CBS MORNINGS; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Weijia Jiang, Matt  Gutman, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Jo Ling Kent, Ramy Inocencio, Skyler Henry, Rob  Marciano, Kelly O`Grady, Jericka Duncan, Carter Evans, Celine Gounder,  Roland Fryer \par \uc2 14,125 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re following a big retreat from The White House on their nearly $2 billion payout fund. Plus, we`ll take you inside a landmark lawsuit against \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  over safety concerns. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener," it is your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The White House backs off what critics call a $1.8 billion slush fund for Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Trump is claiming that the slush fund is dead for now, but Democrats will not stop until it`s well and truly buried.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tensions flare over a growing Ebola outbreak and a quarantine facility just for Americans.\par \par \uc2 MAN: An American White people quarantine facility.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s not done yet. Serena Williams is returning to professional tennis after four years.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN A. SMITH ("The Stephen A. Smith Show" host): I don`t think she`s coming back just for doubles. I think that`s a warm-up act.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Heads up to all Taylor Swift fans. The pop superstar will have a new song in "Toy Story 5" released this Friday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You can`t park there. Oh my God! He opened the door.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A very curious bear was caught red-handed breaking into a U-Haul van, looking for a snack.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: He got what he wanted.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We have a thief.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ZOHRAN MAMDANI (D) (New York City): Who`s ready to watch the \uc2 Knicks\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (CHILDREN answer "me.")\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: New York City mayor, \uc2 Zohran Mamdani\uc2  wants the city`s children to be able to watch the \uc2 Knicks\uc2  in the \uc2 NBA\uc2  final. So he signed an executive order repealing bedtime this Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("The Daily Show" host): By the way, that`s the good news, \uc2 Knicks\uc2  will win -- to follow the bad news, Victor Wembanyama is still growing. As of this morning, he is up to 14-and-a-half feet.\par \par \uc2 If this series goes to seven games, I just don`t know.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait to see Wemby in the Garden.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re not afraid of him though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are not afraid.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re not afraid, Jon. We are not afraid.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what? Wemby is no joke, bro!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And neither are the \uc2 Knicks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you, Nate and Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are not afraid!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we`re going to be talking basketball a little bit later, but let`s begin this morning with some major developments on two major issues for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials say they are backing off a planned $1.8 billion payout fund, which critics said was designed to reward Trump allies. Meanwhile, the latest fallout from Iran saying it is halting all communications with the U.S. about ending the war.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is following all this from The White House for us. Weijia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Vlad. Good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 Iran hasn`t responded to the latest U.S. offer, but President Trump says talks are ongoing, and he remains optimistic for a peace deal.\par \par \uc2 As for that anti-weaponization fund that critics called a slush fund for Trump supporters, in a rare reversal, the President is pausing his plan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: President Trump promoted the so-called anti-weaponization fund as a way to compensate targets of politically motivated prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): These were people that were weaponized and really treated brutally by a system that was so corrupt with corrupt people running it.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But Democrats and several Republicans balked at who might get paid after administration officials refused to rule out all former January 6 defendants as potential beneficiaries.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, House Speaker Mike Johnson met with Trump to talk about the fund amid GOP backlash. Shortly afterward, CBS News learned the administration planned to scrap it, and the Justice Department agreed to comply with a court order temporarily blocking the fund.\par \par \uc2 Democrats vow to take action to make that permanent.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER (D-NY): Trump is claiming that the slush fund is dead for now, but Democrats will not stop until it is well and truly buried and can never see the light of day.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Meanwhile, Trump remains locked in a frustrating stalemate with Iran, which had threatened to break off talks with the U.S. on Monday for what it called ceasefire violations, but in an interview, Trump said Iran hadn`t expressed that to him.\par \par \uc2 He added, "I think we`ve been talking too much, if you want to know the truth," and that "... going silent would be very good," but less than two hours later, on social media, the President insisted talks are continuing at a rapid pace with the Islamic Republic of Iran.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: President Trump said he spoke to Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu and representatives for Hezbollah, and both agreed to stop shooting, but there are already signs overnight that short-lived ceasefire isn`t holding, possibly further complicating fragile negotiations with Iran -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Weijia, should thank you very.\par \par \uc2 It`s primary day across the country, including in California, where some very close races are playing out. The governor`s race, though, is wide open. In Los Angeles, Mayor Karen Bass is facing challenges from both the left and the right, including reality star, Spencer Pratt, who has been endorsed by President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Our chief national correspondent, Matt Gutman, is in L.A. with more on the story. Matt, it went from no way, no how to Spencer Pratt to maybe. The President`s record is pretty good at endorsing, too.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And running neck and neck. Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a jungle primary we have in California, and that means that the top two candidates, regardless of party, advance to November. For the governor, it`s a race to run the fourth largest economy in the world, high stakes, but so far low voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 But, as you mentioned, that`s not the case with the L.A. mayoral race, one of the most fiercely fought races in the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: In Los Angeles, the seat for City Hall is still anyone`s game. There`s incumbent Democratic Mayor Karen Bass --\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KAREN BASS (D) (Los Angeles, California): Nice to be --\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: --who is facing an unlikely challenger, former reality T.V. star and Republican, Spencer Pratt.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re supporting you --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mi casa su casa.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: -- and Councilmember Nithya Raman, a Democratic socialist.\par \par \uc2 NITHYA RAMAN (D) (Los Angeles mayoral candidate): Thank you for your support.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: All fanning out across the city for one final push, as the latest polling shows Bass in a virtual tie with Ramen and Pratt coming in third.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "four more years.")\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Bass is looking to overcome criticism she faced during the 2025 Palisades Fire.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KAREN BASS (D): It was one of the worst moments of my life to not be here when my city needed me.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And one of her most ferocious critics has been Pratt, who told our Adam Yamaguchi it`s what encouraged him to join the race.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER PRATT: I didn`t want to run for mayor, but had to step up, so that my sons one day come back here and live in the L.A. that I lived in. Beautiful, safe. I`m standing in what happened because of failed politicians.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: As for Raman, she is leaning into fighting homelessness.\par \par \uc2 NITHYA RAMAN: They are tired of how high the housing costs are here, how it is driving families out of the city. How our response to our homelessness crisis has been lackluster.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: As for the election of governor here, it`s also a wide open race to replace Governor Gavin Newsom. Xavier Becerra, a former Biden administration official, is in the lead.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA (D) (California gubernatorial candidate): I`m not running against them, I`m running for governor. Let them try to catch me.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Followed by environmental advocate, Tom Steyer.\par \par \uc2 TOM STEYER (D) (California gubernatorial candidate): I am running because Californians can`t afford to live here anymore.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And former Fox News host, Steve Hilton, he`s aiming to change the political direction of the nation`s largest blue state.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HILTON (R) (California gubernatorial candidate): If we don`t get a Republican in the top two, we have no chance for change.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Now, affordability is a huge issue for Californians right now. The latest CBS News poll shows a whopping 70 percent of voters find the cost of living to be unmanageable, and whoever wins will manage a state with a roughly $4 trillion economy and a lot of work ahead of them -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Matt, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Over in Maine, supporters of the Democratic frontrunner in a crucial Senate race are sticking by him. That`s after Graham Platner`s campaign confirmed he sent sexually explicit texts to other women after he got married.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns has reaction from other lawmakers and the voters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ll probably, you know, grit my teeth and vote for him, because you know I`m a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Many voters in Maine tell us Democratic Senate candidate, Graham Platner`s latest controversy will not change their vote.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I probably wasn`t going to vote for him anyway.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I can separate what somebody is doing in their personal life and the mistakes, or whatever, that they`ve done from how they run politically.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I don`t think any differently about him since that story came out.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Platner, a 41-year-old Marine veteran and oyster farmer, is pushing back after his campaign confirmed he sent several women sexually explicit text messages while married.\par \par \uc2 Platner`s wife, Amy Gertner, alerted campaign staff last summer about the messages, worried they could be a liability in the race. Platner has also responded to other controversies in his campaign, including saying he didn`t know a chest tattoo he got while drunk in 2007 was a recognized Nazi symbol, he has had the tattoo covered up.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM PLATNER (D) (Maine senatorial candidate): This far more represents who I am now.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And a now deleted Reddit post that he has since apologized for. He called himself a "communist" and asked, "why don`t Black people tip?"\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats have been hoping Platner could flip the Senate seat long held by Republican Susan Collins, and some are still supporting his campaign.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): Maybe rather than worrying about Graham Platner`s marriage, worry about what`s happening to the working families in this country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And other Democratic senators, like Elizabeth Warren and Ruben Gallego, say that they are standing by Platner, despite these controversies.\par \par \uc2 And Platner will actually be here in Washington today for a previously scheduled meeting with Democratic senators -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Caitlin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to a landmark lawsuit against OpenAI and its CEO, Sam Altman. The state of Florida accuses them of concealing dangers, putting kids at risk, aiding mass shooters, and encouraging self-harm.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent is following all of this. We have some serious charges.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMES UTHMEIER (Florida Attorney General): Sam Altman and ChatGPT have chosen the A.I. race over the safety and security of our kids.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In a scathing statement on Monday, Florida`s Attorney General accused OpenAI and its CEO of deceiving users.\par \par \uc2 JAMES UTHMEIER: People like Sam Altman should not be designing these products to be addictive, to go after kids, and to encourage them to do dangerous things.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The state has filed a civil lawsuit accusing Altman and OpenAI of ignoring warnings about the dangers of its prized product, ChatGPT, saying the company knew the chatbot was capable of advising users on how to commit dangerous acts, but according to the complaint, the company expedited the release of updates and allegedly disregarded safety testing.\par \par \uc2 The suit alleges that ChatGPT has aided and abetted mass shooters, encouraged people to take their own lives, all while collecting data from young people with little parental oversight.\par \par \uc2 The Florida Attorney General referenced numerous incidents, including a deadly shooting at Florida State University last year, where he alleges ChatGPT played a role in helping the suspect prepare for the crime.\par \par \uc2 Although OpenAI is controlled by a nonprofit entity, its business operations are for profit, and Wall Street analysts believe the company is preparing for one of the largest initial public offerings in history, but with the expected IPO comes even more scrutiny, says UCLA Law professor, Julia Powell.\par \par \uc2 JULIA POWELL (Executive Director UCLA Institute for Technology, Law and Policy): The risks are not being managed, and it really deserves a kind of product pipeline where you have verification and proof of safety before these products are released in the world.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In a statement, OpenAI said it has put in place industry- leading protections for young people and has given parents more tools to monitor their use.\par \par \uc2 The company denies that ChatGPT was responsible for the deadly incident at FSU, saying it did not encourage the suspect to do what he allegedly did -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Before you go, OpenAI is expected to go public very soon. Its biggest rival, Anthropic did it first, filing for an IPO yesterday. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Nate, this is big news.\par \par \uc2 Anthropic says it confidentially filed paperwork, becoming one of the first major AI companies to officially go public later this year. It comes just days after a massive funding round that valued the company at nearly a trillion dollars with a T. What`s interesting here is the timing.\par \par \uc2 Anthropic has beat OpenAI in this race to Wall Street. OpenAI widely expected to file for its own IPO as early as today, and of course, SpaceX expected to go public on June 12th in what could be the biggest IPO ever, likely making Elon Musk the first ever trillionaire, a lot of people, a lot of money -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jo Ling, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now to the Ebola outbreak in Central Africa that threatens to become a catastrophe. Doctors on the frontline say they should have learned about this much earlier after reports the virus began spreading months ago.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, locals are protesting U.S. plans to treat affected Americans in Kenya, not in the United States of America. Ramy Inocencio has the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: This is what health workers have been doing every day here in Bunya, the epicenter of this Ebola crisis. Now, at least 321 people have tested positive, and at least four dozen have died. The International Rescue Committee tells CBS News the current outbreak may have been spreading undetected as early as January.\par \par \uc2 But there is hope Ebola can be beat again, as these frontline nurses did. Now declared Ebola-free after they contracted the disease while treating patients.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "Coming out of this illness alive is an indescribable joy," said this medic.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to Dr. Abdou Sebushishe, a frontline medic in the Congo.\par \par \uc2 When do you think this could potentially finally get under control?\par \par \uc2 DR. ABDOU SEBUSHISHE (Senior Global Health Advisor with the International Medical Corps): We believe it can go beyond six months before this outbreak could be put under control.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Would you say that the outbreak is surging faster than the aid and assistance surging in?\par \par \uc2 DR. ABDOU SEBUSHISHE: I think the outbreak is outpacing the current response, and there are lots of adjustments that are being made to ensure that we catch up.\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: To stop it from spreading further out into the world.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. attempting to halt it in Africa and at a quarantine facility in Kenya, two people were killed on Monday in protests against the Trump administration`s attempts of a facility only for Americans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And the International Medical Corps tells us that more testing needs to be done. Some don`t believe Ebola exists, and they`re using home remedies, and contact tracing is only getting about one in four people. There are now three vaccines in development for this strain of Ebola -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 An interstate bus driver faces new charges for a deadly accident in Virginia over the weekend. The crash on I-95 north of Fredericksburg killed five people, including a family of four going to a wedding.\par \par \uc2 Records show the bus company and the driver were involved in other incidents in the past few years.\par \par \uc2 Skyler Henry has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The bus driver involved in this deadly crash is now facing felony charges after a Virginia grand jury indicted him on three counts of manslaughter Monday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The bus was traveling from New York City to Charlotte early Friday morning when it slammed into stopped traffic, killing five and injuring dozens more.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS CHAPMAN (National Transportation Safety Board): It seems fairly clear that if there was any breaking, there wasn`t much, because of the speed and the severity of the collision.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The force of collision led to a harrowing escape.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You have blood -- people bleeding, people screaming, the bus smoking, a stranger came, bust the window open, and let us all out.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The bus was operated by E&P Travel, a company that lists four coaches registered to this Charlotte area apartment complex. Rob Carpenter is a motor coach industry expert.\par \par \uc2 ROB CARPENTER (Motor coach industry expert): This is not a huge bus company, but they are pumping out a lot of miles.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: And as a CBS News investigation found, a spotty safety record. Police records show a similar incident in 2024 when a bus operated by the same company failed to slow and struck a vehicle that injured nine near Greensboro, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 In the past two years, the company has been cited three times for speeding 15 or more miles per hour over the speed limit, and yet two months ago the company received a satisfactory rating from federal regulators.\par \par \uc2 Just because they have a satisfactory rating, that doesn`t exactly mean that it`s safe.\par \par \uc2 ROB CARPENTER: That`s correct. We`ve seen some really egregious companies who have had catastrophic crashes and still maintain a satisfactory rating.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: E&P Travel didn`t respond to a phone call or e-mail.\par \par \uc2 CBS News --\par \par \uc2 And no one was home when we tried to knock on the door.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: In a statement to CBS News, the federal agency that regulates bus safety set by law, safety ratings only include the prior 365 days, and E&P Travels 2024 crash fell outside that window. The driver involved in Friday`s crash was actually set to appear in a Maryland courtroom this week. He`s a 48-year-old New York City man, he was involved in a separate speeding incident while driving an E&P travel bus -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Skyler, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In today`s weather, we are tracking a severe storm threat in the middle of the country. CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is with us.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Look, what happened in Denver yesterday. This isn`t snow piling up, this is hail piling up, they are the size of golf balls, so severe weather in the Denver Metro area. We had winds gusting over 60 to 70 miles an hour, over about a hundred wind reports in Alabama. Today, the threat`s going to shift up to the north, into Rapid City, up through Spearfish Sturges, down through the Badlands, up through Bismarck, and then pushing off towards Sioux Falls.\par \par \uc2 And then the heaviest rain over the next three days will be across West Texas and southeast parts of New Mexico, stretching down I-10 just a little bit as well, but no flash flood threat there.\par \par \uc2 Beautiful weather across the northeast, you feel that low humidity. It`s amazing outside right now. Temperatures will be on the warming trend as we go through Wednesday, Thursday, and look at Friday, temperatures getting up into the lower to mid-90s. That will feel more like summer, so enjoy that, if you like.\par \par \uc2 That`s a check on what`s happening nationally, it is time now for your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, a huge scare for Sabrina Carpenter after a man allegedly tried to force his way into her house.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Serena Williams is back. After four years away from tennis, what she is planning for her big return.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" a major announcement from T. Swift, a brand new song for a major motion picture. Wait until you hear the details.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Stanley Steemer. For floors, furniture, airducts and more. Visit StanleySteemer.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A little Ed Sheeran in the morning, we welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s 7:30 around here. That means one thing. Time to take a look at the stories that "Caught Our Eye." Doing the honors today, Kelly O`Grady on deck.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Hey, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re learning more about the impact of last week`s massive explosion of a Blue Origin rocket. So, Blue Origin CEO revealed that damage to the launchpad wasn`t as extensive as originally feared. He said a lot of critical equipment was "in good shape."\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, NASA partnered with the Jeff Bezos-owned company to help return Americans to the moon in 2028. Despite the setback, NASA believes that timeframe is still possible. Florigen says it will "fly again before the end of this year."\par \par \uc2 Turning now to another stunning explosion, also caught on video. I want you to take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 So, fireworks in a factory in Malta were erupting in flames. Our partners at the BBC report that all the factory`s workers have been accounted for. None were on site at the time, thankfully, but two men working nearby were taken to the hospital with minor injuries. An investigation into the cause of the blast is underway. That footage is absolutely stunning.\par \par \uc2 And shocking video out of Florida, where a speeding car slammed right into a house. So, doorbell cam footage shows two cars colliding, sending one flying into this home. Now, people say three other -- police say three people in the vehicles were transported to the hospital with one in critical condition. CBS News Miami reports no one in the house was hurt, but of course the building is no longer livable because of the damage. No word yet on what caused that crash.\par \par \uc2 And finally, New York City Mayor Zohran Mamdani has issued a new executive order in honor of the Knicks making the NBA finals. Parents, though, might not be happy about it. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ZOHRAN MAMDANI: Bedtime is repealed. All of you can watch the Finals. How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 (CHILDREN answer "glad!")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Now, don`t worry, the repeal on bedtime, it`s only temporary. The order will expire once "The Knicks complete, and hopefully win the historic championship run."\par \par \uc2 The Knicks, of course, take on the Spurs in game one tomorrow night. I didn`t know the Mayor was in charge of bedtime. I should have pulled that line with my parents.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, but, Kelly, all of those little kids are saying, I don`t know what repeal means, but okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, but I am down for it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sounds good!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For them, that means, I don`t have to go to bed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, sounds good! Sounds good.\par \par \uc2 Good order from the Mayor. Thank you, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Tennis great Serena Williams is getting back into the game. Yes, please. We like that. Four years ago, she left the sport that she dominated for more than two decades. Now, the 23 time Grand Slam winner, 23 times, is going to London to play in a warm-up tournament for Wimbledon later this month.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan is here with more.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, this is so exciting. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, so exciting. I had to dress for the occasion. Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 Serena Williams has been busy the past four years, growing her family, focusing on business ventures, and of course appearing in that Super Bowl halftime show, most recently becoming a spokesperson for a weight loss drug. Well, it appears when it comes to tennis, she still has some unfinished business.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Serena will take one final battle.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It turns out Serena Williams never fully retired from competitive tennis. She was just in between sets.\par \par \uc2 Monday, the 23-time Grand Slam winner dropped a video for her 17.8 million Instagram followers confirming her highly anticipated return.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS (Tennis player): I`ve got to change my number.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The 40-four year old mom of two will play under a wild card entry in the doubles bracket of the Queens Club Tournament in London, which begins next week. Williams hasn`t competed since losing in the third round of the 2022 US Open.\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: I`m just so grateful to every single person that`s ever said "Go Serena" in their life. I`m just so grateful because, yes, you got me here.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: At the time, she told "Vogue" Magazine she was "evolving away from tennis" in part to focus on growing her family. A few months later, Gayle King caught up with Williams and her husband, Alexis Ohanian, and asked about a possible comeback.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is the door open just a sliver?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WILLIAMS: It`s hard to say. I would say no for now, no, because it`s just -- I`ve literally given my whole life to tennis, and it`s time for me to give my life to something else.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Whether or not Williams will use the upcoming tournament as a warm-up to Wimbledon, which she`s won seven times, remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER CLAREY (Sports journalist): Serena Williams walking onto the grass at Wimbledon without a racket and waving would attract attention.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re coming back to actually play. I don`t think there`s any question that, because of her stature and the stage in life she`s at right now as an athlete, that they`ll be huge interest in what she does on the court.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Now, this comeback may not come as a total shock for fans who have really been watching. About two weeks ago, Williams` younger daughter appeared in this video where Williams wrote, "Rumor has it, I`ve got a new trainer."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So cute!\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: She is!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her and Little Adira! I am so excited about this.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am as well.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I am, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We just want her to get back on the court and kill it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you feel that, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve always said the hardest thing for the greats, the greats of the game --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It is. Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- to do is to walk away and stay away.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I mean, that`s why she said evolving. I feel like she always left it open.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Even though it was seen as a retirement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Retirement. And even when she said I`d have to say no for now.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right. And she`s such a phenomenal athlete.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she is such a -- yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the thing!\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I mean, the best to ever --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Even, you know she`s a little bit older, but she is still magic on the court.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Don`t count her out!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you look at other athletes like Tom