************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour, what may be another potential benefit of those GLP-1 drugs? Can they reduce the risk of breast cancer? Dr. Celine Gounder is here to sort it all out for us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President Mike Pence is in studio with a lot to say about President Trump and the GOP, why he feels the party has lost its way, and what he wants to do about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Pink is coming up, so you better get this party started. The Grammy winner talks with Gayle about hosting the Tonys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think she is ready. All that and more. The 8:00 hour of CBS MORNINGS starts --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right now!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I know she`s very excited. She`s ready. I just saw the Vice President.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With his wife, Karen, is here. A lot of people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Karen is here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He seems ready, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He seems ready. He`s talking a lot about the conservative movement, he`s got a new book, "What Conservatives Believe." We`re going to talk to him about it. Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we have a fun hour coming up, so stick around for the entirety of it.\par \par \uc2 But we start this hour with Russia launching one of its largest ever attacks on civilian targets in Ukraine. Dozens of missiles and drones hit apartment blocks in the capital and other major cities overnight. At least 18 people were killed, and more than 100 injured.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio has more from London. Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Nate, yes, good morning. There were multiple explosions, barrages of explosions were heard, seen, and felt last night over Kyiv and much of the east of the country. Ukraine`s Air Force said that Russia attacked with 73 missiles and 656 drones. Local media report that more than 30 of those missiles got through.\par \par \uc2 This is Russia`s third heaviest assault in just under a month, and authorities in the capital, Kyiv, warned residents to take cover. You can see them there, people sheltering in the subway system as those strikes hit.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy confirmed that dozens of residential buildings and civilian infrastructure were also damaged, and many people are feared trapped beneath all of that rubble, and the death toll has been continuing to rise over the past few hours.\par \par \uc2 Moscow warned just last week of systemic strikes on Ukraine, and had already told foreign embassy staff to leave Kyiv. For its part, Russia has been criticizing Ukraine for attacks on its own civilians -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it, Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In this morning`s "Health Watch," we`re focusing on a new study of breast cancer and the use of those popular weight loss drugs. Researchers from Penn Medicine found that women taking the GLP-1 drugs like Wegovy and Zepbound were less likely to be diagnosed with breast cancer. Now that sounds great, but we`re hearing now there`s so much more to it.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in our medical correspondent, Dr. Celine Gounder. She`s editor- at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Celine.\par \par \uc2 When I read this this morning, I thought, this is really exciting, but I hear you`re going, wait, not so fast, not so fast.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So, Penn Medicine looked at health records from over a hundred thousand women who had mammograms and other breast imaging. These hundred thousand or more women were overweight, and about 15,000 of them were on GLP-1 drugs, and you`ll hear this number: 30 percent lower risk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Now, so that sounds big until you look at the actual numbers. So, among women not on the drug, 2.3 percent were diagnosed with breast cancer; among those who were on the drug, 1.6 percent so it`s a difference of less than one percent. So the math here matters.\par \par \uc2 And so you know, I don`t want people running home and thinking, "Oh, I need to be on a GLP-1 to prevent breast cancer." That is not the message at all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But can we say that the drugs caused a difference in a positive way?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: We don`t know, so the women were not randomly assigned to the drug. They did not take into account many other factors that we know are predictive of whether somebody develops breast cancer or not. Some of that -- socioeconomic status, exercise, drinking, family history, genetic risk, really important one. What other drugs they were on?\par \par \uc2 So any of those other factors could actually explain why the group that were on the GLP-1s had a little bit lower rate of cancer. So, this is really a starting point to be asking questions, not definitive proof.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And yet these drugs are all over social media. I mean, it has blown up. This is a big conversation, people.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is a really big conversation, and this -- I think what you tend to see on social media is extremes, miracle drug or oh, GLP- 1s are unnatural, they`re not good for you.\par \par \uc2 And I do want to call out, there was one oncologist from Stanford who posted on Instagram, I thought this was a really balanced comment, she said, none of this data suggests people who do not have weight or endocrine issues should be taking GLP-1 drugs, and yet she was speaking optimistically about some of the results.\par \par \uc2 Again, it`s not a miracle drug. This is not necessarily bad for you. We just need to wait for more data.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Adding some much-needed perspective.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, is the classic teen summer job disappearing? That`s the question. We`ll find out why teenagers are opting out and what they`re doing instead.\par \par \uc2 Plus, former Vice President Mike Pence is sharply critical of President Trump. Pence will be here to explain why he believes the Republican Party needs to change course, change it now.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In case you missed it on CBS MORNINGS Deals, we showed you the HigherDOSE Red Light Therapy hat that may help with hair growth.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD (Knockings lifestyle expert): These hats are so comfortable, but that`s not what they`re for. Do you know about red light therapy?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tell me.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: Okay, so it`s like a charger for your hair follicle, and it ignites the hair follicle to promote hair growth and thicken your hair, so it has this little chargeable piece. Clip, pops right on, put the hat on, go for a walk.\par \par \uc2 This red light therapy is going to target your hair follicle while you`re doing whatever you want, just 10 to 20 minutes a day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And with our exclusive discount, you can get it for 25 percent off the retail price. To shop for that and other items, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Summer is right around the corner, and that means that many teens are looking for jobs, but a new report finds that teens getting those jobs is expected to fall to its lowest level in nearly 80 years.\par \par \uc2 Last year, just over 800,000 teens had paid summer jobs, that`s down 25 percent from the summer before. So, what is going on with this decline?\par \par \uc2 Harvard economist and CBS News contributor, Roland Fryer joins us now. How you doing, man?\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: Good. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so you say this isn`t a new problem, that the teen job market has been declining for decades. So, what is the deal?\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: I don`t know. People are making a big deal about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you for coming. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: People are making a big deal about it. Look, the issue is not that the jobs have walked away from the teens, but the teens have walked away from the jobs, because doing other things in the summer has just gotten a lot more valuable.\par \par \uc2 The research lab, the SAT tutor, et cetera, and the premium to go into college, meaning how much more you earn if you go to college relative to high school has doubled over the last 40 years. So, look, teens are making rational decisions to do things that are more valuable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, how much does family income factor in it --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was going to say --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --when you say that they`re walking away from jobs. Because I think it`s very -- I had a summer job.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I had a summer job because that`s how I made -- that`s how I paid for stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I made sure my kids had summer jobs, too. How much does family income factor into all of this?\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: It`s a huge problem, and I, of course, had a summer job. I got fired from it because I was throwing tight spirals in Albertsons in the toy aisle, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a whole -- you`ve got a whole another side.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, surprised with that story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: But look, the teens that are most likely to walk away from these jobs are the affluent teens, because they can afford to have an unpaid internship in an interesting lab.\par \par \uc2 My worry is that kids like I grew up, who come from those types of incomes, the lower-income kids, because when they don`t have summer jobs, research from places like Harvard and Chicago tell us that they actually have a higher probability of death, 20 percent more likely to have to -- for something to happen to them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because they`re getting into the wrong things.\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: They are getting to the wrong things, and that`s why I had that summer job, for two reasons: One, I want to put a little money in my pocket.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: Two, frankly, it kept me out of trouble in the afternoons --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: -- from the things that my peers were doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how tight is the job market for the teens that you said are not -- that don`t come from affluent families.\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: Yes, for kids in inner cities, Gayle, the labor market is quite tight. I mean, just here in New York City last year, they had 200,000 applications for a hundred thousand jobs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People are -- go ahead. Go ahead, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, I was going to say that, you know, the teens who work drive the economy as well. I mean, especially during the summer, businesses rely on teen -- young people working in ice cream shops and hotels.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I worked at the mall during the summer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: During Christmas break.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The pools and beaches.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pools and beaches. So, there is an economic -- overall economic issue here as well.\par \par \uc2 ROLAND FRYER: Yes, there is, and it`s both teens and, frankly, i