, let`s start with the topic. Let`s go with the news of the day\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Personal values, multiple candidates are running for office. You`ve got Democrats like Graham Platner, Republican Ken Paxton in Texas, they`re facing questions about their personal lives, things like infidelity. Do you believe issues like adultery should be disqualifying for a candidate? And this is about the people that are running today, not -- we are not looking in the past.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, first off, thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thanks for coming.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: You know, I write about the principles that have defined the conservative movement for the last 50 years, and certainly the issues of integrity and character have been embraced by our movement through the years, and they seem less significant today.\par \par \uc2 But the reason I wrote the book is because -- for all these decades the Republican Party has been defined by a conservative agenda, a strong defense of limited government, traditional moral values, including character, they battled against the progressive left, which, as we speak, seems to go farther and farther toward the progressive agenda, and even a socialist agenda.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the reasons President Trump came to power in 2016 and has such enduring popularity is because the way he`s fought the progressive left\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: But the reason I wrote the book, Vlad, is because what I want Republicans to know is that there`s a new threat to that conservative agenda from a populist right that would -- that would walk away from those timeless values, that would embrace big government solutions, protectionism and isolationists questioning our allies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I want to ask you about that. I want to ask you about that. I want to ask you about that, but I just want to get 