ent off today. This is like, get one for your car, get one for your husband.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tuck it away.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN: One for your closet. Everyone is going to want to steal these, and there are tons of colors on CBSDeals.com, so definitely go see, get the one you want. There`s a cloud one, too.\par \par \uc2 Now we have to clean up, okay, this is the Zippi, you just turn it on, and it`s going to get all of those chunky things. I have a senior dog whose dog food goes all over the kitchen, and even my highest power to the wall vacuum won`t get her dog food. This is getting everything.\par \par \uc2 It is going to give you the versatility of getting in all those corners, because of the shape, light on the front, and then when you`re done, check this out, turn it off, and then you just go over the trash can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN: And --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s that easy.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN: It`s that easy, and then you`re done. Put it away, it`s good to go. You are saving $60.00 today. Get the Zippi.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Ash, thank you so much. Now we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw, scan the Q.R. code on your screen, or visit CBSDeals.com\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with Pink.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Raise Your Glass"/Pink/LaFace Records)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that is Pink`s song, "Raise Your Glass." We remember it, featured in "Moulin Rouge: The Musical." It`s one of two Pink songs featured in Broadway productions.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the other is in the musical "And Juliet." I know that one, too.\par \par \uc2 PINK (Singer): That`s a good show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know that one, too.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Grammy Award-winning entertainer, known for singing while performing. First time I saw this, my jaw was on the frickin` floor -- aerial acrobatics -- is hosting the seventh -- look at her, the 79th Annual Tony Awards this Sunday on CBS, and so we are so happy that she`s joining us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Hi!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did I mention we are so happy? Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When I heard you were doing this, I thought, lucky us that you agreed to host the Tonys.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So when they came out to you, I`m assuming it was immediate yes, was it?\par \par \uc2 PINK: It was an almost immediate yes. It was a yes, but or yes, and let me get permission from Willow, because this is her lane. I try to stay out of her lane. She`s all about Broadway. She goes to Performing Arts High School, and she was like, "Do I get a seat?" I was like, "I think so." So, here we go.\par \par \uc2 Also, it`s -- yes, I`m in a yes place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does this mean, I`m in a yes place? What does that mean exactly?\par \par \uc2 PINK: I`ve always just been a touring artist. I tour, I put my head down, and I beat the pavement, and I do it all over the world and back again a thousand times, and I don`t really do a lot of other things.\par \par \uc2 And this year was, I was like, you know what, we`re following Willow to New York City.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: I`m going to just say yes to things that I wouldn`t normally say yes to.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talk about the decision, I love that you guys have done this, to follow Willow to New York City. Why? What`s her dream?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Her dream is to be on Broadway. She is musical theater obsessed. She has followed me around the world her whole life, on and off tour. And I told her, it`s your turn, we`ll follow you, and that`s what we do for our kids. We show up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: If we can, if we can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: And I`m lucky that we can. And she`s phenomenal. And I would tell -- she`s very talented, and she`s also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you are just saying that because you`re her mother.\par \par \uc2 PINK: No, she`s no -- I would be -- I would be like, you can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you say that if --\par \par \uc2 PINK: I would say it in a nicer way? But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How would you say it?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Maybe! Also science.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You know Dentistry is good.\par \par \uc2 PINK: She`s more talented than I ever was at her age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where were you guys watching the Tonys last year?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Last year, we were at home, Willow was watching the Tonys on television from the coffee table. She stood up on the coffee table, she was dressed in her Gatsby costume that she designed herself on tour, which is four quadrants, the four main characters of Great Gatsby. It`s adorable, and she was just bright-eyed and bushy-tailed, and now she`ll be in the seat watching.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes watching mom.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Making fun of her mama.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Watching mom, also watching the show. What are you most excited about? Because number one everybody judges from the opening number, Pink.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you have plans for that?\par \par \uc2 PINK: It is goofy and over the top, and it`s sort of a nod back, it`ll all make sense, and there`s going to be 170 people on stage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: It`s going to be so fun, and I`m an idiot from the very first moment, which was very important to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To make fun of yourself?\par \par \uc2 PINK: To make fun of myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why -- why did you want to do that?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Because I haven`t been on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 PINK: And I wanted to just speak to the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You want people to know, I know I haven`t been on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 PINK: I know, I know I haven`t been here. I know that I`m an absolute idiot. And my whole point this year is to celebrate all of you, and we just - we`re just going to have fun. We`re going to have so much fun. I`m excited!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am, too. I am, too. What are you most looking forward to of the evening? Because you know they always think there`s got to be surprises. They always think there has got to be --\par \par \uc2 PINK: There are so many surprises\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- that`s unexpected.\par \par \uc2 PINK: There`s a lot of really cool anniversaries. "The Book of Mormon," and there`s "Chorus Line." The "In Memoriam" is going to be amazing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because whoever is doing that, that will be good\par \par \uc2 PINK: And also all the shows that are on this year, it is going to be a really fun year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love Broadway, I love a good musical, I love the it can transform you and take you to a totally different place, and all the kids, just think of all the kids, like your daughter, who was sitting there dreaming of one day, I want to do this.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, you know, she gets this opportunity that you know, a lot of people think I would kill to do this.\par \par \uc2 PINK: I want theater to be available for every kid, because I think more than most things are our art classes, and our theater classes are the first things being stripped from our schools, and I find those to be the only places in towns and cities where kids can be goofy together, and it`s -- and they can be in a safe space, and they can be fully themselves, or they can be fully something else other than themselves, and they come together as this group of supportive, nobody`s a weirdo, and it`s just the most wholesome, beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 I want it for every single kid, and that`s again why Broadway is always -- theater has always been really important to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you just say, will there be any acrobatic moments?\par \par \uc2 PINK: There will be acrobatic moments, but not in the way that you would normally see me doing acrobatic moments.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, I don`t know what that means.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Neither do I yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know what that means.\par \par \uc2 PINK: But I know there is a harness or two.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh boy! I don`t know what that means. Just very excited, cheering you on. I can`t wait for Sunday.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Me, too!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I cannot wait.\par \par \uc2 PINK: It`s going to be awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I cannot.\par \par \uc2 You can watch Pink host the 79th Annual Tony Awards live this Sunday. Tune in at 8:00 Eastern, 5:00 Pacific, right here in streaming on where, Pink?\par \par \uc2 PINK: CBS and Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260602em6200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 U.S. and Iran Trade Shots as Ceasefire is Tested; Iran War`s Impact on Economy and Politics; Spirit Spent $100 Million on Extra Fuel Costs; New Look at United Crash Damage; Gunman Opens Fire Blocks from White House; Blake Lively and Justin Baldoni Settle Dispute; Quarantined Cruise Ship Heading to Spain; Deadly Fireworks Factory Explosion in China; Supreme Court Restores Abortion Pill Access; Britney Spears Guilty Plea; Delta Ending Some In-Flight Snacks; The Glitz and Glam of the Met Gala; Dolly Parton Cancels Las Vegas Residency; Justice Neil Gorsuch on State of the Supreme Court; Designer Zac Posen on Glamour of the Met Gala; How Gen Z Women`s Anxieties are Being Amplified; Revealing Nominees for the Tony Awards; Oprah Selects "John of John" by Douglas Stuart; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Holly Williams, Ed  O`Keefe, Kris Van Cleave, Nicole Sganga, Jericka Duncan, Celine Gounder,  Adriana Diaz, Jan Crawford, Tom Hanson, Rob Marciano, Kelly O`Grady, Meg  Oliver \par \uc2 14,578 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on this Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, the ceasefire in the war with Iran looks even more shaky today, plus we`ve got all the big looks from last night`s Met Gala.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and Iran exchange fire as the military launches a new operation to escort ships through the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Are you worried that this actually may cause the cease fire to collapse?\par \par \uc2 RAMAN KAPOOR (Indian oil tanker captain): Yes, anything could go wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: An American stranded on a cruise ship hit by deadly viral outbreak tells his story to the world.\par \par \uc2 JAKE ROSMARIN (Travel blogger): There`s a lot of uncertainty, and that`s the hardest part.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dolly Parton cancels her Las Vegas residency, but says she`s doing really well.\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON (Singer): Hopefully, I`ll be up to snuff again soon. Maybe I shouldn`t have said snuff.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Britney Spears has accepted a plea deal in her driving under the influence case. DUI charges were reduced to reckless driving.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Fashion`s biggest night did not disappoint.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: The stars were out for the Met Gala.\par \par \uc2 BEYONCE (Singer): Juicy, curvy, being tall, whatever, just celebrating whoever, whatever God gave you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, listen, I can`t eat a raisin, but I am in the dress.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): If you had a Spirit flight booked over the weekend, good news you were upgraded to not having one.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON ("The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon" host): When they heard \uc2 Frontier\uc2  was like, oh no, are we the worst airline now?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Writing to our guests, all flights have been cancelled and customer service is no longer available, as if it ever was to begin with.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Which, coincidentally, was also Spirit`s motto. One more for the road, baby.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you put it that way, all flights cancelled and no customer service.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. It`s perfect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good luck.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good luck.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with concerns that the ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran could be on the brink of collapse.\par \par \uc2 Both sides, as you may have heard, exchanged fire, Monday, when the U.S. began a new mission to escort commercial ships through the Strait of Hormuz. Iran also attacked an energy facility in the United Arab Emirates. You can see the aftermath of that right here.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has the latest. She is in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. military has come under fire as it works to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, the narrow waterway that normally carries around 20 percent of the world`s oil, but which Iran effectively shut down after the U.S. initiated a war back in February.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): So we did a little detour, and it`s working out very nicely.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: President Trump maintained yesterday that the conflict with Iran is going very well.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They have no Navy, they have no Air Force, they have no anti-aircraft equipment.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Yet, Iran state media claimed this video showed the Iranian Navy firing warning shots at American destroyers. CBS News cannot independently verify it.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Central Command said two American flagged commercial ships passed through the Strait of Hormuz, protected by an air defense umbrella, including warships, helicopters and fighter jets. The U.S. military said it shot down Iranian cruise missiles and drones and sank six Iranian small boats, all used to try to disrupt the mission.\par \par \uc2 On social media, Sunday, Trump called the initiative to reopen the Strait a humanitarian gesture.\par \par \uc2 With around 2,000 ships stranded and food on board, some of them running short, but ending the Iranian blockade would also strip Iran`s leaders of their most powerful bargaining chip.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN RAMAN KAPOOR: Ever since the war started, we have witnessed maybe more than 200 missiles over our head.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Raman Kapoor is the captain of an oil tanker stranded in the Persian Gulf since the war began in February.\par \par \uc2 It must feel as if your fate is not in your own hands.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN RAMAN KAPOOR: Not at all. As a Captain, it`s my responsibility to keep the ship and its crew safe all the time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: President Trump warned in an interview yesterday that Iran would be blown off the face of the Earth if it attacks U.S. ships in the Strait of Hormuz. A senior Iranian official posted on social media this morning that Iran is "just getting started" -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Holly Williams in Israel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, the fallout from the war continues to grow. Gas prices are up to $4.48 a gallon on average, and just six months before the midterms, voters are paying close attention.\par \par \uc2 Vice President J.D. Vance heads to Iowa today, a key state for control of Congress. Ed O`Keefe is in Des Moines.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Vlad, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Now, vice presidents don`t come to early presidential caucus states by accident, and that`s a big part of the reason why we`re here. But before worrying about J.D. Vance`s future, he and The White House have to worry about current opposition to the war and how it`s driving up prices at the pump.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Having the gas at this price, it puts people more into struggle to pay even rent, paying for car, for food, it adds up, you know?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Gas is now just over $4.00 a gallon here in Iowa, but prices have climbed across the Midwest in recent days, like $4.83 in Michigan and $4.78 in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 It is sending President Trump`s approval ratings falling.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I don`t hear them talking about what they`re actually going to do to make the gas prices go down.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On Monday, the President insisted again, the high prices are temporary.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I see it going down very substantially when this is over.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Today, Vice President J.D. Vance is headed here to Iowa, where competitive races in at least three congressional districts this fall could decide control of the House of Representatives. Both parties believe this state once again up for grabs because of the economy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Where are we economically? Where`s the farmer?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The farmer, especially critical to the Iowa economy is hurting.\par \par \uc2 So how`s business?\par \par \uc2 AARON LEHMAN (Iowa Farmers Union president): Business is rough. Almost every farmer in Iowa will tell you that they`ve had difficult times.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Farmers like Aaron Lehman have taken several hits. First, tariffs shrunk global markets for Iowa corn, soybeans and livestock. Then the war with Iran caused the price of diesel and fertilizer to jump.\par \par \uc2 You`re getting hit in all sorts of ways.\par \par \uc2 AARON LEHMAN: All of it`s happening at the same time, and we`re not responding to it well.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Lehman says recent tariff relief checks helped, but they weren`t enough to cover rising costs.\par \par \uc2 So when this White House says it`s the best friend a farmer has ever had, you say --\par \par \uc2 AARON LEHMAN: That doesn`t say much about my friends, because people are out here hurting.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Aaron Lehman told us the President`s rhetoric about this being the Golden Age of agriculture doesn`t line up with reality here on the ground. He says he wants lawmakers in this state and other Midwestern farm states to speak up for farmers, especially with the elections approaching - - Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the fallout over the collapse of Spirit Airlines. This morning, we are learning new details about how the deal to save Spirit fell through, and how other airlines are stepping up to help.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Hundreds of former Spirit employees gathered outside the company`s Florida headquarters to remember the airline many saw as more than just a job.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re all experiencing, I think what feels like a death, you know?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Flight attendant, Colleen Burns, worked at Spirit for a decade.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN BURNS (Spirit Airlines flight attendant): We all feel like our wings were clipped, and even though I`m sure we`ll land other places, it`s not going to be the same.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Newly filed court documents reveal Spirit management learned last Thursday, the $500 million government lifeline was off the table. The cash strapped airline had already burned through $100 million in additional fuel costs since March 1st, as the Iran war sent jet fuel prices soaring.\par \par \uc2 HENRY HARTEVELDT (Atmosphere Research Group travel industry analyst): Spirit was a no-win situation. It just had, unfortunately, not enough time to turn itself around,\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: While the Spirit counter at Fort Lauderdale Airport was a ghost town, Monday, JetBlue announced 11 new routes from the airport, all previously served by Spirit, but in Atlantic City, without Spirit, there is no longer daily air service.\par \par \uc2 Allegiant offers some flights, and Breeze says it is adding several former Spirit destinations in the coming months, but only one will be daily by the fall.\par \par \uc2 For the time being, airport director, Tim Kroll says customers will have to adjust their travel plans.\par \par \uc2 TIM KROLL (Airport Director): Significant impact right now. Allegiant does hit a lot of those markets, but not all of them, and the frequency isn`t there, so short term, unfortunately, they`ll probably going to have to choose another airport.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Spirit was about 85 percent of the Atlantic City Airport`s business, they`re working to add new airlines. The Department of Transportation says the nation`s four biggest carriers have helped nearly 100,000 Spirit flyers get home since Saturday -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Kris, you`re at Newark also covering the United Airlines flight that hit that truck. What`s the latest on that investigation?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: An NTSB investigator was here at the airport yesterday to look at the plane and speak to the flight crew. We`re getting a closer look at the damage to the aircraft. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 You`ll see a gash in the side of the airplane. That is a hole that was opened up from the impact, either with the light pole or that big rig. We also got a look at one of the landing gear wheels. You can see there`s a mark there, as well as the front of the semi, we can see the windshield is busted out. The driver of that vehicle, by the way, is out of the hospital. He is home recovering.\par \par \uc2 The NTSB plans to look at everything from weather to pilot decision making to the runway and air traffic control -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Kris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In Washington, a shooting near The White House on Monday has police wondering if it was another potential attempt on the President`s life.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sganga reports on the investigation after a gunman opened fire on Secret Service personnel in broad daylight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Go! Go! Go!\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The White House briefly went under lockdown after a suspect allegedly shot at Secret Service just four blocks away.\par \par \uc2 Here by the Washington Monument, the Secret Service said officers spotted a gun and tried to stop a man who allegedly fled and fired in the direction of the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW QUINN (U.S. Secret Service Deputy Director): Not long before this shooting occurred, the Vice President`s motorcade did transit through this area.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Officers returned fire, hitting the suspect, a young bystander also was struck in the crossfire.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW QUINN: The juvenile did not sustain any life threatening injuries, but he is also receiving treatment at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The shooting happened just nine days after an alleged assassination attempt at The White House Correspondents` Dinner, but Secret Service said in a statement, "... there is currently no known nexus between the incident and The White House."\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW QUINN: We`re patrolling this area and every site we do, 24/7, hard core, whether or not it was directed to the President or not, I don`t know, but we will find out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The suspect has not yet been publicly identified, but as federal investigators search for a possible motive here, law enforcement sources tell CBS News, we could see charges filed as soon as today, including multiple offenses like weapons violations and assault on a federal law enforcement officer -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to a surprise settlement in a very bitter Hollywood battle, Justin Baldoni and Blake Lively were set to go to court in just two weeks. They made a deal after a judge dismissed her claim that he sexually harassed her while making the movie, "It Ends With Us."\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan is here with all the details. Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this was always a possibility. Ever since "It Ends With Us" hit the box office a year-and-a-half ago, the two have been locked in a bitter he said-she said battle that`s played out in the press and social media.\par \par \uc2 Well now, their multi-million dollar legal battle is finally over.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Actor, Blake Lively graced the steps of the Met Gala Monday night, the same day she settled her high profile legal battle with her, "It Ends With Us" co-star and director, Justin Baldoni.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The terms of the settlement were not disclosed. In December of 2024 shortly after the release of the film, Lively, filed a complaint accusing Baldoni and the production company of sexual harassment and retaliation.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In it, she alleged Baldoni launched a smear campaign against her and conspired with publicists to destroy her reputation after she privately accused him of sexually harassing her on the movie set.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Baldoni denied the claims against him and his production company, clapping back with a defamation lawsuit against lively and her husband, actor, Ryan Reynolds, which was dismissed last June.\par \par \uc2 In April, a federal judge tossed out Lively`s sexual harassment claims against Baldoni, but left intact two retaliation claims which would have let a jury hear her other allegations.\par \par \uc2 CBS News legal contributor, Caroline Polisi.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: That`s probably one of the biggest driving factors of the decision for a settlement in this case, I would think on Lively`s side, remember, this case is no longer about alleged sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Attorneys on both sides released a joint statement Monday, saying, in part, "... we recognize concerns raised by Miss Lively deserve to be heard. We remain firmly committed to workplaces free of improprieties."\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: By all accounts, this is a huge win for Justin Baldoni. Blake Lively, I personally don`t feel would have settled this case had she believed that she was going to have, you know, a good chance of prevailing in court.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And in that joint statement, both sides also say they are looking to put the drama behind them and refocus on the movie`s message of reclaiming strength for survivors of domestic violence -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jericka, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A cruise ship quarantined off West Africa is now expected to go to Spain`s Canary Islands after two people with the dangerous hantavirus are airlifted off the ship. Three passengers have died.\par \par \uc2 Health officials now say the illness may have spread between passengers.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson has more from an American on board.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAKE ROSMARIN: We`re not just a story, we`re not just headlines, we`re people -- people with families, with lives.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Jake Rosmarin is one of 150 passengers, including more than a dozen Americans stuck on board the MV Hondius cruise ship. This morning, the World Health Organization said the ship will continue to Spain`s Canary Islands after two passengers with the dangerous hantavirus are medically evacuated.\par \par \uc2 The cruise ship is currently off the coast of the West African nation of Cape Verde.\par \par \uc2 JAKE ROSMARIN: All we want right now is to feel safe, to have clarity and to get home.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Seven passengers are believed to have gotten hantavirus. Three have died, and one person who is critically ill has already been hospitalized. The crew set sail from Argentina more than four weeks ago on a journey to remote locations, including a visit to Antarctica, but the suspected hantavirus outbreak, which is typically spread through contract with rodents, has left them stranded.\par \par \uc2 MARIA KERKHOVE (WHO Director of Epidemic and Pandemic Preparedness): They were doing, you know, a lot of things with wildlife, so our assumption is they were infected off the boat and then joined the cruise.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Tom Hanson.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so let`s talk about this with medical correspondent, Dr. Celine Gounder. She is also editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So if people aren`t familiar, this is the same virus that killed Gene Hackman and his wife. So what is it exactly, and how can you become infected?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So hantavirus is a family of viruses that infect rodents, so rats and mice, you get infected, usually by breathing dust that has been contaminated by rodent droppings or urine.\par \par \uc2 I think I need to emphasize, this is exceedingly rare. There are fewer than 900 cases over 30 years in the United States. Now, we know there have been at least three deaths related to this particular cluster, as we said, two people airlifted, at least one person in the ICU in South Africa, and there are additional people who are currently being tested.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it spreads from person to person?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, rarely. So there`s a particular form of hantavirus called Andes virus, which spreads in Argentina and Chile, and so this cruise ship took off from Argentina, which is why we`re wondering, is it the usual kind, or is it a kind that can be transmitted person to person?\par \par \uc2 Now, the biggest outbreak of that was in 2018 in Argentina, involving 34 cases, 11 people died. So it`s quite a high case fatality rate. But I think it`s important again, to emphasize that this is a virus that requires prolonged close contact. This is not a pandemic kind of virus.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How can you protect yourself from it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So cleaning, if you`re going to be cleaning droppings, other kinds of things related to rodent contamination, you want to have a respirator, gloves. You don`t want to be sweeping or vacuuming, because that can kick up the dust.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dust!\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Use watered down bleach to help it settle. They`re also going to have to investigate the cruise ship. What`s the source? Who -- you know, getting the testing done. How was it transmitted?\par \par \uc2 And this is really the work of public health agencies like the CDC that`s been gutted, and we`ve pulled out the WHO so we`re a little bit, you know, caught flatfooted here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha! Dr. Gounder, thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s check the weather now, including damaging storms in several parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is watching those storms and today`s threats in the South.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we`ve got a couple fronts out there. This one brought to over 60 mile an hour winds to parts of Michigan. It`s going to be bumping into some pretty warm air here across the Northeast, but the main player is going to be here, Texarkana to Little Rock, Jonesborough on to Memphis. This is later on tonight through the overnight. We could see big time hail, damaging wind gusts, and the possibility and certainly threat for tornadoes, not just today, but tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Backside of this, by the way, winter storm warnings are up for Denver. You`ve got four to eight inches on the way there tonight and tomorrow, but here we go tomorrow, across Shreveport, Monroe, getting up through Jackson and through Birmingham, Alabama. These two will have all hazards in play as it heads off towards the north and east.\par \par \uc2 Behind it, 80s across the Northeast, enjoy that and then big drop off behind this front.\par \par \uc2 That`s a wrap of what`s happening nationally, time now for your local weather,\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, the most fashionable night of the year. We will have all the highlights from last night`s Met Gala.\par \par \uc2 Superstar designer, Zac Posen will be here win an exclusive look at a dress he designed for a major A-Lister!\par \par \uc2 Plus, prayers for Dolly Parton after she cancels big plans due to her health -- what she is telling fans!\par \par \uc2 And an update on Britney Spears after her DUI arrest. We will tell you what happened in court.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Ashley. Inspiring the love of home!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It is 7:30, which means around here, time for the stories that caught our eye.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady doing the honors this morning.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Kelly!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Hey, good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are going to begin this morning with a devastating explosion at a fireworks plant in China. This happened Monday afternoon. Footage that you see there, it shows massive clouds of smoke coming up from the rooms and extensive damage to multiple buildings.\par \par \uc2 Now, the blast left at least 26 people dead and dozens of others injured. State media reports Chinese President Xi Jinping has called for an investigation to determine who should be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 And the Supreme Court has restored the ability to obtain the abortion pill mifepristone through the mail, at least for now. So on Monday, Justice Samuel Alito temporarily paused a lower court`s order blocking access to the drug via telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 This comes after the state of Louisiana sued the FDA. The state claimed the drug`s availability by mail circumvented its near total ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 The pause is set to last until May 11th, as the Supreme Court takes a closer look at the case.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Britney Spears has pleaded guilty to charges of reckless driving involving alcohol and drugs. This comes after her arrest back in March on suspicion of a DUI.\par \par \uc2 The pop star did not appear in court during Monday`s arraignment, but an attorney entered the plea on her behalf.\par \par \uc2 Spears voluntarily checked into rehab last month. As part of the plea, she was placed on 12 months` probation, order to pay a fine and complete a three-month DUI program.\par \par \uc2 And finally, if you`ve got a flight booked on Delta, you might want to grab something to eat before you board. So beginning May 19th, the airline is eliminating snacks, drinks and in-flight meals on flights under 350 miles. That affects about 450 flights a day.\par \par \uc2 Delta told CBS News, it is "creating a more consistent experience across our network." If you`re flying up in front in first class, though you`re in luck, the carrier says those customers will still receive full service.\par \par \uc2 I just want my Biscoff cookie, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come on!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We protest. We protest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, come on!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How can you create a more consistent experience?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right! Right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think they should get rid of the hot towels and stuff in first class. That could cut down costs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we like the peanuts and the snacks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the drink.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, all of a sudden --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who can we call?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: BYOG -- bring your own goodies. Come on, Delta.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to fashion, nothing, and I mean nothing is bigger than the Met Gala. You know, they call it the Super Bowl of fashion.\par \par \uc2 Last night`s event benefiting the Metropolitan Museum of Arts Costume Institute drew a who`s-who of guests, I am talking about Beyonce, Madonna, Cher, Rihanna and even some famous faces with last names, too. Who`s that? I can`t see. Who`s that, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know. He looks good!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Bad Bunny!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I know that`s Bad Bunny! Who is that with the black wavy thing? Who is that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve got to find out!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This year`s dress code -- who is it, Brian?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN: Madonna?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was Madonna.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s Doechii.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s Doechii.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Doechii.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This year`s dress code was Fashion is Art.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Saturday Morning" co-host, Adriana Diaz was there on the carpet looking lovely. She`s got the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: At fashion`s biggest party of the year, fans were treated to some of the loudest wardrobe choices of the season, worn by some of entertainment`s biggest stars.\par \par \uc2 DWAYNE "THE ROCK" JOHNSON (Actor): What I`m most excited about is exactly what`s happening right now, which is just this iconic walk in these stairs. It is cool! It`s our first.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You always knock out of the park. How did it feel tonight? Does it feel different because it`s the Met Gala?\par \par \uc2 DOECHII (Singer): I think that it does feel different, because I think that this is -- this is artist night. We represent culture, our voices. We speak for this art and we speak for this craft.\par \par \uc2 This is like the Super Bowl of fashion, girl. We`re here. We`re here.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The modern Met Gala, as we know it uses the 60 or so steps to be the event before the event, because the fundraiser inside is closed to press, no cameras, no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 "Vogue" editor, Anna Wintour has been throwing this party since the early 90s. This year, it`s being co-chaired by Nicole Kidman, Venus Williams and Beyonce, who arrived in a skeleton inspired look, appearing at her first Met Gala in a decade alongside her husband, Jay-Z and 14-year-old daughter, Blue Ivy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Beautiful smiles! Beautiful family!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What are you most thrilled about? Who have you decided to sit with?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beyonce!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Our own Gayle King dazzled in a flowing golden gown. Monday night`s dress code, Fashion Is Art left plenty of. Room for interpretation.\par \par \uc2 I see you`re projecting technology and you want -- you`re talking about how technology is taking over nature. Is that what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 JANELLE MONAE (Actor): That`s how you see it? Well, this is great! This is great.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Many were unrecognizable, like Heidi Klum, who transformed into a living sculpture and Bad Bunny who aged himself.\par \par \uc2 Met Costume Institute curator, Andrew Bolton.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW BOLTON (Met Costume Institute curator): The idea is that we actually look through fashion -- through the lens of fashion, rather than the way around.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s extraordinary seeing everyone here this evening and how they`ve interpreted the theme.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Which emoji describes how you feel?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m in Atlanta. Georgia peach. I`m a peach, and I`m giving peach. So, yes, this is cute. I love it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You look great!\par \par \uc2 MAN: This guy!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The head explosion guy? Is that how feel?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Absolutely!\par \par \uc2 TEYANA TAYLOR (Actor): Easy! Something like a peach!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes!\par \par \uc2 TEYANA TAYLOR: A lot like my booty look good!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Lauren Sanchez Bezos did walk the Met steps, but her billionaire husband, Jeff Bezos did not. The two were Honorary Chairs of the gala and faced backlash over their involvement.\par \par \uc2 Outside, protesters gathered in colorful outfits holding letters that spelled out "Tax The Rich." We saw one protester who tried to rush the red carpet get detained.\par \par \uc2 People who feel like things like this are inaccessible, why are events like this important?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION (Actor): To pay for the programs that allow art to be more accessible. So much of where we come from and how we got here is because somebody else gave a damn.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh, it was so amazing. It was also announced before the start of last night`s event that the Gala had raised a record $42 million breaking last year`s previous record of $31 million.\par \par \uc2 How was it, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was fantastic. But I think you make such a good point. That the whole purpose, I know it`s Lights, Camera Action. You see all these rich people in great clothes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see that. I get it. But the main point is to raise money to keep the Costume Institute alive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And keep the arts alive and that really is so important. That said, it was fun!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You looked amazing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You looked amazing.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh