But the Knicks looked so good last night.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, I`ve got the Knicks on my card. I don`t know about y`all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up in our next hour, who run the world?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Girls!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Girls!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, but author, Freya India says they are being exploited by social media companies. She is here with the lesson on how to fight back, not just for them, for parents and grandparents as well.\par \par \uc2 Plus, some dreams are coming true today only on CBS MORNINGS, we will announce the Tony nominations with some All-Star help, but right now, here is a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, Jan Crawford sits down for a rare interview with Supreme Court Justice Neil Gorsuch. He responds to attacks on the court by President Trump and shares his hopes for the next generation of American leaders.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour designer, Zac Posen was at the Met Gala last night. I got to go, too. Can`t wait to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 He is here first on CBS MORNINGS, and he brought the dress that he designed for Kendall Jenner.\par \par \uc2 Let`s just say it was a moment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also got a conversation you`ll want to hear about the pressures faced by young women on social media. Author, Freya India says there are ways to fight back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ve got the top Tony nominations. We`ll announce six major categories only on CBS mornings, and we`ll have some help from some big stars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They know what it`s like. All of that and more in this 8:00 hour of CBS MORNINGS, which is going to start when?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right now! Wooh!\par \par \uc2 You can tell how excited I am for the 8:00 hour of CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Going with the Ric Flair!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right! Wooh!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wooh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is saying that we will have Zac Posen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Zac Posen is here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uzo Aduba.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is bringing the dress --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Darren Criss, yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- that like Kendall Jenner wore last night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony nominations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony nominations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony noms are coming up. It`s a big hour.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Say the wooh again!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a big hour. Wooh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wooh! Plus, "Mean" Gene Okerlund in the house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s begin this hour with alarming developments in the Strait of Hormuz, where shots were fired by both Iran and the United States. The U.S. says two destroyers came under attack Monday, while defying Iran`s blockade of the Strait, but neither was hit.\par \par \uc2 Iran also attacked an oil facility in the United Arab Emirates, sparking a massive fire. It`s the first such attack since the ceasefire, which is now hanging on by a thread.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. military has come under fire as it works to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, which Iran effectively shut down after the U.S. initiated a war back in February.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Central Command said two American flagged commercial ships passed through the Strait, protected by a defense umbrella, including warships, helicopters and fighter jets. The U.S. military said it intercepted Iranian cruise missiles and drones and sank six Iranian small boats all used to try to disrupt the mission.\par \par \uc2 On social media, Sunday, Trump called the initiative to reopen the strait a humanitarian gesture. Ending the Iranian blockade would also strip Iran`s leaders of their most powerful bargaining chip, but there are fears in this region, it could see the fragile ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran break down.\par \par \uc2 President Trump warned in an interview yesterday that Iran would be blown off the face of the Earth if it attacks U.S. ships in the Strait of Hormuz. A senior Iranian official posted on social media this morning that Iran is, "just getting started" -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Holly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has made some big decisions in the past week. Yesterday, the court temporarily restored nationwide access to a widely used abortion pill, and last week, justices made a ruling in a case that could impact the midterm elections, but Justice Neil Gorsuch, he still found some time to have a conversation with our Jan Crawford. They discussed the climate of the court today, the lessons of our past and his new children`s book.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH (U.S. Supreme Court associate justice): I try to remind myself that democracies are supposed to be a messy place, that we`re supposed to disagree.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What about at the court? I`ve covered the court a long time, and it does seem more divided now. Do you feel that at the court?\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH: Do we disagree on hard cases? Yes, Jan, we do. You give us the 70 hardest cases in the entire American legal system every year, that`s our diet. There are nine of us appointed by five different presidents over the course of 30 years. Can you get nine of your best friends to agree on where to go to lunch?\par \par \uc2 So, if you give us those 70 cases, we`re unanimous about 40 percent of the time. How does that happen? It happens because we listen to one another. I`ve never heard a voice raised in the conference room.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Never?\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH: Never.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You know the President is constantly taking shots at the court. The other day, he said, you`re showing very little loyalty to the man who appointed the three of you. Just does a justice owe a president loyalty?\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH: No, my loyalty is to the Constitution, the laws of the United States. That`s the oath I took and so do I care people say left, right, center about me? Nah!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Justice Gorsuch says what he cares about is the next generation, which inspired him to co-write his children`s book, "Heroes of 1776" focusing on the everyday people who sacrificed for America`s independence. He starts the book with this powerful quote --\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH: "You will never know how much it costs the present generation to preserve your freedom. I hope you will make good use of it." John Adams.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What is your goal here, in doing this book and sitting down and doing these interviews?\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH: It is to inspire young people to realize what a heritage they have and what a responsibility they have, right? And do I worry? Jan, yes, of course, I do. You know and if I worry about anything in the world, I worry about America fulfilling its own promise, right?\par \par \uc2 We are our own worst enemies. But are these things fixable? Of course, they`re fixable. Of course, they`re fixable.\par \par \uc2 Teachers, parents, children, we can do this.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Jan digs even deeper in this conversation. You can watch an extended interview with Jan Crawford and Justice Gorsuch on our CBS MORNINGS YouTube channel and our social media.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right ahead, are tech companies hurting young women in America? Author, Freya India says yes, and she`s got some advice on how to push back.\par \par \uc2 Plus fashion designer, Zac Posen was at the Met Gala last night, and now he is here, first on CBS MORNINGS. He had a late night, but he`s here this morning with us. Zac, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 He`s going to talk all things Met Gala and will show us one of his stunning creations from last night.\par \par \uc2 Before we do that, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Coming up, look who`s here! It is author, Freya India out with her new book and some advice on how to help girls cope with social media.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to help us figure it out.\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA ("Girls" author): Yes. Yes. I am going to give you all the solutions.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Okay. Perfect. You`re going to want to stick around.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We love a fashion moment around here!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is our resident fashionista!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, stop it, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He goes, keep going, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 There is no bigger night for fashion than the Annual Met Gala which was held last night. So I was there, I can confirm that the stars really embraced this year`s theme, costume art taking inspiration from all over.\par \par \uc2 So, one very dramatic reveal came from the lovely, Kendall Jenner. She was wearing a custom Gap Studio design by -- look at him -- Zac Posen. He is executive vice president and creative director at Gap.\par \par \uc2 And we are very happy that he is in the