: Inspiring people is all --\par \par \uc2 ZAC POSEN: You never ever know how it will change your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As great as it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Zac Posen --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Getting you to the museum.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you to much. Shoutout to you. Shoutout to Gap studio as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congrats on all the success.\par \par \uc2 ZAC POSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, a huge reveal: The Tony nominations in six top categories. Past nominee, Uzo Aduba will -- and two- time winner, Darren Criss will be here to help with the big announcements.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Girls"/HBO0\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Well, unlike the characters from the series, "Girls," our next guest says, Gen Z women do not have a manual. Her new book aims to change that.\par \par \uc2 Freya India is a Substack writer focused on women and technology. Her book, "Girls" is out today.\par \par \uc2 Now, India writes social media and tech companies have turned girls from people into products. Scan the Q.R. code on the screen today to buy her book.\par \par \uc2 And Freya India is also a contributing writer for The Free Press, which, of course, is owned by our parent company.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Freya. Really appreciate you being here.\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: Good morning. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Okay, so let`s start here. You write that girls have become "products on display, things to be sold." That`s a very powerful, bold statement. So what do you mean by that? And who`s profiting?\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: Yes, I think previous generations of women were, of course, sold products and procedures, but I think with my generation, we really are the product.\par \par \uc2 So I think since the rise of social media, girls have grown up seeing themselves as really nothing but an object in a marketplace. So we`re packaging up our personal lives for Instagram. We`re displaying ourselves on dating apps. We turn ourselves into these professional, personal brands, and then I think we wait for the reviews and ratings of other people, and that influences how we feel about ourselves.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it`s very different from how previous generations grew up. And yet, as you say, we`re really treating ourselves more like products than people, and I think that`s why we`re unhappy because we`re no longer human.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did you write this book?\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: I felt --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Something going on with you?\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: Yes, I felt very anxious growing up. I felt anxious about marketing myself online, and I thought I was the only one who felt like that. And then gradually I started to see these psychologists come out, like Jonathan Haidt and Jean Twenge, and they were mapping out all of these charts, you know, rising rates of anxiety and depression.\par \par \uc2 And it was kind of weirdly comforting for me to see these graphs, because I felt like, oh, maybe this is not just me, but I felt there was another part of the story to be told. So there was the story of a generation falling apart, but I wanted to tell the story of a generation being remade. So what are we being turned into? What is the pressure on us? And so that`s my attempt with "Girls."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But people could say, hasn`t it always been this way? Does this feel different to you?\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: Yes, I think that a lot of the anxieties I talk about in the book, every generation of women relate to. My mom relates to, my grandma relates to, but I think that they`re so intense now, and they`ve never been more profitable.\par \par \uc2 So not only --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the difference of the profitability part.\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: So not only does the modern life sort of amplify those anxieties, but I also think there`s industries that can cash in on them like never before, because they have constant access to us through our phones and social media.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So we are raising a three-year-old girl. How do we help her navigate this world? What`s your advice to the girls who say that they feel a sense of powerlessness?\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: Yes, I think for me, even writing the book, it allowed me to zoom out and actually see what was happening. Because I think for young women my age, we`ve never known any other world. We`ve never known any different and so, we look at people taking selfies and worrying about how they come across online, and that`s just growing up. That`s normal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Normal, yes.\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: And so I think that actually, for girls who are coming of age now, it will be a lot easier, because young women my age are now coming forward and realizing and putting together what happened to us, and there`s much more of a conversation about it.\par \par \uc2 And so I think if girls can notice when they`re treating themselves like a product, and notice the industries that are involved, because I couldn`t even see it until I`d mapped it out. And so I think the next generation actually have a lot more hope.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Really quickly. I mean, these social media companies -- Meta, Instagram, TikTok et cetera -- they do have restrictions and resources for folks to sort of seek help.\par \par \uc2 You know, are they doing enough? And by the way, is it their responsibility? Because their duty, the way our financial system is set up, is to their shareholders.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Make money.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: Yes, I would say there`s not -- I`m not hopeful that there`s much they can do, because I think the nature of the platforms is a certain way.\par \par \uc2 So for example, there are loads of thoughtful influencers online. There`s genuinely helpful information. But that`s not what these platforms reward.\par \par \uc2 And so I think no matter what they do, no matter what features they tweak, it`s just the nature of these platforms to create competition, and then influencers have to become more extreme to get more clicks.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FREYA INDIA: And so I think especially for young girls, they don`t have the context to put this in. So they`re learning about relationships from social media or porn companies before they`ve even had one, and they`re diagnosing themselves before they`ve even --\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: We could talk about this forever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Freya, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your book is really good.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It is, and it is on sale now. Scan the Q.R. code. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You recognize that!\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re kicking off Our Road to the Tonys with an exclusive announcement. We are excited to reveal the nominees for six major categories for the 79th Annual Tony Awards right here on CBS and to help us this morning, we have two very special and talented guests.\par \par \uc2 Uzo Aduba was nominated for a Tony in 2022 for Best Featured Actress in a Play for her role in "Clyde`s" yes, and Darren Criss, he made history last year as the first Asian actor to win Leading Actor in a Musical for his performance in "Maybe Happy Ending."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Maybe Happy Ending"/Belasco Theatre)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so good!\par \par \uc2 VLADI