G: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: That`s theater.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: We love to say it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But you`re tight with Sara.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Yes. Sara and I went to college together. She is a dear, dear friend of mine, and has been amazing and hilarious, might I say, since day one, such a remarkable talent and Caissie being here in New York, coming up in the theater together. It`s just such an incredible category of astonishingly talented --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is always good. Listen, but I saw "Chess." I`m a big fan of Lea Michele. I`m very disappointed that she was not in this category. But let`s talk about Marla Mindelle.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Marla -- I saw -- I`ve been watching versions of that show since I saw it in a strip mall in L.A. like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: -- maybe around 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: So, it is a long road. It was off Broadway, it was in the West End.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: It has seen a lot of iterations, and she`s always been batting a thousand as that role. So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt!\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: You know, congratulations to her, because that these things don`t happen overnight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, exactly! Even though they`re all first time nominees, they don`t --\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes, exactly! They don`t give that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Surely, you`ve been doing this for.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Darren, you`re back up with a very competitive category.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes, indeed. These are the nominations for Best Performance by an Actor in a Leading Role in a Play.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Punch")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Will Harrison, "Punch."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Arthur Miller`s Death of a Salesman")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Nathan Lane, "Arthur Miller`s Death of a Salesman."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Giant")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: John Lithgow, "Giant."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Every Brilliant Thing")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Daniel Radcliffe, "Every Brilliant Thing."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Oedipus")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Mark Strong, "Oedipus."\par \par \uc2 Those are some heavies. Heavy guns!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some heavy guns.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the categories are so good.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re talking to Nathan Lane tomorrow. I saw John Lithgow last night, who`s 80, says he still doesn`t know how he`s doing it, but he is doing it and killing it.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes. I mean, when you`ve been doing it that long, you can`t help but be fantastic. I mean, you don`t stick around that long by accident.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: You know? And I have to shoutout young Will Harrison, who is fabulous in "Punch" and that`s his Broadway debut in the category with some of these, you know, more seasoned vets is really a pretty cool thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Uzo, so you`re up again.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Okay, so the nominees for Best Performance by an Actress in a Leading Role in a Play are --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fallen Angels)\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Rose Byrne, "Fallen Angels."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bug")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Carrie Coon, "Bug."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Liberation")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Susanna Flood, "Liberation."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Oedipus")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Lesley Manville, "Oedipus."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fallen Angels")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Kelli O`Hara, "Fallen Angels."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nine times for her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nine -- you know, I`ve interviewed Kelli last year, and we talked about her record then, now it`s the ninth time in a row.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In a row! Yes.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: I mean, it makes sense. You know, talk about, I just said, you know, batting a thousand, but like, if there`s any higher number than that, I think Kell is definitely that. She never misses. So that makes perfect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then Rose Byrne had an Oscar nomination and a Tony nomination.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which is hard to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Quite a tremendous year for her.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Uzo, you`re up again.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Okay, so the nominees for Best Play are --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Balusters")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: "The Balusters."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Giant")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: "Giant."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Liberation")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: "Liberation."\par \par \uc2 Clip from "Little Bear Ridge Road")\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: "Little Bear Ridge Road."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right!\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: A lot of cool plays.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A lot of cool plays.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: I think what`s really exciting, we`re starting to see this like wonderful, rich new tradition of new plays that are really exciting and this is great to see this range of stories being told this year.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: This one is always one of my favorite categories, because going to drama school, I didn`t do musical theater at all. This, to me, was the Super Bowl, because these are the plays that I would hope to maybe be in one day, that we would maybe do a production of in college or in my own theater company one day.\par \par \uc2 So this was always a really cool way to kind of get a cue from the theater community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: These are play plays. "Liberation" won Pulitzer Prize.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pulitzer.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Just won the Pulitzer --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yesterday.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Pulitzer Prize, yes, right.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: All the details, so major congratulations to them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Darren --\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Major works.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, definitely you won big in this final category.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes, we did! Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Take it away!\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes, yeah, I`ll celebrate that one last time, I will pass it on to the next folks.\par \par \uc2 The nominees for best musical are --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Lost Boys")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: "The Lost Boys."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Schmigadoon!")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: "Schmigadoon!"\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Titanique")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: "Titanique."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Two Strangers (Carry a Cake Across New York)")\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: "Two Strangers (Carry a Cake Across New York)"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wouldn`t want to be voting on this one?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Exciting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What?\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Yes, man! It is going to be like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they say there`s no clear front runner. Lorne Michaels is up for it -- for his "Schmigadoon!"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is producing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Matt Rogers and Bowen Yang are also in there for --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Kiefer Sutherland for "The Lost Boys."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes!\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: -- Wilson as well. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This was so amazing, right? I mean, this is -- I don`t envy the people who have to make these decisions, because this is an incredible year.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Uzo and Darren, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 DARREN CRISS: Thank you so much for having us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You guys will announce the full list of the Tony nominations from the Sofitel New York after our show, which is at 9:00 A.M. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 To watch, visit the Tony Awards Official YouTube page or TonyAwards.com and of course, you can watch the 79th Annual Tony Awards on Sunday, June 7th, at 8:00 P.M. Eastern, 5:00 P.M. Pacific, right here on CBS and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Time now for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today only on CBS MORNINGS, we love when this happens, Oprah is going to reveal her latest Book Club Pick.\par \par \uc2 We recently sat down with her and the award-winning author, and we discussed the novel that Oprah calls a complex and compelling tale that ultimately showcases the transformation and the power of love.\par \par \uc2 Here`s that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I sort of feel like Mariah Carey, you know, how she does that "It`s time!" That`s how I feel about Oprah when it`s time, time to reveal her latest Book Club Selection. Oprah, who did you select? What did you select?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Media personality): I selected "John of John" by Douglas Stuart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even the title is good.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: "John of John."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because "John of John" is about a young man called back to his rural Scottish hometown where he has to hide his queer identity.\par \par \uc2 When he returns home, he slowly discovers that his deeply religious father and his profanity-loving grandmother, let`s just say she had a mouth on her, are keeping secrets of their own, secrets that threatened to tear the family and the community apart wide open.\par \par \uc2 We make it easy for you to buy this book. You`ll like this, Douglas, all you have to do is scan the Q.R. code on your screen to buy it. Along with Oprah, we`re joined by "John of John`s" award winning author, that would be Douglas Stuart.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Douglas Stuart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hello, Douglas Stuart.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART ("John of John" author): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you think when Oprah called you?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: Oh, I mean, it`s mind blowing. You know, it`s a Wednesday evening, and you`re sat by your phone, and then you get a phone call, and very quickly you realize, oh, this really is the real Oprah Winfrey.\par \par \uc2 It was one of the most wonderful and most surreal phone calls in my entire life. So thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Douglas, it was my pleasure to do it because I was so moved by this book.\par \par \uc2 So here`s the thing, I started reading the book, and Abraham Verghese, whose book I chose for "Covenant of Water" sent me a text saying, I get sent a lot of books. And the best book I`ve read all year is this book called "John of John." And I said, I was reading it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I said, "I`ve got to get that."\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I was reading that, and I love that he says "... it`s the most satisfying novel I`ve read in a long time. Stuart`s tale is soulful, tragic, comic, uplifting, and ultimately, so very satisfying, destined to be a classic."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow! That`s great. Wow!\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Says Abraham Verghese.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: Nice! He is a phenomenal writer.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s quite the endorsement.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, yes, yes! And the reason why I was so impressed by this novel is that what it takes to repress who you really are, what it takes to do that means you stifled your life. You`ve been enslaved to somebody else`s idea of who you should be, instead of being who you should be.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I find the novel to be liberating for people who have been suppressing themselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it would be torturous to live that way.\par \par \uc2 Where did the story line come for you? Because you had gone to Scotland, to an isolated area.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: I spent 16 weeks on the Outer Hebrides, which is an archipelago up off the northwest coast of Scotland. And I was traveling around thinking I was going to write about loneliness and about the search for love, but when I was doing my research, I was going from little town to little town, and there was always bachelors or unmarried women, and I was hearing about how they`d missed their chance for love, or how they couldn`t be bothered with the opposite sex.\par \par \uc2 And I was listening to this for some weeks, and I said, well, of course, perhaps one or two of them are gay. And the person went, "Oh, no, no, no, no! That`s not possible."\par \par \uc2 And I suddenly thought, oh, of course, the whole family that I`m writing about has secrets. And to keep secrets, not to be cruel or mean, but to protect each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody, though, has a secret, including dad. Why did you want everyone to have a secret?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: Because I think when we grow up in a place we assume that we belong and that we fit in. And I wanted all of the characters to feel some kind of outsider in their own life, in a way.\par \par \uc2 But the novel is so much about how men spend their entire life avoiding the emotional truth, while women have to carry all the knowing quietly. You know, they have to carry it inside themselves.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: The father and son never say what they want to say, because their conversations are always censored.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: That`s right, whenever they speak on the phone. And so actually, they`re both tweed weavers, and the only time they can really, truly be honest with each other is when they`re discussing their work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: So they`re absolutely exacting when it comes to color and pattern and technicalities. And yet, in their own lives, they sort of hold their emotional selves at a distance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ultimately, though, you said the book is transformational, it`s a power of love.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: That`s right. The family love each other very deeply, and they go to great lengths to protect one another and to make huge sacrifices for one another.\par \par \uc2 But also, what do we inherit from our from our fathers and our grandmothers? You know what sort of behaviors and lies, and one of the keys the book is the grandmother, Ella says to John, the father, when they`re sort of discovering that Cal is gay, they say, well, how do you tell a man who hates himself that his son is made in his image?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: And I think so much --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: That was a jaw drop line.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a jaw dropper to me.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was!\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, well done, Douglas.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: "John of John." Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 "John of John" goes on sale today, wherever you like to buy your books. As I said, we make it easy for you. Scan the Q.R. code to get your copy today.\par \par \uc2 Check out CBSMORNINGS.com for a reader`s guide. And guess what? He`s going to read it, a free excerpt from the book with that accent. I love your accent!\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS STUART: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you can watch Douglas Stuart`s conversation with Oprah on "The Oprah Podcast" available on YouTube or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she`s very excited about this book. She gets very excited about all of her books.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All her books.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She spends a lot of time reading and searching and finding just the right thing that she thinks will have a mass appeal and this one is no different.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very well done, Oprah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. All right, we will be right back. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re still thinking about the Met Gala red carpet last night in particular, Colman Domingo, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He looked fantastic!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Men are really changing the game in terms of how they`re dressing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, look at that!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When doesn`t he show up and look fly?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Colman!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This was my favorite of the night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Naomi Osaka.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Naomi Osaka.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When she takes it off --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, right here!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- there is the red dress!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fire!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Naomi told me she`s right -- that was my favorite.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Chase Infiniti.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chase Infiniti.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us! We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Infiniti and beyond!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260505em5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Secret Service Shoots Gunman Near Washington Monument; United Flight Was Only About 20 Feet Above New Jersey Turnpike When Landing Gear Hit Truck; Passengers Not Allowed to Leave Suspected Hantavirus-Stricken Cruise Ship After Docking Off Africa; U.S. Strikes Seven Iranian Boats, Trump Says, Amid Operation to Move Ships Through Strait of Hormuz; What to Know as Supreme Court Temporarily Restores Access to Mifepristone By Mail; Missing U.S. Soldier Was Trying to Save Colleague Who Fell in Ocean Off Moroccan Coast; Controversy at 2026 Met Gala Over Honorary Co- Chairs Jeff and Lauren Sanchez Bezos; \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  Pilot Has Impromptu Retirement Party on Southwest Flight, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nicole Sganga, Kris Van Cleave, Tom Hanson, Holly  Williams, Nancy Cordes, Chris Livesay, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 3,864 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to be with you. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 The breaking news right now: A Secret Service officer opens fire blocks from the White House after someone raises a gun at the officer. The White House press corps evacuated off the North Lawn as President Trump holds an event inside. And all of it just days after that third assassination attempt. Tonight, what we know about the suspect.\par \par \uc2 Also developing tonight, near disaster dash cam video shows the moment a \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  jet with 231 people on board hit a bakery delivery truck in a light pole on one of the busiest highways in the U.S. What authorities are saying tonight. And that deadly outbreak aboard a cruise ship, the public health threat so dangerous, 149 passengers, three of them sick, now stuck on board.\par \par \uc2 Dolly Parton health update what she`s calling a good news, bad news situation. And what about those sold out shows in Vegas?\par \par \uc2 The Met Gala, money, power and politics collide at fashion`s big night out. We`re there as the stars arrive.\par \par \uc2 And the good stuff, a pilot`s last flight canceled. And yet somehow sweeter because of what happened next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News with Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And good evening. Several major stories as we come on the air tonight, including what federal investigators are now officially calling an accident. That plane on final approach to Newark International Airport yesterday passing over Interstate 95 and coming in so low that the landing gear clipped a truck.\par \par \uc2 You`re looking at the driver`s dash cam at the moment of impact. The plane also hit a light pole and just barely cleared that concrete divider there. We have a full report on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, the breaking news out of Washington, D.C., the White House on lockdown for a time this afternoon after the Secret Service shot a gunman near the Washington Monument just blocks away. Homeland Security and Justice Correspondent Nicole Sganga has made her way to the scene.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, good evening to you. What have you learned?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Good evening, Tony. The confrontation took place just behind me, across from the Washington Monument, four blocks away from the White House. Around 3:30 this afternoon, a Secret Service officer spotted a man that appeared to be carrying a weapon. Moments later, the suspect opened fire. Several agents returned fire, wounding him. A juvenile was also hit, though it`s not clear by whom, and is being treated for non-life- threatening injuries.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service deputy director was asked if this was an attempt on the president`s life.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW QUINN (Deputy Director, \uc2 United States Secret Service\uc2 ): I`m not going to guess on that. I can tell you that every time, one, we`re patrolling this area and every site we do 24/7 hardcore. Whether or not it was directed to the president.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The White House was briefly locked down. Members of the media were evacuated off the North Lawn. The president was also hosting an event at the White House. It was not interrupted. Law enforcement sources tell CBS News the suspect now faces multiple charges and remains in custody at a local hospital.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nicole Sganga, leading us off from Washington. Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Over the past two years, U.S. commercial airlines have collided with a helicopter in flight, a fire truck on the runway, and now a semi-tractor trailer on one of the busiest highways in America. Senior Transportation Correspondent Kris Van Cleave joins us now. Kris, good evening. This plane came within feet of catastrophe. What happened?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Well, Tony, \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigators now say based on damage to the plane, they consider this to be an accident in terms of their investigation.\par \par \uc2 Now, think about this. A tractor trailer is about 13.5 feet tall. The landing gear on a 767, about six feet tall. So, that puts the body of that airplane only about 20 feet above the pavement. The question now, why was it so low?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Dramatic video from Sunday. Truck driver Warren Brodley Jr. was on the New Jersey Turnpike when out of nowhere his tractor trailer is hit by a \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  767 about to land at Newark Airport.\par \par \uc2 Watch again in slow motion. Out of the driver`s side window, you can see part of the plane, then what appears to be the landing gear. And now this picture has surfaced, appearing to show the moment of impact between the jet with 231 people on board, coming from Venice, Italy, and the truck. Police say the plane also struck a light pole, which hit an SUV.\par \par \uc2 Look closely, you can see the bent light pole here. The plane was landing amid gusty winds on the shortest of Newark`s three runways.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT (Former NTSB Chair): This airplane was just feet away from striking the ground.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Former NTSB Chair Robert Sumwalt.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: This runway, as a former airline pilot, I would consider this runway to be relatively short. And so, I suspect that the pilots wanted to make sure that they were not landing long, but unfortunately it appears that they got too low too soon.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): There were no injuries on board the plane.\par \par \uc2 TOWER: They felt something over the threshold and there`s a hole in the side of the airplane.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): United has removed the flight crew from duty and is pledging a thorough investigation as the NTSB has launched its own probe. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy:\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY (U.S. Secretary of Transportation): This is unacceptable --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY: We have really well-trained pilots. This is one. This should never happen in America.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Runway 29 butts up to the turnpike, making scenes like this of airliners descending relatively low over passing traffic not an unusual sight. Court documents reveal Spirit learned the government lifeline was off the table on Thursday. After burning $100 million in additional fuel costs since March, Spirit ran out of money, stranding tens of thousands of flyers and leaving roughly 7,500 Spirit staffers abruptly unemployed. Flight attendant Colleen Burns worked for Spirit for a decade.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN BURNS (President, Spirit AFA Local 77): We all feel like our wings were clipped. And even though I`m sure we`ll land other places, it`s -- it`s not going to be the same.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The Department of Transportation says airlines have helped bring home nearly 100,000 stranded Spirit flyers since Saturday. But just this morning at the Fort Lauderdale Airport, we watched as a woman came in with bags in her hands thinking she was going to get on her Spirit flight, a flight that`s never taking off.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to the west coast of Africa, where the suspected outbreak of a rare and deadly virus has killed at least three people and left a cruise ship stranded at sea. Here`s Correspondent Tom Hanson with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): Tonight, the cruise ship MV Hondius is docked off the West African nation of Cape Verde. Officials revoking permission for passengers to disembark. The ship set sail from Argentina more than four weeks ago, promising its 150 passengers a journey to several exotic places, culminating in Antarctica.\par \par \uc2 Now passengers are stuck on the ship. Medical investigators believe three passengers have died from the Hantavirus. Three other cases are suspected. Hantavirus is a rare respiratory disease that can kill up to 50 percent of those infected. It`s the same illness that killed actor Gene Hackman`s wife last year.\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: This really is a detective story. So, they`re going to have to figure out, okay, where was the boat? Where were the people? When could they possibly have been infected with rodent droppings or urine or saliva?\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): The World Health Organization is helping to evacuate passengers that are showing symptoms.\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: Right now, the World Health Organization is saying there`s no big risk to the general public. But of course, you`re always going to play it safe. You`re going to be as cautious as possible and get as much information as possible.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And Dr. LaPook says this virus is not like the norovirus, which can rapidly spread on a cruise ship and really wreak havoc. The World Health Organization is working to see if this virus is the strain that can be passed from person to person. Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tom, thank you very much. We turn now to the war with Iran, still officially in a ceasefire, but with missiles and drones crisscrossing the region again today. U.S. stocks fell, the Dow dropping more than 500 points. That`s more than one percent. And oil prices up more than five percent to around $114 a barrel. But two U.S. cargo ships that had been trapped for months in the war zone made it out today, according to officials, raising hopes that more could follow. Here`s Senior Foreign Correspondent Holly Williams with more tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R) (U.S. President): We did a little detour, and it`s working out very nicely.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): As the U.S. military works to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, President Trump said today that the conflict with Iran is going very well.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Central Command said the military intercepted cruise missiles and drones launched by Iran to try to disrupt the mission, with no American warships or other U.S.-flagged vessels hit. The president said the U.S. struck seven Iranian military speedboats.\par \par \uc2 As usual, Iran gave a different version of events, denying that any commercial ships passed through the Strait of Hormuz. The country`s state media claimed that this video shows the Iranian navy firing warning shots at American destroyers, though we cannot verify it.\par \par \uc2 On social media yesterday, Trump called the initiative to move ships through the Strait of Hormuz a humanitarian gesture, with around 2,000 ships stranded and food on board some of them running short.\par \par \uc2 But ending the Iranian blockade would also strip Iran`s leaders of their most powerful bargaining chip. That`s helpful to a president who`s under pressure to end the conflict.\par \par \uc2 RAMAN KAPOOR (Indian Oil Tanker Captain): Ever since the war started we have witness maybe more than 200 missiles over our head.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): Raman Kapoor is the captain of an oil tanker stranded in the Persian Gulf since the war began in February.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Are you worried that this actually may cause the ceasefire to collapse?\par \par \uc2 RAMAN KAPOOR: Yes, anything could go wrong at any time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: There are fears in this region that the fragile ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran could now crumble. The United Arab Emirates, an American ally, said it was targeted by Iran today with a drone strike injuring three people and sparking a fire at an oil port.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly Williams for us in Tel Aviv. Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Rudy Giuliani is in a Florida hospital tonight recovering from pneumonia, according to a spokesperson. The former New York City mayor and personal lawyer to President Trump suffers from what`s known as restrictive airway disease and was put on a ventilator over the weekend. He says the illness is tied to exposure to toxins at ground zero following the September 11th attacks. Giuliani turns 82 later this month.\par \par \uc2 Most abortions today are obtained through medication, often accessed by mail and without an in-person visit to a doctor, something opponents have tried to stop and for a time the Trump administration tried to require. But the Supreme Court has, at least for now, restored access.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in Chief White House Correspondent Nancy Cordes. Nancy, good evening.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Tony, good evening to you. This order from the Supreme Court is going to allow the abortion drug Mifepristone to be prescribed once again via telemedicine and shipped to patients in the mail for at least another week until the Supreme Court decides how it wants to handle this case.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Supreme Court stepped in today after a lower court on Friday blocked telehealth providers from prescribing these pills nationwide. And today`s order was signed by one of the court`s conservative justices, Samuel Alito, but this is just an administrative stay, so it doesn`t indicate how the full court might rule going forward.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is one of two drugs commonly used in conjunction to bring about an abortion early in pregnancy, so a permanent ban, Tony, would have a major impact on abortion access around the country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, sure. Nancy, remind us why the lower court blocked access in the first place.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Sure. Well, the state of Louisiana had sued the Food and Drug Administration arguing that the availability of Mifepristone by mail violated Louisiana`s ban on abortion. There are 13 states that have enacted near-total abortion bans since the repeal of Roe v. Wade four years ago, and access to this particular drug has really been at the heart of the fight ever since because medication, as you noted, is now used to bring about two-thirds of all abortions. Mifepristone is commonly used to manage miscarriages as well, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 At a campground outside Oklahoma City last night, someone opened fire injuring more than 20 people, though it`s unclear at this hour how many of them were shot. The police say it was a lakeside party promoted on social media, and they`re investigating.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to northeast Africa, where a search is underway for two U.S. Army soldiers in Morocco for a training mission and now missing after going on a hike during some downtime. CBS News Foreign Correspondent Chris Livesay has details.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good evening, Tony. It is a real-world rescue operation now for what started off as an exercise.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY (voiceover): Multiple aircraft, drones, divers, and vessels have been searching along Morocco`s southern coast for the last three days for two missing U.S. soldiers. Defense officials tell us they were off-duty on a hike when one soldier is believed to have fallen off a cliff into the surf below. Other soldiers witnessed the fall and attempted a rescue but could not reach the service member. A second soldier jumped in the water but struggled to return to shore, so a third jumped in. Only the third soldier made it out.\par \par \uc2 The soldiers were at this base along with more than 7,000 service members for the annual African Lion exercises. We spoke to one of the technology developers.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: I choose to look around and measure the work we do today as a metric for the number of lives we`ll save in the future.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: That future was sooner than expected. Using drones, he designed himself to search for his fellow soldiers. The military says this is still a search and rescue mission.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Chris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the broadcast, the star-studded Met Gala celebrates fashion, also power, also fame. What`s the controversy? Some big names are boycotting this year. We`ll get into it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a bear on the loose in Los Angeles and the rare move to keep kids safe.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you can afford a reported $100,000 a ticket and are deemed cool enough to be invited, tonight`s a biggie. The annual Met Gala, officially a charity for the museum, unofficially a showy gathering of big names and even bigger money. None more controversial than this year`s honorary chairs, Amazon founder Jeff Bezos and his wife Lauren Sanchez Bezos.\par \par \uc2 CBS Morning Saturday Co-Anchor Adriana Diaz is there and filed this report.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Hey, Tony. Tonight, the Metropolitan Museum of Art is the center of the fashion world, drawing the biggest names from film, fashion, music, art, and tech. Hundreds of people are expected to arrive, and we`ve already seen some high-profile arrivals.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voiceover): Tonight`s theme, costume art, celebrates the museum`s new exhibit opening later this spring. Guests are expected to dress as expressions of art, not just style, each one a walking canvas.\par \par \uc2 VENUS WILLIAMS (Tennis Player): I wanted to go for timeless, but also statement and, like, also art.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voiceover): But this year`s gala is also facing controversy, particularly around honorary co-chairs, Amazon founder Jeff Bezos and his wife Lauren Sanchez Bezos. Critics cite Amazon`s labor practices and are calling for a boycott.\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: Shame on you, Jeff Bezos.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voiceover): Last night, a video featuring Amazon workers angry over the way they`re treated was projected on the facade of Bezos` Fifth Avenue apartment. A handful of celebrities have decided to sit out this year`s gala. And New York City Mayor Zohran Mamdani is skipping the event, saying he`s focused on affordability in one of the country`s most expensive cities.\par \par \uc2 There are no cameras allowed inside of this invitation-only fundraiser, but the cost of individual tickets has soared, now $100,000 up from $75,000 last year.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And despite all the controversy and high-ticket prices, there is so much excitement in the air to see how everyone will interpret this year`s theme. People are especially excited to catch a glimpse of BeyoncAc, one of the co-chairs. She hasn`t been here in ten years.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Adriana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Dolly Parton has a message for her fans, what she calls the good news and a little bad news.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pop superstar Ed Sheeran bought a piece of his favorite soccer team back in 2024 only to see them lose their way out of the Premier League. They`re back now though. Here`s a celebration.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s Sheeran with a pint of Guinness in hand leading the celebration as the players and coach of Ipswich Town sing word for word Sheeran`s 2011 mega hit, "The A Team." That is them.\par \par \uc2 Dolly Parton is canceling her upcoming Las Vegas residency citing health issues and breaking the news as only Dolly Parton can.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON (Singer-Songwriter and Actress): This thing of me is like an old classic car that once restored can be better than ever.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The 80-year-old says she`s healing from among other things, kidney stones but is improving every day as she works on her new Broadway show which opens later this year.\par \par \uc2 A lot of us love to get out into nature less so when nature comes to us as was the case today in the Los Angeles neighborhood of Pacoima where a large bear wandered around roaming through backyards walking along fences even hanging out on someone`s patio. It prompted a lockdown at an elementary school in the area but those tranquilizer darts, they work.\par \par \uc2 The bear passed out on a wall after wildlife officials hit it with one this afternoon and carted the bear off presumably for release back into the wild.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a very special flight for a father and son.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back with the good stuff. The story of a pilot`s retirement after 47 years. The plans broke down and yet it was all somehow sweeter because of what happened next.\par \par \uc2 Captain Jon Jackson`s final commercial flight was scheduled for Saturday from Fort Lauderdale home to Baltimore and his son Chris, who`d followed him into aviation, was supposed to be on that flight with him. Then dad`s company, Spirit, shut down overnight, canceling everything. They did have a flight home at least via Chris` airline, Southwest. But what happened next is proof that while the world of business may not care if you have your day, your son still does. And if you`re really lucky, the people in your field do too.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL (voiceover): Chris` colleagues arranged for his dad to be welcomed with a traditional double water cannon salute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did it feel when you touched down and the water cannons were going over the top of the plane?\par \par \uc2 CAPT. JON JACKSON (Pilot): It`s pretty emotional. I was, you know, quite overwhelmed because it wasn`t expected at all.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL (voiceover): There was also a whole group waiting for Jon at the gate along with a bottle of champagne.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON (Southwest First Officer): You know, I love my dad. He was one that literally taught me how to fly. And I`m just thankful to see, you know, all the hard work he`s done throughout his career and be rewarded and be able to be turned around on him for once.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jon told us he`s proud of his career in aviation but even prouder of his career as a dad. And now he`s a granddad too. Chris` son is a little over a year old. And yes, they are planning to teach him to fly.\par \par \uc2 That`s another day in America. I`m Tony Dokoupil. We`ll see you right back here tomorrow. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020260505em5400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Tornadoes, Blizzards and Extreme Winds Wreak Havoc; Storms and Shutdown Disrupt Air Travel; U.S. Allies Push Back on Trump Security Demands; Trump Chief-of-Staff Diagnosed with Breast Cancer; Iran Strikes on Embassy in Iraq; Iran Retaliates Amid U.S. and Israeli Strikes; Trump Suggests U.S. Could Take Cuba; Judge Blocks RFK, Jr.`s Vaccine Policy Changes; Dog Rescued From Train Tracks; Kouri Richins Found Guilty of Killing Husband; A.I. Money Management; Grammy Winner, Melissa Etheridge on New Album, Tour and Career; Behind-the-scenes with Bubba Wallace and 23XI Racing; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Viewer Faces Her Fear of Water; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Dave Malkoff, Ian  Lee, Rob Marciano, Kris Van Cleave, Nancy Cordes, Imtiaz Tyab, Celine  Gounder, Andres Gutierrez, Caroline Polisi, Jill Schlesinger, Cristian  Benavides, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 14,259 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on your Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, very nasty weather and long lines at the airports create a travel mess for millions.\par \par \uc2 Plus, breaking news, Israel claims to have killed two of Iran`s most senior leaders. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A growing travel nightmare is just one of the many problems nationwide, extreme weather is hitting millions from coast-to-coast.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We have absolutely no power. Our entire house shook. We can`t even enter our front door because we have live wires all across our front door.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: America`s allies refuse to answer President Trump`s call for help protecting ships in the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): My attitude is, we don`t need anybody.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There`s a verdict in the trial of a Utah mom accused of killing her husband then writing a children`s book about grief.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE: Guilty of aggravated murder.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: President Trump threatening to take over Cuba as it deals with a crippling nationwide blackout.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I can do anything I want with it.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Team Italy facing an exuberant Venezuela team in the World Baseball Classic.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: The hit parade continues, and so does the fiesta.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: In a four-two winner, Venezuela paces Team USA in the WBC championship.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Actor, Michael B. Jordan celebrating his Oscar winning style with a burger and fries at a Los Angeles In-N-Out.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Michael B. Jordan got Best Actor with his incredible performance of two parts in the movie "Sinners." I`m starting to think this guy could be the Michael Jordan of acting.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive. Making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We love all things, Michael B. Jordan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There`s only one Michael B. Jordan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. There is only one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he likes In-N-Out burgers, apparently. So that means he gets extra points in my book for that.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin though, of course, talking about the weather. I know you`ve heard about it. It`s all kinds of nasty weather all over the country.\par \par \uc2 A tornado tore the roof off this warehouse. This is in North Carolina. The winds there got up to 85 miles an hour. Yikes!\par \par \uc2 In the Upper Midwest, a blizzard slammed the region, including Green Bay, Wisconsin. At one point, a huge fire broke out at a paper factory. The conditions there made it very, very difficult to put it out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Meantime, airports are also in chaos with delays and cancelations from the weather and the continuing fallout from the partial government shutdown. We`ve got team coverage on all of this, but we`re going to start with Dave Malkoff in Atlanta, where storms left a trail of destruction.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: They definitely did, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Let me just show you this. This is just a smaller piece of the tree that came down on Tonya Steve`s (sp?) house as we pushed in up there.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you what happened to the side of the house. That is her mother`s bedroom closet and bathroom. She was in there at the time. So what happened here?\par \par \uc2 This was not a tornado, although the \uc2 NWS\uc2  is investigating those around town. This was straight line winds, and they`re telling us these winds were strong enough to compare to a Cat 1 hurricane.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Here in Georgia, destructive winds toppled this tree, barely missing the bed where Tonya Steve`s 95-year-old mother slept.\par \par \uc2 TONYA STEVE (Georgia resident): I heard like metal breaking, glass breaking, but she got out just in time. She was in her bed.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Further north, an EF Zero tornado packing winds of up to 85 miles an hour, touched down in Charlotte, North Carolina, ripping off a chunk of this HVAC company`s roof while overnight, severe storms moved through the Philadelphia area. Trees crashed down onto power lines and homes and wind gusts up to 68 miles an hour slammed Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My little one was starting to get very frightened. I said, okay, we`re going to go shelter.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The storm sent a trampoline flipping on top of this car and trees falling down on her driveway.\par \par \uc2 And in Alabama, an unusual sight for March as snow blanketed roads in Huntsville. In Green Bay, Wisconsin, blizzard conditions hampered firefighters in their hours` long effort to battle flames at a paper factory, Monday.\par \par \uc2 Our Ian Lee is in Green Bay.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Green Bay continues to dig out and pull cars out after experiencing an historic snowstorm. The last time they saw this much snow in a single day was 1889.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: A record 26 inches of snow fell there through Monday, while other parts of the Upper Midwest are still digging out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: And remember, this is St. Patrick`s Day here in Atlanta, and you can see your breath here. It is 30 degrees, a little colder up where Rob Marciano is. We`re going to him in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Rob, it shouldn`t be this cold down here or in places like Huntsville.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: No, really! Excuse me, but it`s one of the coldest St. Patrick`s Day on the East Coast that we`ve seen in quite some time. Matter of fact, here in Chicago, it`s been 80 years since we`ve seen a St. Patrick`s Day this called, a far cry from what they had on Saturday, when they had the big celebration with temperatures in the 60s. My goodness!\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s go through the radar and show just how dynamic this storm was. All of those severe weather reports, over 700 severe weather reports over the last two days. Big winds across the East Coast there. Philadelphia -- look at JFK, 72 mile per hour winds. You want to talk about chaos in the skies. There it is.\par \par \uc2 And then behind it, we`ve got the cold air and the lake effect snow, even this time of year, we get lake effect snow. That`s rare to have that sort of difference in temperature between the cold water and even colder air, and they are your temperatures this morning.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow morning, they`ll be similar. So we`re not going to warm up immediately. We will eventually, but the next 48 hours are going to be real, and it is biting. I can tell you that firsthand right now along the Lakeshore Drive.\par \par \uc2 All right, earliest heat warning on record for Phoenix coming this week. We`ve got heat warning signing up through Southern California. Some of these numbers are jarring.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the coin, obviously, huge ridge is what`s driving the heat there, and it`s driving the cold down off towards the East as well, 97 expected in Los Angeles today for a high temperature, my goodness, 96 again tomorrow, and then potentially the earliest 90-degree day in Sacramento on record.\par \par \uc2 So talk about a yin and yang, those temperatures would feel pretty good right now. Something about the cold in the middle of March, it just hits differently, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`re right about that, Rob. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we mentioned how difficult air travel is right now. Well, this is what we mean.\par \par \uc2 More than 17,000 flights were delayed or cancelled on Monday. Frustrations are growing as thousands of TSA officers continue to work without pay.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleve has the latest from Phoenix Sky Harbor Airport.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It has certainly been a double whammy for flyers. You have weather disruptions and that unpredictable TSA line situation. Fewer cancellations today, airlines looking to recover, but the situation at the nation`s checkpoints does appear to be getting worse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The winter storm that lashed much of the country this week wreaked havoc on flyers` plans. Roughly 9,000 flights cancelled and 25,000 more delayed since Sunday, which is when Tammy Sam Pornero (sp?) was supposed to leave Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY SAM PORNERO (Traveler): -- yesterday, our flight kept getting delayed over and over and over. We got like two hours of sleep and came back and said, here we are probably going to miss the next flight, too.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: She was greeted with TSA lines topping two-and-a-half hours at points, Monday. Staffing shortages forced one of Atlanta`s checkpoints to close.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, TSA sick calls have more than tripled since the start of the partial government shutdown last month, and they`re getting worse.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, TSA officers missed a paycheck, and nearly 8.5 percent of the roughly 50,000 screeners called out sick. On Saturday, that jumped to nine and a quarter percent.\par \par \uc2 By Sunday, it was more than one in every 10 officers calling out. That`s more than five times a normal day.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY JONES (TSA officer and Union official in Dallas): I fully anticipate that more and more people will be unable to come to work.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Johnny Jones is a TSA officer and union official in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY JONES: The majority of the employees work paycheck to paycheck, so without getting a full paycheck last time, they are definitely struggling.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Denver`s Airport is asking flyers to donate $10.00 and $20.00 grocery or gas gift cards to help officers while food banks are opening at some airports.\par \par \uc2 More than 360 TSA officers have quit during this shutdown.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY JONES: If I was the traveling public, I think I`d prepare to get to the airport four hours early instead of two.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And several airports have sort of taken the brunt of this. Houston Hobby, JFK, Atlanta, the San Juan Airport in Puerto Rico and New Orleans, all have seen a lot of these issues, but they can happen sporadically, really, at any airport. And of course, we`re right in the middle of spring break -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Kris, before we let you go, domestic airfare prices are also going up this week. Why is this happening?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, a lot of it has to do with jet fuel and certainly some demand from spring break, but jet fuel prices are about double where they were a year ago. That`s 30 percent of the cost for the airlines. If their costs jump, you can bet, passengers are going to end up paying for some of that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 Kris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s get the latest now on the war in Iran.\par \par \uc2 President Trump faces strong pushback from his U.S. allies who he has asked to help protect oil tankers in the region.\par \par \uc2 Now, Iran has blocked most international shipping 