n`s book about grief after her husband`s death, was motivated by money and a plan to start over with another man.\par \par \uc2 ATTORNEY: She wanted to leave Eric Richins, but did not want to leave his money.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: The digital trail Richins left behind may have helped seal her fate. Prosecutors pointed to internet searches about poisoning, a forged life insurance application, and intimate text messages to her then boyfriend, where she said, "I think I want you to be my husband one day."\par \par \uc2 The defense rested without calling a single witness and claimed the prosecution didn`t prove its case.\par \par \uc2 ATTORNEY: The investigation in this matter was nothing but sloppy. It was driven by bias.\par \par \uc2 AMY RICHINS (Eric Richins` sister): It`s been a long time coming. So just very happy that we got justice for my brother.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: Sentencing is set for May 13th on what would have been Eric Richards 44th birthday.\par \par \uc2 AMY RICHINS: Our focus is now on honoring Eric`s life and supporting his boys.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Andres Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CBS News legal analyst, Caroline Polisi is with us to sort this all out.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you at the table.\par \par \uc2 So the jury took a little over three hours. Many people think that was a very quick decision. What do you think went into their thinking process? Because some people say, if it`s a quick decision, definitely guilty, some people say, definitely innocent. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: Yes, well, you know, rule of thumb, quick decision, definitely guilty. So, I knew sort of immediately.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: But you know what it tells me is that they walked in there with unanimity. There was no sort of hold-out jurors. They basically spent -- I mean, there were a lot of charges here. They basically 