ar \par \uc2 And just everything about how I look, to how I was dressed, to how I behaved, to you know, the sleeping my way to the top, to the gold digger, to this -- in all the words that Lisa used, the husband stealer, which, by the way, that expression really bugs me. And I`m not trying to make light obviously, of adultery, it`s not that, but this notion of women stealing men, like I would walk in and duct tape and zip tie a guy and, like, unwittingly drag him out and steal. Like the man has no role in this.\par \par \uc2 This is -- you know, and for me, like I was the face of all of this. There was no one talking about Andy`s hair, shirt, watch. There was no one questioning -- and maybe anyone here can correct me, I never read or heard anything about wow, did he become a CEO by sleeping his way to the top? No, and that`s all anyone would say about me.\par \par \uc2 It was unfathomable that I might have achieved and worked and hustled and built a career I`m incredibly proud of that was not on the table.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is an important conversation, because it just gives an insight into the way society treats men and women --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, very much so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- who find themselves in circumstances like this, where there is something happening between them, they are with other people. In this case, she was separated from.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was separated and she said, I was there at the taping, number one. It was very interesting to watch, because the audience is primarily women, and many of you were sitting there -- Harumph! Harumph!\par \par \uc2 But the more she talked, the more people`s hearts opened, the more their minds opened. He had told her that he was also separated, going through a divorce. They had not had an affair. Clearly, they were close.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They were not -- in other words --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They had not been intimate. They had not been intimate. They were clearly close. They were bonding over their mutual separation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got caught up in a moment at a conference.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they got caught up in a moment. That was the first time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her husband was there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her soon to be ex was there. She was more worried about him being on the Jumbotron. But it was interesting, the way women in particular were the ones that -- the main ones that came after her -- women.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Attacked her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, the people would show up at her house, looking in the window, calling her all sorts of names, threatening the children.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Meanwhile, he escaped from all of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s had job offers, she`s had none, and she --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s is the duality we live in these days, though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wait until you see this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The interview is out on Oprah`s YouTube channel or wherever you listen to podcasts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- I kick it with Bubba Wallace in the car.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Nate Burleson. That`s Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour. Melissa Etheridge, she`s here in the studio today to talk about her brand new music. Her latest single is called "Being Alive" and she`ll explain what it`s all about.\par \