ning, a little earlier than usual, but that`s okay.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, what`s up? What do you have?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, my "Talk of the Table" is I`m feeling a little salty, and on a level of saltiness between like pretzel and Pacific Ocean, big ocean salt, about what Sarah Michelle Geller is talking about, which is "Buffy the Vampire Slayer" reboot that was suddenly just cancelled.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Buffy the Vampire Slayer)/20th Century Studios)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, before it even came out.\par \par \uc2 So Sarah Michelle Gellar says she and "Hamnet" director, Chloe Zhao, yes, that Chloe Zhao, Academy Award winner Chloe Zhao had been working for years to bring back the much beloved series that went off the air in 2003.\par \par \uc2 Geller said in a statement: "We had an executive on our show who was not only not a fan of the original, but was proud to constantly remind us that he had never seen the entirety of the series and how it wasn`t for him. So that tells you the uphill battle that we`ve been fighting since day one, when your executive is literally proud to tell you that he didn`t watch it."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe somebody else will pick it up, though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t get it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But how does that happen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe somebody else will pick it up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve got --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s hope that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve got the original star that made this show such an iconic show.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Got one of the greatest working directors in Hollywood right now who came to her and said, I want to do this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Buffy the Vampire Slayer)/20th Century Studios)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s hope. Let`s -- fingers, legs, eyes, toes crossed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Salty!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Salty. Shawna, our executive producer is salty, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We don`t like him being -- we don`t like Vlad -- we don`t like him being salty.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" -- we like to keep him happy around here. It`s a nonprofit GYRL Wonder, G-Y-R-L is how it is spelled, hosting its Inaugural Women of Impact Gala.\par \par \uc2 So I was the woman -- I was one of the people who was honored by the group last night.\par \par \uc2 For more than a decade, GYRL Wonder has been building a network of development and opportunity for ambitious women of color between the ages of 17 and 23.\par \par \uc2 Other honorees this year included Olympic gymnast, Jordan Chiles, Asia Milia Ware, beauty editor at "The Cut" and beauty entrepreneur Danessa Myricks, who had us all in tears. She is founder of Danessa Myricks Beauty.\par \par \uc2 In her speech, she has talked about her experiences growing up, weren`t always so pretty. She faced tremendous bullying. She was often referred to as "most ugly" by her peers in high school. You know, you get voted Most Likely to Succeed, Friendliest --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was called Most Ugly. It broke my heart.\par \par \uc2 After being passed over for that award, Myrick said a teacher told her something she would never, ever forget.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANESSA MYRICKS (Danessa Myricks Beauty founder): She was like, what happened to you today, what happened to you over and over and over in your life because of who you are and what you look like. And I think that was the first time where I really felt like doing anything special in the world be somewhat impossible.\par \par \uc2 I hope my presence here tells a story of possibility for you.\par \par \uc2 And my deepest prayer for you is that you find the courage to disappoint all the people who try to make you feel small.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I was so smitten with her. She has a very successful beauty business, and we should really do her for women who make a difference.\par \par \uc2 But she talked about when she was in sixth grade, she was told, look, you won the contest. It was about intelligence. She passed. They`re going to call your name. When it came time to call her name, they called another little girl`s name, and she went to the teacher and said, why? The teacher said, because of how you look, this is going to happen to you over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What? Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Being told that you`re ugly, being told that your life doesn`t matter. And when it came to launch your beauty brand, they said, we wish we had somebody that was better looking to promote it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh come on!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So to have all of that guys and then to still be the success that she is, cheering you on! Thank you, GYRL Wonder!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She has the beauty and the brains right there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, GYRL Wonder. Thank you, GYRL Wonder. Thank you, Danessa. It was a great night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was a great night, that`s for sure!\par \par \uc2 Stick around. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We are going inside the garage of one of NASCAR`s newest and hottest teams, the Michael Jordan co-owned 23XI Racing.\par \par \uc2 Their driver, Tyler Reddick, recently set a NASCAR record winning each of the season`s first three races. So, we traveled to North Carolina to spend time with another 23XI driver, Bubba Wallace.\par \par \uc2 We learned about the training and strategy behind their success.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What sounds like thunder and what looks like a tornado of car mechanics is actually a well-rehearsed, well-choreographed game plan.\par \par \uc2 Racer, Bubba Wallace is one of the sport`s most popular drivers.\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE (Race driver): There`s one guy that told me, if you know you`re going to go into something and lose, then don`t compete.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who said that?\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: M.J.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: M.J.\par \par \uc2 Michael Jordan`s passion for competition has helped spin 23XI Racing into NASCAR`s hottest thing. His race shop operates like a professional football or basketball team.\par \par \uc2 JON CARVIN (23XI pit crew): The car comes in --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A state of the art film room, a fitness center with strength and conditioning coaches, all housed on the team`s campus just outside of Charlotte, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 JON CARVIN : You`re trying to, in football, find the best techniques to gain yards. We`re trying to find the best techniques to cut tenths of seconds off of our pit stop time, like five hundredths of a second. It matters to us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Josh Shipplett and Jon Carvin run the pit crews for 23XI. They both have a background in sports other than racing.\par \par \uc2 The two have used that experience to build a roster stacked with military veterans and former college athletes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHIPPLETT (23XI pit crew): Three-tenths of a second is the amount of time it takes you to lose a spot or gain a spot.\par \par \uc2 So if we come down right behind somebody, if we have a three-tenths of a second faster pit stop, we will go out in front of them.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Let`s go!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Last season, 23XI`s pit crew were among NASCAR`s best.\par \par \uc2 This year, the team is helping its racers find victory lane at a record setting pace.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Tyler Reddick --\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: From the time we get to start finish line to turn one, two seconds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wallace is near the top of this year`s NASCAR standings.\par \par \uc2 My heart is racing. Does your heart still race?\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: Sometimes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How is your heart?\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: Sometimes, it does, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The 32-year-old has three-career NASCAR Cup Series wins.\par \par \uc2 All right! All right! All right!\par \par \uc2 And has been racing for Jordan`s team since 2021.\par \par \uc2 I think people that are unfamiliar with racing, they think it`s just the driver in the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: Hundred percent. There`s a lot more to it than that.\par \par \uc2 We`re fighting for thousands of an inch.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thousands of an inch.\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sheesh!\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: That`s what -- we`re trying to find every little low hanging fruit to capitalize on to make the full potential of our car that much better.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Can you believe? You just said it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jordan`s impact is being felt beyond just this garage. Last year, the team settled a landmark antitrust lawsuit with NASCAR, one that both sides agree will increase protections for teams and elevate competition for fans.\par \par \uc2 The teams argued in the lawsuit that NASCAR was operating like a monopoly, controlling too much of the T.V. deal money and which teams are guaranteed to race each week.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JORDAN (23XI co-owner): I`ve said this from day one. The only way this sport is going to grow is we have to find some synergy between the two entities, and I think we`ve gotten to that point.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How do you explain M.J.`s love for racing to people who might not know about it?\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: It`s surprising to know that M.J. has such a knack and such a passion for NASCAR and for motorsports in general.\par \par \uc2 Now that I know him, his car collection, his super bike team that he had. Now, he is the owner of a NASCAR team. It could all make sense. He loves to compete. He loves cars. He loves going fast.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Going fast is what Wallace does best, but he also understands it is one of the most dangerous jobs in all of professional sports.\par \par \uc2 It`s like the elephant room nobody really talks about. But do you fear death on the track?\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: I do. I fear death because everything is right here. You know, I`m enjoying life with our beautiful baby boy, my beautiful wife, our beautiful home that we just built, and I get --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A sweet spot right now.\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: Right. Everything is just clicking, and when that`s all done, but that`s what scares me the most, not retiring done, but, like, done, done death.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUBBA WALLACE: You know that`s it! Climbing into the car that is always there. We`re doing an incredibly dangerous sport. We go, we react, and we deal with the consequences later.\par \par \uc2 And I think that takes a lot of courage from my wife, my family to trust in that and know that I`m okay climbing in, and it could be my last time that I never climbed back out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So 23XI is on fire right now. So shout out to them. Everybody involved that opened up their doors to me.\par \par \uc2 Tyler Reddick and Bubba Wallace are one and two in the standings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are cooking right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yup!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Denny Hamlin, who is also the co-owner of 23XI just won this past Sunday. He races for another team, but he co-owns it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: 