Never Too Late" dreams. We were grateful to hear from so many of you, including Peggy Marie Tucker from South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER (From South Carolina): I have a fear of water. I almost drowned as a child.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Peggy Marie Tucker has designed her life around her fear of water.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Yes, that one`s mine.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This is yours.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Yes, that one is mine.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Life jackets in the car. No water on her face in the shower, all because as a little girl, she experienced trauma at the school pool.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Someone jumped in on the back of me, and the next thing I knew, they were pulling me out. I have more than a fear of it. It goes beyond the fear.\par \par \uc2 It`s the dreams that I have. Water is coming at me. Water is chasing me.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Do you have them once a year? Once a month?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Oh, no, several times a month.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The terror has been a constant in her life through raising kids, teaching elementary school and even beating kidney cancer. But now there`s a stronger feeling -- determination.\par \par \uc2 What inspired you to send a video to CBS MORNINGS?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Gayle King, when she went to outer space.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Blue Origin)\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: She had to let go and that`s what I`m hoping to be able to do, is to let go. I mean, even literally, figuratively, to let go.\par \par \uc2 And I would love to get in the pool with my son.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And then you can have a little drink and walk across the pool!\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: There you go!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes!\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: You know my dream.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I do.\par \par \uc2 So we called in the best.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Jones is just accelerating along --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Two time Olympian, Cullen Jones.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES (Olympic swimmer): You`ll be all right today. I promise.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Despite his four Olympic medals, Jones relates to Peggy Marie`s phobia. He almost drowned at five years old at a water park.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: You passed out.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: Oh yes. I had to be resuscitated.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: Oh yes --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: First, they set goals.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Maybe tomorrow I can put my head under the water, hopefully, yes.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: Yes, yes. Tomorrow? Why not today?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And used visualizations.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: If you`re always in the water, you think of it as tissue.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Oh, okay!\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: You always blow out, you would never blow in.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Taking incremental steps toward blowing bubbles.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: We`re just going to do the bottom lip first and touch.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: I can`t breathe.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: Yes, you can.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: By the end of day one, her confidence was growing.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: You`re getting fancy not holding on. Oh you`re getting fancy!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What do you hope people watching this take away?\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: That you`re only limiting yourself, especially around water. I`m speaking specifically to Black and Brown communities, but for anyone. Sixty percent of Black Americans don`t know how to swim, 54 percent of Latino Americans and 36 percent of Caucasians don`t know how to swim.\par \par \uc2 We are not built for water, but you can learn to swim.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Everything okay?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Just a call.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh, my God! We have a call. Oh, I think it`s for you. Miss Peggy Marie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hi, Peggy Marie.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: That`s a call you take.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so excited for you, because I hear you`re putting on your big girl panties and you`re going to take the plunge and get in the water, and you`re in good hands with Cullen.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Blue Origin)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I understand your fear. I was terrified when I went to space, but I said, I`m going to do something.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Step outside my comfort zone. How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: I`m excited. I`m like, let`s do this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I love that you said, "When I conquer this."\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It will be freeing.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Yes! What a wonderful surprise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay! Yay! Yay!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With a boost from Gayle, Peggy Marie, was ready for day two.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: So you take a deep breath and blow out. That`s it!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Which was a mental challenge.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: Okay, take a minute. Good! Some -- yes!\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling? You better be proud. There ain`t no other word that should come out of your mouth.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Okay, then I won`t say anything.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: Yes!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: When asked to put her whole face in the water, she froze.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: And when you`re ready, deep breath in and blow out.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: I did it. Oh my God! Okay.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You just put your face in the water.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: I did. I put my full face in the water.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You never -- she never --\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: No drama!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She never does this.\par \par \uc2 CULLEN JONES: She did it this far.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: I still feel like crying.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You feel like crying?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Yes!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s part of that letting go that you`re doing, right? Oh, Peggy Marie!\par \par \uc2 PEGGY MARIE TUCKER: Yes!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: By the end of day two, the woman who didn`t want to let go is moving forward.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I love you Peggy Marie and Cullen Jones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Goosebumps! Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good job, Peggy Marie.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She says she feels that her "Never Too Late" fear is conquered.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She is going to takes some lessons this summer. She`s got it all set up. She has got a --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Cullen is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a great coach he is.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: He is so good! He works with Diversity in Aquatics. You can go to DiversityInAquatics.org to find a pool near you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So important!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations, Peggy Marie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are pulling for you Peggy! That will do it for us. Have a great Saint Patrick`s Day!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A leprechaun in the building!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Jerry!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s nice, Jerry --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260317em3h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Clintons Set to Give Epstein Depositions; \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  Admits Affairs, Denies Illegal Acts; Epstein Documents on Trump Accuser May Be Missing; Cuban Troops Kill Four Onboard U.S.-Registered Boat; U.S. and Iran Holding High Stakes Talks in Geneva; FBI Raids Home of L.A. Schools Superintendent; Mother and Son Badly Burned in Massachusetts Explosion; Record Dry Weather Fuels Fire Near Denver; Heart Disease and Stroke on the Rise Among Women; Surgeon General Nominee Grilled by Lawmakers; Fans give Olympic Hero Hughes a Warm Welcome; Video Captures Vince McMahons 100-plus miles per hour Crash; Mexico Violence, Measles Could Impact Spring Break; Author, Jonathan Haidt on the Risks of Social Media; Astronaut Sickened Aboard ISS Reveals Identity; BBC Investigating BAFTA Racial Outburst; The Evolution of Hollywood in America; "Heated Rivalry" Producer on Show`s Popularity; Note to Self; Eliminated Castaways on Show`s Season Premiere; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Nicole Killion,  Nancy Cordes, Charlie D`Agata, Carter Evans, Celine Gounder, Cristian  Benavides, Jo Ling Kent \par \uc2 14,283 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on your Thursday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 We are following new fallout from the Epstein files, including testimony from Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , plus why some files involving Donald Trump are missing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re also learning more about a deadly gun battle off the coast of Cuba, involving U.S. residents.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The Epstein backlash continues with more prominent figures under scrutiny, and there are new questions about missing files.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROBERT GARCIA (D-CA): Why aren`t the documents there? Why have they been removed? And Congress under the Transparency Act and the subpoena have a right to see them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, tension is building in the Caribbean after Cuba says it killed four people aboard a boat that came from Florida.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (U.S. Secretary of State): Suffice it to say it is highly unusual.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  searches the home and office of the Los Angeles Schools Superintendent in a dramatic daytime raid.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They had a guy in camo with a rifle pointing at him.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Jack Hughes, who scored the game winning goal for the men`s team, got a hero`s welcome in his first game back at the \uc2 New Jersey Devils\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  has reached a deal to stay in business. The budget carrier said it has reached an agreement with creditors to exit bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Apparently, the last minute, somebody stepped in with 800 bucks, bought the whole company.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Put your underwear where you put the coffee grounds.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: A \uc2 TikTok\uc2  influencer facing backlash over a so-called hack to wash her underwear while traveling.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: She says, just pop them in the Keurig they keep in your hotel room.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Blow dry those bad boys, and you`ve got yourself a cleaner pair of underwear.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: We aren`t using Keurig in hotels anymore ever again!\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: I was happy to see hotels are getting like \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and nicer coffee pods. You know, like -- it ruined it!\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Are you going to try it out?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: Hell! I mean, heck no!\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think I speak for all of us, that`s a big pass.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Cafe, what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, cafe what? Just think -- the picture is not good. It is not pretty.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with this though. We`ve got some major, major new developments in the fallout from those Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  are set to give depositions to Congress starting today in its probe of the Epstein files. There are also new questions this morning over allegations against President Trump and whether key documents involving his name and an accuser were withheld by the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 There has also been an apology from billionaire, \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  over his association with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is in Chappaqua, New York, where the Clintons will give their depositions.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning. This continues, the fallout continues, and we keep hearing there`s more to come. We`re also hearing that another member of the global elite has become the latest casualty of the Epstein files. What`s this one about?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, that`s right, Gayle and good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 We are learning that the CEO of the Davos Forum has resigned, the latest member of the global elite to face consequences for ties to \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meantime, here in the U.S., the Clintons are set to appear behind closed doors for tape depositions after being threatened with contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Images in the Epstein files show powerful men like President Trump, former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  co-founder \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  in undated photos with women, all three adamantly deny any criminal wrongdoing and have not been charged in connection with Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES (Microsoft co-founder): Every minute I spent with him I regret and I apologize that I did that.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Microsoft mogul apologized to the Gates Foundation staff Tuesday and admitted to having extramarital affairs with two Russian women, but added "I did nothing illicit," in a recording obtained by "The Wall Street Journal."\par \par \uc2 In two 2013 e-mails with the subject line "Bill" that Jeffrey Epstein appears to have sent himself, the convicted sex offender said he had been helping Bill to get drugs in order to deal with consequences of sex with Russian girls, including antibiotics to surreptitiously give to Melinda, Gates` ex-wife spoke earlier this month on an NPR podcast.\par \par \uc2 MELINDA FRENCH GATES (Bill Gates` ex-wife): For me, it`s just sadness. Sadness for you know, I`ve left -- I had -- I left my marriage. I had to leave my marriage. I wanted to leave my marriage.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But the focus now isn`t just whose names appear, it is what some lawmakers say isn`t there.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROBERT GARCIA (D-CA): It`s very clear the evidence is pointing to these documents are missing.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The top Democrat on the House Oversight Committee is raising concerns that documents related to allegations by an Epstein survivor who accused Trump of sexual misconduct may be missing from the documents that were released to the public.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROBERT GARCIA (D-CA): The survivor has made serious allegations of the President and that needs to be investigated.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Something another Epstein survivor, Elizabeth Stein says goes beyond any one person.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH STEIN (Epstein survivor): Any time that we find anything that could potentially be missing, we need to call attention to it, regardless of who it`s about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And Harvard University announced its former president, Larry Summers will relinquish his remaining roles following an internal Epstein review. Summers served as Treasury Secretary under former President Bill Clinton.\par \par \uc2 Former Secretary of State, Hillary Clinton will be deposed here today, while her husband will follow tomorrow -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nikole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to a violent confrontation off the coast of Cuba. Officials in Havana say four U.S. residents were killed after opening fire on Border guards from a speed boat in Cuban waters. Several others were arrested.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at The White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning, so what are we learning about this?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Well, The White House so far hasn`t commented on the incident, but Secretary of State Marco Rubio who was traveling in the Caribbean said the U.S. is working to find out what happened. He called this very unusual.\par \par \uc2 The Cuban government called it an armed attack from a speed boat registered in Florida that Cuba says came within about a mile of its North Central Coast, and then when Cuban Border Guard troops approached the vessel to ask for identification, Cuban authorities say that the men who were on board the boat opened fire, injuring a Cuban commander.\par \par \uc2 Cuban officials say the troops shot back, killing four people aboard the boat, and then arrested the other six. They say everyone on the speed boat was a Cuban national living in the United States, some of them with violent criminal records. It`s not clear if any of them were U.S. citizens as well.\par \par \uc2 Cuba says it seized assault rifles, handguns, Molotov cocktails and bulletproof vests from the boat, and alleges that the group had intentions of carrying out terrorism. This all comes, of course, amid already heightened tensions between the U.S. and Cuba, after President Trump ordered the blockade of nearly all oil shipments to Cuba, even as he says, the diplomatic talks between the two countries continue -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A new round of talks this morning could be the last chance for diplomacy before a potential new American attack on Iran. President Trump is trying to push Iran into signing a deal to give up its nuclear program. Iran`s government has threatened widespread retaliation for any new airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Israel, which could get caught in any crossfire.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Indirect talks began this morning in Geneva with Mideast envoy, Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner, President Trump`s son-in-law heading up negotiation.\par \par \uc2 Iran`s nuclear program may be the focus of today`s meetings, but Secretary of State Marco Rubio says it goes beyond that.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO: Iran possesses a very large number of ballistic missiles, particularly short range ballistic missiles that threaten the United States and our bases in the region.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Last June, during the 12-Day War with Iran, Israeli residents experienced firsthand Iran`s capabilities.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News camera captured the moment missiles penetrated Israel`s vaunted Iron Dome Air Defense System and U.S. interceptors striking residential neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 This is just a hint of the aftermath. These buildings bore the brunt of it, but you really have to take a 360-look between the shrapnel and the pressure of the blast. The damage is everywhere, and this is in the heart of Tel Aviv, one of Israel`s most populated cities.\par \par \uc2 Adi Gaffney (sp?) lives in an adjacent apartment block.\par \par \uc2 That must have been terrifying.\par \par \uc2 ADI GAFFNEY (Tel Aviv, Israel resident): It was insane. It really was insane. And I cannot explain it with words enough. At some point we just had, like heard and felt like a boom!\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: US forces were given advance warning of the Iranian missile attack on America`s largest base in the Middle East last summer, but even then had to respond with the single biggest deployment of Patriot missiles in U.S. history, the Pentagon said.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Now all eyes on those last ditch talks in Geneva. Iran`s Foreign Minister said he is hopeful a deal can be reached. U.S. officials I spoke with this morning say President Trump has been given a number of options that are ready to go if diplomacy fails and he decides to take military action -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Charlie D`Agata reporting from Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Los Angeles, the FBI has raided the home and the office of the school superintendent who oversees the nation`s second largest public school district. Alberto Carvalho is his name. He`s been leading L.A. schools for the past four years and is a long-time critic of the administration, including President Trump`s immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans is following this investigation for us and has the latest.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning to you. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Well, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The FBI says the searches were court authorized, but it`s still unclear exactly what they`re looking for, because the records are sealed.\par \par \uc2 Now, witnesses say federal agents came to Carvalho`s San Pedro home, that`s just outside L.A. just after 6:00 A.M. on Wednesday, with guns drawn and cameras were rolling when several agents were leaving the home with boxes. The FBI also targeted the L.A. Unified School District headquarters.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the nation`s second largest school system, says it is cooperating with the investigation, but it is unclear exactly what wrongdoing, if any, Carvalho is accused of.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement sources tell CBS News, a property linked to Carvalho was also searched in the Miami area. Carvalho served as Miami-Dade School Superintendent for 14 years before coming to Los Angeles in 2022.\par \par \uc2 And a source with direct knowledge of the investigation tells CBS News, federal agents are looking into allegations that Carvalho may have received kickbacks from a business while he was running the Miami school system.\par \par \uc2 CBS news reached out to Carvalho, but we have not heard back -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Carter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning to weather right now, the massive snowfall in Northeast is still causing chaos. Fire crews in Massachusetts struggled to find fire hydrants buried in snow as a three family home went up in flames. Witnesses say they heard a big explosion before the fire. A 25-year-old mother was severely burned after she went back inside to get her two-year-old who was also hurt.\par \par \uc2 The cause of the fire is still under investigation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the West, a wildfire broke out just north of Denver, Wednesday, fueled by dry and windy weather. A high school was evacuated and five people were hurt, including four firefighters.\par \par \uc2 So let`s bring in CBS News` meteorologist, Rob Marciano, who has more on this near record dry weather in that part of the country and where it`s raining right now.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning. A lot going on.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Yes, a lot going on, and really a scary situation yesterday in Denver. Thankfully, they got that under control. But you know, they`ve had very little precip there.\par \par \uc2 For one thing, it`s the least snowiest February on record, at least tied right now with just 13 inches of snow. Meanwhile, in Providence, Rhode Island, it is 68 inches of snow. That`s the snowiest February on record. We know what happened there in the last couple of days.\par \par \uc2 We do have a little bit of rainfall that`s coming into the Southeast. This is an area that also needs the rain, including Florida, where there are red flag warnings up from Orlando out towards Melbourne and Daytona, and there`s some fires burning across parts of Southwest Florida. We talked about that.\par \par \uc2 Some heavy rain today across parts of the mid-Atlantic and the Carolinas and the Southeast, and then eventually getting down into Florida tomorrow night, which we will take any way we can.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about what`s coming in after that. Another front unfortunately, from Canada means a little bit more in the way of colder air, not quite done with winter just yet. This is going to bring some winds to the Great Lakes, including Chicago, Milwaukee, Saginaw back through Detroit tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 As that cooler air comes in, it does have the potential for seeing a little bit more in the way of wintry precipitation as we go towards Sunday morning into Sunday afternoon, maybe another batch coming into Monday. Details here a little bit loose, but it looks a little bit better than when you gave me grief yesterday, Gayle. So, an improvement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Rob Marciano, I take it back. I never give you grief. It does sound a little better today. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: You got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a new warning from the American Heart Association that women -- women -- need to pay attention to this big time.\par \par \uc2 The organization says nearly 60 percent of women in this country could develop some type of cardiovascular disease by 2050, that is a significant jump from the 44 percent of women who are currently living with heart disease.\par \par \uc2 So here to talk about this is CBS News medical contributor and editor-at- large for Public Health at KFF Health News, the lovely Dr. Celine, Gounder.\par \par \uc2 I heard the numbers, and they sound very scary to me. What`s behind it, do you think, the big increase?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Right. So, we are seeing increased rates of high blood pressure, diabetes and obesity, all of which are risk factors for cardiovascular disease. Those conditions are starting earlier, and women are living longer, so that adds up to a longer lifetime risk of developing cardiovascular disease.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always pay attention this because my Dad died at 47, heart attack. Mom died at 60. I`ve outlived both of my parents, so my heart history is very, very bad, but I keep hearing that it`s really bad with Black women in particular.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: That`s right. So, we were just saying by 2050, women in general, you see rates of 60 percent of women having cardiovascular disease. We already have that level of cardiovascular disease in Black women, 60 percent of women over the age of 20 who are Black already have cardiovascular disease.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why? Why is that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, they have higher rates of high blood pressure, diabetes and obesity, which are driving a lot of this, and much of this is also related to the quality of the food you`re eating, ultra-processed food and so on. Do you have time and the ability to exercise on a regular basis, you know? And a lot of this goes back to socioeconomics. Do you have access to these things?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, women of all color, what can we do to protect our heart health? You mentioned a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, pregnancy, young women, I just want to say those are two really big takeaways here. We`re seeing, again, rising rates of obesity, high blood pressure, diabetes in younger women.\par \par \uc2 If you are of age to be pregnant, you are of age to have cardiovascular disease and what we`re seeing is sort of a two-way direction here, where pregnancy is a major stress test in the heart. If you develop whether it`s preeclampsia or gestational diabetes, that is a sign that there`s something wrong.\par \par \uc2 But if you also are planning a pregnancy, you should do everything possible to make sure your blood pressure, your diabetes, your weight are under control heading into that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Celine, let me change topics here.\par \par \uc2 President Trump`s surgeon general nominee, Dr. Casey Means, faced some really sharp questioning on Capitol Hill yesterday during the Senate confirmation hearing.\par \par \uc2 I want to play a little bit of an exchange that she had with Republican senator and physician, Bill Cassidy. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Do you believe that vaccines, whether individually or collectively, contribute to autism?\par \par \uc2 DR. CASEY MEANS (Nominee for U.S. Surgeon-General): The reality is that we have an autism crisis that`s increasing, and this is devastating to many families, and we do not know as a medical community what causes autism, and until we have a clear understanding of why kids are developing this at higher rates, I think we should not leave any stones unturned.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Celine, what do you make of a response? And she`s also facing some scrutiny because she let her medical license lapse, which you say could affect public confidence.\par \par \uc2 So, those are sort of two really important issues that the American people are looking at.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Look, do we need to study what causes autism and how best, especially, to treat it, manage it, support families where somebody has been affected? Absolutely! Absolutely!\par \par \uc2 But whether MMR, measles vaccines cause autism is literally one of the most studied questions in Medicine. And so to spend more taxpayer money studying something that we actually have a very good answer to, well, that`s just a waste of time and money.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And pushing the narrative.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: And pushing the narrative, and to not be clear that, look, we have studied this, asked and answered. Of course, there`s a place for uncertainty and nuance in many public health conversations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: But this is one where you need to be clear, especially in the middle of measles outbreaks around the country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: That you should be getting your MMR vaccine, you should be getting your flu vaccine, that these things actually work and save lives.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And letting your license expire, your medical license, that seems pretty basic. True?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: That`s pretty basic stuff, and it really calls into question, are you keeping up with continuing medical education? She`s a residency dropout. She never finished residency, so she hasn`t actually practiced in the health care system.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very concerning. Dr. Celine Gounder, always great to have you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, newly released video of a car crash involving WWE co-founder, Vince McMahon. Why he told State Troopers he was driving over 100 miles per hour. But first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, the producing team behind the smash hit series, "Heated Rivalry," first on CBS MORNINGS, as they talk about what is next.\par \par \uc2 Plus, in "What to Watch" members of the U.S. women`s hockey team respond to comments from President Trump. We will also talk to both of the people no longer on the island after the "Survivor 50" premiere.\par \par \uc2 Nate, your torch is out. I guess the tribe has spoken.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You were eliminated last night?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no, not of the island. Off the desk. Gayle is the sole survivor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Winner, winner, chicken dinner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So goofy!\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Freshpet. Healthy food fresh from the fridge.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Devil`s fans, lets hear it for Number 86, Jack Hughes!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jack Hughes got a hero`s welcome from his hometown fans last night. The gold medal winner took to the ice with the New Jersey Devils. They were the -- this was the first time since winning the big gold in Milan that he shared the stage with his teammates, and also shared the spotlight with his Team USA teammate, Tage Thompson and they spoke to the crowd.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JACK HUGHES (Team USA Olympic hockey player): I am so proud and I am so happy the men`s and women`s USA hockey teams brought gold medals back to the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hughes said he still gets goosebumps watching his game- winning goal as we all do, and yes, he is still missing parts of his front teeth. Shoutout to him!\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead, psychologist, Jonathan Haidt told us long ago that the internet was putting kids at risk. Guess what? He was right! And now he is back with a new book to show us what he found.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 This caught our eye at 7:30. Police officers raced to a burning apartment building in Silicon Valley last Saturday to rescue a family trapped inside. You can see the officer racing to the scene. Smoke rising from the building, flames in the doorway.\par \par \uc2 Officers told the parents on the second floor to drop their child. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: I`ve got her. Drop her. Drop her. How many other people?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. The three-year-old toddler was okay and just moments later, police actually caught the family dog there, too. Look at that! Fortunately, no one was hurt in the fire. That is some scary like business.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those are heroes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Up to the task.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Once again, they know what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got newly released video of a high speed pursuit where former WWE CEO, that`s Vince McMahon crashed his $300,000.00 Bentley.\par \par \uc2 So this happened back in July. A Connecticut State Trooper was tailing McMahon when he hit another car and smashed into the divider. He said that he was rushing to his -- wow -- to his granddaughter`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Did you see me trying to catch up to you behind you?\par \par \uc2 VINCE MCMAHON (Former WWE CEO): I couldn`t see that. I saw the lights, but it didn`t look normal.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Yes, I`m trying to catch up to you, and you keep taking off.\par \par \uc2 VINCE MCMAHON: No, I am not. I am not trying to outrun you.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: I know, I know, I understand that, but that`s why I was very confused. It wasn`t like you`re running, it was just like you`re going -- you almost hit I think 115 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Very lucky that day, no one was badly hurt here. McMahon was ticketed for reckless driving.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After this long and cold winter, many people may be looking for some warm weather during spring break, and rightfully so, but the recent wave of violence on Mexico`s West Coast have some people worried about new attacks in popular destinations, and in this country, there`s a new health scare in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides has more on the safety concerns this spring break season.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: As unrest erupted across Mexico this week, hotels were locked down. Video showed local businesses on fire. Flights in and out of popular tourist destinations were cancelled.\par \par \uc2 Even as things return to normal, the U.S. State Department says travelers should exercise increased caution throughout Mexico, with stricter warnings for specific states, including Jalisco, where resort town, Port-au-Prince Vallarta is located.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN COFFEY (Travel risk expert): It shows how fluid issues can be any time we travel.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Travel risk expert, Kevin Coffey says vacationers should sign up for State Department Alerts, share their locations with a loved one and purchase travel insurance that covers evacuations and cancellations.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN COFFEY: You really have to do your research on exactly what`s going on in that destination and will it impact where you`re going to be traveling into or traveling out of.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Another travel concern, health risks.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Aileen Marty is an infectious disease expert in Florida, the state currently has the third most measles cases nationwide.\par \par \uc2 DR. AILEEN MARTY (Infectious disease expert in Florida): People who are coming to areas where there is an outbreak of measles, whether it`s South Florida or Cancun, they should make sure that they are fully vaccinated before they come.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from City of Miami Beach T.V.)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Miami Beach is trying to curb concerns with its latest spring break campaign.\par \par \uc2 ERIC CARPENTER (Miami Beach City manager): We are making a deliberate pivot away from a party-centric spring break of the past.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The city previously discouraged all spring breakers, but is welcoming visitors back this year with new measures to keep crowds safe.\par \par \uc2 MITCH NOVICK (Sherbrooke Hotel owner): I`m cautiously optimistic.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Sherbrooke Hotel owner, Mitch Novick says the new approach will hopefully attract rule-abiding visitors.\par \par \uc2 The new message is, let`s try to bring some people back.\par \par \uc2 MITCH NOVICK: The people are going to -- are coming here. It`s just, are they going to be well-behaved, or is it going to be absolute chaos?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Previously, Miami Beach has had rowdy crowds, has been overrun by spring breakers, often leading to dangerous shootings and fights.\par \par \uc2 So, the crowd that Miami Beach is going after this year is one that is a beach a bit more health conscious. They`ve got a number of events planned, including outdoor activities, things like marathons and workout events at their outdoor gym known as Muscle Beach -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s hope it works.\par \par \uc2 Cristian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, author, Jonathan Haidt is here to talk about big lawsuits against Big Tech and what they can mean for your family.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You may have heard there are multiple legal cases underway alleging social media platforms like Instagram, YouTube, TikTok and Snap are designed to be addictive and can be harmful to minors, so what do you need to know about the dangers.\par \par \uc2 Our guest, Jonathan Haidt is the co-author of a new book written specifically for young readers called, "The Amazing Generation: Your Guide to Fun and Freedom in a Screen-Filled World." He is also a social psychologist who studies the effects of smartphones and social media on development.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT (Social psychologist and "The Amazing Generation" co- author): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so you`ve done extensive research on the impacts of social media, specifically around a child`s development. Do you think that these cases will change the behavior of these social media companies?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: Oh, yes. Because there is a gigantic hole in our legal system in our society. If there was any other consumer product that was used by 95 percent of all children, that had contributed to the deaths of thousands of children that had addicted millions of children, at some point, they would have been sued into oblivion or forced to change, and that never happened because of an oddity of an American law passed in 1996, we can`t sue them for what the companies show us, but what the new lawsuits are saying is, okay, fine, but we can sue you for the design. You guys made this addictive, we have memos, we have e-mails, we have internal stuff. We know you did this to hook our kids to go after preteens and so take responsibility.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the tech companies know what they`re doing. They know how addictive their product is.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They know.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: They know, and we know that in two ways. One is again, all of these memos where they talk about it, they use the word "addiction" in their own internal discussions, and the others just look at their behavior.\par \par \uc2 Where do the -- where do the moguls of Silicon Valley send their kids to school? They often send them to the Waldorf School or other schools, specifically because there`s no tech.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: And you know, video game makers, some of them don`t let their own kids play video games. They design it for addiction. They want your kids to get addicted. They don`t want their kids to get addicted.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let me just read a statement from Google, the parent company of YouTube to the CBS News. They say: "The allegations are simply not true," and a Meta spokesperson told us, "The evidence will show, the plaintiff "... faced many significant, difficult challenges well before she ever used social media." This is regarding the plaintiff and TikTok and Snap have settled out of court.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: That`s right. So this is so -- Meta is really the main offender here, in that we have so much evidence against Meta in their own words, and what I find particularly revolting about Meta is their main defense is, it`s just a correlation. Like, okay, people use social media a lot are more depressed, that`s true, but this didn`t cause that.\par \par \uc2 And then they try to use the same strategy on the parents, that is, they try to say, yes, your daughter spent 16 hours a day, you know, on Instagram. But you know, that`s just correlated with her death. She was actually depressed before.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is those pre-existing things you have to worry about. It`s not our fault is really what they are saying.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: Exactly! Always the parents fault or the kids fault. No, it`s the goddamn company`s fault!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, parents are sitting at home, and they might be delighted to hear that, because most of the time they hear that it`s their fault, parents, here`s what you`re not doing, here`s what you should be doing. And I know that you had a previous book to this that was a bestseller, and it was "The Anxious Generation" that was for the parents --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still a bestseller.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- still a bestseller.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still on the list, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And this is for teens and tweens. So, what is your message to young people that are watching, because, surprisingly, there are teenagers that get a lot of our content now that we are split across all these social media verticals.\par \par \uc2 So, what is your message to them that are dealing with this a very addictive form of entertainment?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: That`s right. So in this book, so lots of parents asked us, asked me for like, Is there something I can give to my kids? And so I teamed up with Catherine Price, and we wrote this book. And it`s not like, you know, old professors saying kids get off your phones. A lot of it is, we`ve got quotes from older teens saying, "I lost my childhood. I missed out on childhood."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got you!\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: All of these kids have seen movies that took place in the 80s and 90s. They know there used to be a thing called childhood, where kids were out having fun and adventures. That`s been gone for 15 to 20 years now, and this book is about getting it back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, you`re saying it`s more than just giving up the phone.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What --\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: You can`t just give up the phone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t just -- but what you want us to do is what?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: Reclaim childhood in the real world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say play based childhood, not a phone based --\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: That`s right. Humans are mammals. All mammal babies play. We have to play to wire up our brains, that`s how you become an adult. And ever since 2012, which is the year when most kids had a smartphone and social media, life has become this, and the brain doesn`t wire up, right if you spend half your day doing this.\par \par \uc2 And most of our kids spend half of their free time, or more of their free time doing this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of the things that struck me in the book is when you said this tech is just as damaging as we were warned about tobacco back in the day. You know, they used to do candy cigarettes. We thought it was so cool to do that. You said, this is just as damaging as that?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: Yes. So, if we`re looking at actual death toll, no, not as many people have committed suicide and drug overdoses from Snapchat drugs as tobacco. But if we look at the number of lives diminished, the number of people whose lives are now worse and will be worse permanently, for many of them, that is possibly most children. That is the short -- now we`re learning.\par \par \uc2 In the book, I focused on mental health, and especially girls, and it`s not most girls, but it`s about a third are harmed by it, roughly, but the short form videos, I now believe are harming the majority of children born on this planet since the late 1990s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you even say, don`t let your kids do short form videos.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIDT: Yes, that would be an absolute advice to parents. You know, look stories, long videos, movies are good. I`m not saying no stories, but the short form, I would say zero. No TikTok at all, no Instagram reels, no YouTube shorts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jonathan Haidt, really important information for parents out there. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 "The Amazing Generation" is on sale right now. Scan that Q.R. code on your screen to buy it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, up next, the stories you`ll be talking about in "What to Watch." Right now, it is 7:44 time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead in our next hour, we hope you caught the premiere of "Survivor" season 50 last night. It was incredible. First on CBS MORNINGS, we love when that happens, we`ll talk with the two castaways who left the island. How are they feeling today?\par \par \uc2 Plus, what is next for the smash hit show -- have you seen it -- "Heated Rivalry."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another first on CBS MORNINGS, we are talking to the producing team right here in the studio. So, that`s coming up in our next hour, but right now, Claus and I are heading over the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you get some more advice from Jonathan Haidt?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, tons of advice for Celine. We`re going to take a quick break. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes! Dolla` Dolla` Bill, y`all starting with "What to Watch" with some bad news.\par \par \uc2 You`re hearing, of course, the Wu-Tang Clan, I was so excited that they were nominees for the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame. Well, this morning, we found out that founding member, Oliver "Power" Grant died on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Grant was the driving force behind the group and also founded the popular Wu Wear fashion brand. Wu-Tang was nominated for the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame yesterday, of course, so far, no word on the cause of death.\par \par \uc2 Oliver "Power" Grant was 52. All the members of Wu-Tang have been paying tribute to him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s hard. He was 52.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was instrumental -- yes -- in building that group to what it is today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So young.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very young.\par \par \uc2 Okay, moving on to the members of the U.S. hockey team. They`re talking now about President Trump`s joke that did not land well for some.\par \par \uc2 The men`s team visited The White House Tuesday after an invite from the President. In a phone call to the U.S. locker room after Sunday`s final game, the President seemed to put down the U.S. women`s team, which had also won gold. Let`s listen to what the President said.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R) (President of the United States of America): We`ll do The White House the next day. We`ll just have some fun. We have medals for you guys, and we have to -- I must tell you, we have to bring the woman`s team. You do know that.\par \par \uc2 If we don`t do that, I do believe I probably would be impeached. Okay?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so the men are taking some heat for laughing along with the President at that joke at the expense of the women`s hockey team.\par \par \uc2 Jack Hughes says people are making something out of nothing, and both teams support each other. Yesterday, his teammate Jeremy Swayman and the women`s team captain, Hillary Knight, the legendary Hillary Knight, said this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEREMY SWAYMAN (Team USA Olympic hockey player): We should have reacted differently. We know that we are so excited for the women`s team. We have so much respect for the women`s team, and to share that gold medal with them is something that we`re forever grateful for.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY KNIGHT (Team USA Olympic hockey player): There`s a genuine level of support there and respect, and I think that`s being overshadowed by sort of a quick lapse. I think this is just a really good learning point to really focus on, you know, how we talk about women, not only in sport, but in industry. Women aren`t less than and our achievements shouldn`t be overshadowed by any anything else, other than how great they are.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Period! Hillary Knight, a legend.\par \par \uc2 The majority of the U.S. men`s team, of course, attended the State of the Union earlier this week. The women were invited, they declined. They are being celebrated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can see why the women are being -- I could see why they`re offended by it. I get it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I like him saying that we should have responded differently.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I will say this. There was somebody in the background that was somebody in the background that was yelling, two for two.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I heard that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two for two, which is basically saying we went two for two in the gold medals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Stanley Tucci, Megan Thee Stallion, and talking about, boy, he`s been Flavor Flav --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Flavor Flav, that`s right, no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 All right, the creator and executive producer of "Heated Rivalry."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, the one and only, Diane Warren shares a Note to Self she has -- listen to this -- 17 Oscar nominations, go Diane -- for some of the most iconic songs in movies, and she has got a great story to tell.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King, that`s Nate Burleson, and that`s Vlad Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody`s excited for this. The producing team of the massive hit show, "Heated Rivalry," they are here today, first on CBS MORNINGS. They`re talking about what`s next for the series.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN (Songwriter): Dear 13-year-old Diane --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Songwriter, Diane Warren has 17 Oscar nominations, and she`s written many iconic songs, but she`s also faced some big struggles along the way. We will have her powerful Note to Self.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor 50"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And first on CBS MORNINGS, meet the two people eliminated from last night`s premiere of "Survivors" season 50. Wait until you see who they are.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor 50"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But we begin this hour with a shocking confrontation about a mile off the coast of Cuba. Four people are dead and at least six wounded in what Cuban authorities say was a shootout with a speedboat carrying U.S. residents. They say it happened in Cuban waters, and the people in the speedboat fired at Border guards first.\par \par \uc2 Cuban officials say the boat was carrying 10 people who had assault rifles, handguns, Molotov cocktails and bulletproof vests. Havana says those on board were trying to "carry out an infiltration for terrorist purposes."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has not confirmed those details. Secretary of State Marco Rubio says an investigation is underway.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right now to an update on a story that we brought to you last month. A mission in space that was cut short because an astronaut needed urgent medical care.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, we learned the name of that astronaut. Fifty-eight-year-old, Mike Finck. He revealed that he was the crew member aboard the International Space Station who needed to come home mid-January. Now, this was the first time NASA had ever ended a mission early for health reasons.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Finck did not say what his medical issue was. He wrote, "I`m grateful to share that the results are very reassuring and that I`m firmly on the path to a complete recovery. The decision to shorten the mission was deliberate and thoughtful, ensuring I could receive that comprehensive evaluation on the ground. It was the right call."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re glad he`s going to be all right. We still don`t know what was wrong, but glad he`s going to be okay.\par \par \uc2 There is new fallout from a controversial moment at the BAFTA Awards in London on Sunday. A man with Tourette syndrome shouted a racial slur at Michael B. Jordan and Delroy Lindo while they were on stage and people watching the BBC`s coverage of the event, everybody heard it.\par \par \uc2 The BBC has told its executive complaints unit to carry out a fast-tracked investigation, saying the broadcaster made a serious mistake by not editing out the remark. You think?\par \par \uc2 John Davidson, the man who shouted this slur has apologized. He says it was a verbal tic that he cannot control. He is the subject of the film, "I Swear."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It sounds like they`re trying to do the right thing to figure out what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t know that you need a full on investigation. Just got to figure out who made the call.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And let the people know, because let`s not forget, the BBC is a state-funded broadcaster.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, they are held accountable by the people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they had plenty of time to edit out the remark.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, because it is delayed, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t make any sense. By like two hours.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. We can appreciate the apology.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But at the same time, you can`t make people feel bad for being offended, if they are --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- by something that is despicable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or were offended.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and were offended, for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead first on CBS MORNINGS, the two men behind the smash T.V. hit, "Heated Rivalry." They will tell us about the effort to keep their vision of the series intact.\par \par \uc2 And we will talk to the first two castaways eliminated from "Survivor 50: In the Hands of the Fans," which debuted last night.\par \par \uc2 But first it is 8:03, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, it is Hooray for Hollywood! It`s our latest edition of "USA to Z." We`re going to talk to some big names, including actor Sean Aston, about the future of showbiz.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Netflix CEO, Ted Sarandos is set to visit The White House today as the bidding war for Warner Bros Discovery heats up.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s unclear if Sarandos will meet with President Trump. Netflix`s bid to take over the entertainment giant is in flux after a new offer from Paramount Skydance, the parent company of CBS. So, it`s fitting that the subject of our America 250 series "USA to Z" this morning is H for Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent explores its history and future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In so many ways, Hollywood is iconic, from the sign to the Walk of Fame to the studio lots where dreams of making it big spring to life.\par \par \uc2 SEAN ASTON (Actor and SAG-AFTRA President): Hollywood is actually like a physical place, but it`s also an idea, and the idea has lit the world for a hundred years.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Lord of the Rings: Return of a King"/New Line Cinema/Wingnut Films)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: But in 2026, that idea is changing rapidly, says longtime actor and Screen Actors Guild president, Sean Aston.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rudy"/Columbia Tristar)\par \par \uc2 SEAN ASTON: I think it`s pretty easy to say that most of us have developed very short attention spans, but sometimes you want to stop scrolling and sit down in a movie theater and watch something together.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Casablanca"/Warner Bros)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Titanic"/Paramount Pictures/20th Century Fox/Lighthouse Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The movies and celebrity culture became one of our most important exports, shaping the idea of America overseas.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "LalaLand"/Lionsgate)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Legendary film critic, Leonard Maltin.\par \par \uc2 LEONARD MALTIN (Film critic): Well, in some ways, the evolution of Hollywood is the evolution of technology, and it`s often on the cutting edge of technology.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Avengers: Endgame"/Marvel Studios/Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures)\par \par \uc2 LEONARD MALTIN: Look at CGI, computer generated imagery. No one could have predicted the impact that Netflix would have. They`ve really disrupted business as usual.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: How would you describe the state of Hollywood right now?\par \par \uc2 TED SARANDOS (Netflix co-CEO): I think there`s anxiety, and there should be.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Ted Sarandos is the co-CEO of Netflix.\par \par \uc2 TED SARANDOS: We are intentionally located right now in the center of Hollywood. The idea of this was, is that we wanted to kind of plant a flag and say that we are going to be part of the next century of Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 Technology has kind of driven every advancement in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The pandemic and recent strikes have sent many Hollywood jobs into thin air, and some leading figures warn that A.I. could further erode livelihoods here.\par \par \uc2 People believe it`s a dream come true to work in T.V. and movies, but that`s becoming harder. Is the entertainment industry dying here?\par \par \uc2 TED SARANDOS: Absolutely not. Every development requires a skilled team of people to do the work. Those people are here in California.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: But so many people are saying to us, we`re not getting enough work these days.\par \par \uc2 TED SARANDOS: I think what happens is there`s been a lot of competition for production and California, with all of its history and all of its legacy, they also have to compete more. Georgia and New Jersey most recently.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE COLLINS (Filmmaker and actor): Hollywood is shifting.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Filmmaker and actor, Nicole Collins and her daughter, Aadyn Encalarde, who recently appeared in "Sinners" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sinners"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: -- just moved back to New Orleans from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE COLLINS: I knew that coming back home was the right choice for us mentally, emotionally, financially. Los Angeles feels like it`s even dried up.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: But for Sarandos, making movies and T.V. shows anywhere in America is what matters.\par \par \uc2 TED SARANDOS: No one in the world is better than the United States at telling the world stories. It`s also really rooted in our own First Amendment. I think that freedom that this country stands for has led to the great storytelling that has led to this great industry.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Transformers"/Paramount/Dreamworks Pictures)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: However, Hollywood transforms the future, it is that ideal that keeps so many people watching.\par \par \uc2 What do you hope Hollywood will bring to Americans going forward?\par \par \uc2 SEAN ASTON: I hope Hollywood continues to be an idea that lights the world of inspiration, with joy, with meaning.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends with Benefits"/Screen Gems/Sony Pictures)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jo Ling Kent in Hollywood, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What a great piece!\par \par \uc2 I am cautiously optimistic. I want to believe what Ted Sarandos says. You know, there`s always been disruption in the film industry. Famously, when there was sound added to films, that disrupted the industry, people thought it was the end of movies --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- as we knew them then in the silent era. And of course, it led when color came in, when wide 35 millimeter came in. It`s all been disruptive, and it`s better for us, the viewers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m a big Ted Sarandos fan. Something tells me he knows what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 To be continued, for sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up Jacob Tierney and Brendan Brady, the producing team behind that wildly popular series, "Heated Rivalry" -- have you seen it? Yes, you have. They`re joining us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Can`t wait. When we come back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The wildly popular series, "Heated Rivalry" is taking over your T.V.s and social media. It follows rising Hawking stars, Shane Hollander and Ilya Rosanov.\par \par \uc2 The story begins with a secret fling between the two rivals and eventually turns into a years` long journey of love, denial and self-discovery.\par \par \uc2 So the series is based on the "Game Changers" book series. It`s written by Rachel Reid. It has been so popular, it has averaged more than -- listen to this number -- 10.5 million viewers per episode --whoa -- in this country alone. And get this, according to the ticket selling platform, Seat Geek, weekly ticket sales for National Hockey League games increased 20 percent after the series release.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look at the show.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jacob Tierney is the creator. He`s the executive producer. He`s the director. What can this guy can`t do? He`s also the writer of "Heated Rivalry."\par \par \uc2 Brendan Brady, no slouch either. He`s an executive producer of this series, and we`re happy to say, they join us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Welcome and congratulations, guys. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY ("Heated Rivalry" executive producer, director and creator): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY ("Heated Rivalry" executive producer): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m trying -- I said I`m going to try not to drool, because I love this show so much. I watch it because of all the heat, I go. What is this about? I don`t think it`s for me. I watched the first episode, then I watched them all.\par \par \uc2 But it started with a book. I didn`t even know that, Jacob, you read the book. What did you see that you thought this could be something?\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Well, there -- so there`s kind of two answers to that. One, I love the book. I became a fan right away. It was one of the first romance books that I read, and I really -- I just really liked it.\par \par \uc2 Rachel is a great writer. It`s funny, it`s sexy, but it`s also really sweet and tender.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: And then I also -- what I basically did, what I said to Brendan, once this kind of occurred to us to do is romance is the kind of I.P. -- everyone`s looking for I.P. in Hollywood, right? What`s an intellectual property that we can get?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Intellectual property.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: And romance is really underserved. There`s not a lot going on, and there was definitely none in this space. And this was a Canadian book. We`re Canadian producers, and we thought we could maybe -- this could be something, because we -- what we knew is we would be the only ones out there peddling a gay, steamy hockey romance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hockey story. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Every one of those words makes it less likely that it gets made.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And when you make the pitch, Brendan, what is the initial reaction? Because I don`t think you had a lot of takers in the beginning, did you?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: Yes, so when Jacob pitched me, I was taken back because we had started our company focused on comedy, but ultimately we also really liked the idea of genre, and romance is just a great genre, and it was underserved in a way that we immediately saw like there was something there.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: And ultimately, you get into business with creatives.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: You want to trust their visions. And Jacob was really excited about this. And I said, okay, if this is what you want to focus on, let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But so many people said, no, you have to make changes. Weren`t you told you have to make changes. And you said, no, that`s okay.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Yes. What was interesting about the show was that there was a lot of lean-in factor. You know, you pitch it again, like what I said before, no one else is pitching the show.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of people were like, tell me more about that. And then when they would read it, they were like, oh, you weren`t kidding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: And I was like, no, I wasn`t kidding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: And so we did. We definitely had a lot of dancing to the edge of doing it with people who then backed away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Is that the way --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: I don`t know --\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: I`m trying -- I was told don`t make double entendres, so I`m trying to focus on single entendres here, if I can.\par \par \uc2 And yes, so we did -- but we really did find the right partners in Crave, in our home broadcaster who I`ve been working with, we`ve both been working with for many years.\par \par \uc2 I did a show called "Letterkenny" with them for a long time and they trusted us. They trusted -- you know, they trusted me, but they also trusted the material and the audience that already loved this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Because what struck me was that there`s a lot of people who think that they`re smarter than the audience that loves the book, and I don`t think they are and I wanted to do a fairly -- it is a very faithful adaptation of the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But why do you think, guys, that it resonates so well with so many different types of people -- Black, White, male, female, gay, straight -- what do you think it is? I know what it is for me. What do you think it is?\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: I think is ultimately because it`s very romantic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: I think that`s what it is, is that it has the hallmarks and trappings of a real classic romance, years of longing and yearning and trying to get together and do the right thing for each other, and not being able to and then eventually a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 And so I do think that that you know, and it`s -- and what the show is that I think, is a rarity in terms of a televised version of a romance is that it is laser focused on that love story. There`s not a lot of diversion from it, and I think that`s what the audience responds to, is kind of the intensity and the focus of the storytelling, because it`s just -- you`re really watching Shane and Ilya miss each other and then meet each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Meet together and come together.\par \par \uc2 What did you think, though, Brendan, when it started to click? Because I just think that it seemed to come out of nowhere for me. All of a sudden, everybody was talking about it, and it`s my understanding, you guys were literally working on it 10 days before we first saw it.\par \par \uc2 So when it first started click, what were you thinking?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: Well, I was thinking we`ve got to get another episode in the can so that we can now keep this momentum going, because we were delivering episodes 10 days before they were going online.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: So, I think it was -- it was wild to be in this moment where we`re just trying to make sure that we`re getting the best possible show out to the fans.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heated Rivalry"/Crave/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: And then when we finally delivered and that sixth episode aired, we had a moment to kind of look around and say, oh my God, I can`t believe this is happening.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: And I remember very early on, Jacob, after second -- the second episode or third episode, he looked at me and said -- I thought -- I forgot that this is going to keep happening every week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: Because we`re so used to, you know, delivering streaming shows, which all just drop on the same day.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why are you guys making us wait so long for season two? I`m really starting to feel irritated about this.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: I know, I know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As lovely as you are, Jacob.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: I`m doing it to you on purpose.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, as is your way.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: As is my way. Listen, as I said, I mean, like, you know, the reason is because we didn`t know we were going to sell this show. We didn`t know anybody was going to watch it.\par \par \uc2 I had not written the second season. I did not know I was going to get a second season, but it`s all happening. We`re writing it now. We`re getting ready to start shooting this summer, and there will be more "Heated Rivalry" on your T.V.s as like, truly, as soon as humanly possible.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: And as I like to say, I want to say this right to the camera, which camera --\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Give him a camera, which camera? You`ve got to give him one.\par \par \uc2 It`s three.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: Hey, like the best parts of this show, just enjoy the yearn.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you can enjoy going to the cottage, because, according to Airbnb, you can spend the time in the cottage.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And?\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Thanks, Airbnb.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: Thank you, Airbnb.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You guys start shooting in April.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: No, no. We start shooting in August.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: August. Okay. April 2027, cannot wait! Jacob, Brendan, congratulations. I can`t --\par \par \uc2 JACOB TIERNEY: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BRADY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see what you do.\par \par \uc2 All episodes of "Heated Rivalry" is streaming right now on HBO Max.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is Tony Braxton with the classic "Unbreak My Heart" written by the iconic, Diane Warren.\par \par \uc2 Warren`s resume is incredible. It`s so hard to believe we really just had to show you her many accomplishments. We are talking massive hits for legends like Barbra Streisand, Aretha Franklin, Elton John, Cher Tina Turner, Gloria Estefan, Celine Dion, Mariah Carey, NSYNC, Rihanna, Justin Bieber, and that is the short list, folks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 Warren`s songs have earned her an incredible 17 Oscar nominations. Her latest called "Dear Me" is actually a letter to her 13-year-old self, and it is perfect for our "Note to Self" series.\par \par \uc2 Let`s listen to what she says about her amazing career and her unlikely path.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: Dear 13-year-old, Diane, I know you feel alone and that you don`t really belong. You`ve always felt different, and kids are not very kind to those who don`t fit in. You never have.\par \par \uc2 Don`t worry, this will be your superpower. You don`t know it now, but those who don`t fit in end up being the ones who stand out.\par \par \uc2 You`re sitting in your room listening to the radio. You hate school, doesn`t everybody? The radio is your school. You are listening and learning because you want to be a songwriter. Not want to be, you are a songwriter.\par \par \uc2 You sit in your room with that little guitar your dad brought you from Tijuana, and you write and write and write. This is what makes you feel alive. You don`t know it now, but it always will be.\par \par \uc2 There will be hard times and a lot of challenges. You will be kicked out of schools, put in juvenile hall after your parents catch you with pot, made fun of, bullied and molested, but somehow young me, you`ll get through it. Writing songs will get you through it.\par \par \uc2 Music will always be your safe space.\par \par \uc2 Your mom doesn`t understand. She`s more of a realist than a dreamer. Your dad does because he`s more of a dreamer than a realist. You`ll realize someday how lucky you were to have both of them, one to prove wrong and one to prove right.\par \par \uc2 You will also prove all those main kids wrong and have a lot of fun doing that.\par \par \uc2 You will realize proving people wrong is one of your favorite things to do.\par \par \uc2 Dear 13-year-old me, you`re going to do all right, kid.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rhythm of the Night"/El DeBarge)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: After years of handing out demo tapes and just trying to get heard, you will get your first big break after writing "Rhythm of the Night" for DeBarge.\par \par \uc2 You will go from chasing your dream to actually getting paid for it, but you will soon learn the publishing deal you signed wasn`t great at all. You will get no share of the publishing income from your chart topping song, and will only get paid $200.00 a week.\par \par \uc2 Not being in control of your songs and still needing your dad`s help to make a living will be extremely discouraging, young Diane. It will be hard, but you will have people who believe in you in your corner.\par \par \uc2 They will encourage you to start your own publishing company, Real Songs. This will be the best thing that ever happened to you.\par \par \uc2 You will own all your songs from that moment on, and everything will begin to shift. Your music will travel far beyond that little room you started writing in. You will start to feel heard.\par \par \uc2 Some of the world`s greatest artists will be singing your songs. Celine, Dion --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "These are Special Times" T.V. Special)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: Whitney, Houston,\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Learned From the Best"/Whitney Houston/Arista Records)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: Aerosmith.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Don`t Want to Miss a Thing"/Aerosmith/Columbia Records)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Was Here" (Live at Roseland)/Beyonce)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: And, of course, Cher.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "If I Could Turn Back Time"/Cher/Geffen Records)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: These songs will become classics. One day you`ll give Cher a demo with your vocals of the song, "If I Could Turn Back Time." She`s going to hate it, but keep being relentless, like you always have been and always will be. You`ll eventually convince her by holding down her leg in the studio until she agrees to try it out.\par \par \uc2 The rest is history, right Cher?\par \par \uc2 You will write a song for a film about love, but it will also be an opportunity to thank your Dad for always believing in you. That song will morph into a soundtrack for countless father and daughter dances and so many weddings.\par \par \uc2 Remember when I told you, music will always be your safe space. Well, that feeling will be proven even more so when you write till it happens to you for Lady Gaga. It will bring up unexpected emotions for you, but it will also be a very healing song for you. And guess what? A very healing song for a lot of other people as well, especially when you watch Lady Gaga sing your song with other survivors of sexual assault on stage at the Oscars, you realize what a safe space a song can be.\par \par \uc2 In your 60s, you will write one of your most personal songs, "Dear Me." Kesha, will sing it for a documentary about your life. I wrote it for you, 13-year-old Diane.\par \par \uc2 You`re starting the most difficult time in your life. I know you`re at your most miserable right now, but like I wrote in the song, it`ll get better soon, you`ll see.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dear Me"/Kesha/Kemosabe Records)\par \par \uc2 DIANE WARREN: "Dear Me" will speak to so many people and will even be nominated for an Academy Award. It will be your 17th nomination.\par \par \uc2 You won`t believe this, but you will become the most nominated female songwriter in the Academy`s history. They will even give you an Honorary Oscar, the first time they`ve ever given that to a songwriter.\par \par \uc2 You will dedicate that award to your Mom, who wanted you to be a secretary, and your dad who always believed in your dream. You`re going to go through a lot of challenges you don`t think you`re going to get through, but guess what? You`ll get through it.\par \par \uc2 You are both stronger than you even know. If I could turn back time -- see what I did there? I tell you, you`re in for a sometimes bumpy but wonderful ride.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Diane Warren is such a force. You see 17 Oscar nominations. I`m hoping that this is the year for her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m hoping this is the year she gets such a well-deserved Oscar.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like what she says, radio was her high school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And she was relentless, and that music can be a safe space. We all love music, and we know that. Music is a safe space.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like, "Those who don`t fit in, are the ones that stand out." Proving people wrong is her favorite thing to do.\par \par \uc2 Diane, we are pulling for you. I hope this is your year for you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And celebrate you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We all --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also, we all know that those "Note to Self" pieces are hard to do because it`s no track. Producer, Sumitra, we`re talking about you -- Batalia -- bravo, bravo! Camera, audio, Luke Thomas, Don Hale, bravo! Editor, Brian Cunningham, editing always, always sells a piece. You can have great stuff, but if it`s not edited properly --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Shout out to Cunningham. He does this stuff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt, I am a little bias.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chris Stover, Maureen Honeymeyer, a lot of people make this happen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little bias, but we have the best people working here at CBS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do say that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I just wanted to throw that out there.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you caught last night`s "Survivor 50" season premiere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will have the first to be eliminated in studio, coming up next, and you`ll never guess who.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Last night, "Survivor" returned for the premier of its 50th season right here on CBS. And you know, we are really into the show as you can tell by the set, guys. The set looks so good.\par \par \uc2 It was filled with twists and turns and surprised and, of course, a tribal council. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor 50"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good luck, guys. I`d say it with my teeth clenched, good luck, guys! Jenna was not the only castaway who was eliminated, we`re sorry to say. And a surprise -- this was a surprise. Kyle Fraser was medically evacuated after hurting his Achilles tendon during the immunity challenge.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor 50"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh man!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jenna Lewis Dougherty and Kyle Fraser join us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both.\par \par \uc2 Kyle, I felt for you because we could tell you did not want to go.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER (Eliminated "Survivor" contestant): No, not at all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve had the surgery.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Yes, I got surgery.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Yes, I`m running three weeks ago, so we`re still recovering, but we`re on up and up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, hold that thought for just a second. Jenna, I really want to say to you, you were robbed.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was -- I was screaming at my television. And I love when you said, "Good luck, guys," because after that you also said, "I hope they lose every challenge." It sounds like Petty Bitty to me.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Heck yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I understand. Were you surprised? I was very surprised.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: I wasn`t, I wasn`t -- I think I felt like I was playing from the bottom the second we landed.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor 50"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: It felt like there were these already made connections that I didn`t have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alliances.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Yes, so I did feel that. That`s why I was trying really hard to get Savannah and Joe, because I felt like those were probably newer players, the people that didn`t have as many connections, like I`ve been out of this game for 22 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but when, when the elimination comes, are you mad? Did you cry? Did you -- what did you do?\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: I didn`t cry. No, I didn`t -- I walked out and I was like, I damned them, you know, I hexed them. I was like, "Lose everything." That`s what I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What about you, Kyle? I was wondering if you cried because you we could see the pain on your face, not so much from the injury, but you did not want to give up.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Yes, you know, it`s tough, because two weeks earlier I had won. The whole world got to see me win a million dollars.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor 50"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: And then to just tear your Achilles two weeks later, and know that I get pulled from the game only four days in, it was heartbreaking. I loved playing with my tribe.\par \par \uc2 I was optimistic at first, but that first night, when I was in my hotel room. I mean, I cried like I hadn`t cried before, and I just -- I missed out on the experiences.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: When you look at that first episode, there was just so many cool things that happened in the game, and I was excited to experience a lot of that myself. And, you know, I`ve had tons of other experiences after, but it was a tough one to swallow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you hiding out all this time? Because you guys finished in July, right? So, you`ve had to carry this secret now until now. Have you been hiding all this time? What have you been doing?\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: So, I had to come back earlier than the whole cast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: So I had to come back and get surgery immediately because when you tear your Achilles, you can`t start recovering until you they actually tie it back together again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that true, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is asking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a football injury right there.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: It`s athlete`s injury, right? You know, me and Tyreese, but I had to hide basically in my apartment because I couldn`t let people see me on my scooter and my boot while everyone else was still on the island. But, you know, I got tons of quality time. I just got married, so my wife --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh! Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is having his first baby.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congrats!\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Thank you. Thank you. Daughter coming in May.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Gayle is a great name!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: My mom would love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gayle Fraser.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: My mom would love that, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Jenna, you were voted out for playing and scheming very, very hard taking aim at Cirie. Did you think heading into the Tribal Council that you were -- and to me, what you were doing is how you win the game.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is how you play the game.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is how you play the game.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you played so well.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: And if you`re going to go out, I`ll go out playing too hard, as opposed to, like, rolling over and dying. So, I was playing that hard, because I did kind of feel like I was on the bottom. And I did feel like Cirie and Ozzy could, but, you know, could vote for me, but I really did feel like Joe and Savannah might really understand that they`re on that bottom, too.\par \par \uc2 So we did have those conversations. They just didn`t listen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but were you surprised when they voted against you? Because I thought --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, I was surprised.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cirie had serious mistakes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right?\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Yes. I think that when I heard that all of the names -- as soon as I saw Cirie written in my name, I was like all the rest are mine. I knew it! I knew it at my core.\par \par \uc2 But I think that with only three days, I think they were kind of feeling on the outs, too. So they weren`t sure what to do. They were just trying to stay one more day themselves, like I could have thrown Savannah under the bus and I could have tried to stay three more days, but they were trying to be --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Maybe you should have!\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Maybe I should.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Vlad, you`re cold-blooded, brother.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Give Vlad out there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want to win!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt about it. Speaking of winning, Kyle, you are one of three previous winners, and we saw in that premiere episode Dee and Savannah, they already had targets on their backs.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, in football terms, when you win the Super Bowl, that next season, every single team is gunning for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There are no days off. Did you feel that type of energy? And then, how does that approach the way you play "Survivor."\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Yes, you know, I mean, just like sports, I felt that target. I saw it when I was out there immediately. But I was like, okay, I have to go back to my fundamentals. I won my season by building relationships, playing with people, getting to know them first. Because as I said in the show, it`s not chess, it`s literally people.\par \par \uc2 So you have to build a relationship first before you can play the game. So I was like, I`m going to go back out there. I`m going to do exactly what I know how to do, which is get to know people, and then if they like me, we can play the game together, and that`s how I was able to navigate.\par \par \uc2 And I think it was working out for me while I was out there, because, like, there was so many things to learn about my other tribe mates. We were such an eclectic group, but we got along, and I think that I was at the heart of that. So I`m proud of that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In your experiences, I`m curious, is it better to genuinely build a relationship or to play the person, that way you can pivot at any time.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: I think you have to do both.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have to do both.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Every person --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What tends to work better, though, especially --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nate`s like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It comes down to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is ready. He is ready to join the tribe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because it is psychological. There`s warfare that you`re playing with somebody else`s mind.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Their heart, their soul, their spirit --\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: I went the furthest\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- just the manipulation that you`re trying to --\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Yes, but you have to trust somebody. But I went the furthest when I had a true trusting group that I could trust like that`s how I got to third --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When Jeff was here, he tells us, everybody is changed by the game once they go through it. True?\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Absolutely!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For the better?\par \par \uc2 KYLE FRASER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Jenna.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: No, it absolutely is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it!\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: You definitely learn more from --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will you keep watching? Will you guys keep watching the show?\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Yes, I`m a fan, so I am excited to see the rest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s why we love the show. That`s why we love the show. We love y`all too. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jenna and Kyle, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Next Wednesday, we will have an exclusive preview of next week`s episode of "Survivor 50." It is Wednesday night at 8:00 Eastern, 7:00 Central right here on CBS and streaming on Paramount+, of course, and then next Thursday, we will be joined by another eliminated contestant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who do you guys predict? Who do you predict?\par \par \uc2 JENNA LEWIS DOUGHERTY: Cirie!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Manifesting it. Manifesting it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you missed CBS MORNINGS Deals, we showed you an 18-piece bath towel set that you can get the set from Spirit Linen at a 49 percent off discount.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER (Knockings lifestyle expert): Last time my mom was at my house, she went to the bathroom and came out and said, "Can I get you some towels for that guest bath?" And you know what? I love my mom, but she might have been saying, my towels are a little tired.\par \par \uc2 For many of us, we have not upgraded or updated our towels.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: This is a set of 18. It`s from Spirit Linen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hotel quality.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Hello?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Five-star hotel.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes! So, this is premium cotton. It`s looped, so it`s soft, also super absorbent. You`re getting 18 towels, six towels, six of the wash, six of the hand.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Eighteen.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And they match.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To shop for these are other items, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, you sold it so well, I bought the sea foam and the bay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait to get them!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I am talking about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260226em2q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 U.S. Official: Ukraine Agrees to Peace Deal; Violent Tornado Destroys Homes Near Houston; Coast-to-Coast Storms Hitting the U.S.; Unofficial Start to Thanksgiving Season; ATC and TSA Workers Get First Full Check in Weeks; Charges Dropped Against Comey and James; Pentagon Launches Probe into Sen. Mark Kelly; What Black Friday Spending Says About Economy; Local Football Coach Disappears Mysteriously; Alleged Porch Pirate Ring Busted on Long Island; White House Turkey Pardon; Study: Risks to Stopping GLP-1 Before Pregnancy; FDNY Commissioner on Decision to Resign; Singer- Songwriter Amanda Shires; Tips for Caregivers in Sandwich Generation; Celebrating 40 Years of the Cult Classic, "Clue;" \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Holly Williams, Karen  Hua, Rob Marciano, Kris Van Cleave, Kris Van Cleave, Scott MacFarlane,  Nancy Cordes, Kelly O`Grady, Anna Schecter, Ed O`Keefe, Vladimir Duthiers,  Celine Gounder, Chris Stover, Antony Mason \par \uc2 14,443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 November 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpet Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got everything you need to know as the Thanksgiving rush officially kicks off. Let`s go!\par \par \uc2 Plus, breaking news. There may be a breakthrough in a Ukraine peace deal, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Tornadoes carve a path of destruction in Texas as powerful storms across the country threaten travel plans.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: Minnesota and Wisconsin, we`re talking possibly getting up on Thanksgiving morning to possibly up to a foot of snow for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Breaking news overseas, a U.S. official tells CBS News, Ukraine has agreed to the framework of a deal to end the war with Russia, but how will President Putin respond?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There are new twists in the case of the high school football coach disappearing during an undefeated season.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON: What you have here is really a mystery.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The official White House Christmas tree has arrived, and it got the seal of approval from First Lady Melania Trump.\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP (First Lady of the United States of America): Merry Christmas!\par \par \uc2 VINCE (ShamWow): Hi, it`s Vince with ShamWow. You`ll be saying wow every time you use this towel.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The ShamWow guy is running for a U.S. House seat in Texas.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: The filing lists his name as Offer Vince ShamWow Shlomi.\par \par \uc2 VINCE: I don`t know it sells itself.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Naked Gun: From the Files of Police Squad"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Variety.com, put together a list of the hundred greatest comedies of all time, according to them, and at number one, "The Naked Gun: From the Files of Police Squad."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Naked Gun: From the Files of Police Squad"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so silly and stupid, you can`t help but laugh at it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was casted perfectly, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really was. It was very well done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a classic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to talk more about that a little bit later on, but we`re going to start with this, because you all know what today is. It is the unofficial start of the Thanksgiving travel rush. Cannot wait. There could be record crowds at airports and on the roads, too.\par \par \uc2 Nasty weather is threatening to make a big mess with major storms from coast-to-coast including a tornado outside Houston that has already caused major damage. That`s not good.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got correspondents covering all of it, and we`ll get to all of that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just in a moment, but we`re going to be with some breaking news in the peace talks for Ukraine. We may have a framework of a deal.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get right to Holly Williams in London who is tracking this for us.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. official tells CBS News that Ukraine has agreed to the terms of a peace deal. This comes as U.S. officials are in Abu Dhabi today for meetings with their Russian counterparts.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The details of what the U.S. and Ukraine have agreed are unclear, but we do know what the sticking points were.\par \par \uc2 President Trump`s 28-point peace plan demanded that Ukraine give up swaths of its territory to Russia, cut the size of its Army and agreed to be barred from joining \uc2 NATO\uc2 , the U.S.-led alliance that could protect it from future attack.\par \par \uc2 More than three years after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  launched his invasion, the Trump plan seemed to mirror many of Russia`s demands. Last night, around 400 miles from the front line in Kyiv, Russia, again, hit an apartment building, killing at least six.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "We don`t believe in the peace plan," said this survivor, "Putin won`t stop until he finishes us off."\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s wartime president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  initially responded to the Trump plan by saying it could mean the loss either of Ukraine`s dignity or of its partnership with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 But last night, Zelenskyy was more upbeat, saying changes have been made to the plan, and it may become workable.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: For many Ukrainians though, any agreement that means they`re forced to give up parts of their country permanently to Russia is unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "What did our soldiers die for?" said this woman, "That`s why we cannot give up territory."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: There`s unconfirmed reporting that the veto on \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership for Ukraine may have been removed from the peace plan, as well as the limit on Ukraine`s military, but remember, those changes could mean that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  rejects it. A senior Ukrainian official says that President Zelenskyy may visit Washington later this week -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Holly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back now to the Thanksgiving travel rush in the U.S. and the destructive tornado which blew through a Houston suburb. Karen Hua is in Spring, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Karen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the storm may be over, but the cleanup is just beginning here. You can see these piles of fallen trees and debris behind me, which is what much of this neighborhood in Spring looks like, and while residents say they did get several warnings ahead of this quick storm, it was far more intense than they thought it would be.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: She just touched down.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: Texans watched as this massive funnel cloud loomed over the sky, Monday.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: That`s an actual tornado where Houston suburbs took a direct hit and its ferocious winds were captured on this fire engine`s camera.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It was really almost like narrow, quick and it traveled really fast.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: The destruction spread far and wide, with more than a hundred homes and buildings in shambles, including this emergency services building, where its roof was ripped away and two employees rode out the storm inside.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA SEVCIK (Harris County Emergency Service District 11 PRO): One of them managed to hold on to a rack. The other one ducked underneath the table. You can imagine how terrified they were, but they were just fine.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: Beyond the damage to structures, the storm snapped and uprooted a massive number of trees.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All of these huge, beautiful trees, you know, down.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: Leaving the area virtually impassable, and first responders overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 FIRST RESPONDER: We still need a lot of chainsaws and heavy equipment to move some of these trees out of here.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: That work continued throughout the night, with residents in the streets clearing broken branches and scattered debris in the pouring rain.\par \par \uc2 DORIS WARFORD (Spring, Texas resident): We have pine trees blocked the street here, one of the pine trees fell, and it was in the neighbor`s yard across the street.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: We met up with Doris Warford, who rode out the storm in a closet with her family.\par \par \uc2 DORIS WARFORD: It was just like, oh my God! What`s happening? How long is this going to last? But it was over pretty quickly, and we came out and started to look around, and you looked out in the backyard first, and the deck had just folded like a deck of cards.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: Now this area also has several power lines down, which even sparked a small fire here yesterday. Officials had to turn off gas to a generator to reduce that threat.\par \par \uc2 Now, as the sun comes up today, officials are going to be continuing to survey this area for damage. They`re also looking into the Austin area to see if there was any potential tornado there as well -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Karen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on the national picture, let`s bring in CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 What is it like on the roads today?\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Well, in the south, it is certainly a mess; in the north, we`ll get to the snow in a second, but that line of storms that move through Houston now move to Jackson, Mississippi. They had some damage west of town there, and big time damage across parts of Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 You see that bow echo there. Over 40,000 people without power right now in Alabama and that`s not the only round that`s going to come through. We saw the front that`s going to drift through later on today. So about two million people in this area susceptible to more in the way of damaging winds, tornadoes, and then by the time tonight, rolls around across parts of Atlanta, that`s where we`re going to see some issues for travel.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk winter weather. The north side of this thing is a mess as well. Rain heading to Cleveland, Pittsburgh, eventually to New York, but look at the pinks on the map here. Winter storm warnings now, poster for Minneapolis. You go from rain to snow tonight and blowing snow overnight, we could see three to six, maybe eight inches of snow by tomorrow morning, and then cold air, kind of winds in behind this. We look for lake effect snows to be drifting, and that will pile up as well.\par \par \uc2 So travel today, Atlanta is going to be a problem. Birmingham, you saw that, a little bit of yellow on the map across the Northeast. Obviously, Minneapolis, as we turn that rain to snow, and the next system coming into the Pacific Northwest, so Portland, Seattle, you may see some issues with that as well.\par \par \uc2 A little bit of quieter day on Wednesday, but we`ve got another storm coming in Saturday and Sunday and in between that, temperatures are going to fall off the table here, dropping 20 degrees from Minneapolis and New York.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, over to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t look so great, Rob. Thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 If you thought that you were going to beat the crowds by leaving today for Thanksgiving travel, think again. Kris Van Cleave as a reality check just for you. He`s on the tarmac at Dulles International Airport that, of course, is located outside Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning. What can you tell us about what`s happening there?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You beat the crowds by being out here on the ramp. They are inside the terminals. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says today will actually be its busiest day as far as scheduled flights go about 250 of those will be United flights leaving here at Dulles, crews working around the clock to make sure they get out on time, and also watching the weather.\par \par \uc2 You heard about the weather issues in Texas that resulted in hundreds of delays and cancelations, particularly in the Dallas area, but also impacted Houston.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight to Phoenix had to divert to Houston after the crew reported fumes in the flight deck and cabin. Four flight attendants and one passenger were taken to the hospital as a precaution, but that plane did land safely.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, some good news, air traffic controllers and TSA officers are getting their first full paychecks in more than a month today, along with back pay from the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 We asked Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy how the system is recovering from that shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What do you say to flyers who are still a little unnerved after what they saw during the shutdown?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY (\uc2 U.S. Department of Transportation\uc2  Secretary): If you can still get a ticket and you can still fly, and your family still wants to see you, book that ticket, jump on a flight, and don`t be afraid to fly this season, it`s --\par \par \uc2 Again, I can`t control the weather, I can`t control the mechanics of an airplane, but things look good from the air traffic control side. We are back to staffing levels that we had before the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is watching for weather disruptions around Atlanta, the world`s busiest airport, from D.C. up to New York, and you heard Rob talk about the potential snow around Minneapolis, that`s another big airplane hub -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kris, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 In other news, President Trump`s effort to punish two of his most outspoken critics in the courts are dead for now. A judge threw out indictments against fired \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and New York State Attorney General Letitia James, saying the acting U.S. attorney who filed those charges was not properly appointed.\par \par \uc2 The charges can be refiled, and the Justice Department is looking for a way to do that. Scott MacFarlane is with us from Washington. He is following it.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Tony.\par \par \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and Letitia James did not have the opportunity to defend themselves on the merits of these controversial charges. These cases were ended, at least for the time being over a procedural matter, but the ruling is a major defeat for the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This morning, \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and Letitia James are no longer criminal defendants. A federal judge ruled Monday, the prosecutor who filed the cases, former Trump White House aide Lindsey Halligan, was unlawfully serving in the post.\par \par \uc2 The ruling says The White House exceeded its power installing Halligan as an acting federal prosecutor without necessary approval from Congress or the courts. U.S. Attorney General Pam Bondi stood behind Halligan Monday.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI (U.S. Attorney General): We believe we will be successful on appeal. Lindsey Halligan is an excellent U.S. attorney, and shame on them for not wanting her in office.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Halligan, who`d never prosecuted a case before, took over the role after Trump ousted the former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Virginia, when he wouldn`t move forward with these cases.\par \par \uc2 Comey had been facing charges of making a false statement to Congress back in 2020 and he welcomed the ruling.\par \par \uc2 JAMES COMEY\uc2  (Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): This case mattered to me personally, obviously, but it matters most because a message has to be sent that the President of the United States cannot use the Department of Justice to target his political enemies.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: An attorney for Letitia James who was charged with mortgage fraud, said, "This case was not about justice or the law, it was about targeting Attorney General James." Both Comey and James have denied the allegations against them.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: This ruling is much more consequential for \uc2 James Comey\uc2  than it is for Tish James.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Caroline Polisi, a criminal defense attorney, argues it will be particularly challenging for prosecutors to refile charges against Comey because the case against him is now beyond the five-year statute of limitations.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: The question is, can President Trump nominate somebody and get them Senate confirmed who is willing to bring this case?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and Letitia James also argued they were the victims of vindictive prosecution in a politically motivated case that was called for directly by the President in a September social media post, but those arguments won`t be heard in court. As the cases are now closed, those arguments, Gayle, are now moot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Scott for now, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There is major new fallout from the effort by a group of Democrats to remind members of the military that they should refuse to obey illegal orders. The Pentagon says he is now investigating one of those Democrats that would be Arizona Senator Mark Kelly, to see if he should be punished for misconduct.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at The White House with the latest on this.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, before he was a senator, Mark Kelly was a Navy pilot and an astronaut who retired at the rank of captain. Now, the Defense Secretary is threatening to recall him and possibly court martial him over that video he released with five Democratic colleagues.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): Like us, you all swore an oath.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Pentagon says this one-and-a-half minute video could get a sitting senator court martialed for interfering with the morale, good order and discipline of U.S. troops.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): Our laws are clear, you can refuse illegal orders.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The video caught the President`s attention on Thursday. He called it seditious behavior punishable by death, and has been posting about it ever since.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): Senator Mark Kelly well knows the rules of the military and the respect that one must have for the chain of command.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Kelly wasn`t the only veteran in the video.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRIS DELUZIO (D-PA): Former Navy.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON CROW (D-CO): Former paratrooper and Army Ranger --\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But as a former senior officer, he is the only one eligible for recall to a military service.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): We had a very successful mission.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Kelly, who flew on four space shuttle missions, said, "If this is meant to intimidate me and other members of Congress from doing our jobs and holding this administration accountable, it won`t work."\par \par \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  is Kelly`s fellow Arizona senator.\par \par \uc2 REP. RUBEN GALLEGO (D-AZ): Mark Kelly is a patriot. There is no reason why they`re going after him.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): Like us, you all swore an oath --\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRISSY HOULAHAN (D-PA): To protect and defend this Constitution.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In the video, Kelly and the other five lawmakers did not claim that Trump has issued illegal orders.\par \par \uc2 But on "Face The Nation" this weekend, Kelly said he does worry Trump will.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): This President, from before he was in office, as he was a candidate, to the time he was in office in his first administration, has said things that cause us great concern.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Court martials are usually reserved for serious criminal offenses, and punishments can range from reduction in rank to confinement and even hard labor.\par \par \uc2 Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said that Kelly`s conduct will be "addressed appropriately," but this is a very novel accusation, a novel case he is making against the senator, so it`s unclear where exactly all of this is going to lead -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we will stay tuned, and Nancy, I love that coat by the way.\par \par \uc2 All right, in this morning`s "Money Watch," we are nearing the Super Bowl of holiday shopping, Black Friday and Cyber Monday, as we know, and we are just 30 days away from Christmas.\par \par \uc2 A new report finds that more than 80 percent of people say they will be making purchases during these shopping events. Here with everything that you need to know, "Money Watch" correspondent, Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so what does the new report say about consumer spending this week, and for people who want to spend money in person, what do they need to know?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, I tell you everything you know. Let`s start with that report, okay.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  says that consumers are actually going to pull back a little bit this holiday weekend. The average consumer is expected to spend 622 bucks, which still sounds like a lot this weekend, but that is down four percent versus last year.\par \par \uc2 Overall, though we have seen so many estimates when it comes to consumer spending, even one from the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2  that says overall this season, people are expected to spend more.\par \par \uc2 I think the bottom line here is, people are looking for deals.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  says 90 percent of people are going to go shop at a discount retailer. Okay, it`s a lot, actually --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And the 50 percent would consider giving a dupe -- a fake product to save money.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So people are trying to find ways. You know, they`re still going to spend though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: They`re likely going to trade down from your dining out, your travel. It`s the holiday magic. They want to give the gifts. But it is a tale of two shoppers.\par \par \uc2 You have your higher income earners, they`re doing the heavy lifting, and then the lower income folks are going to pull back. That being said, if you are going to be out there shopping, okay, don`t expect to line up at the mall after your turkey. All right, don`t be a crazy\par \par \uc2 All right, wait until Friday. Most places aren`t open. You`ve got 5:00 A.M. You`ve got Kohl`s, \uc2 JCPenney\uc2 , 6:00 A.M. \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , target. Gosh, Macy, a few of the others. 7:00 A.M., \uc2 Nordstrom\uc2 , I think the door buster deals, they are there for those early birds. But remember, if you want to be like me in your jammies, you can still do that stuff online and get all the deals.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You could still do that online. I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Great way to maximize your money.\par \par \uc2 All right, Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, the latest on the search for a well-known high school football coach who vanished during his team`s undefeated season, but first 7:17 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Anthony Mason talks with Grammy winning musician, Amanda Shires about her vulnerable new album made in the wake of her split from Jason Isbell.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, we`ll look at the legacy of the movie "Clue," the box office flop, which became a cult classic.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of classics, there`s a new list of the greatest comedy films of all time. Which would you choose? Think about it. We`re going to talk about it. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New York City loves a champion. The members of Gotham FC they took a ride through Lower Manhattan yesterday, celebrating their second NWSL championship in three years.\par \par \uc2 A rally at City Hall followed after that. The city also celebrated Gotham FC`s first trophy in 2023 because they did not have a parade after that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, by the way, New York fans, the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 , Mets, Giants, Jets, \uc2 Knicks\uc2 , Nets, Rangers and Islanders haven`t won a title in quite a few years. So you should get out and celebrate those ladies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Remember Rose and Emily were here yesterday, and they were really looking forward to the parade.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They were still floating from that victory.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure. Congrats to them again.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, our coverage of the year`s most famous pardon. You`re looking at three famous turkeys, Gobble, Waddle and our Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ouch!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Saying Ed needs to join a runner club?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I heard you, Nate. I heard you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turkey trot, Ed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, he heard me. I`m sorry, Ed. I am sorry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He didn`t mean it, Ed. He didn`t mean it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we are back, 7:30, CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Newly released body cam video shows the moment \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  rescued a man trapped after an explosion blew the roof off an apartment building. You can see the officers are pulling the man to safety. He had been trapped in the rubble between stairwells. This all happened last week.\par \par \uc2 The Fire Department said the man was taken to the hospital. He`s in stable condition. The blast displaced 19 families. Big impact. The investigation into the cause is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very lucky man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, very lucky man.\par \par \uc2 The mysterious disappearance of a well-known high school football coach has rocked the community in rural Virginia and baffled investigators. Police are ramping up their search for Coach Travis Turner. He was last seen five days ago amid his team`s undefeated football season.\par \par \uc2 Anna Schecter has been following this case.\par \par \uc2 Anna, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, according to local reports, Turner was last seen walking off into the woods near his home wearing a gray sweatshirt, gray sweatpants and glasses, and local police tell CBS News they haven`t seen a trace of him since.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: The Union High School Bears in Southwest Virginia are undefeated this season, led by Coach Travis Turner.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS TURNER (Union High School Bears coach): When it was going to be a battle, a four-quarter battle, and I thought our kids did a good job.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: But last week, just days away from a playoff game, police say Turner disappeared.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON: What you have here is really a mystery. All of a sudden, for some unknown reason, he disappears. Is there some criminality afoot, or is there something else that might have caused him to go missing?\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: Virginia State Police told CBS News on Thursday, special agents in their criminal division were headed to Turner`s home as part of the early stages of an investigation. They would not reveal the nature of that probe, but said they were not planning to arrest him. While enroute, agents found out Turner was not home and he hasn`t been seen since.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Wise County School District where Turner works said a member of its staff had been placed on administrative leave while an external agency reviews an allegation and that the situation involves an active law enforcement matter.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON: There`s still a lot more work that the Virginia State Police need to do to be able to track down the coach and bring home to his -- bring him home to his loved ones.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: Local police have deployed drones and K9s, but five days into their search, they say their top priority is locating Turner alive and safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: Well, law enforcement sources told us that the drones help give them a bird`s eye view and heat sensor drones are especially effective in a situation like this, while the K9s track the scent.\par \par \uc2 The Virginia State Police have been in charge of this search so far, and we`re watching to see if the \uc2 FBI\uc2  steps in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very scary story, Anna. I hope it has a good outcome. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just as all those holiday packages start coming in, a highly sophisticated ring of accused porch pirates is what they`re called has been busted on Long Island, New York. Officials say 14 people have been indicted for allegedly targeting \uc2 FedEx\uc2  deliveries of cell phones and other electronics.\par \par \uc2 The Suffolk County District Attorney says the thieves obtained inside information how they did that, still trying to figure that out, including names, addresses and tracking numbers from deliveries from Verizon and \uc2 AT&T.\par \par \uc2 Officials say they would strike shortly after the drop off, and the phones were eventually resold here in this country and internationally.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And up next, we will head to our nation`s capital to talk to our senior White House and political correspondent, oh, Gobble and Waddle in there, too. What`s up, Ed?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Nate, you know, you know, you talk to a lot of turkeys in this business, but very few of them actually have feathers, meet, Gobble and Waddle before they head to the White House for their pardon, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for an annual tradition in Washington. President Trump will pardon two lucky turkeys today at the White House. This year`s birds, they`re named Gobble and Waddle, and they were selected from a farm down in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the Willard Intercontinental Hotel in Washington, where, apparently, these turkeys have been staying before their big day. Ed, they don`t even let us stay there. It`s too expensive. It`s a five star hotel.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It is out of our per diem, but it is definitely in for these guys. They`ve been staying here for years. Good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 You know in the morning show business, we`re always looking for exclusives. Well, we have one for you this morning before they get their presidential pardon, Gobble and Waddle are with only CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Gobble, by the way, weighs 52 pounds, if you could tell which one it is, I don`t know, but Gobble weighs 52 pounds and is a big fan of Bojangles biscuits, while Waddle weighs in at 50 pounds and is a big fan of sweet potatoes with extra marshmallows. And these two are joining a really proud presidential tradition.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Harry Truman was the first to be presented with a turkey by the \uc2 National Turkey Federation\uc2  ahead of Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States of America): You are hereby pardoned.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But the more modern presidential pardon for any potential foul play dates back to George H.W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 This year, Travis Pittman from Wayne County, North Carolina, had the honor of raising Gobble and Waddle.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS PITTMAN (From Wayne County, North Carolina): We strive every day to do the best we can do raising our birds and this is a dream come true.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Typically, Pittman raises flocks of at least 19,000 on his farm for Butterball, the nation`s largest Turkey producer.\par \par \uc2 And Butterball CEO, \uc2 Jay Jandrain\uc2 , is also chairman of the \uc2 National Turkey Federation\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 JAY JANDRAIN\uc2  (Butterball CEO): There will be about 30 million turkeys that are on the Thanksgiving table.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Oh really? This is the peak week.\par \par \uc2 JAY JANDRAIN\uc2 : This is the peak.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Why do you deserve a pardon? What did you do? What didn`t you do?\par \par \uc2 So how did Gobble and Waddle stand out from the rest of the flock?\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS PITTMAN: They were normally the two that came to us first every day, or even they weren`t first closest to us, they were the ones that you could like stand on the table and fan out, they were more comfortable.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Pittman credits his young son, Carter for helping feed and interact with the winning birds. A reminder, farming is a family business and family wide commitment.\par \par \uc2 What would be your message to people as they sit down this week to have this very important meal with their family and friends,\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS PITTMAN: Most people that are in that industry work countless hours, and not only do they work hours in whether they`re in their flock of birds with turkeys, or if they`re in the farm fields and the tractors and all, they carry this job home at night, also, as my wife would say, you really never get away from it.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So be thankful for the farm.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS PITTMAN Very thankful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There we go. So you may be wondering, what do they do for the rest of their lives? Well, right after their White House pardon Gobble and Waddle are getting on a ride back to NC State, where they will live out their life of freedom, they`re going to try to hit the road before they hit the rush this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Guys, always thankful to work with you guys. This is our third year hanging out with the \uc2 National Turkey Federation\uc2 . And good to see these birds. They`re going to have fun today at The White House.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And a reminder, too, to be thankful for the farmers, because while they`re spared, you know, a lot of their cousins are going to be sitting on our tables.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m very excited about that. I`m glad Gobble and Waddle are going to be all right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, I think they heard you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Woho! What did she say?\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, we will have the stories that you will be talking about in "What to Watch" but right now, it is 7:41, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up for you in our 8:00 hour, including Anthony, as in Mason, with singer-songwriter, Amanda Shires how she learned to be a witness for herself for her deeply honest new album.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`re celebrating 40 years since the release of the hilarious movie of the board game "Clue."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that game. We`re going to talk with some of the cast, but first, Claus and I are heading over to the table because Vlad is in his room.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, Vlad!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So have you thought about what your favorite comedy film of all time is?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE 