ages were seared into her memory.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Gayle, you spoke to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and she was only seven years old. Yes, we went to her place. And you know, when you`re a hundred plus, your memory phase, but she was very clear, very vivid about that particular day.\par \par \uc2 Just imagine at seven years old, you don`t know the political ramifications. You just know as a little kid, you are very afraid.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re terrified.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re terrified and you see that your family is afraid, so --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Your neighbors.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your neighbors, you don`t forget that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You don`t forget that ever.\par \par \uc2 There is still one remaining survivor, Lessie Randall. She`s also 111 years old. She`s six months younger.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. How about that!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is still one person still alive to remember.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to move on to something completely different now. We`re going to talk about "Variety" Magazine who has come out with its 100 Best Comedy Movies of All Time. There has been so much debate in the control room, here in the studio.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Naked Gun: From the Files of Police Squad"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let`s talk about "The Naked Gun" starring Leslie Nielsen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is funny.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is number one, though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a great movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s number one on the list.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a good movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s pretty funny.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a great movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is. It is a great movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that movie. But is it number one?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I think it could be. I mean, that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can make an argument for it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can make an argument for it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me show you the --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The top five.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Number two is "Some Like It Hot," which I think should be number one. "Some Like It Hot" is probably one of the greatest -- it is the greatest comedy movie of all time --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You like it hot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- in my estimation. "Annie Hall" comes in at number three. "The Great Dictator," I love Charlie Chaplin. I`m a huge Charlie Chaplin fan. "The Great Dictator" is one of the greatest comedy movies of all time.\par \par \uc2 Now this one, "Waiting for Guffman." Christopher Guest. It`s a great movie. I love all of Christopher Guest`s movie, but I would have put in "Trading Places."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Eddie Murphy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel you on that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Trading Places" has got to be in there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am surprised Eddie Murphy isn`t in the top five.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Trading Places" Eddie Murphy has got to be in the top five.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somewhere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen, there is not a Black comedy in the top 25. And listen, I`m not making it about race because I feel like real classic comedies, they are color blind to who their actors are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But like come on, you`re telling me "Trading Places" or "Beverly Hills Cop" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Beverly Hills Cop."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They can`t crack the top 25?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes, yes! No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Coming to America" can`t crack the top 25?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where is "Coming to America"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Forty-six on the list.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Forty-six is wild.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let me show you the rest of the list. Let`s talk about "Monty Python and the Holy Grail," "Duck Soup," "Fargo." Right? "Young Frankenstein," "Groundhog Day." That rounds up the top 10.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Groundhog Day" is a great movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve already talked about "Trading Places." I also love "Something About Mary."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What -- oh, I love that. I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Something About Mary" is --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Something About Mary"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What about "Harry Met Sally"? That`s got to be on the list somewhere.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t know how -- I don`t think that`s on the list, because I feel like that`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If it is, it`s kind of way down, it`s a romantic comedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I think that that`s hilarious.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What about like a Jim Carrey movie or an Austin Powers character?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Austin Powers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like "The Truman Show" or something like.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s on the list, Shawna?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Ace Ventura."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Ace Ventura."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Ace Ventura: Pet Detective." All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Ace Ventura: Pet Detective."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is "Old School" on there?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Old School."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Will Ferrell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh! I love those.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a funny movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I also love all stupid comedies of the 70s and 80s, like "Caddy Shack," "Animal House," usually problematic now, probably, but they`re still really fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is "Tropic Thunder" on there? "Tropic Thunder."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, hey, hey!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Tropic Thunder is not on there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hundred percent with you on "Tropic Thunder."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Tropic Thunder" is --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It stands up to re-watching it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whenever there`s a list like this, there`s never an agreement.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There`s never an agreement. Everybody is debating.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But there`s an agreement that you can put two or three Eddie Murphy movies in the top 25.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Eddie Murphy is the greatest.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- comic of all time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, it barely cracked the top 50.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I love this "What to Watch" because this is all my favorite stuff. We`re talking about -- oh, it just went away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We ran out of time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jon`s mad at me because we were having too much fun!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You were going to confess that you paid $9 million for a Superman comic book! Is that it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Superman number one, $9 million.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sheesh!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It wasn`t you. It wasn`t you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It wasn`t me, but if I was, if I had the money, I`d be buying that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, stick around. Anthony Mason is going to sit down with Amanda Shires, Granny winners, talk about her emotional new album.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After the break, new information on those popular GLP-1 weight loss drugs in pregnancy. Dr. Celine Gounder is here with what you need to know. That and more when we come back.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, singer-songwriter Amanda Shires is painfully honest in her new album. It`s all about her divorce. She spoke to Anthony, as in Mason about looking for closure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 The holidays can be a tricky time for those in the so-called "sandwich generation" helping care for older people and kids. We`ll have some tips on how to keep it all together.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We will also celebrate 40 years of the much beloved mystery-comedy classic "Clue" by talking to some of the whodunnit characters from the cast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that game, Colonel Mustard. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at 8:00."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Breaking news in the peace talks for Ukraine, we may have a framework of a deal.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: A U.S. official tells CBS News that Ukraine has agreed to the terms of a peace deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nasty weather is threatening to make a big mess with major storms from coast-to-coast including a tornado outside Houston.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: While residents say they did get several warnings ahead of this quick storm, it was far more intense than they thought it would be.\par \par \uc2 DORIS WARFORD: It was just like, oh, my God, what`s happening? How long is this going to last?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A judge threw out indictments against fired \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and New York State Attorney General Letitia James saying the acting U.S. attorney who filed those charges was not properly appointed.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI: Lindsey Halligan is an excellent U.S. attorney, and shame on them for not wanting her in office.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Garfield")\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Someone was complaining about their workout today, and it wasn`t me.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: That is Luna expressing her displeasure about her workout. She has been trained to walk on the treadmill in Colorado. Luna usually exercises every day.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: I love that.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Just like a regular person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the sound of that cat. That`s so me. I wish I like working out. I know you have to do it. I know you have to do it. That`s how I feel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t know you could train a cat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cats can get chubby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, they get chubby, but you can train a cat?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I didn`t know you could train any cat to do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The cat didn`t like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Cat needs like some headphones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t like it either.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour, though, with this morning`s "Health Watch." There`s new research on weight loss, drugs and pregnancy. Study in "The Journal of American Medicine" looks at patients who stopped taking GLP-1 drugs before or during pregnancy. CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Celine, good to have you at the table. I was very confused when I heard this, because if you`re pregnant, why are you taking a weight loss drug? But that`s not what they`re referring to here. It`s because you`re taking it before you get pregnant, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: That`s right. And just to step back a little bit and provide some context, over 30 percent of pregnant women in this country are obese, and the lack of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before they get pregnant?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Before and during their pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, and the lack of information on these medications, these GLP-1 weight loss medications during pregnancy, is a major gap, because obesity and the complications of obesity drive up a lot of the maternal illness and mortality that we see in this country. And by the way, our rates of maternal mortality are three times higher than other high income countries, so this is a major gap.\par \par \uc2 In this particular study, the researchers looked at 150,000 or so pregnancies. A few hundred of those women had been on these medications prior to pregnancy, and they look to see what happened when you stopped, because right now the recommendation is you should not be on these medications during pregnancy. We just don`t know the safety.\par \par \uc2 And what they saw is, when women stopped, they regained the weight quickly, and then you saw a slight increase in risk and things like gestational diabetes, high blood pressure, preterm birth. And so we really do need more information about whether women can continue these medications during pregnancy safely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So are there they saying, if you take the medication before you`re pregnant, should you stop X amount of months before you get pregnant?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, right now --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are they saying?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, right now the recommendation is to stop two months before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two months.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Because it takes some time for these medications to wash out of your system. But given the complications that women suffer related to weight gain, especially if they`re already obese, you know, we really need some more information, more research. And of course, pregnant women are always left out of so many clinical trials because people are afraid of what might happen to the baby.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: But here we may actually be putting those babies at higher risk by stopping the medications.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you should never take it during pregnancy, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: As of right now, no, because we don`t have the data, but we need the research.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Quick note about something airing tomorrow, we hope you will tune in for the New York City Fire Commissioner`s first interview since he announced plans to resign. Announcement just hours after Zohran Mamdani was elected the city`s next mayor.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was the moment when you realized this mayor-elect, if elected, is incompatible with me, myself, my personal beliefs?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT S. TUCKER (New York City Fire Commissioner): I think the whole campaign really reflected that for me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tomorrow, Commissioner Tucker explains how religion is part of his decision and why he thinks Mamdani he still has to earn the support of many first responders, says he has tremendous amount of work to do there. We`ll have that interview tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m really looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, "Clue" is the whodunnit movie fans love more now than ever, even 40 years after its release.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will hear from two cast members about what`s behind the staying power. But right now, it`s 8:05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Way it Goes"/Amanda Shires/ATO Records)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Grammy winner Amanda Shires latest album chronicles the ending of her marriage to Jason Isbell. Ahead, she tells Anthony Mason about the surprising inner strength she found while starting over.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you know that November is National Family Caregivers Month? Neither did I, and our next guest hosts a podcast about navigating life as a caregiver.\par \par \uc2 So according to AARP, 63 million Americans are balancing the role of caregiver. That`s a lot of us, and one in three of them are part of the so- called "sandwich generation." That means raising kids under the age of 18 while caring for an adult loved one.\par \par \uc2 Candace Dellacona is host of the podcast "The Sandwich Generation Survival Guide." She`s also an estates and trust lawyer and also a very smart human being.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got a lot -- you have a lot on your resume, but I want people to hear about your background, Candace, because you`re a former caregiver, you`re a real estate planning lawyer, you`re a member of the sandwich generation. And yet, with all of those qualifications, even you felt what, while you were taking this on?\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA ("The Sandwich Generation Survival Guide" podcast): Totally overwhelmed and totally ill-equipped to handle all of the responsibilities that were coming my way, and so it was really difficult.\par \par \uc2 I had a ton of resources as you point out, I`m an estate planning lawyer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: And I do this for a living, and I found myself on the other side, and I was really looking for a way to share those resources with other people to make it maybe a little bit easier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the biggest mistake we make?\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: I think the biggest mistake we make is taking on too much, not asking for help, and not having good boundaries. I think that`s one of the things that I try to impart to our listeners, to my clients, and to anyone who will listen, that this is a community issue, and we have to rely on members of our community and share our resources.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there are resources, though, out there, like?\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: There are. In fact, there`s one that I think really has flown under the radar called The Guide Program, which is administered through the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid, and it is an excellent resource for caregivers and those who have dementia and Alzheimer`s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s great about it? I mean, you mentioned it, but what`s great about it?\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: Number one, it`s free.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: So that is something that`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because it can be expensive, right?\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: That`s correct. Resources can be expensive, so any place that you can find to receive resources at a low cost or no cost, take advantage. Take advantage -- and I think it`s really important. And what it is Gayle, is a care navigator gets assigned to you as the dementia or Alzheimer`s patient and your caregiver, and the care navigator sets up a plan of care for you and makes sure that you have access to vital resources that you need and it`s a 24/7 hotline to ask questions, to find resources that are in your own community, if you have dementia or Alzheimer`s.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s really great to have this conversation right now as families gather for the holidays, because there`s going to be a moment when several members of that sandwich generation around the same age are sitting there. Maybe there`s a bottle of wine on the table, and you`re talking about how you`re going to deal with --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- everybody else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uncle Bob.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. What are the important things that should be discussed when you have everyone under one roof together.\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: I think one of the things that`s important to think about is these conversations are difficult to have. It is facing someone`s mortality, perhaps a diagnosis that`s really difficult. So starting that conversation from a place of love and a place of care, I think, is step number one.\par \par \uc2 I`d probably leave the alcohol at home and not have the conversation over a bottle of wine, but certainly having everyone together and having transparency among all of the family members and the people who will help with the care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And documentation. You were talking about documents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No documentation.\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: Yes, documentation is so important, and we`ve talked about this, having those powers of attorney and those health care directives to make sure that the person that you want to speak for you has the legal authority to do so.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, delegate responsibility, take advantage of free programs, documentation. We are listening, taking notes. Candace, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CANDACE DELLACONA: I love it. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, wait a minute. Why is Vlad still here?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because he loves us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly, right. Also because, Nate, we`re celebrating 40 years of the classic mystery movie "Clue," and nobody can leave this room - -\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ta-da-da!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly! Until we discover why it`s so beloved.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. We`re actually going to be hearing from the cast\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Heaving breath, flames on the side of my face. That is the late, great Madeline Kahn as Mrs. White in the classic comedy "Clue."\par \par \uc2 The movie actually broke new ground. Why? Because it had multiple endings, but a lot of people didn`t get it, and so at the time, it wasn`t a hit. Why not? It`s a mystery for some, but if you`re a fan, you`ll know 40 years later, it`s a huge cult classic. Our in-house clue super fan, Chris Stover caught up with some of the cast.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Six strangers --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: -- at a murderous dinner party with a mysterious host.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: The plot of the board game, "Clue" coming to life on the big screen to answer three questions --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Forty years later, can you even believe that we`re still talking about "Clue"?\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN ("Clue" actor): You know, it`s amazing, really.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Lesley Ann Warren is part of the ensemble cast. She plays Miss Scarlet.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: The sultry D.C. madam who collects secrets on the Washington elite.\par \par \uc2 How much of Lesley Ann Warren is Miss Scarlet?\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: She takes no (bleep). She takes no prisoners and in real life, even though I`d love to be that way, no, I don`t -- you know, you have to temper that stuff. So it was -- it was a great chance to kind of explore all of that kind of behavior.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN CAMP ("Clue" actor): I literally put on a French maid`s outfit. I walked into the interview. I did the entire interview in character, and I just wanted to be in this movie.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Colleen Camp plays Yvette, the maid, an informant for Miss Scarlet.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Tell me what it was like on set.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN CAMP: It was fun. We would watch each other in the scenes. We applaud each other. We were like a real family.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: Honestly, we were laughing at each other`s work all the time.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: The mystery on screen played out in real life. The cast didn`t learn the movie would feature multiple endings until nearly the end of filming.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: Scenes we didn`t really know who was to be trusted and who wasn`t and how that was all going to work out. You know, it created an element of suspense for us as well.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Do you have a favorite ending?\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: my own. Come on!\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: In 1985, "Clue" debuted to mixed reviews.\par \par \uc2 Roger Ebert, his quote was "Fun, I must say, is in short supply." That was in his review.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: Originally.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Yes. Originally.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: Yes. I wouldn`t go by those originally.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: That`s because a decade after its release, a new popularity emerged, thanks in part to cable T.V. and eventually streaming services.\par \par \uc2 When did you realize there`s something bigger going on here?\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: Honestly, it was when Martin Mull called me on the phone and said, are you getting a lot of fan mail from "Clue"?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: Like, a crazy amount of fan mail. And I was like, yes, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN CAMP: It was years later where all of a sudden I would hear people react to this movie, and they were obsessed with it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: The cult following has led to spoofs like this drinking game version in Brooklyn. The stage production is interactive and can get as campy as the original.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: The humor, I think, is written in such a way that it`s accessible for all audiences.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It just has this fun, buzzy cult following, and it`s never not just like quick and zany and silly.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: It`s a movie that transcends time.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: And perhaps the answer to that mystery is right in front of us.\par \par \uc2 Forty years later, what is the ultimate legacy of "Clue"?\par \par \uc2 LESLEY ANN WARREN: I think that "Clue" brings people a lot of joy. It makes them laugh. They love the suspense. And I think it`s -- you know, it`s just created a life that lives now on its own.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Chris Stover in the Broadcast Center with the microphone.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very good.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: A very quotable moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that Chris Stover goes, get it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We got it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We got it.\par \par \uc2 You know, I have never seen that movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You need to watch it, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It looks kind of goofy, though.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Really. Well, it`s a movie I think you need to see multiple times in order to get it, because it`s filled with double entendres. It`s filled with subtle references, even Colleen Camp, who was in there, says, every time she watches it, she notices something new. She was in it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: So, it is just one of those movies --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still love the game, though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The game is amazing, but also the film, when it came out, it wasn`t popular because audiences didn`t get it. They didn`t -- they thought they were going to see an Agatha Christie type of film.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And they got this screwball comedy that was a throwback to, like, the 50s and 60s, but you had this murderous row of Madeline Kahn, Tim Curry, Michael McKean --\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: So good! So good!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Leslie Ann Warren.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: And they still go on tour on these screenings, these special screenings. Leslie is going on tour in January, up for a second part of her national tour. She is going to meet fans and interact with them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Because it is -- it means that much to them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m always fascinated guys with movies that are skewered in the beginning, and then as time goes, people see them differently and say, that was great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. That`s one of those movies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This seems to fall into that category.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Also highly quotable. Communism is just a red herring. Probably one of my favorite lines.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS STOVER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Clue"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I say, Chris Stover, you look very nice. Chris and Vlad are in a band. You look very nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are in a band together.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Way to go, Chris, or as I like to call you, Stove Top, cooking things around here.\par \par \uc2 All right, Tony, what`s up, baby?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As long as you don`t call him Muffin Top, Stover, nicely done. Really nice job. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on "Talk of the Table" a major car CEO says thousands of jobs with six figure salaries, no one will work them. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, there is Eddie Murphy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Party all the time!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Party all the time.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you not party all the time, but stories that we call "Talk of the Table" because it`s a table and we be talking. You are in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got an interesting . My "Talk of the Table." \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2 , CEO \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  saying they have 5,000 mechanic jobs that pay $120,000.00 a year, and they cannot find enough skilled people to fill those jobs. But he also points out, this is not just about Ford: "We are in trouble in our country." He told the business podcast last week, "We have over a million openings in critical jobs, emergency services, trucking, factory workers, plumbers, electricians and tradesmen," and he added "A bay with a lift and tools and no one to work in it. Are you kidding me? Nope."\par \par \uc2 This caught my eye because, of course, I can barely build a birdhouse and because I`ve covered this issue of trade school enrollment before, on the show. Now, enrollment was up in the last couple of years, according to "The Wall Street Journal," but there are still 10 times as many people graduating from four-year colleges than there are young people completing vocational program.\par \par \uc2 So in other words, for every trade school grad who might fill one of those jobs, there are 10 college grads now competing with A.I., and it`s not just four that`s noticed. More than 400,000 open manufacturing positions are on the books right now, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 So we are not just talking about this enough according to Farley, and I agree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is why there are wait times for like, things you want to get fixed, you want to build, why we can`t just rush ahead into the future.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does put things in perspective. And you`re right, most people don`t know the story that you just told.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which is why trade schools are important and also learning skills.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That will ever be forever be around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And as you sit around the table, and someone`s considering it be like, go for it, young man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they are well paying jobs, too. That`s the thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very good paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you`re afraid of A.I., go in the opposite direction and learn things, learn how to build things with your hand.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so we came in with Eddie Murphy, the singer, not the actor, by the way, I wish I was around at that time when he was making that song -- "Party All The Time." Okay?\par \par \uc2 But my "Talk of the Table" is about his movie career. So the Hollywood star, Eddie Murphy is revealing three blockbuster movies he regrets turning down. So the "Coming to America" actor has plenty of box office hits under his belt as we know.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Coming to America"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Told The Associated Press about the films, but he says he did have really good reasons for saying no.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EDDIE MURPHY (Actor): Ghostbusters, I was supposed to do "Ghostbusters" and didn`t do that and "Rush Hour," and "Who Framed Roger Rabbit." Those are -- those are my big three.\par \par \uc2 With "Ghostbusters," I did "Beverly Hills Cop" instead. So that was like the -- it was do this or that. So it worked out cool.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It did workout cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love this. It did work out cool for him. But, you know, I think about the butterfly effect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What would have happened if Eddie Murphy would have starred in "Rush Hour" or "Ghostbusters" instead of "Beverly Hills Cop," which really shot him to the top of being one of the most popular actors. And of course, "Who Framed Roger Rabbit" is one of my favorite movies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you never really think about that side of actors, especially the big blockbuster stars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like how he said it worked out cool. What I also like is the way it`s like he was reflecting in real time, he says, I didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because he`s been off the grid for a while.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m glad that Eddie Murphy is back in our faces and doing more projects.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And also that doc is very good at Eddie Murphy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is. I saw that Gayle, it is good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did, too. It is good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, what have you got, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is what has become a widespread problem throughout Southern California. I was in L.A. last week, and I saw this on local news. I said, people need to know about this.\par \par \uc2 Thieves are using technology to steal expensive cars. Here`s a look at how it works. So you see a person standing in front of someone`s house. They`re holding a wire above their head. Police say what that person is doing. They`re using the wire like an antenna to intercept the signal from the car owner`s key fob from inside the house.\par \par \uc2 Then they transmit that signal to an accomplice who opens a car, turns it off and then takes off. I didn`t know you could do this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. So you`re standing -- one lady said I thought they were coming to rob me, but they`re holding the wire up. It gets a signal from the key fob. Police say the issue is that fobs never turn off, they are always transmitting those signals. So how can you prevent this? What you need to do is get your signal by storing your key fob away from the front door, blocking it with bags or leaving it, leaving it far away from the windows and the front doors.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They need to make a car --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t even know that was possible, Tony./\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want -- I want manual windows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Old school.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The key, I put into the side of the steering column and go - - give it to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t want that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is something to think about.\par 