s attached.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love a good cross body. I do. I rock them. I got a bunch of them.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GRACIE: Well, and you know, you guys that wear that big fat wallet in your back pocket that`s so easy to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nok, stain in the jeans or --\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GRACIE: -- and it could get lost. So this will carry your phone. It carries your credit cards, cash, lip balm, keys, anything you need right in the handle on your strap.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GRACIE: Easy-peasy. Great gift for everybody on your list, 30 percent off, $31.00. That`s $13.00 off for each one. Get multiples.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GRACIE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Carolyn, thank you so much. And we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything that you saw, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Piece of Mind"/Amanda Shires/ATO Records)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Amanda Shire is looking good. Her latest album, "Nobody`s Girl," captures the ending of her decade long marriage to Jason Isbell. The Grammy winning artist, reunited with Anthony, as in Mason at Lucinda`s right here in New York City. I want to know what they`re laughing about, to talk about heartbreak and newfound happiness.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It hasn`t been an easy year for Amanda Shires.\par \par \uc2 You had a car accident.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES (Singer): I had a car accident with a deer.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She hit a deer driving near her Tennessee home.\par \par \uc2 How many stitches did you have to have?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Fifty-three.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ah! Fifty-three.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: And I broke my nose.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Wow!\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: I`m God`s favorite because I`m still here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. But you lost your dad in May.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: I lost my dad in May, my grandmother in February.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: It`s funny because I went into 2025 thinking this year is going to be better.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: But 2026 is soon.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Piece of Mind"/Amanda Shires/ATO Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s all come around the release of her album, "Nobody`s Girl" -- about the breakup of her 10-year marriage to singer Jason Isbell.\par \par \uc2 Music which shires was reluctant to share.\par \par \uc2 Why didn`t you want to do it?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: I was thinking, there`s more important things in the world.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But friends persuaded her to put it out.\par \par \uc2 What did writing these songs do for you?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Well, they allowed me closure.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: I learned how to be a witness for myself.\par \par \uc2 For that song, "The Details" like, there`s high chance I`ll never play that song live.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: But I needed a place to put that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: So I could feel relieved of the burden.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Does it work?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yes, it works. And the way that I make sense of things is by making something. I think anybody that is having some kind of problem, they can -- you can make anything. You can make a song, you can make a painting. You can make a meal. It doesn`t have to taste good.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Better if it does.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Right, depends on who you`re serving.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Her ex has also written about the split. Isbell`s album was released earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Did you listen to it?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yes, I did. I didn`t like put it on repeat or anything.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: So at some point, I might put it back on again, but I`m not really looking to do that right now.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jason Isbell: Running With Our Eyes Closed"/HBO)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The streams in their marriage were exposed in the HBO documentary, "Jason Isbell: Running With Our Eyes Closed."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jason Isbell: Running With Our Eyes Closed"/HBO)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Shires acknowledged them when we talked in 2022.\par \par \uc2 So what was going on?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: There was a disconnect. It was just a war in the studio. It was like a war he was having with himself and with me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In that conversation we had three years ago, what`d you find?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Myself.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Had that been missing?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Nope.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There was an epic --\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Double fisting of the babes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Timeout.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You all right?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She`s quit the habit at the urging of her daughter, Mercy.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: But I love vaping, and I miss it, but I`m not doing it. It`s like a bad boyfriend. Toxic.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Three years ago, Shires believed she and Isbell had moved past their problems.\par \par \uc2 You thought you were okay.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you weren`t.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: I know. Isn`t that something.\par \par \uc2 To quote myself: There is a way love goes sometimes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Way It Goes"/Amanda Shires/ATO Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So you were surprised?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yep. And not like a Christmas surprise way either. More surprised now at the inner strength that I found along the way.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Your own?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yes, and like, realizations like, you`re never too old, it`s never too late, you`re not too much and you`re not too complicated. I shouldn`t say it, but I`m kind of happy now.\par \par \uc2 I bought this pen.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. It`s a really beautiful pen.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yes. And I also keep the house at whatever degrees temperature I like. I have thermostat freedom.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Amanda Shires has also taken up Backgammon.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think you ended up gravitating to Backgammon?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: I think because it involves a lot of thinking, and it`s involved enough that you can`t let other thoughts come in.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: And then it`s like a hitting game. You have to learn how to hit your friends. You move the other one, too.\par \par \uc2 Do you have a guest bedroom?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: I am moving in.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You`re moving in? Oh, good, so you`re going to teach me?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: Yes. We`re going to play backgammon and we`re going to paint and we`re going to use our pens.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SHIRES: And I`m coming to you Christmas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This is Amanda`s ninth studio album. I really do think it`s her best. I mean, by far, it`s extraordinary album. Another piece of good news. As you may know, she`s in a group called the High Women with Marin Morris, Brandi Carlile, Natalie Hemby. They are all getting back together very soon. So there`s new music coming from them, which excites me enormously.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s still doing a lot of thinking, though, Anthony, I`m watching her.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Wouldn`t you be?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, a 10-year marriage. They were -- you know, they played in the same band together. She won a Grammy together. You know, it clearly hit her hard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your recall in these stories and your ability to connect the dots from past conversations to present is really amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, you can see, I mean, it`s so interesting to see where people move over time, you know. I mean, that`s part of what -- part of the joy of getting to talk to people again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love her voice and love her music. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Before we go though, we want to wish -- talking to you, A.D. Trishman.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A very happy retirement. Listen. Look at this woman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She started as a production assistant on the morning show 41 years ago. She`s been working for us ever since.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020251125elbp00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For October 22, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 John Lauritsen, Margaret Brennan, Charlie D`Agata, Weijia Jiang,  Carter Evans, Maurice DuBois, Dave Malkoff, Elaine Quijano, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump`s expanding White House construction project now includes demolishing the entire East Wing to make way for his massive ballroom.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): We`re very proud of it. It`s gotten great reviews. It`s gotten really great reviews.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Another U.S. military strike on an alleged drug boat, this time in the Pacific -- the growing number of casualties and concerns about the operation.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A school bus bursts into flames. The mom who raced to the rescue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN (Kayaker): These are all monkeys jumping in.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And it`s raining monkeys in Florida. What`s behind this mass primate plunge?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We begin tonight with President Trump`s expanding plans to architecturally transform the White House.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Which we learned today includes the complete destruction of the East Wing.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The first images that emerged on Monday of the ripped-down walls of the first lady`s wing sparked criticism from those who want to preserve the history of 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And those who point to the president`s promise last summer that the massive privately funded ballroom he`s building would not touch or interfere with the current building.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The president calls the project a much-needed modernization. Critics call it a desecration.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Senior White House correspondent Weijia Jiang leads us off from the North Lawn -- Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, President Trump says the ballroom construction will cost $300 million, up from -- up from $200 million, which was his original estimate, all paid for, as you mentioned, by private donors.\par \par \uc2 Senior administration officials tell me it was always the plan to modernize the East Wing and demolishing it completely makes the most sense.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): As construction crews continue to tear down the East Wing of the White House...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Nobody`s actually seen anything quite like it. And I think it`ll be one of the great ballrooms anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: ... tonight, President Trump showed off the plan.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It`s really an honor to add that great ballroom to the structures in Washington. It`s going to be one of the great ballrooms of the world. And I think it`s going to be something you`re all going to be very proud of.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The 90,000-square-foot ballroom, once finished, will be nearly double the size of the main White House building. The demolition has taken many by surprise after Trump said this in July:\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It won`t interfere with the current building. It won`t be. It`ll be near it, but not touching it and pays total respect to the existing building, which I`m the biggest fan of. It`s my favorite.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The first lady`s office has already been demolished. A family theater and a gift shop will also be flattened.\par \par \uc2 In a new letter to the Trump administration, the \uc2 National Trust for Historic Preservation\uc2  wrote that the addition will overwhelm the White House itself and urged officials to pause the demolition until the plans have been reviewed by \uc2 the National Capital Planning Commission\uc2 , which approves plans for federal buildings.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Green was a commissioner until July.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN CLARK GREEN (Former Commissioner, \uc2 National Capital Planning Commission\uc2 ): This is a building that belongs to all of us as people. This is part of our heritage. This is part of our story as Americans. And we deserve to have that conversation about its fate and what happens to it.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Tourists we spoke with on site today were torn over the president`s project.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I think it`s disgraceful. It`s a desecration, really, I think. I don`t know that anybody particularly wants a big ballroom.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Nixon had his bowling alley. JFK had his swimming pool. So he`s adding his touch. And it`s going to be beautiful when it`s done, absolutely beautiful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, Weijia, you mentioned \uc2 the National Capital Planning Commission\uc2  has to review these renovation plans. Is the White House engaging with them right now?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Yes, a White House official says they will submit construction plans to the commission at an appropriate time, adding, the group does not have oversight over demolitions.\par \par \uc2 CBS News spoke with former first lady Laura Bush`s chief of staff, Anita McBride, who said multiple East Wing alums across administrations are stunned by the demo images, sharing this quote from former first lady Betty Ford: "If the West Wing is the mind of the nation, then the East Wing is its heart."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Weijia Jiang at the White House, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Turning now to the president`s widening war on alleged drug trafficking, not only in the Caribbean, but now also the Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A U.S. military strike last night off Colombia was the eighth strike in recent months against suspected smugglers. At least two of them were killed, bringing the total now to 34.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Here`s senior national security correspondent Charlie D`Agata.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): New video posted by Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  appears to show a speedboat half loaded with large black bundles slicing through the waves, a flash of bright light, followed by an explosion, then the vessel engulfed in flames.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Every one of those boats that gets knocked out is saving 25,000 American lives.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It`s the first known military operation in the Pacific, the boat taken out off the coast of Colombia, which produces 90 percent of the cocaine that eventually is sold in the United States.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They make drugs. They refine drugs. They make cocaine. They have cocaine factories.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The previous seven strikes, killing at least 32 people, were conducted in the Caribbean near Venezuela, where the U.S. military footprint includes 10,000 troops and eight warships. The president has argued the boats were carrying drugs headed for the United States, but most of the drugs trafficked through the Caribbean are headed for Europe.\par \par \uc2 As in those Caribbean strikes, no evidence was given about who was on board the boat hit last night or what exactly they were carrying. The administration has designated the cartels as terrorist organizations and claims the strikes are legitimate because the U.S. is engaged in an armed conflict.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (U.S. Secretary of State): They are people traveling on international waters headed towards the United States with hostilities in mind, which includes flooding our country with dangerous, deadly drugs.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Former top DEA agent Mike Vigil disputes that assessment.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL VIGIL (Former DEA Agent): It really violates the Uniform Code of Military Justice. It violates human rights. And there`s no proof that these boats were carrying drugs because they destroyed them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: That former DEA agent also told us that blowing up a few boats will have little impact on the cartels, that they`re adept at changing routes, and, if Trump intends on making an impact, he will need to target their infrastructure on land.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Charlie D`Agata in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump said this evening it was he who called off his anticipated second summit with Russia`s \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They were to meet in Hungary to discuss an end to the war in Ukraine, but the president says it didn`t feel right to meet with Putin at this time, so he canceled.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The president at an Oval Office meeting with the secretary-general of \uc2 NATO\uc2  also discussed new U.S. sanctions against Russian oil companies.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation moderator Margaret Brennan here now to take us through this.\par \par \uc2 So, Margaret, just a week ago, I know it seems longer, but President Trump was talking with Zelenskyy and Putin, but, if anything, things are looking worse in Ukraine this week as we speak. So does that explain why the \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary-general is there in Washington?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Two European officials tell me that Trump now has some realization that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  needs to be pushed in order to come to that negotiating table and that he cannot demolish a kindergarten and pay zero cost for it. Today, we saw those images from a preschool that took a direct hit from Russian missiles, and you can see in the video parents coming to find their young children as they were evacuated.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, none of those kids were seriously injured, but this strike came just 24 hours after President Trump announced that he had pushed off planning that peace summit. So, today, the \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary-general is here to try to keep cease-fire negotiations on the table urgently.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, Margaret, if the president is now engaged in a new realization, what exactly happens now?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we will see how far that realization takes him. We know today he committed to more sanctions on Russian oil and gas companies to dissuade their trading partners from continuing to buy the products.\par \par \uc2 And, on the battlefield, my sources tell me that the administration has turned back on the intelligence sharing that allows Ukraine to make more precise long-range strikes inside of Russia. That`s for targets that include energy sites and military infrastructure, but not anything that would be politically sensitive.\par \par \uc2 And President Trump said today he does have a limit here. He will not be providing American-made Tomahawk long-range missiles to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Margaret Brennan in Washington, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 A tow truck driver had to be rescued today after a crash left his truck dangling from an overpass near Dallas. Police say the driver swerved -- swerved to avoid hitting a disabled vehicle, hit a concrete barrier, and nearly went over the side. He escaped without serious injuries.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The \uc2 Louvre Museum\uc2  reopened in Paris today, three days after Sunday`s daring jewel heist. Thieves stole France`s crown jewels worth more than $100 million before escaping on motorbikes. The museum director says no external cameras saw, captured them on video. She submitted her resignation, but it was rejected by the government.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And more than 400 items, including Muppets and props, from Jim Henson`s storied career are going up for auction, the first auction ever from his archives. Online bidding opened today, and the auction closes next month. Henson`s production company this year is celebrating its 70th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now to a terrifying scene of a school bus bursting into flames captured on a Ring camera in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All on board narrowly escaped, thanks to a quick-thinking mom.\par \par \uc2 John Lauritsen of CBS News Minnesota spoke with her.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KARI THORP (Help Rescued Children): She got about halfway to the driveway and I`m like: "Fire, fire."\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAURITSEN (voice-over): The end of the school day is normally routine in Kari Thorp`s neighborhood. But a couple weeks ago, as her daughter was getting dropped off, Kari noticed smoke and flames coming from under the bus and alerted the driver.\par \par \uc2 KARI THORP: It was crazy. I mean, I`m just thankful that he was very calm.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAURITSEN: They worked quickly to get all 22 kids off safely. Her Ring doorbell captured the East Bethel Elementary students gathering in front of her house as the flames began to spread.\par \par \uc2 KARI THORP: Kids were crying. Kids were scared, freaking out. It was quite emotional and kind of frantic.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAURITSEN: Within minutes, the fire had taken over.\par \par \uc2 KARI THORP: It moved pretty fast, yes. I mean, it was probably within 15 minutes that thing was engulfed. It was crazy to see the tires explode.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAURITSEN: School staff and volunteer firefighters arrived quickly. They got the flames under control. And it was when the bus was finally towed away that you could see just how intense the fire was.\par \par \uc2 She and the bus driver were recognized by parents and school leaders for their heroic efforts, as investigators work to figure out what caused the fire.\par \par \uc2 John Lauritsen, CBS News, in Bethel, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead on the CBS Evening News: an experimental breast cancer treatment that could keep the cancer from coming back.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Plus, Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: I`m Carter Evans just outside Salt Lake City. Cargo theft has become a huge problem in the U.S. We will show you how one Utah bike manufacturer tracked down hundreds of its bikes in Los Angeles. That`s next on Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, later, the flying monkeys of Florida putting on quite the show.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN (Kayaker): Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN: It`s raining monkeys.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Breast cancer survival rates continue to improve thanks to advances in detection and treatment, but still, with aggressive cancers, roughly 30 percent of patients can relapse.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A recent clinical trial showed success in identifying and treating breast cancer survivors at a higher risk because of dormant sleeper cells that hide in their bodies.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Elaine Quijano met with a patient who has renewed hope.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voice-over): Illeana Casiano-Vazquez and her husband, Sam, cherish every minute together. Six years ago, she was diagnosed with an aggressive form of breast cancer, triple negative, which has fewer treatment options and a higher rate of recurrence.\par \par \uc2 ILLEANA CASIANO-VAZQUEZ (Cancer Patient): The first thing I thought about were my boys, so -- and the same feeling of, oh, my God, am I going to die? Sort of was this immediate like, mmm, I`m not going to die.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Chemotherapy and a double mastectomy eliminated signs of cancer, but naturally she`s still worried.\par \par \uc2 ILLEANA CASIANO-VAZQUEZ: The mental struggle that comes with the fear of recurrence is real and strong.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Dr. Angela DeMichele is Casiano-Vazquez`s oncologist.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELA DEMICHELE (Professor of Medicine and Epidemiology, \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 ): So she had about a 25 percent chance that the breast cancer would come back within five years.\par \par \uc2 So, numerous here, but really...\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: She co-leads a team conducting a clinical trial that targets breast cancer sleeper cells, cells that break away from the main tumor and can resurface later to spread cancer throughout the body.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELA DEMICHELE: The way that we follow patients, we`re not going to right now find the cancer until it`s already found its way to another part of the body. And, by that time, it`s incurable.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: There were sleeper cells in Casiano-Vazquez`s bone marrow. So Dr. DeMichele gave her drugs to attack the cells.\par \par \uc2 After the six months of treatment, what did you find?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELA DEMICHELE: We were so thrilled. We could no longer see these dormant cells in her bone marrow.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: There were 51 women in the trial. After seven years, only two people saw the cancer come back, far below the expected average.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELA DEMICHELE: The next steps are to take this to larger trials to confirm this, and then to really see if this could also apply to other cancers.\par \par \uc2 ILLEANA CASIANO-VAZQUEZ: Two, three, four.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Casiano-Vazquez counts herself incredibly fortunate and is now part of the follow-up study.\par \par \uc2 ILLEANA CASIANO-VAZQUEZ: The only chance I have is if this clinical trial continues to work. It`s a chance that other women have to give them hope that it won`t come back.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Elaine Quijano, CBS News, Pottstown, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : How millions of dollars in goods are being stolen not by force, but through deception.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Tonight`s Eye on America looks at a significant factor in the rising costs of goods, cargo theft. The Sheriff`s Department in San Bernardino County, California, says it`s broken up a ring of thieves that stole millions of dollars in cargo through a scheme that involved legitimate trucking companies.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans learned how this type of highway robbery hurts businesses and consumers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: If you`re inside, come out with your hands up.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voice-over): With a show of force, investigators made their move in Bell, California.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Make yourself known. We know you`re in there.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Hidden in these warehouses, about a million dollars of stolen goods, household items, medicine and beauty products.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Dave Navarro investigates cargo theft with the \uc2 California Highway Patrol\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LT. DAVID NAVARRO (\uc2 California Highway Patrol\uc2 ): Several pallets of Alba sunscreen.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Cargo theft accounts for up to $35 billion in losses in the U.S. every year, often while products are in transit.\par \par \uc2 LT. DAVID NAVARRO: People obviously are driving the freeways, and they see commercial trailers up and down the freeway all day long, but no one really knows if that is a stolen load that they`re driving next to and/or if it`s just going from point A to point B.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Highly visible retail thefts get a lot of attention, but losses from cargo theft cost everyone.\par \par \uc2 Who`s ultimately paying for this?\par \par \uc2 LT. DAVID NAVARRO: Oh, consumers. The loss to the businesses are temporary. All they`re going to do is increase their prices and then who pays for it is the consumers.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Lieutenant Navarro says many cargo heists look like legitimate transactions. Thieves first steal a business` identity, then trick shippers into handing over entire container loads.\par \par \uc2 That`s how Utah-based Ari Bikes lost a truckload of almost 350 bikes.\par \par \uc2 TYLER CLOWARD (Director of Product Development, Ari Bikes): It was a brand-new e-bike that we had just launched.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Tyler Cloward is director of product development.\par \par \uc2 And someone got between you and that trucker.\par \par \uc2 TYLER CLOWARD: The company who signed up to take this load, bring it up to us, they were actually impersonating a legitimate trucking company`s e- mail. It looked completely real. Everything checked out.\par \par \uc2 I`m trying to get the word out, looking for these bikes.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So he put out a call for help online, and the biking community came through. A local shop sent him this video of a man bringing one in looking for parts.\par \par \uc2 TYLER CLOWARD: I immediately called the sheriff`s department and go, hey, there`s a guy with our stolen product. We have this.\par \par \uc2 We really appreciate it. We were able to recover most of these bikes.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: He got most of them back, but is still finding listings for the remaining bikes at deep discounts online.\par \par \uc2 I like a good deal online. I know a lot of people who do. Could we unknowingly be supporting some of this theft?\par \par \uc2 LT. DAVID NAVARRO: Yes. If you`re going after a high-value item, do a little bit of homework. Call this phone number, see if the phone number works. Go to the Web site, see if the Web site works.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Turns out, when a deal looks like a steal, it just might be.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Up next: The wild monkeys of Florida go for a swim.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We end tonight with a big splash.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Wild monkeys putting on an impressive display of synchronized swimming, of all things.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Dave Malkoff.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voice-over): If you`re wondering what that is flying like a sack of potatoes from a tree in Ocala, Florida...\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN (Kayaker): This is, like, crazy.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: ... we will let Trenda Kitchen tell you between fits of laughter.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN: It`s raining monkeys.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The kayaker says her peaceful ride through Silver Springs State Park was interrupted last week when a group of rhesus macaques began leaping from the surrounding trees into the water around her.\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN: These are all monkeys jumping in.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN: And one jumped in right here by me.\par \par \uc2 It was overwhelming excitement.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Kitchen told CBS News she hoped to see the monkeys, but didn`t expect this.\par \par \uc2 TRENDA KITCHEN: And I just was, like, totally flipping out. Like, this is crazy fun.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Rhesus macaques are an invasive species first introduced to Florida`s ecosystem by a tour boat operator in the 1930s and have thrived at Silver Springs ever since.\par \par \uc2 We met up with some primates at the Greensboro Science Center in North Carolina. Jessica Hoffman thinks she knows what`s going on here.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA HOFFMAN (Greensboro Science Center): Some primates do really just enjoy swimming. Like, humans are one of them. We love to swim, right?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: They`re doing a cannonball to do a cannonball.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA HOFFMAN: They -- kind of.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: We share 93 percent of our DNA with these guys and 100 percent of our sense of adventure.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff, CBS News, Greensboro, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Oh, my. At least it wasn`t pigs flying, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At least.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : At least, yes. Yes, an umbrella is not going to help you with that.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Nope.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That`s the CBS Evening News. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020251023elam00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Israel Launches Ground Offensive in Gaza City; \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Says Israel is Committing Genocide on Gaza; U.S. Strikes Suspected Drug Trafficking Boats; Sources: Kirk Murder Suspect Confessed Online; \uc2 Oracle\uc2  Could Play Role in Deal to Save TikTok; Court Blocks Firing of \uc2 Fed Reserve\uc2  Gov. Lisa Cook; Trump Sending Federal Law Enforcement to Memphis; Electric Vehicles Safety Test Results; Key Hearing Today in Mangione Murder Case; Safety of Menopause Medication; Weighing Hormone Therapy for Menopause Symptoms; Trump Makes History with Second State Visit to the U.K.; Elin Hilderbrand co-Writes Book with Daughter; Chef Vinnie Cimino`s Flavors of the Midwest; Actor, Robert Redford Dies at Age 89; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Brett Goldstein and Imogen Poots on "All of You," \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Imtiaz Tyab, Carter  Evans, Jo Ling Kent, Nicole Valdes, Kris Van Cleave, Jericka Duncan, Nikki  Battiste, Vladimir Duthiers, Ed O`Keefe, \par \uc2 15,552 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot of ground to cover today, including breaking news out of the Middle East, plus, a potential deal to keep TikTok from shutting down, but first, here is today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The latest on Charlie Kirk`s alleged killer and the Trump`s administration to use his shooting to mobilize their supporter.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R) (Vice President of the United States of America): You have a role, too. So when you see someone celebrating Charlie`s murder, call them out and help, call their employer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The U.S. military destroys another boat from Venezuela and the President warns drug cartels to watch out.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): When they come by land, we`re going to stop them the same way we stop the boats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Luigi Mangione, the accused killer of UnitedHealthcare`s CEO is back in court.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: They will be arguing that he had inspired some of these more recent killings.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The White House says it has reached a framework deal with China to save the future of TikTok in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Nick Chubb! Touchdown Texans! They are on top.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Houston leads by five. Baker and the Bucs` one last shot to win the game with six seconds left.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: And a touchdown, Tampa Bay.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Who are you most excited to meet tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 OWEN COOPER (Actor): I think everyone knows that Jake Gyllenhaal is my top number one.\par \par \uc2 JAKE GYLLENHAAL (Actor): Now, like, I am nervous. Now, I feel nervous.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Before 15-year-old Owen Cooper made history as the youngest male actor to win an Emmy, he got a surprise visit and a gift from his acting hero.\par \par \uc2 JAKE GYLLENHAAL: I got nominated for an Academy award, and a friend of mine sent me this before. It is just a lucky duck.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A lucky duck! Well, it worked. That one is on Owen. He has got to pay it forward and give that duck to someone else.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive. Making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doesn`t that just give you tingles all over.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so nice when people -- young people meet people they admire so much, and that Jake Gyllenhaal really is lovely, and they are as lovely as they appear to be.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is even nicer, yes, exactly. They are the person that you thought they were and more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that lucky duck is going to go somewhere special, I am sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The lucky duck, the luck with the skill of that young man, he is going to have a long career.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly! He is just getting started.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us, we are going to start with breaking news overnight from the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Israel says it launched the main phase of its offensive to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza`s largest city. Military officials say ground forces are moving deeper into the area.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is tracking all of this from London.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, good morning.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, Gaza City residents have told CBS News they are being subjected to "heavy and relentless bombings as the Israeli military launches its ground invasion there and as the United Nations inquiry has found that Israel`s nearly two-year war on Gaza is in their words, a genocide.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Israel`s military has started what it has called the main step of its ground invasion of Gaza City, where tired neighborhoods have already been reduced to ruins and hundreds of thousands of Palestinians have been forced to flee.\par \par \uc2 It comes as Israeli leaders have reacted angrily to the U.N. Commission of Inquiry`s Genocide Report, denouncing it as "distorted and false."\par \par \uc2 DANIEL MERON (U.N. Israeli Ambassador): The report relies entirely on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  falsehoods, laundered and repeated by others.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Investigators found "reasonable grounds" that Israel has committed four of the five acts of genocide under the 1948 U.N. Convention.\par \par \uc2 NAVI PILLAY (U.N. Independent International Commission of Inquiry in Occupied Palestinian Territory Chair): The goal of the Israeli government is abundantly clear, as we continue to witness the destruction of Palestinians in Gaza. The Commission finds it is genocide.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The four acts Israel is accused of committing since the start of the war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , following the brutal 2023 October 7th attacks include, killing members of a group, causing them serious bodily and mental harm, deliberately inflicting conditions calculated to destroy the group, and preventing births.\par \par \uc2 The report not only sites the patterns of conduct by Israeli forces as evidence of "genocidal intent," but also the statements of Israeli leaders by alleging Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , President \uc2 Isaac Herzog\uc2 , and former Defense Minister Yoav Gallant, have all "incited the commission of genocide."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And a number of international and Israeli human rights groups and scholars have also accused Israel of genocide against Palestinians in Gaza. This, while the \uc2 International Court of Justice\uc2  is hearing a case that accuses Israeli forces of genocide, a case Israel has called wholly unfounded and based on biased and false claims -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imtiaz, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 For the second time this month, President Trump says that he ordered the U.S. military to attack a boat that he says was carrying illegal drugs from Venezuela to this country.\par \par \uc2 Now Trump posted this video yesterday saying it shows a moment that the boat was destroyed, killing three people.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata covers The Pentagon for us.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning to you. What can you tell us about this latest attack?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Yes, good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 So, this latest strike comes about two weeks after the U.S. military struck another Venezuelan boat, as you said, allegedly trafficking drugs, killing 11 people. The Trump administration has built up its presence in the Southern Caribbean over the last month, saying it is to combat the flow of illegal narcotics into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Video posted on President Trump`s Truth Social account shows what appears to be a stationary vessel in open water. Seconds later, an explosion leaves it engulfed in flames. The result of what Trump said was a targeted U.S. military strike against identified Venezuelan narcoterrorists on Monday morning.\\\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We have proof. All you have to do is look at the cargo that was -- like it is scattered all over the ocean. Big bags of cocaine and fentanyl all over the place.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The President said, three people were killed aboard the boat. It comes just about two weeks after another U.S. strike killed eleven people aboard another vessel in international waters. Sources tell CBS News, that boat had attempted to turn back before it was repeatedly fired on.\par \par \uc2 Trump said those on board were members of the designated foreign terrorist organization, TDA or Tren de Aragua, something Venezuelan officials have denied.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Adam Schiff, says he plans to challenge President Trump`s authority to carry out such strikes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I don`t want to see us get into some war with Venezuela, because the President is just blowing ships willy-nilly out of the water.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Venezuela`s government has been encouraging its citizens to join local militias and familiarize themselves with the use of firearms amid what it calls U.S. aggression in the region.\par \par \uc2 President Trump warned that the U.S. military operation against drug traffickers won`t be limited to suspected smugglers at sea.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And you know what? We`re telling the cartels right now, we`re going to be stopping them, too, when they come by land, we`re going to be stopping them the same way we stop the boats.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The U.S. also deployed F-35 fighter jets to the region after two Venezuelan F-16s came close to a U.S. Destroyer there. Democrats and some Republicans have expressed concern that the U.S. military actions in the region might violate international law -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We have got a number of developments in the investigation into Charlie Kirk`s assassination. CBS News has confirmed a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " report that the suspect, Tyler Robinson, appeared to confess in an online group chat just before his arrest.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Vice President J.D. Vance stepped in to host an episode of Kirk`s podcast, urging Trump reporters to report and call out anyone celebrating Kirk`s death.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans is covering all of this from the courthouse in Utah.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The alleged shooter is expected to make a virtual appearance in court from jail when those charges are filed today, and as a possible preview of what`s to come, a probable cause affidavit listed aggravated murder, felony discharge of a firearm, and obstruction of justice as reasons for his arrest last week.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL (\uc2 U.S. Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  director): There was evidence, DNA evidence --\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel said Monday, DNA evidence found on a screwdriver at the scene of the shooting and on a towel wrapped around the alleged murder weapon had now been linked to the suspect.\par \par \uc2 Patel added, his agency is reviewing the alleged gunman`s online activity and cellphone data.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL: He had a text message exchange. He, the suspect, with another individual, in which he claimed that he had an opportunity to take out Charlie Kirk and he was going to do it, because of his hatred for what Charlie stood for.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY EARHARDT (Fox News Channel Host, "Fox And Friends"): Why didn`t that person say something?\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL: You know, our investigation is ongoing. That`s a great question.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: A discord spokesman and two law enforcement sources told CBS News, an account appearing to belong to the suspect claimed responsibility for the shooting in a group chat around two hours before his arrest last Thursday. Writing in part quote, "It was me at UVU yesterday. I`m sorry for all this."\par \par \uc2 The messages were first reported by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." \uc2 Discord\uc2  has said it hasn`t found any evidence that the suspect planned kirk`s assassination on the platform.\par \par \uc2 While no motive has been identified, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director said the suspect`s family and friends told investigators, he subscribed to a left wing ideology.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT J.D. VANCE: We have to talk about this incredibly destructive movement of left-wing extremism that has grown up over the last few years and I believe is part of the reason why Charlie was killed by an assassin`s bullet.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Hosting his friend`s podcast late Monday, Vice President J.D. Vance vowed to prevent what he called festering violence on the far left, and appearing as a guest, President Trump`s Deputy chief-of-staff \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , argued Kirk`s death was the result of an organized campaign of terrorist networks, though he offered no evidence to support that claim.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER\uc2 , (Deputy Chief-of-Staff for Policy and Homeland Security adviser): We are going to use every resource we have at the Department of Justice, Homeland Security, and throughout this government to identify, disrupt, dismantle, and destroy these networks and make America safe again for the American people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now for now, investigators say the suspected shooter is believed to have acted alone and we`re expecting to learn more about that later today in court.\par \par \uc2 Also today, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director, Kash Patel, is expected to face questions about his agency`s handling of the case when he testifies on capitol hill -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Carter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There are major developments in the fight over TikTok. The Trump administration says it struck a deal with China that would stop the ban of the app, but there are still some pretty big questions over the details.\par \par \uc2 Senior business and tech correspondent, Jo Ling Kent has more.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Sources with knowledge of the negotiations tell CBS News, the tech giant, \uc2 Oracle\uc2 , is part of a group of companies that would allow TikTok in the U.S. to continue operating.\par \par \uc2 According to sources, the structure of the final deal is unclear, but it will include multiple firms. There`s no word on what level, though, of involvement there will be from Chinese companies, including TikTok`s parent company, ByteDance and what their role will be in the deal or who will ultimately control TikTok`s algorithm, that recommendation algorithm, that`s the key technology that serves up the videos that you see, keeping users scrolling TikTok.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes after Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said Monday, the administration and China have struck a framework deal to meet the requirements of a law that now requires TikTok to be owned by a U.S. entity or face a ban.\par \par \uc2 Oracle\uc2 `s founder and chairman, \uc2 Larry Ellison\uc2 , is an ally of President Trump and you may remember back in 2020, \uc2 Oracle\uc2  briefly reached a deal to buy TikTok, but then that fell apart. We should point out, his son, \uc2 David Ellison\uc2 , is now the owner of \uc2 CBS\uc2  through a merger combining his media company, \uc2 Skydance\uc2 , with CBS News` parent company, Paramount.\par \par \uc2 Of course, CBS News, we`ve reached out to \uc2 Oracle\uc2  executives and a White House spokesperson for comment and a TikTok spokesperson declined our multiple requests for comment -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so Trump`s deadline to sell TikTok expires Wednesday, two days before Trump and Xi are set to discuss the final details. What are we expecting?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Yes, this is a great question for everyone on TikTok. It`s unclear what will happen, but experts do not expect the app to come down like it did back in mid-January.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Bessent says a deal would address U.S. National Security requirements, but keep Chinese characteristics of TikTok.\par \par \uc2 So, if a deal does fall through, a TikTok ban could impact roughly seven million small businesses who heavily rely on the platform, 170 million TikTok users here in the U.S. -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jo Ling, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A federal appeals court late yesterday blocked President Trump from firing Federal Reserve Governor Lisa Cook. Now, the move clears away for Cook to participate in this week`s crucial two-day meeting on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you may recall tried to remove her from the Board, alleging mortgage fraud, even though Cook has not been charged with a crime. Critics say the President`s push to remove her is a threat to the Fed`s independence.\par \par \uc2 Also late Monday, the Senate voted to confirm Stephen Miran, he is one of President Trump`s top economic advisers to serve on the Federal Board of Governors. He is also expected to vote at the Fed meeting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Memphis, Tennessee could be the next city to see an influx of federal agents and National Guard troops, that`s according to the President himself. He says the effort is to crack down on violent crime, and like other cities sited by the administration, the mayor of Memphis is a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes is in Memphis with the reaction now to the President`s decision.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We are sending in a lot of help.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: President Trump mobilized federal law enforcement agents to deploy to the streets of Memphis on Monday. Trump says federal agents from multiple agencies including the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , \uc2 Drug Enforcement Administration\uc2 , and \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  will join \uc2 Tennessee National Guard\uc2  troops as part of a task force to combat crime in the city.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has already deployed National Guard troops to the streets of Los Angeles and Washington, D.C. But Memphis will be the first city in a red state.\par \par \uc2 Many local Democratic officials are against the decision.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON (D) (State Representative): The militarized occupation of our city is not a resolution to the problems that we have.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: A CBS News data team review of \uc2 FBI\uc2  statistics show that Memphis had the highest rate of violent crime of all major U.S. cities last year, but Memphis Police data shows there was a decrease in violent crime in the first nine months of this year.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLER\uc2  (Restaurant owner): We support our community.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: \uc2 Mike Miller\uc2  has owned Patrick`s Restaurant in East Memphis for twenty years. He is optimistic a federal task force could help make the city safer.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLER\uc2 : It should make a difference. It should help. It should not be a negative.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Amber Sherman grew up here. The lifelong activist worries the presence of federal troops could do more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 AMBER SHERMAN (Activist): No one is really talking about the real root issues of crime and the fact that so much federal funding that has been cut from the city of Memphis that actually was working to combat crime.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Now, this is not the only city that could see a federal law enforcement presence soon. The President yesterday threatened Chicago again, despite the opposition from that city`s Democratic mayor and governor.\par \par \uc2 And Gayle, another city the President mentioned yesterday, St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re learning about the results of new safety tests for popular electric vehicles. The \uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  looked at how seven E.V.s performed in collisions and avoiding pedestrians. Kris Van Cleave has got the results.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In tests simulating a crash between vehicles traveling in opposite directions at 40 miles an hour, four of the seven vehicles tested, the \uc2 BMW\uc2  i4, Chevy Blazer E.V., Tesla Cyber Truck, \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  ID. Buzz earned good ratings. The \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Model 3 earned a passing, acceptable, but two have room to improve.\par \par \uc2 The Nissan ARIYA SUV rated marginal, because the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety Injury Metrics showed a high risk of chest injuries to backseat passengers. And the Ford F-150 Lightning earned the lowest score, a poor rating because the rear seated dummy showed a high risk of chest, head, or neck injuries and the dummy`s lap belt moved from the ideal position on the pelvis vision on to the abdomen.\par \par \uc2 RAUL ARBELAEZ (\uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  vice president): That is not something we want to see in any crash.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 IIHS\uc2  Vice President Raul Arbelaez.\par \par \uc2 The common sensibility is a big vehicle, like a full size pickup should do well in a crash.\par \par \uc2 RAUL ARBELAEZ: Most people think bigger is better, heavier is better, and certainly we had that with the Ford F-150 Lightning. The vehicle structurally holds up really well in this crash, but fell short in protecting the rear seat position.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 IIHS\uc2  also looked at pedestrian crash avoidance technology, and most performed well. The \uc2 BMW\uc2 , Ford, and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  models all scored top marks joining the ARYA with good ratings.\par \par \uc2 When it came to headlights, five of the seven earned acceptable ratings. But both the \uc2 BMW\uc2  sedan and the Cyber Truck had some lights earning a poor rating.\par \par \uc2 Automakers including \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , say they are evaluating the results and will continue to work with \uc2 IIHS\uc2  in all matters related to customer safety. Ford adds safety is a top priority. We are always working to continuously improve.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For more on what the automakers say about these test results, we invite you to go to cbsnews.com.\par \par \uc2 I think it is so important that they do these, even though good cars are destroyed in the process. If you`re in the market for getting one of these E.V.s, this is going to be very helpful for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure, I wonder how many cars they destroy a year for our safety?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I do, too, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and some people say that especially traditionalists who love old school cars, they say these news cars, they are not made like they used to be. So, you have to test them out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, definitely not as much metal in them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s something else, when they break down.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, Luigi Mangione is back in court today. Prosecutors may try to link the killing of a health insurance CEO to the broader climate of political violence. But first, 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, one of "Food & Wine`s" best new chefs of 2025 gives Tony a taste of his delicious midwestern cooking. And Deborah Norville will join us for "What to Watch." She has got a new game show and she is here to challenge us all. I think we all know, I am a fan of useless trivia, my childhood friends were books and movies, so that gives me an edge.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Geno Smith`s first home game in Las Vegas was a nightmare. The new Raiders quarterback threw three interceptions and then lost to the L.A. Chargers. In the other game, Tampa Bay won in the final seconds beating the \uc2 Houston Texans\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, stick around, Luigi Mangione heads back to court. How Charlie Kirk`s assassination could impact the case. We will break it down. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back, 7:30, CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Somebody get the crane. That is a truck being swallowed rear first by a sink hole in Mexico City. There was soda on board. It is a bit shaken now. Be careful when you open it. This was Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Authorities believe a very old drainage network underneath the roadway collapsed and the truck went down with it. It was removed a day later. No reports of injuries.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wild!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I thought that was CGI for a second.\par \par \uc2 All right, back home, Luigi Mangione is in a New York City court this morning. The 27-year-old faces state and federal murder charges in the case of killing \uc2 UnitedHealthcare\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , last year.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  warned farther political violence -- not political -- excuse me, potential violence against prominent figures following Thompson`s death in the assassination of Charlie Kirk.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent, Jericka Duncan is at the courthouse in Lower Manhattan. Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 It is hard to see with all the traffic, but there is a line of people, some with signs saying "Free Luigi," getting ready to go into the court. Now keep in mind, Mangione is facing charges in three separate jurisdictions, but today is about the eleven counts that he is facing right here in New York.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Luigi Mangione has pleaded not guilty in the shooting and killing of 50-year-old UnitedHealthcare CEO and father of two, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , last December.\par \par \uc2 Today, Mangione could learn which prosecutor he`ll face first.\par \par \uc2 KAREN FRIEDMAN AGNIFILO (Defense attorney): Like every other defendant, he is entitled to a presumption of innocence.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: CBS New legal analyst, Caroline Polisi.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: I think the most charges here come from federal prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Federal prosecutors are also pursuing charges, including murder with a firearm, which could carry the death penalty. Because of that, Mangione`s attorneys argued the federal case should be tried first.\par \par \uc2 It almost feels like the state and federal prosecutors are trying to compete for which case is going to go first.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: That`s absolutely right. We`re seeing a very public turf war here and we don`t know the outcome. I suspect that a large portion of the hearing will be dedicated to this timeline.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mangione`s hearing comes days after the assassination of conservative activist, Charlie Kirk, and on the same day Kirk`s suspected killer will appear in court in Utah. Kirk`s death, like Thompson`s, has ignited conversations and anger around the political violence in this country.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: You will see prosecutors using this recent assassination of Charlie Kirk as an example of why Luigi Mangione is so dangerous. Federal prosecutors, especially, will be using this idea of the follow-on murder, the copycat murder, the inspiration.\par \par \uc2 The fact that Luigi Mangione had a rally of supporters behind him, he has become sort of this folk hero for a certain sector of individuals online.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mangione has pleaded not guilty to all of the charges brought against him so far. As far as the federal case is concerned, he is due in court for that in December -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jericka, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, some important health news for women. We are going to show you the latest research on menopause therapy and how one patient and her doctor are weighing the benefits against all the risk.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In today`s "Health Watch," we`re talking about the safety of hormone therapy for women struggling with menopause symptoms and struggle is the word here. The treatment can offer relief for symptoms like hot flashes and night sweats, the worst. New research finds hormone therapy does not increase heart disease risk for menopause women under the age of 60.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste spoke to one of the women that is weighing the benefits of the therapy against the risk.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with a woman in her 50s who suffers from perimenopause symptoms, but like many women, has been nervous about trying menopause hormone therapy. That caution lingers from a study about two decades ago that showed an increase risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 A recent study found only about five percent of menopausal women are on hormone therapy today, even though about 80 percent have symptoms.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN (Patient): This is my best friend, my fan.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Hot flashes.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: It`s called Fanny.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And sleepless nights are disrupting Isabel Kallman`s life.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I keep her in my bag, and just to cool myself off when I need to.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: At 53, Kallman`s symptoms are part of perimenopause, the phase before menopause when a woman`s hormones start to decline. The most common medication is hormone therapy, which uses estrogen and if needed, a progestin to alleviate symptoms.\par \par \uc2 Are you considering it?\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I am. I`ve been on the fence about it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Many women have been wary of the treatment because they were concerned it would increase the chances of breast cancer. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has long mandated so-called black box labels on the medication, warnings about stroke, blood clots, dementia, and breast cancer. But the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is now considering removing some of those warnings.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER (Columbia University Irving Medical Center Integrated Women`s Health Director) This is a public health priority and this is a sign that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  sees that.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Mary Rosser has been Cal has been Kallman`s doctor for five years.\par \par \uc2 Is the fear of the risk of breast cancer fair?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER: It`s been over blown.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Rosser believes flawed research from about 20 years ago overstated the risks of breast cancer because that study focused on older women.\par \par \uc2 As today`s new study shows timing matters. A woman`s age when she begins hormone therapy, along with dose and delivery method can substantially mitigate risks.\par \par \uc2 Generally, when would a woman begin hormone therapy?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER: So, we have said to begin as close to the final menstrual period, and then you continue until about the age of 60.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Rosser spent the past nine months working through the risks and benefits with Kallman, who has a family history of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I`m hoping, even if I do it for a year or two, that there is an option for me that can help me in the short-term.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new study says the ideal age when most women should take hormone therapy in order to mitigate risks is between 50 to 59. Recent research shows the right timing of treatment can even reduce the risk of diabetes and improve cognitive function.\par \par \uc2 If you are considering treatment, of course, it`s important to always speak with a doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the most important thing. I am sitting here so disappointed with myself, because I was one of those women that believed that study years ago and didn`t take anything, and have spent years and years struggling with all of these different symptoms and now, it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 So, is there anyone who shouldn`t take the hormone replacement therapy?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new study shows if you are 70 or older, you should not take hormone therapy. If you`re in that 60 to 69 age range, use caution.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rosser at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  told me there are -- for some women who have had certain breast or endometrial cancers, they wouldn`t qualify for systemic hormone therapy, but new studies show they might be able to safely take some local forms like a gel or a cream.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, your number surprised me in the beginning. You said 80 percent of women are going through the symptoms, but only five percent are taking it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is a wild number.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: I mean, it is the power of that study.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even though we know that it is better for us.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes, and the stigma, which I think is slowly fading.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do think --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And it is large in part due to social media, we`re talking about it here. We`re talking about it. The other point I want to make, it can be difficult to find a doctor who really understands menopause, because 20 years ago, the education in Med School was minimal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It has gotten better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: But I think it`s important we talk about it. It`s important for men to know about it, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Segments like this help destigmatize it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nikki, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a special edition of "What to Watch" with Deborah Norville, that`s right! But right now, it is 7:42. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have got some big things coming up for you in our 8:00 hour including the stars of the new romantic drama, "All of You," actors Brett Goldstein and Imogen Poots. The movie explores the idea of soulmates, and it is really fascinating. Would you take a test? Let`s discuss.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Tony visits one of the country`s best new chefs, according to "Food & Wine." How that chef is being honored for his Midwestern nice approach to his food and his employees. It`s ne