today. Investigators say he confessed online.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Trump says he ordered U.S. military forces to destroy another boat from Venezuela, claiming it was carrying illegal drugs to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We have proof, all you have to do is look at the cargo that was -- like it is scattered all over the ocean.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: The crowd are on their feet. They are roaring on. Is it his fourth world record of the year? Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the world`s best pole vaulter breaks his own record, but it was the announcers, well, they went way over the top.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Call it a sweep. He`s held us all in the palm of his hand and he has delivered the knockout pole again. We will never, never forget the night that Mondo Duplantis lit up the stadium and captured the headlines and the hearts of everyone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh God!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will we never forget the night?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Never, Gayle!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Held us in the palm of his hands.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also, it helps when the picture is in slow motion.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it was quite a free fall there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was! It was!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It does go on forever. Twenty feet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fourth World Record is pretty impressive.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. No competition.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have got to begin with the news at the top of the 8:00.\par \par \uc2 The president, President Trump is leaving for a state visit to London. He will become the first U.S. president to be honored with a second such visit. Security is very tight in Britain`s capital after a huge anti- immigration protest over the weekend showed growing discontent with the current government there and support for the pro-Trump Reform Party.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe has traveled from Washington, D.C. to London for us and made it in time.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tony, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 You know, U.S. Presidents serving a second term don`t normally get a state visit. They might get lunch or tea with the monarch, but this commander-in- chief has made no secret of his affinity for the Royals and having the red carpet rolled out for him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Mr. Prime Minister, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: This latest chapter in the special relationship began with a surprise invitation delivered by the British Prime Minister in February.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER (British Prime Minister): It`s an invitation for a second state visit.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The invite was part of an urgent appeal to win over the American President as he upended the global economy with new tariffs.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: The Royal family, no pun intended, is the biggest trump card this government can play at big moments of international statesmanship and diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: CBS News Royal contributors Roya Nikkhah and Julian Payne note American presidents have long met with British monarchs, but in 2003, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was the first American president to make an official state visit. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  received one in 2011, and Trump`s first was in 2019, when he and Queen Elizabeth reviewed troops at Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 During that visit, he also connected with then \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  who he has known for at least two decades.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: This is state to state, but this is a genuine personal relationship that is there as well.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: This state visit, the traditional greeting and Review of the Troops will take place at Windsor Castle, where a state dinner will be held for senior members of Trump`s Cabinet and top business executives.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: There is a huge amount of lobbying to get that ticket.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now hold on, spill the tea. What do you have to do to lobby the palace to get a seat at that state dinner?\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: Well, if I told you that, then you would know how to get into the next one. I`m afraid there`s only so much I can tell you.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: When we start to have a ticket.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: There`s a level beyond which I cannot go.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I tried. I tried.\par \par \uc2 Also on the schedule, is a first of its kind military flyover as the British call it a fly past involving British and American military jets, and tomorrow, we are expected to see William and Kate, the Prince and Princess of Wales, alongside the King and Queen as they welcome the President and First Lady to Windsor Castle -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ed, a lot going on. But back to that anti-immigration protest, I think, it was over the weekend, we have all seen the pictures. What is the political backdrop there?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, so massive protests over the weekend; mostly against current U.K. immigration policies, and among others, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  addressed the crowd remotely saying that violence is coming to you and calling for revolutionary government change here in the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 The Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  who is set to meet with the President on Thursday also facing some troubles as at least three senior officials left his government and there are a lot of questions among the British public why the Royal family and the government are spending so much time and manpower on an American president who in recent polls only earned somewhere in the mid to high teens -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very interesting. Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, our series, "The Dish" is going to take you to Ohio today. Tony is going to take us actually. He is going to introduce us to one of "Food & Wine`s" best new chefs who is being recognized for embracing the flavors and hospitality of the Midwest. Whatever that is, I want a bite.\par \par \uc2 Plus, best-selling author, Elin Hilderbrand is here along with her co- writer -- hey co-writer-- also known as Shelby, her daughter, Shelby Cunningham. Their new novel, "The Academy" is set inside a boarding school, but first, it is 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, the new movie, "All of You" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All of You"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- explores an intriguing question, whether each of us has a soul mate. We will talk to the stars, Brett Goldstein and Imogen Poot about that exact question and their sizzling on screen chemistry.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The queen of the beach read is starting a new chapter. She`s writing with her daughter. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best-selling author, Elin Hilderbrand has written dozens of novels -- look at that -- set on her beloved island of Nantucket. It can fill all the shelves.\par \par \uc2 Her new novel, "The Academy," is a first in a series co-written with her daughter, that would be the lovely Shelby Cunningham. It takes place over a year at a place called Tiffin Academy, which is a fictional New England boarding school filled with a lot of scandal, a whole lot of secrets involving the administrator, the teachers, and of course, the students.\par \par \uc2 We are happy to say, first on CBS MORNINGS, we love when that happens, there they are, Elin Hilderbrand and Shelby Cunningham join us live in the studio.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations to you, Shelby.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM ("The Academy" co-author): Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 I can`t recall you ever having a co-writer, Elin. Is this the first time you`ve ever had one?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND ("The Academy" co-author): This is the first time I`ve ever had a co-writer and it is going to be the only time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa! Uh-oh! What happened?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, how did it come about? Did you have the idea? Did you have the idea? Start us of, Elin, how it --\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: So, Shelby attended St. George`s School in New Port, Rhode Island, and she went as a sophomore the year after COVID in 2021, and when she got there, she was calling four or five times a day, having a wonderful time, but the stories were so mind blowing that eventually in the middle of her sophomore year I said, we have to write a novel.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because some mothers would just say, okay, well that`s interesting, or that`s great. But you said, this is a book.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: This is a book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, Shelby, when she said we have to write a novel about this, what did you think? Were you game?\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Don`t tempt me with a good time, yes. I did say that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you say your mom is your best friend.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Yes, she is my best friend. I tell her everything already, so it was super easy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I like that. What was the process?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: So, I am because the novelist, we created the characters and the arc of the characters together, and then basically, I started writing it, and I would give Shelby assignments, like a teacher to a student, and I`d say, okay, you write the scene where Davi goes and tells Charlie that she has to go to First Dance, and so Shelby would write the scene and then, send it to me via e-mail and then I would edit her.\par \par \uc2 And then when I would write scenes, I would send them to Shelby, and she edited me and that was where it became very humbling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, let`s talk about that, Shelby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Editing your mom who is a best-selling author.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You weren`t intimidated to tell her the truth?\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: No, she`s not a teenager, so since she is my mom, I could be as honest as I needed to be. There were so many times where I was like, we can`t say this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So give us an example of that.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: She had people wearing Converse and listening to Rolling Stones, and I`m like, they are wearing Golden Gooses and listening to house music, it is not the same thing.\par \par \uc2 So there was a lot of instances like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And was she always receptive?\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Yes, yes. A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Yes, I needed the input very badly. I felt very Gen-X.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Yes. Very old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what would you say is your favorite part about working with your daughter? Because this is a different capacity.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know your daughter --\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve worked with her before.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Different spaces, but it is different when you are writing together.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: That`s right. Well, combine the two things I love the most -- writing books and parenting, and you know, we got to spend -- and this is so much fun being able to do, we need to get together, but writing the book together, we always had stuff to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Our phone calls sort of went to the next level, because we -- any time Shelby experienced something at boarding school, I am like, straight to the pages? And she`d be like, yes, straight to the pages.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What did you learn about your daughter?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: That`s a really good question.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That maybe you didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Well, I mean, the thing is, is that boarding school makes her very, very good with adults, because they have such close relationships with the faculty --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: -- and the staff. And so she is excellent with adults and I was blown away by how mature she is, really.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I was blown away by the wild stuff that happens at boarding schools.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is really this crazy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She said, "oh yes."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: It is really crazy. Well, it`s a bunch of teenagers living together like in dorms together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boys and girls.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: No parents.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Yes, so you can only imagine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t have --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or you can read.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or you can read the book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we don`t have to imagine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, the stories go straight from story to the page, but now they are going to the screen as well. You already have someone who is going adapt this for streaming. We can`t say what platform yet. But who do you want to play the lead?\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: That`s a really good question. Which one?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: I feel Kerry Washington for --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For Audre.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: -- for Audre, right? Kerry Washington for Audre.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes! Oh, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: And then we`ve got to find some new talent, I think for the kids, don`t you think so?\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is already picked up. A streamer has picked it up.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to guess Netflix because they get -- that`s what I am going to guess.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shelby, would you be interested in being on screen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, good question.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She said, definitely.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Of course. I can`t pass that up, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you? Would you? Which character would you want to play?\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Oh, I don`t know if I would want to be a character, maybe just one in the backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am still just so haunted by the Golden Goose and the Converse thing. I wear Converse and my 16-year-old son asked for Golden Goose recently and I was like, these are ridiculously expensive.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: No. I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No way. Wear a Converse. He`s like, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shelby goes, yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 But the beauty, too, is the way you guys treat food and music. That hasn`t changed.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: That hasn`t changed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, bravo, you guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Classic.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY CUNNINGHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see it on Netflix is my guess.\par \par \uc2 Elin and Shelby, thank you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, our series, "The Dish" visits a restaurant overflowing with what the owners call modern grandma. Cordelia is the name. It`s in downtown Cleveland. It is an unabashed homage to the culture, cuisine, and community of the Midwest. That in the words of "Food & Wine" was just named executive chef, Vinnie Cimino, one of the ten best new chefs in America.\par \par \uc2 I recently met with him to see how he puts Midwestern nice into each and every bite.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Walking into Cordelia feels a bit like walking into family dinner, with food that is classic and yet dialed up for a new generation.\par \par \uc2 Like this tender pork belly, or this asparagus carbonara, and don`t miss the smash burgers topped with Kool-Aid flavored pickles.\par \par \uc2 Wait, wait! A Kool-Aid pickle? What`s that?\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO (Chef): Kool-Aid pickle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s part of what Chef Vinnie Cimino says, modern grandma.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: So my grandmother, when I would go over to her house would have an entire shelf at her refrigerator of jars of pickles and she would spike them with different packets of Kool-Aid, so we kind of took that a step further. We make our own pickles in-house, and then we, essentially reverse engineered how to make Lemon Lime Kool-Aid.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wait, so it is your own rendition of Kool-Aid.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: It is our own rendition of Kool-Aid.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Food & Wine" in its just released 37th Annual Class of the Best New Chefs in America, says Cimino is part of a next generation of culinary superstars, which came as quite a surprise to this not so new, new chef.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: I`m 42 years old, you know, like the thought of Best New Chef, it is incredibly wild to me to think about. I tell everybody, I was a vagabond chef, I was smashing burgers on flat tops in parking lots. I was cooking in people`s homes.\par \par \uc2 I was really doing anything I could to put food on my table with my wife and my kids.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Part of what makes Cordelia special is its authenticity, from the cutlery --\par \par \uc2 Everything feels so idiosyncratic, like my fork doesn`t match your fork.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: No! No!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the knives are different and every plate is different.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: We wanted it to feel familiar. You know how many people come to me and like, it`s like, my grandma had these same plates, and I am like, mine too. This is fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To the decor.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: We built this space to be you know, resemblative of who we are as people, of how we grew up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vintage cans of booze on the walls? Old cans of beer?\par \par \uc2 Even the name is authentic.\par \par \uc2 There`s an actual Cordelia and that is her, right?\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: That`s her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who is she?\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: Cordelia is my partner, Andrew`s great grandmother. He attributes her, teaching him a lot about hospitality, bringing people together and that`s what this restaurant does, like the burger is meant to be shared. We want you to pick up something, pass it around, you know talk, build connection. This is what family and food is all about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s also all about the flavor of the Midwest and beyond.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: We cook with spices, you know, and seasons and you know, we just utilize Midwestern flavors and techniques to kind of develop all of that.\par \par \uc2 We do understand that, you know, being in the Midwest, sometimes people think mayonnaise is spicy. You know --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sorry, did you say mayonnaise is spicy?\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: Some people think mayonnaise is spicy in the Midwest, you`d be surprised.\par \par \uc2 Mushroom patty, four, please, Chef.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Chef.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What guides Cimino is decency, including salaries and benefits for his entire kitchen staff. That is just one of the reasons he stood out to "Food & Wine" according to editor-in-chief Hunter Lewis.\par \par \uc2 How has the definition of a Best New Chef changed over the years?\par \par \uc2 HUNTER LEWIS ("Food & Wine" editor-in-chief): About ten years ago, we began to look up from the plate. It`s not enough to make a great plate of food and to do that consistently night in and night out. They have got to be leaders in their kitchens and then also leaders in their community.\par \par \uc2 I love that Vinnie wears his heart on his sleeve, and the food is just a ton of fun. It is a romp!\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: We are only going to toast one side.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We saw that firsthand with his fresh made tomato toast.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: We have beautiful fresh tomatoes that just walked in the back door from --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fresh from the Ohio dirt.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: Fresh from the Ohio dirt. Tomato salt, which is a bunch of seasons, all kind of them mixed in there. Look at that. We`re making this - - we are making this like amalgamation of --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s great.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: -- of a tomato salad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mixed with cherry vinegar, the tomatoes are placed on top of a bed of pimento cheese aioli.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: All right, now, we`ve got cheers for ourselves. All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To tomato season.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: To tomato season.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beyond the dollars and cents, how do you measure or define a good night here?\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: By the vibes. You know, by how people feel. By, you know, hearing the laughter in the room.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When it works, it works.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: When it works.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every damn day, like the sign says.\par \par \uc2 VINNIE CIMINO: Every damn day, always.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, I want to go. I want to go, tomato toast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can go. I know a guy or two over there. Now, it is a wonderful place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They take it straight from the farm. They come up with creative expressions of it and it is delicious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vinnie, I want to come to your house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right coming up, "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want a burger with the pickles.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stick around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the tomato toast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is a \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King with Tony Dokoupil and Vlad Duthiers here in New York.\par \par \uc2 Robert Redford was one of the greatest names in modern Hollywood. The 89- year-old actor died early this morning at his home in Utah. That`s according to his publicist.\par \par \uc2 Redford made his name as an actor starting in the 1960s with the movies we all remember, "Barefoot in the Park," "Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid" made him a big screen heartthrob.\par \par \uc2 He was only nominated once for an Oscar, but by the 1970s, he was the biggest star in the world. I so remember this.\par \par \uc2 He moved on to be the director of classics like, "A River Runs Through It" and "Ordinary People" winning an Oscar for that one. The Sundance Film Festival was his idea. It became one of the biggest movie showcases in the world.\par \par \uc2 Redford was an environmental crusader.\par \par \uc2 And once again, Hollywood legend, Robert Redford has died. He was 89 years old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s bring in Vlad Duthiers. Vlad, this really hits me. I mean, he --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does, Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel that, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was such a model of manhood, masculinity. The rugged good looks, the charm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was a beautiful human being as well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All of those things you mentioned, Tony, were encompassed in the roles that Robert Redford played in those iconic movies that Gayle mentioned.\par \par \uc2 You talk about the charismatic actor, well, of course, that was "The Sundance Kid" in "Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid" alongside Paul Newman. The charming conman in "The Sting," right, we all remember that role --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- of "The Sting" in 1973. The romantic leading man with the rugged good looks, opposite Barbra Streisand --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Barbra Streisand. That`s what I was thinking, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- in "The Way We Were."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the first one I thought of.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everyone will remember that scene --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- when Barbra just sort of tussles his hair in that iconic film.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And then the serious consciousness of this nation when it came to the Watergate scandal in "All the President`s Men" in 1973, alongside Dustin Hoffman.\par \par \uc2 And the artist himself, as a director, Gayle, you mentioned, "Ordinary People." that movie won four Oscars. Not only did it win Best Picture, it also won him his Best Director, and guys, it was his debut as a film director.\par \par \uc2 And there were sort of the fun movies. We all remember "Spy Game" with Brad Pitt, "The River Runs Through It."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Out of Africa" another leading man role, so there were so, so many.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there is something though, Vlad about hearing Robert Redford. Listen, he is 89, that`s a very good run and I know we all get older, and it happens to all of us, but there`s something about hearing that that takes you back to so many things, it hits different to me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because we remember him with such vitality.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Larger than life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know he was ill. Do we know? We don`t --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No one knew that. I didn`t know he was ill.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he also didn`t just lead on camera. There are so many young actors right now that modeled themselves after who he was off screen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because as you said, he also cared about humanity, the environment, and you think about individuals like Leonardo DiCaprio, who decided, you know what? I can also be a super star and care for this globe we`re standing on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was one of the early actors to take an active role in trying to protect our environment, and let`s not forget, he also founded one of the most important film festivals in the history of Hollywood, The Sundance Film Festival, which is where iconic films --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like that. That was his idea.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- that was what Robert Redford created.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I remember when that started, Vlad, look at what it has become today?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because you remember, Gayle, when it started, it was tiny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it was very tiny. But look how big it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They just had it the other day. You know, it is the type of thing that we don`t know the cause of death yet, so maybe he wasn`t ill. I don`t know. It could have just been natural causes, but there is still something to think that Robert Redford is no longer with us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will all be looking at --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s shocking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The one thing that always sits with me with individuals like this is that, their legacy will live on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, he will be missed and our thoughts and prayers go out to his friends and family, but when you are such an iconic person on and off camera, what you left with us will be a gift that keeps on giving.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Long after you`re gone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think of him as a believer. He believed in the power of movies, he believed in democracy, he believed in the country. He is an optimist. He hated cynics.\par \par \uc2 I was telling you about that quote I found where he has very low regard he said of cynics. He believed cynicism is the beginning of dying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I have to believe, as we announce his passing today, he died an optimist because that`s the way he lived his life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because his movies had that spirit of optimism running through them, and as you point out, Tony, as all of us have pointed out, it translated to what we call Americana. Robert Redford was Americana personified in everything that he did in everything, and in every role that he sort of he played.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is anyone going to mention the salad dressing? I mean, it is not for nothing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Paul Newman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is Paul Newman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Paul Newman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they were -- but they were very tight.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They were very tight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And they starred in that iconic movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I could see where you get --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Butch Cassidy and The Sundance Kid."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that was --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everyone will remember.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am sorry. I am a man of a certain age. And my apologies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My apologies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I could see why you thought that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everyone will remember that last scene.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`ve just got something guys from his publicist. Robert Redford he says, passed away on September 16, 2025, at his home in Sundance, in the mountains of Utah, the place he loved. He was surrounded by those he loved. He will be missed greatly, of course, and the family requests privacy at this time.\par \par \uc2 We want you to know that our coverage of the life of Robert Redford will continue on our streaming channel, CBS News 24/7 and on your local news, and tonight, of course, on the "CBS Evening News."\par \par \uc2 Many of you will now return to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 This has been a \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, and Vlad Duthiers -- all of us here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time now for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. We`ve got several