aw today, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit cbsdeals.com. We make it easy for you.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, who do we have in our greenroom?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always say this, Nate, you meet the nicest people in our progressive green room and this day is no different. Actors, that`s Brett Goldstein and that`s Imogen Poots.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS ("All of You" actor): Hi!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In a moment, they will tell us about their new movie, it is called, "All of You" and why it questions the idea, Brett of soul mates.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN ("All of You" actor): That`s correct, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is there only one? That`s the question.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Nice question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is there only one, Imogen?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Oh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the question. And what would you do for love?\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are very excited about our next guests, you just saw them a minute ago, Brett Goldstein and Imogen Poots.\par \par \uc2 The actors are in a new romantic drama called, "All of You" that`s why John Legend is saying, "I want all of you." That`s right.\par \par \uc2 They play best friends from college, Simon and Laura, who live in a world where -- listen to this, you can take a test that will tell you who your soul mate is. Sign me up.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, they are waiting at the clinic for Laura to take her test, Tony says no --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no, no!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- which is something Simon does not want to do.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All of You"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Call me intrigued. Brett Goldstein also co-wrote this film, he and Imogen Poots, we are glad to say are with us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Good morning. Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I left this movie, watched this movie and I was filled with so many emotions. I was so conflicted about, should they, should they not? Anyway, I won`t give anything away, but this is what they say about the two of you, Brett and Imogen, there is sizzling chemistry between the two of you. How difficult it is to have sizzling chemistry with him? And with her?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: We`ve got over easy, didn`t we, in the beginning?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: What`s funny is there`s like sexy scenes in the film.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And there is one in particular that is very steamy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: But the reality of shooting that scene is, we were in a fruit and vegetable market that was rotting. It was really late at night and we were like behind the curtain and the entire crew on the other side of the curtain and we were doing this steamy scene, but we can just smell rotting vegetables. And the curtain falls and everyone claps like we are in some --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, may I say you guys are very good actors then, because we couldn`t tell that -- it would be so hot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I can see right now why you guys got the giggles on the set.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can see that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is about the chemistry between you two? Because you`ve worked with different actors before.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: It is kind of mystifying. I don`t know. I think you just meet people along the way and you just have a real natural synergy with each other and an ease and a trust and we just got along, didn`t we?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: It happened instantly. It happened when we first -- I first met her on a Zoom, and like five minutes in, I was like, I feel like I`ve known you forever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And I figured, that`s what --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she jumps out in the Zoom, that`s pretty good. But you co- wrote this though, Brett.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: She really pops in the Zoom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You co-wrote this.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, I wrote this with --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`ve been working on it for a long time. Where did the idea come from? Set the idea first.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Well, the idea sort of came from me and Will Bridges, who I co-wrote it with and directed it. This ideas -- we were just talking about love and the idea of a soul mate that we have, that there`s one person meant for you and when you really think about it, you go, that`s a lot of pressure on one person --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: -- to take care of everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t believe there`s just one, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: No, I think there`s a bunch, and that people -- they are very surprised when they come into your life and they change the course of it and it`s always something new and unanticipated. That`s what makes it so cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, if the technology existed today, would either one of you take it?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: I would not, I have decided, because we`ve been asked this question a bunch, haven`t we? And I just think, I would not choose to take it.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: I would not either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I definitely would not.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Why not?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I might take it and then depending on the results, I might say, well no, that didn`t work because I think ultimately, the best way to meet somebody is through friends introducing you, or you just have a natural encounter on the street.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s the best way to do that.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Romance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Old school love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imogen, I so love the romance of it all, but you decided, you said, what if you could meet a soul mate, is that what you guys were talking about?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: So, yes, it was like, some with that was a test --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: -- that could scientifically, a hundred percent tell you who your soul mate is and what happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And then we just made this like -- it is like an ethics story, because it is however many years, but it is just very intimate because it is just these two people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And they are best friends, and she takes the test and he is not her soul mate and yet, and yet, they are still drawn to each other however their life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And yet, and yet, and yet. And she`s married and you have a child.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And yet, and yet, and yet.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: So, it is kind of an interesting predicament for the audience --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: -- to look at and sort of see how they feel about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hence why I was so conflicted.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes. Good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What are some of the elements that a soul mate must have? I`m talking to you two. Not your characters. What do you think a soul mate must have?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not cell mate.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Not cell mate, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want a tight cell mate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a completely different movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s number one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a different movie. You can write that one later.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: That`s -- yes. I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What are some elements a soul mate needs to have?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Listen, we -- I mean, and Will Bridges already have talked about this in so much detail, because I think there is a reality of like, if this person is meant for you, is the perfect fit for you, then they might not be the most fun. They might be the most challenging.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It might not be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That might be the perfect fit. They might be there to challenge you.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, they might be there to --\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Like activate something in you that you didn`t know, you know, like kind of complete you in a different way and they like give you yourself back to you as a gift, you know?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Imogen!\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, you are preaching right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did it resonate with you? I see it started from Brett and his friend working --\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes, so much of what you`re saying, Gayle is the idea of just looking at these characters and the predicament they are in and that situation, and you can`t morally evaluate love in that way. I don`t think you can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: And desire and these things which are out of our control and we`re in such controlled culture these days. So i think it`s to do with that, that`s what you read into it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But your co-writer was getting married at the time.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did he marry his soul mate?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: You know what`s funny about this and he hates me saying it, but I think it`s a nice 