Manhattan. Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 It is hard to see with all the traffic, but there is a line of people, some with signs saying "Free Luigi," getting ready to go into the court. Now keep in mind, Mangione is facing charges in three separate jurisdictions, but today is about the eleven counts that he is facing right here in New York.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Luigi Mangione has pleaded not guilty in the shooting and killing of 50-year-old UnitedHealthcare CEO and father of two, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , last December.\par \par \uc2 Today, Mangione could learn which prosecutor he`ll face first.\par \par \uc2 KAREN FRIEDMAN AGNIFILO (Defense attorney): Like every other defendant, he is entitled to a presumption of innocence.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: CBS New legal analyst, Caroline Polisi.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: I think the most charges here come from federal prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Federal prosecutors are also pursuing charges, including murder with a firearm, which could carry the death penalty. Because of that, Mangione`s attorneys argued the federal case should be tried first.\par \par \uc2 It almost feels like the state and federal prosecutors are trying to compete for which case is going to go first.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: That`s absolutely right. We`re seeing a very public turf war here and we don`t know the outcome. I suspect that a large portion of the hearing will be dedicated to this timeline.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mangione`s hearing comes days after the assassination of conservative activist, Charlie Kirk, and on the same day Kirk`s suspected killer will appear in court in Utah. Kirk`s death, like Thompson`s, has ignited conversations and anger around the political violence in this country.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: You will see prosecutors using this recent assassination of Charlie Kirk as an example of why Luigi Mangione is so dangerous. Federal prosecutors, especially, will be using this idea of the follow-on murder, the copycat murder, the inspiration.\par \par \uc2 The fact that Luigi Mangione had a rally of supporters behind him, he has become sort of this folk hero for a certain sector of individuals online.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mangione has pleaded not guilty to all of the charges brought against him so far. As far as the federal case is concerned, he is due in court for that in December -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jericka, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, some important health news for women. We are going to show you the latest research on menopause therapy and how one patient and her doctor are weighing the benefits against all the risk.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In today`s "Health Watch," we`re talking about the safety of hormone therapy for women struggling with menopause symptoms and struggle is the word here. The treatment can offer relief for symptoms like hot flashes and night sweats, the worst. New research finds hormone therapy does not increase heart disease risk for menopause women under the age of 60.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste spoke to one of the women that is weighing the benefits of the therapy against the risk.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with a woman in her 50s who suffers from perimenopause symptoms, but like many women, has been nervous about trying menopause hormone therapy. That caution lingers from a study about two decades ago that showed an increase risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 A recent study found only about five percent of menopausal women are on hormone therapy today, even though about 80 percent have symptoms.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN (Patient): This is my best friend, my fan.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Hot flashes.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: It`s called Fanny.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And sleepless nights are disrupting Isabel Kallman`s life.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I keep her in my bag, and just to cool myself off when I need to.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: At 53, Kallman`s symptoms are part of perimenopause, the phase before menopause when a woman`s hormones start to decline. The most common medication is hormone therapy, which uses estrogen and if needed, a progestin to alleviate symptoms.\par \par \uc2 Are you considering it?\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I am. I`ve been on the fence about it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Many women have been wary of the treatment because they were concerned it would increase the chances of breast cancer. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has long mandated so-called black box labels on the medication, warnings about stroke, blood clots, dementia, and breast cancer. But the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is now considering removing some of those warnings.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER (Columbia University Irving Medical Center Integrated Women`s Health Director) This is a public health priority and this is a sign that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  sees that.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Mary Rosser has been Cal has been Kallman`s doctor for five years.\par \par \uc2 Is the fear of the risk of breast cancer fair?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER: It`s been over blown.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Rosser believes flawed research from about 20 years ago overstated the risks of breast cancer because that study focused on older women.\par \par \uc2 As today`s new study shows timing matters. A woman`s age when she begins hormone therapy, along with dose and delivery method can substantially mitigate risks.\par \par \uc2 Generally, when would a woman begin hormone therapy?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER: So, we have said to begin as close to the final menstrual period, and then you continue until about the age of 60.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Rosser spent the past nine months working through the risks and benefits with Kallman, who has a family history of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I`m hoping, even if I do it for a year or two, that there is an option for me that can help me in the short-term.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new study says the ideal age when most women should take hormone therapy in order to mitigate risks is between 50 to 59. Recent research shows the right timing of treatment can even reduce the risk of diabetes and improve cognitive function.\par \par \uc2 If you are considering treatment, of course, it`s important to always speak with a doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the most important thing. I am sitting here so disappointed with myself, because I was one of those women that believed that study years ago and didn`t take anything, and have spent years and years struggling with all of these different symptoms and now, it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 So, is there anyone who shouldn`t take the hormone replacement therapy?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new study shows if you are 70 or older, you should not take hormone therapy. If you`re in that 60 to 69 age range, use caution.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rosser at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  told me there are -- for some women who have had certain breast or endometrial cancers, they wouldn`t qualify for systemic hormone therapy, but new studies show they might be able to safely take some local forms like a gel or a cream.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, your number surprised me in the beginning. You said 80 percent of women are going through the symptoms, but only five percent are taking it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is a wild number.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: I mean, it is the power of that study.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even though we know that it is better for us.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes, and the stigma, which I think is slowly fading.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do think --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And it is large in part due to social media, we`re talking about it here. We`re talking about it. The other point I want to make, it can be difficult to find a doctor who really understands menopause, because 20 years ago, the education in Med School was minimal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It has gotten better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: But I think it`s important we talk about it. It`s important for men to know about it, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Segments like this help destigmatize it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nikki, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a special edition of "What to Watch" with Deborah Norville, that`s right! But right now, it is 7:42. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have got some big things coming up for you in our 8:00 hour including the stars of the new romantic drama, "All of You," actors Brett Goldstein and Imogen Poots. The movie explores the idea of soulmates, and it is really fascinating. Would you take a test? Let`s discuss.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Tony visits one of the country`s best new chefs, according to "Food & Wine." How that chef is being honored for his Midwestern nice approach to his food and his employees. It`s never bad to be nice, it is not so hard.\par \par \uc2 All of that is coming up in our next hour, but right now, Tony and I are heading over to the table. We have got a special edition of "What to Watch" with Deborah Norville.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: CIA, Men in Black, Vlad Duthiers.\par \p