s commander-in- chief has made no secret of his affinity for the Royals and having the red carpet rolled out for him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Mr. Prime Minister, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: This latest chapter in the special relationship began with a surprise invitation delivered by the British Prime Minister in February.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER (British Prime Minister): It`s an invitation for a second state visit.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The invite was part of an urgent appeal to win over the American President as he upended the global economy with new tariffs.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: The Royal family, no pun intended, is the biggest trump card this government can play at big moments of international statesmanship and diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: CBS News Royal contributors Roya Nikkhah and Julian Payne note American presidents have long met with British monarchs, but in 2003, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was the first American president to make an official state visit. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  received one in 2011, and Trump`s first was in 2019, when he and Queen Elizabeth reviewed troops at Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 During that visit, he also connected with then \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  who he has known for at least two decades.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: This is state to state, but this is a genuine personal relationship that is there as well.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: This state visit, the traditional greeting and Review of the Troops will take place at Windsor Castle, where a state dinner will be held for senior members of Trump`s Cabinet and top business executives.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: There is a huge amount of lobbying to get that ticket.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now hold on, spill the tea. What do you have to do to lobby the palace to get a seat at that state dinner?\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: Well, if I told you that, then you would know how to get into the next one. 