at`s Brett Goldstein and that`s Imogen Poots.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS ("All of You" actor): Hi!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In a moment, they will tell us about their new movie, it is called, "All of You" and why it questions the idea, Brett of soul mates.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN ("All of You" actor): That`s correct, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is there only one? That`s the question.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Nice question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is there only one, Imogen?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Oh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the question. And what would you do for love?\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are very excited about our next guests, you just saw them a minute ago, Brett Goldstein and Imogen Poots.\par \par \uc2 The actors are in a new romantic drama called, "All of You" that`s why John Legend is saying, "I want all of you." That`s right.\par \par \uc2 They play best friends from college, Simon and Laura, who live in a world where -- listen to this, you can take a test that will tell you who your soul mate is. Sign me up.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, they are waiting at the clinic for Laura to take her test, Tony says no --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no, no!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- which is something Simon does not want to do.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All of You"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Call me intrigued. Brett Goldstein also co-wrote this film, he and Imogen Poots, we are glad to say are with us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Good morning. Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I left this movie, watched this movie and I was filled with so many emotions. I was so conflicted about, should they, should they not? Anyway, I won`t give anything away, but this is what they say about the two of you, Brett and Imogen, there is sizzling chemistry between the two of you. How difficult it is to have sizzling chemistry with him? And with her?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: We`ve got over easy, didn`t we, in the beginning?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: What`s funny is there`s like sexy scenes in the film.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And there is one in particular that is very steamy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: But the reality of shooting that scene is, we were in a fruit and vegetable market that was rotting. It was really late at night and we were like behind the curtain and the entire crew on the other side of the curtain and we were doing this steamy scene, but we can just smell rotting vegetables. And the curtain falls and everyone claps like we are in some --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, may I say you guys are very good actors then, because we couldn`t tell that -- it would be so hot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I can see right now why you guys got the giggles on the set.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can see that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is about the chemistry between you two? Because you`ve worked with different actors before.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: It is kind of mystifying. I don`t know. I think you just meet people along the way and you just have a real natural synergy with each other and an ease and a trust and we just got along, didn`t we?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: It happened instantly. It happened when we first -- I first met her on a Zoom, and like five minutes in, I was like, I feel like I`ve known you forever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And I figured, that`s what --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she jumps out in the Zoom, that`s pretty good. But you co- wrote this though, Brett.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: She really pops in the Zoom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You co-wrote this.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, I wrote this with --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`ve been working on it for a long time. Where did the idea come from? Set the idea first.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Well, the idea sort of came from me and Will Bridges, who I co-wrote it with and directed it. This ideas -- we were just talking about love and the idea of a soul mate that we have, that there`s one person meant for you and when you really think about it, you go, that`s a lot of pressure on one person --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: -- to take care of everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t believe there`s just one, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: No, I think there`s a bunch, and that people -- they are very surprised when they come into your life and they change the course of it and it`s always something new and unanticipated. That`s what makes it so cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, if the technology existed today, would either one of you take it?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: I would not, I have decided, because we`ve been asked this question a bunch, haven`t we? And I just think, I would not choose to take it.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: I would not either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I definitely would not.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Why not?\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I might take it and then depending on the results, I might say, well no, that didn`t work because I think ultimately, the best way to meet somebody is through friends introducing you, or you just have a natural encounter on the street.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s the best way to do that.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Romance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Old school love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imogen, I so love the romance of it all, but you decided, you said, what if you could meet a soul mate, is that what you guys were talking about?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: So, yes, it was like, some with that was a test --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: -- that could scientifically, a hundred percent tell you who your soul mate is and what happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And then we just made this like -- it is like an ethics story, because it is however many years, but it is just very intimate because it is just these two people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And they are best friends, and she takes the test and he is not her soul mate and yet, and yet, they are still drawn to each other however their life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And yet, and yet, and yet. And she`s married and you have a child.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And yet, and yet, and yet.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: So, it is kind of an interesting predicament for the audience --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: -- to look at and sort of see how they feel about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hence why I was so conflicted.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes. Good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What are some of the elements that a soul mate must have? I`m talking to you two. Not your characters. What do you think a soul mate must have?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not cell mate.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Not cell mate, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want a tight cell mate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a completely different movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s number one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a different movie. You can write that one later.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: That`s -- yes. I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What are some elements a soul mate needs to have?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Listen, we -- I mean, and Will Bridges already have talked about this in so much detail, because I think there is a reality of like, if this person is meant for you, is the perfect fit for you, then they might not be the most fun. They might be the most challenging.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It might not be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That might be the perfect fit. They might be there to challenge you.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, they might be there to --\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Like activate something in you that you didn`t know, you know, like kind of complete you in a different way and they like give you yourself back to you as a gift, you know?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Imogen!\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, you are preaching right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did it resonate with you? I see it started from Brett and his friend working --\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: Yes, so much of what you`re saying, Gayle is the idea of just looking at these characters and the predicament they are in and that situation, and you can`t morally evaluate love in that way. I don`t think you can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMOGEN POOTS: And desire and these things which are out of our control and we`re in such controlled culture these days. So i think it`s to do with that, that`s what you read into it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But your co-writer was getting married at the time.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did he marry his soul mate?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: You know what`s funny about this and he hates me saying it, but I think it`s a nice story is that when we first were writing this, he said, she is obviously not my soul mate. I don`t believe that`s the thing. She just happens to be someone I have known for years, but however, ten years later, married, kids, the more we talk about it, I am like, you`re soul mates.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Probably soul mates.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will give her that. No!\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: No, I think she is his soul mate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250916el9g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For September 15, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Kris Van Cleave, Carter Evans, Charlie D`Agata, Leigh Kiniry, Mark  Strassmann, Nikki Battiste, Nancy Cordes, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson,  Lonnie Quinn \par \uc2 4,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : For the second time, President Trump orders an attack on a boat in the Caribbean allegedly carrying drugs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (Vice President of the United States): J.D. Vance here live from my office in the White House complex.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Also tonight: The vice president fills in for Charlie Kirk. As a memorial to the conservative activist grows, we will have the latest on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : New research could give women the answers they`re looking for about the safety of medication for menopause.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OWEN COOPER (Actor): You do not tell me when to sit down!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And an adolescent actor earns an Emmy in record time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OWEN COOPER: If you listen and you focus and you step out your comfort zone, you can achieve -- you can achieve anything in life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The U.S. military has again attacked a boat in the Caribbean alleged to be carrying drugs from Venezuela bound for the United States.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump made the announcement on social media, saying he had ordered the attack and that three people on the boat were killed.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : This is the second such U.S. attack in two weeks, as the president makes good on his threat to use the U.S. military to traffic drug traffickers.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata at the Pentagon has details.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Yes, John, this is the second strike this month, and it comes as American stealth fighters have arrived at the Caribbean. And today, just today, the Venezuelan president claimed the U.S. is trying to drive him from power.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): In the video posted by the president, a motorboat is floating in the water before being struck. The president called it a drug boat and the three men aboard narco-terrorists. And he warned: "If you`re transporting drugs that can kill Americans, we`re hunting you."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes asked the president about the strike.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Do you plan to provide proof that these were narco- terrorists who were on their way to the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): All you have to do is look at the cargo that was -- like, it`s spattered all over the ocean, big bags of cocaine and fentanyl all over the place.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT NICOLAS MADURO SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Today, prior to the strike, the Venezuelan president, Nicolas Maduro, lashed out against the United States for illegally seizing a fishing boat Saturday and occupying it for eight hours.\par \par \uc2 The Venezuelan government released video showing the U.S. destroyer in the distance as members of the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  approached the fishing boat. This comes as F-35 stealth fighter jets arrived in Puerto Rico, part of the growing military buildup in the region, joining at least eight U.S. warships already there.\par \par \uc2 Today`s strike is the second against an alleged drug boat, which killed 11 drug smugglers earlier this month, according to the president. CBS News has learned that vessel had turned around and was fired on anyway. Democrats and some Republicans questioned whether that strike violated international law.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAND PAUL\uc2  (R-Kentucky): If this is the new policy, that we`re just going to blow people up we think are drug dealers, that`s what -- you`re just going to see cannons going off all off of Miami and California coasts, and we`re just going to blow up ships, that just isn`t who we are.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Charlie, you mentioned the F-35s are now in Puerto Rico. What role are they expected to play?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Well, Maurice, they can be used for surveillance, as well as attacking, but they were sent there after two Venezuelan F-16s buzzed by a U.S. destroyer in the region, Jason Dunham.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has warned that any warplanes that are interfering with U.S. operations there would be shot down. And, today, he warned any drug smugglers who are intent on smuggling drugs by land will meet the same fate.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Charlie D`Agata at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The suspect arrested in the assassination of conservative activist Charlie Kirk is expected to appear before a state judge in Utah tomorrow. Tyler Robinson will not be in the courtroom. He will appear virtually.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Robinson will hear the charges against him, expected to include aggravated murder. \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel said today Robinson texted someone before the shooting, saying he had the chance to kill Kirk and was taking it out of hatred for his politics.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans has more from Spanish Fork, Utah.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voice-over): As prosecutors build the case against Charlie Kirk`s alleged killer, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is combing through social media accounts the suspect, Tyler Robinson, used searching for signs anyone encouraged the shooting or had prior knowledge.\par \par \uc2 On the eve of the suspect`s first court appearance, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel laid out some of the evidence, which he says points to a motive.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL (Former Deputy Assistant to President Trump): He had a text message exchange, he, the suspect, with another individual in which he claimed that he had an opportunity to take out Charlie Kirk and he was going to do it because of his hatred for what Charlie stood for.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: According to Utah Governor Spencer Cox, the alleged shooter was in a romantic relationship with a roommate, who is now cooperating with investigators. Robinson, however, is not.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (Vice President of the United States): He was a joyful warrior for our country.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: As a tribute to Kirk, Vice President J.D. Vance took over his late friend`s radio show today.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT J.D. VANCE: We don`t believe in political violence, but we do believe in civility. And there is no civility in the celebration of political assassination.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Deputy Chief of Staff Stephen Miller went further.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER (White House Deputy Chief of Staff): It is a vast domestic terror movement.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Miller said that -- quote -- "in Charlie`s name," the administration would dismantle and destroy left-wing organizations they claim are encouraging, inspiring and funding violence.\par \par \uc2 But he offered no evidence to support that claim. The shooting prompted the White House to request an extra $58 million in security funding to help protect officials in the executive and judicial branch.\par \par \uc2 In St. George, Utah, near where the suspect grew up, a vigil.\par \par \uc2 Jaida Funk is a former classmate.\par \par \uc2 JAIDA FUNK (Former Classmate of Tyler Robinson): I saw his picture and immediately pulled out my yearbook and started recalling all the memories I had with him. And it was just really surreal. From the 10 years that I did know him, he was always just a kind, respectful, very sharp and smart guy.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: On Sunday, several \uc2 NFL\uc2  teams paid pregame tributes to Kirk, while, on the campus of Utah Valley University, a memorial continues to grow.\par \par \uc2 Luke Jangula (sp?) says he and other students are still coming to grips with what happened.\par \par \uc2 LUKE JANGULA (Student): I have been trying to stay off social media. I know a lot of people can just bring other people down. It`s not easy to tell why, why they would try to hurt other people. Like, there`s so many people in our world that`s struggling and you can never see it. So, you just try to do your best every day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: We will learn more about the prosecution`s case against Robinson when charging documents are released tomorrow ahead of his first court appearance in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Carter Evans, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 Officials in the Gaza Strip say Israeli forces launched a heavy wave of airstrikes today in Gaza City. The CBS News producer in Gaza says explosions could be heard for miles. In recent days, Israel has destroyed a number of high-rise buildings as it ramps up its military offensive to take over the largest city in the Palestinian territory. Everyone in Gaza City has been ordered to clear out.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump is sending the National Guard into Memphis to fight crime there. He signed the order today. The president is also sending federal agents from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , DEA, ICE and the Marshals Service. And he said Chicago will be next.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent says the U.S. and China have agreed to the outline of a deal that would transfer ownership of TikTok. The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has been pushing for TikTok`s China-based parent to sell the social media platform over national security concerns.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A new report is raising questions about the safety of the air we breathe when we fly on commercial jetliners. "The Wall Street Journal" says toxic fumes can leak from jet engines into the cabin.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The CBS News Data Team found \uc2 Airbus\uc2  accounted for about 61 percent of reported incidents last year, nearly three times more than \uc2 Boeing\uc2  aircraft.\par \par \uc2 Senior transportation correspondent Kris Van Cleave has been looking into this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): This was Delta Flight 876 back in February, the cabin filling with hazy smoke. The plane made an emergency landing. Smoke or fume events like this happen on airliners at a rate of more than three a day, according to a CBS News review of \uc2 FAA\uc2  incident reports. Experts say those fumes can be toxic.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT HARRISON (\uc2 University of California San Francisco\uc2 ): The most common problems I see are generally the nervous system problems.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Dr. Robert Harrison is an occupational medicine specialist who has treated over 100 flight crew members for toxic exposure.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT HARRISON: A person breathes it into their lungs, it circulates around, and then it gets into their brain and can affect the other parts of the nervous system.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It`s an issue flight attendants have been raising alarms about for years. Vanessa Woods told us, back in 2016, she passed out after a chemical odor filled the cabin on a flight she was working.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA WOODS (Flight Attendant): I felt like that I might die, and I thought, I wonder what my mom is going to think. This -- I got -- I went to work, and I may not make it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A "Wall Street Journal" investigation found the fume issue was most common on the popular \uc2 Airbus\uc2  A320 family of narrow-body jets.\par \par \uc2 Association of Flight Attendants\uc2  president \uc2 Sara Nelson\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 SARA NELSON (President, \uc2 Association of Flight Attendants-CWA\uc2 ): Every aircraft should really have the filtration system on it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Nearly all commercial jets bleed fresh air from the engines. It`s then fed through an air conditioner and into the cabin. But if an engine seal fails, oil and other potentially toxic particles can be released. Fumes, incident reports say, often are compared to the smell of dirty socks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says these incidents are rare and are investigated. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  insists the air on board its planes is safe. Delta says air travel remains the safest form of transportation.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Airbus\uc2  says it is working with its operators and regulators to ensure the best possible cabin environment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Kris Van Cleave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead on the CBS Evening News: what a new study says about the safety of menopause medication.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also tonight, this story:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m Mark Strassmann Los Angeles. In a city that can make people feel anonymous, its recovery from wildfires has created a small-town community feel.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And barely out of acting school, Owen Cooper takes an Emmy and breaks a record.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We want to go back now for a moment to the story of Charlie Kirk.\par \par \uc2 After Kirk`s death, President Trump ordered flags lowered to half-staff in his honor. There was no such honor earlier this year after the assassination of the Minnesota House speaker.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes asked the White House asked the president about that today.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In retrospect, given all of the moving ways that this White House has paid tribute to Charlie Kirk...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Right.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: ... do you think it would have been fitting to lower the flags to half-staff when \uc2 Melissa Hortman\uc2 , the Minnesota House speaker, was gunned down by an assassin as well?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m not familiar. Oh, the who?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Minnesota House speaker, a Democrat...\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: ... who was assassinated this summer.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, if the governor had asked me to do that, I would have done that. But the governor of Minnesota didn`t ask me. I didn`t -- I wouldn`t have thought of that. But I would have, if somebody had asked me. People make requests for the lowering of the flag. And, oftentimes, you have to say no, because it would be a lot of lowering. The flag would never be up.\par \par \uc2 Had the governor of Minnesota asked me to do that, I would have done that gladly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that was Nancy Cordes reporting at the White House.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : There is a new study out today on medications used to treat the symptoms of menopause.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Nikki Battiste reports it could help women weighing the benefits against the risks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN (Patient): This is my best friend. It`s my fan.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice-over): Hot flashes...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: It`s called Fanny.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: ... and sleepless nights are disrupting Isabel Kallman`s life.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I keep her in my bag and just to cool myself off when I need to.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: At 53, Kallman`s symptoms are part of perimenopause, the phase before menopause when a woman`s hormones start to decline. The most common medication is hormone therapy, which uses estrogen and, if needed, a progestin to alleviate symptoms.\par \par \uc2 Are you considering it?\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I am. I have been on the fence about it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Many women have been wary of the treatment because they were concerned it would increase the chances of breast cancer. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has long mandated so-called black box labels on the medication, warnings about stroke, blood clots, dementia and breast cancer. But the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is now considering removing some of those warnings.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER (\uc2 Columbia University\uc2 ): This is a public health priority and this is a sign that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  sees that.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Mary Rosser has been Kallman`s doctor for five years.\par \par \uc2 Is the fear of the risk of breast cancer fair?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER: It`s been overblown.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Rosser believes flawed research from about 20 years ago overstated the risks of breast cancer because that study focused on older women. As today`s new study shows, timing matters. A woman`s age when she begins hormone therapy, along with dose and delivery method, can substantially mitigate risks.\par \par \uc2 Generally, when would a woman begin hormone therapy?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARY ROSSER: So we have said to begin as close to the final menstrual period, and then you continue until about the age of 60.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: Insomnia is not as bad as it used to be.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Dr. Rosser spent the past nine months working through the risks and benefits with Kallman, who has a family history of breast cancer. She says she`s still hesitant, but she realizes hormone therapy may be her best chance at relief.\par \par \uc2 ISABEL KALLMAN: I`m hoping, even if I do it only for like a year or two, that there is an option for me that can help me in the short term.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, Nikki, according to this new research, when is the safest time for women to take estrogen or hormone replacement therapy?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new research shows that the ideal time for a woman to be on hormone therapy in order to mitigate risk is between 50 and 59, and the right timing can even reduce the risk of diabetes and improve cognitive function.\par \par \uc2 But for anyone who is considering hormone therapy, it`s really important to speak with a doctor first.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: What about the dangers, Nikki? Are there women who just need to stay away from this altogether?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It`s a great question. Dr. Rosser of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  told me that some women who have had certain cancers may not qualify for systemic hormone therapy, but newer studies show they may be able to safely take some local forms of the treatment, which is hopeful for them.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All right, Nikki Battiste, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Recovery from the Southern California wildfires is a marathon.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : How runners are helping is tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In Eye on America, we often look at an issue facing the nation or one corner of it.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we focus on Southern California and the ongoing recovery from the wildfires. After flames raced through the community, a running club is helping it to heal.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Mark Strassmann.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): You could ask any of these runners. Los Angeles feels changed since the wildfires. They have changed too.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m really excited to have you all here with us today.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Carl Walton joined the Pasadena Run Club two years ago for a sense of community. He got so much more.\par \par \uc2 CARL WALTON (Pasadena Run Club): Oh, this is the living room right here.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Walton, an Uber driver and massage therapist, took us to his old address in Altadena. Last January, L.A.`s Eaton Fire roared through this neighborhood. In minutes, the house he was renting became a fireball.\par \par \uc2 CARL WALTON: Wow, I can`t believe this.\par \par \uc2 Like, literally burned up right in front of me.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: That must have been...\par \par \uc2 CARL WALTON: Oh, it was hard.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... awful.\par \par \uc2 CARL WALTON: It was hard.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It was also the moment when the Pasadena Run Club became more than exercise for Walton. Roughly a dozen members had lost their homes. The club responded with temporary shelter, food, clothes. They organized fund-raisers.\par \par \uc2 What does the run club mean to you today that you never knew about before the fire?\par \par \uc2 CARL WALTON: They have been here the whole time. They have been helping me the whole time just on all levels.\par \par \uc2 RONNIE FERRAZ (Co-Founder, Pasadena Run Club): Every day, we would learn that there was somebody new affected by the fire, and it`s still going on today.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Ronnie Ferraz co-founded the run club four years ago.\par \par \uc2 RONNIE FERRAZ: Do they have food? Do they have clothes? Like what do they need?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The club is no longer just about the running.\par \par \uc2 RONNIE FERRAZ: It`s much more of a community than anything else.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Altadena is slowly recovering. The wildfires destroyed two-thirds of this town`s structures; 97 percent of those lots have now been cleared.\par \par \uc2 CARL WALTON: So, all that was yard.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Walton`s still recovering too. He`s staying with relatives, trying to save money to live on his own again. But, some nights, he sleeps in his car.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Keep it going, Carl, the last homestretch.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: His run club is his relief from all that.\par \par \uc2 CARL WALTON: By running, I`m kind of like putting everything to the side for a minute, and the hope comes from the people, the people around me that`s helped.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: You got it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In the Pasadena Run Club...\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Come on. Whew.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... no one runs alone.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Great job. Good job.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For Eye on America, I`m Mark Strassmann in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally here tonight: Owen Cooper has become the youngest male Emmy winner.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That is for his portrayal of an adolescent charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Kiniry now on an actor coming of age.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY SWEENEY (Actress): The Emmy goes to Owen Cooper, Adolescence.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY (voice-over): When you`re 15 years old and you win an Emmy, you hug your mom and thank your teachers.\par \par \uc2 OWEN COOPER (Actor): Honestly, when I started these drama classes a couple years back, I didn`t expect to be even in the United States, never mind here.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR: Police! Suspect found!\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY: Owen Cooper was awarded for his performance as Jamie, a 13- year-old boy from a blue-collar town in Northern England arrested for murdering a female classmate.\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS: You need to sit down.\par \par \uc2 OWEN COOPER: I don`t want to sit down.\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS: If you don`t sit down, I have to stop this discussion.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY: This series dealt with dark themes like online misogyny radicalizing young boys. But his drama teacher Esther Morgan told us the actor in real life is nothing like his conflicted character.\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MORGAN (Drama Teacher): He`s just what I`d call like a normal lad, just a normal boy.\par \par \uc2 OWEN COOPER: Don`t you dare!\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY: The performance in Adolescence was an incredibly intense and difficult performance. Did you know he had this kind of performance in him?\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MORGAN: I think we knew he was fantastic and I think we knew he had great potential, but he surprised us all.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY: Becoming the youngest male ever to win the statue.\par \par \uc2 OWEN COOPER: I was nothing about three years ago. I`m -- I`m here now. So I think, if you listen and you focus and you just step out your comfort zone a little bit, who cares if you get embarrassed, you know? Anything can be impossible.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY: Owen Cooper, an inspiration in adolescence.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: He was nothing three years ago? That`s an impressive kid.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : He`s a lot now.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The rest of us were doing nothing...\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Right, at age 13.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: ... at that age, right? Playing baseball, delivering the newspaper, that was my resume.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also, that idea of getting out of your comfort zone...\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : ... when you`re 13, that`s really hard. When you`re 57, it`s not so easy either.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And it gets even tougher.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Out of the mouths of babes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: There you go.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That`s the CBS Evening News. Evening News Plus is coming up on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250916el9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 SATURDAY WEEKEND NEWS for September 13, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Haley Ott, Willie James Inman, Andrew Kozak,  Camilo Montoya-Galvez, Lilia Luciano, Elizabeth Cook \par \uc2 3,676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, new information about Charlie Kirk`s alleged killer. Just miles from the crime scene, the suspect is behind bars and booked on murder charges.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: I`m Carter Evans in Spanish Fork, Utah, where the accused gunman is being held and evaluated to determine if he`s a risk to himself or others.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Plus, Charlie Kirk`s widow breaks her silence.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA KIRK: No one will ever forget my husband`s name and I will make sure of it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, in London, more than a hundred thousand rally in the streets.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: I`m Haley Ott in London, where 10s of thousands of right-wing protesters have come out to demonstrate for free speech and against illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Poland`s latest drone threat and President Trump`s tough talk for Russia.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: I`m Willie James Inman traveling with the President in New Jersey as he demands \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies stop buying Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Weather alert.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: I`m meteorologist Andrew Kozak, tracking the potential for flash flooding. I`ll have details in your forecast.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And later, healing powers. The doctor mixing medicine and music in the operating room. Dr. Esserman is a miracle doctor.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening, everyone, and thank you so much for joining us on this Saturday.\par \par \uc2 We begin in Spanish Fork, Utah. That`s where Tyler Robinson, the 22-year- old suspect in the murder of conservative activist Charlie Kirk is being held. While officials have yet to identify a precise motive for Wednesday`s shooting, we are learning new details about the suspected killer. His family members say he recently became more politically active. \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Carter Evans, who has been following the new developments, joins us with the very latest. Carter.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Tyler Robinson`s being held in jail behind me in a special housing unit where he`s being evaluated right now to determine if he`s a risk to himself or others. Meanwhile, Charlie Kirk`s widow has shared her -- her message for his alleged assassin.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERIKA KIRK: You have no idea what you just have unleashed --\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): In her first public comments Friday night, Erika Kirk was emotional, but filled with determination that the murder of her husband will not end his life`s work.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA KIRK: If you thought that my husband`s mission was powerful before, you have no idea. The movement my husband built will not die. It won`t.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Thursday, the conservative activist`s body was flown back to Arizona where he lived, escorted by Vice President \uc2 J.D. Vance\uc2  on Air Force Two. The manhunt for his alleged assassin lasted 33 hours. The suspect was taken into custody Friday at his parents` home in Washington, Utah, about three and a half hours southwest of the murder scene.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say his father recognized his son and urged him to turn himself in. The suspect told his dad he would rather die by suicide. That`s according to two law enforcement sources who said the family called a youth pastor who notified \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  . The Marshals arrested Tyler Robinson and turned him over to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Robinson lived five miles away from his parents in St. George, Utah. \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Andres Gutierrez is there.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: This is the apartment complex where the suspect lived. Neighbors tell me that Robinson did have a roommate and they mostly kept to themselves. On Friday, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  hauled away several boxes of potential evidence. Cameron Turnbow (sp?) is a neighbor.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON TURNBOW: Just my heart breaks.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: What do you think of the parents doing this?\par \par \uc2 CAMERON TURNBOW: I just break. I break for the father. However, I also commend him as a man and a father to be like, look, you did it. You got to turn yourself in.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: In Phoenix, at the mortuary where Kirk`s body lies, there`s now a growing memorial.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It hurts me that people have so much hatred in their hearts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The suspect has no prior felonies or misdemeanors on his record. And prosecutors are expected to file formal charges on Tuesday ahead of his first court appearance. And today, plans were announced for a memorial to be held for Kirk a week from Sunday at the home stadium of the \uc2 Arizona Cardinals\uc2  . Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Carter Evans for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 As President Trump praised the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for its speedy response to Charlie Kirk`s murder, he also signaled a new position on Russia`s war in Ukraine. Today, he said the U.S. will agree to additional sanctions on Moscow, but only when our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies stop buying Russian oil. This weekend, the President is at his golf club in New Jersey. \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Willie James Inman reports tonight from Bridgewater. Willie James.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Jericka, good evening to you. Romania and Poland today scrambled aircraft in response to Russian drone activity. Heightened tensions come as President Trump encourages allies to turn up the sanctions on Russia.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): In a blunt letter addressed to \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries, President Trump says he is ready to impose sanctions on Moscow if the alliance first stops buying Russian oil. The President posting on social media saying, "As you know, NATO`s commitment to win has been far less than 100 percent, and the purchase of Russian oil by some has been shocking."\par \par \uc2 The President also called on \uc2 NATO\uc2  nations to impose secondary tariffs of 50 percent to 100 percent on Chinese goods to put pressure on Russia`s top oil consumer.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Trump previewed what additional sanctions on Russia could look like.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Well, it`d be hitting very hard on with sanctions to banks and having to do with oil and tariffs also.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Since 2023, \uc2 NATO\uc2  member Turkey has been the third largest importer of Russian oil. Alliance members Hungary and Slovakia also buy Russian oil. The President has grown increasingly frustrated with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as his diplomatic efforts to end the war have stalled.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Yeah, it`s sort of running out and running out fast. But it does take two to tango.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Trump`s \uc2 NATO\uc2  ultimatum takes place as he also focuses on his crime crackdown here at home. With a plan to deploy the National Guard to more cities, starting with Memphis, Tennessee. Residents have mixed reactions about the operation.\par \par \uc2 TONYA OWENS (Memphis, TN Resident): I`m for any help coming to Memphis to put aside the -- the crime that`s going on.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT MCNEAL (Memphis, TN Resident): I know it`s a lot of crime here, but as far as the guard, we don`t need them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: On Saturday, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported about the existence of a Pentagon plan with details about a deployment of 1,000 National Guard troops on a crime-fighting mission to Louisiana. Now, a Pentagon official told CBS News that leaked documents should not be treated as policy. Jericka?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Willie James Inman in Bridgewater, New Jersey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In London today, more than 100,000 people took to the streets in what`s believed to be the largest anti-immigration protest in British history. Organizers called it a Unite the Kingdom rally. CBS Haley Ott reports tonight from London.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Good evening. A minute of silence was held for Charlie Kirk at the massive march in London, which drew an estimated 110,000 people.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT (voiceover): Marching through the streets of London, one of the largest right-wing demonstrations to take place in the U.K. in modern times. Organized by the far-right anti-immigration activist \uc2 Tommy Robinson\uc2  , it was billed as a celebration of free speech.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Illegal immigration is out of control, we`re -- and we`re losing -- losing our voice. You know what I mean? Freedom of speech, I feel, is being eroded.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Close by, a much smaller counter-protest organized by anti- racism activists.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I`m just really against racism.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: So close, at one point, that right-wing marchers began throwing things at them and pushing police. But some demonstrators were looking beyond Britain`s borders. Among the Union Jacks and St. George`s Crosses were MAGA hats and American flags.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Me and my partner decided to have an American flag as well to show our solidarity with America, basically, and the relationship that we have, and more specifically because of the death of Charlie Kirk.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Do you feel a connection to Charlie Kirk?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I like what he stood for. I like his integrity.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Tens of thousands of people came out today to hear speakers at these screens. The crowds were so large, they stretched all the way down the road behind me. And then down behind, past Westminster, across the bridge, over the Thames River to the south of the city.\par \par \uc2 One of those speakers, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  , who called for a change of government in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK: What I see happening is a destruction of Britain.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Rob traveled to London from Canada just to attend the march.\par \par \uc2 ROB: I want to basically get an idea as to what they`re going through, so I`ll get a more clear picture as to what -- what`s coming to us.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: What do you make of when people call rallies like this racist?\par \par \uc2 ROB: Oh, that`s just a buzzword that they`ve been using for years to try to discourage it. They want to change the West completely and put it under more of an authoritarian rule.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: London`s Metropolitan Police said a number of its officers were assaulted by the right-wing demonstrators, Jericka, and that 25 people were arrested.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Haley Ott, thank you for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago today, a protest against President Trump`s immigration crackdown. A large group of Catholic clergy led what they call a people`s mass outside the Great Lakes naval base. Now, the base will also be used by ICE agents as a staging area for deportations. Immigration reporter Camilo Montoya-Galvez shared what he learned after spending a day with ICE agents in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: All right, we`ll stay safe.\par \par \uc2 CAMILO MONTOYA-GALVEZ: ICE agents gathered in a parking lot in the early morning. Their plan was to arrest two men who they say are in the U.S. illegally and have serious criminal histories. At the first location, they discovered their target was not living there. During the second attempted arrest, no one opened the door. Marcos Charles leads ICE`s deportation branch.\par \par \uc2 MARCOS CHARLES: The community leaders in Chicago and these sanctuary areas have been in -- you know, giving information to these illegal aliens, telling them not to answer the door.\par \par \uc2 CAMILO MONTOYA-GALVEZ: Charles would not say how many federal agents are involved in the Chicago immigration operation. He stressed as agents are going after criminals, not day laborers.\par \par \uc2 You`re not targeting Home Depots?\par \par \uc2 MARCOS CHARLES: No, we are not targeting Home Depots.\par \par \uc2 CAMILO MONTOYA-GALVEZ: Today, there were protests outside an ICE facility near Chicago following Friday`s fatal shooting of Silverio Villegas- Gonzalez. Officials say Villegas-Gonzalez tried to evade arrest, injuring an officer. ICE says Villegas-Gonzalez was in the U.S. illegally and had a history of reckless driving.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: This is a test of our justice system. Will ICE follow the law or will they keep operating like a secret political police?\par \par \uc2 CAMILO MONTOYA-GALVEZ: Tension between protesters and homeland security agents escalated Friday. Chicago Area Minister Quincy Worthington is calling on all sides to de-escalate.\par \par \uc2 It feels like the tensions right now are really high.\par \par \uc2 REV. QUINCY WORTHINGTON: Yeah, I think they`re extremely high. And I -- and I -- from my perspective, I feel like that`s the result of dehumanizing our opponents.\par \par \uc2 CAMILO MONTOYA-GALVEZ: He says dialogue is needed now more than ever. Camilo Montoya-Galvez, CBS News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right. Well, turning to the weather where this tornado touched down in Navajo Territory in rural southeastern Utah. It sent debris flying, damaging some mobile homes. For most of the country, though, September is bringing some unseasonable heat. Let`s check in with \uc2 CBS\uc2  meteorologist Andrew Kozak for more on that.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yeah, good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 September heat, just a few degrees warmer than average across the East Coast. Very nice weekend, but across the midsection of the country, an omega block pattern forms. That`s when the jet stream forms the Greek letter omega, trapping some of the cooler and stormier air to the West, midsection of the country, steaming in the 90s. Areas like Des Moines, Iowa could be setting records mid-90s, and that pattern will stay in tune for your Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Of course, today, across the four corner states, we were tracking severe weather, including some incredible pictures of that tornado there across southeastern Utah. We get into the day on Sunday, and we`ll see some of that push a little further north and east. So, let`s talk about that.\par \par \uc2 A chance for not only storms north and east on your Sunday, but also a chance to get in on some very heavy rain. In fact, we could see some potential flooding for the Dakotas as we get into your Sunday afternoon. We`ll watch that with storms.\par \par \uc2 Quick check of the tropics, Pacific, the remnants of Mario, still giving some very heavy rain to Mexico. Atlantic`s been quiet, but we`re watching a cluster of storms. Good chance of development in the week ahead. Jericka?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Thanks so much, Andrew.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, we`ll take you to the city in Peru that`s benefiting from one of its most famous former residents.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a daring whale rescue off the coast of Australia. We`ll show you how this tale turned out.\par \par \uc2 And a surgery with a song. How one doctor is personalizing hospital visits. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Pope Leo XIV turned 70 years old today. At the Vatican, the newly appointed American ambassador presented the first American-born pope with a chocolate birthday cake, one of my favorites.\par \par \uc2 Well, before becoming pontiff, he spent over two decades as a missionary and a bishop in Peru. The city where he led the church has become a destination for the faithful. \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Lilia Luciano reports from Peru.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: That Pope.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: That Pope.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): It`s a long way from the south side of Chicago to the heart of Chiclayo. We went to the city in northern Peru because of a simple phrase spoken on a balcony in the Vatican by the first American pope.\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV: -- y en modo particular a mi querida diocesis de Chiclayo.\par \par \uc2 In particular, to my beloved diocese of Chiclayo in Peru.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: How did people react? Como reaccionaron cuando escucharon Chiclayo?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Una alegria muy grande --\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: "Some cried, we laughed, and remembered all of those gracious moments he spent with us here in Chiclayo."\par \par \uc2 And on that Sunday in early May when Cardinal Prevost ascended to the throne of Saint Peter, the town was lifted as well, becoming an instant tourist attraction.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: The fact that recently a new pope has been elected from here just makes everything more special.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: City officials told us the election of Pope Leo touched off a pilgrimage here by Catholics from all over the world. And that is a blessing for the economy. Tourism could give this city a $40 million boost. Restaurants, hotels and tour companies are scrambling to meet demand.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried?\par \par \uc2 MARIA ISABEL ESPINAL: No, I`m so happy.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Maria Isabel Espinal runs a local tourism agency.\par \par \uc2 MARIA ISABEL ESPINAL: Really, we`re going to have a lot of work, with everybody. With hotels, tour operators, restaurants, with the tour guides.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Like the Stations of the Cross, tourists retrace Pope Leo`s steps here, walking from church to church along his unique path to the papacy. Even stopping at his favorite lunch spot.\par \par \uc2 This is where the pope would sit.\par \par \uc2 Served by none other than the pope`s favorite waiter, Carlos.\par \par \uc2 (CARLOS speaking in foreign language).\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Who told us he prays every day for his old friend, the Pope.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano, CBS News, Chiclayo, Peru.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  expands its reach, sending two dozen satellites into orbit. We`ll explain what that means.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Now to California`s Vandenberg Space Force Base. It`s where today \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  launched a Starlink mission that will add an additional 24 broadband internet satellites to orbit. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  now has more than 8,400 satellites up in space.\par \par \uc2 Off the Australian coast, a humpback whale is free tonight after getting tangled up in fishing ropes. Rescuers fought rough seas and high winds to free the massive creature. They say the whale remains still through the effort as though it was trying to cooperate. Well, after several tries, the ropes were finally cut and the humpback swam away.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, she`s hitting the right note in the operating room. But first, a look back at this day in history.\par \par \uc2 Graphics onscreen: This Day in History--\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Scooby-Doo, Where Are You")\par \par \uc2 This Day in History: 1969 - "Scooby-Doo, Where Are You" Debuts on \uc2 CBS\uc2  from Hanna-Barbera\par \par \uc2 This Day in History: 1977 - GM Debuts Frist U.S. Diesel Car: The Oldsmobile 88\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s an Olds diesel V8. And the beauty of a diesel is that without an ignition system or carburetor, it never needs a tune up.\par \par \uc2 This Day in History: 1997 - Mother Teresa`s State Funeral Watched by Millions Worldwide\par \par \uc2 PAULA ZAHN: The world says farewell. Mother Teresa receives a full state funeral in Calcutta as her order vows that her work will go on.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Finally, this evening, surgery can be one of life`s most daunting experiences. But one doctor is changing the tune, making the process more personal while striking a calmer chord. In tonight`s Weekend Journal, CBS`s Elizabeth Cook highlights the high notes resonating with patients.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK (voiceover): When Dr. Laura Esserman, a leading breast cancer surgeon prepares for a surgery, she goes beyond expectations to make the operating room comfortable.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURA ESSERMAN: (INAUDIBLE) for you.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: And she gives them this --\par \par \uc2 (Dr. Laura Esserman singing)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: A special song chosen by the patient.\par \par \uc2 Why did you start doing this?\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURA ESSERMAN: Everything about my practice is all about trying to figure out how to personalize medicine.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Melissa Parker needed a doctor 14 years ago for treatment for her breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA PARKER: So, I did some research and found an article about Dr. Esserman. I crafted this heartfelt email to her. I thought, she`s a rockstar surgeon. She is not going to get back to me. But the next night I got a phone call at 9 P.M. and it was her.\par \par \uc2 (Dr. Laura Esserman singing)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Two weeks later, Parker, then 32, was in the operating room.\par \par \uc2 And at the end, you`d actually changed the lyrics to the song.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURA ESSERMAN: I did.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: What did you change them to?\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURA ESSERMAN: If you say, you know, it well may be that we will never meet again, and it`s supposed to be in this lifetime, but of course it`s more appropriate to say in this OR. So, let`s hope, right?\par \par \uc2 (Dr. Laura Esserman singing)\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURA ESSERMAN: Never again, let`s hope.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Unfortunately, they did meet again.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA PARKER: This time it was triple negative cancer. And I knew immediately that this is the worst type of breast cancer. And then my next thought was, I need to email Laura Esserman.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Melissa Parker underwent a special treatment Dr. Esserman arranged.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA PARKER: That was a miracle treatment, because Dr. Esserman is a miracle doctor.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Her surgery now is to remove a mass made benign in a successful trial.\par \par \uc2 (Dr. Laura Esserman singing)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Dr. Esserman`s own research is among those that have shown music helps heal, helps lower blood pressure and anxiety in patients during difficult times.\par \par \uc2 (Dr. Laura Esserman singing)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Elizabeth Cook, CBS News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yep, Dr. Esserman has many talents.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on FACE THE NATION, Margaret Brennan`s guests include House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 For now, I`m Jericka Duncan in New York. We thank you for watching. Have a great night and we hope to see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020250914el9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 4, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson, Ramy Inocencio, Caitlin Huey- Burns, Jon LaPook, Holly Williams, Lilia Luciano, Norah O`Donnell, Lonnie  Quinn \par \uc2 3,880 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARIA CANTWELL (D-Washington): You`re a charlatan. That`s what you are.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Health Secretary Kennedy on Capitol Hill, under fire and firing back.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (U.S. Health and Human Services Secretary): I don`t even know what you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Mr. Secretary...\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR.: You`re talking gibberish. I`m not taking vaccines away from anybody.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Justice Barrett`s first interview since joining the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT\uc2  (U.S. Supreme Court Associate Justice): The court should not be imposing its own values on the American people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The inking of America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TRACEE GLAB (Executive Director, Flint Institute of Arts): It`s about freedom and how you want to express yourself and your body.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And remembering the king of fashion, \uc2 Giorgio Armani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  is responsible for nothing less than the health of the people of the United States. But, on Capitol Hill today, there were a lot of questions about the way he`s doing his job.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The president`s health secretary was criticized over, among other things, health care costs and availability, COVID and vaccines.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News poll out today found 74 percent of Americans believe the government should make vaccines more available. But 39 percent say Kennedy`s policies are making them less available.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: There was a lot of shouting at the Senate Finance Committee hearing. Dr. Jon LaPook will help us cut through the noise.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But we begin with Caitlin Huey-Burns, who was at the shouting location, the Capitol -- Caitlin.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, and that hearing was as dramatic as advertised.\par \par \uc2 But unlike most events up here on Capitol Hill, it didn`t fall on partisan lines. Kennedy faced really tough questions from both Democrats and Republicans alike. But he remained defiant through it all.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): For over three hours on Capitol Hill today, lawmakers from both parties grilled Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , much of it over his agency`s approach to vaccines.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MICHAEL BENNET (D-Colorado): I`m asking the questions, Mr. Kennedy, on behalf of parents and schools and teachers all over the United States of America, who deserve so much better than your leadership.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-Wyoming): I`m a doctor. Vaccines work. Secretary Kennedy, in your confirmation hearings, you promised to uphold the highest standards for vaccines. Since then, I have grown deeply concerned.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kennedy fired and replaced all 17 members of a key CDC vaccine advisory panel, which is set to meet later this month to discuss vaccine recommendations. Last month, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  limited access to the COVID vaccine to those over 65 or with underlying health conditions.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (D-Massachusetts): Will you tell America that all adults and all children over 6 months of age are eligible to get a COVID booster at their local pharmacy today?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (U.S. Health and Human Services Secretary): Anybody can get the booster.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN: Anybody is eligible to get a booster by just walking into the pharmacy?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR.: It`s not recommended for healthy people.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN: No, no. If you don`t recommend, then the consequence of that in many states is that you can`t walk into a pharmacy and get one.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , a physician who helped Kennedy get confirmed only after he got assurances from Kennedy that he wouldn`t restrict vaccine access, said this today:\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY (R-Louisiana): I would say, effectively, we`re denying people vaccine.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kennedy also defended the recent ouster of his handpicked CDC Director Susan Monarez, who wrote in an op-ed today that she was told to preapprove the recommendations of a vaccine advisory panel newly filled with people who have publicly expressed anti-vaccine rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON WYDEN (D-Oregon): Did you in fact do what Director Monarez said you did, which is tell her to just go along with vaccine recommendations, even if she didn`t think such recommendations aligned with scientific evidence?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR.: No, I did not say that to her.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON WYDEN: So she`s lying today to the American people?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR.: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kennedy once praised Monarez as unimpeachable. After her exit, several top CDC officials resigned last week.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR.: We need bold, competent, creative new leadership at CDC.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Caitlin, after all of this today, where does RFK Jr. stand with President Trump when it comes to keeping his job?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Well, more Democrats up here on Capitol Hill called for Kennedy`s resignation today, but that is highly unlikely because he still enjoys the support of the president. And, in our polling out today, a vast majority of Republicans approve of his job performance, even if the broader public does not.\par \par \uc2 So he may be called for further testimony at other committees, but, for now at least his job is safe.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Caitlin Huey-Burns on the Capitol -- at the Capitol on the Hill tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we are joined now by our chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook.\par \par \uc2 So, you are a practicing physician, Dr. LaPook. What did you make of this hearing?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You know, one of the reasons behind such a heated hearing is, decisions are being made outside of the usual process. For example, the CDC`s vaccine advisory committee usually meets over two to three days, tons of research material is available, and various stakeholders weighing in, because the stakes are so high.\par \par \uc2 What`s often happening now is, decisions are being announced over social media without the evidence behind it being available for people to really do a deep dive on. So, as my math teacher used to say, show me your work.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We have been hearing today and other days recently people making the case of getting a vaccine should be between you and your doctor. Is it as simple as that?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Well, first of all, childhood vaccinations work, with research estimating that more than 10,000 deaths and more than a million illnesses are prevented every year.\par \par \uc2 But I do understand the instincts of a parent to say, don`t tell me what to do with my child. I want to take the chances, for example, of an infection like the measles. What they may not realize is that, when they`re rolling the dice for their child, they`re also rolling it for millions of people who are immunocompromised, 3.5 million babies who are born each year who usually don`t get the measles vaccine until 12 months of age.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All right, Dr. Jon LaPook, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 An American was among the 16 people killed when a streetcar derailed yesterday in Lisbon, Portugal. More than 20 others were injured.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: It`s from here that Lisbon`s famed historic Glory electric streetcar, the Elevador da Gloria, hurtled out of control downhill more than 800 feet, before it smashed into a building in a mangled wreck.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two dozen others were injured. Some people ran to help. Others scrambled out of the way. One of Portugal`s top engineers told us he believes the cable snapped and that both the manual and automatic brakes couldn`t handle the 20-ton load of metal and people. The streetcar`s operator says it has been maintained regularly. An investigation is now under way.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : There are troubling signs in the job market. The private sector added just 54,000 jobs last month, well short of the 75,000 economists had expected. The Labor Department will put out the full jobs report for August tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Alligator Alcatraz, the migrant detention facility in the Florida Everglades, will remain open for now. A federal judge last month ordered it shut down, and many detainees were transferred out. But, today, a federal appeals court panel pushed that order and paused it while the state and federal governments appeal.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It was two months ago today, the Fourth of July, that raging floodwaters caught Central Texas by surprise, killing more than 130 people, including 27 children and counselors at Camp Mystic.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Grieving parents demanded new safety rules to protect kids at summer camp. And, tomorrow, Governor Greg Abbott is expected to sign them into law.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jason Allen, who covered the floods, spoke to a family that lost a daughter. It is their first television interview.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN (voiceover): At 8 years old, Chloe Childress followed a family tradition of spending summers at Camp Mystic. This year, she turned 18, going from camper to counselor.\par \par \uc2 WENDIE CHILDRESS (Mother of Chloe Childress): She was very excited to return and excited to take care of those little girls and was even very worried about that. She want -- just like everything else, she wanted to do a good job. That was very important to her in the week before she left for camp.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: She probably put herself back in the shoes of those girls.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CHILDRESS (Father of Chloe Childress): Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WENDIE CHILDRESS: Probably.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CHILDRESS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WENDIE CHILDRESS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Wendie and Matthew Childress learned on July 4 that Chloe was missing after the flood. They began trying to piece together what had happened.\par \par \uc2 You felt like there are clearly things that are not in place here that could have prevented this.\par \par \uc2 WENDIE CHILDRESS: Something is not right here.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CHILDRESS: It was mostly that everything was preventable.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: They joined the parents of other girls demanding lawmakers make immediate changes for camps. New rules will prohibit cabins in flood zones, provide warning systems, require training for emergencies and strengthen communications when there is no power or cell service.\par \par \uc2 Some camps told lawmakers they were concerned about the financial burden of the new laws.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CHILDRESS: We think that that is an unfortunate stance for them to take, because they should be embracing this, because it is positive for the camps, and longer term they will thrive if they actually make the appropriate changes.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: In an editorial, Childress wrote it was complacency in the old rules that left him and other parents in the tortured position of questioning their own role in their daughters` deaths.\par \par \uc2 You wrote: "Did I kill my daughter?" What do you mean when you wrote that?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CHILDRESS: My daughter, I taught her right from wrong. I taught her those things. She obeyed the authorities. She followed directions.\par \par \uc2 WENDIE CHILDRESS: But, because the rule was to stay in place, they stayed in place, and it cost all of those children their lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Green ribbons are still around the trees in this Houston neighborhood in memory of those girls from Camp Mystic.\par \par \uc2 The Childress family will be with other families in Austin tomorrow to see those bills signed into law. They hope, though, this is just the first step, that it sets the tone for other states to look at their own camp safety rules.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jason Allen in Houston.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jason.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, Norah O`Donnell with \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , her first interview since becoming a Supreme Court justice.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And he dressed the biggest stars on the red carpet -- the death today of \uc2 Giorgio Armani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Have a look at this, hail the size of golf balls, divots and all. It fell in Central Kansas yesterday, as storms moved through the area, knocking out power to 5,000 customers around Wichita.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn has more about that and a reduced fire threat in the West -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: All right, we will get to everything, guys.\par \par \uc2 I want to start off with Kansas, because the good news is, for Kansas, tonight, it`s a much quieter picture. If you go back however 24 hours, here`s the cell. You`re going to find it right here pushing through the Wichita area, putting that big hail down, some of those hailstones recorded at three inches in diameter.\par \par \uc2 Now, the story of hail is a fascinating one, so bear with me. It all starts with a really big thunderstorm that is super high in the sky. It`s important. No, it`s got to be higher than that guys, way up. Push it way up, up, up, up. This is where the air is well below zero.\par \par \uc2 So, as that snowflake -- it starts as a snowflake -- falls to the earth, it starts to melt, becomes a raindrop. But that storm`s got big updrafts and downdrafts. It pushes that rain back up into the sky. Now, it freezes into a little piece of ice. That then starts to fall to the earth.\par \par \uc2 As that ice is falling, now it`s starting to melt. But before it becomes a raindrop, it gets pushed back up again and it refreezes bigger this time. Each time, it gets bigger and bigger, last night, three-inch hail. The record in Kansas, 2010, seven-inch hail. Imagine that.\par \par \uc2 Now, I do want to talk about the fire threat. It`s reduced. Places like Arizona will be picking up a lot of moisture. It`s monsoonal moisture. And that`s over the next few days. But, for California, we need the rain. Unfortunately, for the next week, I don`t see rain.\par \par \uc2 The saving grace for California, your temperatures are going to be significantly cooler outside -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn controlling the movements of the clouds.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  is defending her vote to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In a new memoir, she says Roe, which made abortion legal, got ahead of the American people, who she said had not always considered abortion a fundamental right.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And in her first interview since joining the court five years ago, Barrett talked with Norah O`Donnell about Roe and whether marriage and contraception, two other rights she considers doctrine, might also be up for revision.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You wrote in the book: "The court has held that the rights to marry, engage in sexual intimacy, use birth control and raise children are fundamental, but the rights to do business, commit suicide and obtain abortion are not."\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT\uc2  (U.S. Supreme Court Associate Justice): Right. I`m describing the doctrine. I was a conlaw professor for many years. Yes, I described the doctrine in the book. And that is the state of the law, which I -- yes, which I described in the book, because I want people to understand it.\par \par \uc2 I want Americans to understand the law and that it`s not just an opinion poll about whether the Supreme Court thinks something is good or whether the Supreme Court thinks something is bad. You know, what the court is trying to do is see what the American people have decided.\par \par \uc2 And, sometimes, the American people have expressed themselves in the Constitution itself, which is our fundamental law, sometimes in statutes. But the court should not be imposing its own values on the American people. That`s for the democratic process.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And so when \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , for example, says, what`s next, she said: "My prediction is the court will do to gay marriage what they did to abortion."\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT: Well I think people who criticize the court or who are outside say a lot of different things. But, again, the point that I make in the book is that we have to tune those things out.\par \par \uc2 So...\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But you also say in the book that the rights to marry and engage in sexual intimacy and use birth control and raise children are fundamental.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT: Yes, again, I`m describing what our doctrine is. And that is what we have said.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And you can see more of Norah`s interview with Justice Barrett this coming weekend on Sunday Morning here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The growing popularity of tattoos, but is there still a stigma about them?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: In Eye on America, we hold a mirror up to the country. And, in that mirror, you may have noticed there are millions of tattoos. They`re a major part of our culture, a way for individuals to make a statement, more elaborate, more expansive than ever.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano takes a closer look at works of art on a human canvas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): We met Lily Abitia (sp?) at this tattoo parlor in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 This is your dog who passed a year ago.\par \par \uc2 LILY ABITIA (Customer): Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Marking a memory of a great love.\par \par \uc2 Of course, you want your dog immortalized.\par \par \uc2 LILY ABITIA: A hundred percent, yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: To commemorate a milestone, to stand out, or even to fit in, the reasons for getting inked haven`t changed much over the last 5,000 years.\par \par \uc2 CHEYENNE ENDERSON (Tattoo Artist): There`s been a bigger shift when it comes to openness and individuality.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Tattoo artist Cheyenne Enderson tells us she works on hundreds of clients every year.\par \par \uc2 What do you think are common misconceptions about people who have tattoos?\par \par \uc2 CHEYENNE ENDERSON: Maybe some rebellious overall kind of crowd, which is definitely not the case anymore.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In a country where a third of American adults have at least one tattoo, is there still stink over the ink?\par \par \uc2 When I think about judgment around tattoos, I`m thinking, OK, risk-takers, might not be as ambitious. How much of that is wrong?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM CHOPIK (Associate Professor of Psychology, \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2 ): Almost all of it.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Bill Chopik, an associate professor of psychology at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2 , studies the stigma around people with tattoos.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM CHOPIK: You know, a lot of people have these formed assumptions about what people with tattoos are like. But it doesn`t really correspond anyway to their lifestyle and their personality.\par \par \uc2 Even studies done in the last five years show they`re less likely to get the job. We`re less likely to trust people with tattoos.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Despite that stigma, more than 40 percent of millennials, people born between 1981 and 1996, have a tattoo, a significant jump when compared to 13 percent of Baby Boomers.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to political party, roughly the same percentage of progressives, conservatives and independents of all ages sport one.\par \par \uc2 TRACEE GLAB (Executive Director, Flint Institute of Arts): That phrase ink addict, it`s a real thing.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It took Tracee Glab, the executive director of the Flint Institute of Art, a decade to get buy-in for this exhibit that features the work of 33 Michigan tattoo artists.\par \par \uc2 TRACEE GLAB: I think there was still hesitation, just because people didn`t understand they`re pushing the boundaries of art.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this is a very cool, abstract one.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: This is my favorite type of tattoo.\par \par \uc2 TRACEE GLAB: So, I think it`s about freedom and how you want to express yourself and your body.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: So, what about regret? Twenty-four percent of people wish they hadn`t gotten at least one tattoo.\par \par \uc2 CHEYENNE ENDERSON: Sometimes, you get those that are pretty hesitant, and they don`t want to regret getting it. But I`d like to think that, one day, our nursing homes will be full of tattooed residents.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: A different look for the next generation.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Lilia Luciano in Chicago, with more than one tiny tattoo and no regret.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Whole different look.\par \par \uc2 He adorned people not in ink, but fabric. The \uc2 Giorgio Armani\uc2  story, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We end tonight with \uc2 Giorgio Armani\uc2 . The Italian fashion designer died today at his home in Milan. He was 91.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Holly Williams has more about the man praised today as a giant, a visionary and a legend.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): His name is synonymous with a very Italian type of elegance, refined and luxurious. Born in Northern Italy, \uc2 Giorgio Armani\uc2  served briefly in his country`s military before selling his \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  to fund his first collection in 1975.\par \par \uc2 Just seven years later, he was on the cover of "TIME" magazine.\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 GIORGIO ARMANI\uc2  SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: When he was interviewed by CBS News Sunday Morning in 2006, his fashion house was already a multibillion-dollar empire.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: But you never formally studied. You never really went to fashion school, no?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GIORGIO ARMANI\uc2  (Fashion Designer) (through translator): No. This really shocks people. I learned everything on my own.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Suits are what made Armani`s name, soft and deconstructed, first for men made famous in the film American Gigolo, and later for women, he said a response to the feminist movement.\par \par \uc2 In Italy, he was known as Re Giorgio, or King Giorgio. On the other side of the Atlantic, he and Hollywood had a decades-long love affair. His designs were ubiquitous on red carpets.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, his company said \uc2 Giorgio Armani\uc2  worked until his final days, a man who came from nothing to rule the fashion world.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I first heard about him when Pat Riley, \uc2 L.A. Lakers\uc2  coach, the Showtime, the whole slick suit -- that was special.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : First time I ever knew of a name that had anything do with clothing, it was his.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: He was the guy.\par \par \uc2 That is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  Everything News. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . See you soon on EVENING NEWS Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250905el9400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Severe Weather Disrupting Holiday Travel; Dangerous Flood Risk; Diddy Verdict Drama; House to Vote on Big, Beautiful Bill; Kohberger to Plead Guilty today in Idaho Murders; Gaza Aid Distribution Controversy; \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  Settles Trump Lawsuit; Evacuations After California Fireworks Warehouse Explodes; Safety in the Skies; Televangelist Jimmy Swaggart Dies at 90; New Breast Cancer Risk; IVF Playbook for Men; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Summer Beach Hacks; Hot, New Beauty Trends; City as a Canvas; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Michelle Miller, Nikki Battiste,  Rob Marciano, Elise Preston, Jericka Duncan, Caroline Polisi, Caitlin Huey- Burns; Carter Evans; Debora Patta, Kris Van Cleave, Celine Gounder \par \uc2 14,598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey there and welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, July 2nd. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And I`m \uc2 Michelle Miller\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle and Nate are off, but we`re in wonderful hands. We`ve got another day of extreme weather affecting travel.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to break down a big decision from the jury as well in the \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  trial. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Heavy rain, flooding and lightning hit the Northeast just as millions try to travel for the holiday weekend. We`ve got the absolute latest.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was totally flooded. You couldn`t see the sidewalks. You couldn`t see -- it kind of went above the sidewalks, above the curb.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The jury reaches a verdict in the \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  trial on every count but one.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: The RICO charges, by far, in a way, the most complicated charge in this case.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: President Trump`s spending bill advances with what could be major cuts to Medicaid for millions.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): This is no longer just a House bill, it is not a Senate Bill, it is a bill of the people.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A fireworks facility in California caught on fire and exploded.\par \par \uc2 BOY: I was like, really scared.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: The biggest win of her career.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: A shocking day in Wimbledon.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Number two ranked Coco Gauff lost in straight sets to unseeded Dayana Yastremska of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 COCO GAUFF (Tennis player): I think I just couldn`t find my footing out there, too.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A Broadway star had a parenting moment, Jennie Harney Fleming performing a solo concert when her three-year-old daughter interrupts by shouting, "I want to go home."\par \par \uc2 JENNIE HARNEY FLEMING (Actor): Come to mommy. It`s too loud, I know.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: But obviously, she continued singing with her daughter, Olivia in her arms.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, you`ve got to.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I can so relate, been there on stage.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What can I say?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And Alan Alda in the audience, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And what happened?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Mason was like, "Yo, mommy, time to go home."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s such a beautiful moment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mom is singing it all.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A parent can relate. Every parent can relate.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to begin this morning, though, with some very nasty weather, taking the fun out of the Fourth of July holiday week for many Americans, major storms brought flooding yesterday to the Northeast, including Philadelphia and Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 More than 1,500 flights were canceled. Hundreds more have been canceled today. Nikki Battiste is at LaGuardia Airport right here in New York City for us. Nikki, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Vlad, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This is one of summer`s busiest travel weeks. According to AAA, a record 72 million Americans across the country are expected to travel. More than six million of them plan to fly, but severe weather is making that option challenging.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: A mix of thunder and heavy rain pounded the East Coast Tuesday afternoon, flooding roadways up and down the I-95 corridor.\par \par \uc2 In Fairfax County, Virginia, firefighters believe lightning caused a small fire at this town home, no one inside was injured.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It definitely scared me. Got up, looked around, didn`t think anything of it. Maybe 10 to 15 minutes later, started to smell smoke and then heard a siren, and got out of my house.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And here`s what the visitors` dugout looked like Tuesday night at Citizens Bank Park in Philadelphia. The \uc2 Phillies\uc2  postponed their game against the \uc2 San Diego Padres\uc2  after thunderstorms.\par \par \uc2 REP. NIKKI BUDZINSKI (D-IL): Finally landed after a four-hour delay.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The weather didn`t stop House members from traveling to D.C.\par \par \uc2 REP. RUSSELL FRY (R-SC): I`m not going to chance being stuck in Myrtle Beach in this historic opportunity.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Ahead of today`s critical vote on President Trump`s tax and spending bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-IL): My flights got canceled, and so we`re going to drive overnight.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Illinois Representative Raha Krishnamoorthi hosted a \uc2 Zoom\uc2  town hall on his drive to the nation`s capital after his flight was canceled.\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-IL): We`ve got some snacks, and away we go to Washington, D.C., and so wish me luck. In 14 hours, I`ll be there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The congressman said his GPS showed an arrival time just before the 9:00 A.M. vote.\par \par \uc2 For anyone else hitting the road, AAA says the busiest driving days are today and Sunday. So if you are heading out, try to leave before noon -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, good advice, Nikki. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on the weather and the potential impact on the holiday, let`s bring in CBS News` meteorologist, Rob Marciano.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning. How is it looking?\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Some slow improvement today across the Northeast. In the morning, though, it`s going to be a little bit on the wet side, especially around D.C. This is where our front kind of dragging around. So still some wet roadways and the airport is going to be playing catch up. We should be much better today, maybe D.C. a little bit in the way of some delays there.\par \par \uc2 Another pulse coming through the upper Midwest today. We`ll get to the northeast tomorrow, not nearly what we saw yesterday. And then the southeast, we`re looking at action across the Mid-Atlantic. The Carolinas, you`re going to see some of this as well, but Florida really is going to be the sore spot here. Tampa, if you`re flying in there, we`re going to see delays, not just today, but through tomorrow as well.\par \par \uc2 And now the National Hurricane Center is giving this area a 40 percent chance of developing into something tropical. You know, whether it develops over the next seven days or not, it`s going to bring some heavy rainfall across this area, especially the West Coast. Fort Myers to Tampa, 48 inches. This is just through Thursday, so this is going to be your sore spot for the holiday. The rest of the country doesn`t look too shabby for the Fourth of July on Friday -- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Still be prepared. Rob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, at the \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  sex trafficking trial here in New York, jurors say they have reached a verdict on four of the five charges against the hip-hop mogul, but they say they are stuck on the most serious charge, racketeering and conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent, Jericka Duncan and CBS News legal contributor, Caroline Polisi are here with us.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 You know, you`ve been covering this trial from the start. Where do we stand?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right now, we`re talking about these deliberations that will take place this morning. Jurors are expected to deliberate this morning. Yesterday afternoon, they told the judge they had come to a decision on sex trafficking and transportation to engage in prostitution charges, but the jury said it could not decide on the racketeering conspiracy charge because jurors had "unpersuadable opinions."\par \par \uc2 The judge urged them to keep deliberating. While all this was happening, Combs was seated in court surrounded by his attorneys in an intense huddle. He has pleaded not guilty to all the charges. Earlier in the day, the jury asked to see transcripts from the testimony of Combs` former longtime girlfriend, Cassie Ventura. That suggests the jury was analyzing, possibly that sex trafficking charge that Combs faces in relationship to Ventura.\par \par \uc2 The jury also asked to review transcripts from the testimony of an escort who says he was paid to have sex with Ventura on multiple occasions. So, a lot to parse out here, but ultimately, we still don`t know what the outcome is. We just know that they`ve reached a verdict on four of those five counts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very interesting. Caroline, for parsing the outcome, some analysis here. What makes the racketeering conspiracy charge so complicated?\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: Yes, it is complicated. It has the most elements, and frankly, it is what some people have criticized the government for, perhaps an overcharging, right.\par \par \uc2 RICO, racketeering conspiracy, you think mob, Mafia, organized crime syndicates. What the government is alleging here is a little bit more amorphous. It`s that Sean Combs was sort of the head of this enterprise, the object of which was to promote his reputation, promote his brands, fulfill his sexual desires and cover up crimes related to achieving that goal, essentially.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a rotating cast of characters, what they`ve alleged. So it`s rotating bodyguards. It`s his top chief-of-staff, KK, and they didn`t put on the stand any of those people. So it may be difficult to convince all of the jurors that this enterprise existed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the jury`s note to the Court cited "unpersuadable opinions." What does that mean exactly? And what could be happening in that deliberation?\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE POLISI: Yes, I wasn`t surprised that the judge gave what`s known as a modified Allen charge, which is essentially telling them to go back try to get to a verdict. It`s complicated, though, because you don`t want a coerced verdict, right? So the judge said, you know, stick to your reasonably and conscientiously held beliefs. Don`t abandon them, but talk to your other jurors. Keep an open mind, with the object towards getting a verdict.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And being in that courtroom, some of those jurors were very expressive. So when you hear those words, "unpersuadable," to me, that means we`ve tried, and we`ve got people in here who are like, I don`t see it.\par \par \uc2 So now they`re going to have more time as they should, and if they don`t reach a verdict today, we`re told there`s a possibility that the judge would keep the court open on Thursday, even though technically it`s closed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But we`ll see if that`s even needed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Serious business, you guys have been a great team covering this all the way.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, you did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate it again today. Thank you Jericka and Caroline.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to Washington and more drama, drama, drama over President Trump`s spending bill. The Senate approved it yesterday, despite concerns about cuts to Medicaid for millions of Americans who rely on it for health care.\par \par \uc2 The House takes it up today, but the path may not be an easy one.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns has the story for us from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R) (Vice President of the United States of America): The bill as amended, is passed.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Vice President J.D. Vance cast the tie-breaking vote to pass the President`s so called Big, Beautiful Bill through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Miss Murkowski --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Republican leaders spent hours courting the vote of Alaska Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , she ultimately backed the bill after securing provisions for her home state.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LISA MURKOWSKI (R-AK): I advocated my state`s interests. I will continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But on the other side of the Capitol, some Republican House members were quick to rain on the Senate`s parade.\par \par \uc2 REP. RALPH NORMAN (R-SC): What the Senate did is unconscionable. What they did to our bill was unconscionable. I`ll vote against it here, and I`ll vote against it on the floor until we get it right.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: House Speaker Mike Johnson acknowledged the tough road ahead after the Senate`s changes, including steeper cuts to Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): I encouraged them to amend it as lightly as possible. They went a little further than many of us would have preferred.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In a social media post, President Trump had a message for House Republicans: Stay united, have fun and vote yay. The bill would extend four and a half trillion dollars in tax cuts while adding new tax relief on tips and overtime pay, but it also cuts roughly a trillion dollars from health care programs like Medicaid, which 63 percent of nursing home patients rely on.\par \par \uc2 So if there are changes or cuts to Medicaid, are you concerned about your ability to operate?\par \par \uc2 NANCY MASON (Martinsburg Healthcare Center senior executive director): I would be.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Nancy Mason runs a nursing home in Martinsburg, West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 NANCY MASON: It helps to meet our operational expenses, everything from pharmaceuticals to food to utilities to recruitment of staff, and you know, all of our wages.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, Democrats are united against this bill, arguing that it favors the wealthy, and there is a new study from the \uc2 Wharton School\uc2  at Penn that finds that the lowest income earners, this will cost them about $970.00 per year by 2030 while the top 10 percent of earners stand to benefit the most.\par \par \uc2 Now the White House argues that the tax cuts in this bill stand to benefit all Americans -- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Caitlin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, 30-year-old Bryan Kohberger will be in court today to plead guilty to the murders of four college students back in 2022. In exchange, he could be spared the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans is in Boise ahead of what could be an emotional day in court. Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Good morning, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 The hearing is set for 11:00 A.M. local time. You can see people are already lining up outside court here to get a seat inside the courtroom, where 30-year-old Bryan Kohberger is expected to change his plea today from not guilty to guilty. It`s part of an agreement with the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Now, in exchange he will avoid the death penalty. He`ll spend the rest of his life in prison without the possibility of parole or appeal. Now, Kohberger is accused of fatally stabbing four students in Moscow, Idaho in November of 2022. The victims, Kaylee Goncalves, Madison Mogen, Xana Kernodle, and Ethan Chapin were killed in an off campus home, their families are divided on how they view this plea deal.\par \par \uc2 Some are expressing support, like the father of Madison Mogen, but the Goncalves family is outraged. Their attorney says they only found out about the agreement on Monday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHANON GRAY (Kaylee Goncalves family attorney): If you were taking the victims` families into any consideration at all, you would not have scheduled it for July 2nd. I`ve never seen such a short notice on to victims` families who don`t even live in the town.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, the Goncalves family is planning on attending the hearing today. Kohberger`s family says they don`t plan to comment or answer any questions -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Carter Evans for us this morning. Thank you, Carter.\par \par \uc2 President Trump declared overnight on social media that Israel has agreed to terms for a 60-day cease fire in Gaza. There`s been no confirmation of this from Israel, but it does raise hope for Gazans who risk their lives just by lining up for food every day.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta looks into a controversial food distribution group that has been heavily criticized for its activities in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Doctors and eyewitnesses we`ve spoken to say Israeli soldiers have repeatedly opened fire on people waiting for food near the U.S. backed Gaza Humanitarian Foundation sites, which are secured by private American contractors.\par \par \uc2 We spoke via \uc2 Zoom\uc2  to the Foundation`s new head and former spiritual adviser to President Trump, Reverend Johnnie Moore.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND JOHNNIE MOORE (Gaza Humanitarian Foundation): I`m not diminishing at all these reports. We can`t control what happens outside of our -- outside of our distribution sites.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: How many people will have to die before you change the way you operate?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND JOHNNIE MOORE With respect, I think the way you frame that question is just -- is inappropriate, okay.\par \par \uc2 Our whole reason for existing is to get food to these people so they can live.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Why not do it the way that`s been tried and tested?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND JOHNNIE MOORE: We`ve said again and again and again to the United Nations, the \uc2 World Food Programme\uc2 , join us in this effort.\par \par \uc2 Debora, I mean with respect, you know -- you know, why don`t you join us? I`m asking you to help us run the operation if you think there`s a better way of doing it.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: We asked Moore how they verified who was receiving aid, which he says is not being diverted to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . His response --\par \par \uc2 REVEREND JOHNNIE MOORE: I just have a question for you, Debora, do you have anything positive to say whatsoever about what we`re doing?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Do you think it`s acceptable that people die every day trying to access aid? How are you going to make sure that there is a better way?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND JOHNNIE MOORE: We`ve made a decision, and the decision is that it`s worth operating in a war zone with all the complexity and all the risk as deadly as it is, because Gazans deserve food.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Debora, as always, you really held truth to power during that interview, but how much did you actually learn about how the group operates?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, we still don`t have answers from Moore, Vlad, the criticism from groups like the \uc2 World Food Programme\uc2 , whom I`ve seen deliver aid in many war zones around the world, is that the GHF has replaced 400 sites with just four. The IDF has then been accused of using live fire to control the huge crowds that then gather.\par \par \uc2 Comparatively, the \uc2 World Food Programme\uc2  gives a month`s worth of food to registered recipients only, while GHF rations are handed out randomly and only last three-and-a-half days -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Debora Patta in Jerusalem for us. Debora, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In other news, \uc2 CBS\uc2  and \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 , which of course, is our parent company have announced a settlement of a lawsuit by President Trump. This happened overnight.\par \par \uc2 As you`ll recall, the President had sued over a "60 Minutes" interview with his opponent in the 2024 presidential election. You`ll recall that was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His lawyers had claimed that the interview was edited deceptively, something that Paramount and "60 Minutes" have always denied. Under the terms of this settlement, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  says it will pay $16 million to settle the lawsuit. Part of that will cover fees and costs for Trump`s lawyers. The rest will be donated to Donald Trump`s future presidential library.\par \par \uc2 Importantly, no amount will be paid directly or indirectly to President Trump, personally, and the settlement does not include an apology.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Coming up, a dramatic explosion at a fireworks factory ahead of the Fourth of July. We`ll show you what happened. But it`s 7:17 now, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead in "What to Watch" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 ESPN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- some major upsets for Americans at Wimbledon. We will tell you who`s out and who`s in, and why are \uc2 Yankees\uc2  tickets going for above regular price? It has to do with "Seinfeld" the show.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Ashley, inspiring the love of home.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Take a look at this scene in Northern California, where a fireworks warehouse blew up before the Fourth of July holiday, massive fire, forcing evacuations as well.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has our story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: A massive explosion caught on video that sends a gigantic plume of smoke into the air. Also capturing the moment, CBS Bay Area`s chopper catching this dramatic mushroom cloud in the moments immediately following the explosion at a fireworks warehouse.\par \par \uc2 It went up in flames Tuesday night in a rural area in \uc2 Yolo County\uc2 , outside of Sacramento.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters arrived at the facility that stores fireworks as they exploded, sending projectiles into the air.\par \par \uc2 CURTIS LAWRENCE (\uc2 Esparto Fire Protection District\uc2  fire chief): Units responded. Arrived on scene to find a few commercial buildings well involved, as well as numerous explosions.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Fire officials say the explosion sparked multiple fires across approximately 80 acres. The blast launched debris around the property and sent a fireball into the air that could be seen for miles.\par \par \uc2 People living nearby say they felt their homes shake --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And the windows were just, you know, going back and forth.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: As a result of the fire, a one-mile evacuation zone was set up. Firefighters say the cause of the explosion and fire has not been determined.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Elise Preston in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, Kris Van Cleave shows us how pilots train in case air traffic control goes offline. Yikes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ominous skies in Northern Portugal over the weekend, you`re looking at what`s known as a roll cloud. It looks like a tsunami, and it scared beach goers. But scientists actually say this is not dangerous. It is rare, though, in Europe, it happens when hot and cold air masses slam into each other, making the air roll like a log. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 Another factor here a serious heat wave in Europe right now. Temperatures in Portugal had reached 107 degrees.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Fourth of July travel Rush is underway, and it could set a new record. The TSA expects to screen more than 18.5 million flyers over a seven-day stretch, but what will pilots do if there`s a major disruption with air traffic control?\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave decided to find out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Blacked out air traffic control screens, communications issues in Newark, Denver and Jacksonville, all since April, signs of an aging air traffic control system.\par \par \uc2 AIR TRAFFIC CONTROLLER: Currently, we are stopped on departures, approach control off the radar again.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY (\uc2 U.S. Department of Transportation\uc2  Secretary): Well, I don`t want those to happen.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY: The pilots, when that happens, they know that they have to look to another center or TRACON or tower to communicate with and also to pay close attention and stay away from each other.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Ahead of the next big summer travel push, we wanted to see that in action.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN (United Airlines Flight Training Center managing director): We operate on a system of multiple layers of redundant systems.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Captain Miles Morgan oversees United Airlines Flight Training Center in Denver.\par \par \uc2 Was losing air traffic control on the list of things you practice?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: This is not the first time this has happened. One of the first things you learn as a pilot when you`re getting your instrument rating in a small airplane is what to do if you lose communication with the air traffic control facility.\par \par \uc2 All right, ready to go?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: Clear for takeoff.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Inside a 737 Max simulator, we are at the controls for a firsthand look, taking off from a simulated Newark Liberty Airport.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: Unannounced.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Once airborne, we are dialed into air traffic control on an assigned radio frequency.\par \par \uc2 Air traffic control goes out right now. You`re over New York City. What happens?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: So the first thing is, I`m on this primary frequency. I would immediately go to the frequency that I was last on, which is now in my stand by.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Another radio is set to an emergency channel that all ATC Centers and other aircraft can hear. If that doesn`t work --\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: The third layer of redundancy we have is this text messaging system.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: While flying, the 737`s transponder sends a signal identifying the plane to controllers and other aircraft. Pilots can change that number to 7600 the code for comms trouble.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: Hey, this aircraft has lost communication, so they`ll start separating aircraft around from us.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: All airliners also have an on board collision avoidance system known as TCAS, that`s scanning the skies for other planes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Traffic, traffic.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: So now I`m going to stop everything that I`m doing. I`m going to look at my navigation display, and I see that I`ve got traffic encroaching on me 600 feet above.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What happens if you`re not a safe distance away?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: So my aircraft would give me a command. If my aircraft was told to descend, that aircraft be told to climb, and that would increase the separation.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: If there`s one of these ATC outages and I`m in the air, I`m a passenger, am I less safe because of that?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN MILES MORGAN: Absolutely not less safe. You might be inconvenienced because we`re going to immediately have delays.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Those delays happen because air traffic controllers will immediately limit the number of aircraft operating in the chunk of air space that`s having issues. Air traffic controllers also have backups, backup radio channels, and if one center does go dark, other \uc2 FAA\uc2  facilities can track the airplanes in the air, so no one is lost in space, if you will. And all of this is going on amid the push for billions of dollars to modernize the air traffic control system -- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Oh, Kris, we did it a different way back in the olden days. I`m sure they`re picking up on those lessons now. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, some other news now and Jimmy Swaggart`s family announcing yesterday that the once hugely successful televangelist has died.\par \par \uc2 Swaggart was a cousin of Jerry Lee Lewis and Mickey Gilley, but chose preaching instead of performing. His T.V. Ministry had millions of followers in the 1970s and 80s built on his fiery Pentecostal sermons, but a connection to prostitutes led to his downfall.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY SWAGGART (Televangelist): I have sinned against You, My Lord.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Swaggart was defrocked after this tearful apology to parishioners in 1988. His T.V. ministry is now led by his son, Donnie. Jimmy Swaggart was 90.\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up, we`ve heard a lot about hormone therapy for menopause symptoms. Now, there`s a new study on the effects of certain hormones and the risk of breast cancer in women.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder is here to show us what it could mean for you.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: In this morning`s "Health Watch," a new study of women having hormone therapy to treat menopause symptoms found a link between a certain type of treatment and higher rates of breast cancer before the age of 55.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, and I have to take a breath now, because I know a lot of women watching are having whiplash hearing that headline, because they`ve been told one thing, told something else, and now we`re back into this sort of nebulous area, because the devil is really in the details.\par \par \uc2 So break it down for us.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is another great example of science evolves. We heard a lot about that during COVID. This is another example of that, where there was a study that was published in the early 2000s called "The Women`s Health Initiative Study," where they looked at hormone replacement therapy, but what distinguishes that study from this new one that was just published, is the age of the women in the study and how long it had been since menopause before they started hormone replacement.\par \par \uc2 So in that study, the women were significantly older -- 60s, 70s -- and they may not have started hormone replacement therapy until 10 or so years after going through menopause. This newer research is focused on what actually happens in real life, which is women who go through menopause in their 40s or 50s start treatment around that age, and what are the risks and benefits in that setting?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: So what are the findings? Break it down for us.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So what they found, they are focused on women under the age of 55 and they broke it down into two groups, women who still have their uterus, so have not had a hysterectomy, versus women who do not have a uterus anymore.\par \par \uc2 And so for the women who do still have a uterus, you have to give estrogen plus progesterone together. Estrogen alone will cause a thickening of the uterine lining, which can cause a different kind of cancer, what we call endometrial cancer, so you have to give the combination.\par \par \uc2 In women who no longer have a uterus, you can give estrogen alone. And so it does matter here. What they found is estrogen alone reduced the risk of breast cancer. It`s the progesterone that appears in the women who need to take both there seems to be a slight increase in risk in breast cancer if you also took progesterone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so the study also found that there`s another type of hormone therapy that may actually lower the risk of breast cancer. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, so that`s the estrogen alone. And I think this is going to be surprising to a lot of people, because a lot of women think that you`ll take estrogen and that`s actually going to increase your risk of breast cancer, and that`s not what we found in this study.\par \par \uc2 And I think the really important takeaway here is that hormone replacement therapy can be very safe. It is effective. In fact, it is the most effective treatment for a lot of perimenopausal symptoms, and you really need to sit down and talk to your doctor, depending on family history and other things, what is the appropriate thing for you.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, important news at the right time. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dr. Gounder, always great to have you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the stories you will be talking about today, including some very big upsets for Americans at Wimbledon. That`s ahead in "What to Watch." Though, right now it is 7:42, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A very personal story, he is opening up about male infertility, what it can mean for men, all that in our next hour. But first Vlad has got the stories you will be tough about today --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come on over, baby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- in "What to Watch." You know, people looking to shoot the fireworks off early. July 2nd is when the Declaration of Independence was actually formally announced by the Continental Congress. So go ahead, light a fuse. "What to Watch" is next.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What is he saying? He is changing everything.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is changing everything up.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Fasenra. For more information, visit fasenra.com\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Out goes the second seed, the Roland Garros champion, Coco Gauff.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, time now for "What to Watch." It was a very tough day for the American women in Wimbledon`s opening round. Second seeded American star Coco Gauff lost in straight sets to an unseeded player from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Gauff said she was mentally overwhelmed after winning the French Open three weeks ago. Dayana Yastremska is who beat her.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile American, Jessica Pegula, the three seed also suffered an early exit. She lost to a 116th ranked player from Italy.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her name Elisabetta Cocciaretto.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like a little bit of spice there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little bit of spice on that. But on the men`s side, 10th ranked American Ben Shelton pulled out a victory.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: After a tight contest with Australian, Alex Bolt, so --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: So, it goes to show you, you know, I mean --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What does it go to show?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It goes to show, you only have to be as good to beat them the day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On any given day --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- in professional sports, anyone can beat anyone at anything. You could lose to anybody.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s exactly right. You could be a 116th seed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s why we watch.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Much better said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The greatest reality T.V. show is sports.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is sports.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Absolutely. You know what else is really cool, BTS, Tony. Did you recognize that song that we came in?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did not, but I do know BTS.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Do you Michelle? A little BTS action.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, no, but they`re huge. I know they`re huge. Tell me about it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are huge, and they are potentially coming back together again after completing mandatory military service in South Korea last month.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from BTS/Weverse)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For the first time in nearly three years, all seven members --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Look at t