.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, Corey Parchman is here to talk about something that affects millions of men, including himself, and he`s here to remove the stigma.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I`m \uc2 Michelle Miller\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`re also following the latest beauty trends with "People`s" Brittany Talarico, the hair, the nails and the clothes you might see this summer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also got some beach tips for you just in time for the July 4th weekend, so you can enjoy your time in the sun with your family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to it. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 Severe storms cause major travel delays all across the country ahead of the holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Another pulse coming through the Upper Midwest today, it will get to the Northeast tomorrow, not nearly what we saw yesterday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The jury reaches a verdict on all but one charge in the trial of music mogul, \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The jury said it could not decide on the racketeering conspiracy charge because jurors had "unpersuadable opinions."\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): I encouraged them to amend it as lightly as possible. They went a little further than many of us would have preferred.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: After a close vote in the Senate, President Trump`s spending bill heads back to the House, but it could be a tough slog.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And \uc2 NBA\uc2  superstar Victor Wembanyama proves he`s an all- around athlete during a soccer match in Japan.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 MAN: No way.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Athlete means athlete.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Apparently so.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to the eight o`clock hour here. We`re going to begin with the extreme weather news. Major storms brought flooding to the Northeast yesterday, including to the suburbs outside of Philadelphia, and take a look at this lightning in the skies, all making travel difficult for many people this holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste has made her way to New York`s LaGuardia Airport, where a lot of other people will be going today.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, good morning. How are people feeling there?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Tony, I`d say the mood here is mixed. I just spoke with a group of travelers headed to Orlando who said their flight is delayed three hours. Several others have said their flights are on time.\par \par \uc2 Nearly six million Americans plan to fly to their destination, but hundreds of flights here at LaGuardia and across the country were halted yesterday. Severe weather all along the East Coast is to blame for these delays and cancelations. Yesterday, we saw thunderstorms from the D.C. area all the way to New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 You can see cars in the Philadelphia area driving through three inches of rain and to Baltimore, rescue crews responded to trapped cars in knee deep water. The weather is really throwing a wrench in travel plans for many Congress members who are trying to get to D.C. for today`s critical vote on President Trump`s spending bill. Many had to hop in the car. They are among the millions who will be on the road nationwide.\par \par \uc2 AAA projects more than 61 million people will drive, that`s 1.3 million more people than last year. Experts say the worst days to drive are today and Sunday. But if you are heading out, try to leave before noon.\par \par \uc2 With Fourth of July falling on a Friday, travel experts say people are more inclined to travel to take advantage of the long weekend -- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: A busy one all the way around.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, Florida could also be looking at a very wet holiday weekend