y 3rd that July 2nd should be the day, okay? That was the day.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: And the wives are always right. So you just -- you always side with that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: America.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I say, do it all three day. Light all the fireworks. Get the hot dogs and the grill going. Have a good time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh boy. Rob Marciano, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanks for the call back on that one. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Ahead, a topic that`s often not discussed among men. Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, Corey Parchman is here. He`s going to tell us about the challenges he and his wife -- oh, there they are with their little baby -- he is going to share that advice for us. Stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 Right now, though, it is 8:05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Coming up, we`ll share tips to help you have a safe and fun day at the beach, including one easy hack to create a sand free area.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we look at this summer`s biggest beauty trends. "People" style and beauty director shares what`s in when it comes to hair, nails, perfume and a whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guest is shining a light on the stigma around male infertility. At least 30 percent of infertility cases are, in fact, related to men, yet they are less likely to talk about it or have the tools to understand it.\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player and now real estate developer, Corey Parchman is trying to change that. In his fantastic new book, "IVF Playbook for Men," Parchman writes about his own personal and challenging experience with infertility and IVF offering reflections for men who feel isolated in their journey to fatherhood.\par \par \uc2 Corey Parchman joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Corey, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN (Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player and "IVF Playbook for Men" author): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Page 41, you talk about you`re struggling to conceive, you and your wife, and you`re told it`s male factor infertility, and you feel benched in the game of life. You`re right. I think a lot of guys will identify with that passage, but they do not talk about it. Why did you want to do it?\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Just because, as I went through the process, there was nothing out there for men that`s going through the process. There are a ton of information for women, and it should be, because women, they carry 99 percent of the load. But as I was trying to navigate through the process of infertility and IVF, there was nothing out there for men, so I wanted to create this book as a resource for men.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did it feel when he got that news? Because, I mean, as a guy, there is something that we pick up, either culturally or from somewhere else, that makes us feel like, if we can`t do this thing, we`re of less value.\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Well, I started to do a recheck. I`m like, you`re not talking to me, are you? You got the right paperwork?\par \par \uc2 So it`s a shock when you first hear it, because you know, as you grew up, you think, this is the process of having kids, and it`s as simple as ABC. And then when you realize, wow, it is just not it, then you`ve got to figure out, how do I put a strategy together? And the part of -- the hurt part was, I felt like I was being the weakest link on the team. I felt like I let my partner down, Elisha down, and she`s an amazing person. So I felt like I was the weak link when I first got the news about IVF.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You and your wife, Elisha, who`s here with your beautiful son, Corey, went through five IVF cycles over the course of two- and-a-half years. And you write about the challenge --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, he is so cute. I can`t get over a Little Corey -- but you write about those challenges, including miscarriages and the emotional toll it took on you and Elisha.\par \par \uc2 Can you talk a little bit about that and how you came to feel that you could share some of these very personal details about your lives to the world?\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: The question -- so the hardest part for me was standing on the sidelines, because once the process starts as a male, you know, you have to depend on your doctor and your wife everything else.\par \par \uc2 So I`m a person that always, I want to fix things. I`m a fixer, you know, I put strategies together. I fix things. But this situation, I had to sit back and put the faith in my doctors and my wife, and that process was hurtful in some aspects, because once again, I`m an active person. I want to be active. I want to be a part of this, but you`re often limited to that with that process.\par \par \uc2 So I had put a lot of faith in my wife, who let`s say did an amazing job, and also the doctors. And kind of, once you kind of get out of your own way that it is not about you, it`s about your family, then the process becomes a little bit easier. So I had -- it was an easy time putting faith in my wife and the doctors.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I`ve had so many friends go through IVF treatment. You have a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: So many people don`t. It tests marriages. I`m curious, how can you better support not just, you know, your own mental health, but that of your wife or partner?\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Yes, so definitely, you know, that`s kind of -- I am talking about the book, it is twofold, how to support yourself and support your partner. And you know, my partner and wife, know, she went through the physical and emotional process of it. You know, going to the appointments, have taken shots, all of those things, and you see that, but at the same time, we`ve got miscarriages, it hurts you also.\par \par \uc2 So you`ve got to figure out, okay, how can I make sure I take care of my wife? And one good part about our marriage was we talked a lot, and what happened was we`ve got an open space and a space that was a safe space, because now you say things you may not necessarily mean, but we knew we kept it inside that room right there, and then, once the day went on, we kind of forgot about it, but just create an open space for your partner and yourself to be able to talk about it, because you`re the only one that knows about it. So --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s talk about friends and family, because a lot of well- meaning friends and family, once you lock down with somebody and the timing seems to be right. They want to ask questions. How`s it going? Hi, are you trying?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And give advice.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Give advice, I mean, and it can be -- it can cause all kinds of feelings. So what`s your advice to friends and family who are -- who are rooting for you, but maybe, butt out of it.\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: I will say this, definitely give grace. What I found was a lot of my friends and family, they meant well, but oftentimes they didn`t have the right advice, because they were trying to say things that were comforting, but oftentimes was so far off.\par \par \uc2 You know, I had friends tell me -- eat three eggs and spin around, jump. It will help you --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What?\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Yes, it was like, you know, thanks for the advice, but I`m listening to the doctors. Because I`ve tried that already anyway.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Corey, in the book, in your final thoughts, you write that the IVF journey is much more than just medical procedures. It`s about transformation. Other than being a parent and a dad, what else is transformed in your life and Elisha`s life?\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Rewriting masculinity.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wait, say that again?\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Rewriting masculinity.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Rewriting masculinity.\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Yes, yes, I thought masculine was being strong and running. It`s being vulnerable. It`s opening up. It`s creating a space for yourself to express how you feel with your partner or your friends. So I want to be someone that can speak out and tell men, it is okay to speak out about your feelings. It`s okay to speak out about thoughts, because we all have them, and once you kind of open up your lives, everyone is the same.\par \par \uc2 So it`s one of those things where I want to redefine what it means to speak out, what it means to express yourself, what it means to be able to open up about any problems you may have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, we really appreciate you opening up today. I think the books can be passed among men for many years, and it`s valuable contribution to this conversation.\par \par \uc2 Corey Parchman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 COREY PARCHMAN: Thank you for us. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The book is "IVF Playbook for Men." It`s available on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and you can find it with other retailers nationwide.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Up next CBS MORNINGS Deals.\par \par \uc2 Gabrielle Kerr will show us a rechargeable sweeper mop that could make your cleaning much easier.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Time now for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. We`ve got several items at exclusive discounts of up to 50 percent off. To start shopping now, scan that Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Knocking