kar Noir.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes. Lauren --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I still smell it sometimes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It hasn`t worn off yet. I told you, you were wearing too much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You sound like my mom.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Bring us back in.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Okay, fragrances, so right now the fragrance mists and body mists are huge, so it`s not like this really heavy cologne or perfume people are wearing. They want a mist, they want to spray it all day. It reminds me of like walking through the mall as a high schooler, or like going to a school dance that you`re spraying your whole body, right? That is what`s in again. And you can, kind of, it`s like this really, like, just fun fragrance spray whenever you want. Yes, in your hair, too.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, that was a college thing for me.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is mermaid core? And what does it have to do with makeup?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: So mermaid core is another fun trend we`re seeing that`s huge. It`s all about, kind of, this iridescent, fun eye. You have a little bit of blue liner happening. I see, I think that`s a little, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, cool.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: There`s a little something maybe.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I thought it was a little --\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yellow and then something on top.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s got shells on her ear.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yes, so also, we have shells in our hair. It`s kind of bringing, like that, sea vibe. We also have something called sardine summer that`s happening.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What is that?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Also, it is bringing in like shells and big fish and like that kind of jewelry and charms.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sardine summer.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: And you can wear them on the neck.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Just not the smell.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yes. Yes. Not the smell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You said it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not the smell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not me.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yes, it is a fun one. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, sun protection. Tony`s favorite topic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, well, I do like it because it could be kind of makeuppy being give you a little glow as well.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yes, now it`s like, so easy to practice safe sun. I love like an SPF stick that I can keep in my purse. I keep it in my car. I put it on myself, on my kids. But you can also go to the beach with a full face of SPF makeup, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: SPF makeup?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Now -- yes, make --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Tans glow --\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Literally, it is like a beach beat on your face, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: You go, people have -- you can get a tint in your skin tint. You can do it in your blush. It`s lip oils, lip balms SPF, so it`s never been easier to practice safe sun. But like, look super glam.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want that beauty by Joseon, that Korean brand that makes that SPF stuff, which is like --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Are you selling?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: You`re a beauty --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m a beauty hyper. I`ve got to keep it looking fresh, Brittany.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: He`s Metro. He is on Metro. On Brittany Talarico, it was all fun.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY TALARICO: Oh my God. This was so fun. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: So informative, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: We thank you so much. So you can check out "People" for more beauty trends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And ahead, we`ll show you how the largest public art program in America is transforming the city of Philadelphia and changing lives as it does it.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In Philadelphia, art is everywhere you look.\par \par \uc2 More than 6,000 murals bring Philly`s buildings and blocks to life, earning it the unofficial title of The Mural Capital of the World. And for the second year in a row, "USA Today" calls it the best city in America for street art. Much of that art is commissioned by Mural Arts Philadelphia. The program not only brings color to the community, but it`s also transforming the lives of the artists behind the work.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan introduces us to some of those artists.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: With miles worth of murals in Philadelphia, you could see why they`ve become the main attraction, bold colors, vibrant expressions, backdrops that match the mood of the neighborhood and its people.\par \par \uc2 ALLOYIUS MCILWANE (Mural artist): I just get inspiration from all sorts of environments and just things that happen in life.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A wall like this is a canvas for 43-year-old Alloyius Mcilwane. His pockets stuffed with the paint he sprays in search of reaching a new level and new people through each mural.\par \par \uc2 ALLOYIUS MCILWANE: When I first saw my first mural, I just remember that the scale just being so amazing. You know, I just knew immediately that that`s what I wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And for the last 15 years, he`s been creating works of art around Philadelphia. Mcilwane paints for clients and himself with nearly 400 murals in the city and about a dozen more around the world, including the Bahamas, Japan, and France.\par \par \uc2 Where do you find the time?\par \par \uc2 ALLOYIUS MCILWANE: I don`t sleep. That`s pretty much it, I don`t sleep. It`s my job and it`s my hobby.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How would you describe your style?\par \par \uc2 ALLOYIUS MCILWANE: Well, there are blends of many different genres and styles in there. I have, like, signature shapes that I call kudos clips, which are positive affirmations in symbol form. So all of my artwork has these positive affirmations in there.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOLDEN (Active mural painter and community organizer): I just love the fact that he saw the murals before he even started working with us and thought that could be me and that is exactly right. That`s what we want artists to think.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Jane Golden has been working with thousands of artists for more than 40 years, starting with the Anti-Graffiti Network.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOLDEN: When we started out, it was like we were just sort of feeling our way through it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Nowadays, it`s called Mural Arts Philadelphia, the largest public art program in the country.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOLDEN: The walls of our city speak to you, tell your stories. I call it the autobiography of the city of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people that we work with are creative, have talent, but they don`t necessarily know it, and so art is, as the kids used to say in Anti- Graffiti, art had become a lifeline for them.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Becoming an artist has been a long time dream of 45-year- old, Reese Juelg.\par \par \uc2 REESE JUELG (Muralist): It gives people hope. It gives you something to look at.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: For years, she struggled with addiction. In her attempt to get clean, her case manager called her with a rare opportunity.\par \par \uc2 REESE JUELG: That weekend, I got a call saying, do you want to come to this program called Color Me Back. It was a mural arts program that my case manager signed me up for.\par \par \uc2 Knowing every day that I come here, that I`m doing what I want to do when I was pretty hopeless, thinking that my life was pretty much over.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: When Juelg picks up her brush in search of the perfect blend of colors, she becomes fixated on the work. It will be her first painting for Mural Arts Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 What is it like for you to have your own mural?\par \par \uc2 REESE JUELG: It feels like I`m living a dream. I would get a little bit like imposter syndrome about that, feeling like I should have already been doing it and not wasted my life. But the good thing is, I could combine street art, graffiti and murals altogether.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right. And it`s not a waste, because here you are today.\par \par \uc2 REESE JUELG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: New artists get inspiration from the more experienced ones. Juelg watched as Mcilwane put the finishing touches on his latest mural, which shows a little boy eating his positive affirmations.\par \par \uc2 Different paths to mural art connected by a calling to create.\par \par \uc2 ALLOYIUS MCILWANE: I have to get my ideas out, or else I feel anxious. I just need a place to put it.\par \par \uc2 REESE JUELG: I have art. I know it`s not alive per se, but it`s a living thing to me and without it, I don`t think I would be here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Powerful words, without it, she doesn`t think she would be here. Reese Juelg`s first mural is expected to be completed in August. I spoke to her last night. She is still doing well.\par \par \uc2 As for Alloyius Mcilwane, I`ve known him for about 15 years. He`s the brother of a dear friend of mine. Shout out to Karisma (sp?). I also knew he had talent back then, but he was truly and has truly taken things to another level. Just recently, he freestyled this piece in Philadelphia a few days ago. He said it took him about five hours to finish this piece.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What a beautiful story.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Incredible artist.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Ricky Irving, your producer.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony Furlough on the camera lens.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay. Shoutout to Tony Furlough.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony Furlough, did he smile?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He smiled, all right. We love that.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ever wonder who creates the sounds we hear in television shows, films, video games? Well, tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we`ll introduce you to Sanaa Kelley. That`s her job. Her credits include the acclaimed series "Shogun," which won her an Emmy this year.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shogun"/FX Productions)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She showed me how she created the specific sounds for just basic movements needed for some scenes. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SANAA KELLEY (Emmy Award Winning Foley artist): I had to learn to re-walk. So basically to shuffle your feet and not move and for the mic was really difficult. So the way to do it, if you get your knees and put them together, and then you could do little scuffies. And anytime they go like this with their arm, I have to do cloth for it. Anytime they sit, cloth.\par \par \uc2 It was just so detailed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You put as much thought and consideration into how to replicate these sounds as actors do in how to deliver their lines.\par \par \uc2 SANAA KELLEY: Yes, we`re like acting with sound.\par \par \uc2 You could just go --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Sanaa gave us a tour of her studio, which looks a bit like a playground. It`s very cool. I can`t wait to show you guys this piece.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did you even find out about this?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Movie nerd.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: He`s like, I`ve got my sources.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, just a movie nerd and like, I followed her on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and she`s got this very cool \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . You guys will really appreciate her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another way to appreciate movies, Vlad. I love it.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us. Stick around for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250702el720002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For June 4, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti, Imtiaz Tyab, Celine Gounder, Jo Ling Kent,  Jill Schlesinger, Jonah Kaplan, Maurice DuBois, Lonnie Quinn, John  Dickerson \par \uc2 3,757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Casualties of a trade war.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN (Founder and CEO, Munchkin): We now have 80 percent tariffs on a metal soft-bite spoon for babies.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : As the latest tariffs hit businesses and families.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: American consumers and businesses are caught in the middle of a trade war. New Trump tariffs today doubled import taxes on steel and aluminum to 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That will raise costs for businesses who depend on those metals. And it is likely to force them to raise prices on their products.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This is the day the president set to have America`s trading partners submit proposals to avoid still more tariffs next month. He said China`s President Xi is extremely hard to make a deal with. Canada`s prime minister said negotiations are under way, but, if they fail, he is ready to retaliate.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Today, the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  said tariffs will slow the economy. The president believes they will eventually boost U.S. manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Right now, some U.S. manufacturers say they`re being hurt by the tariffs and their customers will feel the pain.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent is in Los Angeles tonight -- Jo Ling.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 When we think about these 50 percent tariffs on steel and aluminum, you often might think of those big-ticket items, when it comes to cars, trucks, refrigerators, washing machines, but what we found in Van Nuys, California is, these steel tariffs are hitting everyday smaller items too.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voice-over): We went to the headquarters of Munchkin, one of the most innovative baby brands in the country, where steel tariffs are impacting their biggest products and their smallest.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN (Founder and CEO, Munchkin): No one thinks about a baby spoon as a steel product.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Steven Dunn is Munchkin`s CEO and founder.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: Tariffs should be strategic. We need a tariff policy that doesn`t have the unintended consequences of putting 80 percent tariffs on a metal spoon.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Dunn says making their popular soft-bite baby spoons will cost 80 percent more because of the 50 percent steel tariffs, plus the existing 30 percent tax on Chinese imports.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: It`s a tariff on parents and it`s a tariff on small and medium businesses throughout the entire country.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Steel and aluminum tariffs are now the highest they have been since the 1930s. And, this week, the global economic group the \uc2 OECD\uc2  downgraded its forecast for U.S. growth, pointing to tariffs and policy uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Tariffs affect almost all of Munchkin`s 500-plus products. The vast majority are made in China. Dunn says his profits have already taken a hit and after tariffs were announced he had to lay off workers for the first time in 35 years. \uc2 Munchkin\uc2  is also discontinuing some of its most popular items.\par \par \uc2 You have stopped ordering these entirely?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: It just will become unaffordable if we pass on the cost of the tariffs. It will make it too expensive. We don`t believe consumers will pay for it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Why can`t you make these kind of products in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: There isn`t the manufacturing base, the tooling equipment, the automation.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: So how do you run a business?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: It`s like being blindfolded, throwing darts at a rotating target.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jo Ling, there`s some new economic data that seems to back up what your reporting is telling you.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: That`s right, John.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to hiring momentum, it slowed down in May, according to the private payroll provider ADP. In fact, small businesses are bearing the brunt. Small businesses lost 13,000 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Jo Ling, what are analysts expecting for the May jobs report? That`s a big number and it comes out on Friday.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Maurice, it`s really all about what this number shows. The market is expecting a slowdown from May compared to April. But since tariffs began, we heard from businesses that it would be 60 to 90 days until we started to see the actual real-world impact on jobs. And we are sitting at that 60-day mark right now.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Jo Ling Kent tonight in Los Angeles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says President Trump`s spending and tax cut package will add nearly $2.5 trillion to the debt over 10 years. The CBO also says nearly 11 million more Americans could lose health insurance.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A U.S.-backed aid group that provides food to Palestinians in Gaza was forced to suspend distribution today.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab in Tel Aviv has more on this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Well, we were able to make contact with the representative for the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation...\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): ... who told us they will not be resuming operations tomorrow. Now, the group had paused aid deliveries today in order to -- quote -- "enhance safety." For three days straight, there were large-scale deadly shootings not far from the U.S.- and Israeli-backed group`s hubs in Southern Gaza, which are staffed by heavily armed American private contractors.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Israeli military said it allowed 157 trucks of aid into Gaza, but our CBS News team on the ground reports, so far, none of it has been distributed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A federal judge today blocked the government from deporting the family of the alleged Boulder, Colorado, firebomber to ensure they get due process.\par \par \uc2 The Egyptian national is accused of injuring 15 people in the attack on a demonstration in support of Israelis held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And a cargo ship is burning off Alaska. The Coast Guard says the ship is carrying thousands of cars and trucks, including 800 electric vehicles. The crew was rescued.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And a man from Washington state is facing federal terrorism charges for allegedly providing chemicals for a car bombing last month at a fertility clinic in Palm Springs, California.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is following this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The bomb that exploded outside the clinic was made with ammonium nitrate, a volatile fertilizer the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says was secured by 32-year-old Daniel Park of Washington state.\par \par \uc2 AKIL DAVIS (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director in Charge): Park was in possession of an explosive recipe that was similar to the Oklahoma City bombing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Park was arrested at New York`s JFK Airport overnight after an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation led to him being detained in Poland. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  discovered he traveled there after the bombing, tracking him down using online chats and travel itineraries.\par \par \uc2 He appeared in federal court in Brooklyn this afternoon, charged with providing material support to terrorists. He waived his right to a detention hearing. Investigators say he connected online with the man he allegedly helped, Guy Bartkus, over their shared nihilistic ideology that life has no meaning.\par \par \uc2 According to an affidavit, in January, Park shipped about 180 pounds of the fertilizer to Bartkus` California home before meeting him.\par \par \uc2 BILL ESSAYLI (U.S. Attorney for the Central District of California): Park spent approximately two weeks visiting Bartkus` residence in Twentynine Palms in late January and early February, running experiments in Bartkus` garage.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says Bartkus then drove the bomb to the Palm Springs clinic, where he carried out what is now being called a suicide bombing.\par \par \uc2 Is there any indication that there were other targets to follow?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT DIRECTOR AKIL DAVIS: No indication that there were other targets, and our analysis is still trying to determine exactly how much explosives were used.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says neither man appeared on their radar before the attack.\par \par \uc2 Park will now be transferred to California to face his charges. If he`s found guilty, he could face up to 30 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The commissioner of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is promising to conduct a review of mifepristone, a medication that can end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dr. Celine Gounder is a CBS News medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, remind us again why mifepristone is the subject of so much attention.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Mifepristone is a medication that is used for medication abortion, which now accounts for over 60 percent of all abortions in the United States.\par \par \uc2 But you have anti-abortion groups that question the FDA`s authority to regulate this, as well as the safety of the drug. Last year, the Supreme Court upheld the availability of mifepristone, but there are still debates about its use and its regulation. And this is despite the fact that the drug was approved some 25 years ago and is also used to manage women undergoing miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You had a look at some of the evidence that the administration cited for this review. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Gosh.\par \par \uc2 Well, the authors are not medical professionals. The study -- well, study - - was not reviewed by experts in the field. This was not published in a scientific publication.\par \par \uc2 And I think also, importantly, they did not reveal the sources of their data or their methodology. And this is important, because Senator -- or -- sorry -- Secretary Kennedy has made a big deal about the importance of being able to reproduce scientific research. And without the data source and the methodology, you can`t really do that.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Real fast, where does this go from here?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Really depends on if the \uc2 FDA\uc2  strictly follows the science and its own extensive safety reviews. We know that this is a drug that is at least as safe as Tylenol.\par \par \uc2 But it depends, science versus politics.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn on the record heat in the Northeast and that spreading smoke from Canada.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also tonight: His daughter was killed in an antisemitic attack in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM (Father of Sarah Milgrim): Usually, a parent tries to mold their child. Sarah molded us.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: I`m Jill Schlesinger in New York. Economic uncertainties are making many Americans feel queasy as they approach retirement.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Two weeks ago tonight, 30-year-old Yaron Lischinsky and 26-year-old Sarah Milgrim, in love and planning to get married, became victims of antisemitic hate. They were gunned down in cold blood in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They had worked together at the Israeli Embassy. The suspect is charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 Milgrim`s parents, Nancy and Bob, talked to Jonah Kaplan of our CBS Minneapolis station WCCO about their loss.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM (Father of Sarah Milgrim): She was the perfect child. She loved people. She loved family. She loved her community. She loved her religion.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN (voice-over): Sarah Milgrim also loved her job. And that`s where her parents, Bob and Nancy, and her brother, Jacob, told us she met and fell in love with Yaron Lischinsky.\par \par \uc2 On May 21, the couple was leaving an event at the Capital Jewish Museum in Washington when a gunman opened fire, killing them both.\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM: Usually, a parent tries to mold their child. Sarah molded us. I`m a different person now than before. This happened from learning, but from learning so much about what Sarah did and her courage.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: What have you learned from her?\par \par \uc2 NANCY MILGRIM (Mother of Sarah Milgrim): She was really strong. Her desire to reach her goals and to see a better future was stronger than any of the hate that was directed towards her.\par \par \uc2 JACOB MILGRIM (Brother of Sarah Milgrim): This attack was in the beating heart of the free world. For it to happen there, it says that it can happen anywhere.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: We`re sitting down now about two weeks since the shootings and in the immediate aftermath of another attack on a group of Jews, this one in Boulder, Colorado. How does it feel to be a Jewish family in America?\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM: Threatened, insecure. Because of what happened to Sarah, I think I have been hardened by it, that I now -- I`m not scared, because she gave the ultimate sacrifice. And I can`t -- I can`t be scared.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: According to the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 , 2024 saw the highest number of antisemitic incidents in 45 years, while a survey from the \uc2 American Jewish Committee\uc2  found, 93 percent of American Jews, they said they believe antisemitism is a problem in America, John and Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So important to share their story.\par \par \uc2 Jonah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you, Jonah.\par \par \uc2 Now to some potentially dangerous weather. It`s headed here in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn is following the record heat and the smoke that is wafting its way down from Canada -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Guys, that`s where I want to start because some of that smoke from the Canadian wildfires has made its way here to New York City. Now, it`s upper-level smoke.\par \par \uc2 So what that means is, take a look at our skyline. That iconic view you see right there, there`s just a little bit of haze out there. It`s not like what we experienced two years ago, where the entire sky turned orange. It smelled like a campfire walking down Broadway. That`s not the case. So where is the worst-quality air right now?\par \par \uc2 It`s in the Ohio Valley, stretching over towards the Great Lakes. And where`s it going to go from this point moving forward? It all depends on how the jet stream flows. As of right now, that jet stream has been flowing south from Manitoba into, say, Northern Minnesota, into the Ohio Valley. Then it does a U-turn and it pushes well up to the north, north of Maine, and it exits.\par \par \uc2 Basically, every day, we will get better as you go through the next two or three days. The temperatures are going to be a story, because I told you that jet stream goes well to the north like up around Maine. If you`re south of that, big temperatures. Manchester hits 95 degrees tomorrow. Springfield is 93 tomorrow. New Bern, North Carolina, with some rain cool there, you`re only 77. So that`s a big change right there.\par \par \uc2 I do want to tell you one thing to put this all in perspective. The worst air quality we have had from this event was in Northern Minnesota, and was between 250 and 300 on the AQI scale. Two years ago, and you guys probably remember this, here in New York City, we had the worst-quality air anywhere on the planet. It was between 400 and 500.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was something else. And this is nothing like that event for us here in the city -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes, it was dark in the middle of the day back then, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Something else.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Let`s hope that doesn`t happen again. Exactly. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Will economic turbulence lead some Americans to make a U- turn on the road to retirement?\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Up-again/down-again stock prices, on-again/off-again tariffs, up again and again prices, it`s enough to give current and future retirees motion sickness.\par \par \uc2 Workers in the first quarter of the year saved a record 14.3 percent of their income for retirement, but the value of the average 401(k) fell by 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, Jill Schlesinger checks back with a couple we met last year as they were looking down the road to retirement.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER (voice-over): Dinner at the Gomez home outside Boston provides a textbook image of the sandwich generation.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ (Planning For Retirement): Here you go.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Three sets of relatives living under one roof.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: The club sandwich has a lot of layers, and we have a lot of layers.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: It`s not the easiest way to save for retirement, as they told us when we first met them last year. Back then, their nest egg was healthy and growing. Stocks were climbing, hitting an all-time high by February of this year, but they cratered with the start of the trade war in April, only to climb back and recover most of the losses.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: I feel like I`m on a roller coaster. You just hope that, if we`re going to be on the downturn now, we would be on the upturn when we...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: ... we decide to retire.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Today, like millions of Americans, Alicia, 57, and Chu, 59, are experiencing the waves of an uncertain, seesawing market.\par \par \uc2 It was about almost a 20 percent slide. Did you feel like you could go through that again?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: No.\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ (Planning For Retirement): I don`t want to go through it again.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: If you`re paying attention to the market, you`re going through it every day.\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI (Labor Economist): Forty-three million people are about that age.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: These gyrations can trigger rash decisions, says labor economist Teresa Ghilarducci of \uc2 The New School for Social Research\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: We have a name for living through that kind of volatility, and it`s called scarring.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: How do I help those folks or prevent that scarring from causing them to act?\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: Act in a way that`s...\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: That`s detrimental.\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: In times of financial crisis, do not talk to your friends or your family about what to do. Take a breath, take a minute and rely on expert advice.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Alicia, who holds down two jobs, had thought maybe she`d cut back work at 62. Chu, who works in logistics, thought 65. Now they have adjusted that mind-set.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: It`s probably going to be 67, at least. I think there`s still a lot of unknowns.\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Would you stop putting money in your retirement accounts? Would you pull back on funding something else?\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: The first place would be pull back on the retirement funding, because we are maxing that out.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Adding to their anxiety, fear that the Social Security system could run dry. There`s been a 13 percent jump this year in people claiming retirement benefits early, despite permanently reduced payouts.\par \par \uc2 Would that then argue that you should claim Social Security early, because...\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: No, not yet.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: No? OK.\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: Not yet. Wait for the maximum benefit. Don`t haircut yourself now, anticipating it will be cut later.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: The Gomezes say their retirement investments are up by about 3 percent this year, so they will simply sit tight and work hard to hold on to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: You can only take so many hits. We don`t have due overtime.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: No.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: In the long run, saving for retirement really is more like riding a roller coaster than a merry-go-round.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Jill Schlesinger in Boston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Some people drink coffee as they read their newspaper. In tomorrow`s Eye on America, you will meet a couple who sell coffee to support a newspaper.\par \par \uc2 Now, have you ever had one of those days? Well, Jim Marshall had one on October 25, 1964. His story is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally tonight, one of the most infamous plays in sports.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It`s 1964. Minnesota Viking Jim Marshall carries the ball triumphantly into the end zone.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But, uh-oh, it is the Vikings end zone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Watch Marshall, number 70, pick up the loose ball.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Marshall had recovered a 49er fumble, got disoriented, and ran 66 yards the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Instead of a Vikings T.D., he scored a safety for San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On the way home, his teammates joked with him that Marshall should fly the plane so it would land in Hawaii, instead of Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But it would be unfair to judge Marshall by one gaffe. The two-time Pro Bowler was an iron man. His 282 straight games as a defensive end set a record.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Marshall twice left the hospital to be in a game and once played after accidentally shooting himself.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And about that game, Marshall said: "Think about making your worst mistake in front of 200 million people and getting teased about it every day of your life."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Jim Marshall died yesterday. He was 87. What a career, four Super Bowls with the famed Purple People Eaters defense.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Man, but that many games in a row, extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 That`s the CBS Evening News. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . The news continues on Evening News Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7. See you there.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you right back here tomorrow. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250605el6400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Deadly Weather Slams the Country; Trump Stumps in Michigan; Trump`s \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Dispute; Ports Prepare for a Slowdown; Economic Uncertainty Impact; New Details in School Crash that Killed Four; Dramatic Testimony in Karen Read Trial; Southwest`s Big Changes; Immigration Overhaul; Cuts to Victims` Grants; Chewing Over the President`s Record; "The Girl Who Was Too Big for the Page;" How to Survive Menopause; A Century of Good Vibes; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Megan Shinn, Jessica  Burch, Ed O`Keefe, Carter Evans, Kelly O`Grady, Ian Lee, Kristina Rex, Kris  Van Cleave, Vladimir Duthiers, Scott MacFarlane, Caitlin Huey-Burns,  Jericka Duncan \par \uc2 14,054 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets for your Wednesdays. We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We are following dangerous storms barreling across the country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll also have Tony`s exclusive border interview with DHS Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Violent storms turn deadly. Multiple tornados carve a path of destruction across several states, and there could be more on the way.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: Another round of storms firing up, not just severe weather, but a lot of rain coming over the same areas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The President claims success in his first hundred days, while polls show he is losing support with everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): We`ve just gotten started. You haven`t even seen anything yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A key prosecution witness gives dramatic testimony in the Karen Read murder retrial.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MCCABE (Witness): She told me she didn`t remember being there, saying could I have hit him? Did I hit him?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: \uc2 Blue Jays\uc2  center fielder, Daulton Varsho turns a blooper into a highlight reel.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: He fell and he caught that ball.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Police in Sarasota, Florida were racing to this unmanned boat, circling at 40 miles per hour after the driver fell overboard.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Then made their James Bond move as one officer furiously just jumped over into the speeding vessel.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Claps)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nice job. Good job.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: This wasn`t the Australian outback. This was Alabama.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: This runaway kangaroo named Sheila back home now, after she shut down an Interstate.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: No doubt, by tonight, has her own \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: If you on your Bingo card for 2025. Kangaroo down I-85 in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the music they chose to play with the kangaroo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love all of the traffic in the other lane. You know, people were like, what`s going on up there? Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re in Alabama. Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sheila said, I`m gone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve got stuff to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we`re going to get to the show.\par \par \uc2 We have to begin with deadly weather in several states, from the Plains to the Northeast, at least two people were killed near Pittsburgh, which was hit by some of the strongest winds ever recorded there. Nearly half a million people don`t have power.\par \par \uc2 Farther south, flash flooding is causing trouble from North Texas to Southwest Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Megan Shinn from CBS News Pittsburgh is in Penn Hills, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Megan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: Yes, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 You can see the damage here just outside of Pittsburgh in Penn Hills is extreme. This is an example of it. This tree toppled over, uprooted all the way over, across the road, blocking it on top of a car, as well as the wires. And it`s widespread damage across the Pittsburgh area caused by intense winds, all part of a system that slammed a dozen states yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: A chaotic week of severe weather continued Tuesday, spawning damaging winds, tornados and flash floods from New York to Oklahoma. Near Pittsburgh, large trees fell onto homes after wind gusts up to 80 miles an hour ripped across parts of Western Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN RANDAL (Allegheny County, Pennsylvania resident): We both ran in and we ran to the basement like right away. I didn`t want to take any chances.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: In nearby Beach View, the destructive storm swept the roofs off multiple stores and sent wooden beams, insulation material and other debris flying into the middle of the street.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I just can`t believe this. I mean, I was driving around and there`s trees down everywhere, and as soon as I pulled up, I couldn`t believe what I could see right now.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: Fallen power lines took out electricity to hundreds of thousands of customers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Dave was the humblest guy. He was such a kind man, 59 years old, unbelievable painter.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: Pittsburgh`s Public Safety Department says one person died after he was electrocuted while trying to move a live fallen wire with a stick after it fell onto his truck.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Is that rotation up there?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: In Southern Missouri, a tornado struck just outside Springfield with winds up to 85 miles an hour that destroyed roofs and crushed buildings.\par \par \uc2 South of St Louis, Potosi dealt with a one-two punch of severe winds and heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, man, I`ve never seen it like this.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: The torrential rain also fell in parts of North Texas where pounding waters spilled onto streets.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN SHINN: And back here in Pittsburgh, while no tornadoes were reported, the severe winds, according to the National Weather Service were so strong they were actually comparable to tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 Nate, sending it back to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. How about that?\par \par \uc2 Megan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on where the system is headed, let`s go to meteorologist Jessica Burch from CBS News Bay Area.\par \par \uc2 Jess, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA BURCH: Good morning to you, too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Let`s just dive straight into it. Over a hundred million people today alone are gearing up for flash flooding risk ranging from Dallas, Texas over into St. Louis, Missouri, across the Ohio Valley as we head into the evening hours tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now keep in mind, just within the past 48 hour hours, it was areas like Texas that saw up to around a hundred mile per hour wind gusts reported. On top of that, hail, about five inch in size, swept throughout some parts of Northern Texas, and we`re not done just yet. That was storm number one.\par \par \uc2 Here is what we`re expecting for storm number two as it continues to impact millions from Ohio across the Central Mississippi Valley and up into the Ohio Valley today, lasting into our forecasted hours tomorrow, we could see heavy amounts of rainfall, flash flooding is possible. Our Severe Storm Risk today alone stretches all the way up into the Northeast, too.\par \par \uc2 We continue to keep a close eye on those rainfall totals, anywhere around three to six inches expected today. Heavy rain and winds will continue as we head into the evening hours tonight, on top of tornado activity, too.\par \par \uc2 Back to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you. Jess.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Trump marked the 100th day of his second term with a campaign style rally in Michigan last night. He used most of the speech to attack his opponents and then boast about his actions in office so far, even as the polls show that he is losing a lot of support.\par \par \uc2 He also had a very public dispute yesterday with one of the tech titans who stood behind him at his inauguration.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe covered the rally for us. He`s in Detroit with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The President back out on the campaign trail shouting familiar themes seems suggesting that his term so far has been a success, despite the fact that his approval rating is on the slide and some tough economic reality.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Hello, Michigan. Hello.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: A hundred days in, President Trump back in campaign mode, rallying his supporters in a battleground state.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: In 100 days, we have delivered the most profound change in Washington in nearly 100 years.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Recent CBS News polling shows immigration remains among the issues the president scores best on, so it`s no surprise he talked it up.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You finally have a President who is defending our borders and our nation.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But in a striking moment, the American President used propaganda video shot by the El Salvadoran government to show how he has deported undocumented immigrants to that Central American nation.\par \par \uc2 The President`s milestone day came as consumer confidence plunged again in April amid concerns about his planned tariffs, but Trump came to Michigan with relief for automakers, they`ll still face a 25 percent tariff on imported cars and parts, but not an additional tariff on other critical materials, including steel and aluminum.\par \par \uc2 And in an interview, he defended his decision to impose 145 percent tariffs on China.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They were making from us a trillion dollars a year. They were ripping us off like nobody has ever ripped us off.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President argues his policies will end up benefiting auto workers like Scott Duty and Tabitha Noble, who were furloughed just before Trump took office.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they`re feeling the pinch.\par \par \uc2 What does that then cause you to have to worry about in terms of costs on a day-to-day basis?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DUTY (Former auto worker): Well, just daily living and you know, your food -- your food went up, gas went up, our wages went down.\par \par \uc2 TABITHA NOBLE (Former auto worker): Utility bills have went up, groceries have went sky high.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Both agree tariffs could eventually bring more jobs to the U.S., but there`s currently uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 The state of the economy right now, in your view, here in Michigan is -- TABITHA NOBLE: Not very good. Unemployment went up. Every day, somebody is closing their doors because nobody is going in to buy anything, they can`t afford to stay open.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Even with Consumer Confidence levels dropping to its lowest level in over five years since the beginning of the pandemic, the President pushed back in that interview over the idea that the economy is in tough shape. He admitted there could be a transition period, would be a transition period, but said everything is, "headed in the right direction" -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ed, while we have you, what more can you tell us about this White House disagreement with \uc2 Amazon\uc2 ? It led to a call, apparently, from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, it all started with a report that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  was going to start publishing on its site the cost of things and what exactly was costing you because of the tariffs. And that, of course, caused the White House to get quite upset. They called it a hostile and political act.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  later claimed this was only an idea for its \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Haul site, for the lower cost items, and that it was never actually implemented.\par \par \uc2 Before coming here to Michigan, the President said he gave \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  a call, and that they were able to quickly sort out the issue. Critics suggest that this is yet another example of corporate leaders bending to the President`s will -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Ed, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a question, could the President`s tariffs soon lead to empty store shelves as company cut imports from China? Made in China is on a lot of things. Well, we are already seeing a slowdown at America`s ports, and container shipment bookings are down nearly 50 percent from a year ago. A lot of stuff comes on those boats.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans checked out one crucial part of America`s import system.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2  is the busiest in the Western Hemisphere, but now it is preparing for a dramatic slowdown, according to executive director \uc2 Gene Seroka\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do you anticipate happening over the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA (\uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2  general manager): In two weeks` time, Carter, we will see a drop on our import side of the business, by about a third. It could be as much as 40 percent the week to follow.\par \par \uc2 Most American importers are saying that they`re stopping shipments out of China because they simply weren`t going to pay two-and-a-half times the cost of the goods.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: With a 145 percent tariff, a container of Chinese goods worth $100,000.00 will now cost importers an extra $145,000.00.\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA: With information coming out of Washington, seemingly by the hour, it`s tough to get a line of sight on what`s next.\par \par \uc2 Inventory on hand right now in the United States will last for about six to eight weeks. After that, shelves will be there, we won`t be able to buy as much online.\par \par \uc2 MARYAM AL-HAMMAMI (The Game Chest owner): All of these games come from China.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: At The Game Chest, owner, Maryam Al-Hammami has been stockpiling toys and games that arrived before the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 MARYAM AL-HAMMAMI: So I`m buying what I think I will sell between now and September.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: You sound like a hoarder.\par \par \uc2 MARYAM AL-HAMMAMI: I feel like a hoarder. If you see my stores, you would see the hoarding in the back rooms.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Eighty percent of her merchandise comes from China, and board games are her biggest sellers.\par \par \uc2 It seems like you should be able to make a board game in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 MARYAM AL-HAMMAMI: It`s true. It does look easy, but it`s filled with lots and lots of different parts and pieces, and there`s just not enough manufacturing here to be able to do that successfully.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: At this point, even if the tariffs are reduced significantly --\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA: It`ll still take about 10 days to two weeks to get these ships back in line over in China, then it`s a two-week trip here to L.A. so we`re starting to cut it pretty close with respect to inventory in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So we might see some empty shelves even if the tariffs are brought down in the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA: We`re running out of time on that, and if we have fewer items to select from, that`s going to put a pinch on the American buyer.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That doesn`t sound good. All this uncertainty may already have an effect on how you`re spending your money.\par \par \uc2 "Money Watch" correspondent Kelly O`Grady is following all that, lucky for us, and she`s going to help.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, Kelly, help sort it all out for us.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I`ll try.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how bad is the economic uncertainty? How -- that didn`t sound so great. And how do you think it`s going to impact our spending?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So you heard in that piece --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: -- Consumer Confidence, the numbers that we`re seeing in terms of how people feel about the future of the economy and their finances, lowest level since 2011 and there seems to be particular concern about the labor market and employment. The worst numbers we have seen since 2009, the Great Recession.\par \par \uc2 And so the fear is that all of this could impact spending, right? And two- thirds of our economy is fueled by the consumer.\par \par \uc2 Now we haven`t seen that yet, and I was actually looking at Visa earnings last night, and they say, hey, we`re still seeing a resilient consumer. But remember, we know people tried to get ahead of the tariff deadline, tried to buy --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: -- and so we could see that change soon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I remember that.\par \par \uc2 We just heard in Carter`s piece that store shelves could be empty in the coming weeks. What kind of items do you think will be on short supply?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So we know that some places stockpiled, so it is going to depend on the company that you`re shopping at. But one is apparel. So think about some of the cheaper clothing and shoes that might come from China. Another is back to school shopping.\par \par \uc2 You actually have companies ordering that stuff now. So think notebooks, writing devices, and if you can believe it, even some holiday presents, like toys you could see impacted on the shelves. So I`ve got two pieces of advice for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: One, don`t panic buy. Okay, we remember what happened during COVID, and all of those empty shelves, panic buying the toilet paper.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was panicky, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, okay, all right, so don`t panic this time, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The second is, if you want to get a head start on your shopping, know your budget, know what price you`re expecting to see for that item, and know what you`re willing to accept and don`t stray from that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The President tells one story about the numbers, and then the numbers tell a different story. If I was a consumer, who do you believe? It all seems very confusing.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, well, it is showing what the economy is right now versus what may be coming. That`s the disconnect.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Valuable information. Kelly, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, we are learning more about the victims of that horrific accident in Central Illinois when a car plowed through a school building. Vigils were held yesterday to honor the four girls who were killed.\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning more details about the woman behind the wheel. Ian Lee has the latest from Chatham, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Tonight, we gather in the shadow of grief, but we do not gather alone.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: In this tight-knit community, many here in Chatham are turning to each other as they mourn the loss of four young lives they say were taken away too soon.\par \par \uc2 MARK LEWIS (Rylee Britton`s soccer coach): She had her whole life ahead of her, and she was just -- just a fantastic kid.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Mark Lewis was 18-year-old Rylee Britton`s soccer coach for five years. He said she had dreams of becoming a teacher, which is why she participated at the Why Not After School Youth Camp, but that dream was shattered when a car barreled through the building on Monday, killing her and three young girls, including seven-year-olds Alma Buhnerkempe and Kathryn Corley and eight-year-old Ainsley Johnson.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Somebody drove through the daycare.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Those poor kids.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Police identified the driver as 44-year-old, Marianne Akers, a former food service employee at Chatham Elementary School.\par \par \uc2 Illinois State Police say they don`t believe the crash was intentional, but want to know why her car left a road, crossed the field and plowed through the building.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR GODWIN (Witness): My wife and I both heard several loud bangs. We had seen a car had hit, and so I called the police immediately, thinking that a driver had gotten to an accident.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Neighbor, Taylor Godwin, who witnessed the crash, says this center is always packed with children.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR GODWIN: You never imagine that can happen in your community. It`s a terrible situation to see anybody who has been killed, especially a child.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Here at the crash site, there is a growing memorial. You can feel the sense of community as they unite behind the words "Chatham Strong," city leaders have ordered flags to be flown at half-staff, and an ice cream parlor yesterday offered free scoops to bring some cheer during this troubling time.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the driver has not been charged and her toxicology results are pending -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So many questions to that heartbreaking story.\par \par \uc2 Ian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Another reminder about the fragility of life. Scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, as you know, Tony went to the border for an exclusive interview with Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 . Hear what she had to say about those mistaken deportations.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a key witness takes a stand in the Karen Read trial, what she says about a frantic phone call on the night that Read`s boyfriend was found dead.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, a police chase in Florida trying to catch a fast moving boat with no one behind the wheel. We`ll show you the dramatic bodycam.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" see what led to this moment when an \uc2 NBA\uc2  superstar confronted another player`s dad.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Fresh Pet. It`s not dog food, it`s food, food.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More dramatic testimony at the retrial of Karen Read. She is accused of hitting her police officer boyfriend with her car then leaving him to die.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, a key witness described what Read told her the morning he was found.\par \par \uc2 Kristina Rex of CBS News Boston was in the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MCCABE: Hi, I`m Jennifer McCabe.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA REX: Key witness, Jen McCabe told a Massachusetts jury Tuesday about a frantic phone call from Karen Read in the early hours of January 29th screaming that her boyfriend John O`Keefe was missing.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MCCABE: Jen, Jen -- and then she is screaming that John didn`t come home.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA REX: The night before, McCabe testified, she invited O`Keefe and Read to a party at her sister`s Canton, Massachusetts home. She said she saw Read`s car outside the house.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Did they ever make it in the house?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MCCABE: No, they did not.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA REX: McCabe said the next morning, Read did not remember being at the house and was concerned about a cracked tail light.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MCCABE: She told me she didn`t remember being there. She started saying, could I have hit him? Did I hit him?\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA REX: The defense argues that O`Keefe did enter the home that night and was beaten to death by another law enforcement officer before being left outside overnight in the snow.\par \par \uc2 McCabe was driving with Read when she discovered O`Keefe on the 29th.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MCCABE: And all of a sudden, Karen is screaming from the back, there he is or something -- something to the effect of there he is, let me out, just for like -- just him, just like frozen.\par \par \uc2 McCabe`s calm demeanor Tuesday was in stark contrast to her testimony in Read`s first trial, which ended in a mistrial.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: In the first trial, you had a combative witness in Jennifer McCabe. You had an angry person who did not have the same aura of credibility that the new Jennifer McCabe has.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA REX: A digital forensics expert said O`Keefe location data placed him near a flag pole outside the home shortly after midnight, but under cross examination Tuesday, testified that the phone could have been within a large radius, including inside the home.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Kristina Rex, Dedham Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, major airlines makes a big change, and it`s going to cost you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Look at this scene in the water off Sarasota, Florida. Police pursuing a runaway boat with no one at the helm. Officers responded to calls of a boater in distress on Monday. Here`s what happened. The driver had fallen out of the boat, which was going nearly 40 miles per hour. It ended with a Police Lieutenant, Bruce King making a daring jump and brought the other boat to a stop. No one was seriously hurt, but the story of Bruce King is stuff of legends and will be told forever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Nate, where`s the boater? The boater that fell out of the boat? What happened to him?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- the boater is okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, no. What happened to the boater?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They saved the boater and the boat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Good, good, good. That`s good to know.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re going to take a look at big changes coming to \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 . For decades, this carrier prided itself on its unique customer service, you could say. But soon, Southwest is going to look a whole lot more like the other airlines we`re used to.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is at San Diego International Airport with what you need to know for your summer travel.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The want to get away \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  that had open boarding and free bags, that is changing very quickly, but let`s start with some good news. As soon as tomorrow, Southwest will start slowly rolling out its new cabin, which means extra leg room. Seats will be available to you for free, at least for now. That, of course, will change, but you`ll start seeing them as soon as tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Later this year, Southwest will start selling tickets for those seats with extra legroom, those choice extra fares will come with early boarding and free checked bags, but they will cost more than other seats. There will also be a preferred section, which is standard seating, but closer to the front of the airplane. That will also come with general boarding.\par \par \uc2 Standard or choice seats will be located closer to the back of the plane. Then we`ve got, of course, another big change to tell you about, starting in late May, just after Memorial Day, most Southwest passengers will have to pay to check a bag. That`s a huge change.\par \par \uc2 The airline says frequent flyers, credit card holders, and certain higher fare levels will still get that perk for free. The airline also plans to launch its most restrictive ticket ever, a basic fare. Why is Southwest making these changes? They`ve lagged behind some of their competitors, who already offer these choices to customers, and they`ve also had an activist investor takes seats on the board, pushing the airline to be more profitable at the same time as demand is starting to slow down -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My mom is home right now. I`m sure taking notes on all of this. Kris, she`s got status on Southwest, a lot of miles, whatever, you name it. She`s following it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kris, it`s a lot to remember. Also, you should tell people about that REAL I.D. I was at the airport yesterday. They are already looking for the REAL I.D. and the lines are very, very long already.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s true. It`s true. Got to get on it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Kris, thanks again.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our exclusive conversation with Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  at the U.S.-Mexico border. Noem talks about a whole number of things, including the deportation of an undocumented immigrant mom who brought her two-year-old child with her. What`s the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Trump won back the White House promising tighter border security and a crackdown on illegal immigration, and those things have happened.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol apprehended nearly 138,000 people at the southern border illegally in March of last year, and that number fell to 7,000 just a year later, it`s a decrease of about 95 percent in 12 months. Deportation numbers, though, have been slower to change.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, we went to the border and spoke with Homeland Security Secretary, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  about the administration`s goals for immigration control and its limits.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In a convoy of all-terrain vehicles, we travel through the El Paso sector of the southern border, shadowed by Mexican forces across the Rio Grande and seeing little more than sunshine and dust in a region where federal data shows more than 55,000 people cross the border illegally in just one month under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Up ahead of us, on an ATV of her own is Secretary of Homeland Security, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , who has made a point of getting out into the field like this, but what you may dismiss as a photo op, she says, is part of a strategy.\par \par \uc2 So all of these dramatic pictures of raids, of CECOT, of that prison in El Salvador, the message there is self-deport now --\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM (U.S. Homeland Security Secretary): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- or this could be you.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: Absolutely. That is absolutely the message, it is that you have the option to go home on your own terms. We`ll help you with a plane ticket. We`ll get you booked. We`ll facilitate it, and you get the chance to come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That process though to cross again legally into the US is often a long and tricky one.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: How far back do they go?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And a few miles ahead at what is the only legal border crossing in El Paso, we watch as the Secretary is shown images of chaos in years past.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from El Salvador Presidency Press Office)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But when it comes to removing the millions of undocumented immigrants who have already crossed into the U.S. or overstayed their visas here, the Trump administration has faced challenges.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: We`ve been doing this correctly. We`ve been building cases. We`ve been going after the worst of the worst and doing it the right way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A number of federal judges aren`t so sure, including most recently, in the case of an undocumented mother deported to Honduras, Friday, with her two-year-old, a U.S. citizen along with her, and before the court could clarify the child`s interests. The result wrote a Trump appointed federal judge is the "... strong suspicion that the government just deported a U.S. citizen with no meaningful process."\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: I fundamentally disagree with that. I`m sure that these judges will continue to challenge every single thing that this administration does. We have several activist judges across the country that have made claims such as this, but that mother made a choice for her child and wanted to keep her child with her, so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, if a relative here in the U.S. wants that child back, and the mother agrees, the child will come back.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: Yes, absolutely, and that`s the process. You know, this mother gave us documentation and fully said she wanted her child with her, and we honored that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is harshness part of the message when it comes to the removal of mothers who have American children?\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: No, I think that with that, with families, we recognize that families can stay together, and so these mothers get the option to take their children with them, which I think is absolutely where President Trump`s heart is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Should those non-American mothers have that option, or should the American children have a right to stay here?\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: I think the mothers should have the option to have their children with them, and then if those mothers do leave and choose to register and self-deport, them and their children can come back, or if they want to have their child cared for by somebody else in the United States, that`s an option that they can pursue, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On a training range at Fort Bliss, meanwhile, we watch as the Border Patrol`s Special Ops group BORTAC demonstrates its work for the Secretary.\par \par \uc2 Nobody wants would-be terrorists or gang members in the country or violent criminals of any kind.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nobody -- nobody forgets or discounts the pain of families who have lost loved ones to those people.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How do they know that those people on the flights in the chains are actually terrorists and gang members?\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: We have spent hours and hours building cases against them. We have the victims that speak up that they have perpetuated crimes against.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But without a trial in any traditional sense, without that due process, how can voters be sure that the people going away are, in fact, as bad as you say?\par \par \uc2 Are you saying that your administration has never and will never make an error in determining who should be deported?\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: Well, that`s a big statement.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course, it is. Of course.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: But obviously, we`re relying on the expertise of our investigators, our teams, double checking, triple checking, going through the paperwork, making sure that we`ve done everything absolutely correctly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One area where the government has already admitted an error is in the case of Kilmar Abrego Garcia sent to a prison in El Salvador, the one country a court said he shouldn`t go.\par \par \uc2 His family denies the government`s allegation that he`s a gang member, and the Supreme Court has said that the administration needs to facilitate his release in El Salvador and handle the case properly here.\par \par \uc2 What have you done, Madam Secretary, to help facilitate his return to the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: You know, this is the President`s decision and the White House`s decision, so we follow his direction and his legal scholars and attorneys. And you know, he is not under our control.\par \par \uc2 He is an El Salvador citizen. He is home there in his country. If he were to be brought back to the United States of America, we would immediately deport him again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You would fight that underlying, do not deport ruling, which is at the heart of all this.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: I would fight the underlying do not deport?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, there was an earlier ruling in this case.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: Yes. Well --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That said he had a credible fear.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: and I understand that you think that no longer holds, but this gets into where people are, like --\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- wait a minute. Are they following the courts, or are they not? Are they defying the courts, or are they listening to the court?\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM: No. President Trump and his administration has adhered to the court and respects the court and its decisions. This individual is not under the United States of America`s jurisdiction, and he is not one of our citizens. He is home in his home country, and that`s up to that country to decide what to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you can hear what`s tough to discern, because on the one hand, she says it`s President Trump`s decision what to do with Garcia, but on the other hand, she`s saying that they`re listening to the courts, but those statements can`t both be true --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because there`s some confliction there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re in conflict. Yes, the courts are asking the Trump administration to do some things that they`ve yet to do or yet to talk about doing.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, all of this tough talk made me wonder, actually, about whether there was any softer side to the Secretary and to the administration. I did ask about the role of heart in all of this, and it was interesting what she said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she said what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She said, "There`s heart in all of our immigration conversation. It`s about families and it`s about people," I followed up by wondering, are we still a welcoming country for legal immigration? And her message was yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 The idea that the administration has is they have to get through this tough period now, clean up what they see as a big mess from years gone by, and then the door will open again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, your mom used to always tell you, actions speak louder than words, and their actions are scaring and off putting to a lot of people. In the meantime, Mr. Garcia is still there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there is still no evidence that he is a gang member that they have shown to us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not that we`ve seen publicly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not that we`ve seen publicly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thank the Secretary for bringing us down there. It was an interesting conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she was very candid with you. I will say that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She answered everything you asked her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, the stories that you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:46, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up for you in our eight o`clock hour.\par \par \uc2 Oscar winner, Geena Davis is coming in today. She`s got her first children`s book. She`s a writer and she`s the illustrator -- that`s different -- and it was inspired by her own childhood.\par \par \uc2 Plus, comedian, Samantha Bee, her one-woman show is all about the absurdity and, dare we say, the hilarity of menopause. Hilarity?\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour. But first, Klaus and I were just talking about menopause, it is so funny. We`re heading on over to the table. Don`t we think --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "What to Watch" is so bright this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad is here getting ready for "What to Watch.".\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got to wear shades.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is time for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: An unbroken --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wait, wait. Don`t start. Don`t start. Beep-beep-beep-beep.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All rise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gang is all here. Gang is all here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Samantha Bee just got here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on in here, Sunshine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just went to say hello.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s go, Vlad. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let me get going because we`ve got a very limited time here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are going to start with a long overdue honor.\par \par \uc2 Eighty years ago, nearly 900 members of the only Black all-female Army battalion to serve in World War Two were deployed to England, and unit was known as the Six Triple Eight.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 National Archives and Records Administration\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Its job was to clear a backlog of 17 million undelivered letters and packages critical to the morale of American troops, and they did the job brilliantly.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday at the U.S. Capitol, the unit, was honored with Congressional Gold Medal. Members from both parties, led by House Speaker Mike Johnson, presented the award to descendants of Lieutenant Colonel Charity Adams, who led the battalion.\par \par \uc2 The only reaction I have is that was Lizzo song about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Damn time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And if you haven`t seen Tyler Perry`s "Six Triple Eight" on Netflix.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Go check it out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now is a good time to see it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You should go check that film out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, last story, while seen at the \uc2 NBA\uc2  last night, emotions running high after the \uc2 Pacers\uc2  knocked the Bucks out of the playoffs. The \uc2 Pacers\uc2 ` Tyrese Haliburton hit the game winning layup with just a second left as Indiana began to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at Haliburton`s Dad storming the court. He stood right in front of Bucks` star Giannis Antetokounmpo seemingly taunting him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh no.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Shortly after Giannis got face-to-face with Haliburton`s dad and the two exchanged, it would appear to be some heated words, Haliburton`s father later apologized on social media.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The father was out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 All right, stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Getting fired up, Dad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Samantha Bee is here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Got get in Giannis` face.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS) **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Samantha Bee is here with her outrageous and courageous new one-woman show. It`s about something not enough people talk about. It`s called "How to Survive Menopause."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Our "Three Meals" series returns 100 days after President Trump took office, we head to Georgia and North Carolina to find out how voters are feeling now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Oscar-winning actor, Geena Davis is in the building. She`s got a new kid`s book, so I guess she`s now an Oscar-winning author as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He said it. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Deadly storms strike millions across the country, knocking out power and leaving a path of destruction.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I just can`t believe this. I mean, I was driving around and there`s trees down everywhere. As soon as I pulled up, I couldn`t believe what I could see right now.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Hello, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Trump celebrates 100 days in office with a campaign style rally as polls show public support is slipping.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: State of the economy right now, in your view, here in Michigan, is --\par \par \uc2 TABITHA NOBLES: Not very good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A key witness testifies in the Karen Read retrial, recalling a frantic phone call with Read before her boyfriend was found dead.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MCCABE: She is screaming that John didn`t come home. She left him at the waterfall. She was hysterical. I was very high to follow what she was saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the world`s tallest and shortest dog meetup for the very first time. It`s cute. Let`s just call it puppy love.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You can see through the photos and the videos, the love we have, you know, for our pets. They are part of the family, and they are incredible. You know, they are -- they are really amazing, truly amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is a very cute picture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a cute picture, really.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you just say, Nate?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What did you recall --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh that I feel around these two, two of my favorite people in the world, Patty and Gisele, tiny things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just have to appreciate --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The tiny twins over there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, the tiny two. They are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We love the crew around here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not even taller than the cameras.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour with news that has many people concerned all around the country.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is taking away federal dollars from hundreds of community programs that can play a big role in public safety.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane obtained a list of these Justice Department cutbacks, and he joins us now to talk about the potential impact.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you. What did you find out?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Many of the nonprofit groups with whom we spoke were shocked by this news. They run programs to combat hate crimes, school violence and opioid abuse, but the Department of Justice is standing by the cuts.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The funding cuts are far and wide, from a neighborhood crime fighting program in San Francisco --\par \par \uc2 MAN: I once took lives, now, I save lives.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: To an opioid abuse project in North Carolina and a hate crime reduction initiative in Louisiana named for Emmett Till.\par \par \uc2 Abrianna Morales, who says she survived a sex assault as a teenager now works for the National Organization for Victims Advocacy.\par \par \uc2 ABRIANNA MORALES (National Organization for Victims Advocacy): It sends a really painful message that this doesn`t matter, that their experiences don`t matter, that the needs that they have, the connection that they deserve, the healing that they might be pursuing is deemed wasteful.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: CBS News has obtained the notification the groups received, saying their programs, including for rural crimes, senior citizens, and trafficking victims no longer effectuate Department of Justice priorities.\par \par \uc2 At least 360 federal grants have been halted touching all corners of America from Sioux City, South Dakota to Boston to Baltimore to Tallahassee.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department told CBS News, it is confident these cuts are consistent with the administration`s priorities, while at the same time protecting services that tangibly impact victims.\par \par \uc2 Were you surprised when you got this notice?\par \par \uc2 RENEE WILLIAMS (\uc2 National Center for Victims of Crime\uc2  executive director): Surprise doesn`t begin to describe it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Renee Williams, who runs the \uc2 National Center for Victims of Crime\uc2 , feared the freeze on her group`s $2 million grant would shutter their National Crime Victims hotline, which receives 16,000 calls a year and will squeeze similar groups nationwide.\par \par \uc2 RENEE WILLIAMS: From what we are hearing from many of our partners in the field, it means the shutting down of these programs completely, and in some cases, the shutting down of entire organizations.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Department of Justice says the groups have about a month to appeal to get some of their funds restored. So far, only two groups have gotten their money back, including that crime victims hotline.\par \par \uc2 But Gayle, a New York based group that helps provide sign language interpreters for some crime victims, they`ve already begun layoffs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s tough to hear. Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ll hear from voters in a special edition of our "Three Meals" series. It is focusing on President Trump`s first 100 days.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Geena Davis is here. She`s just arrived. Guess what? She`s brought her new children`s book that she hopes will help kids feel more comfortable in their own bodies. That`s always a good thing. But first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead, we`ll talk to the always funny Sam Bee about her latest one-woman show full of tips and tricks for surviving menopause.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To mark 100 days of the Trump administration, we are bringing back our series, "Three Meals" where we hit the road and have breakfast, lunch and dinner with voters to find out what`s on their minds.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what the polls say: The latest CBS News polling finds 45 percent of Americans approve of the job President Trump is doing, and 55 percent do not. When it comes to his handling of the economy, one of the top issues, of course, 42 percent approve, 58 percent disapprove.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns went to two battleground states, North Carolina and Georgia, to hear from voters, from everyday people on the streets, about what they think the first 100 days has done for them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: We began our day at Pressley Park Restaurant in Charlotte, North Carolina, a mom and pop institution that`s been serving up the classics for more than 60 years.\par \par \uc2 Harvey Cassells is a regular here, starting every morning with a little prayer.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY CASSELLS (Charlotte, North Carolina resident): I just want to say thank you for this wonderful day --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And a side of grits.\par \par \uc2 What`s a word that you would use to describe these first hundred days?\par \par \uc2 HARVEY CASSELLS: Unchartered is a word that I`ve been using.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Castles voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in November, but says the back and forth over tariffs has him concerned.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY CASTLES: Are we going about it the right way? Not sure that we know yet whether we are. You know, sometimes courage and stupidity can be a thin line.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Jimmy Greene says the economy was top of mind when he voted for Trump.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY GREENE (Charlotte, North Carolina resident): I think we -- the country, is already trying to save money in some ways with, you know, some of the things that DOGE is doing, I would like to see us be able to give a little bit back to the American people.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Do you look at the stock market much?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY GREENE: I just think we kind of have to be a little patient. I let my 401 (k) do what it`s going to do.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Our next stop, Habersham County, which went overwhelmingly for Trump in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The number one industry in this county is agriculture -- chicken, pork, peaches. And here in this small town of Cornelia, apples.\par \par \uc2 Fenders Diners` claim to fame a hot, melty smash burger.\par \par \uc2 But for the Dodd family --\par \par \uc2 AVERY DODD (Habersham County resident): That`s plenty of syrup.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It says pancakes for dinner kind of night.\par \par \uc2 Avery Dodd, who voted for Trump, says it`s hard to keep up with the pace of the news.\par \par \uc2 AVERY DODD: A lot of whiplash, I guess, fast, not super thought out decisions from an outsider`s perspective.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Candace Kraft (sp?), an Independent who didn`t vote for Trump, says she can`t look away.\par \par \uc2 CANDACE KRAFT (Habersham County resident): Well, I`m watching the stock market, and, you know, it`s bouncing like a trampoline. It worries me, you know? I mean, they`re talking about all these tariffs going in place and how much it`s going to cost us.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: One table over --\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE DEZOTELL (Habersham County): He`s just -- I think he`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Neighbors, Catherine Dezotell and Ron Dundore (sp?) now bond over their shared love for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Ron didn`t vote for him in the last election, but now he wishes he had.\par \par \uc2 RON DUNDORE (Habersham County): I`ve been very pleasantly surprised with what he`s doing for the country. I think he`s got a really good Cabinet in here this time.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE DEZOTELL: being from California and Arizona. To I dealt with the immigration crisis and saw how it affected our daily lives. I believe he`s a man of his word, and I really like that because I had given up on politics.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Next up on our road trip is Peach Tree City, Georgia, just about 20 miles south of Atlanta in Fayette County. It`s a political battleground Trump won with just 51 percent of the vote, but The Curious Pig has something for just about everyone.\par \par \uc2 This upscale barbecue joint was filled to the brim on a weekday afternoon, but diners told us off camera, they were hesitant to talk to us, because in a town divided, no one wants to upset their neighbors, but like every great pit master knows, patience pays off.\par \par \uc2 LISA JIMENEZ (Fayette County, Georgia resident): Rome wasn`t built in the day, and this is not going to be fixed overnight.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: We found Trump voter, Lisa Jimenez catching up with a friend over lunch. She told us good policy requires patience, too.\par \par \uc2 LISA JIMENEZ: People are starting now to say, okay, we`re going in the right direction. If these jobs are not here, what happens when everything that we have is outsourced? And that is exactly what has been going on for decades. He`s looking to bring it back.\par \par \uc2 If President Trump is watching us, I love you dearly.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: On the other side of the restaurant, a different feeling.\par \par \uc2 MARCIA HARVEY (Fayette County, Georgia resident): It`s been a long hundred days.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Marcia Harvey is a waitress at The Curious Pig. She`s a former paralegal who voted for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MARCIA HARVEY: Every day, it seems like you`re waking up to something new, and it`s hard to feel safe.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Cornelius Blanding works with farmers across Georgia in conjunction with the Department of Agriculture, which is facing restructuring under Trump.\par \par \uc2 How are things feeling for you?\par \par \uc2 CORNELIUS BLANDING (Fayette County, Georgia): The one word I would use is uncertain. What affects farmers impacts all of us. We`re all consumers, we`re all eaters, and we eat what farmers grow and what farmers produce.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Have you guys seen a big impact in terms of tariffs right now?\par \par \uc2 CORNELIUS BLANDING: I`m worried about how much is grocery going to cost, how much are clothes going to cost, how much everything is going to cost based on these tariffs.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Blanding didn`t want to tell us who he voted for, and he says it doesn`t matter. He`s just rooting for the country.\par \par \uc2 CORNELIUS BLANDING: I hope for the opportunity where we all can talk and figure out how do we move in the same direction?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, maybe over a beer.\par \par \uc2 CORNELIUS BLANDING: Definitely over a beer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, the new children`s book from Oscar winner, Geena Davis. She`ll talk to us about creating a positive and inspiring message for kids, and she is sitting in our greenroom.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS) (Clip from "Thelma & Louise"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Iconic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Iconic is right.\par \par \uc2 Geena Davis made herself a legend in the ground breaking movie "Thelma & Louise" and she has been stealing scenes and our hearts ever since.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Oscar winner has found a new calling as a children`s book writer and illustrator. "The Girl Who Was Too Big for the Page" is inspired by her own childhood.\par \par \uc2 Geena Davis, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS (Actor and "The Girl Who Was Too Big for the Page" author): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Oh, I am great. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Looking good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now you say that you have been tall your whole life.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You were even a tall baby.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So we can say that this book is inspired by your life story.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Since it`s about being tall, yes. It is pretty safe to assume, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What was it like preparing for this book and actually thinking about you being this tall girl who sometimes had to deal with that awkwardness?\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Right, right. Well, you know, all I wanted to do was fit in and not stand out --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: And you know from being so tall.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: And I was always the tallest kid in class, not just a girl, and all I wanted to do was be smaller and shrink and not be noticed as much. And it wasn`t until I was an adult and I stopped growing that I realized, you know, that it`s okay to take up this much space, and so that`s kind of the message of the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nope, and to own it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember being in sixth grade, taller than everyone in the class, and they used to call me "Goofy Gail. Goofy Gail. Uh-huh. Uh-huh. Uh-huh." I`m not traumatized or anything.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I have to stand in the back of the line. I know, to this day, I think maybe I`m a little traumatized, but now, when you get older, you realize I used to slouch down to look like everybody else.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did it have an effect on you as a kid?\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Oh, yes. Yes, no, definitely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How so? How so?\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Well, and my mom knew I was going to be tall, so she would not let me slouch my shoulders, but I found ways to -- there is even an illustration in the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: She is standing with her hips stuck way out so she could be shorter.\par \par \uc2 But my nickname in high school, the boys called me "Kareem Abdul Jabbar."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How was your -- how was your hook shot?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Right. Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But not only did you write the book and the message about your -- about you.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was fascinated that you did the illustrations, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is this -- listen, she is an archer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s an actress. I`m thinking, what? Do you make your own clothes?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pumpkin carver.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A pumpkin carver?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She does make her own clothes, and she did carve a pumpkin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Oh, dear me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you were so meticulous in your illustrations.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What were you trying to convey there? I love the illustrations and detail of it.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Well, thank you. Thank you. I mean, I`ve always been very interested in drawing, I always have drawn, but I didn`t ever consider myself like an artist or something, but I knew exactly how I wanted the character to look, so I decided, let me just try, you know?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Gayle mentioned archery -- being an archer, that wasn`t just a passing thing. You almost qualified for the Olympics as an archer.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: I was a semifinalist for the Olympic tryouts at 43, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: -- yes, because I take everything too far.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: That`s my modus operandi.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, what attracted you to the archery, though, of all things?\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: I had to learn some sports for various movie roles that I played, and like taekwondo and ice skating, horseback riding and baseball, and I was kind of good at all of them. And I had -- I had no interest in sports when I was a kid, I was too shy to -- you know, they begged me to be on the basketball team.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Of course, but -- but I thought, well, maybe I have some, you know, untapped athletic ability. Let me take up something --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have so many -- listen, we were talking about your iconic goals, "A League of Their Own," "Thelma & Louise."\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But let`s stick to the book for now.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Sheila isn`t just tall, she also has a voice too big to fit on the page.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She frets about being worthy of being a main character, but in the end, she learns a big lesson.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you read that part for us?\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Oh, yes, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I like how you --\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: At the very end --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At the very end. For the Show and Tell.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Her friend has told her she should be as big and loud as she - - as she should be. So she -- Sheila says, I know the answer. I`m -- I`m not too big. I just need --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To --\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: -- a bigger page.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s so good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: I love that reveal. Yes, it`s really fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m in the trenches of reading books to kids at home. They`re going to love that. They love the flip up page.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Oh yes, oh yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we also hear that you speak Swedish.\par \par \uc2 GEENA DAVIS: Well, yeah, I was a foreign exchange student.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: