SAMANTHA BEE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When did you know that this was funny material, though, because --\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Well, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- I never thought it was funny.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: I -- for sure did not think it was funny, at first at all. But then as I grew more into it and I got more comfortable with it, I was like, nobody`s talking about this in a way that I, as a person from the comedy world can understand.\par \par \uc2 Like, everything is very anodyne.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Everything is very generic. Like, talk to me about why my hair is falling out in clumps like, why? Why I`m like, my inner core is like Vesuvius when someone knocks into me at a Whole Foods with their grocery cart, like, talk to me about the real deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, real deal is right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But what`s the response been like? Because I imagine some of those people at Whole Foods are like, oh my God. And they just want to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: They do want to talk about this. I swear, everywhere I go, if I even -- if I even grace the topic of perimenopause, we`re all talking about it within five seconds.\par \par \uc2 I was in your hair and makeup room talking to your incredible team, and we all started talking about it like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: -- if you find someone, if you can, like, identify your people, you are in conversation together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Identify your people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was talking with my wife about it the other day --\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- and she had to pull me in on it. She is like this is the transitional period before menopause, because we were talking about how she`s changing.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s heating up at certain times, certain things are happening, and I don`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Well, you`re the perfect audience for my Audible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, exactly.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s why it`s perfect. What advice do you give to people out there that are with someone going through that period?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Well, first, my first step is, obviously to listen to my Audible show.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: You know, I think, like, everybody has to bring a lot of compassion to the table. Everybody has to, kind of, like, put their egos aside and it is very important for people to find community. Because it is actually very important to talk about it out loud.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: We are Gen X people. We say hard things out loud, like we are like pioneers of saying all the gross stuff that happen to our bodies so that everyone can feel comfortable in the future.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: And I think it`s just like an -- it`s the most essential part --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: -- to feel normal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s the first thing to do, is to talk about it. Because you said so many times, perimenopausal women are invisible. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Invisible. I feel like I could walk into a Dick`s Sporting Goods like right now and steal a full canoe.\par \par \uc2 It is like, put it on my head and walk out the door.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Walk out.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: No one`s going to stop me. No one`s going to see me like you do, and you face this kind of reality. I embrace the invisibility.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: It`s hard to come to, because it is hard to -- you don`t always want to feel invisible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Perimenopausal women also carry everything with them at all times. Like -- what do you mean?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Gayle, if you touch my purse right now, like you can barely lift it. It has so many supplies in it, because perimenopausal women have to be prepared for every eventuality, whether it`s low blood sugar, nuclear war. I don`t know. There`s always -- there`s always food in my purse. We could live for a week.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You say once you have a menopause role model --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A menopause buddy, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A buddy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: You have a menopause buddy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And a role model and a buddy. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Because you don`t -- you can`t remember anything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: You`ve got to -- like it takes two people to come up with the name of a movie. You know what I mean? It`s like -- that`s like teamwork.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so true.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: It`s so true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so well done. It`s so well done.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Bravo, and I`m so glad you`re doing it. Because people hear menopause, they think dry vagina, they think aging, They think you`re done, and that`s just not true.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Sure. No, I mean, that`s like, partially true, but you know what I mean? like, there`s a whole --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am going to let you guys talk about this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Samantha Bee, thank you. Thank you and bravo, bravo.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "How to Survive Menopause" is available now on Audible, a podcast and audio book company.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 National Endowment for the Arts\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Marshall Allen performing with the Sun Ra Arkestra. He`s a living legend and is still going strong after a century.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent, Jericka Duncan spoke to him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Dressed in a gold color cap and a jacket that sparkles, Marshall Allen takes the stage knowing he is a star.\par \par \uc2 His sound is unique because he doesn`t just play the written melody. He adds random notes at random times that reflect how the songs make him feel.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN (Musician): You have to depend on the spirit of the sound.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What may be even more impressive, at the golden age of 100, Allen is on tour promoting "New Dawn," his first solo album with his name.\par \par \uc2 So you`re a hundred years old, a hundred years wise, does it feel like you`ve been on this Earth that long?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: No.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What does it feel like?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Little aches and pains here and there, but that`s a normal thing. I feel good.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Allen grew up in Louisville, Kentucky, listening to the sounds of Duke Ellington`s jazz orchestra. The clarinet was the first instrument Allen learned to play, then came the saxophone while serving in the Army band during World War Two.\par \par \uc2 But how Allen played and his reasons why, changed in the late 50s when he met Herman Poole Blount, also known as Sun Ra.\par \par \uc2 The composer and leader of Sun Ra Arkestra later welcomed Allen into his group, but not without criticism.\par \par \uc2 He didn`t want or care about your elite training.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: He said it did sound good, had a tone and this -- but it`s not what I want. I said, now, what it is that you want? He said, you in a square, what you know, I want to hear what you don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Sun Ra`s fascination with outer space and the true freedom it could offer inspired his cosmic blend of jazz, Afro futurism, and spiritual expression. He encouraged his artists to play from the heart and improvise when they "felt the spirit."\par \par \uc2 How did you learn to play with that?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: I had to go through a lot of ridicule.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: For years, the group lived together in Philadelphia and practiced relentlessly.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: We rehearsed day and night. I was restricted to my running habits and my good time habits. All of that was --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What were your good time habits?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Go chase some girls.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Chasing some girls.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Do this and you know, the usual thing. No, not with him.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So you had to be more disciplined.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Disciplined. I had to get my discipline with him.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But when Sun Ra died in the early 90s, Allen became the new leader, and for the last 30 years, he has been teaching musicians the art of playing what they don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Do you think jazz is just as important today as it was when you were just starting out?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Yes. Yes, of course, that`s what kept me really alive.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The routine of preparing for a show and testing his instruments never seems to get old for Allen, who recently performed in Brooklyn, New York.\par \par \uc2 So however you feel today will come out in the music.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Well, of course.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Does this feel like home for you when you`re out here?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Yes, I`ve been doing this too long -- 1970s, 80s.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Seventy, eighty, ninety years.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Time well spent doing what he loves, making people feel something.\par \par \uc2 What is it about jazz? What is it about what you do that keeps you going, because you said jazz kept me alive?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: The spirit of things.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The spirit --\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: I am not knowing everything, I don`t know nothing. Look at the world as it is with everybody knowing everything. It is chaos, and then the spirit of the thing is left out.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What is your advice for the next group of musicians of jazz bands?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL ALLEN: They have to earn it. They have to listen to the spirit. You have to play what you don`t know.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mr. Allen is clearly a national treasure. He will turn 101 next month.\par \par \uc2 And if that concept of playing what you don`t know is hard to sort of wrap your head around, think about it as being open and being vulnerable, because so much of what we do is learned as humans and rehearsed, and it`s predictable. And he says, no, be free.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that he is still going strong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He looks great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think being vulnerable --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250430el4u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For March 19, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Kelly O`Grady, Jan Crawford, Charlie D`Agata, Meg Oliver, Lilia  Luciano, Janet Shamlian, Rob Marciano, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,919 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : He was seeking freedom in America.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But it appears he wound up here, in one of the most dangerous places on Earth.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : President Trump and his border czar, Tom Homan, said the mass deportation of migrants in this country illegally would begin with dangerous criminals, the worst of the worst, they said.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But CBS News has learned that among hundreds of alleged gang members deported this past week to El Salvador was a Venezuelan migrant with a job and no criminal record in the process of seeking asylum.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : His family believes he is now being held among some of the most dangerous prisoners in the world.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And they have no way to get in touch with him.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano now with his story -- Lilia.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: We traveled to Northwest Arkansas to meet with a man`s wife who`s cross-country traveling because she fears she too could be targeted, but more so is in fear that her husband is being mistreated indefinitely in a place filled with very dangerous people.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice-over): The pictures are striking, more than 200 Venezuelan deportees flown on \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  planes out of Arlington, Texas, last week and brought here, one of the world`s most notorious prisons, El CECOT in El Salvador, heads shaven, arms shackled.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration says these men are all members of criminal gangs. But CBS News has learned at least one of the men believed to be deported here has no known criminal record. Franco Caraballo is a 26-year-old barber who was living with his wife in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 He turned himself in at the southern border more than a year ago and requested asylum, and was detained last month during a routine check-in with ICE. His attorney, Martin Rosenow, showed us DHS documents confirming Caraballo has no criminal history.\par \par \uc2 So now they`re saying on paper he`s believed to be a gang member.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN ROSENOW (Attorney For Franco Caraballo): With no known criminal history.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: No criminal history and no explanation.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN ROSENOW: No explanation whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Venezuelan authorities provided information that says there`s no evidence Caraballo was in a gang or had any criminal record there.\par \par \uc2 Rosenow says Caraballo was deported to El Salvador and has not been able to communicate with him.\par \par \uc2 He had no suspicion, no clue that he could end up in a place like CECOT?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN ROSENOW: We had no idea, not him, not his wife, not anyone.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Is his family concerned that CECOT is worse than Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN ROSENOW: They are concerned that it`s worse. I`m concerned that we have no access. Who has custody over our client? Who has jurisdiction? El Salvador? ICE? The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 ? We have no idea.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In the DHS deportation document, agents highlight Caraballo`s tattoos, but don`t explicitly say they are connected to gang activity.\par \par \uc2 Why did he think that they were going to deport him for the tattoos?\par \par \uc2 (JOHANNY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Because of a clock?\par \par \uc2 (JOHANNY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: A clock that marks the time when your daughter was born?\par \par \uc2 JOHANNY (Wife): Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: His wife, Johanny, says she last spoke to her husband the day before he was deported.\par \par \uc2 What worries you about him being in this prison?\par \par \uc2 (JOHANNY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: That he`s innocent.\par \par \uc2 (JOHANNY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: You think he`s being mistreated in there.\par \par \uc2 (JOHANNY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The deportation of these Venezuelan men is in front of a federal judge who ordered the Trump administration to not send them to El Salvador in the first place and gave the government a deadline of tomorrow at noon to explain why they did not comply with the order.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lilia, I remember, when you reported on CECOT, and you said it was essentially a black hole. Is that why it`s so hard to find out if Caraballo is in there?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: That`s absolutely right, John.\par \par \uc2 It`s a place that has a half-of-a-mile of a radius where cell phone signal is blocked, where the prisoners will never get visitors, where they cannot make phone calls, hear from family members. And this is a government that is known for a lack of transparency.\par \par \uc2 I asked the Bukele administration about this case and the rest of the Venezuelans there, and they said, we will share these images, but not any more information.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lilia Luciano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The commander in chief is in another court battle, this one over removing the estimated 10,000 to 15,000 transgender people in the military. A federal judge yesterday blocked him at least temporarily from doing that.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Charlie D`Agata introduces you to two transgender service members who wanted nothing more from their country than the opportunity to serve it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): What made you want to join the services?\par \par \uc2 PETTY OFFICER 2ND CLASS PAULO BATISTA (\uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 ): You know, I just -- I made a promise to my father.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Petty Officer 2nd Class Paulo Batista worked for years to join the ranks of America`s armed forces.\par \par \uc2 PETTY OFFICER 2ND CLASS PAULO BATISTA: Nobody wanted to help me. I went through at least 22 different recruiting offices. I wanted to serve.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The amateur bodybuilder completed his gender transition before enlisting at the age of 38.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, President Biden lifted the ban on transgender people in the military. Four years later, the Naval intelligence analyst could be kicked out.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Our warriors should be focused on defeating America`s enemies, not figuring out their genders.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The White House says service members receiving transition-related health care are not physically capable of meeting military readiness requirements.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  has been given a 30-day notice to come up with a plan. The Army was the first to announce new measures banning transgender troops from joining its ranks, pausing medical treatments, and putting an end to promotions, with immediate effect.\par \par \uc2 PARKER MOORE (U.S. Nuclear Electronic Technician): This is my second Navy and Marine Corps achievement.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: San Diego is also the home port of the USS Lincoln, where Parker Moore (sp?) works as a nuclear electronic technician. The transgender sailor trained for more than two years for that role and has been actively serving for more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 PARKER MOORE: I`m married. I have a 6-year-old son. This doesn`t just affect me as an individual. It affects my family and my entire command. If one day I`m not allowed to do that job anymore, it affects the other 500 people in my department, who would now have to pick up that slack.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A 2017 analysis found discharging transgender troops would cost nearly a billion dollars, far more than the roughly $52 million the Pentagon spent over the last decade on transition-related health care.\par \par \uc2 PARKER MOORE: I think that most people who do have an issue with transgender service members are uneducated. I do not think they actually understand the process or may not have ever interacted with a transgender person.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Parker told us, despite the ruling temporarily blocking the ban, it`s impossible to live with the uncertainty, and they`re planning on leaving the military. Paulo said he will fight until the end.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has until Friday to file an appeal.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Charlie D`Agata at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Jan Crawford in Washington is our chief legal correspondent.\par \par \uc2 So, Jan, the president involved here in dozens of legal cases. What do you make of all this?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, Maurice, just like President Trump flooded the zone with all these executive actions, we`re now seeing judges flood the zone with decisions that are against him.\par \par \uc2 So far, though, his lawyers are insisting that they`re not defying those orders. They`re making some creative legal arguments, but Trump is saying that he`s going to follow the process and appeal. And as we have already seen, those appeals courts are going to reverse some of these lower court judges for being wrong on the law.\par \par \uc2 Now, the concern is, what if Trump`s patience with the process runs out and he openly defies a court order? That could then escalate up to the Supreme Court. And no president in history has ever defied a direct order of the Supreme Court. That would be a constitutional crisis -- Maurice.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Jan -- Jan Crawford for us in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jan.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now more some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is suggesting the United States take over Ukraine`s power plants. In a phone call today with President Zelenskyy, Mr. Trump said it is the best way to protect Ukraine`s energy infrastructure. Despite Vladimir Putin`s promise just yesterday to not attack that system, Russian forces hit Ukrainian substations that supply electricity for trains.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Federal investigators say the commuter plane that crashed in Alaska last month was 1,000 pounds overweight for the weather conditions. All 10 people on board were killed.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And back on Earth after nine months in space, astronauts Butch Wilmore and Suni Williams started post-flight rehab today. They got medical checkups and worked out with trainers at the gym.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  left interest rates unchanged today. The Fed said economic growth is now expected to slow this year, even as inflation ticks up in parts because of the trade war.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady asked Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  about the possibility of a slowdown -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Well, John, the key theme today was uncertainty. And Powell admitted that uncertainty is weighing on consumers, which power much of the U.S. economy. So I asked him if people spending less at retailers and restaurants is a bigger worry than higher prices.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2  (Federal Reserve Chairman): It`s a pretty good economy, but people are unhappy because of the price level. And I do completely understand and accept that.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Why are you still projecting two rate cuts this year if your own projections show inflation higher for longer?\par \par \uc2 Does that mean you see a slowdown in economic growth as a real threat?\par \par \uc2 CHAIRMAN JEROME POWELL: Remember, we came into this with -- at the December meeting, the meeting was two cuts. We do understand that sentiment has fallen off pretty sharply, but economic activity has not yet. And that probably has to do with turmoil at the beginning of an administration that`s making big changes in areas of policy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: While Powell thinks a significant slowdown is unlikely, retail giants like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and Target are already warning of a more cautious consumer.\par \par \uc2 If people spend less, that could complicate the Fed`s war on inflation. Now, the next time we will hear about the path forward on interest rates is May -- John and Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Kelly O`Grady in the nation`s capital tonight, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the CBS Evening News: a tornado threat in the heartland.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: I`m Meg Oliver in Asheville, North Carolina. Studies show digital addiction can change the structure of your brain. It`s now referred to as brain rot. We will show you what it looks like. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A woman faces 20 years in prison for something that`s legal in many places. I`m Janet Shamlian in Houston with a story about a Texas law and a national debate next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Texas has one of the most restrictive abortion laws in the country.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Last night, we told you that a midwife and her associate had been arrested for violating that law.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, Janet Shamlian takes a closer look at the case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voice-over): Forty-eight-year-old Maria Rojas is a certified nurse midwife, charged with performing illegal abortions and practicing without a medical license. A man who worked for her was also charged.\par \par \uc2 According to the arrest warrant, Rojas ran three clinics near Houston. Her patients called her Dr. Maria, but she`s not a licensed physician.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY SHEARMAN (Tomball Birth Center): I can`t picture her doing this. I don`t believe that she did.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: You don`t think she performed abortions?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY SHEARMAN: No, I do not.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: She didn`t do that in any capacity for you?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY SHEARMAN: No. No. Midwives don`t do that, period.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Holly Shearman owns the Tomball Birth Center and employed Rojas as a midwife once a week. She`s known her for eight years.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY SHEARMAN: She was a doctor in Peru, an OB, came to America. And America doesn`t recognize physicians from other countries. So then she became a midwife.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Has she ever presented herself, as far as you know, as a medical doctor?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY SHEARMAN: No, never.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Court documents charge that Rojas provided medication to induce an abortion in March and alleged she told the patient her pregnancy was likely nonviable. She allegedly performed another abortion on a different patient in January. The method is unclear.\par \par \uc2 A search warrant found misoprostol in Rojas` car and one of her clinics. The medication can be used for a variety of medical purposes, including to induce abortion.\par \par \uc2 Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  announced the arrest. He said in a statement: "Individuals killing unborn babies will be prosecuted to the full extent of the law."\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler is a law professor who studies abortion-related issues.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER (\uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , School of Law): Attorney General Paxton was trying to find someone who was not a licensed physician to argue that providers posed a threat to Texans in general.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Shearman says she`s worried about how Rojas is doing.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY SHEARMAN: In my mind, she`s a hero. She helps people.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: You see her that way?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY SHEARMAN: Yes, definitely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Janet Shamlian joins us now from Houston.\par \par \uc2 So, Janet, what`s next in this case?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Well, tonight, Rojas is still in jail. Her bond is set at $700,000. Both of the charges against her are felonies. The abortion charge is punishable by up to 20 years in prison, and there are potential civil penalties as well.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general is also asking for a restraining order. He wants to close her three clinics.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All right, Janet Shamlian for us in Houston.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Janet.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On this day before spring, winter is getting in its final blows.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A storm brought blizzard conditions today to the Midwest and the Great Plains. Some places could get as much as a foot of snow before it`s over.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Rob Marciano is tracking the storm for us -- Rob.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Guys, this has turned into some storm. We have got that blizzard warning on the backside, on the front side, severe weather.\par \par \uc2 This is a live picture out of Chicago. They`re under a severe thunderstorm warning right now. We have seen flashes of light. You obviously see a lot of rain with this, and that`s the front side of this system. You can see on the ground here that front side. Those yellow boxes, that is where we have been having tornadoes, tornado watches up until about midnight tonight.\par \par \uc2 But the low itself is between Kansas City and St. Louis. And it is not moving that quick, and it continues to get wound up. Look at the satellite picture. This is amazing, that classic comma shape. But I want you to look down here towards Texas. See that brown? All right, that is the dust we talked about last night in New Mexico and Texas.\par \par \uc2 It`s being drawn up into this system, and it may very well be rained out of it. Look at this video out of Arkansas, just south of Little Rock on Interstate 530. They had dust there dangerous enough to make visibility horrific there on the highway.\par \par \uc2 And this is something I haven`t seen in a long, long time out of the National Weather Service, a blowing dust warning in Northeast Arkansas until 9:00. So that`s out. And anyone that lives downstream of this may very well get dust delivered to them from Texas and New Mexico if it rains east of this system -- guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Dust to snow. We got something for everybody tonight.\par \par \uc2 Rob Marciano, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They`re called smartphones, but overusing them may not be wise.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We will show you what excessive scrolling can do to the brain in Eye on America next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: How much time do you spend doing this? According to a survey, the average smartphone user scrolls the equivalent of 78 miles a year. That`s three marathons. It may be good exercise for your finger, but there is evidence it may be hurting your brain.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voice-over): Katy Paige Rosenberg, a freshman at the University of North Carolina, Asheville...\par \par \uc2 MAN: Two successes, two failures.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: ... recently realized what too much scrolling was doing to her.\par \par \uc2 How many hours were you spending on your phone?\par \par \uc2 KATY PAIGE ROSENBERG (College Student): Oh, God, probably like nine.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Nine hours?\par \par \uc2 KATY PAIGE ROSENBERG: I was just kind of constantly on it.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: You may have heard stories like hers before.\par \par \uc2 KATY PAIGE ROSENBERG: I wasn`t able to focus because I would have to take out my phone every couple of minutes.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: But now you can actually see the science behind it.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENT NELSON (Newport Healthcare): Smartphones have wide-reaching changes all over the brain, and, specifically, it grows here and it shrinks there.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Psychiatrist Brent Nelson is applying this new science at Newport Healthcare, which has mental health treatment centers for teens across the country.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENT NELSON: This is a brain that`s addicted to a smartphone.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: These are MRI images from a recent study in Korea. All the red indicates increases in brain activity, the effects of smartphone addiction.\par \par \uc2 Do you want your brain to be this colorful?\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENT NELSON: You don`t.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Why?\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENT NELSON: Well, because this is showing where the brain is working extra hard compared to a non-addicted brain when asked to do actually a pretty simple task.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Addicted smartphone users` brains were so colorful, so active, it made them less attentive and more easily distracted, what`s now informally called brain rot.\par \par \uc2 What does that look like in real life?\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENT NELSON: Yes, let`s take school, for example, sitting in class and you`re trying to focus. They`re going to be looking around, not attending to what the teacher is trying to teach them.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Dr. Nelson says emerging research points to even greater risks.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENT NELSON: We`re just starting to see these changes, and we know they`re connected to behavioral changes, depression, anxiety. The dangers are hiding in there.\par \par \uc2 KATY PAIGE ROSENBERG: Social media had really influenced me in a lot of ways. TikTok would kind of push these videos of people popping an edible before school. And I was like, if I do this, maybe I will be cool. And I started self-medicating.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: To deal with that, last year, she checked into a treatment facility.\par \par \uc2 If you hadn`t gone to treatment, where would you be today?\par \par \uc2 KATY PAIGE ROSENBERG: I don`t think I`d be here. It was really bad.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Katy had to give up her phone in treatment. There, she found other outlets, from drawing to playing guitar, that helped rewire her Gen Z brain.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The key, perhaps, analog antidotes reminiscent of another generation.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENT NELSON: Playing in the dirt, drinking from the hose, sort of the Gen X kind of mentality, is shown to actually allow folks to recover, to feel better, to make it easier to kind of go about their day.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In short, it`s good to stop scrolling and start strumming.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Meg Oliver in Asheville, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Katy used to spend nine hours a day on her phone. Now she is down to less than two. Good for her.\par \par \uc2 In tomorrow`s Eye on America, from Quinter, Kansas, the lingering impact of COVID on an area that once had the highest COVID death rate in the country.\par \par \uc2 John and I will be back in just a moment with an attempt to take the blood out of a blood sport.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally tonight, in the battle between bullfighting and animal rights activists, Mexico City has come up with a compromise.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Rather than ban bullfighting, the legislature voted to make it kinder and gentler.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Bullfighting without violence, it is called.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Killing the bull is no longer permitted or even injuring the animal. No sharp objects allowed.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And to protect the matador, the bull`s horns will be covered.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The fight is limited to 15 minutes. How the winner is determined, not clear.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Animal rights activists said of the new law, ole.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And opponents? Well, the rough translation, bull feathers.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ernest Hemingway would have had something to say about this.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Bull feathers?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Or the bullfighting itself?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The bullfighting.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 That is the CBS Evening News. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . Evening News Plus is coming up on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you right back here tomorrow. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250320el3j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Delta Plane Crashes and Flips on Tarmac; Deadly Storm Recovery; Snow and Cold Grips Country; US and Russia Officials Meet; Federal Workers Layoff; Fewer Consumer Protections; Building Back Efficiently; Overlooked No Longer; Oprah`s Book Club Pick; CBS MORNINGS Deals; Road to Recovery; CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Tom Hanson, Kris Van  Cleave, Nicole Valdes, Jessica Burch, Holly Williams, Nancy Cordes,  Vladimir Duthiers, Jill Schlesinger, Nancy Chen, Meg Oliver \par \uc2 14,304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on a Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A plane crashed. It`s upside down. Fire Department is on site. It`s upside down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A Delta flight with 80 people on board crashes and flips upside down on a Toronto runway, and everyone survives.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The South braces for more severe weather as it tries to recover from devastating floods.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): One of the most significant natural disasters we`ve seen in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More mass fire rings of federal workers by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and the Trump administration. Some say the reason they were given doesn`t make sense.\par \par \uc2 MONICA (Former \uc2 FAA\uc2  employee): I was in complete disbelief.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Four deputy mayors in New York City have resigned a result of the alleged quid pro quo between Mayor Eric Adams and the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: NASCAR Hall of Famer Jeff Gordon says he wants a sequel to the 1990 film "Days of Thunder." He`s been in touch with Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Days of Thunder.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Stand up straight. Stand up straight.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Wait. Wait. Wait. Wait.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We look -- well, look at us taking a picture with you.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HART (Comedian and actor): You`re so stupid. That`s why I don`t have no clothes with our sizes.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Kevin Hart got roasted when he stopped to take photos with a few WNBA players at the All-Star game this weekend.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`ve got to bend down low. That`s great --\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HART: No.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It should be.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HART: At the end of the day, if you could be this small and this dope, you would be this small, too.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You know, I wouldn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kevin has a very has a very good sense of humor about all the teasing he gets all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He goes if you be the small and dope, he ain`t wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, shoutout to all the short kings out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right. He is all right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking at him roll with that wide angle, anyway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this morning, though, with the incredible stories of survival after the terrifying crash of a Delta flight guaranteed you`ve never seen anything like this before. Take a look. The video shows the plane as it skids, catches fire and then flips upside down. That`s right, a plane upside down on the tarmac as it lands at Toronto`s Pearson Airport.\par \par \uc2 Eighty people on board when this happened on a flight. It started in Minneapolis. Many were injured, but the big news, the great news, the good news here is nobody -- nobody died in this crash.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is at Toronto`s Pearson Airport with more on this story. Tom, you`ve got 80 grateful people this morning. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Yes, no doubt, Gayle. Good morning to you as well.\par \par \uc2 The two runways involved in this crash are still closed this morning as the investigation continues. We`re learning more about a couple of the people who were more seriously injured, a 40-year-old woman, a 60-year-old man, both in critical condition this morning, 16 other passengers walked away with minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 But still, there are so many questions about how this crash happened in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: New video shows the moment a Delta regional jet crash landed at the Toronto Pearson Airport Monday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 You see it catch fire as it skids across the tarmac before rolling over surrounded by a plume of black smoke.\par \par \uc2 Video obtained by TMZ shows the crash landing at a different angle.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This aircraft is upside down and burning.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Our plane crashed.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Video of the aftermath shows the plane upside down on the tarmac, its wings and tail ripped off and landing gear pointing toward the sky.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everything drop it. Come on.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Flight attendants and crew members rushed to help stunned passengers strapped upside down in their seatbelts escape the wreckage.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON (Passenger): It sounded -- I mean, it was just cement and metal, you know.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Pete Carlson, who was traveling to a paramedics conference, was on the frightening flight.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: All of a sudden, everything just kind of went sideways, and then next thing I know, there is a kind of a blink and I`m upside down, still strapped in.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: He says he knew he had to get off the plane quickly.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: I took my seatbelt off, which I had obviously left fastened for the landing, and as I took it off, crashed on to the ceiling, which had become the floor.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Passengers looked on in shock as emergency responders immediately began spraying down the plane`s charred fuselage.\par \par \uc2 Despite the devastating damage, officials say all 80 people on board, including 76 passengers and four crew members survived.\par \par \uc2 TODD AITKEN (Toronto Pearson Fire Chief): It`s really important that we do not speculate. What we can say is the runway was dry and there was no cross wind conditions.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: At the time of the incident, visibility was down to six miles, and there were wind gusts up to 37 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 Air Traffic Control was heard warning the pilots.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Might get a slight bump in the glide path. There will be an aircraft in front of it.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Billy Nolan, former acting \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator, calls it a remarkable escape.\par \par \uc2 BILLY NOLAN (Former acting \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator): A bit miraculous to wind upside down, and yet you`ve -- we`ve got 80 folks to walk off this plane and with no fatality. So I`ll mark that as a win for us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And I want to read you a statement from \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  that they released. They say the hearts of the entire global Delta family are with those affected by today`s incident.\par \par \uc2 Tony, nothing short of a miracle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it really is.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk more about it with Kris Van Cleave. He is going to talk about the investigation and how this all could have happened.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning to you. You`re a veteran of covering aviation and general transportation. You`ve seen the videos as we have. What do you make of what you`re looking at?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, I think what you can see from the TMZ video looking behind as it is coming into land, this appears to be a very hard landing followed by a collapse of one of the rear landing gear, perhaps because you see the plane sort of shift down to the side right before that engine starts to flame.\par \par \uc2 That will be a good starting point for investigators. You know did the impact the hardness of that landing result in the gear collapsing? Did the gear collapse because of other reasons? That`s not going to be told by the video, but certainly, these new images give us a sense of what appeared to be a very hard landing.\par \par \uc2 You don`t see the typical kind of flare up that you would see as a plane would come in to land, where you bring the nose up just a little bit right at landing to sort of smooth it out.\par \par \uc2 This one looks like they came in, almost like a carrier landing, straight in a hard landing, they were dealing likely with winds at that time. We had heard gusts up to 40 miles an hour when they were coming in.\par \par \uc2 So all of that will be significant. This video does give investigators a pretty good starting point as they make their list of what they want to know and what they need to find out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tom Hanson describe it as a miracle. I certainly believe in miracles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you look at this --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean putting context for us. If you could, Kris, one of the passengers said that they were upside down like bats in a cave, just hanging there with the seatbelts on.\par \par \uc2 How unusual is it in any kind of airline incident for the plane to be upside down at the end?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, it`s certainly very unusual, but clearly something that was thought about when they designed not only the aircraft, but those seats. The seats are designed to withstand many times the force of gravity and if you ever wanted a lesson as to why people tell you to wear your seatbelts on the airplane, this was it. Staying in those seats saved lives.\par \par \uc2 The flight attendants, you know, you`ll hear when you`re coming in to land that they need to prepare the cabin for landing, and they go through and they collect glasses and everything else, things that could become projectiles, they tell you to put your bags away and your laptops away.\par \par \uc2 The reason they do that is for a scenario like this, where things go wrong and those items could be flying about the cabin and injure passengers by securing them.\par \par \uc2 It also means that there`s not clutter on the way out of the airplane. In this case, the exit path was the ceiling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is such a good reminder, Kris.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is great reminder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hadn`t thought of it that way, even though we hear it time and time again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great reminder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really good. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Kris. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 In our next half hour of our program, we`re going to talk to a pilot who has flown into that very airport there in Toronto, many times in similar conditions, find out her thoughts on what happened.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, speaking of weather, let`s go to the brutally cold weather in much of the country. It feels like it`s 50 below zero this morning in parts of Montana and North Dakota, Gayle will say out here in New York, but the cold weather is also blasting the South and the East.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky has seen the worst of the bad weather. Twelve people are now confirmed dead after weekend storms caused severe flooding, and another storm is on the way.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes is tracking the recovery effort.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): We just got hit by one of the most significant natural disasters we`ve seen in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Kentucky is recovering from deadly storms that battered parts of the state, flooding out roads and buildings.\par \par \uc2 First responders have been working around the clock, like Mason Michael.\par \par \uc2 MASON MICHAEL (First responder): The water had, at that time, risen to almost a hood level.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: He leads the fire department in Horse Cave. His team rescued a couple from their truck on a flooded street.\par \par \uc2 Since the rain started to fall on Saturday, how many calls have you and your team gotten?\par \par \uc2 MASON MICHAEL: There has been a significant increase.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Communities in Tennessee and West Virginia also felt the wrath of the storms, and now many are trying to see what`s left.\par \par \uc2 This couple got help from family, who went to check on their house with kayaks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Everything in the house is dry.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Back in Kentucky, Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  surveyed the damage in hard hit Pike County, which saw devastating flooding in 2022.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA LUCAS (Kentucky resident): Many have PTSD from the floods that they suffered last time.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Amanda Lucas lives in nearby Letcher County. This year, storms flooded her antique shop`s inventory, but her focus is on her community.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA LUCAS: These are my friends` houses.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people around here have no income, and I just feel like things were just kind of starting to somewhat feel normal again, and now a lot of people is going to have to start all over.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Here in Kentucky, we are expecting a snowstorm to start tonight and last through tomorrow morning and get this, here in Bowling Green, we are not expecting to see any temperatures above freezing for a single hour until Saturday. That is bad news for communities like this that are still dealing with high flood waters -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bad news indeed.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Bay Area meteorologist, Jessica Burch is watching the rest of this winter weather blast.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So how bad will it be today?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA BURCH: You know, we`re taking just a look at the next 48 hours, and we`re already starting to see the storm strengthen, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Over the Central Plains right now, and it will continue to track off into the South and all throughout the Mid-Atlantic as we head into the rest of this week`s forecast, pushing offshore just within the next 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 But it`s important to note a couple of things. It comes just days after that major flooding in Kentucky, and we timed the storm out. Throughout the next 12 hours, watch how it starts to gain strength from Kansas into Missouri, pushing over the Mississippi as we head into the overnight hours tonight, early morning hours tomorrow, making recovery very difficult for our friends in Kentucky as we head into those early morning hours on Wednesday, covering that whole state with snow.\par \par \uc2 That continues to track off into the East and to the rest of this week`s forecast. And the other big story has to do with these cold temperatures that potentially could break records today, like Kansas City at six degrees. You have that wind chill that`s all the way down to negative 12.\par \par \uc2 We`ll keep you updated on that -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jessica, got it. Always good to see you. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 The most extensive talks in years between the US and Russia began in Saudi Arabia this morning.\par \par \uc2 Now, the war in Ukraine is top of the agenda, even though Ukraine is not at this negotiating table.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is following the talks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Secretary of State Marco Rubio sat down with his Russian counterpart this morning, high speed diplomacy aimed at ending the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ignited the conflict nearly three years ago with his invasion, and some say today`s meeting is already a victory for Russia, which has been isolated by sanctions and condemnation.\par \par \uc2 Many Ukrainians feel betrayed.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Ukraine did not know anything about these talks," said Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  yesterday. "We cannot recognize any agreements about it that are made without us."\par \par \uc2 European leaders held an emergency meeting in Paris on Monday. Many in Europe fear that if Ukraine`s allies don`t guarantee its security, Putin could agree to a ceasefire then re-invade in the future.\par \par \uc2 The British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  says his country is willing to provide peacekeeping troops, but cannot protect Ukraine alone.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER (United Kingdom Prime Minister): There must be a US backstop, because a US security guarantee is the only way to effectively deter Russia from attacking Ukraine again.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: He`ll go to Washington next week to discuss that with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: President Zelenskyy says he`ll travel to Saudi Arabia tomorrow. Meanwhile, Russia released another detained American yesterday, a man arrested this month for allegedly possessing marijuana. That was apparently a gesture of good will -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Back in this country, Trump`s acting Social Security Commissioner stepped down over the weekend. According to multiple reports she had resisted letting Elon Musk`s Department of Government Efficiency look inside the agency`s most sensitive records.\par \par \uc2 The White House is saying that DOGE is looking to eliminate waste and fraud. And this morning, Nancy Cordes has the story of one of the thousands of federal workers already fired since Trump took office.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MONICA: And I was in complete disbelief.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Monica, who asked us not to use her last name was hired by the \uc2 FAA\uc2  in December to be an aviation safety assistant at Dallas Fort Worth Airport. She was part of the team that filed inspections and reports on the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  fleet.\par \par \uc2 MONICA: I personally did not feel that our positions were at risk for the simple fact that we are in the Flight Standards Safety Division. It says its name right, flight and safety.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But on Friday, she was let go. With less than a year on the job, she had not yet received civil service protections. The stinging dismissal e-mail blamed her performance and said, "You have not demonstrated that your further employment at the DOT FAA would be in the public interest."\par \par \uc2 MONICA: There have been no negative comment or feedback until I got this letter about performance.\par \par \uc2 MICHAL SHINNAR (Joseph Greenwald & Laake Employment lawyer): What this really does appear to be is just a purely false stated reason.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Employment lawyer, Michal Shinnar says the reason Trump`s team has been citing performance in firings is because, by law, probationary federal workers can only be removed for performance or misconduct.\par \par \uc2 MICHAL SHINNAR: We don`t let an agency get around these congressional directives by just terminating our probationary employees and mass claiming it is performance when it`s not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: She says the situation is ripe for class action lawsuits while Trump officials insist these cuts are just a drop in the bucket.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy posting last night: "The \uc2 FAA\uc2  alone has a staggering 45,000 employees. Less than 400 were let go." We`re still waiting for the exact numbers of cuts at most other agencies like the CDC and \uc2 NIH\uc2  -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, another Trump administration target for cuts is a key consumer protection agency. \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here and will show us how that could affect you. But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Oprah Winfrey exclusively shares her new Book Club Selection, and we talk with the author.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is highlighting some remarkable people whose achievements were overlooked for decades, we meet the writer who is bringing their stories back to life.\par \par \uc2 This CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . Save when you bundle auto, home, or motorcycle insurance.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Money Watch," we are looking at the Trump administration`s push to gut the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 , but what does it actually do?\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to break it all down.\par \par \uc2 All right, so why should Americans care? And what are some of the safeguards that exist because of the \uc2 CFPB\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So the \uc2 CFPB\uc2  was created out of the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It was an agency that was very laser-focused on consumers, so everything from mortgages to credit cards to payday lending - - all of that.\par \par \uc2 So some of the things that they have done include really trying to skinny up a lot of documentation, like when you get a credit card bill and all of that gobbledygook and mortgages, so that`s called the Know Before You Owe.\par \par \uc2 Then there is restriction of credit card late fees, that`s in action, that`s been enacted, but I`m sorry -- that has been a rule that`s put in place, but has not been enacted.\par \par \uc2 And then we also have that -- we`ve talked about this, the expunging of medical debt from your credit report, that`s huge it will impact millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 So there are things that really help people on the ground.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So investigations at the \uc2 CFPB\uc2  are on hold. Many staffers are on leave. So where should consumers turn to now?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So there are other agencies that are available for consumer complaints. So one is looking at the \uc2 FTC\uc2 , the Securities and Exchange Commission. They all have consumer arms, right? We also have something for employees and their retirement plan, and states have a lot of insurance and securities arms, as well as consumer divisions that are very helpful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so you mentioned there are other agencies. If there are other agencies, does the Trump administration have a point in gutting this one?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, this has been a point of Republicans since the beginning of the \uc2 CFPB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Since 2008.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : They said, hey, there are seven other agencies that have consumer arms. Why do we need this?\par \par \uc2 And they really push back that payday lenders actually went to the Supreme Court to defund it. The Supreme Court found against them.\par \par \uc2 Here is what I think is important. There are very few agencies whose sole purpose is to focus on the consumer, and that`s why I think this has been good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha. Consumer protection.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Consumer protection.\par \par \uc2 However, I also want to be clear that when big institutions like \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 `s \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  complain about regulation, I`d like everyone to look back 15 years and see just how much money those banks have made over those years. They`re doing just fine regardless of the regulation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They have been cashing in.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Consumers take action. Be clear, there`s no one agency that is really out there fighting for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love the way you make this all digestible. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much, Jill. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, we`re going to look into the Toronto plane crash through the eyes of a pilot. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Drop it. Come on.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, these pictures still amaze me.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 The crew of that Delta jet that flipped over after crashing on the tarmac in Toronto is being praised for getting everybody out of that plane safely. All 80 passengers and crew on board survived this. At least 18 of them were hurt.\par \par \uc2 Captain Laura Einsetler, she`s a longtime commercial airline pilot who has flown into that area -- flown into that area and that airport many times, joins us now with her expertise.\par \par \uc2 Very good to see you, Captain.\par \par \uc2 I was really looking forward to this because I know you know the airport, the plane and the conditions. So first, I want to get your initial thoughts when you saw this plane upside down.\par \par \uc2 Most of us have never seen anything like this. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER (Longtime commercial airline pilot): It`s very disturbing. This is not something, of course, that we hardly ever see. And now, as the details are coming out and the investigation is taking place, we`ll be able to determine what the mitigating factors were in the situation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How tricky is the airport -- this airport during this time of year? Because it was interesting to me that the pilot made no mayday call to indicate that he was concerned -- he or she was concerned or that something was wrong.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: Basically, on the video, it shows that everything happened pretty fast, flying in and out of Toronto myself many times in the wintertime, as we know in the Northeast, weather can play significant factor. So they`re going to be looking at things like wind gusts, wind shear -- you know, wind shear, things like that; icing on the runways, the condition of the aircraft, and what the pilots were seeing and thinking and experiencing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Captain, can you talk a bit about the training that pilots have? We`ve heard that this is a proven airline design that type of airplanes landed in this area a lot for decades, pilots obviously trained for tough conditions. What kind of prep would have been in place for a landing like this?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: Well, in this kind of winter weather conditions, you`re going to be thinking about again, icing, visibilities, what type of icing might be taking place, what type of wind conditions, wind angles, different types of gusts that might affect the aircraft, cross wind components for those type of airplanes, the capability of the aircraft and the capability of the pilots involved.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Captain, can you walk us through the communication between the pilot and air traffic control, and from what you`ve heard about this incident, could something else have been said that could have led to a safer landing?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: That`s something that the investigators will definitely look at and determine, based on air traffic control, based on the cockpit voice recorder, the flight data recorder, things of that nature, all the different factors involved in this tragic situation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, this is either the fourth or fifth airline/airport mishap in recent months, we should say, recent weeks. Does this rattle you as a pilot?\par \par \uc2 I sit here very rattled, but I`m a very nervous flyer anyway but do you think that the flying public should be concerned? I know they`re all different, too, but should the flying public be concerned?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: I want to reassure the flying public that, again, air travel is the safest mode of transportation. We have 90,000 flights that take off and land safely around the world every day, carrying millions and millions of people.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you want to pay attention. You want to be on apps and things that your airline has for you to keep you updated if there`s any type of delays or weather conditions that might affect your flight, and you always want to listen to the safety briefings of the flight attendants, always, anytime you come on board with us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but I still don`t know how you`re not rattled by this because you simply know more than we do, because you`re the expert here. I just don`t know why it`s not unnerving.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: It is concerning and in aviation, these are the kind of things that we learn from so that we can keep that safety bar as high as we possibly can, and it`s what we`re trained for.\par \par \uc2 We`re experts in compartmentalizing, so you can be assured that we put our lives on the line every day to keep everyone safe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we appreciate the investment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we know the numbers are good. The statistics are in your favor when you get on an airline.\par \par \uc2 Captain Laura Einsetler, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: Absolutely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead Americans are showing incredible resilience after the wildfires in California and other disasters.. We visit a company that claims to have a better way to build things back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As we know, national disasters can -- natural disasters can devastate entire communities. More than 16,000 homes and other structures were destroyed just in California`s January wildfires. Rebuilding them will be time consuming and costly.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News analysis found the cost of home construction supplies across the country has increased by 47 percent since 2016.\par \par \uc2 Now, one company says it can rebuild in a matter of weeks for less money. Nancy Chen looks at how it could also help tackle our country`s housing shortage.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CORONADO (Palm Beach, Florida resident): Over here, we have the living room. There`s a little balcony out there.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Jessica Coronado (sp?) has lived in this apartment near Palm Beach, Florida for almost a year.\par \par \uc2 Did you have any idea that this building is constructed differently than others?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CORONADO: I didn`t know at all. I was very surprised. I thought it was just going to be a regular apartment complex.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But this is no ordinary apartment complex. These four buildings contain 96 units and are the first of their kind in the country.\par \par \uc2 The inspiration behind the new technology, \uc2 Lego\uc2  bricks. Just 11 workers constructed the walls, floors and ceilings of each building in only eight weeks.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY (Former US representative): We built that much faster than we would have had if we`ve done it with wood or concrete, sort of a traditional material.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Patrick Murphy is a former congressman and now managing director of RENCO, the company behind this technology.\par \par \uc2 How quickly can you turn a house out of here?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: We can basically produce about 16 homes a day in this facility.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Their factory in Jupiter, Florida is scheduled to begin scaling up operations in April.\par \par \uc2 It`s no coincidence that you`re doing this here in Florida.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: No coincidence.\par \par \uc2 So we probably should have started in an easy area, but we decided to start in the toughest area, and so did years of testing to ensure that it could withstand Category Five hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The company says it can endure 240 mile per hour winds and is more fire resistant than most brick and wood and also water resistant.\par \par \uc2 Will they withstand the test of time?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: They have such a long lifespan that the insurance on our first building is about 20 percent less expensive for that sort of long term insurance.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: It doesn`t rot, it doesn`t rust. Pests don`t eat it, termites don`t eat it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: While the RENCO bricks are more expensive, Murphy says they still save 20 percent because of decreased time and labor.\par \par \uc2 We saw this wall go up in less than nine minutes. What does that mean for the speed in which these buildings can be assembled?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: Basically, it means we can get a lot more product built a lot faster with unskilled workers, right? You saw how easy it was. This entire 2,000 square foot home can be built with two people in one day.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Even an unskilled worker like me can use aerospace glue and a mallet to hammer in a brick.\par \par \uc2 That actually comes together really quickly.\par \par \uc2 Your company calls this the most disruptive innovation in construction in more than a hundred years, which is a big statement.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: The last big breakthrough there was reinforced concrete that was a hundred, a hundred and twenty-five years ago. So now all of a sudden we have this new material that`s displacing all of that.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Is this a time of change for construction?\par \par \uc2 ILLYA AZAROFF (Architect): It`s a huge time of change.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Illya Azaroff is an architect and teaches sustainable construction at \uc2 City University of New York\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ILLYA AZAROFF: Globally, last year, 114 million people were displaced for natural and man-made disasters. So when I think about that, this has to be a moment of change.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: He said, while products like RENCO have design limitations and tend to be blocky, the structural and time-saving benefits are crucial..\par \par \uc2 ILLYA AZAROFF: When I see innovation in the marketplace like this, yes, we can build to fire resistance, earthquake resistance, to hurricane force winds, tornado force winds. We just have to have the political will to do it, and we have to have the guts to try new ways of doing things.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: RENCO says it costs about $30.00 per square foot to build the walls, floors and roof, including labor and transportation. And the company said it compared the cost of that apartment building in Florida to similar ones made with concrete and saved 20 percent, which goes a long way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what is it made out of?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes, it`s a good question. It`s a composite made of glass, recycled plastic, as well as other materials, including a resin that keeps it all together. But it`s very lightweight, which can save a lot of money in terms of transportation as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really cool. The Lego component is very fascinating.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Teddy is definitely watching right now, and thinking, I want to build a house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he will be able to\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I will bring the ante.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead the stories that you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:43 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re very psyched today about our eight o`clock hour. Why? Because Oprah is on the show today. Only on CBS MORNINGS, she is going to share her latest Book Club pick, and we`ll also hear from the author. We`ll introduce him today.\par \par \uc2 Plus the story of resilience from "Inside Edition`s" Alison Hall. Remember her? She was here last month. Why she has chosen to be so open and so honest about her experience with breast cancer and what she hopes other people will learn from her journey.\par \par \uc2 So all of that is coming up in our next hour, but first, Klaus and I heading over to the table because Vlad has entered the building.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Getting ready for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Talking asteroids and Safari. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am going to put those two together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the connection? "What to Watch" right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 Cancel your plans. We`ve got an asteroid coming.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how lucky are we feeling?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pretty lucky.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony pauses.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t like these odds.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me tell you why.\par \par \uc2 There`s an asteroid that`s heading towards us, and scientists have now doubled its chances of hitting Earth. The asteroid, it`s called, YR4 was spotted by high-powered telescope last year.\par \par \uc2 At the time, \uc2 NASA\uc2  estimated there was a one percent chance of it colliding with Earth in 2032.\par \par \uc2 Now that estimate has grown to 2.3 percent. It doesn`t sound like much, right? But astronomers call that percentage uncommon.\par \par \uc2 Scientists say the asteroid is the size of a building and could possibly wipe out a major city.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which one? I will avoid that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle asking the right questions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which one. Give us that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s why she`s an award winning journalist.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nonpartisan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t want to tell us?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It doesn`t say. I mean, wherever it lands, if there`s a city in its path, that city could potentially be wiped out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on. Come on. Someone`s doing the math. Someone knows the answer to this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is also a small chance it could completely miss Earth and hit the moon, there is that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re telling me, in 2032, there is a two percent chance a city on this planet is gone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By an asteroid.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So here is a couple of things to remember. As --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you writing down the date?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s only seven years from now. We could all still be here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We most likely will all still be here. Yes. I am hoping that we are all still here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, until the asteroid comes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 So a couple of things to remember. In 2022, \uc2 NASA\uc2  did launch a DART mission that successfully pushed an asteroid --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- out of its orbit. So we do have this technology that exists to move these asteroids, depending on their size, out of orbit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I bit like that movie, "Armageddon."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But back in 2013, there was an asteroid that did hit Earth, and a couple of homes in Russia were damaged by the breakup of that asteroid.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yowzer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Remain calm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All is well.\par \par \uc2 All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s good, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Simone Biles also felt that very same way on a Safari in South Africa. The Olympic gold medalist is celebrating her second anniversary with her husband, Jonathan Owens. They couldn`t go on a honeymoon after tying the knot, so this trip is extra special.\par \par \uc2 Biles documenting their adventures on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  with photos of giraffes and elephants and more. One encounter, though, had a little bit too close for comfort for them, that`s an adult lion. That`s not -- there it is -- an adult line strolling past her vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Biles captioned the video saying she nearly blanked herself. But this is not uncommon. I experienced something very similar in May of 2023. Marian and I took our entire family to South Africa for a Safari. We had a phenomenal time, including -- this is our entire family.\par \par \uc2 But look at this, and I don`t know if we have the sound up. You could hear our guide saying, "Remain calm." Like I don`t know if we have the sound up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is two of them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, there are two and you can see as they approach, and you`ll see how close they are actually to our vehicle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, were you actually calm?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look, I`m filming. You can see, I don`t know if it`s like in a show -- my hand, there we go. There I am. That`s how close we are. Chill. Chill.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Vlad. Coming up, we have actor Nicole Ari Parker joining us for a special CBS MORNINGS Deals.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very impressive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stick around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very impressive.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, Oprah reveals her latest Book Club selection, and the author tells us what it meant to get the call from Oprah herself.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, only on CBS MORNINGS, and we love when that happens, Oprah`s latest Book Club pick, and she likes it, really likes it. She`ll tell us all about it, and we`ll meet the author.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 "Inside Edition`s" Alison Hall is here with a very personal story about her recovery from breast cancer. Why she feels it`s so important to share her journey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Ari Parker, the very talented actor and businesswoman is here for special CBS MORNINGS Deals. She wants to help people with their exercise game and Gayle, we think it`s all going to work out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she wants you to look good, too.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everything, drop it. Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All 80 people on board a Delta flight survive after their plane crashes on the tarmac.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: The next thing I know, it was kind of a blink and I`m upside down.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s just devastation and heartbreak.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another winter storm heads for the South as flooded communities struggle to recover.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m numb. I`m trying to process it all.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Top officials from the US and Russia discuss peace in Ukraine without Ukraine`s involvement.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Meanwhile, Russia released another detained American yesterday, a man arrested this month that was apparently a gesture of good will.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Days of Thunder.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: NASCAR`s Jeff Gordon reveals he has been in touch with Tom Cruise about what? A potential sequel to "Days of Thunder." Yay.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Days of Thunder.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope that happens.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really hope it happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like Jeff Gordon, I like Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So excited.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let me out of car, Cole. Let me out of the car. One of the only movies I can quote.\par \par \uc2 I love "Days of Thunder." Rooting for it.\par \par \uc2 All right, but we`re going to begin with the news. The tough news out of Toronto, that terrifying plane crash yesterday involving a \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  flight. The video that came in this morning shows the plane making its approach to the runway before crashing in a ball of fire.\par \par \uc2 The good news, 80 people on board, all safely evacuated. Many were hurt, but remarkably, everyone survived this incident.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is at Toronto`s Pearson Airport for us.\par \par \uc2 Tom, what a morning. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hey there, Tony. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the scene here at the airport vastly different than it was yesterday, both of the runways that were involved in the crash, those remain closed as investigators hit the ground to figure out what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 But I want you guys to take a look at some of this jaw dropping video that is coming out of the crash. Take a look at this. This video shows the heart stopping moments when the plane crash lands on the tarmac here at Toronto Pearson Airport, you can see the plane catch fire as it skids across the tarmac before rolling over.\par \par \uc2 We know the \uc2 Delta Airlines\uc2  plane left Minneapolis just before noon, then around 2:15 PM, crashed and flipped upside down here.\par \par \uc2 Miraculously, all 76 passengers and four crew on board made it off the plane. Eighteen people were hurt, two are listed in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 Video of the aftermath shows the plane upside down, its wings and tail ripped off. Flight attendants and crew members rushed to assist passengers evacuate the plane as fire crews went to work immediately spraying the plane`s fuselage. One man who was on the plane, shared his experience.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: As we made our descent and made touchdown. It was just a very forceful event where all of a sudden everything just kind of went sideways, and then next thing I know, it was kind of a blink and I`m upside down, still strapped in.\par \par \uc2 The most powerful part of today was -- there was just people. No countries, no nothing, just people together, helping each other.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And Monday`s plane crash marks the fifth high-profile plane crash in the last three weeks in North America. And I want to read you a statement from \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 . They say that "... the hearts of the entire global Delta family are with those affected by today`s incident." -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, once you see it, you can`t unsee it, Tom, that`s for sure. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Oprah exclusively reveals her latest book club selection, and then the author joins us in the conversation. But first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, one woman`s years long quest to highlight important figures in our history whose achievements were overlooked.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "Inside Edition" correspondent, Alison Hall will tell us about her recovery from breast cancer and why she`s grateful to actor, Olivia Munn for sharing her experience.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we honor Black History Month with a story about paying tribute to people who were once overlooked.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, an editor at "The New York Times" noticed a big disparity in the newspaper`s obituaries. They feature mostly men and not many women and people of color.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver introduces us to the devoted obituary writer who is making sure we know more about the remarkable people who blazed a trail.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: What do you find down here?\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI ("The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " obituary editor): This place is absolutely gold for any obit researcher.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Deep below street level in the morgue at "The New York Times," is where obituary editor, Amy Padnani goes digging.\par \par \uc2 From an old school card catalogue to rows of file cabinets, the room is stuffed with more than 170 years of history.\par \par \uc2 How hard is it to get in to "The New York Times" obituary?\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: You have to have done something pretty remarkable, invented something everybody uses, changed the way of life for society at large or a group of people.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Since 1851, "The Times" has published thousands of obituaries from heads of state and scientists to celebrities and athletes.\par \par \uc2 But during a research project in 2017, Padnani realized many trailblazers, especially women and people of color, were overlooked people like Sylvia Plath and Ida B. Wells.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: I thought, yes, why is that? So when I approach my team to ask this question, they said, well, obituaries are a rear view look at society at a time when women and people of color weren`t invited to the table to make a difference. I just didn`t find that answer very satisfying.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In 2018, she launched "Overlooked," a series in the obituary section to remember notable women who were left out.\par \par \uc2 She eventually expanded the following year for Black History Month to include people of color. Seven years later, it`s still going strong.\par \par \uc2 This month, she is publishing the obituaries for Maria Stewart, the first Black woman to speak publicly about racial and gender equality in the 1830s to Annie Easley.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: Annie Easley, for instance, is a scientist who worked for \uc2 NASA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: And Lena Richard.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: Lena Richard was the first Black person to host a cooking show.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The first Black woman to host a cooking show in 1949 before she could eat at the lunch counter.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: How was she overlooked?\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: We ask this question all the time.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The overwhelming response from readers compelled her to publish a book featuring 66 more stories of remarkable people, including Diane Patrick`s grandfather, Sinclair Clark.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe your grandfather?\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK (Sinclair Clark`s granddaughter): I never knew him, so I can only describe him as a rockstar, taxidermy tanner.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: A rockstar taxidermist.\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Clark`s work is on display at museums around the world, including the \uc2 American Museum of Natural History\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 What did it mean to you to see your grandfather`s obituary printed?\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: It meant that I had something that I could now show to some of the institutions that he worked in.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Patrick discovered her paternal grandfather two years before he died. Even then, she didn`t know that he worked on the iconic Hall of African Mammals in the museum.\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: His methods became the standard for these dioramas in this museum and other museums.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The gold standard around the world?\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: It seemed to me he might have been kind of quiet about his own achievements, so I just think he didn`t get the attention he deserved in his lifetime, which is part of the reason "The Times" may not have heard of him at the time that he died.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Overlooked trailblazers finally receiving their due recognition.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Meg Oliver in New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what I love about that? It is that not every trailblazer is loud. They may be proud, but they`re not loud about their achievements.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we don`t know about them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we don`t know about them,\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now, we do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is a way to celebrate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it`s a great idea. It really is, a great book as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is, and we will continue to celebrate Black History Month tomorrow. We have someone who is on the frontlines of Civil Rights.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-five years ago, four teenagers challenged a Whites-only lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina. We spoke to one of them, Air Force Major General Joseph McNeil, about the historic protest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Where did you find that courage from to be so young?\par \par \uc2 AIR FORCE MAJOR GENERAL JOSEPH MCNEIL (Member of Greensboro Four): We had strong convictions. We faced adversity. It gives you a strength. I never seriously done much of God, but I suddenly had faith.\par \par \uc2 And I`m sure it made the students the same way. We had all gotten together and determined that we can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Powerful conversation. He was just 17 years old, and you can see more of our interview with General McNeil tomorrow here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really looking forward to that, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, we will reveal Oprah`s latest Book Club selection and hear from Oprah and the author, too.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. We will right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Only on CBS MORNINGS, we are revealing Oprah`s newest Book Club selection. She said to us that this book changed her thinking. We sat down with Oprah and the author, to talk about why she liked it so much.\par \par \uc2 Here`s that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are so excited -- this is my excited face to have Oprah, as in, Winfrey, there`s only one in our studio to announce her latest Book Club selection. Drum roll. Please.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (TV personality): Drum roll. It is called --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , what is it?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : "Dream State" by Eric Puchner. I am so happy to be able to announce this book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so excited to talk about this book, because "Dream State" takes place over the course of 50 years in rural Montana. It follows a young bride who decides to leave her "picture perfect husband," that`s in quotes, after falling in love with his best friend. What?\par \par \uc2 Despite the betrayal the three remain intertwined in each other`s lives. Eric Puchner, we`re happy to say and Oprah is here.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Good morning. Congratulations to you, Mr. Eric Puchner, and this -- this is what I learned for the first time, sitting here with you and Oprah, because I was saying, "Oh, I like your suit." She said, "I picked out the suit --" to what?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Match the book cover.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. It does. Do you approve?\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER ("Dream State" author): I`m so honored. I can`t believe it. I really made it now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am always fascinated by Eric, by anything that involves betrayal, but certainly betrayal among best friends. So I was surprised that you would pick a book about betrayal about best friends. I love reading this kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Well, one of the reasons I really appreciated "Dream State" so much is because there`s this point in everybody`s life where you make a decision and that decision changes the course of your life, right?\par \par \uc2 And what if you had made the other decision? What if you had chosen to marry the other guy? What if you had not married the person that you`re with?\par \par \uc2 Everybody, I think at some point gets cold feet. I remember you telling me when you were many years ago --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: -- getting married that your mother said --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do I know?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: How do I know? And your mother said, oh, you`re just having cold feet. And so I think that this brings that story into full focus about what if you`d made another choice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the thing is, guys, this is more than just cold feet. She actually married the man and then ends up with his best friend.\par \par \uc2 Eric, where does that story come from?\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I think, Oprah, what you`re saying about making a decision and possibly wondering if it was the right decision, and then living in the rest of your life thinking about this sort of ghost life that you could have lived. I think it`s -- it`s very seductive to think that life is elsewhere and to fall through the trap door of regret.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I think.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Oh, that`s a good --\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: But that`s doesn`t mean that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ain`t that good, the trap door of regret.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: -- you would have been happier living this other life. You may, may have been just as happy, or you may have been less happy.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: What it says is that we can make a decision and you can make happiness wherever you are.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Yes, I think part of that happiness is not falling through the trap door of regret and accepting that this is the decision I made, and not dwelling on the other possibilities.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And can I just say up front, your lovely wife is here watching us at this time, you have not fallen through the trap door of regret.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You and your wife are very happy. This is not a personal story for you.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Not at all in that respect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she`s a novelist. Tell us her name.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Katharine Noel is a wonderful, wonderful novelist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I want to read Katharine`s book. Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So this has nothing to do with your own marriage, because whenever I read these kind of books, I think is it something they can relate to in their own personal lives?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : One of the reasons why I, too was drawn to the book "Dream State," is because it is in a dream state. It`s set in the dream state of Montana, and I know that that`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which is a character in the book.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, which was its own character in the book. I have loved Montana since the river runs through it, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I mean, I adore Montana, and Katharine and I have been going to Montana for the past 25 years and staying at a particular house, which is a house that`s in Katharine`s family.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And that`s the house that`s in the story.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: And it`s the house, it`s not the actual house, but the house in "Dream State" is based on a real house that I adore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and I don`t want to give the story away, because --\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I can`t replace --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- right up until the end, you keep us -- you keep us guessing right up until the end. Because I kept thinking the book looks like it`s going to about to end, but I still don`t know the answer to fill in the question that I had during the whole book.\par \par \uc2 How were you able to keep them still friends after 50 years? I was really fascinated by that.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I think friendship is really complicated, and I think that one thing I haven`t read enough of in fiction are stories about male friendship. And so I was very interested in this male friendship.\par \par \uc2 Charlie and Garrett were two of the main characters, have been best friends since college, and they form this powerful bond, and then there`s this profound betrayal. And so I was fascinated by how that might play out over 50 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I love this story, because whether you`re Gen Z, a millennial or you`re my generation, there`s something for everyone. I was talking to your producer, who is of the Gen Z generation, who recently married, was saying that she related so much to the story, and I loved having the opportunity to talk to you about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the book. Eric, congratulations, but before you go, I always like to know how people reacted when you got the call. Where were you?\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I was in my office, which gets horrible cell reception, so I had to go out into my car. I was kind of grumbling about it, because it`s this kind of depressing parking lot, and because I was sitting in my car hunched over, and then I get this phone number comes up on the screen that I`ve never seen before, and I answer it, and it`s \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Hello, Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I was just so flabbergasted. I was astonished.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, not only that, but I get -- I get the books ahead of time. I get the like reading proof copies, and I called you later to say, you know, I think there`s a couple of errors here, because you`re telling the story and you`re saying that he`s this age, but at the wedding, he was that age, and he goes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You found errors in the book? Of course, you did. Of course, you did.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: It was an early galley.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: It is an early galley and he goes, okay, that`s been corrected, Oprah. It`s been corrected.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good thing. Oprah is on the case.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations, Eric and thank you, Oprah.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Dream State" is on sale now. You can go to wherever you like to buy your books, but you can go to CBSMORNINGS.com for a reader`s guide and a free excerpt from the book, and get Eric Puchner`s conversation with Oprah on the Oprah podcast, available now on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  or wherever you go to get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 You can tell that Oprah was a good student in school. She`s catching errors in the galley, pointing them out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a very good read, very good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good habits, and it sounds like a good book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. Very good read.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead in a moment, "Inside Edition" correspondent, Alison Hall will share her experience going through breast cancer treatment and her journey to recovery. She`s hoping her story will help others.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. And Tony, it`s on you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, I`m excited to talk about this video that surfaced from Brazil.\par \par \uc2 A man from an isolated indigenous tribe along the Amazon River made contact with a more developed part of Brazil, I mean, essentially the modern world.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Validated Doc)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And this is the footage. He had these two logs with him. It looked like he was banging them together and people think he was trying 