par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does stage zero mean? I`ve never heard that phrase before, stage zero. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: So stage zero means that the cells have just turned into cancer. They`ve just turned malignant. It has not spread anywhere else yet, and it`s the earliest --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is there.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: It is there. And it is the earliest stage that a cancer can possibly be caught. So, it`s pretty incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So after the cancer was caught, you had some treatment options, some decisions to make. Walk us through those decisions and why you ultimately went with the double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes, absolutely. So I learned about my cancer, and they said that I could do a lumpectomy and radiation and hormone therapy, or I could do a double mastectomy, which would treat the cancer that I had on my right breast, but also dramatically reduce my risk for future cancer.\par \par \uc2 So I took a look at both options, and knowing at the stage of life that I`m in being a young woman, I hope to have a family one day, I wanted to do something that would reduce my risk.\par \par \uc2 My doctor has told me that I now have a less than three percent chance of ever having breast cancer again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. This is -- wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is so good.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Which sounded pretty good to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but take me through the night before the surgery. I don`t want to be inappropriate or impolite, but I`m curious about your feelings, because I was thinking, if it was me, what would I do? I would stand in the mirror and just look at my boobs one more time. Might have a conversation with myself. What did you do?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: That`s exactly what I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you do that?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: That`s exactly what I did. I took a shower, I had a good cry, I listened to my favorite music. I looked in the mirror --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which was what? What? What did you listen to?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: The Head and the Heart, a folk band I love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: And I looked in the mirror and I thanked my breasts. I said, Thank you for your service. You`ve given me 33 good years, and it`s time to move on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I`m ready for this new chapter, and I`m ready to not live in fear.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At the risk of being inappropriate, I`m just asking a follow up, because I was curious too, and as a man, I don`t know how to ask, do you miss your breasts?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was my next question, too.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes. You know what? I really don`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I decided to go the reconstruction route. So I`m doing a two part reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: So when I woke up, I did look down and things looked a little different.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: But I have started the reconstruction process where I have temporary implants currently, and then they`ll replace them with more permanent implants in a few months.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: But I still feel like myself, because this new body of mine is built from a place of strength and control.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: And I don`t look down anymore and think what`s going on in there. Is there a cancer there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re pleased with the results?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes. They look surprisingly great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, you are back to work, and a few people that -- a few people that you may be familiar with, wanted to send you a little message. So check this out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM MORET ("Inside Edition" correspondent): Welcome back, Alison.\par \par \uc2 STEVE FABIAN ("Inside Edition" correspondent): Hey, Hall, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH NORVILLE ("Inside Edition" anchor): Alison, I`m so proud of you.\par \par \uc2 LISA GUERRERO ("Inside Edition): Alison, you are an inspiration. Thank you so much for your courage, your guts. You are a warrior.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH NORVILLE: Thank you for teaching us that we have to be proactive about our health, that we have to ask the right questions, and most importantly, as you did, learn to trust your gut.\par \par \uc2 MARY CALVI ("Inside Edition Weekend" anchor): How you`ve handled this with such courage and such grace is such an inspiration to me and to countless women across the country.\par \par \uc2 JIM MORET: You have grace and empathy, intelligence and tenacity, and you are changing the world one story at a time.\par \par \uc2 STEVE FABIAN: We missed you so much, and we`re so glad you`re doing okay.\par \par \uc2 LISA GUERRERO: We appreciate you, we honor you, and we`re so excited to have you back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We really are. That`s so nice.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those are your colleagues, of course.\par \par \uc2 I know you -- this story came to you through Olivia Munn, and we showed Olivia Munn reach out to you through us, and given a contact, given a message to you. I have heard that you two have communicated with each other?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes, she`s been absolutely incredible. After our segment here on CBS MORNINGS, she reached out privately and offered even more words of encouragement and love, and it just meant so, so much to me. I will forever be in debt to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How much time did you take off? Because it seems only yesterday, honestly, that you were here. I know it wasn`t, but how much time did you take off?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I was off work for a full month. Four weeks, which felt really good to me. And of course, "Inside Edition" has been incredible saying if I needed more time or if I need to take a day here and there, but after about four weeks, I was really anxious and eager to come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, and so the takeaway for people is, get that screening.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Get that screening, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Alison Hall, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Alison. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So we`re so impressed, we`re so happy you`re doing better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you want to see more of Alison`s reporting on "Inside Edition," you can, all you`ve got to do is check your local listings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yup. That will do it for us. We will see you tomorrow right here on CBS MORNINGS. Your favorite morning show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250218el2i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for February 14, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Margaret Brennan, Scott MacFarlane, Steve Hartman,  Lilia Luciano, Maurice DuBois, Lonnie Quinn, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If you check the Justice Department`s Web site, as we did today, you will find at the top of the mission statement the words "to uphold the rule of law."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 And on that same Web site, under the heading "Our Values," you will see "independence and impartiality."\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But after yet another federal prosecutor resigned in protest today, is the department, under the influence of President Trump, living up to its mission or its values?\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Seven U.S. prosecutors have now quit, rather than follow orders from Trump allies in the department to drop criminal charges against New York City`s Mayor Eric Adams.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But the implications of this go far beyond the Adams case. They go to the integrity of the justice system throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane -- Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A day of absolute turmoil inside the Department of Justice amid these concerns about efforts to dismiss the case against Mayor Adams, who pleaded not guilty to trading official government actions for gifts and travel.\par \par \uc2 But the concerns are spreading into concerns that prosecutors in communities big and small could be on a short leash and under the president`s thumb.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): The aftershocks of the corruption case against New York City Mayor Eric Adams might be more powerful than the initial earthquake itself. A behind-the-scenes battle has unfolded inside the Department of Justice between the veteran prosecutors and the new Trump-aligned leaders.\par \par \uc2 It surfaced yesterday and has escalated. Trump`s one-time personal defense attorney Emil Bove, who now helps run the Department of Justice, has one at a time ordered top prosecutors in Manhattan and Washington to intervene and dismiss the case against Adams, arguing, in part, the administration needs Adams to be free from the case to help the president`s immigration enforcement efforts in New York City.\par \par \uc2 One after another, those prosecutors have quit in protest, including an assistant U.S. attorney in New York who not only refused to drop the charges against Adams, but in a letter announcing his resignation said: "I expect you will eventually find someone who is enough of a fool or enough of a coward to file your motion, but it was never going to be me."\par \par \uc2 Tom Dupree served in George W. Bush`s Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this back-and-forth, this mass resignation wave?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE (Former U.S. Deputy Assistant Attorney General): Well, it`s reminiscent of the Nixon era, where you saw the president basically try to find someone who would carry out his orders in the Justice Department and had to go through several officials before he finally was able to find someone who would carry it out.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The president today denied any involvement.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Well, I don`t know about it. I mean, obviously, I`m not involved in that.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Further fueling the controversy, Adams appeared side by side with Trump`s border czar in media interviews today after denying his team offered a quid pro quo to work with Trump`s team on immigration to get the case dropped.\par \par \uc2 Some fear the Trump administration is sending a loud message to 93 federal prosecutors nationwide in communities big and small, from Seattle to St. Louis to Savannah to Scranton: Don`t cross the president when you pick a case.\par \par \uc2 This feels like it`s bigger than just Eric Adams.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE: This goes way beyond Adams. This goes really to the Justice Department as a whole. And, make no mistake, in my view, the deputy attorney general was trying to send a message throughout the Justice Department that people need to follow the orders, and, if they don`t follow the orders, they need to leave the department.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Scott, those 93 federal prosecutors around the country you mentioned, how are they reacting to all of this tonight?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Maurice, this commanded their attention all day. They watched. They exchanged text messages.\par \par \uc2 This is all so disorienting for them. There are a couple hundred public corruption cases prosecuted in states across the country each year, sheriffs, school administrators, mayors accused of misconduct. It`s unclear if they have to check with the White House now before some of them prosecute the case in front of them.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott, how does this Adams business end from the Justice Department standpoint?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Just after the doors closed here tonight, John, they finally made the filing. A prosecutor here in Washington, along with Emil Bove, the number three at the Department of Justice, submitted the paperwork in court.\par \par \uc2 A judge will have to allow this case to be dismissed, Maurice and John. There may be a hearing and hear some arguments before the judge agrees.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott MacFarlane at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: If there were any doubts among America`s European allies that there`s a new sheriff in town, they were erased today when Vice President J.D. Vance said those exact words in his first major speech on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : At the Munich Security Conference, where much of the focus is on the Russian invasion of Ukraine, Vance lectured European politicians about free speech, immigration and the need for cooperation with the far right. And he downplayed the threat of the strategic alliance between U.S. adversaries Russia and China.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (Vice President of the United States): The threat that I worry the most about vis-a-vis Europe is not Russia. It`s not China. It`s not any other external actor. And what I worry about is the threat from within, the retreat of Europe from some of its most fundamental values, values shared with the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we are joined now by Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 OK, that`s what J.D. Vance is worried about, Margaret. What are -- how do the Europeans receive that, and how does that affect U.S. national interests in this tight moment?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, it sounded to a lot of these allies of the United States like they were being described as adversaries or autocrats.\par \par \uc2 It was really, the focus here, two things. One of them was migration and the second was what Vice President Vance calls censorship. He was trying to bring that MAGA message of the enemy within to the global stage. And, specifically, he says that European leaders aren`t showing that they have strong democracies when they sideline controversial groups.\par \par \uc2 He went out of his way to meet with the head of the AfD. That`s a political party that`s under investigation in Germany because it`s so far right and for potentially conducting extremism. And he mentioned that car ramming in Munich recently conducted by an Afghan migrant as an example of what he says is out-of-control migration. He says that`s proof you have got to bring immigration skeptics into the government.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Margaret, he also met with Zelenskyy. How did that meeting go?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you know, Maurice, there are no diplomatic talks yet under way to actually end the war between Ukraine and Russia, though there`s a promise to try to get there.\par \par \uc2 What we saw today was the president of Ukraine meeting with the U.S. and talking about a joint venture, an investment in critical rare earth minerals. These are the things that you can use in electronics. President Trump is trying to shore that up in order to sort of have it as a reward for all the billions of dollars in weapons the U.S. has provided.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Margaret Brennan there in D.C., thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Margaret will be along Sunday with Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 In the investigation of the deadly midair collision near Washington, the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says the pilots of the Army helicopter may not have heard two messages from air traffic control and the Black Hawk may have given the crew bad data about its altitude. Kris Van Cleave also reports, there`s growing evidence the plane attempted evasive maneuvers right before the collision. Kris will have more on \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS PLUS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : President Trump signed an order today to stop federal funding for schools that require COVID vaccines. It`s not expected to have much impact because most of those mandates have already been dropped.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And a Texas judge fined a New York doctor $100,000 for sending abortion medications to a woman in Texas, where most abortions are banned. The same doctor faces criminal charges in a similar case in Louisiana, pitting the governors of Louisiana and New York against each other.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan talked with a prosecutor in that case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY CLAYTON (Louisiana District Attorney): We`re prosecuting the doctor because she violated Louisiana laws for aiding and abetting in committing abortion. Bottom line is, come answer the warrant.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voice-over): Tony Clayton is the district attorney in Louisiana who filed the criminal charges against New York doctor Margaret Carpenter.\par \par \uc2 TONY CLAYTON: If the abortion crime were a tailor-made suit, it would custom-fit the actions of Dr. Carpenter.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How much time is she facing? What`s the penalty?\par \par \uc2 TONY CLAYTON: That`s up to a judge. So it ranges from five to 15. It depends on what...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Five to 15 years behind bars?\par \par \uc2 TONY CLAYTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The doctor sent pregnancy-ending medication to the mother of a 17-year-old in Louisiana. The drugs mifepristone and misoprostol are illegal in the state, with few exceptions. Clayton alleges the mother forced her pregnant daughter to take the medication. He now wants the doctor to face a jury.\par \par \uc2 TONY CLAYTON: If you`re innocent, Dr. Carpenter, then these 12 people down here will tell you you`re innocent. If you`re guilty, they`re going to tell you you`re guilty. What`s wrong with come and face the music?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  refuses to extradite Carpenter, who co-founded a group that has advocated making abortion medication available by mail even to states where it`s restricted or banned.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): I will make sure that everyone in the state knows, keep your hands off this doctor.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The governor of Louisiana, the attorney general, the prosecutor we spoke to all say you are not respecting law in Louisiana. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: I say I`m respecting the laws of New York. Am I supposed to make those subservient to laws in another state?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Governor Hochul signed legislation earlier this month expanding a New York law that protects doctors who provide medication abortion care outside of New York.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: The Supreme Court may have to get involved in resolving a dispute between two states` interpretation.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Do you expect this case to go to the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: I expect that I will stand firm to protect this doctor. So she`s staying right here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Jericka Duncan joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, what happens if this doctor travels outside of New York and then gets pulled over or something?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You know, that`s the thing here. Governor Hochul said that she cannot protect Dr. Carpenter if she leaves the state.\par \par \uc2 So this is a situation where the governor says, as long as she`s here, she has my support. But if she steps outside, she very well could find herself in a situation where she has to go answer those charges in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So then where does the case go from here?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So, because this is uncharted territory and we`re talking about a case with these two contradictory laws, it could go before the Supreme Court, as you heard in the report, Governor Hochul saying she`s willing to take on that fight.\par \par \uc2 Also, Clayton, the prosecutor there in Louisiana, saying the same thing. But this is what some people did expect with the overturning of Roe v. Wade, when you have so many states operating under different laws in different jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS: Lonnie Quinn tracking a storm that could bring flooding to the South.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we will have these stories as well:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: I`m Lilia Luciano. We went into one of the world`s most notorious prisons, a place where the U.S. may send some deportees.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I`m Steve Hartman. Forget expensive chocolates. If you want a long, happy relationship, this couple says you can have it for a song.\par \par \uc2 The story when we go On the Road to Sioux Falls, South Dakota, coming up on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The United States this week began deporting Africans and Asians who are in this country illegally, not back to their home countries, but to Panama -- excuse me -- Panama.\par \par \uc2 Panama, along with Guatemala and El Salvador, has agreed to take people deported from the U.S. no matter where they are from.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : El Salvador`s president said he will accept deported criminals for a fee. The money, he said, would keep his prisons running. The State Department has long warned about harsh conditions in those prisons.\par \par \uc2 And Lilia Luciano took a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice-over): It takes about a 90-minute drive from El Salvador`s capital to the most notorious prison in the country. We knew we were close when we noticed all cell phone service was blocked.\par \par \uc2 The official name of this place is the Center for the Confinement of Terrorism. Inmates call it by its acronym, CECOT.\par \par \uc2 I have been asking for over a year-and-a-half for access into this place, and this is the first time we`re getting it.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: These are the most violent criminals in the country. And this is where El Salvador`s president has offered to house some deportees from the United States. The stated capacity is 40,000 inmates.\par \par \uc2 We were surprised when we learned it`s only half full. The U.S. would send deportees convicted of crimes. Exactly what kind is unclear. We saw cells with anywhere between 80 to 100 men crammed inside.\par \par \uc2 We are not allowed to interact, to speak to any of the prisoners. They are all staring at us. It`s so striking to see them. Inmates are outside of their cell for just 30 minutes a day, and no visitors are allowed.\par \par \uc2 Is it possible to get a tattoo like this if you`re not a gang member?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN VASQUEZ (Inmate): No. You got to kill.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Marvin Vasquez (sp?) belongs to the infamous MS-13 gang. He was arrested here in El Salvador. The government declared war on the gangs in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to be here the rest of your life?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN VASQUEZ: Yes, the rest of my life. We murdered a lot of people, and this is the consequence of what happened to us. It`s like that Titanic, that we were a big and strong gang, but we got hit with the iceberg. Like, we tried to act strong in the day and cry in the nighttime.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: You cry at night?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN VASQUEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Most Salvadorans support President Nayib Bukele`s efforts to lock up gang members. But advocates for migrants in the United States worry this is no place for anyone but the most dangerous people.\par \par \uc2 Noah Bullock is the executive director of Cristosal, a human rights organization.\par \par \uc2 When you heard that Bukele offered to receive U.S. deportees, what came to mind?\par \par \uc2 NOAH BULLOCK (Executive Director, Cristosal): I worry about the idea that people who are undesirable in the United States can just be sent to basically a transnational penal colony without any guarantees of rights or due process.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Lilia Luciano joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Extraordinary access, Lilia. I know you had to be afraid, but...\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: ... how likely is it that migrants from here are going to be sent there?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: OK, that`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 Look, the agreement is that the people who would go to prisons would be convicted of crimes. But El Salvador can receive deportees of all kinds. We have seen in Guantanamo, we thought it was going to be people who were violent, and half of them don`t have a criminal record.\par \par \uc2 And what I did learn about the system in El Salvador is that there are, according to the studies and the advocates, thousands of people who are held without evidence indefinitely, without communication to the outside world, in basically what they`re calling a black hole of human rights, and that those prisons are worse in terms of conditions than CECOT.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Wow. Black hole is right.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now to some potentially dangerous weather.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn is following the flooding in the South and ice in the Northeast -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Guys, believe it or not, both those threats that you`re talking about are all connected to one system. It`s a storm right now around Chicago, where it looks like it`s snowing, right?\par \par \uc2 Well, you know what? Let`s open the curtains here and I will show you what`s going on. Daley Plaza, what looked like snow on the radar picture is not making its way down to the surface. The air is just a little too dry. Now, that system is going to become stronger, because, as it pushes to the east, it starts pulling in some Gulf moisture.\par \par \uc2 So, on Saturday, you betcha, the Northeast is dealing with snow, but it`s going to transition with warmer air on Sunday. That becomes rain, sizable rain. And look at this. In between, so Saturday night and early Sunday morning, look at that. The pink color on the map, that`s some icing. And this is a lot of icing anywhere from wherever you see that pink color, the Poconos toward the Southern Green Mountains, a quarter-of-an-inch of ice, if not more, on everything, roadways, car tops.\par \par \uc2 It`s just a tough thing to try to maneuver your vehicle in. And if the talk -- and talking about the flash flooding still relates to that same system. You see it right here. Look what we`re dealing with in the Tennessee Valley. It`s a four out of four scale for flash flooding for a place like Bowling Green.\par \par \uc2 The amount of rain that we`re talking about in a 24-hour period, ready for this number? Let`s bring the map up and open it up here nice and wide for everybody. Nine inches of rain from 7:00 tomorrow morning until 7:00 on Sunday morning. Portions of the Tennessee Valley could very well be underwater because of that -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 (WHISTLING)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Coming up next on this Valentine`s Day, the secret to a happy marriage.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : From Steve Hartman On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now, on this Valentine`s Day, the story of two lovebirds.\par \par \uc2 It`s been nearly seven decades since he said "I do" and she said "I do." And all these years later, they still do every night.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Steve Hartman On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voice-over): In less time than it takes to make a cup of tea...\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT (Sioux Falls, South Dakota, Resident): Two minutes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... I will explain the secret of a long and happy marriage...\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT (Sioux Falls, South Dakota, Resident): There.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... as told to me by 94-year-old Don Barnett...\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: Here you go.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... and his 93-year-old wife, Marilyn, of Sioux Falls, South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: I will get this done.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Their public displays of affection made possible, they say, by their private nightly ritual.\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: It`s very simple.\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: And it`s very easy to do.\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: We wouldn`t go to sleep without that.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It happens...\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: You want a song?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... right before bed.\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Don sits down beside his bride of 68 years and starts to sing.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: I just look forward to it.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: And he has a beautiful voice.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: They started doing this about 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: And I discovered a couple of things. First of all, it pleased her. And, secondly, it calmed us. And if we had a disagreement or something before, it was gone by the time we went to sleep.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A magical musical elixir....\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: You got it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... that their son Doug says happens without fail.\par \par \uc2 Whether they`re here or in a hotel room or in the hospital?\par \par \uc2 DOUG BARNETT (Son): Doesn`t matter. I`m not aware that they ever miss it.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Last month, Marilyn fell and broke her hip.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And, sure enough, her nightly serenade continued without skipping a beat.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: When you sing to your spouse, she realizes you really care enough to go out of your way, rather than just saying: "Good night, honey."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: Thirty-one across.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This Valentine`s weekend, couples will be searching for just the right words to show their love.\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: Yes, that works.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But for Don and Marilyn, there`s nothing to say...\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... that can`t be sung.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman On the Road in Sioux Falls, South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 DON BARNETT: Geez.\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BARNETT: Oh.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, but there is more coming up on EVENING NEWS PLUS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We will have details of a new wave of layoffs in the federal government, and we will tell you about a new medication that relieves pain without the risk of abuse or addiction. That`s on EVENING NEWS PLUS streaming on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we hope you will join us again right here on Monday. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250215el2e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 California Wildfire Emergency; Trump`s Controversial Comments; Lying in State; AI Used in Cybertruck Blast; \uc2 Matter of Fact\uc2 ; Alison Hall`s Breast Cancer Battle; Remember Peter Yarrow; New Year, New Reads; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Julia Michaels Live; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Jonathan Vigliotti,  Kris Van Cleave, Alex Biston, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Nikole Killion, Andres  Gutierrez, Kelly O`Grady, Vladimir Duthiers, Carter Evans \par \uc2 14,560 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 January 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this Wednesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, of course, is on assignment.\par \par \uc2 The big story from California. You`ve seen these terrible pictures, the California wildfires. We`ll have more on that with Jonathan Vigliotti and Kris Van Cleave in just a second, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Apocalyptic scenes of destruction around Los Angeles, as wildfires fueled by high winds force tens of thousands to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: You can`t even see through the smoke. This entire mountainside is covered in flames, the wind pushing them up to 50 feet into the sky.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President-elect Trump refuses to rule out using military force to try and take control of Greenland and the Panama Canal.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President-elect of the United States): No, I can`t assure you either of those two.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  is honored in Washington flying in state at the Capitol Rotunda.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice President of the United States): He lived his faith. He served the people.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Peter Yarrow, the legendary folk music trio of Peter, Paul and Mary has died. Among their hits was "Puff the Magic Dragon."\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG\uc2  (\uc2 Meta\uc2  CEO): Factcheckers have just been too politically biased.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  says \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is getting rid of factcheckers.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON ("After Midnight" host): Is this just what \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  looks like now?\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: I guess someone got the Kendrick Lamar album for Hanukkah.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): President-elect Trump held a press conference today in Mar-a-Lago and spoke for more than an hour.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): He covered all the big issues, including his plan to rebrand the ocean itself.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We`re going to be changing the name of the Gulf of Mexico to the Gulf of America.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): So now, if I understand this correctly, the Gulf of Mexico will become the Gulf of America. New Mexico will be Dry America, and Cinco de Mayo will be the Fifth of Mayo.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got to get, of course, right to the story. I don`t know about you guys, I stayed up late just watching it over and over and over again, and you feel so helpless sitting there watching it, but I can`t take my eyes off of it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s incredibly difficult to see what people are going through in Los Angeles right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is. It is so upsetting. And we`re going to begin, of course, with that. The extremely dangerous conditions around Los Angeles as those multiple wildfires continue to just burn out-of-control, including the one you see here.\par \par \uc2 This is the Palisades Fire, which has destroyed multiple homes and the businesses forced tens of thousands of people to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is just one of three fires in the area that is fueled by the high winds, including the Eaton Fire that`s northeast of LA and the Hurst Fire that`s in the northern outskirts.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we`ve got team coverage all over this. We`re going to start with Jonathan Vigliotti. He is in Malibu.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And Gayle, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Simply put, this is a hellscape. There are so many homes on fire. It is unusual to see a home that is not.\par \par \uc2 We`re here on the Pacific Coast Highway in Malibu, and look at what 80 mile per hour wind gusts are doing to these flames. This home devoured the one next to it about to go up in flames as well.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, fire crews here stretched thin. We have seen very few here on the road, as they are battling fires throughout this area.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: A community completely destroyed by the ferocious wind- driven Palisades Fire on the west side of Los Angeles in Pacific Palisades.\par \par \uc2 One man seen trying to use a hose to put out the flames got burned in the process. We visited one neighborhood overnight, which is a total loss.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got this one over here across the street, another one up in flames. You pan over here, homes lost on this street. A home here gone.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters aren`t trying to save anything here. They`re trying to put out these flames to prevent them from jumping to other neighborhoods. I`ve never seen anything like this.\par \par \uc2 The Palisades Fire broke out Tuesday morning, and within 20 minutes, exploded to more than 200 acres, creating the walls of flames you see here.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CROWLEY (Los Angeles Fire Chief): Los Angeles is currently experiencing an extreme fire weather condition. With the winds as unpredictable as they are, there is very, very much at risk.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: While evacuation notifications were sent out, traffic built up on the few main routes in and out of the area, leaving vehicles stuck in the gridlock as flames advanced closer.\par \par \uc2 The flames moved in so quickly, people who were trying to evacuate, they panicked. Every car that you see here represents someone or a family that in that state of fear thought the best option was to abandon their cars and flee on foot.\par \par \uc2 A bulldozer cleared 100 cars from the roadway to create a path for fire engines.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Come on guys. We`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Our own \uc2 CBS\uc2  news crew nearly escaped flames as the fire grew to encompass the whole town.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  urged residents to heed evacuation orders.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Listen to these evacuation orders. We`ve all been to memorials. I`ve never been to a memorial for a building.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Throughout the night, flames threaten landmarks like The Getty Villa.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My heart is in my throat. Pray. But God doesn`t stop this -- this wind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: In all of my years covering wildfires, I have never seen anything quite like this. I mean home after home, an iconic Malibu destroyed. There are more homes on fire than men and women that can get to them.\par \par \uc2 The LA Fire Department calling on all backup reserves off duty to come into service for the first time in 19 years, and this wind will continue to pick up over the next few hours -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, please be safe.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to Kris Van Cleave in Pasadena, which is also under severe threat.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning. What are you seeing there? Wow.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 This is the Eaton Fire. Last update was about a thousand acres, it is likely bigger than that, but we`re actually nowhere close to that fire. These winds are so strong they`re picking up embers, and wherever they land, they start a fire.\par \par \uc2 This house, firefighters have been trying to save, they weren`t able to. Now, it is fully engulfed.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you what we saw just a little while ago up here.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: This house is a goner. The effort now is, can they save the neighboring house? They`re trying to keep the fire off of the fence so it doesn`t jump onto the neighboring home.\par \par \uc2 But there`s so much wind right now and these red, hot embers coming from all over. Wherever they land, they can spark a fire. If it lands on a house, that house can go up in minutes, and there just aren`t enough firefighters to fight every fire right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: You can hear the screams there. This was a family that got an evacuation order, and the evacuation orders come "Leave now."\par \par \uc2 It`s terrifying for families in this area and across the Southland who have been told to get out. Also frightening moments, a retirement community here in Pasadena, they brought in any vehicle they could find -- city bus, SWAT vehicles -- to get folks out of this retirement home that was in the potential path of the fire and to somewhere safe.\par \par \uc2 It is a dangerous evolving situation, and because of these super strong winds, you`ll get these gusts that are almost hurricane force winds, they pick up those embers. They can blow them a mile or more away.\par \par \uc2 The main Eaton Fire, guys, is nowhere near us. It`s about a mile up the hill.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow. Kris Van Cleave reporting.\par \par \uc2 Kris, stay safe. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And check out these live pictures coming into us from the Pacific Palisades. This is from CBS Los Angeles. It shows just how hard the battle to fight the flames is right now.\par \par \uc2 And for more on that, we`re joined by Captain Sheila Kelliher of the Los Angeles County Fire Department.\par \par \uc2 Captain Kelliher, thank you so much for taking some time to be with us this morning, I know under incredibly, incredibly stressful situation.\par \par \uc2 Let me just begin. You said yesterday you expected the worst of the wind and the fire to come overnight. As daylight approaches in Los Angeles, where is the fire now? How is it currently spreading?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN SHEILA KELLIHER (Los Angeles County Fire Department): Listen, that`s exactly what happened with those winds. They increased overnight. I was over there all evening. I just came over the other side to get cleaned up and changed, and those winds just got worse and worse, and they`re ferocious.\par \par \uc2 So, it is hurricane force winds, and you can imagine trying to fight fire in those winds. It is really incredibly challenging and volatile and unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, Sheila, I was watching last night. I`m so haunted by so many pictures. I saw a shot of a man in his home with a dog surrounded by flames. I kept waking up thinking, God, I wonder if they got out all night.\par \par \uc2 I can`t imagine what it`s like for you all who are covering this fire. What`s your biggest challenge today? I know you said the winds, you said it was going to be a rough night, and clearly it was.\par \par \uc2 What`s your biggest challenge now? And what are you focusing on?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN SHEILA KELLIHER: I think, besides the winds and the topography, I think because we have so many major incidents that are going on at the same time, so we now have three major incidents, and that`s just a big strain and stretch of resources.\par \par \uc2 So it takes a small village, a small city, actually, to run each one of these fires. So we`ve been up 24 hours plus, as we keep into all day long.\par \par \uc2 So the challenges is going to be, how much is going on and we do a lot with less sometimes, but that`s going to be tested today and tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Captain Kelliher, as Gayle mentioned, we have seen these videos that are just shocking. People trapped in their homes or even in their cars surrounded by fire. So what do you tell people that might catch themselves in that situation?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN SHEILA KELLIHER: Don`t get caught in that situation. Get out when those warnings come. Pack up, get ready to go.\par \par \uc2 We have an app in Southern California called Genesis App, which is incredible because it gives you all the up-to-date current evacuations for your area. It`s an incredible resource. People need to get on the website. It is free. And they can see where their region stands. So, that`s really important number one.\par \par \uc2 And then sometimes, as you can see, the cars had to be left behind, and a lot of times, just even getting out on foot, you`ve got to get going.\par \par \uc2 So, it has been crazy. I sat -- I was just on the other side of PCH by the ocean and watched it come down that whole hill and we had 300-foot flame links, and it just ripped across that and like you`re saying, those embers are -- it looks like snow. It looks like driving sleet. It is how hard those embers are blowing past us and it burned down several lifeguard towers, you know, on the on the sand, so after jumping the 101 -- or the Pacific Coast Highway.\par \par \uc2 So it`s extreme fire behavior, and it`s hard to explain that. You can see it in the pictures, but you`re going to have -- we`re going to have our hands full for a while.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it looks like it. Captain Sheila Kelliher, thank you so much for joining us. Strength and courage with your team.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have mentioned high winds and weather conditions that are playing a big role in these fires. Meteorologist, Alex Biston from our Los Angeles station, KCAL joins us now with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Alex, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So what is the situation with the wind out there?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BISTON: Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 You know what? It is all about those very strong offshore Santa Ana winds. We have seen wind gusts anywhere from sixty, seventy, even 80 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you the wind forecast because we`re looking at those peak winds through the morning hours. It is still going to be blustery as we head into this afternoon. You know we are under that extreme fire risk for some portions of Ventura and Los Angeles County today.\par \par \uc2 I want to give you a look at some of the wind gusts that we`ve seen. I mean, again, it`s been sixty, seventy, eighty miles per hour. Those winds will die down somewhat as we head into this afternoon and into this evening, but we`re still seeing that offshore flow, and it is going to impact much of Southern California.\par \par \uc2 We are under a Red Flag Warning. Again, much of Southern California under a high wind warning and a Red Flag Warning -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Alex Biston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is other news this morning.\par \par \uc2 President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he will not rule out using the military to expand the territory of the United States. He also told reporters that people charged or convicted for the January 6 on the Capitol can expect -- in his word -- major pardons.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns was there for the news conference.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ignited controversy on Tuesday saying he wants the United States to take over the Panama Canal and Greenland, and that he won`t rule out the use of military force to do it.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : No, I can`t assure you on either of those two, but I can say this, we need them for economic security.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The US built the Panama Canal, but transferred operational control of it to Panama in 1999 and Chinese shipping has increasingly used the key trade route.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And we gave the Panama Canal to Panama. We didn`t give it to China, and they`ve abused it.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The Arctic island of Greenland is a territory of Denmark.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We need Greenland for National Security purposes.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But as his son, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Jr. landed in Greenland`s capital Tuesday for what he called a personal visit, the Danish Prime Minister made clear that territory was not for sale.\par \par \uc2 The president-elect also suggested using economic force to annex Canada and make it the 51st state.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : You get rid of that artificially drawn line, and you take a look at what that looks like. We`re spending hundreds of billions a year to take care of Canada. We lose in trade deficits.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  responded: "There isn`t a snowball`s chance in hell" his country would become part of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Trump also said he wants to rename the Gulf of Mexico the Gulf of America.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : What a beautiful name, and it`s appropriate.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And Trump didn`t rule out pardons for those who were charged with committing acts of violence on January 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Are you planning to pardon those who were charged with violent offenses?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Well, we`re looking at it. People that were doing some bad things weren`t prosecuted, and people that didn`t even walk into the building are in jail right now.\par \par \uc2 So we`ll be looking at the whole thing, but I`ll be making major pardons, yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And also here in Florida, yesterday, a judge temporarily blocked the final report of Jack Smith`s investigations into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from being released. This is as Trump`s legal team had won it.\par \par \uc2 Now, later today, Trump will head to Washington to meet with congressional leaders ahead of attending Jimmy Carter`s funeral there on Thursday -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And this morning, former President Carter is lying in state at the US Capitol, where people are passing by his casket to pay their respects. This follows a day of ceremonies honoring the only president ever to graduate from the \uc2 US Naval Academy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion has more on the farewell for Carter in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The sun set over the US Capitol as the flag-draped casket of the late President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  was carried up the East front steps, linked on both sides by a Military Honor Guard, as his family and former Cabinet members looked on.\par \par \uc2 Inside the ornate rotunda, dignitaries from the vice president to members of the Supreme Court and Congress, paid their final respects with bipartisan praise.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: James Earl Carter, Jr. loved our country. He lived his faith. He served the people and he left the world better than he found it.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): When \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  took his oath of office, I was just four years old. It`s obvious now to me, as an adult, why he captured everyone`s attention.\par \par \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  was a member of the greatest generation.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The ceremony capped a day of tributes that began at the Carter Presidential Center in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 The 39th president departed his home state on Special Air Mission 39 arriving at Joint Base Andrews in Washington. From there, he was transported to the US Navy Memorial with a formation of midshipmen honoring the fellow submariner.\par \par \uc2 His remains were transferred to a horse-drawn cassone making the trek down Pennsylvania Avenue. It mirrored the same route Carter took during his Inaugural Parade, onlookers once again lining the streets.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: As members of the public pay their respects here at the Capitol, several groups with ties to the late president are also expected throughout the day. Tomorrow, a state funeral will be held at Washington`s National Cathedral -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nikole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, how artificial intelligence helped plan the cybertruck explosion in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, how an "Inside Edition" correspondent`s life may have been saved by the celebrity she was covering. It`s a story that could help you, too.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s alarming new information about the cybertruck explosion outside the Trump International Hotel in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the Army Green Beret who was driving used artificial intelligence to help build the explosive devices.\par \par \uc2 Andres Gutierrez has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: Las Vegas Metro Police said Tuesday that Matthew Livelsberger used ChatGPT to help plan his cybertruck bombing calling the development a game changer.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCMAHILL (Las Vegas, Nevada Sheriff): I think this is the first incident that I`m aware of on US soil where ChatGPT is utilized to help an individual build a particular device.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: Livelsberger asked a chatbot for information on fireworks and other explosive materials, as well as the speed of bullets fired from certain guns.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  which developed ChatGPT said their models are designed to minimize harmful content, and warned Livelsberger against conducting illegal activities.\par \par \uc2 Police also released new video that shows Livelsberger pouring racing fuel into the rented cyber truck prior to his arrival at the hotel. The vehicle was full of at least 60 pounds of explosive material, including fireworks and 70 pounds of birdshot.\par \par \uc2 Police say the muzzle flash from the gun he used to shoot himself might have triggered the blast.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH COOPER (ASAC ATF San Francisco, California Division): It is important to note that the consumer fireworks are designed specifically to not mass detonate. This limited the explosive effects.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: Officials also showed additional writings by Livelsberger, where he claimed he had no intention of killing anyone besides himself.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Andres Gutierrez in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, an update from California on the devastating wildfires.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where we continue to follow multiple wildfires engulfing neighborhoods around Los Angeles, including the Pacific Palisades. This is an extremely volatile situation.\par \par \uc2 Many homes have already been destroyed. Tens of thousands of people have evacuated the area, and hundreds of thousands have lost power.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is on the frontlines of the Palisades Fire, which is spreading with dangerous speed.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: This has been a terrifying 15 hours where every minute, the destruction seems to only get worse. We`re here in Malibu on the Pacific Coast Highway, and you`re looking at easily 10 homes engulfed in flames.\par \par \uc2 We have wind gusts exceeding 80 miles per hour. Flames reaching 50 feet into the air.\par \par \uc2 This fire began yesterday morning. We were quick to arrive in the Pacific Palisades, and we watched as blow torch conditions devoured easily hundreds of homes.\par \par \uc2 There are so many structures on fire right now, there simply aren`t enough men and women of the Fire Department to hold these flames back. The wind will continue to grow for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 When all is said and done, the hope is this afternoon, aircraft will be able to launch and drop their retardant here. Thirty thousand plus people under a mandatory evacuation, and many of them will return home to nothing left -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jonathan, thank you. Be safe.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we`re going to turn now to the changes coming to \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the owner of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Founder and CEO, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , announced yesterday, the social media company is getting rid of its fact-checking program. Instead, it will rely on users to correct inaccurate and false posts.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady looks at what the change means for you and what could be driving it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG\uc2  (\uc2 Meta\uc2  CEO): It`s time to get back to our roots around free expression on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . The factcheckers have just been too politically biased and have destroyed more trust than they`ve created.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: \uc2 Meta\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  announced a slew of changes. The company plans to eliminate restrictions on hot button issues like immigration and refocus its automated moderation on illegal content.\par \par \uc2 The biggest change: An end to the platform`s fact-checking program.\par \par \uc2 So what does this look like? Whether it`s \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  or Threads, you`ll see a similar community notes label as you do on X. For example, this \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  post, users added this context.\par \par \uc2 But critics warn removing factcheckers could breed misinformation. The International Factchecking Network, one of the third parties used by \uc2 Meta\uc2 , tells \uc2 CBS\uc2 : "It`s unfortunate that this decision comes in the wake of extreme political pressure," but some are cheering the move, including President-elect Trump.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Honestly, I think they`ve come a long way, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you think he is directly responding to the threats that you have made to him in the past with?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Probably.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: CEO, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  not only met with the president-elect last month, he installed a Republican lobbyist as Meta`s chief policy officer.\par \par \uc2 DAN IVES (Wedbush Securities Global Head of Tech Research): I think this is really Zuckerberg reading the room.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: \uc2 Wedbush Securities\uc2  Global Head of Tech Research, Dan Ives says this could be a good business move.\par \par \uc2 What does \uc2 Meta\uc2  have to gain with this change?\par \par \uc2 DAN IVES: Well, this can increase advertising. This could be a positive for \uc2 Meta\uc2 , positive for \uc2 Instagram\uc2  in terms of engagement and interaction, and that ultimately is a positive for advertising.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the biggest threat here, it was regulation of the Trump administration, this is a little sort of waving of the white flag with Trump coming in.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And in this new Trump era, add UFC President and CEO Dana White to Meta`s makeover. Zuckerberg has announced White, who is one of President-elect Trump`s biggest surrogates, will be on its Board of Directors, and it is part of a broader shift we`re seeing in the tech business community.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is licensing a documentary on Melania Trump and others, Big Tech Titans, they are donating to the President-elect`s inauguration fund.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is a business decision.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nothing more, nothing less.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It definitely is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nothing more, nothing less.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what gets lost when it comes to these platforms, because it`s so near and dear to us, we forget that the ones at the top are also making business decisions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a business decision, but the game has changed.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, first on CBS MORNINGS, "Inside Edition" correspondent, Alison Hall is here. She`s going to reveal to us her health struggle and tells us why a story about actor Olivia Munn may have saved her life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Olivia Munn/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: First on CBS MORNINGS, "Inside Edition" correspondent, Alison Hall is here. She is sharing her breast cancer battle and how actor, Olivia Munn`s story about her own cancer treatment may have now saved Alison`s life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Olivia Munn/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Munn credited a breast cancer risk assessment tool for her diagnosis and had a series of surgeries, including a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 So in the course of reporting that story, Hall took the same risk assessment test, and she, too was startled by what the doctor told her.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Your risk comes out to be 36 percent.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL ("Inside Edition" correspondent): Wow. Okay, that feels high.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that was such a moment.\par \par \uc2 Alison Hall joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Alison number one, thank you for being here. Thank you for sharing your story. I think it`s so important and so brave what you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To open yourself up this way.\par \par \uc2 So you take the risk assessment test. What is it exactly?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: So it`s really just a series of questions. It`s a questionnaire that could be administered by your doctor or even online for free. It asks you things like height and weight, family history of cancer, anything that you`ve ever had come up with your breasts in the past, any exams that you`ve had.\par \par \uc2 You answer all of those questions, and then it gives you a percentage, your lifetime risk of breast cancer. You take that score and you use that information.\par \par \uc2 So if you have a score of over 20 percent, that means your risk is fairly high, and you should use that to perhaps get early screenings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now you do have a history of breast cancer in your family. Your mom had it when she was --\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: When she was 50.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When she was 50, yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: You were 32 when you took this test.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you had had -- you had done some testing on your own, and all the results had been really good for you, correct?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes, a few years ago, knowing that my mom had breast cancer, I went and got the BRCA gene test, and I was negative. I was in my 20s, and I thought, "Great, I don`t have the BRCA gene. I`m good." Every doctor I had spoken to at the time said, "That`s great. Get tested when you`re 40."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So then -- but you didn`t you. You also got a mammogram. That was fine.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I actually never had a mammogram. I had -- I didn`t have one until just this year because of this test.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because of this test.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was fine. So you think, whew, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes, exactly. Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then you take the MRI and what happened?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With this MRI?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yep. So the doctor who I interviewed for "Inside Edition," after that score was revealed, said "Let`s get you on a regular screening schedule. Let`s get you on mammograms and on MRIs every six months for one or the other." Basically, I went for my mammogram in the spring. I went for my MRI in October. The MRI showed a couple of abnormalities, and they asked me back for biopsies.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks after the biopsies, I got a fateful call from my doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did she say?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: She shared that I had breast cancer, but she said, this is actually good news. This is stage zero. This is the earliest it could possibly be caught, and we have the opportunity to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Alison, you -- we just said it earlier, you were reporting on Olivia Munn`s journey through the very same thing.\par \par \uc2 Can you just describe -- we hate being the news as journalists. We don`t want to be the news story. But what was going through your mind when that doctor on camera delivered that information to you? What did you think?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: It was pretty surreal. I was prepared to do the test in order to promote awareness for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Especially with it being a subject so close to my heart, but I really was not ready for that information and for it to change my life in this way. I didn`t expect it to happen so soon, especially.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now that you`ve gotten that information, what have you now decided to do?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: So I`ve decided to get a double mastectomy next week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Next week.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes. So I`ll be tackling the current cancer that I have head on, and I will also be doing this to dramatically decrease my risk for future cancer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How do you feel about going in --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alison, let`s talk about that for just a second. Because as women, you know, a lot of women, myself included, place a lot of value on a breast. How do they look? How are they doing? What went in -- and you`re so upbeat about what you`re about to do next week? This is huge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And how are you feeling about going into that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you. I feel really good. I mean, as a child who had a mom who had breast cancer. My mom had breast cancer that was much later stage. She had four children that she was raising at the same time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I watched her harrowing, but incredible experience, and knowing that I have caught this so much earlier --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I want to do whatever I can to prevent that for my future self and my future family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I think most people would understand that decision. Take them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and Alison, before you go, we reached out to Olivia Munn about your breast cancer battle, and she sent us a statement, and I would like to read it right now: "Dear Alison, when I heard your story, my heart broke wide open. I am so happy you took the risk assessment test, and you are now on the path to survival. I know all too well the moments of fear and uncertainty that can come in overwhelming waves, but you will soon discover reserves of power within yourself you never knew you had."\par \par \uc2 "You have faced this diagnosis with so much determination and bravery, and by telling your story, you are creating a ripple effect of hope in saving lives. I hope you remember that you are never alone in this fight. I am wishing you strength every step of the way. Love, Olivia."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: That`s really nice. Thank you for sharing that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How do you feel about those words?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Just incredible. It`s so wonderful to hear from her. I`ve been thinking about her a lot over the last six months, especially the last few weeks, and I am just so grateful to her.\par \par \uc2 As journalists, we`re not often part of the story, but we certainly believe in the power of storytelling. And by Olivia sharing her story, she had a direct impact on my life. And so --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And now you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now, you sharing your story, we are so grateful to you, Alison. Please know we are cheering -- your husband is here. You call him your human support person. We`re grateful to him, too, and we are cheering you on all the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 For Alison`s full story, you can tune into today`s episode of "Inside Edition." Please check your local listings.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead the stories --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are all still thinking 