hit in 1976, "Torn Between Two Lovers." Remember that song, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Feeling like a fool."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In a statement, Yarrow`s daughter said he believed with his whole heart that singing together could change the world.\par \par \uc2 Peter Yarrow was 86 years old.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the legacy of that music, not just Peter, Paul, and Mary, the stuff that he did solo and "Leaving On A Jet Plane," one of my favorite songs --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, mine, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- from Peter, Paul and Mary. He`s going to be missed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, yesterday, we revealed some of the nominees for this year`s NAACP Image Awards, and they include Danielle Pinnock from "Ghosts" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . She was just here last month. She`s up for Outstanding Supporting Actress in a Comedy Series.\par \par \uc2 So let me show you her very heartwarming reaction when she learned the news.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PINNOCK ("Ghosts" actor): This is nuts hearing it like -- I`m like, I got to rewatch and make sure they didn`t a mistake. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Danielle, they did not make a mistake. You are nominated.\par \par \uc2 She shared that video on social media, of course. We love her here. We love everybody on "Ghosts." We are wishing her --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- a big congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is nice to see people so happy when they get -- because this -- these Image Awards mean a lot to the people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They really do. They really do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can see that from her reaction.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is such a bright light.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her story is so incredible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I loved it when she came on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the light that she brings on that show, all the actors are incredible, but we especially love Danielle, and so we`re cheering you on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before we go, I just want to say that we`re that thinking about Meredith Vieira today. When you`re talking about Peter Yarrow. She lost her husband.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She did lose her husband.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Over the Christmas Holidays. They have been married over 50 something years, a long time illness.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You struggled with multiple sclerosis for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For a very long time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was first diagnosed when he was only 25 years old, and he told her on one of their early dates that he had it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And he struggled against it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Thinking of Meredith and her family today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are thinking of Meredith.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, an update on the California wildfires, including how our Carter Evans tried to protect his own neighborhood and family from the flames.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, we will have the latest on those fast-moving wildfires threatening tens of thousands of people in Southern California, and an update from a top LA County official.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some reading recommendations for 2025 from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Books` Sarah Gelman, including books to help you with those New Year`s resolutions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Plus, six-time Grammy nominee, Julia Michaels has worked with nearly every superstar in the business.\par \par \uc2 Now only on CBS MORNINGS, she is sharing some brand new music featuring Maren Morris.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s good. It involves scissors.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 MAN: Come on, guys. We`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Breaking overnight: Powerful winds fuel raging wildfires around Los Angeles as crews desperately try to fight back those flames.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN SHEILA KELLIHER: You can imagine trying to fight fire in those winds. It is really incredibly challenging and volatile and unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In a wide-ranging press conference, President-elect Trump refuses to rule out using military force to take over the Panama Canal and Greenland.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  lies in state of the US Capitol ahead of tomorrow`s state funeral.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: He left the world better than he found it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We saw some literally flaming debris, it landed in the road.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And California residents share their terrifying experiences dealing with the raging infernos around Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We are stuck here. Our car cannot get out of the garage because the garage door is electric.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: There were cars piled on top of each other. There were boulders and debris that fell off of the mount -- of the canyon that was on fire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This story just feels so different for all of us who are watching this. How many times can you say, "I`ve never seen anything like it before," but that is, never seen anything like this before, and that`s where we`re going to begin this hour with those catastrophic wildfires in Southern California. Catastrophic, really is the word here.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters are battling right now against three fast-moving fires in LA County, including this one. This is in the Pacific Palisades neighborhood of Los Angeles, which has already destroyed homes and forced more than 30,000 people to evacuate. That`s a huge number.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is there.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, I know it was a very rough night. What can you tell us about what`s happening this morning?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Gayle, it is hard to make sense of the scale of destruction that we`re witnessing this morning. We`re here on the Pacific Coast Highway in Malibu. You`re looking at one of easily a dozen homes on this stretch alone, absolutely gutted by these massive flames are being whipped around by gusts exceeding a hundred miles per hour. For some perspective, that`s the equivalent of a Category 2 hurricane ripping through this area.\par \par \uc2 There are fire crews here on scene. They are stretched thin. Overnight, this fire exploding to nearly 3,000 acres. It first broke out yesterday morning in the Pacific Palisades, about five miles from where we are. It moved so quickly that evacuees, as you can imagine, panicked with guidance from officials, they actually abandoned their vehicles and fled on foot. Bulldozers later brought in to clear those vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Fortunately, no deaths have been reported yet, but more than 10,000 homes currently threatened, and from what we have seen here on the ground overnight, I would say more than a hundred structures easily have been destroyed so far.\par \par \uc2 Making matters worse, you have a second brush fire that broke out last night in Altadena, that is north of LA, the Eaton Fire, as it is being called, has scorched at least a thousand acres so far and some desperate scenes are merging there.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 95 seniors had to be evacuated from an assisted living center in wheelchairs and stretchers. They were later loaded onto city buses and even SWAT vehicles.\par \par \uc2 This truly is the worst case scenario for firefighters. Resources stretched thin, crews split between the fires. The LA Fire Department calling on off duty staff to come in and assist, the first time they`ve had to do so in 19 years, calling out local news to get the word out.\par \par \uc2 As we`re back here, I`m not sure if you can make out all these embers they`re blowing, in some cases, more than a mile away, starting new spot fires.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, 30,000 plus people under a mandatory evacuation. Those numbers continue to grow as this fire continues to explode out-of-control this morning. The wind is only going to pick up until it starts to calm down later this afternoon -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Jonathan, I`ll take it from here. Thank you for chasing the flames for us.\par \par \uc2 Please, you and the crew be safe.\par \par \uc2 And our very own Carter Evans has been covering wildfires for us for years. Now the Palisades Fire zone, which is his own neighborhood, he`s dealing with that as well.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: This is really concerning.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got a fully evolved structure here. You`ve got the wind blowing embers. You can hear the smoke alarms going off inside the home, and there are no firefighters around to fight this fire.\par \par \uc2 I`m in the back of this home trying to help the firefighters, because they asked me for help because he couldn`t get over the gate.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And I`ll feed you the hose.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Okay, so I`m trying to get the flames out. Hopefully we can save this home.\par \par \uc2 This is getting near. We need a little more hose.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m going to give you this, brother.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Okay, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s the flames we need to get to here. We`re going to get it now. All right, we got it. We`re going to save this home. We`re going to try.\par \par \uc2 Here is another home that is a total loss, and these were beautiful homes with panoramic ocean views.\par \par \uc2 I`m in an elementary school playground right now. In fact, this is where my daughter went to school. You see those embers blowing behind me? This is what catches homes on fire.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, I was texting with Carter and his wife, Lauren, yesterday, because I`m very close to them, just because I knew that that neighborhood was where they had, recently, in the last couple of years, just bought their dream home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I wanted to be sure that they were okay. And Carter told me, essentially what we just saw there, that firefighters, there are not enough of them, and they told him to start grabbing a hose, which is what we just saw.\par \par \uc2 The kids are not there in the home. They`re staying with some other folks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I think anybody, if you have anybody in that area, that`s what I did, too last night, you just call people to say, are you okay? And a lot of people I know have been evacuated. But even if you`re not close to the fire, it`s just scary, just sitting here in New York, where we`re safe from that --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s just scary watching the heartbreak that people are going through.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This does feel very different to me. We`ve never seen cars that are abandoned and just bulldozed off the road, and to see it in such real time and the way they`re taking these homes --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the correspondents who are themselves facing danger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And dealing with it with their own families, but still showing up for work for us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re appreciative of that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, ahead, more on those fires, including an update from an LA County supervisor, but first it is 8:07, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heaven"/Julia Michaels/GFY Records)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is six-time Grammy nominee, Julia Michaels. Ahead we have an exclusive first look at her new song and video with Maren Morris. Michaels will tell us all about the inspiration behind the music.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow. You are looking at pictures of one of the fast- moving wildfires burning in and around Los Angeles. This is Pacific Palisades, California.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now for an update, is Lindsey Horvath, who`s a member of the Los Angeles County Board of Supervisors.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, ma`am for joining us this morning. Can I just ask you, before we get into the substance of our discussion, how you are doing?\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH(Los Angeles County Board of Supervisors member): I appreciate that, I am okay. I`m just heartbroken, though, for my constituents who are really struggling through this fire, and I am, you know, just praying for those who are on the frontlines doing all that they can to fight it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Supervisor, we heard earlier from a fire captain in the show said that the worst of the wind and the fire came overnight. What are the latest developments as daylight starts to creep in on Los Angeles?\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH: Yes, we had predicted that the worst of the winds were from 10:00 PM last night to about this time this morning, so our teams are out there doing assessment and continuing to fight this fire and bring everything that we can to it.\par \par \uc2 You know, we went into Unified Command on this Palisades Fire within two hours, and that means the city, the county, and the state are all working together. We also brought in federal resources. So all resources are dedicated to fighting this fire and making sure that we`re doing all that we can to keep people safe.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had evacuation orders underway. There are thousands of acres that are burning in the Palisades Fire alone. In another part of my district, there`s also the Hurst Fire, which has about 500 acres and counting that are burning, and I know that there`s also a fire in the Fifth District, which is the Eaton Fire. So there`s a lot of activity.\par \par \uc2 Our LA County firefighters are working on all three of these fires, as well as whatever smaller fires have popped up. So they`re all hands on deck.\par \par \uc2 We had the governor here with us yesterday. The president was in the district as well, and so all resources are focused on keeping people safe and doing all we can to get this under control.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, Lindsey, these pictures are so tough to watch. I was talking to people yesterday who live in that area who just say they have to evacuate. They don`t not know what they`re going to be coming back to, which is also very, very frightening.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any idea when you guys can expect a break? I keep thinking about your firefighting teams and how exhausted they must be. It`s all hands on deck. It`s 24/7.\par \par \uc2 Have you been told that, you know we expect to get a break? The winds are dying down. Things are improving. Do you have any of that kind of information as we sit here this morning? I realize it`s a very fluid situation.\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH: It is. We`re not there yet. We knew at the end of last week and over the weekend that these high winds were going to continue, probably through the end of tomorrow, if not into Friday. So we`re still not out of it.\par \par \uc2 We know that there was a peak time, as I mentioned earlier, but there`s still a lot of work ahead. And actually, you know, we had the Franklin Fire in Malibu just before the holidays. So I was out there with -- and Unified Command, with all of our folks addressing that fire just before the holidays.\par \par \uc2 So they are relentless. This is truly a culling. This is not just a profession that somebody signs up for to get a check. They are driven to keep people safe, to keep our communities safe, and they are doing all that they can do to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lindsey, you mentioned the assistance that you are getting on a state and federal level, but what more do you need right now? Because certain areas, it just seems like the people that are there that have boots on the ground are overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH: Well right now, the thing that we need most is partnership. We have partnership in alignment on government from city, county, state and federal levels, but we also need the partnership of community members. We know that each of these homes is incredibly valuable, not only in terms of dollars and cents, but in terms of how hard people have worked to create and build them.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s hard to walk away and think that you might not have much to walk back to.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH: But if we don`t get people safely evacuated, it`s not only a danger for the people who are living in those communities, but also for the people who are on the frontlines. We really need people to follow the evacuation orders.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lindsey, do you know what started this fire? How did this start? Is it unusual to be dealing with this in January?\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH: It is incredibly unusual for us to be dealing with this in January. About this time last year, I was alongside other officials talking about the floods and those landslides that we were experiencing as a result of extreme rain.\par \par \uc2 And so this is incredibly unusual that we know that climate change has impacted our region. I knew coming into this office a couple of years ago, I was advised that this is the most disaster prone district of any county in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 And so as I said last year, we had to Topanga Canyon closure from a landslide resulting from the rains, and now, we have these fires where we had to evacuate Topanga Canyon entirely overnight.\par \par \uc2 So we are all hands on deck to keep people safe. We do emergency preparedness drills throughout the year to keep people informed at a time where they`re not in a state of emergency, but we know that sometimes that information just doesn`t get through to them until a moment like this, and so we`re doing all we can to keep people informed and keep them safe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hopefully people are getting out and being safe, and the people that are fighting the fire are also safe as well.\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lindsey Horvath, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY HORVATH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As 2025 gets underway, day eight, we`re looking at different ways to reset this January with popular New Year`s resolutions.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Books editorial director, superstar, Sarah Gelman joins us with some book recommendations that she says can help us all learn to be better versions of ourselves. I want some of that.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, get ready to dazzle us again. I marvel every time you come because you do it with no notes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN (Amazon Books editorial director): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You just rattle it off.\par \par \uc2 All right, so here we go. We`re going to start with a book called " "Defy: The Power of No in a World that Demands Yes." You recommend it. Why?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So I think, I hope that people have a resolution to say no more. I think especially parents, we just need to say no to more things. This book will teach you how to say no.\par \par \uc2 So we think of defiance as something that`s bad. I think that`s how we`re conditioned. If you say a kid is defiant, they`re a bad kid.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: But Dr. Sah actually wants us to think of defiance as something good. It`s acting in accordance with your true values, particularly when there`s pressure to do otherwise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So in this book, you`re going to learn how to get to your true yes and your true no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And actually be defiant, which is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody told me once that no is a complete sentence, and sometimes I always feel like --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I have to explain "Well, I want to, but" -- no is a complete sentence.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Make it your year of no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Sarah, well, you said yes to a cookbook that you even made some recipes from.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: This cookbook has my heart. I love it. It`s called "Ambitious Kitchen." Monique Volz. I have cooked so many things out of this book.\par \par \uc2 So whether your resolution is to cook more this year, eat at home more, eat more plant based food, or even just get more joy out of your time in the kitchen, this is the perfect cookbook.\par \par \uc2 It has, I would say healthy and an indulgent healthy recipes. So nourish, oh, that`s a soup. Oh, no, that`s one of the recipes. It has nourishing smoothies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here`s the soup.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I made -- that`s my soup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right, Sarah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sarah got some skills in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I am a food stylist on the side.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So yes, that`s a chicken soup with turmeric and garlic and ginger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, that`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Everything from smoothies to salads to more indulgent things like an enchilada skillet. And I feel like a lot of people feel controlled by food, and if you do, this is a great book, because it`s not only joyful, but the author has a really interesting back story that she shares in the book that I found super inspiring.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. You`ve also got a book that you call "a break glass in case of emergency read."\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What does that mean? I like that, right?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: This book is called "Open When," Dr. Julie Smith.\par \par \uc2 Dr Smith is a clinical psychologist, and this book is you open when you`re going through different situations. So, if you`re having trouble getting over a breakup from last year, if you`re paralyzed by indecision, if you feel like you`re being a bad parent, you open when and every chapter --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: -- is like a therapy session with this wise person.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you just find like what it is you`re going through --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- and you open the book and find --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- that advice there.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And it just -- you feel like you`re getting a hug and like a warm mug of tea in each chapter. I love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like this next book. I love the title. You said it`s good if you`re trying to get on track with your money. And the title is --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: "You Deserve to be Rich."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I feel like a Gen Xer wrote that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Master the Game of Inner Wealth and Claim Your Future." You like it, why?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I like it because it talks about how the American Dream is a lie, also very provocative, that this idea of working hard doesn`t always equal financial freedom, especially in Black and Brown communities.\par \par \uc2 So this book is going to teach you how to get to financial freedom and even generational wealth, using tips like five things to get your debt back on track, a month by month guide to get your finances back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A book on optimism.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The last one.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. So this is called "The Bright Side." I think maybe we all need this book this year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: This is a book about how to be an optimist, why it`s healthy to be. Optimist and how being an optimist is actually a moral obligation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she did it again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Sarah --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did it again. Four minutes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You deserve to be rich.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Sarah, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want that book, too.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, what a man, what a man. That`s some good lyrics.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mr. Burleson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Me?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories we "Talk of the Table." What a man, what a man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, stop.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m just a little boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate goes, "Oh stop." But keep talking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just a little boy over here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, you`re a man, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is good Nate, because you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am in pole position.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And my "Talk of the Table" is \uc2 CBS\uc2  bringing back the classic game show, "Hollywood Squares."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m taking you behind-the-scenes. You know the show, it`s a tic-tac-toe style game --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hollywood Squares"/CB S)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- where celebrities representing X`s and O`s answer questions from me, the host. Contestants decide then to see if those answers are true or not for a chance to win big cash prizes, even vacations.\par \par \uc2 Drew Barrymore sits in the center square, and we both are so excited to share this with you. Here is what Drew had to say.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I am lucky enough to be center square.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DR