ON: Come on. Come on in here.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You won`t sleep hot. You won`t sleep cold. It will help you regulate. $77.00 and up. They`re up to 33 percent off. We have lots of colors and sizes for every bed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, how much is it?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: They start at $77.00 and they`re up to 33 percent off.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is really comfortable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate it. All right --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And you`ve got your massage and your sheets.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elizabeth, we thank you so much. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 We partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything that you saw, scan the QR code on your screen, or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Let`s head over to Gayle in our progressive greenroom, because I know you`re tired of looking at me and Vlad in the bed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I never get started looking at you and Vlad in the bed, but I like looking at me and Julia because she`s a six-time Grammy nominated singer-songwriter, Julia Michaels, and she`ll exclusively share a new music video and a single featuring -- who is a single featuring?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS (Singer-songwriter): Maren Morris.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a one-two punch with these two. Wait till you hear it.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Issues"/Julia Michaels/Republic Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is the song "Issues" by six-time Grammy nominee, Julia Michaels.\par \par \uc2 Michaels began her career writing for other artists when she was only 16 years old. She has co-written songs for Dua Lipa, Ed Sheeran, Justin Bieber, Olivia Rodrigo, oh, Lady Gaga, oh, Britney Spears.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On and on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She has worked on Sabrina Carpenter`s latest album, which is up for Album of the Year at this year`s Grammys. This Friday, Michaels is releasing a brand new song called "Scissors" with Maren Morris and only on CBS MORNINGS, we have a first look at the video. Check it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Scissors"/Julia Michaels/GFY Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh. Julia Michaels is here with us. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the line, though, "If you want to cut ties, I got the scissors. I`m not even bitter." Bravo.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, that`s -- so this is metaphorically about cutting people from my life, which is something that I -- a lot of us struggle with.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, maybe Gayle, you don`t, but like, I struggle with cutting people out of my life. So what inspired you to write this song?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: I think it`s just a really funny way of saying that I`m unbothered.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Scissors"/Julia Michaels/GFY Records)\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: You know, my skin is thick, and I really could care less if you stay or leave. I`m very okay with it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "I`m okay with it."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I think this is a good time for you right now, Julia --\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you recently decided to leave your record label.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said, no bad blood, but you decided.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: No bad blood.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did you decide you wanted to leave?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: I just think that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To go out on your own.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: -- it was time. Yes, it was time and I felt like it had come to a point where I just felt a little bit misunderstood, and I wanted to have the opportunity to bet on myself and take the chance and they were so wonderful about letting me leave.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Scissors"/Julia Michaels/GFY Records)\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: And now I`m on this journey which is so exciting. And I`m in love with Maren Morris. I just think she`s one of the coolest people in the world, and I`m so thankful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What was it like working with her?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says the same about you, by the way. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: It was -- it was amazing. The first time I wrote with Maren was in 2020. We had written a song together called "Circles Around This Town," which was nominated --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that song, too.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: -- for Best Country Song for the Grammys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also good video.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Thank you, yes, and we had written a song last year called "Cut" together. And she was like, "Would you be on this song with me?" And I was just so flattered and so honored. And I was like, "Speaking of, I have a song called `Scissors` as well, would you want to do this song with me?" And she heard it, and she was like, "Are you joking? I would love to."\par \par \uc2 Just getting to do the music video with her was so fun. We have very similar senses of humor, very satirical and dark.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Scissors"/Julia Michaels/GFY Records)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But this is the thing about you that`s why I point it out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the people that you`ve worked with, you wrote Justin Bieber`s "Sorry." I love Sabrina Carpenter`s song, "Taste."\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just know when you`re kissing me, your taste -- when kissing you, you`re tasting me. I love that line.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve quoted that song before.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that line.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Go Gayle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle, is that autobiographical?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about me, I know. But here is the thing, when you were writing for all of these people, was there a part of you that thought, "I can sing too. I want to do this too."\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: I think that for a long time, I, just out of insecurity, never really pursued.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: And I`ve always written songs for other people, that`s just like a true love of mine, and I think I`ve consistently put myself on the back burner in order to write songs with, and for other people. I love it. I`m so grateful to get to do that.\par \par \uc2 But I think this is kind of the first time in my life where I`m putting myself first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why you have a tattoo on your neck that says, "Speak up."\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: I do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That says something.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So speaking of --\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- speaking up and through your own voice, talk to me about a potential new album that you might be working on.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Yes, I am definitely creating new music. I haven`t decided yet if I`m going to put out an album. I have the freedom to just put songs out as I make them and as they come, and create a visual world for them to live in, which is new for me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heaven"/Julia Michaels/GFY Records)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I just say I`ve heard some of the new music, "Heaven" too. I love it.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love your writing. I love your voice and the way you put your phrases together. In addition to this, though, your music is going great, your personal life is going great. Very few people come on and say, ask me about my hot new boyfriend.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Can we see the picture? Let`s see the hot new boyfriend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s his name and what -- what is it about him that you know he is the one in less than 30 seconds, Julia. Ten seconds.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Ten seconds. He`s just so supportive and wonderful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Supportive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s hot.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we support you.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MICHAELS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250109el1800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for November 16, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jason Allen, Ali Bauman, Ramy Inocencio, Itay Hod, David Wade,  Elizabeth Palmer, Andrew Kozak, Nicole Valdes \par \uc2 3,682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Tonight, gunfire strikes at Southwest jet.\par \par \uc2 PILOT: Southwest 2494, we have to return to the gate. Need to have an inspection done on the aircraft.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: A bullet hits a 737 ready for takeoff in Dallas. No one injured, but it`s another troubling incident in a week of problems on planes.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: I`m Jason Allen at Love Field in Dallas, where investigators are trying to figure out where that bullet came from.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Also tonight, spoiling for a fight.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: I`m Ali Bauman in New York, where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected to return with a spotlight on his provocative Cabinet picks.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Final talks: Presidents Biden and Xi of China meet as Trump and uncertainty loom large.\par \par \uc2 CHUN JU (Hong Kong University): I would not want to invest too much in China.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: You play as America.\par \par \uc2 CHUN JU: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Plus, weather warning.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh no.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: King Tides in Oregon nearly sweep a woman away with several states this weekend set for a blast of winter. We`ll have the forecast.\par \par \uc2 And later, driving on sunshine.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: How fast does this car go?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Itay Hod takes a spin in a car that`s powered by the sun.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANTHONY\uc2  (Co-CEO Aptera): This is the future.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Good evening, Jericka is off. I`m David Wade.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we begin with rattled nerves on a runway in Dallas. A bullet pierced a Southwest jetliner just feet from the cockpit as it taxied for takeoff. It is the latest incident in a week of problems for planes and passengers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jason Allen leads us off tonight at Love Field. Jason.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: David, you know that there are all kinds of sounds you hear when a plane is getting ready to take off. Well, \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  tells me, in this case, the crew of the plane heard something above all of that that was out of place. It turned out to be a bullet hitting the side of the plane as they were headed out toward the runway, about to take off.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PILOT: Southwest 2494. We have to return to the gate. Need to have an inspection done here on the aircraft.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN (voiceover): The passengers and crew on the 737 were moments from takeoff when a bullet hit the right side of the jetliner just below the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says no one was hurt. The aircraft returned to the gate and passengers exited. A Southwest source tells CBS News it appears to be a stray bullet.\par \par \uc2 We asked travelers about it at Love Field today.\par \par \uc2 Does any of that cause you any hesitation before you`re getting on a plane today?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are bothered. But I know, I ain`t going to Haiti, though.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  stopped US airlines from flying to Haiti this week after gangs shot three US jets. Former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  chair, Robert Sumwalt spoke to my colleague, Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from November 11, 2024)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: How dangerous is it for a plane to get struck by a bullet?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: Obviously, when you have people shooting at an airplane, it`s not a good situation.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: In Denver, a rush to the exits. One hundred eight passengers evacuated on this Southwest flight, Friday, a cell phone battery caught fire as the plane was preparing to depart.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We were lucky, so we were delayed 30 minutes. If we hadn`t been delayed, that phone would have caught on fire in the air.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: And trouble on a \uc2 Scandinavian Airlines\uc2  flight headed to Miami. The jet with 254 passengers on board shaken by severe turbulence over Greenland, Thursday. It turned around landing in Copenhagen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: The plane that was hit by the bullet is still here. It is out of commission until it can be repaired. There is an \uc2 FAA\uc2  approved repair process, believe it or not. Four bullet holes in the body of a plane.\par \par \uc2 Aviation experts we talked to told us it`s important not to draw any links or conclusions between all of these events, but they are happening, David just about a week before that very busy holiday travel season gets underway.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Wow. Jason Allen, in Dallas. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, President-elect Trump is in New York City for a night out in his hometown, and his picks for his Cabinet just keep coming.\par \par \uc2 Trump revealed he wants Chris Wright, CEO of a fracking company, to be his Energy Secretary.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ali Bauman joins us live outside of Trump Tower in Manhattan. Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: David, President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in the city tonight to attend an Ultimate Fighting Championship event at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . But the real fight is going on down in DC, where several lawmakers are raising concerns about his latest Cabinet selections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN (voiceover): As President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  departs from Florida for New York City Saturday, many on Capitol Hill are spinning over his Cabinet picks.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): I`m trying to be respectful. I believe in a smooth transition of government. His Cabinet picks leave me worried.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: While selections like Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  for Secretary of State were expected, others are raising eyebrows, including Trump`s pick for National Intelligence, former Congresswoman \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 ; RFK, Jr. to lead Health and Human Services; Fox News host, Pete Hegseth for Defense Secretary, and former Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  for Attorney General.\par \par \uc2 Gaetz had been the subject of a House Ethics Committee inquiry into allegations of sexual misconduct and illicit drug use before resigning from his seat Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Despite calls from senators on both sides of the aisle, House Speaker Mike Johnson says he does not think the committee should release its findings.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): Stick to the tradition to not release a report on a former member of the House, because it would open a dangerous Pandora`s Box.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: An attorney for two women who were witnesses in that investigation tells CBS News one of his clients testified she saw Gaetz having sex with a minor at a party in Orlando in 2017. Gaetz denies the allegations.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from WGME, November 15, 2024)\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R-ME): I am confident that all of the information that is in that Ethics report will come out through the process.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: And CBS News has learned that Vice President-elect JD Vance has been speaking with Senate Republicans about the confirmation process, engaging support for Trump`s picks.\par \par \uc2 Now Trump still has about a dozen more Cabinet roles to decide on, and next week, he is expected to choose his economic team, which includes Treasury Secretary. David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Ali Bauman in New York City, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Peru now in a meeting between President Biden and China`s President Xi. It comes as relations between the rival superpowers face new uncertainty. The leaders gathered at a hotel on the sidelines of the APEC Summit. For Biden, it was the last chance to directly challenge Xi, after working to avoid open conflict with Beijing these last four years.\par \par \uc2 But China`s focus is less on the lame duck president and more on the next president. \uc2 CBS\uc2  Ramy Inocencio is in Hong Kong, where all the talk is about Trump`s tough talk on tariffs and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voiceover): Beijing is bracing for a new dawn in China-US ties, wary about the second coming of an emboldened president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President-elect of the United States): And I`m doing the same thing with China. We`re going to tariff the hell out of them.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "Last time, Trump was not friendly to China," this woman told CBS News in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "Personally, I like Trump, but he does things that are outrageous," said this man.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s top threat is economic, eye-watering tariffs of 60 percent on all Chinese imports to protect American manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 CHUN JU: In terms of retaliation, choices for China, it`s very limited.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Chun Ju (sp?) at Hong Kong University, says that`s because China relies on America for crops and semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 CHUN JU I would not want to invest too much in China.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: The play is America.\par \par \uc2 CHUN JU That`s right.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: On top of China`s slowing economic growth, its property market that`s crashed, and its youth unemployment that`s hit a record high, the biggest flash point of all --\par \par \uc2 MAN: The sovereignty over Taiwan is the red line of all the red lines.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Beijing says the democratic island of 23 million is a separatist province and has vowed to retake it. The US is legally committed to supporting Taiwan`s defense, but many fear Trump may not.\par \par \uc2 To what extent is an isolationist America good for China?\par \par \uc2 MAN: China does not want to replace United States as the top dog. China needs to put his own house in order.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And Xi Jinping`s job is looking more difficult with Trump naming more China hawks to his Cabinet, like Representative Mike Waltz for his National Security adviser and Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  as Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 Rubio has called China the US` most advanced adversary, and Beijing has sanctioned him twice.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio, CBS News, Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: To the Middle East now and tonight, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leaders in Lebanon are reviewing a US ceasefire proposal, as Israeli forces push deeper into that country and in Gaza, the latest Israeli strikes have claimed at least 26 lives with no peace in sight for war-weary civilians.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elizabeth Palmer is in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER (voiceover): Almost two million Palestinian Gazans have endured more than a year of violence and trauma.\par \par \uc2 In desperation, they`re looking to a new government in Washington for help.\par \par \uc2 Rakan Abdul Ahman (sp?).\par \par \uc2 RAKAN ABDUL AHMAN (Palestinian in Gaza): The winning of the Trump to stop the war on the Gaza Strip and end our suffering.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT-ELECT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m not going to start a war. I am going stop wars.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Gazan Palestinians know that in his victory speech, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he`d stop wars, and they hope he means this one.\par \par \uc2 (ABDUL HADI FARAJAT speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: "Will you send him a message," says, Abdul Hadi Farajat (sp?), "That we`ve had enough of killing, destruction, all the disasters afflicting Palestinians."\par \par \uc2 But Mustafa Barghouti, a Palestinian lawmaker in the West Bank, warns, be careful what you wish for.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA BARGHOUTI (Member of the Palestinian Legislative Council): I think we`re going through a very dangerous time for two reasons. First of all, you have very extreme Israeli government. And from the other side, you have President Trump appointing one pro-Israeli politician after the other.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Even if Trump does convince Israel to end this war, Barghouti says Palestinians will be facing another long standing battle that is halting the expansion of Jewish outposts on what was Palestinian land in the West Bank, and he`s worried about pro-Israeli officials in a Trump Cabinet like \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 , his pick for US Ambassador who said there`s no such thing as Palestine.\par \par \uc2 But analyst and former diplomat, Michael Oren says it may not be so simple. Just look at Trump`s first term.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL OREN (Former Israeli Ambassador to the United States): He was against unbridled, unfettered settlement expansion, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has signed on what he called the deal of the century, and that deal was a two- state deal, and believe me, the Israeli far right actually knew it then and rejected it; today, they seem to forget it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The war here has to stop, almost everybody agrees on that. But what lies beyond, David, particularly, whether or if the Palestinians get independence, is anybody`s guess.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Elizabeth Palmer in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well tonight, there are winter weather warnings all across the US with snow expected in several states, and the first King Tides of the season have also arrived on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video, a woman in Oregon, lucky to be alive after getting caught in the dangerous high tide. The woman just walking along the beach when she was knocked off her feet and nearly swept away. Fortunately, she was able to pull herself out.\par \par \uc2 From tides to storms, time to check in with CBS News meteorologist, Andrew Kozak. Andrew.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yes, David, glad she is okay.\par \par \uc2 A King Tide, well, that`s formed by several factors. Let`s talk about it for a second. Waxing gibbous phase of the moon, that means 98.4 percent full. That`s what we saw Thursday, over Oregon. We also were at perigee, which means the orbit of the moon was closest to the earth. And of course, we also had several strong cold fronts moving through, another one just about to enter the Pacific Northwest, with heavy rain across the coast and several areas under winter storm warnings and winter weather advisories.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, we could have a soaking rain from Portland to Seattle, higher elevations, including areas across Idaho, Montana could have an additional foot of snow. We`re also watching the Southern Plains, Oklahoma and Texas, could be looking at severe weather, including isolated tornadoes and very dry once again, up and down the East Coast. Elevated fire danger once again in the Northeast, a lack of rain, low humidity and continued gusty winds into the week ahead. David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: All right. Andrew, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a lawsuit seeks to clarify a controversial abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a fitting finish for an American skiing sensation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Tonight, emotional testimony in a lawsuit over Idaho`s strict abortion ban, but the legality of the ban itself is not the issue here.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nicole Valdes speaks with one of the plaintiffs, who says she was forced to travel hundreds of miles even though her pregnancy and her life were in danger.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES (voiceover): Thirty-two-year-old Kayla Smith found out she was pregnant for a second time two years ago.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA SMITH (32 years old): I never thought I would need an abortion. It is the most traumatic experience that I`ve had in my whole life.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: At 20 weeks, she learned the son she was expecting had little to no chance of survival, diagnosed with several critical heart defects in the womb.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, nearly all abortions are against the law, except after rape, incest, or when it is necessary to prevent the death of the mother.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA SMITH: The likelihood of me developing preeclampsia again was very high. I remember asking, how sick do I have to be, right? And she couldn`t give me an answer.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: So she drove over 500 miles from Boise, Idaho, to a hospital in Seattle, Washington, taking out a loan to pay for the trip and the procedure.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA SMITH: I had an induction of labor with the same medication that they would give for a medical abortion.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Now she and five other women are suing the state of Idaho not to overturn the ban, but to clarify and expand the exceptions.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA SMITH: As a medical professional, they`re not coached to go and ask, you know, legal advice and whether they could intervene.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: One of the plaintiffs is Dr. Emily Corrigan, an Idaho OBGYN, who admits she`s felt forced to turn patients away.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY CORRIGAN (Idaho OBGYN): I am weighing my own personal freedom to stay out of prison against the needs of my patient.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: But the Idaho Attorney General`s Office has doubled down.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Idaho Fourth District Court)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Unborn children have a fundamental right to life. How to best protect those lives through Idaho`s laws is a policy decision for the Idaho legislature.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: We reached out to the Attorney General`s Office, and it declined to comment on the case. We also reached out to several Idaho State lawmakers, and none returned our requests.\par \par \uc2 The trial is expected to last at least another week, and there`s no timeline on the judge`s decision.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes, CBS News, Boise, Idaho.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Still ahead on the CBS Weekend News, fight night, the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  rumble in the ring and on screens.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Tonight, American skiing star Mikaela Shiffrin is celebrating after winning the first Women`s World Cup Slalom of the season. The race in Finland was her 98th career victory. She has reason to smile. No other skier, male or female, has ever won more than 86.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Mike Tyson and \uc2 Netflix\uc2  are both bruised and battered after the 58- year-old made his much-anticipated return to the ring.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , MVP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: The boxing legend lost to internet star turned boxer, Jake Paul, a lot of rust on old Iron Mike, meanwhile, people watching live on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  had their own battle with streaming issues, some posting pictures of their frozen screens on social media. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  says the fight is now available to watch for everyone. I guess you just have to pretend that you don`t know who won.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, runaway pig and the police pursuit that followed. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: You`ve heard of a suspect being on the lam, well, tonight we have a police chase for something on the ham. You see when a pig got loose and went hog wild in Tacoma, Washington, officers were sent on a comically mad scramble.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tacoma Police Department)\par \par \uc2 The snorting suspect was dubbed the Notorious P.I.G. Police eventually cornered Piggy Smalls and took it into custody, and noted, despite all the squealing, the pig is just fine hanging out on a local farm.\par \par \uc2 When we return, no gas pump, no problem. Why sunshine is all you need for this ride.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Finally, tonight, hitting the road in a car that doesn`t need gas and doesn`t need to be charged.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s "Weekend Journal," \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Itay Hod in San Francisco shows us a vehicle that`s rolling into the future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD (voiceover): It`s a new twist on traditional travel, no gas or plug required, all this car needs is a tank full of sunshine.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANTHONY\uc2 : This is the future.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Called the Aptera, this two-seat, three-wheeled car looks more like a flying machine, especially when you open its doors and wait until you see how you do it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANTHONY\uc2 : There you go.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: \uc2 Chris Anthony\uc2  is Aptera`s co-CEO. He says, this car priced at about $35,000.00 is all about efficiency.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANTHONY\uc2 : There`s combustion vehicles, there`s electric vehicles, but there`s no vehicle that creates its own fuel throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: The global solar vehicle market is just revving up from $450 million in 2024 to $2.5 billion in 2030.\par \par \uc2 The Aptera is covered in solar panels, which, according to the company, can generate enough energy to drive up to 40 miles per day for longer drives that can be charged like a regular electric car.\par \par \uc2 How fast does this car go?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANTHONY\uc2 : Zero to 60 in about four seconds, and the top speed is 110 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: We took it for a spin to see how it handles San Francisco`s steep terrain.\par \par \uc2 I have to say it`s doing a pretty decent job.\par \par \uc2 But while these cars have plenty of fans, Steven Zoff, a former Transportation official and Stanford researcher, says they may not be for everyone.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN ZOFF (Former Transportation official): The tradeoff is very small, very lightweight, limited carrying capacity are going to be deal breakers for a lot of US customers.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Then, of course, there`s the weather.\par \par \uc2 What happens if you live in a place like San Francisco, known for its fog?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ANTHONY\uc2 : Yes. San Francisco is a great use case for the Aptera. In Southern California, we get about 11,000 miles of free driving a year. In San Francisco, Seattle, New York, you get more like 8,500 miles a year.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Solar cars steering people towards a brighter future where the best way to move forward is to look up.\par \par \uc2 Itay Hod, CBS News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: And a whole new meaning to Sunday drive.\par \par \uc2 That`s the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Saturday. First thing tomorrow, we have "Sunday Morning with Jane Pauley," followed by "Face the Nation." Margaret Brennan`s guests include Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  and Dr. \uc2 Deborah Birx\uc2 , and Representative Jim Himes.\par \par \uc2 I`m David Wade in New York. Hope you have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020241117ekbg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 60 MINUTES, November 3, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Scott Pelley, Sharyn Alfonsi, Jon Wertheim \par \uc2 7,579 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: 60 Minutes\par \uc2 SXMN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Election day comes in an anxious time.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Why should Americans have confidence in this election?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : We have tons of people, processes and policies in place in all 50 states and the district to assure that the outcome of the election is correct.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Correct, but maybe not as fast as our nerves would like.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: So on Election Night, don`t expect to know the winner. We probably will have a pretty good idea who won the presidency maybe around Thursday or Friday.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Tonight, you will hear from doctors who say the new abortion laws in Texas are creating unintended consequences for medical professionals and the women they care for.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: Five years ago, I could counsel a patient on all the various treatment options. And now, it is a dangerous situation for me.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: "Dangerous" because?\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: Because I could face life in prison just for having a conversation with my patient about evidence-based care.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): There is very little rotten in the State of Denmark. This is the land that gave us Lego, province of pedaling, all fishing nets and safety nets. Today, the spike in demand for the Danish- made drug Ozempic is fattening the country`s economy, creating thousands of jobs and keeping mortgage rates low.\par \par \uc2 And the pharmaceutical giant behind it all, \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 , now has a market cap larger than the Denmark`s entire GDP.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: I`m Lesley Stahl.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: I`m Bill Whitaker.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: I`m Anderson Cooper.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: I`m Sharyn Alfonsi.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: I`m Jon Wertheim.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA: I`m Cecilia Vega.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: I`m Scott Pelley. Those stories and more tonight on 60 MINUTES.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ELECTION TRUTH\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Tuesday, Americans will consider a question more vital than who wins the White House. They`ll decide whether they have faith in the ballot, whether January 6, 2021 was the end of chaos or the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Election fraud lies are aimed at destroying confidence. You`re about to hear why they are lies. \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2  is a messenger of election truth. He`s the Republican chief operating officer for the Georgia secretary of state who runs elections there. Georgia was the center of the scheme to overthrow the 2020 election. Sterling says these are the stakes this Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : People have to have faith in the outcomes. And for 200 and some odd years, we had to have the acceptance of the person who came in second or lost to say, "You know what, I lost. I will come back again and fight in two years." The reason we have ballots is to avoid bullets. And if you say, "Ballots don`t matter anymore," there`s only one logical direction to go. And that`s not healthy in any democracy.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Why should Americans have confidence in this election?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : Every state in this country now has poked and prodded and looked at their processes. And we know that while we all do it slightly differently, everybody assures that there`s one person in one vote. Everybody assures that there`s a single ballot cast.\par \par \uc2 Nearly every state in the Union now has audits after the fact. Every state in the Union tests their equipment beforehand in a public way. We have tons of people, processes and policies in place in all 50 states and the district to assure that the outcome of the election is correct.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: And there are millions of people who are skeptical about what you just said.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : There`s always some level of skepticism. It`s always there. But it`s reached a different level in the 2020 and 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): In the 2020 election, President Trump lost Georgia by a slender 11,779 votes. Two recounts confirmed his loss, but he said this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from November 26, 2020)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (45th President of the United States): Nobody wants to see the kind of fraud that this election has -- has really come to represent. We are looking at things that are so bad in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Four days before the vote was to be certified on January 6th, Trump called Sterling`s boss, Georgia`s Republican Secretary of State, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : So, look, all I want to do is this. I just want to find 11,780 votes, which is one more than we have.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): In a state where Republicans controlled every branch of government, Raffensperger stood up to Trump on the call.\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER: We have to stand by our numbers. We believe our numbers are right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Trump`s lawyers brought shocking tales of fraud to the legislature. But investigations by the state police, \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Raffensperger`s office found none was true.\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER: They said there were 66,000 underage voters. There were zero. Then they said that there were 2,423 non-registered voters. There were zero.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: But you`re a Republican. He was your president.\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER: I work for the people of Georgia. I respond and I work for the voters of Georgia. My job is to run a fair, honest, accurate election.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): But fairness and honesty were met with threats of violence.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : It has all gone too far.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Death threats to his election workers snapped Republican Gabe Sterling`s patience.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : It has to stop. Mr. President, you have not condemned these actions or this language. Senators, you have not condemned this language or these actions. This has to stop. We need you to step up.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re going to take a position of leadership, show some. Stop inspiring people to commit potential acts of violence. Someone`s going to get hurt. Someone`s going to get shot. Someone`s going to get killed.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): His warning was five weeks before January 6th.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Fight for Trump. Fight for Trump.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Help!\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): More than 1,000 rioters have been convicted -- just part of the election lie chaos. Trump filed 64 lawsuits and lost 63. Lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was sued to bankruptcy after falsely accusing Georgia election workers of fraud.\par \par \uc2 Fox News paid $787 million to settle a suit that said Fox knew it was lying about the election. And Trump was indicted in Georgia. He`s pleaded not guilty to election interference. But four co-defendants did plead guilty to related charges. Sterling hoped his message would get through to everyone. But it hasn`t.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : And there`s some you`re never going to convince. And you just have to accept that you`re never going to convince them. But for the vast majority of Americans who have questions, basically, they think, "Well, if there`s this much smoke, there must be something. Nobody would lie this much about it."\par \par \uc2 So I think there`s savable souls there, who basically, they want to understand. We have to tell them over and over again, "Voter suppression`s fake. Voter fraud is fake. It`s used to raise money and get you angry."\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Trump riled supporters with election denial from the very first time he was ever on a Republican ballot. He lost the Iowa caucus in 2016 and wrote, "Ted Cruz didn`t win Iowa, he stole it."\par \par \uc2 Later, when Trump won the national electoral vote, but not the popular vote, he posted, "I won the popular vote if you deduct the millions of people who voted illegally." Later, a fraud commission he appointed disbanded when it couldn`t find the evidence.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: People are going to say that the election was stolen. It`s inevitable.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: And to them, you say what?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : I`m sorry that your candidate lost. But the rules are the rules. The law is the law. And the count is the count.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Georgia, in many ways, is a model. They do a lot of things exceptionally well.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Few people know elections like David Becker. At the Justice Department, he helped enforce voting rights. Later, he led the creation of a voter registration data center that is used today by half the states. Becker is a CBS News election consultant.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: One of the things everyone should understand is the claims about election theft and fraud are not driven by the actual policies that exist in the states. They`re driven by one factor, and one factor only. And that`s whether your candidate won.\par \par \uc2 Sadly, tens of millions of Americans have been targeted for this disinformation. They`ve been preyed upon by losing candidates and foreign adversaries. And importantly, they have donated money because a lot of this is financial. There`s a financial incentive for those who`ve spread the lies, who have raised, let`s face it, hundreds of millions, if not billions of dollars off the lie that our elections aren`t to be trusted.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Let`s look at some of the conspiracy theories of fraud. We`ll take them one at a time. "Bogus votes are electronically inserted into tabulation machines."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: They`re not connected to the internet. They are under strict physical chain of custody. It is really hard to get access to a physical machine and do anything to it. And even if there were some attempt to hack the machine or, probably more likely, have some kind of malfunction of the machine, the audits would catch it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Another of the conspiracy theories is that illegal immigrants are voting by the millions.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: That is a hundred percent false. Every single voter has to give a driver`s license number or Social Security number, which is matched against files, databases that are held to make sure the voter is who they say they are, and they`re eligible to vote. And we also know that states who have gone looking for non-citizens voters have found shockingly few, even potential non-citizen registrants.\par \par \uc2 Ohio just recently announced that in a period of time of over a decade, it had found six possible cases of non-citizens voting.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: But fraud does happen, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: It happens very rarely. We know how much fraud in this country happens. We know it`s not zero, but it`s very, very close to zero.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: We`re not talking about millions of votes or hundreds of thousands of votes?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: No, we`re talking about dozens of votes in a big national election. It is, for all intents and purposes, impossible to steal a big national election. We are so decentralized with almost 10,000 different jurisdictions run by Democratic and Republican election officials. Our voter lists are as accurate as they ever have been before, and they`re checked regularly.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): David Becker told us the key to confidence in this election is the paper ballot.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Ninety-five-plus percent of all Americans are going to vote on paper ballots, which are verifiable and auditable to confirm the count was accurate. And then we audit those ballots, which means we do hand counts of the ballots to compare them to the machine counts, to make sure the machine`s got the right result.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Why should we trust the people who are working the election?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: I can tell you I`ve been working with election officials for over a quarter of a century. And when you talk to these election officials out there, Republicans and Democrats, they don`t do this because it makes them rich and famous. They do this because it`s a calling.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): A calling heard by George Harrison, Beverly Woityra and Paul Petruska, trained poll workers near Atlanta. They told us, when there`s a problem, it`s nearly always a mistake the voter made. And there`s a saying at state headquarters, "If you don`t know how anything works, everything`s a conspiracy."\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARRISON: Sometimes they just want to vent. Sometimes they just want to have somebody listen to their question and say, "Hey, I have this question. What about this?" I try to have all of my voters, by the time they leave, be in a better mood than when they came in.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Election doubters are welcome to volunteer. Like two skeptical women who took the four-hour training course recently.\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY WOITYRA: And they just wanted to see for themselves, and they were both convinced by the time they left training that everything was on the up and up.\par \par \uc2 PAUL PETRUSKA: I would say to the voters that are watching this, stay off the Internet. Listen to the people. Come down here and talk to us. Fact check with more than \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and TikTok. That`s the -- what I would want people to take away from this whole entire conversation, is that gossip -- gossip kills.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Gossip isn`t the only menace.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: (Inaudible). Have a great day (sp?). Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: OK.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): Many Georgia poll workers have a 911 panic button on a lanyard. Offices are armed with the antidote, Narcan. Last year, Fentanyl was mailed in an attack on election workers just doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: They want to ensure that all eligible voters, and only eligible voters can vote in a system that is accurate and breeds confidence.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): But confidence remains under attack. This post last week claimed illegal Haitian immigrants are voting with Georgia driver`s licenses. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  traced it to Russia. Election consultant David Becker told us, there`ll be more disinformation and a few breakdowns, and mistakes, none of which should be confused with fraud.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: What should we expect in terms of results on election night?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: So on Election Night, don`t expect to know the winner. Definitely don`t expect to know who controls the \uc2 United States House of Representatives\uc2 . There are a lot of very close races in -- even in places like California, that will take days or maybe even weeks to resolve.\par \par \uc2 The Senate we might know a little bit sooner, but unclear. We probably will have a pretty good idea who won the presidency, maybe around Thursday or Friday.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (voiceover): That`s because the race is essentially tied, including in Georgia, where \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2 , the Republican secretary of state`s chief operating officer, hopes America can endure its next close election without coming apart.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: What is it like to be the chief operating firewall against lies and myths in the election?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : I`ve never heard it put to me quite that way. Wild horses couldn`t drive me away from this job right now. I mean, I know our system is great in the state. I know the elections across America are safe and secure.\par \par \uc2 I know that 2024 will be the safest and most secure election in history. It will be the most scrutinized election in history. And whoever the winner is, be it \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , we will know that they are the correct winner at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: How Georgia is keeping election workers safe.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : A texting tool for every candidate that wants to opt in. It`s sort of a see something, say something.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: At 60minutesovertime.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 UNINTENDED CONSEQUENCES\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022, 20 states banned or severely restricted abortion. Six states voted to protect access to it. And on Tuesday, voters in 10 states will decide on measures that would add abortion rights to their state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 To understand the impact of the changing legal landscape and its complexities, we went to the first state to change its abortion laws: Texas.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you will hear from doctors who say, in Texas, the laws designed to stop abortions are creating unintended consequences, hurting women with desired pregnancies and the people who care for them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: My mom is a doctor and she is probably the coolest person I`ve ever met. And I grew up, you know, with her coming home in her white coat. And I honestly just wanted to be my mom.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): In 2021, Dani Mathisen was following in her mother`s footsteps, on her way to becoming an OB-GYN. She was 25 years old, in her final year of medical school in Texas, married to her high school sweetheart and ready to start a family.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: We planned it out perfectly. I was going to get pregnant at this time. And it was going to be great.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You had it planned.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: I had it planned down to, like, the week.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Mathisen was thrilled when she learned she was pregnant. Early scans and testing showed a healthy baby girl. But a routine fetal anatomy scan at 20 weeks did not go according to plan.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What did they tell you was wrong?\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: Her brain was not formed correctly. She only had one kidney. Her spine was so bent that it put pressure on her heart. It was honestly a blur. I either said, "Is it bad? Or is it lethal?" Her answer was, "It`s lethal," meaning my daughter that I planned out to a tee, and we had already started to get little outfits and bath toys, was going to die.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What did you do?\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: Screamed, cried, got a second opinion, got a third opinion. And it wasn`t a question as to what we were going to do. It was a question of how we were going to do it without getting arrested.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: A motion to concur --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): That`s because just two weeks earlier, a new Texas law went into effect, known as SB-8. Senate Bill 8.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: And this bill says we`ll protect that life when that heartbeat is detected.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): The law banned abortion at six weeks with no provisions for victims of rape, incest or severe fetal abnormalities, like Dani`s case.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: The motion is adopted.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): The law also included a novel enforcement method, deputizing citizens to sue people for aiding or abetting an abortion and rewarding successful lawsuits with a $10,000 bounty.\par \par \uc2 The bill included an exception for medical emergencies, but didn`t define what those were. Mathisen says her doctors seemed scared and confused.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: It wasn`t clear what counted as aiding and abetting somebody getting an abortion. So they couldn`t even counsel me and say like, "Yes, we recommend you have an abortion or at least look into it, even if you go to another state."\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): So Mathisen and her husband turned to her mother, the physician, for help. Several calls later, her mom secured an appointment for her to end her pregnancy at a clinic in neighboring New Mexico, a non-ban state.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: She put her license on the line for that, because I`m her baby and that`s what I needed. She booked our plane ticket. She booked our hotel and gave us an envelope of cash.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You were afraid to use a credit card --\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: -- or have your name attached to anything.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: Yeah, we paid for the abortion in cash so that there wasn`t paper trail of our Texas credit cards paying for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): A year after SB-8 went into effect and Roe was overturned, Texas enacted another, more restrictive law, banning all abortion from conception except when the mother`s life was in immediate danger.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, Dani Mathisen joined 19 women with similar stories in a lawsuit against the Texas government for denial of care.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHRUP: It is the first lawsuit in which individual women have sued a state.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): The lawsuit did not seek to overturn the bans, rather, to clarify which exceptions were allowed under the law.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I was in septic shock.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): The Texas Supreme Court ultimately rejected their case.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Mr. Speaker --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): But after the women filed their lawsuit, Texas legislators quietly passed a new law to include two exceptions to the ban - - one for ectopic pregnancies, when a pregnancy occurs outside the uterus, the other, when a woman`s water breaks prematurely.\par \par \uc2 But according to the \uc2 Cleveland Clinic\uc2 , those instances only make up 5 percent of all pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: The inconsistencies, the misunderstanding, the confusion. This is why women will lose their lives because of these rules.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Dr. Emily Briggs practices family medicine in Central Texas.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: Hey there. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Her office is papered with the photos of some of the babies she`s delivered over the last 15 years. She`s overseen hundreds of complicated pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: Five years ago, I could counsel a patient on all the various treatment options available in these medically complex situations. And now, it is a dangerous situation for me to have that comprehensive conversation with my patients.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: "Dangerous" because?\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: Because I personally, as a family physician, could face losing my license. I could face life in prison. I could face huge fines, just for having a conversation with my patient about evidence-based care.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): So far, that hasn`t happened. No physician has been prosecuted for violating the ban, which is a felony in Texas.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: How does the office look today?\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): But Dr. Briggs says the threat of prosecution has created such fear that today, it`s not unusual for hospitals to require physicians to consult with staff attorneys when treating complicated pregnancies, even miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: This is not the medical care that those of us in medicine signed up for. This is not what our plan was, or is, when talking to a patient about their care. It should be between me and the patient.\par \par \uc2 There`s so much guilt when you lose a pregnancy. So add to that the slap- in-the-face of having the risk department of the hospital come in and be involved in that conversation between you and your physician. And it`s because of the legal ramifications from our legislature.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: When there`s a sense of urgency, like, "We need to deal with this right now" --\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: -- how do you proceed in this moment?\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: With great caution. In these situations, time is of the essence. She could lose her uterus. She could lose her life because of these situations. And when we have our hands tied, and can`t act appropriately at the medically appropriate time, we can have worsened outcomes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Texas has only released maternal death data through 2021.\par \par \uc2 But according to CDC numbers, after Texas restricted abortion, maternal deaths rose 61 percent from 2019, compared to 8 percent nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Emily Briggs says physicians can no longer offer comprehensive maternity care to women in Texas.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Have you heard from colleagues who say, "I don`t want to practice medicine in Texas anymore"?\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: Yes. Definitely. Obstetricians, family physicians, yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Last year, the number of OB-GYN resident applicants in Texas dropped 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: That says to me that future obstetricians are acknowledging the complexity of the rules in Texas. So not only do they not want to train here, but that also means that they won`t want to practice here.\par \par \uc2 DR. ADRIANNE SMITH: So we have a patient ready.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Adrianne Smith, who was a resident in Texas, transferred to the \uc2 University of New Mexico\uc2  hospital this year.\par \par \uc2 She told us one of her last cases in Texas still haunts her: a young woman who became extremely ill after she tried to end her own pregnancy with an unknown medication she bought in Mexico. Smith spoke to a supervising physician about the case.\par \par \uc2 DR. ADRIANNE SMITH: I remember being like, "I wish that there was something more I could do for her." And he looked at me and said, "The attorney general is looking to make an example out of somebody. And you don`t want to be that case."\par \par \uc2 And that was when I realized that there are people that are looking to criminalize me for that and send me to jail.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Eve Espey is the chair of the OB-GYN department at the \uc2 University of New Mexico\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What is it that a resident can learn here in New Mexico that they can`t learn in Texas?\par \par \uc2 DR. EVE ESPEY: I mean, so many things. They lack an opportunity to learn trauma-informed care, diagnosing pregnancy complications in the first trimester and in the second trimester. They miss learning miscarriage care, ectopic pregnancy care, pregnancy of unknown location. I mean, the list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: I mean, those sound like --\par \par \uc2 DR. EVE ESPEY: They miss so much --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: -- important things to know how to do.\par \par \uc2 DR. EVE ESPEY: Really important things.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Those things are part of the training required to become a certified OB-GYN anywhere in the U.S. But here`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, some training is no longer offered because of the new laws. That means OB-GYN residents now have to leave the state for two- to four-week rotations to get the required training.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Is that long enough to really learn the lessons of all of these various things you`ve just described?\par \par \uc2 DR. EVE ESPEY: No, it`s not enough time. I mean, our residents have a dedicated rotation in the first year, in the fourth year. But they are working alongside of us throughout their four years of residency.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): And Dr. Espey says her hospital isn`t just absorbing more residents. Data shows more than 34,000 Texas women traveled out of state for care last year.\par \par \uc2 DR. EVE ESPEY: We`ve seen an enormous increase in our out-of-state patient volume. Just in calendar year 2023 compared to 2019, we saw an over 300 percent increase.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Three hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 DR. EVE ESPEY: Over a 300 percent increase. And we, you know, on any given day in 2023, 70 percent, 71 percent of our patients were from Texas.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Today, women making that journey face even more risks.\par \par \uc2 Six Texas counties have imposed "travel bans," which threaten legal action against anyone helping to transport women out of state for abortion services. Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  has filed a lawsuit demanding access to the medical records of women leaving the state for that care.\par \par \uc2 We reached out to Paxton`s office multiple times over the last two months to ask about the issue and got no response.\par \par \uc2 DR. EVE ESPEY: So many of the patients who come to us that we diagnose with a miscarriage, they`re like, "I`m done with Texas. I want my care here. I can`t trust my own doctor to take care of me for a miscarriage or a pregnancy complication."\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What`s the culture here? Is it --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Dani Mathisen was one of them. After the loss of her baby in Texas, she and her husband moved to Hawaii to begin her OB- GYN residency and start a family.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANI MATHISEN: I did not want to be pregnant in Texas. Absolutely not. I think I know too much about what can go wrong in a pregnancy to feel comfortable being pregnant in Texas.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Earlier this year, the Mathisens welcomed Emerson, a healthy baby girl.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: One of the other things --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Dr. Emily Briggs is urging Texas lawmakers to work with doctors.\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: We are not looking at this from a partisan standpoint. We`re not saying, "We`re enemies." What we need to look at this as is we can all come together to make this safer for women in Texas, just by making some changes to these rules.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: If nothing changes, then what?\par \par \uc2 DR. EMILY BRIGGS: We lose physicians in Texas. We lose healthy mothers. We lose families in general. It`s already scary to decide to become pregnant. Throw on top of that that if something medically complex happened, you could lose your life and not have the care that you deserve. Why would anybody stay for that?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 LAND OF NOVO\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: It has the ring of a Hans Christian Andersen fairytale -- if a supremely ironic one. Tiny Denmark is home to six million of the world`s wealthiest and healthiest people, all those muesli-munching cyclists. Even their letter O sometimes comes slashed in half.\par \par \uc2 But Denmark is also home to what is suddenly Europe`s largest company, \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 , a pharmaceutical firm with a market cap of a half trillion dollars, thanks to products that -- wait for it -- combat obesity.\par \par \uc2 Novo`s drugs Ozempic and Wegovy have slimmed down Hollywood stars and millions of non-celebrities worldwide, while adding great heft to Denmark`s economy. We travel to the Baltic to see how a country with a slender ego is coping with this most unlikely injection of fantastic wealth.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Just another Copenhagen commuter headed off to work in the morning, Lotte Bjerre Knudsen goes unnoticed, first on the train, then, pep in her step, walking from the station to the office she shares with two others. Understating matters, as one does in Denmark, you`d never know from appearances that she is the scientist whose research at \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  led to perhaps the most revolutionary drug this century.\par \par \uc2 LOTTE BJERRE KNUDSEN: I`m so grateful. I was always just been a nerdy little scientist who kind of found home here in this company for 35 years.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Is that really your sense of self still these days?\par \par \uc2 LOTTE BJERRE KNUDSEN: I`m proud. But I`m also humble, and really very focused on the fact that it was a -- it was a team effort.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Modesty aside, her discoveries helped create Ozempic and Wegovy, which not only treat Type 2 diabetes and obesity, but are now approved for treating cardiovascular, kidney and liver disease as well. This, of course, has made her a billion -- uh, not so fast.\par \par \uc2 LOTTE BJERRE KNUDSEN: You know, I`m actually not super interested in actually having a whole lot of money. I don`t think that it doesn`t look like it`s making people happy, right?\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Money`s not something that`s important to you --\par \par \uc2 LOTTE BJERRE KNUDSEN: I like to pay my taxes. I like the society that we live in. I like that there`s equal access to healthcare. I really like that.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Long as she brought it up, let`s get this out of the way: there is very little rotten in the State of Denmark. This is the land that gave us Lego, province of pedaling, all fishing nets and safety nets.\par \par \uc2 And today, \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 `s success and the spike in demand for Ozempic is fattening the country`s economy, creating thousands of jobs, bolstering national pension plans, keeping mortgage rates low. Novo now has a market cap larger than the entire country`s GDP.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Ozempic ad)\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Giving rise to a new national emblem: drugs so popular they`ve become embedded in pop culture, at least in the U.S. where the company advertises liberally, including on this broadcast. But in Denmark, pharmaceutical advertising is illegal.\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: This is very, very unfamiliar for Danish persons. This kind of advertisement. They wouldn`t like it. It`s not very Danish.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Peter Lund Madsen is a celebrated neurologist and writer, and like most Danes, delighted that America`s demand for \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 `s drugs is Denmark`s gain.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Help us understand where this company fits in the Danish national consciousness right now.\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: Novo is a part of Denmark. Because we`re a small country and, finally, we have a big company in Europe, much bigger than anything the Swedes have. So, so we like that notion.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: You can hold this over the Swedes?\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: Yes. Yes. Because they`ve always had cars and airplanes and big companies. But now we have Novo.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Take that, \uc2 Ikea\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: Yes, yes, yes. It`s a first time.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Danishness courses through \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 `s bloodstream. The company was founded in the early 1920s, by August Krogh, a Nobel Laureate, and his wife Marie, a doctor. Their motivation wasn`t financial. It was personal. Marie was diagnosed with diabetes, at the time, a death sentence.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: This drug that we`re seeing right here helped save the life of Marie.\par \par \uc2 HANNE SINDBAEK: It did. She was very discreet about it. She did not want anybody to know that she was diabetic because Marie was a doctor. She was "not a patient."\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Hanne Sindbaek, a Danish journalist, has written two books about \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  and the Kroghs. We spoke to her in a lecture theater in what was the Danish Medical School where the Kroghs first met.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Is it fair to say that this origin story of \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  starts as a love story?\par \par \uc2 HANNE SINDBAEK: It is absolutely fair to say. He was teaching her, as she was a medical student. He fell in love with her right away.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): When, in 1922, they heard that Canadian scientists had stumbled upon a miracle cure for diabetes: insulin. They traveled to Toronto and came home with the rights to manufacture the drug in Scandinavia.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Sounds very nice of the Canadians scientists. Did they ask for anything else in return?\par \par \uc2 HANNE SINDBAEK: They asked that nobody should -- person should profit from it. It should be to the benefit of humanity. That was the "price," you can say. It was a way to get this life-saving drug out in the world fast.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Back in Denmark, within months, they set up the Nordisk Insulin Company. In keeping with their agreement, they established a non-profit foundation, which today controls 77 percent of the company`s voting shares.\par \par \uc2 MADS KROGSGAARD: So the agreement was that if there were revenues and proceeds from the sales of insulin here in Scandinavia, it should be returned to society in the form of support for research into physiology and medicine.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): \uc2 Mads Krogsgaard\uc2  is the foundation`s CEO. Today, it is the largest philanthropic organization in the world, bigger than the \uc2 Gates Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LOTTE BJERRE KNUDSEN: This is actually a 384 plate that Karen (sp?) has there. You know --\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): While \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  always focused on diabetes drugs, it did branch out beyond medicine. In the late `80s, young Lotte Knudsen was assigned to the enzyme team, which had the noble goal of making sure reds and whites didn`t run in the wash.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: You started with laundry detergent.\par \par \uc2 LOTTE BJERRE KNUDSEN: Yes, I did. Yup. It`s the same story, right, of just wanting to make a product that`s useful.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): In the early `90s, she came across a new study about a naturally occurring gut hormone: GLP-1, that lowered blood sugar levels and suppressed appetite. She thought, if it could be harnessed into a drug, it could revolutionize treatment for diabetes and obesity.\par \par \uc2 She went to her boss, Novo`s head of research, \uc2 Mads Krogsgaard\uc2 , yes, the same guy who now heads the foundation.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: What do you remember about her?\par \par \uc2 MADS KROGSGAARD: She was the first one to march into my office with red hair, and very fired up, showing me a publication that was not even published yet. She was talking very agitatedly about this. And I was getting excited.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: I understand you also had to convince senior management about obesity, and what it was, that this wasn`t a behavioral choice, necessarily.\par \par \uc2 MADS KROGSGAARD: They felt, "Just go up on a bike. Do some jogging. Do some biking."\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Just get off the couch and exercise.\par \par \uc2 MADS KROGSGAARD: Yeah. And I started trying to convince them that it`s not getting on the bike. If you`re genetically predisposed, living in the environment we are in today, you are at very high risk. And something should be done about that.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): For the next 20 years, they worked on that GLP-1 molecule before Ozempic finally made it to market as a type 2 diabetes drug. It took another four years for Wegovy to be approved for weight loss. It turned \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  from niche player to a company bigger than \uc2 Coca Cola\uc2  and \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The CEO is \uc2 Lars Fruergaard Jorgensen\uc2 , so typically Danish, his compensation package of roughly $10 million, is dwarfed by his U.S. counterparts. His office is a co-working space atop Novo`s Copenhagen headquarters, designed in the shape of an insulin molecule.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: So your next building needs to be the molecule of an anti- obesity drug.\par \par \uc2 LARS FRUERGAARD JORGENSEN: Yes, it should.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Jorgensen is only the fifth chief executive since the company was founded. Ask him about the weight of the job, and -- no CEO God complex here -- he defaults to the company mantra, the \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  way.\par \par \uc2 LARS FRUERGAARD JORGENSEN: So the \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  way is the basic thinking of our founders. And key elements linked to how we treat each other, how we collaborate. And that`s about being open and honest. It`s all about being accountable.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: This sounds almost like a cult, a religious movement, and not so much a pharmaceutical firm.\par \par \uc2 LARS FRUERGAARD JORGENSEN: I think the values are based on ordinary human, decent values.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: You appreciate you`re not sounding very much like an American CEO right now.\par \par \uc2 LARS FRUERGAARD JORGENSEN\uc2 : Well, I think I`m very grounded as an individual. My upbringing has given me a lot of, say, groundness.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): And in Denmark, chief executives are expected to be grounded.\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: In Denmark, it`s very unrespectable to flash your money. Rich people in Denmark, they tend to buy cheaper cars in order to stay out of trouble.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: So the CEO of \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 , if he`s driving around Copenhagen in a --\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: -- Ferrari or a limousine, how does that play?\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: Poorly. But the other way around, if he was driving around in a cheap car, that would be a very good thing for him. "Oh, I like his driving such a car." He`s a true Dane.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): But for all of Novo Nordisk`s Danish high- mindedness, there is a growing chorus of complaint in America.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BERNIE SANDERS (D-Vermont): Stop ripping us off.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): As an avowed socialist, Bernie Sanders may find plenty to like about Denmark. But at a Senate hearing in September, Sanders excoriated Jorgensen over allegations of price gouging.\par \par \uc2 The CEO told the committee what he told us: the benefits of Novo`s drugs to global health will, ultimately, save trillions of dollars. And if anything is to blame for the high prices, it`s the fractured U.S. health care system.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: What is the response to the skeptical American that says, "Come on. This is Big Pharma. This pricing is predatory. They`re making money off people with health problems.\par \par \uc2 HANNE SINDBAEK: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: This is not Utopia. This is just another big, greedy business"?\par \par \uc2 HANNE SINDBAEK: Of course it`s -- it`s "greedy." You have to compete in the world as it is. And I don`t think that \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  has all these values just to be "nice." They have it because it`s good business.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: They`re not blind to capitalism? That there will be rivals.\par \par \uc2 HANNE SINDBAEK: Absolutely not. They are capitalists at heart.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: And in this --\par \par \uc2 HANNE SINDBAEK: But you can be a capitalist with great values.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): And to keep up with the global demand for Novo`s drugs -- less than 1 percent of sales come from inside Denmark -- the company is sinking billions into new plants worldwide. And just a few miles down the road from the cranes, in the Danish countryside, sits this quintessential Scandinavian institution. Half boarding school, half summer camp, and state-subsidized, of course.\par \par \uc2 It`s a health facility for the small portion of Danes who are diagnosed with obesity. Recently, enrollment has declined by almost half. And some of the empty beds are being filled by, get this, newly recruited Novo employees as they try to find permanent housing.\par \par \uc2 LARS JORGENSEN: That`s a way we can gain a little money, you know? Now we have less students, so --\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Bit of an irony, right?\par \par \uc2 LARS JORGENSEN\uc2 : Yeah, it is.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): \uc2 Lars Jorgensen\uc2  has been a therapist and life coach here for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: When you heard these drugs were coming on the market from Denmark, did you think, "Oh, boy, this --\par \par \uc2 LARS JORGENSEN: We did.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: -- this could impact us?"\par \par \uc2 LARS JORGENSEN: Yes, we did.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: So if somebody says, "I`m just going to take these anti- obesity drugs rather than come here," what would you tell them?\par \par \uc2 LARS JORGENSEN: I would tell them to think about what made you eat too much in the first place. What was that about? Why do you need to take medicine? And for some people, it would be a perfect solution. No doubt about that. But for many people, it will not be, because they still have the problem. The obesity is just a symptom.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: That this goes way beyond --\par \par \uc2 LARS JORGENSEN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: -- what the scale says.\par \par \uc2 LARS JORGENSEN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): On a more macro level, \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 `s runaway success is beginning to shrink entire sectors of the global economy -- fast food, big box stores, even \uc2 Krispy Kreme\uc2 . They`re already tightening their belts in a universe where people are less hungry. Still, there are competitors and counterfeits out there. And Chinese companies are already in clinical trials for generics.\par \par \uc2 But for now, the world`s weight surplus remains the Danes` and Peter Lund Madsen`s economic surplus.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Do you own \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  stock?\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: Yes. I have for many years, like many other people in Denmark.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: How are you feeling about your investment?\par \par \uc2 PETER LUND MADSEN: Everybody has the feeling that this will go on forever. And they are very -- they`re very -- yes, happy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 CBS\uc2  SPORTS HQ)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: The Last Minute of 60 MINUTES is sponsored by UnitedHealthcare, reliable coverage for your whole life ahead.\par \par \uc2 THE LAST MINUTE\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Our last minute is a note on Election Day 160 years ago. In 1864, the Civil War wasn`t going well. And everyone knew \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  would not be re-elected. So Lincoln wrote a memo, folded it closed, sealed it, and made his cabinet sign the back. None of them knew what they were endorsing.\par \par \uc2 The memo reads in part: "It seems exceedingly probable that this administration will not be re-elected. Then it will be my duty to so cooperate with the president-elect as to save the union between the election and the inauguration."\par \par \uc2 Lincoln was re-elected. So it was never necessary to open his secret, solemn vow to a peaceful transfer of power, even while the nation was at war with itself.\par \par \uc2 I`m Scott Pelley. We`ll be back next week with another edition of 60 MINUTES.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SXMN000020241104ekb300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For October 28, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nikole Killion, Lilia Luciano, Elizabeth Cook, Nicole Sganga,  Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: With eight days to go, the candidates campaign in the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Last night, we had a great rally at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the fallout from racist and sexist comments at Donald Trump`s Madison Square Garden rally.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY HINCHCLIFFE (Comedian): There`s literally a floating island of garbage in the middle of the ocean right now. I think it`s called Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Will it have an impact on the crucial Latino vote?\par \par \uc2 Plus: In the final week, we get a rare look behind the scenes with the Harris campaign as it aims to boost turnout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Raise your hand if this is your first presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And after fires are set in two ballot boxes, how federal officials are keeping your votes safe.\par \par \uc2 The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 With just eight days to go before Election Day, both candidates are in battleground states tonight. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is barnstorming across Michigan with three events, and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But, tonight, there is fresh backlash against Trump`s rally in New York City on Sunday, including from many top Republicans who are distancing themselves from the vile, racist, and sexist comments that were made.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, CBS News has learned that the campaign claims they struck jokes that contained the C-word. But we have new reporting tonight about that vetting process. In addition to Trump`s unusual attacks on Vice President Harris, the rally featured a comedian who repeatedly insulted Puerto Rico and today is refusing to apologize.\par \par \uc2 Puerto Rico is an American island. Let me say that again. Puerto Ricans are American citizens and the island is a U.S. territory. And there are about a million Puerto Ricans living in battleground states, where both candidates are vying for the Latino vote.\par \par \uc2 We were with Vice President Harris for much of the weekend as she rallied supporters in Texas and Michigan. And we will have more of our interview tonight.\par \par \uc2 We have team coverage, beginning with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, I understand you have new reporting on how those comments were vetted. Good evening, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening to you, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is calling Sunday`s rally great. However, CBS News has learned that his campaign is now trying to distance themselves from those comments, acknowledging that they vetted some of those jokes and even had to cut out a few profane lines.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We have to get all the Christians to get out there and vote.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sat down with faith leaders in suburban Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I think this is a country that needs religion. It`s like the glue that holds it together.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: It was a dramatic turnaround in tone from his \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Nine days from now will be liberation day in America.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The event was billed as the former president`s closing argument. But, instead, Trump was upstaged by his own speakers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: She is the antichrist.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: One after another hurled insults at Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and other Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GRANT CARDONE\uc2  (CEO, Grant Cardone Enterprises): Her and her pimp handlers will destroy our country.\par \par \uc2 SID ROSENBERG (Radio Talk Show Host): She is some sick bastard, that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , huh? What a sick son of a (EXPLETIVE DELETED).\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But it was comedian Tony Hinchcliffe who drew the most controversy...\par \par \uc2 TONY HINCHCLIFFE (Comedian): There`s literally a floating island of garbage in the middle of the ocean right now, yes. I think it`s called Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: ... for a series of crude and racist jokes.\par \par \uc2 TONY HINCHCLIFFE: And these Latinos, they love making babies too. Just know that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TONY HINCHCLIFFE: That`s cool, black guy with a thing on his head. We had a Halloween party last night. We had fun. We carved watermelons together. It was awesome.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  slammed the Trump event.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spends full time trying to have Americans point their finger at each other, fans the fuel of hate and division. And that`s why people are exhausted with him.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mr. Biden now voting.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: President Biden, early-voting in Delaware, also didn`t hold back.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Just simply embarrassing. It`s beneath any president.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Trump campaign says that those jokes do not reflect the former president`s views, but some sources tell CBS News that they are concerned about some of the fallout from that rally and that it could potentially have a negative impact on the campaign in these final days, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The margins are so slim in these battleground states that every vote matters. Philadelphia is a city with the second largest Puerto Rican population outside of the island itself.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano went to the city to find out how the incendiary so- called joke is playing with Puerto Rican voters in a must-win state.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice-over): Backlash to Tony Hinchcliffe`s comments was quick.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: That was the wrong thing to say. Why he said it, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Superstars like Bad Bunny, J.Lo, and Ricky Martin took to social media and their hundreds of millions of followers, blasting out support for Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 In battleground Pennsylvania, where more than half of all eligible Latino voters are Puerto Rican, Jean-Marie Lugo (sp?) says the joke sealed her vote for Harris.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-MARIE LUGO (Pennsylvania Voter): With the comments that happened yesterday, I finally said, no, I have to be in the right side of the history.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She has lived in Philadelphia for 20 years and was leaning towards Trump until the comments.\par \par \uc2 Why did that make you decide on your vote?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-MARIE LUGO: I think it was not respectful. And, yesterday, I said, they are making fun of us in our faces. Puerto Rico is not a trash can or a garbage island in the middle of the ocean. Puerto Rico is more than that.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Puerto Ricans, like most Latinos, have traditionally supported Democrats by wide margins. Former President Trump has made gains in recent years by focusing on economic issues.\par \par \uc2 But, at a local corner shop:\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: The guy made a joke about it, but you know he meant it.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: So you feel like what the comedian said reflects what Trump thinks?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It went through him. You see what he did last time he went down there?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: He threw the toilet paper.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Throw the paper towel, like we were like...\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Trash.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: We`re trash.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: If he would have been strong enough, he would have said, yo, uh- uh, it`s not funny.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And while I have heard from Puerto Ricans and Latinos who dismissed it as a joke, the issue has raised a huge deal of interest.\par \par \uc2 One of the top searches on \uc2 Google\uc2  over the last 24 hours is whether Puerto Ricans can vote for the president. The answer is yes for those of us living in the 50 states, and that`s more than Puerto Ricans on the island -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Some interesting new reporting.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It`s a sprint to the finish, and the Harris campaign tells us the vice president will visit every battleground state in this final week before America decides. Today, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was back in Michigan, part of a blue wall strategy.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of the places we spent time with the Democratic candidate over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voice-over): Touring an assembly line today in Saginaw, Michigan, the vice president is trying to make a dent in voters` view that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would do a better job with the economy.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: When he was president, he sold advanced chips to China that helped them with their agenda to modernize their military. That`s not about what`s in the best interest of America`s security and prosperity.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Hemlock Semiconductor\uc2  secured a $325 million federal grant to expand production of chips.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: You`re going to be hiring 1,300 new workers starting today, good-paying jobs, $89,000 a year, I understand, at least.\par \par \uc2 How are you? Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We met with Harris for two days this weekend.\par \par \uc2 We are waiting to go backstage to see the vice president here. We good?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Getting a rare behind-the-curtain look. It`s a message aimed at energizing first-time voters, like this \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  veteran.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: This is my first rally that I`m ever experiencing. I was one of the young black males that came up, never thought my vote would ever make a difference. So, this time, I`m actually experiencing voting for the first time at 65 years old. I just turned 65 two days ago.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Older voters, but especially younger ones.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: I have never been this close to a politician before, especially someone as high up as the vice president. And I just think it kind of humanized her a little bit.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Raise your hand if this is your first presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And raise your hand if this is the first political rally you have ever been to.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: As we traveled to battleground, Michigan, we met more first-time voters.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you think is the number one issue for women your age?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Abortion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Abortion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Abortion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Reproductive rights?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Really?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You have been clear that that is a priority, but what you have not been clear on is what that bill would look like. You have talked about restoring Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Oh, but I am very clear. Let`s...\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You have not said what restrictions you would support.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Let`s put back in place Roe v. Wade. This was not an issue in terms -- when Roe v. Wade was intact, for 50 years, half-a-century, women together with their physicians -- we`re here at a medical office talking with physicians.\par \par \uc2 Women, in consultation, if they chose, with their priests, their pastor, their rabbi, their imam, were able to make those...\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So you do support restrictions after viability?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I support Roe v. Wade being put back into law by Congress and to restore the fundamental right of women to make decisions about their own body. It is that basic.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But you know there were -- there are restrictions. With Roe v. Wade, there were restrictions after viability.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We would not be debating this if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had not hand-selected three members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  with the intention they would undo the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And what we have seen, as demonstrated last night and every day these last two years, is extraordinary harm that has occurred in America, where women have died because of Trump abortion bans, where women who have survived rape and, girls, incest, and no exception for someone whose body has been violated to make a decision about what happens to their body next.\par \par \uc2 We have seen women who are experiencing a miscarriage around a pregnancy they prayed for, and being denied health care because doctors are afraid they`re going to go to prison, and those women developing sepsis. We have seen extraordinary harm and pain and suffering happen because of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did in intending and effectuating an overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Yes, my first priority is to put back in place those protections and to stop this pain and to stop this injustice that is happening around our country.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So, then why not say what restrictions you would support as part of that?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I have told you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And when you...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Let`s put back in place Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And when you argue that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if elected, would put forward a national abortion ban...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Just read Project 2025.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: ... the former president said that`s not true, he would veto...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: He says everything. Come on. Are we really taking his word for it? He said that women should be punished. He`s -- he has been all over the place on this.\par \par \uc2 But I`m too busy watching what he`s doing to see what he has said.\par \par \uc2 NARRATOR: The one place in America where women still have a right to choose.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This new Democratic ad out today...\par \par \uc2 NARRATOR: You can vote any way you want, and no one will ever know.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: ... is a sign that Democrats see female silent voters as her best chance for victory.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA (Former First Lady): We are more than just baby-making vessels.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s the very same message we heard from \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  on Saturday in Kalamazoo.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And if you are a woman who lives in a household of men that don`t listen to you or value your opinion, just remember that your vote is a private matter.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We were there on the rope line...\par \par \uc2 Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ... as the voter next to us told us she`s heard about a number of Republicans who will vote Democratic.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #5: Right? There`s been kind of a little under-campaign going about, you can vote and no one knows your vote. And it`s been aiming at women.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There are just eight days to go.\par \par \uc2 What would you like to see the vice president address or talk about?\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: I think more details about what she plans to do, how she plans to make things better. There are a lot of people who are afraid who have short-term memory about what things were like under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There have been questions about enthusiasm, as polls show the race in a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 And, given that -- that polling, which I`m always reluctant to put too much emphasis on...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Me too.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: ... but it does suggest that your momentum has slowed. Do you feel like the race is slipping away?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I do not. And, actually -- and I agree with you. I think, certainly, polling is a measure, but, to be frank, if I`d listened to polls, I would have never run from my first or second office. I wouldn`t be here talking with you.\par \par \uc2 And what I am seeing are -- in states such as South Carolina, I mean, North Carolina, Georgia, historic turnout, historic turnout.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Do you think you have had enough time to make the argument about why you should be president?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I`m going to make the most of the time I have.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: CBS News has offered a sit-down interview to former President Trump as well. And the offer still stands.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of ballots were destroyed today in two suspected arson attacks. A possible break in the investigation and how officials are protecting your votes, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating fires that were deliberately set this morning in drop ballot boxes in Portland, Oregon, and Vancouver, Washington. The sabotaging of ballots is something federal officials are warning about in a new bulletin.\par \par \uc2 We get details now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nicole Sganga.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: In Vancouver, Washington, officials say hundreds of ballots were destroyed when they went up in smoke after a fire suppression system inside this ballot box did not work as it should have.\par \par \uc2 A small number of ballots were also damaged in Portland, Oregon. Investigators believe this vehicle is connected to both fires, and they`re investigating a third similar incident at another nearby ballot box earlier this month, this as the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  warns, some individuals online are calling for the sabotage of ballot drop boxes, circulating their methods on forums typically used by domestic violent extremists.\par \par \uc2 A federal bulletin published today and obtained by CBS News assesses that threat actors are likely to leverage claims of election fraud to drive violence before and after the election.\par \par \uc2 Now, Norah, police in Washington state and Oregon say they have stepped up patrols around these ballot boxes, adding that election offices there are safer today than they have ever been before.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The election is safe.\par \par \uc2 All right, Nicole Sganga, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This week marks the start of Lung Cancer Awareness Month.\par \par \uc2 And while rates have been declining for decades, it`s still the deadliest form of cancer. Each year, more people die of lung cancer than of colon, breast and prostate cancers combined. Researchers have recently noticed an alarming rise in diagnoses among nonsmoking Asian American women.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elizabeth Cook examines this troubling trend.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK (voice-over): For five years, Vicky Ni has been battling lung cancer, a diagnosis that came out of the blue after she went to a doctor for pain in her shoulder.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NI (Cancer Patient): He was taking X-rays of my neck and it was only by chance that the bottom corner of the X-ray showed a raised diaphragm.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: What were you thinking when they said you have lung cancer?\par \par \uc2 VICKY NI: I was stunned beyond words.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: The 54-year-old lawyer and mother of two is now part of a medical mystery. Lung cancer in nonsmoking adult Asian Americans had been rising an average of 2 percent a year for more than a decade before Ni received her diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NI: I assumed that I would get chemo and beat it. It was only later when I met with an oncologist that I learned that I was stage 4 and therefore incurable.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: A study by leading California medical centers found that 57 percent of Asian women diagnosed with lung cancer are nonsmokers. For all others, only 15 percent of the women diagnosed had no history of smoking.\par \par \uc2 You didn`t grow up in an area that there was a lot of air pollution?\par \par \uc2 VICKY NI: No.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: You weren`t exposed to any cancer-causing chemicals?\par \par \uc2 VICKY NI: Not to my knowledge.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: And as a nonsmoker, she wasn`t eligible to be screened for lung cancer.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Currently, screening guidelines drive what`s covered under insurance.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Scarlett Gomez and Dr. Iona Cheng, epidemiologists at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , were awarded a nearly $12.5 million grant from the \uc2 National Cancer Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So some of the factors we`re looking at include certainly exposure to secondhand smoke. High exposure to cooking oil fumes is an established risk factor.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: And why Asian American women specifically? Why not Asian men? Is there a hormonal component to this?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: There is some suggestion based on cellular studies just in the past year showing that a particular genetic mutation may predispose people to be more vulnerable to levels of air pollution.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: At the heart of the mystery is the fact that we are all breathing the same air. But why are women like Vicky Ni getting sick while others are not?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELAINE SHUM (\uc2 Perlmutter Cancer Center\uc2 ): We always wondered, is there something that we can do to diagnose them earlier?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: At New York University`s Perlmutter Cancer Center, Dr. Elaine Shum is randomly screening 1,000 Asian women for free.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELAINE SHUM: We are definitely going to need a much larger study to really provide the evidence to try to change the guidelines one day so that other populations can be offered low-dose C.T. scans by insurance companies.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: Detection that will come too late for Vicky Ni and her husband, David, to prevent advanced cancer...\par \par \uc2 DAVID (Husband of Vicky Ni): Like any cancer, it affects the whole family. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH COOK: ... but could offer hope to spare other families that same pain.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Elizabeth Cook in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: With Election Day just eight days away, a quick check on the record number of people who have already voted. More than 46 million early votes have already been cast in person and through the mail.\par \par \uc2 And, on election night, we hope you will join us right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  at 7:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s CBS Evening News. I`m Norah O`Donnell. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241029ekas00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For October 27, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Anthony Salvanto, Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 9,013 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: The race to the White House is neck-and- neck. We sat down with vice presidential hopeful J.D. Vance. And we will have more of Norah O`Donnell`s exclusive interview with presidential hopeful \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): And make no mistake, we will win.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): It doesn`t matter how we win. We have to win this battle to save our country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: With just nine days until Election Day, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  are locked in a dead heat With tens of millions of early votes already passed, they`re delivering their closing messages to drive supporters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Harris is doubling down on her message that Trump is a danger to democracy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I do believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be an unserious man, but the consequences of him ever being president again are brutally serious.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: With voters deeply divided by gender, former first lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  amplified her warning about Trump`s threat to women`s health care.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA (Former First Lady): Please do not put our lives in the hands of politicians, mostly men who have no clue or do not care about what we as women are going through.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The former president, for his part, using some of his sharpest language to date to describe the impact of illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re a dumping ground. We`re like a garbage can for the world. And every time I come up and talk about what they have done to our country, I get angrier and angrier.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And his political opponents.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They`re so nasty. They`re so evil. They are actually the enemy of the people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will have a new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll, plus an interview with Republican vice presidential candidate J.D. Vance. Former Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who`s campaigning for Vice President Harris, will also join us.\par \par \uc2 And we will get the latest from top election officials in two battleground states, Arizona`s \uc2 Adrian Fontes\uc2  and Pennsylvania`s Al Schmidt.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 It is down to the wire in campaign 2024, and this is shaping up to be one of the closest presidential contests in history. Our CBS News poll out this morning shows that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` edge over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is down to just one point in the national popular vote.\par \par \uc2 The two candidates are tied in the battleground states collectively, and all those head-to-heads are within the margin of error. We will have more from that poll later on in our broadcast.\par \par \uc2 Candidates are making their final appeals to voters and pushing hard for Americans to get out and vote. But the events of Friday night, when Israel launched its long-awaited retaliatory attack on Iran, are compounding fears of an expanded Middle East conflict.\par \par \uc2 Our Norah O`Donnell spoke with Vice President Harris on the campaign trail yesterday in Michigan and asked her about the Israeli strikes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Israel launched an attack on Iran that has raised fears of a widening conflict in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 What is your message to Iran?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): Well, first of all, do not respond. It would be a mistake.\par \par \uc2 And we are prepared to defend Israel, as we have before. We will again. But the critical point is that there must be a de-escalation in the region. And we are working through diplomatic channels and other channels to ensure that there is a de-escalation in the region.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Is there any indication, based on the intelligence that you have seen, that Iran wants to respond?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I can`t speak to that, but we have been very clear as the United States that they should not respond and that it would be a mistake.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What are the consequences if they do?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I will keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can watch more of Norah`s interview with Vice President Harris tomorrow on CBS Mornings and on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 While my colleague was in Michigan, we traveled to the battleground state of Pennsylvania and caught up with the Republican who wants to be the next vice president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The last time we spoke, Iran had just launched a ballistic missile attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE (R-Ohio) (Vice Presidential Candidate): Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, this Friday, Israel did retaliate against Iran.\par \par \uc2 At the debate, I asked you about your position, and you said it`s up to Israel to do whatever it is they think they need to do. The thing is, is that doesn`t address the question of the safety of U.S. military personnel in the region, right now about 43,000 of them.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t address the question of whether the United States, as the largest weapons supplier, should have a say, as they do under U.S. law, as to how those weapons are used.\par \par \uc2 In a Trump/Vance administration, would you keep the troops we have in the Middle East, or would you pull them out, as President Trump tried to do in his first administration?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, obviously we have a lot of troops in a lot of different parts of the Middle East. I think it`s reasonable to support Israel as it deals with this extremely unprovoked attack from Iran.\par \par \uc2 And let`s step back a little bit here, because the reason that Iran has all of these weapons to fire at Israel is because they have a lot of money. And where do they get that money from? From the easing of oil sanctions and from the -- the release of a lot of money that the Biden/Harris administration had effectively locked up.\par \par \uc2 They released it. And the promise of releasing it is that somehow it would make the Iranians a better partner, would maybe get them a little bit more on the trajectory to being a reasonable country. That`s not happening. They`re just using that money to buy weapons that they launch against Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`re tightening up some of the sanctions.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: They`re -- they`re -- they`re tightening them up. They`re going in the other direction after making a big mistake.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: And that big mistake was to empower Iran financially to afford a lot of weapons that they`re now firing not just at the Israelis, but also at us. And they`re also arming the Houthis, and that`s obviously affecting global trade.\par \par \uc2 So I think the first and biggest mistake, the thing that we need to change, is, we need to stop giving the Iranians money that they use to make ourselves and our allies weaker in the region. When we talk -- when it comes to Israel specifically, look, I don`t think that we should be taking assets out of the Middle East at a time when our most important ally in the region is coming under significant attack.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that would send the right message. And, most importantly, it`s not just Israel. It`s us. We have interests that are important in the Middle East. We obviously have to deal with -- deal with the Houthis somehow, because we want to reopen the lines of global trade. Very hard to do that if we do a full-scale withdrawal,.\par \par \uc2 But if there are certain countries where maybe we shouldn`t have troops, I`m willing to have that conversation. But, you know, you`re talking about 14 different theaters, 14 different troop deployments.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: It`s hard to make a broad stroke -- broad stroke conclusion about the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, it would be a specific question on Iraq, since the Biden administration said 2026, the year you have to make a decision on troops. Do you have a view on that at this point?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: On Iraq specifically?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: No, I mean, look, I think we need to look at the situation. We obviously need to have a conversation with our -- our allies in Iraq and obviously recognize, where -- what is the direction of Iraq?\par \par \uc2 Are they becoming more and more allies of Iran? Are they becoming a little bit more of a good partner in the Middle East? So, it`s -- it`s hard to say what we`re going to do two years down the road, because we`re not -- we don`t know what the world is going to look like two years down the road.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Staying on national security, as you know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the director of national intelligence on Friday said Russia is behind a fake video that is circulating of someone tearing up ballots in Pennsylvania, the state we`re sitting in right now.\par \par \uc2 They assess Russia will release more media like this in the coming weeks. What price should Moscow pay for trying to manipulate American voters?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, look, I think all -- a lot of countries are going to try to manipulate our voters. They`re going to try to manipulate our elections. That`s what they do.\par \par \uc2 I think the bigger question is, what is in our best interest vis-a-vis Russia, not what price Russia should pay for putting out social media videos. And I think what`s in our best interest vis-a-vis Russia is, in particular, for them to stop supporting the Iranians as the Iranians engage in acts of aggression.\par \par \uc2 And I think, when it comes to Europe, it`s important for the killing to stop in Russia and Ukraine. I don`t think that we should set American foreign policy based on a foreign country spreading videos on social media. I think we should set American foreign policy based on what`s in our best interest as a United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You don`t consider that election interference and crossing a red line?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: I think it`s bad. I think it`s bad.\par \par \uc2 But social media posts and social media videos, Margaret, you want us to go to war because the Russians made a ridiculous video or paid for it?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there are options other than war, as you know, sanctions, other measures. I mean, that doesn`t...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, but -- but this -- this raises a difficult question, Margaret, because every -- everything...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Manipulating voters, that`s part of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Everything that we`ve tried, a lot of the sanctions that we`ve tried, they`ve gone off like a wet firecracker. We promised at the beginning of the Russia-Ukraine war that we would engage in financial nuclear war against the Russians.\par \par \uc2 Biden administration, Harris administration officials talked about how our sanctions would cripple their economy. They didn`t. And so we have to be realistic about what America can accomplish, compare it against our national interests and just be smart about this.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that we should overreact to anything. What we should do is encourage our fellow Americans to be careful. Don`t trust everything that you see on social media. And, of course, we should push back where appropriate. But that`s the big question, is, what is an appropriate response to a country making social media videos?\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to make a commitment to that sitting right here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you have full confidence that the state of Pennsylvania and the election officials here are administering this election fairly? Do you foresee litigation in this state?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Look, I think that they`re doing a good job. I think that they`re doing a better job than they were in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And, most importantly, I think most Americans are seeing, whether it`s voter I.D. in places like Wisconsin or Georgia or a little bit of tightening of the regulations around signature verification in places like Pennsylvania, that I think our elections are in a pretty good spot in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, if something comes up -- we don`t know what the future is going to hold. If we see evidence, for example, of an illegal alien casting a ballot, yes, we`re going to challenge that in court.\par \par \uc2 But seeing what I -- what I see right now, 10 days out from the election, Margaret, I think Pennsylvania is in a really good spot. I think we`re going to win the state, and I think Pennsylvanians, Republicans, Democrats, independents, should have confidence in the elections here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: At a minimum, would you call on Moscow to knock it off, to not post videos like this in the coming weeks?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, of course. I would call them to knock it off. But this is not a schoolyard. I call on Moscow to knock it off, are they actually going to do it?\par \par \uc2 We need to have a real, serious conversation with the Russians, but also with ourselves, about what tools of diplomacy and statecraft we have to dissuade the Russians. So, yes, I condemn the Russians for funding fake social media views, but I don`t think that my job as a statesman, as a person who wants to be the next vice president of the United States, is to go engage in saber-rattling.\par \par \uc2 My job is to figure out how we can best respond to these threats. And, unfortunately, thanks to Kamala Harris`s leadership, we`re in a much weaker space vis-a-vis Russia than we were 3.5 years ago.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about polling and where we are in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Former Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  called you and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  misogynistic pigs. She urged voters to look at what you have said in the past.\par \par \uc2 And she said of you personally -- quote -- "You`ve got a real women problem."\par \par \uc2 Do you think your ticket has a women problem?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: No, I don`t think that we do, but we have to make our best case to women and to men as well as we can.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, women and men are going to decide where the chips ultimately fall. I think it`s a more interesting reflection of Kamala Harris`s campaign that, at this late stage, she`s not talking about how she can lower the price of groceries, because she raised the price of groceries. She`s not talking about how she can lower the price of housing, because her policy saw a 40 percent increase in the cost of housing.\par \par \uc2 She`s going to Michigan with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the person whose father is responsible for the deaths of millions of innocent Arabs and tens of thousands of innocent American troops, and saying, effectively, that, if you elect me, I`m going to have the foreign policy of Dick and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that message is going to fly in Michigan. I don`t think it flies in Pennsylvania either.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On the topic, though, of -- of how the campaign is doing, our \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling shows a significant gender gap here; 40 percent of women say the Trump campaign is paying too much attention to men`s concerns; 56 percent of women say the Trump campaign is paying too little attention to women`s concerns.\par \par \uc2 You say one of your top advisers is your wife, Usha.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Yes, the top adviser.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is she -- so, what is she telling you? Because the data says you do have a women problem.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, what she`s telling me is, we got to make the case to women as well as we can, and ultimately trust in the wisdom of those women to make a determination about what`s in the best interest of their family.\par \par \uc2 All I can say to women voters, and frankly, to men voters too, but to all Americans, is, I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the person who`s going to lower the cost of groceries. I know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the person who is going to secure the southern border. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a plan to open up American energy, to lower the cost of goods and to make our neighborhoods and communities safer.\par \par \uc2 I know a lot of women care a lot about the safety of their communities. What does it say about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` leadership that violent crime has gone up? What does it say about the fact that fentanyl overdose deaths have increased under her leadership? I think that we can make a good case to women, but I`m not going to tell them who they have to vote for.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to try to persuade them. The voters are ultimately going to decide.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You took a vow, first as a Marine and then as a United States senator, to protect and defend...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Of course.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... the Constitution of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 Can you assure the American people that you will do everything you can to stand by that and to make sure that President Trump abides by the Constitution?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Of -- of course.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t worry about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes in the Constitution. It`s why he`s running for president a third time, is because he`s worried that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has broken the greatest economy in the world, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will fix it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He`s talked about suspending it, the January 6 events. I don`t have to retread the territory you well know about challenging the certification of the election.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: I -- I -- I -- I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes in the American Constitution. I know he does. I have seen it in his actions. I have talked to him about it.\par \par \uc2 But you asked about me. And, personally, yes, my first loyalty is to the American people and to the United States Constitution. I think the best way to serve loyally to those people and to that Constitution is to get us back in a place where Americans can afford to live a decent life again.\par \par \uc2 That`s why we`re running the campaign focused on the affordability of goods, American manufacturing -- it`s a big deal here in Erie, Pennsylvania -- and certainly on securing that southern border.\par \par \uc2 I think the threats to our Constitution, Margaret -- and I do think they`re real. The threats to our Constitution come from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who`s saying that she wants to censor Americans, instead of persuade them.\par \par \uc2 Whether you agree with me or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or disagree with us, we believe in the First Amendment. We`re going to fight for your right to speak your mind and think whatever you want in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Vance, you have a busy schedule. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be right back in one minute.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re back with former Wyoming Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who has been campaigning for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (R-Wyoming): Thank you. Great to be with you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have a number of questions for you, but I do want to start where I left off with Senator Vance.\par \par \uc2 He responded to your characterization of him as a misogynistic pig by attacking your father and his role in the Iraq War. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also done so. Do you care to respond?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : Look, I think that they are doing everything they can to try to distract from the fact that the people who know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  best, including retired Four-Star Marine General \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , who is a Gold Star father, have come out and said very clearly and very directly to the American people that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not fit, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself standing near the graves of our fallen service members says things like they are suckers and losers.\par \par \uc2 We all watched on January 6, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sat in the dining room next to the Oval Office for hours, for hours, and watched people with his flags and his name, a mob he had sent to the Capitol, he watched them brutally beat police officers and invade our Capitol.\par \par \uc2 His own vice president is not voting for him. His chiefs of staff aren`t voting for him, secretaries of defense, national security adviser.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, this is completely unprecedented. I don`t think, certainly in my lifetime, probably in American history, have you had a situation where so many of the top officials of the former president are saying they won`t vote for him.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : And I think, you know, we`re going to hear continued assaults and rhetoric from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and J.D. Vance to try to cover that up.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, it`s desperation. The American people are -- are much smarter than that and -- and they see through it, I`m confident.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Given how outspoken you have been, are you concerned about your personal security if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins this election, as he well may do?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : Look, first of all, I am very confident that Vice President Harris is going to win this election. It`s what we`re seeing all across the country, the kind of absolutely unprecedented coalition that`s coming together to support her.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re all going to run through the tape, and nobody is overconfident here, but I do believe she`s going to be the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has ushered violence into our politics in a way that we haven`t seen before. Any violence is unacceptable. Certainly, the assassination attempt on the former president was completely unacceptable and obviously should never have happened.\par \par \uc2 But when you have a situation where, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  suggests that people who disagree with him ought to be put before military tribunals, that the former chairman of the Chief -- Joint Chiefs of Staff should be hanged for treason, and his running mate, you know, doubles down on it, you know, I think that -- that tells you that you`re dealing with a man who doesn`t have any conscience.\par \par \uc2 And the people who worked most closely with him know that. And so I`m confident that he`s going to be defeated next week.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re seeing a really interesting gender divide in this election. And I know you`ve been appealing to people to vote their conscience, and you said they never have to say a word to anybody about who they actually do vote for.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, former first lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  said something similar. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA (Former First Lady): If you are a woman who lives in a household of men that don`t listen to you or value your opinion, just remember that your vote is a private matter.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Regardless of the political views of your partner, you get to choose.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have to say, it`s a little striking. It sounds a little 1950s or 1970s.\par \par \uc2 Are you really hearing from female voters that they`re afraid to tell their spouses who they`re voting for?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : Yes, you know, what we`re seeing is so important and so interesting. You`ve seen a real coalition of women who are pro-life and pro-choice come together to support Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 And that`s because we`ve seen some of the -- just the draconian laws that have been passed in places like Texas and North Carolina that are preventing women from getting lifesaving health care, preventing women from getting medical care that you know will ensure that, if they have a miscarriage, that they can have babies again, just fundamentally a set of circumstances that can`t be maintained.\par \par \uc2 And so you have a growing coalition supporting Vice President Harris. You certainly have had many Republicans who are speaking out to endorse her, which, again, is unprecedented. And there are also many Republicans and independents who are saying, look, you know, I don`t want to bring the wrath of, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and J.D. Vance down on me, so I`m going to vote my conscience, I`m not going to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 And we, you know, obviously, encourage that. Your vote is a secret vote. You should do what you know is right. And -- and I think you`re going to have, frankly, a lot of men and women who will go into the voting booth and will vote their conscience, will vote for Vice President Harris. They may not ever say anything publicly, but the results will speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s an interesting coalition you`re talking about in terms of the pro-life movement and where we are now. And you`re talking about a coalition here.\par \par \uc2 How do you explain to someone at home who may agree, as you do, with the pro-life position that abortion is murder, and reconcile that with putting that conviction aside and voting for Vice President Harris? How did you do that?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : Yes, I don`t think it`s about putting convictions aside.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s about looking at the reality on the ground of what`s happened since Roe was overturned. And, again, when you have a place like Texas, where women need lifesaving care, you know, perhaps it`s a woman who`s had a miscarriage and she may be bleeding out, and she cannot get a DNC, she can`t get the care that she needs, because there`s concern that maybe that care has been criminalized.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, you have a situation also in Texas where the attorney general is suing, literally, he`s suing so that he can see the medical records of women who have sought abortions. And, you know, this is coming from people who support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won`t let anybody see his own medical records.\par \par \uc2 So, the idea that -- that, you know, you`ve got those kinds of policies and state laws being put in place is really mobilizing women to say, look, you know, you don`t have to abandon being pro-life, but this kind of circumstance, this kind of really abhorrent situation where women can`t get medical care they need, that just can`t go on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think that Vice President Harris going to the Ellipse on Tuesday, as she plans to do, to make her closing argument is really the most effective one?\par \par \uc2 Because we see in our polling that, on both sides of the aisle, each party is blaming the other for undermining democracy. Why do you think that would help put Harris over the mark and help win?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : Yes, I think it`s so interesting.\par \par \uc2 I think, when you -- when you look at the polling, first of all, people have to remember, this is the first presidential election post-January 6. And so, you know, you`ve got, in fact, many of the same people who were promising a red wave in 2022 doing the same thing now. We`re not going to see it now. We didn`t see it then.\par \par \uc2 And what Vice President Harris has done, I have watched her do it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : I have sat next to her on the stage as she does it. She talks about a whole range of issues. She talks about grocery prices.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : She talks about women`s health care. She talks about, you know, Donald Trump`s tariff policies...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : ... which are massively inflationary...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : ... and at the same time reminds everybody...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ve got to...\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : ... you have to have a president who obeys the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re hitting a hard break here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, former Congresswoman Cheney.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 With a closer look at our new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll, we`re joined by our executive director of elections and surveys, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Good to be here. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, we have seen in your polling of the popular vote that this is neck and neck. As we know, it comes down to the magic 270, the electoral count. And those battleground states that have those hefty electoral votes with them are really what are being focused on by the campaigns.\par \par \uc2 In your numbers, those are also dead even.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, this is as tight as all this has ever been. Big picture, the national vote is everyone`s voice, of course, and that all counts. But in that race to 270, for electoral votes, it`s going to come down to a handful, we think seven battleground states. Across them it is exactly even. Hasn`t really moved much over the course of this campaign. The individual states themselves are all close. I do think it`s curious, this year, more than others, the national vote is tracking very closely with those battleground states. It could mean we`ve just got a very nationalized election here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have, through this election, particularly since Vice President Harris moved to the top of the ticket, talked about the divide along gender lines. What are you hearing from voters?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: So, that gender gap, which is now, in our polling, the largest we`ve seen yet this year, is driven by a couple of things, I think, starting with people`s views on larger societal factors. And we asked people, what do you think about U.S. efforts to promote more gender equality? When people say those efforts, they believe, have gone too far, and that`s a little more men who say that, they`re voting overwhelmingly for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . When people say those efforts haven`t gone far enough, and many women say that, they`re voting overwhelmingly for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Those numbers are pretty stark.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: They really are. And it`s one of the biggest splits in the - it`s a reminder that the gender gap isn`t just a cross tab on a poll report, it`s people expressing views about the candidates, as well as the larger direction of society, frankly.\par \par \uc2 And then the other part of this is, when they do evaluate the candidates, you see some differences. For example, you see more men saying they believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be a strong leader, although when you ask them they say it`s not necessarily because he`s a man. But at the same time, look at this measure we`ve been following all throughout this campaign, which is, who has the mental and cognitive health to serve as president. Folks will recall we were tracking that when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the nominee, and his numbers dropping on that was important. OK, now Harris has the advantage on that. And particularly among women who are much more likely to say that only she has the mental and cognitive health. So, it also affects the views of the candidates as well, these splits between men and women.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which is kind of a remarkable question to begin with in and of itself. But you have been talking about, for a very long time, the - the huge factor of the economy as a perception of the economy is for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters. The Democrats are putting reproductive healthcare front and center. Is that bet working for them?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, it motivates the Democratic base. And that`s important because we may have a very turnout-determining election here, right? But at the same time, I do notice that the number of people who say abortion is a major factor in their vote has been fairly steady throughout this campaign, hasn`t really gone above the low 50 percent. And one might think that if the Democrats were getting more traction on that, that those numbers would go higher. And, frankly, they haven`t.\par \par \uc2 At the same time on the economy, you know, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had been trying to separate herself from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a little bit. And we do see in the polling more people start to say they`re evaluating her on her proposals. She`s cut a little bit into Donald Trump`s advantage on what really is a central question, are you better off or would you be better off financially under which of these candidates. Trump still has the edge on that, though. She hasn`t cut quite far enough in it. And so he maintains that edge for people who say that the economy is their top issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Rhetoric has been extremely heated throughout this campaign, but both of them are now arguing the other is the threat to democracy itself. How are voters reacting?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: So, a couple of things. One is, you see each side does think the other one is a threat and each side does think that democracy is under threat, albeit for different reasons, number one. Number two though, you do see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has often gotten a proportion of voters who feel that the whole system is broken. And I think that`s really central to understanding part of his appeal. So, that hasn`t changed much.\par \par \uc2 What you do see, and you can ask people how they want the government to work overall. And by and large voters say they`d like the two parties to negotiate and cooperate. There are some hardcore partisans who`d just like their party to run everything. But there is always a component, and there is here, of people who say that they want one strong leader to cut through all of this. It happens to be a little more among younger voters, a little more among young men. And we do see more of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters saying that relative to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  supporters.\par \par \uc2 This, for a larger context, does happen sometimes in democracies, if you have a decline of trust in institutions, which we have also seen. It`s not the majority, but that view is out there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that allows itself to have more manipulation of perception and what voters are thinking. It`s a - it`s a dangerous thing you just put your finger on. And that`s why I want to, Anthony, set some expectations here on clarity. People remember days when, on election night, it was clear who the winner was. In very tight races, it`s not going to be clear and it may take some time. U.S. intelligence is warning there are bad actors looking to manipulate how long it may take and to manipulate your mind.\par \par \uc2 What expectations should we have for the vote count?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: The first word is patience. You`re right that it will take a while for all our expectations. A lot of these battleground states, it`s not just that they`re close, it`s that they`ve got millions of votes to count. And especially some of the larger counties may take them late into the night. It may very well go another day, another couple of days before all those get counted. We may need to see all of those votes before we can determine who`s won. So, patience is the watch word here.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing is, we are at CBS News going to show you how this unfolds all along. I am going to show you from the data desk where the outstanding vote is, what`s been reported, what the implications of all of it are in real time so that you can watch it unfold, along with us. And I think that will be important. You don`t want to just see a few votes get reported and think that`s the way it`s all going to go. You really have to have that patience.\par \par \uc2 I would add this, most Americans may very well cast their ballots before Election Day, right? We`ve got mail vote. We`ve got early in person. That will be over the next week, Margaret, kind of the abstract art painting of the political world. People are going to see whatever they want to see in that. We don`t always know exactly how those people voted. They`ll look at how many older people, younger people have voted, how many registered Democrats, registered Republicans. But since 2020 there have been shifts in the people`s decisions about how to cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: And we don`t know how that will play out yet. So, I urge patience there as well over the next week. And we`ll have those expectations about what the early vote means once we get closer to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re going to speak on this program with the secretaries of state to understand how they are administering those elections and how quickly each state - each one`s different - will know the results.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Turn now to the top election officials in two battleground states, Arizona Secretary of State \uc2 Adrian Fontes\uc2  joins us from Phoenix, and secretary of the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania Al Schmidt joins us from Harrisburg.\par \par \uc2 Welcome. And I appreciate the two of you having this conversation in a bipartisan manner.\par \par \uc2 You are in non-partisan roles as the top election officials in your states. So, very quick question to both of you, Secretary Fontes first, will you commit to certifying the election results even if the winner is of the opposite political party?\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN FONTES\uc2  (Secretary Of State, Arizona): Yes. Without question. That`s our duty. And I believe all of my colleagues across the country would do the same.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Schmidt.\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT (Secretary of The Commonwealth, Pennsylvania): Absolutely. Without any hesitation whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Now that we`ve set the table on the very basics here, I want to ask you about some of the recent news. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  director said this past week it is incomprehensible and unacceptable that election workers have to be worried about their security. But here we are. And we know that the Department of Justice, just this past week, did indict individuals for making threats to workers in both of your states.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Fontes, one of my \uc2 CBS\uc2  colleagues is reporting Maricopa County is significantly increasing security and using drones, using fencing and other measures. I`ve read news organizations say there are going to be snipers on top of tabulation centers.\par \par \uc2 First of all, is that true? That seems extraordinary. And is that based on specific threat intelligence?\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN FONTES\uc2 : Well, first and foremost, the authority and jurisdiction over the specific tabulation centers are within the counties. My office provides support for all of them. And, frankly, we don`t get into the specifics when it comes to our safety and security protocols, particularly the source or frequency or nature of any of the specific threats. We are in the kind of a circumstance right now where we`re taking whatever precautions are necessary to ensure, not just the security of the vote, but of all of our employees, our workers at the polls, at our tabulation centers and, of course, keeping first and foremost in mind the safety and security of our voters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you say, at a minimum, whether that`s increased from what you had to do in 2020?\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN FONTES: It`s absolutely completely different from 2020. In fact, I remember in 2018 our greatest security threat was a rattlesnake in the parking lot at the Pinnacle Peak precinct. So, this is a radically different set of circumstances that we are dealing with. But we are prepared and we`re going to have a secure election.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Schmidt, what have you had to do in the commonwealth?\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT: As part of the lessons learned from the experience of 2020, the Shapiro administration organized an election threat task force that I lead, made up of federal, state and local partners in law enforcement and election administration so that if any of the ugliness returns, that we experienced in 2020, everyone will be ready.\par \par \uc2 In 2020 it was, as Secretary Fontes said, so different than before. And many of us had to scramble to figure out when threats were incoming, where do we refer them to and - and all the rest of that. Now we have open lines of communication. Everybody is very clear about what everyone else`s responsibilities are. So, we`ll be prepared should, again, any of that ugliness return.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Schmidt, you heard me discuss with Senator Vance the specific intelligence that the ODNI and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  revealed on Friday about a fake video made by Russia to trick people into believing that ballots were being destroyed in Bucks County, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you about what U.S. intelligence has uncovered, and why do you think they`re targeting your state?\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT: Well, I`m - I`m very concerned. And it`s - and it`s clear that bad faith foreign actors are seeking to undermine confidence in our election results. And it`s no surprise that they`ve targeted several of the so-called battleground states in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 The video that you`re referring to from Bucks County, to anyone who has years of experience in election administration, was conspicuously fake. But if you don`t, it`s - it`s so easy for people to be sort of deceived by others trying to undermine confidence in our system of government. So, it`s important that our counties remain vigilant, that our commonwealth remains vigilant and our federal partners remain vigilant, and to call out any of these sort of efforts to deceive our voters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Fontes, another specific warning from U.S. intelligence was some of the mind hacking, the lies being sewn in the minds of potential voters. And one specific thing that U.S. intelligence says Russian influence actors are doing are posting allegations about the possibility of illegals, undocumented people, voting in this country. You are a border state. We have heard a number of Republican officials, including on this program, try to argue that this is a real concern. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is arguing that and amplifying that.\par \par \uc2 What are you doing in Arizona to make sure that non-citizens are not voting in federal elections, which is already against the law?\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN FONTES\uc2 : Well, first and foremost, if and when something like that happens, it is absolutely vanishingly rare. No system is perfect. And I`ll admit that right off the top.\par \par \uc2 But the allegation that this is something that might turn an election or is something much bigger than it is, that`s the nonsense that we are dealing with. And, unfortunately, we still have candidates, elected officials and folks with large megaphones lying. They`re directly lying to the American public and they are basically playing the role of useful idiots now we know for sure to foreign enemies who want to sow distrust in our democracy. They want us to be losing that civic faith that we have in one another as Americans and dividing us further.\par \par \uc2 Again, the source for a lot of this information and misinformation, I should say, is coming from outside of the United States. It gets amplified by folks in America who are helping those folks. And that`s just bad. And it`s bad for our democracy. It`s bad for business. It`s bad for the law and education and science and everything else.\par \par \uc2 So, what I encourage folks to do is stop being a Russian puppet, look to the folks who are actually doing the work, ask them what`s actually happening, and go with the truth instead of amplifying lies from foreign adversaries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Schmidt, what are you doing to make sure that non-citizens are not able to vote, which is already against the law?\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT: Well, Margaret, I was a Republican election commissioner in Philadelphia County for ten years. And I assure you, and I assure your viewers, and I assure voters that I care as much about election integrity as anyone possibly could. I investigated hundreds of allegations of voter fraud over that time. Whether it involved double voting or allegations of people voting for dead people, or non-citizens. And what that research showed time and time again was how incredibly rare it was that it occurred, and how easy it is to catch whenever there are incidents of it. There are safeguards in the system from tooth to tail to make sure that - that voter fraud in the few instances it does occur is identified, investigated, and responsibly prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Schmidt, on Friday the Lancaster County district attorney did report incidents of voter registration fraud among a group of about 2,500 ballots. What can you tell us at this point about how many of those ballots were actually fraudulent, and what happens next? Who`s behind it?\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT: The county reached out to us right away as soon as they became aware of it for guidance. As far as we can see, the county is - is pursuing this investigation responsibly. The county hasn`t concluded that investigation so I can`t speak to the number of cases yet. We will be relying on them to make that determination and be supportive of our county partners.\par \par \uc2 As part of our commitment, I visited 67 of Pennsylvania`s 67 counties, to sit down with them and make sure that if there are additional resources they need, additional equipment or anything else like that, we provide that to them so that we have a free, fair, safe and secure election in 2024, just as we had in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it was notable in my interview with Senator Vance that he said he was confident in Pennsylvania. But when I asked about litigation, he raised the possibility of non-citizen voting and said they would be open to litigation if that were to happen.\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT: Well, the good thing about our judicial system, which is really where our disputes should be settled, is it is always something of a put up or shut up moment when it comes to providing actual evidence of actual problems. And that when people are involved in litigation and lawsuits, they have to present that evidence. And I think that`s why time and time again, in many dozens of cases in 2020, every one of those cases upheld that our elections were accurate, and that we`re not susceptible to any sort of widespread voter fraud or anything like that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Fontes, 95 percent of all ballots cast in battleground states will be recorded on paper. However, President Trump said this on Joe Rogan`s podcast about a conversation he just had with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He said to me, that unless you have paper ballots, it can never be an honest election. That`s a big statement.\par \par \uc2 JOE ROGAN, HOST, "THE JO ROGAN EXPERIENCE": That`s a big statement.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We should go to paper ballots. You`re done by 9:00 in the evening, right? Now we have these sophisticated machine, it goes up to heaven, it goes all over the place and down and around and they say, we`ll need two weeks to figure out who the hell won the election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Secretary, do you want to set the record straight and how long will it take you in Arizona to know the results?\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN FONTES\uc2 : Well, first, I`m glad that Mr. Trump agrees with the security of Arizona`s elections, where we have 100 percent paper ballots and have had so in Arizona for several decades.\par \par \uc2 Number two, it`s going to take us as long as it has always taken us. While we have more voters and in many cases and counties a lot more voters, the time that it takes us to process our ballots is based on doing it right the first time, not doing it quickly for news cycles or for the sake of political satisfaction. We will have official results in about 10 to 13 days, but everybody knows that the - the networks and the newspapers will make their prognostications whenever they see fit based on their math. And that`s just the way it works. We`re perfectly confident that we`re going to have what we need, when we need it, which is when it happens, and it will be accurate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Schmidt, how quickly will you know the results in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT: Pennsylvania is at something of a unique disadvantage in that our countries cannot begin processing mail-in ballot envelopes until 7:00 a.m. on election morning. Plenty of other states, red and blue alike, allow that process to begin days, if not weeks, in advance.\par \par \uc2 But a couple of things have changed since 2020. Counties have acquired additional equipment that assists with the processing of those ballots and ballot envelopes. They have a lot more experience than they had in 2020, which is the first time any of us encountered, in Pennsylvania, mail ballot voting in a large scale. And also the percentage of voters, while it`s still widely embraced, the percentage of voters voting by mail has decreased since 2020 during that peak Covid environment.\par \par \uc2 So, there are several factors that while I think contribute to knowing results earlier, even though the law hasn`t changed, but as Secretary Fontes said, it always comes down to, and has always ever come down to, how close an election is before you know who won and who lost.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly. And as our Anthony Salvanto, our \uc2 CBS\uc2  elections and surveys director, was telling us, this is a tight one. So, we will be watching what`s happening in your states very carefully.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for level-setting with all of us.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: America`s elections are -\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Here.\par \par \uc2 What a wild couple of weeks it has been.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And this race continues to be a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It is the issue of economy and inflation that is weighing the most on voters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The vote count will likely extend beyond Election Day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Major Garrett has been fact checking on the fly and in real time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One of the things that`s hanging in the balance is whether truth matters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In an election like no other, turn to the team like none other. "America Decides," CBS News, live election coverage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you can watch CBS News on election night. We will go live from our headquarters in New York City. And we`ll have our teams spread out all across the country. Join us for real time results, analysis and exclusive polling. Our coverage starts at 4:00 p.m. Eastern on CBS News, 24/7, streaming channel, and at 7:00 p.m. Eastern on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  television network.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching. Until next week. For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020241027ekar00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for October 25, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Scott MacFarlane, Ed O`Keefe, Nancy Cordes, Steve  Hartman, Karen Morfitt, Rob Marciano, Carter Evans \par \uc2 3,631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news, Chinese hackers target \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , JD Vance and Democratic officials.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We`re here today, the great state of Texas --\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The battle in the Lone Star State with just 11 days to go. The candidates focusing on key issues, abortion rights and border security.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`re here with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Houston where hometown hero Beyonce makes her first appearance on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 More people in more states are sick in that McDonald`s E. coli outbreak. What you need to know.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Why did you want to join a fraternity?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE MCGEE: To have like a great time.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we go On the Road with a college student who`s achieving his dreams.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Do you think they`ll be friends for life?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE MCGEE: Friends for life.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening tonight from Houston, Texas for this special edition of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS and we are here to cover this presidential race. As both \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were here in the great state of Texas today, Election Day is now just 11 days away and polls show that the race is deadlocked. We`re also on the campaign trail to speak exclusively to the Vice President in an interview you`ll be able to see right here on CBS News and across our streaming platforms, that`s starting on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of news to get you tonight and we have teams covering both presidential tickets. We want to start with that breaking news, we are just learning that Chinese hackers targeting phones used by Trump and his running man. Now, that is according to federal officials. Officials are still trying to figure out if any damage was done, but the National Security implications are significant. And it seems like the hackers didn`t just stop with the Republican ticket.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane has the breaking details. Scott, what have you learned?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yeah, Norah, investigators are now suspecting Chinese criminals were targeting both parties. A potential case of espionage, not election interference. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  confirmed late today it`s investigating unauthorized access to telecommunications infrastructure in the U.S. people who are potentially targeted are now being notified.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s team responded today arguing without evidence that Democrats bear responsibility saying they`d stop at nothing including emboldening China and Iran attacking critical American infrastructure. Telecommunications giant, Verizon, today said, "A highly sophisticated nation-state actor has reportedly targeted several US telecom providers to gather intelligence."\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Scott, we have known that China as well as other countries want to try and interfere in our election. But what do they say China was hoping to accomplish?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Well, cybersecurity experts say the Chinese government would very much want intel on presidential candidates of both parties so close to the transfer of power, it would be an intelligence coup.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS\uc2 , FMR. DIR. CYBERSECURITY & INFRASTRUCTURE SECURITY AGENCY: If this is in fact the ministry of state security out of the People`s Republic of China, they`re very likely not quite interested in you and me, for instance. They are much more interested in national figures. They are more interested in the types of national security and economic security information --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But, Norah, the breadth and scope of this potential cyber intrusion was not immediately clear nor what data might have been taken.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Scott MacFarlane with that new reporting. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Houston, tens of thousands of people you can see pack this stadium to hear from the Vice President and listen to some of the star power tonight including Beyonce. Texas, you know, it`s not a battleground state. In fact, it`s a solid red state but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is here tonight because the Lone Star State has some of the most restrictive abortion rules in the country where doctors and nurses can get jail time for performing abortions.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes who is with me here in Houston. Good evening, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Harris may be trailing here in Texas, but she came because she sees this state as ground zero in the fight over reproductive rights an issue, she`s hoping will drive women to the polls everywhere just as it has over and over again in state races over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): Texas is Trump territory, but that`s almost the point.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): It`s good to be in Texas --\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Tonight, Harris will argue that the near total abortion bans here and in 12 other states are endangering pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Real harm has occurred in our country. A real suffering has occurred people have died.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: They are restricting --\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: With 11 days to go, abortion is a cornerstone of Harris` closing argument.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : For 54 years, they were trying to get Roe v. Wade terminated and I did it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: She has released two new ads about it, just in the past three days.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: He`s bragging --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Bragging about the rights that he stole from American women.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It`s a message the Harris campaign hopes will resonate with some women who once favored her opponent. In 2020, for instance, 63 percent of white women without a college degree voted for the former president. But they are nearly as likely as white women with college degrees to say abortion is a major factor in their vote.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: I don`t think that anybody should be able to control somebody else`s body.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Tonight, Harris will appear on stage with more than a dozen women who say they were harmed by state abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN CASH: We started trying to have a second baby in 2001.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Caitlin Cash is from Austin. She went into hemorrhagic shock after giving birth to her daughter last year, because she needed a procedure typically associated with abortions to remove leftover placenta.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN CASH: I almost bled out and I woke up several hours later in ICU away from my daughter and when we asked about it, the hospital staff said, oh, we don`t do D&Cs anymore. And then we never heard anything else.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: (INAUDIBLE) in Houston. And Nancy, there`s a huge gender gap in this race, what are we seeing the Vice President do in these final days to try and shore up support?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Well, she`s looking to win over women once again this evening with a sit-down interview with Brene Brown, the popular podcaster and relationship guru. And then, of course, a short time from now, she`s going to be appearing on that stage behind me with the Queen B herself, Beyonce here in Beyonce`s hometown of Houston, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, more than a dozen former Trump administration officials today signed an open letter backing up comments from former White House chief of staff \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  who made big news this week by saying Trump meets the definition of a fascist and repeatedly expressed admiration for Adolf Hitler. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe is covering the Trump campaign tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Tonight, new threats from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to hold people accountable not for any potential election fraud this year, but for what he calls rampant cheating in 2020.\par \par \uc2 "WHEN I WIN," Trump said today on X, "those people that CHEATED will be prosecuted to the fullest extent of the Law," and face long-term prison sentences.\par \par \uc2 There`s no evidence of widespread fraud during the 2020 elections, but that isn`t stopping Trump and his allies from raising concerns about fraud in the closing days of the race. Thursday, Maryland Republican congressman, Andy Harris, became the first known lawmaker to seemingly endorse a radical idea pushed by Trump supporters, to assign North Carolina`s Electoral College votes before all the ballots are even cast.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY HARRIS (R, Maryland): I mean, there are a lot of people who aren`t going to get to vote and may make the difference in that state.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Harris said he believes North Carolina 16 electoral votes should be awarded to Trump before Election Day because thousands of voters in rural, historically Republican western counties offended by recent storms might not be able to cast votes for Trump. Late today he said his comments were taken out of context.\par \par \uc2 And North Carolina Republican congressman \uc2 Patrick McHenry\uc2  dismissed the idea.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 PATRICK MCHENRY\uc2  (R, North Carolina): It makes no sense whatsoever to prejudge the election outcome and that is a misinformed view of what is happening on the ground in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Early voting is already underway in North Carolina and officials are encouraged by turnout levels in storm-affected areas. Campaigning today in Texas, Trump didn`t bring up election concerns in person focusing instead on immigration, falsely claiming 325,000 immigrant children are missing in the U.S. And he criticized Vice President Harris for also spending time in the Lone Star State.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She doesn`t want to go to a swing state because she`s losing so badly in those states, so she decided to come to Texas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: While the campaigns were deep in the heart of Texas today, they`re now coming here to Michigan as the Wolverine State begins early voting tomorrow. Trump rallies tonight here in Traverse City. And tomorrow outside Detroit, Vice President Harris is headed to Kalamazoo with the former first lady, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 . Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will see you there in Michigan. Ed O`Keefe, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, we learned today that dozens more people have gotten sick in that E. coli outbreak linked to McDonald`s quarter-pounders. At least one person has died. While the source of the outbreak has not been confirmed, other fast-food chains are removing raw onions from their menus. We get the latest developments from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Karen Morfitt in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAREN MORFITT (voiceover): At least 75 people have been sickened and the number of people hospitalized has more than doubled in the deadly E. coli outbreak linked to McDonald`s, that`s after the CDC reported an additional 26 cases today. Twenty-two people have now been hospitalized and the outbreak has widened to include three new states: Michigan, New Mexico and Washington State.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Herlihy is the state epidemiologist in Colorado where the most people were sickened.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL HERLIHY (Colorado State Epidemiologist): Be on the lookout for symptoms and if you develop symptoms to reach out to your health care provider.\par \par \uc2 KAREN MORFITT: McDonald`s announced late today they will stop sourcing onions from Taylor Farms Colorado facility indefinitely. The fast-food chain previously announced they stopped serving quarter-pounders at 20 percent of their nearly 14,000 U.S. locations.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Farms, a supplier to McDonald`s, has also voluntarily recalled some of its yellow onions. The company told CBS News: "Our priority remains the health and safety of our consumers. We continue to work with CDC and \uc2 FDA\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Bill Marler\uc2  represents two people who say they were hospitalized with E. coli poisoning after recently eating at McDonald`s.\par \par \uc2 BILL MARLER (Food Safety Lawyer): I do expect this outbreak to grow. I would expect the numbers to continue to go up as public health reports more illnesses across the country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KAREN MORFITT: McDonald`s issued a new statement tonight saying slivered onions from Taylor Farms were distributed to approximately 900 of their restaurants with a select number of those being in transportation hubs like airports, which could account for illnesses in additional states. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Karen Morfitt, thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 A month after Hurricane Helene disrupted and displaced thousands of people, there are signs of recovery tonight. We`ll take you there next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Thousands of students in North Carolina returned to school today for the first time since Hurricane Helene tore through portions of the state about a month ago. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Rob Marciano spoke with students, parents and educators about the challenges they face to reclaim a sense of normalcy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It`s going to be the best day.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (voiceover): In the hills of Watauga County, North Carolina, the ride to Valle Crucis Elementary is filled with excitement. Students here are finally back to school.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 2024)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: It was a month ago that floodwaters from Hurricane Helene swept through Western North Carolina carving a path of destruction, filling Valle Crucis Elementary with four feet of muddy water and swamping the school buses parked outside.\par \par \uc2 Now, classes are being held in an old conference center, a temporary space about a mile from the damaged school. Leslie Alexander is the superintendent.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE ALEXANDER (Superintendent, Watauga County Schools): You know, getting kids back in the building is a first step to normalcy but we have to realize that people have been through a lot.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: When Helene hit, over 450 schools were impacted across 28 school districts. Schools in five of those districts remain closed. Some may not open until early November.\par \par \uc2 Many students are still carrying a heavy mental load, pain of losing a loved one or their home or both.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I want you to look to somebody near you --\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Today, extra counselors are on hand, available for any students or staff you want to talk.\par \par \uc2 GEORGIA: I started feeling anxiety from not seeing anyone, so it was really refreshing when I heard that this week we were going back to school.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Twelve-year-old Georgia was with her parents, James and Heather, when their home began to flood, and parts of their community were washed away.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER: When the rain stopped and we went and walked around, she said, I`m not learning academically right now, but I`m learning a lot about survival.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: You get emotional when you talk about lessons of survival, why is that?\par \par \uc2 The water may be gone, but the resilience remains.\par \par \uc2 Rob Marciano, CBS News Valle Crucis, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Affordable housing is a big issue for millions of Americans in the run-up to Election Day. We hear from voters in a key battleground state, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Being able to buy a home or afford to rent one is an important issue for millions of voters this election year. Both candidates for president have ideas to make the American dream a reality for more people, but whose plan will help more? Tonight`s CBS` Carter Evans hears from voters in the battleground state of Nevada.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): For nearly a year, 32-year-old Mason Cunha and his realtor have been struggling to find the right home in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 MASON CUNHA: Everything would have to be remodeled.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: At the right price.\par \par \uc2 What keeps you from buying a home?\par \par \uc2 MASON CUNHA: You know, it just doesn`t really make sense right now to buy a home with the interest rates and where they are and with the inventory what it is.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Harris campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS: We have a serious housing shortage in America.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: If elected, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  plans to work with the private sector to build 3 million new homes. Cunha is a Harris supporter.\par \par \uc2 Is that going to solve the problem?\par \par \uc2 MASON CUNHA: I think it`s going to definitely help if you are to double or triple or quadruple the inventory.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Harris also proposes outlawing price-fixing by corporate landlords and giving first-time homebuyers a boost.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Harris campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS: With a $25,000 down payment assistance so you can just get your foot in the door.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LUM: I would want to review what the qualifications are for that. Where is that $25,000 coming from.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Thirty-two-year-old Andrew Lum is a professional DJ. He sold his home when his family expanded, and he now rents but can`t afford to buy. Lum says his life was better when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LUM: 2020, we were able to buy home. We were able to buy it at an interest rate that was possible. We were able to buy it with, you know, minimal down payments.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : It`s all about the American dream.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Trump says he wants to reduce mortgage rates by slashing inflation, and he says he`ll open limited portions of federal lands to allow for new home construction. A plan the Biden administration is already enacting.\par \par \uc2 More than 80 percent of the land here in Nevada is owned by the federal government. This 20-acre plot in Las Vegas was recently transferred to the county and now all of it is designated for affordable housing.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I will also stop inflation by stopping the invasion, rapidly reducing housing costs.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Trump says his promised mass deportations will make more housing available.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LUM: So it just seems a little far-fetched to me that all the houses are being purchased by immigrants.\par \par \uc2 MASON CUNHA: I think everything that Trump says has to be taken with a really aggressive grain of salt, because he is known to inflate the truth.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Two supporters of the American dream, no matter who they support for president. Carter Evans, CBS News, Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: On the Road is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We end tonight with a story about the many rewards of kindness and acceptance. Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: At \uc2 Clemson University\uc2  in South Carolina --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The ClemsonLIFE program gives students with intellectual disabilities a chance to learn life skills for independent living.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: So we are going to do grocery shopping today.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But as much as the program offers, junior Charlie McGee wanted the whole college experience, from alpha to omega.\par \par \uc2 Why did you want to join a fraternity?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE MCGEE: To have a great time and experience the brotherhood and more joy.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Unfortunately, rushing a fraternity is a process. There are interviews, and mixers and even then, there`s no guarantee of acceptance. In fact, no fraternity here had ever taken a kid from the ClemsonLIFE program, until now.\par \par \uc2 And when Charlie found out he got a bid, he was so excited he ran out of his left shoe and into the loving arms of his new fraternity brothers at Phi Kappa Alpha.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We saw him run towards us and we just lost it. And after that, it was just raw emotion of all of us just taking him in.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: That was one of the coolest moments at Clemson that we`ve had was seeing that right there, so.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The guys told me, at first some of the members doubted a person with Down Syndrome could fit in with the group. But they were willing to do Charlie the favor. Only to learn later that Charlie was the one doing them the favor.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: The joy and light that we were missing in our chapter that we didn`t know we were missing from the beginning is unreal.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: He kind of broke all of the preconceived notions that I thought of that a fraternity man should be like.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from ClemsonLIFE)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Others have come to the same conclusion. This year, a total of six fraternities and five sororities all welcome students from ClemsonLIFE. And according to Charlie, it will be a game-changer, because he says something magical happens whenever you`re accepted, this fully.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE MCGEE: My disability, it doesn`t really exist.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: When you`re with your fraternity brothers, you feel like your disability doesn`t exist?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE MCGEE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Now that`s a rush.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, On the Road in Clemson, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Doesn`t your heart just burst with that story? We are all more alike, than we are different.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell here in Houston. Be sure to watch our interview with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  across all CBS News platforms beginning on \uc2 CBS\uc2  Sunday morning. Until then, have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241026ekap00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Workers Reject Contract Proposal; Trump Campaign in Georgia, Lashes Out at Kelly; Harris Appeals to Undecided Voters in Town Hall; North Korean Troops Training with Russian Forces; Doctor in Lebanon Hospital Compares Chaos in Gaza; DOJ Warns Musk $1 Million Check Giveaways May Be Illegal; Arizona Campaign Office Attack; Remembering Dodgers Great, Fernando Valenzuela; \uc2 FAA\uc2  Probing Close Calls at Our Nation`s Airports; High Levels of Lead Found in Syracuse Water; Trump and Harris Make Last Push to Key Voters; The Backbone of Broadway; Heart Health in the US; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; "Shrinking" Grows; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Kris Van Cleave, Nikole  Killion, Weijia Jiang, Imtiaz Tyab, Debora Patta, Jo Ling Kent, Jarred  Hill, Lilia Luciano, Vladimir Duthiers, Nancy Cordes, Anthony Mason, Celine  Gounder \par \uc2 14,299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets on a Thursday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers reject another contract offer extending their strike. What it means for the company and the economy.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Do you feel like \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has heard that message now?\par \par \uc2 JON HOLDEN (IAM Union District 751 president): Members wanted their pound of flesh, and they`re getting it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With less than two weeks until Election Day, Vice President Harris seizes on reports former President Trump praised Hitler.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (CNN host): Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a fascist?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: North Korea sends thousands of troops to Russia amid the war with Ukraine, prompting a warning from the White House.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): They will become legitimate military targets.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The Justice Department warning \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that his daily million dollar lottery for swing state voters may be against the law.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: LaGuardia airport can now boast about being named the best airport in the country by Forbes Travel Guide.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): For New Yorkers who want to check LaGuardia out, the easiest way to get there is to drive to Newark Airport and then fly there.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  dropped this diss track about Elon Musk`s recent appearance at a Trump rally.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN) (2024 vice presidential nominee): Elon is on that stage jumping around, skipping like a dip (bleep).\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: That is top tier Midwestern dad slam.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I believe the scale goes, Mister, Pal, Knucklehead, Real Piece o` Work, Bozo, Dip (bleep) and silently stands up and walks to garage.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s pretty funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m very familiar with that scale, even though I am not from the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think we all are familiar with that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us. We begin with a breaking story that could have a big impact on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Striking workers at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  voted to reject the company`s latest offer. It means the strike goes on at America`s largest exporter.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is outside a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  facility in Renton, Washington.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Certainly not the way \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executives were hoping they`d be starting the day today, after 64 percent of striking workers rejected their latest labor offer.\par \par \uc2 It was a four-year deal. One perhaps bright side here, it did fare far better than their first offer, which was nearly unanimously rejected here. But nonetheless, this is Day 42 of the strike, 33,000 workers will stay on the picket line, shutting down the bulk of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s airplane production.\par \par \uc2 The rejected deal called for a 35 percent wage hike up from \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s initial offer of 25 percent. It also included increased health and retirement contributions, but the major sticking point for workers remains the loss of their pension. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has said that pension is not coming back.\par \par \uc2 Before the vote, we talked to the union president, Jon Holden about this impasse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Is it realistic to think a pension could come back? The auto workers said they wanted their pension back, it didn`t happen. I don`t know that I can point to a single union that`s won a pension back.\par \par \uc2 JON HOLDEN: Yes, it is the direction society is going, and it`s not right, it`s not okay. But our members are dealing with that, and some want that defined benefit pension, and I understand that fully.\par \par \uc2 We haven`t been able to achieve that, and I can`t say that we will by staying out on strike.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Boeing\uc2  declined comment on the vote, but it came just hours after the company announced dismal third quarter earnings that included a loss of about $6 billion for the quarter alone. The company is preparing to lay off 10 percent of its global workforce to cut costs. There is no timeline for when negotiations may resume -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now taking you to politics and two very different strategies in these final days before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  appeared to be courting the moderates last night in a CNN town hall in Pennsylvania, calling her opponent unfit and dangerous, while former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appears to focus squarely on his own base at two campaign events in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion and Weijia Jiang are following the candidates. We`re going to start with Nikole with the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent much of his time here in Georgia, courting the evangelical vote and firing up conservatives appearing at an event last night alongside fire brands like \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ignited a packed Duluth, Georgia arena Wednesday night with fireworks ablaze.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We will fight, fight, fight and win, win, win.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Blighting into his opponent --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She takes some time off. I`ve gone 52 days in a row, and I`m not taking any days off, that I can tell you.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But he saved his more fiery rhetoric off stage for his former White House chief-of-staff, \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , who this week revealed then President Trump spoke favorably of Adolf Hitler and warned the former president could rule as a dictator if he wins a second term.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2  (Former White House chief-of-staff): He certainly falls into the general definition of fascist, for sure.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In a post on Truth Social, the former president slammed Kelly as a "total degenerate," a "low life" and a "bad general whose advice in the White House I no longer sought."\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: At a town hall Wednesday night, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was asked if she thinks Trump is a fascist.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes, I do. Yes, I do, and I also believe that the people who know him best on this subject should be trusted.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Trump hit back by falsely claiming the vice president called him Hitler. Many rally goers said they still stand with the former president.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD YATES (Trump voter): Well, he may say things he doesn`t mean sometimes, but you know what, we all say things we don`t mean sometimes.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH MANN (Trump voter): Did he run this country as a dictator the four years he was in? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: As early voting surpasses the two million mark here in Georgia, the Secretary of State`s Office confirms that it was the target of a cyberattack that was unsuccessful.\par \par \uc2 Later here in the Atlanta area, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will hold her first joint campaign event with former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and "The Boss," Bruce Springsteen -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 At that town hall in the Philly suburbs, Vice President Harris tried to appeal to persuadable voters, and she was pressed on her positions on immigration, the war in Gaza, and also grocery prices.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is in Philadelphia for us. Weijia, good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Tony. Good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 The Harris campaign tells us they believe that 10 percent of battleground voters are still up for grabs, including right here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 At that town hall, the vice president took questions directly from several of those voters who say they`re not convinced who to choose.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In an appeal to undecided voters, Vice President Harris vowed to bring change to the country.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: My administration will not be a continuation of the Biden administration. I bring to this role, my own ideas, and my own experience.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But at times, Harris struggled to provide specifics when asked about policy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So my question is concerning groceries. What would you do to bring prices down for Americans?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We will have a national ban on price gouging. There are a number of issues that we need to address in terms of bringing down the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What would you do to ensure not another Palestinian dies due to bombs being funded by US tax dollars?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Far too many innocent Palestinian civilians have been killed. It`s unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Harris was pressed on why she now supports a border wall she once ridiculed as stupid.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Let`s talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that border wall.\par \par \uc2 So remember, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said Mexico would pay for it. Come on, they didn`t.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: But you`re agreeing --\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: So come on.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER -- to a bill that would earmark $650 million to continue building that wall.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We -- I pledge that I`m going to bring forward that bipartisan bill to further strengthen and secure our border.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: CNN`s town hall was held in Delaware County, 25 miles outside of Philadelphia. The campaign says it represents former GOP strongholds that could turn blue, where they are focused on finding Independents and moderate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Back in the city, volunteers are canvassing Philly, making sure voters cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Black Men Vote.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Billy Miller (sp?) is the state director for the non-partisan group, Black Men Vote.\par \par \uc2 Can you put into words how important turnout is?\par \par \uc2 BILLY MILLER (Black Men Vote state director): It is the most important thing in this election.\par \par \uc2 If every Philadelphia voter came out and voted one way or another, it wouldn`t matter what the rest of Pennsylvania did.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: A senior campaign official says next week, the vice president will make what they are calling a closing argument speech on the National Mall, the very same spot where Trump delivered his January 6th remarks right before the assault on the Capitol -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Weijia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have a disturbing update about what could be the next war in Ukraine. The US and \uc2 NATO\uc2  say they have confirmed that troops from North Korea have started training with Russian forces. They say if the North Koreans joined the fighting in Ukraine, that would be a dangerous escalation of the war.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab time has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: It`s a scene that has set alarm bells ringing from Washington to Kyiv. North Korean soldiers, around 3,000 reportedly on the ground in Russia dressed as Russian military personnel.\par \par \uc2 The images not independently verified, apparently show the recruits at a training exercise near the far eastern Russian city of Vladivostok, just four hours from the border with North Korea, according to a White House assessment.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): After completing training, these soldiers could travel to Western Russia and then engage in combat against the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In June, when the autocratic leaders of Russia and North Korea, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Kim Jong-un held these direct talks, THEY also signed a Mutual Defense Pact, deepening ties that stretch back beyond the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 Since then, South Korean intelligence officials have said North Korea has sold more than a million artillery rounds to the Kremlin, bringing in desperately needed foreign currency.\par \par \uc2 While President Putin in dire need of more arms and men, is attempting to contain domestic anger at the estimated 600,000 killed or wounded Russians fighting in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): The question that we`re asking ourselves, and we don`t have an answer for right now, is, what does Kim Jong-un think he`s getting out of this?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And the fear is North Korea wants Russia to help modernize its outdated weapons systems, including its nuclear missiles, nuclear missiles, Kim, just this month, warned he would not "hesitate to use" -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imtiaz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In Lebanon, overnight, Israeli forces attacked more targets around the capital Beirut, while \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fired rockets of its own into Israel. Now, the fighting across the region is putting more civilians at risk.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta spoke to one of those eyewitnesses who accuses Israel of using the same tactics in Lebanon as it used in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Starved, besieged, and now forced to move again, Israeli drone footage shows thousands of Palestinians under military orders to evacuate Northern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Israel says it is for their own protection, but residents say there is no safe place to go, fearing they will never return home again.\par \par \uc2 (YINA ABU NADA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "We can`t take it anymore," cried Yina Abu Nada (sp?). "We are being exterminated and no one is helping us. We are innocent."\par \par \uc2 Speaking from the north, Dr. Abu Safieh (sp?), told CBS News, the situation is catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 DR. ABU SAFIEH: Actually, we don`t have medicine, medications, medical supply, food, water, blood unit.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: On Israel`s second front in its war with \uc2 Hezbollah in Lebanon\uc2 , Israeli jets pounded the suburbs of Beirut overnight.\par \par \uc2 American emergency doctor, Mohammad Subeh did two missions in Gazan hospitals and is now volunteering in Lebanon, where he told us, it feels like the early days of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 DR. MOHAMMAD SUBEH (American emergency doctor): We`re seeing kind of the same playbook that was played out.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: He told us ambulances transporting patients to hospitals are being targeted, and that just like Gaza, they are treating an overwhelming number of civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 Israel insists it operates within strict international rules of engagement, but for Subeh, it is the faces of the children caught up in both these wars that haunt him.\par \par \uc2 DR. MOHAMMAD SUBEH: If you ignore everybody else, think about the child, and if that doesn`t move you, I feel like you`ve kind of lost that nucleus of what it means to be a human being.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has said that due to the intense bombardment of Northern Gaza, they`ve been forced to postpone the vaccination of more than 100,000 children against polio -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Debora Patta in Beirut, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been telling you that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is offering $1 million prizes to voters in battleground states. The mega billionaire and his political action committee are backing former President Trump`s 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 Now CBS News has confirmed a Justice Department warning to Musk`s super PAC. CNN was the first to report this.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent has the latest.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Nate, good morning. We`ve confirmed that the DOJ has sent a letter in the last few days to Elon Musk`s America PAC, basically warning that the $1 million checks he`s giving to registered voters may violate federal law.\par \par \uc2 The world`s richest man is randomly selecting registered voters in seven major battleground states who sign a petition supporting the First and Second amendments, and so far, the PAC says it`s given out four sweepstakes-style checks like those, three in Pennsylvania and one in Holly Springs, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 This DOJ warning reportedly comes from the section of the agency that investigates election related legal violations, and the DOJ itself has declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 And now the nonprofit advocacy group, \uc2 Public Citizen\uc2  has also weighed in. They filed an \uc2 FEC\uc2  complaint requesting an investigation as well.\par \par \uc2 Now the federal election law says it is a crime to pay anyone to vote or register to vote, and legal scholars that we`ve been talking to say this appears to be a clear violation, but it could be interpreted in a gray area, since Musk is asking people to sign a petition.\par \par \uc2 Now, Musk himself has already poured $75 million into his own PAC to get Trump voters to the polls. We expect to see more money poured in in these final two weeks. And of course, if you`re on X, you`re seeing it.\par \par \uc2 Musk, who owns what is formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , has been promoting Trump to his 200 million plus followers, and of course, was promised a prominent role in a second Trump White House last month.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve reached out to Musk and his PAC for comment on the DOJ`s letter, and they have not gotten back to us -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jo Ling, thank you very much. Very interesting story there.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, more interesting stories. New effort to stop near collisions at America`s airports. A pair of pilots tell us how they almost faced disaster. But first, it is 7:16, let`s take a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, we celebrate the one thing everyone gets at a Broadway show. "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " has been around for 140, years. Anthony Mason will show us what the theater guide means to audiences and actors. I collect mine.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Brett Goldstein talks about his Apple TV+ show "Shrinking." He co- created the acclaimed series, and he is part of the new season.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll show you Olivia Munn`s revealing and courageous photos for a new ad campaign.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A man is under arrest this morning, accused of shooting at a Democratic Party campaign office in Tempe, Arizona that`s just outside of Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 According to police, it`s the 60-year-old Jeffrey Michael Kelly who had a huge arsenal of guns, I mean, really big stockpile; ammunition, too, inside of his home. They say he may have been planning what the authorities call a mass casualty event.\par \par \uc2 Jarred Hill reports now, the suspect is accused of targeting that campaign office more than once.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEN MCCOY (Tempe, Arizona Police Department chief): Justice does not have a political party. There is no place in the city of Tempe for political threats or intimidation.\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: On Tuesday afternoon, police surrounded Jeffrey Michael Kelly`s car during a traffic stop before hauling him away in handcuffs.\par \par \uc2 He was arrested on terrorism charges in connection with a string of shootings at a Democratic Party campaign office. Officials say the three shootings started back in September, and all happened around midnight.\par \par \uc2 During one incident, Kelly allegedly fired BB pellets and then gunshots at the glass front door and a window.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference, police said that after five weeks of investigation, it was tips by people in Kelly`s neighborhood that led police to him.\par \par \uc2 During his first court appearance Wednesday morning, prosecutors told the court that back in July, Kelly did a \uc2 Google\uc2  search for the DNC office`s address, and officials said that during a search at his home, they found more than 120 guns, over 250,000 rounds of ammunition and .22 caliber casings in his trash can, along with scopes, silencers, a grenade launcher and body armor.\par \par \uc2 Officials say Kelly was seen hanging suspicious bags containing a white powdery substance from several political signs lined with razor blades just a day before his arrest. They say at least one of those bags was found to just contain baking powder.\par \par \uc2 Kelly was released on bail and ordered under house arrest. His attorney says Kelly is a retired aerospace engineer with no prior record and legally owned his firearms.\par \par \uc2 Following their discovery, officials say they believe Kelly was planning a much bigger mass casualty.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jarred Hill.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in a moment, what the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is doing to address a string of scary close calls at our nation`s airports. Yikes.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to want to see this story.\par \par \uc2 Local news, though, coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MLB/October 23, 1981)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 LA Dodgers\uc2  are remembering one of their heroes from the past as they prepare for the World Series this year. On your screen right now, Fernando Valenzuela beating the \uc2 New York Yankees\uc2  40 years ago plus, 1981 World Series. That, of course, was the year of Fernando Mania.\par \par \uc2 Valenzuela won Rookie of the Year and the Cy Young Award for the Best Pitcher in Baseball, pitching the Dodgers to their fifth overall championship. No one has done what he did ever again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He died at the age of 63 on Tuesday. Very sad news, dying way too soon.\par \par \uc2 We asked former big leaguer turned commentator, Harold Reynolds about Valenzuela`s outsized impact on the sport.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HAROLD REYNOLDS (American former baseball player): You look at Fernando coming out of Mexico, he turned Los Angeles upside down.\par \par \uc2 You had people coming from all over to Southern California to come see him pitch, and then wherever he went, he sold games out. So I think he was the first really true international star in our sport.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Major League Baseball will honor Valenzuela during the World Series, when the Dodgers, once again, face the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it wasn`t just Southern California. I was in love with him as a kid in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you remember him well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I could throw left-handed a little bit to this day, because as a kid with a tennis ball in the yard, I would do his wind up, which was distinctive.\par \par \uc2 He would look to the heavens before he dealt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you remember him, Tony on the thing --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh, of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was so huge. Yes, so huge. He was a guest at the White House that summer in `81. That`s how big of a deal he was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At 63, he is so young.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Legendary career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So young.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a legacy he has left us with.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has begun an audit of 45 major airports aiming to reduce close calls on the nation`s runways. Now, just days ago, in San Diego, air traffic controllers briefly put two Southwest planes on a potential collision course. Yikes for that.\par \par \uc2 But they caught it just in time.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave talked about the dangers with two pilots who survived another close call.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Southwest abort.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: As this \uc2 NTSB\uc2  animation shows, a \uc2 FedEx\uc2  767 came within 150 to 170 feet of landing on top of a Southwest 737 at the Austin, Texas Airport last February.\par \par \uc2 It was an extremely foggy morning. Air traffic controllers in the tower couldn`t see the runway, but cleared the Southwest flight to take off and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  to land.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  Captain Hugo Carvajal was at the controls.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN HUGO CARVAJAL (\uc2 FedEx\uc2 ): As soon as the tower cleared them to take off, we knew that there was an issue because we were about three miles, which is too close at that point.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Just moments before possible disaster, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  First Officer Robert Bradeen saw something through the fog below.\par \par \uc2 FIRST OFFICER ROBERT BRADEEN (\uc2 FedEx\uc2 ): I saw a light that turned out be the position light on the Southwest airplane, and quickly saw the silhouette following that and called for a go around.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What goes through your mind as you`re seeing an airplane that shouldn`t be there?\par \par \uc2 FIRST OFFICER ROBERT BRADEEN: Obviously, there`s a moment of disbelief: Am I really seeing this? Is this really an airplane right there? And then training and experience kick in.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Last Wednesday, another close call near the Austin airport, when the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says a Cessna unexpectedly flew within hundreds of feet of an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  737 on approach to land from Chicago, forcing the pilots of American Flight 2587 to take an evasive maneuver before landing safely.\par \par \uc2 And earlier this month in San Diego, controllers briefly put two Southwest planes on a potential collision course after clearing one plane to taxi across the runway the other was using to take off.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Southwest 1478 cancel takeoff clearance.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  chair, Robert Sumwalt was part of an \uc2 FAA\uc2  committee reviewing close calls that made 24 safety recommendations.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: I certainly think things are moving in the right direction. It`s important that those of us in the aviation profession, we not lose vigilance, and that we continue to push for better procedures, better technology, better staffing, and that we don`t get complacent.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says close calls, especially those happening on runways are rare and on the decline.\par \par \uc2 In a new audit, the agency will look to identify any additional gaps in procedures, equipment, or processes at the nation`s 45 busiest airports.\par \par \uc2 Recommendations to further reduce the risk of runway close calls are expected by next year.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Kris Van Cleave. It`s good to know that they`re really looking into this.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever been on a flight where it`s coming in for a landing, and then all of a sudden, the pilot guns it, and you go right back up in the air?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, whatever it is, it isn`t good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whatever it is, it isn`t good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I know. We all want our planes to, you know, get there on time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And do it quickly, but not so quickly that they`re on top of each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that is the problem.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I love this report, because it`s some of the things we don`t think about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, we just want to get to our location, destination, and land.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yet another example of a quick thinking pilot seeing something --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- and making an adjustment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Experience, experience, experience.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So thank you to them.\par \par \uc2 All right ahead, alarming amounts of lead found in the water of yet another American city, this time, Syracuse. Why other communities also have cause for concern?\par \par \uc2 Parents are going to want to watch this in particular.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When you hear the name Flint, Michigan, chances are good, you think of the city`s problem with lead in the drinking water, and now testing has discovered a big problem in yet another American city, Syracuse, New York. Advocacy groups say it has some of the highest levels of lead found in drinking water in decades.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano went to Syracuse, and she joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Look, city officials are pushing back on claims that this is a crisis, saying that the latest water testing this month showed that the levels had improved, but some advocates are questioning those results and methodologies, and residents we spoke with say the city needs to be doing a lot more to protect their families.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you. How was school today?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: When five-year-old Ella showed traces of lead in a blood test, her grandmother, Oceanna Fair was terrified, knowing the effects of lead poisoning, starting with her brother more than 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR (Families for Lead Freedom Now): And is essentially an eight- year-old that me and my siblings care for.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In 2020, Fair fought for federal funding to fully remove lead paint from her home. She was confident her home was lead-free, but as recently as 2022, tests showed her granddaughter was still exposed.\par \par \uc2 How did you feel?\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: Helpless. Helpless because we couldn`t put our finger on it. We didn`t know where she was coming into contact with it.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She says the culprit had been hiding in the basement.\par \par \uc2 How did you find out? Do you have to hire somebody?\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: It`s pretty simple. You can scratch your line and that silver that you see underneath.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: That means it`s --\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: That`s lead.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She only checked this August, after a letter from the city warned of high lead levels in nearly 30 percent of water samples taken from homes with lead service lines. More confirmation --\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: We eventually brought a new fridge that had a filter, her lead levels went down, and we assumed it was from the diet we were feeding her.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: This is a community plagued by lead issues, where nine percent of children have suffered from lead poisoning. In some Black and Brown neighborhoods, it`s one in five.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BEN WALSH (Syracuse, New York): You have the public side of the service.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Syracuse Mayor Ben Walsh has called the results from the spring outliers, stressing the latest testing showed far lower lead levels.\par \par \uc2 But he says the city is working to replace all lead service lines faster than the EPA`s 10-year deadline, and providing filters to families with small children.\par \par \uc2 We`ve heard from people in this community who are extremely alarmed, whose children have tested positive. This is being compared to Flint. What`s your response to people who are -- who are that worried?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BEN WALSH: Well, I don`t think the facts support those comparisons. The specific challenges that we`re dealing with, again, are limited to the properties that had have lead service lines. So if you don`t have any lead service lines, there`s no reason to be concerned.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But he acknowledges, even the city does not have a full inventory of all pipes made of lead.\par \par \uc2 This is a community that is 70 percent renters, meaning people don`t know whether those pipes are made out of lead, bringing water into their homes.\par \par \uc2 Darlene Medley (sp?) says the city is failing her two twin boys who suffered from developmental and behavioral issues from lead poisoning.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE MEDLEY (Syracuse, New York resident): When you tell people, when their kids are poisoned by lead that they will never make it to what they were fully supposed to be, you don`t think that deserves a damn apology? That deserves an apology and a whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The twins had been poisoned because of lead paint, and then to find out that this is in the water, and there are millions of buildings across the country that have these lead lines.\par \par \uc2 This year, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  mandated that states and municipalities must change every lead pipe that delivers drinking water within the next 10 years. The Biden administration, it`s a part of the bipartisan infrastructure law provides $15 billion in funding to replace these let lines.\par \par \uc2 In Syracuse, it is going to take about five years, because there are at least 14,000 of these lines to change. Of course, the community says it needs to happen a lot faster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You feel her passion and her anger about what is happening.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems like this could be avoided.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It can be avoided.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does not have to be.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And there are chemicals that are used to control the corrosion, but clearly in those samples, they didn`t seem to have worked. And the fixtures within the house in some homes are also an issue. It`s not just the lead pipe, so even if you change them, it`s important to get them tested.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel that parent`s pain there at the end of your story though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It was so painful to hear.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Lilia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a stowaway scares dozens of airline passengers. We will tell you what happened in just a bit. But right now, it is 7:43 -- forty-four, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some great things ahead for you at eight o`clock, including an inside look at the iconic Broadway publication, "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ." You`ve seen one of those from our very own Anthony, as in Mason.\par \par \uc2 It`s existed for 140 years. It still looks good. Big things are planned to mark the occasion.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to be joined by the one and only John Legend for special \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals today, we always feel good when he visits the studio, because we`re just ordinary people.\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour, but right now, Scott and I are heading over the table because Vlad has arrived in the building.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Groovin` to a little George Benson there on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Such a good song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Such a good song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a very good song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that how you begin with "What to Watch"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, it has, to do with Anthony Mason and "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, it is Broadway.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get into it because we`ve got a couple of really, really good stories that we want to share with you this morning.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with Olivia Munn, who is bravely showing a new side of herself after undergoing a double mastectomy. The 44-year-old reveals her surgical scars in a new campaign for SKIMS. That`s the shapewear brand.\par \par \uc2 Munn revealed her breast cancer diagnosis back in March, and she says it feels liberating to show the scars. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN (Actor): When I look at the scars, or how my breasts look different, or how clothes fit on me differently, I just feel a lot of gratitude. The way my body looks is just a representation of how hard I thought.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Munn says she hopes embracing her scars will help others with breast cancer and breast cancer survivors feel beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I so admire that`s she is doing this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very brave.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And she looks good in the ad, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she great. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ten percent of US bra sales from SKIMS going to breast cancer, Susan G. Komen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Susan G. Komen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a big number, actually.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a big number.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Congratulations to her. They just had a new baby, her and John Mulaney.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about Ted Danson, right?\par \par \uc2 You remember "Cheers." We all love "Cheers."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Danson is burying the hatchet with his co-star from "Cheers," Kelsey Grammer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Cheers"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know they needed a hatchet to bury.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me neither. But you know, Diane, she got in the way of everything. The beloved sitcom ran for 11 seasons and led to Grammer`s "Frasier" spin off, which has been rebooted on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Behind the scenes, the two apparently didn`t get along. Who knew?\par \par \uc2 During a podcast yesterday, Danson apologized to Grammer for their strained relationship. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TED DANSON (Actor): I have a memory of getting angry at you once --\par \par \uc2 KELSEY GRAMMER (Actor): Yes, you came and told me that one day, and that was good.\par \par \uc2 TED DANSON: And it`s stuck in both of our memories.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY GRAMMER: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 TED DANSON: I missed out on the last 30 years of Kelsey Grammer and I feel like I want -- it is my bad, my doing, and I almost feel like apologizing to you.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY GRAMMER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TED DANSON: I know I don`t feel like -- I apologize.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY GRAMMER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TED DANSON: For you and me --\par \par \uc2 KELSEY GRAMMER: I wish we`d spent some more time.\par \par \uc2 TED DANSON: -- that I sat back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So yes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, it is really cool that they are sort of burying the hatchet here when we didn`t really know that there was one to be buried.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And in such a public way, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was the hatchet about?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For Danson to do that, though, that says a lot about where he is in life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But it also speaks to the fact that they were able to do this incredible show for 11 seasons that are loved by everyone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And we didn`t know they were --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We didn`t know that they weren`t even like pals in real life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No idea. No idea. I`m preoccupied with facial hair. That is a great all white --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really want one of those.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You want one of those?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You are a very hirsute man. You could probably do it very easily.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope so. Not on this show. They won`t let -- they won`t even let me grow a mustache here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This dude has got a beard.\par \par \uc2 All right, I`ve got to get to this story.\par \par \uc2 So we`re talking about a recent \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  flight, left some passengers saying, "I`m tired of these bleeping rats on this bleeping plane."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Samuel L. Jackson, I see what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, yes. All right, all right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Snakes on a Plane," 2006.\par \par \uc2 Okay, this video shows what appears to be a big one above the overhead bin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s not a rat. Come on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The person who posted this video online said the plane was headed from Dallas and Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No way.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Passengers called it a super rat. \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  told TMZ it`s aware of the video and is taking steps to address the matter. Someone said this rat got on at New York -- at the layover in New York.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a slanderer and a liar.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, we have actor Brett Goldstein talking about his new show, "Shrinking."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: If you`ve ever been to Broadway, you`ve probably taken home one of these. This is where they print "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ," the magazine that is marking its 140th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 We will show you how they are celebrating, coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, if you`ve been to a Broadway show -- yes, you have -- you`ll definitely know what a "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " is.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason will tell us how that iconic chronicle of American theater -- I like that -- is celebrating 140 years in print.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 "Ted Lasso" actor, Brett Goldstein is taking on a very different role. He`ll talk about stepping in front of the camera on "Shrinking." A show he also helped create.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 The much loved John Legend, needs no introduction, he is here for a very special edition of \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is love.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  strike continues after workers vote to reject the latest contract offer from the company.\par \par \uc2 JON HOLDEN: We will push for the members` demands as quickly as we can, and we remain on strike.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Who has already voted?\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Former President Trump fires up his base while Vice President Harris reaches out to undecided voters ahead of Election Day.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The question in 13 days will be, what do the American people want?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The US confirms North Korean troops are in Russia as the war in Ukraine grinds on.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): If Russia is indeed forced to turn to North Korea for manpower, this would be a sign of weakness, not strength on the part of the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): I notice my palms are sweating, knees weak, arms are heavy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  channels his inner Eminem in front of the rapper during a campaign event.\par \par \uc2 How`d that go?\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA: I`m a nervous, but on the surface, I look calm and ready to drops bombs, but I keep on forgetting --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA ("The Daily Show"): Did he just come up with that on the spot? I mean, that was so good, it should be a song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, let`s just say he just came up with that on the spot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the fly, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, I do think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- he wasn`t sure how deep he was going to go, and then he realized, I actually know this song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every bar of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He does know it. And he did it very well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the words of Eminem, will the real US president, please stand up. Please stand up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please stand up. Please stand up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Please stand up.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are going to begin this hour --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- with the -- with the campaign for being the next president. Two different strategies out there 12 days before the election.\par \par \uc2 We`ll start with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , two rallies in Georgia yesterday, and they seem designed to appeal to his base. He doubled down on insults. He called Democrats stupid at least six different times and promised his supporters they`d be part of something great if they voted.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  spoke to undecided voters at a CNN town hall near Philadelphia, taking questions about the economy and then responding to reports that Trump had praised Hitler while in office, calling her opponent, "unfit and dangerous."\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House for us to help break all of it down for us.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got these two different events. What do they tell you about the state of the campaigns?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I think what it tells you, Tony, good to be with you, is that this has become a race in these closing weeks that in some ways resembles a normal election, and in some ways borders on the surreal.\par \par \uc2 On one hand, we watched Vice President Harris last night get grilled about her policy plans on taxes, on the Middle East, on immigration, and she`ll be judged based on how specific, how workable her proposals are on those issues as any candidate for president should be.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, you have this larger conversation going on now about just how wedded \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is to the democratic process in the first place. He suggested just this week in an interview that all the battleground states may have cheated in elections. You`ve got former top aides coming forward to warn that he`s drawn to fascism. He`s musing on the campaign trail about using the military to go after the enemy within, and so all of that is kind of drowning out the policy conversation right now -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Nancy, you know, we`re getting down to the nitty and the gritty here, and both campaigns are talking about making their closing arguments.\par \par \uc2 But here`s a question, do you think this has become more about voting against the other person rather than voting for the candidate you want?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Absolutely, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 I mean, let`s just take the Harris campaign, which I cover day-to-day. Not only is Harris spending a great deal of time warning people about Trump in these closing weeks, but so are her top surrogates on the campaign trail, like former President Obama, who has been dedicating most of the time in his speeches to mocking and criticizing Trump, and yes, Trump is giving them a lot of new material to work with.\par \par \uc2 But the challenge for the Harris campaign is that candidates tend to generate excitement and win when voters view them as the candidate of change. That was certainly the case for Obama back in `08, and if you`re really focused on defining your opponent, that leaves you with less time to define yourself, and so that`s the balancing act, and the Harris campaign is acutely aware of that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy, you have been traveling all over this country covering the campaign, what are you watching for in these final days?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Nate, I`m really curious about that closing argument. We learned yesterday that Harris is going to be giving a speech on the National Mall next Tuesday that her campaign is billing as the big final argument. She`s going to be speaking from The Ellipse, which will inevitably lead people to draw parallels to the speech that Trump gave to supporters on January 6, 2021 at The Ellipse, right before they stormed the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 And that is not an accident. The campaign planned it that way. They want that contrast, and so I`ll be watching to see how she makes the case against Trump, while ensuring that her case for her own candidacy doesn`t get overshadowed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, the final countdown. We`ll check in with you later.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, John Legend will join us for a special \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. He`s going to share some products from his skin care brand.\par \par \uc2 But first it is, 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Tony Awards Presents Broadway`s Back/\uc2 CBS\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can, can, can. That`s Danny Burstein, who won his first Tony for "Moulin Rouge." Ahead, Burstein looks back at his Broadway career with Anthony Mason through his "Playbills." Do you save yours, too? As is the iconic magazine celebrates its 140th anniversary -- they`ve been around a long time.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We always thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s no business like show business, and today we`re looking at a business that`s the backbone of Broadway.\par \par \uc2 No matter what show that you see, you`re guaranteed at least one souvenir, a "Playbill." The iconic magazine turns 140 this month, and Anthony Mason shows us how "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " is celebrating this special anniversary.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is delivered almost every day on Broadway, the magazine that`s the guide to the Great White Way.\par \par \uc2 As any theatergoer knows, it wouldn`t be Broadway without a "Playbill."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So I can look back and remember.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You provide a magazine to every Broadway theater?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH (\uc2 Playbill, Inc.\uc2  vice president and chief operating officer): Every Broadway theater, most off Broadway theaters and the biggest classic arts venues in the city.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex Birsh, whose family has published "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " since 1973 supervises the printing plant in Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 How old is this machine?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: This machine here is actually, I believe it has its 40th birthday.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s a beast.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: It is, and it`s our beast, and we love her.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When did "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " actually start?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: 1884.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Which makes it the oldest surviving theater program company in the world.\par \par \uc2 When I think about "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ," I mean, it`s really the history of American theater on its covers.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: I like to think that, too.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: From "Death of a Salesman" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Death of a Salesman")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To "Damn Yankees."\par \par \uc2 "West Side Story" to "Wicked."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wicked")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: For over a century, the cast bios and production details have been featured in "Playbill."\par \par \uc2 So this is how you`re celebrating your anniversary.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This month, every Broadway show has four different "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " covers.\par \par \uc2 And these are meant to mark different eras.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: That`s right. We have the Golden Age, 30s, 40s.\par \par \uc2 So this is the 50s design, and then we have the 60s, with the first showing of the yellow banner. And then we have the groovy 70s.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love the groovy 70s.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: Which was a -- it`s actually kind of a rare design.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s when Alex`s grandfather, Arthur Birsh bought "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ." A printer by trade, he later brought in his son, Philip.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BIRSH (\uc2 Playbill, Inc.\uc2  CEO): We had a basic debate. I thought "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " was a brand, and he thought it was magazine.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Philip pushed for "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " to go online.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BIRSH: And in 1995-`96, we actually were the first company on the internet for the theater industry.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What is "Playbill`s" place on Broadway?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BIRSH\uc2 : "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " is really a treasure box. It could be a souvenir. It could be a guide. The actors, it means a lot, too, because that`s their goal, is to be in a "Playbill."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The 66th Tony Awards/\uc2 CBS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN (Actor): The first time was very, very special.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Danny Burstein made his Broadway and "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " debuts in 1992.\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: I do remember writing my first bio and thinking, oh, my God, holy cow, it`s going to be in a "Playbill." See those two pictures. Holy cow. That is a young man. That is an old man.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How many Broadway shows have you been in?\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: This is -- "Gypsy" is my 20th.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Burstein stars with Audra McDonald in the revival that opens in December.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Lincoln Center Theater)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He has also starred in revivals of "South Pacific," "Cabaret."\par \par \uc2 You were Tevye.\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: I was Tevye in "Fiddler on the Roof." And that was the hardest role I`ve ever done, and the most fulfilling.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fiddler on the Roof.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And this is the one you won the Tony for.\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Tony Awards Presents: Broadway`s Back/CBS)\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: After seven times being nominated.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But who`s counting?\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: But who`s -- well, my mother. She counted.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Your whole history is just right in this pile of "Playbills."\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: I know, this is your life, Danny Burstein.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex Birsh can also track his life through "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Two things you notice right away when you come in here, the sound and the smell.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: Yes. That`s right,\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The smell of ink.\par \par \uc2 You grew up with both.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 Each time I walk in here, that smell makes me feel like I`m, you know, eight years old.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You actually painted these walls as a kid.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: I did, when I was 16. Sadly, the last time they were also painted and I feel embarrassed by that, but it was -- it was a fun summer, and it really taught me the value of an N-95.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex would need an N-95 mask again during COVID, when "Playbill`s" presses shut down.\par \par \uc2 That had to be really painful.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: It was extremely painful. It was the only time in our a hundred and at that point, a hundred and thirty-five plus year history, where we did not know when we would print again.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But the beast came roaring back. "Playbill`s" bright banner has become a symbol of Broadway. And if you were wondering --\par \par \uc2 Why did you choose yellow?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: Well, it`s kind of embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex says they were experimenting with different looks when his grandfather asked the ink salesman --\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: What`s the cheapest color? Yellow. Great. And there we go.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Broadway shows may open and close, but "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " has become a tradition.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The sometimes it`s so simple, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What`s the cheapest color? But it`s the color everybody knows.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is so distinct and it really stands out.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I love the back story of "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, it`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The way you told it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All the 140 years, who can believe it that it`s been publishing continuously since then.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I find I save mine, and then after about five or 10 years, I go, "why am I saving it?" You take yours?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I think that`s exactly -- that`s what most people do. Dave, our audio guy, on the way in, he says, I have a whole drawer full of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know? But it`s the one souvenir you always walk away with.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can think --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It could be temporary or forever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I can think of five I`m definitely going to save on our table right here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at this. Look at this.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " folks were kind enough to put the show on the cover. So there you all are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am definitely keeping this.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s the contemporary version, and then you have each of the four period versions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Isn`t that cool?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Honestly, this is fantastic, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much. I`m definitely saving those.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was very nice you guys to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Anthony, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, an important conversation about your health. Dr. Celine Gounder has got some tips on how you can prevent cardiovascular disease. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In today`s "Health Watch," we`re talking about cardiovascular disease. It is the leading cause of death in this country. Did you know that? Nearly 120 million adults in the US live with the disease. That`s nearly half of adults, and it is expected to get worse.\par \par \uc2 A recent \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  report projects heart disease and stroke will affect more than 60 percent of Americans by 2050, that`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 We are joined by CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder. She is editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 I`m so glad you`re here. I mean, heart disease is one thing I always worry about. Both my parents died of heart disease. I`ve outlived both of them. As I sit here today, I`ve outlived both my parents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is something I think about constantly. I`ve got high blood pressure, high cholesterol, they say it`s all part of your DNA, and even on medication, the numbers are still going up.\par \par \uc2 Why is that? Even when you`re doing the right things.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is a very concerning trend, that by 2050 it`s estimated that six in 10 adults will be living or dying from cardiovascular disease. And so why is that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is really a factor of we tend to emphasize prioritize sick care in this country, not preventive care. If you look at how we spend relative to other high income countries, we spend far more and get far worse health outcomes, and we`re not prioritizing primary care, public health, which are the things that would prevent cardiovascular disease.\par \par \uc2 And so why is that? Well, we don`t have enough primary care providers, and some of this is driven by how we compensate different kinds of doctors.\par \par \uc2 So specialists are paid about 40 percent more than primary care providers. Of course, if you`re in school trying to decide, what am I going to go into, you know that is going to influence your decision making. And then on the public health side, of the $4.5 trillion we spend on health in this country, only four percent of that is for public health.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing. I mean, you make such a good point. We call it health care, but really it`s sick care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It`s not even care.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s not even care. It`s not even care.\par \par \uc2 Could weight loss drugs like Wegovy help turn around these numbers?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So that`s an interesting question. For people who can afford them, access them, and tolerate the side effects, they have been very useful. But not everybody can, and these drugs remain in relatively short supply, and they`re quite expensive.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, I was at the annual meeting of the National Academy of Medicine, and there was a session all about GLP-1 drugs with the top researchers in the space, and there was a lot of enthusiasm, but also a lot of skepticism and caution, and really the acknowledgement that we are not going to be able to treat ourselves with these drugs out of the obesity epidemic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what are the -- what health factors are there that will drive up this increase in cardiovascular disease?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, so we`ve made progress on a couple. So smoking and high cholesterol, we have made progress on smoking. It was really the result of public health educational campaigns, taxes, regulations on how they`re sold. So we`ve made progress there.\par \par \uc2 But the things that we are seeing go up are obesity, diabetes, and high blood pressure. And that is being driven by high calorie, high salt, high fat, high sugar foods that are ultra-processed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, it is good to be preventative.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the good stuff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Dr. Gounder, thank you. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back, 8:30 CBS MORNINGS and it is time to bring you some of the stories that are talk of the table this morning. Was that "Candy Girl"?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I used that as my walk on song for a short -- for a short period. Didn`t get a lot of hits. I don`t know why I picked it, but yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you were hitting on the girls though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that was a big year in a variety of ways.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Candy Girl." Okay, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great song. Okay, Tony.