Studies show, but you did take the role.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You played the role so powerfully.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a very, very difficult role to play. We just saw that. It`s very intense. What kind of preparation research went into it?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Well, if we`re being serious, you know, it`s a playing a drunk driver who`s been responsible for this thing, and I did loads and loads of research as writers in the writers` room, we read lots of accounts of people on both sides of this thing happening.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And listened to lots of sort of people`s stories, so you`re taking it very, very seriously. And then -- and then, kind of, in terms of playing it, we`d already done all the research writing it, you feel like you`ve acted it when you write it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple TV+)\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: You know what I mean? Because it`s the same process of empathizing with the character. And then when I was playing it, I just thought about PTSD. I know what it`s like when you`re in a car accident. It sort of loops in your head.\par \par \uc2 So I would just kind of loop it before a scene, and it would make it very sad -- you know, it`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tough.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, there are some strong themes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- in this -- forgiveness, confronting demons.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s these patterns and triggers. Do you have personal history with therapy?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes. I have a poor therapist who has been stuck with me for some years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m an advocate for therapy.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Did any of your experiences through therapy help you with this role?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: With this particular role? That`s a good question. I don`t know. I think -- I think so much of the show, you know, we have consultants who are therapists, and we -- you know, as much as the show kind of plays with the boundaries of therapy, we also take it seriously, believe in therapy, think it`s a very important thing, so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Having that vulnerability, I`m sure helped.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, I think -- yes, you have to be very -- for this part, you had to be very vulnerable. And having Lukita Maxwell --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who plays your daughter, yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: In that scene, I have lots of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plays the daughter.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: She is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so good just to see you back on TV after "Ted Lasso." You know, now there`s all the rumors about Season Four.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please share with the class what you feel like, because, you know we all wanted to come back and there are rumors, rumors, Brett, you`ve heard them, too.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes. I`ve heard these rumors.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING:, Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: You got any info?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s here, he`s there. He is there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do we dare to dream, or should we just let it go and be gone?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said you`d play Roy Kent forever.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, well, dare to dream. Always dare to dream. I would never stop anyone from dreaming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, the bearded -- the very bearded Brett Goldstein.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going to take that as it is coming back. Thank you, Brett.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Shrinking" is streaming now on \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV+. A new episode is on Wednesday. We`ll see you --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241024ekao00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for October 18, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Meg Oliver, Ramy Inocencio, Scott MacFarlane,  Nancy Cordes, Steve Hartman, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Jo Ling Kent \par \uc2 3,915 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D-Vice President of the United States and U.S. Presidential Candidate): If you are exhausted on the campaign trail, it raises real questions about whether you are fit for the toughest job in the world.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: the battle for Michigan. With 18 days to go, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  crisscross the Great Lakes State.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I`m not even tired. I`m really exhilarated.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The drugstore in your neighborhood may be closing. Why?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAN IVES (Managing Director, \uc2 Wedbush Securities\uc2 ): Prices are going up. Consumers are fed up. That`s bad news for traditional pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, the major shakeup at \uc2 CVS Health\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: You have your name on a building, that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: It has to be someone that you just know would be right.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And it`s Friday, so we go On the Road with a school custodian who teaches us the power of kindness.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BOREN (Swedeborg School Board President): These kids get sick, you think they go to the nurse to start with?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BOREN: They go to her. And she`s there for each and every one of them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the focus is on the critical battleground state of Michigan. Both candidates know turnout there will be key. So Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are flooding the zone. In fact, tonight, they`re holding campaign events in the same county separated by just a few hours and a few miles.\par \par \uc2 Trump will round out his night in Detroit, a city he`s repeatedly insulted and where early voting starts in the morning. Remember, this is a state that Trump won in 2016, and it went to Biden in 2020. And, right now, polls show the Wolverine State is a toss-up.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes is going to lead off our coverage tonight from a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  rally outside Detroit.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): This woman has no idea what she`s doing, and we can`t let her be president. I don`t want to be rude about it. We can`t let her be president.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): Both candidates rolled into Michigan today, one day before early voting gets under way in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D-Vice President of the United States and U.S. Presidential Candidate): So now is the time to make your plan to vote. Make a plan.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Stumping in Grand Rapids, Harris touted her support for unions.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is no friend of labor.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump has taken an unusual tack in Michigan lately, dissing the state`s largest city twice in the past 10 days.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, we`re a developing nation too. Just take a look at Detroit. Our whole country will end up being like Detroit if she`s your president.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Today, Harris called that unhinged.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The former president is insulting the people of the state.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And she brought up a new report claiming Trump aides cited exhaustion as the reason Trump passed on a recent media interview.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: If he can`t handle the rigors of the campaign trail, is he fit to do the job?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump quickly fired back.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m not even tired. I`m really exhilarated. You know why? We`re killing her in the polls.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A Harris campaign memo, first obtained by CBS News, argues her path to win Michigan capitalizes on Trump`s unprecedented weakness in the suburbs, which he narrowly won in 2020. But then came the rollback of Roe v. Wade, which even he seemed to acknowledge today has hurt him with suburban women.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You have one issue. You have the issue of abortion. Without abortion, the women love me.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: With the race essentially tied in Michigan, the ground game will be pivotal. And these rallygoers in Waterford know it.\par \par \uc2 How many of you are planning to volunteer or are volunteering already?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We`re already...\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We are already volunteering.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Are volunteering.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Wow. What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We were doing -- I have done canvassing. I have a phone bank or a group of people that I have to call.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Phone calls, brochures in my neighborhood, 350 this past two weeks -- this past week.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: All right.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #5: All right.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Those women are busy.\par \par \uc2 Michigan is such a prize that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has visited 13 times this year alone, while Harris is making her sixth trip here just since becoming the presidential candidate this summer. And she`s sticking around for a second day tomorrow, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes on the trail tonight.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This news now. Today, the judge overseeing former President Trump`s federal election interference case released nearly 1,900 pages of evidence gathered by special counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 , evidence Trump didn`t want out there until after the election.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane has been poring over all these documents.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Scott. What`s in there?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: There`s a lot there.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is calling the federal judge in this case, \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2 , an evil person for releasing these nearly 2,000 pages of evidence submitted by special counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In one of the depositions released today, a White House aide told investigators, when Trump was informed there was rioting at the Capitol January 6, he responded by saying: "Oh, really? Let`s go see."\par \par \uc2 Then the aide handed him the TV remote control. The aide said he then went to grab Trump a Diet Coke. \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  has argued Trump scrolled his phone and watched the attack on TV that day without immediately calling off the rioters.\par \par \uc2 Trump tried to delay the release of these passages until after the election, but, Norah, Judge Chutkan ruled, holding back the information itself from the public would be election interference.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Scott MacFarlane, thank you for looking through those documents.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the Middle East and the fallout over the killing of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader \uc2 Yahya Sinwar\uc2 . There is renewed hope about reaching a deal to free the remaining hostages in Gaza and end the war. But who that deal would be negotiated with is still a big question.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio reports tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voice-over): In Yahya Sinwar`s hometown of Khan Yunis, a tribute to the slain leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  before Friday prayers.\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOTS)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: For the past several weeks, Israel said its 828th Brigade had been sweeping Southern Gaza, including the Tal al-Sultan refugee camp in Rafah, where \uc2 IDF\uc2  soldiers on Wednesday spotted three masked militants and exchanged grenades and gunfire, killing two.\par \par \uc2 The third, a wounded Sinwar, ran into a heavily damaged building, where the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says a drone found him slumped over. From the street, a tank took aim and fired. Only the next day could Israel confirm this was Sinwar.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Joy was immediate for many in Israel, but not for all.\par \par \uc2 And what went through your mind?\par \par \uc2 MEIRAV LESHEM GONEN (Mother of Hostage): Glad is not the right word, because you never want somebody to be dead.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Meirav Gonen is the mother of Romi, now 24 years old and still a hostage in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MEIRAV LESHEM GONEN: In the tunnels, without daylight. Can you imagine not to see your daughter for a year? I don`t know how she looks like.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Israel says its highest priority is to bring home all hostages, but has resisted renewed calls for peace...\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: ... instead escalating its offensive in Northern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. DORON SPIELMAN (International Spokesman, \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 ): We have made phenomenal military progress, eliminated much of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but we are not at the day after.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: While \uc2 Hamas\uc2  officials in Gaza vowed to fight on.\par \par \uc2 (ISMAEL AL-SAWARTA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "I don`t think Sinwar`s death will impact the war," says Ismael al-Sawarta. "The Palestinian resistance is not led by a single individual."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: But who will now lead \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is still a big unknown. Yahya Sinwar`s brother Mohammed could assume the role. He`s believed to still be alive in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 There`s the group`s top cease-fire negotiator. That`s Khalil al-Hayya. And, also, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` leader before Sinwar, that`s \uc2 Khaled Mashal\uc2 . He`s living in Qatar -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ramy Inocencio, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we`re learning disturbing new details in the 2017 double murder mystery that gripped the nation. As the trial got under way today, prosecutors revealed just how two teen girls died.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver reports from the courthouse in \uc2 Delphi\uc2 , Indiana.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: If you have your electronics, make sure they`re off or in your car.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voice-over): Overnight, people wrapped around the Carroll County Courthouse, \uc2 Delphi\uc2 , Indiana, eager to secure a seat for the long- awaited trial; 52-year-old Richard Allen sat in the courtroom, often glancing back at his family members.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, 13-year-old Abby Williams and her best friend, 14-year-old Libby German, went for a hike along a bridge trail. Their bodies were found the next day less than half-a-mile away, both their throats slit.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutor Nicholas McLeland clean said the case is about three things, "Bridge Guy," a bullet, and the brutal murder of two girls. "Bridge Guy" refers to this image captured on Libby`s phone shortly before she was killed and this audio clip prosecutors say was a man forcing the girls off the bridge at gunpoint.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Down the hill.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The bullet refers to an unused round located near the bodies that prosecutors say matched a gun owned by Allen. Allen told police in 2017 he was on the trail the day of the murders, but he wasn`t considered a suspect.\par \par \uc2 During a review of the case in 2022, police connected the unused bullet to one of Allen`s guns.\par \par \uc2 John Tompkins is a prominent Indianapolis defense lawyer.\par \par \uc2 JOHN TOMPKINS (Defense Lawyer): He had lived in that community before, during, and right up until his arrest was -- was pretty shocking, and there was no clear connection between he and the victims.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Despite the lack of DNA or weapon, prosecutors said Allen confessed to the murders while in prison. The defense insisted he didn`t do so voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The judge has this trial locked down, banning cameras and devices inside court, issuing a gag order, and sequestering the jury for the duration of the trial with no cell phones, only supervised calls and visits allowed.\par \par \uc2 The judge also ruled that the jury will not hear a defense theory that a cult committed the murders -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The motive is still a mystery.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating a close call in the skies over Austin, Texas, two aircraft coming within 350 feet of each other that is shorter than a football field.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL: Did you ever see him the second time around?\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN AIRLINES: One-seven-five. We saw when he flashed us and flew right into us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That was air traffic control on Wednesday morning speaking with the crew of American Airlines Flight 2587. Officials say the pilots took evasive action to avoid hitting a small Cessna plane, which left the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  jet too close to a third plane. The pilots of that aircraft quickly changed course. All three planes ultimately landed safely.\par \par \uc2 Paul Whelan, the former U.S. Marine who was wrongfully detained in Russia for nearly six years, is speaking out for the first time since he was freed in a prisoner swap in August.\par \par \uc2 On this Sunday`s Face the Nation, Whelan tells Margaret Brennan what it was like to learn that WNBA star Brittney Griner was coming home before him in exchange for a notorious Russian arms dealer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your family was really worried about your well-being.\par \par \uc2 PAUL WHELAN (Former Russian Prisoner): Well, I was too. I was too.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Was that your lowest point?\par \par \uc2 PAUL WHELAN: That was. I had been working in the factory. I was called to the warden`s office. The FSB is there, et cetera, et cetera. There`s a phone call for me. I pick up the phone. It`s the White House. It`s one of the officers with the DHS.\par \par \uc2 And he basically tells me that Brittney Griner was going home, that the trade was for Viktor Bout. And I asked him point blank, I said: "So what else do you have to trade?"\par \par \uc2 And he said: "Nothing."\par \par \uc2 I said: "You gave up your negotiation platform. There`s nothing else. How do you now get me back?"\par \par \uc2 And he said: "Well, we`re going to reconvene tomorrow to discuss that."\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Oof.\par \par \uc2 PAUL WHELAN: And I said -- I said point blank to him: "You realize what you have done, that you have abandoned me here."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You can see more of Margaret Brennan`s interview Sunday morning on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Major pharmacy chains are closing stores across the country. Why? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Big news about your drugstore. CVS just announced it`s ousting its CEO and it`s in the middle of closing 900 stores. \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  is closing more than 1,000. And they`re not alone.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent digs into what`s going on with these pharmacies and what it means for you.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voice-over): Today, a major shakeup at \uc2 CVS Health\uc2 , CEO \uc2 Karen Lynch\uc2  suddenly replaced at the nation`s biggest pharmacy chain.\par \par \uc2 KAREN LYNCH\uc2  (Former CEO, \uc2 CVS Health\uc2 ): Health care is so personal to you and me that you can`t lose sight of that human connection.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Facing financial struggles under her leadership, CVS is on track to close 900 of its nearly 10,000 locations in its three-year plan and cut about 3,000 corporate jobs. Industry-wide, an estimated 7,000 pharmacies have already shuttered in the last five years.\par \par \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  just this week announced 1,200 of its remaining stores could soon close, and \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  filed for bankruptcy last year.\par \par \uc2 DAN IVES (Managing Director, \uc2 Wedbush Securities\uc2 ): Older demographics, they want to sit down with the pharmacist, understand the medicine. Younger demographics, they don`t want to interact with pharmacists. They want their medicine, and they want it cheap, and they want it at their doorstep.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: What does a pharmacy look like when it comes to getting your medication in five years?\par \par \uc2 DAN IVES: You could have potentially 25, 30 percent less pharmacies in the next two, three years than there are today.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The retail closures are creating even more drugstore deserts. That has Amazon Pharmacy taking flight, testing prescription drug delivery in Texas, with plans to open pharmacies in 20 more cities next year.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Have you taken this medication?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Changes in consumer habits prescribing a new dose of reality.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Reproductive rights are a key issue for many voters. We will see how it`s playing out in one of the key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: President Donald Trump`s comment at a town hall this week claiming to be the father of IVF sparked a national discussion on the issue of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns hears from voters in the battleground state of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hey. Dr. Bowling (sp?). Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DENNI CLAYCOMB-GARDNER (Mother): Hey. You too.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voice-over): Denni Claycomb-Gardner (sp?) and her husband, Edward, conceived their son Dallas through in vitro fertilization, or IVF, and are now going through the process again in the hopes of growing their family.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everything looked great today.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The Gardners (sp?) had wanted to wait until Dallas, now 18 months old, was in preschool, when they could afford the expensive procedure. But when access to fertility treatments came to the political forefront earlier this year...\par \par \uc2 DENNI CLAYCOMB-GARDNER: You sweet baby.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: ... the Gardners decided they couldn`t afford to wait.\par \par \uc2 DENNI CLAYCOMB-GARDNER: With how big the election is, that that could possibly change, it almost feels like we`re kind of backed into a corner at this point.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD GARDNER (Father): It is something that is very alarming to me that it can -- it`s that her rights to have a kid being taken away.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Is that something that you would have ever kind of thought about?\par \par \uc2 EDWARD GARDNER: I`d never -- until Alabama, I never really connected abortion with IVF.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The Gardners live in North Carolina, but, in February, the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  ruled that frozen embryos were considered children, IVF treatments were paused, and patients scrambled.\par \par \uc2 The state legislature then passed a law protecting IVF, but it thrust the issue into the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D-Vice President of the United States and U.S. Presidential Candidate): Fight for the future.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  blamed former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who has taken credit for overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Couples just trying to grow their family are being cut off in the middle of IVF treatments. They did not want this.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In August, Trump said he supported IVF and that the government would pay for it, though he didn`t say how. This week, he claimed:\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I`m the father of IVF.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But seconds later admitted that the process needed to be explained to him.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And, within about two minutes, I understood it.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: For the Gardners, IVF is more than a political talking point.\par \par \uc2 DENNI CLAYCOMB-GARDNER: The first time you hold him, it`s just so special. And then I know the first night can be rough, but I know our first night, like, it still felt like we were on cloud nine.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: After everything you went through?\par \par \uc2 EDWARD GARDNER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 DENNI CLAYCOMB-GARDNER: Yes, totally worth it, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 EDWARD GARDNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A feeling they hope to have again.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, CBS News, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Steve Hartman`s On the Road is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the story of a school worker who`s being honored for doing so many little things that make a big difference.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman goes On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voice-over): When folks in Swedeborg, Missouri, decided to name their elementary school building, everyone knew it had to be named after someone truly special.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: You have your name on a building, that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: It has to be someone that you just know would be right.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #3: That makes you think of when you think of good.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voice-over): Throughout history, there have been many national figures who fit that bill, also lots of famous Missourians who would have been great choices to celebrate and inspire.\par \par \uc2 But, in the end, they went with Claudene Wilson. Who is that, you ask?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You wouldn`t believe. Meet school custodian Claudene Wilson.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: You can get her, like, a vacuum, and she will appreciate it, because she really liked cleaning.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: Yes, she`s like in the zone. She focuses.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #5: And she just really dedicated herself to this school.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So much so that, over the past 30 years, she has taken on a busload of additional responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDENE WILSON (School Custodian): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: From transportation coordination...\par \par \uc2 CLAUDENE WILSON: How are you guys today?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... to lunch duty, to landscaping, from facility operations to fill-in operator.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDENE WILSON: Swedeborg School, janitor speaking. Can I help you?\par \par \uc2 And the next thing I know, I was leaving at 6:00 in the morning and getting home about 7:30 at night.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And what`s your motivation for doing all that?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDENE WILSON: The kids. The kids, that`s your heart.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BOREN (Swedeborg School Board President): That`s what makes Claudene what she is.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: School board president Chuck Boren.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BOREN: These kids get sick, you think they go to the nurse to start with?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BOREN: They go to her. If they had a bad night, they go to her. I mean, and she`s there for each and every one of them.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And all that is why the school board voted unanimously for the new name and why today there`s really only one person in this whole community who remains unconvinced that Claudene deserves this honor, Claudene.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDENE WILSON: It touches your heart, but I don`t think my name needs to be up on a building somewhere.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So, we will add humility to one of the reasons why you should be up there.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDENE WILSON: I guess.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: In America, building names often inspire greatness.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDENE WILSON: You have done a good job this morning.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And Swedeborg, Missouri, is no exception. They just define greatness a little differently.\par \par \uc2 Would you want to be like her?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #6: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: That`s what everybody should want to be, you know? That`s what I would want to be.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman, On the Road, in Swedeborg, Missouri.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, Claudene just stole my heart too.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241019ekai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For October 13, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anthony Salvanto, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,303 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation, we will get a new poll on where the presidential race stands in the final weeks before the election, plus new concerns over disaster funding after two major hurricanes hit the Southeast in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  returned to North Carolina for a second time in two weeks, packing supplies for hurricane victims and campaigning in the all-important battleground state still cleaning up from Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 And on the heels of Hurricane Milton slicing through Florida last week, communities across six states now face a huge price tag to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 We will get the latest from Homeland Security chief Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, as former President Trump tries to make the storms a liability for his opponent...\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Biden and Harris have done a horrible job on North Carolina and some of the other ones.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... we will check in with House Speaker Mike Johnson on disaster relief, the campaign and more.\par \par \uc2 Florida Congressman Maxwell Frost will also join us.\par \par \uc2 And as the campaign enters the homestretch, we will take the temperature of voters in a new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll and get their thoughts on the future of the country in a focus group interview.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 With just 23 days until Election Day, large portions of the Southeast are in recovery mode. And presidential candidates are campaigning from coast to coast to get out the vote.\par \par \uc2 Our new CBS News polls shows the race for the White House remains a toss- up, with Vice President Harris up just three points over former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  nationwide 51 percent to 48 percent. That`s one point tighter from last month. Among voters in the seven battleground states that will decide this election, it`s just a one-point difference 50-49.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, repair and cleanup efforts are under way in both Florida and North Carolina, where more than 260 deaths have been blamed on two major hurricanes. President Biden is in Florida this morning surveying the damage. Vice President Harris will hold a rally later in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And we begin this morning with Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS (U.S. Secretary of Homeland Security): Good morning, Margaret. And thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Secretary, you oversee FEMA. There is still seven weeks left in hurricane season. FEMA`s director says they`ve already used nearly half of the $20 billion in disaster relief Congress allocated to them.\par \par \uc2 But the speaker of the House says FEMA has plenty of resources, they have been slow to respond and that FEMA hasn`t actually distributed the money. Can you clear that up?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Margaret, FEMA has the money to address the immediate needs of individuals impacted by Hurricanes Helene and Milton, but we need Congress to act swiftly to fund FEMA, and specifically its Disaster Relief Fund, because hurricane season is not over.\par \par \uc2 And, also, seasons are less and less important now, given the effects of climate change and the increasing gravity and frequency of extreme weather events. And I should say, FEMA has not been slow at all. It already has distributed more than $470 million in relief to individuals impacted by Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 I take great pride and this nation should take great pride in the extraordinary work of our FEMA personnel and all first responders.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, this is obligated versus distributed. That`s where you two are sort of disagreeing with each other, but -- it seems.\par \par \uc2 But, before Hurricane Milton hit, you said October 2: "We do not have the funds," FEMA doesn`t have the funds, "to make it through the season and what is imminent."\par \par \uc2 So, if another hurricane hits before the election, are there funds to deal with that kind of emergency situation?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: We will address the immediate impacts.\par \par \uc2 But, remember, immediate impacts speak of immediate needs, meals, potable water, and the like. But, also, we have to ensure that individuals can recover from these extreme disasters, such as rebuilding their homes, repairing damage and the like.\par \par \uc2 And so we need Congress to act swiftly to fund what should be a nonpartisan, apolitical phenomenon, and that is providing relief to all individuals with respect to the impacts of these extreme weather events.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, immediate versus rebuilding. So, you could wait until after the election? Do you know how much you`ll need?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: We don`t. We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: But we are still, for example, assessing the damage from Hurricane Milton, which is of historic strength.\par \par \uc2 And it also led to approximately 27 different tornadoes. So we don`t know the impacts. We don`t know what`s coming tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: ... whether it`s another hurricane, a tornado, a fire, an earthquake. We have to be ready.\par \par \uc2 And it is not good government to be dependent on a day-to-day existence, as opposed to appropriate planning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ve seen some dangerous misinformation being amplified online.\par \par \uc2 Florida`s Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  hit back hard at some of these claims that, if you got help from the federal government, somehow, they`d seize your assets in his state. FEMA has been trying to counteract some of these lies as well.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned when you see this and how widespread it is that it`s a preview of what`s to come with the upcoming U.S. election as well, attempts to manipulate people?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Incredibly concerned.\par \par \uc2 And the misinformation -- I should say it`s disinformation -- false information deliberately spread to impact people`s behavior and perceptions, it is extremely pernicious. We have individuals in need of assistance who are entitled to assistance who aren`t seeking it because of the false information.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Who is driving it?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Well, I will tell you what we need.\par \par \uc2 We need individuals, elected officials, people who have the platform to really debunk this false information. And we`re not seeing enough of that, and I find that to be incredibly irresponsible, and irresponsible to the people who are survivors of these extreme weather events.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have a big portfolio at Homeland Security. And, of course, the U.S. border is part of that. Border crossings are now at a low for the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 But our \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling shows 65 percent of Trump voters believe the Biden administration is trying to intentionally increase the number of migrants at the border. And among the people who do believe that, three-quarters of them say it`s because the administration wants them to vote.\par \par \uc2 And the Speaker of the House just told us that he thinks noncitizens are going to vote, even though this is already illegal under established law.\par \par \uc2 What mechanisms can you tell the public about that exist to prevent this from happening?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: So, what a -- what a powerful example of disinformation.\par \par \uc2 There is no facts underlying these assertions. They are extremely damaging. It causes people to lose confidence in the integrity of the election system. And we need people in positions of authority to actually communicate accurate information to the voting public. We -- the numbers that we are experiencing now at the border are actually lower than they were at this time of year in 2019.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: And so it`s not just over the last three years, but well before them in the pre-COVID times.\par \par \uc2 And the notion, the notion that we in law enforcement have sought the -- to intentionally allow individuals to cross the border illegally for the purpose of voting is preposterous, and everyone should condemn that rhetoric, everyone, regardless of party affiliation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, on the issue of the actions you`ve taken to bring these border crossings down, this was President Biden`s presidential power.\par \par \uc2 He`s essentially disqualifying most of those who are entering illegally from being able to get asylum. This was meant to be a temporary policy change. So you took this without having to go to Congress and change the law, in short.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: We took it after Congress failed to take the enduring step...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: ... of legislation, which was bipartisan in nature.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But -- but, just in doing that, by doing this through presidential power, aren`t you essentially doing what Republicans said was possible throughout, which was, with the stroke of a pen, President Biden could stop all of this?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: No.\par \par \uc2 And remember where we were. We were enforcing Title 42, the public health authority, until May of last year. We then sought from Congress additional funding, additional Border Patrol agents, additional Homeland Security Investigations personnel. They did not fund the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  to enhance our border security.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: We sought that funding again, again rejected.\par \par \uc2 A bipartisan group of senators actually presented a...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: ... the most dramatic change in legislation in over 20 years. Congress was poised to pass it, and then politics got in the way.\par \par \uc2 Some people would prefer the problem to persist...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: ... deliver a solution for the American people. The president acted.\par \par \uc2 And, remember, we had to build the capacity to implement so successfully the president`s proclamation, and that is indeed what we have been doing month after month.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So -- but on this issue of who is in the country I know, practically speaking, it is very difficult to deport, for example, to the country of Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , on the campaign trail, talks a lot about a prison gang from that country, and he argues there`s already laws on the books that would allow for the Biden administration to dismantle criminal networks. He says he`d do it.\par \par \uc2 Are there those laws? You know, are you doing everything you can to combat this transnational criminal organization?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Yes, we are. And I think you`re speaking of Tren de Aragua, TDA.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Margaret, I was a prosecutor for 12 years. We had criminal gangs to battle back then. We are indeed doing everything we can to dismantle criminal gangs and transnational criminal organizations.\par \par \uc2 And, quite frankly, we`ve devoted an unprecedented level of resources and personnel and focus to this effort. This is not a new phenomenon, but we are bringing new tools, new capabilities and new resources to the fight.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you quickly explain what the status is with this arrest this past week -- or this prosecution we learned of this past week of this Afghan national who was apparently planning an Election Day terrorist attack on behalf of \uc2 ISIS\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 He was in this country. He had immigration status. Was he radicalized before he came to the United States?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: So, Margaret, it is an ongoing prosecution, so I won`t speak of the facts.\par \par \uc2 But the viewing public is getting an idea of the breadth and diversity of the missions that we in the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  tackle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it gets to the issue of vetting versus whether he was radicalized here.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Well, remember, the individual came in through parole, an Afghan national. And when we vet, and we do so intensively, when we vet an individual, it`s a point-in-time screening and vetting process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: If we obtain information subsequently that suggests the individual could be of danger, we take appropriate law enforcement action. That is exactly what we did in this case,\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Mayorkas, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We spoke earlier with House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 And we began by asking him whether Congress will approve more disaster funding for hurricane victims.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have visited some of these disaster zones yourself.\par \par \uc2 And Punchbowl News quotes you as saying that Congress may eventually have to pass an aid package that could reach as high as $100 billion.\par \par \uc2 Last Sunday on this program, \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 , the senator from North Carolina, said to us he`d like for Congress to provide some certainty by voting now on aid and then come back after the election to do more.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers aren`t scheduled to come back for another month. Why do you think it can wait?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): Well, it can wait because remember, the day before Hurricane Helene hit -- made landfall in Florida, and then went up through the states and wound up in Senator Tillis` state of North Carolina, Congress appropriated $20 billion additional to FEMA so that they would have the necessary resources to address immediate needs.\par \par \uc2 And so we put that into the coffers. I just checked, Margaret. As of this morning, less than 2 percent of that funding has actually been distributed, right around 2 percent of it. So we need FEMA to do its job. That -- those funds, that money is provided so that storm victims can have the immediate necessities met.\par \par \uc2 And then what happens after every storm is that the states have to assess and calculate the actual needs, and then they submit to Congress that request. As soon as that is done, Congress will meet and, in bipartisan fashion, we will address those needs. We`ll provide the additional resources.\par \par \uc2 But it would be premature to call everyone back now, because this -- these storms are so large in their scope and magnitude, it`s going to take a little bit of time to make those calculations.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the FEMA director says there`s only $11 billion left from that $20 billion that was allocated. So, that`s a different accounting than this 2 percent you say was distributed.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So they`ve obligated some funds, but they`ve only distributed 2 percent. The rescue and recovery effort is still going on, and then we address the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 But FEMA was slow to respond. They did not do the job that we all expect and hope that they will do. And there`s going to be a lot of assessment about that as well in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But hurricane season, as you know, lasts through the end of November. You`re from Louisiana. You`ve dealt with this before.\par \par \uc2 NOAA\uc2 , the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2 , is forecasting this could be one of the busiest seasons on record. So -- so, why not get ahead of this? Isn`t it a bit of a gamble to wait?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: No, it`s not.\par \par \uc2 Margaret, as I just said, Congress can`t meet and just send money on a guess or an estimate of what the damages are. The way supplemental disaster funding is provided is that, you know, the state sends in actual needs. It`s assessed by Congress and then handed out that way.\par \par \uc2 But, again, remember, they have billions, tens of billions of dollars that were already sent to FEMA...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: ... one day before Helene made landfall. So they -- they have plenty of resources.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, the administration Secretary Mayorkas said himself, DHS -- FEMA is a division -- a division of Homeland Security -- he said just a few days ago on his telecast to the media that they have what they need right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that $100 billion is a correct assessment that you made?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Well, look, that`s my guesstimate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: But, you know, I -- I was on the ground in the worst-hit areas in Florida. I went where Helene made landfall. Whole communities are wiped out there. It`s devastating. It goes all the way inland. The agricultural areas are devastated by it as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: And then you go up to the Carolinas, Tennessee, Georgia, it is a broad scope.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are the speaker of the House, which means you are one of the officials with some say about the security situation leading up to January 6 and election certification.\par \par \uc2 Can you assure the country we will not see the violence that this country experienced in 2021? What are you doing to stop it?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Yes, I don`t think we`ll see anything like that.\par \par \uc2 I certainly pray and hope that`s true. There`s a lot of great work that`s been done at the federal, state and local level to prevent the chaos that ensued after 2020, the COVID election year, when all the states were changing their laws and regulations without the legislatures approving that.\par \par \uc2 That brought up constitutional questions. There were all sorts of concerns about fraud and irregularity and all those things. The -- the good thing is -- and I think everybody should be encouraged that, since that time, most of the state legislatures went to work to shore up their systems to ensure that those kinds of things didn`t happen in the future.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that`s going to give us a high degree of certainty, and certainly hope that this will be a free and fair and legal election. Congress will follow the Constitution. I can guarantee you that. I have made a career of that.\par \par \uc2 You know, I have demonstrated it over and over and over throughout my life and as speaker of the House. And we will take care of this. So, everybody can -- can have a sense of certainty about that.\par \par \uc2 Now look, there -- there are members on both sides of the aisle who may object to slates of electors. That is commonplace now. In fact, Democrats have objected to slates of electors after every single Republican presidential victory this century.\par \par \uc2 So there`s nothing really irregular about that. And when people say that they`ve got to watch it closely, they`re saying that they`ll do their job. So...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But back in...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: We`re going to have the peaceful transition of power. Yes, I believe President Trump`s going to win, and this will be taken care of.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, back in 2020, you supported a legal challenge to the outcome of the election.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Supreme Court rejected that attempt to challenge it. Since then, the Electoral Count Reform Act has been passed.\par \par \uc2 Are you certain that, at the nation`s Capitol, the lawmakers who you work with won`t be challenging the outcome?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Look, we`ll see what happens. I can just tell you that we`re going to follow the law, and we will ensure that our colleagues follow the law the Electoral Count Act and the Constitution itself requires.\par \par \uc2 The reason for the objection in, you know, the last election cycle, in 2020, was what I referenced earlier, is that we had all sorts of changes to election laws in the states, and choosing electors, the mechanism by doing that under Article 2, Section 1, Clause 2, says that only the state legislatures can choose those systems.\par \par \uc2 So, when elections officials and secretaries of state and some state court judges and whomever, the governors, when they changed their rules because of COVID, they did it in an unconstitutional manner. And that was an important legal question. The Supreme Court never addressed it, because, remember, they just rejected all that on standing.\par \par \uc2 But we`re not relitigating what happened in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and 62 legal -- no, I`m not. But 60...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: No, we`re talking about 2024 and...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly. And 62 legal challenges, as you know, failed with the Trump challenges to the outcome.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Yes, but not on that basis. Margaret, Margaret, wait a minute. Wait a minute. Not on that basis. That legal question is, I think, objectively proven to be true.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, everyone knows the legislatures weren`t meeting in 2020 because of COVID. So it`s Article 2, Section 1, Clause 2. Everybody, \uc2 Google\uc2  it and read. It says only the legislatures can make those rules.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I`m confident I can tell you, I think, this year...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But on the security picture...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: ... that`s not an issue, right?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On the security picture at the nation`s Capitol, what measures are you taking?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Look, we have an all-hands-on-deck approach on this. \uc2 The Capitol Police\uc2  and the architect of the Capitol and everyone have done extraordinary measures. They`ve hardened the facilities around the building to prevent anything in the future from foreign terrorists or anyone else who might want to, you know, try to invade the Capitol, OK?\par \par \uc2 That`s taken care of. But I think the greater issue and the bigger story is that you`ve had really great work done in most of the states to shore up their systems and to make sure that we have a free and fair election.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, as President Trump says all the time, and I agree, our side, what we say at the rallies, you`ve heard, is, we`ve got to make it too big to rig. I think that`s really important. I think there is going to be some cheating in this election. I think noncitizens are going to vote.\par \par \uc2 Look, case in point...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know that it is against the law...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: ... \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 , the governor of the Commonwealth of Virginia...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... for noncitizens to vote in federal elections. That`s established law.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Of course it is. Of course it is, but -- of course it is, but here`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 There`s a number of states that are not requiring proof of citizenship when illegals or noncitizens register to vote. We know that`s happening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, respectfully, Speaker, you...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: We want -- everybody should want the law to be followed\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Respectfully, Speaker, you both, in the course of this interview, said that you do believe that states have taken measures that will help the integrity of this election...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... and then you just also seemed to undermine confidence in the integrity of the state elections.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: No, no. Margaret, hold on. Wait a minute. Wait. Wait just a minute. It`s not me undermining it. It`s the actions of the Biden/Harris administration and some of these states.\par \par \uc2 Noncitizens are not allowed to vote under federal law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, if your issue is with certain governors, shouldn`t you be talking to them?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: We have been. We have been. And the Republican governors have done heroic work. They`ve done their own audits of the -- their voter rolls to try to ensure and do their best duty to make sure that this we -- this is a free, fair and legal election.\par \par \uc2 We`re calling on all the governors to do the same thing, Democrat governors as well. And Democrat-led states in the legislatures, they need to do the same thing. A lot of good work has been done since 2020, but there are still questions out there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Speaker of the House Mike Johnson, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Florida Democratic Congressman Maxwell Frost. He joins us from Orlando.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST (D-Florida): Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman, President Biden is in Florida today to assess damage from Hurricane Milton. At least 60 Democratic lawmakers, some from Florida, have called for Congress to immediately return to Washington to vote for aid.\par \par \uc2 You haven`t joined that call. Why?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: I believe I have joined that call. But -- but, if not, I do believe that Congress needs to return to make sure that we pass money for more aid.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But how immediately do you need that to happen? How in need is your community?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: Our community`s very much in need across Florida.\par \par \uc2 But the thing we have to understand is, yes, FEMA has the resources necessary to deal with the current situation, but like was mentioned in the previous segment, \uc2 NOAA\uc2  is predicting and we`ve seen that this is one of the worst hurricane seasons we`ve seen. And we`re not done with this hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t end really until the end of November. And so I think it is important. Why leave up to chance when we can ensure that FEMA has the resources it needs, and not just FEMA. SBA also has a program, and different agencies have programs that help us to respond to these hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 Why would we leave it up to chance and wait until something happens, when we can make sure that we do that work now, especially when a lot of my colleagues on the other side of the aisle are working at politicizing these storms, when we can actually do something about it?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you need this vote to happen before Congress is set to return November 12, or you can wait until November 12?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: I think we should do it as soon as possible. Why wait until November 12? We don`t know what`s going to be happening in terms of natural disasters or storms.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: I think it`s important that a agency like FEMA have even...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: ... not just what they need, but even more than what they need to ensure that they have...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: ... the resources necessary, of course, to help with the current operation...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have got to...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: ... which they have but also with future things.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... take a break here, Congressman, and continue our conversation on the other side of the commercial.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We return to our conversation with Florida Congressman Maxwell Frost.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, you are the youngest member of Congress, it looks like, and you`ve been on college campuses trying to shore up support for Vice President Harris. In our \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling from earlier this summer, before Harris entered the race, we did see, among 40 percent of young people who responded, they said politics over the last few years makes them feel like there`s nothing else they can do. Another 40 percent said they want to tune out and watch something else.\par \par \uc2 Young people may not be apathetic, but they certainly don`t seem to be excited about the political system as a way to effect change. How do you change that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: Well, I appreciate the question.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just up to me, right? It`s up to all of our leaders, all of our organizers, people on the ground to make sure that young people understand that, if they step away from their civic power that they have, there`s people who don`t have their best interests at heart that are more than happy to step into that power for them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you mentioned, you know, a number that comes before \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  being the top of our ticket. I have seen, as I have traveled this country - - did a back-to-school tour just a few weeks ago -- a lot of enthusiasm and excitement around this election, especially with young voters wanting to vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, you know, there`s this notion that young people are not being involved in the political system. And we can do better, but people should know that young people are voting at the highest numbers in our country`s history. 2020 was the highest youth voter turnout in the history of our country. 2018 and 2022 -- 2022 are the highest youth voter turnout in a midterm in the history of our country too.\par \par \uc2 So we`re really -- we`re trending in the right direction. We just need to continue to reach out to young people, not take those votes for granted.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. And there`s that question of enthusiasm actually translating to votes. You`ve said vibes don`t mean votes, I saw you say.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: Mm-hmm. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On another issue that we know is of concern to the Harris campaign, I want to ask you about remarks that Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  made earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 He was speaking to campaign volunteers in Pittsburgh, and he raised the concern about low enthusiasm and turnout specifically among black men. He said -- quote -- "Part of it makes me think that, well, you just aren`t feeling the idea of having a woman as president."\par \par \uc2 Do you share the former president`s concern that this is a dynamic impacting the race? How much of an issue is it for Vice President Harris?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: I think it is an issue with a lot of different voters across this country.\par \par \uc2 And it`s something that our country -- you know, we`ve come a long way, as -- in terms of women`s suffrage. We`ve come a long way in terms of making sure of equity in this country.\par \par \uc2 But there`s still a lot of this bigotry in this country in terms of sexism, in terms of racism. And we still have to work at getting over that. Those things are still here. They still exist in a lot of communities. And we still have a lot of work to do here.\par \par \uc2 And so I think President Obama was just having some very serious, tough talk, right, tough love, with a lot of -- especially -- he was speaking directly to young black voters, young black men specifically, and making sure that they understand, look, sometimes you have to take a step back and look at your own bias.\par \par \uc2 What`s the bias you have? What are the real reasons you`re voting against someone or for someone? Is it a legitimate policy thing, or does it have to do more with the vibe of something And then look more into that as well. I think it`s really important.\par \par \uc2 This, like, politics of vibes, we see it a lot here in Florida with someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who claims to be someone who`s for the people and for democracy, yet, every chance he gets, he acts like an authoritarian.\par \par \uc2 Then I go to voters who have family members who have escaped countries with authoritarian leaders, and I ask them, why are you voting for someone who`s emulating what your parents escaped, and they can`t quite explain it. It has to do with the vibes, with the atmosphere the politician has created.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXWELL FROST: And I think we still have to contend with that as a country. And that`s what the president was talking about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman, thank you for sharing your insights. We`re going to have to leave it there for today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For more on the race for the White House and our latest poll, we`re joined now by CBS News executive director of elections and surveys Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, the polling show`s an already close race has tightened further. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Let me start by reminding people just how tight this is.\par \par \uc2 When you look at our estimates across each and every one of the battleground states, one after another is effectively even. So it`s a race that either candidate can and may win.\par \par \uc2 But one reason I think this week was highlighted is that you see how differently these candidates and their supporters define what is, not just what ought to be going forward.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you a couple of different examples. Start with the debate over hurricane relief. Trump supporters say that they believe hurricane relief is not going to people impacted by the hurricane. In fact, they accept the claim that FEMA money was diverted to people in the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 And Harris voters say speed of FEMA was just about right and that aid is going to people impacted. But that`s just one example.\par \par \uc2 Take another, the economy. Harris voters say that the economy is largely good. They say or are more likely to say that the stock market is up this year, that the U.S. has been adding jobs, Trump voters much less likely to say those things.\par \par \uc2 And one more example, the border. Harris voters say that crossings of the border, the U.S. and Mexico border, are down. Trump voters say that crossings are up.\par \par \uc2 So what that does is, it all underpins this idea it`s not just competing ideologies going forward. It`s different views of what is right now in the country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s no agreed-upon set of facts...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... on things that are measurable, arguably.\par \par \uc2 So, if they get their information differently, who do these individuals trust to provide it to them?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We started by asking people how much they use social media. And both camps, both Trump and Harris voters, do use it. They don`t necessarily trust everything they see on it.\par \par \uc2 But one thing they say is that they feel social media makes it easier for them to find people that they agree with. And that`s a reminder to everyone, when we look at the role of information, it`s not just that people are maybe getting right or wrong things or are steered in different direction. It`s that sometimes they very affirmatively go out and look for things that reinforce existing beliefs.\par \par \uc2 The other part is, look at sources of information. For Trump voters, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a trusted source of information, as much as their family and friends and more than a lot of what`s called experts. But they`re not as trusting across the board of all those things, whereas Harris voters also trust Harris very much, but they also trust other sources, like the ones that I mentioned, across the board.\par \par \uc2 One thing we saw that both camps share is, they`re concerned about foreign interference, that foreign countries are trying to influence them, the voters, to try to steer this election.\par \par \uc2 And what tends to happen is, each camp thinks that Iran, China, Russia wants the other candidate to win this. Where all this comes out is, when you look ahead, what happens after the results are counted, Donald Trump`s voters are more likely than Harris` to say that his camp should challenge the election if he loses.\par \par \uc2 And they`re more suspicious that there will be widespread fraud in this election. That`s something, talk about different views of what is, that we need to keep an eye on once the race is over.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Salvanto, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We spoke late last week with a group of voters from all seven battleground states to discuss the election, their outlook for America`s future and more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How many of you voters are going to cast your ballots for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (SOME VOTERS RAISE THEIR HAND)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mary, you raised your hand. You are in the very important state of Arizona. What`s driving your decision?\par \par \uc2 MARY (Trump Supporter): Well, my decision is, I live right on the border in Yuma, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 And I know, in the last 3.5 years, we have had just horrible, horrible problems in Yuma with a lot of illegal immigration coming through. Our economy is awful. Yuma`s unemployment is 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 So the economy is a big factor as well, just a combination of things. Our groceries have gone up. Just -- just everything has just really gone downhill in the last 3.5 years for us in Yuma and Arizona completely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Cole, you are in Georgia, also a very key state. Why do you plan to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 COLE (Trump Supporter): For me, I do not look necessarily at the candidate specifically, but just the broader party and the direction they`re hoping to take America.\par \par \uc2 For me, some of the top three issues would definitely be our economy, immigration, and then I would also say, as we have come out of the pandemic, just seeing how the two different political parties handled the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the Democrats are a little bit too extreme on the issue of abortion. I do not support the idea of allowing abortion at 20 weeks of pregnancy, and certainly not with no restrictions. And so I would prefer candidates who are more aligned with my pro-life position.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mike, you`re in Nevada. You said you will be voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but he makes it hard. He makes it hard for you to feel good about that vote.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain what you mean?\par \par \uc2 MIKE (Trump Supporter): There`s parts of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  which I admire and parts of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  which I cringe, for lack of a better term.\par \par \uc2 And in the debate, when he said -- they just asked him, January 6, would you change anything, I think he had an opportunity there to step up as a leader and take some ownership. People went to jail. I think -- I think that would have showed a lot of courage for him to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, I like things on the economy, tightening up inflation, yes, yes, and yes. I think those are the primary issues in this election. You know, as you get into the periphery and just how he conducts himself sometimes, it`s -- you know, it`s hard to put a Trump sign in the front yard based on some of his behaviors.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you`re still doing it. Why?\par \par \uc2 MIKE: Well, because I`m a fan of the economy and a fan of success, right? I think there`s been -- there has been no driver in the driver`s seat in our economy for the last 3.5 years.\par \par \uc2 And I think we have suffered mightily for it. That drives a lot for me. I don`t have to like people personally. I don`t have to agree with some of the stuff they do, but I have to kind of go for the greater good.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Christopher, you are in North Carolina. How are people doing there amidst the fallout from this storm? And what makes you an enthusiastic Republican right now?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER (Trump Supporter): I have always been enthusiastic Republican since I voted for \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in 1980.\par \par \uc2 I have been fortunate enough to be part of Trump`s ground campaign in 2016. And I met some very amazing people. I really, really like the maturation process that he`s gone through. He got his feet wet in 2016. He learned the hard way. He got tagged a few times. He stepped in it a lot.\par \par \uc2 It`s easy to look in the rearview mirror and say coulda, shoulda, and woulda. But when you`re in the moment, you make the best decision you can at the time. I trust him to do that. I trust him to make a decision.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But do you, Christopher, ever feel uncomfortable, like Mike said he did, with things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER: At some times, I have.\par \par \uc2 But in this day and age, with the -- what we`re being faced with the -- on the global stage and the not-so-nice characters that will look for a weak spot in the United States of America to bring her to her knees, I`d rather have in a -- in the ocean full of bloody water shark -- great white sharks, I`d rather have an orca in there than Flipper protecting me.\par \par \uc2 And I know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will get in there and he will fight for us. He may not say what we want to hear, but he will execute on our behalf. And it`s obvious. The man nearly -- nearly died.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER: He took a bullet for us and he got back up again. And you cannot dismiss that. And his instinct was to say, fight.\par \par \uc2 So, that right there tells me this man doesn`t -- he doesn`t have to be there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How many of you are planning to vote for Vice President Harris in November?\par \par \uc2 (SOME VOTERS RAISE THEIR HAND)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to dive into exactly why that is.\par \par \uc2 Lynda and then Kathy, why are you supporting Vice President Harris for president?\par \par \uc2 LYNDA (Harris Supporter): I first voted for Reagan in `80 as well. And all of my voting years, I was told character counts.\par \par \uc2 And as I look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I either have to say character counts and not vote for him or say it doesn`t count anymore. I did vote for him in 2016. I voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020. And I will vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s interesting that you voted for Trump, then Biden. Now you`re going to vote for Harris. These are very different people.\par \par \uc2 What is driving your decision now?\par \par \uc2 LYNDA: I have listened to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the things she has to say. I truly believe she cares about people. I think her life shows she cares about people. And she truly wants to work to make our day-in-and-day-out lives better now.\par \par \uc2 And I care about now, but I have grandchildren, and I care about what kind of a world we`re going to give them to build on as they become people to vote in the next few years. And, again, I go back to character counts. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a proven liar. He has said and done too many things that I think should be disqualifying, from January 6 to those poor people in Springfield, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 They must just be terrified.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Kathy, you`re in Pennsylvania. Why are you voting for Vice President Harris?\par \par \uc2 KATHY (Harris Supporter): I hear and see the fears that people have just contemplating another four years with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I don`t have anything that I can think of that I would say I agree with him on. And I, like Lynda, would say, to me, character counts. So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a no for me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What about you, Mauricio? You are joining us from Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 MAURICIO (Harris Supporter): January 6, to me, is beyond unacceptable. That`s sacred territory that was stormed. He riled up that -- it was not a protest. That was an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 The plans were to overthrow a legitimate election. That will never be tolerated, in my viewpoint.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you believe that Vice President Harris, if she is elected president, that she will be very different from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Or do you see her continuing the issues and agenda that he has pursued for the past four years?\par \par \uc2 MAURICIO: I`m sure she`s probably a little bit more liberal. But she would accept the results if she lost. And that`s big to me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Lynda, back to the original question I asked Mauricio as well.\par \par \uc2 As a Harris supporter, do you believe that the vice president would continue Joe Biden`s policies? Or do you think she has a different vision for the country?\par \par \uc2 LYNDA: I think she will do some of the things President Biden has done. And some of the things Biden has done has helped our country quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 I would like to hear more specifics of what she plans to do, but I would rather see someone want to give tax breaks to the middle class than the most wealthy people in our country, which is what Trump wants to do.\par \par \uc2 COLE: I just wanted to follow up on this idea of how a President -- a future President Harris might or may not be different than President Biden.\par \par \uc2 And I will be honest with you. I do not envy \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , because she has to defend President Biden, and she also has to kind of thread the needle of following his policies, maybe making some of her own.\par \par \uc2 Well, listen, there have been some failures in this administration. And I think whether you`re on the left or the right, you can hopefully see that. And, for me, some of those -- those things off the top of my head were, we did not have a good withdrawal from Afghanistan, prices are through the roof for consumers. And we can debate how we got at that.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, inflation is out of control. And there have been a record number of illegal border crossings under the Biden administration. And so I understand that she has to appeal to the base and she doesn`t want to alienate any Biden voters.\par \par \uc2 But it`s very shocking to me that there`s not one thing that she could think of that she would have done differently than President Biden.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Show of hands, who is concerned about the issue of abortion and reproductive health care going into this election?\par \par \uc2 (SOME VOTERS RAISE THEIR HAND)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Mary, you`re not raising your hand. You live in a state where abortion access is currently permitted up to 15 weeks. You`re good with that, where it is? It`s not a motivating factor for you, even though abortion will be on the ballot in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 MARY: The way I feel is that I`m more concerned about women`s health. And I think that we need to concentrate on prevention, prevention of unplanned pregnancies, not using abortion as birth control.\par \par \uc2 That`s the way I feel. I`m a mother and a grandmother, and I think that that`s what it should be. I think 17 weeks is a good amount of time. Now, of course, just like everyone else had said, rape, incest, problems with the pregnancy, things like that, of course, I can understand having an abortion, and you find out later in your pregnancy that something`s wrong with the baby.\par \par \uc2 OK, that`s a different story.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you, Mary, like to hear candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and candidate J.D. Vance echo your beliefs? They avoid talking about the specifics.\par \par \uc2 You were very specific at 17 weeks. Would you like the candidates to be that specific?\par \par \uc2 MARY: At the federal level, and the reason why it was brought back to the states, is that different states have different feelings.\par \par \uc2 California is much more liberal than Arizona or Texas or Oklahoma or Georgia or other areas of the country. And just like Trump said, look at Kansas. Kansas has become more liberal than they used to be. And that`s very true.\par \par \uc2 But I -- the main focus is women`s health. Women and what`s taught in schools, nothing anymore.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Kathy, you raised your hand that this is an issue that`s important to you.\par \par \uc2 KATHY: It is. I have daughters and granddaughters. And I would hope that the decisions of something that serious would be allowed to be made by them and their health care provider, not by someone sitting in a -- on a bench or in a boardroom or wherever making that decision for them to be able to or not to be able to control their own body.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not realistic to believe, if you like this liberal law better, you -- everyone can just move where they want to. Those things just aren`t realistic.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can see the extended interview on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page or our Web site.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for Face the Nation, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020241013ekad00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Storm Hours Away from Devastating Landfall; \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  on Milton`s Track and Conditions; Some are Unwilling or Unable to Evacuate; Bracing for Another Impact; Central Florida also in Track of Monster Storm; Trump`s Relationship with Putin; Election Terror Plot Thwarted; Israel and Gaza Continue Exchanging Strikes; TikTok Sued by States; Jim Cantore on Areas Milton is Expected to Impact; Desperate Journeys; \uc2 FEMA\uc2  Administrator on Milton Preparations, Helene Clean Up; Jenna Fischer Reveals Breast Cancer Diagnosis; Jets Fire Coach Robert Saleh; Fat Bear Revenge Win; New Trump Revelations?; Harris Visits Colbert; Three Meals in California; Dishing on Diversity; "7 Rules of Self-Reliance"; Glamour Woman of the Year Awards; "Ive got a bone to pick with you..."; "All The Smoke"; Taylor Tomlinson Live, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers;  Stephanie Abrams, Nicole Valdes, Manuel Bojorquez, Nikole Killion, Nicole  Sganga, Haley Ott, Jo Ling Kent, Jim Cantore, Tom Hanson \par \uc2 14,402 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this Wednesday, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s a matter of life and death and that`s not hyperbole. It`s a matter of life and death.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JANE CASTOR (Tampa, Florida): This storm is going to affect everybody.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Time is running out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The window is closing for people to evacuate from the path of Hurricane Milton. We`re on the ground in Florida. We`ve got the latest.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Donald Trump`s relationship with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is under scrutiny again over claims they`ve spoken multiple times since Trump left office.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH) (2024 vice presidential nominee): Is there something wrong with speaking to world leaders?\par \par \uc2 (CROWD answers "no.")\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More than a dozen states are suing TikTok claiming the social media app is knowingly harming teenagers.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA (California attorney general): They specifically prey on young people`s vulnerabilities.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The \uc2 New York Jets\uc2  fired their head coach. Robert Saleh is out. The Jets are two and three. Saleh`s record was 20 and 36.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): You and I have been texting every day. You`re usually sending me text like, "Hey, Stephen. It`s Kamala. For just $5.00 you can make a difference in this campaign. Text stop to quit." I`ll go stop like that. And you know, I don`t mean it, because they don`t stop.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made appearances today on "The View," Howard Stern and "The Late Show" with Stephen Colbert.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: With all of these appearances, Harris is trying to connect with younger voters, Black and Latino audiences, and older Americans. I think I know which demographic we represent. I said, I think I know which demographic we represent.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m guessing the young Latino voter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is that who he was screaming at?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s who he is going for.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every voter counts. Every viewer counts. Come on, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s -- you`re so right about that, especially when it`s this close.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin, of course, you`ve heard about this, it is Hurricane Milton. He is barreling down on Florida. The storm, as we`ve heard, is massive. It`s just hours away from slamming into the coast. Officials say it could be the worst hurricane to hit Florida in a century. Let that sink in for just a second. This is serious.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got correspondents all across the state, from one coast to the other.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start with Vladimir Duthiers. There he is. He`s in Tampa, leading our coverage there. Vlad, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The hurricane isn`t even here yet, but we`ve already been getting drenched overnight by heavy rain from a separate front that is saturating the ground, and it`s making it that much more difficult to deal with the water to come.\par \par \uc2 You can see behind me across Hillsborough Bay, that is Tampa General Hospital, where patients and staff are being moved off the first floor, and where flood barrier that held during Hurricane Helene two weeks ago might not hold this time if the most dire predictions turn out to be true.\par \par \uc2 Scattered throughout neighborhoods across this area, that has been the most heartbreaking thing to see guys, debris left over from the last disaster that threaten to become projectiles as winds hit later today.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the roads, let`s talk about that. They remain jam-packed, as they have been for days, as people try to make their way out. Gas shortages have also become a problem, and get this, most stations around here have run dry, while elsewhere, people are waiting hours just to reach a pump.\par \par \uc2 Now take a look at how huge Hurricane Milton is, seen from the International Space Station yesterday, covering much of the Gulf of Mexico. On one hand, it`s absolutely beautiful, and on the other, it is absolutely terrifying.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Vlad, good morning.\par \par \uc2 People should be finished with their preparations or wrapping them up this morning. The monster that is Hurricane Milton will come ashore tonight, into tomorrow. It will act like a buzz saw as it speeds across the state, maintaining its hurricane strength, and it will still be a hurricane once it is offshore tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see the strongest winds where it makes landfall, but I want to emphasize that you will feel impacts of the storm outside of the cone. Gusty winds will reach well away from the center, which could take out power at the coast and inland. So please use flashlights over candles to prevent accidental fires and fill up your tub so you can flush your toilet if you lose water.\par \par \uc2 The turbulent onshore flow will bring the water up along much of the West Coast and even along the northeast coast of Florida. We will also get water from the sky, with the heaviest rain, into Central Florida, which gives us the potential of flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 Rivers will also come out of their banks, and remember to stay out of both fresh and salt water, as water is the biggest killer in hurricanes. We will also see tornadoes quickly spin up.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, the National Hurricane Center warns, and I quote: "Milton has the potential to be one of the most destructive hurricanes on record for West Central Florida."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve heard that from the mayor as well.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, just hours before landfall, many folks are still trying to flee this part of Florida, but talking to folks around Tampa Bay, we found some people are either unwilling to leave or they`re unable.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: -- local emergency.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Despite days of evacuation warnings from local officials.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Quickly secure your home or business and safely evacuate the area.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Some in the Tampa, St. Petersburg area are refusing to heed the call.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to ride this storm out in this boat right here?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH MALINOWSKI (Resident unable to evacuate): Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH MALINOWSKI: Because I had nowhere else to go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Joseph Malinowski (sp?) has lived on this boat since March.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH MALINOWSKI: This is my home. This is my everything. This is all I have right now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He ignored calls to evacuate during Helene anchoring in the same area.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH MALINOWSKI: And as long as my Nash don`t break or anything like that, I`ll be all right. For the last one, Helene, I slept through the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Helene made landfall roughly 200 miles to the north of Tampa. Milton, on the other hand, is expected to hit much closer, potentially bringing double the storm surge.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH DAVIS (Florida resident): This was the outside water line, and it was about the same inside.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Deborah Davis (sp?) also chose to ride out Helene at home.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH DAVIS: Lower cabinets have been pulled, counters and things set aside.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The 65-year-old former teacher lives in a retirement community in St. Petersburg, many of her and her neighbor`s belongings were destroyed by two feet of flood water here and are now piled outside the condo.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH DAVIS: A lot of my neighbors were surrounded by their memories, and their memories are now sitting in that dump pile in the parking lot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Davis says she`s staying with friends nearby, unable to leave town after her car was totaled in the flood.\par \par \uc2 Her friend Jerry (sp?) told us he and his two cats are staying put in this complex.\par \par \uc2 JERRY (Local resident): I don`t have anywhere to go, and I`m afraid to get stranded somewhere on the interstate. If you run out of gas, what do you do?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tampa`s mayor says officials are going door-to-door citywide, offering transportation for those in need. Shelters have also opened up across the region.\par \par \uc2 So what is your message to people who decide to stay after all that you`ve said, all the warnings you`ve given.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JANE CASTOR: If you`re in a single story home and a 12-foot wall of water comes, that is un-survivable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dire words there from the mayor.\par \par \uc2 Another part of Florida expecting to take a big hit from Milton is Fort Myers, which is about two hours south of where we are. The area is already reeling from Helene, and it has spent the past two years rebuilding from another major hurricane.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes is there.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Vlad, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Sadly, this is not the place anyone is going to want to be in just a few hours. City leaders have been warning people of the potential lethal force of this storm, calling this scary and significant. It`s why that storm surge forecast in some places is up to 12 feet, and that`s what we`re looking at here when you see these businesses that have put layers on layers of protection.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking everything from tape to plastic wrap, sandbags, even large tubes that act as really an oil boom to try to divert all that water away. The question is, will it be enough?\par \par \uc2 Now, yesterday, just off the coast the island of Sanibel, told everyone to get out. Winds from this hurricane could reach 70 miles an hour or more, but it`s that storm surge that many fear the most.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, Sanibel was devastated by Hurricane Ian, and it actually washed away parts of the causeway, leaving locals stranded there for weeks.\par \par \uc2 Now, despite that looming threat, some say they aren`t going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: We had the sheriff tell us yesterday, for those who choose to stay, we`re not going to force anyone out of their homes, but at a certain point, no help is coming.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We know that we`re staying and we know that the officials know that we know they`re not going to be there. We don`t expect that. We don`t expect them to be there on our beck and call.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Of course, an emotional and tough choice, whether you choose to stay or evacuate.\par \par \uc2 Back here in downtown, this restaurant was set for their grand opening, look at that, this month. Now, there`s just no telling when that will happen -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you for that reporting. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Milton`s fury, folks, is not going to be limited to just Florida`s West Coast. It is expected to sweep across Central Florida overnight, bringing high winds, rain and floods, and that`s where Manuel Bojorquez is at Orlando International Airport.\par \par \uc2 Manny, good morning. What`s happening there on the ground?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Vlad, you know this airport is usually teeming with people, families and children here for the theme parks. This morning, as you can see behind me, the TSA line is empty. That`s because the airport will cease operations at 8:00 AM Eastern Time and not reopen until it is safe to do so.\par \par \uc2 Let`s show you video of the other concern for this area, and that`s the demand for gas that has surged as people try to fuel up ahead of Milton. At the pump, some have found gas stations running low on fuel or completely out.\par \par \uc2 About 17 percent of gas stations in the state are out of fuel. It`s created a situation where the Florida Highway Patrol has actually been escorting fuel trucks to gas stations along evacuation routes to try to replenish the supply.\par \par \uc2 Major theme parks have adjusted their operations. \uc2 Disney\uc2 `s theme parks here in Orlando are slated to close starting at one in the afternoon. Universal Studios Florida and Sea World are doing the same, and it`s likely most of these places will be closed through Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The other big concern for Central Florida, the power outage potential. If the current track holds, it could be worse than Helene, when at least two million people in the area lost power -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is a big fear. Manny Bojorquez for us. Thank you, Manny, very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have much more from Florida in our next half hour for now, though, let`s send it 