e watching CBS MORNINGS, we always appreciate that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, TikTok faces major lawsuits accusing the site of being bad for children. More than a dozen states and the District of Columbia sued TikTok yesterday. Congress has already moved to ban the site owned by a Chinese-based company because of security and data concerns.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent joins us from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 So Jo Ling, good morning, but what does these new lawsuits -- what do they allege?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 These 14 attorneys general claim TikTok is not only misleading the public about how safe it is, but also knowingly harming children`s mental health.\par \par \uc2 These lawsuits were filed yesterday in state courts after two years of investigations, and they allege that TikTok violates consumer protection laws and claim the social media app relies on addictive features that keep users on the app.\par \par \uc2 Now, some of those features include notifications that can interrupt kid`s sleep patterns and video that auto plays to entice users to spend more and more time scrolling.\par \par \uc2 TikTok`s viral challenges were also cited in the lawsuits. States are claiming that those challenges promote dangerous behaviors that have caused injuries and even death.\par \par \uc2 Now in a statement, TikTok told us it`s been trying to work with these attorneys general for more than two years. The company says it provides what it calls robust safeguards and has rolled out new safety features for users under 16 that limit screen time and increase privacy.\par \par \uc2 Now as for that federal ban you already know about, it requires the company be sold or shut down by mid-January of next year, but TikTok has sued, that could drag on for years -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jo Ling Kent, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS from Tampa, where Hurricane Milton remains an extreme threat this morning.\par \par \uc2 The National Hurricane Center says it could be a historic and devastating storm.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist Jim Cantore, with our partners at the \uc2 Weather Channel\uc2  is also in Tampa.\par \par \uc2 Jim, good morning. What can we expect when Milton barrels across the state tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Well, I think you`re going to see a lot of regret for those who didn`t evacuate, okay, especially from about Pasco County, which is north of Tampa, all the way down toward Fort Myers Beach.\par \par \uc2 This will be a life-changing and a beach-changing hurricane with the kind of storm surge that we`re expecting. That is the biggest threat to the loss of life for those that did not evacuate, as they were told to do.\par \par \uc2 So let`s time this out for you. Now again, as I show you this, this is one model and one run of it. It, to me, seems a little bit fast. So this thing could come in anywhere, let`s say from nine all the way to about 1:00 AM in the morning or maybe midnight. Okay, so there`s a little wiggle room as to when it comes in.\par \par \uc2 But I want you to look at the wind in Tampa, in Clearwater, in Sarasota and Port Charlotte. We`ve got wiggle room, whether this goes to the north of Tampa or to the south of Tampa, you`re still going to have some issues.\par \par \uc2 And then this eye goes right across the state. It`s going to be twice as big as it is right now, maybe three times as big, depending on what happens after this eye wall replacement cycle. And we`re talking about damage, flooding, obviously, storm surge on the west side and in the east side, that will get enhanced once the storm goes offshore tomorrow morning. We`ll still have parts of the western eye wall on the east coast of Florida.\par \par \uc2 So this is going to be a devastating hurricane, probably the worst hit that we`ve seen, certainly, on the west coast of Florida in over a century.\par \par \uc2 So Vlad, I hope everybody heeded those warnings, especially in the surge zone, because it`s coming in at night and you`re not going to know what hit you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, indeed.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: I`m telling you. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, exactly. And we talked about why Tampa is so vulnerable to that storm surge.\par \par \uc2 Jim Cantore for us this morning. Jim, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 thousands of people on Florida`s Gulf Coast are still trying to get out of Milton`s path. But folks, it is not easy, because of all the traffic.\par \par \uc2 Our Tom Hanson found a way to get from Sarasota to Daytona Beach yesterday, and that is a 200-mile trip. Along the way, he met a lot of other people preparing for this dangerous storm.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: We began our trip heading inland from Sarasota to Arcadia. Carrying water and pets, evacuees trickled into this middle school gym, now their home through Hurricane Milton.\par \par \uc2 LEONA RUDISELL (Evacuee): It can fizzle out. It can get worse, but you`ve got to go what is recommended.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: For Leona Rudisell, who is disabled, it was the safest option. Others said they couldn`t afford to go further. But many Floridians are choosing to leave the Gulf Coast altogether, and this is how some are doing it.\par \par \uc2 We are heading east. We`re going to end up in Daytona Beach, but first things first, we have to find a gas station.\par \par \uc2 We pulled into a Circle K packed with cars.\par \par \uc2 No gas in that pump. No gas in that pump. But we`re going to keep our fingers crossed that this line will have enough gas when we get to the front of it.\par \par \uc2 Lucky for us, our tank filled up fast. But for others --\par \par \uc2 Look how slow that is going.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, it is going really, really, slow.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: While waiting, we met Dale Dorman, a first responder in nearby Polk County.\par \par \uc2 DALE DORMAN (Polk County first responder): If you don`t leave, I`ll give you two words, how you`re going to be the next day -- dead. Well, that`s one word.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: With a full tank, we forged ahead.\par \par \uc2 That one has bags over it. We`re passing entire towns that don`t have gas at this point.\par \par \uc2 In Winter Haven --\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Pump number 11 is open.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: It was controlled chaos at one of the few stations still in service.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this line. You can see it goes down the block. Virtually everybody is waiting in this line.\par \par \uc2 LUXY SUMERALL (Waited an hour for gas): Not knowing really what`s to come and how it`s going to hit, it is better then to be prepared.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: As we pulled away, the station announced it also ran out of gas, leaving many with empty tanks as the sun set on the last day before the storm.\par \par \uc2 We`re driving right along the projected path of Milton.\par \par \uc2 Finally, we closed in on our destination.\par \par \uc2 One of my big takeaways from this is, you know, it is a headache for people to pick up everything, board up their windows, leave their homes, but they`re willing to deal with the headache far more than risking their lives, and that is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And Florida officials here on the East Coast are taking this threat very seriously. According to the city of Daytona Beach, the entire county, Volusia County is under a mandatory evacuation order that is set to begin in less than 30 minutes -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Tom, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s send it back to Tony in New York.\par \par \uc2 Tony, as you can see, people are heeding those evacuation warnings, which is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that is a good thing. Tom Hanson is right ahead.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, thank you. We`ll check back with you in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, \uc2 FEMA\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  will talk to us about the preparations for the hurricane and also how to avoid and get fooled by misinformation.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s your message to the people who are spreading misinformation?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JANE CASTOR: I hate to sound like a mom, but shame on you. You know, shame on you. Politics doesn`t belong in a life and death situation like this. These hurricanes don`t know who votes for who.\par \par \uc2 We are Americans, and we need to come together as Americans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is Vlad speaking with Tampa Mayor \uc2 Jane Castor\uc2  yesterday about the dangers of misinformation when it comes to these disasters.\par \par \uc2 Misinformation is also impacting the \uc2 FEMA\uc2  workers who are trying to help people in the wake of Hurricane Helene, while they`re now preparing for Hurricane Milton. It`s a mess.\par \par \uc2 FEMA\uc2  administrator, that`s \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Miss Criswell.\par \par \uc2 I know you`re on the ground in North Carolina, where you`re still working as the clock is ticking, getting ready for Milton. What`s your biggest concern as you sit here right now, while we`re waiting for this thing to hit?\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2  (\uc2 FEMA\uc2  administrator): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 You know, as we`re watching Hurricane Milton, you know, it increases in strength, decreases and increases and what I want to make sure that people are doing is that they are still listening to their local officials.\par \par \uc2 This is a massive storm that is going to bring catastrophic wind, but also flooding and storm surge, and so listen to your local officials. Nobody needs to die from this storm.\par \par \uc2 I want people to get out of harm`s way. You may still have time to get out, but listen to your local officials, they will tell you the best place for you to go and the best actions for you to take right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know how you break through, though I saw officials saying, look, write your name and your Social Security on your arm if you insist on staying, because we will not be able to help you. How do you break through when people just won`t listen because they say, look, this is my home.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 : Yes, I mean, I can understand. People want to stay in their home and protect their home, but this storm is going to be like nothing that they have ever seen before. And I think the message that I try to get across is when we think about evacuation, we often think about these long lines of traffic going out of state, when in reality, you may only need to go a few miles.\par \par \uc2 I remember talking to the St. Petersburg mayor who said their safe evacuation shelter was just two miles inland. That`s what we need to focus on, is know exactly where to go. It may not be far. It will get you out of harm`s way for the storm.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Miss Criswell, for those that might not have the means to travel out, that have to hunker down where they are, what advice do you have for them?\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 : Pay attention to the local officials. Make sure you`re charging up all of your devices right now so you have them available to use.\par \par \uc2 And if you need immediate assistance, you know your local officials, local first responders, call 911, and we`re going to have additional teams in the area to support search and rescue efforts as the storm passes, but make sure that you`re just making yourself as safe as possible right now, and continue to listen to the local officials so you know what the risks are and where they`re at and when they`re going to be where you are specifically located.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Miss Criswell, I`d love to talk a bit about the resources that \uc2 FEMA\uc2  has right now. You`re in North Carolina. You`ve got teams there in the south, you`ve got other teams that are working on the last hurricane that hit Florida, the debris there, and there`s a report in "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " suggesting that only 10 percent of the overall resources of \uc2 FEMA\uc2 , 10 percent of the overall staff are actually available to work on the aftermath of Milton.\par \par \uc2 That`s concerning to people. What do you have to say on the subject?\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 : Yes, I mean, I read the report in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," and we have a layered approach to staffing, and the numbers that they report are a very specific part of our workforce, and that`s our Disaster Management workforce, but we also have all of our other employees.\par \par \uc2 In fact, when everybody signs on to work with us, we make them sign a statement that says every employee is an emergency manager. So we start to repurpose some of our steady state employees from headquarters, but we also move people around from noncritical, life threatening offices or open field offices that we have to support the immediate needs and then we`re just one part of the team.\par \par \uc2 We bring in the whole federal family, the entire federal government, to support the efforts that are going on across all of the states that have been impacted by this storm.\par \par \uc2 But I`m not saying it`s not going to be challenging. We know that there`s a lot going on with all of these efforts, but we plan for this, and we`ve done this before.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Administrator Criswell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right now, it`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- including our series, we call it "Three Meals," Jo Ling Kent dines out with voters and talks presidential politics in a hotly contested California district. That`s a new one.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "After Midnight"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, comedian Taylor Tomlinson is here. She`s killing it, as you know, on season two of \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "After Midnight," and on her new stand up tour, it is called "Save Me" like that.\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour, but first, you know, Vlad is on the road, so Klaus and I heading over the table because Nate is going to have this --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s go Mets. I`ve been on the road, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s go Mets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did the Mets win, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mets won last night, and they are going to close it out tonight. Oh, sorry, Philly. Love you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, it`s time for "What to Watch." Let`s get right into it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hit it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is time for "What to Watch" and Vlad is on assignment, so let`s get right into it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We start with Jenna Fischer. She is from "The Office" as we know, sharing a personal memo.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Office"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fischer who played Pam on the popular sitcom, revealed on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that she was diagnosed with an aggressive form of breast cancer last year. Now she said it was caught early, which is great news and she had a lumpectomy and it was removed, the tumor was removed in January.\par \par \uc2 The 50-year-old said, after chemo and radiation, she is now cancer free, which is something we all love to hear, those two words.\par \par \uc2 Fischer said, she announced this for a few reasons, in her own words, one is, "I`m ready to ditch the wigs, which we get. Two, to implore you to get your annual mammograms," and she said, if she waited six months longer, things could have been much, much worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s good she is talking about it. I have a mammogram today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, you`ve got to stay on this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it`s important to remind people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is something that we need to do on a regular basis. I love that she`s talking about it and really glad she is all right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, really glad she is all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She has millions of followers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is using the platform to spread that message --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- like you said, Gayle. People need to hear it.\par \par \uc2 All right, so the \uc2 NFL\uc2  is firing season started a little bit earlier than expected.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 New York Jets\uc2 , they dumped their head coach Robert Saleh after a disappointing two and three start losing their last two games. The Jets began this season as Super Bowl hopefuls by most people`s ideas, because they had the return of Aaron Rodgers.\par \par \uc2 Now they have not made the playoffs in 13 years. That is longer than any other team in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . Jets` owner, Woody Johnson wrote on X, "I believe now is the time for us to move in a different direction."\par \par \uc2 He announced defensive coordinator, Jeff Ulbrich will be the interim head coach after Saleh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always hate that phrase, "different direction."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you surprised by that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was surprised, yes, because I wouldn`t necessarily blame him for the woes of this team. There have been some struggles to blame, even last game.\par \par \uc2 As much as I love Aaron Rodgers, he threw three interceptions that were really on him. So, it`s been tough. The spread is on everybody, not just the head coach.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Nate, I love the idea of firing season in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . How many coaches usually lose their job in a given year?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, not that much. This happened a little bit early.\par \par \uc2 All right, so for this year`s Fat Bear Contest in Alaska, revenge was on the menu. All Hail the Queen, Grazer is the winner for the second straight year after putting on impressive pounds for winter.\par \par \uc2 The 20-year-old female beat out Fat Bear favorite, Chunk, he actually killed one of Grazer`s cubs over the summer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know that`s a twist in the story right here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She won by more than 40,000 votes, so a little payback for what happened to her cub.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you get for winning Fat Bear Contest?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just love from the streets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do they even know? Are they aware what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up how to build your personal brand and set yourself apart from everyone else. A top expert is explaining that in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back. companies.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, the leaders of the new school of sports media, this ain`t your momma`s \uc2 ESPN\uc2 . The hosts of "All The Smoke" Podcast will be right here in the building.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in the 8 o`clock hour, Stephen Jackson and Matt Barnes, they were stars on the court in the \uc2 NBA\uc2 . Now they`re stars on the mic in their hit podcast, "All the Smoke." We`re talking about that and they got a new book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. For our series "Three Meals." Jo Ling Kent travels in California, not a swing state but a swing district known for great diversity and great food.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We also have Taylor Tomlinson in the building, host of CBS`s "After Midnight," about staying real and being funny on her new stand-up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, she`s killing it.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye-Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re going to ride the storm out in this boat right here.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH MALINOWSKI: This is my home. This is my everything. This is all I have right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: While millions evacuate coastal Florida, some people say they have to stay even with Hurricane Milton expected to make landfall tonight.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: It will act like a buzz saw as it speeds across the state, maintaining its hurricane strength and it will still be a hurricane once it`s offshore tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  secretly sent COVID tests to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  early in the pandemic, according to a new book by Bob Woodward.\par \par \uc2 BOB WOODWARD: These are precious assets to anyone in the world and the country and he gives it to him for his personal use.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More than a dozen states are suing TikTok, claiming it`s addictive and harmful to children`s mental health.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  continues her media blitz with a visit to "The Late Show with Stephen Colbert."\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: It`s not the first time the vice president has graced the stage here at the Ed Sullivan. She`s been on six times, six times seven, if you count when she was on to promote her acoustic album, "When We Folk, We Win."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour, though, with Hurricane Milton, as you`ve heard, already set to make landfall tonight. You can see here how massive this storm is. It`s off Florida`s Gulf Coast and it`s heading there strong. It`s a Category 5, which is just about as dangerous as it gets.\par \par \uc2 Vlad is down in Tampa leading our coverage for us. Vlad, good morning again. I always find it so interesting when they tell people, "Get out, get out." But Vlad, please go down there and tell us the story. We want you to be safe as well. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah. Good to see you, Gayle. And that is the message from officials all across the state. Milton and its 160-mile-per-hour winds are still more than 200 miles off the coast, but we`re already getting soaked with rain from another system, and that is going to mean worse problems for flooding later today.\par \par \uc2 Now, here in the Tampa Bay area, meteorologists are warning that the storm surge could get as high as 15 feet, potentially putting some of these spots all around me underwater.\par \par \uc2 Over six million people are affected by evacuation orders. You can see the mass exodus from the Naples area, a scene that`s being played out all across the region. Fourteen counties are under mandatory evacuation orders, and if you`re living in any of the evacuation zones, look, thanks for watching us, but, folks, you should have left by now. That`s a message hammered home by emergency management officials. Listen.\par \par \uc2 CATHIE PERKINS (Director, Pinellas County Emergency Management): I can`t stress enough that this is not an if event. It`s a when event, and we are into the final hours. We`re going to get hit, and it`s going to be hard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now in terms of where exactly the storm is and when it`s expected to make landfall, let`s turn to \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  and our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Vlad, Hurricane Milton is currently in the Gulf of Mexico, but it is making a beeline for Florida. Preparations should be finished or wrapped up, and people should be in their safe place.\par \par \uc2 The storm is massive in size and strength. It will make landfall tonight into tomorrow along the West Coast of Florida. The outer bands will spin up tornadoes in central and south Florida. By tomorrow, Milton will be off the east coast, and believe it or not, still a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 The winds will do the most damage where it comes ashore and within the eye wall as it crosses the state, but its large wingspan will bring gusty winds along the peninsula and outside of the cone.\par \par \uc2 That means we`ll see significant and widespread damage to structures, trees, and power poles. Use flashlights, not candles, to avoid accidental fires. The surge will be in the double digits on the west coast and high in cities well away from where it makes landfall. Florida`s northeast coast will also see the water rise.\par \par \uc2 The north side of the storm is where we could get several months of rain over a short period of time, 8 to 12 inches or more. That gives us the greatest chance of flooding in central Florida.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, Milton will alter the coastline and change lives forever. We hope everyone stays safe.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s send it back to you in the studio in New York, Tony.\par \par \uc2 And as the mayor of Tampa told us, if you`re still in your home when those 10-foot waves and those surges come aboard, you`re in a coffin.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. And you just heard Stephanie there. This will alter the coastline and change lives forever. That really cuts through, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in a moment, our series "Three Meals." We hit the road in California to talk to voters about a crucial race that could help determine control of Congress. But first, 8.05. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Global communications strategist, Maha Abouelenein has helped build some of the biggest brands in the world, but she started her career as an intern with CBS Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN (Digital and Savvy CEO): The ticket could change your life. A single winner from today`s Powerball drawing could take home an estimated $3 million annually for the next 20 years. And today --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And ahead, she will share some lessons on how to unleash your superpowers.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every week before the election, our series "Three Meals" is on the road talking to voters over a meal or three. Today we take our forks and knives to California.\par \par \uc2 Now usually we head to battleground states, but this time it`s a Democratic stronghold that has one of the most competitive congressional races. California`s 45th district battle between GOP incumbent Michelle Steele and Democrat Derek Tran could help determine control of the U.S. House.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 40% of the population there is Asian or Pacific Islander and 31% is Hispanic. Jo Ling Kent hit the road to see what`s on folks` mind and found that many people are focused on the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: From the mountains to the beaches and redwood forests, the Golden State is one of the nation`s most geographically diverse. California is also one of the most racially diverse with the biggest population of eligible Vietnamese voters in the country.\par \par \uc2 That`s why we`re headed to a neighborhood called Little Saigon. Garden Grove is hailed as one of the best places to get Vietnamese food in the U.S., Pho Redbo is our first stop. Known for its broth made with high quality Wagyu beef.\par \par \uc2 JAMES BUI (Co-owner, Pho Redbo): Everybody is enjoying pho. You can have pho on a hot day, cold night or early morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: So pho for breakfast?\par \par \uc2 JAMES BUI: Pho for breakfast. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Okay, I`m down with this. That`s a great idea.\par \par \uc2 James Bui opened Redbo three years ago and hopes the next president will give small businesses more tax breaks.\par \par \uc2 JAMES BUI: We`re coming due our tax right now and the bill is pretty hefty.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Is it higher than it`s been in past years?\par \par \uc2 JAMES BUI: It`s just high.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Inflation, which cuts into his bottom line, will be a deciding factor in how he votes in November. Restaurant inflation is still rising by 4% nationwide compared to last year.\par \par \uc2 JAMES BUI: Inflation plays a major role because everything we see price increases on the weekly basis. I`m just a person trying to make his ends meet. We bring the best product, we bring the best meat and that`s how we`re going to attract eaters.\par \par \uc2 A few tables away we met Zachary Fritz (sp?) and Monica Yee (sp?).\par \par \uc2 So who are you going to vote for in the presidential?\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY FRITZ (California voter): Kamala. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY FRITZ: And I will be the first to say I don`t like her, but the choice is still clear to me. It`ll always be somebody who at least respects democracy and respects the vote of the people.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Yee was born in China. Her main concern is immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 MONICA YEE (California voter): It`s stupid to judge people by colors, by nationalities. The beautiful part is diverse. I love that part. But as long as it`s good people, not like illegal people. So that`s my point.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: One thing everyone agreed on, the pho was phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 So we`re back in the car and we`re leaving behind the flavors of Southeast Asia.\par \par \uc2 And keep in mind here in California`s 45th, the Hispanic population makes up a third of the district. So we`re heading for a Southwest staple. We`re going to get some tacos, my favorite.\par \par \uc2 We landed at award-winning Amor y Tacos, founded in Cerritos a decade ago. The food reflects the community, Mexican with both Asian and American influences.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is heavenly. It`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 Lunchtime is packed, but Chef Thomas Ortega is feeling the heat. He and co- owner Todd Tsujioka tell us soaring costs will impact how they cast their votes.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling about the economy here?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS ORTEGA (Co-owner, Amor y Tacos): Our food cost is 30% higher. It feels like everywhere we look, higher, higher, higher.\par \par \uc2 TODD TSUJIOKA (Co-owner, Amor y Tacos): People aren`t ordering dessert. They`re laying off an extra cocktail. So it kind of all chips away at things to make it difficult.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Many have cut back on how often they eat out, like Susana Solano (sp?) and Ernie Ortega (sp?).\par \par \uc2 ERNIE ORTEGA: The cost of everything, fuel, food. You need to have like half a million dollars you need to be making every year just to afford a house right now. It`s crazy. And you ask me who I`m going to vote for, it`s going to be Trump.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Are you going to vote for a Democrat or a Republican?\par \par \uc2 SUSANA SOLANO (California voter): Oh, I used to be Democrat. And now I think I`m more Republican, even though I`m Mexican. And people maybe hate me, but I`m not really a fan of Trump. But now I think he`s the best option.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: A few tables over, Vicky and Michael Allen (sp?) tell us their main focus is also the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 VICKY ALLEN (California voter): My number one priority this election is preserving democracy. Social security, Medicare, preserving women`s health care rights, all of that is at stake.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: So I think I know who you might be voting for.\par \par \uc2 VICKY ALLEN: I am going to vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I don`t want to go backwards.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: All right, we`re rounding out our day in the northernmost part of this crescent-shaped district. We`re going to Brea. We`re going to go check out an Irish pub known for its loyal group of regulars.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to downtown Brea and Macallans public house. This Irish tavern is bustling, keeping spirits filled to the brim.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s menu features a bourbon and beef pairing. Regulars Kelly and Mark Irwin (sp?) are focused on the local election.\par \par \uc2 You guys live in the 45th district. This district could change the balance of power in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 KELLY IRWIN (California voter): Yes.\par \par \uc2 Do you know who you`re going to vote for yet?\par \par \uc2 KELLY IRWIN: Michelle Steele.\par \par \uc2 MARK IRWIN (California voter): We feel that her policies align with what we think`s appropriate and what`s needed.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Both also plan to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , pointing to the economy.\par \par \uc2 How do you feel about saying that out loud, 2024 versus 2020 and 2016?\par \par \uc2 KELLY IRWIN: I feel good about it, because I feel like in 2016 and 2020, it was taboo to say his name and that I`m a woman, that I would be voting for him, because I think a lot of people don`t think that he is very respectful in that way. I`m happy to be saying that now. I`m saying it out loud.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL O`BRIEN (California voter): You can look if you want. I won`t look. I`ll look away.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Professional magician Michael O`Brien performs at Macallans every week. He wishes Trump would disappear.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL O`BRIEN: I`ve been a little lukewarm with Harris, if I`m being honest with you. But just living through the four years of the Trump administration, that`s definitely something that I`m happy is in the past, and I would not like to see him come back again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: So interestingly, guys, at each of the restaurants we went to, customers really were evenly split between supporting Trump and Harris in her home state, making this congressional race between Steele and Tran that much more pivotal for control of the House of Representatives in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And can I also just say thank you for assigning me this? I am eight months pregnant. This was the best possible assignment to get. I had no trouble eating my way through all three meals, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 And from everybody here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 , I`m being told you`re welcome, you know, dinner for two, anytime you want it on us.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done with your three meals.\par \par \uc2 All right, Matt Barnes and Steven Jackson went from \uc2 NBA\uc2  stars to podcast stars to sports media giants. They join us in studio to talk about everything under the sun.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is here to help us unlock our inner superpower. She says everybody has one, do we?\par \par \uc2 Maha Abouelenein is a global communication strategist who`s represented brands like all the biggies, \uc2 Google\uc2 , and \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . Her new book is called "Seven Rules of Self-Reliance, How To Stay Low, Keep Moving, Invest In Yourself, And Own Your Future."\par \par \uc2 Maha, it`s good to see you. We just showed the clip of you as an intern way back in the day at \uc2 CBS\uc2 . I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You look good, by the way.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: I`m an OG CBSer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then here you are.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: I can`t believe it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like -- I want people to know a little bit of your back story. You talk about being the only diverse kid in your class.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Minnesota, back in the day. And you said all people knew about Egypt was pyramids and camels.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you said you even turned that into your superpower, which led to one of your rules.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yeah, so I`m 100% Egyptian. I grew up in Minnesota. I was one of the only brown girls in my class. And you know, when you`re younger, you want to fit in. You don`t want to stand out.\par \par \uc2 But what I did was I turned my differences, being both American and Egyptian, into a business opportunity. How can I help educate people about other markets, other cultures? And that`s what I ended up doing. I lived half my life in the U.S., the other half in the Middle East, and I moved back home during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you were offered this job as an office manager, which you said was beneath you, and you were overqualified for. But your dad, your dad is your North Star, he said, "Take this job even though it`s beneath you."\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yeah, so I got offered to be office manager for a chairman, a billionaire of a company, and I thought, "That`s not what I`m qualified to do." And he said, "Girl, you just moved here. You don`t know anybody in Egypt. You put your head down, you put the work in, and let your work speak for itself. And turn it into what it can be. Add value. Show him what you`re capable of. But first, you need to do what he wants you to do first."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What have you learned about how other people can follow your path by finding their superpower?\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: I think you need to understand, like, where can you add value to other people? And it starts with a simple question. How can I help?\par \par \uc2 When you ask somebody, "How can I help?" You understand, what are they passionate about? What do they care about? What are their pain points? And then pull that string and see where you can bring the most value to them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: First of all, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Nice to see you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re fantastic. Congratulations on everything.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, rule number six, along those lines, be a long-term player. We`re in a day and age where people want instant gratification.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Instant rewards. I do something, I need something right now.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you say give and expect nothing in return.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yeah, so we live in a society. You want to tap to pay to get out of the way. We want to download the whole season right now. We want to wait -- we don`t want to wait three seconds for a video to load.\par \par \uc2 I`m talking about being a long-term player. What can you do to put deposits in other people`s trust banks? Give with good intention and trust me, you will get the returns.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t be a waiter. Be a creator.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Yeah, don`t be a waiter. Often in life, one of the rules of self-reliance is not waiting for other people to bring you opportunities. Come to the table with something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Don`t wait for permission. Don`t expect other people to bring you opportunities. Invest in yourself first. What can you do? Come to the table with relationships, with knowledge, with skills, and bring value.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And your networking tip, because I`m terrible at it.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: So networking is so hard, right? You know, especially through social media or through Zooms or remote working environments. What`s the one way you can try to connect with somebody and make that relationship matter?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In less than 30 seconds, you say everybody should build their personal brand. That drives me crazy, because I say don`t build your brand. Do your job.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Okay So, Gayle --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Work hard and do your job.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Take the word "personal brand" out of your vocabulary and replace it with the word "reputation." And don`t you care about your reputation?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 MAHA ABOUELENEIN: Nothing matters more.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dropping gems. We appreciate you. "7 Rules of Self-Reliance" is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Talk of the Table" coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It`s time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. And Gayle is grooving in the middle seat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s on you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, my talk of the table is, this year`s c ended out last night. I`ll just say this, everybody cleans up nicely when they know they`re about to hit the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 The honorees include, I was not an honoree, I was just a looky-loo. The honorees included there, there are Suni Lee, Olympian, tennis great Serena Williams, that`s Taraji P. Henson, Pamela Anderson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow, wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Allyson Felix was there, she was on your show yesterday, Tony, she was there. That was my table last night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s your what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sitting at the table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re kidding me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was my table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those are just some of the stars that lit up the night. A teary-eyed Beyonce was there. To hear her mom, Tina Knowles, another honoree, tell the crowd how her daughters are her rocks.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Glamourmag)\par \par \uc2 TINA KNOWLES (Beyonce`s mother): My daughters have brought me such incredible joy and pride in my life, far more than anything else. They are my crew, my tribe, my rocks, my ride or dies. They have been right there by my side at the lowest points of my life, and they have cheered me on at the highest points of my life, making me feel like I could conquer anything.\par \par \uc2 KELLY ROWLAND (Singer and actor): `Cause you can.\par \par \uc2 (Audience cheers)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a very emotional moment. That was Kelly Rowland who said, "`Cause you can."\par \par \uc2 Glamour did something different this year. They honored mothers in addition to the nominees. Donna Kelce, who as you know is Travis and Jason`s mom. Mandy Teefey is Selena Gomez`s mom. Maggie Baird, Billie Eilish, and her brother, Phineas, were there. Allyson Felix was also an award-winning. But I did just so happen to be sitting next to Beyonce. They placed me there, but it was really nice.\par \par \uc2 Before Tina went up, Beyonce, come over here, Tony, was like rubbing her shoulder, like, you got this. You`re going to be great. The relationship they have, we all know this, is so tender and so sweet, and they are each other`s biggest cheerleader.\par \par \uc2 And Tina said this, "I`m most proud when they do something about our daughters that is artful that they love. I`m proud to say that I do see things that I instilled in them. They treat people well. They`re not stuck on themselves."\par \par \uc2 Because Beyonce never responds to any of the negativity, but Tina says, "Sometimes I can`t help it."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, and you know about that special relationship between mom and daughter, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Oh, I do, I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So I know it was emotional to see their connection, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was very nice. All eyes on Beyonce. She walked in looking like a blonde bombshell. Kelsea Ballerini was performing, and even she gave a shout out to Beyonce from the stage.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, she looked good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, she looks good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You were lucky to have that seat next to her, because I`m sure people were trying to move their name tag right there. Oh, look at that!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know I was going to be there. I walked in, and I was like, whoa.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo to Glamour.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bravo, bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Glamour`s night for glamour indeed.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is a fun new way to fight with your neighbors. It starts this time of year, every year. You got to know about a skeleton named Skelly that sells for $300 at \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 . There`s an image of Skelly. People put it up on Halloween.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The trouble begins is because people then don`t take Skelly down. And the homeowners association --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That could be a problem.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --get upset. They say it`s scaring the children, it`s January, can you get this thing down? And so what people do is then they get around the rules by decorating Skelly in different seasonal attire for the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Caleb Frost Wall Street Journal)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So there`s 4th of July Skelly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s kind of funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We got back to school Skelly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s kind of funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, there`s like football jersey Skelly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s hilarious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, that`s Cinco de Mayo, I think. I`m not sure, can`t make it out. But so, I mean, this is a season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is this like leaving Christmas lights up all season? Or is this different?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is such a crazy trend that they sell out every year. People have 300 big ones that they spend on a skeleton, and they limit one per customer, because otherwise there`d be a huge black market around it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think I might get involved. I might get involved.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re going to get on the skeleton business?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I might as well, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`d put a Skelly in front of your house?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, me and my nephews are cool, though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would like to know what Mrs. Burleson will say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She won`t let me do it.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, the host of the mega popular podcast in digital series, "All the Smoke" are here.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with former \uc2 NBA\uc2  stars, Matt Barnes and Steven Jackson, about their new book and so much more.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from All The Smoke Productions)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, that`s Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who recently joined former \uc2 NBA\uc2  stars Stephen Jackson and Matt Barnes on their "All the Smoke" podcast and digital series.\par \par \uc2 Over their 200-plus episodes, Jackson and Barnes have interviewed dozens of fellow basketball legends, including Steph Curry, Magic Johnson, Kobe, as well as Hollywood stars like Kevin Hart and Ice Cube.\par \par \uc2 Now they are out with a new book, "All the Smoke, All the Stars, All the Stories, No Apologies." It shares interviews and insights on everything from Michael Jordan to fatherhood.\par \par \uc2 Matt Barnes and Stephen Jackson joins us now. What`s up, fellas? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: Good morning, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: Before we get started, Nate, I want to congratulate you, man. Your transition has been unbelievable, bro, and you`re really inspiring and inspirational for a lot of us, man.\par \par \uc2 So I just want to salute you before we get started.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We feel that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We talk on camera all the time. Iron sharpens iron. I appreciate you all doing what you guys are doing.\par \par \uc2 Matt, we`ll start with you. So take us back to 2019. How did this start? What was the inception and the idea that launched everything?\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: I never thought it would be in media, to be honest with you. You know, as a player, there`s kind of that fine line. And I felt at the time media had crossed that line and I just really didn`t want to be part of sports media.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: You know, but we both kind of fell into \uc2 ESPN\uc2  and Fox and we`re both getting positive feedback. We`re at my place in the Bay just talking about, you know, what can we do together? What`s next? And I threw the podcast idea out.\par \par \uc2 And although neither of us really knew what it was, we just knew there was a little bit more freedom than the other two networks we mentioned. And he trusted me with the, with the vision. And I went out and made it happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you reluctant at first, Stephen? Because the thing, the beauty of this podcast, I think, is that you`re both uniquely yourselves.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At all times, with all people. Were you reluctant at first to do it?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: I wasn`t because I was open to doing it. You know, I`ve been doing \uc2 ESPN\uc2  and Fox and all that. But following him, you know, I knew he would put me in a situation where we`ll both benefit from it. And it`ll take us somewhere we want to go. So I never second guessed it. I`m a loyal teammate. Tim Duncan called me the ultimate teammate, so I`m going to continue to be that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so speaking of being teammates, you guys were teammates at one point in your careers. What was your first impression of each other? Keep it real.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Well, I was looking for the first smoker on the team.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: I got to say, keep it real.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: And I have found Matt, and we just clicked like that. But during that time, we always talk about it. You know, I was coming from some trouble in Indiana when I got to Golden State, and as soon as I got to Golden State, he lost his mom.\par \par \uc2 So around that time, we became-- you know, I stayed with him during the whole time, and we became brothers more than teammates.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: And that`s why it`s so easy to work together now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like seeing in the book, you said, people didn`t really know the real you. They had an impression of both of you. They didn`t really know the real you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Oh, they didn`t-- when the broadcast first started, they didn`t want me. Really?\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: I had to fight for Jack, and I had to tell them that if it wasn`t him, I wasn`t going to do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You weren`t going to do it.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: Yeah, so it was our loyalty. But, I mean, to your point, yeah, I think there was such a misconception, because we caught the end of social media during our careers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You guys caught almost the worst of it.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: Right, so what was put out there was the gospel, whether it was \uc2 ESPN\uc2  or a newspaper or articles, whatever they said about us was it. So, you know, once we kind of got to this platform and understood, man, they`re starting to listen to us and trust us, and we can tell who we are.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think it was a huge blessing, and it allowed us not only to express who we really are, but allow our brothers and sisters in the space to do the same.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the topics that you guys do. For instance, mental health, because most people don`t think black men are comfortable with mental health. I love you talking about fatherhood.\par \par \uc2 My favorite section is mothers, but then you have a whole thing about fatherhood. And you get people to open up in ways, I don`t think that they do with other people.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: Yeah, I mean, I just think creating a safe environment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: You know, we have a good time. We come and hang out, and we`ll partake in a few different activities, have some music and food, and really just get a--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does "partake in a few different activities" mean? - What does that mean, Mr. Barnes?\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: cut right to it. We might have some of our Anillos mezcal, or, you know, maybe smoke a little bit of cannabis, just to kind of relax and take the edge off. And we have fun, so when we get out there, they feel comfortable. And I think, you know, Stephen--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They trust you.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: I`ve been through so much stuff that, you know, the way we frame questions is very relatable, so they feel comfortable enough to kind of let that guard down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So let`s talk about the cannabis. All the smoke, that`s not just a metaphor for conversation, right? You guys are involved in the business. It matters to you personally.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When you spoke to Vice President Harris, she said that she supports legalization.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What would that mean for you, for everybody, if it happened?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Well, for us, I`m just happy it`s legal in basketball. In professional basketball, they`re not getting drug-addicted no more.\par \par \uc2 We know how important it was for us to be able to come home from games, not have to take all this kind of medication to come down and relax and be able to sleep.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Therapy insides.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: All the stuff that gets you addicted to.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The opioids.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have to find after you retire, and now you`re addicted to it. So I`m glad that it`s legal now for the players to be able to decompress and relax after games the right way and not to do any --\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: And to piggyback, I think it`s bigger than that, too, because, I mean, black and brown people have been targeted throughout this journey with cannabis, and there`s a ton of people still in jail or in prison for it.\par \par \uc2 So I just think that next step is being able to, you know, low-level offenders to be able to be released and have an opportunity in this space. We`re talking off-air on just how saturated this space is now, you know, but to still have this kind of opportunity for the black and brown community is what I`m really looking forward to as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love your guest list. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, go ahead, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So I described you guys earlier as the leaders of the new school of sports media because there has been a shift, and, you know, people are looking for information from players, and now players are taking that side of the job more seriously. How do you guys feel that you guys have changed the way fans look for information on your sport and everything outside of the lines?\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: I think there will always be the big industries. There will always be the Foxs and ESPNs, but I think there has been a shift where you don`t need the big machine behind you to be heard, to have a platform to go viral, or to be a trusted voice in the space, and that`s what we take, you know, very serious, is being a trusted voice.\par \par \uc2 You know, with the list of names we`ve had, you know, we put that list up against any show across the board, and I think it just goes to show that, you know, we are trusted, we`re real, and we`re understood, and we give people an opportunity to speak their own piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And, Matt, that`s where I was going about being a trusted voice. The fact that the vice president came on your show was like, "Whoa."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clearly she trusts you.\par \par \uc2 So, and you`ve done practically everybody, so as you sit here today, who are you trying to get?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Oh, for me--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I would think it`s a phone call.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: For me, Michael Jordan, or Denzel Washington. Some guys that I`ve admired, been admiring for a long time. And just like everybody on our show, we`re fans of, so that`s why we don`t mind giving them their flowers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Michael Jordan is great, yeah.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: You know, we want to know them as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, you know what? I know somebody that knows both of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, we`ve been trying to get Michael Jordan. It`s been a hard no. I bet you could get him.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: I just think the ask is a little easier now with the VP under our belt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: So, you know, obviously, current people -- Caitlin Clark, Angel Reese, Gayle, we wanted you to come on the show.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATT BARNES: You know, MJ, LeBron, there`s still a list out there, Dr. Dre, but, you know, we just enjoy having high-level conversations with people that as Stacks said that we`re fans of.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah. Well, listen, we`re fans of y`all. We appreciate you pulling up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congrats on all the success.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congrats, it`s a beautiful book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "All The Smoke"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you mind if I hold the book?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- it`s on sale now. Make sure you go get it. Yeah, beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Comedian c is here. Ahead, we`ll hear about Season 2 of her \uc2 CBS\uc2  show "After Midnight." I love this show and her stand-up tour. I haven`t seen it, but I`m sure it`s good.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are very excited to talk with comedian Taylor Tomlinson, host of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  late-night show "After Midnight."\par \par \uc2 Last year, she headlined the seventh highest grossing comedy tour according to Billboard, it ain`t bad and she is on the road again with her "Save Me" tour Tomlinson is also back on TV with the second season of "After Midnight." Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "After Midnight" \uc2 CBS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Taylor Tomlinson`s with us now. I`m a big fan of the show. I think it`s so self-deprecating, self-referential and also brings in all this stuff I wouldn`t see otherwise and you`re funny about it constantly.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: Thank you so much. Yeah, we try really hard to be self- aware ---\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: Self-deprecating so that means a lot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So found your rhythm, you feel good?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: I think so. Yeah, I think so. What I love so much is that \uc2 CBS\uc2  really encouraged us to experiment which is the best thing about late night, I think that space, you`re able to try new things and be weird. And our show`s very weird.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very weird. It`s very weird. We were just talking about the scoring system.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I was saying, I don`t understand the scoring system. I said, I can`t wait for you to come and explain it to me. And I shouldn`t?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: No, no, no, no. It`s absolute nonsense. I`m giving away the points.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay. You`re doing that.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: And if the points meant anything, they wouldn`t trust me with them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: It`s just me with a button.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So that`s part of the bit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s set up like a game show.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there`s categories and you have comedians. And then how much is it ad-libbed and riffed and how much is it sort of like you`re doing a skit?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: Well, we have an amazing writers` room that prep the guests. So they have, like, an arsenal of jokes if they want them. But also, we have so many comedians that come in that just write their own jokes.\par \par \uc2 And I think people go in feeling so prepared that they feel ready to riff and, like, do stuff off the cuff as well. So it`s a real combination.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would think it would be hard, Taylor, working with comedians. You`re a comedian, too. Maybe it makes it easier.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: It`s hard working with myself.\par \par \uc2 I am a nightmare --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would think that that would be hard `cause you never know where it`s going to go. That`s part of the fun, too, of watching it.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: Yes, that`s what I love about -- I mean, stand-up comedians don`t get a chance to play around with each other. Like, when we have improvisers on the show --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: They just crush it because they`re used to that, like, yes, and play around, you know, mess with each other. But stand-ups don`t get that opportunity very often. It`s usually just us on stage alone.\par \par \uc2 So that`s what I love about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So who`s your dream guest?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: I would love to get Conan on the show. I think he`d be great. I just think he`s, like, one of the funniest people on and off stage and screen. So I`d love to get him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How`s tour going?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: Tour`s going great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why is it called "Save Me"?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: It`s called "Save Me" because I grew up in church, and so I have a lot to say about that, both positive and the challenges of growing up that way. And this hour is a lot about that. Like, I started in churches. I had a lot to sort of work through and deconstruct over the years. And I wanted people coming to the shows, the live shows, to know that it was a lot about that. It`s also about coming out and sexuality and whether or not to have kids.\par \par \uc2 So there`s other stuff as well, but a good portion of it is that. And so I wanted to make that clear to people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you picked up a prop, right?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: Yes, we did pick up a prop. So I do this section at the end of the show now, which I`m really excited about. There`s an app called Community that allows fans to text you through an app on your phone. So it`s not your personal phone number, because that would be crazy.\par \par \uc2 But I have a list of questions on screen and in a pamphlet at the show that people can text in their answers to. Because I love doing crowd work on the road. But you end up only being able to hear people who were able to afford the first few rows of seats.\par \par \uc2 So this is a way for me to get to know people all over this, like, 3,000- seat crowd. So we found a pew.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A pew? That`s the prop.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: It`s a sit-in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very churchy. There it is.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Tomlinson, thank you. "After Midnight airs weeknights" 12:37 AM. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241009eka900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 VP Candidates Square Off in Debate; Voters React to VP Debate; \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  on Takeaways from VP Debate; Iran Launches Missile Attack on Israel; US Reacts to Iran Missile Attack on Israel; Dockworkers Strike Impact; New Diddy Lawsuits; \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  Turns 100; DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  Weighs in on VP Debate; Remembering John Amos; Rascal Flatts Reunion; Searching For Survivors; Vance and Walz Comparison; VP Candidates Square Off in Debate; Ed O`Keefe on Takeaways From VP Debate; Politics In The Peach State; "Barefoot Contessa" Live; NY Liberty Near WNBA Finals Return; Gayle "The Queen" Would Win Survivor; Cloned Sheep: A Baaaad Idea?; A Focus on Fertility; "We Are Free, You & Me", \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Jericka Duncan, John  Dickerson, Ed O`Keefe, Kris Van Cleave, Chris Livesay, Weijia Jiang, Lilia  Luciano \par \uc2 14,773 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets on a Wednesday, hump day, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, but we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN) (2024 vice presidential nominee): So the rhetoric is good. Much of what the senator said right there, I`m in agreement with him on this.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH) (2024 vice presidential nominee): And I think that Governor Walz and I actually probably agree that we need to do better on this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The vice presidential nominees strike a respectful tone in their debate while they sharply disagree on policy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Israel vows to hit back after Iran launches its largest ever missile attack against the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2  (\uc2 US State Department\uc2  spokesperson): We have been clear that there must be consequences for this attack.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A massive port workers strike enters its second day, threatening major damage to the US economy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Those greedy companies are making billions of dollars and they don`t want to share.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Tributes are pouring in for actor, John Amos, his family revealing he died from natural causes back on August 21st.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): The most vice important night of the entire campaign.\par \par \uc2 The vice-excitement was palpable. These two men were both a heartbeat away from being interesting this evening.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I believe Senator Vance wants to solve this.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): I agree with you. I think you want to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I agree with a lot of what Senator Vance said.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): I actually agree with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA ("The Daily Show"): Wow. Maybe you two should run together. Yes, maybe a date?\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA: It seems like you have a lot in common. Hello, \uc2 CBS\uc2 , it`s Tim. We`re going to share an Uber home.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It almost did feel like they could share an Uber home.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There was decorum.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We call it civility.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, civility. I like civility.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s where we`re going to begin. As Senator Vance and Governor Walz held their debate, we got a fresh reminder that whoever wins the presidential election is going to be tested by multiple crises here and abroad.\par \par \uc2 Just in the last 24 hours, Iran unleashed its largest missile barrage ever on Israel in response to Israeli strikes targeting \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders.\par \par \uc2 And in the United States, searchers found dozens more victims of Hurricane Helene, a storm that has left thousands cut off from food, water and electricity.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Vlad it is against that backdrop that Senator Vance and Governor Walz offered very different views of American leadership last night at the only vice presidential debate of this election.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News poll shows viewers split evenly on who won, but most did really like the tone. Eighty-eight percent thought it was generally positive. Ed O`Keefe was in the spin room for the debate here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 , and right now he joins us in the morning.\par \par \uc2 Long night for you, too, Ed. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Sure was, Gayle. But it was worth it, because for 90 minutes, the vice presidential nominees made their case, and while it was a mostly civil affair, their differences were still on full display.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The vice presidential debate began with a handshake, but then just hours after Iran fired missiles into Israel, the candidate sparred over national security.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  recognized that for people to fear the United States, you needed peace through strength.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Iran is closer to a nuclear weapon than they were before because of Donald Trump`s fickle leadership.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On domestic issues, the economy consistently ranks as a top concern for voters, and both men made their pitch.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has said to do the things she wants to do, we`ll just ask the wealthiest to pay their fair share. When you do that, our system works best.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Honestly, Tim, I think you`ve got a tough job here, because you`ve got to play Whack-a-Mole. You`ve got to pretend that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t deliver rising take home pay, which of course he did. You`ve got to pretend that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t deliver lower inflation, which, of course, he did.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The future of abortion services has been a major focus for Democrats, but Republican, JD Vance shared a personal connection to the issue.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): I grew up in a working class family in a neighborhood where I knew a lot of young women who had unplanned pregnancies and decided to terminate those pregnancies because they feel like they didn`t have any other options. And you know, I -- one of them is actually very dear to me, and I know she`s watching tonight, and I love you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And made this admission.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): My party, we`ve got to do so much better of a job at earning the American people`s trust back on this issue where they frankly, just don`t trust us.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Around the same time, in a social media post, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said for the first time he would veto a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 But Democrat \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  criticized Republican arguments that the issue should be decided state by state.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): The fact of the matter is, how can we as a nation say that your life and your rights as basic as the right to control your own body is determined on geography?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Walz also got personal when asked about gun control.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): All the parents watching tonight, this is your biggest nightmare. Look, I`ve got a -- I`ve got a 17-year-old and -- and he witnessed a shooting at a community center playing volleyball. Those things don`t leave you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Tim, first of all, I didn`t know that your 17-year- old witnessed a shooting, and I`m sorry about that and I --\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Christ, have mercy. It is -- it is awful.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And while the debate was overwhelmingly civil --\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Well, first of all, Tim just said something that I agree with.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I agree with a lot of what Senator Vance said.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It ended on the divisive issue of what might happen later this year.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I will tell you this, that when this is over, we need to shake hands, this election, and the winner needs to be the winner. This has got to stop. It`s tearing our country apart.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): It`s really rich for Democratic leaders to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a unique threat to democracy when he peacefully gave over power on January the 20th, as we have done for 250 years in this country.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But then Walz pressed Vance on Trump`s decision not to accept his loss to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Did he lose the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Tim, I`m focused on the future.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): That is a damning non-answer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Of course, Trump had urged supporters to challenge the results and then pressed then Vice President Pence not to certify the Electoral College results.\par \par \uc2 Also last night, Vice President Harris reiterated her call for a second presidential debate. Trump repeated that he is "not looking to do it again" -- guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Ed, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan watched last night`s debate in a very interesting place, Grand Rapids, Michigan. This is one of those towns that voted for Trump in 2016 then flipped over to Biden in 2020. Everyone is battling for it once again.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you. Good morning to everyone.\par \par \uc2 It is a very competitive district here in Kent County, Michigan. About 350,000 people voted in the last presidential election. But following last night`s debate, we asked the voters, who do they think won? They said JD Vance. Most of them said that he won this debate.\par \par \uc2 But take politics out of this for just a moment. They said they were most impressed with the civility that we saw on full display last night. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA LEDESMA (Harris voter): This debate that I saw made me happy that again, the politics is going back to us, treating each other with respect.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think that my biggest takeaway from Vance is that he is able to articulate himself, his feelings, his policies, in a respectful way. That`s one of the things that I dislike about Trump.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY PANIWOZIK (Trump voter): They both complimented each other when they needed to, and gave respect to each other. They got along as two gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Another issue that was top of mind for those voters was gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELLI BERKEY (Undecided voter): They both agreed that they need that something needed to be done, but there really wasn`t a plan to do, to fix anything. The only thing that I heard was Vance saying that he wanted more security in the schools.\par \par \uc2 If someone were to have access to a gun in my kid`s school, I have no idea what I would do.\par \par \uc2 ARICK DAVIS (Harris voter): I got asked more questions when I was getting a dog from the shelter than when I was getting my gun. And we`re just now getting to a point where it seems like maybe there`s going to be like, actually, like, real support on both sides to do like, basic gun safety things.\par \par \uc2 But I feel like the Republican Party has not prioritized or taken like, some of these basic common sense gun things, like seriously.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Before the debate started, two of the voters said that they were going to vote for Trump. Two said they would vote for Harris. Two said they were undecided, but by the end of it, those two undecided said they would vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 So clearly, you know, these voters didn`t see eye to eye on all the issues, but there was one thing that they said they do all agree on. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Is democracy on the line in this election, Bill?\par \par \uc2 BILL (Voter): Yes, sure is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Arick, why are you laughing?\par \par \uc2 ARICK DAVIS: Because it absolutely -- it just absolutely is. And I had to catch myself, but it`s like, oh my God, is it? Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Holly?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY PANIWOZIK: Same. I feel the same way. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA LEDESMA: Yes, definitely.\par \par \uc2 MAN: More than ever in my life. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: "More than ever in your life?"\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Elli?\par \par \uc2 ELLI BERKEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Nice discussion that we had there. We`re going to put the full interview eventually on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . So check it out.\par \par \uc2 With 34 days left, though, all of these voters said that they want to see another debate, that they wouldn`t mind, I should say. Some of them said that they felt like they could get a little bit more clarity if they could see one more debate between Harris and Trump -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really interesting stuff, Jericka. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to our wisest of all wise heads of all things political --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- our chief political analyst, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . He also hosts "The Daily Report" with \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : You`ve got to keep the bar low, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our burbling fountain of all wise things. Please tell us. What were your takeaways last night?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, it was a kind of a, you know, basically everything that that`s been reported so far, it was civil. It was -- you know, and that`s notable mostly from JD Vance, because you look at who`s running at the top of the ticket, so it`s like you work for a boss who comes in, in cutoff jeans every day, and then guy comes in wearing a suit and tie, and you think, wow, it`s like the old days. It`s like it used to be, you know, where people believed in the norms of civility, they debated politics.\par \par \uc2 That isn`t to say that there wasn`t a lot of traditional stuff that irritates people about politics, but it was so much different than what we`ve heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out on the trail. So that was the biggest thing, I think, probably though it kind of passes through and we move back into the race. That doesn`t really change anything too much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, to me, one of the most talked about moments of the night was January 6th and the difference of opinion between the two.\par \par \uc2 JD Vance seemed to say, look, I want to move forward. Stop looking back, accusing the media like we always bring it up all the time.\par \par \uc2 Can you still say that January 6 is in the past?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes. JD Vance said he`s focused on the future. So a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 One, when you spend a lot of the debate talking about how great things were in the Trump years, you can`t then say, wait, but I`m focused on the future. You can`t just remember the stuff you say is the good stuff.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is, you know, who`s focused on this a lot and built his whole campaign around it? It is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So he`s quite focused on this.\par \par \uc2 But then there are two reasons I think this is really important, that that Senator Vance couldn`t give a simple yes, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the election. Simplest question in the world. Still can`t do it. And we interviewed Senator Tom Cotton, Republican from Arkansas, couldn`t do it either.\par \par \uc2 This is why it matters, two things. When you talk to national security experts who are worried about violence around the next election, they say the biggest thing fueling the possibility for violence around the next election is the lie that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the last one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So that fuel is out there in real time now, and then when you look at the answers from Vance and Senator Cotton, democracy exists by common agreement. Everybody agrees that elections matter and that the votes should be counted and that the power given to the people who win the election then allows you to make laws, and everybody will agree to that. If that agreement doesn`t exist, you have violence.\par \par \uc2 Well, that agreement only exists if everybody chooses to agree to it. So when you see Senator Vance and Senator Cotton making excuses, not saying easily, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost, they are creating room for the lie to keep living.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : This is not a small -- this is not like a debate over tax cuts. This is about a fundamental idea of whether 81 million people should be disenfranchised because a guy didn`t like that he lost.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Who said that? Not me, but the Republican leaders in the House and Senate at the time who said the lie from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  led to the violence. That`s real and alive right now. It`s not in the past.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. That`s what worries me. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, John.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you call him? A burbling --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A burbling fountain of wisdom?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a programming note for you, too. "60 Minutes" will air a primetime election special, Monday, October 7th, at eight o`clock.\par \par \uc2 For more than half a century, you should know that "60 Minutes" has invited the Democratic and the Republican tickets to appear on its broadcast as Americans head to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris will speak with correspondent Bill Whitaker. After initially accepting "60 Minutes" offer for an interview with Scott Pelley, former President Trump`s campaign has now decided not to participate.\par \par \uc2 We want you to know the original "60 Minutes" invitation to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still stands.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s turn to our other top story.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from Iran`s largest ever missile attack on Israel. Iran fired nearly 200 missiles at targets inside Israel last night, saying it was in retaliation for its assassination of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders. Israeli officials say the bombardment caused only minor damage.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay has more from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Some 180 missiles tear through the night sky as Israel shoots, most of them down, shrapnel falling like embers, some snuck through Israel`s defenses and falling debris killed a man in the occupied West Bank and lightly injured several others.\par \par \uc2 From the Western Wall in Jerusalem to Tel Aviv, the entire population was told to move into bomb shelters, including us.\par \par \uc2 You can see everybody is just piled into this parking garage that`s doubling as a bomb shelter.\par \par \uc2 There are children here. There`s dogs here.\par \par \uc2 Above ground, our colleague Elizabeth Palmer watched the onslaught from a balcony.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The alarms have sounded again for the second time this evening for people to take shelter. We`ve had one volley of Iranian missiles. There`s a second one coming in now, and you can see flashes of light in the sky where they`re being blown up in flight by Israel`s interceptor system.\par \par \uc2 (REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The Israeli military says the attack will have consequences, and that Israel will act at a time and place of its choosing.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the damage was still being assessed.\par \par \uc2 A ballistic missile left a massive crater in this residential area outside Tel Aviv, just smashing buses and cars like this one and leaving twisted metal.\par \par \uc2 Violence brought to the doorstep of Israel`s largest city against the backdrop of a widening war. Israel has launched what it calls a limited ground offensive into Southern Lebanon, targeting \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rocket positions and uncovering a network of tunnels and stashed weapons.\par \par \uc2 The fighting here has reached an intensity not seen since the last Israel- Lebanon War in 2006 when Israel launched a full ground invasion.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Chris Livesay, Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two \uc2 US Navy\uc2  ships in the Middle East shot down some of those Iranian missiles last night. President Biden`s National Security Adviser calls this attack a significant escalation.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang change at the White House with more on that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to everybody. You`re absolutely right, and now the US vows severe consequences for Iran, but what those are remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was watching the strikes from the Situation Room alongside Vice President Harris and his National Security team. The Pentagon says it was Biden`s order that those two \uc2 US Navy\uc2  destroyers fired roughly a dozen interceptors to take down Iranian missiles.\par \par \uc2 After the attack, both presidential candidates weighed in. Harris said she unequivocally condemned the attack and said the US would continue to work with allies to hold Iran accountable. Former President Trump declared the world has been "unraveling" since he left office and accused Biden and Harris of leading the US to the brink of World War III.\par \par \uc2 Biden has pushed for a ceasefire in Lebanon to avoid a wider conflict, but he stresses the US is fully supportive of Israel -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it, Weijia. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another issue that could have a big effect on the 2024 election, the dockworkers` strike, something people weren`t talking about two weeks ago. Now, it`s in its second day.\par \par \uc2 The economy was already top of mind, and it could become an even bigger issue for voters because of these images on your screen.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has everything you need to know.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: For a second day, 14 ports from Boston to Houston shut down after dockworkers walked off the job. Those ports handle about 80 percent of the coffee, tea, spirits and beverage imports, as well as about 60 percent of medical and surgical instruments coming into the US. Also halted, about 76 percent of vehicle exports.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mondi is among at least 25,000 union workers on strike demanding better wages and job protections against automation.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MONDI (Union worker on strike): We`re looking ahead into the future, going to work now at the risk of losing our jobs five or 10 years down the road is very shortsighted.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Morton Williams grocery stores in New York City say they stocked up on extra perishables to buy time.\par \par \uc2 MARC GOLDMAN (Morton Williams Supermarkets produce director): If it goes a few days, there won`t be much of an effect. If it goes weeks, a month or more, there could be a very big effect. We`ll definitely have higher prices, and we might have empty shelves.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The strike could cost the economy up to $5 billion a day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Apparel, footwear, consumer electronics, home goods, decorations, food products. You know, anything you can think of is coming through these ports, so they are vital to the economy, and it is critical that we get them back up and running.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Still, New York`s governor, again, urged people not to rush the stores.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): It`s not time to be panic buying groceries or other products.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kris Van Cleave reporting for us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in a moment, dozens of new sexual misconduct allegations against \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2 , and we`re going to hear from a lawyer who says he personally represents 120 victims, but first, 7:18, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, TV`s Barefoot Contessa, Ina Garten talks with us about her candid new memoir about her life`s highs and lows.\par \par \uc2 And our series "Three Meals" goes to the battleground state of Georgia. Tony asked voters what it would take for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to get their support.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Verizon.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  is facing another long list of sexual misconduct allegations. He is in jail awaiting trials on charges of trafficking women for sex parties. He has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 An attorney in Houston says he represents 120 people who say Combs abused them as well. The allegations are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY BUZBEE (Attorney for Combs` accusers): Her youngest victim at the time of the occurrence was nine years old. We have an individual who was 14 years old. We have one who is 15.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-five of the 120 individuals who are plaintiffs in these cases were minors at the time of the acts complained of.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Combs` lawyer said he denies any claim that he sexually abused anyone, including minors.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we ride with the National Guard troops in North Carolina searching for hurricane survivors.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CBS News exclusive video shows former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  -- there he is -- and his family celebrating his 100th birthday, yesterday. Long, long life.\par \par \uc2 Four military jets flew over his hometown of Plains, Georgia. Carter is the first US president to live a full century.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He has been in hospice care, as you know, at home for the past 19 months. He keeps defying all the expectations.\par \par \uc2 According to his son, Chip, Carter`s next move, his next goal is to make it to Election Day so that he can vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is super interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is very moving.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I said yesterday how \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  likes to win. Somebody who profiled him once told me that when he was entered into hospice care, his goal was to break the record for longest stay in hospice care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He just wants to keep he wants to see what happens. He cares about this planet and the people of the United States.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the people care about him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People care about him. Exactly. I think he`s going to make it, too. I think he`s going to be able to do that goal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because he`s never not hit his goals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, President Biden goes to North and South Carolina today to inspect the terrible, horrific damage from Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 Officials now say more than 160 people have been found in six states, from Florida to Virginia. North Carolina`s National Guard is helping many of the survivors.\par \par \uc2 And Lilia Luciano shows us how they`re carrying out that mission.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In a helicopter high above North Carolina`s remote Blue Ridge Mountains, Tuesday, CBS News rode with first responders as they combed the devastated landscape, searching for survivors.\par \par \uc2 We`re embedded with the state`s National Guard, following several missions. Some are delivering supplies, some are able to land and bring people to safety, and some have to hoist survivors.\par \par \uc2 We saw them in action over the small town of Burnsville, where the National Guard rescued seven people.\par \par \uc2 All right, the clear signal was given, the person is now being hoisted into the helicopter slowly.\par \par \uc2 And 60 miles south in Hendersonville, three survivors waved from the ground, grateful as the helicopter descended to bring them in.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling right now?\par \par \uc2 MARIYA KARPENKO (Rescued from Hendersonville, North Carolina): Grateful to be alive. Grateful that everyone is safe.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But not all rescues have had a positive outcome.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Helene is now blamed for more than 160 deaths across six states.\par \par \uc2 And in the area where you were, were people safe? Are people injured?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Everybody is safe. Everybody is safe and healthy. They desperately need fuel, things to start rebuilding the road so they can get more food and water in there.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Damaged roadways, heavy debris and unreliable phone service has made it particularly hard to reach some of the mountain communities her\par \par \uc2 MAN: There`s still hundreds of people that were getting reports are stranded.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And hundreds of thousands remain without power and running water.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D-NC): If you need help, we are working around the clock to reach you. We`re working together to open more major routes to push supplies forward.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And Governor Cooper added that there`s now about 1,100 people who are housed in 29 shelters across the state -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 People all over the US are reaching out to the victims of Helene with offers of help. You can go to CBSMornings.com to learn what you can do or check out CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  or X.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in just a moment, DNC chair, \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , we`ll talk about last night`s vice presidential debate and what`s next in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Last night`s vice presidential debate, likely the last debate before the presidential election, Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  were largely civil to one another, but there were stark contrasts between their views on everything from immigration to the economy and who is the larger threat to American democracy.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  for more on the debate and his reaction.\par \par \uc2 It was civil. It was cordial.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2  (\uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  chairman): It was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But there is daylight between the two parties.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : A lot of daylight.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , who I`ve known for almost 20 years now, was definitely Minnesota Nice last night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a little wobbly in the beginning, though, Jaime, were you worried?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : But, you know, listen, on any debate stage like that, when you get on for the first time, you can -- you can have the butterflies.\par \par \uc2 But when it really counted, when he really started getting into the issues, we saw the differences between him and JD Vance. And JD Vance was as slick as an eel last night.\par \par \uc2 Line -- every time he opened his mouth, a lie fell out. I mean, on abortion, on climate change, on all of these issues, gun violence, right?\par \par \uc2 But what was really, really important? There are two moments that stick out for me. The first moment is on the discussion on abortion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : And the governor basically saying, you know, for a woman to have control of her body should not have to be dependent on which state she lives in. Right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Men can control their own bodies. It doesn`t matter -- the three of us can do whatever we want with our bodies. But why can`t Gayle? Why can`t women have complete control over their bodies?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Why should they have to ask \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or some right-wing Supreme Court in order to do so?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Jaime, we can sit here and talk about the policy differences --\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- and political scientists think about , strategists too. But when I go out and talk to voters, a lot of people are just picking up on tone, style, presentation, and you`re right, JD Vance was a smoother operator here.\par \par \uc2 JD Vance or \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  came in, he was the most liked candidate, arguably on any ticket, most favorable. But do you think he stuck the landing on that?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Well, listen, when looking at the \uc2 Google\uc2  Trends last night, they weren`t looking at who was smoother on the debate stage? They were looking at abortion.\par \par \uc2 It was the number one searched topic every, every state, all 50 states, number one searched topic, because that is what really matters to people. Can you control your own bodies? Do you have freedom in this country?\par \par \uc2 There`s only one party that is fighting for the freedom of all Americans, and that`s the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about the campaign. I expect you to say it`s going well.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think she`s going to win. I expect you to say that, but recent polling shows that there`s a disconnect with many Black men. I actually know some of them that say they`re going to go for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Is the party doing anything to reengage that part of the electorate?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Well, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And are you worried about it?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : I`m not worried about it. But you know, you always worry about every election, but you make sure you`re not taking anybody for granted. You make sure that you`re talking to people about the issues that are important.\par \par \uc2 For the last three-and-a-half years, I`ve been going crisscrossing this country, having discussions with Black men, with Latino men about the issues that are important to them, about security, making sure that they can protect their families, economic security, but also physical security.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What about voter suppression? Many people are worried about that.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Voter suppression is a huge issue and we should worry about it, because it`s something part of the tactics that the Republican Party has.\par \par \uc2 I, as Democratic Party chair want everybody to vote, and I want them to vote Democratic, but I believe that fundamentally everyone should have the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 We have built, at the DNC, the largest voter protection program that we`ve ever had in our history, because we know part of the tactics, we see it in Georgia, we see it in a lot of other states, of the Republican Party to put up barriers to make it more difficult for people to vote.\par \par \uc2 If you -- and I want to say to -- I don`t know where the camera is, but --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All over.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : All over, all over.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just look at Vlad.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : But I want folks to understand this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : I don`t care if you`re a Democrat, Republican, or Independent. You run into any trouble at the ballot box, you go to your precinct and they say that you can`t vote, text VOTE to 70888. We have lawyers on the ready.\par \par \uc2 And again, I don`t care your party affiliation. Every eligible American should have their right to vote count.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jaime, let me just ask you. Vance was factchecked on the mistruths he was spreading about Haitian immigrants eating pets in Springfield, Ohio. Do you think the damage, though, has already been done with that type of rhetoric, and how do you in the last couple of weeks of this race combat that?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Well, it`s racist, it`s bigoted. Think about this, to lie on people who are your constituents, these people are legal in Ohio, and to lie about this? I don`t think there`s a bigger -- a bigger slight than you can put on somebody that is supposedly a public servant.\par \par \uc2 And Vance stood on that stage and lied about those people and there are consequences to that. When little kids have to go to school and are scared to go to school because of bomb threats, when businesses have to shutter because, you know, they`re scared of people attacking them and the Republican mayor said, that`s a lie. The Republican governor said that`s a lie, said Senator, stop saying it and he continued to do that.\par \par \uc2 And looked in the camera and said, you know what? Yes, I`m lying. I`m trying to make a point. No, we don`t do that in America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , thank you very much for coming by.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have Governor Walz come by as well. I think that`s imperative, which Vance has been on TV a lot. We`d love to see the governor as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He doesn`t need to be nervous. He can come by.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come on by.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to have the stories you`ll talking about and "What to Watch." Right now, though, it is 7:43, time to check a local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a lot going on in our eight o`clock hour today, including the Barefoot Contessa. That`s author Ina Garten, who`s known for her recipes that most people she says can make. Oh, we will see.\par \par \uc2 Now, she is sharing some very personal stories in a new memoir about her life, and she would join us in the studio to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our series, "Three Meals" takes us to the battleground state of Georgia. Tony hits the road for some good old southern cooking and great conversation with voters about the election. Two of my favorite things. Good food and good conversation.\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour, but first, Klaus and I are heading over to the table --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Head on over. You`re walking again. I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- where Vlad and Tony have already --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Georgia is my fallback state by the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- taken up residence.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, your fallback state from --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The food is great. It`s affordable. You can buy a home. You`re going to see it.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All night long.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Who doesn`t love this song?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all love this song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rascal Flatts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we`re going to be talking about Rascal Flatts --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love Rascal Flatts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But I want to begin by paying tribute to a 1970s, 1980s, 1990s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- forever TV icon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ah, yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Roots"/Wolfer Productions)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the most iconic roles in television, played by that man, John Amos, famous for that role in "Roots," "Good Times."\par \par \uc2 He has died at the age of 84. His family revealed that he died of natural causes back in August, but let`s just break down this incredible career.\par \par \uc2 He earned an Emmy nomination for his powerful portrayal of the adult Kunta Kinte in the groundbreaking 1977 miniseries. You can go on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and find clips of him if you haven`t watched the entire thing, the scenes when Fiddler dies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And he expresses that Fiddler is finally free, one of my favorite scenes.\par \par \uc2 His career spanned more than five decades. He broke barriers though first as James Evans, Sr. on "Good Times."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Good Times"/Tandem Productions)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of the first programs showing a two-parent Black family on television.\par \par \uc2 He advocated for positive African American representation in entertainment throughout his career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Roots"/Wolfer Productions)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: James Evans, Sr. represented something that most people have never seen on television before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A father who was very involved in the life of his kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it changed the narrative of what people thought a Black family in America was at the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A Black family was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And he wasn`t just an actor on that show. He`s talked about how the all-White writing team would have scripts, and then he`d have disagreements, and he would say,. "How long you been Black?"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 And finally --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kind of shuts down the conversation, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`ve been watching Mary Tyler Moore. He played Gordy -- of course cast her on that show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I forgot about that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Remember about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And King Jaffe Jafar -- James Earl Jones just died a few weeks ago, and now, Mr. McDowell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. We saw --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Big Mick.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was surprised here he died in August.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He died in August.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m glad they`re letting us know, but it was sad to hear that news yesterday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He will be missed. What an icon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s talk about Rascal Flatts reuniting to mark its 25th anniversary. The group`s "Life is a Highway" Tour will hit 21 cities next year.\par \par \uc2 Rascal Flatts had planned a farewell tour in early 2020, but the COVID pandemic sidelined it. Band members said they took time to focus on themselves, and now they`re back together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tickets will go on sale Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay. Yay. Yay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, every year, we highlight Fat Bear Week, a head-to-head competition that celebrates Alaskan bears preparing to hibernate.\par \par \uc2 This year, the start of online voting was delayed due to a different head- to-head battle, one with life and death consequences. Horrified fans watched a live stream video as a male bear known as Number 469, killed a female bear called 402. The fight happened in the waters of Alaska`s Brooks River.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Katmai National Park and Preserve says, "National parks like Katmai protect not only the wonders of nature, but also the harsh realities." The statement said, "Each bear seen on the webcams is competing with others to survive."\par \par \uc2 So online voting begins today and goes through next Tuesday. Like you said before, Tony, what are bears?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re wild animals.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are wild.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They appear to be wild animals.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They be bearing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Were you watching the webcam live?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t think I would be able to watch that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, \uc2 NFL\uc2  star, Colin Kaepernick and his wife, TV and radio host, Nessa Diab will be in studio talking about a new children`s book.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN (Barefoot Contessa): Perfect. Browned on that side, you can tell if the chicken bounces back, it`s perfectly done. Onto the sheet pan.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, I`m hungry. That`s Ina Garten on her hit TV show, "Barefoot Contessa."\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ll talk with her about her new memoir and the secret to developing a successful and tasty recipe.\par \par \uc2 Nam.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Tony takes a road trip for our "Three Meals" series, He ate well, we like when that happens. And he talked about what matters to voters in the Great State of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Speaking of eating well, author and chef, Ina Garten is here. She`s known as the Barefoot Contessa. And her new memoir, she writes about overcoming the sour notes in her past to build a very sweet present.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab will also be here first on CBS MORNINGS. Their new kids` book is all about activism and the importance of education.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Something they know about, but first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): The winner needs to be the winner. This has got to stop. It`s tearing our country apart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The vice presidential nominees clash over former president Trump`s actions after the 2020 election at their only debate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): It`s really rich for democratic leaders to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a unique threat to democracy when he peacefully gave over power on January the 20th.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Iran launches its largest ever missile attack on Israel, pushing the Middle East closer to all out war.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: CBS News flies with the North Carolina National Guard during a mission to rescue survivors stranded after Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 MARIYA KARPENKO: Grateful to be alive. Grateful that everyone is safe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Stephen Colbert highlights the differences between JD Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: It was the ultimate showdown between two very different Midwestern vibes. Hillbilly versus Minnesota nice. Attack dog versus folk hero. America`s dad versus America`s dad. Could you come pick me up? JD Vance just walked in here. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A lot of material for the late night comics. A lot of material.\par \par \uc2 So let`s begin with that debate last night with what`s likely the last debate before election day. Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  clashed on policy and about the character required to lead the country. But the exchanges were largely civil.\par \par \uc2 On gun violence, for example, they`re miles apart on how to address the issue, but they agreed that it`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): We know, unfortunately, that a lot of kids are going to experience this terrible epidemic of gun violence. And of course, our hearts go out to the families that are affected by this terrible stuff. And we do have to do better.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I 100% believe that Senator Vance hates it when these kids, it`s abhorrent and it breaks your heart. I agree with that. But that`s not far enough.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ed O`Keefe was there watching the debate and he`s here now with us. Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Good morning, how are you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You live to tell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So it`s interesting, the CBS News poll found 88% of debate watchers said it was generally positive. And that sort of gives me a little insight into this world where people say that, oh, they like to see the WWE aspect of politics. They don`t, if you believe this kind of polling.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Yeah, and look, we fully anticipated that this could become a slugfest. Both sides had channeled that they were prepared for that. But instead it became like two Midwestern dads cracking a beer in the middle of the street between their houses discussing the finer details of gun control policy.\par \par \uc2 And that`s kind of what, frankly, after so many years of acrimony, I think you`re right. A lot of Americans were kind of okay with that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I took solace in it.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My blood pressure was falling. Key issue, they did get into abortion. \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  from the DNC was here saying that was a big issue for them. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tweeted or truthed on his platform during the debate and he said he would veto a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s something new from him. And JD Vance also tried to talk about this issue in a new way. What stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Well, what he was saying is, while we don`t necessarily depart from our current viewpoints on the idea that this should be a state by state concern, we as Republicans have to do a better job of discussing this issue and embracing and appreciating the concern and the risks that women face.\par \par \uc2 That, to me, was a white flag that says, yeah, we`re not winning on this issue anymore. We have the potential to lose a lot of voters over this. Sorry, we`re going to try to do better in at least discussing it.\par \par \uc2 But the position didn`t change. State by state, Roe v. Wade is gone. Let the states decide. The bigger deal to me was that he now, on social media at least, and let`s see if he ever verbalizes it, says that he would veto an abortion ban. The reason why, and we`ve discussed this before, if that`s now his position, go ask his evangelical base what they think of that and whether there`s any potential they stay home or become less enthusiastic about supporting him and Republican congressional candidates that might agree.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Super interesting. I can`t get that image out of my mind of the two dads cracking a beer and talking to his neighbors, but then what really matters is when they go back into their own houses and they shut the door, what do they say about one another?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why I ought to --\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know, they like each other. Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in our series, "Three Meals" I hit the road in the battleground state of Georgia to talk with voters about the presidential candidates and the issues. And I also enjoy some southern cooking and a lot of sweet tea, too much in fact.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8.04, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player Colin Kaepernick and his wife Nessa Diab share their new children`s book. This is their first TV interview together and we`re looking forward to this.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every week until the election, we are driving through key states to talk with voters for our series "Three Meals." And this morning, I go to Georgia, where voters backed a Republican in every single presidential race dating all the way back to 1996 until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  edged out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But the margin of victory there essentially amounted to an overflow crowd at a minor league baseball game. I played in front of bigger crowds in this margin. The latest CBS News poll shows Trump and Vice President Harris, it is very close. They are in a statistical tie in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Top issues in that poll, the economy and rising prices. But as I found out, the people of Georgia have a whole lot of other stuff on their minds.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Peaches")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t we all, Justin Bieber? But what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  most want out of the peach state.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I`m thrilled to be back in the great state of Georgia. I love Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is votes.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): Good evening, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A Harris victory here depends on running up the margins in and around Metro Atlanta, where the population is booming. And starting the day over coffee at West Egg Cafe in the city`s West Side neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We just moved to Georgia from California.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We learned a lot of newcomers like Marion and Brian Smith (sp?), they tend to vote blue.\par \par \uc2 Would \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  be better for you economically?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN SMITH (Georgia voter): I wouldn`t vote for him even if, you know, if it meant another hundred grand in my pocket.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Hello, Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won this state in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So you`re voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 There are warning lights for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ISAAC SIMPSON (Georgia voter): I feel like he aligns more with America and American values than she does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Isaac Simpson (sp?) explained those Trump values while I made some gains on a delicious potato biscuit and omelet.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC SIMPSON: I just know that once you start doing away with things like the second amendment and altering it to your own, what you think is sensible, then it`s a problem because then who`s the arbiter of that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yet over by the counter.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WALKER (Georgia voter): A lot more of the country became more hopeful once Kamala became a nominee.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some counter points from Christopher Walker (sp?).\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WALKER: Democrats are focused on actual issues that are of concern to Americans and solving the systemic problems that we all face.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Afterward, we hit the road and made some U-turns along the way.\par \par \uc2 Heading into the suburbs of Forsyth County.\par \par \uc2 Part of a Republican stronghold that the Trump campaign is counting on. And sure enough, as we settled into the station house off US 19 for lunch.\par \par \uc2 Oh thank you.\par \par \uc2 Over a chef`s salad and shrimp and grits.\par \par \uc2 MAN (Georgia voter): To me, he`s more for the people, the hard working people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We heard a whole lot of Trump support.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Georgia voter): I did like my lifestyle when Trump was elected. I liked that groceries were much more affordable, gas was much more affordable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mixed with general exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Georgia voter): I can overlook some of the nonsense rhetoric that comes out of his mouth. I mean, ultimately I feel like he`s worried about our country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: His talk of border control is what motivates Republicans like Butch and Judy Harrell (sp?).\par \par \uc2 BUTCH HARRELL (Georgia voter): You`re paying for all these immigrants, you`re keeping them up in a hotel, you`re giving them food, you`re giving them health care, free.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You feel like the border has been too open under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JUDY HARRELL (Georgia voter): Oh, absolutely. It`s just a mess.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, how are you?\par \par \uc2 A few tables away and with a serious sugar buzz starting to get the best of me.\par \par \uc2 I should not have gotten the full sweet tea.\par \par \uc2 MATT HUTZ (Georgia voter): Half and half, you got to always go half and half.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We met Matt Hutz (sp?).\par \par \uc2 MATT HUTZ: We had a great economy with the person that I voted for.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who says there`s very little the Harris campaign could do to scoop away his or his mother Sharon`s vote for the man they`ve supported since 2016.\par \par \uc2 So he was like, I think I`m gonna vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and you were like, honey, me too, and then you guys brought it in for a hug.\par \par \uc2 MATT HUTZ: Honey, me too. This is my mom.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yeah, you`re right. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 In need of a little time for the sugar to find its way out of my system, we headed south.\par \par \uc2 Down to Peach County, one of the so-called pivot counties in Georgia, that voted for Obama twice before voting for Trump twice. They love their two for ones here.\par \par \uc2 Did you know two people can eat for $13 today?\par \par \uc2 As we found an irresistible deal at the 478 Country Buffet in Byron. As you might expect in this purple county and in this restaurant.\par \par \uc2 What`s good here?\par \par \uc2 Quite a few folks still mulling their choices.\par \par \uc2 So you`re undecided. What more do you need to know?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Georgia voter): I don`t know yet. I just kind of want to hear like all the options, like everything that both of them have to say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Marcotte Anderson (sp?) knows who she wants.\par \par \uc2 What is the biggest issue that makes you support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MARCOTTE ANDERSON (Georgia voter): Right now, abortion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 MARCOTTE ANDERSON: Yeah, Because I`ve been put in scenarios that if I didn`t get a choice, it would not work for me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But she doesn`t always feel like she can express that support in public.\par \par \uc2 I can`t put nothing Harris related on my car. If not, it might be vandalized.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Has that happened before?\par \par \uc2 MARCOTTE ANDERSON: No, but I don`t want.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t want to tempt anybody.\par \par \uc2 And yet.\par \par \uc2 Hold on to your Biden-Harris hat.\par \par \uc2 Consider the story of Greg Hall (sp?), proudly sporting his support for Harris. He`s a Democrat who gave his Republican neighbors a ride to the polls last election, raising an interesting hypothetical this time.\par \par \uc2 You, Greg, a Democrat, drive a Republican to the polls, thereby putting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the top and making him president again. If you knew that that was what you would be doing, would you still do it?\par \par \uc2 GREG HALL (Georgia voter): I would make sure they get to the polls because I believe in the right to vote and the responsibility to vote more than I believe in my own candidate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo to Greg there. Greg`s a great American. You missed a great little sound bite at the end there.\par \par \uc2 So I met a voter in Georgia who drove his Republican neighbor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I saw it back there. Oh yeah. Yes, yes, yes, I saw it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I just feel, you know, we`ve talked about civility and people being cordial again. I think that`s good too. We`re all Americans, we`re still neighbors. We have political differences, but drive each other to the polls.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah, well done, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Ahead --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done, Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah, well done, Tony. Oh, well that`s always a given. I don`t even --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Remember, half and half on the sweet tea.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Half and half on the sweet tea, brother.\par \par \uc2 Another great conversation. The barefoot contestant, Ina Garten, is here to talk about her new memoir. We`re coming back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Barefoot Contessa" Food Network)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She makes it look so easy. That`s Ina Garten decorating her famous flag cake on her Emmy award winning cooking show, "The Barefoot Contessa." I love that. The self-taught cook is the author of 13 bestselling cookbooks. Did you hear that? Number 13.\par \par \uc2 Now she`s opening up about her life. The new memoir, "Be Ready When the Luck Happens." She reveals details from a very difficult childhood, her love story with her husband, Jeffrey. I love that part. And burnout working in the White House, which, by the way, led to her very successful career change.\par \par \uc2 We`re very happy to say that Ina Garten is alive and in color in the studio this morning. We`re so glad to have you here, Ina.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN (Author, "Be Ready When the Luck Happens"): I`m so happy to be here. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Set the scene for us. You`re at this job at the White House, a little doll. And you see an ad in the paper that says what? No cooking experience, no nothing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was just a tiny ad. It was the first day it was advertised. And it`s in the business opportunity section of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " where they like advertise like dry cleaners in the Bronx and frozen yogurt stores. And it said especially food store in the Hamptons. And I thought, oh, that sounds really interesting.\par \par \uc2 And I went home and Jeffrey said to me, I said to him, I just I`m not a government person. I don`t like working on policy. Nothing happens in my job. And he said, just do what you love. If you love it, you`ll be really good at it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. But you have --\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: You know, I saw this ad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes,\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: And said, let`s go look at it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go look at it. But you have no experience cooking, no experience running a store.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: I`d never had employees. I never worked in the food business. I mean, I knew how to make 12 brownies for my friends, but I had no idea.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Brownies, by the way, you say the homemade brownie is a culinary equivalent of love. Isn`t that nice? I so believe that food is love for me.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: It really is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, it`s true.\par \par \uc2 You also talk about your childhood here, which involved physical abuse, verbal abuse. Whenever I ask people questions like this, I know you write it down in a book and you pick the words you wanted to use to express it. What do you want to get into here? I mean, and why did you want to get into that?\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: You know, I didn`t tell the story of my childhood. Certainly people have had worse childhoods than I did. But I just wanted people to know that you could come from a really difficult childhood and rewrite your own story. You know, that doesn`t have to be the rest of your life. That my parents were really critical. They were really harsh. My father was, would hit us. It was it was really rough. And I kind of tried to disappear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know it was more than critical and harsh. Your mom said you`ll never amount to anything. Your dad said no one will ever love you.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: Can you imagine telling a teenage girl, nobody will ever love you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: But under this great thing. I walk up Madison Avenue and somebody leans in almost every block and says, I love you. And I think it`s this great cosmic joke. And I think say to myself, I guess he was wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. He was wrong.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the things I loved about your story, and this is a page in the book where you write about being your authentic self and what you know works. I think anybody who`s in front of a camera will have those feelings where some marketing exec will say, you need to do this.\par \par \uc2 And you say, no, I know what connects to the viewers. You write this that while the cameras were rolling, I picked up and took a bite, actually chewed it. "Delicious" I said. Director yell "Cut. You can`t talk with your mouth full. Martha would never do that."\par \par \uc2 What did you say to yourself in that?\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: I said, I`m not Martha.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: I`m Ina. And I want to do it my way. And there was this push pull of doing it. I`m just making sure that I that it was who I am. And I just I mean, you know this better than I do. It`s the only thing that works is if you if you don`t try and act like somebody else on TV.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of my favorite work, one of my favorite parts, it was Jeffrey, because the love story that you and Jeffrey have, I want to know what he taught you, what you`ve learned from him, because you`ve been married a gazillion years. And at one point you decided to separate because you thought this isn`t working for me right now.\par \par \uc2 Tell me what you`ve learned --\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: You know, in my 20s, because I`d had such a difficult childhood. I always think that he kind of brought me up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: He taught me how to be in the world. He`s happy. He`s grounded. He`s smart. He`s funny. And he really taught me that if we the two of us want two different things, it`s not like he gets to choose or I get to choose.\par \par \uc2 Let`s figure out how we can both do what we want to do. And he`s just extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You found love in your life.\par \par \uc2 I`m with you, Gayle. I do think food is love.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: Yeah, I do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s so amazing going back to that comment in your childhood, no one will ever love you. It`s like not only have you found love in your life, but by teaching people to cook, they can say I love you to the people in their life.\par \par \uc2 And it just it goes on and on. It`s a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: And that`s what I love about writing cookbooks. It`s not like I`m giving people something. I`m giving the tools to do something for themselves. And when they put down a coconut cupcakes in front of their friends.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your Boston cream pie is on my bucket list.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Ina Garner. The name of the book, "Be Ready When the Luck Happens" is in store now. "Talk of the Table" is next. The commander is looking to add to their winning streak this Sunday when they take on the \uc2 Cleveland Browns\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning. Ms. King.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. My "Talk of the Table" is the WNBA playoffs. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I was there last night to watch the New York Liberty move one step closer to the finals. The \uc2 Liberty\uc2  are now up two games to none against the Las Vegas Aces in that playoff series. Now this is a rematch of last year`s WNBA finals where the Aces beat the Liberty to win their second title in a row.\par \par \uc2 League MVP A`ja Wilson, adore her is hoping for game three win at home on Friday to keep Las Vegas alive in the best of five series. And as much as I love the lovely A`a, A`ja I have to go with the hometown team. It was so exciting.\par \par \uc2 At one point toward the end, it looked like they were going to go into overtime and all I could think of was please don`t go in overtime I got to get home to watch the debate please.\par \par \uc2 Are you aware that you can watch \uc2 CBS\uc2  Live in the car?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes I am.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re aware. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On the CBS News App.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 CBS\uc2  Live in the car.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I finished watching the debate, got home and then watched, finished the debate and all the analysis, so I was up very late last night. But it`s worth it staying up late on a school night for that --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For the for the women`s team, I love these guys and for the debate last night.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m really, really hoping that this is the year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think a lot of people are hoping along with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also one more thing at the end of the game the women come over and actually thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh no way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never seen that before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not everybody.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not all 25,000 --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People who got good seats. People who got them good seats.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe the first row there. How long is the thank you line?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up in the air going A`ja! A`ja!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never seen anybody come over and thank you for coming to the game that`s the only point I make it. Vlad!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s celebrity row down there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My "Talk of the Table" is about one of the favorite shows in America not just around here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m talking about "Survivor." It`s been on \uc2 CBS\uc2  for almost a quarter century and so we decided to do something fun. We posted a video on social media asking Jeff Probst, the host of Survivor, out of Gayle, Nate, and Tony, who would win Survivor?\par \par \uc2 People sounded off in the comments. Don`t underestimate Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, you can.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s from your producer Katie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh Katie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who I guess still has a job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you talking about Willis?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Another said my man Nate he sees everything from every angle.\par \par \uc2 Another comment said Tony would outsmart them all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this morning we can finally reveal Jeff`s opinion. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST (Host, "Survivor"): While Nate and Tony and Gayle would make a great final three, sorry fellas, there is absolutely no question who wins in a version of Survivor. The Queen, Gayle, always.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Jeff is dead wrong. When I played that game, and I`m not just saying that because Tony`s here, I told everybody Tony would definitely win.\par \par \uc2 And Nate is very fit too. I get it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not about fit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate is very smart.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You got to be smart, you got to be intuitive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who would you pick? I`d definitely think Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As much as I would love to bask in this, I think a former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player is going to do better than me. You know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I appreciate the confidence.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know though Gayle, the game, Survivor is so popular because it`s the people who you don`t think would win.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it`s more about the athleticism. It`s more than just the athleticism.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t think, I wouldn`t even say it`s about the athleticism.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s the strategy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New season of Survivor, 47, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Season 47, tonight, 8/7 central, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And surprise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thank Jeff for the vote of confidence.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Surprise, none of us are competing in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is an only in America wild story. Something out of a sci-fi film.\par \par \uc2 An 81 year old man in Montana who`s been charged with federal felony wildlife crimes for cloning a giant foreign sheep here in the US for hunting purposes.\par \par \uc2 Let me try to explain this. So this 81 year old man, his name is Arthur Schubarth. He imported sperm, tissue and testicles from a Marco Polo sheep, the largest in the world. And then successfully took that material and made a full living sheep out of it. Then he crossbred that sheep with a Rocky Mountain bighorn sheep and created a hybrid sheep that he called the Montana Mountain King.\par \par \uc2 And the plan was to sell those, which he actually did, to people who wanted to stock preserves exclusively for hunting. The authorities were not fans of this move and they`ve given him a six month sentence.\par \par \uc2 The judge said this is to deter anyone from "changing the genetic makeup of the creatures on this earth."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like how you said he imported. I would love to see the image, the TSA image, of the importation of the testicles and the semen going through that machine. What it would show up on the screen. The guy, when I look at him, he`s like, "What the?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do you want to see that, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m just curious. Imagine what it would look like to that person.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well now that you have that image in your mind, here`s the other complication here. These are living animals now on the earth. I mean, he successfully created the clone. So people would be wondering, that would be the clone of the Marco Polo sheep that he created here, which is usually native to Kazakhstan, is going to go to a zoo. So that`s going to be living.\par \par \uc2 The other hybrid sheep are not going to be used for hunting. He`s got until the end of 2025 to sell them and the meat will be eaten by somebody. That`s what they say anyway. Just a crazy story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is a wild story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s very apologetic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Baaaad idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s what it said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s very apologetic, by the way, contrite.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to meet the founder of a group that helps give young women hope when they face a major health challenge that can affect their fertility.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for your local weather.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I just got that.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our series "Changing the Game" highlights extraordinary women. Did you hear that part? Extraordinary women making a difference. It`s Breast Cancer Awareness Month. We want to introduce you to one woman trying to make a difference for those fighting the disease. And boy, is she working overtime on this.\par \par \uc2 Her name is Amanda Rice. She`s a founder and CEO of Chick Mission. Think of that name. That`s going to be very relevant in just a second.\par \par \uc2 She`s a two-time breast cancer survivor and a skin cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two time, wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right, Tony, two-time.\par \par \uc2 Chick Mission helps young women who have been newly diagnosed with cancer preserve their fertility.\par \par \uc2 So this is when a person`s eggs are removed and saved before treatments that can cause infertility. Chick Mission works with 35 fertility partners in eight states, has helped families save more than $7 million in fertility preservation costs.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Rice, bravo, bravo to you and welcome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE (Breast cancer and skin survivor): Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did your own experience, number one I just I bow to you, three cancers before the age of 40. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody`s looking out in your life for sure.\par \par \uc2 How did your own experience though lead to this?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Yeah I mean when you`re diagnosed with cancer as a young adult it is very isolating.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes and devastating.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Most of the people you know are postmenopausal, they`ve already lived these long lives thus far and it doesn`t mean it`s right to be diagnosed with cancer, it`s just very unique as a young woman especially.\par \par \uc2 I was a working woman in New York City working in finance and all of a sudden stopped in my tracks by those three words and ultimately I was one of the lucky ones. I was a woman who worked in finance, a white woman, a privileged woman who had access to great care and phenomenal doctors who told me about the risk of infertility.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately that`s something you have to think about before you even start treatment if you want to have biological kids one day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then when you went to freeze your eggs you found out that it was not covered by insurance, you had to pay for it and it`s very costly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Incredibly costly and I actually, I had fertility benefits at the time but when I went to freeze my eggs the insurer told me I wasn`t infertile yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How old were you then? How old were you?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: I was 38.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well so only 18 states offer to pay for fertility coverage. You`ve advocated for more states to require coverage for cancer patients but you`ve also been instrumental in changing the law in one state.\par \par \uc2 Tell us about that because that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Yeah I mean we there is a broader coalition across the country, different organizations that have band together. This past year we worked on Oklahoma with a group of survivors and in honor of somebody that we lost, Corinne.\par \par \uc2 And we worked in Oklahoma, found some authors and ultimately were able to change the law this year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was gonna say talk to us directly about this mission of Chick Mission where the name comes from.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the name.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah the name and then the future of it.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Yeah so we, we`re a triple threat mission. So we give need- based grants as soon as you`re diagnosed it`s a whirlwind of information. So one of the things hopefully you are told is that there may be an impact on your future fertility.\par \par \uc2 So we cover these grants for women that are aged 18 to 40. Any type of cancer but predominantly given the prevalence of breast cancer, those are those are our patients. They are given a small window to freeze their eggs. We don`t have them go out of pocket. We reimburse the practice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow Amanda.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: So we can just take that burden off of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So someone listening to you right now is sitting in tears going oh my gosh maybe she can help me. Who`s eligible? How do they get in contact with you? How does that part work?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: So we, we receive DMs on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . We get contacted on a daily basis from those that have just been diagnosed or have completed treatment. So we say reach out to us. There are a ton of resources.\par \par \uc2 They`re not all in one place but if you live in the eight states where we currently work, you`re diagnosed, you haven`t started fertility threatening treatment yet, you make under $150,000 per year. They`re need-based.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: We`re gonna we`re gonna cover your fertility preservation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And your health today before we go?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: I`m great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Six years in remission.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can see how important this mission is to you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now we understand why. Amanda Rice, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guests are Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab. We`ll talk to them about their new children`s book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There they are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "We Are Free, You And Me." We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Activist and former \uc2 NFL\uc2  quarterback Colin Kaepernick and TV and radio host Nessa Diab are here with us now for their first TV interview together. As a 49ers player Kaepernick protested police violence by kneeling during the national anthem, the couple co-founded the Know Your Rights Camp in 2015 with the mission of advancing the well-being of black and brown communities through education and self-empowerment.\par \par \uc2 Now they are releasing their new children`s book. It is called. "We Are Free, You & Me" Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab, welcome, so good to have you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK (Co-Author, "We Are Free, You & Me"): Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Best-selling authors in --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 So I was just saying to you before during the commercial as a new dad, a girl dad, I absolutely love this book. Why did you choose to write it together? What was that impetus?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Yeah --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whose idea was it? What Colin?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: No, it`s collective conversation. We, at this point I had written another children`s book, a graphic novel. We just had our daughter.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB (Co-Author, "We Are Free, You & Me"): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: And we`re also looking at the work we were doing at Know Your Rights Camp. We`re like, how do we bring this message to a younger audience that they can feel that they have agency, that they can be courageous like they really get to take control of what their future and destiny looks like.\par \par \uc2 And we created this book around the ten points of Know Your Rights Camp.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: So that`s what led to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, Nessa, you said you would have loved a book like this when you were younger.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: well, I just feel like it`s so the message is so easy to understand and it`s just a reminder that wow I do have the right to be happy, to be healthy, to do what I want to do and just to have that confidence.\par \par \uc2 And not saying like the other books I read, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That it didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: But in a different way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: But this is, it`s a lot of fun and you know, I just, I love that a lot of everything we symbolize within the Know Your Rights Camp, you get to see it. You know, all of our volunteers come from different parts of the country in the world to come and give back and you know, it`s the community feeling which you get to see throughout the book and that`s really important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The artwork is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was gonna say that, the illustration.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The outwork is so great. I just want to read very quickly. The Know Your Rights Camp ten points. You have the right to be free, healthy, brilliant, safe, loved, courageous, alive, trusted and educated. And you look at this list and you go. Well, that`s pretty self- evident, but it`s not in many communities around this country.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: Right, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: And that that`s been our experience and as we work with youth, we`ve seen that show up with them where they may not feel like they have the right to be safe or the right to be healthy based upon circumstances or environment or situation.\par \par \uc2 And we want to reiterate that message to them so they can take that and be like, you know what? I can look at this differently, I can go out and try to accomplish and achieve these things and pursue them and do that with the best of intentions of how we make our community better, how we make the people around us better and to be able to have a show up with this, like this, with the amazing artwork from Gladys, it is incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I appreciate you earlier trying to keep the peace in the children`s literature world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like how she did that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you know kids are honest and kids are frank.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So your little one, when you read this to your daughter, how`d she feel about it? What`d she like?\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: Okay, so Colin won`t say it, I will. So she loves to see Dada. So anytime there`s Dad, that`s her favorite page. So actually there`s an Easter egg in the back towards the end in the spread. Actually what`s in the backdrop right now, you see Colin and myself and our baby in a stroller --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yeah.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: A background which we absolutely prefer, saying hello to other family and friends, part of the Know Your Rights Camp, but she loves seeing her dad. Her face lights up, it`s all about Dada.\par \par \uc2 I mean I --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And her mom too, who`s a radio superstar.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who`s been on the air 20 years, she`s a legend in this town when it comes to radio, and I know you`re both very involved in your advocacy work.\par \par \uc2 And Colin, it says that you were still getting up every morning, you were still working out, you were still practicing, and when I read that I went "Really?" and then I thought "Why?" Is it because you do really want to play football again or are you trying to make a point?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Both.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Both.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: I think it`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: I mean, when I look at my career, it was 15 years of work and dedication before I ever stepped foot on an \uc2 NFL\uc2  field. And to have that passion and chase it for that long, you don`t just give that up, you don`t just walk away. You chase that, you pursue it, and to your point on "Do you make a point around that?" There will never be an instance where I`m not ready. And there will never be an instance where "Oh, it`s because he doesn`t want to anymore."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You want them to know that you want to.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But it`s such a sad situation in so many ways because you clearly could do it, and this is what you worked your life to do, and then to have it taken away because you feel it`s important to express yourself on this other issue, which is protected by the way, and which you have a right to express yourself on.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever feel sadness when you pick up that football and wish you could have done two things at the same time?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Well, I think that`s part of the point of like continuing to pursue and chase is, I actually had one of the youth at one of our Know Your Rights Camp ask me that. It was like, you know, I love playing football, but I also really care about my community, and I saw what happened. How do I do both?\par \par \uc2 And for me, I`ve had so many conversations like that, and my response is always, "You don`t have to choose one or the other. You can do both." And I think that`s the environment that we should be pushing forward with.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You both are amazing. Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab, thank you both for being here. The book is called "You Are Free, You & Me" on sale now wherever you buy your books.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Best-selling authors in the house.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241002eka200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Israel Strikes on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Kill Hundreds; Biden Speaks at \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2 ; Tracking a Strengthening Tropical Threat; Harris and Trump Focus on Pennsylvania; Suspect Allegedly Wrote Confession Note; Police Offer Reward for Alabama Shooting Suspect; \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  Sued Over Plastic Pollution; Missouri Execution Controversy; Weight Loss Drug CEO Faces Tough Questions; Reggie Bush Suing NCAA; Phone Ban in Schools; Porch Pirate Thwarted; Rising Middle East Tensions;"Proper" Hamburgers Only; Cheese Curds and Conversation; Candid Talk on Menopause; Princeton Ranks #1... Again; And "The Most Fun State" Goes to... ; Tackling Malnutrition; Kristen Bell Live, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Adriana Diaz, Chris Livesay, Weijia  Jiang, Stephanie Abrams, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Scott MacFarlane, Cristian  Benavides, Ben Tracy, Vladimir Duthiers; Jericka Duncan, Anna Werner;  Samantha Vinograd; Elizabeth Werner \par \uc2 13,720 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey there. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, September 24th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And I`m Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but we`re in great hands. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden addresses the \uc2 UN\uc2  as Israel strikes \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  killing hundreds in Lebanon and increasing fears of an all-out war.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli prime minister): The \uc2 IDF\uc2  has warned you to get out of harm`s way. I urge you take this warning seriously.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The vice president weighs a trip to the border while Trump repeats false claims about Haitian immigrants.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): You have to get them the hell out. You have to get them out. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The CEO of a company behind popular weight loss drugs faces tough questions on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): You`ve got to end this incredible greed.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Reggie Bush suing his alma mater, the \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , and the PAC 12.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: To recoup money made on his name, image and likeness.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Launching down the middle, intercepted.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Snagged by Damar Hamlin, first ever interception for the Bills` safety, who famously collapsed on the field and nearly died.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: And the bill steamrolled the Jaguars.\par \par \uc2 DAMAR HAMLIN (\uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  safety): No worries for it, man. I`m still processing it as I go.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): In a new ad, Walz made his most convincing argument yet -- truck.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN) (2024 vice presidential nominee): My 79 International Harvester Scout and my five-year-old Black Lab Boxer Scout.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: He named his dog after his truck. That is peak middle American Dad.\par \par \uc2 Hey there, these are my kids, Dodge Ram and right over there is the apple of our eye, \uc2 JD Power and Associates\uc2  best class midsize sedan, named her after grandma.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I have to say that the \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  vehicle there, particularly in that color, maybe my favorite car of all time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a beautiful vehicle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a truck.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A classic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, they get notes from people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Classic truck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It would be very hip in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to be with the news, the aftermath in the Middle East, deadliest day of the war so far.\par \par \uc2 Growing conflict between Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . Lebanon now says that nearly 560 people died yesterday during a barrage of airstrikes that Israel says were aimed at \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , rocket launchers that have menace Northern Israel for almost a year now.\par \par \uc2 Mass evacuations inside Lebanon underway in the southern part of the country, and there are fears now of a possible Israeli ground invasion.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And we`ve learned that President Biden, who`s speaking today at the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2 , is sending a small number of additional US troops to the region. We`ll have more on the president`s remarks in a moment from Weijia Jiang at the \uc2 UN\uc2 , but we begin with Chris Livesay in Israel.\par \par \uc2 Chris, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good morning, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 The US military is bracing for a wider conflict, sending a small number of additional troops to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Pentagon would not specify how many, only that they would augment the 40,000 already in the region in light of the increased tension.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Wave after wave, missiles landed across Lebanon, 1,600 strikes in all, according to the Israeli military. The attacks continued overnight, setting fire to buildings and homes.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Hundreds have been killed and thousands wounded according to Lebanon`s Health Ministry. The \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  claim it sent thousands of text, audio, and video warnings to Lebanese civilians to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said this of the militant group \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : It placed rockets in your living rooms and missiles in your garage. Those rockets and missiles are aimed directly at our cities, directly at our citizens.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The Israeli military said these videos capture secondary explosions after the initial air strikes as those hidden weapons detonate.\par \par \uc2 Cars choked the highways as people fled into the night, heading north towards the capital, Beirut, looking for shelter in schools and anywhere they can find it.\par \par \uc2 After a week of crushing blows to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , a ground invasion is almost certain, says retired Israeli Army Colonel Kobi Maron.\par \par \uc2 KOBI MARON (Retired Israeli Army Colonel): Is Biden administration going to like it? No, but they understand, when two million people right now under attack, Israel should do whatever we can to stop the fire, to make the war as short as we can.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Can Israel afford to have a multi-front war?\par \par \uc2 KOBI MARON: Well, unfortunately, we already have it. It`s very tough.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The Pentagon says Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  has been pressing Defense Minister Yoav Gallant for a diplomatic solution to the fighting. The \uc2 US State Department\uc2  is warning all Americans to leave Lebanon while commercial flights are still available -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Chris Livesay in Israel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s \uc2 UN\uc2  speech will cover a wide range of topics, including the global conflicts, but many will be listening specifically for what he says about Israel and the ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 For more on that, let`s go to Weijia Jiang, at the \uc2 United Nations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning to you, Nate, and good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 As Chris mentioned, the Pentagon announced just yesterday that the US is sending those additional troops to the Middle East, that`s to bolster those already stationed in the region as part of an effort to avoid a wider conflict.\par \par \uc2 Now, the ongoing issues in Israel and Lebanon will be just part of the focus when the president addresses the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  for his final time as commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 Biden will highlight America`s role in defending Ukraine`s sovereignty and expanding \uc2 NATO\uc2 , as well as managing competition with countries including China.\par \par \uc2 Biden is also expected to talk about all his efforts to broker a ceasefire deal between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Now, that includes passing the US led \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  resolution back in June calling for an immediate ceasefire, though that came after the US vetoed similar measures in the past.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is also expected to have one-on-one meetings on the sidelines of this \uc2 UN\uc2  gathering, but as of right now, one with Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu is not on his schedule -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Notable.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the latest on the growing storm system in the Western Caribbean that is expected to become Tropical Storm Helene.\par \par \uc2 Forecasters think it will strengthen into a hurricane tomorrow as it moves toward Florida.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning. Where is this all going?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Adriana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Today, our system is moving to the northwest. Right now we just have a broad circulation in the Caribbean, but the thunderstorms will get more organized today into tomorrow, and that means it will strengthen into Hurricane Helene as it snakes through the Caribbean and the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 It will then rain out over the southeast and the Ohio River Valley. It`s going to be a sizable storm, so expect wind gusts over 70 miles an hour on the West Coast of Florida and over 60 miles an hour along I-95. These winds will also churn up dangerous waves and surge.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start to see some of those outer bands today into tomorrow. Landfall is expected Thursday into Friday, potentially as a major hurricane that is a Category Three or higher. It will leave at least five to eight inches of flooding rain as far north as the Carolinas.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, the coast is going to take the brunt of the storm, but we have to remember, there are going to be major impacts inland as well.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, thanks for the warning.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Election Day, Election Tuesday, just six weeks away. Republican nominee, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  campaigned in Pennsylvania yesterday, while Democratic nominee, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will return there tomorrow, and our CBS News Battleground Tracker shows in that key state, they are tied.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns has made her way to Indiana, Pennsylvania that`s outside of Pittsburgh. She`s following all of this.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Well, this town is the birthplace of the late actor, Jimmy Stewart, who, of course, was the star of "Mr. Smith Goes to Washington."\par \par \uc2 And if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  want to go to Washington and go to the White House, they`re going to have to win this state, and that`s why they are both here in Pennsylvania this week as early voting starts soon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you very much, Pennsylvania. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Rallying support in the Keystone State, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  focused on his campaign`s two key issues -- the economy --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I will get Pennsylvania energy workers pumping, fracking, drilling, and producing like never before.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And immigration again, bringing up Springfield, Ohio where many Haitian immigrants live legally.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : The fact is, and I`ll say it now, you have to get them the hell out. You have to get them out. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting "Bring him back.")\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: This as new CBS News polling shows a vast majority of Trump supporters say immigrants are making American society worse.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is considering a visit to the southern border later this week, when she campaigns in Arizona. Both campaigns are on a battleground state blitz this week, with Harris returning here to Pennsylvania tomorrow and Nevada this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Trump will stop through Georgia, North Carolina, and Michigan, all as CBS News polling shows Harris narrowing the gap with Trump on the economy, yet the gender gap remains wide, and in an apparent attempt to appeal to women voters, Trump tried to argue abortion is no longer an issue.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Women will be happy, healthy, confident and free.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : You will no longer be thinking about abortion.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, the former president argues that this issue will go back to the states, but polling and recent election data show that abortion is a motivating issue for many voters.\par \par \uc2 Now next weekend, Trump will return here to Western Pennsylvania for a rally in Butler, Pennsylvania, that, of course, is the site of that first assassination attempt -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We all remember.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And a note for our viewers, CBS News will host the one and only vice presidential debate between Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  next Tuesday here in New York City. Join us for live coverage, moderated by Norah O`Donnell and Margaret Brennan on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , CBS News 24/7, and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 A man arrested after a confrontation near Trump`s Golf Club in West Palm Beach, Florida is now likely to be charged with trying to assassinate the former president.\par \par \uc2 At a court hearing yesterday, a judge ordered Ryan Routh to be held without bail after prosecutors revealed what seems to be a handwritten confession.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: To convince a judge to hold Ryan Routh in jail, new details were revealed in court Monday. Prosecutors argued, Routh brandished this gun with 11 rounds, including one in the chamber. They say a Secret Service agent spotted Routh`s face then the gun barrel in the bushes near the sixth hole of Trump`s South Florida golf course, and opened fire when Routh`s gun moved.\par \par \uc2 RONALD ROWE (Acting \uc2 United States Secret Service\uc2  director): He made swift decisions and took a swift action to be able to mitigate that. No shot was fired at the former president.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: When police later caught Routh, the Feds say they found six cell phones, two additional license plates, a passport, and directions to Mexico in the car.\par \par \uc2 Months earlier, Routh allegedly left a box with a friend who discovered a handwritten note after Routh`s arrest, which said: "Dear World, this was an assassination attempt."\par \par \uc2 Why was it necessary to put that handwritten note, allegedly written by the defendant in public?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE (Former Deputy Assistant Attorney General): The federal prosecutors have to persuade the judge to keep this man detained and incarcerated until trial and it`s not enough for them to say, we think that he was trying to assassinate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they actually have to introduce evidence that shows what he was trying to do.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Prosecutors also allege Routh had handwritten notes on Trump`s summer schedule, and its cell phone records showed Routh near Mar- a-Lago multiple times from mid-August to mid-September.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE: For now, it`s a firearms case. It will not be so for long. Within weeks, the federal prosecutors will expand the list of charges.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Routh is due back in court Monday to be formally charged. This week, Congress is likely to approve more than $200 million for the agency to beef up its protection of officials, including, Adriana, the presidential candidates.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Scott MacFarlane, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Alabama, where police still haven`t found the gunman behind a mass shooting in Birmingham Saturday night. Four people were killed and 17 more injured. Police are now offering a $100,000.00 reward.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides spoke with the mother of one of those victims.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CANDANCE KEMP (Anitra Holloman`s mother): She had a life ahead of her. She had a mom who loved her dearly. That was my best little friend.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Candance Kemp`s 21-year-old daughter, Anitra Holloman was one of the four people killed Saturday night outside the Hush Lounge in Birmingham`s popular Five Points South District.\par \par \uc2 Officials believe the shooters were targeting at least one individual waiting in line outside the nightclub.\par \par \uc2 CANDANCE KEMP: They hit somebody that I really, really love.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Anitra`s mom says her daughter was an innocent bystander, needlessly gunned down.\par \par \uc2 CANDANCE KEMP: My baby didn`t deserve it.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Police say they`re investigating whether any of the suspects were armed with a so-called \uc2 Glock\uc2  switch, which can turn a semiautomatic weapon into a fully automatic one.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham`s mayor says these federally banned devices are a growing menace in his community.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RANDALL WOODFIN (Birmingham, Alabama): If you have lax gun laws or no gun laws, you put this hand behind my back. If you put us in a situation where gun switches are easily accessible, then you put this hand behind my back.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Another recent victim of gun violence in Birmingham, Anitra`s former partner and the father of their one-year-old daughter, Skilar.\par \par \uc2 Skilar is now forced to grow up without both of her parents.\par \par \uc2 I just wonder how you come to terms with something like that?\par \par \uc2 CANDANCE KEMP: How do you? I just know that I am going to be the best grandmother that I can be to my Skilar and I`m going to protect her very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Anitra`s mother told me that her daughter wasn`t able to go out with friends as much as other 21-year-olds, because she would often stay home to take care of her baby.\par \par \uc2 She was also in college, studying to become a teacher, so this was a rare night out for Anitra that ended her life, that her partner`s killing remains unsolved -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A bright future she now won`t have. Cristian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in just a moment, plastic bags, plastic bottles, they`re very hard to recycle and one state is suing a major oil company for allegedly lying about that fact for decades. We`ll give you all the details.\par \par \uc2 Plus, how come some weight loss drugs are so much more expensive in these United States than in other countries? The CEO of a major drug company is in the hot seat today. We`ll tell you all about it. How come? But first, it is 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning for our series, "Three Meals," Nate apparently went to every gas station in Wisconsin and ate cheese curds, oh, and along the way, got a good idea of what voters in Wisconsin are saying 42 days out from Election Day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. The cheese is good.\par \par \uc2 This is not your average "Three Meals."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In today`s "Climate Watch," a first of its kind lawsuit against \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2 , and it has to do with recycling. We previously told you about what`s going on in Texas, where only a small fraction of plastic actually gets recycled through a program partly funded by \uc2 Exxon\uc2 , well now, California`s attorney general is accusing the oil giant of deceiving the public.\par \par \uc2 Ben Tracy was on that early report and he joins us once again.\par \par \uc2 Ben, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  is one of the biggest plastic makers on the planet, and in this lawsuit, California`s attorney general alleges the company lied to the public for decades to convince us that recycling works so we`ll keep buying plastic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Plastic waste now pollutes every corner of the planet.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: Imagine a future where plastic is not wasted --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And plastic makers have long said recycling is the solution.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA (California attorney general): And they knew from day one that that was not possible.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: California Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  accuses \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  of a decades` long campaign of deception that falsely promoted all plastic as recyclable, when in fact, the vast majority of plastic products are not and likely cannot be recycled.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: We are demanding that \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  stop lying, stop deceiving the public, stop manipulating consumers.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Only about five percent of plastic waste in the US gets recycled. Most of it ends up in landfills, incinerators, or pollutes rivers and oceans.\par \par \uc2 California wants \uc2 Exxon\uc2  to pay billions of dollars to fund cleanup efforts.\par \par \uc2 Exxon\uc2 , obviously, is not the only company that makes plastic, but they are the only company you`re suing. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  is the biggest producer of the building blocks of the plastic products that we see in our daily lives, and two, they are one of, if not the biggest liars when it comes to perpetuating the myth of recycling.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The shiny metal shows the new technology that we brought in --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: CBS News recently reported on Exxon`s claims that it can now recycle any kind of plastic through what it calls advanced recycling.\par \par \uc2 Is this just your industry trying to make consumers believe that plastic is recyclable so they`ll keep buying more plastic?\par \par \uc2 RAY MASTROLEO (ExxonMobil Advanced Recycling manager): Advanced recycling is real. It`s happening. We`re doing it.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: California`s attorney general calls this a public relations stunt, citing that 92 percent of plastic waste fed into Exxon`s system does not become new plastic, but is turned into fuel that is then burned, creating planet warming emissions.\par \par \uc2 We asked \uc2 Exxon\uc2  about the AG`s investigation before the lawsuit was filed.\par \par \uc2 What is your response to the attorney general of California saying you basically lied for decades about recycling working?\par \par \uc2 RAY MASTROLEO: Obviously, it`s an open investigation, and I prefer not to comment on that. We recognize plastic waste is a challenge and our goal is ultimately to address plastic waste.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Now, in a statement responding to the lawsuit, \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  called California`s recycling system ineffective and blames state officials here for suing rather than working to fix the problem.\par \par \uc2 The company also doubled down claiming that its advanced recycling process does work -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ben Tracy, with the tough questions, we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Lawrence on the move, launching down the middle, intercepted over through the man and Damar Hamlin. A huge roar as one of the most popular, inspiring Bills comes up with the takeaway.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What a moment. That`s a big play for Damar Hamlin, the player who returned to the \uc2 NFL\uc2  after suffering a cardiac arrest on the field less than two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Hamlin, who snagged his first interception of his career during Buffalo`s blowout of Jacksonville on Monday Night Football. The Bills mafia went wild for the 26-year-old defender.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Blowout on Monday Night Football, not a good look.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So such good news for him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, happy for him.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 In other news, Missouri plans to execute a death row prisoner tonight, despite significant questions about his guilt.\par \par \uc2 Marcellus Williams was convicted of murdering newspaper reporter, Felicia Gayle in 1998. He has always denied it, and some prosecutors in that office -- in the office that convicted him say the case is filled with holes.\par \par \uc2 Missouri`s governor says it will not stop the execution.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan has been following this case.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys for 55-year-old Williams not only maintain his innocence, but they claim prosecutors botched the case, alleging evidence of racial bias and investigators mishandling of the murder weapon before the trial.\par \par \uc2 Now, despite the many questions surrounding Williams` conviction, the execution will go forward barring a last minute change.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: With only hours left before his execution, Marcellus Williams is nearly out of options.\par \par \uc2 Last night, the Missouri Supreme Court rejected his request to cancel the execution. Writing: "There is no credible evidence of actual innocence."\par \par \uc2 Missouri Governor \uc2 Mike Parson\uc2  also denied his request for clemency. Writing: "Mr. Williams has exhausted due process and every judicial avenue. Nothing from the real facts of this case have led me to believe in Mr. Williams` innocence."\par \par \uc2 Williams is on death row for the 1998 murder of Felicia Gayle. He is convicted of stabbing the newspaper reporter 43 times with a butcher`s knife inside her suburban St. Louis home.\par \par \uc2 Police say they found Gayle`s personal items and her husband`s computer in Williams` car, but his defense team argues male DNA found on the murder weapon and at the scene did not match Williams, and that there were no direct witnesses to the alleged crime.\par \par \uc2 He and his family have always maintained his innocence.\par \par \uc2 MARCELLUS WILLIAMS, JR. (Marcellus Williams` son): I want a full exoneration, because I believe wholeheartedly that my father is -- that he is innocent.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Williams` fight has been ongoing for more than two decades. Hours before his scheduled execution in 2017, then Governor \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  granted a stay due to unresolved questions about the DNA.\par \par \uc2 He ordered a five-person board to examine the case, but when he left office, the board dissolved without making any recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Since then, defense attorneys, the current county prosecutor, and even Felicia Gayle`s family have all asked to take the death penalty off the table.\par \par \uc2 Have you seen anything like this before?\par \par \uc2 TRICIA ROJO BUSHNELL (Attorney for Marcellus Williams): Certainly this is an extraordinary case.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We spoke to Williams` attorney, Tricia Rojo Bushnell before the governor declined to halt the execution.\par \par \uc2 TRICIA ROJO BUSHNELL: I think what many of us struggle with when we see a situation like this is what`s the point? How can this be a criminal justice system if this is the kind of outcome it delivers?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A lot of people questioning that this morning.\par \par \uc2 Williams` last chance at sparing his life sits with the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 . His legal team and county prosecutors told us they will work until the very last moments to stop Williams` execution.\par \par \uc2 We did reach out to the Attorney General`s Office, but have not heard back. And I spoke to one of his attorneys this morning, who said, you know, they have these three pending, you know, cases to the Supreme Court. They`re hoping that it makes a difference, and the family has said that they`re at peace with whatever decision is made, because they`ve been here before.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: They`ve been here before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The idea of going forward with a death sentence when you haven`t worked out the questions of guilt or innocence seems crazy to me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And what`s so odd about this one, you all, is you have not only the family, the victim`s family, saying, wait a minute.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You have prosecutors saying that, and just the support and the amount of people that are really looking at this case to see what happens next.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Jericka, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, why Americans are paying as much as 10 times more for popular weight loss drugs like Ozempic and Wegovy, 10 times more than folks in other countries.\par \par \uc2 Anna Werner is digging into that question to get some answers, too. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In today`s "Health Watch," the CEO of a company behind the popular diabetes and weight loss drugs, Ozempic and Wegovy will face questions from US senators today, that`s because research shows that Americans are paying far more for those drugs than people in many other countries.\par \par \uc2 For example, Wegovy costs about $1,300.00 a month here in these United States versus just over $90.00 in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Our senior consumer investigative correspondent, Anna Werner has been digging into these high prices and asking questions. She joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Anna, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the question: Why does a company price a drug at under $100.00 in a country like France and 10 times as much here?\par \par \uc2 Senator Bernie Sanders, who will be leading that hearing today, says he has a simple answer -- greed.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 cYou`ve got to end this incredible greed, which is taking place literally on the lives of people.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders says drug companies are legally allowed to set their prices as high as they want in the US and the prices \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  has set for Ozempic and Wegovy here are, in his words, ripping off patients.\par \par \uc2 A Senate committee report in May found the company charges Americans with type two diabetes $969.00 a month for Ozempic, while prices are set at $155.00 in Canada, $71.00 in France, and $59.00 in Germany. The list price for its weight loss drug Wegovy is $1,349.00 a month in the US, but the same product is just $137.00 in Germany and $92.00 in the UK.\par \par \uc2 The senator says he`s been pressuring \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  for months.\par \par \uc2 You have these conversations with them. Are you saying, Look, I`d like you to lower the drug price by 50 percent, or 40 percent or 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): I`d like them to not charge us any more than they are charging on average people in other countries.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: And their response to that is?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): It`s complicated. We have a very complicated health care system in America. We`re working on it.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The company told us that 99 percent of commercial health care plans in the US cover Ozempic and about half the plans cover Wegovy going, and that 80 percent of US patients with insurance pay $25.00 or less per month for the prescriptions.\par \par \uc2 But some employers have stopped covering the drug because of the high cost combined with high demand. In April, North Carolina stopped covering weight loss drugs, including Wegovy for state employees like Tralene Williams.\par \par \uc2 TRALENE WILLIAMS (Wegovy patient): Once they terminated the coverage, it was just like a train hit me.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Williams works in IT for a state agency, morbidly obese in 2009, she had bariatric surgery, but later found her weight creeping back up.\par \par \uc2 So in 2021 she started Wegovy paid for by the state`s health insurance plan. Now, with coverage dropped, she can`t afford the over $1,300.00 a month price tag.\par \par \uc2 TRALENE WILLIAMS: That`s like a mortgage payment that I simply cannot afford.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: A Yale study published in JAMA earlier this year found the drugs could likely be manufactured for prices far below current prices. How much? One of its authors told Senator Sanders at a meeting last week --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Semaglutide is the generic name, can be properly manufactured for just under five dollars per month.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2  Professor Stephen Schondelmeyer studies pharmaceutical markets.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN SCHONDELMEYER (\uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2  professor): You can make this drug for the cost of a cup of coffee, and it`s selling for a thousand dollars, so something is broken in the market, or that doesn`t seem to be priced fairly, and where the US is paying a price that`s five to 10 times what it costs in Europe.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 `s response: "We appreciate that it is frustrating that each country has its own health care system, but making isolated and limited comparisons ignores this fundamental fact."\par \par \uc2 But for Williams --\par \par \uc2 TRALENE WILLIAMS: I feel disappointed. I feel like simply because we`re the USA, they feel like they can charge us more, and that`s just simply unfair.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: In response to Senator Sanders` comment about \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  being greedy, the company told us it`s proud of the work its scientists have done to advance innovation and improve people`s lives.\par \par \uc2 It also said that even when it lowers its prices, too often, patients in the United States don`t get the savings. It says insurers and pharmacy benefit managers, along others play a role in pricing too, and they make money in the system as well -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Anna, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, California makes a big decision about kids and cell phones in schools. That`s next in "What to Watch" but right now, it is 7:43, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to need you to stick around, because we`ve got a lot coming up at eight, including actor, singer and producer, Kristen Bell will be here in the building.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Nobody Wants This"/Netflix)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Her latest show is a love story, and it`s called, "Nobody Wants This," but it`s definitely something fans will want to see.\par \par \uc2 Plus, I always get hungry when watching our series, "Three Meals," so it`s my turn. I shared good food and good conversation with voters in Wisconsin and the people had some surprising things to say. You have to stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 All of that in our next hour. But first, Vlad will have the stories you`ll be talking about in "What to Watch" --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did you really eat all the cheese at all the gas stations in Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy. I wouldn`t want --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: See any Packers fans?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Wisconsin. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey, you guys don`t know this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, I`m Lasso.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little pelvic disruption here on a Tuesday with Tom Jones. "It`s Not Unusual."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You knew what I was talking about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said pelvic disruption?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pelvic disruption. You ever see Tom Jones?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When he said that --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Uh-oh-oh-oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s how you ended up with Celine. That takes care of that. All right, come on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Once and only.\par \par \uc2 All right, time now for "What to Watch" -- we talked a lot about a big change in college sports. Players can now make big money for their name, image and likeness.\par \par \uc2 I have that picture in my head, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Now, a college football hero from 20 years ago wants a little bit of that money. We`re talking about 2005 Heisman Trophy winner, Reggie Bush who is suing the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2 , the PAC 12 Conference and the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  for using his name, image, and likeness back then.\par \par \uc2 His lawyers say Bush was never compensated for any TV contracts, merchandise sales, and media rights. The \uc2 NCAA\uc2  didn`t change its rules to allow NIL until 2021.\par \par \uc2 Bush is also suing the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  in a separate defamation case. USC says it has not received the latest lawsuit. There`s been no comment from the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  or PAC 12.\par \par \uc2 Nate, Reggie Bush.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Reggie Bush is known as the greatest college football player of all time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was on the cover of my "Madden" --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- game when he was still in college.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, when he was still in college. College, professional sports, USC -- they all made money off of his name, image and likeness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For sure.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But can you have back pay?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But -- yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: For name, image and likeness?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the question.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you go back to pay him, you have to pay everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You open the floodgates.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So I hope he wins, so I can be paid, too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You have to pay Nate. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re all rooting for you, Reggie because my boy, Nate needs to get paid.\par \par \uc2 All right, California is the latest state to restrict the use of smartphones in schools. Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  signed the Phone Free School Act requiring every school district and charter schools to come up with a policy to ban the devices by 2026.\par \par \uc2 Eight states now have measures like this, part of an effort to stop distractions in the classroom, as well as online bullying.\par \par \uc2 You guys have kids in school. We are about to have kids in school.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You shook your head no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no. I`m fully in favor of this.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You`re in. Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our kids learn nothing with smartphones in the room with them. They need to be locked up in these little pouches. You get them, you put them in at the beginning of the day, you get them out at the end of the day. If you need to reach your kids --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the parents would say --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- you just call the front office. It worked in the 90s.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It would still work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is true.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I agree.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It