ons in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Nobody Wants This"/Netflix)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That`s Kristen Bell and Adam Brody in the new series, "Nobody Wants This."\par \par \uc2 Ahead, she will tell us why she loves the story about how opposites attract.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil. Coming up in this hour, a look at America`s role in the Middle East amid fears of an all-out war there. National security contributor Sam Vinograd will tell us whether Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  can step back from the brink.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m Adriana Diaz. The charming and hilarious Kristen Bell is here to talk about her new romantic comedy series. We`ll ask if she found inspiration from her real-life love story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. I shared three meals and great conversation with voters in the Great State of Wisconsin. Was there cheese? Oh yes, there definitely was.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There had to be cheese.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Cheese is good, but first, here`s today`s "Eye-Opener At Eight."\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Don`t let \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  endanger Lebanon. Please, get out of harm`s way now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mass evacuations are underway in Lebanon after hundreds died, Israeli airstrikes on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The US military is bracing for a wider conflict, sending a small number of additional troops to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We got to take our country back from these horrible people because if we win Pennsylvania, we win the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In a wide-ranging campaign speech, former President Trump slams Vice President Harris and President Biden.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS:: And if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  want to go to Washington and go to the White House, they`re going to have to win this state.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The head of a company that makes popular weight loss drugs faces questions on Capitol Hill about why they`re so expensive.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): I`d like them to not charge us any more than they are charging on average people in other countries.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And former President Trump is asked how he tries to stay healthy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I try and eat properly. I try, I do the best. And I try --\par \par \uc2 SHARYL ATTKISSON ("Full Measure with Sharyl Attkisson): But you eat hamburgers.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I do, but -- but proper hamburgers.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I only eat the most proper hamburgers. These are hamburgers wearing a necktie, a little top hat, and a sash that says Mayor McCheese. That`s the only...\par \par \uc2 (Audience laughs)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And we begin this hour with the vicious fighting in the Middle East after Israel launched a wave of airstrikes yesterday, killing 560 people nearly in Lebanon. The Israeli military says the target was the militant group \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , which has fired thousands of rockets into Israel since the start of the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The US is now sending additional troops to the region and warning Americans still in Lebanon to leave if possible. To help us understand the situation, we are now joined by Samantha Vinograd, a CBS News national security contributor and former assistant secretary for counterterrorism at DHS.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Sam. So glad you`re with us on a morning like today. It seems like this just continues to escalate with more airstrikes in different parts of the border with Israel. Explain to us what the latest situation is and your reaction.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Well, Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  have both been redefining what is off-limits since October 7th from a targeting perspective. Just for context, since the mid-1980s, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been calling for the destruction of the State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 After October 7th, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  upped the level of its cross-border strikes into Israel, supposedly in solidarity with the Palestinian people. Since that time, cross-border strikes have certainly escalated. And more recently, Israel has launched targeted airstrikes into Beirut, into the capital with mass civilian casualties. And \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been launching strikes at a broader range of targets in Israel and much further into Israeli territory.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For context here, though, you know, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists invade Israel on October 7th, almost a year ago, and then \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  starts firing rockets in the north, a whole different part of the country. Tens of thousands of Israelis haven`t been able to go back home since then. And now you have all this fighting, it`s very difficult for Lebanese citizens. Why does \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , which also has a role in politics and in public services in Lebanon, why is it doing this to its own people? Why did it get involved in something that was between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Well, as I mentioned, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been focused on the destruction of the State of Israel since the mid-1980s. It is a designated foreign terrorist organization, in addition to being a political force within Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been attacking Israel since the mid-1980s, and there`s been a series of conflicts between the two countries.\par \par \uc2 But Tony, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is a very sophisticated fighting force. They have a huge arsenal of weapons, they are highly trained, and they have the backing of the regime in Iran, like \uc2 Hamas\uc2  does. But \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is a highly sophisticated fighting force that would present a challenge to the State of Israel if Israel did escalate further by launching a ground operation in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When I was in Israel reporting on the aftermath of the war before the counter-invasion of Gaza, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  was telling me that the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fight is like a fly and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is like an elephant. They just militarily are on a whole other scale.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, and as Tony mentioned, it is widening regionally. But do you believe that this could escalate to an even larger-scale war, which is the worry of the surrounding parties?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Well, there`s already a regional conflict. Israel and Iran have traded direct strikes. Iran has activated its proxies all over the region. What the US is seeking to avoid is Israel launching a ground operation in Lebanon and a direct confrontation between Israel and another country, another nation state in the region. And that`s why the US has sent additional troops and is working both diplomatic channels and trying to deter both Israel and Iran and its proxies from escalating further.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the president is going to talk at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  later today. Are things going to be solved by tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: I don`t think President Biden will find an off switch at the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 . I think instead there is behind-the-scenes activity to try to convince the government of Lebanon, as well as Iran and the proxies it supports, that escalating further will not end well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The off switch search is really a -- we`re fumbling around in the dark looking for that switch.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The world is.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And the US has been trying to help diplomatically, but like we reported, they have sent some troops to the region, additional troops. Could that be a slippery slope? How much more can the US get pulled into this conflict?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Well, we are already pulled in. We came to Israel`s defense when there was a barrage of aerial assets directed at Israel. The forces that are in the Middle East, the additional forces, are meant to help provide for Israel`s security, to deter further escalation, and also to come to the defense of US citizens if they need it. Both American forces, American diplomats, and American civilians.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of dual citizens living in countries like Lebanon if the situation escalates further.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Samantha, thank you so much for this context.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Nate hits the road in the key battleground state of Wisconsin to talk to voters about what`s on their minds and sample plenty of cheese curds for our series, "3 Meals."\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8.06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, actor Kristen Bell will talk to us about her new series "Nobody Wants This" and how the rom-com story is like her real-life relationship with husband, Dak, Dax Shepard. It`s in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Each week until the election, our series "3 Meals" is hitting the road in different states to break bread with voters and ask, what matters the most?\par \par \uc2 This morning, we go to the important battleground state of Wisconsin. Recent CBS News polling shows Vice President Harris and former President Trump in a statistical tie there. We took a road trip through the Badger State, home to America`s largest cheese producer, and home to 10 critical electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 And yes, we were able to find some amazing food, but it was the conversations that filled us up.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wisconsin, one of the dairy machines of America. It`s a place where people like their politics, how they like their cheese, sharp and straightforward. Around here in Eau Claire, they like their portions hearty. We stopped for dinner at 4 Mile Restaurant and Bar, a community staple since the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 MARK GAGNON (Wisconsin Voter): I would never vote for a convicted felon for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Conservative Mark Gagnon says, instead of Trump, he`s picking the Harris-Walz ticket.\par \par \uc2 MARK GAGNON: I voted for him the first time he ran. I was gravely disappointed. I think we need younger people. I mean what`s an 80-year-old person know what a 30-year-old family needs?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What did you say, you`re what?\par \par \uc2 JIM WEBER (Wisconsin Voter): I`m a Trumper.\par \par \uc2 DAVE YOUNG (Wisconsin Voter): I`m a liberal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And somehow, you guys are friends.\par \par \uc2 JIM WEBER: And people say Trump`s an (beep) I don`t disagree.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good friends Jim Weber and Dave Young live right in the neighborhood, but their political views are worlds apart.\par \par \uc2 JIM WEBER: I think we need a businessman to run this country. It`s a mess.\par \par \uc2 DAVID YOUNG: Yeah, I think it comes down to character. I think Trump is unfit to be a leader, and that automatically disqualifies him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s the key to getting along? Seems like a simple question, but..\par \par \uc2 JIM WEBER: Respect each other.\par \par \uc2 DAVID YOUNG: Exactly what I was going to say, respect.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Before you ask, yes, I pump my own gas. And yes, these are cheese curds.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we`re headed south on I-94 before we rebase for breakfast in Sauk County.\par \par \uc2 We started our morning in the small town of Reedsburg, Wisconsin. And the flat-top grill at Greenwood`s Cafe has been crackling since sunrise. Greenwood`s has been helping fuel the town for more than half a century.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MIHLBAUER (Wisconsin voter): I`ll tell you what I say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you say, Terry?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MIHLBAUER: You know why circuses, they can`t have all their shows and everything anymore?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MIHLBAUER: Because all the clowns went into politics.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Terry Mihlbauer is retired. This election, Trump has his vote because of his positions on immigration and energy.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MIHLBAUER: But what I like about him is he wants to control the border.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Control the border.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MIHLBAUER: He wants to keep drilling some oil so that the gas prices don`t go up to $4 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 DAWN POTTER (Wisconsin voter): Trump has said things about women that totally inflame me. But I am a Republican from the word go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAWN POTTER: We cannot discuss politics.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sisters Dawn Porter and Sharon Peterson have different ideas about where this country is headed.\par \par \uc2 You said that Trump has said inflammatory things about women. He also brags about overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 DAWN POTTER: And that`s wrong. I`m a Republican, but nobody has the right to tell me what I can do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With your body.\par \par \uc2 DAWN POTTER: With my body.\par \par \uc2 SHARON PETERSON (Wisconsin Voter): Trump, I disliked from day one, and I don`t trust him. And every time he opens his mouth, there`s a fricking lie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you believe that we, as a country, are ready for a woman to be president?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN RORAFF (Wisconsin Voter): I don`t think they are. But a lot of white women feel -- `Cause I live in a small town, and I hear people talking. A lot of them are factory workers. Men -- Women are (bleep) when they`re in positions of power.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You hear that.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN RORAFF: All the time. Yes. So I think being a white woman, I think we`re our worst enemy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We needed to stop for more gas, but more importantly, more cheese curds. But let`s not get sidetracked.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re headed to Waukesha County, a county just outside of Milwaukee that Trump won in `16 and in `20.\par \par \uc2 We made our final stop in the town of Oconomowoc. Lunch today was at the Crafty Cow, where this family-owned spot pairs some of the tastiest food and some of the warmest hospitality in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Do you know who you`re voting for?\par \par \uc2 JUDY PIERCE (Wisconsin Voter): Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And who is that?\par \par \uc2 JUDY PIERCE: Do we say? Yes, I know, it`s Harris. When Trump calls us losers and suckers, because I`m a veteran, how would that make you feel? I mean, would you vote for him? Would you say, "I really like you. I`m going to vote for you"?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY LUCAS (Wisconsin Voter): But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can`t relate to being a veteran. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can`t relate to growing up in the Midwest on a farm. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can`t relate to the common person.\par \par \uc2 LILY NYMAN (Wisconsin Voter): I`m going to vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ELLIOT SWONGER (Wisconsin Voter): I`m also leaning more towards Kamala as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: 21-year-old Lily Nyman and 22-year-old Elliot Swonger worry about abortion access and worry about how divided the country has become.\par \par \uc2 LILY NYMAN: As a woman living in this country, I`m definitely worried about, like, abortion access. I`m not very well-equipped to have kids right now, and so I think that there are a lot of women out there that, like, in the off chance that, you know, I get pregnant for whatever reason, I want to make sure I have safe access to get a procedure like that done.\par \par \uc2 ELLIOT SWONGER: In my family as well, there have been some differences in political opinions, and that`s driven us apart.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ELLIOT SWONGER: I wish it didn`t have to be that way. I wish we didn`t have to play teams like red team versus blue team.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want to say thank you to 4 Mile Restaurant, Greenwoods Cafe, and the Crafty Cow -- Crafty Cow for their hospitality, which rivals the Southern hospitality. The people in the Midwest were great.\par \par \uc2 But when we drove through those cornfields, just about every line had a Trump sign.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Flags everywhere, billboards everywhere, Trump campaign offices almost on every other block. It was a surprising number of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  supporters.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I just feel like this election is more balanced in areas that we might assume could be leaning one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you know what`s real? We talk about the silent Trump voter who doesn`t say yes to a poll. From your reporting and from other reporting, it seems like there`s a silent sexism voter as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s not ready for a woman.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To be the boss.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, and we picked up on that.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Well, I was jealous you got Wisconsin, by the way, because I got Wisconsin for "3 Meals" during the midterms. But I`m so glad you went to Waukesha. Waukesha is one of the WOW counties, Waukesha, Ozaukee, and Washington counties, which are outside of Milwaukee, and those are critical for either side to win.\par \par \uc2 And it really is, like you said, it`s anyone`s race, and that`s where our polling shows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, and another thing I found fascinating was Trump supporters, who were strong in their stance, almost the common thread was, he says things I don`t agree with, but I still am going to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s always that but in the sentence.\par \par \uc2 You had a lot of cheese, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, I ran it off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did you? Vlad mentioned the pelvic disruption earlier. You doing okay? -\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, I`m doing fine, I`m doing fine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, "The Good Place" star Kristen Bell will be here to talk about her return to TV and the new romantic comedy, "Nobody Wants This."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Good Place" NBC)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Nobody Wants This" \uc2 Netflix\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why she wanted Adam Brody to star opposite her as the hot rabbi.\par \par \uc2 It`s true, he`s a hot rabbi in this. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This morning, we are getting an early look at today`s episode of "The Drew Barrymore Show" one of my favorites. It features candid conversations about menopause with our friend, CBS News contributor, Lisa Ling, and actor, Valerie Bertinelli.\par \par \uc2 This is Lisa`s first public conversation about her experience with menopause.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Drew Barrymore Show")\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It has hit me like a ton of bricks.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE (Actor and film producer): Really?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Honestly, I`m always--\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Tell me everything.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: I will tell you everything. I`ve always taken care of myself. I`ve always eaten healthy. I`ve been a cardio junkie. And so a couple of years ago, really when I turned 50, I started experiencing horrible brain fog and mild anxiety.\par \par \uc2 I never really got too many hot flashes every now and then.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: That`s right, Ling. - Say it out loud, say it proud.\par \par \uc2 (Audience Laughter)\par \par \uc2 VALERIE BERTINELLI (Actor): Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Well, and then Drew, so I went to my GP, who is a lovely woman, and she started talking to me about getting a referral to see a psychologist and possibly taking some SSRIs. Not once, and I was 50 years - -\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRMORE: What`s an SSRI?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: They`re like psych meds, meds for depression, things like that. Not once, and I was 50 years old when I had this appointment, not once did she mention menopause. And then I heard you talking about menopause and perimenopause, and you started talking about brain fog, and I started thinking, could that be what I`m going through?\par \par \uc2 And sure enough, when I finally went to an OB and started talking to other women in my life, I realized that I am going through a major hormonal imbalance as a result of what I and every woman on the planet will go through, which is menopause and perimenopause.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I can`t believe this is such a miracle.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Okay, I have to watch this later. The Drew Barrymore Show`s menopause episode featuring Lisa Ling and Valerie Bertinelli airs today, check your local listings.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, we have "Talk of the Table" local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. And it`s time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Tony, you are in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, my "Talk of the Table" is the US News and World Report college ranking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The list of the schools you almost def