ike, one of your sisters. I was giddy, you know, when, like, you guys were having the chemistry. I was like -- and then he said something, but he said something, and I`m like, oh, they`re so funny. They`re meant for each other.\par \par \uc2 It is so good. Now, you`re also executive producer, so what other things does that entail? What is your favorite part of being executive producer besides also the acting part?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I like paying attention to where the storylines are going, because when you`re an executive producer, you know a lot of behind-the- scenes info about where your character`s going to end up, and you can add little, like, Easter eggs or seeds and sort of play the character in a in a fun way. But I also really like the scheduling of it all to be honest.\par \par \uc2 I`m kind of like an AD at heart. That`s the person who like, writes down the schedule and tries to make it the most efficient, and I`m really good at like our family calendar.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you`re A type.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I guess.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Do you put color coordination on your on your calendar?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: No, I`m not --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Or do you like -- not at that level.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I`m not that good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you at that level?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I just try to make sure they don`t overlap.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so what is Dax like in your, in your personal relationship when it comes to finding the balance. You said you were scheduled --\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You figure things out. Is he your opposite?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: No, he`s pretty good at schedule as well. He`s my opposite in every other way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every other way.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: Yeah, yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That seems like a lot of ways.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: It`s all the ways.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you say opposites bring the best out of each other.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I think so. It certainly does in my relationship because we`re constantly disagreeing about things and it just keeps it fun and interesting, but there`s a level of trust where I always want to hear what his opinion is, even if I disagree. It`s very stimulating.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got another question in the vein of art and life affecting one another, so you`ve got two daughters, you play a relationship podcast host in the show. So you`re giving advice on relationships. Do you and Dax have ideas? What kind of advice you`re going to give your daughters as they get older and go out there in the world?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I have no idea. I think that`s really going to depend on the need of the child.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I mean, what if they want it desperately want to get married at 18? What if they don`t ever want to get married? What if they want to have kids? What if they don`t? I think it`s so individual.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re just going to say "as long as you`re happy."\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I mean kind of --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: Yeah. Unless they ask for advice.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And maybe not put it out -- all out there on a podcast like your character.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: No, not about our kids.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Although she hesitated there. She was like maybe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve been in this industry for a long time. I mean "Veronica Mars" is what 20 years ago? There are young boys and girls that are looking to get into this industry, and take this journey, which you know has its highs and lows.\par \par \uc2 What is your best bit of advice for them?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: You got to hustle. You got to hustle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: I mean for me, I never expected it to come to me, I knew I just had to keep putting myself out there and you just have to make sure you`re okay with doing that in the midst of a lot of rejection.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And you`ve done such good work that I think makes the world a happier place.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, we appreciate.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Anna from "Frozen" is how you`re best note in my household so many amazing projects.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wait, are you the voice of Anna from "Frozen"?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Get right out of my head, oh my god.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And you sing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kristen Bell.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: Yeah, it`s all I`m a singing voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You`re so talented.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was today years old, Kristen Bell, thank you very much. "Nobody Wants This" her current project premieres Thursday on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . Find someone you love, sit down and watch it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That does it for us. Tony and I are going to go upstairs and watch "Frozen" for the first time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let it go, let it go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- CBS Mornings, you know the song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course, I know all of it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240924ek9o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 More Deadly Lebanon Explosions; Battleground New York; Diddy Denied Bail; Kentucky Highway Shooting Suspect Found; DOJ Sues Owner of Ship in Bridge Collapse; \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  files for Bankruptcy; YouTuber MrBeast Lawsuit; Federal Reserve Cuts Interest Rate; New Study on Alcohol and Cancer Risk; NASA`s Hidden Figures Honored; Football Hall of Fame Nominees; Batty End to Yankees-Mariners Game; Revealing the Cast of "Beyond the Gates," A Look at Israel`s Mossad Agency; Ordaining Women; Saving on Car Insurance; Scarlett Johansson Transformed; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Imtiaz Tyab, Michael  Morell, Nikole Killion, Jericka Duncan; Jill Schlesinger, Carter Evans,  Celine Gounder, Chris Livesay, Lisa Ling \par \uc2 13,917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The sound of trumpets on a Thursday, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is on assignment today, but we`re still here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A second wave of deadly explosions rocks Lebanon, as Israel says, a new face of the war has begun. We are in Beirut with the latest.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANIA EL HALLAK (Emergency room doctor): We started seeing people all blown up. It was horrific. I can`t even explain the scenery at that time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Both candidates miss out on a crucial endorsement as the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  declines to pick anybody for president.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): They looked at her, they said, we are not going there. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Music mogul, Sean "Diddy" Combs remains behind bars this morning after a second judge denies him bail.\par \par \uc2 MARC AGNIFILO (Attorney for Sean Combs): We`re going to fight this case with all of our might until we don`t have to fight any longer.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: They have products in nearly every kitchen, but \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  is filing for bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: To facilitate a sale process.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Taco Bell has teamed up with the folks at National Day Calendar. So that National Taco Day will always be on a Tuesday, the first Tuesday in October.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): The first time a corporation has managed to change the calendar since Target moved Christmas to September.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): For the first time since the pandemic, the Fed cut interest rates by half a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 RONNY CHIENG ("The Daily Show"): I guess this is a big deal. Lowest interest rates mean lower car payments, credit card payments, and, of course, cheaper home loans. So you know that house you couldn`t afford? Well, a half point decrease means that now you can afford a bus ticket to drive past it.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 RONNY CHIENG: American Dream alive.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s making jokes, but they are saying, it`s going to make a big difference for people trying to buy a house, thinking about buying a house.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you`re close, it will get you a little closer, but he is not wrong.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and they were expecting a quarter point. They got half, that`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s very good.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with this, though, that second wave of deadly explosions across Lebanon, which \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  blames on Israel. On Tuesday, it was a pagers that exploded -- you remember that -- and yesterday, it was handheld radios that are like walkie talkies that blew up, including at a funeral in Beirut for some of those who had been killed in the first attack.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (People screaming)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lebanese officials say this new wave killed at least 20 people and injured hundreds more, including a lot of children.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is in Beirut with the latest on this story. Imtiaz, good morning. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Gayle, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, frankly, we are now in uncharted territory after a second day of synchronized explosions of hundreds, if not thousands of communication devices turned bombs here in Lebanon that are being blamed on Israel, with many people here telling us they no longer trust their own handheld devices.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: A funeral in southern Beirut for four people, including a child killed in Tuesday`s pagers turned bomb attacks, quickly became a scene of terror after a second wave of mass explosions, this time walkie talkies carried by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  members detonated right before their eyes.\par \par \uc2 The explosions were once again simultaneous, killing at least 20 people and wounding over 450.\par \par \uc2 Although it was smaller in scale than the pager explosions just 24 hours earlier that killed 12 and left over 2,700 wounded.\par \par \uc2 After two days of what appears to be coordinated explosions, hospitals like this one in Central Beirut are now at breaking point, with so many here saying they`re worried about what will happen next.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANIA EL HALLAK: I`ve seen injuries --\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Including emergency doctor, Dania El Hallak, who has treated over 200 seriously injured patients.\par \par \uc2 What went through your mind when you learned what happened?\par \par \uc2 DR. DANIA EL HALLAK: That was shocking, because we use pagers in hospitals, and we were scared for ourselves that any pager in the hospital might explode at any time.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: As \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  scrambles to uncover how Israel may have breached and weaponized its communication networks, CBS News has learned a Taiwanese manufacturer, Gold Apollo, authorized a Hungarian company to use its logo to produce the pagers sold to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But in a statement, Hungary`s government told us the company was only a trading intermediary with no manufacturing site.\par \par \uc2 Security analysts believe the apparent shell companies could have been part of an elaborate front by Israeli intelligence, something Israel has neither confirmed nor denied.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And as \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  vows revenge for the booby trapped devices, Israel`s defense minister has announced a "new phase of the war," saying the center of gravity has shifted to the north and forces from Gaza are now being moved to the northern front, with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  sparking fears an all-out regional war is closer now than ever before -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Imtiaz Tyab for us in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on this, we are joined by Michael Morell, CBS News, national security contributor and former Acting CIA director.\par \par \uc2 Mike, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Welcome, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  as a terror group in the eyes of the \uc2 US government\uc2 , dozens of American dead on their hands, civilian, and soldiers alike. They`re also responsible for thousands of Israelis not being able to live in the northern part of the country.\par \par \uc2 And yet, one wonders what the goal of this attack was, how it accomplishes any long-term military ends for Israel, and I also want to get to the how first. How might this have happened?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: So three steps, Tony. The first is to get in between the manufacturer of these devices and the delivery of those devices to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Multiple ways to do that, you can break into a warehouse where they`re being stored for a long weekend, you know, make the changes you need to make, or you can actually become part of that supply chain and be a partial manufacturer. You can be a wholesaler. So that`s step one.\par \par \uc2 Step two is to get inside the device, put an explosive charge, put a detonator. Step three is to put some sort of triggering device into these communications pieces that you can make a phone call and set them all off at once.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As a covert operation, if indeed this was Israel, it is a stunning tactical victory. But what`s the goal of these explosions? If Israel is behind it, what do they accomplish?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Tony, I think this is preparation of the battlefield for an Israeli military operation over the next few weeks to a month or so of going into Southern Lebanon and trying to push \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  back north so that tens of thousands of Israelis who have not been able to return to their home since October 7th can go home.\par \par \uc2 So I think this is making it more difficult for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to communicate, command and control when those military operations by Israel begin.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So possibly a prelude to that military goal, I do wonder, though, long term, the solution here, I imagine, is not a military one, but a political one. Does this bring that any closer to reality?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Tony, I don`t know of any national security problem that has ever been solved with only military means.\par \par \uc2 You often need both military means and most importantly, a political solution and I think that`s what`s required here as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, political solutions have one thing that have very much been lacking in that region.\par \par \uc2 Michael Morell, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s turn now to the presidential race. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is getting some high profile campaign help today from \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rallied with supporters in New York, where a series of close congressional races could decide which party controls the House in January.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back in deep blue New York, an unlikely battleground.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s the worst kept secret in America. The battleground state for the House of Representatives is New York.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: With several critical down ballot races that could impact control of Congress, the former president rallied supporters on Long Island, Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We are going to win. New York.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Security was in force as Trump is now receiving the highest level of protection on par with that of a sitting US president, according to Secret Service. He recounted the latest apparent assassination attempt at his Florida golf course.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Thankfully, our outstanding Secret Service agent, and they are outstanding --\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : -- spotted the barrel of his rifle in the bushes. Can you believe that?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The former president also heaped praise on the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , claiming he won their endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I love the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . We won the overwhelming majority of the local chapters and the members.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But for the first time since 1996, the Union did not endorse a presidential candidate, stating that neither Trump nor Vice President Harris provided serious commitments to the group.\par \par \uc2 SEAN O`BRIEN (\uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president): It sends a message to both parties that if they truly want to support working people, they have to reevaluate and understand that nothing is given, it is earned.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In Washington, Harris also courted the Latino vote, addressing the Congressional Hispanic Institute on Immigration, as a new poll shows a majority of Americans surveyed support Trump`s position to deport migrants.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice President of the United States): How is that going to happen? Massive raids? Massive detention camps? What are they talking about?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The vice president campaigns tonight, headlining a virtual unity rally with Oprah. Meantime, the former president will remain here in New York, where he meets with Jewish Americans in Brooklyn -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Music mogul Sean "Diddy" Combs failed for a second time yesterday to be let out on bail, while awaiting federal charges including racketeering and sex trafficking. Jericka Duncan was there in court, and she joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Of course, as I was in the courtroom, I saw Combs was wearing the same clothes for the second straight day in court. When he walked into the hearing, he seemed confident, looked over at his family and waved, but after nearly two hours of listening to both sides, District Judge Andrew Carter, Jr. refused to grant Combs bail.\par \par \uc2 The 54-year-old tried for home detention, offering to post a $50 million bond, give up his cell phone and internet access and even restrict female visitors to family and the mothers of his children, but the judge said he had serious concerns about potential witness tampering and sided with prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 The once self-proclaimed "Bad Boy" was arrested earlier this week and pleaded not guilty to federal charges of sex trafficking, transportation to engage in prostitution and racketeering.\par \par \uc2 The multimillion dollar Grammy winner has lost his most valuable asset, his freedom. He is now being held at the Metropolitan Detention Center in Brooklyn, New York.\par \par \uc2 Combs faces up to life in prison if found guilty at trial, but it could be at least a year before that even begins.\par \par \uc2 Combs` lawyer maintains his innocence -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jericka, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Police in Kentucky say they believe they found the body of the suspect in a series of shootings alongside Interstate 75. The gunfire there wounded five people nearly two weeks ago, and police have been looking for a man named Joseph Couch ever since, after finding the shooter`s rifle and vehicle.\par \par \uc2 The body was found yesterday in a heavily wooded area near the scene of the attacks. The coroner has not yet identified the body, but police say they did find items alongside that are connected to Couch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Justice Department is demanding more than $100 million from the owner and operator of the cargo ship that knocked down the Francis Scott Key Bridge in Baltimore. That civil lawsuit claims negligence and cost cutting decisions led to the destruction of the bridge back in March after the ship lost power and collided with it.\par \par \uc2 The disaster as you may recall killed six construction workers who were on the bridge at the time and shut down the Port of Baltimore for months.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ calls this tragedy entirely avoidable. The ship`s owner and operator have not responded to our request for comment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: An iconic consumer brand is filing for chapter 11 bankruptcy protection. \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  has been around for nearly 80 years, but sales have been dropping recently.\par \par \uc2 The company says it intends to keep going while it looks for a new owner. It`s a big shock for a brand that literally became part of the language in America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  advertisement)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Tupperware\uc2 , just the name can evoke kitschy thoughts of women in the 1950s gushing over kitchen products. Truth is, when it was introduced in 1946, the product was game changing.\par \par \uc2 BOB KEALING ("Life of the Party" author): It was a revolutionary product because it worked and it kept food fresher longer with the vacuum seal.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bob Kealing is the author of "Life of the Party."\par \par \uc2 Invented by Earl Tupper, \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  wasn`t exactly flying off the shelves, so they were removed from stores.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  advertisement)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And sold through \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  parties where the containers could be demonstrated to curious housewives.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  advertisement)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The revolutionary airtight seal, the \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  burp --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  advertisement)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- was a sales practice spearheaded by divorced mom, Brownie Wise.\par \par \uc2 BOB KEALING: She would actually put grape juice inside a bowl and then seal it up, and then in someone`s very nice living room, she`d toss it across the room, and it would fall and thank goodness it didn`t spill open all over the nice carpeting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  parties gave women a toehold in the job market, and the \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  name became synonymous with food storage worldwide. Its products sold in 70 countries.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Minions: The Rise of Gru"/Universal Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And has been a mainstay in pop culture, but other less expensive, more easily available storage systems have bitten into \uc2 Tupperware\uc2 `s business, ultimately putting the iconic kitchen brand`s future in question.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Gayle, you were asking why --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I said, why is it going out of business? Can you show that part?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, there`s a lot of cheaper options, but consider that when it debuted, it was for selling for 39 cents. Now it`s about $43.00 for a three-piece set.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But everyone of a certain age has had a family member, an aunt or a mom who went or participated or held a \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I use the cheaper -- the cheaper stuff.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re questioning it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you`re part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it never occurred -- but I swear to you, it never -- the rare times I do make something, but it never occurred to me that it would be hurting \uc2 Tupperware\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Well, yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  is such an iconic brand.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look, it helps cut down on food waste. The one thing someone needs to invent is a way to keep track of the lids. Where do the lids go?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where are my lids?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, that is so true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where are my lids?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Where are those lids?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, MrBeast dominates \uc2 YouTube\uc2  these days, but now he`s facing sweeping accusations of harassment. We`ll show you what a proposed class action lawsuit is all about.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, our series, "The State of Spirituality" with Lisa Ling introduces us to the female priests who live for God, even if the Roman Catholic Church has rejected them.\par \par \uc2 Plus Scarlett. Johansson talks with us exclusively about starring in the highly anticipated "Transformers One."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Transformers One"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Find out why she loves voice work and how the role brought out her inner kid.\par \par \uc2 But up next, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is right here in our greenroom to break down that Fed interest cut and what it means for you exactly and your wallet.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by EGO, the number one rated brand in cordless outdoor power equipment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In today`s "Money Watch." For days now, we`ve been expecting some big, big news from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , and now it has finally happened.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the Fed announced a half point interest rate cut. That`s the first since March of 2020, so this is big.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to tell us what this means in normal people terms you could say.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Jill.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been hearing about this for a while, half a point. People say that doesn`t sound like very much, but it`s still bigger than what many thought.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes. We were looking at maybe a quarter of a percentage point. We got a half of a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 And what`s interesting about this is it`s a recognition by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that after fighting inflation for the last few years, it now needs to turn its attention to the rest of the economy. The Fed has got two main jobs, make sure the economy is strong enough to create enough jobs for anyone who wants one, and also control prices.\par \par \uc2 With inflation coming down, the Fed now says, okay, we`ve got to concentrate on the economy, dropping rates. They`re likely to do another half of a percentage point in total by the end of the year, and then get to about a three percent Federal Funds Rate over the course of the next two years. But it will happen gradually. It`s not going to happen all at once.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what does it mean, though? How are we going to see the difference? If you`re walking around the world.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : The way you`re going to see it is, you`ll see interest rates overall start to come down. So it means that when you`re borrowing money, maybe to buy a new car, maybe to finance that credit card bill that you have, you`ll start to see interest rates slowly go down.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re paying down that credit card balance, you`re going to get a chance to pay it down at a lower interest rate. If you`re going to buy a new or used car, those interest rates should come down.\par \par \uc2 If you`re going to get a new mortgage or refinance, those rates are not directly controlled by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , but they`ve already started to come down.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re if a borrower, rates come down. If you`re a saver, rates come down, not such good news.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Jill, part of the reason people have big credit card bills is because prices are still high. I was just in Georgia shooting a story in Peach, in Forsyth County, and people complain about prices constantly. They are still high, right? That`s true.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Absolutely. This is a huge point of confusion, and I get it. You say, well, wait a minute, the inflation rate is coming down. That`s what Fed Chair Powell said.\par \par \uc2 But prices are still high. That`s because the rate of increase of prices is coming down, but prices overall, they`re still high. Remember, we always have inflation in a growing economy.\par \par \uc2 Usually we have inflation of about two percent, but we saw inflation spike up to seven percent, that big spike pushed all prices up.\par \par \uc2 So even though they`re not going up by that much, they`re still a lot higher than they were five years ago, 20 percent higher or so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s big.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what needs to happen so people can say, okay, prices are coming down. We know gas is coming down. What needs to happen?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : What needs to happen is our wages start to go up faster than the inflation rate. And over time, if your wages are going up and the inflation rate -- it is going up by here and your inflation rate is here, you`ll feel better at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 It will take some time, and I bet, about another few years, before we put this period behind us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  with that news that you can use, love it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead some alcohol come with cancer warning labels like cigarettes. Should that happen? We`re going to look into a new report on drinking and cancer and find out what you can do to reduce your cancer risk.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- to CBS MORNINGS. Take a look at this dramatic video from outside Detroit. This deputy helped save a man`s life by climbing into a moving vehicle.\par \par \uc2 It started with a report of erratic driving, but it turns out the man was having some kind of a medical problem. The bodycam video from Macomb County Sheriff`s Deputy Nicole Miron shows her crawling through the driver`s side window, putting the truck into park and turning it off.\par \par \uc2 The driver was hospitalized. Good news, he is now recovering.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is unbelievable that she was able to do that while it was moving on the highway.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: While it was still moving, on the highway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On a highway. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Having a medical problem while driving is like a chronic fear of mine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Deputy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s an amazing step in by the --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, you hope that if you do have one Deputy Nicole Miron is there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To rescue you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Call Deputy Miron.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 All right, moving on now. One of the world`s best known online personalities, MrBeast, faces a lawsuit this morning. The suit, which seeks to be certified as a class action lawsuit accuses MrBeast production company and Amazon Studios of "chronic mistreatment and sexual harassment of contestants" in an upcoming reality show.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans looks now at the controversy kicked up by a \uc2 YouTube\uc2  giant.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MrBeast)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Twenty-six-year-old Jimmy Donaldson, better known as MrBeast is the biggest star on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , with more than 300 million subscribers.\par \par \uc2 He`s built an empire offering huge prizes to viewers on his videos.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MrBeast)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But now, five unnamed contestants in the upcoming \uc2 Amazon\uc2  reality show "Beast Games" are alleging Mr. Beast`s production company and Amazon Studios created a toxic and hostile environment that "laid the groundwork for sexual harassment."\par \par \uc2 They cite an alleged production guide called "How to Succeed in MrBeast Production" encouraging obscenities and directing staff to do everything to help the talent "be idiots."\par \par \uc2 Contestants also claim they were forced to endure unsafe conditions with a lack of access to medical care, food, and water, and they weren`t paid a fair wage.\par \par \uc2 These people were competing for $5 million. Are they contestants or are they employees?\par \par \uc2 LIZELLE BRANDT (Plaintiff attorney): You can call them whatever you want to, but if they meet the definition of an employee, it doesn`t matter what you title them.\par \par \uc2 Our argument is that they are employees under California law.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: The hope, dream, and expectation that you might get a payment is not the same as being an employee.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Jessica Levinson is a CBS News legal contributor.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: Certainly, I think there`s a real legal battle here that the plaintiffs will have to convince a judge that this group who signed on to be part of a show should be considered as employees, not contestants.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Amazon Studios and MrBeast production company declined to comment, but the accusations come after YouTuber, Jake Weddle, who claims to be a former MrBeast employee, recently alleged mental abuse during a 100-day isolation challenge.\par \par \uc2 JAKE WEDDLE (Former MrBeast employee): It got to a point where, like, they weren`t turning the lights off, you know, I asked him, I said, can we have, like, nighttime hours, you know? They said no.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A new report highlights the link between alcohol and rising cancer rates. We`re going to talk with Dr. Celine Gounder about the report and how to reduce your risk. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In today`s "Health Watch," a new cancer report is highlighting the deep link between alcohol and cancer rates.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  says excessive alcohol use may be one factor behind an increase in at least six cancers, including breast cancer and colon cancer.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder is a CBS News medical contributor and editor-at-large for public health at KFF and she joins us now, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So there will be a lot of people who will watch this, and they`ll say, you know, every couple of years they tell me that a glass of wine is good, and that helps me live a better life. And now you`re saying even any amount of alcohol can lead to cancer in these six types of cancers.\par \par \uc2 So what is this link that the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  has identified?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So we are seeing that with excessive alcohol use, so for women, that would be three or more drinks per day, seven or more drinks per week. For men, four more drinks per day, 14 or more drinks per week.\par \par \uc2 With excessive alcohol use, we are seeing an increased risk of various cancers -- head and neck, esophageal, stomach, liver, colorectal cancer, and for ladies, breast cancer. Some of this is happening through chronic inflammation. We also know that alcohol changes the microbiome. So those are the bacteria that live in your gut, and that can also increase the risk of cancer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So is there such a thing as a safe amount of alcohol?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re right?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: There is no "safe amount" of alcohol. Other studies have shown that any amount of alcohol consumption can increase your risk of cancer, particularly if you have underlying medical conditions.\par \par \uc2 But look, you know, I`m not sober. I drink just like you know, I think all of us at this table do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not Gayle, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t because I`m thinking, does that mean I`m going to live longer?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: You know, but I think it`s everything in moderation, right? And we have tradeoffs in life, and we take on certain risks because we enjoy them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you hear the headline though, Celine, and it just sounds so extreme that there`s a connection.\par \par \uc2 It`s almost like saying to people you shouldn`t drink at all. You`re saying moderation.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: If you don`t drink, don`t start drinking. And then if you do drink, really try to keep it within moderation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So where do you come down on the question of whether alcohol should have a warning label ala cigarettes?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, so the \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulates tobacco, the Department of Justice and the Department of Treasury regulate alcohol. So there are some differences in terms of jurisdiction, what authority they have.\par \par \uc2 There was a law passed in 1988 that allows for the labeling of alcohol with the warning that you can have issues if you`re drinking while pregnant, if you drink while driving, but it would require either an amendment to that law or a new law to have the authority to be able to put that kind of warning on alcohol.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is there anything you can do to prevent cancer? Sometimes it seems so random to me. People get lung cancer who have never smoked, or people get breast cancer who have no history of it in their family. Sometimes it feels so much like the luck of the draw. Is there anything that you can realistically do?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, there is sort of a checklist I can give you. So in addition to cutting back on drinking, quitting smoking. So anything that causes smoke could be smoking tobacco, cannabis, anything that causes smoke is going to increase your risk of lung cancer and other cancers. So quitting or cutting back on smoking.\par \par \uc2 Obesity we know is linked to cancer, so losing weight; exercise also helps, in addition to the weight loss. Wearing sunscreen when you`re out in the sun. And then finally, the HPV vaccine, human papillomavirus vaccine is really underutilized, and we`re seeing rates of cervical cancer and oral cancer related to HPV go up.\par \par \uc2 We, as the US, do a really bad job of vaccinating against HPV compared to other high income countries, and there`s a lot of room for improvement here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Dr. Gounder, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the stories you will be talking about today in "What to Watch" but right now, it is 7:41, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up for you in the eight o`clock hour, including a big ti