, but I didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. And actually, I think we`re on a really interesting cultural kick right now where we are going back in time and saying who are the people who, on their merit and by all rights, deserved credit back then, and giving it to them now, and in many cases when they`re still alive and that`s a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and I love that it was a bipartisan effort, that`s something we could all use --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- in this country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Mike Johnson shouted it out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Mike Johnson -- yes, very big deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we have a new list of nominees for the Pro Football Hall of Fame. It has 167 candidates, including quarterback Eli Manning, running back Marshawn Lynch; pass rusher Terrell Suggs, and quarterback Tony Romo, who`s now, of course, a big part of the \uc2 NFL\uc2  on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . Romo played for 11 seasons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do we get to vote?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t think -- somehow, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I vote for Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But we do love Tony Romo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course, he played for the Cowboys, a pro bowler for four times. The nominees will be whittled down. The newest inductees will be announced during Super Bowl Week.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All of these of are great names. Eli -- all of them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, Eli is getting --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Marshawn -- yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, let`s not get it twisted. Eli is getting in. Marshawn Lynch is getting in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Suggs is getting in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony is probably getting in, right? Am I right -- any football fans?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Klaus says no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s call Nate. Where`s Nate? He is in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is getting in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You don`t think Tony Romo is getting in?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yikes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Klaus doesn`t mean it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Broadcasting Hall of Fame for sure though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right, so that`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It looks like Klaus and Tony know each other.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I think Tony is going to be like the next time he sees you on Sunday, he is going to be like, why I ought to -- why I ought to --\par \par \uc2 Okay, let`s talk baseball now. You`ve got to see this one. This is one of the crazier plays you`ll see in the game. Let me set the scene.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Seattle Mariners\uc2  were down a run in the bottom of the 10th with a runner on third when this happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Two-two. Swing and a miss.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You just saw the bat fly out of the hands of the Seattle player that he struck out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Randy Arozarena.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 The runner had -- the runner on third had to dodge the flying bad, but ran too far from the base. \uc2 Yankees\uc2  quickly got the ball to third and tagged him out for a double play.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The third base coach is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So he is --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So he dodged the bat. He is taking a lead there, and so the game is still active. I mean, it`s in play, and so he`s got to go back to the bag and he doesn`t. He just does a little tap dance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Tony, isn`t he supposed to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Watch the third base coach. The third base coach has got his head on straight still is going -- he gets there. He has got his arms up. He`s going, what are you doing? What are you doing? Run. Run.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Tony, he had to dodge the bat, though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He can`t just stay there and get hit by the bat.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, you dodge the bat, but then go back to the base. But he dodges the bat, and then he did one of these, like, whew. Like whew. And he stood around. It was like -- and then he got tagged out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Then he got tagged out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can dodge, but then you can`t marinate in the dodge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Yankees\uc2  won that game. All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Tony is a baseball player.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We do know that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve never seen that before. That`s not a regular thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. This is very unusual, which is why we`re showing it in "What to Watch" --\par \par \uc2 We want to show you all of those unusual moments.\par \par \uc2 Also exciting and exclusive moments, including this announcement revealing the first cast members of the new \uc2 CBS\uc2  daytime drama "Beyond the Gates." It will be the first hour-long daytime soap on television featuring a Black cast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The first time, wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The show is set in an affluent suburb of Washington, DC. The first actors to join the cast are Tamara Tunie, Daphnee Duplaix, and Karla Mosley.\par \par \uc2 They are all part of the powerful and prestigious Dupree family, but behind their glamor, behind the elegance, there are dark secrets and scandals.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Look at that. You could be a Transformer voiceover actor yourself.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Beyond the Gates." Production begins this fall and is expected to premiere on \uc2 CBS\uc2  daytime early next year. Tun-tun-tun.\par \par \uc2 That`s what they do to commercial. This is a big deal, folks. This is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was amazing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re like what`s happening, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is an all-Black cast for a daytime soap opera.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. For the first time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s being done in conjunction with \uc2 NAACP\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I will be watching.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s going to be huge.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up -- coming up, ahead in a moment, Lisa Ling meet some women who are defying the Catholic Church as priests.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Scarlett Johansson is one of the voices in the new movie "Transformers One." Have you heard of it? Only on CBS MORNINGS, she talks with us about the role and why she is so excited to bring Transformers to a new generation.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in our eight o`clock hour, only on CBS MORNINGS, Oscar nominee, Scarlett Johansson talks about the new movie. Have you heard of it? "Transformers One."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Ling spends time with rebel women priests for our series "The State of Spirituality." Why they say fighting the Vatican is an expression of their Catholic faith.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also got a car insurance buyer`s guide from Consumer Reports. With prices skyrocketing, it is something a lot of people need -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, cupcakes. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Electronic devices explode across Lebanon for a second straight day, killing at least 20 and injuring hundreds.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Hospitals like this one in Central Beirut are now at breaking point. So many here saying they`re worried about what will happen next.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sean "Diddy" Combs denied bail again while awaiting trial on a racketeering and sex trafficking charges.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Combs faces up to life in prison if found guilty at trial, but it could be at least a year before that even begins.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  advertisement)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The widely imitated household brand, \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  files for bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 BOB KEALING: It was a revolutionary product because it worked.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cuts interest rates by half a percentage point. It`s the first rate cut in over four years.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Many economists predicted a quarter point cut now. Now, quarter, half -- I mean, it might not sound like much, but cutting a quarter as opposed to a half is the difference between cute bangs and cute bangs.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stephen Colbert, always on point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Always.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour, though, with this, that second wave of deadly explosions in Lebanon. You`ve heard about this. Those handheld radios now blew up across the country yesterday, including at a funeral for some of the victims of Tuesday`s attacks.\par \par \uc2 The radios are typically used by the militant group \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . The attacks are being widely attributed to Israel, which many say has a history of unusual tactics using those.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay reports from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: It`s the stuff of spy novels -- pagers and walkie-talkies exploding in the hands of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters. While Israel has not claimed responsibility, the attacks appear to bear the fingerprints of its feared foreign intelligence agency, \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , like in 2020 when a remote operated machine gun hidden in a pickup truck killed Iran`s top nuclear scientist, \uc2 Mohsen Fakhrizadeh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Two years before that, \uc2 Mossad\uc2  agents infiltrated a warehouse and stole the plans for Iran`s secret nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  showing them to the world.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli prime minister): Fifty-five thousand pages.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: And in 2009 Israeli and American intelligence agencies planted the infamous Stuxnet cyber worm into the centrifuges that enriched uranium in Iran`s Natanz facility. But while the Stuxnet worm brewed a hole into software, the operation detonating thousands of pagers in Lebanon drew human blood.\par \par \uc2 Guy Mizrahi is a cyber warfare expert who has worked with Israeli military intelligence.\par \par \uc2 GUY MIZRAHI (Skilled hacker): Now we know, when we -- you know, we have proof that cyber can hurt people. You know, do some kind of kinetic problem to the real world.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: It can physically hurt you, not just digitally or electronically.\par \par \uc2 GUY MIZRAHI: Yes. Yes. So it is not just ones and zeros, it can go boom.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Now, the big question in Israel is, why now? And will we see a cyber war evolve into a ground war?\par \par \uc2 Now, as both sides brace for a possible escalation, the US official confirms to CBS News that Israel`s 98th Division has been moved to Israel`s northern border where exchanges with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  are growing more fierce by the day -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Chris Livesay in Israel for us. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our series, "The State of Spirituality" with Lisa Ling meets the women pushing to revolutionize the Catholic Church. They want women to be ordained as priests, but first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, we continue our "Transformers One" countdown --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Transformers One"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- with award winning actor, Scarlett Johansson.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, we`re continuing our series, we call it "The State of Spirituality" with Lisa Ling that explores different paths to faith, God, and religion.\par \par \uc2 Today, we`re going to take a look at a grassroots movement, they are pushing to end a tradition that`s dating back nearly 2,000 years in the Roman Catholic Church, 2,000 years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CBS News contributor, Lisa Ling traveled to Albany, New York, to meet the women who are leading communities that are defying the church`s leadership.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: For hundreds of years, this has been the exclusive body at the helm of the Catholic Church -- men.\par \par \uc2 But inside this warehouse-looking building in upstate New York --\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN singing)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Rebels are convening, and they want change.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK (People`s Catholic Seminary founding member): Change begins by somebody doing it.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: This is the Upper Room described as an inclusive Catholic community led by congregants and a woman priest, Reverend Mary Theresa Streck.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: Continue to share the good news.\par \par \uc2 People are looking for what we`re offering, and they want to see ordained women celebrating mass.\par \par \uc2 BISHOP BRIDGET MARY MEEHAN (Association of Roman Catholic Women Priests): Mary Magdalene --\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: At today`s church service, the sermon is delivered by Bishop Bridget Mary Meehan.\par \par \uc2 BISHOP BRIDGET MARY MEEHAN: -- inspires us to create a renewed model of church.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Both women are part of the Association of Roman Catholic Women Priests, a worldwide movement to ordain Catholic women.\par \par \uc2 Around 64 percent of US Catholics support the idea of women priests.\par \par \uc2 Why the pushback from the institution? From the Vatican?\par \par \uc2 BISHOP BRIDGET MARY MEEHAN: I think they`re afraid of change.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: The Roman Catholic Church does not allow women to be ordained, arguing in part that clergy should be unmarried men in the image of Jesus and his 12 disciples.\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN praying "Your spirit moves through the love within us.")\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: These women disagree and have formed a resistance.\par \par \uc2 There are other Christian denominations that allow women clergy. Why remain part of a faith where you are considered illegitimate?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: Because it`s in my bones. I am a Roman Catholic.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Like many organized religions in the 21st Century, Catholic Church membership is declining. Many citing the way the church has dealt with sexual abuse claims involving priests and how it has historically handled divorce, the queer community, and women.\par \par \uc2 So did you leave the Catholic Church for a while?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY PERRY-COON (Upper Room parishioner): I think the Catholic Church left me.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: That is a sentiment echoed by most members of the Upper Room.\par \par \uc2 For many here, 2010 marked a turning point with their Catholic faith, after the Vatican listed ordaining women as a grave offense alongside pedophilia.\par \par \uc2 Would you say that these women priests brought you back to Catholicism?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY PERRY-COON: Most certainly they did. We are the Catholic Church here, too. So we are not apart from them.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: The Catholic women priests movement began back in 2002 when seven women were ordained on the Danube River in Germany.\par \par \uc2 The Vatican excommunicated all known to be involved and all those who have come after.\par \par \uc2 BISHOP BRIDGET MARY MEEHAN: Our response was, we do not accept this excommunication. We`re leading the church we love into a renewed model of priestly ministry to respond to the needs of Catholics all over the world.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Both Bridget Mary and Mary Theresa are former nuns, raised devout and proud Catholics.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: By the time I was in third grade, I wanted to be a nun. I knew in third grade.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: At just 17 years old, Mary Theresa says she was called to become a nun, until 18 years later, another call came.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: I met a young priest --\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Love.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: I knew that I loved him dearly, and he loved me dearly, so we both resigned.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And your faith never wavered.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: Oh, no, I think it was stronger than ever. We were actually married right within the public housing project where we ministered for many years.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: But after Jay died of cancer, Mary Theresa began to explore a new call -- the Catholic priesthood.\par \par \uc2 Did you know you`d be excommunicated by becoming ordained?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: When I first spoke with Bridget Mary, the very first call, the first thing that she said to me is, if you do this, you will be excommunicated. And I said, okay, well, that`s that doesn`t mean anything, really, because I will continue doing everything that I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: In 2013, hundreds were in attendance as Bridget Mary ordained Mary Theresa in Albany, a first in the area.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: Since 2013. we have 10 women who have been ordained. I think people are saying, I`m not going to wait for the church to decide it`s okay.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Around 300 women worldwide have now been ordained within the larger movement. Mary Theresa and Bridget Mary, who both have Doctorate Degrees in Ministry, created a process and seminary curriculum for ordination in the US.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that you will live to see women priests accepted in the Roman Catholic Church?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: Probably not. But that`s not a good reason not to do it.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Earlier this year, in a rare interview, the Pope addressed the issue of women clergy with CBS News` Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: For a little girl growing up Catholic today, will she ever have the opportunity to be a deacon and participate as a clergy member in the church?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS: No.\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN "Take and eat, this is my very self.")\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: But these women say that won`t stop them. They hope they`re laying the groundwork for future generations of women priests.\par \par \uc2 If you had an opportunity to have an audience with the Pope and those in the Vatican, what would you say to them?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND MARY THERESA STRECK: Do not be afraid of us. We have a lot to offer.\par \par \uc2 BISHOP BRIDGET MARY MEEHAN: We spent our whole lives just creating a church where there`s more justice, more equality, and that everyone is so welcome at the table of God`s love.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It sounds very Jesus-like.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: We reached out to the Albany diocese for comment, and it responded that it is not associated with this group. The Vatican also did not respond.\par \par \uc2 Next month, Mary Theresa and Bridget Mary are headed to Rome during the Vatican`s gathering of bishops, and in defiance of the church, the women will ordain six more people then, including a transgender priest.\par \par \uc2 They`ve also reached out to the Vatican in hopes of meeting with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , but have not received a reply -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lisa, you know, Lisa, part of what inspired your series is the fact that younger people are not joining organized religions of any kind. And so you might think, well, the Catholic Church should modernize, welcome women into the fold, make them priests, but is it just that they`re afraid they`ll lose the older contingent who`s been going to church this whole time?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Well, that`s the thing. I mean, the people have been leaving the Catholic Church in droves, and these women say that it`s time that the Catholic Church wakes up, because they believe that God is an inclusive God, and that is the ministry that they are preaching.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so interesting to see your visuals, Lisa, when you said, rebels are convening on the top floor, and then you see this group of women who are clearly determined, and then to hear that this is 2,000 years that they`re fighting against, a rule that, just because somebody said so. That`s what strikes me about it.\par \par \uc2 Somebody just made the rule and said, no women allowed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and somebody is not God. It wasn`t like from on high.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is not Biblical.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Somebody is are men.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are men.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re basically talking about men.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So do you see -- do you see this changing?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Well, I asked the women that question, do you think that you will ever see women become clergy in the Roman Catholic Church? And they don`t believe that in their lifetime, they will.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and in an industry journalism where you don`t often get clear answers, Norah O`Donnell asked the Pope, will we have female priests? No, he said. One word.\par \par \uc2 Lisa,. thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another eye-opening report, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the man, the myth, the hair, Consumer Reports, Brian Vines is here with some ideas on how you can save on car insurance, a sexy man with an unsexy topic, whether you can save some money.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Brian said, I look good.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This morning, we are sharing tips for drivers about how to save on those costly car insurance bills. The average price for full coverage in the US is more than $2,300.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 According to the latest government report, motor vehicle insurance rates are up 16.5 percent from last year.\par \par \uc2 To help drivers, Consumers Reports has now a buyer`s guide on new car insurance. Their deputy editor of special reports, Brian Vines joins us with some tips for lowering your car insurance payments.\par \par \uc2 Brian, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES (Consumer Reports deputy editor): Yes.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am sorry, I was looking for parking. I apologize.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He still -- this dude drives a car New York City.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I have big car insurance problems because I got my license suspended. But we`re not going to get into that. We`re not going to get into -- that`s a whole other story. I`ve told it before. You know the mugshot.\par \par \uc2 You can save $200.00 a year just by shopping and staying vigilant.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And taking a defensive driving course. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: All of these things are true. Number one, we should all be looking at those policies once a year as they renew. Don`t sleep on your car insurance policies. It might feel comfortable for your insurance person to call you up and say, I`m sending over the new policy. Sure. One less thing off your plate, but you can be saving money by really examining those things and shopping around at least once a year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It never occurs me to shop around for insurance. You know, you sign it with the same -- I only get frustrated when you go to make a claim, and then you get -- they do the hokey-pokey and turn themselves around not to pay you.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I just automatically just sign up for another year. It never occurs me to shop around. You say that`s foolish. We need to start doing that.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: It is imperative that you shop around, because rates can be competitive. They`re going up, but there is a lot of room among the competitors, so much --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Tony, you were asked about defensive driving, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, there is class you can take, right?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So there are things that they will -- if you do this, they`ll give you that, right?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Yes, if you do a defensive driving class, your insurer will give you a break on your premium. So just by taking those classes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How much of a break?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: You can save as much as $233.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, there it is. Okay.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: And it makes a difference. All of these things add up --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Add up.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: Especially if you know someone who`s 16 or 17, and put in that teenager on your insurance, it can drive premiums up by as much as $2,000.00, so it`s a great idea at any age.\par \par \uc2 And on the other end of the spectrum, if you`re over 70, that`s when your insurance rates start to creep up again, because 16-year-olds have high rates of collisions, and by the time you reach 70, those things start to creep up a little more so defensive driving for seniors.\par \par \uc2 AARP offers a discount for a AAA class that gives seniors tips on defensive driving to keep those seasoned drivers safe and keep their rates lower.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that word "seasoned."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, very good. Seasoned drivers.\par \par \uc2 But you mentioned something interesting. You talked about collision, and you say that another way to save potentially a thousand dollars --\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is to drop collision and comprehensive insurance, which to me sounds risky, even when I`m renting a car, I`m like, give me everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I only sign it for -- yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Give me everything to make sure nothing happens.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: You have that calendar -- you`re like, put it on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Put it all on there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VINES: So one of the things that you really should be diligent about is go to that Kelly Blue Book and see if your premium for your collision and the other is comprehensive.\par \par \uc2 If the value of your car is within 10 percent of what your premium is, you might want to renegotiate. In other words, you might be paying an 