ictures)\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT JOHANSSON: She has a healthy ambition. She believes in her ability. She has, you know, all of these qualities that really make a leader. And that`s like, what she is born to be.\par \par \uc2 She`s really born to be in a leadership position. You know, she`s a little bit of a control freak, which I may or may not know something about. Hard to say. You have to ask Colin.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, it`s definitely, you know, I think we sort of imbibe the characters with our own, you know, our own flavor that comes really from us as people. And I think that`s why the characters feel, you know, human in a way, these sentient sort of robots have that human quality.\par \par \uc2 Like you need the nuance to really make the story, I think, to have the story connect with the audience in the way that it does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Scarlett, when we had Keegan-Michael Key on and he described his approach to voice work, he said he runs in the booth if the character is running, he falls in the booth if the character is falling.\par \par \uc2 Do you take that same involved, fully committed approach? How do you do it?\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT JOHANSSON: Yes, I mean, there`s -- it`s interesting, like, when you go in and so they`ll be -- I`ve had to do so much voice work for these kind of, like, big action films because, of course, I did all the Avengers movies for like a decade of time.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m used to doing just absurd things in the sound with whatever helps you kind of get the feeling of movement. Because you really can`t keep -- if you`re just standing there and you`re doing these kind of grunts and efforts, it sounds very stayed so you have to use there`s like a bar that they put in front of you, and you find yourself doing all kinds of crazy stuff.\par \par \uc2 But hopefully nobody has any video recordings of it, because it definitely would be extremely embarrassing to see out of context, because you`re like, squattin