c2 ELIZABETH WERNER (Lifestyle expert): Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: It is so good to see you.\par \par \uc2 We went to college together. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So National Preparedness Month. This is when as a family, we take stock and get ready for any natural disasters -- weather, tornadoes, hurricanes, like we had at \uc2 University of Rhode Island\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So I went online with my hubby. We printed out the FEMA checklist of what you need to have to be ready. Number one, on their checklist, food, water.\par \par \uc2 Number two, a charger, right, for your phone, just in case. Number three, a radio with NOAA alerts with batteries or a hand crank. And number four, a flashlight.\par \par \uc2 This has all of the above, not the food in the water.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: But this is from ETON. Inside of this AM, FM, shortwave radio, and NOAA alert, so it will actually turn on and tell you there`s a tornado, there`s a wildfire, get moving. So it saves lives. Really amazing.\par \par \uc2 It`s got power. You can plug in all your devices to this. It`s got a 5,200 mAh battery built in, so you`re going to be able to use a USB and charge all your devices.\par \par \uc2 To power this, you can plug it in, but also three AAA batteries, a hand crank, solar, and finally, it said, flashlight. We`ve got your flashlight also and a lantern. This has everything you need. $97.99. It`s 25 percent off. This can save lives.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it. This is in my go bag.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My wife thinks I am nuts of having one, but it`s instrumental to be preparing.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Very, very important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, I love this thing.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, I do.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Three out of 10 people last year said that they had their identity stolen. What do people do? They dumpster dive. They go through your trash. They get your personal information.\par \par \uc2 This is the solution. It`s called ID Defender. You can see a pattern here. It`s patented. You`re going to take it and just roll it over your personal information just like this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let`s show people how I use this.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because I don`t want people looking through my garbage. I am a man of a certain age has some pills. This doesn`t work with a Sharpie.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: No, you can still see through it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You think it does. You can still see through.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When you use this.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: But now, we can actually go through our packages, our bottles, and we`re going to be able to eliminate all of that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No one will ever know what kind of medication I`m on.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That`s it. Set of three, $24.99. We don`t want to know. Forty-eight percent off.\par \par \uc2 Okay, laundry detergent. Here`s what most of us have been using. It`s big, it`s heavy, and it`s full of chemicals that are not good for us or for the waterways.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: This is called Clean People. These are laundry detergent sheets. So they come in one sheet. You can rip them in half, 96 loads, eco- friendly, hypoallergenic. Dermatologist tested.\par \par \uc2 They work so well. You throw them right under the washer, any washer, high efficiency, regular top loader, front loader, you name it. You can also hand wash with these.\par \par \uc2 Put it in the sink, put it in the tub, they will dissolve. We`ve got lots of different scents. Go to CBSDeals.com and find yours, unscented and baby.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For the baby. This is what we use for little baby Celine.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Baby. Exactly. $21.50, thirty-five percent off. I`ve switched to these. This is your opportunity. Try them. See what you think.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, and last but not least, where are we going?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes. This is Sporticulture Steinbacher. Now check this guy out. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 In suburbia, we love Halloween. We completely deck out --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not just in suburbia.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And here, too. You`re going to have this in Brooklyn, all right, then my son is in Baltimore. He is definitely going to have the Ravens.\par \par \uc2 So it`s splashed with all your favorite \uc2 NFL\uc2  and college teams. You`ve got the cool Halloween. Take this tailgating. Plug it in. It`s got its own fan in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll stand over here.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, and honestly, this is like the center of attention.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m going to bring this to Tony`s backyard.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Please.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: It has got LED lights so your neighbors will drive by at night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it only just football?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Football. Yep. You`ve got \uc2 NFL\uc2  and college. I hope they have \uc2 University of Rhode Island\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I hope. They better.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: They better, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They better have URI. Rhode Island born, Rhode Island bred.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Having a party, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are having a party.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: I mean, this is the photo op right here, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Elizabeth Werner --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Good to make the place --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Folks, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To buy anything you saw today, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBS Deals.com.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a big goodbye.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Goodbye.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Your favorite show. Have a great day. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240919ek9j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump and Harris Spar in First Debate; Voters React to Presidential Debate; Breaking Down the First Presidential Debate; Explosive California Wildfire; Hurricane Francine Approaches Louisiana; \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  Endorses \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; Dishin` in Michigan; Taking on IUD Pain; Muni in the Bank; Remembering the Lives Lost on 9/11; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Weijia Jiang, Nikole  Killion, John Dickerson, Adam Yamaguchi, Dave Malkoff, Stephanie Abrams, Jo  Ling Kent, Ed O`Keefe, Major Garrett, Celine Gounder \par \uc2 13,728 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this September 11th, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): World leaders are laughing at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I have talked with military leaders, some of whom worked with you, and they say you`re a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A tense night in Philadelphia as vice president Harris and former President Trump face off in their first presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): It will be the end of our country. She`s a Marxist. Everybody knows she`s a Marxist.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Stunning scenes of destruction in California, where out of control wildfires are forcing thousands to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It came ripping through here and we heard a couple bangs. We knew it was a house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Millions are under a hurricane warning this morning as Francine barrels toward Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I wasn`t really taking it serious until now. I see, it`s really real.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: A federal investigation is underway after two planes collided in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: The tail section on the smaller jet ripped off.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: A judge has ordered rapper \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  to pay a Michigan man $100 million.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: It`s because of an alleged sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : They`re eating the dogs, the people that came in, they`re eating the cats. They`re eating -- they`re eating the pets.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Let`s call it time of crazy 9:31 AM.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  says she endorses \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for president. This announcement coming shortly after the debate.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("The Daily Show" host): You know what this means. \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  and I were watching TV, the same program at the same time.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s true. It`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Yes, I think a lot of people felt that. Let`s see what Swiftie nation does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very interesting night last night and you know, it`s a big night in politics when the headlines include Harris, Trump, cats and a Taylor Swift endorsement. That`s what happened last night, using Taylor`s phrase, there was plenty of bad blood last night -- that`s one of her songs -- first and possibly only presidential debate between Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Harris was consistently on the offense against Trump. We`ve got team coverage, but we`re going to begin with Weijia Jiang, who was there in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning to you. How are things looking on this morning after?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning to you, Gayle. Good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 Well, both sides are claiming victory after a contentious debate that was filled with heated exchanges. Vice President Harris repeatedly tried to bait former President Trump and her team says he took it every time.\par \par \uc2 When the debate was over, Harris almost immediately said she`s ready for a second one. Trump says he`s still thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let`s have a good debate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Nice to see you. Have fun.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: That was the most cordial moment of the night, but the debate was on from the first topic the economy.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left us the worst unemployment since the Great Depression.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She doesn`t have a plan. She copied Biden`s plan, and it`s like four sentences like, run, Spot run.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And on the issue of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The government and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  certainly should not be telling a woman what to do with her body.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Each individual state is voting. It`s the vote of the people now. It`s not tied up in the federal government. I did a great service in doing it. It took courage to do it.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In Taylor Swift`s endorsement, she cited Harris` choice of \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  as her running mate, and is standing up for a woman`s right to her own body. Harris tried to provoke her opponent again when foreign policy took center stage.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: It is absolutely well known that these dictators and autocrats are rooting for you to be president again, because they`re so clear they can manipulate you with flattery and favors.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Putin endorsed her last week, said, I hope she wins, and I think he meant it, because what he`s gotten away with is absolutely incredible. It wouldn`t have happened with me.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: A former prosecutor, Harris tried to make the case against Trump by highlighting his legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think this is so rich coming from someone who has been prosecuted for national security crimes, economic crimes, election interference.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: While Trump blamed the Democrats` rhetoric for the July assassination attempt against him.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I probably took a bullet to the head because of the things that they say about me. They talk about democracy. I`m a threat to democracy. They`re the threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: One of the most fiery exchanges of the night came when Harris criticized Trump`s rallies.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: You will see during the course of his rallies, he talks about fictional characters like Hannibal Lecter. He will talk about windmills cause cancer. And what you will also notice is that people start leaving his rallies early out of exhaustion and boredom.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : People don`t go to her rallies. There`s no reason to go. And the people that do go, she`s busing them in and paying them to be there.\par \par \uc2 We have the biggest rallies, the most incredible rallies in the history of politics.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Trump also defended his questioning Harris` racial identity.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I read where she was not Black, that she put out, and I`ll say that, and then I read that she was Black, and that`s okay. Either one was okay with me. That`s up to her.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think it`s a tragedy that we have someone who wants to be president who has consistently over the course of his career, attempted to use race to divide the American people.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And when the former president tried to turn the debate to immigration, he furthered lies about immigrants in an Ohio town.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : In Springfield, they`re eating the dogs, the people that came in, they`re eating the cats. They`re eating -- they`re eating the pets of the people that live there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: A spokesperson for the city of Springfield tells CBS News that there have been no credible reports or specific claims of pets being harmed by immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Following the debate, Trump made his way to the spin room, an unconventional move to get his message out, where he declared it was his best debate ever -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for the clarification on the eating of the pets, Weijia. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion watched the debate last night at Penn Taproom in Doylestown, Pennsylvania, alongside voters with a wide range of views.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning. What did they tell you?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 We are in the heart of Bucks County, which is a competitive swing county here in Pennsylvania. The voters we talked to said that they didn`t necessarily see eye to eye on this debate, but they did agree on a few things.\par \par \uc2 For instance, they appreciated that former President Trump and Vice President Harris shook hands before the debate. They also felt that the candidates should have spoken more about policy and gotten less on the attack. And one issue that was top of mind for all of them, immigration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HEIDI AULI (Supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ): We need to do something with the border. It needs to be closed. I am a business owner, and this is something that I -- you know, struggle with continuously, and I have been struggling with.\par \par \uc2 EDDIE RUHL (Supporting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ): I agree we need strong border controls. I don`t believe that we can deport everybody in the country. It isn`t physically possible, and our economy would collapse if we did.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Did it surprise any of you that this conspiracy theory about Haitian migrants and cats came up during this part of the debate? And what was your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 EDDIE RUHL: Yes, even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I did not think was going to go there.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN BISHOP (Supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ): I was initially -- I had that same reaction. I was like, what is he doing?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI AULI: I was shocked. I was -- I said to myself, what is he doing? So it took me back to when COVID came, and he was calling it the China virus.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: We also talked about some of the exchanges over reproductive rights. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BRIDGET FOLEY (Supporting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ): I think that Kamala stood her ground on her position. I think that she`s always been more pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI AULI: He kind of avoided it. He didn`t want to touch the subject.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Are you concerned about an abortion ban?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI AULI: In that -- in that aspect, I am. I do believe in a woman`s right to choose in certain circumstances.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN BISHOP: She tried to emphasize that it`s -- it`s the voters rights to play like abortion protections and make it a state referendum. He tried his best to try and give a non-answer, deflect it, because that`s really all he could do at that point.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Most of the group was split between Harris and Trump voters who told me that the debate mostly reinforced their views about the candidates. But one voter was undecided. He said after the debate, he is considering Harris, although he`s not completely sold.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DECKLES (Undecided voter): I`ve been getting slightly irritated with actually, the both of them, especially with people like me, as a veteran and as somebody that`s kind of on the fence for either or. They`re not making it easy to choose somebody that I feel as though is actually right. They are not giving any solutions to any of the problems, and from there, it just seems like it`s more entertainment than it is actually debating on problems.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: All of the voters said that they wanted to hear about other topics that didn`t come up last night, like how the candidates may support veterans and also what they would do to bring down the national debt.\par \par \uc2 So they all believe that there should be another debate. Whether or not they get one remains to be seen -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There was a lot of personality last night, that`s for sure, and we`ll see about another debate.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by CBS News chief political analyst, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , host of "The Daily Report" with \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  on CBS News 24/7 to talk about what happened last night. Let`s not get ahead of ourselves talking about this next debate.\par \par \uc2 Your takeaways -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, the biggest takeaway is that Vice President Harris had a plan, she stuck to her plan and what was her plan? Her plan was to bait \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and when he responded in the way she hoped, when he took the bait to then literally point at him, or figuratively point at him and say, we don`t want to go back to that, to create the conditions in the debate itself that would remind people of the parts of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  they don`t like, and then say, we don`t want to go back to that and she successfully executed that plan.\par \par \uc2 In this country that`s so evenly divided, we don`t know how voters will respond, but when you go into a debate and you have a plan and then you can execute it, that`s about as well as you can do if you`re a candidate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why does it matter that he seemed to take the bait? Because surely he must have -- surely he or his team anticipated that she`s going to try to goad you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All the reporting beforehand was, don`t take the bait. Why does this matter? It`s a great question. This is about the presidency. What happens in the presidency? You get hit with things from all different sides, and your natural impulse sometimes will be to take the bait. The presidency is a job of quick action, but it`s also a job of restraint and temperament, and you need to be able to control your emotions and on things that were very small, he seemed to react emotionally.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re trying to look at who can handle the pressures of the job, that`s not what you want to put forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And any other walk in life, I will ask you this question, and the answer really does matter, but I`m going to ask you, as it pertains to the debate last night. Does truth matter because there were some falsehoods being spread, some exaggerations, some mixed truths by Trump. Will that have an impact on his voter base or those that are hanging in the balance?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, one of the things that`s hanging in the balance is whether truth matters. I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said some crazy stuff about cats and dogs, but the craziest thing he said is that he won the last election. I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And still continues to say that, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still to this day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And had an opportunity --\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : To fix that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To fix that last night.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now he`s so far gone on that issue, he can`t walk it back. But I think the important thing to know is, you know, because some of his supporters will say, well, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  changed her positions on things. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in a totally different category.\par \par \uc2 When we talk about mistruths and lies, the important thing to look at is frequency and seriousness and so it -- you know, all politicians shade things a little bit, but it`s like saying, if you have a cook that adds a little too much salt to the meal, well, one cook adds a little too much salt to the meal, the other cook just gives you a box of salt.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the box of salt. He is constantly saying things that don`t have a basis in fact and that matter whether there was a free and fair election is at the center of the democracy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always love your analogies, box of salt, I totally get that.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about the Taylor Swift endorsement. People say celebrity endorsements don`t matter, but it`s interesting the timing of her endorsement, and it was so well thought out when she made reference to AI on the Trump side that, you know, she wants people to do their own due diligence.\par \par \uc2 Will it make a difference? And what did you think of the timing?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The way -- right -- the way she wrote it was, I`m just any other voter out there, and this is what I want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But it was, I was struck, actually. Usually what happens is, when you have a bad night, you announce something big, and I`m not suggesting there was coordination, but usually announce something big to kind of swamp the bad performance.\par \par \uc2 Harris had as good a performance as they could probably want, both in her execution and in the response that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made to her debate performance. So I was a little surprised at that, but Taylor Swift creates her own weather in the world, so she can do whatever she wants and the Harris campaign has got to be thrilled with this kind of one-two punch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think it will make a difference for the voters?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I think if you get everybody`s head that has not been paying attention to politics, to swivel to the campaign right now, this is a good day for them to be looking at \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For what it`s worth, the Harris campaign says there was no coordination, but the voter registration tidbit within the \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  post, that could have a big difference, because people can register and vote early.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , thank you so much. We appreciate you popping in.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we are tracking some dangerous wildfires in the West, which make up most of the 71 major fires burning across the lower 48 states.\par \par \uc2 Nine of them are in California, including the Airport Fire south of Los Angeles, which is burning out of control.\par \par \uc2 Adam Yamaguchi is watching the growing threat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Explosions rocked this neighborhood as the Airport Fire ripped through burning homes and cars in its path. The wind driven wildfires south of Los Angeles raged Tuesday night, crossing over Orange and Riverside Counties north of San Diego and jumping a major highway.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters were seen battling the blaze from the road, clearing the way for evacuees to escape. As the fire devoured surrounding hillsides, residents nearby watch and wait.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s getting a little scarier now. We are trying to as, homeowners, just stay relaxed and calm through this process.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Officials say they believe the fire was accidentally started by a Public Works group that were using heavy equipment in the area on Monday.\par \par \uc2 In LA County, the Bridge Fire near Mount Bald exploded Tuesday evening from 3,000 acres to 34,000 acres in just a matter of hours.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Back here in Lake Elsinore, the Airport Fire has expanded to over 22,000 acres and is still zero percent contained. You can see flareups happening really at any time.\par \par \uc2 The dry brush and the steep terrain make firefighting here extremely difficult -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Adam, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, her name is Francine. Remember this. Francine is now a hurricane in the Gulf of Mexico, heading for Louisiana. Now, it`s due to make landfall today with dangerous winds, heavy rain, and storm surge, all the usual stuff with a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff is in Houma, Louisiana with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Hurricane Francine has already flooded roads in South Texas, and that is barely the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Storm surges slamming the coast, and it`s going to become even more dangerous as it moves north, targeting Louisiana. Those in low lying areas have been ordered to evacuate by parish governments.\par \par \uc2 MONTY MEYERS (Jean Lafitte, Louisiana resident): Man, I just don`t want to stay down here. Just, you know what could happen.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The governor of Louisiana is urging people to shelter in place until after the hurricane has passed.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JEFF LANDRY (R-LA): We remind people that when this storm makes its way on shore, we would ask that you stay in your homes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Barges are coming in from the Gulf, tug after tug pushing them to safe mooring.\par \par \uc2 Three years after experiencing Hurricane Ida, a Category Four storm, everyone is taking this one seriously, like Carol Duplantis here in Houma.\par \par \uc2 CAROL DUPLANTIS (Houma, Louisiana resident): We`re still not finished. We`re not done from Ida yet still, like people, think everything`s all done and over with, it`s not. We still have a lot of places that aren`t done. My son is still displaced.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: She`s concerned the water will enter her home, still being remodeled from the damage that Ida left behind.\par \par \uc2 CAROL DUPLANTIS: We know we`re going to get some water, so I just want to protect what I have.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Carol works in the maternity ward, helping to deliver babies. She did that during the last storm in 2021 and Tony, her next shift is actually during landfall for this one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dave, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 , is watching Francine very closely.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning. When`s it going to hit?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Tony, good morning. We expect landfall later today. The center of Francine is offshore, but we`re already seeing the clouds and showers in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 The rain and wind will get more intense as it nears the coast, and there`s your landfall this afternoon into early evening. Tomorrow, the center will head north, raining out over multiple stays from Arkansas to Georgia.\par \par \uc2 New Orleans is nearly 15 inches above average on rainfall for the year, we could get another five to eight. But this is not just a coastal storm. It will bring a flood threat well inland, as well as power outages as the storm slowly winds down.\par \par \uc2 Right along the coast, Francine is expected to deliver a water rise of 10 feet, along with punishing waves and within the rain bands, especially on the east side of the storm, there is a threat of tornadoes. Our Tornado Condition index is a three out of 10 from Louisiana to Florida.\par \par \uc2 What`s left of the storm lingers into Missouri, Friday, and gives us a few showers in the southeast over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Tony, Francine will be the sixth hurricane in just the last five years to make landfall in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Steph, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have much more ahead, but right now it`s 7:19, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead, Major Garrett eats his way through the state of Michigan and choose the fat with some voters for our series, "Three Meals."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . Protect the stuff you love with renters insurance. Visit progressive.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s 7:30.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  is now in her endorsement era -- get it -- after announcing her support for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  last night after the debate in Philadelphia, she posted this image calling herself a childless cat lady. That cat, by the way, that`s Benjamin Buttons.\par \par \uc2 And writing this, "I`ve done my research and I`ve made my choice." The post already has more than seven million likes. It hasn`t even been 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent has more on why this could be a very big deal.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Look What You Just Made Me Do"/Taylor Swift/Big Machine Label Group)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  is known for dropping late night announcements, and Tuesday night was no exception.\par \par \uc2 The global superstar making her choice for President known on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , backing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  just minutes after the debate concluded, declaring: "I believe we can accomplish so much more in this country if we are led by calm and not chaos."\par \par \uc2 Swift pointed to a number of issues that mattered to her, including reproductive and LGBTQ+ rights. She said her call to action was also motivated in part by her fears surrounding AI. Those fears, Swift says realized when former President Trump posted fake images of her falsely endorsing him.\par \par \uc2 Swift ended her post by trolling Trump`s running mate, JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH) (2024 vice presidential nominee): We`re effectively run in this country via the Democrats, via our corporate oligarchs, by a bunch of childless cat ladies.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Harris` running mate, Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , reacted to the endorsement during a live interview.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN) (2024 vice presidential nominee): This would be the opportunity, Swifties. KamalaHarris.com, get on over there. Give us a hand. Get things going.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Swift hasn`t always been comfortable with jumping into politics.\par \par \uc2 In 2019 she told "Vogue" she wished she was more vocal about supporting \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2016 and in 2020, she endorsed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just one month before the election.\par \par \uc2 With more than 280 million followers on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  alone, Swift boasts a massive fan base of young people. But can she pack the voting booths with those same screaming fans?\par \par \uc2 According to a recent CBS News poll, young people are the least reliable voters. Only 66 percent of registered voters, 18 to 29 say they will definitely vote in this year`s election. That`s compared to 94 percent of voters ages 65 and older. In Tuesday`s post, Swift encouraged her voters to register to vote, saying it`s much easier to vote early.\par \par \uc2 Last year, which was a non-election year, Swift was credited with getting more than 35,000 \uc2 Instagram\uc2  followers to sign up to vote in a single day and in an election that could come down to razor thin margins in a handful of swing states, Democrats are hoping it could make a big difference.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): Her speaking out is a big darn deal. Wyomissing zone, Berks County zone, Pennsylvania zone, \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2 , that`s a huge endorsement.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jo Ling Kent.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You forget about that. She`s got ties to Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right, I forgot about that, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if she can swing just a few people to register and then turn out in that state alone, this could swing the whole election.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you know what I like about it? It`s not like she said vote for her because I`m voting for her. She said, I did my own due diligence. I`m encouraging you to do the same, and then calling it, signing herself as a childless cat lady. I just thought was so -- and the timing of it too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So well thought out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Even though, it came from a celebrity, it`s important just to go vote.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hopefully, you will do your due diligence like she said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And she emphasized, it`s your choice, your research.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re on your journey, but this is where I am.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead reaction to last night`s debate with Republican governor and maybe runner up for VP candidate under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  of North Dakota. He is in Philadelphia. He was in the spin room last night. He`s going to share his thoughts with us this morning. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Understand what it would mean if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were back in the White House with no guard rails, because certainly we know now the court won`t stop him. We know JD Vance is not going to stop him.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I probably took a bullet to the head because of the things that they say about me. They talk about democracy. I`m a threat to democracy. They`re the threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of many sharp exchanges between Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at last night`s presidential debate in Philadelphia. The only one scheduled, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 Joining us from Philadelphia right now is Republican Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  of North Dakota, a spokesperson, really for the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 Governor, you`ve got a tough job this morning. I`ve got a little list here of people on the center or right side of the spectrum -- \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , \uc2 Ben Shapiro\uc2 , the Free Press publication, "The Wall Street Journal" Editorial Board, "The Drudge Report," "The New York Post," \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , none of these folks are complimenting Donald Trump`s performance, but I presume you`re going to this morning.\par \par \uc2 What did you like?\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Well, I think the thing that we have to focus on coming out of a debate like this is the issues that matter to swing voters. And I think that we know, when we get to that election, that`s going to be 54 days from now, is that people are going to be focusing on the economy.\par \par \uc2 And I think when we talk about the things that folks had when President Trump was in office, low interest rates. We did not have the inflation that we`ve experienced. We had secure borders. People felt safer in their cities. And I think that there are people that are saying, hey, if, you know Harris says, we don`t want to go back, there`s a lot of people that felt they were better off under President Trump and we know that in past elections, that`s what`s made the decision when people have come to vote in November.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Governor, obviously, the economy is a huge issue for people. They care about dollars and cents, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not talk a whole heck of a lot about the economy or how he would help people, everyday Americans. He chased a lot of rabbits down a lot of holes. He seemed to take the bait, which I`m sure was not the strategy from your camp.\par \par \uc2 Have you talked to the former president? Did he think he did a good job? Does he regret some of the bait he chased?\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Well, he was -- as a lot of people did, we saw him in the spin room last night. He was feeling very confident about his performance. And I think that, again, he felt that he hit on the points that he knows really, really mattered to some of those voters.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, this is going to be in the eye of beholders, different people you know, wanted different things from their candidate. But I think again, when we get back out on the trail and we see this -- I don`t think there`s anything in this debate that`s going to live in people`s memories, you know, historically, a year or two or three years from now, it`s going to come back down to -- come back down to those swing voters in swing counties, and both campaigns are going to be fighting.\par \par \uc2 We know it`s going to be a close race, but at the end of the day, I think when President Trump is talking about a proven track record of whipping inflation, keeping interest rates down, what we heard from Harris last night was really more of the same.\par \par \uc2 You know, I mean, the one thing we didn`t hear from her was her thanking President Trump for knocking out \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She wouldn`t even be in this position if he hadn`t had such a great debate performance earlier this summer.\par \par \uc2 So debates have mattered in this race, but I think the thing which she talked about, which is, you know, more spending, more regulation, higher taxes, these are the things that states like California, New York and Illinois have been pursuing those kinds of liberal agendas, and people are leaving those states.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Governor Burgum, Gayle here, it`s really good to see you, sir. We remember when you were in the studio. It`s nice to see you again.\par \par \uc2 You said you worry -- you doubt that some things will stick out. But the thing that stuck with a lot of people today on both sides is Donald Trump`s false claim, by the way, that immigrants are killing and eating pets. Now, it`s been proven that that`s just not true. When you hear stuff like that, what do you think? And do you think that helps -- that helps your cause in any way swing undecided voters to your side? It just seems so farfetched to anyone listening to it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Well, I think what is really key to that is it brings the attention back to the strain that this unmitigated illegal immigration is having on our country.\par \par \uc2 When you take a town in Ohio, I`d heard of Springfield, Illinois, I had never heard of Springfield, Ohio until yesterday, and then you take a community that small and with that size of immigration coming in, putting strains. You heard it from your own panel on this show, but putting strains on schools, on emergency services --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Governor --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is a legit conversation that we have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, there is a legit conversation to have about those strains, right? But that`s not what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did last night.\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Well, I think what he did is he brought attention back to this issue that immigration is not just something that`s happening a problem in the border states. And if you`re a family living in, you know, Tucson, Arizona, in the swing state of Arizona, I think you know who came across last night as someone who`s got a plan to actually secure the border. And if you`re someone who`s, even if you`re living in New York City, earlier this year, lots of stories about the strain that was putting on those cities.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Governor, hey, how are you doing? Nate Burleson here. Now, do you wish that -- do you wish that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  answered that question the same way you did, instead of perpetuating a false narrative that immigrants are eating cats and dogs and other people`s pets?\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Well, I suppose in the same way. You know, maybe there`s some people on the Democrat side, maybe not wish that Harris hadn`t twice in the first 15 minutes brought up a false narrative about a project that President Trump --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but Governor, we`re talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this while we have you. You know, when given an opportunity to talk about health care, you mentioned what matters to swing voters. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spoke about a better plan, an alternative plan to Obamacare, and he was given an opportunity. He said, I`m not president, but I do have concepts of a plan.\par \par \uc2 A presidential debate is an opportunity to speak to the people about what that plan is, and he did not go into detail. Do you believe he could have answered that better, and should he do a better job of detailing what that plan is over the next 55 days?\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Well, I think any time either candidate can give more detail around their policies, that`s helpful. But again, this campaign is in this election is not going to swing on health care, but I think it`s going to swing on affordability, and the affordability that is -- you know, there was a time when affordability of health care was the main issue for families, but when you`ve got a -- when you`ve got situations where people can`t afford gas in their car, their electricity bill, their rent, they can`t live the American Dream because of high interest rates, food on the table cost more, and then they`re worried about, you know, wars abroad, some of that goes with the incumbency.\par \par \uc2 And I think that you saw last night, Harris trying to run from the fact that she`s part of the Biden-Harris administration, which their policies of their spending and their bills have led to that inflation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Governor, thank you so much for joining us. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to see you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right now --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right now, it is 7:45, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up in our eight o`clock hour, we`re going to have breakfast, lunch and dinner with Major Garrett. I like that, and likely voters in Michigan for our series, we call it "Three Meals."\par \par \uc2 Plus singer-songwriter, Muni Long is here in the studio today. She`s got a very powerful voice, and she`s up for a VMA tonight. How`s she feeling? All of that`s in our next hour, but first, Tony and I heading over to the table, because Nate is sitting in for Vlad Duthiers --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- who is still on assignment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have some good stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Getting ready for "What to Watch" --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back. Not this Tony. We`ve got another one.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by The Farmer`s Dog. Real, fresh pet food delivered. Long live dogs.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, it`s time for "What to Watch" -- let`s jump right into it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because we have some good stuff.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so "GQ`s" October issue, and the cover model is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Beyonce. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She looks absolutely stunning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Va-va-voom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now in a rare interview, because the superstar doesn`t really do this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doesn`t talk, right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The one superstar who was behind "Cowboy Carter," which we all love, talked about her three children, saying: "One thing I`ve worked extremely hard on is making sure my kids can have as much normalcy and privacy as possible, ensuring my personal life isn`t turned into a brand."\par \par \uc2 She also talked about balancing motherhood in her career, saying: "There was a time when I was pushing myself to meet unrealistic deadlines while not taking the time to enjoy the benefits of why I was working so hard."\par \par \uc2 I feel like that`s beautiful, because we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We oftentimes can identify with that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And she has a lot going on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Especially with her dropping that new album, and the CMA nomination, not being there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I am still feeling some kind of way about no CMA nomination.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but it looks like Beyonce, as she always is, is moving on with grace.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is a class act.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is a class act.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She really is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, get ready for music`s 