nee, and we`ll talk about her remarkable journey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she can sing.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s `Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let`s have a good debate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Nice to see you. Have fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vice President Harris and former President Trump square off in their first presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: When the debate was over, Harris almost immediately said she`s ready for a second one. Trump says he`s still thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Apocalyptic scenes in California as multiple wildfires tear through the state.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You can see fire coming over the ridge now. We`ve had flames coming up. It`s getting a little scarier now.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There`s a lot of variables here that could go from worst case scenario to we are fine, but we have to prepare for the worst case.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hurricane Francine barrels toward the Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You never know how hard and how bad it`s going to be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  endorses Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  after the debate.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I really did not see that Taylor Swift thing coming.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, I mean, she timed it perfectly.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: She did time it perfectly. I wonder why. I wonder why now? Was she waiting for Cheney? Was she waiting for \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  to get in?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The question was, is \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2  waiting for the \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  endorsement of Kamala before she followed? I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t think so either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am going to go out on a limb.\par \par \uc2 Politics was on the docket last night. It is where we begin this hour as well, with the debate between former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Throughout last evening, the vice president seemed determined, you might say, to provoke the former president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And what you will also notice is that people start leaving his rallies early out of exhaustion and boredom.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : People don`t go to her rallies. There`s no reason to go. And the people that do go, she`s busing them in and paying them to be there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now both candidates will attend events today to mark 23 years since the 9/11 attacks, a somber ceremony planned for Lower Manhattan. And Lower Manhattan is where we find Ed O`Keefe as well. We`re going to go back to him to talk about that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But for now, sticking with politics and the debate, Ed, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she had a lot at stake here. A lot of voters say they don`t know her. She needed to define herself. She also went after Trump. Did she accomplish her goals?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You know, in talking to the campaign beforehand Tony, they said she needed to work on defining herself, trying to distinguish herself from President Biden and stand apart from former President Trump. And yes, there was hope that she would be able to provoke him in some way to get him off his mark and fall back on the personal attacks he`s been known for.\par \par \uc2 So in that regard, her campaign would argue they did exactly what they wanted her and needed her to do. It`s going to take us probably about a week, though, to really assess in those seven battleground states that we talk about all the time whether those voters who felt they needed to learn more or wanted to see her in this setting now think any more differently about her, to the point where they`re going to support her.\par \par \uc2 Did she perform better, for example, with White women in Pennsylvania or Georgia or Michigan? And if so, do her numbers rise because of that? Was she able to win over disaffected Republicans or Independent voters? Looking at careful statewide surveys done in those seven places over the next week, 10 days, will give us a sense of that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Ed, \uc2 Taylor Swift\uc2 , a daughter of the state of Pennsylvania, that was a big endorsement that landed pretty heavy and hard last night after the debate. It`s one that a politician would welcome no matter where they are in the spectrum.\par \par \uc2 So how big of a moment is that for the Harris campaign? What do they do now to maximize the effect of it? And do you think it will actually turn out more voters to the polls?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, that`s the hope. And look, they said after the debate they had no idea it was coming, that it was a surprise, but they welcome her in whatever way she`s willing to help. Certainly, an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post posing with one of your kittens helps, signing it off as a childless cat lady rubs it in the face of Republicans. And who is her base, right? It`s women, it`s younger voters, but also, to some extent, is families more broadly. She has become a cross generational star, really, in the last several years. So there`s the potential for someone like her, just as there might be an athlete, a corporate leader, some other political leader to step in and say, I`m voting for her. It might do just enough to potentially sway voters in these states.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to say, Tony, not only because of this, but because of his performance more generally, I texted with one Republican operative overnight who sits at the intersection of congressional leadership, the Trump campaign and the party`s top donors, I asked him, What did you make of this debate? He texted back one word this morning, "depression," because he said he did nothing last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to add to his support, that devastating split screen, they said the fact that Harris clearly practiced her facial expressions on how she would react him, it`s going to be -- it`s going to be devastating for them, they believe going forward.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, depression, devastation, disaster. You hear sometimes two words "the end." Ed, we`re going to leave it there. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We are going to talk about this more though. The presidential election, it is Major Garrett talking with voters in the state of Michigan for our beloved series, "Three Meals." But first it is 8:05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, how a new guidance to doctors could help women who suffer pain when they get a very popular birth control device. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we are continuing our post-debate coverage with our series "Three Meals."\par \par \uc2 Each week until the election, we`re hitting the road in different states to break bread with voters and ask what issues really matter to them. This week, we visit a state at the center of both campaigns` roadmap to the White House -- Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Our new CBS News poll shows Vice President Harris with a one point edge over former President Trump, who won Michigan in 2016 before it went blue in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett traveled there to ask what`s on people`s minds.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: To look for America, we started in Saginaw. Michigan`s only boomerang county that backed Obama, then Trump, then Biden.\par \par \uc2 Boarded up buildings and this shuttered train station may make things around here look forgotten, but we can say this with certainty, national candidates have not forgotten Saginaw.\par \par \uc2 We fueled up for breakfast at Levi`s Food and Spirits on these big pancakes and four egg omelets.\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS (Michigan voter): Enjoy.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Jill Adams (sp?) is a server here.\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS: How are we doing over here?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: For this election, would you say your mind is made up?\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And that is?\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS: Trump.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Okay. Are you a previous Trump supporter?\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS: The first year he won in 2016, I was not.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Did you vote for Hillary?\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS: I did vote for Hillary.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS: I cried when she did not win.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Okay. Then what happened?\par \par \uc2 JILL ADAMS: And then I just felt like he did a good job. I like the fact that he was not a politician.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You believe you were better off four years ago?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CSONGRADI (Michigan voter): Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Small business owner, Andrew Csongradi backs Trump, too.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CSONGRADI: Before Harris stepped in, I thought it was going to be like almost a slam dunk. Now, I`m not so sure.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Retiree, John Brady is backing Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BRADY (Michigan voter): I worry that fear is going to drive people to vote for somebody who`s going to use that fear to take away other people`s freedoms. The abortion rights thing is obviously a big issue, and one of the clearest examples of a loss of major freedoms for women in this country.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would you say Harris excites you in any way?\par \par \uc2 JOHN BRADY: I like her optimism. She reflects more of what I see.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: For lunch, we drove south toward Detroit, stopping in Dearborn at Sahara, a Lebanese restaurant owned for the past quarter century by Sam Hammoud`s family.\par \par \uc2 What is your most popular set of items on the menu?\par \par \uc2 Sam Hammoud (Sahara restaurant): Shawarma. We go through a lot of shawarma.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Nearby, we found next door neighbors, Rachel Knight and Diane Sikora, who recently retired.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SIKORA (Michigan voter): I knew I had enough to make things last the rest of my life. Now, I`m kind of questioning it if I should maybe go back to work part time.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Both friends are backing Trump.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SIKORA: I don`t like him as a person, I`ll admit it, but I think he`s the best thing for the country.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: How will you feel if Trump doesn`t win right now?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SIKORA: Petrified.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Petrified.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SIKORA: Petrified.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Of what may be happening?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SIKORA: All the illegals coming through. They`re going to make it up here to Michigan.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL KNIGHT (Michigan voter): What is going on at the border scares me to death. I just hold my breath every day, hoping that somebody that I love doesn`t get hurt.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Really? You think about it that much?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL KNIGHT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Dearborn, hometown of \uc2 Ford Motors\uc2 , is also home to a lot of immigrants, the largest city with the majority Arab American population anywhere in the country.\par \par \uc2 Many, like Mike Ayub (sp?) lean Democrat, but have broken with the Biden administration over the war in Gaza. And for this community, there really is no other issue right now.\par \par \uc2 MIKE AYUB (Michigan voter): The Arab American community. It`s a one issue and one issue ahead.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you think they`ll sit out the election?\par \par \uc2 MIKE AYUB: There`s a very good chance. Absolutely. I am considering sitting it out.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Next we`re headed right outside the State Capitol of Lansing to Eaton County. Eaton County, you see, is a swing county in a swing state.\par \par \uc2 You see, Obama won Eaton County twice, so did Trump, but Trump`s 2020 margin was less than one percent and when I say less than one percent, I mean, 499 votes.\par \par \uc2 Eaton Place is the place to eat here.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett with \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TERRY DAVIS (Michigan voter): Terry Davis (sp?).\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Hi, Terry. How are you?\par \par \uc2 When we stopped by, these guys on the wall weren`t talking, but these two college roommates were.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MUDRI (Michigan voter): He`s my best friend.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Right, right, right.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MUDRI: So we`re on the same page.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Retired nurse Paul Mudri (sp?) is a Reagan Democrat who backs Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 There`s this idea, and you can certainly hear it in Washington, there`s all this excitement for Harris. She`s raising all this money, all these volunteers. It`s in the bag.\par \par \uc2 MAN: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MUDRI: No, it does not.\par \par \uc2 No, I`m worried. The bag has got a big hole in it.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Really?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MUDRI: Yes, I think there`s a rat eating down at the bottom.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Sloan Davis (sp?) is part of the Harris coalition, but half- heartedly.\par \par \uc2 SLOAN DAVIS (Michigan voter): I`ll vote for Harris. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Sounds kind of meh though.\par \par \uc2 SLOAN DAVIS: I`m a pretty passionate person, and I`m not passionate about either of them. The middle school counselor makes ends meet by helping cook burgers here in her parents` restaurant. But Davis says her vote also is about something more personal.\par \par \uc2 SLOAN DAVIS: Well, I am getting married this year.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Okay, congratulations.\par \par \uc2 SLOAN DAVIS: Thank you -- to a woman, and of course, there is always that burning question in the back of your head, like, what if? Because I don`t want that right to be taken away.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And like many we met in Michigan, she yearns for a brighter future.\par \par \uc2 SLOAN DAVIS: Even if the person I vote for doesn`t win, I do believe in tomorrow, so that`s what I`m hopeful for.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Major joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Major, what did you learn from the voters in Michigan, and did last night`s debate reflect some of those issues?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We`ll get to the debate in a second.\par \par \uc2 So I talked to 20 voters, not one of them was undecided.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not one.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Not one. And headlines come and go, but let me tell you this, support for former President Trump is real and resilient. I encounter that everywhere I went, and that`s something the Harris campaign knows, recognizes, a fact in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Top Democrats in Michigan tell me that they expect the margin in this election could be as tight as 50,000 votes. Biden won Michigan by 150,000 votes. So let`s dig a little deeper.\par \par \uc2 I was in Dearborn, just outside of Detroit, population about 110,000 roughly a third of that is Arab American. That`s 30,000 votes, possibly. If the margin is 50,000 and some significant portion of them, as Mike Ayub, I talked to said, sit this election out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And the Gaza war is of significant importance to that community.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Many people I talked to said they have friends relatives who are suffering. They think about it every single day, something to keep an eye on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And when they vote?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As for the debate --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ys.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I don`t know how much that issue was addressed in Dearborn last night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It was a little bit. The broader spectrum of issues, those two women we also talked to in Dearborn, constantly worried about immigration. I know Trump went down some very seemingly ridiculous rabbit holes about cats and everything else, but that doesn`t mean they don`t still care about immigration and will not still continue to back Trump.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say when the election is this close, every vote matters.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s why they say voting -- not voting is voting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but I like the lady who just said, even if the person I like doesn`t win, I still want -- I still have hope. I want the country to deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a great piece --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Saginaw Island and Simon & Garfunkel is a way to start it. I mean, I just love this country and how different everybody is. Just down the road, different food, different ideas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you had four meals, not three. I`m going to try that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. We work out. We work out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When I get to do one of these.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Major, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a long overdue change from the CDC.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was counting, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It could help women who get a popular birth control device, that is an IUD, people. We`re going to talk to Dr Celine Gounder about what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In today`s "Health Watch" for the very first time, the CDC is recommending that doctors talk to women about pain management for IUD procedures.\par \par \uc2 For years, women have described very painful experiences. Did you get that? Very painful experiences. You know we`re on camera -- painful experiences - -\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We weren`t a second ago. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- of the insertion of the birth control device. So far, there has been little advancement in pain management.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder is here. She`s a CBS News medical contributor and editor-at-large for public health at KFF.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Dr. Gounder. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I keep hearing, we see all the videos of women saying that it`s excruciatingly painful, but they use it. Why? I`m thinking, if it`s this painful, why use this.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: IUDs are by far the best reversible birth control we have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Better than the pill.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Better than the pill, and you actually get a lower exposure to hormones with the IUD than you do with the pill. You have a hormonal IUD, you have a copper IUD. These are more than 99 percent effective in preventing pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do they think they need to now send the warning to advise women. What can we do about it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, it`s not a warning, it`s more that for very long time providing pain control during the procedure, the insertion of the IUD, pain control has not really been offered, and I have had multiple IUDs myself. I was not offered pain control, and it is a very painful procedure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So there`s an alternative gynecological surgical device that got \uc2 FDA\uc2  clearance last year. What can you tell us about it, and how does it compare to what`s already out there?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So I`ll show you what these look like. So this is used to stabilize the cervix. This is the newer device that is \uc2 FDA\uc2  cleared. It works by suction. You can see this looks pretty, you know, gentle. This is the old device, what`s called a tenaculum. So it has these little hooks, sharp hooks on the end. This was used during the Civil War to remove bullets and to pull tissue during an amputation.\par \par \uc2 This, you can see it`s going to be a lot more painful than this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`ve got an announcement about the new device?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So the new device is already in use at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , at the \uc2 Indiana University\uc2 , and then a series of clinics, women`s health clinics called Tia, are partnering with the company that makes this to train their providers. They are training their providers in LA, San Francisco, Scottsdale, New York City so that they will be offering this by the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 And then the hope is for expansion beyond that down the line.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My apologies. I should know this, but that`s still in use to this day?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she got me with the Civil War thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, this still in use today. And as you can imagine, this is not a comfortable procedure to undergo, so making the update in the device, but then also offering women things like lidocaine injections can really reduce the pain.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, wow. Dr. Gounder, I am shocked right now by that tool.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate you. Thank you for stopping by.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Neither one of those look appealing to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Neither one of them.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s 8:30. "Talk of the Table" time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a table and w be talking.\par \par \uc2 Nate, you`re up first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so I`m up first. My "Talk of the Table" is Australia saying it intends to set a minimum age limit for children to use social media. The prime minister says they are still ironing out the plans and the details of it, but the age would likely be between 14 and 16.\par \par \uc2 He says social media is causing real harm to kids, and wants to see them off of their devices and back on athletic fields and in pools, basically outside.\par \par \uc2 Some groups, though, have warned restrictions might drive dangerous online activity underground.\par \par \uc2 Now, we oftentimes talk about social media and its impact on kids. Now, I want to bring this up because I picked up my daughter yesterday, my daughter, Mia, from the bus after school.\par \par \uc2 And I`m like, how`s school? How`s volleyball? She`s like, great, great, great. And she`s like, guess what? You did a story on kids locking away their phones and pouches on CBS MORNINGS, and now we have to do it. And I`m like, what?\par \par \uc2 I guess, the Dean saw the story, was talking to Mia --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And was saying, we`re going to implement that in the middle school. Mia is in high school, so they can have their phones. But I find it fascinating that some of the stuff we cover and talk about that schools are watching and they are now placing into their programs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Some of Mia`s younger friends are saying, will you tell your dad to stop talking?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To shut up, yes. Leave us out of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There has been a revolution in the last decade. My older kids all had phones. Younger kids, Katy and I are not giving them phones until very late in their adolescence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And something Drew Barrymore is working on this season to get at that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a big issue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of people are behind it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Tony, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" -- my very favorite awards event of the year, the Food and Wine Best New Chef Awards. I get to emcee it. The 2024 event took place last evening right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 I love this event because, you know, life is many things, but one of the big things it is, is meals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And these people are pumping out some of the best meals in their respective cities. And not only are they good at what`s on the plate, Food and Wine, does this anonymous survey of the staff of the restaurants to ask them what they`re like culture-wise, how they treat people. So they`re great on that front as well.\par \par \uc2 So I`m going to do a quick shout out here of the different places. There`s Chilte in Phoenix, Arizona, Chef Lawrence "LT" Smith, The Musket Room and Rafts right here in New York City. MYRIEL in Saint Paul, Minnesota, great place. Kisser, Nashville, Tennessee. Bomb Biscuit, Atlanta, Georgia. We`ve got a profile of Erika Council coming out. Holy Basil in Los Angeles, Peche in New Orleans, Goldee`s in Fort Worth Texas, and Neng Jr`s in Asheville, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Check these places.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am hitting all of those spots.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Food and Wine has got a great track record of finding the stars as they`re on their way up. These people are making it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is this event open to the public?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s open to Gayle King, if she would like to go there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, but I mean, do they serve food there, too?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They do serve food.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I would love this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And what happens is, it is a community. So like past winners of the award come back and serve dishes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great cocktails as well. I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I be your plus one next?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are in, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell Katy I am really nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And me as well. Add me to the list.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Now, you`ve got plus twos.\par \par \uc2 Moving on, the VMAs, guess what? They are tonight. We are very excited to be joined by one of this year`s nominees. She`s knockout.\par \par \uc2 Singer-songwriter, Muni Long is here. She`s nominated in the R&B category for her song, I know you`ve heard it, "Nobody Loves Me," made for me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Nobody Loves Me"/Muni Long/DEF JAM Records)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Long whose real name is Priscilla Renea Hamilton, pretty name - - has written songs from Madonna, Rihanna, Mariah Carey, Ariana Grande, and John Legend.\par \par \uc2 So in 2020, she launched her solo career. She has released two albums, including her latest one. It`s called "Revenge." I am waiting to hear the lyrics on these songs. Get this, she has more than seven million monthly listeners on \uc2 Spotify\uc2 . She`s won a Grammy, and she has wowed critics with her nostalgic R&B style and her emotional lyrics.\par \par \uc2 Muni Long, we`re glad to say is here. Hello, Miss Priscilla, also known as Muni Long.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG (Singer): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hello.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wonder what it`s like, because actually, you can really sing, but you`re a songwriter.\par \par \uc2 I like to sing, but I know I can`t, but I wonder what it would be like to be you, that you`re a songwriter, and you`re writing these songs and people are singing, and you go, but I really want to do this, too.\par \par \uc2 Did you have that? Did you have those feelings? Or were you very happy being a songwriter?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: I think there`s a narrative that tell you as a songwriter, if you want to be an artist, you know, you write these songs, you get a number one, you make all this money. Songwriting is where the money is at.\par \par \uc2 I bought into that for a really long time, until I realized, like I did all the things you said, and it`s not taking me where I want to go. So I quit. I`m going to focus on me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and then you realize they don`t even know more than I do.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: They really don`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you feel that?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Revenge"/Muni Long/DEF JAM Records)\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: We don`t -- none of us know. We`re all following our gut.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now this song is huge. What is this? Do you feel that it`s a moment --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, huge is an understatement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your right. You are, right. I love the song. I love it.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: You know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does this moment mean for you?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: It`s like, the lyric says --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This time?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: It`s very corny to say, but this moment is surreal. It`s like, I scroll on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , TikTok and all of these places, and I`m seeing it, I`m hearing it, and I`m wondering like, is this just my algorithm, or is it really that big?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is really that big.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: In Target, in a car that will drive past and be playing it. And that`s how I know, like, people are really using it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh gosh, what is that like when you`re just going about your day, you`re in a store, you`re in a car --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is everywhere. Trends.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you hear you. What is that like?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: TikTok.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: It never gets old. It never gets old. I`m always like, is that my phone? Like, oh no, they`re actually playing it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s not my phone. That`s my song.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, Gayle talked about your journey and kind of realizing the moment where you wanted to take that leap. But I`m sure through the roller coaster ride, there were times where you wanted to get off and say, all right, I`m going to just go pursue something else, because this industry is not showing me the love that I show it.\par \par \uc2 Did you get close to walking away? And how did you refrain from making that decision?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: I think when you`re doing anything that`s like, outside of the box, and you`re living in like the outlier space. Yes, of course, you`re like, this is too hard. I want to do something else, but then I`ll be like, girl, what are you going to do? Like, what else are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are you going to do? Yes.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: I`ve always known that I wanted to do something on the stage in the performance art space, and so I`m glad I didn`t give up. But if you listen to anybody who`s super successful, that`s kind of like a repeating pattern.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody goes through that, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Don`t quit. Never give up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t quit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So for people hearing you for the first time, meeting you for the first time, Muni Long, the name. You say it like money, but it`s spelled differently. Where does it come from?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: I was doing some research. So, like, I`m really into, like, frequency and self-help teachings. And I was researching, like, meditation, came across a story about an ancient sage named Muni, spelled with the two accents over the U and I showed it to somebody I was talking to at the time. They were like, you should pronounce it money.\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was really cool. I Googled it. The name didn`t exist anywhere, so I took it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is really cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You also -- listen, I love the title of the album, "Revenge." You say that it`s really one big diary entry. And last month, I was really sorry to hear you announce the separation from your husband of nine years.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been through that. I know what that`s like. It`s never good, even when you know it`s the right decision. Do you find writing about it helps you along the way to be -- because your lyrics, you were very vulnerable in your lyrics on this album. Was it tough?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or was it helpful?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Personally, it helps me process like what I`m going through, because when you don`t share, you sort of normalize things that are not okay.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Revenge"/Muni Long/DEF JAM Records)\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: And so like saying it out loud and putting it on the songs, I think people are like, oh my God, I can`t believe this is what you went through. Is this really your story?\par \par \uc2 On a personal --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or your story could say, some people would look at it and say, your story is my story.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Exactly, exactly. But then, like in the bigger scheme of things, it might make things difficult relationally, just because, you know, you don`t want your business in the street, so to speak.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, using music as therapy can be very vulnerable, but you are transparent, and you`ve been open about your battle with lupus, and you say eliminating stress, it has helped you feel better.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How do you eliminate stress? Because, baby, we all trying to eliminate some stress. We`re taking notes right now.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: My no is my no. I don`t feel guilty. If I want to sleep, I sleep.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: You know, I just quit putting pressure on myself to be perfect and to like, try to make everybody happy. Reformed people pleaser here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: I just do what feels good like, literally, if it makes me cringe even in the slightest, I just don`t do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like what you said, my no is my no, because I learned a long time away -- it took me a while, a minute to get it, but no is a complete sentence. Don`t have to offer any explanation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that is just done.\par \par \uc2 So tonight is the night, what are you wearing? And then we`ve got to go. What are you wearing?\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: I have a custom savage Fenty look from Rihanna. This is the first time they`ve ever dressed anyone other than Ri on a carpet, so I am really excited about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: It is going to be like really pretty, aubergine color.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, we can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Super excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re going to kill it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So happy for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cheering you on tonight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Muni Long, we appreciate you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MUNI LONG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The VMAs will air live tonight at 8:00 PM on \uc2 MTV\uc2  and other Paramount owned networks, which, like \uc2 CBS\uc2 , is part of the \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  family.\par \par \uc2 Now it`s time to check the local news.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Skyline of New York City. It was 23 years ago now, almost to the minute when a strike at the heart of New York, downtown there, the heart of Washington, DC, the heart of this country changed the heart of this country forever, and we mark those losses.\par \par \uc2 Memorial service are being held nationwide this morning in honor of the 2,977 people who died on this morning 23 years ago, September 11th, the terror attacks on that day, more than two decades ago now.\par \par \uc2 Here in New York City, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are all at the National September 11th Memorial and Museum at Ground Zero in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 And later today, all three will travel to Shanksville, Pennsylvania to honor those who died on that fourth hijacked flight, United Flight 93, possibly one that would have been headed for the White House.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Vice President Harris will also go to the Pentagon to mourn the victims who died there in that strike.\par \par \uc2 At 8:46 AM, those numbers etched in our brains forever, that is the time the first flight, the first plane hit the North Tower. We were going to bring you, as we do every year, because we do not forget that moment of silence, live to commemorate those killed at the World Trade Center.\par \par \uc2 And one also thinks as we bring in our Ed O`Keefe, who is at Ground Zero where the memorial service is already underway.\par \par \uc2 Ed, it`s -- everybody who died that day in those buildings and at the Pentagon and in a field in Shanksville, Pennsylvania, and then the many thousands of soldiers and service people who went into the field to fight the threat that led to 9/11, all of those lives as well. It`s hard to fathom. It`s impossible to forget.\par \par \uc2 Every single day, this sneaks up on me and it catches me in a big, big way. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 As you said, President Biden and Vice President Harris, standing with former President Trump and JD Vance, other New York leaders, they briefly exchanged handshakes and pleasantries and made small talk ahead of this event, as you said. They will be there for those events here in New York and in Pennsylvania and Virginia. And it is, it says something that this is the only kind of event that seems to be able to bring political leaders together anymore.\par \par \uc2 The body language pretty stiff and awkward, but an important day for them to all be together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an important day for us all to be together. I was just explaining this day to my five-year-old, Teddy. It`s a day of remembrance and explanation, and we are approaching that moment, the moment of silence, exactly 8:46 AM. Let`s take it.\par \par \uc2 (Bell tolls for one minute of silence.)\par \par \uc2 (Band plays)\par \par \uc2 (Reading of names of victims of 9/11)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the names, the reading of the names of those lost on this day. That list is long. It`s going to go on for a good long time. And it`s, I say this day sneaks up on me, because --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel the same.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- the summer passes, the fall is so gorgeous, and it was such a beautiful day that day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember it well.\par \par \uc2 I mean, those bagpipes always sound so melancholy. And now we`re getting to the point where people, for some people, this is just a story in a history book, but for those of us who know that day, we`ll never, ever forget it.\par \par \uc2 I`m glad every year they still remember it and remember everybody`s names.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. And it`s such a wonderful example. America is a closed fist when we are attacked, as we were on that day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are resilient.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And to see the bipartisan show of support and honor for those who died that day and then who fought, it is a powerful thing.\par \par \uc2 Those people taking it at home and talk to the next generation, they will not forget.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: May we never forget. That`s why I think it`s so important to see people from both parties there on this day. We all know what it means.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240911ek9b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS WITH Norah O`Donnell September 10, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Weijia Jiang, Nancy Cordes, Major Garrett, Omar  Villafranca, Dave Malkoff, Lonnie Quinn, Elizabeth Palmer \par \uc2 3,854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the debate stage is set. The stakes are high and the candidates are ready. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  meet for the first time ever in what could be their only presidential debate of the election cycle. Can they sway voters in a race that is neck and neck? The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 We are just hours away from the showdown America has been waiting for. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  takes on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the debate stage. Good evening, I`m Norah O`Donnell in New York and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 This is where it is happening, at Philadelphia`s \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 . And it`s hard to overstate how pivotal tonight`s performances from the Vice President and former president could be.\par \par \uc2 Election Day is eight weeks from tonight and right now the race is all tied up nationally and in a handful of battleground states. Tens of millions of Americans are expected to tune in to see the candidates argue their positions in what could be their only debate.\par \par \uc2 The stage is set, you see the podiums there. Harris chose the podium on the right, Trump will be on the left. We have team coverage of this historic night and \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang will start us off with a preview from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening to you, Norah. These candidates have a massive spotlight tonight and all the pressure that comes along with it, especially since this could be their only chance to square off with no agreement yet for another debate. All this comes as the race to the White House is incredibly close.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT) ` WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): Tonight, after months of fighting from afar --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Comrade \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to sacrifice our wealth, kill the economy.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): So hear me when I say, I know Donald Trump`s type.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Vice President Harris and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will meet in person for the first time on this stage inside Philadelphia`s \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 . The two nominees will stand at podiums about six feet apart. There will be no live audience and microphones will be muted if it`s not their turn to talk.\par \par \uc2 Harris` team fears that will limit moments when she can directly engage with her opponent like this one from four years ago.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Mr. Vice President, I`m speaking. I`m speaking.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Tonight will be Harris` first presidential debate and Trump`s seventh. She has been studying his past performances and getting advice from Hillary Clinton --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She doesn`t have the look. She doesn`t have the stamina.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: -- who was the recipient of Trump`s attacks in three debates. The Trump team is expecting tough talk from Harris, too. She showed off her skills in the 2019 primaries, criticizing then-candidate Biden`s record on busing and school desegregation.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : You also worked with them to oppose busing. And, you know, there was a little girl in California who was part of the second class to integrate her public schools. And she was bused to school every day. And that little girl was me.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But CBS News polling shows Harris has work to do tonight. More than a third of voters in three battleground states do not know what she stands for.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2  (D, California): They want to know her why. What`s the motivation. Why she`s not just the Vice President of the United States, but why does she want to be president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Trump got advice from his allies, too.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R, South Carolina): I think President Trump would be wise to explain how he would change the country, stay away from the personal stuff.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Tonight, aides to Vice President Harris tell me she`s feeling great and she`s ready to call out any lies that come from Trump and argue it`s time to turn the page. The former president is expected to paint Harris as a San Francisco liberal who`s out of touch with voters and incapable of change. Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Weijia Jiang in Philadelphia tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on what`s at stake, let`s bring in chief White House correspondent, Nancy Cordes and chief Washington correspondent, Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 And Major, what about that? Trump`s advisors may want him to focus on policy, but he loves the personal attacks.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: He does, but these are nervous times within the Trump campaign, and some of that has crept into the former president himself. Why is it a nervous time for the Trump campaign?\par \par \uc2 Because they know one of the best polls, internal and external, they`re close. They`re not ahead. They were once ahead in this race. They`ve lost the lead and they`ve clawed their way back to a tie. But they know Vice President Harris has more money, more enthusiasm, more volunteers. All of that works to her benefit.\par \par \uc2 So how do you block that? How do you rearrange this race?\par \par \uc2 They`ve basically, given this nervous candidate, something that calms him down, a mantra. What is it? Inflation, immigration, world on fire, whether you agree with those characterizations or not, the Trump campaigns believe all three of those issues work to his benefit, and no matter how difficult this 90 minutes gets tonight, it always go back to inflation, immigration, world on fire. That`s the way they want to try to define Harris as the incumbent, not the change agent, they want Trump to reclaim that ground.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, Nancy Cordes joins us as well.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, but despite that, the Vice President has to be preparing for personal nasty attacks from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He`s done it in the past.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right. And her campaign advisers say she is preparing for the kinds of attacks that she knows are going to come her way. That she`s too liberal, that she`s too soft on crime, that she`s too tough on crime. He`s actually made both arguments, so they say she`ll be prepared for either one.\par \par \uc2 Yes, she will fact check him if he says things that aren`t true, but they say she can`t spend the whole night fact checking because she`s got work to do defining herself, explaining her positions, explaining who she is to an electorate that says it still doesn`t know enough.\par \par \uc2 And Norah, I`m told that no matter how tonight goes, they will be pushing for at least one more debate between Harris and Trump.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We do know voters want to hear more about policy and what they will do in the future.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, Major Garrett, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And our coverage of the presidential debate begins tonight at 8 Eastern, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , followed by a live simulcast of the debate at 9 and then we`ll have \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` expert analysis, so we`ll see you right at 8 PM.\par \par \uc2 Terrifying moments on the taxiway at Atlanta`s \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport\uc2  when two passenger jets collided. No injuries are reported, but the smaller plane was badly damaged. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca is on the scene.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): A run-in on the runway. Just after 10 this morning, Delta Air Lines Flight 295 was taxiing for a trip to Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We just hit something on the taxiway. Could you tell us what it was?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Delta 295.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: When its wingtip clipped the tail of a Delta regional jet that was sitting on an adjacent taxiway.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: The whole tail of that CR jet is off.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The impact almost took the smaller plane`s tail completely off.\par \par \uc2 STACY THIBODEAUX (Passenger, Delta Regional Jet): When it happened, like, we all freaked out. We thought we ran off the runway. And then all of a sudden, there was a bigger plane took our tail out and it was bad, man. It was bad. We were scared.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Some passengers on the bigger plane, like Steven Shelton, said they barely felt the collision.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN SHELTON (Passenger, Delta Air Lines Flight 295): The whole plane vibrated. You know how when you hit a really deep pothole and the whole -- like, your whole vehicle kind of, like, bounces? It was basically bad.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: There were no reports of deaths or injuries. Shortly after the mishap, the smaller plane`s 56 passengers who were headed to Louisiana were evacuated from the plane.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 FAA\uc2 , the number of serious runway incidents in the first six months of 2024 have decreased 62 percent from the same time last year. Delta says there was minimal impact to date to their operations.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating the incident, and affected passengers were rebooked on other flights. Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Omar Villafranca, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tropical Storm Francine is expected to become a hurricane tonight before slamming Louisiana tomorrow. Hurricane and tropical storm warnings are already in effect along the Gulf Coast from Texas to Alabama, with threats of severe flooding, storm surge and damaging winds. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Dave Malkoff reports from Houma, Louisiana, which could get a direct hit from this storm.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): As Francine gains strength, more than two million people and communities along the Gulf Coast are under a hurricane warning.\par \par \uc2 RANDY SMITH (Sheriff, St. Tammany Parish): We don`t take this storm lightly because of the movement towards the east, which is closer to us, and again, we`re prepared for any emergencies that arise.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: In Morgan City, business owners and residents are boarding up and heading out, and gas station lines are getting longer. Some even running out of fuel.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been through a lot of storms.\par \par \uc2 CAROL DUPLANTIS: Yes, I`ve been through quite a few.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Carol Duplantis (sp?) is getting ready for Francine. She lives in Houma and is using sandbags to protect her home that is still under renovation after Ida, a Category 4 hurricane.\par \par \uc2 And you`re still not recovered from 2021, that was Ida.\par \par \uc2 CAROL DUPLANTIS: No, I am not recovered.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: As an essential worker in a hospital maternity ward, Duplantis can`t evacuate. Her next shift is during Francine`s landfall.\par \par \uc2 CAROL DUPLANTIS: We deliver babies even through the hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Born in a hurricane, this is one of the bayous that we`re worried about. As the storm surge flooding pushes up into these communities from the Gulf of Mexico, the lieutenant governor assures me that the levees will hold.\par \par \uc2 But Norah, there are still some communities here that are under a mandatory evacuation order.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wishing them the very best. Dave Malkoff, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So let`s get the latest track on where Francine is heading. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lonnie Quinn is here in the studio. Good evening, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Nice to be here, Norah. The track hasn`t changed at all. This is still going to be Louisiana`s hurricane, even though right now it`s a lot closer to Texas than it is Louisiana. I`ll show you when you look at the satellite picture. It`s about 150 miles offshore from Brownsville, Texas, but it is forecast now to make that arc and start moving to the northeast.\par \par \uc2 So we now believe it`s a Cat 1 as opposed to a Cat 2, making landfall sometime later in the day on Wednesday, big rainmaker, moving into the Tennessee Valley all the while, putting rain down, but losing its tropical characteristics the farther inland it goes. So the big question is, how much rain does this system hold? It`s a little bit weaker than what we saw yesterday, but the rainfall numbers are still pretty impressive out there.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, I thought we`d be seeing six to 12. I`ve trimmed that a little bit. I think it`s more likely a five- to 10-inch range that includes New Orleans. So fresh water flooding and some storm surge, of course, are the biggest concerns with this storm. Norah, that`s the latest.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Lonnie Quinn, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Gaza say at least 19 people were killed and dozens injured today when Israel struck a tent camp for displaced Palestinians. Israel says it was targeting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. The powerful airstrike left craters up to 32 feet deep.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Israeli defense officials are releasing new video of the tunnel beneath a children`s bedroom where six hostages were killed last month, days before troops found their bodies. American Hersh Goldberg-Polin was among the victims found down there. That video was released on the same day Israel acknowledged that one of its soldiers likely shot and killed a 26- year-old American woman in the West Bank last week, a death U.S. officials are calling unacceptable. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elizabeth Palmer is in Israel and retraced the activist`s final moments with an eyewitness.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER (voiceover): Aysenur Eygi`s body was carried in a traditional funeral procession in the occupied West Bank wrapped in the Palestinian flag. We went with Jonathan Pollak to see where she was killed.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN POLLAK: Like, I put my hand under her head to try and stop the bleeding.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Twenty-six-year-old Eygi had traveled from her home in Washington to join Pollak and other activists.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: You can see the two jeeps.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: They were demonstrating with Palestinians against the expansion of an Israeli settlement. Rocks were thrown. The soldiers fired tear gas and the activists ran into the olive groves.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN POLLAK: They`re pointing guns at us.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: But the military was watching.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN POLLAK: At some point I noticed soldiers taking over that rooftop over there.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Mm-hmm. And I`m just looking at it now, it`s a clear line of sight. That`s a clear shot --\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN POLLAK: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: -- from up there to where she was standing.\par \par \uc2 Eygi was hit in the head with live ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Today the Israelis said the soldier had likely been aiming at a different activist. But U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said that`s no excuse.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (United States Secretary of State): Her killing was both unprovoked and unjustified. No one -- no one should be shot and killed for attending a protest.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: He said the Israelis have to make fundamental changes to the rules of engagement that led to the death of an unarmed young woman.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: There had been some hope, Norah, that the shocking violence in this region might stop with a ceasefire before the U.S. Election, but that hope`s pretty much died. There seems no political will on either side to make a deal.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elizabeth Palmer, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Today, Ukraine launched one of its largest attacks on Moscow since the Russian invasion started more than two and a half years ago. Russia says it downed more than 140 Ukrainian drones in several regions, including at least 20 that flew over the capital city.\par \par \uc2 A woman was killed, making this the first time someone in Moscow has been killed in a Ukrainian drone attack.\par \par \uc2 The first commercial spacewalk is just days away after \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  lifts off with an all-civilian crew. But that`s not the only reason this flight will make history. The details, next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: At least 10 large wildfires are burning across California as hot temperatures and dry conditions make the work even more challenging for firefighters. The so-called airport fire in Orange County exploded overnight to more than 9,000 acres. A firefighting plane prevented flames from destroying communications towers. Thousands of people have been ordered to evacuate across the state.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 `s historic Polaris Dawn mission blasted off today from Florida`s Kennedy Space Center. The six-day trip is taking four civilian astronauts, including billionaire entrepreneur Jared Isaacman, about 870 miles from Earth, which is farther than humans have flown in more than 50 years. Later this week, two astronauts will take part in the first ever commercial spacewalk.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next with a close look at election integrity from the men and women from both parties who are on the front lines of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the CBS Evening News, you can listen. Subscribe wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: As \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  get ready to meet for their first debate tonight, we count on the issue of election integrity to come up, especially as the former president continues to make false claims that the last election was stolen. In tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Major Garrett speaks with election officials in battleground states from both parties. They speak with one voice about their stress and anxiety and conviction that our elections are conducted freely and fairly.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT (voiceover): Election officials from seven battleground states convened in Atlanta last week.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Speeding everywhere.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We met four of them. One Democrat --\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2  (\uc2 Michigan Secretary of State\uc2 ): \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2  and my title is \uc2 Michigan Secretary of State\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And three Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SETH BLUESTEIN (Philadelphia City Commissioner): Seth Bluestein, City Commissioner of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES (Maricopa County, AZ Board of Supervisors): \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , and I`m a member of the Maricopa County Board of Supervisors. I represent District 3.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2  (Chief Operating Officer, GA Secretary of State): \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2 , Chief Operating Officer of the Office of Secretary of State, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , State of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: This year`s election fills you with --\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2 : Joy.\par \par \uc2 SETH BLUESTEIN: Hope.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES: Excitement.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : I feel there should be joy, but there is some angst.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Why?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : The biggest thing I worry about is the possibility of violence.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2 : We`re daily receiving threats, whether it`s through voicemails, emails, social media or in person.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You personally?\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2 : Oh, yes. And it`s escalating. And they`re all rooted in lies and misinformation, which is always very disappointing and sad. But at the same time, it`s real.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES: This has unfortunately become a way of life.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  has spoken openly about his need for therapy in the face of hostility driven by election denialism.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES: We`ve invested as a board in metal detectors, in fencing, in cameras. I wish we didn`t have to do this, but we do.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And how are you doing?\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES: I`m doing well. I`ve gone and gotten the support that I need, and I`m feeling great.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Georgia now gives every poll supervisor a direct line to report trouble to authorities.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : It lets us know what it is. Is it somebody yelling at people in the parking lot or is it somebody with a gun?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Voters say to me, I`m afraid that all these illegals are going to vote.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2 : You know, I understand the fear, but it`s an unfounded fear.\par \par \uc2 SETH BLUESTEIN: We`re just not seeing it in any real numbers.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES: This is a boogeyman. This is not something that people should be concerned about.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: If you could, with a magic wand, dispel one piece of election related disinformation, what would that be?\par \par \uc2 SETH BLUESTEIN: That there are magical ballot drops in the middle of the night. That window of time from when the polls close until the networks are able to call the race is really where that window of misinformation can spread.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES: For me, it would be the conspiracy theory that our tabulation machines are connected to the Internet. They`re not.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from December 1, 2020)\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : It has all gone too far.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: After the 2020 Election, \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2  chastised fellow Republicans for inciting unrest with election denying rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 : I think that threat remains viable. For 200 years, the loser accepting the outcome and coming back to fight again in two to four years was the way the system works, and we all accepted it. We have to get back to that being the normal way of dealing with elections.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2 : We`ve now endured four years of that same rhetoric, and that`s why I think all of us do feel a little bit of a heightened state of anxiety going into this cycle, that we could see that rhetoric transform into violent acts in the weeks ahead, and we all have to brace ourselves for that.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES: I continue to be disappointed by many people in the Republican Party, elected officials, who continue to be silent in the face of these threats. We cannot normalize threats of violence against anyone, but particularly those people who are literally running our democracy.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A sadly necessary plea for a nation to return to itself.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Major Garrett in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we thank them for their service to our country.\par \par \uc2 We`ll look ahead to tonight`s high-stakes debate and what could be the only face-to-face meeting between the two candidates. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of the CBS Evening News is sponsored by SOTYKTU. Find it at sotyktu.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Here`s another look at the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  in Philadelphia, the site of tonight`s presidential debate between Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This will also be the first time the two candidates have ever met. Election Day is now 56 days away.\par \par \uc2 CBS News special coverage begins at 8 PM Eastern, followed by the live debate simulcast, and then our expert analysis.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s CBS Evening News. I`m Norah O`Donnell in New York. We`ll see you back here for the debate.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240911ek9a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for August 30, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Cordes, Lilia Luciano, Janet Shamlian, Caitlin Huey-Burns,  Lonnie Quinn, Roxana Saberi, Steve Hartman, Cristian Benavides \par \uc2 3,713 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Attention, passengers.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Labor Day travel rush in full swing after a record- setting summer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CYRUS SARKARI (Managing Director of Customer Service, \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 ): Labor Day is one of the busiest. It`s the unofficial end of summer travel, and it caps off a really busy summer period.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The tens of millions of Americans traveling in what could be the busiest Labor Day weekend ever, plus the good news for those hitting the road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: When the gas prices are a lot cheaper, people want to get on the road and take the car. It`s cost-efficient.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Could thunderstorms and a heat wave upend your holiday weekend? Tonight, the information travelers need to know.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Nancy Cordes, in for Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Tens of millions of Americans are hitting the road and the airports this Labor Day weekend for one last taste of summer. The nation`s air terminals are packed with crowds and long lines, with today being the busiest day of the weekend for air travel. Millions of passengers are expected to be screened today at the airports, with Atlanta, Chicago O`Hare and Dallas Fort Worth among the busiest.\par \par \uc2 AAA says Labor Day travel will be up 9 percent over last year, with most people driving to their vacation destination.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Roxana Saberi will start us off tonight from Newark Liberty International Airport.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI (voiceover): At the nation`s airports today, millions of passengers are squeezing in one last trip of the summer.\par \par \uc2 TRAVELER: We`re traveling to Minnesota to see my brother.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The \uc2 TSA\uc2  expects to continue its record-breaking summer by screening over 17 million travelers this Labor Day weekend, including nearly three million today alone. Airlines are also expecting a record number of passengers.\par \par \uc2 For American, that`s nearly 14 percent more than at this time last year, for United, around 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 CYRUS SARKARI (Managing Director of Customer Service, \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 ): We have been preparing for this for a while. We have added about 1,500 more flights for the Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Helping fuel the trend, lower domestic airfares. Americans hitting the roads today are also finding lower prices at the pump. The national average is now $3.35 a gallon, nearly 50 cents lower than last year.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The traffic is so horrible.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s been really good. I do expect for it to get worse.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: AAA says the best time to avoid traffic tomorrow is in the afternoon and on Sunday in the morning.\par \par \uc2 AIXA DIAZ (AAA Spokesperson): Plan ahead on your trip. Have that route mapped out. That way, you have an alternate route if you do encounter a lot of traffic on the interstate.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Colleen (sp?) and J.D. Rader (sp?) said they didn`t plan ahead for long security lines this morning, when they flew from Austin to Newark for a friend`s wedding this weekend.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN RADER (Traveler): And we barely made it.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Wow.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN RADER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: So you were running to your gate?\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN RADER: Yes, hustling. But we love our friends, who are getting married, so we`re very excited.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN RADER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: And the lines are getting longer here at Newark Airport, as people end the workweek and head out of town. For those returning home after the holiday weekend, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says Monday will be one of the busiest days in the skies next week -- Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good to know.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi in Newark, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 A number of strong storm systems across the country could put a damper on your holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 So let`s bring in \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lonnie Quinn with the forecast.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Hey, good evening, Nancy. Good evening, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the skyline of New York City draped with cloud cover. For New York City, Monday is going to be the pick of the three-day weekend. Right now, if I`m looking at the roughest weather anywhere, I`m going to focus on Detroit. Detroit is under a severe thunderstorm watch with some warnings out there as well for the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 That line will be pushing to the east. So, for tomorrow, it`s more like the mid-Atlantic up to the Northeast. But in terms of flooding, we are focusing on the Gulf Coast, from Galveston over to New Orleans. And this is not a one-day event. This could be a few days in a row, picking up potentially nine inches of rain.\par \par \uc2 But then you get to your Labor Day holiday, and the bulk of the country, not 100 percent, but a good chunk of the country has a great Labor Day. A place like Charleston, South Carolina, could see some showers out there, temperatures around 86 degrees.\par \par \uc2 But that`s the latest. Nance, it`s all yours. Have a very happy holiday.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That`s encouraging. We will take it, Lonnie. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Hockey League\uc2  is in mourning tonight following the death of \uc2 Columbus Blue Jackets\uc2  star Johnny Gaudreau. The 31-year-old New Jersey native and his 29-year-old brother, Matthew, were riding their bicycles when they were struck and killed by a suspected drunk driver not far from their hometown of Carneys Point.\par \par \uc2 State Police said the driver was attempting to pass two other vehicles when he hit the brothers from behind on the eve of their sister`s wedding. The driver reportedly admitted to driving, drinking five or six beers before the crash and was charged with two counts of death by auto.\par \par \uc2 In Dallas tonight, a candlelight vigil for a police officer who officials say was ambushed in his patrol car, shot and killed at point-blank range. Investigators say police exchanged fire with the gunman Thursday night. Two officers were wounded in that shoot-out and the suspect was killed.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re learning the identity of the 46-year-old officer who died, Darron Burks, a former teacher who just completed police training.\par \par \uc2 With only 67 days to go before Election Day, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is facing some backlash from some of his most ardent supporters over comments he made about abortion. The former president said he believes Florida`s new six-week abortion ban is too restrictive and signaled that he would vote to overturn it, only to reverse himself late this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Donald Trump`s sudden claim that he plans to make IVF free if elected has stumped some of his supporters.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): The government is going to pay for it or we`re going to get or mandate your insurance company to pay for it, which is going to be great.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The former president has not yet detailed how the government would pay for the treatments, which could cost billions of dollars a year.\par \par \uc2 But his proposal comes as he faces a significant gender gap in his campaign against \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . CBS News` polling shows the vice president leads Trump among voters who say abortion is a major factor in their vote. The issue will also be on the ballot in Trump`s home state of Florida in November, where voters will decide whether to codify abortion rights into the state`s constitution up to the period of gestation.\par \par \uc2 After signaling an openness this week to supporting that measure, Trump closed the door in an interview on Friday.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: So, I think six weeks -- you need more time than six weeks. I have disagreed with that right from the early primaries, when I heard about it. I disagreed with it. At the same time, the Democrats are radical, because the nine months is just a ridiculous situation. So, I will be voting no for that reason.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But Trump`s criticism of the Florida law and his pledge to have the government fund IVF has worried some top conservative leaders.\par \par \uc2 TONY PERKINS\uc2  (President, \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2 ): He runs the risk of de- energizing, de-motivating his base by moving away from an issue that has been and continues to be a prominent issue. Backing away from that fundamental principle that the party has championed for 45 years is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And, meanwhile, Democrats aren`t giving Trump any credit, arguing he is the one who put IVF in jeopardy by nominating the Supreme Court justices who overturned Roe v. Wade, a decision, Nancy, that Trump told me just last week he has no regrets about.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And now people really trying to figure out where he is on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to have complete coverage of the presidential debate hosted by ABC on September 10, starting with a preview special at 8:00 p.m. Eastern right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . The live simulcast of the debate will be at 9:00 p.m. Eastern, followed by expert analysis. We hope you join us.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has obtained shocking video showing a Michigan teenager driving nearly 140 miles per hour just days before a high-speed crash last November that killed his friend. The victim`s family wants the driver`s mother to be charged, because she had been tracking his reckless driving on a mobile app.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNE VANKER (Mother): Every day, I wake up and it literally feels like a horror show.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): This is what`s left of the \uc2 BMW\uc2  X3 from the crash that killed Anne Vanker`s 18-year-old son, Flynn MacKrell, minutes after he left his home in Grosse Pointe Farms, Michigan, last November.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Should I try to grab him from on --\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Flynn`s 16-year-old friend Kiernan Tague was behind the wheel, police say driving over 100 miles per hour on a residential street where the limit was 25. He survived.\par \par \uc2 Investigators found videos on Tague`s phone showing a pattern of excessive speeding, matched, they say, by records from an app called \uc2 Life360\uc2 , which his mother used to track his car in the weeks leading up to the crash. In just 17 days, the app recorded a quarter of his trips involved speeds over 100 miles per hour, 10 percent over 120 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 Tague`s mother was concerned about his driving, texting him: "It scares me to my bone, and slow the eff down right now."\par \par \uc2 MacKrell`s parents believe Tague`s mother, Elizabeth Puleo-Tague, could and should have stopped him.\par \par \uc2 What kind of justice are you seeking?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I think both of them should go to jail.\par \par \uc2 ANNE VANKER: Gross negligence, manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: They compare the case to that of the Oxford, Michigan, school shooter Ethan Crumbley. His parents were convicted for not securing the gun he used in the 2021 killing of four people.\par \par \uc2 CBS News legal contributor Jessica Levinson says she believes this case could be even stronger.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: She had months and months of knowledge of her son`s reckless driving, and she not only failed to take the keys away. She actually gave him a car that could go faster.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Tague was charged with second-degree murder. Right now, he`s out on bail. But if he`s convicted and sentenced as an adult, he could face life in prison.\par \par \uc2 The family`s attorney had no comment, citing pending litigation -- Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: What an unusual case.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  is aiming for next Friday night to send \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s troubled Starliner capsule back to Earth from the International Space Station without those two astronauts who have been waiting since June for a ride home. Officials have determined the capsule`s thruster problems make it unsafe for the astronauts. So engineers will try to remotely command it back to Earth.\par \par \uc2 Those astronauts, who were only supposed to be in space for about a week, will now come home in February aboard a rival \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  capsule.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  customers, you may have been overcharged for basics like milk and eggs. An executive for the grocery store chain acknowledged to keeping some prices high in areas where \uc2 Kroger\uc2  had fewer competitors even after inflation had eased. A spokesman calls the comments cherry-picked.\par \par \uc2 But all food shoppers are feeling the pinch this Labor Day weekend as they gather around the grill. While inflation -- inflation has fallen below 3 percent for the first time since 2021, food inflation remains a problem.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian digs into the issue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): The unofficial end of summer officially arriving with higher prices for that Labor Day barbecue.\par \par \uc2 NAOMI VEAL (Customer): Prices have gone up. Most all prices are up. The wings here especially have really escalated in price.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This is Bud`s House of Meat in Houston.\par \par \uc2 How much product will you go through this weekend?\par \par \uc2 TIM HINER (Owner, Bud`s House of Meat): We`re going to go through a lot, about 1,600 of leg quarters, 2,000 pounds of sausage, 5,000 pounds of ribs.\par \par \uc2 I need you to go help cut orders.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Owner Tim Hiner also stocks all the barbecue side dishes, including dessert, plus staples like milk and eggs.\par \par \uc2 TIM HINER: Eggs are crazy. Eggs are about $1.50, almost $2 more than my cost was last year.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For shoppers, the price of a dozen eggs is up 47 percent, now averaging more than $3. In one year, hot dogs are up almost 10 percent, ground beef nearly 8, ribs more than 4 percent, and beans 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 That beer you plan to wash it all down with has increased more than 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 What did you buy today?\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL TRIMMER (Customer): Steak.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: How much did it cost?\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL TRIMMER: Twenty-eight dollars.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: What did you think it would cost?\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL TRIMMER: About 10.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Even with inflation cooling, experts say prices are rising due to higher labor costs, drought conditions and, in the case of beef and poultry, low inventory, bird flu also taking a toll.\par \par \uc2 Has it changed your buying habits?\par \par \uc2 NAOMI VEAL: To some degree, yes. There are some things that I just rarely or buy don`t buy anymore. Even some meat products, I don`t buy anymore.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But they are still buying. You can see it`s busy in here tonight right before closing time.\par \par \uc2 More than 70 percent of consumers today will celebrate Labor Day, a majority planning to grill or barbecue with family and friends -- Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Now you have got us all really hungry, Janet.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much, Janet Shamlian, in Houston tonight.\par \par \uc2 A group of renowned authors and major publishing companies take legal action against the state of Florida over its controversial book ban.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Several top-selling authors and major publishing companies are now suing the state of Florida over a controversial law that led to the banning of hundreds of books.\par \par \uc2 We get details now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Cristian Benavides.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voiceover): On 16-year-old Jack Hayes` bookshelf, you will find books that may no longer be available in some Florida classrooms.\par \par \uc2 JACK HAYES (Student): Ooh, "Slaughterhouse-Five."\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: His mother, Judi, said the Florida law about restricting certain books in school libraries is hurting Jack`s ability to learn.\par \par \uc2 JUDI HAYES (Mother): We`re not talking about "Playboy" magazine. We`re talking about "Anna Karenina."\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The Hayes are part of a lawsuit filed Thursday by \uc2 Penguin Random House\uc2  and several other companies that argues the law violates the First Amendment and created a regime of strict censorship in schools.\par \par \uc2 Right now, any county resident can challenge books over their sexual content. That book must be removed within five school days and remain unavailable until the objection is resolved. Classics that have been pulled include Maya Angelou`s "I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings" and Ernest Hemingway`s "For Whom the Bell Tolls."\par \par \uc2 LAURIE HALSE ANDERSON (Author, "Speak"): It is not protecting children.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Author Laurie Halse Anderson`s book "Speak" about sexual assault was removed.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE HALSE ANDERSON: Parents can say, no, I don`t want my child to read that. That`s their choice for their kid. But what they don`t have the right to do is to decide for other people`s children.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The lawsuit names the Florida Board of Education as a defendant. They called it a stunt. Not named is Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who championed the law.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida) (Presidential Candidate): They try to say that this is -- quote -- "banning books." But what you have in a school, you have to make judgments about what`s appropriate or not.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Jack Hayes said politics should not play a role in his education.\par \par \uc2 JACK HAYES: If a student wants to read, they should be able to read that book.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: A \uc2 Florida Department of Education\uc2  spokesperson said there are no books banned in Florida.\par \par \uc2 But PEN America, a free speech organization, said there have been over 3,000 challenges to books in over -- in 11 school districts here in the state between 2021 and 2023. That`s more than anywhere else in the nation - - Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides in Orlando, Florida, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Popular foods like Twinkies and Cheetos could soon be banned from the menus at California public schools. We will explain next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is weighing whether to approve a first-of-its-kind ban on certain artificial dyes in meals served in the state`s public school cafeterias.\par \par \uc2 The bill passed by state lawmakers would prohibit foods and drinks containing sixth synthetic colorings that have been linked to behavioral issues in kids. If approved, the ban would take effect in December of 2027.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next. One of the marvels of being a parent is watching how quickly your kids grow. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman goes On the Road with one family to see how they handle the important milestone.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: On this Friday, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman takes us On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY BISHOP (Mother): Those are big muscles.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): The Bishop family of Festus, Missouri, can smile now. But a few months ago the mood was much more somber, as Courtney, Jake, and their daughter Hazel said goodbye to a very important member of the family.\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP (Father): We had been trying to wean her off of it for some time.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Hazel`s beloved pacifier, affectionately known as paci, had been with her for years. And Jake knew saying goodbye could come with tears and tantrums.\par \par \uc2 So he searched for creative approaches to ease the transition, like cutting off the end.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Broken.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Soaking it in vinegar.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We got to cover them up so they can grow.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Or planting it in a pot.\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: I did it a little bit differently.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A little bit?\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: A little bit differently.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: No, a lot a bit differently.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: OK, it`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 We are gathered here to remember Pass "The Pacifier" Bishop --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: -- one of the greatest pacis to have ever graced the lips of a toddler.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Jake gave paci a full-on funeral.\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: I have Hazel and the paci.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Of course, before the Internet, parents used to just take the thing away and deal with the tantrum. But, today, young parents like Jake, for better or worse, would rather not go to war over a Binky.\par \par \uc2 They`re paci-fists, if you will.\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: You need them to go through some tough times to really grow as a person, but you don`t need to make extra tough times for them. They will -- they will have plenty of those coming up.\par \par \uc2 Put the dirt on top of the paci, yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Parents delight in marking milestones. But those milestones are often bittersweet --\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- because each turning point is a point of no return.\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: She`s transitioning to the next stage of her life. We just got to hold on to those moments and those memories, and this is why we do this stuff. It gives us these memories to look back on, because it goes fast.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 HAZEL BISHOP (Daughter): I want to do it again, please.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Hazel hasn`t asked for it again since, although, shortly after the ceremony, Jake dug it up and buried it again in a keepsake box.\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: I love you so much, OK?\par \par \uc2 HAZEL BISHOP: OK.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Making dad the one who just can`t let go.\par \par \uc2 JAKE BISHOP: Good night, sweet pea.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman On the Road near St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Have a great holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240831ek8u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Accepts Historic Nomination; Trump Visits the Border; Inside Ukraine`s Drone Offensive; Easing Menopause Symptoms; Supporting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; Polaris Dawn; The Heavy Heavy; CBS News Fact Checks \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` Speech; "Don`t Dream It`s Over;" Preserving Political Memorabilia; Giggly Squad; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Making Your Voice Heard, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Jericka Duncan, Major Garrett, Tony Dokoupil,  Weijia Jiang, Joel Payne, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Ian Lee, Celine Gounder, Mark  Strassmann, Ed O`Keefe, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 13,736 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is Friday, August 23rd. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And I am Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle and Nate are off. Tony is at the Democratic National Convention. He`ll join us in just a moment. Here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): Let us write the next great chapter in the most extraordinary story ever told.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Vice President Harris brings down the house as she formally accepts her party`s nomination for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  visits the southern border and promises severe punishments for immigrants who commit crimes.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Impose the death penalty on major drug dealers and traffickers.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We talk to Ukrainian soldiers as they fight to keep control of Russian territory.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Ukraine is keeping up with the drone attacks deep inside Russia.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Evacuations are underway as a volcano spews ash and red hot lava in Iceland, less than 30 miles south of the capital.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D-NC): I`m Roy Cooper, the last guy standing between you and the moment we`re all waiting for.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Beyonce? No, I`m just asking. I`m just asking. I know the DNC said she wasn`t coming, but TMZ said she was.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I accept your nomination to be president.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("The Daily Show" host): By the way, how funny it would have been if at the end she was just like, but seriously, though, not for me.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wow. An anniversary she won`t forget either.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Absolutely. If you`re a Democrat, you got to be feeling cautiously optimistic.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: To Jon Stewart`s point, that would have been a surprise.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It would have been --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Ah nah. I don`t think so. I feel it, but no, not really.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Maybe next time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Maybe next time.\par \par \uc2 So that`s where we`re going to begin this morning at the DNC, with an unprecedented moment in American history.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  became the first woman of color to accept the presidential nomination for a major political party.\par \par \uc2 Tony is leading our coverage in Chicago, and he saw it happen. He was in the room where it happened.\par \par \uc2 Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, good morning.\par \par \uc2 I was on the floor. It was hard to see the candidate at times. So many people were waving placards, hard to hear her as well. There was so much applause. But let`s linger on that candidate, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , this is someone who, just four weeks and five days ago, was not expected to deliver this speech at all, and she has now accepted her party`s nomination.\par \par \uc2 She did it on behalf of, "Everyone whose story could only be written in the greatest nation on Earth." And it was a moment capped by a balloon drop that filled the hall to my waist at times.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, just so happened to fall on her 10th wedding anniversary to husband, Doug Emhoff, and it followed a full week of music and star-studded speeches.\par \par \uc2 We heard from Oprah, we heard from both Obamas, both Clintons, a whole raft of rising Democratic stars. And then finally, Harris herself, who laid out what she called her unlikely journey as a daughter of immigrants who became a prosecutor for the people, as she so often puts it.\par \par \uc2 It`s a story that she said, echoing Obama that is only possible in America.\par \par \uc2 And on the issues, her promise to voters is change and opportunity. From the border to foreign policies, she also pledged security and dignity and balance. And yes, she went straight at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 To help us digest all of this, Weijia Jiang is here as well. She was also in the room.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, I hope you had a better view than I did.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Tony, and good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 You know, from my vantage point, right next to the stage, I could see the vice president`s teleprompter, and she stayed almost exactly to the script.\par \par \uc2 She edited that speech down to the wire, going through each line with her hand in what aides described as a deeply personal message to the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I accept your nomination to be president of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The sound of history in the making.\par \par \uc2 As Vice President Harris vowed to fight for the future.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I know there are people of various political views watching tonight, and I want you to know I promise to be a president for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Harris opened up about how her journey began with the influence of her mother.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: She taught us to never complain about injustice, but do something about it.\par \par \uc2 She also taught us and never do anything half-assed.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And a career enforcing laws.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Every day in the courtroom, I stood proudly before a judge, and I said five words "\uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for the people."\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Harris laid out the case against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , arguing that he is a threat at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I will not cozy up to tyrants and dictators like Kim Jong-un who are rooting for Trump, who are rooting for Trump because you know, they know, they know he is easy to manipulate with flattery and favors.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The vice president hit on every top issue in this election -- the economy, reproductive rights, and the conflict in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: President Biden and I are working to end this war such that Israel is secure, the hostages are released, the suffering in Gaza ends, and the Palestinian people can realize their right to dignity, security, freedom, and self-determination.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Harris` speech capped off a night that highlighted her professional and personal life, including a memorable moment when Harris`s grandnieces Amara and Leela explained how to say their Auntie`s name correctly, something her opponent frequently gets wrong.\par \par \uc2 AMARA AJAGU (VP \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  grandniece): First you say Kama like a comma in a sentence.\par \par \uc2 LEELA AJAGU (VP \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  grandniece): Then you say, la, like, la, la, la, la.\par \par \uc2 AMARA AJAGU: Everybody over here say "Kama."\par \par \uc2 (CROWD answer "Kama.")\par \par \uc2 LEELA AJAGU: Everybody over here say "La."\par \par \uc2 (CROWD answer "La.")\par \par \uc2 KERRY WASHINGTON (Actor): Together --\par \par \uc2 (CROWD answer "Kamala.")\par \par \uc2 (SINGERS singing)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: A sea of white paid tribute to the women`s suffrage movement, but also honoring the first time a Black and South Asian woman is the presidential nominee of a major party.\par \par \uc2 What did it mean to you to be here as the vice president accepted the nomination?\par \par \uc2 ADORA JENKINS (DNC participant): It`s hard to put into words to see this woman who has spent her whole life in service, really coming out here and speaking from her heart in a way that connects with so many people here in this country.\par \par \uc2 As a woman, myself, as a mother, as a Black person, it`s just been just an incredible, incredible night.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Right after the address, Vice President Harris told reporters that she felt good, but it was time to move on to the next thing, and she`s expected back on the campaign trail next week.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the next big thing, Tony, in just over two weeks, she is set to debate Trump.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be following it.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Democratic strategist Joel Payne, a CBS News political contributor.\par \par \uc2 Joel, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 This is your line of work. Who cares what I think? Do you have any notes or compliments for the vice president?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, look, I`m a baseball fan, a tortured \uc2 New York Mets\uc2  fan. She pitched the perfect game last night. But you know, just talking a little bit more about athleticism.\par \par \uc2 I think last night was a feat of political athleticism. Not just last night. That speech was difficult, the valleys that she navigated, the way she continued to keep that coalition together.\par \par \uc2 But look at the last month, look at what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has done in 31 days since July 21. She has consolidated the Democratic base. She`s rewritten the political map, and she shifted this race from a conversation with two octogenarians, near octogenarians, talking about the past, to one talking about the future.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  may or may not win this race, but she has created political relevance for herself that will go well beyond what we saw last week.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a patriotic speech. It was a speech that was very strong on defense, a lot of pride and talk of democracy. What does she need to do now to take this excitement and in the next 74 days, turn it into votes?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Yes, well, Tony, that`s remarkable, too. She reclaimed that freedom mantle. It was one that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  talked about in the frame of democracy. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has reframed it in a way that widens the circle of potential voters that Democrats can go for, potential routes.\par \par \uc2 What she needs to do going forward is she needs to think about what does she want to do in terms of building a winning coalition? Does it look more like Joe Biden`s 2020 winning coalition or does she look like a 2008-2012 Barack Obama coalition, which is more reliant on voters of color, African American voters, young voters?\par \par \uc2 I imagine the folks in Harris high command are thinking about things like that this morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joel, our man in Washington, Major Garrett, is in New York this morning, and he`s got a question for you as well.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Sure thing.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good morning, Joel.\par \par \uc2 When you give a speech like that last night, you have to talk about foreign policy and national security. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did that. Ukraine unifies the Democratic Party, the war in Gaza does not. She was very specific about that. Evaluate what she tried to accomplish and the divisions within the party on that underlying issue.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Yes, Major, it`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I talked about that political athleticism again, and the depth in this. You could tell her -- in the building, she was riding, kind of the emotion and the energy of the crowd. She did it in a way that, frankly, we think about who the previous Democratic nominee was, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That would have been a very difficult speech for him to give. One, given the political baggage that he is dealing with, but also the skill, the ability to have the crowd understand that there are things you need to say, but also getting the crowd to a place where you understand we are the united Democratic coalition, the united Democratic Party is.\par \par \uc2 I think she did that, and I think she continued to give herself flexibility to have credibility on that issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Joel, she really hit the Palestinian-Israeli question, I would say perfectly.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Security for the Israelis, dignity and sovereignty for the Palestinians, that is the equation.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Implementing it of course, is another matter.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: By the way, not all that different from where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is. It`s just a matter of the messenger. And does the messenger have the credibility, and does she allow for the hard work to be done for peace to be brought about in that region of the world? I think she continued to do that, and she was able to manage it politically.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Major, as I send it back to you in New York, I said it was hard to hear some of the speech because there was so much applause in the arena, but I got a copy of it here. There`s so much that stands out describing her approach to leadership and there is a note here where she says to the audience, she`ll be a leader who leads, yes, but also who listens. That stands out this morning as well.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Tony, thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we will be checking back with you in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hopes to get a boost today from Independent candidate, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , who could very likely end his campaign today and then endorse the former president.\par \par \uc2 Both men will be in Arizona this afternoon, where Trump is campaigning for a second consecutive day.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday at the southern border, Trump criticized Vice President Harris on immigration.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns was there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In the battleground border state of Arizona, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seized on a signature issue.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : When we win this November, we will end the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  border nightmare once and for all.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Delivering remarks beside part of the wall his administration built, the Republican nominee repeatedly attacked Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on immigration, an issue where polls show he has an advantage.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : With your vote, we will seal the border, stop the invasion, and launch the largest deportation effort in American history.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A CBS News poll shows 72 percent of registered voters say crossings will decrease if Trump wins, while only 21 percent say the same of Harris.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She will allow more than 100 million illegal aliens into our country. Our country will be overrun, and essentially it won`t be a country.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But what Trump leaves out is that illegal border crossing numbers are at their lowest point in almost four years, due in part to executive actions taken by President Biden in June.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats say Trump is helping to perpetuate the problem by killing the bipartisan border deal earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, Trump returns to Arizona for a rally, and has announced he will be joined by a special guest. Coincidentally, also in the state, Independent presidential candidate, Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. who may suspend his campaign today and endorse Trump.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : There are rumors that he is going to make an endorsement. That would be a great honor for me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And speaking of that rally tonight, you can see people behind me already lined up to see the former president.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a 66-year-old Arizona man was arrested yesterday after authorities say he threatened in social media posts to kill the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And security, as you know, has been ramped up significantly in the wake of that assassination attempt -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Turning to the war in Ukraine, that country`s military is now using drones for long range attacks on Russian territory, including a key air base and a cargo ferry just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Drones are also being used against Russian troops on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 Ian Lee visited a Ukrainian army unit along the border that launches those drone attacks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: A drone is prepped for its one way mission. The deadly payload , a grenade fixed with cable ties.\par \par \uc2 We met up with the 117th Territorial Defense Brigade preparing for a trip to the front to support operations in Russia`s Kursk Region.\par \par \uc2 Private Igor (sp?) pilots the drone on a test run, but a real mission means he could be watching someone`s final moments.\par \par \uc2 Does it ever feel personal?\par \par \uc2 (PRIVATE IGOR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: "Yes, it`s emotional," he says. "But I understand that we chose the right path."\par \par \uc2 (SERGEANT ALEX speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Sergeant Alex (sp?) leads the unit.\par \par \uc2 How important is this drone in the fight against Russia?\par \par \uc2 (SERGEANT ALEX speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: "Very important. This system is far more effective and precise than artillery," he says, and as onboard cameras reveal, that makes drone units like these a prime target for Russian drones and Moscow has more of them.\par \par \uc2 On an average day, this squad conducts up to five search and destroy missions targeting Russian armor, vehicles, and troops. The goal is straightforward.\par \par \uc2 What`s your mission today?\par \par \uc2 (SERGEANT ALEX speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: "We always have a single mission," he says. "Destroy the enemy." An enemy they`re now fighting on Russian soil.\par \par \uc2 Ian Lee, CBS News on the Ukraine-Russia border.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Coming up, a pill that seems to relieve a familiar symptom of menopause without using hormones. We`ll ask Dr. Celine Gounder about the potential impact. But first, it is 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, DNC delegates tell Tony they`re behind Vice President Harris all the way. But do they know about her policies?\par \par \uc2 Plus, Anthony Mason talks to the 80s band, Crowded House about the inspiration behind "Hey, now, hey, now, don`t dream it`s over."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Rock Arena/Australian Broadcasting Corporation)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, you know the song, still a hit after nearly 40 years.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Airsupra. Visit us at airsupra.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In today`s "Health Watch," new data suggests an experimental pill eased hot flashes for women in menopause, a symptom more than 75 percent of females experience during their lives. The pill does not use hormones.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is editor-at-large for public health at KFF. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 I was saying that by the time I get to menopause, I may not ever experience it at the rate that we`re going, which isn`t that far off, but needless to say, how does this drug work?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So when your estrogen levels drop, there`s a part of the brain that starts to rev up and produce a substance called neurokinin, which impacts the nervous system as well as your blood vessels. And that`s why, as you go through menopause, you can have issues with hot flashes, problems with your sleep and so on.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And as far as the element of not using hormones, why is that so important?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, some women cannot take hormones, so maybe they`ve had breast cancer, heart attack, stroke, blood clots, but some women are afraid to take hormones and this dates back to a study that was done over 20 years ago on hormone replacement therapy.\par \par \uc2 There were a lot of problems with that study. And now, in retrospect, we understand for the vast majority of women, hormone replacement therapy is safe, and I think that`s really important to emphasize that, because a lot of women are not using it, who could be using it, but now we have some alternatives for women who can`t or don`t want to take hormone replacement.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: There`s always side effects. Talk about that and when we can expect this to come to market.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So the two main side effects we`ve seen in the clinical trials with the elinzanetant, the new option that we just saw come out published studies yesterday, the two main side effects are headache and fatigue. This is on data that`s been reported out to 26 weeks of use.\par \par \uc2 So obviously women would be on these medications for much longer. Are there longer term side effects? We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And as far as when we could see this come out?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So \uc2 Bayer\uc2 , the company that makes it, has submitted an application over the summer, and they expect to hear back from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  sometime in September.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay, that`s good news for people like myself. Looking forward to it.\par \par \uc2 Doctor Gounder, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Tony`s conversation with folks in Chicago about how well they knew Kamala Harris`s policies before her big speech last night. Their answers, well, they might surprise you.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back, 7:30 CBS MORNINGS, and as they clean up here in Chicago and we process Vice President Harris` speech from last night, we can talk about this: Her goal, to define herself before Republicans have an opportunity to do the defining for her and she laid out some of her big policy positions on the issue. Some, but not all, and not always in that much detail.\par \par \uc2 However, we spoke to delegates who told us they actually don`t really need a detailed platform to feel that they already know exactly what she stands for.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With so much support inside the convention, it`s easy to forget that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was not the Democratic Party`s first choice to take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 When did you become a supporter of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for president?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: As soon as Joe said he wasn`t going to go.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Pretty much as soon as President Biden said we`re going to line up behind her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the first time since the 60s, delegates, including everyone we ran into at the United Center --\par \par \uc2 Hi, I`m Tony.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I know you are, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are backing a candidate who didn`t run in a single primary.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is new. It`s new for everybody. It`s new for Kamala. It`s new for Joe. It`s new for all of us.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is it odd? Yes, but we live in odd times.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (Former US Secretary of State): The choice in this election could not be clearer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  ran for president in 2016, she had more than 200 different policy proposals, a level of detail \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  matched with a 110-page policy pitch in 2020, both packed with lots of interviews.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We have to bring the middle class back. We have to have a health care policy that makes sense.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: How are you doing, Hannah?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Harris, by contrast, hasn`t done a single major media interview or even added a policy section to her campaign website, and that`s perfectly fine, at least for these Democratic delegates.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everything is happening really fast. Give her a minute, and you`ll hear everything that you need to hear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They say voters already know exactly where she stands on reproductive rights, and she started rolling out some economic policies, too.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, they`re not worried.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: She has been at the White House for four years. She knows the policies, she`s been doing on-the-job training for four years. That`s the kicker.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: She`s not going to move far off the policies where we are now, if she moved it all as far as President Biden hatch, after all, she was his vice president.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And yet --\par \par \uc2 She`s not going to take all the policies of the Biden administration, right?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More than a third of Americans say they don`t know where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  stands.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We will chart a new way forward.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And when you get down to specific issues, neither do a lot of the people who voted to nominate her.\par \par \uc2 Energy policy? Is she for all the above? Is she for limiting oil and gas? What?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Is she for all that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Well, I don`t need either.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: You know, I think what we`re looking at is trying to figure that out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s her position on marijuana legalization?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m waiting. I`m waiting to hear with you on the same on those issues.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But whatever the policies of a potential Harris administration, many folks here are certain they`ll be better than the alternative.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We can`t go back to Trump. I think the values of democracy, that I put time and effort to become a citizen, to defend and protect. I feel that`s an insult what happened.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s your number one policy. Can she beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? And you think yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Hell yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And some here even hope what is happening in Chicago is ultimately a model for campaigns of the future.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: People are saying, well, she hasn`t been out here long enough. Maybe our elections shouldn`t run as long as they run, making everybody get torn down, bitter, hateful, divisive. Maybe we need this to show us that we can do an election in three months, maybe six, over and done, and everybody is good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two points, of course, that I grant on the Democrats` behalf is that the other side, well, they have put a mosh pit of policies together over the years, about as fixed as a plate of Jell-O, and then, on the other hand, if she were to roll out all of these detailed policies right away, that would be suspicious as well, who wrote them?\par \par \uc2 The reality is, policy is hard. It takes time to get there, and ultimately, what these voters, these delegates, told me is that they know she`s going to have to work with Congress. It`s not clear who`s going to control those Houses of Congress.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, if she has an idea of what her values are, shares it with the voters and tells you where she wants to go, the details are going to get worked out later.\par \par \uc2 I`ll send it back to you on that note.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony, no one does these on the street interviews better than you. You connect with the people that you`re talking to, and they share with you really, really illuminating and interesting details about how they`re thinking.\par \par \uc2 Another job well done, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Tony doesn`t need to introduce himself, quite obviously.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He does not. He does not.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I know who you are. Come on, let`s talk.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He needs no introduction, Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Let`s talk. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Major, I wanted to get your thoughts. Because you`ve covered so many of these conventions over the course of your career, and I was thinking to myself about indelible moments of conventions past where whether it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Ted Cruz in that moment that Trump appeared on the stage.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Or \uc2 George HW Bush\uc2  "read my lips," the audacity of hope, it felt like this convention, we had multiple moments.\par \par \uc2 We had the Obamas, we had Oprah, we had Tim Walz`s family reacting to him. How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So there was a constellation of moments that all reinforced one thing, Democrats now believe there is a possible victory ahead of them. They did not believe that in their gut when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was leading the ticket and the orientation of this convention, and that speech last night clarifies one aspect of this campaign, who is the challenger and who is the face of incumbency?\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the face of incumbency until he dropped out. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , with that speech said, I`m the challenger. I`m the new person, and I have to introduce myself, explain who I am, what I am in a way that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did in 1992 when he was the challenger, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  did in 2008.\par \par \uc2 She did not give the incumbent administrative speech. She gave I`m the introducing speech. I`m the challenger. This is what frustrates the Trump campaign so much. This arrow of incumbency, has that which incumbency is not just a job title, it`s a state of mind, and it shifted in Trump`s direction.\par \par \uc2 Trump is now the hyper familiar face of American politics for now, going on 10 years, and he`s trying to get out of that. And this convention was a way for Harris to say, I`m the challenger. We have possibilities. There is something new.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Major, real quick, if I could. How much of policy matters? Everyone sort of feels like, if you were at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  or the DNC, those are your people. But how much of the voting population is still on the fringe, and they`re just not sure?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It does matter and this election will be the first national presidential campaign post the end of Roe versus Wade. On that directional matter, Harris is clear, and I believe that will be the underlying, most pivotal, most motivating issue in the country.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Polls will be out in just a couple of days. We`ll see if she gets a bump.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, we`re going to take you inside a pioneering \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  mission that could open the door for new opportunities in outer space.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Four astronauts are scheduled to blast off early Tuesday on a historic six- day mission called Polaris Dawn. They will climb to a record orbit, 870 miles above Earth, that is more than three times higher than the International Space Station. The mission will also attempt something brand new, the first ever commercial spacewalk.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann met the crew who spent two years training for this ambitious quest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , we are a go for two preservation.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In the crew training room at \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  headquarters, Michael Blasco (sp?) and his team play Gremlin.\par \par \uc2 Their job, make life tough for four civilian astronauts.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: One, low pressure. Abort. Abort. Abort.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You`re throwing problems at them.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BLASCO (\uc2 SpaceX\uc2 ): Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Emergencies.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BLASCO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Our favorite part of the job is just stress people out and see how they respond.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Leading this crew, Jared Isaacman, a 41-year-old billionaire entrepreneur.\par \par \uc2 JARED ISAACMAN (Polaris Dawn mission commander): Welcome, everybody to Crew Dragon Resilience.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In 2021, he paid for and commanded Inspiration4, the first all-civilian mission to orbit the Earth. He hopes this mission, Polaris Dawn, will kick start development of new tools for even deeper space exploration.\par \par \uc2 JARED ISAACMAN: I didn`t grow up the time to see humans walk on the moon. I really would like my daughters to you know, see humans walking on Mars someday.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JARED ISAACMAN: Now we need to kind of start, you know, establishing some of the building blocks to get there.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Dragon --\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Isaacman`s three crewmates include retired fighter pilot, Scott Poteet and two \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  employees, Anna Menon and Sarah Gillis.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is go for spacewalk operations.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The mission highlight and engineering challenge, the first commercial spacewalk. Roughly 435 miles above Earth, their Dragon capsule will vent down to the vacuum of space.\par \par \uc2 Two of the astronauts, Isaacman and Gillis, will alternate floating through the open hatch for roughly 15 minutes a piece.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like training for something that essentially has never been done before.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GILLIS (Polaris Dawn mission specialist): A huge part of the last couple years is not only developing the hardware that we need, but also developing the training for how do we go and successfully demonstrate we can do this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: ZV1 is transitioning for egress.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This special crane simulates microgravity. We watch the astronauts practice exiting the hatch and testing their mobility.\par \par \uc2 JARED ISAACMAN: Hopefully \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  can consider this.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You`re getting better at this through sheer reputation.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GILLIS: Oh, yes. Totally. Every single time.\par \par \uc2 It`s a world of difference for the whole operation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Deep pressure five. PSI, 26 percent humidity.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: To make this spacewalk a reality, these spacesuits were redesigned from helmet to toe. They also have to withstand thermal extremes in the vacuum of space.\par \par \uc2 JARED ISAACMAN: Oh, it has to have all the means to sustain life. When it`s pressurized up, what you`re seeing there is a mini spaceship.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And every part has to work.\par \par \uc2 STU KEECH (SpaceX Dragon program vice president): There`s a huge responsibility. Flying people is the most severe thing that we do at \uc2 SpaceX.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Stu Keech is vice president of SpaceX Dragon Program.\par \par \uc2 STU KEECH: We try to design the mission, and then we think about anything that could go wrong and make sure we have a plan to keep humans safe.\par \par \uc2 The same is true for this spacewalk.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The goal with all this stuff is no surprises.\par \par \uc2 STU KEECH: There have to be no surprises.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Space flight is inherently risky, but this crew is confident in their training and in the company about to make space history.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT POTEET (Polaris Dawn pilot): It`s been an absolute amazing journey, training for this mission, and we just can`t wait to go to space.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: There`s some nerves involved.\par \par \uc2 ANNA MENON (Polaris Dawn mission specialist): I have seen the way they do business. I have seen the way they handle change and risk and development. I very confidently put my life in their hands.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For a mission that could open the door to new possibilities in space.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Mark Strassmann in Hawthorne, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: As Jericka says, I can`t wait till we send Mark Strassmann into outer space.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in "What to Watch" --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And bring him back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And bring him back and bring him back, Strassmann. You`re coming back.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have the stories you`ll be talking about today. But right now, it is 7:45, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We`ve got a Crowded House coming up at eight. I`m talking about the band, Crowded House --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Rock Arena/Australian Broadcasting Corporation)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: -- famous for the smash hit, "Don`t Dream It`s Over."\par \par \uc2 They tell Anthony Mason how they wrote the song and how it changed their lives for our series "Mornings Mixtape." All of that in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 But first -- this is where I get to do a little walk and talk here -- Vlad will have the latest stories that he`ll be talking about in "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: What a beautiful walk and talk.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Eylea HD.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is time now for "What to Watch" I will bring myself in for the celebration.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yay. Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because we don`t have a lot of time, and I`ve got a couple of stories to share with everybody.\par \par \uc2 The US Open is underway right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wooh.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: With some very, very big giveaways for fan week.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fans can snag special editions of Marvel Comics. Check this out. This Marvel Comics -- you`ve got the Hulk, you`ve got Naomi Osaka. What can be better than that? Biggest names alongside the Avengers, look at Serena Williams. What?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The stories reflect this year`s tournament theme, which is celebrating the power of tennis.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Is that Nate? No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Captain America. But it could be Nate. Nate is Captain America. I`d buy that.\par \par \uc2 Fans can also watch the players practice, play interactive games and win big prizes before play begins on Monday. Here y`all go all. Here you go. Here is your tennis racket.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Oh, yes. Smallest tennis racket ever.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Today through Sunday, the Wilson Company will give out these free junior rackets --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Junior. Emphasis.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- at the Billie Jean King National Tennis Center, home of the US Open. We`ve got cookies. You`ve got comic books. You`ve got tennis rackets.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, we`re set.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We are set.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: USA.\par \par \uc2 All right, in our series, "Mixtape Moments," we take turns sharing a song from an emerging artist. I`m up today with the folk rock and roll band, The Heavy Heavy.\par \par \uc2 The British band, was formed back in 2019 by Georgie Fuller and William Turner. The lead single from its debut album is called "Happiness." Check it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Happiness"/The Heavy Heavy/ATO Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Out of this? Out of this? Yes, because we love this song. The Heavy Heavy has performed here in studio on "CBS Saturday Morning."\par \par \uc2 You can check out The Heavy Heavy and other songs and artists we feature right here on our \uc2 Spotify\uc2  playlist. Search for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Mixtape on \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or scan the QR code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back, folks. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Hey.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I was told there would be no dancing.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this hour, Anthony Mason talks to the band, Crowded House for "Mornings Mixtape." They`ll tell him the back story of their biggest hit, "Don`t Dream It`s Over."\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, we`re also talking to the host of the "Giggly Squad" about how it became one of America`s most popular podcast.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 With the 2024 DNC in the books, some of the memorabilia from the event will soon be in a museum.\par \par \uc2 We will meet the historian who figure out, what do you do with all that stuff?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Put it in a shoebox.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Put in a museum.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There you go. There you go.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Or shoebox --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I accept your nomination to be president of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  becomes the first Black and South Asian woman to be the presidential nominee of a major political party.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Independent presidential candidate Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. could end his campaign as soon as today.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: After two years of training, a crew of civilian astronauts is ready for the first ever commercial spacewalk.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You`re getting better at this through sheer reputation.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GILLIS: Oh, yes. Totally, every single time. It`s a world of difference for the whole operation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And at the DNC, the Vice President`s grandnieces steal the show with their adorable lesson on how to pronounce her name.\par \par \uc2 AMARA (VP \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  grandniece): First you say Kama like a comma in a sentence.\par \par \uc2 LEELA (VP \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  grandniece): Then you say, la, like, la, la, la, la.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Wait, wait. Slow down, you guys. I am sorry, what was that? Kama, I am sorry. Hold on. Slow down. What was the first name?\par \par \uc2 What was the first name? I didn`t realize -- I didn`t realize there was going to be another cognitive test.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, if you didn`t know; now, you know.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Now, you know. Learn your lessons well, ladies and gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We begin this hour with last night`s historic speech by Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , as she became the first woman of color to be nominated for president by a major US party.\par \par \uc2 Harris used her remarks to put out her biography, of course, and explain some of her vision for the next part of America`s history, while also, of course, criticizing the policies of her opponent, former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: He doesn`t actually fight for the middle class. Instead, he fights for himself and his billionaire friends, and he will give them another round of tax breaks that will add up to $5 trillion to the national debt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Very happy to say this, Tony Dokoupil joins us yet again from Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Tony, our CBS News confirm team, which I spend a fair amount of time with, factchecked that claim and some others in the vice president`s speech, what did they find?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, Major. Good morning to you again.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have a full breakdown on CBSNews.com, but I do want to highlight a few of the findings in the speech that did indeed keep the factcheckers busy.\par \par \uc2 On that first claim that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to pass tax cuts for the rich, adding $5 trillion to the national debt in the process, that`s misleading, according to our CBS News confirm team, which is a term, of course, that could be applied to a lot of our political speech, but I digress.\par \par \uc2 Here`s how they got to that label. Donald Trump`s 2017 tax cuts did indeed lower taxes for the rich, aka high income earners. But here`s the thing, it also lower taxes for most households.\par \par \uc2 So in the short term, most people, not only the rich would benefit from these tax cuts being extended. All of that, by the way, according to the Congressional Joint Committee on Taxation and the nonpartisan, \uc2 Tax Policy Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` claim about the national debt going up, she was right about that, although it increased by $4.6 trillion, not $5 trillion she rounded up by a couple hundred billion on this, according to the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s take a look at the next one.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: All the while, he intends to enact what in effect is a national sales tax, call it a Trump tax, that would raise prices on middle class families by almost $4,000.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So here`s what`s happening here. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supports tariffs on foreign goods, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says that those tariffs would end up costing American families. She calls them a tax.\par \par \uc2 So is it a tax? Well, that`s a partially true claim, according to our CBS News confirm team, and here`s the context they provide.\par \par \uc2 Economists tell us that consumers would feel this tariff because the cost would be passed on and the prices would definitely spike for normal household goods, but Harris` numbers are off. That 10 percent tariff combined with a 60 percent tariff on Chinese imports will cost about $1,800.00 per household in 2025. Significant, yes, but not $4,000.00.\par \par \uc2 All of this, according to the nonpartisan, \uc2 Tax Policy Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So Vlad, to play off a quote from James Madison, "If men were angels and always honest, no factcheckers would be necessary," but for now, I`m happy to say we`ve got our CBS News confirm team.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, they`ve been burning the midnight oil all this week, Tony, and it`s incredibly important, because people will hear these speeches from these politicians, and then they`ll go around and they`ll say to their friends and their family, hey, here`s what they said and here is what I believe. Factchecking is incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 Tony, my brother, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, call them historical dumpster divers. Yes, we meet a duo who collect all sorts of memorabilia at political conventions before it can be tossed in the trash. I`ve done that too, but then I find I have a room full of just junk, but first, it is 8:05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Monday on CBS MORNINGS, it is "Beg-Knows America" with stories that were sent to us by viewers like you.\par \par \uc2 A story of a man paralyzed after a necessary medical operation, who was told he may never walk again, but wait until you see him run.\par \par \uc2 Then a grandfather becomes the oldest person ever to graduate from his Texas university.\par \par \uc2 And a former foster child is beloved for being a foster parent to more than 70 animals.\par \par \uc2 See it all Monday on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Political conventions like the one that just wrapped here in Chicago last only a few days, but there are historians in this country making sure that all the memorabilia lives forever.\par \par \uc2 At both the DNC and the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , we`ve seen a sea of hats and pins and signs, some of which, believe it or not, are now headed to the \uc2 Smithsonian Institution\uc2 , and our senior White House and political correspondent, Ed O`Keefe, an institution himself, explains which items are picked and which are not.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was just in there a little while ago. It`s got that feeling of like a bar when the lights go up and the floor is sticky.\par \par \uc2 And if you`ve ever wondered how they preserve these demonstrations of political party pride, well, we tagged along with a team who`s been looking for some treasures to keep.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Long after the balloons drop, the confetti flies and the signs wave, political conventions live on, not just in memorable one liners --\par \par \uc2 JOHN F. KENNEDY\uc2  (Former president of the United States): And we stand today on the edge of a new frontier.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HW BUSH (Former president of the United States): Read my lips no new taxes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But also at the Smithsonian`s \uc2 National Museum of American History\uc2 . There, visitors can see everything from pins and peanuts to boxes of mac and cheese and ketchup bottles, all of it produced and used at one time to win votes.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE JERRY (\uc2 Smithsonian Institution\uc2  political history curator): It really demonstrates a very active way in which individuals participate in the democratic process.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Jon Grinspan and Claire Jerry are political history curators. You could call them historical dumpster divers in hot pursuit of political memorabilia.\par \par \uc2 JON GRINSPAN (\uc2 Smithsonian Institution\uc2  political history curator): We`ve started since 1988 sending a crew of people, to primaries, to caucuses, to conventions, to protests, to actively collect before it`s garbage.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We first met Grinspan and Jerry at last month`s Republican National Convention in Milwaukee. They came as guests of the GOP to track down items to help tell the story of 2024.\par \par \uc2 Like these signs some delegates made when they quickly wrote JD Vance`s name alongside Trump, once the senator was announced as the running mate.\par \par \uc2 One planned to donate their fast work.\par \par \uc2 I mean, how neat is that that you guys were doing something spontaneous here and now they want to document it for history?\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s actually what I preach about it -- the most about it, the spontaneous nature.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They did it again this week in Chicago, with the Democrats scouring for shirts, signs, and other swag.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 As we walked the floor, Grinspan spotted a hat he`d like to add to the collection.\par \par \uc2 JON GRINSPAN: We love those things that have history from the past, the present, and the future together, those make the best objects.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Back in Washington, hundreds of thousands of these items are cataloged and stored at the museum.\par \par \uc2 JON GRINSPAN: And we have things like that going back to 1844 from the wig convention in Baltimore, you know.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There`s an at-home convention kit Democrats were sent four years ago so they could participate virtually during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE JERRY: You`ve got your credentials and you`ve got a little bottle of confetti. I guess if you wanted to throw it in your home while you were watching the convention online.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Old press passes, plenty of posters and a homemade pineapple hat worn in 1996 by fans of \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . Get it?\par \par \uc2 JON GRINSPAN: But the hats are really useful because they`re not manufactured by the parties. They really are an insight into the workings of one individual.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Jerry explains why they keep what so often would otherwise get tossed away.\par \par \uc2 Why is it so important to preserve it?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE JERRY: I think the objects are really important because somebody used them. It`s not just something they watched on television, but it`s something they wore or they carried and it meant something to them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So the Smithsonian puts a premium on the handmade stuff, because they feel like that`s the best demonstration. But then they look for the more distinct printed ones.\par \par \uc2 For example, remember when we were in Milwaukee, and you saw those mass "deportation now" signs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They grabbed one of those, because they said that speaks to the moment. This might, "Thank you, Joe" you know, explains that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was dropping out of the race.\par \par \uc2 "Doug," because if his wife wins, he`d be the first, First Gentleman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That might be a good example. And then Patty our -- she found these rejects in a reject pile, right? It was like a complete reject pile. Something about the coloring that the producers probably didn`t like.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, so they`re pro-freedom, but they don`t like the color for those particular signs.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They didn`t like the freedom -- exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was a great piece. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from BBC)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That is Crowded House performing, "Don`t Dream It`s Over" at the Glastonbury Festival two years ago.\par \par \uc2 In our "Mornings Mixtape" series, we are revisiting the timeless 1986 mega hit and the band`s ongoing success. Crowded House, you`ll be happy to know is back on tour in the US, starting next week in support of their new album, "Gravity Stairs."\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason sat down with band members Neil Finn and Nick Seymour and Mitchell Froom, the producer who first noticed their potential.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Don`t Dream It`s Over"/Crowded House/\uc2 Capitol Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN (Crowded House): It`s usually good sign when songs come quickly and that one did come quite quickly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. When you`re writing, do you get a moment where you go, ah --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Don`t Dream It`s Over"/Crowded House/\uc2 Capitol Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Something happened here?\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: Yes, yes and sometimes it`s nothing is happening here.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Don`t Dream It`s Over"/Crowded House/\uc2 Capitol Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It happened for Neil Finn in a melancholy moment at the piano.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Don`t Dream It`s Over"/Crowded House/\uc2 Capitol Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: One of the first things I found was phrasing "all my possessions are causing me suspicion, but there`s no proof" like it just kept me interested in moving forward in the song.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Don`t Dream It`s Over"/Crowded House/\uc2 Capitol Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: When you`re really locked in and you`re getting dreamy with music, if you`re keeping your ears open, surprising, wonderful things keep happening.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was 1985 and Finn was forming a new band called Crowded House, with drummer, Paul Hester and bassist, Nick Seymour.\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR (Crowded House): I remember you playing it to me and describing how you got the snare sound by hitting a box of matches.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Finn sent a cassette out to producers.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL FROOM (Music producer): People just passed on it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But not Mitchell Froom.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL FROOM: I think "Don`t Dream It`s Over" was number six or seven on that tape.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: You`re probably the only producer that listened through to track six.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL FROOM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Froom would help the band shape their sound.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL FROOM: I think you had a guitar and I had a keyboard, maybe a bass keyboard, and a little drum machine, and we just tried to play it like songs.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: He recorded it on --\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR: Oh his little blaster box.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: Blaster box.\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR: A little cassette blaster.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: The condenser mic sounded good.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL FROOM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Over Christmas in 1985, Crowded House went into Capitol Studios in LA.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: We did, "Don`t Dream It`s Over" in the morning.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Froom says, one take stood out.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL FROOM: You don`t go and try to analyze why. You just kiss the ground. You just say, okay, yes, we`ve got one. That`s great.\par \par \uc2 It`s mysterious, yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Rock Arena/Australian Broadcasting Corporation)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The song went Top 10 around.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weather With You"/Crowded House/Capitol Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Crowded House went on to have a run of international hits in the 80s and 90s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fall at Your Feet"/Crowded House/\uc2 Capitol Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: but "Don`t Dream" became their signature song.\par \par \uc2 Today, it has 600 million streams on \uc2 Spotify\uc2  alone.\par \par \uc2 Do you have a favorite cover of --\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: I`d get into trouble if I start talking about favorites, but I actually did like Miley Cyrus and Ariana Grande`s version that they did in bunny suits.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Happy Hippie Foundation)\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: I thought they sang it really well.\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR: It was an Italian cover, like an Italian crooner guy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Alta Marea/Heinz Music)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Antonello Venditti recorded it as "Alta Marea" in 1991.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: It`s a huge hit in Italy, and it`s completely different words. I let them do it. I wouldn`t now, but I let them do it at the time because I was so blown away by the idea of having an Italian version of a song of mine.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Alta Marea/Heinz Music)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The video starred a 16-year-old actress named Angelina Jolie.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, when Finn toured as a member of Fleetwood Mac, they performed the song.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fleetwood Mac performance)\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR: And suddenly all these phones came up out of the audience.\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR: I was very proud. I was very proud.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: I gave you a shout out before.\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR: I know you did a great job, too.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: So did --\par \par \uc2 NICK SEYMOUR: I got a bit teary. It was really lovely.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Crowded House: Farewell to the World/Network Ten)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Perhaps the band`s most memorable performance of "Don`t Dream" came on the steps of the Sydney Opera House in 1996 in front of 150,000 fans.\par \par \uc2 What do you think people saw in that song? Still see in that song?\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: It`s been amazing to see how many occasions that song seems to have found resonance for people and it is not deliberate, and it`s one of those beautiful mysteries and accidents of magic that music can produce every now and again, where you write something and it just -- it makes you feel some way, and it is always that like that for me.\par \par \uc2 I`ll think of a line, immediately have a feeling attached to it. I actually don`t really know what I`m singing about yet.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What is it like for you as a band to perform a song like that, knowing the response you get every time.\par \par \uc2 NEIL FINN: I don`t tire of it, I got to say. I am so grateful that I liked the song that became the flag bearer.\par \par \uc2 It might not have worked out like that, so I`m really happy still singing it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Confirmed. Songwriters kiss the ground. Crowded House kicks off its US tour in Saint Augustine, Florida, next Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Mitchell Froom, who produced their early records, now plays keyboards. Neil Finn`s sons, Liam and Elroy, they`ve also joined the band.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes. A memorable one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bittersweet -- junior prom, dateless, back against the wall, making out.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Back against the wall.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not me. Me, wallflower.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Hey now. Hey now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wallflower I mean.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I want to go to these two women. They are taking their popular podcast, "Giggly Squad" on the road. They`re up next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Giggly.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 To quote our next guest, "Hello, gorgeous gigglers."\par \par \uc2 "Giggly Squad" co-hosts Hannah Berner and Paige DeSorbo are here. They met on a Bravo reality show and started recording \uc2 Instagram\uc2  Live videos during the pandemic. Those videos folks turned into the "Giggly Squad." It is one of the most downloaded podcasts on \uc2 Apple\uc2 , and in this clip, they discuss meeting a fan on the street. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER (Giggly Squad co-host): I`m sorry like ran up to you, but like you just look so normal.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO (Giggly Squad co-host): Honestly, in the world we live in, what a compliment, like, what? A, just, let`s normalize just being like, hey, you just -- you look average, you look normal and average.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: And I know she meant to just like you just seemed like a normal person.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: And I was like, no, that`s me, normal for sure. And she was so sweet. We took a photo, and I walked off, and Dez came in. He`s like, what`s up? And I was like, I think someone just called me ugly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh. The "Giggly Squad" is bringing its podcast to the people with a live tour across 45 cities in the United States and in Canada. Hannah and Paige join us here on CBS MORNINGS, first on CBS MORNINGS, we love what we can say that. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: Thank you so much for having us.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: A debut.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You are far from normal. You are not normal.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: You`re saying that I am weird.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, no. See, like that`s what I love about the podcast. How you guys see the world and your hot takes on things, but also you`re very well-informed takes on the issue.\par \par \uc2 This all started as an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  Live during the pandemic. Did you ever think it would get to this level where you`re like, going on tour like rock stars?\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: No, this started as a bit.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: We were depressed. We were depressed during COVID. It was kind of a joke. People were like, you`re going to run out things to talk about because there`s nothing going on. And --\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: We didn`t.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: We didn`t.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: But we never thought it would become a podcast. It was really to pass the time when we were in quarantine, and then we missed doing it, and it really was like therapeutic for us.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Giggly Squad)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve called it, Paige, a one-hour escape. How do you create that space for your listeners?\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: I think it`s just so -- our relationship is just so organic that it just ends up naturally coming out and it feels like a safe space for us, and I think we just give that to the gigglers, and then they feel like it`s a safe space.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: I think it`s important to have natural chemistry, because --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which you guys have.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER -- when people want to listen, they want to listen to two people actually enjoy each other. Because if you`re listening to podcast hosts who like, actually feel weird with each other, you`re like, this is just like me going to work every day. I don`t want to hear people be awkward with each other all day.\par \par \uc2 I just want two people laughing, so we --\par \par \uc2 And also, it`s our favorite part of the week and I think the gigglers feel that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Hannah, you`re very, very open. Both of you are, but you in particular, Hannah, as a comedian, you`re open about your marriage, mental health, body. I mean, in just that clip that we play there, even your sex life.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything -- would you guys say there`s anything that is off limits? Is there anything off limits?\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: Well --\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: I was going to say so far, we haven`t found something that`s off limits.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: We`re trying.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: We`re trying every day.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: We`re pushing the boundaries every day, but I do think part of "Giggly Squad" is the therapeutic-ness of us being able to be like unfiltered, unedited, and just show our conversations without feeling like we have to be anything other than ourselves.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so if our viewers are not familiar with the gigglers, with the giggler squad, yet, we want to get them to know how the giggly squad works by playing a game that we are calling giggly or not so giggly.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH BERNER: We love a prop.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I have this. I`ve got a bunch of topics, and we`re going to go with, we`re going to say whether they`re giggly or not giggly. And I`m going to play along, too. All right, you guys, ready?\par \par \uc2 So here are the topics. Here are the themes, flip flops.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE DESORBO: Not giggly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not.\par \par \uc2 HANNAN BERNER: But I think it`s giggly because it`s hilarious, like having your do