You said in a bun.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh, I am thinking of food.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was thinking of a hamburger bun.\par \par \uc2 JOEL FAGLIANO: In a bun.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240823ek8n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for July 9, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mark Strassmann, Elise Preston, Chris Livesay,  Nicole Sganga, Lonnie Quinn, Omar Villafranca, Scott MacFarlane, Ed  O`Keefe, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 4,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: Democrats divided over President Biden`s future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Are you all on the same page?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 STEVE COHEN\uc2  (D-Tennessee): No.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What do you mean you`re not on the same page?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 STEVE COHEN\uc2 : Not even in the same book.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: As the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit with world leaders begins here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Welcome to the 2024 NATO Summit.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The critical week ahead for the president to calm concerns within his party.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PETE AGUILAR (D-California): Let`s see the press conference. Let`s see the campaign stops. Let`s see all of this, because all of it is going to be necessary.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 President Biden just spoke at the NATO Summit here in Washington, D.C., a speech that has become as much about his fitness for office as it is about his foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 The president spoke about Ukraine policy and maintaining a united front to prevent another world war. But the world`s focus wasn`t only on what he said, but how he said it, as he tries to fight back calls within his own party to step aside.\par \par \uc2 And just hours ago, a new Democratic defection, a New Jersey congresswoman and former \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  pilot saying: "Because I know President Biden cares deeply about the future of our country, I am asking that he declare that he won`t run for reelection."\par \par \uc2 But a lot of her colleagues are either sticking behind Biden or staying quiet, at least in public.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is on the Hill talking to lawmakers, and Ed O`Keefe is at the White House and kicks off our team coverage.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Tonight, President Biden welcoming members of \uc2 NATO\uc2  to Washington. Questions swirl about his political future.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s a pleasure to host you in this milestone year.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The annual summit is set to have a big focus on Ukraine. That country`s fate could hang in the balance if Democrats lose the White House.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : But make no mistake. Ukraine can and will stop Putin.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And now even European leaders are facing questions about the American president`s future.\par \par \uc2 (CHANCELLOR OLAF SCHOLZ speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The German chancellor, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , today saying he`s not worried about Biden`s ability to handle the summit.\par \par \uc2 The White House remained on the defensive, trying to move on from the president`s debate performance --\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): We do want to turn the page.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: -- but facing more questions again today about the president`s health.\par \par \uc2 PRESS SECRETARY KARINE JEAN-PIERRE: The White House Medical Unit, his -- his doctor, they don`t believe that he needs anything more than what we have been able to provide.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Overnight, the White House released a letter from the president`s physician, saying the commander in chief hasn`t seen a neurologist outside of his three annual physicals and that a neurology specialist who visited the White House at least eight times in the past year was seeing other patients.\par \par \uc2 Campaigning in Las Vegas this afternoon, Vice President Harris turned her attention to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): So, make no mistake. If Trump gets the chance, he will sign a national abortion ban to outlaw abortion in every single state. But we are not going to let that happen.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Trump said Monday night he still expects to face the president this fall.\par \par \uc2 (Audio clip from Hannity)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): You know, it looks to me like he may very well stay in. He`s got an ego, and he doesn`t want to quit.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He will hold a rally tonight in a Miami suburb alongside Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , a potential running mate.\par \par \uc2 And, in Washington, House Speaker Mike Johnson says President Biden needs to go sooner; the Cabinet should use the 25th Amendment to remove him.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): What we`re projecting from the White House is not strength right now at a very dangerous time around the world. And that`s why the American people are so alarmed by this.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But CBS News has contacted all members of the Biden Cabinet to ask whether they have ever considered or heard any discussion about invoking the 25th Amendment. The answer among those who`ve responded so far is universal: No, they haven`t -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe at the White House with new reporting tonight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 And nearly two miles down Constitution Avenue at the U.S. Capitol, Democrats held a series of high-stakes closed-door meetings. On the House side, they were even asked to check their cell phones at the door.\par \par \uc2 And, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports, top Democrats are torn on whether President Biden should continue his campaign.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): On a sweltering day in Washington --\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES CLYBURN (D-South Carolina): We are riding with Biden.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Was there conversation about Vice President Kamala --\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES CLYBURN: We are riding with Biden.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Was there Vice -- was there conversation about Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in there?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES CLYBURN: We are riding with Biden.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The political heat is hovering over the president`s top allies.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): As I have said before, I`m with Joe.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: His surrogates urge colleagues to line back up behind President Biden.\par \par \uc2 You don`t sense concern among your colleagues?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (D-Delaware): I said, many of my colleagues have concern. But what the president has done is listen to those concerns, acted on them.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But some of the most powerful Democrats in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  said the president has more work to do to convince skeptical voters and lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 How much time is left before a final decision has to be made, in your estimation?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD DURBIN\uc2  (D-Illinois): I can`t say. I mean, it isn`t like there is a definite deadline here.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In the U.S. House, another Democrat defected. New Jersey Congresswoman \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  said the president should withdraw.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MIKIE SHERRILL\uc2  (D-New Jersey): And just advocating as strongly as possible that we have someone that can really take that case strongly and energetically to the voters.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Though the president has secured vocal support from the Congressional Black Caucus, Democrats exited a party meeting today without a unified plan.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Are you all on the same page?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 STEVE COHEN\uc2  (D-Tennessee): No.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What do you mean you`re not on the same page?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 STEVE COHEN\uc2 : Not even in the same book.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: There are still dozens of congressional Democrats who haven`t or won`t declare their position on the president`s reelection.\par \par \uc2 Norah, we will find out in the coming days if that hurts or helps the president`s prospects.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Asking the tough questions.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane, on the Hill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to weather and the aftermath of Hurricane Beryl. Tonight, people in Texas are picking up the pieces, with hundreds of thousands still without power, even as the brutal heat moves in.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): One day after Hurricane Beryl dumped flooding rains, Southeast Texas is now sweltering.\par \par \uc2 In the Houston area, nearly 12,000 workers are rushing to restore power to 1.6 million customers, as temperatures soared today into the 90s.\par \par \uc2 ACTING GOVERNOR DAN PATRICK (R-Texas): We are urging them to work as quickly as they can to restore the power in the safest, but in the quickest way they can.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Harris County has opened cooling centers to help, giving residents a space to stay cool and charge their devices.\par \par \uc2 In Channelview, just east of Houston, Yvette Carrasco and her family have been without power since Monday morning, staying outside because it was too hot inside. While we were talking, this happened.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE CARRASCO (Homeowner Without Power): I`m about to --\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Are you OK?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE CARRASCO: -- about to not be OK.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: You`re -- you`re not?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE CARRASCO: No. Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Are you OK? OK, let`s -- should --\par \par \uc2 YVETTE CARRASCO: Can I get a chair from back there? Hurry up.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Can we get a chair?\par \par \uc2 After we found some shade and a chair, we continued.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever wonder that, if you will survive the hurricane, but you won`t survive the aftermath?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE CARRASCO: I don`t know. I don`t -- I mean, we have so far, but it gets tiring. You know, it gets tiring of doing this over and over and...\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: One thing frustrating residents is that they can`t check \uc2 CenterPoint Energy\uc2 `s outage map to see if their power has been restored because it was knocked offline by another storm in May.\par \par \uc2 Houston`s mayor says that he plans to hold CenterPoint accountable if they cannot restore power to one million customers by tomorrow, as promised -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Really important reporting.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s bring in \uc2 CBS\uc2  New York`s Lonnie Quinn for a look at the flood and storm threats and the extreme heat on both coasts.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: OK, good evening. Nice seeing you as well, Norah.\par \par \uc2 And hello, everybody.\par \par \uc2 I want to jump right into the current status of Beryl. It`s no longer a -- a tropical storm. It`s a rainmaker, but you have got winds associated with this, now, not sustained winds, but twists in the atmosphere. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 Indiana to Illinois, that`s where we find it as of right now. And tonight, Indiana all the way to Michigan, you could pick up four to six inches of rain. We have already had two confirmed tornadoes touching down in Kentucky and Indiana. And the tornado watch is in effect for you folks until 11:00 p.m. Central time.\par \par \uc2 The amount of rain we`re talking about, all right, as it pushes through the areas, like I said, anywhere from, say, Indiana into Michigan, you`re talking about that four-to-six-inch range. But take a look once it gets into Upstate New York and into Northern New England.\par \par \uc2 With the orographic lift, as that air mass drives up and over the mountains, you`re pulling out up to nine inches of rain in about 12 hours. But the story thus far we have been talking about day after day after day has been the heat. It`s centered -- it`s centralized on the West Coast and on the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 I want to start off with the East Coast. Look at this. Philly tomorrow hits 96, crazy humidity. It feels like 110. Columbia, South Carolina, you`re 99, feeling like 109. Houston, where you could use some air conditioning, well, it`s 94, feeling like 100. Hopefully, they can get -- they can get that situation fixed for you folks.\par \par \uc2 And out West, numbers are even bigger, but, as you know, the humidity not as much a question -- an issue for you out there. Boise, it`s 110, Las Vegas, 119. Any relief? Well, for the East Coast, possibly, a Friday, Saturday setup give you a little bit of a setback from all the big numbers -- Norah, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Lonnie Quinn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now look at tonight`s other top stories.\par \par \uc2 And we start with this breaking news. A member of Supreme Court Justice Sonia Sotomayor`s protection detail shot a carjacking suspect near the justice`s home.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nicole Sganga has the details just coming in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: We`re just learning tonight, Norah, two deputy \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  opened fire on a suspected carjacker near the home of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA (voiceover): Eighteen-year-old Kentrell Flowers sustained a non-life-threatening injury to the jaw after allegedly tapping this gun against the car window of a deputy U.S. Marshal standing post near Sotomayor`s home.\par \par \uc2 Flowers is now charged with armed carjacking and carrying a pistol without a license. D.C. police are still investigating, but say there`s no indication the suspect targeted the Supreme Court justice or knew who he was attempting to carjack.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to New Mexico, where Alec Baldwin`s involuntary manslaughter trial is now under way. And opening statements could begin as early as tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston was in the courtroom for jury selection.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The fate of Alec Baldwin will be decided by 12 jurors and four alternates here in Santa Fe, Norah.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling this morning?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): On the first official day of his trial, Baldwin arrived at the courthouse, while, inside, attorneys questioned a pool of 70 potential jurors about their ability to be impartial.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from News Nation)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Just three of them said they never heard or saw anything about this widely publicized case. Jurors were also asked their thoughts on workplace safety, gun ownership and their personal connections to the film industry. If convicted, Baldwin could face 18 months in prison.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And overseas now to Barcelona. With summer travel season under way, tourists are filling the city and protesters are spraying them with water. Why?\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Chris Livesay explains.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Norah, the Trevi Fountain is on a lot of bucket lists, maybe too many. An estimated 1,000 tourists visit this Rome landmark every hour.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps no city is more disgruntled than Barcelona. Take a look at protesters in the Spanish city squirting tourists with water guns. Over the weekend, thousands of marchers blamed them for raising the cost of living and lowering the quality of life. It`s bad in Venice too, where the city has introduced an entrance fee, a little more than $5 a day for day trippers.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: When a Wyoming highway collapsed outside Jackson Hole last month, it threw a spotlight on something important. Two very different Americas often live side by side. In the U.S., the top 1 percent now owns nearly as much wealth as the bottom 90 percent.\par \par \uc2 As CBS` Mark Strassmann shows us in tonight`s Eye on America, Wyoming is home to one of the wealthiest -- the country`s wealthiest communities and also one of the most unequal.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): In Jackson Hole, Wyoming`s Shangri-La in the Tetons, you need silly money to be taken seriously.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SMAELLIE (Construction Supervisor): A family like mine can`t live here, not even -- not even an inkling of a chance.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: John Smaellie is a construction supervisor in Jackson. Like so many workers here, he can only afford to live on the other side of these mountains, priced out of Jackson Hole`s new Gilded Age, vacationing 1 percenters competing for a second home or a fourth.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SMAELLIE: The billionaires are buying out the millionaires.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It sounds crazy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SMAELLIE: It does. You`re making a great wage anywhere else in the country, but, here, you`re poverty-stricken.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For most people here, real estate prices are wildly unaffordable. This county`s median income is $108,000 a year, but the average listing price of a single-family home is more than $7 million.\par \par \uc2 Driving home the inequality, Smaellie`s commute over this mountain pass from his house in Driggs, Idaho. It`s an hour drive or more to downtown Jackson with its Old West charm and sticker shock. That`s where we met Jessica Sell Chambers.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SELL CHAMBERS (Jackson, Wyoming, Mayoral Candidate): A lot of these stores, your average working person, this is not for us.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: She`s a Jackson Town councilmember and mayoral candidate. Sell Chambers told us this downtown project is typical, luxury short-term rentals.\par \par \uc2 What would you like it to be?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SELL CHAMBERS: I would love it to be a multifamily affordable housing.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But that would have been costly and was never an option.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SELL CHAMBERS: This is beyond gentrification. This is super- gentrification.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You can make $200,000, $300,000 a year...\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SELL CHAMBERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... and not afford to live here?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SELL CHAMBERS: Correct. It`s unsustainable too. Who is going to run the place? Who`s going to work and be the backbone of all the services?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Even for people with all the money in the world, there`s a moment of reckoning coming?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SELL CHAMBERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: That moment flashed briefly last month. The mountain pass collapsed, 80 feet of it gone, a landslide impassable for three weeks, further separating the two worlds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You`re on the TV.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Feeling it in Driggs, Idaho, Pete and Sarah Wilson and their daughter, Harper. Sarah`s a media creative director. Pete`s a firefighter paramedic in Jackson, their hometown.\par \par \uc2 You feel excluded from the place you grew up in?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILSON (Firefighter Paramedic): A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 SARAH WILSON (Media Creative Director): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILSON: I`m the help, even though, like, I`m a firefighter paramedic. Once we`re done doing the job, we`re expected to kick rocks and get lost.\par \par \uc2 SARAH WILSON: If you`re looking at the American dream or whatever, like, if you work hard enough you can have what you want. But you can`t.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It`s not just gone for now. It`s gone forever.\par \par \uc2 SARAH WILSON: It`s gone forever.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Sell Chambers` Jackson home has exploded in value, but the property taxes may drive her family out.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SELL CHAMBERS: We don`t want to go anywhere. It`s -- the money is not important to us. It`s the community.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The mountain pass has reopened. The divide stays in place.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Mark Strassmann in Jackson, Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s a good piece.\par \par \uc2 And our new investigation is next with a look at how Texans` taxpayer dollars are funding groups that aim to stop women from seeking an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We turn now to a CBS News and \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  investigation into crisis pregnancy centers, which aim to stop women from seeking an abortion. Lawmakers say they are critical to supporting women after birth.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns reports, there`s little accountability for how that money is spent in a state that leads the nation in funding them, Texas, also a state where abortion is almost entirely banned.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Every day, nurse Megan Wheeler (sp?) drives across Texas` Brazos Valley caring for new young mothers.\par \par \uc2 AVA BRADLEY (Mother): Hey, how are you?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN WHEELER (Nurse): Good morning. How are you?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Like 16-year-old Ava Bradley (sp?) and her 1-year-old son, Mateo (sp?).\par \par \uc2 AVA BRADLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: They connected through the Nurse-Family Partnership, a free program that pairs nurses with low-income first-time moms.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN WHEELER: Any fever for either of you, or just...\par \par \uc2 AVA BRADLEY: No.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MONTEZ (Nurse-Family Partnership): Nurses have been able to intervene and be able save our women and children from dying.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But the program is struggling to meet demand.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MONTEZ: We`re able to serve maybe a quarter of -- of the referrals.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So, last year, they asked the Texas legislature for an extra $19 million. They got $2.2 million. Meanwhile, the state program called Alternatives to Abortion, which includes funds for crisis pregnancy centers, got enough extra funding last year that it had $14 million left over for future use.\par \par \uc2 For years, Democratic State Representative Donna Howard has questioned how Alternatives to Abortion operates.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE DONNA HOWARD (D-Texas): I don`t know of any other program that the state administers that is treated in the same way, not one.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A CBS News and \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  investigation uncovered a payment model that allows the program`s largest contractor, Texas Pregnancy Care Network, to charge the state $14 for handing out a pamphlet as part of its services.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE JEFF LEACH (R-Texas): If taxpayer dollars aren`t being spent appropriately, then we will hold those responsible accountable.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Republican State Representative Jeff Leach is one of the program`s most ardent supporters.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE JEFF LEACH: This program is really meant to provide the full weight and resources of the state government not just before pregnancy, but after pregnancy as well.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But those resources are not reaching some struggling families, like at this Austin Diaper Bank line --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: -- where a stream of cars lines up for free supplies every week. Diaper banks sought state funding last year, but got nothing.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY MCDANIEL (Executive Director, Austin Diaper Bank): There are families out there, I would love to give them diapers, but diapers don`t go on trees.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A spokesperson for the state said it takes stewardship of taxpayer dollars very seriously, and Texas Pregnancy Care Network told us its services are responsive to clients` needs.\par \par \uc2 Also of note, last September, Alternatives to Abortion got a new name, Thriving Texas Families -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We need to support these families.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Two passenger jets appear to come close to colliding in midair. We will explain what happened next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating a frightening sight in the sky over Syracuse, New York, where it appears two passenger jets flew too close to each other.\par \par \uc2 Police dashcam video shows what looks like two planes nearly crossing paths, one taking off, the other landing. According to Flightradar, one of the planes flew within about 700 feet above the other. Both made it to their destinations safely.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet a Miami student who knows how to keep a promise. From P -- pre-K through 12 grade, Keith Holmes Jr. never missed a day of school, thanks to a pact that he made with his late father.\par \par \uc2 After Keith`s father died in 2018, he stuck to his promise and also completed nearly 5,000 hours of community service. Keith says working with children with disabilities puts everything in perspective.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEITH HOLMES JR. (Student): I had a village of people behind me. Put your mind to it, do it, keep your faith and have determination and perseverance and never give up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Keith Holmes Jr., he`s tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240710ek7900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For June 30, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jan Crawford, Major Garrett, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,087 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Democrats defend President Biden after a disastrous debate. And a new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll reveals whether it changed the way voters see the candidates now.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s team sought out Thursday`s debate to draw contrasts with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and hoped it would soothe concerns about the incumbents` age and stamina. Instead, after a meandering, frequently hard-to-follow performance, not even Mr. Biden`s closest supporters could ignore the obvious.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): It was a slow start. That`s obvious to everyone. I`m not going to debate that point.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES CLYBURN (D-South Carolina): That was strike one. If this were a ball game, he`s got two most swings.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president went on damage control...\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): When you get knocked down, you get back up.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... and spent the weekend at fund-raisers, as critics outside some of those events and the nation`s leading newspapers called for him to step aside.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden said he`s staying in the race, but some voters are voicing doubt.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I`m not sure he`s as sharp as he should be.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: After what happened last night, Democrats should wake up today with a plan B.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will have results of a new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll.\par \par \uc2 Maryland Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 , who is campaigning for President Biden, will join us.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, despite repeatedly making falsehoods on stage, former President Trump claimed victory.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We had a big victory against a man that really is looking to destroy our country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But his legal problems could cut that short, as the Supreme Court prepares to rule tomorrow on whether former presidents are immune from criminal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 We will hear from possible Trump running mate Ohio Senator J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will check in with Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2 , the top Democrat on the Intel Committee, and get the latest from the head of \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 , \uc2 Catherine Russell\uc2 , on the humanitarian crises putting millions of children at risk around the world.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with reaction to the first presidential debate of 2024. Our polling unit asked voters how they view the candidates now, including their cognitive health. The results are not encouraging for the president.\par \par \uc2 The number of voters who say President Biden has the cognitive ability to serve has dropped from 35 percent earlier this month to 27 percent after the debate. That`s the lowest number since \uc2 CBS\uc2  began asking voters that question last September.\par \par \uc2 Nearly three-quarters of all registered voters now say he shouldn`t be running, and nearly half of Democrats say he shouldn`t remain the nominee, an extraordinary number for an incumbent who didn`t face a competitive primary. We should also note that half of all voters also say former President Trump does not have the cognitive health to serve.\par \par \uc2 Voters give former President Trump a significant edge, though, when asked which candidate explained his ideas clearly, inspired confidence, and appeared presidential.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss all of this is Maryland Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 . He`s in Milwaukee, where he has been campaigning for President Biden`s reelection this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE (D-Maryland): Thank you so much, great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, you know it wasn`t a good night for the president. And -- and I will save us time, because I know you`re going to tell us, look at presidential performance, not debate performance, everyone has a bad night, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  once had a bad night.\par \par \uc2 But you know, at 81 years old, this is a different set of factors for President Biden. Why was he struggling Thursday?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Well, I -- I think both candidates struggled. I don`t think either candidate had a -- had a very good night on the -- on the debate night.\par \par \uc2 But I also do know that it does matter when we`re looking at presidential performance, and not necessarily debate performance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, respectfully, Governor, the president doesn`t do many interviews. He doesn`t do things where we he -- we have to see him be quick on his feet. He`s not here. You are.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Well, I...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He`s not on the campaign trail as often...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... as surrogates like you are. Isn`t that demonstrating that there are alternatives, Democratic alternatives, who are younger and able to go out and be quick on their feet and on the trail?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: I -- I know I got a chance to see the president when times were difficult and see him in trials.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: And I have seen him been able to be a remarkable partner to us. And that`s what I remember. And that`s why I continue to believe that -- that the Biden administration deserves another four years. And I`m excited about what that can deliver for the American people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why was the president struggling?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: I think the president had a tough night.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: I think all -- all of us have difficult nights.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think that the president had a -- had a difficult night, just like every single one of us do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, I know that Democrats believe that abortion is a winning issue in this election. Here`s what the American people heard from the president when he was asked about abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Look, there`s so many young women who have been -- including a young woman who just was murdered and he -- he went to the funeral.\par \par \uc2 The idea that she was murdered by a -- by -- by an immigrant coming in, and they talk about that. But here`s the deal. There`s a lot of young women who are being raped by their -- by their in-laws, by their -- by their spouses, brothers and sisters, by -- just -- it`s just -- it`s just ridiculous. And they can do nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Abortion is an important issue for Democrats. This should have been a layup. He was struggling to make the point. How do you explain that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Margaret, that -- that -- that wasn`t a great moment for the president. And no one can argue that it can.\par \par \uc2 He was also standing feet away from someone who was talking about politicians who take babies away after they`re born and killing them. He was also talking -- he was also standing feet away from somebody who was debating whether or not the limit on -- on reproductive health should be six weeks or nine weeks or 12 weeks, or saying that any politician should have a say about what happens with a woman and her body, when that conversation should exclusively be between her and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. Donald -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was saying it`s for the states...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: And so it wasn`t a great moment for the president. And we can`t defend that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. The...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: No. Well -- well, I -- I think -- and you -- and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is also claiming responsibility for the turndown of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: So -- so, I mean, it wasn`t a great moment for the president.\par \par \uc2 But let`s be clear. His position on making sure the reproductive health and reproductive rights should be ironclad, his position that we should have Roe v. Wade as the law of the land is very, very clear...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: With \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I want to be also clear that that is not his position.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I -- I understand you want the conversation to be about the policy and the false statements made by his opponent.\par \par \uc2 But "The Atlanta Journal-Constitution" in Georgia, a key state for the president, came out in an editorial saying: "This wasn`t a bad night. It was confirmation of the worst fears of some of Biden`s most ardent supporter. Age has finally caught up to him."\par \par \uc2 It referred to excuses like what you`re making as insulting to the American people. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: I think that what we are watching, when you`re watching a presidential performance, when you`re watching a president who was able to lead us to now where we have record low unemployment, when we`re able to watch how the black -- black wealth inside of this country since the pandemic has risen by 60 percent, when you`re able to watch a presidential performance that`s been able to do things like raise wages for our workers, and making sure that our rights are protected, that that does matter in this conversation.\par \par \uc2 And so I understand people who are, you know -- and I understand...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: ... and I get it, the arguments, about age and the complications of 81.\par \par \uc2 But the number 81 is an important number, but so is watching historically low unemployment rates. And I do not think that people should lose sight of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure, but we`re not just talking about three-and-a-half years in the past. We`re talking about four more years, bringing him to age 86.\par \par \uc2 Here is what the president responded when he was asked about the national debt, and how he would tax billionaires.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We`d be able to right wipe out his debt. We`d be able to help make sure that all those things we need to do, childcare, eldercare, making sure that we continue to strengthen our health care system, making sure that we`re able to make every single solitary person eligible for what I have been able to do with the -- with the COVID -- excuse me -- with dealing with everything we have to do with -- look, if -- we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president lost his own point.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: The -- we also have to remember that this election is a binary choice.\par \par \uc2 It`s an election between President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and, frankly, even the conversation around the national debt, you know, someone whose tax cuts at a time when over the next decade, we are going to have the largest transfer of wealth in the history of the world...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You need young voters to turn out.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: ... and a president who -- and a -- and a former president...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democrats need young voters to turn out.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How are they supposed to see themselves...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... reflected in that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: I think they`re also going to see themselves reflected in all the people who are going out and being the president`s surrogates.\par \par \uc2 I think you have to go out and earn it. You know, I`m -- I`m -- I`m the youngest Democratic governor in this country, the only African-American governor in America. And we`re out here all the time, and we`re out here in conjunction with other surrogates. We`re out here in conjunction with the president, who`s out here campaigning, and the vice president.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to the people and we`re making our case to the people as to why this kind of partnership matters. Progress does not happen by accident. The work that we are seeing -- I look at the state of Maryland. We now went from being 43rd in unemployment when I was first inaugurated to now having amongst the lowest unemployment rates in the entire country for 12 straight months.\par \par \uc2 We went from having some of the highest homicide rates.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So would you pursue the Democratic nomination if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  were to -- to take himself out of this race?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: I -- I will not. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not going to take himself out of this race, nor should he. He has been a remarkable partner.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you confident that the only person, the only Democrat in America that could meet -- beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is our nominee. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is our leader.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has earned and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  deserves the confidence, the respect, and, frankly, the partnership that we now have to provide to him. And so I will be in Chicago. I will proudly be supporting the president in Chicago. I will work through November to make sure he gets reelected.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I do think that President Biden has earned the respect of Democrats and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be our leader going forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor Moore, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Ohio Republican Senator J.D. Vance. He joins us this morning from Cleveland.\par \par \uc2 And, Senator I should say, we`re having some technical issues, so you`re with us on Zoom. Hopefully, our uplink stays solid throughout, because I got a lot of questions for you, sir.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE (R-Ohio): Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a better night on Thursday during that debate by many measures.\par \par \uc2 But according to our poll, he fell short on at least one of them. Fewer voters thought the former president was truthful compared with President Biden. Mr. Trump falsely claimed states are passing legislation to execute babies. I think you know that killing people is illegal in every state.\par \par \uc2 He falsely claimed that the speaker of the House at the time turned down 10,000 soldiers that he had offered to keep the peace ahead of January 6, something his own acting secretary of defense testified to Congress did not happen.\par \par \uc2 If he has such a strong platform, why make false claims?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, Margaret, I think the media is running interference on a lot of this stuff. We all know and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  herself has admitted on camera that she could have requested more National Guard troops. She bears some responsibility for the fact that they weren`t there at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 We know that -- that multiple Democratic governors and states and even some Democratic senators and congressmen have tried to pass laws that would effectively legalize abortion up until the moment of birth. And, most importantly, we know that the media seems totally uninterested in fact- checking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from any of the number of false claims that he made, including he said...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, I lost track, sir, I have been told the media is on every single side of this and everything`s our fault.\par \par \uc2 But let`s get back to the candidate you`re here to talk about.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: But I`m...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Chris Miller said 10,000 troops, he was never ordered by the president to send those to the Capitol that day.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has said on camera, Margaret, that she bears some responsibility for the fact that the National Guard didn`t play a bigger role.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, we know the speaker of the House has an extraordinary amount of influence over the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 . It`s not in dispute, Margaret. And, more importantly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that no troops died on his watch, even though 13 American service members died...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... thanks to his botched withdrawal from Afghanistan. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made multiple statements of falsehood during the debate.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of folks in the media, yes, seemed totally uninterested in fact- checking him. And the reason, Margaret, is because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just performed so much better. There`s -- there was -- there was this 24-hour period where effectively everyone was honest that there was an incredible contrast between Donald Trump`s energy and command to the facts, and Joe Biden`s obvious inability to do the job as president.\par \par \uc2 And now, of course, we`ve transitioned to this new media cycle where folks are trying to run cover. Look, the American people saw what they saw. Trump can do the job. Biden can`t.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, you might not have heard it, but I did raise a lot of those issues to \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 , the Biden surrogate who was on before you.\par \par \uc2 As to where you stand on some of these issues, tomorrow, at the Supreme Court, it may be a significant day as we get -- expected to get that decision on presidential immunity from criminal prosecution. And that`s directly relevant to the federal charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as you know.\par \par \uc2 You`re a lawyer. I wonder. If you become the vice president and you`re in a Trump-Vance administration, do you believe a president could pardon himself for federal crimes?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, look, I`m focused on electing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president. Whether I`m serving in some other roles or serving as the United States senator, I think the Trump agenda has worked, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 And on this particular question...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But would you object if the president were to try to do that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Margaret, we know that the president has to have immunity to do his job.\par \par \uc2 Should Barack Obama be prosecuted for droning American citizens in Yemen? There are so many examples of presidents Democrats and Republicans who would not be able to discharge their duties if the Supreme Court does not recognize some broad element of presidential discretion.\par \par \uc2 I`m very confident that they`re going to be able to do that. And I`m very confident that the fundamental principle here is, the president`s got to be able to do his job. In the same way that police officers, judges, prosecutors, enjoy some immunity, that principle has to apply to the president too.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you do believe that a president could pardon himself for federal crimes?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: I believe that the president has broad pardon authority, Margaret, but, more importantly, I think the president has immunity. It`s not about whether he should pardon himself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: It`s about whether he should be prosecuted in the first place for discharging his official duties.\par \par \uc2 So, in that way, I sort of reject the premise of the question here. We need to have some recognition that the -- you know, look, a Democrat wins the presidency, they try to throw the Republican president in jail. A Republican wins the presidency...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... they try to throw the Democrat president in jail. That is the pathway to unraveling 250 years of American constitutional tradition and making the president totally unable, regardless of party, to do their job.\par \par \uc2 That is not a good thing, and it`s not something I think any Republican supports.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 To that point, President Trump on that debate stage on Thursday, suggested that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  could be criminally prosecuted after he leaves office. It wasn`t clear exactly what crime he was alleging. But he mentioned something about the U.S. border.\par \par \uc2 In a Trump/Vance administration, would your Justice Department prosecute \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? And, if so, for what?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, first of all, that would be the responsibility of the attorney general, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not say that he`s trying to throw his political opponent in jail. That is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who has, in fact, already tried to do precisely that. And, importantly, what he said is that if you apply the same standard that Joe Biden`s Justice Department has applied, then there are a lot of Democratic officials who could go to prison.\par \par \uc2 He`s making a fundamental argument about constitutional fairness. It`s so extraordinary that people could say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the one trying to use lawfare against his opponent sometime in the hypothetical future...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... when, in the very real present, that`s exactly what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is trying to do. This is a danger...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But do you object to the premise?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: And I think that what we need to recognize is that applying a consistent standard is what really matters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you would not want the Justice Department to prosecute \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for any alleged crimes, correct?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: I want people who -- Margaret, I want people who commit crimes to face the appropriate response in law.\par \par \uc2 What I do not think is reasonable is for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to weaponize his own Justice Department...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  any number of crimes, some of which have already been thrown out, a number of which I think will be thrown out, including on Monday by the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, the -- the problem that I have Margaret is not with which Democrats should prosecute which Republican and vice versa. It`s let`s get out of the prosecuting of people based on their politics. Let -- let -- let`s let voters decide who the president should be, not judges and prosecutors who are politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator, you are, as everyone knows, on this very short list of potential running mates for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, for our viewers at home, you are 40 years old. You`ve been in the Senate for less than two years. You haven`t held elected office before this. If you are selected, alongside a nominee who is 78 years old, you will be a heartbeat from the presidency.\par \par \uc2 What do you think your biggest accomplishment in the Senate has been to date?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well Margaret, again, I`m not running for vice president, and it`s important for us to remember that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been a very good president. He will be a very good president again.\par \par \uc2 I think, in some ways, these vice presidential conversations serve to distract from the fact that we have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president was a success, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president has been a failure. Let`s get back to success. Let`s get back to peace and prosperity.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: My attitude on the vice presidential thing, Margaret, is look, if he asked me, I want to help him, and, of course, I would be very interested in the job.\par \par \uc2 But you asked, what are my accomplishments in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ? In 18 months, Margaret, we`ve done a lot of good work for our constituents. We`ve got hundreds of millions of dollars to the Great Lakes. We`ve done a lot to help the people of East Palestine deal with the terrible train disaster.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, we`ve done a lot of work on making sure that Ohio has gotten defense resources that make not just Ohio, but our country stronger. So there`s a lot we can hang our hat on. But I like being a senator. I`m not trying to leave the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . It`s an honor to serve the people of Ohio. And, frankly, if you ask me, that`s where I expect to be in six months. That`s where I expect to be in a few years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, J.D. Vance, we`ll be watching. And, hopefully, we`ll have you back in studio next time.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Tomorrow, the Supreme Court is set to conclude a blockbuster term and issue a landmark decision on whether presidents are granted a degree of immunity from criminal prosecution, a decision that could impact the 2024 race.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by CBS News chief legal affairs correspondent Jan Crawford and chief Washington correspondent Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 A lot to get to with both of you.\par \par \uc2 But, Jan, the big question of tomorrow morning, what will the court rule?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, we will see.\par \par \uc2 But, I mean, I do expect a decision that at least leaves open the possibility of a trial before the election. I mean, I see zero chance they`re going to embrace Donald Trump`s argument that he has absolute immunity and can`t be prosecuted. I think they`re going to say that there is some immunity for the official actions of a president.\par \par \uc2 And why is that important? I agree -- and you heard some of that in your conversation with Senator Vance -- they are concerned that this case will apply to future presidents. They are concerned that, after a bitter campaign, as one justice pointed out in argument, that the winning candidate could throw the loser into jail. They`re worried about that.\par \par \uc2 And, as Trump said during that debate, he believes Biden`s policies on immigration have been criminal. So, they see this as the case that goes well beyond \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s why I think they`re going to wall off those kind of official actions of a president, but leave open the possibility of prosecution for unofficial actions of an office-seeker.\par \par \uc2 And as Trump`s lawyer argued at the oral arguments, conceded at the oral arguments, a lot of what`s alleged in the indictment is unofficial acts. So \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  could have those papers ready to go and say, he`s conceded this is unofficial acts that he can be prosecuted for. Let`s get this trial going.\par \par \uc2 And I think the judge could do that very quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s hard to get your head around something being an unofficial act by the president when he is doing it in office at times from the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A lower court...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you explain that?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Because, as a lower court in a different case on a similar issue explained, unofficial acts are the acts of an office-seeker, a candidate, speeches you may give on the campaign trail at rallies.\par \par \uc2 So, a lower court has already laid out the groundwork for how you can make these divisions in the D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals. The official acts will be those core powers that presidents have, immigration policy, decisions you make about drone strikes. Those cannot be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: That is -- that`s going to be walled off. But there`s a lot of things a president does that could be unofficial and criminal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There is so much we need to dig into on the legalities and then with you, Major, on the practicalities, because, as you just mentioned, there could be a trial before the election. I just...\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: We can`t rule it out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re going to have to talk about what that looks like and the impact on the trail.\par \par \uc2 Please stay with us, and we will have the rest of that conversation on the other side of this commercial break with Jan and Major.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 And we return now to our conversation with chief legal correspondent Jan Crawford and chief Washington correspondent Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Major, I want to pick up with you. Jan has laid out for us that there is the expectation the Supreme Court should - could say some, but not total immunity.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What does this mean for the timeline for special counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As a practical matter, the Trump legal effort has already succeeded. It has delayed a resolution of this matter for a very long time. Oral arguments were April 25th. Many legal scholars have said, what is the court waiting for? This is not that hard a question. But by waiting, even there is room available to special counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  to prosecute, his choice is to launch that trial in September or October at the absolute earliest, in the very teeth of a presidential campaign, in which former President Trump says, because I am potent, because I am politically leading, I am being prosecuted. Is that the political and legal terrain \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  wants to wade into? We`ll find out. It`s the most consequential prosecutorial decision in the history of the country if it comes to that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And on two case -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: One directly related to 2020 and attempts to steal the election.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The other on classified documents. That playing out in Florida.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Precisely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is the timeline on that case?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Much, much later. Because Judge Aileen Cannon has briefed and heard arguments on so many underlying issues in that case, it does not appear ripe for a prosecution under the most favorable calendar before the election. The January 6th case, however, does.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jan, how would the immunity ruling that you are expecting affect the classified documents case in Florida?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I don`t think it would because that again - that case is about what he did after he left office. And I agree with Major, I see no way that the classified documents case goes forward. I think that is a very inexperienced judge and she`s taken on a lot of issues. So, I see no way that one goes forward because, again, that`s - that`s about behavior that after, you know, he`s no longer a sitting president.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to point out, you know, remember the Supreme Court did agree to Jack Smith`s request to expedite this case. He asked the court if they were going to decide it, to take it up this term and have a decision by the end of the session. And that`s what he`s going to get.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people who wish, on the conservative side, that the court had just said, no, we`re going to just do it regular course of business. We`ll come back and have arguments in October, we`ll give a decision in December or January, and that means there is no trial. So the court - and I - I - I understand there is frustration among Democrats that the court seems to be dragging their feet on this, but that`s not true. I mean the court has expedited this. They added this to their calendar. And they are going to give \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  what he wanted in his filings, a decision by the end of the term that allows him to go forward with the trial.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, there`s frustration - there`s frustration among Democrats that the attorney general didn`t move faster either.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: OK, look -\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And bring charges.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Just a quick rejoinder on that point. A three judge panel, the D.C. Court of Appeals issued a 57-page opinion on this that was unequivocal. The Supreme Court could have taken that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And - and the state (ph) didn`t like that decision because it essentially said there is no immunity. There`s none. And the court believed, and I think from this conversation you can see, there is some immunity for official acts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And so the court believed, let`s decide it now, instead of having this go through the trial court process and come back up here, challenging this. Trump would be challenging it in September, Octoer all over again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jan, Friday the attorney general said he was disappointed that the court ruled 6-3 to limit a law that has been used to charge hundreds of Capitol riot defendants, as well as President Trump. How will this affect the potential case against Mr. Trump?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: That case again - you know, that came up - one of the January 6th defendants, along with a couple others who were charged with obstruction of an official proceeding, challenged the use of that charge in their cases and the Supreme Court said on Friday that prosecutors may have overreached. They looked at the law that was at issue as part of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act after the Enron accounting scandal and they said the justices said on Friday that it has to be tied to some kind of destruction or hampering of evidence. So, that was a big win for the January 6th defendant in this case, Joe Fisher, and a couple others, but it doesn`t mean it`s a big win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is going to file a motion to dismiss assuming this immunity case goes like we believe it will. He`ll file a motion to dismiss those charges against him. And \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 , I`m sure, already has legal papers to say, let me show you how this obstructions charge is still going to apply to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And why is that important? That carries a 20-year prison sentence. It is a felony. It is the most serious charge. And I believe that, based on, again, another opinion in the D.C. Circuit that lays out a groundwork for how Trump can still be charged -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: They will be able to show that he was trying to interfere with evidence. The evidence of those certificates where they were counting state electors.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: He was trying to disrupt that evidence and the counting of those votes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Very quickly, Margaret. Just under 1,500 people have been charged with the January 6th riot. This affects less than 2 percent of those cases.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Major, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For President Biden to be removed from the top of the ticket, he would have to agree to do so.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: He has to agree. DNC rules are absolutely clear on this. The threshold to be the nominee is 1,968 delegates. President Biden currently has 3,894. Unless he steps down, there is no mechanism to dislodge him from becoming the renominated candidate for the Democratic Party, period, end of story.\par \par \uc2 There are lots of people who are talking about it, but unless he removes himself, or is somehow otherwise incapacitated, this is a closed matter.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s a family matter at the moment.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A very important family matter, not just the family, but the broader Democratic Party family.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you both for your reporting and your analysis.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Connecticut Congressman Jim Hines. He`s the top Democrat on the House Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES, (D-CT): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you were recently briefed regarding those arrests in Los Angeles, Philadelphia and New York City and the eight Tajiki nationals with suspected links to ISIS-K. Do you know where we are in regard to understanding whether they were a cell working together, whether there were direct links?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Yes, so, law enforcement, as you might expect, is making pretty good progress determining who these people were talking to, what the plans were, other people involved in the network, whether an attack was imminent, whether there were specific plans for an attack. And - and this isn`t new, right? In other words, you know, as you know, shortly after these individuals entered the United States, not stopped because there was no derogatory information on them at the time, very quickly some derogatory information was developed, and the decision was taken to watch these guys.\par \par \uc2 Now, the reason you watch these guys instead of instantly arresting them is that their behavior and their communications can really paint a very specific picture of a plot of a conspiracy if there is one. Obviously, they took the decision at one point that the risk-reward there was such that they made these arrests. But, of course, they continue to work to understand whether there were plans and, if so, who else might have been involved.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, it sounds like intelligence is still being gleaned from these eight individuals. So, how can we say there is no active threat?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Well, Margaret, we can never say there is no active threat. You know, there is always a baseline threat of a terrorist act in the United States. There is absolutely nothing we can do to change that fact. So, you can never say that there is zero risk.\par \par \uc2 What you can do is you can look at the period of time since 9/11, the tragic attack on 9/11, and say how many Americans have actually died in a terrorist attack engineered by foreigners. And the answer to that question is - is vanishingly small. Our people are very, very good, but you can never have zero risk of a terrorist attack.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you support a public hearing with the intelligence chiefs to lay out the facts as we know them? I know Director Wray said we are at the highest possible level of threat right now.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Yes, and, Margaret, look, I think it`s - it`s really important for people to keep this in context. That may true, and it`s probably true because the world is a more complicated place than it was ten years ago, in particular with the war in Israel and Gaza. We see every radical Islamic group from the Houthis to all the Iranian-backed proxies interested in doing things that they might not have wanted to do ten years ago. So, Director Wray may be right and, in fact, you know, I think our intelligence agencies and law enforcement are on alert in a way they haven`t been in a very long time.\par \par \uc2 And look, the Tajik story is a success story. They were arrested. They did not conduct a terrorist attack.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your Republican colleague, Mike Turner, was on this program a few weeks ago and he said, quote, "we have terrorists that are actively working inside the United States that are a threat to Americans."\par \par \uc2 Is that an accurate characterization, and - and, if so, why now?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Well, I - the - the Tajik case is, as far as I know, the only case that we have been briefed on, on the Intelligence Committee of our intelligence community, our law enforcement community, following people that we think could be involved in a plot. I`m certainly not aware of other situations like that.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is almost certainly true, in a country of 350 million people, that there are some people out there who are thinking about undertaking acts of violence. We see a lot of violence in this country. Most of it is domestic. Most of it is - is not related to transnational terrorism.\par \par \uc2 But again, you asked earlier about a public hearing around the facts here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: And I didn`t answer it directly, so I`ll answer it directly right now. We`re probably not at that stage because of what we started talking about, which is the absolute necessity of law enforcement really understanding the full contours of that Tajik group and doing the work that they need to do, which is best done in secret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In terms of what`s happening and the connection to the southern border, President Biden said at the debate on Thursday, quote, "I`m not saying no terrorist ever got through." So, he seems to be acknowledging this unknown element here.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the intelligence community has the resources they need right now to deal with the threat and the vulnerability at the border?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Of course a border in which people are entering, and we don`t know who they are, is a risk. No question about it. And I wish that we had seized the opportunity of the bipartisan bill, negotiated by Jim Langford and Chris Murphy -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: A conservative Republican and a progressive Democrat, to actually do something about that. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said no. He said don`t do it, I want to run on this issue. So, I would have loved to have seen that get done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: But again, you know, people need to put this into context.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: How many Americans have died in a terrorist attack by somebody who snuck across the southern border? The answer to that question is zero. So, resource allocation should the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , should the CIA be laser focused on the southern border, I don`t know. Clearly it is a risk and a vulnerability. But, you know, a lot of these plots we pick up because of our collection ability abroad.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you think the resources are adequate to intelligence collection abroad?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Well, I think if you had the head of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or any of the people who were involved in this effort they would say we could really use more resources, right?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: But one of the challenges we have that we haven`t talked about, Margaret, is remember that for a decade now, more than a decade, we`ve been talking about the pivot to China, right? China invading Taiwan is an outcome that is catastrophic in ten different dimensions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: If we`re serious about pivoting to Asia, if we`re serious about supporting the Ukrainian fight against Russia -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Inevitably, because we don`t have infinite resources, some things, like counterterrorism -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Are not going to get the full amount of resources that you would like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before I let you go, how do you think America`s foreign adversary`s viewed President Biden`s performance on Thursday night?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: I suspect that, you know, pretty much everybody watched that debate and thought that the president did not perform the way we would have liked to have seen him perform.\par \par \uc2 However, I`ve spent time around three different presidents, Margaret, and I will tell you that the president`s job is enormously hard and involves all kinds of things, none of which are standing and doing a debate for 90 minutes on TV. The president`s job involves passing legislation. I would hope that people would compare this president`s record in that regard with the last president`s record. I think the president`s job involves setting the tone.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Being quick on your feet is kind of important to the job.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM HIMES: Well, yes. Yes. And again, I think he`s acknowledged, and the vice president acknowledged, that that was not the performance we were looking for. But I`m not so cynical as to believe that the American people are going to choose a president based on a 90 minute debate, rather than a four-year record of startling legislative achievements and of setting a tone that the rest of the world says, wow, you know, America is back to the decent leader that we used to believe that it was prior to the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman Himes, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the executive director of the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agency that helps disadvantaged children in the world`s toughest places. \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 `s \uc2 Catherine Russell\uc2  joins us from New York.\par \par \uc2 Good to speak with you again.\par \par \uc2 I know you`re just back from Sudan, which is the largest humanitarian crisis on the planet right now. What did you learn?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 , EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 : Yes. I learned that it is a catastrophic situation for children there. And there are compounding crises. First, it`s the largest displacement crisis for children around the world. So many millions of children have moved either from their homes to neighboring countries, like South Sudan, Egypt, or Chad.\par \par \uc2 Many millions are moved inside Sudan to other places in the city trying to - or in the country trying to find some refuge. It`s also a huge challenge in terms of malnutrition. We have 4 million children who are severely malnourished. Almost - more than half a million of those children are severely acutely malnourished, which means that they really are on the verge of starvation. And then, shockingly, almost every child in Sudan has been out of school for the last year, which is incredibly destabilizing for them, for their future and certainly for the country as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, 17 million children who are not in school.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Yes, shocking.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Apart from the humanitarian concern here, I know the U.S. intelligence community has highlighted that Sudan could become a terrorist safe haven due to this ongoing civil war. What are the conditions like for those next generations?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : The conditions are absolutely terrible. I have to say, I was at a center that \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  supports where we were providing all sorts of services for children, education, which, you know, as we discussed, a horrific problem of them not being in school, trying to provide health care, providing psycho social support for children who are so traumatized. It`s just almost hard to imagine.\par \par \uc2 It`s also - it`s just such a desperate place in so many ways. They are so - you`ve they`ve been so traumatized by so much violence and they`ve seen things that no child should ever see. And that`s really something that long term is hard to imagine how they get over it.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, we are there. We`re working hard. We are getting to these children. But this ongoing conflict makes it almost impossible to provide a decent future for these kids.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A little closer to home in Haiti, I know this past week the first U.N.-backed foreign law enforcement forces from eight different countries arrived. This is part of a U.S. supported effort.\par \par \uc2 How quickly do you think that will make a difference for the children there?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Well, I will say, hopefully it will be quick because the children there - I mean, honestly, I feel like I`m sort of a broken record when I tell you how bad it is in so many places. But Haiti is really challenging because there`s so much violence. I was shocked by what I saw when I was there several months ago. So many children who have seen violence directly, who have experienced violence, high, high rates of sexual violence. So, it`s been incredibly important to try to stabilize that situation.\par \par \uc2 I think this is the first step. It`s going to take a lot of work, I think, to get it done, but at least we`ll start to see some semblance, hopefully, of some security, which will make it easier for us to operate, but also give these children some prospect for a decent future that isn`t defined by violence and hunger, which is, unfortunately, what we`re seeing now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the U.S. remains the largest donor to many of these humanitarian causes I know.\par \par \uc2 There was one rare piece of good news this past week in the Middle East. I read 21 children with cancer were permitted to be medically evacuated from Gaza. This was the first evacuation since May. Why is it so hard to get sick children out?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Yes. I`ll say this. You know, everything in Gaza is hard. It`s just the most challenging environment for us to work in, I have to say. I think that, you know, the main problem is a lack of security, and that makes it difficult for the children who live there. They`ve been displaced so many times. So many children have multiple times moved, trying to seek refuge, trying to get away from the bombardments. I think it`s incredibly challenging. We know - we continue to see that we`re on the verbal of famine there. So, children - it basically - it just means they don`t really know where their next meal is coming from.\par \par \uc2 They also have not been in school, right? So, it`s an incredibly challenging place for children. And I think, you know, it is great to find some shred of - shred of good news. And, you know, when you think about it, you forget, in these humanitarian situations, the humanitarian crisis is so devastating that you forget that there are routine problems that children face, right? They`re not getting their vaccines. They`re not getting treatment for things like cancer or other sort of routine diseases and challenges that children all over the world face. So, getting some of these kids out of there has been, as you say, a bright spot.\par \par \uc2 But I think overall we continue to see real challenges in our ability to operate there. We need to get that situation sorted out so that children have some prospect, and I think this applies to all three of the situations you`ve talked about, children are the future. We talk about that all the time. But what does that mean if we don`t give them a future, if we don`t make sure that they have some - some prospect of hope.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : And I think it`s incumbent upon all of the adults in the world to come together and to do better by these children.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I couldn`t agree more with you on that.\par \par \uc2 Director Russell, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nearly half of the countries in the world will hold elections in 2024. And today, French citizens cast ballots in a snap parliamentary election. French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  made the surprising decision to hold it after his centrist party was trounced by the far right in the recently European elections. It signals once again the uncertainty about the political changes ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): President Macron called his shock decision a test of truth. The rising floodwaters of the far right, as Macron put it, cannot be ignored. With a call to stop the migration, this man, 28-year-old Jordan Bardella, may become France`s next prime minister. With a platform promising to crack down on crime and restrict citizenship and rights for foreign residents, his national rally party is poised to potentially win a parliamentary majority.\par \par \uc2 The party, led by Marie Le Pen, and founded by her Holocaust-denying father, has renounced its anti-Semitic past, but for years has rallied on anti-Muslim rhetoric and described migrants as threatening, particularly to women.\par \par \uc2 Macron, who will remain as president for another three years, warned that the far right risks sparking a civil war.\par \par \uc2 And in Germany, police broke up protests this weekend outside the \uc2 AFT\uc2 , or Alternative for Germany Convention. That far right party pulled off its best performance ever in European parliamentary elections, worrying Chancellor Olaf Schultz, and moving one of AFD`s leaders, Bjorn Hocke, from the fringe to the fore. Hocke said he deplores the Nazis but he`s led chants of their slogans at this rallies, which landed him back in court this past week since German law bans the use of Nazi phrases.\par \par \uc2 The AFD party advocate for tough deportation policies, is suspicious of climate change and the government response to Covid. Waves of migrants, mainly from Syria, Afghanistan and Africa have been fleeing to Germany since Europe`s 2015 refugee crisis.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, this viral video of dancing young Germans chanting "foreigners out" caused outrage.\par \par \uc2 Despite these emerging trend lines in Europe, it appears the center did hold in those parliamentary elections, at least for now. Center right \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  is expected to remain European commission president.\par \par \uc2 But this Monday, Hungary takes over the rotating presidency of the European council, and Prime Minister \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  has promised to, quote, "make Europe great again." The slogan a nod to his friend \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the far right still ascendent in parts of Europe, it has left some to wonder if America is any less immune. And leaves open the question of what happens next when democracies elect leaders who flirt with anti-democratic tendencies.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020240630ek6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Debate Face-Off; Biden Debate Concern; Voters Respond to Debate; Breaking Down the Debate; Supreme Court Rulings; July 4th Travel Rush; Oklahoma Bible Controversy; Former Uvalde Police Chief Charged; David Shows Success Measured in Smiles; Rip Currents Risk; Raising a Glass; Discovering Dasha; Note to Self; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nancy Cordes, Weijia  Jiang, Ed O`Keefe, John Dickerson, Jan Crawford, Janet Shamlian, Joel  Payne, Douglas Heye, Vladimir Duthiers, Robert Costa, Nancy Chen \par \uc2 14,916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this Friday morning, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS been a long week.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I`m dealing with everything we have to do with -- look -- if --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Biden faces backlash from inside his own party after a very shaky debate performance\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Some House Democrats talking about talking to the White House about having to step down.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I really don`t know what he said at the end of that sentence .I don`t think he knows what he said either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Supreme Court rejects a multibillion dollar opioid settlement that would have protected the Sackler family.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: This keeps the door open for lawsuits down the road.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Criminal charges are filed against Uvalde`s Former Schools Police over his response to the mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 JESSE RIZZO (Niece, Jackie Cazares was killed in Robb Elementary shooting): I am happy to finally see that there`s judgement coming.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Oklahoma is now requiring its public schools to teach the Bible in fifth through 12th grade lessons.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Los Angeles Lakers\uc2  select Bronny James.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Lakers made history by selecting the 19-year-old son of LeBron James.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Bronny will have the chance to play on the same team as his father.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: First ever father-son duo in the \uc2 NBA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Trump supporters including the former president said that Biden would be jacked up on drugs for this debate.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("The Daily Show" host): Let me just say after watching tonight`s debate.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART: Both of these men should be using performance enhancing drugs.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART: I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that, of course, is where we are going to begin with last night`s presidential debate. I`m sure many of you saw it. Both presumptive nominees were hoping to shake off concerns over their age and their fitness for office, but President Biden failed to land any knockout blows and his stumbling performance is raising a lot of alarm bells for many Democrats this morning.\par \par \uc2 There are even some reports that many are calling -- now calling for him to step aside and let someone else be the Democratic nominee for president.\par \par \uc2 Our chief White House correspondent, that`s Nancy Cordes in Atlanta where the debate took place. Nancy was there.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning. It`s never good when members of your own party are basically saying, Houston, we have a problem. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right, Gayle, and they are using words like "disappointing" and "painful" to describe what they saw last night.\par \par \uc2 Some of them think that President Biden still has an opportunity to rebound, but others worry that last night`s performance was a sign of things to come and they`re starting to ask whether there`s anything the party can do, whether there`s anything the president should do between now and Election Day, just four months away.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: From the very start of the debate, it was clear something was wrong.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You know, the fact of the matter is that --\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It wasn`t just the president`s voice weakened by what aides described as a day`s long cold. It was also his answers, which sometimes meandered or trailed off.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Making sure that we`re able to make every single solitary person eligible for what I`ve been able to do with the -- with the COVID -- excuse me with -- dealing with everything we have to do with -- look -- if we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump seized on this halting answer about immigration.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I`m going to continue to move until we get to a total ban on the -- on the total initiative relative to what we`re going to do with more Border Patrol and more asylum officers.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I really don`t know what he said at the end of that sentence. I don`t think he knows what he said either.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The president`s struggles drew attention away from a blizzard of false claims from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on healthcare --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m the one that got the insulin down for the seniors.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: On his economic record --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We had the greatest economy in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: On his legal troubles --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Because he indicted me because I was his opponent.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And on abortion --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : He is willing to as we say, rip the baby out of the womb in the ninth month and kill the baby.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : That is simply not true. We are not for late-term abortion. Period. Period. Period.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: By the end of the debate, panic was spreading through the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES\uc2  (CNN political commentator): That was not what we needed from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and it`s personally painful for a lot of people. It`s not just panic, it`s pain.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL (MSNBC political commentator): I do think people feel like that we are confronting a crisis.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Even the vice president didn`t sugarcoat it.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): It was a small start, that`s obvious to everyone. I`m not going to debate that point.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, this election and who is the president of the United States has to be about substance and the contrast is clear.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Both candidates provided some truly surreal moments. Like this exchange about Trump`s hush money verdict.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The only person to this stage who is a convicted felon is the man I`m looking at right now.\par \par \uc2 Having sex with a porn star on the night while your wife was pregnant. I mean, what -- what -- what are you talking about? You have the morals on alley cat.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I didn`t have sex with a porn star.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And for voters who worry both men are past their prime, this clash about their golf games was not reassuring.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I got my handicap, which when I was vice president, down to a six. And by the way, I told you before, I`m happy to play golf, if you carry your own bag. Think you can do it?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : That`s the biggest lie that he is a six handicap of all.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I was eight handicap.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Eight, but I haven`t -- I mean, I haven`t --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`ve seen you swing. I know how you swing. Let`s not act like children, Joe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You`re not holding your ground here --\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH (CNN anchor and political correspondent): President Trump, we are going to turn --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Twenty-one run --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Let`s not act like children.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Some Democrats are now openly musing about whether it is too late to replace Biden on the ticket, but California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  who may have presidential ambitions of his own said: "You don`t turn your back on someone because of one bad performance."\par \par \uc2 In the end, though, there`s only one person who could actually make that decision and that`s \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`ll talk about that in just a bit. Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang, she watched last night`s debate with voters from across the political spectrum. She`s in North Carolina where President Biden will be today.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, what did you hear?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Nate and good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 Well, our watch party included five voters who were very different, but they all agreed on two big things: They say there was no clear winner and they were also deeply frustrated that there was a lack of policy detail from both of the candidates to answer how their daily lives would improve.\par \par \uc2 That includes three undecided voters who say last night`s debate did not help answer who they will support in November.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SMITH (Unaffiliated voter): We`ve probably gotten what -- three to five minutes of substantial content on what they actually plan on doing.\par \par \uc2 With the new rules, I expected a lot more rather than following like a normal debate procedure with more civil discourse between the two, it turned more into a once his time is up, I get to get my punches in, and then my time will end and he gets his punches in.\par \par \uc2 And I really was hoping for something different than that.\par \par \uc2 MARY ANNE PAYNE (Unaffiliated voter): It didn`t really add a lot of new information. We didn`t go through and really address any particular questions.\par \par \uc2 JADEN CARLESS (Unaffiliated voter): So once they answer more questions about education or immigration and give better viewpoints to the people, I feel like that will give the people in me a better chance to actually vote for who I want to vote for.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Another voter is a staunch \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  supporter who admitted that she was really worried, especially during the first half of the debate, because she said that Trump was clearly outperforming Biden, although she said the president was able to find his footing.\par \par \uc2 By the end, she does not believe it was enough to get any new support.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FELECIA ROBINSON (Democratic voter): I mean, I`ll be honest, I don`t know if this debate really did move the needle, like others have agreed there is more depth they could have been saying.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if you were not already decided. For the most part, I think people are decided.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: As for the voter who is backing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he said he wanted to see a "more mature and disciplined" Trump than he did in 2020.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER TANNER (Republican voter): He got better, but there were periods that, you know, still got into knockdown drag out, but they both did.\par \par \uc2 But that`s kind of -- he`s a little more animated with it, but I liked, he closed good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Now, one interesting note is that our group of voters which included two 19-year-old said that age probably would not be a factor in their ultimate decision since the candidates are only three years apart, although they did say age really started to show when they started bickering about who was better at golf -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, not a high point.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by our chief political analyst, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , host of "The Daily Report" and senior White House correspondent -- our political correspondent for us, Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you very much for being here. That wasn`t one bad performance from the president. There`s been a lot leading up to this.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been covering the age concerns for two years and at times you were made to feel crazy for even asking the question, and voters are worried. We`ve seen it in polls. You`ve got some information from inside the Biden camp on this, but first, I just want to go to you, John, big picture. Americans are processing something that could be very monumental today.\par \par \uc2 What stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They are.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what their processing is, I mean, you have two candidates the country has said they don`t want either of them, told pollsters that regularly and they got a debate that affirmed their underlying view that they don`t like either one of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 The bigger problem is for the incumbent president, who as many supporters in his own party have said he had one test last night and that was to overcome these questions about his age by his performance.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , a lot of times will say to the questions about his age, he will say, "Just watch me." People did for 90 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Nancy said in her piece that some Democrats believe that Biden can still rebound. Last night was supposed to be the rebound from these questions.\par \par \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  says you can`t just throw someone out after one bad performance. That`s incredibly true. The question is in the White House, whether those who are closest to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , in their heart of hearts think last night was closer to Biden`s best performance than it was his worst performance, which is to say that there is a problem embedded with the candidate that won`t go away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they were --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They kept saying, you know, he had a cold, but this is a thing, when you have a cold, there`s many things you can take for it. We didn`t know he had a cold until he stood up.\par \par \uc2 A cold doesn`t force you to lose your train of thought. A cold doesn`t force you to just throw things out randomly that too many people made no sense.\par \par \uc2 And so how long do we continue to act like that we didn`t see what we just saw last night? I thought for many people, they the word I keep hearing, it was very painful to watch.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well saying something that is different than what people saw and experienced themselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That`s Donald Trump`s trick and then what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did last night, and so you don`t want to be in that position if you`re trying to argue that this is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does.\par \par \uc2 But in the debate don`t last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rewrote the history of the January 6 attack on the Capitol. He rewrote the history of Charlottesville and his reaction to it. And he rewrote the history of the election that he lost that he claims he won.\par \par \uc2 That`s his lane and he performed it, as he always does last night with an unending river of lies and distortions.\par \par \uc2 But the question is whether Biden is now an impediment to Democrats be able to make that case.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Ed, so what are you hearing from the Biden campaign? I see you have a slight smirk on your face. And also, what are you hearing from the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right. Well, the Biden campaign says, look, we raised a lot of money last night in small dollar amounts from people who were watching across the country, the kind of grassroots fundraising they wanted from an event like this.\par \par \uc2 The problem is the perception, as John was describing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Raising money and raising concerns are two different things.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It is now that -- exactly. Exactly. And raising expectations or hopes and dreams of your party, he didn`t do that last night.\par \par \uc2 So the conversations we`ve all heard from friends and family and the guy on the train about is he too old, because there`s something we can do to get rid of him are now happening among the people who could do something about it in about eight weeks.\par \par \uc2 Eight weeks from this morning, the party will wake up with a nominee. Will it be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? You know, right now, probably, because there is no sense among those that are having the conversation about what can we do about what to do?\par \par \uc2 Is it the vice president? Is it \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 ? Is it \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it the First Lady? Is it the First Lady?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Someone else that someone steps up --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, now we are hearing so many whispers about potential replacement.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, we have whispers right now and let`s see through the weekend, whether or not they turn into actual substantive conversation, whether they get to people like \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  of South Carolina, the First Lady, the president`s sister -- those are the kinds of people who turn to him and say, sir, it`s time to consider stepping aside.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Ed, you said --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Unless or until people who are talking -- those people are talking to him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: This is just conversation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I see what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Ed, you said --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But it is happening among those who said do something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said -- you said last night while we were on the air that there are --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That there are people --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That people are already calling for him to step aside.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Last night, in the middle of the debate.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: House Democrats who were gathered together in Washington watching it, governors and people who work for them. Those are super delegates. Those are the ones that can move the levers of the party to somebody else.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But the conversation about who would that be? Do we have enough time to think -- you know, what would that do to a general election race are the ones who are --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has the legislative record to run on. He can`t explain and communicate the power of that record to the American public. He has had many, many opportunities. It is not the first time.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And that`s exactly --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Among other things. It`s a communication job, Tony, as we talked about.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Two things can be true for Democrats trying to wrestle with this question today.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can have an admirable record that is something that the Democratic Party should run on and he may also be incapable of running on that record in a way that gives people confidence about four more years in the office. Those two things can be true at the same time.\par \par \uc2 But the important point Ed is making, I would just add to it, the Democratic Party has a tradition of hand wringing and use the term they use, bedwetting, so there are a lot of Democrats who will say, yes, there`s all this chatter, but there`s always a lot of chatter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : What makes it different, of course, is that the stakes are so high and the chatter is coming from not the usual corners.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not just yet. Exactly. I`m more --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The decision makers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am more in touch with the guy on the train or my mom`s neighbors in West Virginia than political strategists and they`re worried.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, people are worried -- they think it`s sad, that`s the other thing I`m hearing from people.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to talk more about it at 7:30. John and Ed, we have the whole show on this