s. That unforgettable day.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian is there with reaction.\par \par \uc2 Local news though coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 The first criminal charges have been filed in Uvalde, Texas, over the botched police response to the massacre at Robb Elementary School two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Former Schools Police Chief Pete Arredondo who was fired three months after the shootings was arrested yesterday in connection with the deaths of 19 children and two teachers.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian is in Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 Janet, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 The Sheriff`s Office says that Arredondo was in custody here at the police department at the jail in Uvalde last night charged with 10 counts of child endangerment, those are felony charges, but he was released late Thursday night after posting $10,000.00 bail.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETE ARREDONDO (Former Uvalde Schools Police Chief): Sir, if you can hear me, please put your gun down. We don`t want anybody else hurt.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Uvalde Schools Chief Peter Arredondo was among the first to arrive after a gunman opened fire at Robb Elementary.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Wait, Chief is in there now. Chief`s in charge right now. Hold on.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: His slow moving and botched response as onsite commander while he and hundreds of others waited for more than 70 minutes before confronting and killing the shooter was broadly condemned in multiple investigations and led to his eventual dismissal.\par \par \uc2 PETE ARREDONDO: We cleared off the rest of the building.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Reports found Arredondo wasted precious time during the shooting.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: What are we doing here?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And even directed officers at several points to delay making entry into classrooms as shots rang out and terrified children made calls to 911.\par \par \uc2 For Jesse Rizzo who`s niece, nine-year-old Jackie Cazares was killed, Arredondo`s arrest means accountability.\par \par \uc2 JESSE RIZZO: It`s an emotional day. It`s something that we`ve been waiting for a long time.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: He says the indictment is not enough.\par \par \uc2 JESSE RIZZO: And I`m surprised that a lot of other officers weren`t charged.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Brett Cross agrees. His 10-year-old Uziyah had dreams of becoming a police officer. Thursday, Cross stood outside the jailhouse, hoping to send Arredondo a message.\par \par \uc2 BRETT CROSS (Uziyah Garcia`s legal guardian): I wanted him to see me and I know he saw me and I know that he saw the sign that I was carrying which is the same sign that I used two years ago when we first started protesting for him to get fired and it said, "Their blood is on Pete`s hands," and I believe that a hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: There are reports, another officer, Adrian Gonzalez was indicted on similar charges. Of course, we know there were hundreds of officers, close to 400 involved in this response. No word yet on whether more indictments are coming.\par \par \uc2 We did reach out to Arredondo`s attorney and the District Attorney`s Office and have not heard back -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will stay tuned as families look for closure.\par \par \uc2 Janet, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, more coverage of the fallout from last night`s presidential debate. Two very smart people, Joel Payne and Doug Heye will be here in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot of questions and they`ve got answers.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a main topic of conversation this morning. We`ve got more now on last night`s presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 For a lot of people, it was very tough to watch. I keep hearing words like painful, trainwreck, epic fail, with voters facing a stark choice in November.\par \par \uc2 Joining us to talk about how the campaigns move forward from here are CBS News political contributor and chief communication officer for Move On, that`s Joel Payne, and the former national communications director for the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , Doug Heye.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both.\par \par \uc2 This is your wheelhouse, guys. You know exactly what we saw last night and how people are translating it, but we`re having a conversation like if this was a family member and you watched this, you would go to dad and say, Dad, we need to take the keys away from you now. Now is the time.\par \par \uc2 What do you say, Joel about how President Biden can and should move forward from here? Can he recover from this?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, typically the way you would fix a moment like last night where he wasn`t his best is more of him, you would expose him more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: More of him?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s typically.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Typically if you wanted to ease people`s concerns, I think therein lies the challenge. Right? More of him was that as John said earlier, was that closer to his best day or to his worst day?\par \par \uc2 I do think you can perform the job of the president and not necessarily perform the job of a candidate well. Those things don`t necessarily overlap like a Venn diagram and I think we saw he had some challenges performing as a candidate last night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And for President Trump, Doug, I mean, listen, many people say he didn`t have a good night either, because most of the things he said were not true. At one point when he said, I offered the 10,000 troops to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . I said to Ed O`Keefe, I don`t recall him doing that. When did that happen? He goes, "It didn`t." You know?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Democrats are so -- feel so upset by this is because they were softballs, politically speaking that a candidate who was with it and fully present --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Could have knocked it out the park.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Could knock it out park, and the swings weren`t coming and that seemed to be what the takeaway was.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE (Republican strategist): Yes, if you`re the Trump campaign, words and the split screen is everything you wanted last night.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had the debate that he wanted to. He put his -- he was on offense and on basically every issue, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was on defense, and when he wasn`t talking, and we saw the split screen, he didn`t look like he was all there.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll tell you, I`m such a Frank Sinatra fan that I hosted an hour on "Seriously Sinatra" a couple of weeks ago, and I thought last night of seeing Frank Sinatra at the end of his career. One minute, "Come Fly With Me" is going very well, and few minutes later, he can`t remember the words to "My Way."\par \par \uc2 And to Joel`s point, this is the problem of, you fix this with more of him. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to have more nights like this, more days like this than fewer because he`s at an age where he ages faster and he`s still in a job that ages you faster as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Doug, do you call it a good night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  even though most of the time he was not telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Doug, you said that the fact that he wasn`t telling the truth doesn`t really matter.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE: It`s all baked in with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We know this and maybe that`s unfortunate. I agree that it`s unfortunate. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a ball of energy.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last night was not a ball of energy and just judging by text messages and e-mails I was receiving from Republicans and from Democrats, great night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , despite the things that you`re talking about. Terrible, existentially bad night for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: The fact, Gayle, that your first question to me was about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That`s the problem, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lied, obfuscated, did a lot of harm and put forward a lot of misinformation and the fact that as a Democrat, I can`t lead with that. Right? That`s difficult for me to go and offensively lead with because the performance of the president is kind of what`s on the matinee --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: -- after last night.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joel, whether --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you surprised at his performance last night?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Of President Biden`s performance?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I think I was surprised to the extent that he really struggled in this format, but look, it`s not uncommon for incumbent presidents to struggle in debate settings like this. Of course, we all know about \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2012, George Bush struggled in some of his debates with \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joel, the big difference was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the difference is with \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . Yes. Go ahead, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The day after that 2012 Romney debate where he was seen as losing the debate, he gave one of the greatest, most stirring performances.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s a competitive guy, and he went out there and he crushed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And no one questioned, is he up for the job? They said, now, that was an off night for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , but nobody questioned whether he had the ability to do the job.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE: Twenty minutes into the debate, the Biden campaign was putting out to reporters, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a cold. That tells you that they know it wasn`t a good night.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE: And by the way --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE: -- did any of us think that of the two candidates, the one that would say "Let`s not act like children" was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, you`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE: And that tells you a whole lot in one sentence.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Joel, I don`t know how the Democrats move forward here, because we`ve had Democrats on for months and months, and we asked them the age question, you know, are you concerned, voters are concerned. Tell us what it`s like, you`re with the president behind closed doors and they all act like he`s doing trigonometry and a Rubik`s cube, and spinning a basketball, and balancing the budget.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE: All at the same time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s hard to believe that now. So what can they possibly say?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, again, I think now you fix this with showing a counter narrative, counterprogramming, putting him out there.\par \par \uc2 Last night after the debate, he was at the Waffle House in suburban Atlanta. He went to a victory party, showed more vigor, showed more energy.\par \par \uc2 I`d imagine you`ll see more of President Biden out on the trail.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At the Waffle House?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I imagine reporters will have more access to him, which is something that I know that reporters have been frustrated with, because you fix it with dispelling people`s concerns by putting him out 