**************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in his eight o`clock hour, we are going to talk about last night`s presidential debate with our own Robert Costa. He`s got the inside scoop on how the campaigns are reacting and what can happen next.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. We`ve got an emotional "Note to Sell" for International Pride Day from a woman whose voice is known to millions and finally she`s able to live in this world as herself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 If the news ever makes you feel like you need a drink, we`ve got you covered. First on CBS MORNINGS, "Bon Appetit" reveals its list of the best new bars in America.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of people needed a drink after last night. But first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Former President Trump and President Biden go head-to-head in their first presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The only person on this stage who is a convicted felon is the man I`m looking at right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Idaho must allow emergency abortions to protect a woman`s health after a major Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The court will hand down more decisions. We`re still waiting on those two big ones involving former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The holiday travel rush could set new records today ahead of the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Folks, there`s a chance that we`re going to screen three million people nationwide for the first time in our history. What do you need to do? Get to the airport early.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Doesn`t even have his driver`s license yet. He`s already accomplished so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And 15-year-old golf phenom, Miles Russell is his name makes his big debut on the PGA Tour. He may not be a driver yet, but boy can he putt.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #1: This for his first birdie on the PGA Tour.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #2: Give it to him. Well earned.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Calm, cool, and collected.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour though with one the potential impact of last night`s debate between President Biden and former President Trump where both presumptive nominees were looking to convince voters they are fit for office.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Especially when he left office and I`m going to continue to move until we get a total ban on the -- the -- the total initiative relative to what we`re going to do with more Border Patrol and more asylum officers.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN anchor): President Trump.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I really don`t know what he said at the end of that sentence. I don`t think he knows what he said either.\par \par \uc2 Look, we had the safest border in the history of our country. The border -- all he had to do is leave it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As you see Biden stumbled through many of his answers last night while Trump repeatedly made several false claims about his record in office.\par \par \uc2 CBS News chief election and campaign correspondent, that`s Robert Costa joins us now from Atlanta. He was there last night.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Listen, we all know you have some of the best sources in the business. I`m curious about what the Democrats are saying to you this morning.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Gayle. Good to be with all of you.\par \par \uc2 Look after anyone goes to a concert or to a movie, there is always that chatter in the aftermath and we are still living in the immediate aftermath of last night`s debate.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve been working the phones, Gayle, talking to people close to President Biden, working inside the White House, who work at the highest ranks of the Biden campaign and let me be clear, based in my reporting, there is no push to get President Biden to leave the stage, to somehow give up the nomination at the convention in Chicago this summer.\par \par \uc2 This is someone who has been in public office since 1972 when he was elected to the Senate, before that in local office in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 For a half century, he has navigated power, hung on, climbed back from different defeats, and they don`t see him walking away, despite all of the clamor even inside of his party right now.\par \par \uc2 They are listening, though, to elected officials, especially those who are speaking publicly, not privately. And they know they need to get better in terms of his performance in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 They expect Biden to work hard, to maybe meet more voters, do more interviews. They acknowledge it`s a poor performance, but there is no move to get him off the ticket.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how do they explain the poor performance like that when the stakes are so high? He had one job to do last night, Bob, just one really.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So part of the argument I`m hearing from top Democrats, Gayle, is that it`s not just President Biden who is going to be making this case. You`re going to see more from Vice President Harris, more from top Democrats, and they`re going to pour millions and millions into television advertising to try to remind voters that in their view, this election is about former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his character, his conduct, not about one debate.\par \par \uc2 Now this debate has had a searing effect on how a lot of people in the party perceive President Biden, they know that. They`re in a pain position today, a lot of them, when I`ve spoken to them on the phone, all these text messages at the same time in politics, a day can sometimes be a lifetime and there are many days ahead of November.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Bob, we don`t have the ratings on last night`s simulcast debate. It was on CNN. It was on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . It was on ABC. It was on everybody.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know what the numbers were, but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the president who gave fewer press conferences and major interviews than any president in decades. You have to go back to Reagan or even before that.\par \par \uc2 He passed up the Super Bowl interview on \uc2 CBS\uc2  this year, just didn`t want to do it. That`s tens of millions of people. What do you -- how do you expect him to make this pivot that apparently people inside the White House expect him to do when he has not been out there? Not in 2020, and not until now.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s not so much a pivot, it`s going to be shining an even brighter spotlight on Trump. They look at the schedule, and they look at the calendar and they say Trump has sentencing in New York. He faces a possible jail sentence on July 11th. We`ll be covering that closely.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s the Republican Convention, the running mate. Can he bring the attention to democracy and Trump? Can he bring it back to abortion rights? He tried during the debate. I`m told by his top advisers, they wish he did more of it at times.\par \par \uc2 And in terms of the interviews, he is not someone who really likes the press at this time. Since day one of his campaign, I have been told by his closest friends, he`s been frustrated with press coverage. People, he thinks in the press think he`s too old. He`s covered in a way he believes is ageist, and that has really influenced how he does interviews, how he interacts with the press, and that`s just the status quo inside of Biden world.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, look, I work for the -- I work for the media, I`m in the press, but I talk to regular people, that`s my whole job and regular people have those concerns and that`s why we`re talking about him.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much. Appreciate that reporting. We`ll stick with it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Nancy Chen takes us to the ocean to learn about dangerous rip currents and share important safety tips for your family. But first, it is 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up Monday on CBS MORNINGS, David Begnaud introduces us to a California man who has something nice to say to everyone who drives by in the morning.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Good morning, young man. So nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 You are so beautiful, you`re going to charm everybody today.\par \par \uc2 Don, so good to see you, and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I make it a point to learn the names because if you want to make people feel important, you`ve got to learn their names.\par \par \uc2 You all have a wonderful workout.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: If he misses a day, everybody asks where he is.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I want them to feel important. I want them to feel beautiful. I want them to feel needed.\par \par \uc2 And I want them to feel safe. You see, even sometimes, I direct a little bit of traffic.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we love it.\par \par \uc2 Monday on "Beg-Knows America," David shows us what made the successful businessman giving all love in exchange for making people feel good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Successful businessman.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I love this story.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Summer is off to a dangerous start because of powerful rip currents along the Atlantic and Gulf Coast beaches. At least six people drowned over a recent two-day period in Florida, and North Carolina lifeguards have rescued more than 150 people just over the past week.\par \par \uc2 The fast-moving water can surprise even experienced swimmers.\par \par \uc2 Our Nancy chin is on Rockaway Beach in New York to show us how to avoid rip currents and what to do if you are caught in one.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This is the beach where two teens disappeared into the water a week ago today, a reminder of how dangerous the surf can be.\par \par \uc2 And with so many of us hitting the shore next week for July 4th, we learned some tips to keep you and your family safe this summer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: With their toes in the surf and chairs in the sand, beachgoers might not be aware of a hidden danger lurking in the water, rip currents.\par \par \uc2 Why are rip currents so dangerous?\par \par \uc2 DAN GEORGE (Long Branch, New Jersey chief lifeguard and beach safety manager): They`re dangerous because they can be deceiving.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Dan George is the chief lifeguard and beach safety manager in Long Branch, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 DAN GEORGE: You can`t always see them especially if you`re not sure what to look for.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: In terms of all the dangers that you and your team are look hang out for on the beach. Where do rip currents rank?\par \par \uc2 DAN GEORGE: They rank number one. Last year, we had 1,200 rescues.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Rip currents are among the most common beach hazards and can pop up on even the nicest days when waves are just two to three feet high. They can be faster than an Olympic swimmer and are the third leading cause of weather deaths, killing more people on average than hurricanes, tornadoes, or lightning.\par \par \uc2 A rip current is formed after waves crash on shore and encounter an obstacle in the beach`s topography, including low spots and sandbars or around piers on the way back out to the open water.\par \par \uc2 The fast moving channels can flow as quickly as eight feet per second and account for 80 percent of lifeguard rescues on the beach.\par \par \uc2 ANAYA GORNER (Lifeguard): Rip currents are oftentimes one of the main reasons why we`re going in for rescues.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It`s lifeguard Anaya Gorner`s (sp?) fifth summer watching over this coastline.\par \par \uc2 It`s absolutely gorgeous around us right now.\par \par \uc2 ANAYA GORNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But a rip current could pop up even now.\par \par \uc2 ANAYA GORNER: Absolutely. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So oftentimes, people think that recurrent only pop up during crazy storms, but even a day like this today where the water is pretty flat, rip currents can be anywhere.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: How do you spot one?\par \par \uc2 ANAYA GORNER: So it`s actually quite simple.\par \par \uc2 Oftentimes, you`re able to see a swirling in the water and oftentimes a discoloration or like a murkiness to the water. You will oftentimes see that there aren`t any waves, particularly during a day where there are waves.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Then it was time to get in the water for Gorner to show us how to escape danger firsthand.\par \par \uc2 You showed us how to spot a rip current.\par \par \uc2 ANAYA GORNER: Right.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: What do you do if you`re actually in one?\par \par \uc2 ANAYA GORNER: Now, of your air rip current, the number one thing to do is remain calm. Rip currents are essentially channels of really quick moving water that will push you out to sea.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re in when you want to remain calm, let the water take you back.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: So if we were in a rip current right now, what would we do?\par \par \uc2 ANAYA GORNER: The number one thing you do is first wave to the lifeguard just like this, and then you want to swim parallel to shore.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Tips so a day in the ocean really can be a walk on the beach.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Also, good to remember that not all rip currents are the same size and can range between 50 to 100 feet wide.\par \par \uc2 So if you are caught in a rip current, make sure to keep swimming parallel to shore until you stop feeling the pull of the water. And Nate, always remember also to swim near a lifeguard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Helpful tips. Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Vlad will be talking with "Bon Appetit`s" editor-in-chief, Jamila Robinson. She`s going to reveal the list of the best new bars in America. Can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, first on CBS MORNINGS, "Bon Appetit" is revealing their list of the best new bars in the country.\par \par \uc2 The 11 bars all offer a unique experience and creative cocktails. They include an Equipment Room, a bar and underground listening room full of vinyl records in Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Nine Bar, a Chicago speakeasy that you get to by going through a Chinese takeout restaurant -- I love this.\par \par \uc2 And right here in New York, Superbueno, a Mexican American spot that is in the heart of Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 "Bon Appetit" editor-in-chief, Jamila Robinson joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Jamila, what is up?\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON ("Bon Appetit" editor-in-chief): Oh, I`m so happy to be here and thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 We`re so excited about these 11 new bars. Some of them have only been open a year, maybe 18 months, but what is happening is that we`re seeing that bars really are places for more than just cocktails. They are cultural experiences, they are destinations, and we`re seeing so much is happening in bars that even now -- and what`s really important is that even going to a bar doesn`t even mean you are consuming alcohol anymore because there are so many non-alcoholics. We`re really excited about this list.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so let`s get into it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a twist -- I mean, these are incredibly looking -- like they look amazing, so I`m down for it. We`ve got a take on a traditional vodka soda.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: Yes, so these are from Superbueno right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: This is a really, really important bar with all Mexican flavors. You`ve got so many ingredients.\par \par \uc2 Guava, it`s a take on a traditional vodka soda, but you`ve got all of these Mexican ingredients.\par \par \uc2 And so --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Asante.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: Asante. You get a little bit of that, that salt and that spice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is good. The salt is perfectly balanced with the sort of sweet tangy flavor.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: And this is what`s happening with cocktails where you are getting all of these different flavors, big blocks of ice.\par \par \uc2 But I want to draw your attention to the michelada.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is a take on a michelada.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But this is called?\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: This is michelaNA-- michelaNA for NA, for non-alcoholic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Non-alcoholic.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: So there are spirits in here and that is something that we`re seeing across bars across the US.\par \par \uc2 Zero Proof is one of our bars on the list. But it is so important that, you know maybe you don`t want to be consuming alcohol. So, so many bars are using different kinds of ingredients -- shrubs, herbs, and different kinds of spirits to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want to try it. I want to try it. Let me just get to it.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: This one has costeno peppers, and it`s got a little bit of chili, some kiwi in it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nice. That`s nice.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: These are really special cocktails.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So makes this cocktail?\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: Well, these are -- this is also from --these are from Superbueno.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: These are both from Superbueno, head bartender, Kip Moffitt, who made these cocktails here for us.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: Made them for us this morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This morning.\par \par \uc2 All right, so this trend that you`re seeing, these non-alcoholic cocktails, one of the things we were talking about is price. Because people imagine, if I`m getting a Shirley Temple, I`m not paying a Manhattan price.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: Well, sure. Yes, actually, you`re not getting a Shirley Temple.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the point.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: What you`re getting is a new invention of a beverage. It might include fermentation, it might have different kinds of shrubs and grubs. They might have -- they are inventing a whole new beverage. They might be using all kinds of technology.\par \par \uc2 So it might cost more, it may not be less than your craft cocktail.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To that end, you a non-alcoholic bar from Los Angeles on the list. Tell me about that.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: That is called Stay Zero Proof. And even though the zero proof movement has been happening for years and years, this is actually the first fully non-alcoholic bar in in LA.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: So it`s a really, really special bar where you`re going to be getting really inventive cocktails.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me talk to you about the spot for music lovers real quick before we go. What is this spot?\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: Oh my gosh, this is so -- this is such a -- if you me think about like a Japanese bar where you`re going to also have jazz, they are going to be spinning viral. You`re going to be sipping cocktails. You`re going to have A size and B size of cocktails, so exciting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so we are going to drink these backstage.\par \par \uc2 JAMILA ROBINSON: We`re going to fill up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are going to have Gayle try one of these, these non- alcoholic cocktails because she doesn`t drink alcohol.\par \par \uc2 Jamila Robinson, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 For the full list of "Bon Appetit`s" best bars in the United States, just head over to their website.\par \par \uc2 Sending it back to you, Nate. I know you`re ready to down one of these because that`s what you do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. Gayle might not drink, but you know me, you, and Tony can drink for her.\par \par \uc2 All right, in case you missed it on CBS MORNINGS deals, we showed you an electric toothbrush bundle from AquaSonic. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER (Lifestyle expert): And you know when you leave the dentist office how your teeth feels so clean.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You love that feeling. It`s all about having the right tools, so they recommend having --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: -- an electric toothbrush. This is from AquaSonic. It has 50,000 vibrations per minute so check this out. We`re going to turn it on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, oh.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Those 50 -- look at the bubbles. Now you go ahead and do the manual. What`s happening there?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You`re not getting anything. So these bubbles that you`re seeing, well this -- what this represents, the 50,000 vibrations per minute is that you`re going to decrease the plaque in your mouth by up to 21 percent.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to decrease you`re gingivitis --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, with our exclusive discount. You can get the toothbrush bundles starting at $49.99. That`s 37 percent off.\par \par \uc2 To shop for these and other items, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com and make sure you follow \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to our most popular deals.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, we have top of the table.\par \par \uc2 But first, your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It is time to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Gayle had to skip out early because you know, she`s always working, but the boys are back in town.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Boys club.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve got this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That should have been the intro song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bunch of good dudes. Boys back in town.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Boys back in town.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I had some of those drinks from the best new bars.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t get a lot of sleep last night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, so this is about to be good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know how this is going to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s go baby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know why so many Italians are named Tony?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because they got on a boat that said "To NY."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really? Is that a true story?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It might be. It might be a true story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s pretty good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s who my --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what happens when the boys are running things.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s how my great grandfather got here and some American Italians might be going back. Any American in fact, because there`s an amazing new initiative in Italy, pay attention people, this is real.\par \par \uc2 The region of Tuscany is looking for anyone really who wants to move there and buy a home. They`re offering up to $32,000.00 to help defray the cost of renovations if you buy a home in any one of 76 small towns, some are on your screen right now, that have suffered a population loss in the last few decades. So they`re trying to rebuild the region.\par \par \uc2 There are some conditions and again, this is serious. I think some people will really be thinking about this. You get up to 32K for the renovation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have to commit to a tenure residency and this has to be your permanent home. You can`t really be living in another place and taking your vacation over there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh really? It can`t be your vacation home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It cannot be your -- it has to be your primary residence.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because they are seriously trying to repopulate the area.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And these are small towns with 5,000 or fewer residences.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Residents. See, I told you, I had a couple of drinks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Residents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But so --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People have been hearing about this online. So, this is a real thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have until July 27th, people. To apply, go to the Tuscany region website.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For real.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People really do retire to Tuscany, to the south of France. I know a lot of British expats do. I mean, so this is really appealing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If you`re a retiree. I think it`s hard if you because I was thinking I`d do it as a vacation place for --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One catch is, I looked at some real estate in the area and just now to prepare for this and there was one farmhouse listed as a ruin and they were asking 230,000 euros. So, hey, you`ve got to -- you still have got to -- you still have got to pony up some dough.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, I see.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So it is not 32,000 all in, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, okay, so my "Talk of the Table" is so-called Modern Day Hippie Movement, who will not be letting it all hang out next week.\par \par \uc2 The Rainbow Family of Living Light began gathering at California`s Plumas National Forest in 1972 for its annual event, usually five to ten thousand free spirits would come together during the first week of July to pray, meditate, and focus on world peace.\par \par \uc2 This year, the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2  has ordered the group to vacate, saying it would overwhelm the area and its natural environment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I always -- I kind of side with hippies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I do, too, man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know, I am a --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m a hippie deep down.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me, too. I feel like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel like we all are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- at heart.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We all are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You don`t feel like a hippie?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, he said him, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s just a little weird for three guys in a tie to be like, you know, I really identify with the hippie movement.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, that`s why we get along.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The ideals and the principles of the hippie movement, I can get behind it, man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let them do their thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Peace and love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If they are not bothering anybody.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, what have you got, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, my "Talk of the Table" is our series called "Mixtape Moments." That`s when we take turns sharing a song from an emerging artist.\par \par \uc2 I`m up today and I want to introduce you all to country artists, Dasha. The 24-year-old musician started performing at local coffee shops in California just 10 -- when she was just 10 years old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her viral single, I love this song, it is called "Austin (Boots Stop Workin`)" peaked at number 28 on the Billboard Hot 100 charts. The musician even choreographed her own line dance for the song, which has taken TikTok by storm. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Austin"/Dasha/Warner Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Dasha says the song was written out of a lot of rage after her heartbreak. It`s written about a woman who is planning on running away with the guy when she gets to where they`re supposed to meet up, my man is gone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s gone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s gone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So she`s mad as hell and that`s what the song is about. I love it. The lyrics are amazing.\par \par \uc2 Check out more of Dasha and other songs and artists we feature right here on our \uc2 Spotify\uc2  playlist. Just search for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Mixtape on \uc2 Spotify\uc2  or scan the QR code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of music, don`t miss out on your chance to be featured in our "Mixtape Moments" series.\par \par \uc2 All right, so we just launched the \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Mixtape Music Competition. Now if you are a singer or a band, you can create an original and upbeat cover up Johnny Nash`s iconic song "I Can See Clearly Now." Now go to mixtape.CBSNews.com to submit your version of the song and find the rules and details of the contest there. It`s pretty simple.\par \par \uc2 The grand prize winner, check this out: You will travel to New York to record the cover and be featured in our national show`s promotional campaign.\par \par \uc2 Now get your entry in before the deadline on July 9th and maybe you will pull up in New York and record their version of this song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is going to change someone`s life. This is a big change kind of moment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A hundred percent. Hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: First of all, it`s an iconic song and I love interpretations. I love covers of iconic songs. And you get to come here and perform it right here in New York City for a national audience of millions of people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s huge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Big deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaking of that national audience, right now, ahead, our series "Note to Self" shares an inspiring message from a woman with a famous voice. It`s a message to her 18-year-old self. I love this series of ours, but first time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On this International Pride Day, we are celebrating the LGBTQ community with the story of someone using her voice to share who she is, and you I don`t know the name, Bernie Wagenblast, but for millions of New Yorkers and visitors to the city, her city -- her voice, rather is famous.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BERNIE WAGENBLAST (Transgender woman): There is a Local 2 train to Flatbush Avenue-Brooklyn College approaching the station.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s Wagenblast. And Wagenblast is also a transgender woman, and for our series "Note to Self," she writes to her 18-year-old self, someone who had not yet transitioned and she writes from where else? The New York City Transit Museum.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BERNIE WAGENBLAST: Dear Bernie, you are about to graduate high school.\par \par \uc2 The dream you`ve had since fifth grade of being on the radio is going to come true in less than a year. An adventure lies before you, but a secret is holding you back.\par \par \uc2 Radio is your passion, but it will also distract you from that constant desire you`ve had from your earliest memories: The wish you had been born a girl.\par \par \uc2 I know why you`ve kept this part of you hidden all these years. You`re afraid what sharing it will mean. It seems as if there is no way you can live what you see as a normal life.\par \par \uc2 Your career in broadcasting would never get off the ground, and you fear you`d be condemned to a life of loneliness.\par \par \uc2 In just a few years, you will start to share who you really are, but in very limited ways. The first person you will tell will be a college girlfriend. But shortly afterward, she will break up with you, confirming your deepest fears.\par \par \uc2 The next person will be the woman you marry. You feel you must tell her your secret before you ask her to spend the rest of her life with you. It`s the most terrifying conversation you`ve ever had.\par \par \uc2 You`re almost sure she will break off the relationship, but after you shed many tears, she will tell you she still loves you and that you can stay together, as long as this remains a secret between the two of you.\par \par \uc2 I know it seems impossible from your vantage point in the mid-1970s to believe this, but the world will change and people like you will become more visible and accepted.\par \par \uc2 There will be stars on television and in films. They will be elected to office or play sports. They will also be everyday people doing everyday jobs. You will become friends with many of these people.\par \par \uc2 A new word will be used to describe people like us: Transgender.\par \par \uc2 I know you`ve often asked yourself and God, why you have this burden? What purpose does it serve? It will take decades for you to get an answer to that question, but it will finally be revealed to you. You will be able to use who you are as an example to help others.\par \par \uc2 In the years ahead, you will meet and talk with other transgender people. They will help you not feel so alone. They will help you better understand yourself. Listen and learn from them.\par \par \uc2 Your sense of humor will also help you handle your emotions. You`ll find what`s funny in everyday life and that will allow you to make yourself and others smile.\par \par \uc2 You will also see how the universe seems to have a sense of humor. For example, you`ll discover that due to a typo on your birth certificate, you`ve always been registered as a female. You don`t know it yet, but there`s this thing called a podcast. Consider it a modern form of radio that will allow you to do interviews and share it worldwide. It will help your voice reach so many more people, to share your story, and let others know they are not alone.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 BERNIE WAGENBLAST: One day, millions of people will know your voice.\par \par \uc2 . going through Queens. I am Bernie Wagenblast --\par \par \uc2 BERNIE WAGENBLAST: It will be used in places that you can`t even imagine.\par \par \uc2 There is a Local 3 train to New Lots Avenue. Approaching the station. Please stand away from the platform edge.\par \par \uc2 It won`t be until 2017 that you finally take steps to live on the outside how you`ve always felt on the inside. You`ll see others who have transitioned.\par \par \uc2 A final push will come when a friend helps you to attend a support group event as your true self. Your fear of being a laughingstock will disappear and you`ll realize you can do this.\par \par \uc2 When you do transition, it will bring great happiness, but it will also cause pain. Your four decades` long marriage will come to an end.\par \par \uc2 However, many friends, neighbors and colleagues will support you. You`ll return to your high school and tell a group that includes a former teacher and current students who you are.\par \par \uc2 Believe it or not, they will applaud and congratulate you.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in your life, you will be comfortable in your own skin.\par \par \uc2 Throughout life, you`ve had to deal with bullies. When hatred makes you upset, think of all you`ve accomplished in life and the good you are doing to help others.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the only person`s actions you are responsible for are your own.\par \par \uc2 If there is one final bit of advice that I can offer, it is to trust yourself. If you are true to yourself and care for others, you won`t go wrong. You will make mistakes and have trials along the way, but they will teach you never give up hope.\par \par \uc2 You will eventually live that life you`ve always hoped for and you will thrive.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll call that one a note to self-acceptance.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so Vlad, what have you got going next week?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m on a staycation next week. Marian and I are taking some time off. We`re not going anywhere. We`re just trying to get our brand new Brooklyn apartment up to speed, up to snuff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up to snuff.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I can`t deal with mess, man. And my daughter, Celine is a little like me. I was telling Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She sees even a little piece of garbage, she is like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little OCD.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Garbage.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A little OCD.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a lot on the street.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy International Pride as we close out the month.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Happy Pride.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Pride.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That does it for us. We will see you Monday on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, let`s take a look back at everything that mattered this week. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I am going to continue to move until we get to a total ban on the total initiative relative to what we`re going to do with --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Last night`s presidential debate, for a lot of people, it was very tough to watch. I keep hearing words like painful, trainwreck, epic fail.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I really don`t know what he said at the end of that sentence. I don`t think he knows what he said either.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Breaking flooding that has ravaged parts of the Midwest. It is now being blamed for at least two deaths.\par \par \uc2 JIM PROUTY (Union County sheriff): To the families, the homeowners. This is their lives. I mean, this is starting over from scratch.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Supreme Court allows hospitals in Idaho to provide abortions and emergencies to protect a woman`s health.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bon jour. Last night, at the NBA Draft, there was a particular theme. The second year in a row that a French player was drafted number one. Two more Frenchmen went in the top six picks.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Josh Naylor whacks one down the line and waved out. Yes, and caught by the one guy up there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yep. Drink and a phone in his other hand and he still makes the catch.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love the gladiator like, "Are you not entertained."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I love that.\par \par \uc2 The winner of the world`s ugliest dog contest was just crowned. Congrats to Wild Thang. It is a face only a mother could love and a windswept dude that would make Fabio very, very jealous.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New York Liberty mascot, Ellie the Elephant channels her inner Beyonce for a show stopping halftime performance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is so cool. I love it.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : That`s a rump shake.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You guys should --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Geselle was trying to hide from us. It`s her birthday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s her birthday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s her birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a shot of her boyfriend here with their dog, Harry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am still thinking about Geselle`s reaction on her birthday. Is she wanted by the law?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s hiding.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There she is. Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Take it away, Vlad. There we go. Night Fever.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Quiet Place: Day One"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The highly anticipated film, it is called "A Quiet Place: Day One."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We don`t know a lot about these creatures that are hunting humanity.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WOLFF ("A Quiet Place: Day One" actor): Once you get to know them, they`re very sweet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People are saying the cat, Frodo is the MVP along with all of you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you a cat person by nature?\par \par \uc2 LUPITA NYONG`O ("A Quiet Place: Day One" actor): I`ve been terrified of cats my entire life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Terrified.\par \par \uc2 LUPITA NYONG`O: Oh, terrified. Well, I just found them suspicious. You know, they are like little lions.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God. Oh my God, it`s Travis. Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Taylor Swift --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- kicked off her cool summer with a special guest in London. There he is. Travis Kelce.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He said he replaced one of the other backup dancers because there`s one that`s very, very hot. His name Jan Ravnik.\par \par \uc2 The reason I know this is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How do you know this?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Guys, when we went -- we went to the show in Paris. Katy noticed one of the dancers and then I noticed her --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Katy noticed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Katy noticed one of the dancers. She was like --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So when I got -- when I got my phone back and I thought I would take was some family photos on there and there was a lot of tights.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then we Googled hot backup dancer of Eras Tour and Jan came up so I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jan must have been sick if Travis was up there.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240628ek6s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for June 27, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nancy Cordes, Jan Crawford, Robert Costa, Jo Ling  Kent, Suzanne Le Mignot, Kris Van Cleave, Elizabeth Palmer, Omar  Villafranca, Major Garrett \par \uc2 3,783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the historic rematch. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  arrive in Atlanta, set to meet on the debate stage. The candidates trade blows ahead of the high-stake showdown.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): You`re fired, Joe. Get out of here, Joe.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The threat that Trump poses is greater in his second term than his first.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, our new reporting context and analysis. The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 The debate that could mark a turning point in the race for president just five months from Election Day. Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell in New York tonight and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Millions are expected to tune in for the matchup between President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the two meeting in the earliest presidential debate in history. It is a chance for voters to hear the candidates vastly different visions for the future for America, and the contrast between the two could not be more stark in both policies and personalities. The one thing they have in common, however, is age.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is 81 years old and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is 78, making them the oldest candidates to ever meet on the debate stage. And you can bet that will be a topic. We have team coverage starting with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): President Biden was embraced by supporters after touching down in Atlanta this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Four more years. Four more years.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Former President Trump got a hero`s welcome, too, when he arrived a couple of hours later. The two men meet face-to-face tonight for the first time since their second presidential debate in 2020, nearly four years ago.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from October 2020)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : If I thought you did a good job, I would have never run.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You know his character. You know my character.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In a preview of the notes they hope to hit tonight, both candidates launched new ad blitzes today, Biden knocking Trump on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Biden campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Two years ago, Trump overturned Roe v. Wade. Because of abortion bans across the country, women`s lives are at risk.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump knocking Biden on age.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Do you think the guy who was defeated by the stairs, makes it four more years in the White House?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Going in to this debate of the seven battleground states, six are currently rated as toss ups, while one, North Carolina, leans Republican.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE ALVAREZ: He`s relaxed. He`s confident.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Danielle Alvarez is a senior advisor on the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE ALVAREZ: We expect President Trump to win because he`s going to focus on record, his record.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: One new feature tonight, a pair of green lights on each candidate`s podium that will alert him when his microphone is on. The mic will shut off when it is the other man`s turn to speak.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 2020)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The question is --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : (Inaudible) Supreme Court Justices. Radical left.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The question -- will you shut up, man?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Who is your -- listen, who is --\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It comes after Trump interrupted Biden or the moderator more than a hundred times in their first debate in 2020.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Advisor): This is who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Biden advisor, Cedric Richmond, was part of the team that prepped with him at Camp David for the better part of a week.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND: We just wanted to make sure that he was prepared for the antics of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and hearing lie after lie after lie and making sure that he is talking to the American people and make sure that they understand that he wakes up every day focused on them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: One open question tonight, whether these two men who have made no secret of their mutual dislike, will shake hands when they walk on stage. They didn`t in 2020, but that was due to COVID protocols at the height of the pandemic, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes in Atlanta for us. Thank you, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 Also in Atlanta, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Chief Election Campaign Correspondent, Robert Costa, who has been speaking with both campaigns.\par \par \uc2 And Robert, President Biden hold up for a week at Camp David. What does he need and want to accomplish tonight?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Great to be with you, Norah. For President Biden, according to conversations with his top advisors, it is not about so much addressing the age question, it is about the issue of the economy. Can he really broadcast tonight that he`s on the side of working people, union members? And it`s about bringing up the issue of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump`s character, look for him to talk about January 6th, talk about Trump being now a felon based on that conviction in New York and reminding voters that there is a character issue in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 For Trump, I`m told it`s about focus. Yes, he has his grievances, but it`s also about the economy and immigration for him.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s right. We`ve never had a twice-impeached, convicted felon running for president. What about Trump? How has he been preparing?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: I`ve been told that he`s been deliberating, Norah, behind the scenes about whether to come out early with a lot of energy and, rhetorically speaking, take a punch. But look for him tonight to start -- at least start on low simmer, because he believes he was perhaps too aggressive in 2020, interrupting then-candidate Biden and paid a political cost with voters who like a little bit more of a conversation, civility, when it comes to these debates. But we`ll see if he can do that for 90 minutes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. We`ll see if there is a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on low simmer. Robert Costa, I know you`ll be watching with us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The battle over women`s reproductive rights is taking center stage at tonight`s debate. And today, just over two years after overturning Roe versus Wade, the Supreme Court cleared the way for Idaho hospitals to provide emergency abortions, at least for now. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): A day after a draft opinion became public, the Supreme Court made it official, ruling that emergency abortions when a woman`s health is at risk, could resume in Idaho, at least for the time being. An unusual coalition of conservative and liberal justices joined to dismiss the case and let it play out in lower courts.\par \par \uc2 Idaho is one of six states with restrictive laws that do not include any exceptions for a woman`s health beyond saving her life. And Idaho doctors can be charged with a felony if they violate it. Since the law was enacted two years ago, one-fifth of the state`s practicing obstetricians have left Idaho.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA KROLL (President, Idaho College of Emergency Physicians): To have our kind of flip-flopping of how we practice is very hard for us. And I want to make it very clear. This doesn`t mean, oh, you`re going to get fined if you do this or that. It`s five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Biden administration had sued Idaho to expand access, pointing to a federal law requiring hospitals to provide stabilizing care in health emergencies, care that could include abortion.\par \par \uc2 Also today, the justices threw out a multibillion-dollar bankruptcy settlement with the manufacturer of OxyContin that would have provided $6 billion for treatment and programs to combat the opioid crisis, including $750 million to victims and their families in exchange for shielding members of the Sackler family from future lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from December 2023)\par \par \uc2 ELLEN ISAACS: And they need to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Ellen Isaacs, whose son died of an overdose, said the deal left the Sacklers off the hook.\par \par \uc2 ELLEN ISAACS: Give me the ability to get a jury by trial of my peers.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: She may now get that day in court after the 5-4 decision that saw conservatives and liberal Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  joining to reject the plan shielding the Sacklers.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, the court will release more opinions tomorrow and again next week as it looks to wrap up its session before the 4th of July holiday. We are, of course, still waiting on those two major cases involving former President Trump, whether he can be prosecuted for his actions around January 6th, and if so, for what. Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Very important. Jan Crawford, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 One of the top issues heading into the debate tonight is the economy. While unemployment is down and wages are up, so are mortgage rates and the price of groceries due to stubborn inflation, although they`re starting to go down.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Senior Business and Tech Correspondent, Jo Ling Kent spoke with voters about what they hope to hear from the candidates themselves.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I want to keep building the strongest economy in the world.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Under Biden, the economy is in ruins.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): What the candidates have to say on the economy is vital. Watching in Minnesota will be two working moms, both independent voters, who are split on how they feel.\par \par \uc2 Are you better off now than when President Biden took office?\par \par \uc2 MADDIE WALLACE (Minnesota Voter): No, we can`t sit down to dinner as a family without it costing a small fortune.\par \par \uc2 LOAN HUYNH (Minnesota Voter): I would say that I`m better off now. It`s not that much better, but definitely economically as a family, we are doing well.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Loan Huynh plans to vote for President Biden again in November.\par \par \uc2 Maddie Wallace, who voted for Trump in 2020 and Obama in 2008, says she`s still undecided.\par \par \uc2 MADDIE WALLACE: I don`t know that I want to repeat of the last four years. I also don`t know that I want to repeat of those four years before that.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: CBS News polling found young voters overwhelmingly feel it`s now harder to buy a house, raise a family, get a good job or start a business. And despite a strong jobs market and low unemployment, both Minnesotans agree prices have gone up under President Biden.\par \par \uc2 MADDIE WALLACE: The average American isn`t looking at economic data. They`re making the decisions based on, oh, my gosh, I can`t afford groceries.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: From 2019, when Trump was in office until now, the price of a dozen eggs has nearly doubled, a loaf of bread costs 53 percent more and gas prices have jumped 32 percent. Tonight, voters will be listening closely.\par \par \uc2 LOAN HUYNH: We have to ultimately make a decision as to who we want to be as a country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Another tough reality, housing prices have risen more than wages. And with just one interest rate cut expected this year, new mortgages are still too expensive for so many people. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s a good point, Jo. Let`s also talk about that big news today from \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 . The pharmacy giant plans to close a significant number of stores, its stock dropping 22 percent. What does that mean for consumers?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Well, Norah, look, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  CEO saying that they`re losing money due to higher prices in their stores. Profits are also reportedly down on its diabetes and weight loss drugs. Customers can expect to see more discounts soon and store closures for underperforming locations. That could total more than 2,000 stores. Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Jo Ling Kent, thank you. And we will have complete coverage of the first presidential debate hosted by CNN tonight, starting at 8 p.m. Eastern on our streaming network, CBS News 24/7. The live simulcast of the debate will be right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  at 9 p.m. Eastern, followed by our expert analysis, and we hope that you can join us.\par \par \uc2 Now, a look at tonight`s other top stories, starting with a frightening freight train derailment outside of Chicago. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Suzanne Le Mignot has new details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE LE MIGNOT (voiceover): No injuries have been reported after 25 rail cars derailed. Norah, I`m on Main Street, and this leads right to the train derailment. We`ve been seeing a lot of heavy equipment being moved in to help remove those mangled cars.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, about 300 people had been evacuated as a precaution. The train`s owner, \uc2 Canadian National Railway Company\uc2  says the cars were carrying a residual amount of propane.\par \par \uc2 At this time, there`s no danger to public safety and residents are being told they can return home. And tonight, the cause of the accident remains under investigation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And tonight, there`s new trouble for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . The aircraft maker slapped with sanctions from federal safety officials. The aerospace giant is accused of misconduct in the investigation into how that door panel came off an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  plane mid-flight in January. Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Norah, we`re inside the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737 MAX factory. It`s rare access, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  says at each step along this production line, they`ve made changes to improve safety and quality.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): But the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says during this media tour, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  shared non-public details about the door panel blowout, violating the investigation`s rules.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH LUND (Senior Vice President of Quality, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 ): We know the move crew closed the plug. They did not reinstall the retaining pins. That is not their job.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is now cutting off \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s access to investigative information and plans to subpoena company officials for an August hearing. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  says it deeply regrets comments intended to make clear our responsibility in the accident overstepped the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 `s role.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now overseas to Iran, where the country will vote for its next president on Friday. The election comes about a month after the country`s last leader died in a helicopter crash. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Liz Palmer reports now from Tehran.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Sixty-one million Iranians are eligible to vote here tomorrow, Norah. The real question is how many of them will actually turn out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: There are four presidential candidates, three hardliners and one moderate. But all of them were approved by a panel of Islamic clerics. Government loyalists don`t mind this stacked deck and they will vote. But there are millions who have had it with hardship brought on by sanctions and a system that tolerates corruption and repression. Those people are likely to stay home in droves.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And tonight, 70 million Americans are facing a heat wave. That includes \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca who`s in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The heat dome is baking parts of the South. And here in Plano, Texas, people are flocking to pools and splash pads to try to cool off as the feels like temperature hits 107 to 111.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Cities from Atlanta to Dallas could see temperatures in the triple digits heading into the weekend. More rain is in the forecast for areas around the edges of the heat dome, including the flooded states in the upper Midwest where more severe thunderstorms are ahead.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And thanks to our CBS News teams from around the globe. Eye on America is next with advertisers looking to mend the political divide in their outreach to consumers.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So far, the 2024 campaign has been simmering with anger and contempt. And at tonight`s presidential debate, it`s likely to reach a full boil. In tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Major Garrett looks at the race for the White House through a different lens, not Pennsylvania Avenue, but Madison Avenue.\par \par \uc2 The advertising world is betting that more Americans actually favor unity and compromise than division.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Everything \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  touches turns to (expletive) --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : That other guy, that loser.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT (voiceover): The state of our politics inflamed, vulgar, antagonistic.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE SCHMELZER (Johnsonville Marketing Executive): Anger and outrage are biologically addictive.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Jamie Schmelzer is not a mental health professional. Schmelzer sells sausage as a marketing executive for meat processing giant Johnsonville.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE SCHMELZER: We do not pretend that we have what it takes to save America. We know the world is full of serious problems that sausage can`t solve.\par \par \uc2 We couldn`t keep up.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: But Schmelzer and Johnsonville believe they`re onto something.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Johnsonville commercial)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The company`s new ad campaign begs Americans to turn down the temperature, to find common ground and chill.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Johnsonville commercial)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Is this campaign political?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE SCHMELZER: We think this campaign is cultural more than political. But we also fully recognize that those two things have become kind of inseparable.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Johnsonville had a hunch the country was on edge. It took a stab at something politicians do, polling, and found that eight out of 10 Americans are exhausted by the anger and negativity in the country, and that many Americans are getting together less than they used to.\par \par \uc2 Is isolation good for your product?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE SCHMELZER: No. So Johnsonville makes hangout food. We like to say that sausage, for one, almost doesn`t exist.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And it`s not just Johnsonville preaching calm and togetherness.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Miller Lite ad)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You can find similar echoes in Miller Lite and \uc2 La-Z-Boy\uc2 `s ad campaigns.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from \uc2 La-Z-Boy\uc2  ad)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Selling less fighting and more relaxation.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANDREW COHEN: They`re paying attention to our own thoughts as a society.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Andrew Cohen is a cultural sociologist who specializes in advertising.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANDREW COHEN: It`s a great place for these brands to play of saying, you know, we can`t deny the reality that people are heated. That it`s really hard to go to your family`s cookout and not get in a fight with your uncle over some political views that you don`t agree about.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We can find --\par \par \uc2 KRIS STUBBS (Georgia): Common ground.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We traveled to two battleground states, Georgia and Wisconsin, to show voters the ads and ask how they view the country.\par \par \uc2 KRIS STUBBS: Everyone in the country feels a little on edge.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAINE WILLIAMS (Cobb County, Georgia): Everybody`s anxiety and emotions are high and we just need to chill.\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE REAGLES (Kenosha, Wisconsin): People seem to isolate themselves too much. Isolation, it`s a bad thing, you know?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That matches Johnsonville`s research and recent warnings from the Surgeon General about the quote, "epidemic of loneliness" and the toxicity of social media.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It`s a lot of drama everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Miller Lite ad)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The advertisers hope that bridge building and slowing down is a message that sells to an anxious nation in short supply of unity.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Laughter, our shared language again.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE SCHMELZER: It`s kind of a pep talk for America to remember to just make time, take a break and have some fun with some people that you like.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: When marketing wades into deep waters of national anxiety and estrangement, something has shifted. Something our summer of political strife is unlikely to settle. For Eye on America, I`m Major Garrett in Johnsonville, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sausage and bacon, these are things that can bring us together.\par \par \uc2 All right. The \uc2 NBA\uc2  draft makes history, setting the stage for the league`s first father-son duo.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news tonight, \uc2 NBA\uc2  history. The \uc2 Los Angeles Lakers\uc2  have selected Bronny James in the second round of the \uc2 NBA\uc2  draft. He`s the 19-year-old son of basketball legend LeBron James.\par \par \uc2 This set the -- sets the stage for the first father-son duo playing together in the \uc2 NBA\uc2 . Bronny James was sidelined for nearly five months last year after going into cardiac arrest while practicing with the USC Trojans.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America, takes us to where Pride Month began. Tomorrow marks the 55th anniversary of the Stonewall riots, a six-day uprising that started after the \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2  raided the Stonewall Inn in 1969. The riots marked a turning point for the gay rights movement in the United States.\par \par \uc2 That`s why current owners, Stacy Lentz and Kurt Kelly started the Stonewall Inn Gives Back Initiative, a nonprofit that`s committed to creating safe spaces for the LGBTQ community around the world.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STACY LENTZ (The Stonewall Inn Owner): We continue the fight that started here at the Stonewall Inn to make sure that we`re funding and amplifying the voices of marginalized community members that live in those places where it`s still tough to be LGBTQ plus.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Stacy Lentz and Kurt Kelly, they are tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell in New York and we`ll see you later for CBS News` special coverage of the presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240628ek6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for June 24, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Kati Weis, Cristian Benavides, Lilia Luciano,  Imtiaz Tyab, Nancy Cordes, Mark Strassmann, Vanessa Murdock, Jonah Kaplan,  Carter Evans \par \uc2 3,854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The structural integrity of the dam has been in question for a long time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: the new threat in the Midwest, a major dam at risk of immediate failure after catastrophic flooding hits the region.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The devastation is severe, and it`s widespread.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The cleanup tonight with millions under flood alerts and millions more facing excessive heat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALEX WALLACE: Ooh, it has been hot times, and more areas are going to feel that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Deadly and destructive floodwaters ravage Iowa, Minnesota and South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans are under threat, as rivers continue to rise, with some reaching record levels. Hundreds of homeowners have fled to higher ground, as authorities brace for even more rain. The record flooding has reached the rooftops of buildings, cut off entire neighborhoods and shut down roads and major highways.\par \par \uc2 And new tonight, authorities are warning that a key dam in Minnesota is in danger of an imminent failure. That is a breach that could lead to a mass evacuation, now, all this as dangerous heat bakes more than a dozen states, where it will feel like 110 in several cities.\par \par \uc2 With all that news, we have team coverage of the wild weather.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonah Kaplan will start us off from a rain-soaked Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN (voiceover): In farm country, rain is usually a blessing, but not like this.\par \par \uc2 SARAH FRY (Flood Victim): Where do you start?\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Jeremy and Sarah Fry (sp?) took us inside their home as they searched for anything they could salvage.\par \par \uc2 Which part is so distressing?\par \par \uc2 SARAH FRY: The memories, our marriage license.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY FRY (Flood Victim): The marriage license. Our photo albums are down in our storage room.\par \par \uc2 SARAH FRY: Photos, everything downstairs.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY FRY: Items can be replaced, but not that stuff.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: The Little Sioux River is cresting nearly six feet above its previous record.\par \par \uc2 This neighborhood in Spencer, it`s about a half-a-mile away from the Little Sioux River, and, already, you can see how high the water was and how much lower it`s gotten. Those cars were fully submerged. Still, the disaster, it`s really only just begun. Most of these folks here, they don`t have flood insurance, including Stephanie Henrich (sp?), who runs a center for sexual assault victims and a food bank.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE HENRICH (Flood Victim): We help people.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE HENRICH: And now, for the first time in a long time, I`m asking for help.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: An hour to the west, in Rock Valley, a levee gave way, another rush of water pouring into this town of 4,000 residents. In Mankato, Minnesota, this 110-year-old dam failed, the state`s governor calling out the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (D-Minnesota): We have about 18 inches of rain over the last couple of weeks. What`s happened is, we have inundated the ground. And I think the term people are using is unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Another term they`re using is united. Back in Spencer, Mark (sp?) and Cindy Hinton (sp?) just lost their home of 35 years.\par \par \uc2 MARK HINTON (Flood Victim): We will get through it. You know, we always do; 48 years, we have been through a lot of things, and we will get through this.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Despite the waters receding -- despite the waters receding here, there are still flood warnings from the National Weather Service for about a dozen waterways.\par \par \uc2 And they all flow into the Mississippi and Missouri rivers, both of which are still rising. And as if that wasn`t enough, there are still risks of storms this week -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jonah Kaplan, thank you for being there.\par \par \uc2 For more on the storms and flooding and where that brutal heat wave is heading next, let`s bring in \uc2 CBS\uc2  New York meteorologist Vanessa Murdock.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Vanessa.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA MURDOCK: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, it is windy here. These were the winds of change. They`re tropical-storm-force winds, but it has brought the temperature and humidity down here in New York City after a four-day heat wave, our weather here today pristine compared to what I know is being dealt with out West.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about it, the unprecedented rain. About a foot-and-a-half of rain has led to the historic flooding. Flood warnings are in effect through tomorrow for the Upper Midwest. In addition, tonight, severe thunderstorms are expected. That is through portions of Wisconsin up toward Minnesota, strong winds, damaging winds, maybe an isolated tornado, hail as well.\par \par \uc2 That severe threat spreads to the South tomorrow. As we check out the heat, it will be extreme for the midsection of the country into the Deep South, heat advisories, extreme heat warnings. It will feel like 109 in Little Rock tomorrow, as hot as 107 in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 Then, for Wednesday, that heat starts migrating back to the East Coast. We just wrapped it up. It`s very brief. D.C. tomorrow will feel as hot as 99 once again -- Norah, back to you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That is hot for June.\par \par \uc2 Vanessa Murdock, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, there are mounting problems at the International Space Station, with today`s scheduled space walk called off at the last minute due to a water leak in one of the astronaut`s space suits, all this as \uc2 NASA\uc2  has again postponed the return of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s Starliner`s first crewed mission to the ISS.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from June 5)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Ignition and liftoff.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Just hours after Starliner`s June 5th launch came the first sign of trouble.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , June 5)\par \par \uc2 MISSION CONTROL: Starliner, looks like we picked up a couple more helium leaks.\par \par \uc2 BUTCH WILMORE (Starliner Test Flight Astronaut): All right, we`re ready to copy and find out exactly what you mean by picked up another helium leak.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The \uc2 Boeing\uc2 -built spacecraft had launched with one small helium leak. Now it has five. Starliner also lost five small maneuvering engines on its final approach to the space station. All but one were recovered. But the glitches convinced \uc2 NASA\uc2  to delay Starliner`s return to Earth.\par \par \uc2 STEVE STICH (Program Manager, \uc2 NASA\uc2  Commercial Crew Program): We want to give our teams a little bit more time to look at the data, do some analysis and make sure we`re really ready to come home.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: CBS News space analyst Bill Harwood:\par \par \uc2 BILL HARWOOD: It`s not like they`re stranded on the space station, but \uc2 NASA\uc2  just wants to get as much information as they can before the crew comes home.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: So far, all these problems have been in Starliner`s service module. But it gets thrown away before the capsule reenters the Earth`s atmosphere, meaning engineers need to examine the faulty hardware while in space.\par \par \uc2 BILL HARWOOD: That`s why they`re making this big effort to get as much data as they can, because that`s what they`re going to have to base their decisions on downstream.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Starliner could stay in space at least a few more weeks before it would have to return to Earth.\par \par \uc2 And until it does, astronauts Butch Wilmore and Suni Williams will be extra hands to help out on the space station -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago today, the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Never before has the court granted and then taken away a widely recognized constitutional right, in this case, the right to an abortion. And the battle over a woman`s reproductive rights is now taking center stage in the election rematch between President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Our bodies, our choice!\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): Protesters returned to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  today, where one decision two years ago has led to a seismic shift in reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Since Roe v. Wade was reversed, nearly two dozen states have either banned or significantly restricted abortions. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hailed the change in a new podcast interview.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from All-In Podcast)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I got that done with the selection of three great justices. I got it done. And it was a big thing.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Today, several of his top V.P. contenders called it a big thing, too. South Carolina Senator Tim Scott said: "Two years ago today, our prayers were answered."\par \par \uc2 And Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  said: "Now America can lead in being a pro- life, pro-family nation."\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): We have seen attacks on IVF.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In Maryland, the current vice president argued a second Trump term would lead to even more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Now, he wants us to believe he will not sign a national abortion ban. Look, enough with the gaslighting.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd cheering)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Two years in, 60 percent of Americans continue to believe that abortion should be legal in all or most cases, according to a brand- new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll. About half of likely voters say abortion will be a major factor for them in November.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Send me back to the White House, and I will fight like hell to restore Roe v. Wade and protect American freedom.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Biden huddled with more than a dozen close advisers at Camp David today, prepping for Thursday`s debate.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: How should I handle him? Should I be tough and nasty?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: After questioning Biden`s vigor for months, Trump is now trying to raise expectations for Biden, even falsely claiming that Biden will take performance-enhancing drugs.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: So, a little before debate time, he gets a shot in the ass. And that`s -- they want to strengthen him up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Late today, Trump went a step further, suggesting he and Biden undergo drug tests on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 I asked the Biden team about that, and they told me Trump is so scared of being held accountable for his toxic agenda that he is now -- quote -- "resorting to desperate, obviously false lies" -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, CBS News will have complete coverage of the first presidential debate hosted by CNN this Thursday, starting at 8:00 p.m. Eastern on our streaming network, CBS News 24/7. Then, the live simulcast of the debate here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  will begin at 9:00 p.m. Eastern, followed by our expert analysis. Hope you join us.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  says the intense phase of the war in Gaza is about to end, though he says the fighting there will continue. Netanyahu also announced Israel is moving troops to its northern border with Lebanon, where the risk of war with the Iranian-backed group \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  increases by the day.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab reports tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voiceover): Flanked by security, Prime Minister Netanyahu said the heaviest fighting against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Rafah was nearly over.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "It doesn`t mean that the war is about to end, he said, "but the war in its intense phase is about to end in Rafah."\par \par \uc2 But when asked about the Israeli proposal President Biden outlined weeks ago for a cease-fire in Gaza and hostage release deal with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , Netanyahu said:\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "Our terms are not to end the war," a statement he later walked back, saying he was in fact committed to the proposal, a proposal the families of hostages are also desperate for.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, three of them, including the parents of American Hersh Goldberg- Polin, allowed for the release of videos of the abductions that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters filmed.\par \par \uc2 But it was Netanyahu`s comments that troops in Gaza would be redeployed to the north that has intensified fears Israel is readying for another major war, this time with the powerful Iranian-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 We traveled to Northern Israel, where many communities are now ghost towns after the Israeli military and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  began trading cross-border fire back in October. Israel`s Defense Minister Yoav Gallant was heckled by protesters as he arrived in Washington for a series of high-level meetings.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now, Gallant`s closed-door talks about a potential war in Lebanon comes as CBS News has learned the U.S. aircraft carrier Eisenhower has left the Red Sea and is now in the Eastern Mediterranean, as concerns about a full-scale conflict breaking out on Israel`s northern front only mount -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Major developments tonight. Thank you, Imtiaz.\par \par \uc2 Now look at tonight`s other top stories, starting with the rising death toll after an attack on churches, a synagogue and police station in the Southern Russian republic of Dagestan.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano reports, at least 20 people were killed and dozens more injured.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): Eyewitness video of one attack shows multiple gunmen firing automatic weapons at a police traffic post and a church in Makhachkala.\par \par \uc2 Another attack was unfolding 75 miles away in the coastal city of Derbent, where assailants shot at parishioners and killed a priest inside an Orthodox church and also fired at a synagogue, before setting it on fire.\par \par \uc2 Investigators are calling it a coordinated terrorist attack, Norah. And, tonight, there`s growing concern over the rise of extremist violence in the region.\par \par \uc2 This new attack comes just three months after gunmen killed 145 people at a concert hall near Moscow, which the Islamic State later claimed responsibility for.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to rough surf and strong rip currents as the summer beach season gets under way. The dangerous conditions led to the deaths of at least seven people in Florida over four days.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Cristian Benavides speaks to those trying to help.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Double red flag warnings are up here in Panama City Beach. No one is supposed to be in the water, and yet take a look behind me. All these folks are in the water. Lifeguards have been going out on patrol trying to warn people that, even though the waters here may look calm, they could be deadly.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voiceover): On beautiful beach days like this --\par \par \uc2 MAN: The water is too dangerous to get in.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: -- it`s an endless effort for lifeguards to keep people out of the water and away from deadly rip currents.\par \par \uc2 DARYL PAUL (Beach Safety Director, Panama City Beach, Florida): I just hope that people are heeding the warnings of the lifeguards and the flag system. Don`t just throw caution to the wind.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Those who do risk not only their lives, but a $500 fine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Colorado is set to ban products that contain toxic chemicals called PFAS.\par \par \uc2 Investigative reporter Kati Weis with CBS News Colorado breaks down the reasons behind the new law.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: Norah, these are some of the products that are included in the new law. Colorado will ban the sales of cookware, outdoor clothing, menstrual products and even dental floss that`s containing toxic PFAS.\par \par \uc2 PFAS are called forever chemicals because they`re extremely durable and great at making things waterproof and nonstick. The problem is, PFAS can build up in the body and can increase the risk of serious health problems, including cancer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Thanks to our CBS News teams from around the globe.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next, with property owners across the country dealing with the growing problem of squatters and the potential solutions.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Property owners across the country are facing never- ending legal battles from squatters, who illegally take over homes. Now at least six states have passed laws to deal with the problem.\par \par \uc2 In the first of our two-part Eye on America look at the issue, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans shows us how some homeowners are not waiting for the new laws to take effect.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: When Jean, an 81-year-old grandma in Idaho, inherited a plot of land in Los Angeles, she thought her financial worries were over, until squatters claimed the property.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff won`t go there and cut the locks for you?\par \par \uc2 JEAN (Grandmother): No, they`re not allowed to. The squatters have rights.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: At least 20 people in a dozen R.V.s took control and barred her from her own land. Neighbors told us people inside were armed, and they weren`t happy to see us.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: You think, if there were some murderers in here, they would enjoy you coming up to the crack and looking through the camera?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Who has more rights here, you, the property owner, or the people living there illegally?\par \par \uc2 JEAN: Oh, I have rights. I get to pay my property taxes and pay for liability insurance.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Over the last three years, she`s spent her savings, about $100,000, to cover legal fees, taxes, and lost rent. She finally found a buyer that will take the property with the squatters, but it`s going to cost her.\par \par \uc2 JEAN: Eight hundred thousand dollars, I dropped the price.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Eight hundred thousand?\par \par \uc2 JEAN: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So you lost almost a million dollars on this?\par \par \uc2 JEAN: Yes, I have.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Squatters` rights go back to the British legal system, the idea, to ensure abandoned or unused property can be put to good use by people who need it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: No comment.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But the good intentions of centuries-hold long --\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: A couple accused of squatting, then killing a woman inside her mother`s apartment.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- have created some modern-day nightmares.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: These squatters have turned this $4.5 million Beverly Hills mansion into a party hub.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: This is the front line, if you will, in the battle to keep squatters out of an abandoned property. This property`s been vacant for four years. It`s an eyesore in the community here.\par \par \uc2 The owner died during COVID, and when no family claimed it, squatters did. See the broken windows there, the graffiti? And someone`s been living in this.\par \par \uc2 TERRI CORTEZ (Neighbor): It`s been a horrible nightmare.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Terri Cortez lives next door and wants the city to tear it down.\par \par \uc2 TERRI CORTEZ: I think the neighbors and I are very scared sometimes of what kind of people come up, and people sometimes come in.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Since law enforcement can`t do much and court battles can take years, others --\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Back up.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- are now stepping in with different approaches.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: It`s not right to not pay your utilities or your rent.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: I was about to move, too until you turned off the lights.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Lando Thomas and Kimrey Kotchick run a company called Squatter Squad.\par \par \uc2 What happened when the owner came to clean the place after the \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  ended?\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: The \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  guest refused to leave.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And that`s it?\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: Yes, that`s it. And Los Angeles says he can stay.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: They break locks squatters install and put up cameras to monitor them 24/7. But, so far, nothing has chased this squatter away.\par \par \uc2 MAN #7: The police`s hands are tied. The sheriff`s hands are tied.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Squatting is a problem for landlords reaching far beyond Southern California. According to a survey from the National Rental Home Council, cities in Texas, Georgia and Florida have more squatters than any other metropolitan area.\par \par \uc2 MAN #7: Knock, knock.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But there are solutions, as we will show you tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 MAN #7: Sanjay (sp?), you in there, bro?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For Eye on America, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up: A surfer beloved from Hawaii to Hollywood is killed by a shark. We will have the story next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Hawaii`s surfing community is in mourning over the death of a beloved surfer who was killed by a shark; 49-year-old Tamayo Perry, who appeared in several movies, including Blue Crush and one of the Pirates of the Caribbean films, was found in the water off the island of Oahu.\par \par \uc2 Officials say he appeared to have been bitten more than once. This is the fifth deadly shark attack of Hawaii since 2019.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America with a neonatal nurse who is paying it forward.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-two-year-old Hayley Good was born a micro-preemie at just one pound, nine ounces. Good spent the first four-and-a-half months of her life in the NICU at Nationwide Children`s Hospital in Columbus, Ohio, fighting to stay alive. She`s now following in the footsteps of the amazing nurses who helped save her life.\par \par \uc2 Good just graduated from Cedarville University, earning her bachelor`s degree in nursing. And, next month, she will start working at the same NICU where she was born.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HAYLEY GOOD (Nurse): I knew growing up that I just wanted to give back to those nurses and those people that took care of me so well. And so, growing up, I just always knew I wanted to be a nurse and I wanted to do that for other people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nurse Hayley Good, that is tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 I just love nurses. That made me -- I just got the goosebumps listening to that. I loved it.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240625ek6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For June 23, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Mike Morell, Anthony Salvanto, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 8,506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Excessive heat blankets the country, as we begin a crucial week on the 2024 campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 It`s hot outside and, in some cases, getting hotter, but that sentiment can be applied to a lot more than just the heat, as the stakes for the great presidential rematch of 2024 get higher too.\par \par \uc2 With the first presidential debate just days away, we`re seeing the candidates prepare in two very different ways.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): When you say prep, I think this is prepping.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was out and about on the campaign trail in Philadelphia Saturday, checking out the cheesesteaks at a local sandwich shop and teasing reporters about his running mate pick.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Have you decided who your vice president is?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: In my mind, yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do they know?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: No. Nobody knows.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, how is debate prep going?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden is off the trail and away from cameras and voters, preparing for Thursday`s debate at the Camp David presidential retreat.\par \par \uc2 We will look at some of the key issues facing the candidates, abortion, immigration and national security, with guests, including New Mexico Democratic Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , former Trump National Security Adviser \uc2 Robert O`Brien\uc2 , and CBS News senior national security contributor Michael Morell.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll tells us what the under-30 voters are looking for this fall.\par \par \uc2 And our Listening to America segment is a snapshot of the battleground states that you won`t want to miss.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with an update on the heat dome covering much of the country. Roughly 100 million Americans are living under a heat advisory today.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann has more from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): Especially in the Northeast, Sunday`s heat will feel hellish.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Best thing I can tell you, don`t come outside.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Potentially record-setting temperatures, definitely dangerous.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: The heat, it is being felt from the Ohio Valley all the way into the mid-Atlantic; 98 today in D.C. will feel like 104.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It`s too hot today.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Too hot, definitely. It`s difficult to walk around today.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: We`re barely into summer, yet \uc2 NOAA\uc2  says there`s a 100 percent chance 2024 will be among the top five warmest years on record and a 50 percent chance it becomes the hottest ever.\par \par \uc2 Parts of New Mexico feel hellish, and they look it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: And thank God we`re safe and...\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Have somewhere here.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: We have somewhere. There`s a lot of people that don`t have anywhere to go.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Twin wildfires, only partially contained, have charred more than 24,000 acres, hundreds of homes destroyed, thousands evacuated, and two people killed.\par \par \uc2 These are images of Ruidoso, this resort village an outdoor paradise in ruins.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Everything that they have worked for their entire lives just lost in a matter of seconds, it`s very devastating.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Also potentially devastating here, widespread flash flooding, an outgrowth of the wildfires and heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 Flooding and mountains of muck could push over land scarred by the fires.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: I have never seen anything like it in my entire life. It was absolutely the most scariest thing I have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That was Mark Strassmann reporting from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to New Mexico Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 . She joins us this morning from Santa Fe.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D-New Mexico): Good morning, Margaret. Thank you for having me on this morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know President Biden did issue that emergency declaration for your state due to the fires, but you also had a 200-mile- long dust storm, catastrophic flooding.\par \par \uc2 What problems are most acute and what do you need?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Well, we want to continue the -- an open door for federal resources.\par \par \uc2 FEMA is incredible on the ground in an emergency. They bring personnel, and they open the door for federal direct monetary assistance to the state and, more Importantly, the individuals.\par \par \uc2 FEMA Administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  was on the ground, actually in Ruidoso yesterday. And I have to say, the Biden administration has really pushed through these emergency declarations quickly so that we can get families back on their feet.\par \par \uc2 But it`s been a hell of a week here, Margaret, between dust, heat, flooding. These are not the only evacuations. We`ve got flooding evacuations in the north, where the largest wildfire in the history of the United States, one of the largest wildfires in the history of the U.S. -- it`s been really tough on New Mexicans.\par \par \uc2 And I`m -- I`m grateful to every first responder who puts them first and stands up to make sure they`re as safe as we can make them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I know all of us wish you -- your state residents well as they deal with all of that.\par \par \uc2 You are a border state. I wonder if all of this excessive heat is affecting the migrants crossing in from Mexico?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : So we haven`t seen the kind of surges outside of the ports of entry that folks in other states have seen.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s not to indicate at all that that doesn`t still have some risk. But we haven`t seen heat-related issues for asylum seekers, certainly not in this first six months.\par \par \uc2 But your point is very valid. There have, in fact, been multiple deaths along the New Mexico border years past, and particularly Texas and California borders, from extreme heat. But, right now, we`re focused on the security aspects announced by the Biden administration, which are very important for states like New Mexico to have more folks at ports of entry, where risks are present every day, to really focus on human trafficking, drugs, guns, and other contraband.\par \par \uc2 And I really appreciate the president`s investment in more border security where it matters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it sounds like you could even use more than that.\par \par \uc2 I know you were here in Washington with the president when he signed that executive order that restricted the ability to claim asylum for those crossing in between ports of entry.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, some of your fellow Democrats were very critical of that, saying it undermines American values. Why are they wrong?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Well, they`re not wrong.\par \par \uc2 I mean, frankly, the president then did a -- a balanced approach. When he`s creating protections and work opportunities for undocumented families, part of a legal right, a U.S. citizen is married to someone who`s undocumented or has other extended family members, and DACA recipients and dreamers can now get protections, here`s what I think he did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that was a new measure this past week.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : He focused on -- oh, the new measure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : So, look, the security -- I need security first and foremost. You can`t protect everybody else.\par \par \uc2 And, make no mistake, New Mexico, just like everywhere else around the country suffers from the number of drugs, guns, and related nefarious issues that are exacerbated by not having people in the right places at the border. I also appreciate that he did something about all these other families.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 But -- but that`s a different issue. For those folks who`ve been living here for a decade and married to an American, that was that effort the president signed off on this past week.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to the executive order, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  is -- is trying to sue the Biden administration, saying that this was legally identical to the Trump ban that they blocked back in 2019. Does that bother you?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : I don`t -- well, it bothers me that that`s the point of view that they`re taking, and certainly understand that we`re going to do any number of challenges and litigation.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the issue. We`re not separating families. We`re making it really clear you can come through a port of entry. I have a president that, unlike Congress, including when I was in Congress, that is willing to focus on security, fairness, whether that`s in one or two different decisions...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : ... and creating, I hope, a pathway for Congress to stop listening to a candidate who continues to try to score political points, instead of solve problems for states like mine and American families. He`s doing both.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have been a very vocal access -- advocate, I should say, for reproductive health access.\par \par \uc2 This week marks two years since Roe vs. Wade was struck down. And yet, in 2023, America had the highest abortion number and abortion rate in over a decade. Why do you think there are more abortions happening at a time when there is decreased access?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Well, I think there are more abortions happening because more women are at risk, which means they can`t get into a provider. They can`t get prenatal care.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got providers who are worried about prosecutions and any number of other issues that interfere with their ability to provide care, less access to contraceptives, less information about public health, less ability to get to your primary care physician.\par \par \uc2 Most families and women live 86 miles from a provider. You create a draconian situation, you`re going to increase risk at every single place. And this is a state that`s clear about protecting women and creating equality.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : But that`s what I believe is the genesis of this situation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Governor, good luck as you deal with all those emergencies in your state. We`ll be tracking them.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we bring into the conversation now \uc2 Robert O`Brien\uc2 . He served as national security adviser in the Trump administration through the end of the former president`s term. And he joins us this morning from Palm Springs, California.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O`BRIEN (Former U.S. National Security Adviser): Thank you. It`s good to be back, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know, when you were last with us, you said that you would be honored to serve with Mr. Trump again, and you laid out in this essay in "Foreign Affairs" what you think a second Trump term would look like.\par \par \uc2 So I want to get to that. Recently, in some interviews, Mr. Trump has refused to say what he would do if China invaded Taiwan. I wonder if you would advise him to have U.S. military forces defend it.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: I think that`s always been the policy of the American government.\par \par \uc2 It`s called strategic -- strategic ambiguity. And we don`t tell the Chinese how we`re going to react to their actions. But the -- the key thing, Margaret, is peace through strength. If we -- if we have a strong Navy, if we move our soldiers and Marines out of Europe, in Germany, where they`re garrisoned, and put them in Guam and Hawaii and the Philippines, and Australia, where we have Marines already, that sends a strong message to the Chinese not to invade.\par \par \uc2 The key is to deter war, not -- not to fight and win a war, which we need to do if it happens. But we need to deter the Chinese and the Communist Party from -- from invading Taiwan in the first place, which we failed to do with Russia in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so strength -- strength will deter the Chinese from invading. It`s not -- it`s not talk. It`s -- it`s how they -- they see our force posture.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So -- well, the largest contingent of all overseas U.S. military forces is in East Asia and the Pacific already.\par \par \uc2 You`re calling to send the entire Marine Corps to Asia. When Mr. Trump was president...\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, the...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sorry. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: The fighting force, the Marine Corps. We will still have Pendleton and Camp Lejeune and the logistics tail.\par \par \uc2 But, like World War II, when Asia was key for us, we should have the Marines in Asia, we should have the -- the Air Force and the Army and -- and parts of the Navy in Europe and the rest of the world. But the Marines are perfectly suited for the Indo-Pacific. And we should have our fighting force there to deter the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 We don`t want a war, Margaret. We want to stop a war. And the way to stop a war is through strength. And moving the Marine Corps to the Pacific and moving a carrier battle group to the Pacific -- Pacific would show the kind of strength needed to deter a war.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, so you`re not moving all 170,000 Marines to Asia, but some of them to...\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, when Mr. Trump was president, though, he was -- he publicly pressed multiple times for the U.S. to withdraw the 28,000 U.S. troops that are in our treaty ally South Korea.\par \par \uc2 He also threatened to pull out of Japan. And I wonder, is -- if you think that threatening to pull out of Asian-allied countries like that shows daylight in a way that emboldens China.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: No, what President Trump was trying to do -- and he did this with \uc2 NATO\uc2  as well -- is, we need our allies to step up.\par \par \uc2 America can`t do this alone. The American taxpayer can`t deter China -- China alone. We need help from our allies. And President Trump made -- made sure that the South Koreans and the Japanese and our European allies paid their fair share and helped burden-share with us.\par \par \uc2 So, part of the negotiations...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well they already were helping to...\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... to pay for some of the costs related to housing those troops in their countries.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Some of the costs, but not enough.\par \par \uc2 So, the American taxpayers took a tremendous burden. And we`ve got a massive federal deficit. We`ve got inflation at home. We`ve got burdens here in America. So we need our allies to step up to the plate and participate in the same way we do.\par \par \uc2 We pay almost 4 percent of our GDP for defense. South Korea is -- is coming up higher. Japan is coming up higher. And that`s a direct result of President Trump and his tough negotiations and his tough policy. So...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But doesn`t...\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: ... I would look at what happened, and not -- not -- not look at the negotiating rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But at a time when you are looking at a more aggressive China, threatening to pull out U.S. troops would seem to backfire on your ultimate strategic intent.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, our strategic -- strategic intent is to be as strong as possible in Asia. And -- and we got there with Trump.\par \par \uc2 And we`re -- and it`ll go back again with -- with President Trump when he returns in six months, because when the Japanese are engaged, when the South Koreans are engaged, when the Australians are engaged -- and, keep in mind, all of these countries have raised their defense spending very significantly because of President Trump and in Europe as well.\par \par \uc2 And that all started -- that didn`t start under Biden. That started under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: That makes us stronger against China. So, sometimes, you have to be tough. You have to show tough love to your allies.\par \par \uc2 And just like with family members, you have to be -- you know, sometimes, you have to be a little tough with your family members. But the -- the Chinese aren`t going to divide the family. They`re not going to divide the -- the allies. But we -- we need -- we need to make sure that the allies pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, what would \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do to divide the axis, the new alliances that we are really seeing grow between Russia, Iran, China and North Korea? \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  was just driving around North Korea with Kim Jong-un this past week.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Yes, that`s a great question, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 And that alliance has gotten much stronger under the Biden administration because there`s been a lack of American leadership. We haven`t shown peace or strength. So the first thing we can do is increase our energy production in America.\par \par \uc2 These -- these -- these countries are relying on Russian energy for their - - to run their economies. We need to increase our energy production. We need to sanction the Russian Federation`s Central Bank, which \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  and I called upon the president to do before the invasion of Ukraine, and start cutting back on Russian oil sales.\par \par \uc2 We need to put maximum pressure back on the Iranians who are causing so much trouble all throughout the Middle East. And -- and so those are some of the steps we can take, and then rebuild our military, rebuild our Navy, get our shipyards producing ships again. Those are the things that`ll -- that will divide the alliance...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: ... the -- the -- this axis of evil.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s getting harder to sanction when -- when Russia is protecting some of those -- those rogue states, and China too.\par \par \uc2 But, on -- on Russia, during the first Trump administration, the president then argued he was going to pull out at least 10,000 of the 35,000 U.S. troops stationed in U.S. ally Germany. You wrote an op-ed at the time arguing in defense of that and saying that keeping troops in Europe was an obsolete Cold War practice.\par \par \uc2 Do you think, in hindsight, that helped embolden \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: No, what we did with -- with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is, we stopped the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline. That was his number one foreign policy objective, was to get that pipeline built and develop energy dominance over Western Europe.\par \par \uc2 And we stopped it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: The first thing President Biden did when he came into office was, he opened the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  Pipeline and canceled the Keystone XL pipeline to -- to further diminish American energy production.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The invasion of Ukraine ultimately killed that.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, it -- it killed it because Biden opened the -- the pipeline again. And Russia took that as a green light to invade Ukraine, along with our debacle in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 But going to the true question, what I said is, it wasn`t troops in Europe, Margaret. It was troops in Germany. Germany is no longer a front-line state. And we have too many troops garrisoned in Germany.\par \par \uc2 And I said we need to move some of those troops to the front line, to Poland and Czechoslovakia -- the Czech Republic and Slovakia. And we need to move some of the American territories in the Pacific, like Guam, Hawaii, the Aleutian Islands, and Alaska, to deter our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Just having garrisoned troops in Germany doesn`t help us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well -- well, \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  have a different account of what happened during that time, saying that you supported pulling out and bringing people back to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 But, on the campaign trail right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is talking about Ukraine a lot. And he said, apparently, according to the Hungarian prime minister, \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , that he won`t give a penny to Ukraine if he`s reelected, and, by cutting off that money, that will end the war.\par \par \uc2 That sounds like that would be ending the war in Russia`s favor, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Well, keep in mind, Margaret, we`re the first administration to give lethal aid to the Ukrainians, the Javelin missiles which stopped the Russian invasion to start with.\par \par \uc2 And I will give the Biden administration credit for -- for getting them some aid afterwards, but it was always too little, too late. We -- we need to bring \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to the table. And the way to do that is what \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  and I called for and what President Trump has called for, is to put massive sanctions on the Russians to bring Putin to the table, so we kind of negotiated a peace treaty.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to stop the killing in Ukraine. We got to stop the killing of Ukrainians. We got to stop the killing of Russians.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: And we need peace in the world. And -- and our -- our weakness, there -- it`s too little, too late telling the Ukrainians they can have some weapons, but they can`t have others.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: They can use some weapons, but they can`t use others, the half-measure sanctions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: None of that`s helping in the war in Ukraine. That`s -- it`s a lack of leadership under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER ROBERT O`BRIEN: Trump will get this war settled very quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ambassador, thank you for your time. It was an interesting read.\par \par \uc2 We`ll leave it there and be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by former CIA Deputy Director \uc2 Mike Morell\uc2 . He`s also our CBS News senior national security contributor.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Good to be here, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You just had that "Foreign Affairs" article that got all this attention: "The Terrorism Warning Lights are Blinking Red Again."\par \par \uc2 You compare the moment we are in now to what happened in the lead-up to 9/11.\par \par \uc2 And I want to play something \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  said earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2  (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): Our most immediate concern has been that individuals or small groups will draw a twisted inspiration from the events in the Middle East to carry out attacks here at home.\par \par \uc2 But now, on top of that, increasingly concerning is the potential for a coordinated attack here in the homeland, not unlike the ISIS-K attack we saw at the Russia concert hall back in March.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s chilling. The White House says the president is briefed regularly on threats.\par \par \uc2 If that is true, do you think he`s doing enough?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Hard for me to say whether he`s doing enough, because a lot of what needs to be done, we wouldn`t see publicly.\par \par \uc2 What I would say is, I ran into a lot of current -- former intelligence -- current intelligence officers and current policymakers after we published the article. The response was almost universal in: We`re glad you wrote this. It`s really important.\par \par \uc2 I read that as maybe there`s a lack of sense of -- of a sense of urgency here. And that`s really important.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A lack of sense of urgency among members of the public or the government?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: The administration, yes, and Congress, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 There needs to be a sense of urgency about this. And I think the American public needs to understand what the threat is. That`s why we called for a public congressional hearing just on the terrorist threats to the homeland, right, not a hearing on threats broadly, but threats to the homeland.\par \par \uc2 And then we need to hear what the administration is doing about this in a broad sense, right, not the details, but in a broad sense.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I asked the chair of the House Intelligence Committee, Republican Mike Turner, about exactly your proposal.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Right. I saw that, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And he -- he really kind of dismissed it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Yes, he said...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He said: Oh, we`ve covered that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: He said: We already covered it.\par \par \uc2 They haven`t.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. He -- he did call for the administration to declassify information.\par \par \uc2 Our colleague Sam Vinograd, who ran vetting at the border for DHS, said basically that the information that feeds those vetting lists, the watch lists, is dependent on how much good intelligence is collected, and that has been under-resourced.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: I -- I agree with that 100 percent. We`ve shifted resources from the counterterrorism community to the China community.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s understandable to some degree. It`s been significant. So I think there`s a cost to the intelligence we`re collecting. The vetting system -- beyond not having the information, the vetting system does not provide all of the information that the government has.\par \par \uc2 There was just a DHS inspector general report...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: ... that outlined all the problems with the vetting system.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s lack of information, and then it`s the system itself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That -- and we have it on a graphic. The report said: "Customs and Border Protection could not access all federal data necessary to enable complete screening and vetting of noncitizens seeking admission into the United States."\par \par \uc2 This is the government saying: We can`t vet everyone properly.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL: Right.\par \par \uc2 And Customs and Border doesn`t have the technology, right, to even connect. There are all sorts of issues here that need to be resolved.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Mike Morell\uc2 , stay with us.\par \par \uc2 I have to take a break, but there`s much more I want to talk to you about.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We return now to our conversation with CBS News senior national security contributor \uc2 Mike Morell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mike, I want to ask you about some video that \uc2 CBS\uc2  broadcast earlier this week. "60 MINUTES" obtained it. It`s Saudi national Omar Al Bayoumi walking around the U.S. Capitol back in 1999. We`re seeing that video now. It was shot within 90 days of the time when senior al Qaeda planners were deciding on 9/11 targets according to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the time you were at the CIA. We know now the \uc2 FBI\uc2  identified this man, Al Bayoumi, as an intelligence operative with close ties to two of the 9/11 hijackers. But in that 9/11 Commission report, it said there was no credible evidence that he was a violent extremist or aided extremists.\par \par \uc2 Now that you have seen this video, what do you think it reveals?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MORELL\uc2 : No doubt in my mind that it is a casing video. That it is a casing video for some sort of terrorist attack, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, pretty clear to me that Al Bayoumi was - was either working for al Qaeda or was al Qaeda. Did he know about the 9/11 attacks? Probably not. Did he know that the two individuals he was interacting with were 9/11 hijackers? Probably not. But - but no doubt in my mind that al Qaeda tasked him to do this casing video.\par \par \uc2 The video is chilling. It`s chilling in terms of what he was - what - what he was videotaping. His narration over the top of it, which - which is part what tells you it was a casing video, and his - his - his extremist comments.\par \par \uc2 Let me just give you two examples, Margaret. On - on the casing part, at one point he says, "I will get over there." He`s looking at the Washington Monument. "I will get over there and I will report. I will report to you in detail what is there." He`s talking to somebody, right? He`s - and - and he talks about a plan -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Not like a tourist would.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MORELL\uc2 : Not like a tourist video. And then in terms of the extremism, he`s - he`s - he`s looking at the Capitol and he says, "they say that our kids are demons. However, these are the demons." What he`s looking at.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  concluded he was not a threat. The 9/11 Commission report concluded he was not a threat. You`re saying it`s clear he was al Qaeda and living under the noses and examination of law enforcement undetected. He`s now living in Saudi Arabia as we speak. That`s pretty - that`s a pretty big oversight by U.S. law enforcement and intelligence. Did the CIA know about this video?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MORELL\uc2 : We did not. You know, I`m 99.9 percent confident that we did not have this video.\par \par \uc2 I was the president`s briefer at the time. If somebody had shown me this video, I would have shown it to the president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It was, as I understand it, U.K. officials, U.K. intelligence, that scooped up this video.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MORELL\uc2 : Yes. So - so - so when he left the United States, he went to the U.K. And after - after 9/11, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  discovered that he had signed - helped - helped - helped the two 9/11 hijackers get their first apartment. He - and - and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  learned that. They learned that he was in the U.K. So, they go to the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  and they say - they - they share all this information. The \uc2 British government\uc2  arrests him, detains him, interrogates him, gets all this material. They say they provided it back to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it just stayed at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , apparently?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MORELL\uc2 : It looks - it looks that way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A lot more to come on this, including on "60 MINUTES" in the fall.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for your analysis, \uc2 Mike Morell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MIKE MORELL\uc2 : You`re welcome. You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: With our CBS News polling showing a dead heat in the presidential contest, voters under the age of 30 could prove decisive. That`s potentially 25 to 30 million votes depending on whether or not they turn out.\par \par \uc2 Our tireless polling unit has been surveying these young voters to see what their mood is like in 2024. And, by most measures, they say it is tough going out there, especially when they compare their generation to previous ones.\par \par \uc2 Eighty-two percent of young Americans say it`s harder for them to buy a home. More than three quarters of young voters think raising a family will be more difficult. Seven in ten say it`s harder to get a good job. And six in ten think it`s harder to start a business.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is our executive director of elections and surveys, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, so what made you focus in on this generation? How are they different?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, we`re going to look at all the key groups in this election, but I wanted to start with this one. What`s interesting about them is they feel like they`ve already been tested in their lives. This is a generation, the younger part of them, who were in high school or college during Covid and the lockdowns, and most of them tell us, that interrupted their education. They also look back, say they were more concerned about gun violence during their - those years than older generations.\par \par \uc2 But what`s really interesting here is, they are also feeling like the older generation has handed them, has left them a world that`s more dangerous and a climate that`s worse. And that motivates a lot of the issues that they care about.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, Margaret, you can look at polling from the `60s, when the baby boomers were the young generation then, from the `90s, when it was gen-x. There have always been generation gaps in America. But for this generation today, they`re the most racially diverse generation that the country has ever seen. And that comes out in their politics too. They tell us they feel the politics would be better, not just with more young people in it, but also with more minority voices in politics, with more women in elected office. And they feel that way much more so than the oldest generation does.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So they have all this frustration. What are they going to do about it? Are they going to vote?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: That is the keys question. For all the concerns that we just laid out, they don`t vote, and they don`t say they`re going to vote, as often as the older generation does. It`s -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s kind of typical, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, it is in that way. So, you`ve got about two-thirds of them who say this year they`re definitely going to vote, and you`ve got almost all the folks, say over 65, who say that they`ll definitely vote.\par \par \uc2 Part of the factor here is age and how they see the candidates. They think that the candidates, being the ages that they are, cannot relate and understand the issues that pertain to young people. And they`re also a little more disappointed in their choices.\par \par \uc2 What`s key about that is when young people say they`re disappointed in their choices, they`re less likely to vote. But for older folks, even when they`re disappointed in their choice, they still turn out.\par \par \uc2 So, that motivation is going to be a key question. And I`ll button it up with this. They are leaning towards \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . In fact, they`re voting for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the ones who will vote, in about the same numbers that they did - that young people then did in 2020. But it`s that turnout dynamic and that relative lack of turnout that`s really going to be a key thing to watch in this group for the campaign.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, what makes them start to view the ballot box as a way to change what they`re frustrated about? Are there issues that would make them say, OK, I will drive and cast a vote, or I will drop that vote in the mail?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, so big differences are, beyond the economy, which everybody cares about, they`re more likely to point at issues like abortion, to point at climate and climate change, which I mentioned earlier, and also race and diversity issues as being important relative to what older generations say.\par \par \uc2 In all those things, those - that - those accrue to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , right? The people who care about those issues are voting for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , which underpins some of his support. But having said that, they don`t always - they aren`t paying as much attention to the campaign and a substantial portion of them aren`t sure what the candidates would do on those issues. There`s a substantial portion who aren`t sure what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  say would do on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Now, what`s important about that is, that means when we watch this campaign going forward, can the candidates, not just motivate them, but also inform them about what they would do so that they can make ostensibly a more informed choice.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anthony Salvanto, always interesting. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Friday evening we spoke with six voters, evenly split between who they`re going to vote from, from four of the seven battleground states. And the conversation was a feisty one, with the participants veering frequently to former President Trump. One of the few things they all agreed on was that this election is critically important.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 LYDIA (Georgia Voter): It is critical because of the state of our nation. There`s so much going on, on border security, infiltration by people that could cause extreme harm to our country, the economy, and the weakness of our current administration on the national stage.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do you mean by infiltration?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Because of our open borders, I feel very strongly that there have been people coming in - because nobody`s being vetted, nobody`s asking questions, and I think we are being infiltrated by people that could cause another 9/11 very honestly.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP (North Carolina Voter): We see these numbers. Illegals crossing the border. And we`re not talking about Central and South America. We`re seeing Haitians. We`re seeing Chinese. We`re seeing Central Africans. You know, we`re seeing Arab nation immigrants come across who are not immigrants but illegal immigrants come across. So, this - it`s - it looks like an invasion of some sort.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I think both parties recognize a broken immigration system. There is concern about a terror threat. But you`re talking about this as if it is a choice to allow for these risks. Why do you think that that is a choice versus a tough situation America finds itself in?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Because day one the president, current President Biden was elected, he immediately, by the stroke of a pen, undid everything that President Trump had done in closing the border. We had security at the border. We were vetting the people that wanted to come in. And with the stroke of a pen, day one, he opened the - the border. He opened the doors. Without any vetting process.\par \par \uc2 So, it - it was his choice. He has to take responsibility for that. He and his administration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You do know that there is vetting that border officials do when they encounter someone?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: No, they don`t.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They do.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`ve been to the federal facilities. When someone is detained, when they are captured, when they cross illegally, they are taken into federal custody, and those agents do vet people. The success of that vetting may be in question. The efficacy of it. But - but they are. If someone sneaks in undetected, then they`re definitely not vetted.\par \par \uc2 And have - have you heard from either candidate like a specific vision on how to fix the issue you see with the border?\par \par \uc2 MARLENE (Wisconsin Voter): I`ve heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that he wants to shoot people on - when they cross. That`s what I`ve heard.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: I have not heard that at all.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: So, I`m saying -\par \par \uc2 WILDREYETTA (Georgia Voter): Well, I do believe the border, it`s a crisis. It truly is. But what is the solution? If we all can`t get together and decide on a solution to the problem, how - how we going to solve the problem? Because when you have -\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jeremy, I see you raising your hand. You can jump in.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY (Arizona Voter): Well, two things. Just to dispel a myth, if you look at statistically, the number of crimes committed in the United States, the highest - much higher number, by native white citizens. That is a fact. The second thing is that the - the number of illegal crossings has remained roughly the same. I have driven past the border, Arizona, California, New Mexico, many times. And you can`t - you can`t drive, you know, a mile or a mile and a half without seeing a border patrol vehicle agent. There are people there. They`re doing their job.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How many of you plan to watch the presidential debate on Thursday? Raise your hand if you`re going to watch.\par \par \uc2 Do any of you feel like you could be persuaded by these candidates to vote for the other one? Like if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says something really compelling, would you switch from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or vice versa?\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: I would fall over if he said anything worth listening to.\par \par \uc2 WILDREYETTA: Well, Trump always said he wanted to be a dictator. That was enough for me. This is (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY (Wisconsin Voter): Well, I don`t - I don`t think you can take any of their words literally anymore. It`s mostly political theater and comedy.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY: So, there`s some entertainment value there to be had.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Yes. Yes, that is indeed for sure.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: Yes, I think that`s a good point.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So none of -\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: Well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  means it when he says he wants to be a dictator. He means it.\par \par \uc2 WILDREYETTA: I know he want revenge as well.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: So, if - if I can jump if here. It`s a show. He is from New York. He is a showman. He may be a blow-hard, he may say things, but in - in the end, he knows how good this country has been to him in making him a success. So, the things that he says, we basically listen with one ear, goes in one ear, it goes out the other, and you try to go through the weeds to find out what really is going on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s so interesting you say that, that you - you put aside what he says. Do you have kids?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Yes. I have two sons who are in the Air Force.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s sort of the opposite of how you parent, right? Like, you probably don`t tell your boys, say whatever.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You probably - you probably held them to account when they said something that they shouldn`t have said.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think you - you view this man, who is going to be potentially commanding your sons into the battlefield, to a different standard?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Well, I don`t hold him to a different standard. I`m not saying I appreciate or even like the type of stuff that sometimes comes out of his mouth. His show, "The Apprentice," you know, again, he had to be a showman. So, that - does it make it right? No, I don`t agree with that at all.\par \par \uc2 However, we`ve had four years with him. A lot of us liked, you know, what he did for our country, but that doesn`t mean we liked the things he said or how he sometimes acted.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: A huge part of the reason why I`m for voting for Trump is economics and inflation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, raise your hand if any of you feel that you are better off today than you were four years ago.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: Probably the same.\par \par \uc2 TONY: Same.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You feel - you feel the same.\par \par \uc2 I see you there with your hand up, Jeremy. You want to weigh in?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY: Yes. One of the things that people often forget is that American consumer spending has been rising, even in relation to inflation. Wages have been soaring high along with it. Some say that might have caused part of the inflation problem.\par \par \uc2 I raised my hand about being better off four years ago because my employer has given significant raises across the board to everyone. And I guess I don`t buy the kinds of things with the inflationary prices. I mean if I pay an extra 20 cents a pound for asparagus, I think I`ll live. So, it just depends on individual circumstances.\par \par \uc2 WILDREYETTA: Yes, because today, in this economy, anybody that wants a job can get one. And that`s a fact. Anybody that wants to work can get a job now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Raise your hand if any of you think about, for example, the state of our democracy. Does that concern you, and is that a reason that you would - wow. You are jumping right at it, Marlene. That - I didn`t finish the sentence. What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not want what`s good for America. He does not want democracy. He wants to have everything his way. He wants to place people in power that will do whatever he says this time.\par \par \uc2 If it - if he wants so and so to be in the Justice Department because they`re going to do whatever he says, then he`s going to go after all of his political opponents, even if they haven`t done anything wrong, he`s going to have complete control. And that`s what he wants.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: So, the same can be said about President Biden. He forced all the car manufacturers to - to convert to EVs. And now they`ve - they did it because they had no choice. They were going to be penalized if he - they didn`t. And now they`re finally pushing back and saying, no, there`s - there is no sales for these vehicles.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Lydia, do you think somebody`s making you buy a car?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Yes. I`m - I`m not buying an EV.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: By 2030.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: By - he made it -\par \par \uc2 (CROSS TALK)\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: He has made it mandatory.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Just - Americans won`t be required to drive electric vehicles. The car manufacturers, there was a goal set for the transition to happen, but Americans won`t be required and car manufacturers will still make combustion engines too.\par \par \uc2 Does everyone here trust their state government to fairly and safely administer the upcoming presidential election?\par \par \uc2 WILDREYETTA: I do.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Lydia, you believe the state of Georgia will administer your election fairly?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: Well, there`s been a lot of changes since the last election. So I feel -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You don`t think they did in 2020?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA: I - 50-50. Well, I`ll leave it at that.\par \par \uc2 WILDREYETTA: I, too, am from Georgia, and I agree. The election is going to be correct in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TONY: I just think that the concerning thing is that in most areas the population is split almost so evenly that it really doesn`t take a lot to tip the balance one way or the other. And I guess just here in the state of Wisconsin, I don`t have 100 percent confidence that, in Dane County, in Madison, or in Milwaukee, where it matters, where at 10:00 p.m. before you go to bed on election night it looks like one thing, and then magically ballots are found overnight. It just doesn`t give you that sense of confidence.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, will all of you stand by the 2024 election results?\par \par \uc2 TONY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Show of hands.\par \par \uc2 And do all of you expect your preferred candidate to accept the 2024 election results?\par \par \uc2 Tony, you were like, I don`t know. Yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY: I can`t speak for what someone`s going to do. I don`t - I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: Yes. Well, he pretty much already said he`s not going to accept it if he loses.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Donald Trump`s 78. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , 81. Does anyone think that older age is an asset?\par \par \uc2 TONY: There`s some benefit to wisdom, experience, but I think you`ve got to question the fitness, ability, cognitive ability. And this is not a criticism. I mean age is what it is. You can`t stop it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY: It can`t be helped.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: The \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  we`ve got in the past two years, especially two years, has been a definite decline in the cognitive functions (ph).\par \par \uc2 MARLENE: I saw little decline, but not like what you guys are saying. You`re acting like he`s fumbling around on stage (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 TONY: OK, but half - half the time he is.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIP: He is.\par \par \uc2 WILDREYETTA: Oh, you don`t see the decline in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , huh?\par \par \uc2 TONY: Half of the people in this group says that anything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says it`s - it`s garbage. We don`t want to listen to him. Whatever. But we don`t even get the opportunity to hear what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says because he won`t take questions. He walks away. You don`t even get to interact. There`s no transparency. There`s no sense of, hey, here`s what I think about this or why we`re doing this.\par \par \uc2 So, what`s worse? Something that you say isn`t even worth listening to or not even being able to hear the message?\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The full hour of our conversation can be seen on our website and our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I hope you`ll join me, Norah O`Donnell, Gayle King, and the rest of our \uc2 CBS\uc2  political team for our live coverage of the first presidential debate, which is hosted by CNN this Thursday night. We`ll be on this network at 9:00 p.m. Eastern on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , our CBS News 24/7 streaming special coverage begins at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020240623ek6n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Eyes on Alberto; Record Setting Heatwave Continues; Wildfires Devastating in New Mexico; More SCOTUS Rulings Today; Ten Commandments in the Classroom; NY Social Media Ban for Kids; Fourth of July Travel Rush; California Prison Reform; Simone Biles Rising; New Approach to Egg Freezing; Legendary Rickwood Field; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Colors of Change; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Dave Malkoff, Lilia  Luciano, Stephanie Abrams, Jan Crawford, Jo Ling Kent, Kris Van Cleave,  Vladimir Duthiers; Gianna Toboni, Nikki Battiste, Major Garrett, Elise  Preston \par \uc2 13,756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this 20th of June as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Record breaking heat continues to bake the Midwest and Northeast while Tropical Storm Alberto batters the Texas Coast.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: What`s it like in the middle of all this?\par \par \uc2 TONY JUAREZ (Delivery man): Raining and raining, you know? It`s not fun. But we`re out here doing it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A landmark bill in New York is banning "addictive social media feeds for kids." Could it be a model for other states?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Our kids are in distress.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We go inside California`s prisons to see why Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is prioritizing rehabilitation over punishment.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: Is everybody on board?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Not everybody. I mean no one`s naive.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: State Police in New Mexico now say a second person has died in fast-moving wildfires.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: The \uc2 Washington Nationals\uc2  baseball team`s tradition of doing a president`s mascot race took a turn.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Thomas Jefferson clobbers Abe Lincoln from behind with a folding chair. Then George Washington does the same to Teddy Roosevelt.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: A Vermont Republican state representative is now apologizing for her "disrespectful conduct" after video caught her pouring water into her Democratic colleague`s tote bag.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Over the past few months, Representative Carroll has been repeatedly finding his tote bag drenched in water without any indication of what caused it.\par \par \uc2 It`s a political scandal that some are calling water-gate.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is very bizarre though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You just pour water in your colleague`s tote bag, so they set up a camera to catch the person. She admitted she did it She`s on tape, but she doesn`t say why. I don`t get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, just to drive him crazy, I guess. It would definitely drive me crazy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But they`re supposed to be the adults in the room.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all adults here at this table. Happy to say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today, by the way is the first day of summer. It`s going to be the longest day of light for all of us. It`s going to feel like it, too, in much of the country and then some because why? That brutal heatwave is expected to continue for days, setting high temperatures in many areas in this country.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this. It was hotter in Northern Maine yesterday than it was in Miami. How is that possible? In the meantime, Tropical Storm Alberto is pounding the Texas Gulf Coast. It has left at least three people dead there in Eastern Mexico. Some parts of Texas were left underwater by the storm surge.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff is along the coast in hard hitting Corpus Christi with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Dave, yesterday at this time, we saw you standing in pouring rain. What`s the situation like for you now?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Yes, Gayle, we can`t even get to the spot where we were yesterday because the flooding is actually worse than it was before. We are 1,500 feet from the shoreline and it is like this all the way back.\par \par \uc2 Just seconds ago, we lost power to that hotel. There is a stalled car here. This is just all happening right here right now. And it`s not just here. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Flooding from Tropical Storm Alberto submerged cities along the Texas Coast before it even made landfall in Northeast Mexico early this morning.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK WILSON (Visiting Galveston County, Texas): We didn`t think he`d get up as far, you know, so, but they said Jamaica Beach is one of the worst, I guess.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: In Surfside Beach, four inches of heavy rainfall and storm surge up to four feet high flooded homes making them look like they were in the middle of a lake and leaving abandoned cars underwater.\par \par \uc2 The water rose so high, police in Surfside Beach were cruising through the streets in rafts rescuing people from stranded cars.\par \par \uc2 All of those outer bands are reaching here and causing a real threat of storm surge. It`s not just this one street, but many all throughout Corpus Christi and Galveston.\par \par \uc2 In downtown Galveston, a day off work to celebrate the Juneteenth holiday was rained out for most making roads impassable, while people in many areas in the storm`s path fled to higher ground, not everyone could.\par \par \uc2 We found Tony Juarez working in the heavy rain delivering porta-potties.\par \par \uc2 TONY JUAREZ: It is not fun, but we`re out here doing it.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: You have to do it because everybody`s got to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY JUAREZ: Everybody has got to go. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Kudos to those workers who are out there doing their job even in all this. It is still windy, and Nate, we`ve just started hurricane season here.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a very warm gulf. We`ve got possible La Nina over there. In other words, brace yourself for this hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it looks like we`ll be checking in with you real soon.\par \par \uc2 Dave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 About 80 million Americans are under extreme heat alerts today from the Midwest to the Northeast. In Philadelphia, it has been years since it was this hot in June. Temperatures could hit 96 degrees there tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano rode along with EMTs who are very busy because of the heat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: This is the biggest concern for you guys.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Geriatrics. Also kids; even just people in their day-to-day, you know. I can`t even imagine what it`s like being out in the city. We have little to no respite from this.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Emergency responders in Philadelphia say they`ve had a growing number of calls for heatstroke and heat exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 TIM HINCHCLIFF (Burholme, Pennsylvania EMS managing director): Today could be a nonstop day. It`s one call after another.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And it`s starting sooner. The city has already declared its first heat health emergency of the year.\par \par \uc2 Did you expect this much heat this early in the season?\par \par \uc2 TIM HINCHCLIFF: Not really. We don`t usually get it this soon, but if this is what the summer is like, like we are going to expect, we`re going to have to really gear it up.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It`s not just Philadelphia; cities like Portland, Maine; Canton, Ohio; and Wheeling, West Virginia are all expecting peak temperatures more than 10 degrees higher than normal today.\par \par \uc2 SHEL WINKLEY (Climate Central meteorologist): It is virtually impossible to experience temperatures like this at this time of the year without the inception of manmade climate change.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Meteorologist, Shel Winkley with the research nonprofit, Climate Central says earlier extreme heat is a worrying and dangerous trend.\par \par \uc2 SHEL WINKLEY: This is record territory for a lot of cities across the Eastern US, but one of the things, too, is it is coming fast, right? We haven`t had a chance to acclimate our bodies to this heat because this is something we haven`t built up to over the hot months.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a little more dangerous because our bodies aren`t ready to take on this much heat this rapidly.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Here in Philadelphia, there`s a community effort to beat the heat. Civic scientists with \uc2 Drexel University\uc2  launched a neighborhood cooling project working to build dozens of cooling benches with umbrellas, spraying water, and plants around the city\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA MILES-CHAMBLISS (Empowered CDC volunteer): There is so much cement and concrete that you have no place to go to get cool, and this is the best thing that they could ever have done.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And as part of that heat health emergency, the city of Philadelphia is opening 153 cooling centers across the city and also deploying specialized field teams to make in-home visits for anyone who needs it -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lilia in the City of Brotherly Love. Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get more on the heat now and the storm from meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with the heatwave. Our average high in DC is 87. The actual air temperature will be in the upper 90s through the beginning of next week with our Heat Index topping out around 105.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just the nation`s capital. Today in New England, the Heat Index will be above 100 and though it might be the first day of summer, this excessive heat is twice as likely due to the changing climate.\par \par \uc2 Now to an update on Tropical Storm Alberto. The center is heading towards Mexico, but it is still spitting out rain over Texas.\par \par \uc2 Today, the showers will be more scattered. Tomorrow, the flow off the gulf continues to deliver clouds and thunderstorms on already saturated grounds. And because of that, the rain through Friday could cause flooding even though we`re only going to see a couple more inches of rain. Of course, locally we will see more.\par \par \uc2 The winds will be a little bit breezy today. Tomorrow, it will be much improved.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Tony, believe it or not, this weekend, we could have yet another system in this same area.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not good news.\par \par \uc2 Steph, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wildfires raging in Central New Mexico have now killed two people and destroyed as many as 500 homes.\par \par \uc2 The South Fork and Salt Fires have burned more than 23,000 acres since Monday. Both fires were zero percent contained yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Most of the 8,000 people who live in Ruidoso evacuated the town just ahead of the flames. Heavy storms moved into the area yesterday raising the threat of flash flooding there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We go to Washington now where the Supreme Court plans to release more decisions this morning and there are a lot of big issues to be decided, not a lot of time left for the justices to do it.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court where she is going to show us what might be released today.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there`s 21 cases left in the Supreme Court, so we are going to get a few of those later this morning. We don`t know which ones unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest one left, of course, is that one involving former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and whether he is immune from prosecution for his alleged efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the court has got another case on whether Trump along with several hundred other January 6 defendants can be charged with obstruction. That carries a 20-year prison sentence.\par \par \uc2 There is also another case on abortion coming off the court`s recent decision. They refuse to limit the availability of that so-called abortion pill. This one looks at abortion access and emergency room care.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s another gun case. Last week, the justices said it was up to Congress to ban bump stocks, but they still have to decide this case about a gun ban for people who are under domestic violence restraining orders.\par \par \uc2 There are also big cases on social media and free speech. There`s a challenge to a bankruptcy plan involving a big company that is considered responsible for the opioid crisis and whether that can go forward.\par \par \uc2 I mean, all of this, Tony and more coming up this week and the following week. They are hoping to wrap up by early July.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Jan, while we have you there, I want to get you on the record with this situation in Louisiana. There`s a law there, it feels like it`s headed for the Supreme Court. The state will be the first in the country in decades, I think since Kentucky in 1980 to require the Ten Commandments be displayed in every public school classroom and the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  vows to file a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 They`ve said: "The law violates the separation of Church and State and is blatantly unconstitutional." But the governor there in Louisiana says I can`t wait to get sued.\par \par \uc2 So Jan, what`s the precedent here? Is there one for law like this going before the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, I mean, the court, 40 years ago overturned a similar law from Kentucky. It has allowed, you know, Ten Commandments displays in some public parks, but that`s part of like a bigger historical display.\par \par \uc2 So I mean, this seems blatantly unconstitutional. I mean, that`s totally different. A public park with a bunch of other stuff around as a historical display, than in a classroom where kids are required by law to be, and the government is saying that the Ten Commandments have to be a central focus.\par \par \uc2 Now, Tony, some people are saying, well, that was 40 years ago, and this is a new conservative court. And it`s true, I mean, this is a court that is letting people you know, have more religious expression in public life.\par \par \uc2 But that is different than the government telling public schools that they have to put in a display, a Ten Commandments display, whether it`s a Christian display, in this case, but a Muslim display, a Jewish display, where school kids have to be by law.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we`ll be talking about this one for sure. Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here in New York, Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is expected to sign a landmark bill into law today, banning what she calls addictive social media feeds for kids under 18.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, Jo Ling Kent sat down with the governor to discuss what is driving this groundbreaking legislation to whether New York will consider a complete cell phone ban for students in school.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BERNICE SAI (Mother): I`m excited that we are making progress and taking action.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For mom, Bernice Sai (sp?), the battle over social media addiction with her 16-year-old daughter has been a struggle.\par \par \uc2 BERNICE SAI: It makes me mad because I see how social media is kind of using her. I know it`s not healthy for her.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: She is one of many parents who`ve spoken up in support of the Safe For Kids Act in New York, which Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  will sign into law today, making it illegal for social media companies to use "addictive feeds for users under 18."\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Our kids are in distress. They`re not living carefree lives, because they are being held captive to powerful forces outside their own control, algorithms that are intentionally addictive, intended to pull them in and keep their attention.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Why did New York choose to target the algorithm?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): But the most important part, in order to liberate our children, we have to get right to the source of the trauma that`s been inflicted on them. So it`s a novel approach, but we`ve checked to make sure we believe it`s constitutional.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: That means, in New York State, no suggested posts will be allowed in kid`s feeds, no notifications from 12 midnight to 6:00 AM without parental consent, and new age verification tools to be set by the state attorney general.\par \par \uc2 If the social media platforms do not comply, the attorney general can fine them up to $5,000.00 per violation and beyond.\par \par \uc2 How confident are you that New York State can keep up with the technological innovations of the most powerful innovative companies in the world and actually enforce this law?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Our attorney general helped propose this with me. The first start is to just change the law, put the companies on notice that it is a new day in New York. We`re here standing with our children.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: NetChoice, which represents \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 , among other major tech companies says the New York law will violate the First Amendment and censor free speech online.\par \par \uc2 CARL SZABO (NetChoice vice president and general counsel): The attorney general will be responsible for enforcement and they can enforce at will. That`s a decision that should be left up to parents, not government, and not Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: What`s your response to that?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): The parents are the ones helping lead the charge. They`re the ones who started this whole effort.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Today`s signing comes just days after the surgeon general proposed that Congress create a tobacco-style warning label on social media and the Los Angeles Unified School District`s Board voted to ban students from using cell phones during school hours.\par \par \uc2 Do you want to see that here in New York?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): I`ve already said we`re studying that. We are absolutely looking at this seriously. And I`ll tell you why, people want to know their children are safe. That all can be accomplished on a flip phone, right?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The New York law is set to go into effect 180 days after the state attorney general determines what the exact rules and guidelines will be. And of course, we reached out to all the social media platforms.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  says while it does not agree with every aspects of this bill, it supports legislation requiring App Stores to obtain parental approval to download apps and TikTok declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s something happening though, Jo Ling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, there really is.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope it really does pick up steam that the parents buy in. And the kids -- kids actually want structure. They actually want rules.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They say they don`t, but they really do want structure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m so glad you bring that up, because if you ask them, do you want social media? A lot of them don`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: That`s exactly what the daughter of Bernice Sai said, "Mom, I wish I sometimes didn`t even have a phone."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: And what we`re seeing is states doing patchwork regulation. Congress, they`re still stuck. Not much has moved yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And what the governor said about the flip phone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I bet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We were talking about that yesterday. Give them flip phones.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much. Good work.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we could see record number of travel numbers for the Fourth of July, we`ll show you where to find a last minute deal in just a bit, but first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, part two of our "Facing Fertility" series looks at a new program to help women who need to freeze their eggs but can`t afford it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we continue to look at California`s efforts to make prisons more humane and find out what prison guards and crime victims think of it.\par \par \uc2 This CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Freshpet. It`s not dog food. It`s food-food.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In today`s "Money Watch," Fourth of July travel is expected to be the busiest ever for the holiday. AAA predicts nearly 71 million Americans will travel 50 miles or more. That`s up five percent from last year.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is at the very busy John Wayne Airport with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Kris, what`s the story?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 You know, it is hard to believe that people don`t think the airport is the happiest place on earth given that millions of people are going to be passing through them around the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 When we look at the TSA, they are bracing for perhaps three million people to be screened in a day. That will be the first time ever and it could happen any day now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Flying out of Washington Dulles Airport, Joenne Weaver is starting to trip expected to be very popular this Fourth of July, an Alaskan cruise.\par \par \uc2 JOENNE WEAVER (Traveler): The weather is supposed to be a little better, and Alaska now and the mosquitoes aren`t quite as big yet.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: AAA predicts nearly seven percent more Americans will fly somewhere for Independence Day. Since just May 17th, TSA has seen seven of its busiest days ever.\par \par \uc2 Shannon Lann and family are heading to Greece this week to miss that rush.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON LANN (Traveler): We`re hoping that us going at this time, there will be less crowds. We`ll enjoy our time together better.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Most of the nearly 70.9 million expected to travel 50 miles or more will drive, and they`ll find gas prices about 13 cents cheaper than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 AIXA DIAZ (AAA spokesperson): The numbers just keep going up and we keep setting these new records and we sound like a broken record, but that`s because the records are being set year after year.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Is the new norm just going to be every summer is a record breaking summer for travel?\par \par \uc2 AIXA DIAZ: You would think the bubble is going to burst at some point, but we haven`t seen any indication of that happening yet.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Despite surging demand to fly, airfare tracker, Hopper is still seeing last minute travel deals to places like Myrtle Beach, Charleston, even Copenhagen and Stockholm for under 600 bucks.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: If you`re looking to get the lowest airfare departing on the Fourth of July and coming back on Tuesday or Wednesday of the week following, it is guaranteed to get you the lowest price. You will save over $100.00 per ticket on domestic routes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Flexibility is key when looking for those deals. If you`re driving the Wednesday before July 4th and Sunday will be the busiest on the roads -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Kris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, part two of our look inside California`s prison system.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back, 7:30, CBS MORNINGS and we`re looking at California`s prison initiative, a new one that prioritizes rehabilitation over punishment.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, if you recall, we got an inside look at the last days of death row at San Quentin all part of a larger effort to reshape the state`s prison system overall.\par \par \uc2 And today in part two of our series, we take a deeper look at what people are calling the California Model, an effort to rehabilitate general population prisoners.\par \par \uc2 The initiative offers inmates who demonstrate good behavior programs from therapy to sign language classes to coding.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is contributor, Gianna Toboni.\par \par \uc2 Gianna, we liked yesterday`s report. We`re looking forward to this one.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I have been working on a book about the death penalty titled, "The Volunteer." And at a recent family event, I introduced myself to Governor Newsom. We have a family connection.\par \par \uc2 He agreed to an interview back in April. We visited two state prisons to witness how he is implementing California`s new approach to incarceration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When I first came to prison, I couldn`t be honest with myself about what I had done. I have caused the end of someone`s life, murdered someone.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: These incarcerated women are trying to get to the root of why they committed crimes to avoid reoffending in the future.\par \par \uc2 Therapy groups like this one are part of the California Model, a taxpayer- funded initiative being rolled out across eight prisons statewide, prioritizing rehabilitation and education.\par \par \uc2 California`s Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  says his goal is to prevent people from coming back into the system.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): There are people that absolutely never should be released. Absolutely. I believe that in my core. There are people that absolutely should be released, but are not prepared yet to be released because they are not fully rehabilitated.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: The initiative which offers everything from job training to dog handling is based on a model from Norway.\par \par \uc2 Leading the charge is Newsom`s Prisons Chief Jeff Macomber, who started his career as a guard.\par \par \uc2 Back when you were an officer, would you have been on board with something like this?\par \par \uc2 JEFF MACOMBER (\uc2 California Department of Corrections and Rehabilitation\uc2  secretary): No. I didn`t have the vision back then that I do now.\par \par \uc2 And you know, a lot of people say why are you doing this? Why should you spend money for incarcerated to go to college? And I say, well, it is costing you a lot more to keep people in prison and people coming back and I don`t want to create more crime victims out there.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: He thinks the approach will also keep prison guards safer, a group with a suicide rate 39 percent higher than other professions.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MACOMBER: If I could reduce violent behavior by the incarcerated, staff assault rate goes down. Injuries go down.\par \par \uc2 When Norway started their change, it took them 10 to 20 years. So it`s going to take time, but we`re going to get there.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: But getting there is not only going to take time, but buy-in from correctional staff.\par \par \uc2 Many of these correctional officers have also been assaulted by inmates. Is everybody on board?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Not everybody. I mean, no one`s nave, but this couldn`t be done without them. We said you`re going to be around that table making decisions with us.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: We wanted to know what the guards think about the California model. We sat down with a few of them and their boss.\par \par \uc2 MAN: When I first heard about it, like a lot of us it was met with skepticism.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPHINE SOLIS (Correctional officer): I thought it was a great opportunity to just treat people like people. Treat them like humans. What happens if we give them that respect? Do they give it back?\par \par \uc2 JOHNATHON CUSKE (Correctional lieutenant officer): Probably 95 percent of our population is going to get out and I feel like our job as police officers is what are we providing to that person to not create more victims?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Here`s a picture of Kyle and I playing up in the snow. This is a before I married his mom. And here we are, the three of us, Kyle and his mom and myself. Those are definitely good times.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: John Sparry`s stepson, Kyle Myrick was beaten to death by a co-worker when he was just 28 years old. His killer is now serving 15 years to life.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people who have been convicted of murder in this state who are now gaining access to rehabilitative programs. What are your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPARRY (Stepson was murdered): I do feel like the California Model is chipping away at the justice that we`re supposed to receive. And perpetrators, they served some time and now, their time in prison is going to be even easier. So how is that justice?\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: Do you feel that people who are convicted of murder, do they deserve a second chance?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPARRY: I don`t think they`re going to be fixed by any amount of dog training or horse petting or any other therapies that they offer I think they`re probably just beyond that.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: What is the experience of having to mourn a child?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPARRY: It`s absolutely horrific. Even before I knew for sure that Kyle was dead, and we were searching for him, it was -- it was so painful just to be alone with my thoughts, and it hurt everyone -- everyone that he knew -- it is really just, just brutal.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: Does the pain never go away?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPARRY: No.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: There are family members who have lost loved ones who say these people should exist in punitive environments.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: Right? They shouldn`t get these privileges. And what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Look, no one is arguing that folks should be released, that have committed heinous and horrible crimes, that had been sentenced or condemned. In that context, I`m deeply empathetic to the victims.\par \par \uc2 And look, not all the victims are on board, but many are.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: The state argues the California Model is about public safety.\par \par \uc2 Washington and Oregon are adopting similar initiatives, and Jeff Macomber believes other states will follow.\par \par \uc2 Do you think this will drive national policy?\par \par \uc2 JEFF MACOMBER: Yes. Yes, I do. And I think this is where we`re headed.\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: What do you say to folks who are maybe in other parts of the country who are looking at this and saying, what are these California liberals doing? This must be a political strategy.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MACOMBER: We`re doing this because we think it works. Ninety percent of the folks that are incarcerated here and throughout California are going to go back out and be on the streets. Do you want those folks to have a job ?Maybe get treated for mental illness while they`re here? Maybe get the education they didn`t have the opportunity for?\par \par \uc2 If we know those individuals have a twenty, twenty-five, thirty percent lesser chance of coming to the prison, why wouldn`t I do that? Why wouldn`t I try that?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GIANNA TOBONI: When I was with Secretary Macomber there at the prison, he told me about when he was a warden at a high-incident facility, so a place where there`s a lot of violence and he noticed that the folks who were in the classroom were not the ones causing trouble.\par \par \uc2 And then when he got the top post, the data reflected his personal experience, which I thought was very interesting -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gianna, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Wow, that is eye opening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is eye opening and then you can`t help but think what that father was saying though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, the families of the victims.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I get the idea of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just think about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- rehabilitation, preventing future victims.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It does save money. You don`t warehouse people. It respects human dignity. But man, when you sit in that father`s chair, you feel differently.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but I am conflicted.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because if 95 percent of prisoners are going to be released.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Then what are we doing to rehabilitate?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rehabilitate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`ve often heard they go into prison. Sometimes they come out worse than when they went in. It only makes you a better criminal. So clearly, something needs to be done, I`m not quite sure what the answer is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. It`s eye-opening though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gianna, great stuff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right ahead, part two of our series, "Facing Fertility." As the cost of freezing eggs soars, some women are turning to a new program offering to do it for free, but find out what they have to do in return. It is a win-win situation for everybody involved.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Olympic gymnast, Simone Biles is talking about her mental health struggles and her stunning decision to drop out of the 2020 Tokyo Games in a new docuseries.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SIMONE BILES (Gymnast): I knew it would be a long journey, but to me it wasn`t done.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes you have to take that power back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, she got her power back.\par \par \uc2 The trailer for the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series called "Simone Biles Rising" was released yesterday. It shows the superstar breaking down in tears at one point along with training for her Olympic comeback.\par \par \uc2 We also see other big moments in her life like -- I like this -- her wedding to \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, Jonathan Owens.\par \par \uc2 This series will be released on July 19th, and that`s just a week before the four-time gold medalist hits the biggest global stage once again at the Paris Olympics.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait to see this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody is cheering for Simone. I want to see this, too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Listen, there`s athletes that feel pressure --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- and then there`s other athletes who carry the weight of the world on their shoulders.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I feel like the Olympic athletes, some of the best ever do it like her, Michael Phelps.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a different type of pressure that they have to face, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They can clearly carry it. I mean, she`s strong because you`re on the rope.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Powerful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to see her training scenes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Simone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, a massive bid to buy the catalogue of a legendary rock band, Queen. Yes, that`s right. We`ll show you how much. It is a big time dollar amount.\par \par \uc2 But right now, it`s 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some big stories coming up for you at eight o`clock, including a look at the skyrocketing costs, that`s how you say that, of dealing with infertility.\par \par \uc2 For many women, these treatments as you know are unaffordable. But there is a new approach to egg freezing that is giving some families new hope, but it comes with some strings.\par \par \uc2 Plus, for this Pride Month, we`re exploring the history of a powerful symbol of the Gay Rights Movement.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk with one of the creators of the iconic striped rainbow pride flag about its origins. Do you know where it started? Nope.\par \par \uc2 All of that in our next hour, but first, Klaus and I are heading over to the table because Vlad has entered the building. We will, we will rock you.\par \par \uc2 That`s a big old clue.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a tease.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: CBS MORNINGS sponsored by Inspire. No mask, no hose, just sleep.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch" with pep squad leader, Vlad Duthiers. What was that -- what were we doing there?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We were doing Queen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Queen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "We Will Rock You." Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why were we doing Queen?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am going to explain why.\par \par \uc2 So Queen`s music catalogue is reportedly selling for a price that could make you ask, is this the real life? Is this just fantasy?\par \par \uc2 Shawna is sitting over there thinking, we`re going to be in so much trouble for all this Queen music that we have been singing all morning long?\par \par \uc2 "Variety: says Queen is close to selling its catalogue to \uc2 Sony\uc2  Music for more than $1.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: $1.2 billion. This would include of course, the classic songs like "Bohemian Rhapsody," "We Will Rock You," "We Are The Champions," and "Another One Bites The Dust" and many, many others.\par \par \uc2 Artists like Bruce Springsteen and Bob Dylan have sold their catalogues in huge deals, rumored to be worth hundreds of millions of dollars, 