"Jeopardy" host): Reporter Vladimir Duthiers has translated French and the language called Haitian this for Anderson Cooper.\par \par \uc2 Dylan?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN (Contestant): What I Creole?\par \par \uc2 ALEX TREBEK: Good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you know that you were going to be on?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I think -- I think I feel like they -- I feel like I got an e-mail from the "Jeopardy" producers asking me to factcheck something and that`s how I knew that I was potentially in the mix.\par \par \uc2 But maybe, you`re famous so this was probably easy for them. They didn`t have to like -- they just Googled you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nobody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know the way he said your name though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. That was Alex.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, the legend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I get a clip of that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, we will take you to the oldest professional ballpark in America where the late great Willie Mays played and will be honored tonight along with Negro League history.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in our series "Facing Fertility," a new option for women who can`t afford to freeze their eggs.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, part two of our "Facing Fertility" series. The cost of IVF is soaring, you know about this, but for some, it`s the only chance to have a baby. We`re going to show you a program where some women can freeze their eggs for free.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 We`ve all noticed those familiar rainbow pride flags during Pride Month, but do you know where this symbol came from?\par \par \uc2 One of its creators will tell the story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We also visit the historic ballpark in Alabama where Willie Mays used to play. It has been part of American history for more than a century.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait to hear that story. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 The first tropical storm of the season drenches parts of Texas and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We kept our stuff packed and if they say, we need to evacuate or anything, we will be prepared.\par \par \uc2 TIM HINCHCLIFF: If this is what the summer is like, we are going to have to really gear it up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tens of millions of Americans suffer under brutally hot temperatures.\par \par \uc2 SHEL WINKLEY: It is virtually impossible to experience temperatures like this at this time of the year without the inception of manmade climate change.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Supreme Court`s term is winding down with several major cases left to be decided.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The biggest one left of course, is that one involving former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and whether he is immune from prosecution for his alleged efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: New York State moves to ban addictive social media feeds for minors.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): They are being held captive to powerful forces outside their own control.\par \par \uc2 CC SABATHIA (Former professional baseball player): He was an American icon and he is a part of American history. You know, not just Black history or you know the Negro Leagues history, American history.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And as tributes pour in for baseball great, Willie Mays, Birmingham, Alabama unveils a new mural just miles from the historic Rickwood Fields.\par \par \uc2 CC SABATHIA: And to see everybody here and to see all of this stuff going on is because of him and it makes you feel good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CC Sabathia speaking what a lot of people are feeling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: YES.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so nice to hear all the tributes to him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To Willie Mays.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are so many. It`s a celebration of what has been a long life and a wonderful career.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, 93 is a good --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It says a lot about the footprints that Willie Mays left behind, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think so too. Ninety-three is a really good run.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour though with tropical storm, he`s got a name already, Alberto, now blamed for at least three deaths in Northeastern Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Heavy rain and strong winds continue to hammer that area this morning. People along the Texas coast, they are getting a view of the damage left by the flooding that swamped the region yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff is in the coastal city of Corpus Christi.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good morning to you once again.\par \par \uc2 Last hour, you were blowing in the wind. It still looks like the wind is kicking up a bit there. Hello.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: It is still blowing in the wind, Gayle. But think about this, the storm is hundreds and hundreds of miles from us. We can now report that it has made landfall in Mexico and is pushing on to the coast, so it will continue to lose power.\par \par \uc2 But look at what it`s done behind us, all the way back there in Corpus Christi, it is just flooded. This is the main way in and out of North Beach, so a lot of people are waking up and not able to get over there.\par \par \uc2 So let`s go over to Surfside Beach so you can see what was happening yesterday. A lot of people trying to drive through that as well. Cops had to get out on rafts and rescue people from stranded cars.\par \par \uc2 The same thing was happening in Galveston, where you saw people driving through that flooded zone. You don`t know how deep that water is. You probably don`t want to go in it, especially if you have a low-lying vehicle. We are seeing that everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And if we come back here to Corpus Christi, you can see that we had not only people driving on the road through the storm, and this was one heck of a storm here in Corpus Christi with downfalls of those rain bands coming through.\par \par \uc2 But we also saw workers out there who had to do their job no matter if it is a holiday, no matter if there is a tropical storm coming through.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, I`ve got some bad news for you. Even though this has made landfall here, the National Hurricane Center is keeping an eye on one system in the gulf down south of us and they`re keeping an eye on a system that is way off the coast of Florida.\par \par \uc2 So there are two things out there that could potentially become the next systems in this very active hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Dave. Be safe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we`ll be watching it Dave. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to go inside of the more than 100-year-old ballpark where the late Willie Mays began his historic baseball journey.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett is going to show us how Rickwood Field plans to honor Mays tonight.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our series "Facing Fertility" looks at a new program which helps women tackle the high costs of freezing their eggs, how that program also provides hope to other people looking to start a family.\par \par \uc2 But first, 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, the new Paramount+ docuseries "Chopper Cops" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Chopper Cops"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- takes us to the sky over Florida with an elite police unit. How their high-tech helicopters help fight crime from above.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we look at the history of the rainbow pride flag and share the story of one of the unsung heroes behind the iconic symbol.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In our "Facing Fertility" series, we explore reproductive hurdles and give a voice to the many women and men who suffer in silence with infertility.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re taking a look at the high cost of freezing eggs where a woman`s eggs are extracted, frozen, and stored for potential future pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 But here are some numbers to think about: In 2022, nearly 30,000 egg freezing cycles were performed in this country. That`s up from just 7,600 in 2015.\par \par \uc2 But this procedure comes with a very high price tag. The average cost to freeze eggs is $10,000.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste introduces us to a very unique program that could help women afford the treatment if they donate half of their eggs to someone else.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Ten days of painful hormone shots.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE (Egg donor): Day eight. Shot one --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: To stimulate egg growth in her ovaries.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE: Good morning on my egg retrieval. I am about to head out on the way to the clinic.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It was part of Angie`s journey to preserving her fertility.\par \par \uc2 The final step, an egg retrieval to remove as many eggs as possible and freeze them, in case Angie, now 26 years old and a medical student wants to try to have a baby when she`s older.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We ended up getting 34 eggs, as of now, so really happy.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE: There are so many eggs.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: She is only keeping half. The rest, she is donating them.\par \par \uc2 Could you have afforded to freeze your eggs?\par \par \uc2 ANGIE: No.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: That`s why she turned to the company Cofertility and its split program, which offers a woman the chance to freeze her eggs for free, when she gives half of her mature eggs to a person or couple seeking a donor.\par \par \uc2 Both the donor and intended parent must agree to match.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE: What was super appealing to me was the chance to have my eggs, the freezing covered for free.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The intended parent who has given the other half of a donor`s eggs pays all of the donor`s medical costs and a 10-year storage fee for her eggs, plus an $8,000.00 fee to Cofertility.\par \par \uc2 Split donors must be between 21 and 33 years old and undergo comprehensive medical screenings based on FDA and \uc2 American Society for Reproductive Medicine\uc2  guidelines, as well as have psychological and legal counseling.\par \par \uc2 How does it feel knowing you`ll get to keep half, but half will now go to someone else?\par \par \uc2 ANGIE: It feels so good. I`m really excited for them.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN MACKLER (Cofertility cofounder): The best time to freeze your eggs is the younger you are and frankly, when you`re least likely to be able to afford it. And then on the flip side, so many people need an egg donor today to grow their family.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Lauren Mackler is Cofertility`s cofounder. The company launched in 2022.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN MACKLER: When you pay cash compensation to a donor, she is incentivized by a check at the end. And so it`s hard to know, is she following the protocol?\par \par \uc2 In our approach, we know that the person going through it is mutually incentivized as the intended parent for a great outcome. High-yield, great quality eggs.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Do you worry that some young women may feel coerced to give away some eggs, just so they can freeze theirs?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN MACKLER: I really don`t. It`s an option that is great for some people and not for others.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: She says, for intended parents seeking fresh donor eggs, Cofertility`s model, which never pays a donor a dime is about an $8,000.00 average minimum savings compared to a traditional egg bank where donors are paid money.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN MACKLER: I believe and our team believes really strongly that this is a more ethical way to do egg donation than cash compensation.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Arthur Caplan teaches Ethics and Medicine at \uc2 NYU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR CAPLAN (NYU Grossman School of Medicine Bioethics professor): Think hard about whether you really want someone else to have a biological child that`s related to you, as the price of freezing your own eggs. I`d say make sure you understand the limits of privacy. You`re probably going to know down the road if a child was created and they may want to meet you.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Cofertility does say it cannot guarantee anonymity, but offers matches the option to be in contact or not with each other and any future child.\par \par \uc2 Did you have any hesitation about giving away some of your genetics?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (Egg donor): Yes, absolutely. I was like, this is a big decision. Is this something that I totally feel comfortable with? And I did -- I think it took me about a month-and-a-half to really be like, okay, no, this is good.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Twenty-seven-year-old, Nicole in New York City says she always planned to freeze her eggs, but donating some was a tough decision.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE: So my reservations originally were just the idea of not being involved in my own DNA out there. I`m not really going to be involved in their lives and I realized that this isn`t really about me, it`s about, you know, somebody in their family and I have the opportunity to, you know, create a family of my own.\par \par \uc2 I get really emotional while thinking about what I`m doing for someone and it just -- it really makes my heart happy.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Thirteen hundred miles away in Miami, Maiko, a gay single man needed an egg donor to have children.\par \par \uc2 Nicole gave him 17 mature eggs and she has 17 stored for herself.\par \par \uc2 MAIKO (Intended parent): It is a mutually beneficial relationship both for the donor and for myself. I lucked out. I mean, I think I won the lottery with Nicole. She`s just phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Just last month, after creating embryos with Nicole`s eggs, Maiko learned his surrogate is pregnant with twins due on Valentine`s Day.\par \par \uc2 MAIKO: I just burst out in tears. It really became real that this is happening. And just to get that good news and that it`s the twins and everything, it was just overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Is any of your emotion still that grappling with a decision that you`ve made?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE: No. I think all my emotion is just,. I`m grateful. It`s been incredible and I wouldn`t change it for the world.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Lauren Mackler told us, in the unlikely event a donor is unable to produce any or many mature eggs, she will not be responsible for any cost, the intended parent would still cover it.\par \par \uc2 Cofertility says, it offers a notably diverse pool of donors, 59 percent identify as women of color, and about half have a graduate degree or higher.\par \par \uc2 And I want to make one really important point about egg freezing. I saw the reactions to 34 eggs. So Nicole and Angie both keep about 17 eggs on average for them. That would mean about an 80 percent chance at one baby.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Seventeen eggs doesn`t mean 17 babies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seventeen babies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s clearly a very emotional decision on both sides, really. But -- so what happens if you go through the screening for the split program, and then you find out that you`re not qualified to be a donor?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Cofertility offers a keep program. So if you`re not qualified to be a donor, or you simply don`t want to donate eggs, you can freeze your eggs at a discounted cost. It`s a really good option, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This match program, I`m interested in. So the woman who donated her eggs gets to approve of who gets them?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes, yes. She has the option to say yes or no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some thinking there.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: She can see the profile of the parent interested in her eggs and that`s something that`s very different from most traditional egg banks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What I love about this series is that, as a man, I have to be honest, I thought I knew very surface level understanding about the fertility issues that women face. But this dives so much deeper, it peels back the layers, and allow me to learn a little bit more and it creates a different type of sensitivity.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It`s a really important topic and I think people have a hard time recognizing infertility as a disease and because people don`t say "I have infertility," like they might say "I have heart disease" or "I have cancer."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: So it`s really important to talk about these things.\par \par \uc2 Cofertility, I just want to mention, it is interesting, also offers a baby guarantee.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is that?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: So if a first round doesn`t work, if the intended parent doesn`t end up with a baby after one round, Cofertility will have the intended parent do another cycle with their donor or match them with a new donor and waive their $8,000.00 fee.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: But, the intended parent would still have to pay the cost of the cycle and medication.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, but there`s a genuine cap on the cost and you`re going to get the result you`re looking for.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes, it is a really interesting concept.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikki, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, tomorrow, our "Facing Fertility" series focuses on the complicated emotions around infertility. We`re going to talk with author, Marisa Renee Lee about her book "Grief is Love" and get her advice on living joyfully after experiencing infertility.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new Paramount+ docuseries co-produced by See It Now Studios is giving an in-depth look at how police protect one of Florida`s largest counties from the sky.\par \par \uc2 The series, "Chopper Cops" takes viewers into the cockpit of an elite police department and their helicopter pilots.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Chopper Cops"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Their job is to be the eyes in the sky and fight crime for the Marion County Sheriff`s Office which patrols the area larger than the state of Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Chopper Cops"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can watch all 10 episodes of "Chopper Cops" exclusively on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel I`m going to be hooked by that one.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to take you to the story of the Negro League Baseball field where Willie Mays will be honored tonight. You`re going to see how Rickwood Field has been part of American history for more than a hundred years.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What a day for a ball game.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 St. Louis Cardinals\uc2  will play the \uc2 San Francisco Giants\uc2  tonight in a game honoring baseball`s Negro League. It will be played at Rickwood Field in Birmingham, Alabama, the oldest ballpark in America still in operation.\par \par \uc2 Rickwood was once the home of the Birmingham Black Barons, a Negro League team that a very, very young Willie Mays, still a teenager played for more than 75 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett is inside the ballpark for us. Pretty sweet assignment there, Major.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good Morning, Tony. Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 Willie Mays was one of the greatest and most beloved players in Major League Baseball history, but you don`t have to love baseball or Willie Mays to be entranced by Rickwood Field.\par \par \uc2 The greatest names in the history of the sport played right here, and Negro League Baseball, a source of pride and competition for decades, a source of resilience in the African American community, Negro League Baseball thrived here.\par \par \uc2 American history and baseball history are intertwined and possibly nowhere is that more true than Rickwood Field.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: On a run-down edge of Birmingham, Alabama, Rickwood Field`s weathered seats and retro light towers have not just seen baseball history, but American history.\par \par \uc2 GERALD WATKINS (Rickwood Field chairman): Babe Ruth played here in 1929. Ty Cobb played here, so there`s just so much history here and I`m so glad that we`re going to be able to share this with the country.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: In all, 182 Hall of Famers have graced Rickwood`s grounds, legends like Reggie, Hank, and Satchel.\par \par \uc2 Gerald Watkins runs the nonprofit that`s giving the 114-year-old ballpark a future.\par \par \uc2 GERALD WATKINS: This is a place where people like Willie Mays dreamed of being in the big leagues and they made their dreams come true.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mays was a teenager on the 1948 Birmingham Black Barons, a Negro League team that shared Rickwood Field with the all-White Birmingham Barons.\par \par \uc2 Back then, Birmingham was a thriving baseball town in the segregated south.\par \par \uc2 This section here, tell me about it.\par \par \uc2 GERALD WATKINS: This is where the African American fans had to seat when they were watching the Barons and in turn when the White fans came to see the Black Barons, they would have to sit in this sections as well, so fair was fair.\par \par \uc2 AL HOLT (Black Barons 1962-1963): Rickwood Field to me started when I was eight years old.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Al Holt grew up playing at Rickwood and barnstormed with a Black Barons in 1963. Not long after every Major League club finally integrated.\par \par \uc2 AL HOLT: They were scared. They were scared they will go and come in now and take their job.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: They wanted to keep their spots on the roster.\par \par \uc2 AL HOLT: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Holt, now 83 is encouraged by Major League Baseball recently incorporating Negro League stats into the record books.\par \par \uc2 AL HOLT: Negro Baseball was ignored for a long period of time, and they could play.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH "BULL" MARBURY (Indianapolis Clowns 1957-1958): It`s always exciting to come to Rickwood and play baseball.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Joseph "Bull" Marbury played outfield for the Negro League Indianapolis Clowns.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH "BULL" MARBURY: Sometimes we were called names and had them, very disparaging. We had a hard time sometime finding a place to eat, and sometimes the living conditions were very, very bad.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Despite the hostilities and indignities, Marbury, now 85 remembers Rickwood and his playing days fondly.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH "BULL" MARBURY: If I had a point in my life I could live over, I would take the part when I played baseball. I enjoyed it. I would have played for nothing.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Rickwood is preparing for a rebirth. Its playing surface and dugouts upgraded to Big League standards ready for the first regular season Major League game ever played in Alabama, featuring the \uc2 San Francisco Giants\uc2  and \uc2 St. Louis Cardinals\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MASYN WINN (Cardinals shortstop): Oh, I can`t wait. I can`t wait to be out there.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Masyn Winn is the Cardinals shortstop.\par \par \uc2 MASYN WINN: Being maybe the only Black person on the field, it is going to be pretty special for me to represent that out there.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The game will be played in honor of the Negro League generation, by the next generation of baseball history.\par \par \uc2 GERALD WATKINS: All of these ballplayers that come out here now are going to be talking about coming here forever, because they`re going to be walking on the same kind of field that all those famous players did -- Babe Ruth, Mickey Mantle and of course, Willie Mays.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Speaking of Willie Mays, he grew up six miles from here. His father played industrial league baseball and brought Willie here to Rickwood at age 10.\par \par \uc2 At age 17, Willie was the starting centerfielder for the Birmingham Black Barons.\par \par \uc2 Now, he didn`t travel with the team because he had to attend high school. Willie Mays graduated and changed baseball history.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s game will pay tribute to Willie`s legacy and his Rickwood roots - - everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, those roots are very deep.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Awesome stuff, Major.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Major, you a baseball fan? You like the \uc2 Washington Nationals\uc2 . We were wondering about that as we watched this report.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I am a baseball fan for sure. I discovered baseball at age seven growing up in San Diego. I`m a fan of the \uc2 San Diego Padres\uc2 . I call it the longest informal abusive relationship of my life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know. Fernando Tatis is going to turn it on in the second half of the season. This is your year.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Major, we appreciate it. Thank you so much. Looking forward to tonight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love hearing the history of Rickwood, which by the way, I am never embarrassed to say, I`d never heard of it until Willie Mays died and all of this conversation happened.\par \par \uc2 But I love the player saying despite all the hostility -- Major said, despite all the hostilities and indignities, he said he still looks back on that time. If he could do it over, he wants to play baseball.\par \par \uc2 These roots are deep in this game, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, they are really deep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As I heard you say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they are. They are very deep in Alabama. It`s a big deal that the first Major League game ever is going to be played in Alabama. I`m surprised actually by that fact.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes, yes. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They have had so many great players there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, CBS MORNINGS deals. Elizabeth Werner will show us some games you might want for your backyard, for up to 30 percent off the retail price.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is time for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals.\par \par \uc2 Today, we`ve got several i