Oh.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That actually folds up into this beautiful wooden box. All the kids are loving this. They bring them tailgating, they bring them to college.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really high quality.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: High quality.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because some of the stuff you buy at these stores, they fall apart within days.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Exactly. This is heirloom quality. This is what stays with you, you`re going to use this, 10 years, 20 years, 30 years, 40 years.\par \par \uc2 So these are starting off at $60.00. Right now, they`re up to 30 percent off. They`re going to last a lifetime and just think of the memories that are going to last a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great for the price, really.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, to play --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elizabeth Werner, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always love games. Always.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You play dominoes?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals.\par \par \uc2 To buy anything you saw, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com. Be sure to follow us on \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to our most popular deals.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, for this Pride Month, one of the creators of the rainbow pride flag shares little known story about the origins of that now famous symbol.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, in honor of Pride Month, we`re taking a look at the history and evolution of the rainbow pride flag that we`ve all come to know.\par \par \uc2 While the flag is now a celebrated symbol for the LGBTQ+ community. The full story behind its creation has remained largely untold.\par \par \uc2 You know what? I asked everybody in the studio, there`s a lot of smart people here including Klaus who is shooting me right now, none of us know the origin.\par \par \uc2 Nobody knew the answer to this question. That`s why we`re very glad that Elise Preston sat down was one of the flag`s creators who shared her personal story reflected on how far this symbol has come.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For Lynn Segerblom, most mornings look like this.\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM (Woman behind the rainbow flag): These are rocks, too.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Each day, treated like a blank canvas and always starting off with a splash of color.\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: I want yellow.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Fitting for a woman who used to go by the nickname, "Rainbow."\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: So I had a driver`s license that said Fairy Argyle Rainbow on it since 1976.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: A simple fusion of colors transformed into something more powerful when Segerblom and friends crafted a piece of rainbow artwork that brought a storm of change to California in the summer of 1978.\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: I did work at the community center. I rented a room there to do my dyes in. It was only the second time there was a Gay Freedom Day Parade in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 And they`re like, well, we got permission to use the flagpoles at the Civic Center. Do you guys want to go down there and look at them? Think of what we could do with the flag poles.\par \par \uc2 So we did. We went down, and of course, it gave me ideas.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The idea she says was a striped rainbow flag that soon became the symbol of the Gay Rights Movement.\par \par \uc2 You could have created anything. Why those colors?\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: I really love color, and what is a rainbow besides a beautiful phenomenon of light and color in nature? It`s all the colors. It`s the full spectrum.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: But credit for the flag`s creation has historically gone to Gilbert Baker.\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: Gilbert and myself, this is the Stars and Stripes flag.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: A late friend and activist who Segerblom says helped sew and promote the flags. The problem she says is that he was just one of several contributors who should be collectively recognized.\par \par \uc2 How did it feel to not get that credit?\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: At the time, I didn`t know I wasn`t getting the credit, you know, and this was a labor of love for all of us. Nobody got paid. You`re there because you want to be.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Yes. Do you feel that the lack of inclusion of you had anything to do with your gender?\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: Maybe, but James McNamara got no credit and he is the one that taught Gilbert Baker how to sew. He passed away from AIDS in the 90s.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: I am so sorry to hear about your friend.\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: It`s hard to go through, because it`s your friends, it`s your roommates, it`s your hairdresser. It`s your people that you meet on a Friday. And then they`re sick and they`re not going to get any better, and then they`re gone.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: That hurt turned into hope, as rainbow flags helped wave in a new chapter in history with additional colors and stripes added over the years to promote the diversity and inclusivity of the LGBTQ+ community.\par \par \uc2 TERRA RUSSELL-SLAVIN (\uc2 Los Angeles LGBT Center\uc2  director of policy and community building): We have a number of flags to represent different elements of our community.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The history of the Gay Rights Movement is honored here by Terra Russell-Slavin`s team at the \uc2 Los Angeles LGBT Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TERRA RUSSELL-SLAVIN: The overall sense, right, was that people were fighting for basic, just basic equality. It was a time of people often living in the closet, not being able to be openly out. And for those who did, it took a lot of courage.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: When the flag was created in 1978, how did it galvanize the movement?\par \par \uc2 TERRA RUSSELL-SLAVIN: I think it gave people a symbol or imagery to identify with the movement and I think that is important when you`re trying to create visibility, when you`re trying to create acceptance.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Yet, the symbol made out of love has been met with hate.\par \par \uc2 GLAAD\uc2  reports during Pride Month last year alone, there were at least 145 incidents of anti-LGBTQ hate documented nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: But at Pride parades being held around the world this month, the rainbow flags still wave strong.\par \par \uc2 LYNN SEGERBLOM: I love when I am up and about and I see rainbow flags. I love it.\par \par \uc2 I`m like, yes, please do it more.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: An ode to the power of some paint and a vision can have, even 46 years later.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Elise Preston in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now we know. I think it`s such a beautiful symbol, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like what she said, it represents the full spectrum. That`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good history lesson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240620ek6k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Deadly Florida Flooding Continues; Pledging Support for Ukraine; Escaped Houston Inmate Captured; Abortion Pill Access Preserved; Trump Back on the Hill; Evans Gershkovich Goes to Trial; Scathing Report Slams Phoenix Police; Alex Jones Defamation Hearing; Farm to Lunch Table; New Bird Flu Warning; Best Books of 2024 So Far; CBS MORNINGS Deals, Rachel Chinouriri`s Journey, CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Manuel Bojorquez,  Stephanie Abrams, Nancy Cordes, Carter Evans, Jan Crawford, Robert Costa,  Kris Van Cleave, Omar Villafranca, Roxana Saberi, Celine Gounder, Anthony  Mason \par \uc2 14,623 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this Friday. Happy Fri-yay to you as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is off today, but we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The flooding in South Florida turns deadly after a historic rainfall. The latest on the danger as streets are inundated with water.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s like a beginning to a zombie movie this cars littered everywhere on top of sidewalks, in the median, in the middle of the street. No lights on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A major Supreme Court ruling on abortion rights preserves access to an abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: It doesn`t have anything to do with whether or not abortion is a protected liberty.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Alex Jones could soon pay up for lying about the Newtown school shooting. We speak to some of the families.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE PARKER (Father of Sandy Hook Elementary shooting victim, Emilie Parker): Accountability helps bring another layer of closure.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: \uc2 Florida Panthers\uc2  are one win away from the Stanley Cup after holding on to beat the Oilers four-three last night.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON ("The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon" host): Sunday is Father`s Day.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON: That special time when a scratch-off lottery ticket at the gas station can also mean I love you.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): It`s a lot like Mother`s Day in that your mom organize d the plans for both.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2  ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a meeting with -- oh, he called a meeting with all the House Republicans on Capitol Hill. It was his first visit to the Hill since January 6th. They always return to the scene of the crime.\par \par \uc2 (Laugh)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2 : They sang happy birthday to Trump. He turned 78.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER ("The Daily Show): And the worst part is that Ted Cruz sang it sexy like Marilyn Monroe just --\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Close. You can`t unsee that, everybody.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by progressive making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, thank you, Jordan Klepper. That was quite the visual.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Thank you, Klepper.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "The Daily Show" always brings it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with the historic rainfall that`s drenching Southern Florida. You`ve seen the pictures.\par \par \uc2 This was the view from the sky yesterday. The streets were inundated with water, inundated is the word here. We`re talking about very dangerous conditions. At least two people have died.\par \par \uc2 There are even pictures of fish swimming in the streets. Some parts of Florida have seen up to two feet of rain.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in hard -hit Miami.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Manuel.\par \par \uc2 I`m trying to look at the picture and look at you at the same time. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Fortunately, the storms last night were not as bad as the day before. So we`ve noticed this morning that the water is actually starting to go down in many of the impacted neighborhoods, but there are still pockets of Miami that look like this, and there are more storms in the forecast. The concern is they could unleash a whole new set of problems.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: It is expected to be a fourth straight day of excessive rainfall in South Florida, flooding countless homes and roadways from Fort Myers to Fort Lauderdale, which has already been hard hit.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It looked like a beginning to zombie movie. Just cars littered everywhere, on top of sidewalks, and the median, in the middle of the street.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: North of Miami, the driver of this vehicle drove into a canal mistaking it for a road. First responders had to use divers and a crane to fish it out. The driver was able to escape.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, officials continued to remind people to stay off the roads.\par \par \uc2 MAN: If you don`t have to be out, don`t be out.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Hallandale Beach was the scene of some of the worst flooding earlier this week after storms dropped roughly 20 inches of rain in just a few hours.\par \par \uc2 Although the city is no stranger to intense thunderstorms, for some longtime residents, his deluge has been different.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Thirteen years, there`s been storms, there`s been rain, heavy rain but never, never like this. This is extreme.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: This is the worst we`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Three miles north in Hollywood, Florida, Eric Aragon was forced to evacuate his home after it flooded. He and his family were forced to escape out their window.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you that it`s going to happen again with the storms in the forecast?\par \par \uc2 ERIC ARAGON (Hollywood, Florida resident): Well, to be honest with you, I don`t think I want to sit around here because guess what? It`s only going to get worse. And honestly, I don`t want anything to happen to myself or my family. So yes, we`re going to leave.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: There is a Flood Watch in effect for much of South Florida through tonight, but the rain could last into tomorrow -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Manny, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on the rain and extreme heat in the West, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning. Do you have any good news?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, I wish I did, but unfortunately we`re going to see more rain in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for virtual view reality, we can take you to Miami and show you what the rounds of thunderstorms will look like today. Rain will be falling on saturated ground, so we still have that possibility of flooding.\par \par \uc2 Now the showers won`t be as widespread as the last few days, but there`s still plenty of moisture to squeeze out of the atmosphere. It could be quite intense at times, especially into the Keys this evening. There will be a few hit and miss storms as we head into our Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Now overall, the rainfall totals will be much lower, but we could see locally heavy accumulations. In the Northeast, thunderstorms will stretch from New England to the mid-Atlantic this afternoon and evening. They will continue overnight, but we are going to be clearing out for the rest of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 But that`s when the sultry temperatures will be creeping north. By Monday, over 150 million will have highs above 90 and Nate, I`ve got to say, these temperatures are going to be so hot next week that many, several -- a lot of records are going to be broken.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. Got to hydrate, hydrate, hydrate. It is going to be hot.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: At the G7 Summit in Italy, President Biden is sending a clear message to Russia that the US plans to support Ukraine for the long haul. The president signed a 10-year security agreement with Ukraine. The US is also taking the lead in providing a $50 billion loan paid for by frozen Russian assets.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is covering the G7 Summit.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 A short time ago, the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders welcomed a special guest into their midst, Pope Francis who arrived via helicopter in style here in Southern Italy. It`s his first G7 Summit. He is here to talk with the other leaders about artificial intelligence as they grapple with how to make sure this technology develops in a manner that is ethical and protects the workforce.\par \par \uc2 The other big focus here, as you mentioned, has been Ukraine and how to change the dynamic in this two-and-a-half year war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Ukraine notched a series of victories, Thursday, far from the battlefield. First was the long-term security agreement signed by President Biden and President Zelenskyy. Pledging US military aid and training for the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian president): Today is a truly historic day.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Then all the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders agreed on a new $50 billion loan to help Ukraine rebuild, to be paid for with the interest from frozen Russian assets.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY: It is a vital step forward in providing sustainable support for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The US also imposed new sanctions on foreign banks that do business with Russia and announced that five countries had agreed to ship new patriot missile batteries to Ukraine soon.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): You`ll have some relatively quickly.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: All of this is designed to send a message to Russia`s \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who has more money and more manpower than Ukraine does.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We cannot wait this out. It cannot divide us and we will be with Ukraine until they prevail in this war.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders gathered here also discussed the Middle East with President Biden sounding much more pessimistic about the prospects of a ceasefire deal between \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Israel.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Are you confident it`s going to be done soon, sir?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : No. I`m not -- I`m not -- I haven`t lost hope, but it is going to be tough.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Here in Italy, Biden was also asked about his son, Hunter`s conviction, Tuesday on three federal gun charges.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I said, I`d abide by the jury decision, I will do that and I will not pardon him.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  faces anything from probation to years in jail when he is sentenced later this year. President Biden was asked whether he would commute his son`s sentence and he answered with just one word, "No" - - Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy Cordes in Italy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a breaking news out of Houston where a prisoner has been recaptured after hours on the run. Officials say, Joshua Nigel Thomas Sanders had escaped police custody to a courthouse after holding a woman at knifepoint.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: So approximately, 3:00 AM this morning, our search efforts came to an end when we finally located and captured the suspect.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: More than 12 hours after sparking a massive search in downtown Houston, police captured 35 -year-old Joshua Nigel Thomas Sanders. Police said a special unit known as the Violent Offenders Fugitive Task Force captured the escaped inmate who resisted.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And at that point, our officers and deputies deployed Taser and the suspect was then taken into custody. At this point, he is currently in a hospital.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Police also said they arrested two others on charges of hindering the apprehension of a suspect. Sanders` capture caps a day of searching by sea, by land, and in the air earlier Thursday afternoon using drones and K-9 units on the ground.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Unfortunately, we do believe that he is a dangerous individual.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Sanders had been in court facing burglary and unlawful weapon possession charges.\par \par \uc2 Harris County Sheriff`s Office officials say after that appearance and while on his way back to jail, Sanders was able to escape custody, but didn`t reveal how.\par \par \uc2 Police say Sanders held an employee for the District Attorney`s Office against her will in her car at knifepoint. Then after escaping the courthouse, officials say he accosted her and got into her car.\par \par \uc2 Police said the woman only drove a few feet away before she crashed into another vehicle.\par \par \uc2 MAN: After the car was disabled during the accident, the subject got out of the car and ran down the street.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: They said the woman held by Sanders was shaken up, but is okay.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Omar Villafranca.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We expect more decisions from the Supreme Court this morning. Yesterday, the justices rejected restrictions on a drug used in abortion. It was the court`s first abortion related ruling since it overturn the Roe v. Wade decision. That was two years ago now.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning. This was a big decision yesterday.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, it was, Gayle, and people are saying on both sides that the fight over the pill is far from over, but it is remarkable that the justices were able to find common ground in a divisive case.\par \par \uc2 I mean, unanimously rejecting the idea that access to the abortion pill to be scaled back and saying this group of anti-abortion doctors had no business filing a lawsuit over how that pill was approved.\par \par \uc2 Now the decision like I said was unanimous, written by Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and it reversed some lower court judges, conservative judges who had okayed the lawsuit against the FDA to restrict access to that drug nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s not a ruling on the merits. It`s on standing, but it still has some really important implications. Sixty-three percent of abortions last year were medication abortions, and the lower court ruling, if the court hadn`t reversed it would have ended prescriptions by mail or telehealth and required women to see a healthcare provider in person to get the pill.\par \par \uc2 Now Justice Kavanaugh wrote that, instead of going to the courts, citizens and doctors who object to what the law allow others to do, may always take their concerns to the legislative branches.\par \par \uc2 Now, three states -- Idaho, Missouri and Kansas -- they are vowing to kind of take up the fight and sue, but I think based on how this decision is written, Gayle, it is really hard to see how those states will have legal standing either.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I think people were really surprised as you point out, the ruling was unanimous, nine to nothing. But we know there are a lot of contentious cases that are still coming up.\par \par \uc2 So what other opinions are you watching for these days?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Oh, Gayle, I mean, there is a lot and you are right. These are not going to be unanimous. There`s about a dozen big ones, including some major ones. There`s another abortion case, on emergency room care. We`ve got those two cases involving former President Trump on whether he can be prosecuted for his actions around January 6 and charged with obstruction.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got environment, we`ve got gun control. I mean, get ready. It is going to be a big next two or three weeks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, and the good news, Jan is that you`ll be there bringing it out and sorting it all out for us. We always appreciate that. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  returned to Capitol Hill yesterday for the first time since thousands of his supporters assaulted the Capitol on January 6, 2021. The expected GOP presidential nominee met with Republicans in Congress and said they were unified ahead of the election.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa was there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: A spectacle of support on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): There is tremendous unity in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President Trump returned to Washington, Thursday to huddle with congressional Republicans who treated him with reverence.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m with them a thousand percent. They are with me a thousand percent. I`ve had a really great relationship with just about everybody here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Even Senate GOP Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who once called Trump morally responsible for the Capitol attack offered a hand after not speaking since late 2020.\par \par \uc2 For Trump, the visit was also a chance to reassure his party after becoming a convicted felon last month. In a closed-door meeting with House Republican, sources say he lashed out at the Justice Department, calling it dirty along with an expletive.\par \par \uc2 This was Trump`s first visit to the Hill since the January 6th attack in 2021, and Republicans seem to avoid the topic.\par \par \uc2 Did January 6 ever come up?\par \par \uc2 MAN: It did not.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Still, the Biden campaign put a hot political spotlight on Trump`s return.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: On January 6, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lit a fire in this country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: One person not meeting with the former president, Maryland Republican Senate candidate, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , a Trump critic, but on Thursday, Trump threw a curveball and said he would endorse him in a key race that could decide the balance of power in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I would like to see him win, and we`ve got to take the majority.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Hogan however didn`t welcome it. A spokesperson said Governor Hogan has been clear he is not supporting President Trump, just as he did in in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: To understand how cozy it got, just know this, as senators left the meeting, they told me they presented former President Trump with a birthday cake to celebrate his 78th birthday today -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It seemed like they were all happy to see him.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Moving overseas, Russian officials say American journalist, \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  will stand trial on espionage charges.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" reporter has been detained in Russia for more than a year. He is accused of spying on a state military facility for the CIA. Gershkovich, his new newspaper, the State Department, they deny those charges.\par \par \uc2 If he is convicted, he faces up to 20 years in a Russian prison. Officials did not say when his trial will begin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are all watching this and want him back home.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a very disturbing new report about the Phoenix Police Department, what a massive federal investigation uncovered. But first, it is 7:16 exactly, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, the growing bird flu outbreak that has spread to cows in at least a dozen states. Dr. Celine Gounder will talk about the virus and the potential threat to humans.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a look at one man`s project to feed schoolchildren in his home state. It`s called hydroponics and it could be coming to a school near you.\par \par \uc2 And indie rock legends, R.E.M. reunite onstage at the Songwriters Hall of Fame ceremony.\par \par \uc2 In "What to Watch," we will show you the other names who were honored last night.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A sweeping federal investigation into the Phoenix Police Department found a pattern of discrimination against a wide range of people, including what he called unjustified violence and even deaths.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice released the results of a nearly three-year inquiry yesterday. Kris Van Cleave reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The 126-page DOJ report paints a damning picture of the Phoenix Police Department alleging the agency engaged in a pattern or practice of discriminating against Black, Hispanic, and Native Americans using excessive force including unjustified deadly force, violating free speech rights and unlawfully detaining homeless people.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN CLARKE (Civil Rights assistant US attorney general): The police department turned a blind eye to the data, ignored these unmistakable warnings and failed to uncover its own discriminatory policing patterns.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The Phoenix Police Union flatly rejected the report.\par \par \uc2 DARRELL KRIPLEAN (The Phoenix Law Enforcement Association president): This investigation is a farce. In the end, the taxpayers are fleeced and our community will be less safe.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Among the findings, between 2016 and 2022, more than a third of all Phoenix Police arrests were people experiencing homelessness. Black people were seven times more likely to be arrested for marijuana possession than Whites; Hispanic people, three times more likely; and Native Americans were 44 times more likely than White people to be cited or arrested for possessing or consuming alcohol.\par \par \uc2 Interim Police Chief Michael Sullivan --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SULLIVAN (Interim Phoenix Police Department chief): We are midway through 2024, we`ve made a lot of changes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Sullivan and city manager, Jeff Barton told us they`re still reviewing the report`s findings.\par \par \uc2 You read it, sort of the 30,000-foot takeaway. If you look like me, you`re going to have one experience with the Phoenix Police Department, and if you don`t, you are far more likely to have a much more negative experience.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BARTON (Phoenix City manager): What they`re presenting his findings, they are troubling. And if that is in fact where we are, we will fix it and we will correct it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SULLIVAN: It is important that we look very, very deeply because we have to build trust and you know, those numbers as presented doesn`t build trust.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now DOJ is calling for federal oversight of the police department. Phoenix`s elected leaders will meet later this month to discuss how the nation`s fifth largest city will respond.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very disturbing findings there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The only way you improve the system is to first acknowledge that it is broken.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Acknowledge and say we`re going to fix it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we hear from families of Sandy Hook victims as their fight for justice against conspiracy theorist, Alex Jones reaches a critical stage.\par \par \uc2 Your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 The families of Newtown shooting victims may soon be closer to getting financial compensation from Alex Jones.\par \par \uc2 For years, the Infowars host and conspiracy theorist called the 2012 shooting a hoax. The family sued and won to the tune of $1.5 billion. Jones then filed for bankruptcy, but today a judge will decide if he can liquidate his assets to pay up.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca spoke to two of the families hoping to finally achieve some justice.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE PARKER: The reason that accountability is important for me is because that`s part of the healing process.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: For Robbie Parker, today`s hearing is yet another step in his 12-year healing journey.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE PARKER: There is the forgiveness part, that`s a very internal and very sacred and personal thing, and then there`s the things that you see in your life that have been impacted because of things that Alex Jones has done.\par \par \uc2 And so that accountability helps bring another layer of closure.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Parker`s daughter, Emilie, a first grader was one of the 26 people killed in the Sandy Hook School shooting.\par \par \uc2 In the immediate aftermath, conspiracy theorist, Alex Jones called the shooting a hoax and the parents and children involved, crisis actors. His followers threatened and harassed parents for years.\par \par \uc2 But facing defamation lawsuits brought by the families, he relented in court.\par \par \uc2 ALEX JONES (Infowars host): It is a hundred percent real.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: And judges in Texas and Connecticut ordered him to pay families $1.5 billion,\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE PARKER: I lost my own power. I lost my voice for a long time, I felt like I couldn`t be who I was and I really disconnected from that for a really long time.\par \par \uc2 It took me joining this lawsuit to finally start to be able to find and reclaim some of those things again.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Today`s hearing could lead to a liquidation of Jones` assets to start payment, including his online platform Infowars, where he peddled the Sandy Hook lies.\par \par \uc2 If that platform goes away that he had, is there any satisfaction in that?\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE PARKER: In the sense that that`s unfortunately, in this case, what accountability looks like.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: The amount of the verdicts in these cases in Texas and Connecticut is extraordinarily high, but we have to remember how extraordinary the damages were.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: CBS News legal analyst, Rikki Klieman says if granted, this liquidation could be just the beginning of Jones` financial burden.\par \par \uc2 Is he still on the hook if he starts a new business?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: In a case involving defamation, where the injuries that are sustained come from someone who has acted maliciously and willfully, that debt, if you will, is not extinguished. They can continue to go to seek more.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: For Scarlett Lewis, like Robbie Parker, financial relief is secondary in her fight for her son, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS (Mother of Sandy Hook shooting victim, Jesse Lewis): You can forgive someone, but that doesn`t mean that you forget. They need to be held accountable so that they don`t do it to someone else.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: This hearing comes at a poignant time in these parents` lives. Jesse Lewis and Emilie Parker would have graduated high school on Wednesday. Their former classmates wearing green and white pins to honor their memory.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS: I am really excited to see what they do with their lives. You can definitely find purpose in your pain.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Robbie Parker`s purpose these days is to make sure his daughter`s story is told truthfully.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE PARKER: I love hearing stories about Emilie even if it`s -- they`re not like dad stories that you get sick of after 50 times, right? They are meaningful and they`re amazing and they`re awesome.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The hearing starts at 10:00 AM local time. Alex Jones is on the witness list, but it`s unclear if he`s going to show up in court. We reached out to his attorney, but did not hear back.\par \par \uc2 If he shows up in court, Robbie Parker tells me, it won`t bother him at all. He is more focused on accountability, justice for his daughter.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what everybody is thinking. I hope he does show up. He should face those families face-to-face.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that thing is so painful. I reached out to one of the mothers, Nelba Marquez-Greene, her little daughter, Ana, you know, because we`ve done a lot of coverage about the graduation and she said that this anniversary, this graduation was even more painful than the anniversary. She described it as knee-buckling pain.\par \par \uc2 So just think about it. I`ve just called to check on her yesterday because this has all been in the news. Then you see a story like that, what Alex Jones has done to deny what those people went through.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s heartbreaking --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It makes me very angry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It sets a blaze to other conspiracy theorists out there --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- who attack these families --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- that are dealing with the worst thing a parent can deal with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What he did was odious and repulsive, and it can never be enough money to repay to assuage the feelings that these families have, but I hope today that when the judge renders that judgment --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- that we never have to hear about Alex Jones.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Adds another layer of hope.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope it sends a big message.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, we show you an indoor farm where students are growing their own fresh food giving school lunches a big time upgrade.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, our series, "Rebuilding America" looks at a school lunch revolution in the middle of the country. Now, you probably remember your school lunches as unappetizing piles of meat and overcooked vegetables, but not in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Some schools are not only growing their own food, but also harvesting and serving it the same day.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi shows us how one man`s experience at an urban farm led to fresh and tastier foods for students hundreds of miles away.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: At Ashwaubenon High School near Green Bay, Wisconsin, a fresh take on cafeteria food is flourishing.\par \par \uc2 BOY: When you get a bite of the salad, it is just amazing.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I eat it every day in school.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: So fresh.\par \par \uc2 Do you know where it comes from?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: No idea.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: It was grown and picked just down the hallway.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN (Nutrition coordinator): Fresh food can be grown easily in Wisconsin in the middle of the winter.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: With no soil in sight, this indoor hydroponic garden relies on circulating water, special nutrients, and around the clock light from LEDs.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN: We have done tomatoes, beans, sugar snap peas.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Each month, the machines cost around $360.00 to run, but nutrition coordinator, Kaitlin Tauriainen says the school is actually saving money by growing 850 pounds of produce for up to 2,000 students instead of buying organic greens.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN: A lot of our kids aren`t exposed to fresh foods at home just because it is financially hard for the families to purchase those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Is this the only place where they`re getting fresh vegetables?\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN: It may be, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TYINK (Fork Farms): It sets up in about 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: This system stemmed from the imagination of Wisconsin native, Alex Tyink.\par \par \uc2 Trained as an opera singer, he got into rooftop gardening in New York City between gigs and turned the 2,500-year-old technique into a modern day company called Fork Farms.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TYINK: Food is already having to travel further and further to get from seed to plate. Our food system is failing us.\par \par \uc2 MARK GEIRACH (Runs a food bank near Milwaukee): It`s probably some of the best lettuce I`ve ever eaten.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Mark Geirach got grants to buy two of the $5,000. 00 devices for the food bank he runs near Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 MARK GEIRACH: As the cost of food continue to rise, it becomes more valuable than anything else.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: In Milwaukee Public Schools where school officials say more than 80 percent of students are economically disadvantaged, 80 units have sprouted.\par \par \uc2 What kind of plants are these?\par \par \uc2 THEO HUR (11th grader): Chives and cilantro.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Eleventh graders, Zab Xinjiang (sp?) and Theo Hur (sp?) help manage the largest one in the basement of Vincent High School.\par \par \uc2 So did some plants die along the way?\par \par \uc2 THEO HUR: Yes, a lot.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: A lot.\par \par \uc2 ZAB XINJIANG (11th grader): A lot. Since we do grow culturally diverse vegetables, I`ve learned a lot more because at first, I didn`t know what bok choy or tatsoi was, and I really liked it.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: And teacher Tyler Foote says they`re harvesting more than just vegetables.\par \par \uc2 TYLER FOOTE (Teacher): We want to show students that we are able to grow food wherever and however.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: They`re planting seeds for a healthier future.\par \par \uc2 Can you one day feed your whole school with what you grow here?\par \par \uc2 TYLER FOOTE: It would be a dream of ours to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Roxana Saberi in Appleton, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up ahead, the stories you`ll be talking about today, but right now, it is 7:42, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some big things coming up for you at eight o`clock, including more of Anthony`s exclusive interview with rock legend, R.E.M. along with their first performance in 17 years. They were inducted into the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Anthony was there. He will bring us up-to-date on that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our favorite clips from a special Father`s Day edition of CBS` "The Greatest @Home Videos" with Cedric the Entertainer. Lots to choose from.\par \par \uc2 And first on CBS MORNINGS, the best books of the year so far, that`s according to Amazon Books. All of that in our next hour, but right now Tony is in today. We`re going over the table. Vlad has moved over Nate to getting ready for --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Friday Edition. Fri-yay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Fri-yay to you. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yay-yay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay-yay.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: "Rebuilding America" is sponsored by Real Stihl. Find yours at StihlDealers.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love this song -- Steely Dan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Forever classic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Forever classic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love this song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Reelin` in the Years."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Reelin` in the Years."\par \par \uc2 It is time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 Before Pope Francis went to the G7 Summit today, he invited more than 100 comedians and entertainers to the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 So we`re talking Whoopi Goldberg, Chris Rock, Julia Louis-Dreyfus, Jimmy Fallon, Conan O`Brien, Stephen Colbert -- all part of this large group.\par \par \uc2 The Pope told them, they have the gift to unite people and make them smile. He also said it`s okay to make fun of God as long as it`s not offensive. Okay. Okay.\par \par \uc2 Francis blessed everyone and told them to continue to cheer people up. I love some of the quotes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, wait. Hold on. Colbert said, Am I excited? Is the Pope Catholic?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good Stephen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice. Nice. Well played.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Made you guys laugh. That`s good. Chris Rock said please -- no, there was another one, "Chris, please for safety`s sake. I would just keep Mary Magdalene`s name out your mouth."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh. Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They can`t it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They cannot help themselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what an honor, A. number one to be included.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the material that they will come back with. I think -- I think that is so cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it is the truth. Comedians, they have the ability to unite us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Laughter, it makes us all the same, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Would you not be a little nervous? Like they`re -- like showing the Pope, this is -- Mr. Nate Burleson, this is the material he does, and you`re up there talking about like, something racy and he`s like --\par \par \uc2 GA