hey have delivered a great game consistently --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They have delivered it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And they`ve been pros.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- for the WNBA.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`re handling all of this very well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`re getting attacked for no reason.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Both Caitlin and Angel Reese.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love that sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The other sentiment I love, Songwriters Hall of Fame because I love music. They welcome its newest inductees last night right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2024 Songwriters Hall of Fame Induction and Awards Gala)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s rapper and producer Timbaland --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Timbaland.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- who is part of the 2024 class. He conducted a medley of his songs including Aaliyah`s "Are You That Somebody."\par \par \uc2 Also inducted country music hitmaker, Hillary Lindsey who wrote "Girl Crush." Gayle, I remember that when that song came out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I remember that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You were like, that was your jam for a long time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that song. Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Performed that with Keith Urban. Dean Pitchford, who co- wrote "Fame."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s Kevin Bacon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Kevin Bacon?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Kevin Bacon because he wrote "Fame" -- not Kevin Bacon, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Footloose."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right, "Footloose." He was in that movie, of course, shrined in that movie, and his brother Michael hit the stage to perform that classic.\par \par \uc2 Donald Fagan and the late Walter Becker of Steely Dan were also honored, along with R.E.M. who performed publicly for the first time in nearly two decades.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason will have much more of his exclusive interview with the band in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he was there, so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he was there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He goes every year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he goes every single year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He loves it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He says it`s one of the best events,.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Every year, he comes --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, the top books of the year so far, according to Amazon Books. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When the members of R.E.M. were inducted into the Songwriters Hall of Fame last night, they thrilled the crowd with a surprise performance, a reminder of just how great they were together.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2024 Songwriters Hall of Fame Induction and Awards Gala)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Coming up, part two of our special conversation with the band.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, part two of Anthony as in Mason`s exclusive interview with R.E.M., new inductees to the Songwriters Hall of Fame. We`ve got clips from their first performance together in 17 years. Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Looking for something really good to read this summer, first on CBS MORNINGS, Sarah Gelman will reveal Amazon Book`s top picks of the year.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Cedric the Entertainer is always true to his name, including his new Father`s Day edition of the greatest at-home videos and we will share our top dad picks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two top dads right here, but first here`s today`s -- it`s true -- today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thanks, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Never, never like this. This is extreme.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Flooded communities in South Florida cannot catch a break amid more historic rainfall.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ARAGON: When the water started raising, I got really, really scared, the reason being is because you know, I might lose everything.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Biden and other world leaders pledge not to give up on support for Ukraine at the G7 Summit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Breaking news, a prisoner who escaped from police custody in Houston is recaptured after a massive manhunt.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There`s no more threats at this point from this suspect to any public safety.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey. Uh-oh. Guys, get off the hammock.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A man in Vermont had some very strong words for a pair of misbehaving bears who invaded his yard. He wasn`t playing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They both kind of just looked at me. They were saying, hey, what`s up?\par \par \uc2 Bears, unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 That`s not for you guys.\par \par \uc2 Get out of here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that how --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The bears understands it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now that you have outdoor space, you worried about bears in Brooklyn?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, no. I think in New York City, I`m okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re okay?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But I like how they`re talking to the bear like a person like, hey, that`s not for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Get off that furniture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Beat it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We don`t have to worry about bears in New York City.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad is very right about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Rats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I saw rats yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s New York.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is New York. We do have to worry about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It went across the street walking down the street.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, we`re going to begin this hour with the latest on a dangerous bird flu and concerns that the virus has the potential to become -- here you go the P word, pandemic -- yikes.\par \par \uc2 The H5N1 virus has already spread to dairy cows and a few humans. So far, there have been outbreaks in livestock in at least 12 states. Four cases have been reported in humans in three states. Three people were exposed to infected cows and one person to infected poultry.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is here. Hopefully she`s going to make us feel better, sort this out.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Dr. Celine.\par \par \uc2 I don`t want to feel here we go again, but I remember when we first heard about COVID, I`m not comparing them. It`s like if you weren`t in China and had been to China, don`t worry about it.\par \par \uc2 Bird flu, there was just one case. Don`t worry about it. Nothing to see here. And now, the numbers seem to be climbing up.\par \par \uc2 At what point do we start worrying?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: What really concerns us is how do you develop a pandemic flu? It is where you have two different strains of the virus, so a bird flu and a human flu mixing, sort of swapping body parts kind of like Frankenstein. It`s when you get those Frankenstein flus that you get a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 You have humans, the dairy farm workers who are currently getting bird flu. We have regular flu season coming up in a couple of months. That`s when the danger could really emerge for that kind of pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But just the fact that they`re even using the word pandemic at this stage.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is very triggering to all of us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it true.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It is triggering, but imagine six months before COVID. If we had taken steps then, if we could have avoided what all happened. I mean, that was painful for a couple of years there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so you`re hearing about bird flu and farmers, to most people, it seems like they`re very isolated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How quickly, how widely can this spread?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, flu is highly infectious. So if we are dealing with a pandemic flu, then it`s generally spread person-to-person through the respiratory route. So far, what we`re seeing, we think, is most of the transmission is occurring through the milk, through equipment or you know, other items that are contaminated with milk.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: And then from cow to cow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: But we don`t have a great handle on exactly how things are being transmitted. We have some theories, but without testing enough, it`s very hard to hone in on this is exactly the thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So one of the missteps the last pandemic was we perhaps didn`t get out in front of it early enough. What are we doing now to prepare should this become something we need to worry about?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Good question.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Oh, it`s a great question. So you know, how do you incentivize people to get tested? That`s really the such situation we`re in right now. So how do you incentivize testing of cows? How do you incentivize testing of milk? How do you incentivize testing of people?\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 USDA\uc2  has a program, a compensation program for the farmers, so that they have less of an economic hit if there are infected cows on the farm. So far, that hasn`t led to a lot more testing and they only are required to test if they`re moving cows over state borders.\par \par \uc2 So they could be moving them within the state, there is no testing requirements. In terms of the farmworkers, a lot of them are undocumented. And so they don`t want to be on any kind of government roster, database, whatever.\par \par \uc2 So unless you kind of -- you address their immigration deportation fears, they`re probably not going to want to get tested.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So are you feeling that if we`re not in contact with farmworkers, or farm animals or farm equipment, that --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Drinking raw milk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, or drinking raw milk.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That could be contaminated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That we don`t have to worry about it? Is that what you`re thinking or --\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, in the immediate term, and there are ongoing tests of milk, for example, because depending on how much virus is in the milk supply, pasteurization may continue to be good enough, it may not.\par \par \uc2 And so there`s ongoing testing to make sure is that adequate? But if you do get one of those Frankenstein flus where you have a combination of a bird flu and a human flu.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Then that`s still possible, that`s when all of us really start to need to get worried.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. Well, thank you for adding a little bit of clarity. We appreciate you, Dr. Celine Gounder.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a surprise from R.E.M. at the Songwriters Hall of Fame ceremony, and more of their exclusive interview with Anthony. Part one was amazing. Wait for part two.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Monday on CBS MORNINGS, we are launching a new series with David Begnaud. We will reveal the name of it on Monday, but here`s a preview of the first installment.\par \par \uc2 It is where David and his team go to North Carolina to surprise a young man with a day that made his dream come true. Check it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There`s the man. Hi Quentin. Nice to meet you.\par \par \uc2 We met Quentin Hines Jr. (sp?) at his family`s home in Charlotte, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So you`re 11 years old?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR. (11 years old): Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And you got a professional lawn care business.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I heard you have some dreams about working at the airport.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: So like ever since I was younger, I always wanted to get a contract with the Charlotte Airport.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I`ve got a surprise for you.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: What is it?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Look at this, son. Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Quentin was living his dream.\par \par \uc2 Come on, Mama. What you thinking?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m speechless.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well, we had something else planned.\par \par \uc2 Y`all thought we were done.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: What in the world?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I promise you`re not ready.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Look up.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Wow.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, now that`s a tease. I`m like what is the surprise?\par \par \uc2 We`re going to find out on Monday, so tune in for that.\par \par \uc2 As for us, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE (R.E.M.): We are for people who very early on decided that we would own our own masters, and we would split -- we would split our royalties and songwriting credits equally.\par \par \uc2 We were all for one and one for all.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s rare.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The members of R.E.M. stood together for their induction into the Songwriters Hall of Fame last night.\par \par \uc2 After years, their fans got the chance to see them again onstage, one surprise song, one time only.\par \par \uc2 And of course Anthony Mason was there and has more of his exclusive conversation with the band.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, I know this is one of your favorite events of the year.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s really special. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 R.E.M. stunned the crowd when its members took the stage for a stripped down version of their Grammy winning hit "Losing My Religion."\par \par \uc2 It was a moment even they weren`t sure what happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The four original members of R.E.M. had not played together publicly since 2007.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2024 Songwriters Hall of Fame Induction and Awards Gala)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The band was already plotting this one-time reunion back in February when we visited them at their old rehearsal space in Athens, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS (R.E.M.): We had a lot of fun in here. We created a lot of great pieces of music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The lyrics to "Losing My Religion" were on Michael Stipe`s music stand that day.\par \par \uc2 You are playing at Songwriters, is that right?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: I don`t think we should announce it because we don`t want to really (bleep) it up.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But I mean, you`re thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: We`ll go that far, but you`re sworn to secrecy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Their induction into the Songwriters Hall of Fame is what brought the four back together.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: It truly means the world to us that you`ve recognized us tonight, and for that we thank you for this incredible honor. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What made you want to be songwriters?\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY (R.E.M.): The Beatles.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Really?\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: When I was seven, I heard that and I was like, they changed my life.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Punk rock.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Punk rock?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: You can`t play covers all night long, and --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A lot of people do.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Well, and that`s great. I mean, I`ve done it. But once we -- once we realized we could, it sort of -- it was like the keys to the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mike Mills, Michael Stipe, Peter Buck, and Bill Berry came together at the University of Georgia in Athens in 1980.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: And the other thing about R.E.M. though, is that we didn`t know always write music or songs that people connected with on the first listen.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Sometimes, it took seven or eight or even 10 listens before the melody sunk in. You wake up singing it the next day and then you know you`ve got something.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 What does that say?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: We`re very complicated than other people.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The song, "We All Go Back to Where We Belong" from their final album may be a good example.\par \par \uc2 It was used recently in a popular Italian TV drama.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Nelle Tue Mani"/RAI1)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What does that feel like?\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK (R.E.M.): That was incredibly moving.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Nelle Tue Mani"/RAI1)\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: I just thought, it was so cool that halfway around the world, these kids are singing the song.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Nelle Tue Mani"/RAI1)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`m curious what songwriting means to each of you, personally? What has it given you?\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: Something to do at three in the morning when I can`t sleep. I mean, it`s just an obsession I`ve had that`s not necessarily always healthy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: It`s like making -- building a puzzle and when you come up with that, it`s just very satisfying.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you don`t always recognize that at first, do you?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: No.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: No.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Losing My Religion"/R.E.M./Concord Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The global success of "Losing My Religion" caught them by surprise.\par \par \uc2 When something like that happens to a song. How do you react to that? What do you what do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: About time. I mean, you know, we`ve made a lot of really good records and then just randomly, we had a hit single off this thing with the lead mandolin, that`s like, go figure.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2024 Songwriters Hall of Fame Induction and Awards Gala)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: R.E.M.`s reunion was a brief one, and the band says don`t expect another.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: I just don`t know what I`m trying to accomplish if we got back together.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. You don`t think that there might come a moment where you just want to get together and play all those songs one last time somewhere?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: But then what have you got?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: There is no -- yes, there is no one last time.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Yes, all the reasons you don`t want to do it are still in place.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: We are lucky enough to have a -- I don`t really love the word - - a legacy that we can leave in place and not mess up.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: And you don`t get that opportunity, but one time.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Once you change that, you can`t go back.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. So there`s no check big enough, there is no --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: No, I mean, we did it. We had -- we had our day in the sun.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s my fan video there. The crowd reaction last night. It was an incredibly special night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I have to give a shoutout to Linda Moran, who runs the Songwriters, she`s a force of nature. She puts on an amazing show every year. She couldn`t be there last night for health reasons for the first time in like 20 years.\par \par \uc2 But Linda, you did it again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Amazing show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We were just saying how when we see those old clips, I mean -- you know, R.E.M. in my life was such an important band from going back to "Murmur" and "Document" and "Automatic for the People." And they`re just amazing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Incredible catalogue. Something Jason Isbell who inducted them last night and by the way, did an incredible cover of "It`s the End of the World as We Know It."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He said, you know, you hear all four of them in every song. Their songwriting, their music, that`s what`s really special about them.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, make sure to head over CBS MORNINGS, sorry, \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We have the entire interview up on there for anybody who wants to see this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, it worth seeing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great stuff.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is a great conversation about songwriting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is worth -- yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great stuff. One of my favorites, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 All right ahead, Amazon`s best books of the year so far. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: First on CBS MORNINGS, we are revealing some of Amazon`s best books of the year so far.\par \par \uc2 Amazon Books editorial director, the lovely, Sarah Gelman looking like a flower in a garden on summer. I love your dress.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN (Amazon Books editorial director): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning to you, Sarah.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before you tell us about the number one pick, which was unanimous by the way, just briefly, tell us your process how you guys arrive at this.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So this is the list the Amazon Books editors put together. This is our biyearly list.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How many editors?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So, it is January through June. There are nine of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So we`re reading a ton of books every month, putting together a Best of the Month list, and then we look at books that are published in the first half of the year and we ask ourselves a couple of questions: Is this a book that you could give to nearly anyone? A reader or a nonreader?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Is this a book that stays with you? And these are books that we love and we think customers will love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Unanimous number one is --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: "James" by Percival Everett.\par \par \uc2 This is a retelling of "The Adventures of Huck Finn" and in retelling the story, Percival Everett creates a new classic, at least we think so.\par \par \uc2 So this is told from the perspective of Jim and he learns that he is being sold and he`s going to be separated from his wife and his daughter, and so he runs away and he encounters another runaway who is Huck Finn, and they adventure down the Mississippi together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Big change. All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like that.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It`s just great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You recommend a second book, which was when you came on for Women`s History Month, you liked it?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, it`s "The Women" by Kristin Hannah. I love everything about this book. It is such a wonderful historical fiction novel. No one does historical fiction like Kristin Hannah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so for number three, you picked a memoir that pulls back the curtains on the world of public relations, the dark arts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The dark arts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like -- what the name?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Talk to me about it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: This is a debut memoir that`s like Michael Lewis` "Liar`s Poker" meets Anthony Bourdain`s "Kitchen Confidential," except it is exposing the world of high stakes PR.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the name of it?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It is "All the Worst Humans."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The title is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The title.\par \par \uc