par \par \uc2 (Clip from "\uc2 Red Lobster\uc2 " advertisement)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not all heroes wear capes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some wear clocks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ye, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We are counting down to the 77th Annual Tony Awards.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you aware that Nate Burleson will be on stage?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you heard?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE (Actor): I heard from a little bird.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: That he might be looking fresh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t be trying to big time me when I say hi to you, all right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m joking.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Never.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen to this cast -- Daniel Radcliffe, Jonathan Groff, and Lindsay Mendez.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Real quick, Daniel Radcliffe, how would you describe his performance?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN GROFF (Actor): A musical theater masterclass.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL RADCLIFFE (Actor): We`ve lived kind of alongside each other now over the past couple of years. I`ve had a kid. Lindsay has got married, she`s having another baby. Jonathan has seen Beyonce four times. It`s been a huge time for all of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say hello to my little friend.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh. Dry your hair in the morning.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN (Lifestyle expert): There you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There. Hey, hey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s talk about hot dog and the Hot Dog Eating Contest, which could have a new wiener this year.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in nearly a decade. Oh man.\par \par \uc2 All right, not a really good time. Organizers say --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where is the hook?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What would it take to get you guys back together one more time?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: A comet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, while you were, I think at Will`s wedding, we broke the table. So this is the replacement table. You know we had that glass --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You broke the table?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I came out here just for the record.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it was shattered.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was shattered.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It wasn`t us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And nobody knows how it got shattered.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, we have an unbreakable table.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It wasn`t the boys being boys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It wasn`t us playing ball in the house. I promise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But guys tables just don`t break themselves.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240614ek6e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For June 13, 2024, \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For June 13, 2024,\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Cordes, Jericka Duncan, Ivan Cabrera, Robert Costa, Kris Van  Cleave, Manuel Bojorquez, Jan Crawford, Roxana Saberi, Anna Werner, Norah  O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: the Supreme Court`s unanimous ruling on reproductive rights. The nation`s highest court weighs in with its first opinion on abortion since overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WAGGONER\uc2  (President and General Counsel, \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  ): It isn`t the end. There are three states that will continue to litigate this issue in the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, breaking news: Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  gifted more luxury travel than previously known from a GOP megadonor. The new details.\par \par \uc2 The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 The nation`s highest court rejects an effort to limit the use of the widely used abortion pill mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell. And thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 The implications are enormous. Medications are now the most common way to end a pregnancy, and some providers send them by mail to states that have limited or banned abortions. The decision comes on the same day that Senate Republicans blocked legislation that would protect access for in vitro fertilization and other fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 Democrats brought this bill to the floor because they`re trying to make the battle over reproductive rights a centerpiece of the debate this election year.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court with why all nine justices on this divided court agreed on this ruling.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: My body, my rights!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voice-over): Finding common ground in a divisive case, the Supreme Court refused to roll back access to the abortion pill mifepristone, saying a group of anti-abortion doctors had no business filing a lawsuit over how the pill was approved.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We knew we had no choice but to sue the FDA .\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The decision, written by Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  , unanimously reversed conservative lower court judges who OKed the group`s lawsuit against the FDA seeking to restrict access to the drug nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The stakes were high; 63 percent of abortions last year were medication abortions, and the lower court ruling would have required women to see a health care provider, instead of telehealth, to get the pill.\par \par \uc2 The justices said the lawsuit was flawed from the beginning because the anti-abortion doctors weren`t harmed by the FDA`s approval of mifepristone, and so they lacked legal standing to sue.\par \par \uc2 "Instead of going to the courts," Kavanaugh wrote, "citizens and doctors who object to what the law allows others to do may always take their concerns to the executive and legislative branches."\par \par \uc2 While not a decision on the merits, it sends a message to lower courts to put some brakes on novel legal efforts both sides have used in the fight over abortion.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WAGGONER\uc2  (President and General Counsel, \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  ): It isn`t the end. There are three states that will continue to litigate this issue in the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Those states are Idaho, Missouri and Kansas. Additional states ban mifepristone prescriptions by telehealth, and Louisiana has listed it as a controlled dangerous substance.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): This is not a cause for celebration, because the reality of certain things are still not going to change.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, we expect more decisions from the court tomorrow and then over the next couple weeks as the justices wrap up what`s been an incredibly consequential term.\par \par \uc2 And one of the cases we`re still waiting on, whether former President Trump can be prosecuted for his actions around January 6. I expect that case and all of these cases by the end of the month, early July -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And, Jan, I do want to ask you about that breaking news about Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  , a report revealing he took more flights on a private jet funded by a megadonor and that they were not disclosed by the justice. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  has released some documents that show he took three additional trips with his longtime friend the billionaire Harlan Crow, including a trip to Montana, another trip back to his family home in Savannah.\par \par \uc2 But Justice Thomas has always maintained that those kind of trips with a friend who didn`t have business before the court were personal hospitality and, as a result, did not have to be disclosed under the rules in place at the time.\par \par \uc2 The Judicial Conference changed those rules last year. Justice Thomas says, going forward, he will report all of that travel on his disclosure reports -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jan Crawford at the Supreme Court, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the record-breaking rainfall and rising floodwaters across South Florida. Five counties are under a state of emergency right now after days of nonstop heavy downpours, and it`s still coming down. Floodwaters are stranding drivers, closing schools and causing headaches for homeowners.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Manuel Bojorquez is in the thick of it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voice-over): Heavy rain slammed South Florida for the fourth day in a row, dumping on average four inches of rain per hour.\par \par \uc2 The drenching downpour has left parts of Miami-Dade County impassable, with cars submerged and entire neighborhoods underwater.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think it`s crazy. I mean, look at my pants. Look at me. I`m wet. Every time I walk in the street, the water`s right here.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The storm system now centered over the Atlantic has dumped more than 20 inches of rain in some areas, waist-high water, and some residents waded, kayaked or wakeboarded to get around flooded communities.\par \par \uc2 In Dania Beach, first responders rescued at least 40 people from floodwaters as homeowners sought higher ground. Officials are warning residents to stay off the roads.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLA LEVINE CAVA (D-Mayor of Miami-Dade County, Florida): More than half of the deaths from flooding each year occur in vehicles, so the message is, turn around, don`t drown.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: In Hallandale Beach, this person drove right into a canal, mistaking it for a road. Schools in all of Broward County were closed today.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ARAGON (Flood Victim): Oh, (EXPLETIVE DELETED)!\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Here in Hollywood, Florida, Eric Aragon`s home flooded. They had to evacuate through a window, and now he`s concerned about what`s next.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ARAGON: It`s only going to get worse, and, honestly, I don`t want anything to happen to myself or my family. So, yes, we`re going to -- we`ve got to leave.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: His house is just down this street, where, as you can see, the flooding had not receded before this new round of storms began.\par \par \uc2 Further south, the city of Miami Beach has opened up nine parking garages, so people can move their cars away from low-lying areas -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you for being out there.\par \par \uc2 And for how long these storms will stick around and the heat wave moving east, let`s bring in CBS Miami chief meteorologist Ivan Cabrera.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Ivan.\par \par \uc2 IVAN CABRERA: Norah, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 We have, let`s see, happy ducks. We have got happy iguanas here in South Florida. Everyone else is not. We`re just waiting for the sunshine to come back. And, unfortunately, we`re going to have to wait another day. We are waterlogged here.\par \par \uc2 As you saw, there`s some folks that are getting home by kayak. This is the second no-name storm that we have been dealing with over the last couple of years. Take a look at some of the numbers there; 12 to as high as 20 inches of rainfall have fallen over the last couple of days. Just to put that in perspective, that`s about three months worth of rainfall here across South Florida.\par \par \uc2 You know, we always talk about the atmospheric river over California. Well, we have our own here that has been moving through. We have been tapping into the Tropics, and it has just been relentless rain, tropical rains funneled right over South Florida, and that`s going to continue for at least one more day, in fact, additional accumulations perhaps of two to four inches, which, on top of what we have already had, is going to make things a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 Also following this, severe weather moving out of the Midwest -- that is going to be entering the Northeast as we head in through tomorrow, so heads-up there. We have got gusty winds, in fact, severe wind gusts, 60, 80 miles an hour not out of the question, large hail.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, the big story, the heat dome we have been covering, that expands to the east. So get ready for some of the hottest temperatures that we have seen and experienced so far this season across the Northeast coming up this weekend -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ivan, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  visited Capitol Hill today for the first time since the January 6 riot, a riot he`s accused of inciting, causing millions of dollars in damage. Congressional Republicans are falling in line behind the presumptive nominee, as former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said that Trump was -- quote -- "returning to the scene of the crime."\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Robert Costa.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voice-over): Rousing displays of Republican unity today around the party`s presumptive nominee.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I`m with them 1000 percent. They`re with me 1000 percent.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And a notable show of a truce between Trump and his longtime foil, Senate GOP Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , after not speaking since late 2020.\par \par \uc2 For former President Trump, Thursday`s Washington tour was a chance to play up his poll numbers and reassure his party after becoming a convicted felon last month.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: This was a great meeting. There`s tremendous unity in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In a closed-door meeting with House Republicans, sources say he lashed out at the Justice Department, calling it dirty, along with an expletive. He also criticized Milwaukee, host to the Republican National Convention next month, calling it horrible. A spokesman said he was referring to crime and voting issues.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin Democrat Gwen Moore shot back, saying: "Once he`s settled in with his parole officer, I am certain he will discover that Milwaukee is a wonderful city."\par \par \uc2 This was Trump`s first trip to the Hill since the January 6 attack in 2021. And Republicans today avoided the topic.\par \par \uc2 Did January 6 ever come up?\par \par \uc2 MAN: No, it did not.\par \par \uc2 NARRATOR: On January 6, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lit a fire in this country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Meanwhile, the Biden campaign highlighted Trump`s return, releasing this ad this morning, as a mobile billboard was seen playing video of the January 6 attack across Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Trump has said he would pardon rioters.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They ought to release the J6 hostages.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They have suffered enough.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: As I stood outside the Senate GOP Conference Room, I heard cheer after cheer, laugh after laugh.\par \par \uc2 This wasn`t a reckoning, but a Republican embrace of Trump, despite all of his challenges -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to tonight`s other top stories.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is on the world stage tonight in Italy for the G7. He`s speaking about his son for the first time since Hunter`s conviction.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Nancy Cordes reports, for the White House, the high-stakes summit is about supporting Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): This historic agreement comes as Ukraine struggles to regain momentum in its war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): Late today, President Biden and Ukraine`s president signed a document that pledges U.S. support for Ukraine, including military aid and training, for the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 At their press conference, Biden was asked if he felt his son Hunter had been given a fair trial.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I said I would abide by the jury decision. I will do that, and I will not pardon him.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Biden was also asked if he would use his power to reduce his son`s sentence, which could include jail time. Biden said no.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A new report from the Department of Justice finds the Phoenix Police Department violated civil rights, discriminated against people of color, and used excessive force.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Kris Van Cleave has more from Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Norah, DOJ says it found a pattern or practice of the violations, noting pervasive failings that went on for years.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): The report found officers enforced drug laws and low-level offenses more severely against black, Hispanic, and Native Americans.\par \par \uc2 Phoenix police also unlawfully detained the homeless and violated people`s free speech rights.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The police department turned a blind eye to the data, ignored these unmistakable warnings, and failed to uncover its own discriminatory policing patterns.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The city of Phoenix says it will review the report, and the police union flatly rejected it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  made an alarming discovery during a routine traffic stop, finding an arsenal of weapons, and now investigators want to know why the suspect had the stockpile.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Jericka Duncan has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Norah, police say, around 1:30 Wednesday morning, they pulled over 27-year-old Judd Sanson here in Queens for driving with a blacked-out license plate.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): Inside the car, they found a stash of weapons, including a 9-millimeter handgun, 179 rounds of ammunition, knives, axes, and an \uc2 NYPD\uc2  bulletproof vest. The suspect pleaded not guilty and was remanded back to jail until Monday.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say they don`t know what he was planning, but said it could have been a disaster.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Thanks, Jericka, and to our teams around the globe.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  is investigating multiple deaths associated with weighted sleep sacks and blankets for infants.\par \par \uc2 That consumer alert is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now an important consumer alert for parents and caregivers about weighted infant sleep products.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned a government agency is investigating several deaths associated with the products.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Anna Werner.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER (voice-over): Gloria Gamboa has 7-month-old twin boys and a sleep problem.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA GAMBOA (Mother): The level of exhaustion that you feel as a new parent, it`s just -- it`s almost scary.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So when friends recommended these weighted infant sleep sacks from Dreamland Baby, she bought them, but, when they arrived:\par \par \uc2 GLORIA GAMBOA: I was just like, oh, my God, like, this is so heavy. I was just scared, like, if they couldn`t move, if they couldn`t breathe. My instincts are telling me, don`t use this.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The weighted products are popular, but the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  warns parents not to use them.\par \par \uc2 The agency told CBS News it`s investigating multiple fatalities associated with the products.\par \par \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  President Dr. Ben Hoffman:\par \par \uc2 DR. BEN HOFFMAN (President, \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  ): Anything that limits a baby`s movement, anything that might impact their ability to breathe and move their chest is going to put a baby at risk.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Retailers, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  , have halted sales of the products. But the two leading U.S. manufacturers told us there have been no deaths caused by their products.\par \par \uc2 MANASI GANGAN (CEO, Nested Bean): Related to our products, there is no investigation.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Nested Bean CEO Manasi Gangan says her own company`s study shows the weight of a pouch in their garments did not negatively affect babies.\par \par \uc2 MANASI GANGAN: Our products have always been safe, just as we had designed them. They were designed to be safe.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But the AAP says the study did not test the actual products for overnight unsupervised sleep.\par \par \uc2 TARA WILLIAMS (CEO, Dreamland Baby): It really starts with safety. That`s always been the heart of what we did.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The founder of Dreamland Baby, Tara Williams, says her company has a clinical trial under way now, but admits she did not conduct her own study before she started selling her products in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Isn`t it your job as the manufacturer to figure out whether a product is safe before you put it out on the market?\par \par \uc2 TARA WILLIAMS: And, Anna, I would just push that back to you and tell me, what other companies have a peer-reviewed clinical trial that can take years before ever launching a product? We`re a small business. How would we have innovation in America? I mean, this is how America works.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: To that, AAP President Dr. Hoffman says:\par \par \uc2 DR. BEN HOFFMAN: Show me the data that it`s safe. If you can`t show me that it`s safe, I`m not going to be able to recommend it.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Connecticut Democratic Senator Richard Blumenthal says government regulation of infant products is too weak.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-Connecticut): The \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  has no power to take those products off the shelves.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Senator Blumenthal has asked the \uc2 FTC\uc2  to investigate those companies` advertising and statements about safety.\par \par \uc2 Both companies told us they were responding to his concerns. Nested Bean told us his criticisms were inaccurate. But both founders told us they have now lost more than 50 percent of their business. And Dreamland Baby`s CEO, Williams, says she`s planning to sue the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  over that commissioner`s warning letters to retailers.\par \par \uc2 On that, Norah, the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Anna, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It`s a submarine showdown in Cuba. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to a rare announcement from the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  . Just one day after a Russian sub showed up in Cuba, the U.S. Southern Command announced that its fast attack submarine, the USS Helena, arrived in Guantanamo Bay.\par \par \uc2 Now, the announcement itself is unusual. The nuclear-powered sub is part of the Navy`s so-called Silent Service, something that`s typically kept a secret.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Roughly one in five children in the U.S. don`t get enough to eat each day.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, we take a look at an innovative method that sets up farms inside schools just down the hall from the students who need it most.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Roxana Saberi.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI (voice-over): At Ashwaubenon High School near Green Bay, Wisconsin, past the tater tots and the fried chicken sandwiches. You will find something much fresher.\par \par \uc2 MAN: When you get a bite of this salad, it`s just amazing.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: I eat it every day at school.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: So fresh.\par \par \uc2 Do you know where it comes from?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: No idea.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: It was grown and picked just down the hallway.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN (Nutrition Coordinator, Ashwaubenon School District, Wisconsin): Fresh food can be grown easily in Wisconsin in the middle of the winter.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: And there`s no soil in sight. This indoor hydroponic garden relies on circulating water, special nutrients, and around-the-clock light from LEDs.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN: We have done tomatoes, beans, sugar snap peas.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Adding up to around 850 pounds of produce a month. Nutrition coordinator Kaitlin Tauriainen says that`s enough to feed up to 2,000 students throughout the district.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN: A lot of our kids aren`t exposed to fresh foods at home just because it`s financially hard for the families to purchase those kind of things.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Is this the only place where they`re getting fresh vegetables?\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN TAURIAINEN: It may be, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TYINK (Fork Farms): It sets up in about 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The system stemmed from the imagination of Wisconsin native Alex Tyink.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Trained as an opera singer, he got into rooftop gardening in New York City between gigs.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TYINK: They had squash growing right in the middle of Bushwick, Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Then decided to use what he`d learned to start a company called Fork Farms to help people grow their own food.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TYINK: Food is already having to travel further and further to get from seed to plate. Our food system is failing us.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Which is why he sees this 2,500-year-old technique as the water-and-land-efficient farming of the future. And with nearly one out of every eight American households facing food insecurity, Tyink says units like his can get people fresh food faster.\par \par \uc2 MARK GIERACH (Executive Director, Ozaukee Food Alliance): It`s probably some of the best lettuce I have ever eaten.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Mark Gierach got grants to buy two of the $5,000 devices for the food bank he runs near Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 MARK GIERACH: As the costs of food continue to rise, it becomes more valuable than anything else.\par \par \uc2 If you have the opportunity to have fresh produce on the table versus something canned or processed or nothing at all, how much better is life for you? And that`s what we try to do. We try to make life better.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: So did some plants die along the way?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: Yes, a lot.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: A lot?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: Yes, a lot.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: In Milwaukee Public Schools, where school officials say more than 80 percent of students are economically disadvantaged, 80 flex farms have sprouted.\par \par \uc2 ALEX TYINK: That`s where it gets really exciting, because now you have a community of people that are focused on doing this together and they`re learning from each other.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: And, he says, they`re planting a healthier future together.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Roxana Saberi, Appleton, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a great story. We should make those more available.\par \par \uc2 Good night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240614ek6d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Florida Flooding Emergency; Dangerous Heat Dome Expands; Biden at the G7 Summit; Russian Warships in the Caribbean; Supreme Court Controversial Cases; House GOP Holds Garland in Contempt; Israeli Raid Aftermath; Sandy Hook Survivors Graduate; Eye on Inflation; Dangers of Weighted Blankets for Infants; Remembering Jerry West; IVF on the Line; R.E.M. Reflects; Ariana DeBose Live; I`m Mostly Here to Enjoy Myself; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers,  Manuel Bojorquez, Stephanie Abrams, Nancy Cordes, David Martin, Jan  Crawford, Chris Livesay, Jill Schlesinger, Anna Werner, Carter Evans, Lilia  Luciano, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 14,507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this Thursday morning as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Relentless rain brings flash flooding to parts of Florida stranding people and prompting urgent warnings to stay on higher ground.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I can`t do nothing. I can`t even go to the store. I won`t be able to go nowhere, get no food. Nobody could come, I can`t go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden discusses Russia`s war in Ukraine with world leaders as Moscow`s warships arrive in Cuba.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH (Deputy Pentagon Press Secretary): These exercises don`t pose a threat to the United States.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll honor the late \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend, Jerry West, a literal icon.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: He`s the logo. What where do you want? His silhouette is the \uc2 NBA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: NBA Finals, the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2  have put a stranglehold on the series taking a three-zero lead.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #1: Jaylen Brown.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #2: That`s four wins in a row for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Members of Congress have traded bills for bats at the congressional baseball game.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): At least I hope that`s why Marjorie Taylor Greene had a bat.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS: You going to the baseball game? What game?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): The American Dream is dead all because Joey Chestnut has been barred from the Nathan`s Fourth of July Hotdog Eating Contest.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): He signed an endorsement deal with a vegan meat company, as like if Cookie Monster signed a deal with celery, but he has the world record for eating 76 hotdogs in 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER ("The Daily Show"): By the way, the world record for eating vegan hotdogs, two-and-a-half.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good. The world record two-and-a-half.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Cookie Monster eating celery there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because vegan hot dog doesn`t sound so good to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not for me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s like golf at Augusta, it is a tradition like no other.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We love hot dog eating contests around here.\par \par \uc2 But thank you for joining the show. Let`s start with some weather though and we have some very dangerous conditions in Southern Florida with widespread flash flooding and rainfall of historic proportions.\par \par \uc2 Officials are urging people to stay off the roads, and five counties are under a state of emergency.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in Hollywood, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Manny, good morning. So what are you seeing there?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning to you. It`s almost like an unnamed tropical storm minus the wind came through this area. Just take a look at all the street flooding here, not only stranding cars, but it is impacting people`s homes.\par \par \uc2 There are some staggering new rainfall totals coming in this morning, more than 19 inches of rain have fallen on Hollywood, Florida in the last 48 hours, and the worst may still be to come because there are more storms in the forecast.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: A relentless storm system continues to pummel South Florida causing life-threatening flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 On the state`s east coast the Miami-Fort Lauderdale area saw rainfall rates of over three inches per hour on Wednesday, blurring the boundary between land and sea, fish even swimming through the streets.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Look at my pants, look at me. I`m wet. Every time I walk in the street, the water`s right here.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Knee-deep waters meant to many residents waded, kayaked, or even wake-boarded their way through neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 Despite warnings from local officials, the floodwaters did not deter some drivers who continued to venture into seemingly impassable roadways, while portions of the critical I-95 south in Broward County were temporarily shut down. And intense winds up to 60 mile-per-hour ripped apart trees.\par \par \uc2 The severe weather continued to snarl airline travel at South Florida`s major travel hubs with more than 1,200 flights impacted in the region on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I now have to change car reservations, get a new hotel reservation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It just kept get the delayed, delayed, delayed. So I ended up switching flights.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: On the other side of the state, parts of Florida`s Gulf Coast were in cleanup mode Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Sarasota County saw as much as 10 inches of rain, including nearly four inches in just one hour. A once in a thousand-year event.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER ANDERSON (Public Works Sarasota County, Florida): It was a unique event, and for it to occur as early in storm season as it did, it does help us know where we need to tweak some things in the future.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Broward County schools, the sixth largest school district in the county has decided to cancel classes today due to this flood emergency, and a reminder that \uc2 NOAA\uc2  is forecasting an above-average hurricane season -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Manny, be careful in that water.\par \par \uc2 For more on the rain and some extreme weather elsewhere in the country, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The weather need to be taken seriously yet again today and tomorrow in South Florida. More heavy rain will fall on already saturated grounds on both coasts. Flooding is still a major concern with multiple rounds of storms and high rainfall rates. Travel could be impossible once again.\par \par \uc2 Through the beginning of the weekend, we could still see another half a foot of rain or more. Speaking of heavy rain, it could also fall from the strong storms in the Midwest today, the bigger boomers will fire off this evening. They could put down hail, produce gusty winds, and we can`t rule out a tornado. This will push into the northeast for our Friday. Damaging wind gusts will be the biggest threat.\par \par \uc2 We could really use the rain in the West as there is a fast-moving wildfire in Arizona. At least six structures have been destroyed by what officials call the Rose Fire. And a hundred homes are threatened, forcing evacuations around the town of Wickenburg, northwest of Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 The wind and heat helped the fire to spread to both sides of the highway which was forced to close. It is still zero percent contained.\par \par \uc2 The actual temperature is straight-up hot in the West, but when you combine the warm temps and the humidity in the South and the Midwest, it`s going to feel like the 90s and even the triple digits.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, I`ve got to say, this heat is only a taste of what`s to come. Next week it gets even hotter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so we better prepare ourselves. \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, we have important news from Italy where President Biden is meeting with other world leaders at this year`s G7 Summit. They`ve got a big agenda including talking about more aid for Ukraine as it tries to hold off Russia`s army.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is there in Southern Italy for us. She is covering the \uc2 G7\uc2 . The weather looks good.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It sure is nice, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The big news today is this long-term security agreement that President Biden is going to be signing with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . It essentially commits the US to supporting Ukraine for the next 10 years. We`re talking about military training, weapons assistance, intelligence sharing, and more.\par \par \uc2 Fifteen other nations have already signed similar agreements, and this deal does not commit any US troops to Ukraine`s fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 Now this signing is going to come about a week after Biden met with Zelenskyy during his trip to France, and you might recall Biden publicly apologized during that meeting to Zelenskyy for the six-month congressional delay in approving more military aid to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Here in Italy, Biden and the other \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders are hoping to finalize a plan for a $50 billion loan to Ukraine so that the country can do things like rebuild infrastructure that has been destroyed, produce more of its own weaponry, so it doesn`t have to rely on the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 And here is the interesting part, the loan would be paid back using the interest from Russian assets that have been frozen by countries around the world since Russia invaded Ukraine two-and-a-half years ago.\par \par \uc2 Back to this 10-year agreement for a moment. It is meant to send a message to Russia that the US is not going anywhere, even if this war drags on, but it is not binding. It is not a treaty, which means it could be rolled back by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he wins in November -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot to think about there.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes at the G7 Summit, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 While world leaders discussing Ukraine in Europe, Russia is flexing its military muscles in Cuba just 90 miles from Florida. A Russian nuclear submarine and three other ships entered Havana harbor yesterday to conduct what they say are military exercises.\par \par \uc2 It`s really hard not to think of the Cuban Missile Crisis that brought the US and Russia to the brink of war back in 1962 when we saw these headlines.\par \par \uc2 David Martin is tracking the maneuvers and the US response.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: A surface warship and a nuclear-powered submarine arrived in Havana to a 21-gun salute, beginning with the what the US expects to be the largest Russian military exercises in the Caribbean in five years.\par \par \uc2 US officials say the exercises will also include flights by Russian long- range bombers along the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH: It certainly wouldn`t come as a surprise to us if we see more activity around the United States.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The four-ship Russian flotilla has already passed just 30 miles off the coast of Florida as it made its way down from the North Atlantic to the Caribbean. The warship has been used in the past to test fire one of Russia`s newest hypersonic missiles.\par \par \uc2 The submarine is also designed to carry guided missiles, but US officials say there is no indication it is carrying nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH: We of course take it seriously, but these exercises don`t pose a threat to the United States.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The Russian ships have been shadowed by a small \uc2 US Navy\uc2  task force consisting of two Destroyers along with two vessels specifically fitted with sonar for tracking submarines, patrol planes, even sail drones.\par \par \uc2 The Russians are expected to remain in Havana until next week before they get under way again, perhaps for Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 When they do, officials say, the Navy will start shadowing them again.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m David Martin at The Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Supreme Court is racing toward the end of this year`s term with a huge pile of cases still to be settled. We expect to get the first of those rulings this morning.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court to show us what is coming up in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 You know, the month of June, I mean, that`s always when the justices hand down their most controversial cases, but this June has more controversy and more cases than usual, from abortion access and gun rights to whether former President Trump can be criminally prosecuted for his actions around January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Now on abortion, it`s the first abortion case since the justices overturned Roe versus Wade two years ago. They`re set to decide whether the so-called abortion pill, mifepristone, will be limited nationwide even in states where abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 A thoughted argument tomorrow as the justices, conservative and liberal, they seem skeptical, so I think that suggests the court is not inclined to limit access at this time.\par \par \uc2 There`s two different Trump cases. One whether he is absolutely immune from prosecution for his alleged efforts to overturn the 2020 presidential election, the other whether he and several hundred other January 6th defendants can be charged with obstruction. That carries a maximum 20-year prison sentence.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s a dozen or so either high-profile cases that could have wide-ranging implications for American life. There`s two gun-control cases, social media, free speech, environmental cases. All of these cases divisive, difficult, and of course coming right as the presidential campaign is getting into full swing -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is a lot going on.\par \par \uc2 Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives has voted to hold Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  in contempt of Congress. Yesterday`s vote almost entirely along party lines. Republicans are angry that Garland refused to hand over audio of President Biden talking with a special counsel about his handling of classified documents, and the Biden administration is claiming executive privilege and refusing to release that audio.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning to the Middle East, we are learning more about the raid that led to the rescue of four Israeli hostages from Gaza. So far, most of the images we`ve seen of the raid have come from cameras worn by Israeli commandos.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we are sharing a different perspective. That of an eyewitness who describes the chaotic scene which cost many civilians their lives.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay has our report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Gunfire)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: We`ve seen the dramatic rescue footage of those four Israeli hostages, but what`s been less visible, the aftermath and those who survived it.\par \par \uc2 (ABDER RAUF-LEKDAD speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Our CBS News team in Gaza tracked down eyewitness, Abder Rauf-Lekdad (sp?) who walks us through his bullet-ridden home just across the street from where one of the rescue vehicles broke down under heavy gunfire.\par \par \uc2 ABDER RAUF-LEKDAD (Eyewitness): Go, go, go.\par \par \uc2 (ABDER RAUF-LEKDAD speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "The commandos burst into our apartment," he says, "And questioned us at gunpoint. The commandos bound the men`s hands and put bags on their heads," he says.\par \par \uc2 When it was all over, two of his grandsons had been shot. Sixteen-year-old Moamin Mater (sp?) is lucky to still have his arm. His brother didn`t survive, he says.\par \par \uc2 (MOAMIN MATER speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "He was shot right next to me in the stomach and the leg. He was 12."\par \par \uc2 The Hamas-run Health Ministry says 274 people were killed in the rescue operation. Many hundreds more wounded. Israel disputes that number and blames \uc2 Hamas\uc2  for surrounding the hostages with civilians like these.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to the \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  spokesman, James Elder in Gaza who saw the grisly scenes at the hospital himself.\par \par \uc2 JAMES ELDER (\uc2 UNICEF\uc2  spokesman): It felt again like day one of the war, Chris, walking in this hospital absolutely heaving with people, children, little three-year-olds, seven-year-olds with these grotesque wounds of war -- head injuries and burns. It is the smell of burning flesh that`s hard to get out of one`s head.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: So many child victims in Gaza where almost half the population is under 18. But that ceasefire is still in limbo. A frustrated Secretary of State Antony Blinken said \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , "Waited two weeks and proposed changes." As a result, the war \uc2 Hamas\uc2  started will go on.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Chris Livesay, Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very tough day still.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s take you to Connecticut where 20 first graders who died in the Sandy Hook massacre were honored last night as their classmates graduated from high school.\par \par \uc2 About 60 of this year`s 330 graduates at Newtown High School survived that attack back in 2012t hat also killed six adults. The names of all 20 student victims were read aloud at last night`s graduation followed by a moment of silence.\par \par \uc2 Some members of the class of 2024 spoke before the ceremony saying their memories of their classmates are still very vivid.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I think we can`t forget about that there is a whole chunk of our class missing. And so going into graduation, we all have very mixed emotions.\par \par \uc2 Trying to be excited for ourselves and this accomplishment that we`ve worked hard for, but also those who aren`t able to share it with us who should have been able to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s right about that.\par \par \uc2 Many of the Sandy Hook survivors have become advocates against gun violence.\par \par \uc2 You know, it is hard, every time you see that big wall of faces, those little kids, they were six years old, and I think about that day, just sitting in their classroom -- there they are -- sitting in their classrooms, just sitting at their little desks, and the horror that happened.\par \par \uc2 And to know that now they would have been graduating from high school, I am thinking of all their parents today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Me, too. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a very, very difficult day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Unbelievable tragedy all these years later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will never forget it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead in just a moment, some parents have turned to weighted blankets and sacks to help infants sleep, but are those sacks and blankets safe?\par \par \uc2 Anna Werner on the story. She is investigating, but first it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Anthony Mason talks with the members of R.E.M. in their first interview in decades. You`ll hear what they think about being inducted into the Songwriters` Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The One I Love"/R.E.M./Glastonbury Festival)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, Tony`s host Ariana DeBose will be here first on CBS MORNINGS to reveal what she is planning for Broadway`s big night on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 And we will ask \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  -- what up, Jill -- how the newest inflation numbers could affect your budget.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS sponsored by ARK Encounter. An unforgettable experience.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Money Watch," we are talking about something we all care about -- the high cost of living.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has been slowing down. Last month it was up by only 3.3 percent over the year. That`s still higher than what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  wants to see. So yesterday, the Fed left interest rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here with everything you need to know that can help your bottom line.\par \par \uc2 All right, how are you doing?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. So why are consumers so concerned and will they be feeling some relief soon?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : A little bit. So look at some of the categories that actually went down last month -- gas prices, yay -- right ahead of the summer driving season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Clothing, that`s down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Groceries were flat, but new and used car sales -- sorry -- prices down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Unfortunately, things are still higher than they were, of course.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : We think housing, you think insurance costs -- those are still persistently high.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Biden, he says while prices are still too high, wages are rising by more than the inflation rate. So why don`t people feel better about the economy we`re in right now?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know, what`s happened is prices are 22 percent higher than they were from four years ago. That`s why you don`t feel crazy if you say like I know it`s getting better, but it still hurts.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : And I just want to put that in context. Twenty-two percent higher from four years ago. The previous four years, we had seven percent gains.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So three times higher. Yes, President Biden is right, wages are keeping up with inflation now, but they didn`t for four whole years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When can we expect to see a cut in interest rates?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : All right, so \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  gets up and he says, no cut right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : There`s going to be another meeting in July, but he says they think, the Fed thinks one more cut before the end of the year. We don`t know when, maybe September.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so higher rates for borrowers, as we know.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But for savers, what advice do you give them?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Pay attention. There are still millions and millions of dollars sitting in bank accounts earning less than a half of one percent, and you can get four or five percent on CDs, savings rates, money market rates, so if you have not moved your money --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, by pay attention what do you mean?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Move to a certificate of deposit, three months, six months, nine months -- match to when you think you need the money, college tuition payment and insurance bill.\par \par \uc2 There is free money that is out there waiting for you. Move to a higher- bearing interest account.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Always dropping gems. I appreciate you, Dolla`, Dolla` Jill.\par \par \uc2 Up next, safety concerns over weighted sleep blankets for babies. Stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where we are looking at the growing concern about popular products meant to help infants sleep better. Some sleep deprived new parents have given their children weighted sleep sacks and blankets; however, the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  says those products are not safe for infants and the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  says it is investigating multiple fatalities associated with that sort of terminology with these products.\par \par \uc2 Now some major retailers, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , have dropped them outright, but American manufacturers insist actually they`re totally safe.\par \par \uc2 Our senior investigative correspondent, Anna Werner has been looking into all of this controversy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: As a new mom of twin boys, Gloria Gamboa (sp?) face severe sleep deprivation.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA GAMBOA (New mom of twin boys): I mean, one baby is difficult, two newborn babies, the level of exhaustion that you feel as a new parent, it`s just -- it is almost scary.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Then she heard from friends about these weighted infant sleep sacks, from Dreamland Baby, advertised to help babies fall asleep faster and stay asleep longer.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA GAMBOA: I thought if it`s going to help me, I`m going to use it.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But when the sleep sacks arrived, and she picked them up, she says --\par \par \uc2 GLORIA GAMBOA: I was just like, "Oh my God. Like this is so heavy." It just scared me.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: What were you worried about happening to the baby?\par \par \uc2 GLORIA GAMBOA: I was just scared, like if they couldn`t move, if they couldn`t breathe.\par \par \uc2 I was just like, you know what? My instincts are telling me, don`t use this.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  says the same. It tells parents not to use weighted blankets or swaddles for babies. The agency told CBS News that its staff is aware of multiple fatalities associated with an infant-weighted product and it is investigating those incidents.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  says the products aren`t safe.\par \par \uc2 DR. BENJAMIN HOFFMAN (\uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  president): Anything that is going to put a weight on the chest of a baby who is sleeping for unsupervised overnight sleep, I cannot imagine a scenario in which that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: AAP president, Dr. Ben Hoffman.\par \par \uc2 DR. BENJAMIN HOFFMAN: Anything that limits a baby`s movement, anything that might impact their ability to breathe and move their chest is going to put a baby at risk, and that`s just not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: After one \uc2 CPSC\uc2  Commissioner wrote to major retailers warning about the products, sellers, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  pulled them from sale, including those from the two primary US manufacturers, Dreamland Baby and Nested Bean, but both companies told us their products are not responsible for any deaths.\par \par \uc2 Nested Bean CEO, Manasi Gangan.\par \par \uc2 MANASI GANGAN (Nested Bean CEO): Related to our products, there is no investigation, there are no fatalities that we have been made aware of.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Gangan founded her company in 2011 after seeking sleep solutions for her own infant. The products feature a pouch filled with plastic beads that rests on a baby`s chest, she says mimicking a parent`s hand.\par \par \uc2 MANASI GANGAN: One ounce of weight, which is like a slice of bread.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: She says a study she commissioned showed the weight of the pouch did not affect the baby`s breathing or heart rate.\par \par \uc2 What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 MANASI GANGAN: That our products have always been safe, just as we had designed them. They were designed to be safe.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But the AAP points out that study only measured weights on five babies for two minutes and did not test the actual products in real- world conditions.\par \par \uc2 Dreamland Baby CEO, Tara Williams, also a mom who started her company after looking for sleep solutions said her products too are safe.\par \par \uc2 TARA WILLIAMS (Dreamland Baby CEO): It really starts with safety. We do have a full medical board that`s led by a pediatrician. That`s always been the heart of what we did.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: She says she relied on a study of babies using weighted products in hospitals and there is clinical trial underway now, but admits she did not conduct the study herself before she started selling her products in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Isn`t it your job as the manufacturer to figure out whether a product is safe before you put it out on the market?\par \par \uc2 TARA WILLIAMS: And Anna, I would just push that back to you and tell me what other companies have a peer reviewed clinical trial that can take years before ever launching a product?\par \par \uc2 We`re a small business. How would we have innovation in America? I mean, this is how America works.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: To that, AAP president, Dr. Hoffman says --\par \par \uc2 DR. BENJAMIN HOFFMAN: Unfortunately, the way the system works is that products are allowed to come to market with no demonstration of safety record. Show me the data that it`s safe. If you can`t show me that it is safe, I`m not going to be able to recommend it.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Connecticut Democratic Senator Richard Blumenthal says the issue shows a larger problem, weak regulation of all infant products.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): The \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  has no power to take those products off the shelves.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: And is that a problem?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): That`s a problem. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 No matter how egregious or severe the dangers from a particular product, the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  doesn`t have any powers to mandate that companies stop producing or selling them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Senator Blumenthal has asked the FTC to investigate those companies advertising and statements about safety. Both companies told us they were responding to his concerns, Nested Bean told us his criticisms were inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 But both CEOs told us they`ve now lost more than 50 percent of their business and Dreamland Baby CEO Williams says she plans to sue the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  over that commissioner`s warning letter to retailers. On that, the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  declined to comment -- guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s sort of hard, Anna, when you hear the phrase "multiple deaths caused by the weighted blanket" --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Associated with --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Associate with, your right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Associated with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a very important distinction, associated with weighted blankets, and then you see what the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  says. Then see what the people who are making these blankets say.\par \par \uc2 What`s the difference between swaddling the babies like little burritos, which is what many people do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So you know, they are working on a voluntary standard for swaddles in this voluntary standards process that they have and blankets, but if you talk to the doctors at the AAP what they say is that when you put a weight on a baby`s chest, the question is when those weights in those products are on a normal, healthy infant for overnight, what is the effect on their heart rate and respiration?\par \par \uc2 And they`re saying, look, we don`t have the evidence. We don`t have those trials in the door yet, but the products are already out there and they are concerned that that is a bad idea. They say that it contradicts safe infant sleep practices in their opinion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m having flashbacks here because we used a weighted blanket with my younger kids and we used the baby box which is another thing that`s been taken off the market. It seems like they`re taking options away from parents without giving parents very clear guidelines of what is definitely safe and someone has got to step up and be an authority here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not just take things away and say what is going to work.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: It is hard for parents, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What works.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anna, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve got to do your own due diligence here. There`s a lot at stake there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Anna. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, a celebration of basketball legend, Jerry West.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Please join us in observing a moment of silence in honor of a true legend, Jerry West.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Last night`s \uc2 NBA\uc2  Finals Game between the Celtics and the Mavericks began with a touching tribute to Jerry West, one of the greatest players of all time.\par \par \uc2 West died yesterday at 86 years old with his wife Karen, by his side. The 14-time All-Star won an \uc2 NBA\uc2  title in 1972 and was general manager of eight championship teams with the \uc2 Los Angeles Lakers\uc2  and the \uc2 Golden State Warriors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans has more of a man whose impact spanned generations.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Two seconds. One second. West throws it up.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: They called him Mr. Clutch for a reason.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 NBA\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: When his team needed him the most, Jerry West delivered.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Now to West, look at the moves he has.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: He is the man who inspired the \uc2 NBA\uc2  logo. It doesn`t get any more iconic than that.\par \par \uc2 As a player, Jerry West had roaring crowds in the palm of his hands as he dazzled his way across every court he played on. He was an All-Star every year of his career and reached the Finals nine times, even winning the Finals MVP when his team lost in 1969. He won his only championship in 1972.\par \par \uc2 But the Jerry West Show was only just beginning. As general manager, he created the Showtime Lakers of the 80s and the Kobe-Shaq dynamic duo of the early 2000s.\par \par \uc2 Tributes poured in from across the sports landscape. Magic Johnson, who West drafted in 1979 wrote: "Jerry West was more than a general manager. He was a great friend and confidant. He was there in my highest moments winning five \uc2 NBA\uc2  championships and in my lowest moment when I announced my HIV diagnosis and we cried together for hours in his office."\par \par \uc2 JERRY WEST (Former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player): I was a dreamer. My family didn`t have much.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: In his later years, West was open about his lifelong battle with depression. The basketball court became his oasis after a rough childhood.\par \par \uc2 JERRY WEST: I`d sat alone on our front porch and wondered, if I ever make it to the top of that mountain, what will I see on the other side? Well, I did make it to the other side and my dreams have come true.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: A kid from rural West Virginia who reached the highest peaks basketball had to offer.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Our thoughts are with his family, the man affectionately known as "The Logo" will be missed.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad has the stories that you`ll be talking about today, but first, it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the world`s a stage in our eight o`clock hour including the Tonys, and this morning, we are talking with the host of Sunday`s big show, that`s Oscar winner, Ariana DeBose. We`ll find out what she has got planned. She`s good.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ve got Anthony as in Mason, his exclusive interview with the founding members of the legendary rock group R.E.M. --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The One I Love"/R.E.M./Glastonbury Festival)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are they losing their religion? In their first joint interview in decades, all of that in our next hour, but right now, Klaus and I are heading over to the table because Vlad Duthiers -- what you doing Vlad?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The gray flannel over here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I told you. I am wearing --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean that`s my look.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, like, I wanted to look like you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He has arrived. We are getting ready for "What to Watch." We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fifty shades of gray.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER; This portion of CBS MORNINGS sponsored by Opdivo plus Yervoy. Find out more at Opdivo.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you hear "Funky Town" don`t you think of Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s where I want to take everybody to. Funky Town.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go, Vlad. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let me start with the \uc2 New England Patriots\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They are retiring Number 12 in honor of -- guess who?\par \par \uc2 I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As well they should.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tom Brady.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was inducted into the Pat`s Hall of Fame at Gillette Stadium yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The quarterback played 20 years with the team and won six Super Bowls. \uc2 Bill Belichick\uc2  called coaching Brady an honor.\par \par \uc2 Surprise guest, Peyton Manning said when facing Tom Brady, you had to play your best for he would beat you every time.\par \par \uc2 Brady called it a monumental night saying: "I am eternally grateful. I`m Tom Brady and I am a Patriot."\par \par \uc2 Also folks, Jay-Z came out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s what I want to know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jay-Z don`t normally come out for a lot of things, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, there he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He came out. Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jay-Z was on the scene.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s respect. It isn`t for the money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is kind of huge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I really do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To have like Jay-Z come out and perform.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is huge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So was that bag he got for it. No, I am joking. I`m joking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think he got paid?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is he a Patriots fan?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He got paid, Gayle. Jay-Z don`t come out for free.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. And he sang "Public Service Announcement," which is the song that Tom Brady --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tom Brady loves.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He loves that song.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, Gayle, I don`t know. I am joking. I am just joking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So he is eligible for the NFL Hall of Fame in 2028. Think he`s got a shot?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is a shoe-in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is a shoe-in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, now to a scary moment for folks at historic hotel in Dublin, Ireland. A fire broke out yesterday morning at the Shelbourne Hotel, forcing guests and staff to evacuate. Thankfully, there were no injuries.\par \par \uc2 A reporter with the Irish network, RT, was quick to the scene where guests were gathered outside and listen to what one guest in particular had to say. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HENRY WINKLER (Actor and comedian): When I heard the fire alarm, I thought it was the clock radio. I thought somebody had set the alarm before we got there, you know, like another guest.\par \par \uc2 And finally, I went into another room and it was still buzzing. So I called downstairs and the woman said, in a very calm voice: "Yes, we`re all evacuating. You must evacuate right now." And I left.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I left.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, did they know that that was Henry Winkler?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what I want to know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is not clear. I think the reporter thought she was getting what we call in the business an MOS, a man on the street.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, man on the street.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? We`re not sure if she knew that it was The Fonz or not. But I just think that it`s great that Henry just responded the way a man on the street would, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too. I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which is let me tell you what happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know the who, what, where, when, why.\par \par \uc2 He was in Dublin for his book tour, and of course he tweeted about how great the firefighters are. He shared that photo on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account saying that firefighters run in when people are running out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Running out. So true.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love Henry Winkler. You know, I do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope they \uc2 Google\uc2  them there in the newsroom in Ireland.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it says Henry Winkler, witness. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A witness and a survivor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Survivor, right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- and first on CBS MORNINGS, Ariana DeBose will be here and join us. She`s hosting the Tonys for the third time on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It`s The World As We Know It (And I Feel Fine)/R.E.M./Warner Vision Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Tonight iconic rock band R.E.M. is being inducted into the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, what they told us in their first interview together in nearly 30 --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Anthony is in Mason exclusive with the iconic rock band R.E.M. It is their first joint interview in nearly 30 years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Author, Glynnis MacNicol moved to Paris during the pandemic to find pleasure on her own terms and her book about women actually finding happiness, weirdly controversial. We`ll talk to her about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Oscar winner, Ariana DeBose will host the Tonys for the third time, Sunday, and she`ll tell us how she plans to make it seen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I hear somebody else is going to be on stage and making it seen.\par \par \uc2 That`s a tease, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Flash flooding wreaks havoc in parts of Florida as heavy rain drenches the area.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is the worst I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It is crazy. I mean, look at my pants. Look at me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden is in Italy for a critical G7 Summit with major world leaders.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The big news today is this long-term security agreement that President Biden is going to be signing with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Russian warships and a nuclear submarine arrive in Cuba for military exercises.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH: What Russia is doing in these exercises, they don`t pose a threat to the United States. But of course, we`re going to continue to monitor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Stephen Colbert announces he is going to meet Pope Francis this weekend, along with a group of comedians including Chris Rock. That should be good.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Chris, Chris, please, for safety`s sake, I will just keep Mary Magdalene `s name out of your mouth because the Pope wears a big ring.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stephen Colbert and the Pope and other comedians, I would like to be a fly on the wall for that one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t hear anything of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No reaction.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stephen Colbert and a group of comedians.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was looking at something else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Chris Rock.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you with us now?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am ready. We have a big story here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin -- all right. We`re going to begin this hour with a landmark vote today in the Senate on access to in-vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 The legislation would protect the treatment nationwide at a time, as you know, it has come under fire from conservative groups. That includes Southern Baptists who just made their own decision on IVF. It`s a divisive issue to be sure among its members.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano was at the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Senators are poised for a historic vote today on a Democratic-led package that would protect in-vitro fertilization as a nationwide right.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY DUCKWORTH (D-IL): I had my daughters through IVF, and I think that anybody that wants to start a family should have the ability to start a family.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Senator Tammy Duckworth co-sponsored the bill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY DUCKWORTH (D-IL): It doesn`t force anybody to access reproductive technology, it just says, if you want to do that, you can offer it, you can seek it, you can cover it.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Republicans also voiced their support for IVF in a different bill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): To the best of my knowledge, all 100 senators in this body support IVF. This should be a measure that transcends political divides.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: CBS News polling reflects that sentiment with an overwhelming majority of Americans saying IVF should be legal across party lines.\par \par \uc2 But earlier this year, the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  ruled that frozen embryos are human beings and have the same rights as living persons. In response, the state then passed a law shielding IVF providers that discard embryos from prosecution and lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The ayes have it and the resolution is adopted.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Today`s Senate vote comes just a day after the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 , the nation`s largest group of Protestant Christians passed a resolution opposing IVF.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We need to be very clear that human life begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The resolution states the IVF process most often engages in the destruction of embryonic human life and encouraged its congregation of nearly 13 million to seek adoption and consider the ethical implications of IVF.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH SAHADIK (Southern Baptist member): Just dealing with our infertility, it leaves a wound.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Southern Baptist Meredith and Zachary Sahadik (sp?) already have one child through IVF. Another is on the way also through the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY SAHADIK (Southern Baptist member): I am for the sanctity of life and for the sanctity of embryos. I am against the idea that this technology is so wicked that it cannot be employed.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: At the church`s annual meeting Wednesday, Zachary got emotional speaking about his own experience.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY SAHADIK: And I have 10 embryos that I love and with every bit of my being, we will have or see born into a Christian family.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The Sahadiks plan to use their remaining embryos for their own family or put the frozen embryos up for adoption.\par \par \uc2 When the resolution is adopted, what do you fear that means or what are your concerns?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH SAHADIK: Really confident that we made a decision, sought prayer, sought wise counsel. So I`m not too scared.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And the Roman Catholic Church, which is the largest of any religious group in the country, is also against IVF. But as the action shows on the Hill, this very personal decision is going to continue being a major part of the political conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is such a personal decision.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For some families, that`s the only way they can have a family.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The only option, right?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: There are so many.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: There are so many. People are delaying having children for many reasons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Now, many, many people are resorting to IVF for that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The politics are so fascinating because organizations and groups and leaders who are so pro-family are not necessarily pro this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which is the way that many people are able to have a family.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It could be, I think the reason, Democrats in the Senate are pushing it, it is because it can divide some members of the Republican Party, particularly Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Perhaps, and what this couple told me is look, I think there`s a lot of misinformation. I don`t think people understand the process --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also true.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: -- and the possibility of donating a frozen embryo to another family.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we know we don`t want this politicized. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it happens.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a CBS MORNINGS exclusive, the original members of the rock group R.E.M. sat down for their first joint interview in nearly 30 years. Yes, with none other than Anthony Mason.\par \par \uc2 This is a conversation you are not going to want to miss, but first, it is 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "West Side Story"/20th Century Studios)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s Ariana DeBose in her Academy Award winning performance as Anita in "West Side Story."\par \par \uc2 Ahead, she`ll join us in studio to talk about returning as the host of the Tony Awards on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Losing My Religion"/R.E.M./Concord Records)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That of course is the iconic indie rock group, R.E.M. -- Michael Stipe, Peter Buff, Mike Mills, and Bill Berry started making music more than 40 years ago. They were college students in Georgia at the time.\par \par \uc2 Well tonight, they will be inducted into the Songwriters` Hall of Fame and they sat down with Anthony Mason for a very rare and exclusive interview.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 R.E.M. disbanded back in 2011 and the four original members have not sat down for an interview together, in believe it or not, 30 years. But this honor means a lot to them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How long have you guys been in this space?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Eighty-six.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Eighty-six.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Eighty-five.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Eighty-five.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Eighty-five.\par \par \uc2 Their gear is still stacked up in their rehearsal space in Athens, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Is this everything that`s left over from the -- from touring?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve got a warehouse with a lot of the bigger stuff.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It`s The World As We Know It (And I Feel Fine)/R.E.M./Warner Vision Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s been 13 years now since R.E.M. broke up.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I have all my shoes, actually.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All your shoes?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, from every tour and going all the way back to 1980.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s when the band formed at the University of Georgia in Athens.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Man on the Moon"/R.E.M./Concord Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Michael Stipe on vocals, Peter Buck on guitar, Mike Mills on bass, and Bill Berry on drums -- a college band that would become a supergroup taking indie rock to the top of the pop charts.\par \par \uc2 What does it mean to you all to be recognized for songwriting?\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK (R.E.M.): We lived or died on the strength of our songs. So this is a huge honor.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS (R.E.M.): It is the hardest thing that we do. And it`s the thing that we`ve worked on the most from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY (R.E.M.): Because we had to. I mean, really early on, just to put food on the table, we had to write songs as fast as we could.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The quartet found their sound quickly.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE (R.E.M.): Kind of like kismet to me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It did?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Yes. When it happened, it felt right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Much of their songwriting was done in this studio.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: We`d all show up at one o`clock and bring in any ideas that we had and see if it inspired anybody else to do something with it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mills, Buck, and Berry would write the music, then give it to Stipe to write lyrics.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: And Michael of course, is one of the best melodicists in the world.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: I`ve never been called a melodicist before.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: I am not even sure that`s a word.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: I kind of like it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Some songs came easily.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Losing My Religion"/R.E.M./Concord Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Like "Losing My Religion."\par \par \uc2 You were just messing around with the mandolin.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: Yes, and I still don`t play mandolin.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Where did you pull that lyric from so quickly?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: No idea.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No idea.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: I remember originally, it was, "That`s me in the kitchen." Not the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Losing My Religion"/R.E.M./Concord Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You all didn`t really think that much of that song did you?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: I love the song, but we never thought it was going to be a hit.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: It should not, it`s like a bumblebee. They shouldn`t be able to fly in that song and become a hit.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But boy, did it fly.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Yes, it did.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Losing My Religion"/R.E.M./Concord Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There`s the obligation of songwriting that comes with an album that you have to put out.\par \par \uc2 You know, you`re sitting there, the label is waiting for another record --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: And Peter Buck is waiting for another record.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: Sorry, guys.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Were you the taskmaster?\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: Certainly, like I could get these guys to do anything. They didn`t want to do it.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Somebody has got to drive the train, and we were all more than happy to have Peter be sort of our motivator in terms of --\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: Well, more than happy may not be the phrase that I would use.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Looking back.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: It`s accepting.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Let`s say, there`s a body of work that wouldn`t be there had you not been pushing us as hard as you do.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Everybody Hurts"/R.E.M./Satellite Films)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In 1995, drummer, Bill Barry suffered a double brain aneurysm onstage in Switzerland. He recovered, but left the band two years later.\par \par \uc2 R.E.M. carried on, but never quite regained its equilibrium, before disbanding in 2011.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: I think the main reason was that, at that point, there wasn`t anything we could agree on really musically.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: What kind of music, how to record it, are we going to go on tour? You know, it`s like --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: We could barely agree on where to go to dinner.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: And now we could just agree on where to go to dinner.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: We`re also here to tell the tale and we`re sitting at the same table together with deep admiration and lifelong friendship.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people that do this can`t --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: -- claim that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Why do you think that is? Why do you think you can all sit at this table when so many bands can`t together?\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY (R.E.M.): You go with lawyers.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Not ours.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: I think we quit at the right time. This is a really good place to finish. You know, great tour, great album. Go home.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Any second thoughts about that? Anybody ever?\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: No.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Never.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: No.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: NO.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Pretty happy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Bill, did you ever have any second thoughts about walking away?\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: Of course, I did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You did. You okay?\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: That was a weird time for me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I bet.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: And I made it weird for these guys, too.\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: No, you didn`t.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: No, it was weird. We respected your decision a hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: Yes, because we`re all sitting here friends again.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: Still.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, you`ve had some major health issues that you had to deal with.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: Yes. I`m not going to use that as an excuse.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What would you attribute it to?\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: Maybe that thing in Switzerland, brain aneurysm and successful surgery. It may have lowered my energy level.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: And I just didn`t have the drive I once did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So you knew you had to -- you had to give it up.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: Yes. And I didn`t regret it at the time, and sort of regretted it a little later.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: So yes, but enough about me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All four members of R.E.M. will be honored tonight for their songwriting and an exceptional catalogue of 15 studio albums.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever sort of looked at it as a body of work, because it`s pretty impressive.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: It`s so self-indulgent to do that. I mean, none of us are really quite that self-indulgent.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: Okay, I am.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: Not today.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: And then I went up on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and I watched some live performance. I`m like, wow, I can`t believe we did that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The One I Love"/R.E.M./Glastonbury Festival)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STIPE: And we did it really well from time to time.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`ve got to ask the question that everybody`s going to ask me if I asked, which you know is coming, which is, what would it take to get you guys back together one more time?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLS: A comet.\par \par \uc2 BILL BERRY: Super Glue.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Super Glue?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It ain`t happening is what you`re saying? No. No. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 PETER BUCK: It would never be as good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, you know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because everybody would like to see it and I`m one of the people who would love to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love to see it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, I totally respect their decision and I kind of admire it, because they --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that answer. Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. I mean, well, it`s -- and it`s emphatic across the board.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, they`ve decided, you know, they did their best work. They`ve all moved on to other things and they are in a good place with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The hardest thing for bands to do is come to that finality.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So true. So true, Nate. And I think and I -- that`s why I admire the decision.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, and I admire that they`ve stuck to it because I`m sure people have thrown money at them and lots of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it must be surreal, but also very satisfying to look back at what they did back in the day. To say, God, we were really good and we still can do that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is an incredible catalogue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it really is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, it is 15 albums in 31 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Just the productivity is huge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love "Everybody Hurts."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And then the quality --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The quality.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is astonishing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love "Everybody Hurts." And then the answer when you said, do you regret it? I was surprised by his answer. His honesty in that moment.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In Bill Berry`s answer?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. In --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He was the only one that didn`t respond.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Everybody in the room was surprised including their manager.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That that came out because, you know, we certainly weren`t expecting it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And good on you for going back to it because they all said no, no, no. And then wait, wait a minute.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What did you say?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you say?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And as you can tell, there`s a lot here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Very touching --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of music, 