o 65000 to get early access to all of our deals.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Roll out. Before you roll out to work, stick around, we have something good for you. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 And Tony --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" -- very important to Harvard study trying to explain alien life or UFOs.\par \par \uc2 So often when you see an unidentified flying object, it`s either blamed on a potential alien civilization visiting us or maybe it`s just human technology, weather balloon.\par \par \uc2 Well here comes Harvard with some additional theories.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A third set of options here, possibly the aliens are already among us. They are living underground. Possibly they`re on the moon in a colony there. Magical elves is presented as a theory, I`m not joking.\par \par \uc2 And then the fourth option is that they are truly passing among us taking on human form.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So really my "Talk of the Table" is do you know any aliens that might be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There are some people that --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Passing as people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are they in the studio right now?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Are out of this world with the way that they act.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Are they in the studio, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Klaus thinks they are running Congress. He thinks that they are there. Klaus on Steadicam thinks they are elected official.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That actually sounds about right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That might be right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That might be right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that`s good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good.\par \par \uc2 My talk at the table is Flavor Flav taking his mission to save \uc2 Red Lobster\uc2  to another level. Last week, remember we showed you the photo of the rapper with tables filled with food at \uc2 Red Lobster\uc2  saying he ordered the whole menu after the chain filed for bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 He said, "Ya boy." The company is now partnered with the hype man for a new ad to promote crab fest. Here`s the ad.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Red Lobster\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think guys, this is genius.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is genius.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t -- I don`t know here on -- who on \uc2 Red Lobster\uc2 , that marketing team saw that post and said we need to work with him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They deserve a raise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want -- I would like to interview them, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Honestly, I think it`s such a great idea. And I love that -- I`ve reached out to Flavor Flav by the way. He`s very excited about this whole partnership. And he really does want to make a difference and save \uc2 Red Lobster\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think these commercials will make a difference. They`re really, really good. It makes me want to go get a crab leg. And of course, who doesn`t love a cheddar biscuit?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a \uc2 Red Lobster\uc2  on the block.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ain`t it smart?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We can go down there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s going to save it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we can. And everybody in their life has a \uc2 Red Lobster\uc2  story. Everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that. Not all heroes wear capes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some wear clocks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or bibs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ye boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ye boy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shoutout to Flav. That`s my guy. I know Flav personally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like him, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, okay, so my "Talk of the Table" is the emotional moment when a woman in England got to ring the hospital bells --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- signaling she is cancer free.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We always love these videos, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But believe it or not, it got better. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @Lorraine C.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lorraine Clive says she had no idea her boyfriend was going to propose, of course, she said yes.\par \par \uc2 She had battled cancer, breast cancer for more than a year undergoing radiotherapy treatment that led to hair loss and severe fatigue.\par \par \uc2 Now, she says, she is looking forward to the future. I think this is so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that story. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because we all know when you`re battling something like that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are battling death.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he made a decision --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- that I`m going to live the rest of my life with intention.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With the woman that I love.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And now is the time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Cancer is a family fight. You fight it with your -- you`re with your people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is saying to her, I`m your people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so true. For better or worse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of the rest of your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For real, for real.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For real, for real.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s so nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Well said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we are excited to have Oscar winner Ariana DeBose in our progressive greenroom.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we will talk to her about hosting this Sunday`s Tony Awards for the third time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. Okay. One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at his.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But first, here is a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tony Award Production/White Cherry Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Ariana DeBose performing her show-stopping opening number at last year`s Tony Awards. DeBose is known for her Academy Award winning performance of Anita and Steven Spielberg`s "West Side Story." And she earned a Tony nomination for her role in "Summer: The Donna Summer Musical."\par \par \uc2 Now, she is returning as host of this Sunday`s Tony Awards for the third time and this time, she will also serve as producer and choreographed the opening number.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of course she is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ariana DeBose joins us now. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE (Actor): I`m so good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for coming. So number three, you are hosting this gig, the Tony Awards. How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: I`m feeling good. I am a little tired, perhaps, probably.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: But it`s an exciting time. You know, the lead up to the Tonys for the entire community is always a little grueling, but it`s very rewarding by the time you get to Tony Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of one of those hats, choreographing the opening number.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What goes into that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And producing, too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And producing. It is so big, so grand. It`s the opening number that gets everybody excited.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where do you even begin with that thing?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Well, you know, first of all, I`m not doing it alone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: I am whole choreographing this number with my longtime collaborator, Julius Anthony Rubio and we conceived this whole idea for how we wanted to open the show.\par \par \uc2 And in my opinion, it does feel new and fresh, and it has a different look than my prior openings.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you feel pressure though? Because last year was so good, Ariana.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We had never seen anything like that. You seem to be in three different places at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Did you feel pressure to top that? Or what was your thinking about what you want to do this time?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: No. No. The short answer is no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: I don`t -- I never feel the need to duplicate or recycle anything --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: -- that I`ve done. I think, I come to every year with fresh eyes because every season is a different season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Every show, especially the ones I seem to host in three different locations, they have different needs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Different requirements, and we are at Lincoln Center this year, and there is an energy to that space that is very different from last year. So we`re using the tools that we have in front of us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As a producer, did you feel you had to see most of the shows, if not all of the shows? Because something seems to be happening with Broadway this season to me.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Yes, well, there is. I think Broadway is in a very transformational time. You know, as much as Broadway is back, we are reestablishing who we are. The old playbook, you know, pre-pandemic, it doesn`t necessarily work now.\par \par \uc2 So we had a huge season, and now we get to sort of play with the different audiences that we are cultivating and learn from them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you feel you have to go see all the shows?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just to be better prepared?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: I mean, I think there`s a responsibility in seeing all of it because how do you make a show if you don`t know what`s running?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: That`s common sense.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: You`ve got to have a map, so I make it my business to see everything.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not bad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, I want to take us to a slightly different galaxy of art and performance.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that is the Marvel universe. You are going to make your Marvel debut as Calypso, Kraven the Hunter.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is it like to be in that world?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: It`s a doozy, but it`s fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a doozy.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: You know? Yes. That`s Calypso. That`s what she looks like.\par \par \uc2 I had a wonderful time working with Aaron Taylor-Johnson and J.C. Chandor, our director, and you know, I think -- I think it`s going to be a surprise, which is kind of fun, you know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But listen, you play Calypso, and this year alone, your movies -- an astronaut, an espionage field agent, a voodoo priestess. I mean, you`re taking on such a variety of characters.\par \par \uc2 Are you sending a message to all of us that I can do many things and I want to do many thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: That`s always been my goal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: I believe in versatility. I don`t like doing the same thing twice. And I think it`s good to be an artist with lots of different things to offer. And I think I`ve probably only scratched the surface of what I can do.\par \par \uc2 I like to say I`m in my like Debbie Allen era.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Well, Miss Allen does everything. She directs, she choreographs, she`s always been an incredible dancer. But like she, she`s a leader, like she feels like a culture shifter, maker, mover.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Shaker. And I would like to, you know, attempt to be that as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve also stepped out a little bit in politics as a member of the LGBT community. The Pride Month is June, as you know.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You encourage people to vote saying this: "Our voting rights are intrinsically connected to our rights as LGBTQ people."\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why was it important for you to speak so publicly? Because many people are going, I ain`t going to nothing. There she goes.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: No, I mean, it`s a hot topic, for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: But I do think that this year matters, potentially more so than ever. You have the choice between democracy and autocracy and you have to show up to the polls.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: You have -- if you don`t make your voice heard, then essentially, you`re not participating. And we can`t -- I just don`t think we can have that in the times that we live in.\par \par \uc2 We all need to be represented. Your humanity is part of your rights as a human.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: You know, human rights, they go hand in hand.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: So I just think it`s part of my responsibility with platform. It is hard sometimes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: But I think the risk is worth it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The risk is worth it. No doubt about it, because change is worth it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you aware that Nate Burleson will be on stage on Sunday?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: I heard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you heard?\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: I heard from a little bird.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: That he might be looking fresh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t be trying to big time me when I say hi to you, all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I am joking.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA DEBOSE: Never.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ariana DeBose, thank you so much for joining us and we know that you are going to do awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON:` And you can watch her host the 77th Annual Tony Awards this Sunday, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming of course on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`ll do awesome as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, you`re going to be great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, brother. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he always is. I can`t wait to see what she wears and what she does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the shoes and the jeans right now. Go Ariana.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to meet the author of a new memoir, I love this book, it`s getting a whole lot of buzz.\par \par \uc2 Glynnis MacNicol`s book, there she is -- it is just called -- I love this title, Glynnis, "I`m Mostly Here To Enjoy Myself" and boy did you do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Woo-wee.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Glynnis, Ariana said, "Work." She`ll explain when we come back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, you getting some tips?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m getting it all, Gayle. I am getting it all.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Perfect. Perfect. Look at that picture.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is the author of one of the most talked about books of the year.\par \par \uc2 Her name is Glynnis MacNicol. Her new memoir is also -- I love this title - - "I`m Mostly Here to Enjoy Myself."\par \par \uc2 Listen to the second line: "One woman`s pursuit of pleasure in Paris."\par \par \uc2 So it follows Glynnis` journey to the City of Lights after more than a year of pandemic isolation here in New York, determined to find enjoyment on her own terms. Yes, Glynnis.\par \par \uc2 She writes about her days in Paris full of food.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Friendship.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And some sex.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is a bicycle involved, too.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, Glynnis MacNicol, we`re very happy to say joins us first. We love when this happens.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL ("I`m Mostly Here to Enjoy Myself" author): Hello.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Glynnis, bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some sex.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, bravo, bravo.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bravo is right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I am so excited to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I am like in awe of you and afraid at the same time. I was so excited to meet you.\par \par \uc2 Now, I normally always like to read the blurbs, and one stood out to me. Because it came from Katy Tur, also known as --\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Aka Mrs. Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- Mrs. Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says this: "I thought I knew pleasure when I met my husband, but reading this book makes me think I should have stayed single in Paris. Finally, a model of womanhood beyond kids and marriage, a vision of what it can be to embrace freedom. Every word was a pleasure."\par \par \uc2 Tony, do you have any comment before we go to Glynnis?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I will say --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before we go to Glynnis.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What I will say to men is buy this for your wife. She will be in pretty hot and heavy and you`ll enjoy it, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. Oh. Nice.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I`m here to help everyone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I`m here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Glynnis, I love the premise of the book.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it started after you said, you were in isolation in your studio apartment here in New York feeling what and you decided to do what?\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: So, I spent more than a year alone, like alone, alone, untouched. I say, un-smelled, because it is such a strange thing to not have anyone interact in any way with your body.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Unseen except as we were all seeing each other sort of with the screens.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: And my exterminator for the building would see me sometimes. That was sort of the extent.\par \par \uc2 So that spring after vaccines rolled out, before delta rolled in, I have spent a lot of time in Paris. I had a very dear French group there and I bought a plane ticket and just thought, and if the plane takes off, I`ll be on it.\par \par \uc2 And I landed in Paris, and it was after so much deprivation, it was sort of like that first glass of water and you were like, oh, I am really thirsty.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Truly, and I just hurled myself in to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is the untouched part, because you said touch is essential.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Touch is essential.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said, of all the senses, it is what we cannot live without.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Being untouched really struck you.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Being untouched when I was -- a syndrome called skin hunger and when babies go untouched for too long.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: It has really dire consequences on their development and they`ve done studies on animals who if you -- if they go untouched, they prefer touch over food, which is sort of incredible, so to be untouched and then suddenly, so much touch, really.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of touch, I was going to say. You need touch, but touch is also pleasure, right?\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Touch feels good.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I mean, so much -- I`d had nothing -- not nothing pleasurable. I mean, that was a difficult time for all of us. But it was sort of like, I dipped my toe in and thought, well, maybe this will be enough. But then I was like, nope, that is not enough. I want more and more.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just go in the deep end.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes. I went into --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It looks as if --\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes, I went into the deep end.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But what`s so great about the book actually, is the message that women hear particularly as they are around 40 and above is that touch is sort of no longer for you.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are no longer -- you don`t matter --\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- the same way to the world.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s not the experience you had.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: My experience of aging has contradicted so many of the things I`ve been told aging would bring to me as a woman, and I think the absence of a -- you`re not -- you`ll be increasingly invisible, that you won`t be considered attractive. The absolute opposite has been true.\par \par \uc2 And I think entering into my late 40s in the manner I did through this book was a real reminder of the stories were told do such a disservice to our experiences.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You called it gaslighting.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Gas -- it feels like gaslighting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, gaslighting, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But how much of that is intentional though in how you move?\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: How intentional for me or intentional for --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, for you, because yes, you can be told women of a certain age --\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- your life will start to head down the other side of the hill.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you are also intentional.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of where you put yourself --\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- in what do you wanted in your life.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: When I landed in Paris, it was, I wouldn`t say an act of desperation, but there was a little bit of a frenzy, like I have to -- I cannot spend one more day by myself.\par \par \uc2 And so the intentionality for me at that point was very immediate. It was like instinctual.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I thought, I just want this and I`m not going to think about whether I should get it and then subsequently, I thought, oh, I can have as much of it as I want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But let`s talk about when you did. You went on dating apps.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You had a range, from 28 to 58.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You had a good time.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I had a very good time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You had a really good time with no -- this is what I like about it though, with no apologies.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a grown ass woman. It was consensual --\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- on all level.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: On all levels.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I worried about your safety sometimes.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you said --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s Paris, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Huh?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s Paris.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what -- yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, it`s Paris.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You wouldn`t do that -- would you do that in New York?\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: Probably not. I think that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s Paris.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: I`m not -- I don`t want to, you know, categorize Paris is sort of a blanket statement of being a utopia or --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s pretty good though, Glynnis.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is pretty good though.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: It prioritizes -- the French prioritize pleasure in a different way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: And there is more openness toward it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GLYNNIS MACNICOL: And in that sense I think I felt far more comfortable with my own activities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Glynnis --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Glynnis, we love the book.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is an incredible book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we love the book, you said, "Misery loves company, but so does joy."\par \par \uc2 Book your flights to Paris.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I got you, Gayle. She is overheating a little bit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ll love it, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I got you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240613ek6d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden`s Executive Action on Immigration; Hunter Biden Trial Openin