es are confirmed dead.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Coffins of dead people instead of getting them alive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we will share an exclusive interview with a Texas father who says the state`s restrictive abortion laws turned a family tragedy into a medical nightmare.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON (Father): The conversation is not what`s best for my wife.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Remembering a Cowboys legend. Pro Football Hall of Famer Larry Allen died while on vacation in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): You should be prosecuted for crimes against humanity. You belong in prison, Dr. Fauci.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("The Daily Show" host): Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  testified in front of Congress today and ironically, contracted rabies. Terrible.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): In Congress today, a representative named John Rose of Tennessee brought his son to the office. His son`s name Guy.\par \par \uc2 Guy was killing it on \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN ROSE (R-TN): Regardless of one`s opinion of the current Republican nominee, we`d be well served to remember the long and cherished tradition we have in this country of settling our political differences at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is six years old. He looked like a young Klaus to me. Klaus is on our steady cam and if you can see Klaus, you get the joke. Yes, let`s show a picture, Klaus. Doesn`t he look like a young Klaus?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Show your handsome mugs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There he is right there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, there he is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at that camera there. Oh my God. Yes, it is him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But anyway, I love it when little kids do that because they have no idea.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re not thinking about embarrassing dad. They`re just having a good time. I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But some moms wouldn`t play that though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Going to sit still.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sit down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sit down.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Get the shoe -- get the shoe ready.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But secretly, secretly though, you will be laughing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t say anything, don`t touch anything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`d be laughing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with a big move from President Biden on the issue of immigration. He is expected to sign an executive order later today.\par \par \uc2 Now this would be the toughest move so far to control the number of migrants coming across the border.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House with the details.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning. Good to see you. What do we know so far?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, good morning to you, Gayle, and good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 You know this is such a politically charged issue with Republicans pouncing on the increase in migrants processed at and in between ports of entry along the southern border in recent years.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats blame Senate Republicans for refusing to do something about it, and now President Biden is trying to take action on his own.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It is long past time to act.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: At the US-Mexico border earlier this year, President Biden urged Congress to pass a border security bill.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : They desperately need more resources. They need more agents, more officers, more judges, more equipment in order to secure our border.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But last month, Senate Republicans rejected the measure just as they did in February under pressure from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Biden`s new executive order will allow US immigration officials to deport migrants without processing their asylum claims when the number of illegal border crossings surpasses a specific threshold.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell CBS News that number is likely 2,500 a day, which means it would likely be activated immediately. In May, Border Patrol recorded an average of 3,800 crossings daily.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Mike Johnson says Biden`s action does not go far enough.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): It`s too little too late. Now, he is trying to desperately show the American people somehow that he wants to address the issue that he himself created.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Former Acting Director of ICE, John Sandweg says Biden`s order will help.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANDWEG: These administrative actions are not going to solve the situation at the border. They will make a big difference. They will bring a lot of order to the border.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And serve a political purpose with immigration a top campaign issue among voters.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANDWEG: Just the act of issuing this administrative action gives the president another talking point in upcoming debates.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Mayors of some border communities have been invited here to the White House today for Biden`s expected announcement. His executive order though will likely be challenged in court.\par \par \uc2 Almost all of Biden`s immigration policies have led to lawsuits -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Weijia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Opening statements begin this morning in Hunter Biden`s criminal trial in Delaware. He faces three felony charges related to a gun purchase in 2018.\par \par \uc2 He is the first ever child of a sitting president to go on trial.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is at the federal courthouse in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 After a long day of screening and quizzing, both sides have settled on a jury -- six men and six women from Delaware who will decide Hunter Biden`s fate.\par \par \uc2 The First Lady Jill Biden appeared at jury selection yesterday sitting right behind Hunter and at one point, exchanging a hug during a midday break.\par \par \uc2 President Biden issued a statement ahead of this trial saying: "Jill and I love our son, and we`re proud of the man he is today."\par \par \uc2 This week`s witnesses for the prosecution include Hunter Biden`s ex-wife and the widow of his late brother, Beau, both of whom are expected to testify about the depths of Hunter`s drug addiction in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Biden has pleaded not guilty to unlawfully possessing a firearm while a drug user or addict, making false statements buying the gun, not disclosing he was a drug user or addict.\par \par \uc2 A deal to avert this trial collapsed at the last minute last year, so they will proceed to opening statements this morning, Vlad, followed by the first witness, an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent will testify about Hunter Biden`s text messages.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Scott MacFarlane in Wilmington, Delaware for us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 Now to growing pressure on Israel`s government to make a deal to free the hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza. Israel says it has now confirmed the deaths of four more hostages previously thought to be alive.\par \par \uc2 So far, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has not agreed to the latest ceasefire proposal.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab has more from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Israeli protesters outside the US Embassy in Tel Aviv, demanding their government accept the deal outlined by President Biden, which would see the release of the hostages in Gaza and bring an end to the war.\par \par \uc2 IFAT KALDERON (Ofer Kalderon`s cousin): And now, we are in a critical moment, and if we`re not going to negotiate and we`ll make a hostage deal, there is no hope for this country to Israel.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The demonstrations followed an announcement by the Israeli military that an intelligence gathering operation in Gaza declared four more hostages dead and that their remains are still being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in the ruins of the southern city of Khan Younis.\par \par \uc2 They are Nadav Popplewell, Chaim Peri, Amiram Cooper and Yoram Metzger. This \uc2 Hamas\uc2  propaganda video released in December showed three of them still alive.\par \par \uc2 Around 80 hostages are still believed to be alive in Gaza, alongside the remains of 43 others as Prime Minister Netanyahu continues to insist the war won`t end until \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is eliminated and that he isn`t being pressured to dismiss the Israeli War Cabinet approved ceasefire agreement by members of his own extreme right-wing coalition after two ultra-nationalist leaders threatened to quit and topple his government over the plan, calling it a surrender deal.\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: As the misery across Gaza only deepens, a CBS News team was that the Al-Aqsa Hospital in Deir Al-Balah following an Israeli airstrike that killed several Palestinians, including small children and babies.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "They were all children, sleeping in the safety of their own home," he says. "They did nothing wrong."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And this morning, the US is calling on the \uc2 United Nations Security Council\uc2  to adopt a resolution backing the proposal outlined by President Biden to end the fighting in Gaza and see the hostages released, including calls for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to accept the deal, and I am quoting here "without delay and without condition."\par \par \uc2 But the draft so far makes no mention of Israel accepting the deal -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imtiaz Tyab reporting from Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to our exclusive interview with a Jewish US Army Major who resigned to protest US support for Israel`s war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Until yesterday, Harrison Mann worked in the Middle East Bureau of the Defense Intelligence Agency. In his first TV interview, he tells chief investigative correspondent \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2 , why he can no longer serve in the army.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: When the report started to filter in about a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack of Israel on October 7th, former Army Major Harrison Mann knew what to expect.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN (Former \uc2 US Army\uc2  major): All hands on deck, people working late hours, you know, 24/7 at the outset.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: What was unexpected for him was Israel`s response.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN: We saw even from the first days of the Israeli air campaign, willingness to inflict very high civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: A response this 13-year Army vet working on Middle East issues for the \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2  says was enabled by US weapons and indiscriminately targeted Palestinian civilians.\par \par \uc2 Were the Israelis using American weapons against civilians in Gaza?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN: I can say, almost certainly, yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: But were they doing so intentionally?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN: I don`t know how you kill 35,000 civilians by accident.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Mann resigned in November.\par \par \uc2 This is your letter --\par \par \uc2 No longer able to justify his worth to himself, which he explained to his colleagues in a letter.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN: At some point, whatever the justification, you`re either advancing a policy that enables the mass starvation of children or you`re not.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: You felt your work was directly connected to starving children.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Another \uc2 US government\uc2  official announced today she has resigned.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: More than a half dozen government officials have publicly resigned in protest of US support for Israel`s war. Mann is the first from the military, the first from the intelligence community, and the first who is Jewish.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN: I understand if people are angry that I chose to speak about this, but I didn`t really feel like I had that much of a choice.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: The grandson of Jews who fled the antisemitism of Eastern Europe, Mann doesn`t agree that the cry of "never again" that galvanized Jews after the Holocaust warrants the current Israeli response.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON MANN: They`re not responding in a way that is productive for the security of the State of Israel or Jews worldwide, and I think using "never again" as a justification, I am confident saying it is certainly, you know, some measure of ethnic cleansing, I do not think is in the spirit of "never again."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Powerful reporting from \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to weather right now. We saw extreme conditions in the South yesterday, including winds up to 75 miles an hour in Tyler, Texas. The owner of this home partially crushed by a tree told our affiliate she is thankful to be alive.\par \par \uc2 For more on all of this, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Damaging winds will be the primary threat yet again today. There is also the potential for hail and flooding as energy slides into the center of the country.\par \par \uc2 Storms fire off this afternoon and evening from the Midwest to the South. It continues once the sun sets and this whole system is going to push East as we head into the middle of the week.\par \par \uc2 In the West, it is quite the opposite. We will stay hot and dry thanks in part to our jet stream pulling North allowing heat to surge all the way into the Northwest. Through Friday, our highs and lows will be 10 to 20 degrees above average, even record setting.\par \par \uc2 Sacramento will actually come close to setting a record today with a high of 101. And when it`s that hot inside a car, in just 10 minutes, it can get to 120 degrees; after 30 minutes, 135 degrees, so don`t leave any people or pets inside your car.\par \par \uc2 Nate, summer officially begins with the solstice on June 20th, but it looks like we`re getting a head start.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, the heat is here.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 , who helped lead the nation`s response to the COVID pandemic returned to Washington yesterday to be accused of a series of crimes by Republican lawmakers who had no evidence to back up their claims.\par \par \uc2 Fauci denied allegations that he helped cover up the theory that the virus began in a Chinese laboratory and that his office directly funded Chinese research that could have sparked the outbreak.\par \par \uc2 Fauci called the accusations absolutely false and simply preposterous. He said he kept an open mind to different explanations for how COVID began.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2  (\uc2 National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases\uc2  former director): I don`t think the concept of there being a lab leak is inherently a conspiracy theory.\par \par \uc2 What is conspiracy is the kind of distortions of that particular subject like it was a lab leak and I was parachuted into the CIA, like Jason Bourne, and told the CIA that they should really not be talking about a lab leak.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That Republican-led House committee has spent more than a year investigating how the outbreak began in 2020. Fauci told the panel that most of the evidence points to a natural spread of the virus.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s turn now to India, where the world`s largest democracy just finished holding the world`s largest election. Prime Minister Narendra Modi is expected to win a third term as the country`s leader despite a tighter than expected race.\par \par \uc2 Some 640 million people voted and final results are due later today.\par \par \uc2 India is considered US ally and it has built the world`s fifth largest economy. The ruling government has been accused of cracking down on its critics, especially Muslims.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to some sad news this morning. \uc2 NFL\uc2  fans are remembering Larry Allen, a dominating figure in a position that tends to go unnoticed.\par \par \uc2 The Hall of Fame offensive lineman died unexpectedly on Sunday during a family vacation in Mexico. He was just 52 years old.\par \par \uc2 Allen made the Pro Bowl 11 times during his 14-year career, most of it with the \uc2 Dallas Cowboys\uc2 . He earned a Super Bowl ring in 1996. The Cowboys praised Allen "as inspiration for many other players defining what it meant to be a great teammate, competitor and winner."\par \par \uc2 He is widely known.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know him?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I didn`t -- I didn`t know him personally, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have heard of him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Among the sports world, he was widely known as the best to ever play his position and even better man off the field.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I was sad to get the news. It`s always sad, especially hearing he was on family vacation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On vacation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is so young.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And as a football player --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and he is so young. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It does hurt --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I bet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- because he is part of this brotherhood and fraternity.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- and to see a guy go that young, especially with his family on vacation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We don`t know the details yet.\par \par \uc2 Coming up at 7:30, we have an important story for families want to have a baby in states with strict abortion laws. But this is not a story about abortion. We want to emphasize that.\par \par \uc2 This is a story about a reality that all women face, which is this, nearly 20 percent of pregnancies end in miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 For a Texas father, his name is Ryan Hamilton and his wife, that heartbreak turned into a medical nightmare.\par \par \uc2 He told Omar Villafranca they found out that the baby they were expecting had no heartbeat. Then the treatment that doctors sent her home with, with very little guidance did not work.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: We call the emergency center that we went to, and we say it`s not working. So she goes back in and that doctor says due to the current stance, I cannot prescribe this medicine for you.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: How did you take that?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: Fury.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, what can happen when the line is blurred between medicine and the law. Fury is a very strong word. The story very upsetting.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, the latest on a new criminal charge expected in the Gilgo Beach murders on New York`s Long Island.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . Round out your protection with life, phone, and pet health insurance.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A serial murder suspect on New York`s Long Island will be charged in connection with another killing.\par \par \uc2 Rex Heuermann has already pleaded not guilty to murdering four women out of the 11 whose remains were found at Long Island`s Gilgo Beach more than a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett as more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Sixty-year-old architect, Rex Heuermann will return to a New York court Thursday where he will likely face new charges related to a fifth killing.\par \par \uc2 It comes two weeks after authorities conducted additional searches at Heuermann`s home.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS PFEIFFER (Massapequa Park, New York neighbor): I just saw the helicopter, I knew it was Rex`s house once again, so I don`t know what it is, what they found or what they`re trying to find.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: And in April, police from all over the state combed through a wooded area elsewhere on Long Island, where the partial remains of two Gilgo Beach victims had been found more than two decades ago.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: What we likely have here is some sort of tip, some sort of additional evidence that led them to go back to his home and that now is allowing them to link him to another set of remains.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Heuermann was first arrested last July after investigators use DNA recovered from a pizza box to charge him with the killings of Melissa Barthelemy, Megan Waterman, and Amber Costello. Prosecutors added another charge earlier this year for the murder of Maureen Brainard-Barnes.\par \par \uc2 In total, the remains of 11 people, most of them young female sex workers were recovered from Gilgo Beach,\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: The prosecution is doing what they should. They`re not jumping the gun and saying we found a set of remains and we`re going to charge him with every murder that`s possible.\par \par \uc2 They`re going, they are looking at the evidence, and they`re deciding if there`s enough there for each victim with respect to this defendant.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Heuermann has pleaded not guilty to all charges against him.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Errol Barnett in New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The more you hear, the worst it gets on that story.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, our exclusive interview with a father from Texas. His family faced a terrible tragedy. How he says abortion laws turned their loss into a nightmare, that`s coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS, it is 7:30.\par \par \uc2 In this half hour, we are devoting time to a story about the emotional and at times, unintended consequence of restrictive and unclear abortion laws, and how they can increase a burden on families who aren`t even interested in obtaining an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Now, this story comes to us from one grieving husband. His name, Ryan Hamilton. He is 43 years old. He got a lot of attention on the social media with his post about his wife`s miscarriage. In fact, that`s how we heard about this story.\par \par \uc2 He wrote this: "Unfortunately for people like us because of the current laws in the state of Texas, that was only the beginning of this nightmare."\par \par \uc2 CBS News is not naming his wife at her request because she does not want to speak at this time.\par \par \uc2 In an exclusive interview, Ryan Hamilton told Omar Villafranca, how the loss of their child only became worse after they went to multiple hospitals looking for help.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: When you find out your baby doesn`t have a heartbeat, that`s only the beginning. So the conversation becomes, what do we do?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Ryan Hamilton says his world shattered on May 16th. His wife was nearly 13 weeks pregnant with their second child when she called him with tragic news.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: She said that our baby has no heartbeat, and that`s all she could really get out.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Medical records reviewed by \uc2 CBS\uc2  news show his wife was treated at a Surepoint Emergency Center branch near his home in North Texas, where doctors confirmed the fetus had no heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: We were told she could take a medication that would start the process to finish -- to finish what it already started at home.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Hamilton`s wife was prescribed misoprostol, a drug that induces labor and is used for both miscarriages and abortions.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: They used terminology with us like terminate the pregnancy, nobody uses the word abortion at this point.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Nobody is?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: Nobody.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: She has not said it. You have not said it. The doctors have not said it.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: Nobody has said it. No one has said that word.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Hamilton`s wife was sent home to expel the non-viable fetus and told to return if she needed to repeat the medication. And after two days --\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: We call the emergency center that we went to, and we say it`s not working, so she goes back in, and that doctor says due to the current stance, I cannot prescribe this medicine for you.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: How did you take that?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: Fury. My wife especially. I can`t say what she said.\par \par \uc2 It`s confusing at first and there is no explanation from them, so you just assume this stance of the state of Texas, because of the law.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: When that doctor said that, did you think you were going to get the proper help for your wife?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: No. You start thinking about the women that have to drive across state lines. We`ve heard these stories and you just, as a husband, you go. Is that what we`re going to have to do?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Surepoint Emergency Center declined our request for comment citing patient confidentiality laws and HIPAA laws.\par \par \uc2 Coming up after the break, we`ll show you what happened when Hamilton took his wife to a second hospital where they were denied another treatment, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We continue our look at one family`s story of a miscarriage and how they say abortion laws in Texas made their burden heavier.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Hamilton told us how one hospital refused to help his wife after the initial treatment failed.\par \par \uc2 In part two, he explains what happened when they were denied a procedure at a second hospital.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: You want to panic but you can`t.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: At this point, you`re just thinking, get my wife safe.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: Yes. What are we going to do? Leave the baby inside her so she can get an infection? Get sepsis that can kill her?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: After leaving the Surepoint Emergency Center, Hamilton immediately drove his wife along with her nine-month-old daughter to another hospital an hour away where she was treated for four hours and doctors confirmed again, there was no fetal heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 Hamilton asked CBS News not to name the hospital.\par \par \uc2 What do you think the delay is?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: I think the delay is their confusion on what they`re allowed to do. That`s what it feels like. They feel scared. The doctors feel scared.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: According to Texas law, abortions are illegal once a fetal heartbeat is detected with exceptions for medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Did the doctor say she was okay.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: This is what the doctor said. They said it was not enough of an emergency to perform a D&C. They told us we could have the procedure done. Maybe we could schedule it for a week or two later.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: DC stands for dilation and curettage, a surgical procedure to remove fetal tissue. It is used for both miscarriages and abortions.\par \par \uc2 The law does not require there to be a medical emergency to perform a D&C if there is no cardiac activity, like in the Hamilton`s case.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the hospital told CBS News, it follows Texas and federal laws in accordance with national standards of care.\par \par \uc2 So it`s no longer medical, it`s legal.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: That`s what it feels like, and the conversation is not what`s best for my wife. The conversation is on the hospital`s side, what should we do?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Doctors opted to give her a higher dose of medication, and sent her home for a third time.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: I was in the living room, playing with our daughter and I had a missed call on my phone from her.\par \par \uc2 I go into our bedroom, and then into the bathroom, and she is on the bathroom floor. There`s blood where she fell from the toilet to the floor.\par \par \uc2 She`s unconscious, and her phone is still ringing to call me. So I picked her up. I put her back on the toilet. I had to dress her, and my only goal in that moment was to get her to the emergency room.\par \par \uc2 Got to the hospital, ran inside, told them what was happening and they took her in. And you know what they said? Thank God, you brought her.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Did you think at any point that you were going to lose your wife?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: So what happens next?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: At that point, were able to verify that the baby is no longer with her. It`s hard because you know where the baby went.\par \par \uc2 The baby had --\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Come out?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: I don`t even know the words.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The medicine did what it was supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: So?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: So the baby is no longer there. Then it is just a matter of getting her to the point where she could go home.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Did you feel that she would be okay at home?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: No. She`s still bleeding when she gets home. Still bleeding right now.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: What do you want people to know about your experience?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMILTON: I want people to know that this really happens. My fear is that stories like ours will continue to get told and not believed.\par \par \uc2 Everything in her life right now that she is having to do to get better, is not just a reminder of the baby that we lost. It`s a reminder of what they put her through and she has to do it every day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Hamilton tells CBS News he does not plan to sue any of the hospitals involved, but that he hopes his story can help other families in the future. And he says he and his wife will now be focusing on each other, on healing and therapy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Omar, you`re sticking around as we all try to compose ourselves after this story.\par \par \uc2 To help us understand what happened, let`s bring in CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, good morning.\par \par \uc2 I`m trying to understand this. I think all of us are.\par \par \uc2 Hamilton`s wife took one of two medications often prescribed to complete a miscarriage. What is unusual about her case?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning, Vlad, and to your -- the point you`re making really in your question is one of two medications. She was given me as misoprostol. Very often two medications are given, mifepristone and misoprostol. Mifepristone helps shed the uterine lining and the placenta, misoprostol results in uterine contractions.\par \par \uc2 She was only given misoprostol, one of the two medications. We know that the combination of both medications is more effective in these situations.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, she was quite late in her first trimester and using medications like these to help her pass the miscarriage is somewhat unusual this late in the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Something I want to add here too that Ryan told us is that when he got the medicine for his wife, there was no instructions as far as what to expect. It`s not take two pills orally and call me in the morning. It`s not administered that way. I don`t want to get too graphic.\par \par \uc2 But he says they were in the dark, they didn`t know how to do it correctly, and what to expect and that itself in that situation is scary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What we should say is that the medicine had to be inserted. I think people should understand it, because I didn`t even know that that was part of the story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it adds something to it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It does.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring Dr. Gounder back in.\par \par \uc2 At the second hospital, Hamilton says his wife was denied a procedure called a D&C because they were told that this wasn`t a medical emergency. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, I think they`re confusing care for a woman who wants an abortion with care for a woman who has a frankly, dead fetus, there was no fetal heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 You do not need to have a medical emergency to undergo a D&C when the baby is dead, and it really should be guided one by how far a woman is into pregnancy, and also what her personal preferences are.\par \par \uc2 That would be the way most of these situations really should be managed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know, Celine, if not having a dead fetus inside you isn`t a medical emergency. That`s what I can`t wrap my brain around, that they go and the doctors acknowledge that this baby is not -- there is no heartbeat, and yet there is nothing that they can do.\par \par \uc2 So if people find themselves -- how unusual is this situation? Number one. Number two, if you find yourself in a situation like this, what should you do?\par \par \uc2 To Ryan`s point, he just wants people to know that this could happen to you, depending on where you live.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, and I think people are not aware of how common miscarriages are. One out of every five pregnancies end in miscarriage. This is a common experience for women.\par \par \uc2 And so, it is really scary that here you have a woman going through something that`s actually quite common, and having such a frustrating time getting the care that she wanted, needed.\par \par \uc2 There are ways to report situations not just for care for a miscarriage, but really for any emergency care, any care in the emergency room, where you feel like you`re not getting appropriate care where you`re not being stabilized prior to discharge that can be reported to the government and investigated.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: And one less thing, Ryan just said, he wanted everybody to know that it can happen anywhere to anyone. He wants people to speak up. And personally, he just wants the old fashioned thing, people to put him on his prayer list in his community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Bring food over when a loved one dies. So similar things like that, just to help them heal. He`s not sure if he`s going to get in his community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m very touched by him though that he tells a story so articulately.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar Villafranca, thank you. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you as well.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is 7:47, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got some big things coming up at eight. Actor Dakota Fanning has made a lot of great movies. Now, she is starring in her first horror film directed by the daughter of M. Night Shyamalan.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Watchers"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s called "The Watchers." We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, a big reveal about the most slime-filled award show around. We`ll be sharing the nominees for some of the big categories at the Kids` Choice Awards. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, some troubling questions about some technology meant to protect kids from hot cars.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, a very important story for the summer. We`re going to show you the very latest technology to help keep your children safe from the heat in the cars.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Plus, actor, Dakota Fanning is here with her first starring role in a horror film. It`s called "The Watchers" and it is from a big name in scary movies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. And I`ve got a question. Are you ready to get slimed? The Nickelodeon Kids` Choice Awards are just weeks away and we`ll be announcing some of the nominees.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is always a fun show. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANDWEG: Ultimately, these administrative actions are not going to solve the situation at the border. They will make a big difference, they will bring a lot of order to the border.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Biden is expected to sign an executive order later today, allowing many migrants to be deported before their asylum claims are processed.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: His executive order though will likely be challenged in court, almost all of Biden`s immigration policies have led to lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pressure builds on Israel for a ceasefire deal after former hostages are confirmed dead.\par \par \uc2 IFAT KALDERON: I feel terrible. I feel like part of my family that died.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Opening statements are beginning in a criminal trial of Hunter Biden.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Both sides have settled on a jury of six men and six women from Delaware who will decide Hunter Biden`s fate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And pop icon, Cyndi Lauper announces she will be having fun just one more time on her farewell tour.\par \par \uc2 Go Cindy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Girls Just Wanna Have Fun"/Cindy Lauper)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Didn`t we all just love that song?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that, I was just thinking --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was just thinking -- I was just thinking of myself like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And still do, Nate. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That video --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Makes you happy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Walking down the street, singing it. You know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I thought it was so great. Captain Lou Albano.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it`s true, girls do just want to have fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Boys, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boys, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we want to have fun on the show. We will a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 But we want to start this hour with a fresh warning about the danger of leaving kids in your car`s backseat in hot weather. Automakers are working on new detection technology after facing accusations that existing safety devices are not good enough to prevent tragedy.\par \par \uc2 National Consumer correspondent, Ash-har Quraishi spoke to one family who lost a child that way.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAMELA CESTIA (Mother): I think he can be a troublemaker for sure, making a mess of the house. I always wonder like what kind of student he would be in school. He would be going to kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: On a June morning three years ago, as the temperature climbed in Louisiana, Thomas Cestia sat in a car seat in the back of his dad`s truck.\par \par \uc2 Tyler Cestia didn`t realize he had forgotten his son parked outside his office for six hours.\par \par \uc2 TYLER CESTIA (Father): I ran out to the car to see. I couldn`t touch him. I couldn`t. I didn`t know what to do. I was just so devastated.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Police ruled the death an accident.\par \par \uc2 Tyler takes full responsibility. He explains he was experiencing brain fog after being hospitalized with COVID months earlier, and that day, he was distracted by an audit at work.\par \par \uc2 Mom, Pamela says the family`s usual morning routine had changed.\par \par \uc2 PAMELA CESTIA: I think before this experience, I was a little judgmental on that and thinking that how do people leave their kids in the car and forget their children?\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: And how has that perspective changed?\par \par \uc2 PAMELA CESTIA: That it can happen to anybody.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: To address that, a 2021 law requires automakers install audio and visual rear seat reminder alerts in all new 2025 passenger vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Most have already done this voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 Your truck in this case had a rear seat reminder.\par \par \uc2 TYLER CESTIA: The way we thought all along how the technology worked in the truck was based on weight.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: But his truck like many was not sensing the weight of a child in the backseat, only whether he had opened and closed the rear door at the start of his trip.\par \par \uc2 PAMELA CESTIA: We have a false sense of security with the rear seat reminder.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Six children, including Thomas have died in cars equipped with those rear seat reminders now required by law.\par \par \uc2 Does the law go far enough?\par \par \uc2 JANETTE FENNELL (Kids and Car Safety president): We`ve been working with the \uc2 National Highway Traffic Safety Administration\uc2 , and they know that that`s not really an adequate solution.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: We got a demo of more advanced solutions like radar systems that can even sense the breathing of a baby.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE BEEMAN (Hyundai America Technical Center): If the sensor detects movement within the vehicle, you`re going to have the horn go off and you`re also going to get an alert on your smart app.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: What kind of a difference would that have made for you?\par \par \uc2 PAMELA CESTIA: If we`ve had had the technology in our vehicle that day, Thomas would still be here.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: How much would that detection system cost you? According to government documents we found, as little as $20.00. Less than a set of floormats.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ash-Har Quraishi.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: NHTSA would not talk to us on camera. In a statement, it acknowledges there may be safer options beyond the mandated minimum. It`s now testing existing radar systems before issuing proposed rules this August.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That family`s story is heartbreaking, but you often hear it happens when you break your routine. If you`re not used to taking the child.\par \par \uc2 Somebody told me once, women put your purse by the baby because we would never forget our purse. Man or somebody put your shoe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His shoes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take off your shoe. Put something by the child that you know that you`re not going to forget.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Your wallet, your phone --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anything.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Something that you always have with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because when you break routine, it`s easy. You get into your own sense of, well, I go to work, and this is what I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s heartbreaking. I`m glad they`re sharing their story though. It`s going to help somebody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It will.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, our guest, two-time Pulitzer Prize winner, we`re going to talk with author, Colson Whitehead about the paperback release of "Crook Manifesto." It`s great book.\par \par \uc2 And later we will exclusively revealed nominees for the top category of the Kids` Choice Awards, but first, it is 8:06. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Man on Fire"/20th Century Studios)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that movie. That`s a young Dakota Fanning alongside Denzel Washington. Name of the movie, "Man on Fire."\par \par \uc2 That was 2004, guys.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Dakota will be here to tell us about her first horror film as an adult out this week. Yep, she`s all grown up.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS -- we appreciate that -- on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Our next guest is the only living author to have won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction twice, and one of just four authors ever to do that.\par \par \uc2 Colson Whitehead is the best-selling author of 11 books across a range of genres. His latest is called "Crook Manifesto," the second in what will be his Harlem trilogy out now in paperback.\par \par \uc2 The book follows furniture salesman, Ray Carney and his criminal underworld friends and enemies in the 1970s Harlem.\par \par \uc2 We`re so happy Colson Whitehead joins us again.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD ("Crook Manifesto" author): Hey, how do you do?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How was he able to hold your face on straight when he said the only living author to have won the Pulitzer Prize in Fiction twice, and Colson just sat there --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He just sat there. He didn`t do the shoulder brush.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He didn`t do anything. He just sat there taking it all in.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: I was very fortunate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because you`re -- it is because you`re an incredible and incredibly gifted and talented author.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. There you go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And this book is the second in your Harlem trilogy, which we say centers around Ray Carney, who has always got his foot sort of stuck in the straight world and in the criminal world. It`s the crooked world of criminals that are his friends, some people that he gets along with.\par \par \uc2 There`s an interesting twist here about Ray, but why do you want him to be the linchpin of your trilogy?\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Well, he has a divided nature, I think like all of us. You know, he has a side that wants to be legit and be an upstanding citizen. He has a furniture store on 125th Street in Harlem, the Big Boulevard, but there is this voice in the back of his head that`s always saying, let`s do some crimes.\par \par \uc2 I think, and so, over the over the series of books, we follow him as he embraces and rejects that criminal side and I think we can see ourselves in him as we -- you know, we`re social beings and have to have to act a certain way. But we do have natures that we have to tame.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you have natures that you`re trying to tame? Do you ever think you know, I wish I could do a crime, Colson Whitehead.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: This book isn`t as autobiographical as some other ones but I see myself in him sometimes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re currently working on your third book, too, of this trilogy.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Yes. So it follows him in the in the 60s, 70s, and 80s and that means following the city as well.\par \par \uc2 And so he has his ups and downs and the city is going into the fiscal crisis in this book in the 70s. In the 1980s, it comes out. Wall Street is booming again, early 80s and then of course, at the end of the 1980s, we see the height of the AIDS crisis and the crack epidemic.\par \par \uc2 So it`s following the life of the city and the life of this person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, speaking of those decades, 70s, 80s, you grew up in Manhattan, and I believe we have a photo booth --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we`ve got proof, Colton.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- of you in front of your childhood home in Harlem.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And, you know, they say that Harlem is a stage. You had a front row seat to this stage.\par \par \uc2 How has Harlem impacted your perspective as you write these books?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And which one were you? The little baby?\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: I was in the carriage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because there are two kids. Are you the bigger one or the younger one?\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: The older one, the younger brother is Clark.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The older one. Okay.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Well, yes, it is a great stage. I mean, the city is going through its ups and downs and Harlem is transforming as well.\par \par \uc2 And so we can see his -- the main characters` transformation in the city itself. And of course, for me, I was living there as a kid. I come back when I`m much older and doing research and trying to figure out where Ray Carney lives, where his office is.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s very different, but I felt very at home going back to these -- my earliest memories.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the things that I love about your books, especially your historical fiction books -- I love historical fiction -- is how you place things in context, but how the more things change, the more they remain the same. Here`s a line from the book: "The city was burning. Every night you heard the sirens. Pierce blamed years of misguided policy, but Carney rejected that narrow diagnosis. From what he understood about human beings, today`s messes and cruelties were the latest version of the old ones. Same flaws, different phase, all of it passed down."\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Well, everything is happening at the same time, especially in the city.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you go down 125th Street now, there is Nike and \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  and et cetera, but one block up is the same townhouses that had been there for 150 years.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re constantly building this new city over the old city and we are living the same stories generation after generation, and I`m trying to capture that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, you do it really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I say all is right in the world when there is a \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2 . I love that cheeseburger.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Colson Whitehead, always great to have you. Thank you so much. This book is incredible. You`re incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very much for being here. "Crook Manifesto" is available in paperback today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not too many authors that could paint a picture like you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, find out who could be the big winners at the Kids` Choice Awards. We will exclusively reveal nominees of some of the top categories.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we are excited to reveal some of the nominees for the 2024 Kids` Choice Awards. The awards on our fan favorites across categories including movies, TV, music, sports and more.\par \par \uc2 I`m starting us off with the nominees for Favorite Movie The nominees are:\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wonka"/Warner Bros)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Wonka."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Transformers: Rise of the Beasts"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON:: "Transformers: Rise of the Beasts."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Marvels"/Walt Disney Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "The Marvels."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Little Mermaid/Walt Disney Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "The Little Mermaid."\par \par \uc2 ("Guardians of the Galaxy Vol 3"/Walt Disney Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3"."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghostbusters: Frozen Empire"/Sony Pictures Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Ghostbusters: Frozen Empire."\par \par