I`ve got a little bit of an update.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a lesson in why we shouldn`t jump to conclusions.\par \par \uc2 Last week, we showed you a video of a Michigan judge in disbelief.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Honorable Judge Cedric Simpson/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I saw this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As he watched Corey Harris driving even though he thought, the judge --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- thought that his license had been suspended. Harris had logged in for a virtual Zoom court hearing. The judge ordered him to surrender and he spent two nights in jail.\par \par \uc2 Now, we couldn`t believe our eyes either and poked a little fun. But Tony, who was once booked on a similar charge said not to jump to conclusions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen to what he said last week.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t know why his license was suspended and you don`t know what the underlying reason was.\par \par \uc2 The government does it for dumb reasons sometimes. I know from experience.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s just true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pull over and park the car.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was dumb, I`m sure he would admit that. I`m sure he would admit that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he was on Zoom. You can`t be on Zoom while you`re driving.\par \par \uc2 And then hitting the judge with, "Give me a second. I`m pulling in right now." Man.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, obviously, I was poking fun. But Tony was right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Too bad he is not here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He would love this. He would love to be sitting here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What happened? What happened?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what happened was, apparently and no one in the courtroom knew that Harris` license was reinstated in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so court records prove it and it actually, they didn`t change it in the courtroom, so it was a clerical error when the judge saw it. "USA Today" reports that everything is good now and the case against Harris is now closed.\par \par \uc2 He told Detroit TV station that the whole ordeal was very, very embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was interesting on the Zoom because he didn`t say look, my license has been reinstated.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He didn`t say.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He made it sound like, uh-oh, I`ve been caught.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The way he responded.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He didn`t know at the time that they didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, maybe he assumed that they knew.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I definitely jumped to conclusion.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are sorry, Mr. Harris.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we are. We sorry. We sorry. You was right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You was right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You was right. You was right. We sorry.\par \par \uc2 That`s good clarification though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a good one. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is, they just gave me permission. Favorite son, Will, and my new favorite daughter-in-law, get that part, Elise Smith.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wooh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep, Will tied the knot -- Will and Elise tied the knot on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Very good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: June 2nd.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ah, look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two means something to these two. Their first date was on the second, they met on a panel on the second. They got engaged on the second, so they wanted to marry, June 2nd.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh wow. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The color of the day was blue.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oprah of course was there because it was at Oprah`s house.\par \par \uc2 It is always nice when she lets us use her yard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a nice venue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it`s very nice. The prices are very reasonable.\par \par \uc2 But I love -- I love favorite daughter-in-law, Elise. I call her The Lovely Elise. It was a lovely Amsale gown that was strapless and then later on in the evening, because my favorite picture was at the end of the night. Oprah had arranged this. Nobody knew that this was happening.\par \par \uc2 When they were leaving, they had arranged fireworks that they could walk through as they`re coming down the aisle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, look at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So she could add little puffs to her Amsale dress and the boat was so gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 The thing -- Oprah and I were just talking about this yesterday because my favorite part of the ceremony were the vows. They both, of course, wrote their own vows and at one point Will said, "I know the man I am, but with you I know the man I want to be."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, that gave me goosebumps. Where did that line come from?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And clearly both of them said, you know, I see you. I will always see you. I will always be here. What they are to each other -- I`ve never seen love like the two of them have.\par \par \uc2 You go to weddings, and everybody is in love, but there was something about these two that I thought was very epic, it was next level.\par \par \uc2 So it`s always nice when you can see your own children happy and married.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what you want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. My ex was there and we were all fine -- with his girlfriend. That was all good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. Okay, Gayle. Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yup. Yup. That was all good. It was strictly for family, strictly for family. They`re going to have the --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did you say hi?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I said hi and hugs and everything.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Okay now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was Lovely. It was all great. It was all for just very close family.\par \par \uc2 Jon says where`s the picture? I don`t have a picture.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, you don`t have a picture that one picture of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I don`t have a picture of that. But it was all really -- it was such a beautiful day filled with love and they`re going to have the big dance party for their friends later on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I am excited.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, I feel very excited for both of them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They seem very happy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are excited for you. Yes,\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As they start this new chapter of their lives.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations to them both.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, look who is in our progressive greenroom. Actor, Dakota Fanning in that room. We were both talking, Dakota and I are both single. We`re both of available.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`ll find out about the new horror movie she stars in. It`s called "The Watchers." This is her first one and can we just say, she killed it.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:34, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "War of the Worlds"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Are you okay? Yes, she is. That`s actor, Dakota Fanning, co-starring with \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2  in 2005 blockbuster, "War of the Worlds."\par \par \uc2 She rose to fame as one of the biggest child stars from the early 2000s landing signature roles, and I remember this movie, "I Am Sam." That was with Sean Penn and "Man on Fire," that`s with Denzel Washington, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now she stars in her first horror film as an adult. It`s called "The Watchers." She plays Mina, a 28-year-old artist who becomes trapped in an Irish forest with three strangers and they`re stalked by some very mysterious creatures.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Watchers"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And boy it is quite a show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a coop. It`s a very scary place. We`re happy to say.\par \par \uc2 Dakota Fanning joins us in studio.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Dakota Fanning.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING ("The Watchers" actor): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so good to see you all grown up. I know this is what happens in life.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Grown up and glowed up, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Grown up and glowed -- you`re so right. We`ll talk about your past in just a second.\par \par \uc2 But it`s funny, when I went back in the greenroom to talk to Dakota, she goes, "Is it a horror movie?" I go, "Yes."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, I was scared.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too. It is a horror movie. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am not a big horror guy, so --\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes, it is. It is suspenseful. It is the genre of Shyamalan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s supernatural. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: You know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But this is a first for you.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you read the script, and you thought, this is not unlike anything you`ve ever done. You wanted to do it why?\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Well, I spoke to Ishana Night Shyamalan who is the writer and this is her feature film debut. She`s is Night`s daughter.\par \par \uc2 So Night Shyamalan is the producer of the film, and just speaking to her, I really connected with her as a person and was excited to be a part of the first film that she was directing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she`s 24, Dakota.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes, she is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you are 30. I mean --\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I marvel at the age of both of you that you can pull this off.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell us about that process working with her.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: It was wonderful. She`s clearly -- you know, she`s grown up in an environment and in a family that has fostered such creativity in her and she had such a vision for what she wanted to do and is also very collaborative and had such a youthful spirit about her, and that excitement that she brought to directing this, and so getting to be a part of that was infectious and inspiring.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is based on a book and you said you decided not to read the book.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: I never do. I don`t know, that makes me sound bad. But I`ve met the novelist A.M. Shine. He visited the set quite a bit and he was so great. And I will read the book, but I usually just -- I think you`re making the scripts, you know, and so and novels have a way of creating a world that can only be created, you know, while reading.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A novel. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: And it is a novel, and so I think, I focus on what you`re bringing to the screen, which is, you know--\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it could influence your artistic approach to the role.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: I think, it could. I mean, maybe, maybe I`m making a huge mistake. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, you`re not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t think you are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re doing a great job.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I was going to say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Until you see it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all have that feeling, you`re not.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have this unique ability to steal every scene when you were on camera, and you`ve been doing it since a very young age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But let`s get back to the movie. So your character, Mina is 28 years old.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Struggles with understanding herself. And then also coming to grips with her past.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Watcher"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You say that you identify with some of these aspects of your character. How so?\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Well, I mean, I think just in a way that I`ve not met anybody that can identify with having a bit of a lost period in your life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: You know, where you`re -- you`re not super young, and you`re not old, and you`re in the in between phase.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: And you`re kind of -- that feeling of like you`re waiting for your life to start until you realize that your life has started and you have to seize it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: So -- and that sort of happened for me, like in those late 20 years. I think also the pandemic was going on during those years. So it was like a little bit extra confusing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Like, nothing was wrong with your life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We were all struggling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: I`ve always been very blessed and grateful, but I was a little like -- you know, I felt --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now what?\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: I felt some of those feelings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: So I could relate to that where you know, you`re like searching for something else.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: And then you have to, obviously, in this movie, very strange circumstances lead the character.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there are twists and turns.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, a lot of twist and turns.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes, twists and turns lead her kind of back to herself, but yes, I could relate to that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me ask you quickly about your sister, Elle, who is also a very talented actor, but the both of you have formed a production company. And we were talking earlier about a really interesting project you that have coming up.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tell me about it.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: So it`s a docuseries on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . It`s called "Mastermind: To Think Like a Killer" and it`s premiering at the Tribeca Film Festival on Friday. But it comes out July 11th on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it`s about Dr. Ann Burgess who created a methodology that led to what is now called profiling today in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow. And why did you guys want to do this really quickly? Why did you want to do this project?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: I love true crime. I love, you know --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really?\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: -- behavioral analysis and how that applies to serial killers and profiling and obviously, you know, all of that stuff. So I`ve always been very interested in that and Dr. Burgess is such an interesting person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait to watch that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I want to see that, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love you when you`re all together.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s going to be good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wonder what it`s like for you looking at yourself as a younger kid with Denzel, with Sean Penn, with \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Holding her own, too, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Holding your own.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that is a --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re still here with your head screwed on straight. When you look at your younger Dakota, clearly, you`ve wanted to do this for a long time.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where does all that sit with you?\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: I feel so grateful for it. You know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Like I can really think back to each movie and remember thoughts that I was having and the memories and the people and you take something with you along the way, for sure, from each person I`ve worked with in each experience.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I feel so blessed that I have gotten to do what I love to do for so much of my life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: And I also feel very grateful that the people that I worked with also protected my childhood, too.\par \par \uc2 You know, I look back and I have all these great professional experiences and what now people would call a career.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: But I look back at, I think back of all the memories, and I really, I think as I get older, I`m so appreciative of the people in my life personally and professionally.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: That made sure that I have that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: As a career also.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Dakota, more memories to come for you. What`s the name of your production company?\par \par \uc2 DAKOTA FANNING: Lewellen Pictures.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lewellen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Dakota Fanning. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Watchers" is opening exclusively in theaters this Friday.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s time now for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals where we share some items at exclusive discounts. To start shopping now, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Lifestyle expert, Elizabeth Werner is here.\par \par \uc2 What`s up?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER (Lifestyle expert): Hello.\par \par \uc2 Well, I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have you got for us?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You ready? We`re going to get the outside of our houses and our cars beautifully cleaned.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s that time of year.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, exactly. So a Sun Joe electric pressure washer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Sun Joe, the number one brand in electric pressure washers. We like electric rather than gas. Gas is loud, heavy, requires maintenance. This, you plug in and you`re ready to go. You add your hose.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \u