c2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Antimicrobial, so no sweat, no blisters.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you. How much are these?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Starting off at $12.00 and they`re up to 40 percent off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Perfect. Father`s Day coming up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, did you, I`ve worn the socks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I heard that, Gayle. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Follow us on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  as well and text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240604ek6400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for May 31, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Manuel Bojorquez, Major Garrett, Margaret  Brennan, Ed O`Keefe, Robert Costa, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,725 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s reckless. It`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: President Joe Biden`s rebuke of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : It`s irresponsible for anyone to say this was rigged just because they don`t like the verdict.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The president`s forceful response, as the former president lashes out and vows to fight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): And this can`t be allowed to happen to other presidents. It should never be allowed to happen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What we`re learning tonight about what`s next for Trump.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 The president of the United States breaks his silence on the historic guilty verdict against his predecessor.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said no one is above the law a day after former President Trump was convicted on state charges in New York, 34 counts of falsifying business records to cover up his relationship with an alleged film star -- an adult film star. It`s a sharp contrast with Donald Trump`s angry and rambling off-the-cuff remarks today, where he called the system rigged, the trial a scam, and the judge a devil.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Trump is leaving New York for his country club in Bedminster, New Jersey. But the fallout from the monumental verdict in a Manhattan courtroom is just starting to be felt.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` chief election and campaign correspondent, Robert Costa, shows us how both the president and the former president are reacting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): And it`s reckless. It`s dangerous. And it`s irresponsible for anyone to say this was rigged just because they don`t like the verdict.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Today, an extraordinary moment in American history, President Biden sharply rebuking his predecessor`s response to being convicted by a jury of his peers.\par \par \uc2 Biden spoke just two hours after former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  vented and lashed out at the judge in the atrium of Trump Tower.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): It was a rigged trial. We wanted a venue change, where we could have a fair trial. We didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In 33-minute remarks that pinballed from one grievance to another, Trump accused Biden of using the justice system as a political weapon against him and others associated with the case.\par \par \uc2 Biden fired back with a wholesale defense of the system.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Our justice system has endured for nearly 250 years. And it literally is the cornerstone of America. The justice system should be respected. And we should never allow anyone to tear it down.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: While the president`s campaign has spoken out on the trial, Biden himself has often been muted on it, until today.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The jury heard five weeks of evidence -- five weeks. They found \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  guilty on all 34 felony counts. Now he`ll be given the opportunity, as he should, to appeal that decision, just like everyone else.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trump`s remarks were billed as a press conference, but no questions were taken. And several false statements were made, including this claim about the United States:\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re living in a -- in a fascist state.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trump also railed against Judge Juan Merchan.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You saw what happened to some of the witnesses that were on our side. They were literally crucified by this man, who looks like an angel, but he`s really a devil.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Throughout the trial, there was speculation Trump might testify. He said today he wanted to. He and his lawyers ultimately decided against it.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: The theory is, you never testify, because, as soon as you testify, anybody -- if it were George Washington, don`t testify, because they will get you on something that you said slightly wrong, and then they sue you for perjury.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: To be clear, no one was literally crucified.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa joins us now from outside Trump Tower.\par \par \uc2 And, Robert, I understand you spoke with Donald Trump`s lawyer about his legal options. What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Norah, Trump`s lawyer Todd Blanche told us it`s a tough road ahead for the legal team in the coming weeks ahead of sentencing, and he explained it in an in-depth interview. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Is there any chance the Trump team is going to try to move the sentencing date from July 11?\par \par \uc2 TODD BLANCHE (Attorney For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ): We`re thinking about it, but I don`t -- I don`t think so. The judge set the date. He presumably knew what was happening the next week. And we want to move forward with an appeal. We think an appeal is -- has a lot of merit. We think we`re going to win on appeal.\par \par \uc2 And so, right now, we`re proceeding as if the sentencing is July 11 and getting ready for it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Is the former president comfortable with having his sentencing happen just days before the Republican Convention?\par \par \uc2 TODD BLANCHE: I don`t know if comfortable is the word that I would use about anything that`s happening in this case, and certainly not a sentencing.\par \par \uc2 But he`s resolute, as we are, that getting to an appeal is important for him and important for the American people.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Today at Trump Tower, your client, the former president, called Judge Merchan a devil. Was that advised by his legal team?\par \par \uc2 TODD BLANCHE: I do not advise President Trump on what he should say to the American people. I have not been quiet about our views of Judge Merchan presiding over this case.\par \par \uc2 And, look, he heard guilty 34 times yesterday in a case that he passionately believes there was no crime. I share that belief. So, the idea that he would react with frustration and even anger at what happened, I can`t judge him for that. And -- and I won`t.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Is it possible your client could be in jail during the Republican National Convention?\par \par \uc2 TODD BLANCHE: It`s possible. That`s something that -- that I don`t want to think about. I don`t think it`s going to happen, but it`s -- it`s possible, of course.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But many top Republicans tell me they are thinking about it, unsettled about the possibility, however remote, that their nominee, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , could be behind bars when he accepts the nomination -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A revealing interview.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There was another big headline from the White House today. President Biden endorsed a new Israeli proposal for a cease-fire in the Gaza-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war. It is the first time the president has spoken about the conflict since Sunday`s deadly explosion at a tent camp for refugees in Rafah, where Israel is currently expanding its offensive, in spite of international pressure.\par \par \uc2 We want to show you this new video of Israeli tanks and troops moving further into the city. That`s a place where fleeing refugees were told to evacuate to. President Biden made clear today it is time for the conflict to end.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe reports from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s time for this war to end and for the day after to begin.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): President Biden`s endorsement of a new cease-fire plan came a day after a top Israeli security official said his country is preparing for at least seven more months of war.\par \par \uc2 The president said the latest proposal may be the best chance for peace.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : It`s a road map to an enduring cease-fire and the release of all hostages.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The three-phased plan transmitted by Israeli officials to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leadership by Qatar would start with a six-week cease-fire, new direct talks on a permanent end to the war, a withdrawal of Israeli military forces from Gaza population centers, and the release of elderly and female hostages by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in exchange for hundreds of Palestinians in Israeli custody.\par \par \uc2 And 600 trucks of desperately needed humanitarian aid would be allowed into Gaza each day.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says it wants a cease-fire. This deal is an opportunity to prove whether they really mean it.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said his coalition government authorized the cease-fire proposal, but he said the war won`t end until hostages are returned and the elimination of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` military and governmental capabilities.\par \par \uc2 The president suggested Netanyahu`s goal isn`t possible, but:\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : At this point, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  no longer is capable of carrying out another October 7. That`s one of the Israelis` main objective in this war and, quite frankly, a righteous one.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Cease-fire now!\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And he called on those who`ve taken to the streets around the world to protest the war to direct their pressure at \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : And let the leaders know they should take this deal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The president is heading to France next week to mark the D-Day anniversary and to meet with leaders likely to also push him to help broker peace.\par \par \uc2 And congressional leaders are inviting Prime Minister Netanyahu to address a joint session of Congress before their August recess in the coming weeks -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Significant developments.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It has been an extraordinary day of news today, so let`s bring in our team here in the studio to help provide some context, Major Garrett, our CBS News chief Washington correspondent, and Margaret Brennan, CBS News chief foreign affairs correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Margaret, let`s talk about this other big international news today, President Biden announcing from the White House this road map, in his words. You follow this so closely. How significant is this?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Norah, it`s significant because a lot of what`s in here \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had previously asked for.\par \par \uc2 So, when President Biden says that Israel has blessed this four-page proposal, that`s significant. And President Biden`s handing an opportunity to the politically embattled Israeli prime minister, who`s under pressure from the far right of his party, who want to keep the war going and to militarily occupy Gaza, and from those on the left, who have been protesting in the streets for eight months pressing for a diplomatic deal, not war, in order to bring the hostages home.\par \par \uc2 So, this offer, though, would require a concession, recognition of a future Palestinian state, something \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  does not support. But, in exchange, he`d get this promise of a historic peace deal with Saudi Arabia and for Israelis to potentially get to move back to their homes on that border with Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 And, so far, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is saying it views this positively.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the president saying today that this new proposal has been communicated by the Qataris to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . How much pressure does it put on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Secretary Blinken is trying to build that pressure, because he`s been working the phones while he`s flying back from Europe, calling all sorts of countries with any kind of influence, including Turkey.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So many people want this conflict to end.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett, before the president outlined this new initiative, he talked about the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trial. And he has been reluctant to talk about Donald Trump`s legal troubles. That changed today. Why?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Norah, last night and this morning, there were a lot of conversations in Democratic circles: Should the president really draw a line here and say the president, meaning former President Trump, is now a convict, make that a very clarifying part of the campaign?\par \par \uc2 The president decided not to do this. He rooted this in the state procedures, state due process was followed, appeals can go forward, and this was done by a jury, not by him, not by his administration, drawing a clear line.\par \par \uc2 Now, critics of the president will note that this president offered no misgivings about state prosecutors cobbling together a unique array of misdemeanors and felonies to go after a former president. Institutionally, they may wonder if future presidents, Biden, Trump or any others, might regret that.\par \par \uc2 But the point is, this is kind of a shock to the campaign. It resets it a little bit. The peace deal that he put on the table today and the embrace of the legal procedure sort of gives Biden this idea of peace and law. What did the former president talk about today? Immigration and grievance.\par \par \uc2 That`s kind of the campaign reset that emerges after this historic verdict yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  saying today no one is above the law and the justice system is the cornerstone of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you today. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Texas Supreme Court rejected a challenge to the state`s abortion ban after a lawsuit was filed by a group of women who had serious pregnancy complications. The 20 women who sued did not seek to overturn Texas` abortion ban, but, rather, to clarify for patients and doctors when medical exceptions are allowed under the law.\par \par \uc2 Plaintiff Jessica Bernardo says she was forced to go out of state for an abortion after learning her baby had serious medical conditions and would not survive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA BERNARDO (Plaintiff): My doctor, my OB-GYN, wanted to support me. Her hands were tied. The hospital ethics committee didn`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 And I think, because we still don`t have clarity in this and there`s so much vagueness, I think their hands still would have been tied.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The court, made up of nine Republican judges, voted unanimously against the women who said they were denied medically necessary abortions.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, severe weather is threatening Texas for the 11th straight day, with thunderstorms, hail and possible tornadoes. In Colorado overnight, hail as big as baseballs damaged cars and homes. Parts of Denver looked like a snowstorm had hit, forcing people to use shovels to clean up the large ice chunks.\par \par \uc2 The severe storms are not over yet. Tens of millions of Americans from Colorado to Florida are bracing for thunderstorms, flooding rainfall and hail through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 There is some sad breaking news to tell you about. We are learning that former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  just announced the death of her mother, Marian Robinson, at the age of 86. Robinson was a fixture at the White House during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration, moving into the residence to help raise her granddaughters, Malia and Sasha.\par \par \uc2 Then-President \uc2 Obama\uc2  spoke about her on Mother`s Day 2016.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2016)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): And I`m going to give extra thanks to my mother-in-law for the role model she`s always been to Michelle and the countless selfless ways in which she`s helped Michelle and me raise Malia and Sasha.\par \par \uc2 I am incredibly lucky to have this wonderful woman help me raise, love and look after our girls.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 Obama\uc2  family released a statement saying: "As a mother, she was our backstop, a calm and nonjudgmental witness to our triumphs and stumbles. She was always, always there welcoming us back home no matter how far we had journeyed with that deep and abiding love."\par \par \uc2 All systems are go for a second attempted launch of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s Starliner capsule on Saturday, making its maiden voyage to the International Space Station with two astronauts on board.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez reports from the Kennedy Space Center.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Saturday`s scheduled launch is long awaited after seven years of delays.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: From our crew, from our ground teams, we are ready to go fly this mission.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We do not have a path to proceed.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: A launch attempt earlier this month was scrubbed because of a faulty valve on the rocket. Then, engineers discovered a small helium leak in Starliner`s propulsion system traced to a bad seal.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and \uc2 NASA\uc2  determined the helium leak is so small that it`s not considered to be a flight hazard. But they will be watching it closely during the countdown to make sure it doesn`t get worse.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Slowed down just a tad bit.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Veteran \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronauts Butch Wilmore and Suni Williams will be taking Starliner on a test drive to the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 BUTCH WILMORE (Starliner Test Flight Astronaut): Never in my wildest dreams would I have imagined to be the crew for the first flight of a spacecraft.\par \par \uc2 SUNI WILLIAMS (Starliner Test Flight Astronaut): Every now and then, it`s like, wow, this is cool.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: And liftoff.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: A successful mission would clear the way for \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to begin annual crew flights to the space station, giving \uc2 NASA\uc2  another option, in addition to \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , for ferrying its astronauts to the orbiting lab.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: On top of all of the delays, Starliner`s problems have cost \uc2 Boeing\uc2  more than a billion dollars to fix. Still, hopes are high that the ship is finally ready to fly -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We have an update on the urgent search for climbers stranded on North America`s tallest mountain. That`s just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There`s late word tonight from Alaska. One climber has been rescued, but another died on North America`s tallest peak.\par \par \uc2 Bad weather had hampered the evacuation effort on Denali, once known as Mount McKinley. The two climbers from Malaysia had taken cover in a snow cave at nearly 20,000 feet, suffering from exhaustion and hypothermia. A third climber was rescued on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Lopez makes a big announcement, but her fans won`t like it. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, it`s not the news that fans of Jennifer Lopez wanted to hear.\par \par \uc2 Today, J.Lo canceled her North American tour, her first in five years, just weeks before it`s supposed to start. There have been reports of slow ticket sales. In a statement, Lopez said she was completely heartsick and devastated to let her fans down.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next with a final wish fulfilled for one Marine veteran.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: one man`s long military career and a special hometown effort to honor him.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: So, as we gather here on this Memorial Day --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): At a ceremony in Poolesville, Maryland, American Legion Post 247 paid special tribute to one of its own.\par \par \uc2 JULIEN SINGH (\uc2 U.S. Marine Corps\uc2  Veteran): He was a Marine. He was most proud of being a Marine.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The vet they`re honoring is Gunnery Sergeant Richard Remp. Remp, who everyone called Gunny, quit high school to serve in World War II and stayed all the way through Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CAPPUCCILLI (\uc2 U.S. Marine Corps\uc2  Veteran): Here`s all his ribbons.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Friends Julien Singh and James Cappuccilli say he was highly decorated.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CAPPUCCILLI: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And all those accomplishments, and there was still one thing left undone.\par \par \uc2 JULIEN SINGH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CAPPUCCILLI: High school diploma.\par \par \uc2 JULIEN SINGH: That was his sort of farewell wish.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Fortunately, Pennsylvania law allows for any honorably discharged veteran of World War II, Korea or Vietnam to receive an honorary high school diploma. Unfortunately, it takes time, and Gunny had just days to live.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, his friends, in desperation, reached out to school officials here in his hometown of Sharon, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 JUSTI GLAROS (Sharon City, Pennsylvania, School District Superintendent): Hi.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Superintendent Justi Glaros took the call.\par \par \uc2 SUPERINTENDENT JUSTI GLAROS: I have a lot of passion for the men and women that serve our country. And just everything inside of me said, you know, yes, go, do it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So she expedited the request and then went the extra mile, or more like the extra 550 miles, driving nearly five hours each way to hand-deliver his diploma.\par \par \uc2 SUPERINTENDENT JUSTI GLAROS: It was overwhelming. And I knew that he had been weak, but, in that moment, he wasn`t. Like, he was all in.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Gunny died two days later. He was 98.\par \par \uc2 JULIEN SINGH: The last thing he really remembered was getting that high school diploma from Justi.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This may be the most memorable graduation story of the season, but it is also not entirely unique. This weekend and in the days ahead, about four million people will graduate high school.\par \par \uc2 And behind each diploma, you will find at least one educator who made the moment possible.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, On the Road, in Poolesville, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How much do we love that superintendent and all our teachers? Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240601ek5v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for May 23, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Scott MacFarlane, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Meg Oliver,  Mike Bettes, Omar Villafranca, Nikki Battiste, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 3,729 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: wild weather ahead of Memorial Day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everything I heard was that this was going to be an insane weekend to travel.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We`re tracking severe storms after the wind from the same weather system leads to a deadly stage collapse in Mexico, and tornadoes in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I call my daughter and I tell her, "I`m going to die."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 More than 30 million people under threat of severe weather tonight from Texas to Maine.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell. And thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Thunderstorms, damaging winds, very large hail, and even tornadoes all possible, as tens of millions of Americans hit the roads and airports. More than 12,000 flights have been delayed and more than 700 canceled over the last two days. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says that today is the busiest day for the airlines this holiday weekend, with more than 53,500 flights scheduled at the nation`s airports.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca will start us off tonight with the severe weather that turned deadly across the border in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Shocking video shows the moment a campaign rally in Mexico turned into a catastrophe; 10,000 people were attending an event featuring longshot presidential candidate Jorge Maynez, when wind gusts up to 50 miles an hour blew down the stage he was on.\par \par \uc2 Metal beams, lights, and a video screen crashed down onto the crowd below. At least nine people were killed, including one child. More than 120 were injured. Bystanders scrambled to help victims trapped under the twisted rubble, as the grassy field became a makeshift triage center.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: "It hurts a lot and I can`t move," said this woman.\par \par \uc2 The local governor says first responders are still on the scene.\par \par \uc2 (GOVERNOR SAMUEL GARCIA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: "We have worked hard. We haven`t stopped working since the tragedy happened," he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 The same storm system brought wind gusts over 120 miles an hour to Central Texas, spinning up an EF-2 tornado that tore through the city of Temple.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I couldn`t believe what was going on. Everything was over, but I couldn`t believe what was going on. My house is demolished.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: An emergency was declared after hundreds of homes in a new subdivision were damaged or destroyed, luckily, no injuries, while, in the Northeast, heavy rains caused flooding from New York City to New England. In Rhode Island, traffic came to a halt as flash flooding shut down major roadways, leaving some drivers stranded in their cars.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: About 60,000 people in Central Texas are still without power after those storms.\par \par \uc2 Crews are working nonstop, but, Norah, there is more severe weather in the forecast.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The threat of that severe weather, as he said, is far from over, heading into the holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 . He`s with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  for more details.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 A lot of severe storms in our forecast tonight, tomorrow, and right into the holiday weekend as well. You can see that, through the night tonight, an area through the Plains, it`ll be very anxious with storms, a lot of heavy weather here, including high winds and hail, isolated tornado risks as well.\par \par \uc2 This will work away overnight, a lot of storms through the Midwest. Those storms hit us in Milwaukee, Chicago, middle of the day tomorrow. Now, going into the weekend, Saturday provides a lot of severe weather in the middle of the country once again, and then, on Sunday, a larger area of severe weather from the Midwest all the way down to the mid-South.\par \par \uc2 Memorial Day itself, very active in the East, a lot of issues on I-95, I- 75, I-70, I-40, I-94. Heads up, Chicago and Atlanta could have some delays at the airports. The West, though, high and dry.\par \par \uc2 The other big news happening today is that \uc2 NOAA\uc2  released its seasonal forecast for hurricanes. The average over the last 30 years is 14 named storms each and every year. We have been quite a bit above that the last few years. Forecast is 17 to 25, falling in line with the forecast from \uc2 Colorado State University\uc2 , meaning, Norah, it could be a very active very dangerous hurricane season ahead.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, good information, Mike. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, Louisville police released two new videos of the arrest of the world`s number-one-ranked golfer, Scottie Scheffler. This is from a pole camera across the street. You see Scheffler trying to enter a parking lot before the PGA Tournament at Valhalla Golf Club, then stopping as an officer in reflective gear chases him down.\par \par \uc2 He`s later handcuffed and then dash camera video shows him being walked to the patrol car. Scheffler is accused of felony assault of an officer after allegedly dragging one with his car, something that Scheffler denies.\par \par \uc2 Louisville`s mayor says the city is not aware at this time of any video, other video, at the moment. That includes footage from the arresting officer`s bodycam. That officer has been disciplined for failing to turn it on.\par \par \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 , which famously botched the presale of tickets to Taylor Swift`s Eras Tour, was hit today with a sweeping antitrust lawsuit. The Justice Department is accusing \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  and its parent company, \uc2 Live Nation\uc2 , of running an illegal monopoly and driving up prices.\par \par \uc2 We get details from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (U.S. Attorney General): It is time for fans and artists to stop paying the price for Live Nation`s monopoly.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): Tonight, there`s bad blood between the Department of Justice and Live Nation, the entertainment giant that owns \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ATTORNEY GENERAL MERRICK GARLAND: It is time to break it up. The American people are ready for it.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Taylor Swift`s "Ready for It", Big Machine Records)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Self-described Taylor Swift fan Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  threw some shade, using some of the superstar`s lyrics.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Taylor Swift`s "Ready for It", Big Machine Records)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The DOJ`s civil lawsuit with 29 states and the District of Columbia alleges a litany of unfair practices, including retaliating against venues that work with rivals to blocking venues from using other ticket outlets.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ says the massive live entertainment company controls at least 80 percent of primary ticketing at major venues, manages more than 400 artists, and controls more than 60 percent of concert promotions across the country.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: There was service fees and tax, and it hurts.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In a statement, \uc2 Live Nation\uc2  says the lawsuit "won`t reduce ticket prices or service fees. Artist teams set prices for their tickets."\par \par \uc2 So, if they break up Live Nation, what does that mean for consumers` pocketbooks?\par \par \uc2 JEM ASWAD (Executive Music Editor, "Variety"): In the short term, I would say that the ticketing process could get more chaotic and prices could go up. In the long term, one would hope it would make things better.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Last year, \uc2 Live Nation\uc2  raked in close to $23 billion, with 145 million fans going to live events.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, here at MetLife Stadium, unlike other concerts with sky-high ticket prices, these fans are getting what they want from the Rolling Stones. They can be in the audience for as low as 70 bucks. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sounds like they will get some satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana is now on the verge of becoming the first state in the country to reclassify two commonly used abortion drugs as controlled and dangerous substances.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns is here with the details.\par \par \uc2 It`s a big story. What does the bill do?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Well, this is supposed to be headed to the governor`s desk. He`s expected to sign it.\par \par \uc2 And it is likely to make it -- it will make it illegal to possess mifepristone and misoprostol without a prescription in the state. And that`s punishable by up to 10 years in prison and $75,000 in fines.\par \par \uc2 Now, this drug combination was used in more than half of abortions nationwide last year. And the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says it`s effective.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So abortions are already illegal in Louisiana. So what practical effect does this have?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Well, this could make it harder for physicians to prescribe the drugs, which are used to treat other conditions and to manage miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 The reclassification would require doctors to have a special license to write a prescription, and the drugs would have to be stored in certain facilities. Hundreds of doctors in Louisiana say they oppose this because it could delay care. And Louisiana has one of the highest maternal mortality rates in the country.\par \par \uc2 And, in the midst of all of this, we`re awaiting a decision from the Supreme Court on mifepristone, which could impose a nationwide ban. And the decision on that is expected at the end of June, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will be watching very closely.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 An American soldier is in critical condition at an Israeli hospital tonight, injured in a forklift accident while working on the floating pier off the coast of the Gaza Strip. Two other soldiers were also hurt in a separate accident, but they are expected to return to duty.\par \par \uc2 And humanitarian aid is once again flowing from that U.S.-made pier, according to the United Nations. Deliveries were halted over the weekend after a convoy of trucks was looted by Palestinians desperate for food and supplies.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this evening, police broke up a pro-Palestinian protest on the campus of UCLA. Officers moved in after demonstrators set up tents and barricades and then climbed onto the roof of a building and tried to lock officers out of another building.\par \par \uc2 And, earlier today, UCLA`s chancellor was testifying on Capitol Hill to the handling of campus protests and a rise in antisemitic incidents. The presidents of Northwestern and \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  defended their decisions to end pro-Palestinian encampments through negotiations, rather than police force.\par \par \uc2 Calls are growing for Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  to recuse himself from any cases that involve the January 6 assault, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and election integrity. And this comes after photos surfaced of provocative flags linked to the Capitol insurrection being displayed at two homes owned by Alito.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane has the new developments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): A political distress flag.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, more calls for conservative Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  to recuse himself from two pending cases involving January 6, including on the question of whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (D-New York): The Supreme Court has chosen to conduct itself as if the judiciary is above the law.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This amid reports by "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " an Appeal to Heaven flag was flying last summer outside Alito`s New Jersey vacation home.\par \par \uc2 The flag represented the fight for independence during the American revolution, but it took on new meaning in 2021 when it was seen wielded on the front lines by January 6 rioters. The revelation came just days after Alito acknowledged an upside-down U.S. flag, also carried by January 6 rioters, hung briefly outside his Virginia home days after the U.S. Capitol siege.\par \par \uc2 Alito said he had "no involvement whatsoever", that his wife hung the flag amid a dispute with neighbors.\par \par \uc2 Many Republicans stood by Alito today.\par \par \uc2 Any concern about there being a flag there?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-Texas): People who are judges on the Supreme Court have personal lives. They have families. And I don`t think they`re necessarily responsible for everything their families do or say.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And the GOP is expected to block any efforts by Democrats to pass new ethics rules for the High Court, which has a code, but polices itself.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (D-Rhode Island): There is literally no place else in the federal government in which an allegation against an official is raised, and there is not even fact-finding done.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: New polling shows public approval of the court has plummeted in just the last four years.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA FROST (Professor, University of Virginia School of Law): I think, at the very least, we need more answers to questions about the second flag and about his involvement and about whether he tried to distance himself from it, because a flag outside of his house reasonably appears to represent his own views of the merits of a case.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Alito did not return our request for comment on the flag controversies, but he and the rest of the court are set to rule on two big cases related to January 6 and the 2020 election in the coming weeks, if not the coming days, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 The good news, it probably won`t cost you more to travel this holiday weekend, but it`s a different story when you get there. We will explain.\par \par \uc2 And, later, Eye on America. Young adults are less BYOB and more BYOC, bring your own cannabis.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: If you`re a vacation-bound this holiday weekend, buckle up for higher prices when you get there.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave reports on the high cost of getting away.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Janet Korren is enjoying an early Memorial Day getaway with her daughter and grandson. Beating the rush scored her a deal on an oceanfront Virginia Beach hotel.\par \par \uc2 Does it feel like it`s more expensive to go places right now?\par \par \uc2 JANET KORREN (Traveler): It is, but, yeah, you have to just keep hoping that things will change and still enjoy yourself as you can.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Compared to Memorial Day 2023, the overall cost of travel is actually down slightly. Hotel prices edged lower, airfare descended by nearly 6 percent, and rental car costs slowed by 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 But vacationing in 2024 still costs you 15 percent more than pre-pandemic. And, this year, airlines raised check bag fees ahead of the summer rush.\par \par \uc2 Sally French tracks vacation inflation for \uc2 NerdWallet\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SALLY FRENCH (\uc2 NerdWallet\uc2 ): That could be $5, and then multiply that times two for your round-trip. Multiply that times four for your family of four. And you`re seeing that the cost of travel actually does feel like it`s going up.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Doing things on vacation is more expensive this year, and eating out will take a bite out of your wallet, up more than 4 percent, and nearly 30 percent higher than 2019.\par \par \uc2 SALLY FRENCH: A lot of people won`t budget restaurant prices when they`re making that initial vacation plan. They`re budgeting out the price of their hotel and their airfare.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: For the 38.4 million Americans expected to drive this Memorial Day, gas prices are only slightly higher than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN\uc2  (Head of Petroleum Analysis, GasBuddy): Most of the summer this year could be very close to last year, prices well below their records that we saw in 2022, and not a bad time for Americans to hit the road.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: You can often save money by being flexible when and where you travel, traveling on off-peak days.\par \par \uc2 You know, we saved hundreds of dollars coming to Virginia Beach, instead of Miami Beach. And experts say don`t save those travel points. Use them as you get them to cut your costs, because they do not hold their value over time, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good information. The beach looks nice.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next with the generational shift when it comes to marijuana use in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: With recreational marijuana now legal in 24 states, the \uc2 U.S. Drug Enforcement Administration\uc2  is moving to reclassify the drug to a less dangerous category under the Controlled Substances Act.\par \par \uc2 And, for the first time, a new survey shows that daily marijuana users now outnumber daily drinkers.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, Nikki Battiste reports that it`s a development driven by Gen Z.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voiceover): Behind this cloud of marijuana smoke is a new kind of office for Gen Z`ers.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW EVERETT (Patron): Here, we smoke because it enhances our work. That`s our secret sauce.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Work `N` Roll is a shared workspace in New York City where, for as little as $15, you can toke while you type.\par \par \uc2 This downtown loft space is capitalizing on a trend among today`s young workers. Forget after-work happy hour at a favorite watering hole. Weed is the new way to go.\par \par \uc2 GOLDA MOLDAVSKY (Patron): I think this is like the Mecca of the cannabis industry.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Matthew Everett, Golda Moldavsky and Ellis Sudak, all in their mid-20s, are like the 69 percent of people their age who say they prefer marijuana to alcohol, according to a cannabis research firm.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW EVERETT: I transitioned over into cannabis because I saw that there`s limitless possibilities with the flavors. And I found like, hey, I don`t have a hangover the next day too.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Were any of you big alcohol drinkers?\par \par \uc2 ELLIS SUDAK (Patron): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW EVERETT: I was. I was, yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Marijuana sales among Gen Z women in particular have more than doubled since the pandemic lockdown in 2020.\par \par \uc2 At Work `N` Roll, alcohol is prohibited, but it`s perfectly fine to bring your own cannabis or even have it delivered.\par \par \uc2 Cannabis to your door.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS SUDAK: Cannabis to your door.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: No stigma, no criminality, no problem.\par \par \uc2 COLIN REIFF (Addiction Psychiatrist): It`s alarming.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The alarm, says addiction psychiatrist Colin Reiff, comes from the scientific evidence. One recent study associates schizophrenia with excessive cannabis use among some young people, especially young men, at an age when their brains are still maturing.\par \par \uc2 COLIN REIFF: Legalized age for cannabis should be around 33 years old, when people are outside the window of developing schizophrenia and bipolar disorder and a lot of other things. Or, most certainly, it should be after 26, once the prefrontal cortex is done developing.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Do patients come in and say, I`m smoking pot because it`s better for me than alcohol?\par \par \uc2 COLIN REIFF: Yes, yes. And people come in and they tell me, hey, cannabis is healthy, right? I can use it. Or people say, hey, cannabis is used for pain.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t mean it`s healthy. It means it`s being used as a medicine by those people.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It`s still a drug.\par \par \uc2 COLIN REIFF: It`s still a drug.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Potency is the key. According to the latest figures, the average level of THC, that`s the main ingredient in marijuana that triggers a high, jumped from 4 percent in 1995 to more than 15 percent in 2021, a four-fold increase.\par \par \uc2 But for these friends, those numbers don`t tell the whole story.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW EVERETT: Over time, as I started educating my parents more, they started to understand that cannabis was less destructive to my lifestyle than alcohol. And I even got them to try cannabis.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And how did that go?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW EVERETT: They enjoyed it. My mom, she had cancer, and it helped alleviate some of her symptoms. And, for my dad`s part, he thought it was pretty fun.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Do you ever miss alcohol?\par \par \uc2 GOLDA MOLDAVSKY: I never thought about it being here, honestly.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS SUDAK: I don`t miss it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: This next generation is giving new meaning to the phrase high and dry.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Nikki Battiste in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Coming up: A \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  pilot flies off into retirement after a trailblazing career in the sky.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet Captain Theresa Claiborne from Emporia, Virginia. She is retiring after completing her final flight today from Lisbon, Portugal, to Newark, New Jersey, marking 34 years at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  with a full water cannon salute. Claiborne was the first black woman pilot in the \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2  and was commissioned as a 2nd lieutenant in 1981.\par \par \uc2 But her career of service didn`t end there. She`s also the president of Sisters of the Skies, a nonprofit that mentors young women of color looking to become pilots.\par \par \uc2 Captain Claiborne had this advice for future pilots:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAPT. THERESA CLAIBORNE (President, Sisters of the Skies): Number one, you have to know how to do your job. And that`s what I tell people. Don`t look for any handouts. Just go in there and do it.\par \par \uc2 You know, you work, work, work, work, work, work, work, work, and then, at the end of the rainbow, there`s a lot of color.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, congratulations to Captain Theresa Claiborne. She is tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240524ek5n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For May 19, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Chris Krebs, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 9,165 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: From the courtroom to the campaign trail, election 2024 is in full swing. Crisscrossing key states, former President Trump and President Biden spent the weekend rallying crucial voting groups just about one month before they have agreed to face off in the first presidential debate of 2024.\par \par \uc2 But will it be enough to turn out voters?\par \par \uc2 We will talk to Ohio Senator J.D. Vance, a possible running mate for Mr. Trump and one of the Republicans who showed up to support the former president in New York, where he faces charges of falsifying business records to pay off an adult film star to benefit his 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 And we will hear from Michigan Senator Gary Peters, who`s in charge of defending Democrats` slim majority.\par \par \uc2 Plus: new threats to our elections. What do we need to know about foreign interference and the dangers of A.I.? We will get the latest from cybersecurity expert and analyst \uc2 Chris Krebs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then: Russia`s advance on Ukraine`s second largest city of Kharkiv marks one of Moscow`s biggest territorial gains to date. Ukrainian Ambassador \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2  will join us with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Finally, \uc2 Robert Gates\uc2  once led the CIA and the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , serving under eight presidents. We will get his thoughts on the state of U.S. national security, the 2024 election and more.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 With Election Day less than six months away, President Biden is navigating a host of challenges at home and abroad. His national security adviser is in Israel today for talks with embattled Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , as the war in Gaza enters its eighth month.\par \par \uc2 Netanyahu himself is facing an ultimatum from a member of his war cabinet, \uc2 Benny Gantz\uc2 , who is the president of the United States who is threatening to quit government unless a postwar plan for Gaza is delivered by June 8. Earlier this week, the defense minister also publicly questioned Netanyahu`s strategy.\par \par \uc2 And that conflict overseas is making waves at home. Voters` dissatisfaction with President Biden`s handling of the war in Gaza is overshadowing his commencement address at Morehouse College in Atlanta this morning, where some students and faculty have protested his appearance.\par \par \uc2 The speech is just one of the stops this weekend aimed at shoring up support from black voters. Recent polling suggests he`s falling behind his opponent in key states. A new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll shows the president trailing Trump by five points in Arizona, despite Biden winning that state in 2020. And, in Florida, Trump is comfortably up by nine after winning that state in the last two cycles.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with Nikole Killion, who`s in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): Protesters greeted President Biden outside of Morehouse ahead of his commencement address to graduates at the all-male historically black college.\par \par \uc2 KOLLIN BROWN (Morehouse Graduate): You do want to remain conscious of what`s going on in the world, but, at the same time, it`s a time and place for everything.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hundreds of Morehouse students and faculty petitioned college leaders to rescind Biden`s invitation, arguing the president`s support of Israel`s war in Gaza goes against the school`s legacy of social justice.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT MYRICK (Morehouse Alum): I definitely think it`s an important speech. I think this is an opportunity for Biden to come out, talk -- talk about what he`s done for the black community, as well as address young people and voters of color`s disdain for the actions of Israel in Gaza. I still think it`s an opportunity for him to win us over.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The fact is that, this election, a lot is at stake.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Saturday, Mr. Biden stopped by a popular black-owned restaurant in Atlanta, continuing his push with black voters, as recent polls show him trailing former President Trump in several key battlegrounds, including the Peach State.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You hear about how, you know, we`re behind in the polls. Well, so far, the polls haven`t been right once.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I think you`re a rebellious bunch, but let`s be rebellious and vote this time, OK?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In Texas, the former president picked up an endorsement from the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2 . He encouraged gun owners to turn out, even teasing a third term, which is barred by the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We won a landslide that is, here`s the expression, too big, too rigged.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Back here at Morehouse, some of the students and faculty are holding a silent protest, wearing Palestinian scarves or the color of the flag.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will keep making his case to black voters, heading from here to Detroit to speak at an NAACP dinner later today -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s Nikole Killion in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 And we`re joined now in studio by Ohio Republican Senator J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE (R-Ohio): Morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 So, you were at Mar-a-Lago recently. You were in New York at the Manhattan courtroom. You were in Ohio alongside \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at a fund-raiser. I know you keep getting asked whether or not you`re going to be vice president or not. And you said you haven`t spoken to him about it.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: \uc2 Sure\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But we`re also looking at a pretty tight race in the Senate. So, I wonder, do you think you`re more helpful to him in the Senate or in the White House?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, I will let him make that decision, ultimately. I think he knows how to best run his presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 And what I have said is, I`m happy to be an advocate for the agenda in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . I think that`s the best way for me to help the people of Ohio. I`m certainly interested in helping him in other ways, if that`s what -- what matters, because, look, Margaret, we have to reelect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president.\par \par \uc2 The contrast is so extraordinary between higher inflation at home and war overseas -- that`s the Biden record -- and the Trump record of peace at home and prosperity. That is an incredible thing to run on. And, importantly, it`s an incredible thing to deliver for our country. So I think we need to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  get across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I have spent a fair amount of time with him the past couple of weeks helping him raise some resources, showing up in support in New York. But it`s all about getting him elected president. I actually don`t care that much who the vice president is, because Trump`s ultimately going to govern.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you call yourself one of the most pro-labor Republicans in Congress.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were out there with automakers who were striking a few months back. And you`ve been very broadly supportive of tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Why are you opposed to President Biden, then, putting tariffs on batteries and electric vehicles and other technology from China? It seems inconsistent.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, I think there are two things here.\par \par \uc2 First of all, many of the tariffs that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has endorsed in the last couple of weeks are tariffs that he ran against in 2020. But now that he sees that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He kept them.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... leading him in polls, he`s adopting the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  agenda. That`s not actually being a good policy president. That`s shifting on politics because you know you`re about to lose.\par \par \uc2 This is also important. There`s another...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, he`s targeted these pretty directly.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: There`s a second thing, Margaret, that`s really important here, is, Biden`s entire agenda, such that it exists, has been about protecting green energy jobs, at the expense of the industrial heartland.\par \par \uc2 If you are in Wisconsin, Michigan, or Pennsylvania, you are not being empowered or enriched by Joe Biden`s green energy agenda. So, him applying tariffs on the green agenda stuff, does it help steelmakers? Does it help natural gas workers? Does it help the heart of the American economy? The answer is no, which is another reason why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would make a much better president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you know, it`s, what -- Chinese electric vehicles are like, less than 2 percent of the market. But the point here is...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, but it`s a lot of the supply...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... all tariffs, though on -- on -- but, to your point, all tariffs, which you`re -- you seem to be in favor of, they`re inflationary.\par \par \uc2 So how is the Trump-Vance idea here going to help make things more affordable for people, if you`re putting taxes on goods they`re purchasing from overseas?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, I -- I don`t necessarily buy the premise there, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 If you apply tariffs, really, what it is, is you`re saying that we`re going to penalize you for using slave labor in China and importing that stuff in the United States. What you end up doing is, you end up making more stuff in America, in Pennsylvania, in Ohio and in Michigan..\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That did not happen in the Trump administration, though.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: And I think that -- well, it -- it actually did happen in the Trump administration, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Manufacturing jobs came back?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: You did have -- you did have significant onshoring. You had significant increases in people investing in factory construction.\par \par \uc2 But it takes time, Margaret. And that`s one of the things, one of the reasons why I think that we need a second term of President Trump, is, this stuff is not going to happen overnight. The American heartland wasn`t destroyed in -- in -- in four years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: It`s not going to be rebuilt in four years. But you really need to double down on this policy of bringing good jobs back to the heartland and, more importantly, making stuff in America. We have to be self-reliant as a country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. We haven`t heard a lot of specifics. I mean, there`s been ranges of a 10 percent to 60 percent tariffs. Do you know what the plan is?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, look, I`m not going to speak for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but I certainly agree that we need to apply some broad-based tariffs, especially on goods coming in from China, and not just solar panels and E.V. stuff.\par \par \uc2 We need to protect American industries from all of the competition, because here -- here`s the thing, Margaret. The reason China beats us, it`s not because they have better workers. It`s because they`re willing to use slaves to make things there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. Or it`s state funding.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: We want American -- we want American workers to make this stuff at good wages.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about some of the things you`ve said about American universities. I know you`ve been very critical of them.\par \par \uc2 You gave an interview in February. You said: "The closest conservatives have ever gotten to successfully dealing with the left-wing domination of universities is Viktor Orban`s approach in Hungary. I think his way has to be the model for us, not to eliminate universities, but to give the choice between survival or taking a much less biased approach to teaching."\par \par \uc2 He seized control of state universities and put them in foundations that were then run by his allies. Is that what you`re advocating be done in the United States?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, Margaret, what you`re seeing in the United States actually is that universities are controlled by left-wing foundations. They`re not controlled by the American taxpayer.\par \par \uc2 And yet the American taxpayer is sending hundreds of billions of dollars to these universities every single year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I don`t want taxpayers controlling education necessarily.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: I -- I...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that what you`re advocating for, federal government control?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Margaret, what I`m advocating for is for taxpayers to have a say in how their money is spent. Universities are part of a social contract in this country. They educate our children. They produce important intellectual property. They get a lot of money because of it.\par \par \uc2 But if they`re not educating our children well, and they`re layering the next generation down in mountains of student debt, then they`re not meeting their end of the bargain. I think it`s totally reasonable to say there needs to be a political solution to that problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, America`s universities still attract talent from around the world, as you went to one of America`s very top schools. But -- but...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Look, there`s still good things about American universities, but it`s going in the wrong direction, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But so -- but \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , in particular, as you know, I mean, he -- he rewrote the constitution. He neutered the courts. He has tried to control the media.\par \par \uc2 These are not necessarily conservative principles. So why would you want to mimic him?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, look, I`m not endorsing every single thing that \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  has ever done. I don`t know everything he`s ever done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: What I do think is, on the university -- on the university principle, the idea that taxpayers should have some influence in how their money is spent on these universities, it`s a totally reasonable thing.\par \par \uc2 And I do think that he`s made some smart decisions there that we could -- we could learn from in the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, he was just welcomed at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 And -- and, as you know, Leader McConnell just spoke out on the floor of the Senate this past week after Xi Jinping visited Hungary. He`s trying to broker trade deals. They`re brokering trade deals, not just with Russia, but with Iran. Orban -- because of this -- McConnell said it should be a red flag for anyone seriously concerned about competition with China.\par \par \uc2 So why take any policy cues from a man and a country and a strategy cozying up to America`s adversaries?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, look, Margaret, Hungary is a nation of 10 million people. America is a nation of 330 million people and the most important economy in the world.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that we should take every cue. But I actually have to reject the premise here, because why is \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  getting closer to China? In part, because American leadership is not making smart decisions. We are pushing other nations into the arms of Chinese -- the Chinese, because we don`t make enough stuff, because we pursue a ridiculous foreign policy very often.\par \par \uc2 We have to be more self-reliant. I don`t like China. I don`t like that China has stolen a lot of American jobs. The reason they`ve done it is because American leadership has made bad decisions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: That`s our fault, and that`s something we can fix as Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You -- you`ve talked a lot about the -- the need for the United States to pivot to Asia...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: \uc2 Sure\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... and let the Europeans focus on Europe. But Xi Jinping is focusing on Europe.\par \par \uc2 Why would you cede influence?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re -- because you`ve really been opposed to helping Ukraine in its fight. You`ve said a lot of things that are suggestive...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... that you just want to pull -- pull back.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... let me -- let me -- let me address that point, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I think the reason that we have to be smart in Ukraine is, we don`t have a strategy. What is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  trying to do? What is another $60 billion accomplishing that $120 billion hasn`t? We have to have a smart strategy to spend American taxpayer dollars.\par \par \uc2 But -- but, on this -- but on the Europe...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Eighty percent of it funds the U.S. defense industrial base from the -- the supplemental that just passed.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: But, Margaret, but on -- on this question, Europe and China and the intertwinement between those two, look, the reason Europe has become weaker is because they`ve deindustrialized.\par \par \uc2 And why have they deindustrialized? Because they`ve pursued a green energy agenda, following the lead of the Biden administration, and that necessarily empowers China and Russia. We need to acknowledge that it`s our decisions that are making these countries stronger. We need to fix that, not whine at countries that have 10 million people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Or -- or people just like cheap stuff, no matter where they live, right? So -- and they look for cheaper providers. But...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, they`d love to have cheap energy in Europe, and they don`t have it because of the policies of the green energy lobby.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about abortion...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: \uc2 Sure\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... because we see it in our polling as so motivating.\par \par \uc2 But President Trump has adopted this position that it should be states that control abortion access.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: \uc2 Sure\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said back in 2022 that a proposal to limit abortion access after 15 weeks of pregnancy was something you would support and some minimal national standard.\par \par \uc2 What is the minimum national standard that you want the federal government to have on abortion?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Well, look, Margaret, I think that, first of all, we have to acknowledge that political reality is, I think, really motivating a lot of these considerations.\par \par \uc2 What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said, which is very consistent with what I said during my own campaign, is that the gross majority of abortion policy is going to be made at the state level.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: And I actually think, if you compare his view of saying, look, this is a tough issue, we need to let people debate and decide this very tough issue in this new environment, where it`s been kicked back to democratic legislators...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. But you want a minimum federal standard.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: But -- but -- but, Margaret, compare this to the Biden administration approach is, we want Christians to perform abortions and we want American taxpayers to fund late-term abortions. I think...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s not true.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... the Trump approach -- that is absolutely what Democrats have endorsed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Prohibited under that Hyde Amendment.\par \par \uc2 But -- so you don`t have a -- a minimum national standard?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Margaret, what I have said consistently is, the gross majority of policy here is going to be set by the states. I am pro-life. I want to save as many babies as possible.\par \par \uc2 And, sure, I think it`s totally reasonable to say that late-term abortions should not happen with reasonable exceptions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: But I think Trump`s approach here is trying to settle a very tough issue...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: ... and actually empower the American people to decide it for themselves.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Vance, thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE: Thanks Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Michigan Democratic Senator Gary Peters.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS (D-Michigan): Well, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you just heard us run through a whole bunch of these statistics with our polling data. And you are in charge of helping to defend the slim Democratic majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Are you still confident that you can pick up a seat in Florida? And are you bullish that you will actually be able to pull off a win in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: I am.\par \par \uc2 I`m still very, very confident. We have all the pieces in place to be successful. And, basically, it`s very similar to how we were successful in the last cycle, when folks thought that that was not possible in a midterm with a -- a party in power in the White House.\par \par \uc2 We did. We made history last cycle. We`re going to do it again. And it`s primarily because of candidate quality.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: We have outstanding Democratic candidates, outstanding Democratic incumbents who have served their state against deeply flawed Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 So I`m confident we`re going to be able to win.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 So, just to put up on screen, because I don`t think we did that, the candidate in Arizona I was talking about, Ruben Gallego, is leading Kari Lake by 13 points. That`s -- that`s pretty stark.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, should -you - are you confident?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: It is.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a -- here -- I -- the lead is great. But I -- you know, I take every poll with a grain of salt. We`re still going to have a tough race. We`re going to still be focused on Arizona to make sure that Ruben wins.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the contrast, his service to the -- the state of Arizona through his work in Congress...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: ... versus Kari Lake, who`s been an election denier, she`s been traveling around the country, not in Arizona, people of Arizona want someone who`s going to be there fighting for them. And that`s what they have in Congressman -- there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. But that is what`s happening down-ballot, but President Biden in the state of Arizona is down five points versus \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So what is -- what are they missing at the top of the ticket here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: Well, I -- I`m still confident that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will win in Arizona as well. The -- the election will continue to play out. These are going to be close races.\par \par \uc2 As I said, with his lead, you know, I -- I always take these polls with a grain of salt, in the fact that these are competitive battleground states. By definition, they`re going to be very close. We`re going to win because - - not just because of the candidates, but also because of our ability to run better campaigns, reaching out to our voters, getting them engaged, getting them to the polls.\par \par \uc2 That`s how you win these close races. It`s what we did last cycle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: It`s what we`re going to do again this year in election time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, in Florida and Arizona, we polled in both of those states. By almost 3-1, voters say President Trump would be better for their finances than President Biden.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, Trump has a 10-point advantage on the specific question of, who cares about people like you a lot? And the economy, again and again, number one issue. What can President Biden do to fix that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: Well, President Biden has been focused on the economy.\par \par \uc2 And, actually, if you poll folks about their individual view about themselves, polls are pretty consistent. They feel good about what`s happening in their family. And it`s about focusing on that aspect. Ask people about yourself and are you better off.\par \par \uc2 But, certainly, President Biden, as well as Democrats in the Senate, have been looking to reduce costs. For example, prescription drugs, bringing down the price of prescription drugs for families, it is a focus of the president that will continue.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, you have to look back on the previous administration. You know, folks forget that, when the former president was there, we were in a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: And he just mishandled the -- that pandemic in a spectacular fashion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that is why -- that is why it is...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: ... that caused so many hardships for people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. And that is why it is so stark when people say they think he`s going to be better for their finances. It`s pretty stunning, frankly.\par \par \uc2 On the issue of the border, we know, just on the facts, the number of migrants crossing the Southern border has actually been declining for the past few months. And the Biden administration has talked about, you know, new efforts under way here.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not showing up in any sort of better reviews for the president`s border policy. That is so essential for support in the state of Arizona. He`s trailing by five points. What does he have to do on the border that`s going to help stop the bleeding?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: Well, as you said, the numbers are getting better. They`ll continue to get better.\par \par \uc2 And -- and President Biden, his administration has been focused on that. We also, as Democrats...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does he need to talk about it more?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: He -- absolutely. And that`s what a campaign is about, and the campaign to make sure people have that information. As you also know, Democrats worked with Republicans to have the most comprehensive border security bill that would`ve been passed in decades.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it didn`t.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: That would`ve made it even better.\par \par \uc2 And yet \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, don`t vote for it because it`s going to be a political win for Biden.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: He`d rather see problems on the border. the American people don`t want that. They want solutions. They want folks who roll up their sleeves, get things done.\par \par \uc2 We were on the edge of doing that. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tanked that effort.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, he...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: And Republicans just listened to him. They`re -- they`re basically hypocrites on this issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But he consistently talks about immigration.\par \par \uc2 President Biden very, very, very rarely discusses the things that you were talking about. And, on immigration, we just see that these policies here, 13 percent of Biden -- say Biden`s policies benefit U.S. citizens over immigrants. Trump policies overwhelmingly are viewed as more positive.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s just a perception issue here. How do you battle that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: Well, it -- that -- it is a perception issue. It`s not a fact issue. The facts are clearly wrong when it comes to that.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been addressing this issue. The numbers show it. And that`s what a campaign is about. That`s what we do, is to make sure that there`s a very clear contrast painted. When voters go into the voting booth in November...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR GARY PETERS: ... they will know the facts. They will see that there`s a clear contrast. And they`re going to vote for the person who`s actually delivered, which is President Biden, as well as Senate Democrats across the country as well.\par \par \uc2 That contrast will play out as the -- the campaigns get into full gear.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 And we know President Biden is headed to your home state of Michigan shortly. Thank you very much, Senator Peters.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by the Ukrainian ambassador to the United States, \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 , who is just back from a trip to Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the United States): Good morning. Always good to be back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s great to have you in person.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken was just in Kyiv. You were there. What did you learn from these face-to-face meetings with President Zelenskyy?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, it was, as always, very candid, very good, very productive discussion. The secretary has been there for two days, so not just meeting with the president, which, of course, has been very deep on every aspect of our strategic friendship, but also with prime minister, with Vice Prime Minister Fedorov, so many visits.\par \par \uc2 Most importantly, I think it was good to align what are we going to do, how we are going to -- to put to the best use these funds that Congress has provided. And this $2 billion announcement of FMF support, which could, by the way, go to joint production and Ukrainian-made production is, I think, a great step forward.\par \par \uc2 So, very good visit. Always good to -- to -- to see secretary there in person.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are the weapons arriving fast enough?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, there is no such thing as fast enough when we are up against such a bad enemy, and we have to catch up for a long pause in -- in weapons ordering or starting the supply.\par \par \uc2 So, no, we need it to be faster.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ambassador, we have more to talk about.\par \par \uc2 But I`m going to have to take a commercial break in order to do that and come back with a more in-depth chat with Ambassador Markarova. So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: My colleague Norah O`Donnell interviewed the pope at the Vatican last month.\par \par \uc2 And you can watch it tonight on 60 Minutes, plus a one-hour prime-time special this Monday at 10:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION. We continue our conversation now with Ukrainian ambassador to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, President Zelenskyy has made clear on multiple occasions now that Ukraine needs more patriot missile systems. He says your country only has about 25 percent of the air defense systems it needs.\par \par \uc2 Is the United States going to provide that help?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the U.S.): Well, first of all, let me second President Zelenskyy on the need. I mean clearly I was there just for two days but every day we hear in the news just today, you know, the horrible hit in Kharkiv (INAUDIBLE) again, 27 wounded, five dead already. We don`t know, maybe more.\par \par \uc2 Clearly Russia is doubling down on their war crimes. They found new friends to produce more of these glide (ph) in bombs, horrible. Just trying to destroy as many peaceful citizen in Ukraine as possible. So, the fast way to stop it is to provide us with more than air - more air defense.\par \par \uc2 It`s also the efficient way, you know, because not only it will save lives, but it will save the energy generation. And everything then (ph) we will have to spend a lot of money and effort to restore and rebuild.\par \par \uc2 So, desperately need it now. We are in - in very active conversations. Literally Pentagon. And I would like to thank them as we are preparing for the new Ramstein group meeting next week, working day and night to find it. But, frankly, this is the time when everyone have to give us a little bit of theirs. So, we`re very grateful to those who are providing us with their systems. We are grateful to the U.S. for looking for them, locating them, funding some of them, but we need more and it`s time to literally take some brave decisions and provide us with more of this so we can see them right away where we need them two - at least two we need in Kharkiv and that area.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: But other places need to be protected as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And Germany was looking to provide some help, as I understand it, on that front.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know - you just mentioned an upcoming summit in Ramstein. C.Q. Brown, the chair of the Joint Chiefs, told reporters Ukraine has asked the United States for help to strike inside Russia. The U.S. has been afraid to have U.S. weapons used in that kind of a strike. What is it that Ukraine`s seeking to do?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, first of all, you remember we had this discussion for two years now. We have the right to defend ourselves. This - we are defending ourselves whether we are striking Russian troops on our territory or Russian troops outside of our territory. And we have been trying to do that. But, of course, there were some restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Now, I will not go publicly into discussions where we are on discussions with either U.S. or any of other partners. But I just want to say that it`s clear that Russia is an aggressor here. It`s clear when they are preparing something. So, the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  rules, the international law and every other rules that exist in this country, which Russia violated by the way, give us a clear right to defend ourselves and by striking an aggressor whether on our territory or for - or where they are launching or threaten (ph) the attack from.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you need more training for your troops? I know there`s now a new draft, a lower age, you need more men fighting.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: And the training is ongoing. And, frankly, this is where we are cooperating with the U.S. and with other partners. We would like to see more training been done in Ukraine so that not only we`re training our troops, but we are also becoming institutionally more strong, building their - our army of the future which will be protecting, not just Ukraine but all of us from Russia. So, yes, in training it`s going to be one of the key discussions with the allies.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And in Ukraine. Interesting.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, thank you for providing us that update.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re joined now by CBS News cybersecurity expert and analyst \uc2 Chris Krebs\uc2 , who is the former director of the \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS\uc2 : Good to be back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, in our polling that we just carried out in Arizona in particular, it was a center of election denialism back in 2020 and we`re seeing this again. I mean half - nearly half of Trump`s supporters say the 2024 presidential results in Arizona should be investigated and challenged if Biden wins. Seven in ten from those who identify themselves as MAGA.\par \par \uc2 Is this going to be a repeat of 2020? I mean what - what`s going to happen?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS\uc2 : Well, you know, all these claims are despite any credible evidence being presented in the courts or, you know, to everyone else and the experts out there. We`re continuing to see claims of problems with 2020, even 2022, the midterms. So, I think the incentive structures, the way they`re aligned right now, it - it creates plenty of room and a permissive environment for those to allege that there are problems. Again, despite any credible evidence and continued investments in election security, election resilience, modernizing election systems. You know, back in 2020 we talked about having 95 percent or so of ballots, you know, cast on paper. Now that number`s probably closer to 98. So, we have made improvements over the last several years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s interesting you make that point about paper because on the campaign trail \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  claims 2020 was rigged, claims 2024 might be as well.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what he just said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), (Former U.S. President And 2024 Presidential Candidate): Our goal on election will be one day voting with paper ballots, proof of citizenship and voter I.D. It`s very simple. If you want to save America and your Second Amendment, register, get an absentee or mail-in ballot, vote early or vote on Election Day, I don`t care.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, two things there. Now he is for mail-in ballots. He was against them in 2020. But you just said there already is a paper trail. He`s saying there is none.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: The vast majority of votes cast in the United States right now are with a paper trail. There is a small percentage that are recorded down on election - election systems, but that`s for accessibility purposes, for those that may have visual impairment or otherwise. And it`s important to ensure open and accessible voting processes.\par \par \uc2 The one-day voting - this push, this drive, it`s actually counterproductive. It actually is counter to participation in the democratic system and resilience in security. That longer run up, the weeks, months in advance of the election, allows us to detect if there are any issues, get on top and fix them.\par \par \uc2 If you had an election on one single day, life happens first. Somebody might get sick, you could get in a car accident, you might not be able to get to the polls. But if something happens early on, it could have this cascading, catastrophic impact on getting people to the polls and voting on election day.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m much more in favor of a - of the system we have now with early voting, with mail-in and absentee ballots.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the director of national intelligence, your successor, as I said, Jen Easterly, also testified that they are so prepared, election infrastructure has never been more secure. But then we also hear from U.S. intelligence that China, for example, is experimenting with things like generative AI to influence voters.\par \par \uc2 How do people understand that influence effort versus actually tinkering with ballots?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: I think that`s the right distinction. There`s interference, which is the technical manipulation of systems, and as Jen Easterly, my successor at \uc2 CISA\uc2  said earlier this week in a Senate hearing, the investments we`re making, the continued improvements across systems, it - I would even suggest that, you know, when - when we said in 2020 that it was a safe and secure election, it`s even more safe and secure now because those continued investments, because of continued improvements across those systems.\par \par \uc2 But to your point on influence, the scope, the scale, the technology available to our adversaries, including AI and deep fakes, it is a much more precarious threat environment. The Chinese are active. The Russians are very active. They`ve been using deep fakes in Europe. We`ve seen AI pop up in Moldova, Slovakia, else - Bangladesh. So, it is - it is going to be a tool.\par \par \uc2 My sense, however, though, is threats that are AI powered or AI enabled will be much like what happened in New Hampshire with the robo call. It will be immediately detected, it will be investigated quickly, and it will be prosecuted. And that`s what`s happening right now.\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest concern though is that this is cumulative. It`s accretive. So, rather than one single catastrophic AI enabled event -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: It`s going to be a steady drum beat where we, where the voters, the public, are just going to lose confidence and trust in the overarching information ecosystem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re seeing that in our polling already, a lack of confidence.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: Some might say that`s part of the strategy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s scary.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: CNN was reporting both the Chinese and Iranian governments had tried to use fake AI content back in 2020 but chose not to deploy it. Is that accurate?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: I - I can`t speak to the specific intelligence there. It should not be a surprise though. It - we`ve been talking about deep fakes and AI as a risk vector for years, for half a decade at least. So, to say that - whether it`s the Russians, the Chinese, the Iranians, the North Koreans, whomever, have been tinkering or researching this technology as recently as four years ago, I think that`s entirely credible. We are seeing it now though. We are seeing, as I mentioned, already in Europe. We`re seeing it here as well. And I would expect that between now and the election we will continue to see AI created content pop up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: And this is where it`s important for the tech platforms to ensure they`re on the lookout for it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: That the AI companies are ensuring that their platforms cannot be manipulated. The challenge, of course, though is there are opensource models that can be used.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we need to keep talking to you about this, unfortunately, in the months to come because it sounds like it will be a persistent issue.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to former defense secretary and CIA director, \uc2 Robert Gates\uc2 , who we spoke with on Friday from the \uc2 College of William & Mary\uc2 , where he serves as chancellor. We began by asking him why national security should matter to voters this election year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2  (Former Defense Secretary and Former CIA Director): Our leaders need to bring these issues home to the American people in a very direct way. The world isn`t going to ignore us just because we think we can ignore the world. So, the first thing is to make clear that if we don`t deal with these problems early, they become very dangerous problems and very costly problems for the United States down the road.\par \par \uc2 The other is to explain to the American people, for example, how we need - how we are economically interconnected with the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, if President Trump were to win again, what could he do differently in a second term that would give him a stronger legacy? I know you`ve been critical of some of his behavior when he was in office.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : I think you`d probably see significantly different domestic policies in - in a number of areas. I think one thing he`s been clear about for a long time is the need for tariffs to protect American industry and so on. I think he would also have a different approach in many - in many areas with respect to foreign policy and in terms of trying to end the war in Ukraine. It`s never made quite clear how he`s going to do that or what terms he would have to agree to or how he would handle the issues in the South China Sea differently.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I think that there`s an element of unpredictability in - in the - in his case of - of not knowing what he really has in mind to deal with any of these specific issues, especially on national security.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you be open to voting for Mr. Trump?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : Oh, I`m not even going to begin to go there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because you did write an essay in "Foreign Affairs" a few months ago where you were - you were pretty specific in criticism. You said his disdain for allies, fondness for authoritarian leaders, erratic behavior undermined U.S. credibility. You were also critical of President Biden in his withdrawal from Afghanistan, which you said further damaged the world`s confidence in America.\par \par \uc2 Do you think Mr. Biden has been able to repair that damage?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : I think that he gained a lot of credibility with the speed with which he assembled the coalition of partner countries, allies and friends, before, during and after the Russian invasion of Ukraine, putting together that three dozen countries willing to help Ukraine with money, with military assistance and so on. Being able to warn the allies before the Russians actually invaded so that when they did we had enormous credibility with others that we knew what we were talking about and we knew the nature of Putin`s threat.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of people would agree that there has been some unnecessary delays in getting necessary equipment to - certain kinds of equipment to the Ukrainians. And I`m not talking about the six-month late supplemental that - that was just passed by the Congress. I`m talking about going back a year and a half or two years, whether it was tanks or missiles or aircraft that after long debate and deliberation the decision was finally made to provide them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  was in China this past week visiting his ally, Xi Jinping. And he said Friday, Russian troops are advancing daily in Ukraine. What do you think it will take to stop this momentum?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : Well, this is one of the places where, frankly, the six-month delay in getting the supplemental passed is - has been a problem because - and poses a real crisis, I think. The circumstances in Ukraine right now are - are quite dire. The Russians are moving, not only around Kharkiv, but elsewhere along the front. Putin has taken the last six months to a year to rearm, reequip, to recruit. I`ve read numbers that he`s putting as many as 30,000 new troops a month into Ukraine. They have more troops in Ukraine now, the Russians do, than they did at the beginning of the war.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: During that time Russia has also built up more support for its effort with Iran, with North Korea, supplying it weapons, with China now, U.S. intelligence says, helping to reconstitute Russia`s military industrial base, even jointly producing drones.\par \par \uc2 What consequence should there be for a country like China for helping this war to continue?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : There are a variety of other sanctions that are available to the administration and to the Europeans that - that could bring additional pressure on the Chinese at a time when their economy is not doing very well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would they make a difference?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : They could. They could. The Chinese have very large stakes in other problems, in other relationships around the world, in addition to Russia. And, you know, until a year or so ago they were being pretty careful about what they were providing to the Russians. They still have not, as best we know, have not provided actual weapons to the Russians. That`s been a red line for the United States.\par \par \uc2 And - and, Putin, frankly, and if you look at the cabinet changes that Putin has just made, they`re all focused on militarizing the Russian economy to sustain a huge military for a long time going forward.\par \par \uc2 This is not a one-time problem with just Ukraine. Putin has decided to take Russia in a different -- in a different direction that poses a real threat to all of its neighbors. And the Chinese, by helping them, are enabling that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the Chinese president was just touring through Europe, and he was in France. I wonder what you think about the French president`s statement that nothing should be ruled out when it comes to sending western troops to Ukraine. I know some other European countries have also leaned into that idea. Should it be taken off the table by both Biden and Trump?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : I think the notion of deploying \uc2 NATO\uc2  troops into Ukraine causes a lot of domestic concern, not just in this - in the United States, but in Europe as well. I don`t think you take things off the table, but I - but I also don`t think you put them on the table in an explicit way.\par \par \uc2 You know, part of my problem is that our government talks too much and some other governments talk too much as well. Sometimes it`s better just to do things and not tell people you`re doing them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well -\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : But maybe that`s the old CIA guy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that suggesting more training then of Ukrainian troops?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : I think - I think we are clearly - and there`s going to have to be more training of - of the Ukrainians, particularly with their new conscription law and - and a number of new young people coming into the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Should that take -\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : That training can take place in western Europe or it conceivably could take place in Ukraine as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: One of the guests on our program, J.D. Vance, a senator from Ohio, has likened U.S. support for Ukraine to the U.S. invasion of Iraq, making that argument that there`s like a creeping U.S. role potentially.\par \par \uc2 He also went to the Munich Security Conference but skipped meetings with President Zelenskyy and - and the head of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , saying he wouldn`t learn anything new. What do you say to a leading Republican like him?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : Rather than get into specific personalities, I just think that people need to open their eyes and - and be willing to listen to other points of view and be willing to learn, particularly from people who have been around, like Senator McConnell and others, who - who can help him understand - help him and others understand that - that this is a different kind of aggression by far than what happened in Iraq in 2003. And - and there is no assurance that - that Putin will stop with Ukraine. And - and to not understand that potential threat, and also the degree to which it encourages other aggressors around the world, I think - I think some - some additional information, briefings and education may be required.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about the Middle East, where there already are multiple shooting wars on multiple fronts involving U.S. ally Israel.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with President Biden`s decision to withhold some specific armaments from the Netanyahu government while surging others?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : One of the things that has struck me has been the degree to which the Netanyahu government has essentially ignored the views and - and requests of his closest ally, beginning with more humanitarian assistance and - and taking care of the - of the civilians in Gaza. I think that there are - there are ways that we can pressure Israel.\par \par \uc2 The truth of the matter is, 2,000 pound bombs that are not precision guided inevitably lead to a lot of collateral damage. They basically collapse buildings. I`m all for providing all other kinds of weapons to Israel, including precision guided bombs, and - and other equipment that they may need. But I think when - when our allies ignore us, and particularly on issues that are of huge importance to us and to the region, then I think it`s reasonable to take actions that try to get their attention.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There were extraordinary statements from Israel`s defense minister this past week where he publicly criticized his own prime minister, saying he can`t get an answer to some key questions like what happens at the end of this war. He called on Netanyahu to make a decision, declare Israel will not establish civilian control or military control over the Gaza Strip and start talking to international actors about who is going to govern. What do you think about such a public split like this in the midst of the war?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : Well, it`s pretty extraordinary, but - but I think not unexpected. You know, the \uc2 United States government\uc2  has been asking Prime Minister Netanyahu for months, what`s your plan? What happens after the shooting stops? Where are you going with this? What`s the solution politically? What`s the solution economically and in humanitarian terms? And there - and neither we, nor the Israelis, including his own defense minister, get any answers to those questions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you said you`ve never been a fan of \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 . You said you met him back in 1989. Are your concerns in regard to U.S. national security that he could draw the U.S. into a wider conflict?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GATES\uc2 : My biggest concern, and we need to back up and have a little perspective here. There are, in fact, four wars going on in the Middle East right now. Not only the war in Gaza, but the war on Israel`s northern border with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the Houthis in Yemen and what - the disruption of the global supply chains through their attacks in the Red Sea, and then the militias in - in Syria and in Iraq. There is one power behind all four of these conflicts, and that`s Iran. And what we`re not talking about, we`ve become so preoccupied with Gaza, what we`ve failed to talk sufficiently about is, how do we deal with an Iran that is basically the - the one providing the arms, the planning, and the intelligence in all four of these conflicts, and that Iran is the source of the problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thanks for watching. For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020240519ek5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 UCLA Campus Standoff; Arizona Abortion Law Repeal; Abortion on the Ballot; Swing States in Focus; Dangerous Texas Flooding; \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Hostage Deal Protest; The Brink of Famine; Healthcare Hack Testimony; Protests for the Planet; Return of the Cicadas; Remembering Guitarist Duane Eddy; UMG Changes its Tune on TikTok; Knicks Fans Dunk on Philly; Walker Hayes` Sober Thoughts; Mother`s Day Books; Jeff Daniels Live; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Jericka Duncan, Carter Evans,  Janet Shamlian, Ed O`Keefe, Omar Villafranca, Ramy Inocencio, Nicole  Sganga, Ben Tracy, Dave Malkoff, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 13,988 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle and Tony are off, but we are in great hands. Here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, police warm demonstrators at UCLA a day after their encampment was attacked by counter protesters.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The aggression that we face shows that the university has no choice to just stand by and wait for us to get killed by Zionist aggressors.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Texas faces major flooding after days of severe weather batter the state and more storms are on the way.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: Waking up in the morning here in Texas with flash flooding ongoing.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Arizona is set to repeal its abortion law today as reproductive rights take center stage on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): You have to do what`s right, but you also have to get elected.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  says its fight against inflation is still not over, which means interest rates will stay at a two-decade high.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Did you get the memo about the dress code?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: For "The Fall Guy" premier, Ryan Gosling showed up with Mikey Day to reprise their role Beavis and Butthead, which was hilarious.\par \par \uc2 MIKEY DAY (Actor): Very surreal, very fun, but yes --\par \par \uc2 RYAN GOSLING (Actor): We`re having our moment.\par \par \uc2 MIKEY DAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RYAN GOSLING: You know?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Good news for anybody who likes Dubin Rowley, because the Biden administration announced plans to reclassify marijuana easing restrictions nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Do you understand what this means? It means here in New York, it will somehow be even easier to get pot.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Because based on what I smell walking down the street, I think when you come out of the Lincoln Tunnel, they just pack it in your tailpipe.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We begin with breaking news and live pictures out of Los Angeles where police are raiding an encampment of demonstrators on the campus of UCLA after a day of tensions. Again, these are live pictures coming into the newsroom at this very moment.\par \par \uc2 The group protesting the war in Gaza had been warned to clear out, but many refused to comply.\par \par \uc2 (Blasts)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Again, we just want to reiterate this just happened minutes ago. Police fired what appeared to be non-lethal rounds at some of the protesters. That was the pop-pop-pop that you just heard there. Those folks were sheltering behind a barricade.\par \par \uc2 Again, these are live pictures coming into the newsroom right now where you see, it looks like hundreds of police officers in full riot gear now holding up weapons at those protesters.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans is at UCLA watching it all go down.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: A very tense moment here, Vlad. Let me show you what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 We`re at one end of the encampment right here, and you can see, you`ve got some protesters here facing off with police. As we look up to the steps there, that`s where the encampment is and that`s where the activity is right now.\par \par \uc2 Every once in a while, you`ll hear an explosion. That`s police officers firing those rubber bullets or tear gas into the encampment.\par \par \uc2 There are hundreds of police officers here. There are hundreds of people inside this encampment along with hundreds more who have come to support them and form a human chain outside here.\par \par \uc2 So it is really a standoff of sorts at least on this side while police clear out the campers inside, and this has been going on for more than two hours.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Police dressed in riot gear faced off with protesters as they attempted to clear an encampment that has been set up here at the UCLA campus. Those officers could be seen clashing with demonstrators as they conducted arrests.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the night, \uc2 LAPD\uc2  tried to enter the encampment, but were quickly pushed back and retreated. The development signifies an escalation is officers for hours stood by as protesters vowed to stay and yelled at police.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: It comes one night after law enforcement was criticized for its delayed response to the brutal violence on campus.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "peaceful protest")\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Some demonstrators rammed barricades, used pepper spray, and threw fireworks at the pro -Palestinian encampment.\par \par \uc2 YOUSEF (UCLA senior): They seem to want to terrorize us.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: UCLA senior, Yousef (sp?) said he needed 14 staples in his head after he was hit by a traffic cone and beaten with a piece of wood.\par \par \uc2 YOUSEF: I thought the police would be our saving rescue. Instead, what ended up happening is we saw the police forming in a large group outside simply watching the events unfold.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Police stood by for nearly two hours during that confrontation, 15 people were reported injured including one hospitalization.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Where was everybody? The whole world was watching. Where were they?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: What do you think about the police response to the increase in violence?\par \par \uc2 MAN: There was no police response until very late.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Law enforcement activity is typically the last resort.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Sam Vinograd is a former top counterterrorism official.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: As individuals are allowed to engage in unauthorized activity for attenuated periods, the possibility that things are going to turn violent unfortunately increases.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And so, we have police here still making this effort to get inside the camp. Let`s take a look at some of the live video that`s coming in right now, and you can see, police making their moves trying to breach the barricade area. Campers had been reinforcing that all Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 You could hear the power tools and the hammers and they were also using zip ties to tie the fence together, anything to try and keep this barricade together and keep the police out.\par \par \uc2 But you could see, they have made their way in now. They`ve been using pepper spray, they`ve been using tear gas, they had been using rubber bullets, and they are now making arrests.\par \par \uc2 As for where I am right now, I am on the other end of this encampment. We`ve got a big large contingent of the \uc2 California Highway Patrol\uc2  right here, waiting to make their move in here -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Carter Evans, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Obviously a very, very active scene that it turned to, Vlad and Nate, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, these pictures are remarkable coming into us right now into the newsroom when you see what looks like dozens, if not perhaps hundreds of police officers in full riot gear and they`re trying to get down to clear this encampment.\par \par \uc2 And again, Americans haven`t seen scenes like this since the 1960s when college campuses erupted over protests in Vietnam. And now, we`re seeing this again play out on college campuses all across the country.\par \par \uc2 It looks like now the police are actually moving towards those barricades that protesters have set up. You can see those pieces of plywood that protesters are using to try and force the police back. They seem to be inching inch by inch to try to move these protesters off, but it is going to be very, very difficult. And of course, the fear is that somebody gets hurt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Or even worse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And when you look at the protesters, some are speaking and saying that we are protesting peacefully, and we are looking for support from the police. And then others are saying that the police are not offering that, they`re actually doing the opposite.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, the difficulty, of course, is that when you hear police officials and we heard that yesterday from New York City Mayor Eric Adams that there are outside agitators who are taking part in some of these demonstrations, it is very difficult for police to know who the outside agitators are.\par \par \uc2 Look, it is dark. There are lights there --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Most people are covered.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are tear gas being deployed -- exactly. People have their faces covered, so it becomes really, really difficult. And of course, the danger as you see this police officer trying to tear down a barricade that presumably protests are put up. The danger is that somebody gets hurt.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, a lot of these protests happening across the country. Thank you, Carter.\par \par \uc2 Now to another big headline: Arizona`s governor plans to sign a bill today repealing a 160-year-old abortion ban. It got national attention after the law was revived by the State Supreme Court in a four to two decision last month.\par \par \uc2 As Janet Shamlian reports, Democrats and the legislature found just enough votes to repeal it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WARREN PETERSEN (R) (Arizona State senator): By your votes of 16 ayes, 14 nays, you have passed House Bill 2677.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: After a heated session in the Arizona Capitol, the state`s Republican controlled Senate narrowly voted to repeal an 1864 law that bans almost all abortions.\par \par \uc2 Two Republican senators joined 14 Democrats to reverse the law after the repeal barely cleared the State House last week.\par \par \uc2 It`s now headed to Democratic Governor \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 ` desk who said she`ll sign it. The Civil War era law predates Arizona statehood. It bans all abortions except if the mother`s life is in danger and could allow the state to prosecute abortion providers.\par \par \uc2 The decision divided voters in the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s a victory for women. It`s a victory for the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The repeal allows the state`s 15-week abortion limit, enacted before Roe v. Wade was overturned to remain state law.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN FERNANDEZ (D) (Arizona State senator): We still have a road ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Even if signed today, the repeal will not take effect until 90 days after the legislative session ends. With the near total ban set to take effect June 27th, there will likely be a near total ban on abortions in Arizona for a period of weeks or months this summer.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN FERNANDEZ: The thing that I worry about most is what are we going to do in that gap time when abortion is going to be illegal in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Janet Shamlian in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As we know, abortion is one of the biggest issues in the presidential race and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not shying away from his role in overturning Roe v. Wade during a pair of campaign rallies yesterday in Wisconsin and in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 The latest CBS News Battleground Polls show the presumptive Republican nominee is neck and neck with President Biden in both states.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the White House with more.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Nate, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be back in a New York courtroom today for his criminal trial, but on Wednesday, he was rallying supporters and again says it is states who should decide all matters regarding abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: For the first time since his criminal trial got underway in New York, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was back on the stump in two key battleground states, Wisconsin and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m being indicted for you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But it was the issue of abortion that was front and center Wednesday, as Trump touted his role in overturning Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And we were able to do that after 53 years.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Trump`s comments come after a "Time" Magazine interview where he said it`s up to states whether to monitor women`s pregnancies or prosecute them for having an abortion, and he continued to defend his stance.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : The states decide on abortion and people are absolutely thrilled with the way that`s going on.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But poll after poll shows a majority of Americans oppose the Supreme Court`s decision ending federal abortion protections and Democrats see an opening.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): Under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it would be fair game for women to be monitored and punished by the government.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s why Vice President Harris was in Florida Wednesday where access to most abortion services now ends after six weeks before women often know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And understand who is to blame, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But there are also other issues on voters` minds. A new CBS News poll shows the economy remains a major concern.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : On day one, we will throw out Bidenomics and we will reinstate a thing called MAGAnomics.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Especially for Michigan voters like Lori and Bruce Beyer.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE BEYER (Michigan voter): We`re retired and I`ll tell you what? Inflation is hurting. The dollar doesn`t buy as much as it used to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now President Biden will travel to North Carolina today, another key battleground where he`ll discuss the economy and infrastructure. But first, he`ll meet with the families of the four law enforcement officers in Charlotte, who were killed in the line of duty earlier this week -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Ed O`Keefe at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Southeast Texas, the flood risk is dangerously high this morning. Water levels in some areas are nearly as high as they were during Hurricane Harvey, which dropped more than 50 inches of rain in places, a new North American record.\par \par \uc2 Well this morning, flooding has swamped neighborhoods and blocked off a number of roadways. Two to five more inches of rain could fall before the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is in Houston.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Omar.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 We are getting ready for the rain here in the Houston area, but just north and east of us, they have had plenty of it. Rivers are already starting to spill over their banks from the heavy rains earlier this week, and parts of Southeast Texas got six to 10 inches.\par \par \uc2 All eyes though right now, we`re keeping an eye on the San Jacinto River. Homes near the river were surrounded by high water. The river crested just above 70 feet and a sensor about 30 miles north of Houston breached major flood stage Wednesday for the first time in about five years.\par \par \uc2 Now, some residents voluntarily evacuated before flooding swamped the roads and cut off a path to higher ground. In some of these low lying areas, a few residents and their pets needed to be rescued by emergency crews.\par \par \uc2 Now, the same storm system bringing us rain this morning actually spun up tornadoes yesterday in the Texas Panhandle. More thunderstorms are expected today and tomorrow. So of course, officials here in Harris County are concerned that the flooding could last a little bit longer.\par \par \uc2 Again, we`re keeping an eye on those rivers north and east of us. The Trinity River is expected to crest later today, actually tomorrow, highest water levels since 2017, that`s Hurricane Harvey time -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Omar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the Middle East and growing pressure on Israel`s government to reach a hostage deal. In Tel Aviv this morning, demonstrators blocked traffic demanding a deal with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , even as Israeli airstrikes continue in the Gaza city of Rafah.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio has a report which we should warn you contains upsetting images.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Israel`s government is keeping its options open for both war and for peace. Today, top ministers will discuss both a ceasefire and plans for that invasion of Rafah, but some families of hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  called on the prime minister to "choose life."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Shut down in the morning rush hour, Tel Aviv`s main highway. Families of hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  pleaded with Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  not to invade Rafah, claimed to be the last stronghold of the militant group in Gaza, where their loved ones might be and the last refuge for an estimated 1.4 million Gazans from the war.\par \par \uc2 But just after US Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  departed Israel last night, an adviser to Netanyahu confirmed to CBS News --\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: What the prime minister has doubled down on all week, Israel will attack Rafah.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: As political pressure mounts from his extreme far-right ministers calling for war threatening to collapse the government.\par \par \uc2 In Gaza, with the \uc2 UN\uc2  warning of imminent famine, pressure too from Washington has spurred Israel to build routes for more aid. The Pentagon updated its timeline on this temporary pier it is building off Gaza`s Coast.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH (Deputy Pentagon Press Secretary): We are over 50 percent complete.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: But still elusive, a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Israeli airstrikes shattering families and their futures.\par \par \uc2 In Rafah, two-year-old Mona Gerada (sp?) took her last breaths. Her brother, four-year-old, Kareem (sp?) lying still and shrouded.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "Their mother put them to bed," said their grandmother, "She`s in a coma. I don`t know what she`ll do when she wakes."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And no reply yet from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on Israel`s latest ceasefire proposal -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Ramy Inocencio, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, tough questions on Capitol Hill over a cyberattack affecting millions of you, but first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, legendary actor, Jeff Daniels stars in the new limited TV series, "A Man In Full." He`ll show us what drew him to the role and why he says the character will connect with viewers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fancy Like"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, country star, Walker Hayes is well-known for the viral video of his song "Fancy Like." He tells Jan Crawford about confronting personal struggles and how his family helped him get through.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch," a huge, huge deal to bring dozens of famous performers back to TikTok. Yes, this is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Fasenra. For more information, visit Facenra.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2  CEO says a cyberattack on one of its subsidiaries earlier this year could affect as many as a third of all Americans.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Witty\uc2  apologized during testimony before Congress yesterday. Nicole Sganga has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Bipartisan outrage in back-to-back hearings on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON WIDEN (D-OR): Your company on your watch let the country down.\par \par \uc2 REP. CATHY MCMORRIS RODGERS (R-WA): You have a responsibility to protect the data of the people who have put their trust in you or more bluntly in this case, you failed.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: After a cyberattack on IT provider \uc2 Change Healthcare\uc2  paralyzed insurance payments to hospitals, pharmacies and medical practices nationwide.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WITTY\uc2  (\uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2  CEO): We will not rest, I will not rest until we fix this.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: On Wednesday, a public apology delivered to lawmakers by the CEO of its parent company \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WITTY\uc2 : I`m deeply, deeply sorry.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: \uc2 Andrew Witty\uc2  revealed the company paid a hefty ransom to Russia-linked cybercriminals.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: How much?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WITTY\uc2 : $22 million.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: After he said hackers broke into \uc2 Change Healthcare\uc2 `s portal on February 12th using stolen credentials breaching a server not equipped with multi-factor authentication and compromising patient data.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): Ten weeks is way too long for millions of Americans to not know that their records may be available to criminals on the dark web.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: UnitedHealth says payment systems are mostly restored, and they`ve issued $6.5 billion in assistance loans.\par \par \uc2 But physicians like Dr. Christine Meyer of Exton, Pennsylvania, who first spoke with CBS MORNINGS in March, say they`re drowning under a backlog of claims.\par \par \uc2 How many months is it going to take for you to get back to normal?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTINE MEYER (Physician, CMMD & Associates founder): I honestly couldn`t tell you because I thought by now, if we were having this conversation, we would have been celebrating like we got through it.\par \par \uc2 It could be a year, it could be longer. I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Witty says it is still too soon to know just how many patients had their data stolen. Meanwhile, physicians like Dr. Meyer are pushing for loan forgiveness for medical practices still struggling to get by -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we look at the new and more aggressive tactics of some climate activists.\par \par \uc2 Your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, as we continue to cover the protests on campuses.\par \par \uc2 You are looking at live pictures from the campus of UCLA in California where police are clashing with protesters against the war on Gaza. Officers were clearing out an encampment that had been declared illegal. This has been going on for several hours already, and we will continue to monitor the situation there as it develops.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Indeed, we will.\par \par \uc2 College campuses aren`t the only places where protesters are making their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 This morning, in our "Climate Watch" series, we are focusing on climate activists who are taking direct action to make their point.\par \par \uc2 Last week, one group blockaded the entrance to the global headquarters of \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in Manhattan. They demanded the banking giant stop funding fossil fuel interests.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy takes a closer look at one climate group that says it doesn`t need to be liked to be effective.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Blocking traffic, throwing red powder on a case housing the US Constitution and dousing a glass-covered Van Gogh with soup. Climate protesters are not just marching in the streets, they are finding new and more aggressive ways to demand climate action.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING (Climate activist): We can still cause a little bit of a scene, cause some chaos.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: On a recent Wednesday night in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING: I know exactly the route that we can go.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Twenty-one-year-old Maxwell Downing shared his plan to cause a scene at this nearby hotel.\par \par \uc2 What exactly are you guys doing tonight?\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING: We`re going to a fancy schmancy gala that JD Vance, Republican senator from Ohio is going to be speaking at. JD Vance is one of the top 20 recipients of oil and gas money in Congress.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Downing cased the hotel the day before to find the best escape routes.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING: Who does not have $50.00 in cash?\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: So after making sure that everyone had money in case they got arrested --\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING: This is the client super villain.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: These climate protesters stormed the ballroom.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Interrupting the event until security finally threw them out.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING: Immediately, security guards, hands around the neck, which is not usual.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: They belong to a group called Climate Defiant.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We need to act.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Which says it won`t engage in vandalism or violence.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: But has quickly become notorious for surprise confrontations with oil executives --\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "\uc2 Exxon\uc2  lies and people dies")\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And politicians on both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Senator Vance did not respond when we asked for his reaction to the disruption.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: But a recent protest of Senator Joe Manchin, a Democrat almost came to blows.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You have sold our futures and you`ve gotten rich doing it. You sick (bleep).\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And a protester was shoved to the ground.\par \par \uc2 When you burst into a room and you call somebody like Senator Manchin, a sick F-word. What is the outcome you`re hoping to achieve?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GREENBERG\uc2  (Climate Defiance executive director): We don`t necessarily expect to move Manchin or whatnot, we are trying to make climate change a top issue in American politics. I mean, that reached six million people.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Michael Greenberg\uc2  is executive director of Climate Defiance.\par \par \uc2 MAN: End fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: He says their protests are designed to go viral on social media, attracting new members to their cause, and raising awareness of climate change as an existential issue.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are going to die. Who cares --\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t mind if you die.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GREENBERG\uc2 : We are trying to shake the public awake.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Do you worry about turning people off. They see you as more annoying or more of a threat than actually helping the cause you say you`re trying to help?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GREENBERG\uc2 : Yes, we`re definitely an acquired taste. Not everybody loves us. You don`t need to be popular to be effective.\par \par \uc2 DANA FISHER (\uc2 American University\uc2  professor and author): And their goal is media attention, plain and simple.\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t look at the general population.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Dana Fisher is a professor at \uc2 American University\uc2  and author of a new book about climate activism.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING: He is a criminal.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: She calls these kinds of activists, shockers, not unlike some of the AIDS activists of the 1980s who desperately tried to get people`s attention.\par \par \uc2 How do we know if this is actually effective?\par \par \uc2 DANA FISHER: I think it`s going to be a hindsight thing. I mean, well, I do not think that the whole movement should shift towards these kinds of actions because I think it`ll be a detriment to the movement itself, but it is playing a role in helping to keep the conversation going.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL DOWNING: There`s a lot of climate doom that goes around right now. Oh, you can`t do anything. Well, we`re doing something. We`re confronting directly the people who are selling our futures.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And they have had some success after targeting several Biden administration officials, Climate Defiance got a meeting with \uc2 John Podesta\uc2 , the White House`s chief climate adviser.\par \par \uc2 The group was also part of the pressure campaign that recently led President Biden to pause the expansion of liquefied natural gas exports -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is such a great piece, Ben. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot to digest from Ben`s reporting there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There really is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On one hand, you can understand that they want cameras there. They want these activities to go viral because it does cause people to pay attention. On the other, politicians, including the president of the United States have enacted or have at least.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: . put plans into place to address climate issues. So it`s not as if people don`t know that it`s something that needs to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Certainly, time really will tell in terms of what action is actually taken, what policies are actually passed as a result of bringing attention to something that I think everyone at this point recognizes is a problem.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We love shock value.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we`ll see if this is counterproductive or not in the future.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Thinking of shock value.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Ben, again, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 But let`s turn to another issue that`s related to the environment, trillions of cicada are emerging after more than a decade underground. Why this brings more attention to this issue than I think throwing paint onto a Van Gogh.\par \par \uc2 This is a once in a lifetime phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You recognize that awful noise. It is the sound of a massive insect invasion, trillions of cicadas are emerging across 12 states from the Midwest to the East Coast after more than a decade underground.\par \par \uc2 In Central Illinois, there`s a rare opportunity to see two types of cicadas together for the first time in more than 200 years.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff was in northeast Georgia when the first cicadas began surfacing there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: These are the coming attractions.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL VAUGHN (Tree expert): You can see right here, it`s going to split open.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: A synchronized symphony of cicadas emerging from the Midwest to the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 So this is the first place to emerge in the United States?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL VAUGHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: In Northeast Georgia, it is the moment tree expert, Campbell Vaughn has been waiting 13 years for.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL VAUGHN They are emerging from the exoskeleton, drying their wings, and then they`re going to fly off.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: This guy was living underground for 13 years. It`s not like they create generations underground.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL VAUGHN It`s a big party for 13 years.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Take a second and just listen to this.\par \par \uc2 In real life, it`s hard to tell exactly where the sound is coming from, that`s because it`s all happening in the treetops, 60 feet above us.\par \par \uc2 This is where the action happens, where they are separated from predators that will gobble them up. They`re looking to attract a mate.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The first time this has happened since Thomas Jefferson was president.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: This year, in a rare dual emergence, the 13-year brood is making its appearance at the same time as the one that surfaces every 17 years, a cycle that aligns every 221 years and hasn`t happened since 1803.\par \par \uc2 Some folks in Southern Illinois will get to see the once in a lifetime show.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re going to be looking at trillions of cicadas coming out this summer across Illinois.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Wait, wait. I think I got one.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: We went to the \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2  where they`re not only digging for cicadas.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s a boy.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: But also cataloging generations of them.\par \par \uc2 MAN: These specimens are from the great, great, great, great, great, great, great, great grandparents of the generation that`s coming out this year.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: For cicadas, their long wait can sometimes end quickly. Everything eats them, from birds to squirrels --\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL VAUGHN: Snakes and frogs --\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: And people.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL VAUGHN: And people. You might get lucky to do that.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Is this something that people are actually doing in the neighborhood?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ve never heard of this.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: With a little hot sauce, these are a very rare kind of wings.\par \par \uc2 You just ate it. What does it tastes like?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL VAUGHN: Not bad.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: But everyone gets full, eventually. The survivors will mate and lay eggs, and when the youngest cicadas hatch and fall into the soil, it is their turn to wait a lifetime for their moment.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Dave Malkoff in Lincoln County, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NAT