e campus of UCLA where all night police have been clashing with protesters against the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Officers are working to clear an encampment there, but many in the crowd have refused to comply with orders to leave.\par \par \uc2 This scene that you`re seeing playing out live right now across your screen has been playing out on campuses all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans is at UCLA for us this morning keeping track of what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning. What are you seeing now?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Police are starting to move their line into this encampment. Just a few minutes ago, they probably pushed forward about 15 feet or so and then they pushed us, the media out of the way here. They pushed us back.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the line of protesters there, you can see, they are all geared up. They`ve got helmets on. They`ve got goggles, some of them have got gas masks. They`re holding up boards, tables, chairs, skateboards, whatever they can to hold police officers back.\par \par \uc2 And at this point, police officers have been very measured in their approach into this encampment. They take a few feet in, and then they pause and make another move.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they`re moving us back a little bit further here. It seems like they`re getting ready to make a larger move because they`re trying to get us out of the way here. So we`re going to step back just a little bit. You`ve probably got a couple of hundred officers, at least at this skirmish line here and a couple hundred protesters right on the other side.\par \par \uc2 It is a true standoff, Vlad, now, and it doesn`t seem that either side is willing to back down.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Carter Evans for us live right now at UCLA.\par \par \uc2 Again, as we pointed out, these are the scenes that we`ve been seeing playing out across college campuses all across the country.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yep.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here in New York City, in California, all over and the question, of course, becomes for a lot of people is when you have that large of a police presence, and people who are unarmed for most part -- for the most part, you don`t want anything to go wrong as we`ve seen in the years past.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And you wonder how long they can keep that up in terms of that amount of force and presence.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: To make sure that people are safe and folks are removed from certain areas.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And of course, we will continue to monitor that here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, Mother`s Day is around the corner and Amazon`s Sarah Gilman will be here to show us how we can celebrate by sharing books about moms` favorite hobbies and passions.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Man in Full"/Netflix)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, Emmy-winning actor Jeff Daniels will be here to talk about his challenging new role in the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  limited series, "A Man in Full."\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "You Broke Up With Me"/Walker Hayes)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That is "You Broke Up With Me" by country music star, Walker Hayes.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the singer-songwriter talks candidly about facing addiction and loss and how leaning into his family helped his life and career take off.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fancy Like"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know that song. That is the hit "Fancy Like" by Walker Hayes. The song put the country music singer-songwriter on the map when it dropped three years ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, Hayes is out with a new collection of songs called Sober Thoughts.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford recently sat down with Hayes in Nashville for an emotional conversation about his new music, sobriety and the tragedy his family endured.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: in1\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Y`all Life"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Walker Hayes lives in gratitude.\par \par \uc2 From playful moments with his six kids to choreographed dances with his oldest daughter.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "AA"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Even his music is known for being lighthearted and fine. But listen closely and you can also hear a story of struggle, addiction, and loss.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES (Singer-songwriter): Life has kicked us around and we`ve also just -- we`ve made it through some really tough stuff.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Twenty years ago after playing just one gig at a bar in his hometown of Mobile, Alabama, Hayes and his high school sweetheart, Laney took a risk and moved to Nashville.\par \par \uc2 You were trying to write for others.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: I wanted to be a songwriter.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You did.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Yes, I wanted like Keith Urban to cut my song and me just collect the royalties and he could go out there and tour or whatever, but it just didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: After a decade, Hayes` music career was stalling. He was working the swing shift at \uc2 Costco\uc2  to support his growing family and he turned to alcohol.\par \par \uc2 How much were you drinking like what --\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: All day every day. I mean, it had come to an embarrassing point. I had a handle of vodka in my Honda Accord at all times, and it went in every beverage.\par \par \uc2 Puckett`s kind of saved my life. I mean Puckett`s was like a bright light.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: We got lunch with Hayes at Puckett`s location in Nashville. He used to play shows at Puckett`s enduring those tough times, it helped keep him going in more ways than one.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Puckett`s would feed us, and then they would send us with stacks of to-go boxes every Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So it was a way of you like kind of being in the game musically.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But also like, you mean it was feeding your family.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Literally.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Completely.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But the dark cloud that was Haze`s drinking continued to follow, and one day, he finally admitted he was an alcoholic.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: I woke up, and physically, I`ve never felt like this before.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Like it was the worst -- worse than a hangover ever.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: It wasn`t even a hangover. It just -- it felt like, if I start again today and do this, I might die.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: He got sober and things started coming together. But as his star was rising, in 2018, he and his wife would endure the unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 How did you guys get through it?\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: By the grace of God and God alone.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Laney almost died while delivering their seventh child, Oakleigh. She was still born at full term.\par \par \uc2 You had your nursery ready, that baby bed. You know you had her little clothes.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Like it was all --\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Yes, you know, meanwhile, Laney`s morning, she delivered a kid and came home with nothing.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: After Oakleigh`s funeral and three years sober, Hayes drove to a bar.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Got down there and my wallet wasn`t in my door, so I saw drive back home. First thing I see when I walk back in my house is Laney in the dark living room just sitting in the dark, staring into the wall by herself and, you know that, my grief and anger immediately turned to shame.\par \par \uc2 My favorite person on Earth is all by ourself.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Laney helped him find an ASMAN: meeting and Hayes has stayed sober. He says his priorities quickly shifted.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: I was self-absorbed and I wanted power, I wanted success and so the lens changed for when we lost Oakleigh and I began to cling to the things that matter and one thing, you know, that really matters to me is my kids.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And in clinging to his family, Hayes` career finally took off.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @WalkerHayesOfficial/TikTok)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It was in 2021 when his daughter, Lela created this dance to a song "Fancy Like" and they posted it to TikTok.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: And I was like, oh, we got 6,000, you know, 6,000 people like it. And my daughter goes, "Dad, that`s 600,000."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fancy Like"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The song became a sensation, hitting number one on the Country Music Charts. Now, Hayes is out with new music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Same Drunk"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A collection of vulnerable songs called Sober Thoughts.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like this is a happy song and you listen and you go, there`s a lot going on in there.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: Yes. Right. So, I see my music is just another way for me to have a conversation with people out there and just be like, hey, let`s open the floor.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beat the Bottle"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: For Hayes , Sober Thoughts is his way of tackling heavy topics, but in his usual lighthearted way, and is something he is able to do, because of what he`s learned.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES: I don`t know what the future holds and the fact that life, it ends, and if we all really got to see that from the front row, it would change our daily lives. We would see clearly what matters.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Face in the Crowd"/Walker Hayes/Monument Records)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jan Crawford in Nashville, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A lot of love there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s powerful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m always fascinated by understanding the inner lives of people who put out music that feels really good, and oftentimes, like "Fancy Like" is a great song that wouldn`t give you any insight into the darkness that has shrouded his life for so many years.\par \par \uc2 And so when you learn a little bit more about him through these interviews, like Jan does, and then he puts out this new music about Sober Thoughts.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s credibly insightful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I love it because he talks about seeing things clearly now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And sobriety for him is obviously dealing with what he called alcoholism, but we can all be sober from something. We`re all struggling .\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, with something.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . with intoxication of something.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And when you let go of some of these things, your life starts to take off and then doors start to open.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In a positive way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In a positive way.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I like that just a number of people, a lot of people struggle with alcoholism. And for him to say that publicly and you see this beautiful family he has and the support that he has, I think is truly inspiring.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. That`s beautiful. That was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was wonderful, Jan. Wonderful.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, up next, if you need help with a Mother`s Day gift, we`ll share some book ideas. Sarah Gelman from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  books will show us picks for every kind of mom, even mine.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re a mom.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Middle"/Warner Bros Television)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s Frankie Heck pretending to love her Mother`s Day gift on the TV show "The Middle."\par \par \uc2 If you`re still struggling to find a gift for the mom in your life, listen up. Amazon Books editorial director, Sarah Gelman is here with her book recommendations for all kinds of moms.\par \par \uc2 You have a lovely spread of them, but I want to start with the gardening one.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN (Amazon Books editorial director): Yes. So a lot of moms love to garden.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: But honestly, your mom does not have to be crafty or even have a green thumb to be able to do this book.\par \par \uc2 This is "The Container Garden Recipe Book" by Lana Williams. And the thing about this book, you know the step by step charcuterie board, make this \uc2 Instagram\uc2  worthy charcuterie board.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: This is like that, but for plants.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And you can make anything, you can make arrangements out of things like a hollow log, a blue plastic milk crate is in the unconventional container chapter. And it doesn`t matter if your mom lives in an apartment with a little terrace or if she lives in the country and has a huge yard. Container gardening is really for everyone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have a book for a mom who loves to cook.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. I`m a mom who loves to cook and I love this cookbook right now. It`s called "Cooking in Real Life," Lidey Heuck. So Lidey Heuck was Ina Garten`s apprentice, starting right out of college, and Ina Garten is the Barefoot Contessa.\par \par \uc2 So if you grew up on Ina Garten`s roast chicken and roast carrots, your mouth is watering now, you need to get this book for your mom but also for yourself. It has these modern takes on kind of retro recipes. Everything from Old Bay crab dip with a sweet corn. This is actually the book that I`m making my Mother`s Day dinner out for my mom.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the recipes are fairly easy or are they kind of complicated?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: No, they`re very easy. I`m making a slow roasted salmon with lemony leeks and asparagus. And a Romaine and arugula salad with shaved Parmesan, pistachio and radish.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She like -- you also have that recipe up in your head.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s easy?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I`ve made it. I`ve made it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So, it`s like the ingredients are there. And it is so -- it`s so impressive and I`m not that good of a cook.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It sounds pretty good.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It does sound pretty good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It sounds pretty good.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: You`ll love this book.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What about for music lovers?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Okay, so for a second, do not look at the cover of this book. I`m going to tell you this is a novel about a teenage girl who becomes a number`s runner for the mob in Harlem, gets sent to a reform school where they are infamous for their treatment of their Black students, and she`s also homeless and orphaned.\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m going to tell you, the girl is Ella Fitzgerald. And yes, my jaw dropped. I was so surprised by this book. This is my current Sara Selects Book Club pick. I could not believe the life that Ella Fitzgerald had when she was growing up.\par \par \uc2 And honestly, if it weren`t true, I wouldn`t believe that so much had happened to one person, but it`s ultimately a story of triumph. Great for music lovers, great for moms that love historical fiction, too.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What about literary fiction? You said you have a book that`s a must read.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. I love -- this is "Real Americans," Rachel Khong, you should definitely read this book.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It`s about -- it is multigenerational about a family, and this is really -- if your mom just wants to sit down and read a book, this is three generations. It`s three coming of age stories, spans from China to the United States and it is just all about the decisions that we make for our families and about how far a mother will go for the love for her child.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know I`m going to take that recommendation because every book recommendation -- and you know what I like, so --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, you`re going to really like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Okay, I`m down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so what about for moms who are hard to buy for?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Okay, so moms that are hard to buy for, "The Ministry of Time" by Kaliane Bradley. This is one of the hardest books to describe, and so I think there`s a little something for everyone.\par \par \uc2 So this is a novel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It is a spy thriller, government conspiracy, time travel, love story. It is all of those things at one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Time travel love story.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. It is everything. It is sci fi. It`s love.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It sounds like "Outlander."\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It`s history. It`s really a remarkable book. And I think you could hand this to anyone, male or female, no matter their taste, and they will enjoy this book.\par \par \uc2 It`s set in the near future of London, where they discover a device that brings people back from the past into current day. They call them expats and they`re paired with these people called bridges that are responsible for bringing them into the modern world.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So I feel like what`s so great about books you can take them with you everywhere.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So even if you book a vacation or something --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Or, you know you can still enjoy this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So as moms, what should we complement the book with as we get our gifts?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I love to pair the book with something else, like pair a cookbook with a pretty tea towel or the gardening book with some gardening gloves.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Spa day and book?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, I mean, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ll take that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is that overrated?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I wouldn`t turn down a spa day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ll take a spa day.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: If my small children are watching, I want to book and a spa day.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Maybe a nice bottle of wine, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sarah Gelman, always great to have you. Love those book recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our next guest is the great Jeff Daniels. We will talk -- there he is in our greenroom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are going to talk to the award-winning actor about his new series, "A Man in Full."\par \par \uc2 Local news next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that make you go --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am talking of course about "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nate, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to jump right in because this is going to make you go, "hmm." So my "Talk of the Table" is \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  launching its Icons Program.\par \par \uc2 So here`s what it is. The company is now offering customers a stay in iconic properties real or make believe. One of them, like this Westchester County, New York home that`s been transformed into the Xavier`s Institute for Higher Learning. You recognize that, right, featured in the Marvel animation series "X-Men 97."\par \par \uc2 I want you to take a look at this one. How about waking up and Carl`s house from "Up"? You remember that movie?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so every room --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Balloons --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This New Mexico transformed home can mimic the movie. The whole house can be lifted up by a crane.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not balloons?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, that is absolutely wild. And you can even sleep on a bed that is crafted the same leather as Ferrari car seats in the new bedroom installed at the Ferrari Museum in Maranello, Italy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So my question is, iconic homes that you remember from TV and film, are there any that come to mind that you wouldn`t want to stay in? Like the Fresh Prince of Bel-Air.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I was going to say maybe the Fresh Prince.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fresh Prince.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But I was thinking, honestly, I don`t know why "Green Acres" came to me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing wrong with that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Green Acres. Green Acres is the place to be.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s where you`re going to be at, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  did the Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, but it just got me thinking, what about the house from the "Christmas Story." I need the lamp in here though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The house from the "Christmas Story." The Corleone house in Queens. Was it -- was it in Queens or --?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The McAllister house?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: McAllister --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Beetlejuice house.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I gave this a lot of thought. So I want you to hit us up on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You really did.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or X and let us know what iconic home you would like to stay in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good one.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s a good one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a good one. All right, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So this one will also be a question that I have for you guys. My "Talk of the Table" is how Americans feel about a new law President Biden signed yesterday that could ban TikTok if its Chinese owner doesn`t sell its stake within the next year.\par \par \uc2 So a new poll of 2,000 people shows 42 percent of them support the possible ban, only 23 percent are against it. Another 31 percent or so say, they have no opinion and they basically don`t care.\par \par \uc2 So how do you guys feel about this? I know we`ve talked about the influence this has, the fact that the rules over in China are different in terms of how their children are exposed to TikTok.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And this company is valuated at I think $225 billion. So there`s a lot at stake.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And there are a lot of people who obviously have made money off of TikTok.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I will just say that I sent a TikTok to a friend of mine who used to work in past presidential administrations, and I said, hey, check out this TikTok. And she wrote me back, I don`t do TikTok.\par \par \uc2 And I said, no, but just click on this. You don`t have to have an account. She goes, I`m not even clicking on it. I`ve been in too many national security briefings.\par \par \uc2 So I was like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Does that make you think that there knows something that you don`t know?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, I think she`s just like -- she sort of -- I said what I said.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you`re like, uh-oh, okay. Well, she knows something and --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, the concern is that ByteDance is in cahoots with the Communist Party in China.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And the more information that they have, the more influence, you know, those who control the media control the mind.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And clearly, it is having an influence on our children here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me talk about in influence of another kind.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My "Talk of the Table" is the Global Citizen Now Action Summit, which is taking place right now in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, I had the honor to hand out this year`s Global Citizen Prize and Cisco Youth Leadership Award to six young trailblazing changemakers being recognized for their important contributions to our world.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Global Citizen Now)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is a celebration, a celebration of six exceptional young people from around the world who are taking action today to make a better tomorrow for all of us.\par \par \uc2 They aren`t waiting for solutions to the world`s most pressing issues. Folks, they are the solution.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is -- I wanted to talk about this because we had Ben Tracy doing that story on those climate activists and one of the men -- young men that I profiled was Olivier Ndoole who has basically lost his family in his -- there he is -- he is trying to protect the national forests, Rwanda national forests in the Democratic Republic of Congo. And he is fighting to ensure that that forest stays alive.\par \par \uc2 It is home to millions of indigenous creatures and people and he is doing something about it to the point where he can`t go home to see his family because the government will put him in jail.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is somebody who is actually making a change to protect the environment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His life on the line.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Putting his life on the line and doing it. He`s a lawyer. He`s a human rights lawyer. He is trying to protect the environment while also protecting the people that live there. And he doesn`t have to do it by throwing paint at the Mona Lisa, so I think it is --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, it is nice to highlight that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Global Citizen -- Gayle is always involved. It is a really real thing going on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done doing the emceeing.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, you looked good up there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did I really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I want to see you emcee more.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nerve-racking. Okay, all right, you hear that, folks?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Perfect fit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little more emceeing.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is Emmy-award winning actor, Jeff Daniels who says hie is playing one of his most challenging roles yet. He stars in the new \uc2 Netflix\uc2  limited series, "A Man in Full."\par \par \uc2 It is created by Hollywood heavyweights, David E. Kelley and Regina King, who also serves as a director. Daniels plays tough talking real estate mogul, Charlie Croker, who faces bankruptcy and must defend his empire and his family against enemies attempting to exploit his fall from grace.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Man in Full"/Netflix)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yowza. Jeff Daniels is here. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS ("A Man in Full" actor): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Jeff. Charlie Croker is described as crude, rude, and irrepressible.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: \uc2 Sure\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What --\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Sign me up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what I would ask you. That drew you to this character.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Yes. They are the fun ones, the bad guys or guys that as you describe are so much fun because you get to kind of -- you know, we all have that ability to be kind of to go to the dark side or be less than we should be. And you basically as an actor, you get to, you know, put gas on that and then light it (exploding sound), out comes Charlie Croker.\par \par \uc2 It`s fun. It`s a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You said it`s also the most complicated role. And I`ve seen you -- we were talking backstage, loved you in "Purple Rose," loved you in "Gettysburg." But you say this is the most complicated role you`ve ever played?\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Well, the hard part of a Charlie Croker is that you also have to be funny.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: That`s a smaller target.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Man in Full"/Netflix)\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: And it`s true. Comedy is harder to do than drama. And you still have to be believable as well and then Charlie is so big.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, in every aspect.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: He is larger than life.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: And in star school, you`re taught that in close-ups, less is more.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In star school.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: And I`ve got to be, you know, I`ll tell you what, Nate, I`m going to come at -- I mean, you`ve got to be -- we would finish takes, and I would ask the camera operator, did I break the lens?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You also said you have to learn how to think like this character. How did you do that? What inspiration did you pull from? Who were you watching?\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Well, it`s relevant to today.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: But no, I didn`t worry about Trump or anything like that. Tom Wolfe wrote the book in the late 90s.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: And it was based on two or three guys in Atlanta, and so these guys are around. This is kind of like --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The last of --\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: The dinosaur, the -- when men were men and everyone else was inferior. And so this is kind of like, poking a hole in that guy and watching as a blimp, watching it just freefall in six episodes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Last time we were together, I was asking you about the future and what are you looking for in the next projects? And you said, at this point in my career, I`m taking risks.\par \par \uc2 Was this a risk?\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And why?\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Yes. I did a Zoom meeting with David E. Kelley, who wrote everything and Regina King who directed. Tommy Schlamme also directed and I had read the first episode, and I didn`t know how to do it and so I said, yes. And it`s that, and again, it`s so large.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: That you might be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You might swing and miss.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Big time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you knocked it out of the park.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: But yes, but I remember calling my agent about a month in going, well, this is either really good or you`re going to go what the heck were you thinking?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You nailed it.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You nailed it, brother.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So one of the things that`s really interesting, shows like "White Lotus" and "Succession" are incredibly popular. They feature rich and powerful and sometimes incredibly nasty individuals at the center of those stories, and yet we`re fascinated by them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we`re fascinated by real life, people who are rude and nasty and incredibly powerful. Why?\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: I think part of it is because we want to see them fall. I think it`s Shakespearean.\par \par \uc2 You know, celebrities, the famous people, you know, that once they build you up, they want to take you down. And so especially if you`ve made the climb up on the foreheads or the backs of other people, you know, pushing people down and all of that, and now you`re "Look at me. Look at me. Look at me." You want to see that guy go down and you want to watch him go down as slowly as possible as he spins out.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s part of it. You know?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, there is some --\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: We`re pretending to be a hero, but we all know you`re not and you`re waiting for it to happen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: And you know, for Charlie it does.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Some great actors in here. You`ve got Diane Lane. You saw -- you just saw the picture there of you and Regina King who is the director, Oscar-winning actress. What was it like working with her?\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Well, that was the reason I signed on. I mean, Regina has an Oscar. She actually has one. And so I knew that behind the camera, behind when the director sits that I`d be covered.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: So I could come in big and I kept waiting for this Oscar- winning actor as a director to come around the corner after two takes and go, "Really good. Let`s just turn it down."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Put it away.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But now with Charlie though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re breaking the lens.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . it`s turned it up.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: No, I kept waiting for it. Just to be safe, let`s get one word --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: We`re not so -- and it never happened. Regina --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: Keep going. Keep going.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She let you. Wow. That`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DANIELS: And so that was permission to fly.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jeff Daniels, it`s an incredible role. Thank you for being here. We love having you on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a must-see.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It really is. We`re all hooked on it.\par \par \uc2 "A Man in Full" can be streamed on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  starting today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, up 