hiers, Jericka Duncan, Carter Evans,  Lilia Luciano, Ramy Inocencio, Elise Preston, Stephanie Abrams, Manuel  Bojorquez, Ed O`Keefe, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Celine Gounder, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 13,741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle and Tony are off, but we are in great hands right now. Here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Gaza protests erupt with fights breaking out overnight at UCLA while police crackdown at Columbia. We will talk to New York`s mayor.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D) (New York City): External actors are actively creating serious public safety issues at these protests.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Another round of deadly tornadoes rips through the Midwest and more severe storms can be on the way. What you need to know.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: Once again, another tornado threat.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Florida`s new restrictive abortion law takes effect today. We spoke to a Florida doctor who is bracing for the fallout.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN): There`s fear. There`s uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A major pivot by the \uc2 Drug Enforcement Administration\uc2  on cannabis.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are going to reclassify marijuana as a less dangerous drug.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The D-backs-Dodgers game started late because of this swarm of bees.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #1: A beekeeper removed the swarm with a huge old agent.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #2: They later had him throw out the first pitch.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The criminal trial of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  resumed.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Judge Juan Manuel Merchan saying that he would stop trial so Trump could attend his son, Barron`s high school graduation.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL\uc2  ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): It`s funny because now Trump has to go to Barron`s high school graduation.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Bear in mind in this next story, everyone is fine: A Utah couple reunited with their pet cat after they accidentally shipped her with an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  return. Trapped in the box for six days without food or water, so she wasn`t just shipped to an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse, she is also qualified to work there.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by progressive making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Go --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ouch. Ouch.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hilarious as usual.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, but let`s begin with something we`ve been covering all week. It`s breaking news from both coasts on the campus protests against the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 At UCLA in Los Angeles, a pro-Palestinian encampment came under attack overnight from counter protesters using fireworks leading to hours of fighting between the two groups.\par \par \uc2 And here in New York, we`re getting an exclusive first look at the police clearing out protests in a university building in Columbia.\par \par \uc2 Check out this video, it was released to us by the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  this morning. It shows officers breaking up tent encampments and clearing out the occupied hall at the university. Dozens were arrested.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano and Carter Evans are covering the story on both coasts. We`ll start with Carter Evans in LA.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 It`s been some tense moments here at UCLA especially over the last couple of hours and behind me right now, you see \uc2 California Highway Patrol\uc2  in riot gear kind of moving the last of the counter protesters out of the quad area here.\par \par \uc2 Let me try to explain what has been going on because we`ve got pro- Palestinian protesters, and that`s the encampment that they`ve been here for about a week or so. You can see it`s fortified, they`ve put up a barricade and they pretty much owned this part of the quad for the last week.\par \par \uc2 And then these protesters -- counter protesters showed up tonight and started agitating the crowd here. There were some fights that ensued, clearly some violence and there were some injuries. Some incendiary devices that looked like someone threw some fireworks into the encampment here so it was certainly a night of violence for some time here and police were standing by for most of the time watching commonly as the crowd worked out its disagreement amongst each other and calmed down for the most part and then police kind of moved in and moved out the counter protesters.\par \par \uc2 Now the question is, this has been declared an unlawful assembly here at UCLA right now, this encampment. Police have cleared out the counter protesters. They were clearly on the pro-Israel side, but they have not cleared out the Palestinian encampment yet and we do have a lot of police here right now.\par \par \uc2 You saw that group standing right there. There`s probably 10 or 15 of them there, but there`s another 50 right down the street another 20 or 30 right down over there. So there are a lot of police here and we are waiting to see if they move in on this encampment.\par \par \uc2 So clearly, a night of escalation not just here on the west coast but also in New York where my colleague Lilia Luciano is at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  -- Lilia.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: That`s right, Carter.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, before police showed up here at Columbia, students received a shelter-in-place warning. Hours later, we saw dozens upon dozens of protesters in zip ties taken into city and police buses as their peers, protesters, and even faculty members cheered them on from the outside.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Shame on you.")\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Dozens of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers in riot gear entered \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  around 9:00 PM, some through locked gates, others seen here coming through a second floor window of the building occupied by demonstrators Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 The officers entered Hamilton Hall at the request of the university. While inside, police cleared barricades, conducted multiple arrests, eventually clearing the building. Police also began clearing the tent encampment that had been the symbol of the protests on campus for nearly two weeks.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Then \uc2 NYPD\uc2  moved further north in Harlem towards the city college campus. We were there when dozens of officers breached a gate to clear the encampment and began arresting protesters.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting "shame")\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Dozens more students were loaded onto city buses and detained by the end of the night.\par \par \uc2 Many faculty members were outside like this professor we spoke to who was visibly upset over seeing so many students handcuffed.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I am just devastated that this has happened to every single campus in this country. We`re letting the military in, we are letting the police in. These are just students.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: At a press conference before police arrived at the college campuses, Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  blamed outside agitators for the chaos and urged student protesters to call it quits.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: We cannot and will not allow what should be peaceful -- a peaceful gathering to turn into a violent spectacle that serves no purpose.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: \uc2 Wesleyan University\uc2  President Michael Ross says he has not cleared the encampments at his campus, but he thinks students` efforts should be on legislators instead.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ROSS (\uc2 Wesleyan University\uc2  president): It`s the White House that can make a difference on the ground in Gaza and in Israel, and so I think getting our political energies pointed in the right direction would be -- could be very powerful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Police will now stay on campus here at Columbia until at least May 17th. Yesterday, the university sent out statement that any of the students who were inside Hamilton Hall could face expulsion, but yesterday at \uc2 CUNY\uc2 , I heard from a student who was saying, look we`re all here in solidarity with the suffering in Gaza and any consequence a student face is just paling in comparison -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In our next half hour, we will also be talking with Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , he is the mayor of New York City, about the police`s handling of the protests.\par \par \uc2 In the Middle East, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  has been speaking with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . The US is pushing for more humanitarian aid and a deal to release hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio spoke to the wife of American hostage Keith Siegel, who was a former hostage herself. He joins us now from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Vlad, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  has landed here in Israel for the seventh time since October 7th when \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants invaded Israel.\par \par \uc2 His top line, Washington is "determined to get a ceasefire" that brings the hostages home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thank you, Biden.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thank you, Blinken.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Desperate for more American support, Israelis rallied outside Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  `s hotel in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: After stops in Jordan and Saudi Arabia, more shuttle diplomacy with Israeli President \uc2 Isaac Herzog\uc2  on a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): No delays, no excuses. The time is now.\par \par \uc2 AVIVA SIEGEL (Keith Siegel`s wife): I feel like I am broken up into pieces.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: For Aviva Siegel, her American husband, Keith, is still being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (KEITH SIEGEL speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: It`s Day 207: This proof of life video released just days ago.\par \par \uc2 AVIVA SIEGEL: I know that Keith has had enough. My family has had enough. My country has had enough.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Aviva herself was a hostage released after 51 days. She, her daughter, and the families of other American hostages had a face-to- face with Blinken.\par \par \uc2 Take us inside the room. What was the feeling?\par \par \uc2 ELAN SIEGEL (Keith Siegel`s daughter): The feeling was really grateful. I think we all feel and not only the American citizens, I think Israel feel really grateful for what the United States has been doing since October 7th.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: In Jerusalem., Blinken pushed Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to increase desperately needed aid to Gaza. Israel says it has taken steps to allow more aid in by land and by sea. The \uc2 UN\uc2  has not stopped warning of impending famine, and Netanyahu has warned the military`s invasion of Russia will happen soon.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And CBS News has learned the White House is now considering welcoming certain Palestinians to the United States from Gaza as refugees from the war.\par \par \uc2 The focus is on immediate family members of American citizens or permanent residents, but only, only if they pass eligibility, medical, and security screenings -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Ramy Inocencio in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, the tornado threat in the Central US just won`t stop. A twister killed one person in Westmoreland, Kansas yesterday as it tore through the tiny city northwest of Topeka, the state capital.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston is in Westmoreland with a look at the damage left behind.\par \par \uc2 Elise, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Jericka, it`s just devastating. The tornado destroyed nearly two dozen homes in Westmoreland like this one. Powerful winds forced two trees to crash down on this house smashing it in nearly half.\par \par \uc2 What`s left of the roof is just mangled and twisted about and the family`s belongings no longer inside their house. They`re scattered just everywhere alongside this driveway and side yard with these two totaled cars.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a video of the tornado striking down Tuesday afternoon. It stripped this building down to its metal frame and reduced others to rubble. The tornado also twisted power lines leaving the entire town in the dark. Utility crews have been working all around us for the last several hours.\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma, a twister touched down in Cordell damaging several buildings. It also hit a gas station and the public school. No major injuries were reported.\par \par \uc2 Now the new damage comes just days after more than 30 tornadoes touched down over the weekend across the state.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, President Biden approved a federal disaster declaration providing funding for rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 Here in Westmoreland, the small town of just about 750 people is being forced to stay inside while crews work to clean up the dangerous aftermath of this tornado.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, more storms are on the way hitting several states today from Nebraska to Kansas -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elise, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Severe storms have also caused dangerous flooding in Southeast Texas. Heavy rain on Sunday and Monday left some rivers at the highest levels they`ve seen in nearly five years.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning. It`s not just Texas, the rain has been on repeat through the center of the country since last week. We`ve seen five to eight inches in just the last seven days, great for the drought, but has forced for several rivers out of their banks.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the Trinity River in Texas will stay in major flood stage for the next few days and through Friday. Another couple of inches will fall on these waterways.\par \par \uc2 But deep convection could lead to rainfall rates over two inches an hour causing more flooding in the Lone Star State. Warm, moist, buoyant air will rise up; cool, condense and form storms that could produce hail the size of a large apple and we could also see damaging winds and a few tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 The storm and the flood threat is going to shift east tomorrow evening and it is going to linger into the overnight hours. For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Nate, flooding causes the second most weather related deaths in the US.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are learning more about the circumstances of the ambush that killed four law enforcement officers in Charlotte this week.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez spoke to an eyewitness with a dramatic account of what happened and to a friend of one of the victims.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: This bullet riddled home is what remains.\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: After a dramatic gun battle between a Fugitive Task Force and a convicted felon wanted for firearms possession.\par \par \uc2 From what you saw, the suspect was shooting from --\par \par \uc2 SAING CHHOEUN (Witness): Yes, from that window.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: From upstairs.\par \par \uc2 SAING CHHOEUN: Yes, from upstairs --\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Saing Chhoeun was at home next door when the firefight broke out.\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: He recorded video of officers taking cover behind this vehicle.\par \par \uc2 This is where the officers perished.\par \par \uc2 SAING CHHOEUN: Yes. Two over here and then two on the other side.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The slain officers include Deputy US Marshal Thomas Weeks, William "Alden" Elliott, and Sam Poloche, both 14-year veterans of the State Department of Corrections, and Joshua Eyer, a member of the Charlotte-Mecklenburg Police Department -- all four men had children.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY JENNINGS (Charlotte-Mecklenburg North Carolina Police Department chief): It`s been very tough, and knowing that you have families that are hurting right now --\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Police Chief Johnny Jennings was emotional, Tuesday, talking about the fallen officers.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JOHNNY JENNINGS: Officer Eyer was just a few weeks ago, I`m shaking his hand, congratulating him for being officer to month.\par \par \uc2 Jennings says Eyer was shot while trying to assist a task force member. Eyer was once Deamonte White`s school resource officer and inspired him to pursue a career in law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 DEAMONTE WHITE (Victim`s friend): I will remember that the impact that he made in our school and community as he did every day putting on that badge and come out here and protect and serve.\par \par \uc2 And I remember that there`s really not that many guys like Joshua.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And behind me is Officer Eyer`s police vehicle draped in the US flag and covered with flowers serving as a memorial. The suspect in this case was also killed -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A heartbreaking tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Manuel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A historic shift is coming and the federal government`s drug policy. Many states have legalized marijuana, but it is still illegal at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Now, sources tell CBS News, the Justice Department is ready to reclassify marijuana as a less dangerous drug.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the White House. Ed, what else can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Nate, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 You know, since 1970, marijuana has been labeled what`s called a Schedule I drug, one of the most dangerous alongside ecstasy, heroin, and LSD.\par \par \uc2 Well, now the \uc2 Drug Enforcement Administration\uc2  is recommending marijuana be classified down to a Schedule III drug alongside meds like Tylenol with codeine or anabolic steroids. It would acknowledge the medical uses of cannabis, but it would not legalize marijuana for recreational use.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-four states and Washington, DC have legalized marijuana; 14 others permit its use for medical reasons. There`s been no official comment from the White House, but CBS News has learned the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is signing off on this Justice Department proposal, which would open the door to cannabis companies being able to write off expenses and access bank loans.\par \par \uc2 News of this big potential change comes just six months before the next presidential election. Last year, a CBS News poll found nearly two in three Americans think recreational use should be legal, which shows this had broad public support in addition to bipartisan backing.\par \par \uc2 This is considered just a step in the process and a final ruling by regulators is still likely months, if not a year away -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Ed, we`ll stay tuned. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, reaction from Florida and elsewhere to the state`s new abortion law. Starting today, it will be severely limited after six weeks of pregnancy, but first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, first on CBS MORNINGS, Dame Judi Dench tells Anthony Mason what inspired her new book and talks about the role that Shakespeare has played in her life.\par \par \uc2 Plus, author, Emily Oster will tell us about her new book with advice for women having complications from pregnancy and childbirth.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch," another California zoo is getting giant pandas and we`ll show them to you. Look at how cute they are. So cute.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new abortion law goes into effect today in Florida, sharply restricting the procedure after six weeks before many women know that they are pregnant. That means almost every state in the South has severe limits on abortion; in most, it is nearly outlawed.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Jacksonville.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Well, Florida had seen an influx of patients from out of state as it was one of the last remaining places in this region with fewer restrictions. Well, that all changes today and with this new law, providers are bracing for the impact far beyond the procedure.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN: There`s fear. There`s uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Jacksonville doctor, Shelly Tien stood ready to see patients until the stroke of midnight when Florida`s six-week abortion ban was set to take effect.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN: It is in essence a total and a complete abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: As a physician, what is it like for you operating under the circumstances?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN: \uc2 Sure\uc2 , you know, I think, you know, certainly it`s stressful. We see physicians that are scared to take care of basic issues and we`ve seen patients turned away from emergency rooms, delivering in public floor bathrooms. I mean really, really awful, awful scenarios.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Florida`s new law includes exceptions for rape, incest, fetal abnormalities, and life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 DEAN BLACK (R) (Lawmaker): It`s a victory.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: State Republican law or Dean Black voted for the six- week ban.\par \par \uc2 DEAN BLACK: We think we have a good law, a compassionate law, a moral law. It can serve as a guide for other states.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a Florida resident has criticized the new law, but in a "Time" Magazine interview published Tuesday, he reiterated his stance that the states are going to make those decisions.\par \par \uc2 When asked whether states should monitor women`s pregnancies so they can know if they`ve gotten an abortion, Trump replied: "I think they might do that. Again, you`ll have to speak to the individual states," later adding, "It`s irrelevant whether I`m comfortable or not."\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Did you hear what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just said to "Time" Magazine? It`s shocking.\par \par \uc2 After bragging about overturning Roe, then saying women should be punished for accessing reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And Florida voters will have a chance to weigh in this fall with a ballot measure that would enshrine abortion rights into the state`s constitution and that has attracted the attention of the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will be here in Jacksonville later today as part of her nationwide Reproductive Freedom Tour -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Caitlin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we will talk with New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  about the crackdown on campus protests.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Last night, a pro-Palestinian group was cleared out of an academic building on Columbia`s campus in a massive police operation. It is the culmination of two weeks of protests against the war on Gaza and near the Ivy League campus.`\par \par \uc2 Critics accused protesters of antisemitism who responded by saying it`s not antisemitic to condemn Israel`s conduct of the war.\par \par \uc2 For more on where things stand, we are joined now by Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  of New York City.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mayor, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Good morning. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is the second time in two weeks that Columbia has called upon the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  to help clear out protesters on campus. Is sending the police and riot gear the best way to de-escalate what is already a tense situation?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Well, there were two operations tonight. One was with \uc2 CUNY\uc2  and one was with \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , and once I became aware of the outside agitators who were part of this operation, as Colombia mentioned in their letter and their request with the \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2 , it was clear we had to take appropriate actions.\par \par \uc2 When our intelligence division identified those who are professionals, well-trained, one of them was married to someone that was arrested for terrorism, we knew these children were being exploited and they were in danger and it would have been irresponsible not to reply to requests from \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mr. Mayor, when you say that the protests have been co- opted by professional outside agitators, how do you distinguish between those outside agitators and students who are just protesting peacefully and who is this individual who is connected to a terrorist?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Well, it is not -- it is not singular, it is plural. Several organizations and groups, I want to be clear on that.\par \par \uc2 We did not take this action lightly, and once the \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  team and leadership in their letter acknowledging that outside agitators were on their campus grounds, we looked at our intel and information. I was briefed by the Intelligence Division and they were able to identify organizations and individuals who were not students, but were professional agitators.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve realized after the break-in into Hamilton Hall, some of the tactics, some of the methods, these are clearly being used across the globe, and we understood how really dangerous this situation had become.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, but Mr. Mayor, who -- give us some information on who this individual or individuals who are connected to terrorism found themselves in police custody, which I`m guessing they are now in police custody.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Well, if you look at -- you can find this information, they`re proudly acknowledging themselves on social media platforms and I`m going to let the Intelligence Division do their job on what information should be released and what information should not be released. And when we do an analysis of all those who are arrested, a substantial number of them were not students of \uc2 CUNY\uc2  and were not students of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mayor Adams for those who are protesting peacefully, how do you respect and allow the right to free speech while protecting those involved and enforcing the law?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: And I think that`s so important to point out because that was a protester a child. I protested against the South African apartheid movement, I protested when there were unfortunate interactions when young people were shot by police officers, so I know how to protest and I understand the power of protests.\par \par \uc2 But we must make sure that that protest never turned into violence. When you saw the break-in into Hamilton Hall, that was clearly not protesting -- your right to protest -- that was committing a crime.\par \par \uc2 And what we did as independent outside news journalists reported, we used a level of professionalism, and we made sure that a minimum amount of force was used to eradicate the problem that was taking place in \uc2 CUNY\uc2  and on Columbia`s grounds.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mr. Adams, have you spoken to any of these students from both sides of this conversation? I`m wondering what they`re telling you. But more importantly, how do you then have a larger conversation to make sure all students are safe?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: And yes, I have. I have communicated with many. It`s unfortunate and I just believe we, as a country are making a big mistake. There is a desire to radicalize our children coming out of COVID using social media and methods of really radicalizing our children.\par \par \uc2 When you have only 18 percent of 18 to 30-year-olds of really loving this country, that should be a wake-up signal for all of us, and I have had a series of meetings with students, we have been meeting with various faith- based groups, this is nothing new for me.\par \par \uc2 My breaking bread, building bonds, over a thousand attendees of people sitting down together to communicate. We have to start understanding this diverse city, we must come together and we can`t use violence and calling for the destruction of a people like we are hearing with the antisemitism that we are seeing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So to that question, Mr. Mayor, the antisemitism, the rhetoric that we`ve seen, not just in New York, but across the country, what is the city doing to address that situation? And have you spoken to other mayors about that sharp rise in antisemitism?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Yes, and it is not only across the country, it has become a global movement, but it is more than just the terms of hate, Islamophobia, an attack on Sikh communities, going after LGBTQ+ communities.\par \par \uc2 You are seeing a rise of hate in our country that we must push back on, and I have communicated with all of the mayors. I was in Atlanta a few days ago for the African Americans Mayors Association. We talked about this.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with the mayor of Denver as well. We understand that this is going to impact cities and we are coming together as mayors across the entire country and communicating with our mayors outside the country to talk about how do we safeguard our cities and don`t allow discontinuation of a global attempt to radically radicalize young people in these cities.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  of New York City, we truly appreciate your time. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Ahead, the new recommendation that may help cut breast cancer deaths by 20 percent. Dr. Celine Gounder is here with what you need to know about the latest screening guidelines.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: New breast cancer screening guidelines may help clarify when women should get mammograms and help identify cancers early when they are more treatable.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Preventive Services Task Force\uc2  is recommending women begin mammograms at 40, down from age 50 every other year.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder, CBS News medical contributor and editor-at-large for public health at KFF Health News joins us now. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 So as we were talking before the cameras were rolling, I think the question people have is why now? To tell people a year -and-a-half as opposed to the previous guidelines.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So there are a couple of new trends. So we`ve seen an increase in breast cancer in women at a younger age, so about 10 percent of breast cancers are in women under the age of 45. In addition, Black women in particular, we are seeing younger ages with diagnosis of breast cancer, more aggressive breast cancer in Black women, and they are 40 percent more likely to die from breast cancer than White women.\par \par \uc2 So really more aggressive breast cancer screening guidelines, we are hoping will help address some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So and of course, you know, early detection, the key here, but the new guidelines again, every two years. Some doctors will say no, you should do it every year.\par \par \uc2 How do you make that decision? Is that -- you know, your doctor is going to look at your background and figure out if you`re more susceptible, then you need to fall more in line with every year?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is really based on the evidence available to the \uc2 US Preventive Services Task Force\uc2  right now. This is one area of controversy or where there`s lack of adequate information to others or what to do for women who have dense breasts, how best to screen them and for women over the age of 75 whether they need to continue screening.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: As far as these new recommendations, how do they affect insurance coverage?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Women already had insurance coverage starting at age 40 every year for breast cancer screening.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: These guidelines are one factor in determining breast cancer screening, but they`re not the only one.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, well, very, very important information as I got my first mammogram this year and they even asked if I wanted the AI to help detect anything extra, which I thought was interesting as well.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you again.\par \par \uc2 "What to Watch" is coming up next. Right now though, it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, our show is like a good football team, it is better in the second half than the first, so stick around for our eight o`clock hour including Anthony Mason`s one-on-one conversation with Oscar- winner Dame Judi Dench.\par \par \uc2 They talk about everything from her book on Shakespeare to her role in the Bond movies to how she got her first tattoo in her 80s.\par \par \uc2 Plus, author, Emily Oster, she is here to talk about her new book about helping women take on the challenges of pregnancy including some topics not often talked about.\par \par \uc2 All of that in our next hour, but first, we have Vlad with stories that you will be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Hey, hey, hey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want you to stick around --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh, I am getting a sneak peek.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Getting a sneak peek, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The best is yet to come, all right? We w