NY MASON: She is difficult.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN O`HEA: Yes, yes. It`s all a facade all the kind of Dame Judi stuff.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ahead, more of my conversation with Dame Judi about the role Shakespeare has played in her life.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this hour, Anthony Mason`s conversation with Oscar-winning icon, Dame Judi Dench looking back at a lifetime of work at nearly 90. She didn`t break into the movies until she was in her 50s.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`m Jericka Duncan. Author, Emily Oster`s new book, "The Unexpected" is about what doesn`t often get discussed about pregnancy. She`s here to bust some of those myths.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. In our "Changing the Game" series, we`ll introduce you to two former teachers who created a book and toy company to help young kids and their families learn Mandarin Chinese.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ni hao ma --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, not bad, Nate. You`re learning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Police move in to clear demonstrators from a building at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  while at UCLA, clashes breakout between pro-Palestinian protesters and counter protesters.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Counter protesters showed up and started agitating the crowd here. There were some fights that ensued. Clearly, some violence and there were some injuries.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  meets with Israeli leaders as the US pushes for a ceasefire and a hostage deal with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Severe weather continues to pummel the middle of the country, and there are now concerns about flooding.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Rain has been on repeat through the center of the country since last week. We see five to eight inches in just the last seven days.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The hero of the day. They were chanting "MVP." They`re playing "I Need a Hero."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And when a bee colony delays the \uc2 Arizona Diamondbacks\uc2  game, a local beekeeper gets the call. And then he is rewarded by throwing out the first pitch.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What was it like when you rolled into the building and you get a standing ovation?\par \par \uc2 MATT HILTON (Beekeeper): I thought I was here to just take care of a bee problem, and yes, things were pretty hyped up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Respect.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Respect.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He just threw that mask off and he was like "I`m here baby."\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Not afraid.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to start with something we`ve been covering all morning. Breaking news overnight: The latest tornado damage in the Central US, one person was killed when a twister hit Northeastern Kansas last night destroying homes in the tiny city of Westmoreland.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston is there.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 With the sun up, you can see just how horrific this damage is. As soon as you hit Westmoreland, debris is just scattered about like this. This is a roof that`s been ripped off a building, but we are not even sure if it came from this house because that`s just how much debris is out here.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this home. Trees came crashing down on it in the middle of the storm, smashing the house nearly in half. The family`s belongings are scattered about everywhere, mixed and mingled in sheets of metal and plywood.\par \par \uc2 This is just one of 22 homes police tell us were destroyed by the tornado.\par \par \uc2 Now, take a look at the immediate aftermath here in Westmoreland. We`ve been on the scene for hours and people are stuck inside. You`re not really allowed in and out because it`s just too dangerous with so much debris and crews are working to clean up the most dangerous impacts of the storm.\par \par \uc2 But even when they`re clear, it may not -- clean up may not start immediately, because Westmoreland is in a line of storms that`s expected to pummel from the plane states to Texas -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, Elise, so hard for such a small community. Thank you and your team for reporting on this.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, author, Emily Oster will tell us about her new book, helping women with potential challenges during pregnancy and childbirth. But it is 8:03, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shakespeare In Love"/Miramax)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is Dame Judi Dench in her Oscar-winning performance as Queen Elizabeth in "Shakespeare in Love." The film was an ode to the Bard and so is Dench`s new book.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, she talks with Anthony Mason about her memories of a lifetime of acting in William Shakespeare`s plays.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In her seven-decade career, Dame Judi Dench has played nearly every female character in William Shakespeare`s plays from Juliet to Cleopatra.\par \par \uc2 Dent and her late husband even used to call Shakespeare, the man who pays the rent, and that`s the title of her new book written with her friend Brendan O`Hea and is out now.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, she shares stories from a lifetime of iconic Shakespearean roles, and much more with Anthony Mason.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How long have you been here?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Forty years.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s a gorgeous spot.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Well, I love it. I must say.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Dame Judi Dench was here at home outside London during the pandemic when she got a call from an old friend.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Brendan writing me, and he said, I`m going to come down and talk to you about all the parts in Shakespeare you`ve played.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And what did you think about that idea?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Well, it was saving grace.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Brendan is actor and director Brendan O`Hea.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN O`HEA: I mean, famously you`re not great by yourself. So we just kind of rescued each other and we rescued each other through Shakespeare.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How many hours of conversations that you have?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN O`HEA: I`d say about 120. I mean, a lot of bickering.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She is difficult.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN O`HEA: Yes, yes. It`s all a facade, all that kind of Dame Judi stuff.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Those conversations led to their new book, "Shakespeare: The Man Who Pays The Rent."\par \par \uc2 Shakespeare has always been important to you.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Yes. Always.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: From the moment she saw her older brother in "Macbeth."\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: And he walked on and said, "What bloody man is that?" I thought, this is Shakespeare. This is swearing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Her first acting role wasn`t Shakespeare.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: I was a snail at my kindergarten school. I had to grip on with this enormous shell and all I had to do is put my horns out and then quite suddenly, I got a bit excited. So I stood up and I can remember Miss Mibby (sp?) who was the headmistress was standing at the side.\par \par \uc2 I heard her say, "Get down, Judith." I think I was six.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She was 22 when she made her professional debut as Ophelia in "Hamlet" at the Old Vic.\par \par \uc2 That`s huge, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: It`s fairytale stuff.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But her first reviews weren`t glowing.\par \par \uc2 What did you think when the critics were tough on you?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: I expect I cried a lot.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She was given another part in "Henry V."\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Lawrence Harvey was playing Henry V, thought I would be a total actress, so he only ever looked here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We looked right over you.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: I got -- I tried to stand. It never worked.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Comedy of Errors"/Royal Shakespeare Company)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Some of her now legendary Shakespearean performances have been preserved on film.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Midsummer Night`s Dream"/Royal Shakespeare Company)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But Dench doesn`t like to look at them.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Because you`ve got to change it, and it irritates you so much.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Henry V"/BBC)\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN O`HEA: But you did "Henry V," didn`t you with Kenneth Brunner and Michael obviously playing --\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: \uc2 Michael Williams\uc2  played \uc2 Michael Williams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s her late husband, who also starred in it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Henry V"/BBC)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you watch that one?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Oh, yes, you bet.\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS (Dame Judi Dench`s daughter): Oh, that`s good.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: What`s that?\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS: Is that a Roman soldier?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Oh, is it?\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS: Yes. Oh, it is heaven.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Her eyesight is poor now, but her memory is still sharp. With her daughter, Finty`s help, we went through some of her mementos.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Oh. John Stride. That`s his tree right out there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He has a tree out there, too?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: He does. He is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Dame Judi plants trees in her garden for friends she has lost or for people she admires, like Shakespeare.\par \par \uc2 What does it do for you to name the trees for your friends?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Well, it just reminds me of people. It`s just nice to look at trees and thinking you know, the joyous bright screen very well.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Acting awards clutter her bookcase, including her Oscar for "Shakespeare in Love."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shakespeare In Love"/Miramax)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Pride and Prejudice"/Universal Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Dame Judi`s movie career didn`t take off until her 50s in films like "A Room with a View" with Maggie Smith.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Room with a View"/Gold Crest Films International)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It took you a while to really embrace film.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Yes, well, I was told that I would never do it. I went for an audition once for a film and the director, he said, I`m sorry, but he said, no, you haven`t got the face for film he said.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What did you think when he said that?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: I didn`t expect anything more.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Golden Eye"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But then came Bond.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Golden Eye"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She took over the part of M, 007`s boss in 1995.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Golden Eye"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It made her an international star. She was asked about her eight Bond films during a recent book tour with O`Hea.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN O`HEA: The question was very sophisticated, what does Daniel Craig smell like?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Not like past pros, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Quantum of Solace"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But the role was no laughing matter for Dench.\par \par \uc2 Why were you so serious?\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Too frightened.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You were too frightened.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Well, I knew Bernard Lee very well, indeed and he played M before I took over from him.\par \par \uc2 I felt a great responsibility.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A portrait of Dame Judi as M hangs in her study.\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS: It`s quite fun if you FaceTime her and she is sitting here. You know, it`s pointing upwards and you`ve got Ma and Ma\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Dame Judi will turn 90 in December. For her 81st birthday, she got her first tattoo, the Latin phrase, "Carpe Diem."\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: It is savor the day.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ah.\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS: She sat there like that.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Oh, there`s also that woman lying there.\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Having her whole leg done, wasn`t there?\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS: Yes, there was.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Her whole leg.\par \par \uc2 FINTY WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You didn`t consider that.\par \par \uc2 DAME JUDI DENCH: Certainly not, no.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was her daughter`s idea, by the way to get the tattoo. She`s like, what do you get -- what do you for someone who has everything?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And she said, how about a tattoo? And I love the image of her with her arm out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So much wisdom.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and for somebody who has played so many roles.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, what`s next? Is she considering doing --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, the part of the problem now is she has macular degeneration, so it is very hard for her to see, as we alluded to in there, so it`s hard for her to read scripts.\par \par \uc2 She ask that friends read them to her to memorize, but she has like a photographic memory. So, you know, I think there will be parts, but they are going to be fewer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`d like to see her come back. There is a new James Bond coming and maybe she can come back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt as M.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I would love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, I think everybody would love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She has this humility about her.\par \par \uc2 MARQUARDT: She is a lovely woman. I mean, she`s a lovely woman. She has just a wonderful sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I remember that. I think it was on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  during the pandemic, her and her grandson.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, they did some dancing in the backyard.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And introduced people who were much younger, obviously to who she is.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Anthony, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, straight ahead, author, Emily Oster is here to tell us about her new book helping women navigate pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Author, Emily Oster has been helping women navigate pregnancy for more than a decade. Her first book "Expecting Better" has become a staple for many women as they prepare for childbirth.\par \par \uc2 Now Oster is out with her fourth book called "The Unexpected: Navigating Pregnancy During and After Complications." It includes tips on how to talk to medical providers before, during, and after pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Emily Oster joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER ("The Unexpected" author): Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 So we know there are a lot of books out there about the challenges of having children and after, but what did you see as the need for this particular book? I know your first couple of chapters, you talk about having the material, having the script, knowing what to say.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: Yes, so in the decades since the first book came out, I`ve talked to probably thousands of women about their pregnancies, and many of those conversations are hard and complicated and people are incredibly emotional about the things that have happened.\par \par \uc2 But those complications are not rare. So about 50 percent of pregnancies have been complicated by one of the things we talk about in the book. And we really intended the book to be a guide for people navigating those complications and for the people who love them who want to help them navigate that and that is our goal here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So half of all pregnancies, they include complications.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I feel like this conversation doesn`t happen enough. It`s whispered about, but on a macro scale, why is that?\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: I think there are two things. One is I think often when women experience these complications, there`s a sense of shame or fault that makes them reluctant to talk about it and I think socially, we don`t expect those conversations.\par \par \uc2 When you ask someone how was your -- you know, how did childbirth go? You`re expecting to hear, it is great. The baby is amazing. You don`t want to hear, well, I have some prolapse and here is here`s -- you know, here`s the complicated thing that happened.\par \par \uc2 And yet if we don`t say that, then people feel alone. They feel that they - -\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sometimes, you don`t even ask the question because .\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: Sometimes, you don`t even ask the question.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . you anticipate an answer that you --\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: Exactly. We don`t want it -- we don`t want to hear about the hard stuff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the complications you write about is miscarriage, which happens in at least 25 percent of all pregnancies. What do you want people to know about navigating pregnancy after miscarriage?\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: I think one thing is that many, many, many people have one miscarriage and typically after one miscarriage, there isn`t much of an elevated risk of another. But after multiple miscarriages, it is appropriate to talk to your doctor about are there additional tests that might be helpful in navigating what is going on?\par \par \uc2 So making that distinction, but also emphasizing that this is so common, and that doesn`t make it easy, but it does mean you really are not alone.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You also mentioned mental health and talk about the difference between pre and postpartum depression sort of the differences there? How important is it to understand and again, a lot of your book is based on data and other studies that you`ve looked at.\par \par \uc2 But you`re giving women the tools to not only say it`s okay, and it`s normal, but then what to ask your doctor next.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: Yes, so postpartum depression is a great example of a place where having the right conversation with your doctor at anticipating the possibility is the key to prevention or treatment, about 15 to 20 percent of women will have postpartum depression, about half of that starts during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 One of the things we see in second pregnancies is this is often much better, because people know to be looking for it. They know to be saying upfront, I am at risk for this. How are we going to evaluate to make sure that it doesn`t happen again?\par \par \uc2 We should be doing that for every pregnancy, whether it`s happened before or not.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you heard from thousands of women when you were researching for this book, what do you hear the most?\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: I feel alone, and I want to share my story. I mean, we put out a call for what would people like us to talk about in the book, we got almost 4,000 responses from people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: Just paragraphs and paragraphs of this is what happened to me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am sure from every age, right?\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: This is my story. From every age people many, many, many years later saying I still think about this. I`m still grappling with this. And I think that`s -- that is what I hear the most. I want to tell the story that happened to me.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I know you said fourth book, but we are looking forward to a fifth one. I feel like a lot of women --\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: We will see.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We will be expecting that.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That would be a big help.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Your first one was so good. I mean, it`s on the shelf of everybody who`s had children. It was a big sort of Bible in our house. So thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: It has been a privilege to be here for people in those moments, the good ones and the bad ones.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Emily Oster, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 "The Unexpected" is on sale right now wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead in "Talk of the Table," Formula One champion Lewis Hamilton shows us his driving skills on New York`s Fifth Avenue and he tells Gayle why he is so excited to bring his sport to the US.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: First of the month, all right. Time to tell you .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The first of the month.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: . about the "Talk of the Table." Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is about that time, so let`s see what you`ve got -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve got a little something for you and Jericka, thank you for bringing the flavor. I am not saying you don`t have it, that`s not what I am saying.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It wasn`t a shot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I am here every day, bro.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am saying she is in the house.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m here every day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know, bro. Let me get to my "Talk of the Table" okay, so, my "Talk of the Table" is seven-time Formula One racing champion, Lewis Hamilton, doing what he does best on Manhattan`s Fifth Avenue. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Lewis Hamilton on Fifth Avenue)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Empire State Building/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hamilton warmed up his car and the fans for Sunday`s Miami Grand Prix with a few donuts outside the Empire State Building. The stunt promoted a partnership with Hamilton`s Mercedes Formula One team and WhatsApp, highlighting the messaging app`s rapid growth in the US.\par \par \uc2 Gayle sat down exclusively with the global superstar after the event.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Lewis, you were just on Fifth Avenue. What did that mean to you? What did that feel like for you?\par \par \uc2 It`s a big deal for all the people watching.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS HAMILTON (Formula One race driver): It`s a huge, huge deal. I mean, I grew up watching movies and you know, "Trading Places" and "Coming to America" and dreaming of one day coming to New York and then I eventually came and visited this building with my mom when I was 17.\par \par \uc2 I never looked down and thought, okay, one day I`m going to drive the Formula One car up Fifth Avenue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS HAMILTON: So just very, very surreal and it was great to see also how much the people here in America have embraced our sport.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS HAMILTON: So we had a great turnout. The energy was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Now, I was told that you were told not to do donuts, but then they said but, Gayle, he`s a racecar driver, how can you not do a donut?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS HAMILTON: I was told not to do donuts, but I heard --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what did you do?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS HAMILTON: But I heard that the mayor asked us not to do it, but it was like, it was very short and if I had just driven up firstly that I don`t have a very good turning when I step on this thing. So he can`t do like an easy U-turn.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS HAMILTON: So I had to do donuts, but also that just creates a spectacle and I think that gets people energized.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now you can watch Gayle`s full behind the scenes interview on Monday discussing Hamilton`s future in and out of the sport.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s a superstar.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dude, superstar is like yes, incandescent. He`s like amazing, for sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, speaking of driving, I don`t drive really well, but my "Talk of the Table" is about Spotify`s list of the most popular driving songs for 2024. The songs on the list cover a lot of time. The 1985 hit "Everybody Wants to Rule the World" by Tears for Fears came in number five.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? Okay. The music service says it was featured in 698 road trip lists. The Weeknd`s 2015 hit "The Hills" is number four. Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is fascinating.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You like this?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Noah Kahan`s "Stick Season" is third with 920 appearances in car playlists. J. Cole`s "No Role Modelz" from 2014 is at number two.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ready for the number one most popular driving song in 2024.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s this one.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mr. Brightside/The Killers/Lizard King Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, that is The Killers, "Mr. Brightside" from 2003. Spotify says it was on 1,023 playlists related to driving. All the young people are screaming at me, it`s Noah Kahan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, okay. All right, all right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Noah Kahan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So my question is, what are you if you`re going on a road trip --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There`s only one answer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s Steppenwolf`s "Born to be Wild."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, I like that. I respect it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Born to Be Wild"/Steppenwolf/Dunhill RCA)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Sure\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, that`s the song that you play when you get on the open road.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What do you --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that. I like that. What about you, Jericka?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It depends on the mood. Right now, I`m feeling like old school Kendrick Lamar these days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You know, it`s like, going to be all right, you know, like\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re driving. You`re going to be all right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. I just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what I`d do?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`d just -- I just go 80s. I go -- I hit the decade, the 80s.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Danger Zone."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and I just hit the playlist. You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I can do that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome to Danger Zone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON:, That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Jericka, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, all right, so my "Talk of the Table" is Target`s cutest new team member. This is one-year-old, Azai. His mother Mia post videos of their trips to the store where Azai makes his way around to greet everybody that he sees.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mia Ariannaa/TikTok)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The videos have gotten tens of millions of views. Mia says the staff at Target made him an honorary team member for a day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Cute.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Azai took the role very seriously. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIA (Azai`s mom): Look, you work here now, so you have to come here and greet everybody, okay?\par \par \uc2 AZAI (Target honorary member): Okay.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Oh my goodness. Oh, so solid.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Look at you.\par \par \uc2 AZAI: Hi.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Hello.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Hi, sweetie. I got a chance to meet you. I cannot believe it.\par \par \uc2 Aend1\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Such a cutie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`d spend like a million dollars in there just to hang out with him.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Listen, I was going to say, listen, I thought about you because I remember how much shopping you were doing at Target.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. We did a lot of shopping --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In preparation for Celine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s where we got everything for Celine.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So you should be an honorary member as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I vote for you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was so cute. I like that, you work here now.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes. I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Take this seriously,.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got a job.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: He does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 All right ahead, in our "Changing the Game" series, the power of books for kids. We`re going to meet two pioneering women who have found success by creating a bilingual book company for children. But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Time now for our series, "Changing the Game" where we highlight extraordinary women who are making a difference now and for future generations. We are kicking off Asian-American and Pacific Islander Heritage Month by introducing you to two business women and friends.\par \par \uc2 Lulu Chang and Lacey Bernard started their company Bitty Bao in 2020 after struggling to find Chinese learning books for their kids. They`ve now written and illustrated 15 different bilingual board books to help children and families learn Mandarin and Cantonese.\par \par \uc2 They have sold more than 80,000 books and expanded their business to create toys like Hot Pot Kit to go with them. Lulu Cheng and Lacey Bernard join us now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LULU CHENG (Bitty Bao founder): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD (Bitty Bao founder): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LULU CHENG: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I was saying to you, guys earlier that for my daughter, Celine, we got her the old classics, right, "Bao Bao Learns Chinese," but these board books are changing the game, what inspired you to create them?\par \par \uc2 LULU CHENG: So, you know, as we become parents, a lot of things become crystal clear, like what we want for our kids like how we want to nurture them, and at the same time, I was really lucky to go on a hiatus from teaching and as years went by, it is like, oh it is time to do something more than staying at home.\par \par \uc2 And so this is how I wanted to live our traditions and our language, with my parents being all the way across the ocean. It was the only means to stay connected and have our culture and our heritage and identity for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 So I wanted to bridge like me going back to teaching yet not fulltime and the two blended together and became Bitty Bao.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it started as a home project. Right? I mean, just at home figuring it out before you took it to the next level.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Yes, it was just supposed to be a middle of the night hobby. Lulu, actually, that`s how she got me to do this. She said this is going to be a no-time hobby.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A no-time hobby.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: No time necessary, and you know, my second wasn`t even born yet and it is not a no-time hobby, spoiler alert, it takes a lot of time, but it has been really, really great.\par \par \uc2 Yes, but we do it all from our home. It`s definitely you know, from the ground up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you got involved --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you have the text -- shall we show the checks?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like this is the text that she sent you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The text message that got Lacey involved.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 So crazy thought, I want to write children`s bilingual Chinese books. Totally feasible. Want to be my illustrator?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and that`s how you got involved?\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LULU CHENG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Like literally that text came in at 11:30 at night. Just, hey, checking in. Is your baby sleeping?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Just an idea.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: And I have this idea. You want to do it with me?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And you made it happen.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: And crazy me was like, yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But how about too, how it also introduces other people to this language and the culture, because you have more options, and the more options there are out there, the more you`re likely to not just seek out what`s already or what`s typically available.\par \par \uc2 How important has that been for you when you hear from other parents that are not part of the culture, but now have been introduced because of your books?\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Well, something someone else had said to us once that really resonated with me was you know, I want our books to be not just that other option, I want it to be another option, right? So that for all families to want to pick up because they`re engaging and they are exciting, and they`re beautiful.\par \par \uc2 And you know, they can share about culture and language and, you know, just open up the world for themselves, but also their children.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And then you have the toys to accompany them.\par \par \uc2 LULU CHENG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: So many toys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How do you approach creating the art that accompanies Lulu`s words and vision?\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Yes, so the art is actually all paper cuttings, like little tiny pieces of paper cutouts stacked together.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: When I was younger, I was really into making like little handmade cards. And so when Lulu approached me, I told her, I was like, I`m not an artist in terms of like drawing, but I can cut paper and she`s like, that`s perfect. Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 So that`s what they are.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And Lulu, you started making the wooden toys?\par \par \uc2 LULU CHENG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tell us about that,\par \par \uc2 LULU CHENG: Our wooden toys match with our books, you know. We just wanted to add addition to our own kids and the materials and supplies that, you know, when you go to a shop, you look at all the food options and the toy options. And you wonder, well, we eat other foods, too and we eat plenty of them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, let me ask you, Lacey, you were raised without knowing Chinese.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: As I was telling you guys earlier, it`s really important for my wife and I to ensure that Celine, our daughter is able to communicate with her family --\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her parents, her grandparents. How does this help you reconnect with your heritage and your tradition?\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Yes, so I`m a third generation American-born Chinese. My grandparents, when they immigrated over here, they picked up English and Spanish really quickly to assimilate and we`re super proud of that fact.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, that meant, you know, giving up some of the language and culture, you know, of my family`s heritage. And so for me, it`s really about reclaiming Chinese culture for my kids and really exposing them to you know, the different traditions and the wonderful things that make up their cultural identity, and the makeup of who they are.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s my hope for them is that they`ll be able to connect in a deeper way with their background and their family.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, thank you guys so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: These books are incredible.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know these are children`s books but they`re for adults as well.\par \par \uc2 LACEY BERNARD: Very true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I really appreciate you all so much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Especially those of us trying to learn Mandarin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And go ahead and read the books. Get the toys and support because it`s an awesome cause.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, Gayle Bass shows us how to score this air purifier at an exclusive low, low price.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s time now for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals where we share some items all at exclusive discounts. To start shopping now scan the QR code on your screen, it is right there, or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Today, we have lifestyle host, Gayle Bass back in the house. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS (Lifestyle host): Good morning and we`ve got big deals at CBSDeals.com. We have deals starting at 50 up to 62 percent off and I`m talking about up to 62 percent off the Shapermint.\par \par \uc2 This is shapewear, but what I love about this shapewear that this brand is about making y