ces II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chaos on college campuses.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Free, free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Pro-Palestinian protesters break in and occupy a \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  building, as tensions grow at schools across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Protesters chanting)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with the crackdown on increasingly defiant and dangerous protests on college campuses across the country over the war in Gaza. Tonight, at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , the epicenter of the demonstrations, dozens of pro-Palestinian students have barricaded themselves inside a campus building, and they now face expulsion from the Ivy League school.\par \par \uc2 A White House spokesman today saying -- quote -- "Forcibly taking over buildings is not peaceful. It is wrong."\par \par \uc2 At the University of North Carolina in Chapel Hill, police forcefully removed protesters this morning. More than half of those arrested were not even affiliated with the university. In recent weeks, protests have been staged at more than 60 campuses. More than 1,000 people have been arrested. Many protesters are calling for the schools to cut ties with companies linked to Israel and to be transparent about their investments.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Tom Hanson leads us off tonight from \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): Dozens of pro-Palestinian protesters smashed their way into Hamilton Hall on the campus of \uc2 New York`s Columbia University\uc2  early this morning.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They barricaded themselves in, they brought chairs out, blocked the doors, and they moved outdoor heavy metal tables, blockaded the doors, formed human chains in front.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: All the parents, mothers, fathers!\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Once inside, the demonstrators hung a banner with the word "Intifada," the Arabic word meaning uprising.\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House said President Biden condemned the use of the term, along with other dangerous hate speech. The students are demanding that Columbia divest and sell its investment in companies linked to Israel, which the university has rejected.\par \par \uc2 When you hear the conversation about divestment, how does it make you feel?\par \par \uc2 HENRY SPEARS (Co-President, J Street U, \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 ): Well, personally, I don`t agree with \uc2 BDS\uc2  or divestment, but I believe that -- I believe in free speech. And I think it`s a conversation that should be had on campus.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: This latest escalation comes a day after university officials warned the students to dismantle their encampment or face suspension. In defiance, they set up a second one.\par \par \uc2 How do you think this all resolves on Columbia?\par \par \uc2 YOSAN ALEMU (Pro-Palestinian Protester): I`m not a fortune teller, but I think -- I mean, the students have made -- have made it very clear that, like, what they want is for their demands to be met. And as long as, like, those demands are not met, they will continue to hold their ground.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Jewish students on campus say the atmosphere has become intolerable.\par \par \uc2 ELISHA BAKER (Columbia University Sophomore): When we talk about feeling unsafe on campus, we`re not lying. This is a threat.\par \par \uc2 (Protesters chanting)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Police at the University of North Carolina were outnumbered as students threw debris at them, forcing some to use a chemical agent as they retreated.\par \par \uc2 In New Orleans, part of the Uptown Campus at Tulane University closed today after students and outside protesters clashed with police.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And, tonight, Columbia is threatening to expel student protesters occupying campus. But the school has still not given any indication how they are going to clear the encampment.\par \par \uc2 In a press conference, New York City Mayor Eric Adams says: "We cannot call for peace by using violence" -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tom Hanson, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The third week of Donald Trump`s New York criminal trial started with the threat of jail time for the former president. The judge held the presumptive Republican nominee in contempt of court for violating a gag order, forcing him to pay fines and take down social media posts. Trump is facing 34 felony charges related to so-called hush money payments to porn star Stormy Daniels.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jericka Duncan reports from the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): This gag order is not only unique; it`s totally unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): The $9,000 in fines are the result of nine instances where the court found \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  disparaged witnesses online, against the judge`s orders.\par \par \uc2 Judge Juan Merchan acknowledged the fines might not be enough to deter Trump and warned more violations could result in jail time.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: Judge Merchan did exactly what should be done and probably what was expected to be done.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: As court began, for the first time, one of Trump`s children, \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2 , joined the former president.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors played videos of Trump weeks before the 2016 election responding to allegations he sexually harassed women, concerned about how it could impact his chances of becoming president.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from October 2016)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I have no idea who these women are. If 5 percent of the people think it`s true, and maybe 10 percent think, we don`t -- we don`t win.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The focus then turned to \uc2 Keith Davidson\uc2 , an attorney who represented Stormy Daniels and Karen McDougal. Both claimed they had sex with Trump, which he denies.\par \par \uc2 Davidson first described bringing McDougal`s tale, which he called a blockbuster Trump story, to "The National Enquirer," and warned he would bring it elsewhere if they didn`t quickly pay her. Davidson also says it was the Access Hollywood tape where Trump was caught saying he could grab a woman by their private areas that had a -- quote -- "tremendous influence on interest in Stormy Daniels` story."\par \par \uc2 Davidson believed he had reached a deal with Trump`s attorney \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  in early October of 2016, but says it took longer than expected to finalize. When the prosecution asked why, Davidson said -- quote -- "I thought he was trying to kick the can down the road until after the election."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Now, the gag order does not prevent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from speaking to the press, as you have seen, but it does prevent him from making public statements about witnesses, jurors, or other people connected to this criminal case.\par \par \uc2 Now, on Thursday, the judge is expected to hold another hearing, Norah, that will review four other potential gag order violations.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jericka Duncan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a historic shift in U.S. drug policy is in the works. Sources tell CBS News the Justice Department is moving to reclassify marijuana as a less dangerous drug.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang at the White House. She joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 So this will be a major reform. How is it going to work?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This is the most significant change in U.S. drug policy in more than 50 years. So, marijuana, which is now classified as a Schedule I drug, like heroin and illicit fentanyl, would be reclassified as a Schedule III substance, like ketamine and steroids. Drugs in this category are considered less dangerous and less addictive.\par \par \uc2 It also means the consequences for using them are less severe. However, the DEA still regulates these drugs, so even if pot is reclassified, the change would not legalize marijuana for recreational use. So it`s not like you would be able to get it at your neighborhood pharmacy; 24 states and Washington, D.C., have legalized marijuana and 14 others permit its use for medical purposes.\par \par \uc2 The White House is not commenting on the proposed rule, but it was President Biden who called for a review of federal marijuana law back in 2022, and he pardoned thousands of Americans convicted of simple possession.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a former White House drug policy adviser to Presidents \uc2 Obama\uc2 , Bush, and Clinton is slamming the proposal, arguing there are serious health and mental consequences of using pot, and if the drug is reclassified, he plans to challenge that decision in court -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re learning new details tonight about the deadliest attack on law enforcement in nearly a decade. Four officers were killed and four others wounded as shots rang out when they tried to serve a warrant in Charlotte, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Dave Malkoff has more on the fallen officers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): Police say 39-year-old Terry Clark Hughes, armed with an AR-15 rifle and a 40-caliber handgun, opened fire from an upstairs window of a house as a \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  task force attempted to execute an arrest warrant for him at a Charlotte, North Carolina, house on Monday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Several officers immediately came under fire, as others rushed in to help.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY JENNINGS (Charlotte-Mecklenburg, North Carolina, Police Chief): To me, that`s truly heroic, when you hear the gunshots and the rapid fire and they`re running directly into it because they know that there are people that need help.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Hughes then attempted to leave the house and was shot dead. Two women were eventually taken into custody after an hours-long standoff. Neither has been charged.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are learning more about the four officers killed, Deputy Marshal Thomas Weeks, who leaves behind a wife and four kids, William "Alden" Elliott and Sam Poloche, both 14-year veterans of the State Department of corrections, and Joshua Eyer, a CMPD police officer who died later at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Deamonte White knew Officer Eyer. He was the school police officer who inspired him to serve.\par \par \uc2 DEAMONTE WHITE (North Carolina): You know, when danger strikes, just like any other officer, he`s going to get out there.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Today, remnants of the chaos remain, the house partially destroyed and covered in bullet holes, and a solemn procession carrying the fallen to their resting place.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JOHNNY JENNINGS: The last few days have -- it`s just been very tough.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: I`ve got to tell you, it was moving in that room when Chief Jennings was remembering his friend.\par \par \uc2 The chief was just with Officer Eyer when he awarded him as officer of the month for April. And, Norah, he leaves behind a wife and a 3-year-old boy.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It is just devastating.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. may soon welcome some Palestinians from Gaza as refugees. CBS News has obtained internal documents showing senior Biden administration officials have discussed several options for resettling a small number of Gaza residents. It would include those who have immediate family members who are U.S. citizens or permanent residents.\par \par \uc2 In the last fiscal year, the U.S. resettled 60,000 refugees from around the world. Just 56 were Palestinian.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is in Israel for talks, as the Biden administration presses \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to accept a deal to release some hostages in return for a temporary cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta reports, Israel`s prime minister says, even if there is a deal, he intends to go ahead with a ground invasion of the Southern Gaza City of Rafah.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Protesters chanting)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voiceover): Protesters are turning up the heat night after night, demanding Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  bring the hostages home, whatever the price.\par \par \uc2 For Netanyahu, it would mean delaying the Rafah invasion and alienating hard-liners in his government, a price he`s not yet ready to pay.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "We will enter Rafah because we have no other choice," he said today.\par \par \uc2 As the secretary of state lands in Tel Aviv, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is weighing up the hostage proposal.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): No more delays, no more excuses. The time to act is now.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: He`s here in the last-ditch bid to secure a deal to stave off the Rafah offensive and ensure Gaza is flooded with humanitarian aid.\par \par \uc2 The threat of a ground invasion terrifies Adnan Abu Asi (sp?). He`s done living as a refugee in Rafah and returned to what`s left of his four-story family home outside Khan Younis today.\par \par \uc2 (ADNAN ABU ASI speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "Everyone`s putting up a tent on top of the rubble of their homes," he said, "because they want their lives back."\par \par \uc2 Abu Asi has nothing and walks several hours a day to find water or a tin of food. But, at least here, he has dignity, he says, and his memories of what was once the most beautiful place in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And a glimmer of hope in Gaza today.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  resumed work almost one month to the day seven of its workers were killed in an Israeli strike. They provided 200,000 meals to displaced Palestinians and say they have trucks ready to enter Gaza with enough food for eight million more meals -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: They`re doing heroic work.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta, thanks.\par \par \uc2 There was a horrifying attack today in London. Police say a man drove into a house and then began stabbing people with a sword. Doorbell video shows police using a Taser to subdue the subject -- suspect, who was arrested, but not before a 14-year-old boy was killed. Four other people were hurt, including two officers. Investigators don`t have a motive, but do not believe this was an act of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, millions in the Central U.S. are on alert, as tornado watches are in effect. The latest round of severe weather includes the threat of large hail, heavy rain and 70-mile-per-hour gusts.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist Chris Warren is tracking it all with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 With the ongoing cleanup and recovery efforts, an increasing chance for more storms. That threat continues tonight and again tomorrow for some of the areas that were hit by tornadoes over the weekend, the storms continuing to increase during the nighttime hours. The future radar shows that. The tornado threat is there, the possibility for tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 Winds could be damaging, up to around 75 miles an hour. These storms are also going to be bringing hail and very heavy rain. That flood threat will be with us here from Texas all the way up to Minnesota, and, Norah, once again, tomorrow, another tornado threat.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chris Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says scammers are getting rich off America`s seniors. Today, the bureau reported complaints by elderly victims jumped 14 percent last year. Total losses to those over the age of 60 ballooned by 11 percent.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nicole Sganga has important information on how to protect your savings.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REV. MALCOLM FRAZIER (Elder Fraud Victim): All of a sudden --\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA (voiceover): Reverend Malcolm Frazier was browsing his computer when the screen froze.\par \par \uc2 REV. MALCOLM FRAZIER: A message jumped out, said: "Do not turn off your computer. Please call this number."\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: A scammer persuaded him to transfer tens of thousands of dollars in savings, or lose it all.\par \par \uc2 You thought you were talking to the bank, and you were talking to a scammer.\par \par \uc2 REV. MALCOLM FRAZIER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: It`s called elder fraud crime. And Americans over the age of 60 are now falling victim at record numbers.\par \par \uc2 AARON SERES (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent): Financial fraud is definitely on the rise, and particularly targeting our elderly population.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Last year, $3.4 billion was stolen, according to a newly released \uc2 FBI\uc2  report, with more than 100,000 complaints filed to federal law enforcement last year alone, driven by criminal networks operating in West Africa and Asia.\par \par \uc2 AARON SERES: Organized networks of individuals who are well-financed, well-resourced and are using technology.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Still, experts say the crime targeting seniors is grossly underreported.\par \par \uc2 AARP estimates more than $28 billion is stolen due to elder fraud scams each year. They`re now launching a public awareness campaign nationwide, as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  visits senior centers, churches and rotary clubs.\par \par \uc2 AARON SERES: If you have never met somebody in person, the best advice is to not send them any money.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Convincing members of America`s Silent Generation to stay vigilant and to speak out.\par \par \uc2 REV. MALCOLM FRAZIER: Just don`t fall for it. Hang the phone up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials are also urging financial institutions to create more guardrails, fraud prevention measures that would help stop seniors from sending large sums of money to strangers -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such an important story.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sganga, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A tragic accident in Arizona involving a bounce house -- the details when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There`s a heartbreaking story from Arizona.\par \par \uc2 A 2-year-old boy was killed Saturday in a bounce house south of Phoenix. A powerful gust of wind hit the house and sent it crashing into a neighboring yard. The little boy died at a hospital. A second child suffered a broken arm. A spokesperson for the sheriff called it a tragic accident.\par \par \uc2 There are new guidelines for when women 40 and older should be screened for breast cancer. We have got the details for you next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In tonight`s Health Watch: New guidelines are being issued for breast cancer screenings.\par \par \uc2 Regular mammograms are now being recommended to start younger, at age 40, and women up to 74 should get screened every other year. Previously, the \uc2 U.S. Preventative Services Task Force\uc2  recommended that women get the exams every two years starting at age 50.\par \par \uc2 The updated guidelines do not apply to women with a history of breast cancer or who have a genetic marker such as the BRCA1 or BRCA2 genes that may put them at higher risk. Remember, those patients should consult with their doctors.\par \par \uc2 Breast cancer is the second most common cancer among American women after skin cancer. Earlier screenings are meant to address the rise in breast cancer among women in their 40s and the higher death rate from breast cancer among black women.\par \par \uc2 It`s estimated the new approach will save about 20 percent more lives.\par \par \uc2 We should tell you that experts we spoke with say these guidelines don`t go far enough and that women 40 and over should be screened every year. Talk to your doctor. Important information.\par \par \uc2 For people with sensory issues, getting a haircut can be stressful. We`re going to meet a barber who cuts through anxiety with kindness.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet Billy the barber. Billy Dinnerstein is the owner of Your Kind of Cuts in Nutley, New Jersey. His barbershop gives haircuts to children and adults with autism, sensory issues and other developmental disabilities. Their services are so unique and special that some of Dinnerstein`s customers even fly in from out of state.\par \par \uc2 And it`s made him a big star on social media. The haircuts at his shop have a special brand of patience and kindness customized to each customer`s individual needs. His barbers are trained to make everyone feel comfortable and to make sure every visit is a positive experience.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BILLY DINNERSTEIN (Owner, Your Kind of Cuts): If I can present an atmosphere or environment for you in a tough situation that helps you, a little bit more comfortable moving forward, then that`s my goal.\par \par \uc2 Like, at the end of the day, you know, I`m not here trying to win any award for being barber of the year. I mean, we`re here just trying to help kids.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Billy Dinnerstein, a barber who`s a cut above the rest, and he`s tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240501ek4u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For April 28, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Anthony Salvanto, Mark Strassmann, Debora Patta \par \uc2 8,430 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Pressure builds to try and get the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -held hostage negotiations back on track, and campus protests sparked by the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  conflict spread across the country.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, more \uc2 Hamas\uc2  proof-of-life propaganda featuring another American hostage held in Gaza appearing very much under duress.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden vows that he will not rest until every hostage is returned and sends his top diplomat back to the region.\par \par \uc2 Plus, with $61 billion in aid now headed to Ukraine, Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  apologizes to Volodymyr Zelenskyy for Senate Republicans` role in that delay that took its toll on the country`s war effort.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you feel your party is responsible for those setbacks?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-Kentucky): Many of them, yes. We -- we took too long.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Plus: What`s helping fuel those campus protests from coast to coast? We will tell you.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin today with foreign correspondent Debora Patta in Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): Like most days in Rafah, this one began with mourning...\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: ... after more Israeli strikes hit what is mostly a tent city in the south sheltering over half of Gaza`s 2.3 million Palestinians crammed up against the Egyptian border.\par \par \uc2 There were heartbreaking farewells to tiny bodies wrapped in shrouds. This infant has known only war her entire life. It`s been a brutal week for children. Even miracle baby miracle Sabreen al-Ruh, who was saved from her dying mother`s womb a week ago, lived just five days. She`s been buried next to the rest of her family.\par \par \uc2 Rafah is already under daily bombardment, but Israel keeps threatening a ground invasion. And Palestinians who`ve already fled multiple times keep asking, where will they go? Much of Gaza lies in ruin, the destruction on such a scale it is uninhabitable.\par \par \uc2 This morning, more humanitarian aid was airdropped over the territory, and this \uc2 IDF\uc2  footage shows the floating pier being built by the Americans near the Gazan coastline, which officials say will be completed early May, allowing more supplies to flow into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 But the dire warnings of imminent famine have not gone away. Already, the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says one in three children under the age of 2 is acutely malnourished, prior to October 7, hardly any. And despite an increase in aid, the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says it is still not enough to avert famine, which could spread across the north next month.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Ahead of a possible rougher invasion, there are now last- ditch attempts to revive cease-fire talks.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has stepped up the pressure by releasing propaganda videos that show three hostages, including two Americans, still alive, but speaking under duress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our Debora Patta in Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 And we are joined now by Hanna Siegel, whose uncle, Keith Siegel, is one of those American hostages.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Hanna.\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL (Niece of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Hostage): Good morning, Margaret. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you for speaking to us.\par \par \uc2 I know that the Biden administration has reached out to you and your family. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is analyzing that video released yesterday. What stood out to you? I think this is the first proof of life so far?\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: We -- we`ve always believed that he`s alive. We have to believe that he`s alive.\par \par \uc2 And his wife, my aunt, Aviva Siegel, who was held for 52 days, released in the deal that took place in November, she was with him. And so we knew -- when she came out, she told us that he was alive. But this is the first time that we`re seeing him, that we`re hearing him.\par \par \uc2 It`s -- it`s surreal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you have "205" there. That`s the number of days he has been held.\par \par \uc2 In the video, your uncle, under duress, mentions the protests that have been taking place in Israel pressuring the government for a deal. Do you think that a deal can be reached at this point, and is it up to the United States to close it?\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: I do think a deal can be reached. I mean, we -- we know that. A deal was reached in November. Dozens of women and children were released, including my aunt, Aviva Siegel.\par \par \uc2 So we know that it`s possible. And I do think that`s really important to remember. The United States plays a critical role. It played a critical role in November. I think, you know, ultimately, this is a negotiation between \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Prime Minister Netanyahu in Israel.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that really worries my family and worries me is that it`s arguably not in Prime Minister Netanyahu`s political interest to close a deal. And, you know, for us, this is the moment. These videos -- as you know, there was a video released a couple of days ago of another American hostage, Hersh Goldberg-Polin.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: I do think that these two videos are a signal from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that they are ready to make a deal and a reminder that there are American citizens being held, including -- including Keith.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are 133 hostages that we know of, five of them Americans. Why do you believe it is not in Prime Minister Netanyahu`s interest to cut a deal to bring them home?\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: I think, you know, there is a lot swirling in the political landscape in Israel. And I`m not here to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 But there have been negotiations going on for months now. We`re at 205 days. There have been deals on the table. And they`ve proven elusive. And I worry that that is because of Prime Minister Netanyahu. So I think that now is the moment.\par \par \uc2 I think -- I have faith that we can do a deal now. But I also think that, if that isn`t possible, then the Biden administration should think about what they can do directly to bring our American citizens home.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is flying to the region now and is going to talk about a cease-fire and a hostage deal.\par \par \uc2 Do you know if the Biden administration is considering making a deal to bring the Americans home without the Israeli government`s approval or role here?\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you want them to look at that option?\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: What I know is that the Biden administration has shown unwavering and relentless commitment to my family and the families of all of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 And I know that it is their priority to bring all hostages, including the Americans, home. And so I know that they are doing absolutely everything that they can. Keith, in this video, I mean, he starts the video by saying how much he loves us, he loves our family.\par \par \uc2 And he`s obviously under duress. He`s gaunt. He looks weak. But that`s real. I know that he misses his family. He talks about -- you know, I think the hardest part of the video is, he breaks down talking about being alone on Passover.\par \par \uc2 Those of us Siegels in America got together for Passover and those in Israel got together for Passover. And we had -- we had this little picture of Keith at the table, but he`s not home. And so I have such faith, and I have felt the commitment from the Biden administration to get him back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Keith`s daughter sent a recorded message to protesters who were in the streets in Israel last night.\par \par \uc2 And she said: "I demand the country`s leaders watch the video and see their father crying for help."\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  released this as propaganda. Why do you think watching that video is important? What -- what do you think is not understood by the government?\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: First of all, my cousins, Keith`s kids, and his wife, of course, a hostage survivor herself, are incredibly brave.\par \par \uc2 And they have spent every single day going to anybody who will listen around the world and telling them, reminding them that there are people`s lives at stake. I think there`s so much swirling in the political realm that it`s easy to forget that these are human beings. Keith is a grandfather. He`s a husband. He`s a brother. He`s an uncle.\par \par \uc2 We`re a very, very close family. And that`s what we`re feeling, more than any of the political considerations, more than thinking about the day after and what happens. Those are really, really important questions, but I think this video is a reminder that these are human beings, and they need to come home.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The U.S. says \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is standing in the way of a deal.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that, if an invasion of southern Gaza, of Rafah happens, that your uncle will be at risk of death?\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: Very concerned. I mean he -- he talks in the video about bomb -- hearing bombs all around him.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: And so we worry about the fact that he`s being held underground by a terrorist group, of course.\par \par \uc2 We worry about the fact that he`s starving. When Aviva came out...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: ... she talked to us about how they were beginning to starve.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: And I can`t imagine that that hasn`t gotten so much worse.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: You can see it on his face. And we also worry about the bombs going off around him.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Of course. Of course.\par \par \uc2 Hanna, thank you for reminding us about the humanity here. And we wish you luck.\par \par \uc2 HANNA SIEGEL: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We visited Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  on Capitol Hill Thursday just after he apologized to President Zelenskyy for the delay passing the $61 billion aid package for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Leader McConnell acknowledged to us the six-month process, four in the Senate, was a costly delay for the Ukrainian war effort.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In the time of the delay, Russia`s military land forces have grown back to where they were before the invasion, the army is 15 percent larger, and they`ve reinforced the 20 percent of Ukrainian territory that they hold.\par \par \uc2 These are all the words of the supreme allied commander himself. Do you feel your party is responsible for those setbacks?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-Kentucky): Many of them, yes.\par \par \uc2 We -- we took too long. This issue was like a family reunion, if you will, with a lot of different points of view being expressed around the table. All the Democrats were for Ukraine. There is no question that the debate was in our family, on our side.\par \par \uc2 And there was a lot of skepticism for a long time, but I think it got better. And I think we proved that earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do you think changed minds?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : The actual facts.\par \par \uc2 Once we realized we were not going to get a border result, I think our members really started focusing on the -- the package. It was -- it was clear that it was not going to have a border provision attached to it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL: And there are almost no good arguments against this. Every argument that made it -- made by the opponents is provably wrong. And the facts, I think, were convincing for a number of our members, and they changed their minds.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are leader for another eight months.\par \par \uc2 But you`ve said you`re going to stay and serve out your term. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may again become president. According to our latest \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling, 79 percent of self-identified Republicans told us that the source of information they most trust on Ukraine and Russia is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This sentiment doesn`t seem to be disappearing. How are you going to counter that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : What I want to do and what I`m focused on is not the presidential race, but getting the Senate back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But this isn`t the race. This is persuading public opinion.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 I think the single most important thing I can do is make sure my successor is the majority leader, no matter how the presidential election comes out. I think the fact that our nominee basically decided not to continue whipping people against the package was a good sign. And I`m going to be advocating increasing the defense budget, no matter who gets elected, and preparing ourselves for the long term, which is China, Russia, and Iran.\par \par \uc2 This administration`s budget requests for defense haven`t even kept up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said the Republican nominee decided not to whip against the package, in other words, stopped telling lawmakers that -- not to vote for it.\par \par \uc2 But we know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not a fan of -- of Zelenskyy. \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , the prime minister of Hungary, recently had dinner with Mr. Trump at Mar-a-Lago and then told reporters that Trump said he won`t give a penny to Ukraine and that`ll be the way he forces an end to the fighting.\par \par \uc2 If that`s the fundamental belief of the man who`s going to be commander in chief, how do you stop him?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : What I`m doing is trying to change the Senate, so that we have a majority, no matter who wins the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 I can`t control that. I have some influence here in the Senate. I intend to use it, no matter who gets elected president, to increase our defense budget and get ready for the challenges that we have ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that`s a challenge where you might have to be the firewall against your own party and its leader again.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I have been willing to do that. I had something to do with changing opinion in the Senate on this issue. And I think a lot more of my members now understand the importance of it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: About a month after January 6, you voted to acquit \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after he was impeached.\par \par \uc2 And you said on the Senate floor: "Trump"...\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Didn`t get away with anything yet.\par \par \uc2 We have a criminal justice system in this country. We have civil litigation. And former presidents are not immune from being accountable by either one.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you still believe that former presidents are not completely immune from liability?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Let`s put it this way. I addressed that issue on February the 13th...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s active before the Supreme Court as we speak.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : ... and January the 6th of 2021. I stand by everything I said then.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, it`ll be up to the Supreme Court to decide whether I was correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Part of what you said is part of this case in some ways, because you argued for the Senate not to convict Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 And central to his immunity argument is the claim that a former president who was impeached and convicted by the Senate can be criminally prosecuted. He was not.\par \par \uc2 Do you regret your choice? It`s part of the defense.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I don`t regret anything I said then. I haven`t taken anything I said then back.\par \par \uc2 But the answer to your question is going to be in the courts. The Supreme Court`s going to decide that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you stand by your description of Trump as practically and morally responsible for provoking the events of January 6, and potentially criminally responsible and liable?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I don`t know how many times you`re going to ask me the same question. I stand by everything I have said on January 6, and February 13, 2021.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I`m asking you the question because, since the past few months have passed and our last conversation, you`ve endorsed him for reelection.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : You need to get better research. I was asked that question three years again, if he were the nominee, would I support him?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you said you would support whoever the nominee was.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : And I said yes, because the voters of my party across the country have made a decision. As the Republican leader of the Senate, obviously, I`m going to support the nominee of our party.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you have taken stands on issues you feel are of -- of strong national security interests and morally imperative. That -- that was your argument on Ukraine, and that you were bucking, in some ways, a populist opinion.\par \par \uc2 So, on this one, I`m just wondering how you explain that, when you say it was good enough for a number of Republicans that he`d be the nominee, because that is the populist opinion. It`s not taking the position that he has -- he doesn`t live up to the role.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : The issue is -- the issue is -- the issue is, what kind of influence -- even if I had chosen to get involved in the presidential election, what kind of influence would I have had?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re one of the most powerful Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I`m -- I`m the Republican leader of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 What we do here is try to make law. I like us to be in the majority.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your worldview seems more aligned with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  when it comes to American leadership...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I wouldn`t...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... in these global conflicts...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Well...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... than with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I certainly wouldn`t...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... who has spoken against Volodymyr Zelenskyy, who has not endorsed the package that you just worked so hard to get over the finish line.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Yes. OK, look, I -- I wouldn`t have withdrawn from Afghanistan. I wouldn`t have submitted four budgets in a row for defense that don`t even keep up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 I have got plenty of differences with the current administration. Whether I will have differences with the next administration remains to be seen. I know what -- what I think. And it doesn`t make any difference what the outcome of the presidential election is. I`m going to be focusing on this remainder of my time in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What I hear you saying -- tell me if I`m getting it wrong -- is that you can stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he`s commander in chief. Even if you`re not leader, you`re going to do the most you can to counter this isolationist worldview and to counter...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... or limit what he could do if reelected.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I`m going to counter, no matter who`s elected president, advocating things that I think are not good for America.\par \par \uc2 What I care about is, what does the person who actually gets elected ultimately do?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Should Mr. Trump, as candidate, as representative of your party, go and see Ukraine for himself?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : I -- I`m not going to give him any advice. I -- I`m focusing on turning the Senate Republicans into the majority here and focusing on advocating, as I think I successfully did this very week, for moving away from the isolationist movement that began with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It began with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : It did. He has a huge -- he had a huge audience among rank-and-file Republicans. And I think it was very destructive, very impactful on regular Republican voters and created a big problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because he mimicked Republican propaganda and amplified it, and then that`s been repeated on the House floor, as the House Intel Chairman said?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Well, I certainly disagreed with him. And he certainly ended up where he should have been all along, interviewing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Will you fact-check \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when he says these things? Because he has also repeated some of these claims.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL: I`m not going to give any advice to our candidate in the -- in the presidential election. What I`m focusing on is turning the Senate into a majority Republican.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our full interview with Leader McConnell is on our Web site and our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our Mark Strassmann reports on those college campus protests that have erupted across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Back up! You back up!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): All the clamor on campus keeps spreading, a clash of more than ideas, riot gear at Ohio State, Tasers at \uc2 Emory University\uc2  in Atlanta, mostly pro-Palestinian protesters at times scuffling with police, waved on by school administrators, with hundreds of arrests, communities like the \uc2 University of Texas\uc2  turning on each other.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s war on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has another front line...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Let my people go!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... a free speech and hate speech campus debate that`s anything but academic.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: If you want to protect free speech, then you don`t break up peaceful protests.\par \par \uc2 GREG LUKIANOFF\uc2  (President and CEO, Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression): People didn`t seem to be aware of how bad the situation for free speech and academic freedom already was on campus until October 7.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 Greg Lukianoff\uc2  of the Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression, a group defending the right to free speech.\par \par \uc2 Do you have the sense that any of this is moving the ball forward?\par \par \uc2 GREG LUKIANOFF: Some of the ways the protest has been going on, on college campuses has been alienating more of the public than bringing it in.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 `s west lawn, civil disobedience, this ongoing tent city, galvanized national protests. They want the university to divest investments in Israel and companies they say profit off repression of Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #1: Send them home!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Dueling protests at Columbia, pro-Israel, pro- Palestinian, reflect a larger welter of opinion and emotion, passion and perceived prejudice.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #2: People would be up in arms and protesting that right away. But because it`s the Jews, I feel like nobody cares.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: All this coast-to-coast commotion is well into its second week, and what began here at Columbia has now rippled overseas.\par \par \uc2 From Paris to London, university students occupied buildings and marched in the streets. They`re echoing this U.S. student movement, at times a moment that`s been unflattering, even dark, from intolerant protesters to stumbling universities.\par \par \uc2 GREG LUKIANOFF\uc2 : A lot of campuses are teaching young people to think like activists and less like scholars. When you`re reduced to just shouting at each other, that is a failure of some of the things that makes higher education so special.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will have more on what`s fueling those protests coming up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Join us next week, when we will talk to South Dakota Republican Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  about her new book, "No Going Back."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION and the kickoff of our 2024 battleground tracker polls. Our CBS News battlegrounds are those seven key states where things could go either way. Today we`re looking at three of them that Biden won in 2020, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin. In effect, Trump and Biden are running even. The former president is up 1 percentage point in two of those states, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin. The current president is up two over Trump in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is our executive director of elections and surveys, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 So, Anthony, why are they so close if Biden won them in 2020?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: The reason this time is, I just asked people, to start to campaign, how do these candidates make you feel? And the top answer for each of them was, worried. Then you look at the Biden numbers particularly, and he gets feelings of insecurity, which could be kind of tough for a sitting president, although calm is one that accrues to his benefit. But then for Trump you get feelings of anger, right, that`s particularly true for Democrats, also some insecurity. And neither one of them does very well on eliciting confidence.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The economy is always issue number one for voters. How is it faring in these three states?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: And it is again here. But what`s interesting is this story starts back in 2020. When we polled these states, at the time the pandemic was going on. People then said the economy in those states was bad.\par \par \uc2 So, we asked them now, OK, has it gotten better? And very few people say that it has. In fact, many say they feel it`s gotten worse. And inflation is the reason that they say that.\par \par \uc2 But what`s also interesting is, we asked them, OK, look back to the economy during the Trump years, how do you remember it? And a majority say they remember it as being good.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because they forget about the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, they seem to be skipping over the pandemic. And that`s exactly what that tells you in their memory of this. But it also explains now when you ask them, OK, now let`s look forward, they say - more say they feel they`d be financially better off under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that cuts directly to vote. And that is helping the former president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, in these three particular states, access to abortion is legally protected. How much of a motivator will it be then for voters there to actually go to the polls?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: That`s probably the key question. It is for Democrats. What you see is, these other states where there has been abortion laws, Arizona, with that 1864 law, Florida with their law going into effect, their ban, you know, six-week ban going into effect, voters in these states, Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin, say they`re watching what`s happening in those states. So, what`s happening there is not staying there, in their minds. And what`s important there is it becomes a national issue.\par \par \uc2 And so here we see, especially for Democrats, when they are saying they feel angry that Roe v. Wade was overturned, they`re voting overwhelmingly for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But for the ones who say abortion should be legal but they`re not angry about this, they`re dissatisfied, they`re unhappy about it, the race is much closer. And what that tells you is, for the Biden campaign in particular, it`s going to be an exercise in motivation, it`s going to be trying to put that issue even higher on the issues list. And that is the big thing on which -- on which their, you know, fortunes might hinge.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anthony Salvanto, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we go now to Democratic Congresswoman Summer Lee. She joins us from her district in Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 REP. SUMMER LEE (D-PA): Good morning. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You just heard our \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling in reference to your home state. I know last weekend Governor Shapiro told us, "if President Biden`s pause on natural gas permits goes on a long time it will cost Pennsylvania jobs." And we`re seeing in our polling the perception that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be better, 49 percent of voters said, than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , 33 percent, on these oil and gas exploration issues. This seems to be a headwind for Democrats in your state. Are you concerned?\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LEE: Yes. I think that there`s - I think there`s a lot to be concerned about, right? I think that when we look at these polls, you know, we`ll pull out one issue at a time. But the reality is, is that there are a number of issues mixed together that make voters feel confident, that make voters - some other voters feel hopeless or feel like maybe there`s a little reason to participate. And I think that we`re dealing with that a lot, right?\par \par \uc2 When we talk to voters throughout my election, we talk to a lot of voters who, whether it`s the economy, the price of, you know, goods, gas, food, things of that nature, or abortion, like all of these different issues, made voters feel like there was very little options that they had and that they didn`t want to participate. And we have to figure out something very quickly to address that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In your primary, which you won, your Democratic challenger`s point of attack was your willingness to break with President Biden in his unequivocal support for Israel with its war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza. You`ve called for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 If you believe that this is such a matter of conscience and so important, can you truly ask progressive voters to go and vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in November?\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LEE: Yes, I think that we do that every year. Every two years we ask voters, progressive voters, we ask black and brown voters, young voters, and we ask them to weigh the totality of our options, right? The totality of a candidate. The totality of the things that we care about, that we come to the polling places with. And that`s not new, right? This is an issue that gets a lot of issue. And there are going to be very reasonably a lot of people who will feel apprehension about that. And I think that is not a foregone conclusion that there`s just nothing that Democrats can do.\par \par \uc2 When we look at an election like mine, we`ve seen how popular it is to take a pro-(INAUDIBLE) stance, how popular it is to call for a different direction in the policies that the United States has towards the war in Gaza, towards sending unconditional weapons, offensive weapon to Netanyahu, and our government still has time to react. We still have time to listen to the folks who have feelings about that. And that`s what we have to do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you went to meet with some of the campus protesters out in Pittsburgh recently. You were in support of some of those young people who have chosen to protest against what`s happening in Gaza. Do you hear from them, that they`re excited to go vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LEE: To be honest, you know, we don`t. It`s not a topic that, you know, always comes up, but it does a lot.\par \par \uc2 I think that the number one goal for them right now, you know, just listening to what they have to say, is that they want to see a ceasefire in Gaza. They want to see the indiscriminate bombings and killings end, right? That is the reason why they`re on - there`s a lot of noise around this that - that distracts from that. There`s a lot of noise that tries to take away from that central message. But that is the central thing that they are fighting for. And they believe and they feel like our government can do more. And that`s what they`re looking for. They`re looking for some sort of acknowledgment from our leadership that they hear our needs, that they hear these young people saying that we want our country, we want our government to go in a different direction.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I hear you clearly distinguishing between Prime Minister Netanyahu and Israel, but some people, and I`m sure you know is, hear criticism and understand it as anti-Semitic when it is critical of Israel.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister of Israel said that what`s happening on America`s college campuses is horrific. He said the protesters are anti-Semitic mobs. And he compared it to what`s happening in German universities back in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond to that, or the perception of that, perhaps among some of your constituents?\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LEE: Certainly. You know, his language is intentional. And it`s always been. But the one thing that I know is that \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has not been on a college campus in the United States. He has not talked to these students. He has not seen their encampments. He has not seen or heard the message that they`re delivering. And he doesn`t want to hear it, right? His number one goal is, you know, what he`s doing over there. And anything that - that harms or - or helps - or makes it harder for him to achieve that goal, he`s going to - he`s going to distract from.\par \par \uc2 But this idea that every criticism of Israel is anti-Semitic is dangerous. There is a fair critique that we must make of every government, of every war, of every appropriation. And when we say that students who are engaging in the time honored tradition of civil disobedience, of campus protest and action are inherently bad or inherently anti-Semitic, particularly as we see Jewish students standing in solidarity with Muslim students or Arab students and black students, when you`re on the ground, you see that all of those students have been working together or have been learning and educating together. And they are all in one voice crying for a difference in direction, particularly from \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LEE: So, I`m not shocked that he would want to cast them as evil, cast them as the wrong.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LEE: That`s been his M.O.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congresswoman, thank you for explaining what`s happening within your party and your position. We`ll have to leave it right there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re joined now by the founding director of the Chicago Project on Security and Threats, \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  Professor Robert Pape, who has new findings on the college protests over the Israel- \uc2 Hamas\uc2  War.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the program.\par \par \uc2 I understand you`ve been surveying about 5,000 students, 600 colleges and universities between December and January, because this stretches back to right after October 7th.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE (Professor of Political Science, \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 ): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What did you learn about what`s happening?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: The big thing we learned is that the feelings of fear on college campuses are more widespread and more intense than we have known. Overall, 56 percent of Jewish students report feeling in personal danger. Close behind, 52 percent of Muslim students report feeling in personal danger. And 16 percent of all students who are not Jewish and not Muslim, this is 2 to 3 million college students, also report feeling in personal danger.\par \par \uc2 We also asked them in text boxes why. And over 1,000 students told us why. And what they`re reporting, Margaret, is not TikTok. What they are reporting is observing acts of physical violence and intimidation right I front of them. Jewish students are seeing Jewish buildings attacked. Muslim students are seeing people counter back - counterattack against Muslims. The students not Jewish and not Muslim, they`re just seeing everyone getting attacked. Also, protest chants are playing a role.\par \par \uc2 So, what we are seeing in general is that this is not fading away. This is a direct function of the escalation in the Middle Eastern conflict over there. And this is having spillover effects on our campuses and, of course, in some cities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the speaker of the House, Mike Johnson, went up to \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  this week. You had four Democratic lawmakers, all Jewish, who went, they said, in solidarity with Jewish students there and threatened to withhold federal funding. Is that kind of political involvement helpful or is it putting fuel on the fire?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: It is putting fuel on the fire because it`s so one-sided. If Speaker Johnson had gone together with \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  with an actual plan to bring calm - and we`ll talk about what that might look like - then this is a different story. But by come - making it a surprise, by making threats, you know, if you don`t do x we will fire y and we`re going to do x and this is all a surprise, this is actually not helpful for crowd control at all.\par \par \uc2 So, I also study the actual violent part of this, and the number one thing when you have protests and that are building is, don`t do a surprise. In Portland, during the George Floyd protests, you might remember, DHS surprised Portland. That escalated things. This is a very bad idea.\par \par \uc2 So, you need to have a more thoughtful approach to calming tensions that go beyond crowd control and that are not simply one side is right, everybody else is wrong, and we`re going to hammer it through until we win.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, who is that first line of action coming from? Is it university presidents or are you talking about law enforcement?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: There are roles here for university presidents, law enforcement and national leaders. And, actually, talking to law enforcement here directly, in folks who do this in Chicago, law enforcement is actually much more sophisticated, I think, than you might see from the national (INAUDIBLE). So, that`s why I think it`s so important to focus on the solutions from the perspective of our university leaders, our national leaders, because they are only episodically involved with dealing with violence and political violence. And that`s really why it`s so important to focus on what our university leaders should be doing and our national leaders beyond simply crowd control.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It sounds like you`re saying people have forgotten how to have a civil conversation about a heated issue.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: Well, they`ve -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And isn`t that what university is supposed to be about?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: They are. But know - October 7th caught all of us by surprise. And it caught universities by surprise. So, we are simply - we have policies. We have policies at (ph) the Calvin Report (ph) at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 . These were many of the campus policies and our practices developed in the 1960s. And almost no leader who was running either our government or a university was involved in the 1960s. And the issues we`re dealing with today are new. They`re not completely new, but I would say like 90 degrees new. And that is what we have to come to grips with and not just assume everything was fine before.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: So, they will just fade away and they will be fine again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s a lot of focus on the physical protests right now, but going back to October, you had those three Palestinian boys shot in Burlington, Vermont. You had congressional hearings grilling college university leaders for not reigning in the language used on campus. I mean this has been going on for six months now. Does it stop when the school year ends?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: I don`t think we can count on that for several reasons. Number one, we have graduation season to get through. Number two, many of our colleges now have summer programs that are quite extensive. So, for many colleges and universities, things don`t completely come to a halt during the summer. But also next fall, fall is all - not that far away. And this could also happen again. It could happen in - in the middle of our election season as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You betcha (ph).\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: So, there are many, many reasons, the Chicago convention, there are many reasons why we should take calming steps now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: And again, not just keep assuming, oh, yes, everything`s fine. We just need to get through the next day or two or the next week or two and then things will just fade away. I think that was an assumption by many quite important and credible leaders.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: That assumption now needs to be pushed aside.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Professor, thank you for your insights.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PAPE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to take a closer look at the growing humanitarian crisis when it comes to the world`s children. Catherine Russell is the executive director of \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 , and she is just back from Israel and the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL (Executive Director, \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 ): Thanks, Margaret. Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you met with some of the families of Israeli children who are still being held hostage. There`s a one-year-old. There`s a four-year-old.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you met with Israeli officials about access for aid going into Gaza. Did you get any good answers?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Well, we got some good answers, which is that I think a growing awareness of how critical it was for us to get more aid in, and specifically what we needed, more access points, better security. You know, I`m certainly not the only person who made those arguments and advocated with the government of Israel.\par \par \uc2 But we have seen a little bit of an improvement in Gaza, so that`s a positive that came from it. Obviously, the needs still far, far outweigh what we`re able to do, but at least we`ve seen some progress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You also went to the Palestinian West Bank.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: I did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And 2023 was the deadliest year for children in the West Bank since they began monitoring at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  in 2005. With all of the attention on Gaza, tell me what`s happening to kids in the West Bank right now.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes. Well, it`s - you know, the first day I met with, as you said, the hostage families. The second day I was in the West Bank and heard there about the challenges children face every day just trying to get to school, the barriers they face, the difficulty in sort of moving around, the checkpoints, all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes. And as you said, there is an increase in violence. And I think just in this year alone there have been 40 children, Palestinian children, killed, two Israeli children killed. There`s just an uptick in violence. And it`s, as always, children who are really impacted by that.\par \par \uc2 You also mentioned, you know, I met with the hostage families. I mean they`re devastated by what`s going on. Some children still being held. Others who are traumatized either by being held themselves or by seeing family members killed and hurt.\par \par \uc2 And I think I would just say that overall I came back feeling like there`s just so much pain and misery everywhere you look. And it`s really unfortunate to see it. And just praying for a better day for everyone in the situation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says every ten minutes a child is killed or wounded in Gaza. I mean that just hits you in the chest when you hear that.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes, it`s a shocking number. And it is what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s horrific.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the U.S. called this week for an investigation of this mass grave that was just found in Gaza of hundreds of people. Were there children there? What do you know?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes, we don`t - \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  doesn`t know much about that, honestly. I think that - I would say a couple things. One, there needs to be a full investigation of what happened. And I think that at some point will certainly, I hope, take place.\par \par \uc2 And second, to me, it argues again for the importance of having international press in Gaza. I say this over and over again because you have disputes about what has actually happened, what`s going on. And I think not - you know, look, there are incredibly brave Palestinian reporters and press people there who are doing an incredible job. Many of them have suffered so much. People have been killed. I think having the international press in there is also really important, and I hope that that gets - that that changes sometime soon and international press folks can get in, really make their own assessments of what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And if the Israeli government allows for it -\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I think a lot of news organizations would absolutely go in.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: And they should be there. They should be seeing what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The U.S. military, as you know, is setting up this port that`s supposed to be opened sometime in May. We had \uc2 Doctors Without Borders\uc2  chief on recently.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And she said to me, "There`s a slow motion massacre of people suffering from deprivation of food and water for six months in time."\par \par \uc2 You can`t drop lentils from the sky is what she said.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You need a mass medical response. Is there any plan in place to do this, to revive some of these children who are starving to death?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And will it come before there is an invasion of Rafah?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Well, let me say a couple things. One, the humanitarian situation, especially for children, is incredibly worrying everywhere. We know in the north where it`s been very difficult for humanitarians to get in with food, and as you`re pointing out, children in particular need what we talk about as therapeutic feeding, right? So they need plumpy nut or sort of interventions by medical professionals. So, it`s not, as you say, just dropping in food. That`s not enough. We need to do more for little children.\par \par \uc2 It is definitely not anywhere near where it needs to be. I think that the, you know, the idea of the port, the idea of air drops, you know, all - look we support it`s getting much - getting as much aid in as we possibly can. But the truth is, the most important thing is getting more and more in through road access. That`s the best access. It`s the safest access. It`s the best for humanitarians and it`s the easiest way for us to actually get around.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, it`s very difficult to do that for so many reasons, not least of which the roads are all torn up, right? We have security issues. So, it`s a challenging - it`s a challenging sort of array of problems.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: And I think we need multiple ways to try to respond to it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Please tell me what is happening in Sudan, which is the biggest displacement crisis in the world for children right now.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes, Sudan is terrible for children. It is, as you say, the largest displacement. Millions of children are on the move. Almost no children are in school. I think 90 percent of children are out of school. There`s incredible violence, including sexual violence, that children are suffering and witnessing. Children are suffering from malnutrition. It`s absolutely a devastating situation and we all need to pay more attention to it for sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And these are the next generations that we are talking about.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: You know what, Margaret, that`s the thing about working with children, right? It`s like, on the one hand they`re so vulnerable to everything that happens to them. And, you know, you mentioned Rafah. I mean if there`s an incursion into Rafah, that`s unimaginable to imagine what the violence will be like and what - how they will suffer.\par \par \uc2 But whether it`s in Gaza or in Sudan or Haiti or so many other places, children are suffering so much. And we all talk about children of the future. Well, OK, then we need to protect them. We need to make sure that they are educated and healthy and have some decent future ahead. And that`s critical for them and it`s critical for their countries and it`s critical for all of humanity.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Catherine Russell, thank you for your insights today.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020240428ek4s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for April 24, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford, Nancy Chen, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 3,472 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Your Holiness, what an honor.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, our exclusive in-depth conversation with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  at the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: His message for the world as he pleas for peace ahead of the church`s first World Children`s Day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Can you help negotiate peace?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and welcome to this special edition of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS from Rome. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 I`m Norah O`Donnell overlooking the Vatican, where, just a few hours ago, we interviewed the Holy Father, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 . The head of the Catholic Church shares his insight on the state of the world in his first extensive one-on-one interview with a U.S. television network.\par \par \uc2 His Holiness spoke to us about war, peace, famine, and climate change, all with the world`s children in mind. Our sit-down comes ahead of the Vatican`s first World Children`s Day, with a message that all kids are -- quote -- "precious in God`s eyes."\par \par \uc2 We have been in Rome and Vatican City for a few days now speaking with people from around the world who`ve made the sacred pilgrimage to reconnect with their faith, pray at the Sistine Chapel, and have a chance to see \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , a reminder that people of all faiths turn to \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  for guidance on the world`s most difficult issues, making our conversation with His Holiness that much more important.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Oh, my goodness. Buongiorno, Your Holiness. What an honor. So wonderful to meet you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (Leader of Catholic Church): -- to meet you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Love to meet you, yes.\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Thank you so much for doing this.\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voiceover): We met \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  today at Casa Santa Marta, the guest house where the pontiff lives, instead of the lavish papal palace.\par \par \uc2 At 87 years old, he may have trouble walking, but he had no difficulty sitting down with us for an hour-long conversation.\par \par \uc2 How`s your health?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): My health is good.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In nearly every address --\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- the pope often calls for peace. He condemned \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` October 7th attack, but has also called on Israel to use restraint.\par \par \uc2 There are now pictures of starving children coming out of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What about those that call that a genocide?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): Genocide. Every afternoon at 7:00 p.m., I call Gaza to the parish. There are about 600 people there, and they tell me what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 It`s very hard, very, very hard. Food goes in, but they have to fight for it. It`s very hard.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: I know you call for peace. You have called for a cease- fire in many of your sermons. Can you help negotiate peace?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): I can pray. I do. I pray a lot.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: During World Children`s Day, which is the end of May, the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says over a million people will be facing famine in Gaza, many of them children.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What can be done?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): Not only Gaza. We should think about Ukraine. Those kids don`t know how to smile. I tell them something, but they forgot how to smile. And this is very hard when a child forgets to smile. That`s really very serious, very serious.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Do you have a message for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  when it comes to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): Please, countries at war, all of them, stop the war. Look to negotiate. Look for peace. A negotiated peace is better than a war without end.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd cheering)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: His Most Holy Father is considered one of the most influential people in the world, leading nearly 1.4 billion Catholics. He is the first pope from the Americas, the first Jesuit, and will host the Vatican`s first World Children`s Day next month.\par \par \uc2 I love that you take the kids in the Popemobile with you and bring them up to the Apostolic Palace when you say a prayer. Why do you do that?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): Children always bear a message. They bear a message, and it is a way for us to have a younger heart.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Francis is also the first pontiff to make climate change a priority. In 2015, to make an important point, images of the environment were projected onto St. Peter`s Basilica, the dome designed in the mid-16th century by Michelangelo.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to the deniers of climate change?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): There are people who are foolish and foolish, even if you show them research, they don`t believe it. Why? Because they don`t understand the situation or because of their interests, but climate change exists.\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: His progressive style has made him popular worldwide, with millions coming here to St. Peter`s Square to catch a glimpse of the Holy Father.\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MARY BENNETT (Visitor From Little Rock, Arkansas): It has made us emotional just being here, going into St. Peter`s Basilica, going into the Sistine Chapel. It -- just tears coming into our eyes, because we`re actually -- you can feel the spirit.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sophia (sp?) and Aaron (sp?) came with their family from San Diego, California.\par \par \uc2 How did it make you feel when he came out of the window?\par \par \uc2 SOPHIA (Visitor From San Diego, California): It made me feel good, like butterflies in my stomach.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE singing)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Still, the number of Catholics in America is dwindling. In the U.S., only 20 percent of adults identify as Catholic, and that`s down from 24 percent in 2007.\par \par \uc2 I wonder, if you could, speak to those who don`t go to mass anymore, or maybe don`t see a place for themselves in the Catholic Church.\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (through translator): I would say there is always a place, always. If, in this parish, the priest doesn`t seem welcoming, I understand, but go and look. There is always a place. Do not run away from the church. The church is very big.\par \par \uc2 It`s more than a temple. It`s more. You shouldn`t run away from it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We also asked the pope about the migrant crisis, gay rights, women`s role in the church, and whether he`s thinking about retirement.\par \par \uc2 We will have that and much more on "60 Minutes" on Sunday, May 19, and then a prime-time special on Monday, May 20, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For our interview with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , we also wanted to take a pulse of the Catholic Church back home in the U.S. So we asked Catholics in cities across the country about their relationship with the church, how they feel about this pope, and what message they want to hear from him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from New York City)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I would describe the pope as transformative. He`s a one-of-a-kind papacy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Seattle)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I think he has done a great job talking about the marginalized, the poor, the care for the environment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Miami)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We support him. He is our guide.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voiceover): Like the church he leads, Americans` opinions of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  are diverse.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from New York City)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: He`s got some things that I agree with, some things maybe I don`t agree with.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Seattle)\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: We always need popes that challenge our way of thinking, and I think he`s doing that.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: While a majority of American Catholics view Francis favorably, many we spoke with thought the church needed to evolve; 83 percent of U.S. Catholics want the church to allow the use of contraception, and more than half think the church should recognize gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 (Clips from Seattle)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: I think they do need to bring women into the church.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: They need to ordain women as priests, and they need to allow priests to marry.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Denver)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Everything changes. And, sometimes, I think the church needs to change too.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: A recent survey found more Americans were leaving the Catholic Church at higher rates than any other religion.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from New York City)\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: I feel like I can just be spiritual anywhere. You know, I don`t really necessarily have to go to a church to -- you know, and I feel like that`s the old-school way of thinking.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Denver)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: I think there`s been a lot of bad news about the Catholics, the religion, about the priests in general. And I think people are -- have turned around and walked away from that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Dallas)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #5: We have a lot of young people that grew up in the Catholic Church. But they don`t feel that they`re getting the message.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from New York City)\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: They feel they`re not accepted. They feel they might be ostracized. They feel that, like -- that, like, they don`t belong to the church.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: We spoke with Patrick Egan (sp?), who left the Catholic Church as a teenager, only to come back in search of a community. He`s now a leader at a local parish.\par \par \uc2 Does the Catholic Church feel different under \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK EGAN (Catholic Parish Leader): Yes. Francis has really tried to open the door to participation by people who are -- have been traditionally outside of the hierarchy.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: He blames strict adherence to ideology for the decline, but says that can change.\par \par \uc2 What do you think it would take for the Catholic Church to be able to reconnect with Americans?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK EGAN: They need to meet Americans where they are. We are a very secular society. And so, if the church can figure out a way to help people understand how a spiritual journey and a faith community can really make one`s life more rich and meaningful, that I think is also part of the story.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Nikki Battiste joins us now.\par \par \uc2 And, Nikki, so many of the people we spoke with here thought that \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  has been much more incluses -- inclusive. What did you learn?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The feeling is very much the same here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  has made a concerted effort to soften the message of the Catholic Church. It`s an approach that has angered some of the more conservative followers. But it`s part of the pope`s plan to make all feel welcome -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 We also asked him about those conservative bishops. You will hear about that later.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to tonight`s other top story. Clashes between police and pro- Palestinian demonstrators are intensifying. Protests at \uc2 Columbia University in New York\uc2  are now in their eighth day. And, today, the speaker of the House was on campus calling on the school`s president to restore order.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Chen has more on the growing nationwide tensions.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #1: Let him go!\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voiceover): This was the scene at the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2  after school security tried to stop students from setting up a protest encampment.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of demonstrators surrounded a patrol car after campus police detained one student who was later released.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Free, free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: At the \uc2 University of Texas, Austin\uc2 , police in riot gear and officers on horseback confronted about 200 students participating in a pro- Palestinian rally.\par \par \uc2 On the East Coast, demonstrators set up an encampment on Harvard Yard in solidarity with student protesters at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , where House Speaker Mike Johnson spoke late today and was met with both cheers and boos.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): I`m here today joining my colleagues in calling on President Shafik to resign if she cannot immediately bring order to this chaos.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #2: I`m just standing on the sidewalk!\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: This is day eight of the protests at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . The school said it is making important progress with protesters, but has given students a deadline to dismantle their tents.\par \par \uc2 The protesters say they will not leave until the school agrees to cut its business ties to Israel.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: We need the administrators of these universities to get control of the situation. And if they can`t, then we need the National Guard, the law enforcement, or someone to come in here and take control.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Are you calling for the National Guard to come here?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: Desperate times call for desperate measures.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: For now, New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  says she has no plans to send in the Guard.\par \par \uc2 Columbia announced today that, because of the unrest, there will be a remote option for upcoming final exams. Graduation is set to take place on campus in three weeks, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Chen, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to the Supreme Court and a case that could have wide-ranging implications for women`s health care nationwide. At issue is the conflict between Idaho`s near-total abortion ban and a federal law that requires hospitals to provide emergency medical treatment.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford was there for the oral arguments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): The justices were sharply divided over Biden administration arguments that federal law, which requires hospitals to provide necessary stabilizing treatment, including abortions, overrides Idaho`s near-total ban, which permits abortion to save a mother`s life, but not when her health is at risk.\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice): Idaho law says the doctor has to determine not that there`s merely a serious medical condition, but that the person will die.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Liberal justices provided real-life examples of women who have been denied abortions in states with restrictive laws.\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice): Her life is not in peril, but she`s going to lose her reproductive organs. She`s going to lose the ability to have children in the future, unless an abortion takes place.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But some conservative justices noted that, in Idaho, doctors can perform some abortions if they believe the woman is in danger of dying.\par \par \uc2 Raul Labrador\uc2  is Idaho`s attorney general.\par \par \uc2 RAUL LABRADOR\uc2  (R-Idaho Attorney General): They continue to say that this is putting women`s lives in jeopardy, and it is not.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Idaho officials say the Biden administration is trying to find a backdoor way to broaden abortion access.\par \par \uc2 ATTORNEY GENERAL RAUL LABRADOR: There will be more abortions happening in -- in hospitals because they will create an exception that will allow any type of abortion to happen in the state of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But Stacy Seyb a high-risk pregnancy doctor in Idaho, says physicians are now afraid to perform abortions even if the state law may allow it, over fears they could be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB (High-Risk Pregnancy Doctor): We need to be able to help take care of people at this point.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And, you know, you could face criminal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB: Yes, yes, and jail time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And in the political world today, another state with a near- total ban, Arizona, is now poised to repeal it after three Republicans joined with Democrats in the Arizona House.\par \par \uc2 That will go to the Arizona Senate, where there`s support for the repeal, and then to the governor, who`s expected to sign it. That would mean that, in Arizona, abortions again would be permitted up to 15 weeks -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s a big development.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed a bipartisan national security package into law today. The measure forces TikTok`s China-based parent company to sell the video-sharing app or be based in the U.S. That provision is expected to face legal challenges and disrupt the income of content creators. There are 170 million TikTok users in the U.S. alone.\par \par \uc2 The law also provides $95 billion in long-delayed aid to Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 There are new federal rules for airlines on everything from cash refunds to stopping hidden fees. What consumers need to know -- next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We have a consumer alert tonight about new rules aimed at protecting millions of Americans who fly.\par \par \uc2 One rule will require airlines to automatically provide refunds for things like canceled and delayed flights, delayed baggage, returns and failed in- flight Wi-Fi. The Department of Transportation will also require airlines to be more transparent about extra fees, clearly explaining all additional charges.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we`re also taking you inside the iconic St. Peter`s Basilica for a rare private tour.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, you can listen, subscribe wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Back here in Rome overlooking the Vatican and St. Peter`s Square and the magnificent basilica, the largest church in the world and the smallest nation in the world.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, we`re taking you on a private tour.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voiceover): The beauty by day doesn`t hold a candle to its nighttime majesty.\par \par \uc2 How is it that we`re the only ones here?\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO (Director of Communication, St. Peter`s Basilica): Just for you, for \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is -- this is a very important moment for, I think, English American television.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, ahead of a very important time, for World Children`s Day.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Yes, for me, it`s --\par \par \uc2 (FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: -- the great moment. Is right?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, it`s absolutely --\par \par \uc2 Father Enzo Fortunato tells us, 100,000 children will come here. He`s organizing the Vatican`s first World Children`s Day.\par \par \uc2 We visited one of the Vatican`s most popular treasures, Michelangelo`s Pieta, which shows the mother Mary holding Jesus.\par \par \uc2 Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 Father Fortunato took us where no one gets to go, up close to the masterpiece.\par \par \uc2 This is 500 years old. It`s going to make me cry.\par \par \uc2 (FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: "He always says it`s the women who save the world."\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Oh, thank you for saying that.\par \par \uc2 Why is St. Peter`s Basilica so special?\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Because it`s a house of the pope, but, above all, it`s the -- inside the tomb of St. Peter.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: One of the holiest treasures inside one of Christianity`s holiest sites.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we will have some final thoughts from our exclusive interview with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight, a word about our first-of-its-kind interview with the Holy Father. Historic. And this is something I have dreamt about my whole career at CBS News.\par \par \uc2 It took an entire team of dedicated journalists to make this happen. Today, the pope told us that he hopes his legacy is to welcome everyone.\par \par \uc2 And as one longtime follower told us, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  may not change the lyrics of the Catholic Church, but he is trying to change the tune.\par \par \uc2 You can see much more of our interview in May on "60 Minutes" and in a prime-time special.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell in Rome.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240425ek4o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Clashes at Columbia; TikTok on the Clock; Time Trouble Refunds; Easier to Quit; SCOTUS Abortion Hearing; Catch and Kill Testimony; DOJ Settles with Nassar Victims; Bird Flu Found in Milk; Pig Kidney Transplants; Anything for Love; Caitlin Clark Ties of \uc2 Nike\uc2  Deal; Lennon`s Long Lost Guitar; Playing Fetch with The Thermanator; Turks and Caicos Travel Warning; Sandwich Generation Squeeze; Blake Shelton`s Life Off Stage; Deesha Dyer`s Unexpected Journey; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Natalie Morales, Nancy Chen, Scott  MacFarlane, Kris Van Cleave, Jo Ling Kent, Jan Crawford, Robert Costa, Jim  Axelrod, Vladimir Duthiers, Lisa Ling, Drew Barrymore, Ross Mathews, Cassie  DiLaura \par \uc2 14,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets and welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And I`m Natalie Morales.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, but you see we`re in good hands. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 More demonstrations over Israel`s war in Gaza, but protesters at Columbia say they want amid allegations of antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Our first demand is complete divestment from anything related to Israeli settler colonialism, apartheid, and genocide.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tough testimony for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from his longtime friend, David Pecker, the former publisher of the "National Enquirer," how it could help the prosecution`s criminal case.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I am not allowed to talk, but people are allowed to talk about me.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: First on CBS MORNINGS, we talked to the first woman ever to receive a pig kidney transplant about her experience.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: How sick were you?\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO (Patient): I was pretty much done.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A photographer who worked for Megan Thee Stallion is suing the rap star claiming, he was forced to watch her have sex with another woman.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Clippers and \uc2 Dallas Mavericks\uc2  game two winds up.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #2: Luca Doncic is lethal.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #3: Dallas gets the win. That series is now tied at one game each.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #4: It goes. He is the guy.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER ("The Daily Show"): The second day of Trump`s hush money trial, or they could even get into that they had to hold a separate hearing to find out if Trump violated a gag order. Basically, the prosecution`s argument is that a juror has the right to feel safe while serving on Donald Trump`s jury, while Donald Trump`s argument is that that juror lives at 34 West 52nd Street and maybe someone should pay that juror a visit and straighten him out.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually think would be a very interesting case to be on, but I wouldn`t want anybody to know--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- I was on there.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Very tough to be on there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would not want to be on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know nothing about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Count me out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. To be continued.\par \par \uc2 We are going to begin though with the tensions across this country over Israel`s war in Gaza that stemmed from that October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack. We continue to see the demonstrations in multiple cities now including here at \uc2 Columbia University in New York City\uc2  where the school says students have 48 hours to remove an encampment.\par \par \uc2 In Brooklyn, hundreds of Jewish people were arrested near the home of Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , after holding a Passover Seder, which doubled is a call for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen is at Columbia for us this morning to bring us the latest.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Gayle, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 There were protests overnight here on campus, but it is overall calm right now as conversations with students continue. A representative from \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  tells CBS News there has been some progress made with pro- Palestinian demonstrators, but tensions remain high.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Outside of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 `s campus, demonstrators continue to rally.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: After the school`s administration warned students on campus to dismantle their camps or face consequences, some are seen breaking them down while others remain defiant.\par \par \uc2 From coast-to coast, California to Massachusetts, demonstrations expressing anger over Israel`s bombardment of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 NYU\uc2 , plywood barricades were erected yesterday around a campus plaza.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: After unruly demonstrations Monday night, 120 people were arrested. New York City Mayor Eric Adams blamed the unrest on outside agitators.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D) (New York City): People who peacefully protest for an issue, they are not throwing bottles and chairs.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Earlier in the day at Columbia, protesters reiterated their demands, including an immediate ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in demonstration)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And one of the most crucial components, cutting off any financial interests connected to Israel.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Our first demand is complete divestment from anything related to Israeli settler colonialism, apartheid and genocide.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The words "apartheid" and "genocide" are loaded, but the idea of divestment is a refrain echoed over and over by protesters we spoke to on campus.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Financial divestment, financial transparency --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Financial divestment from any companies that profit off of Israeli apartheid.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: According to Kenneth Stern, who is studying pro-Palestinian campus protests for decades, calls for divestment in Israel have gone nowhere on campuses for more than 20 years, because the issues in Israel are not politically simple.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH STERN ("The Conflict Over the Conflict" author: The campus has decided to divest from investments in Israel or companies that do business with Israel. And part of the reason is that unlike some things that have gotten them to divest, whether it is tobacco, stocks, or things related to climate, this is a political debate where people have strongly divergent views.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And on top of that, divestment isn`t practical.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH STERN: I`ll be very difficult because there are so many companies that deal with Israel. There`s so many mutual funds and other things, it would be very difficult to segregate.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But the practicality may not be the point. The point may just be the protest.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And Speaker of the House Mike Johnson is expected to visit Columbia`s campus later today to meet with Jewish students as we now enter day eight of protests here -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very dicey situation. Thank you very much, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here`s something for you, words turning into action in Washington. Some big changes there that could have an effect on millions of Americans. First tucked into that foreign aid bill that passed the Senate last night, a potential ban on the social media site TikTok, also new rules for airlines from the White House that could save you money when you fly, and finally, the government is deciding an important question for some workers, should your employer be able to stop you from going to a competitor?\par \par \uc2 We`re going to break down each of these stories with our team of correspondents starting with Scott MacFarlane who has got the latest on that possible ban on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 Scott, what do we know?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tony, TikTok is now on the clock. They have a year to be sold or divested by their China-based owner or face the prospect of abandoning the United States within that year, shutting down access to 170 million American users and millions of small businesses.\par \par \uc2 In a Congress that seemingly can`t agree on anything, this passed with overwhelming bipartisan support amid concerns China could use TikTok to exploit and access private Americans` information.\par \par \uc2 One lawmaker called it a spy balloon in your phone, another said it`s a possible tool for propaganda, a big issue as we enter an election season.\par \par \uc2 TikTok has indicated they will challenge this in the courts and file a lawsuit. They argued this is an infringement of First Amendment rights, but Tony, President Biden is expected to sign this bill into law today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Promising, Scott. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are going to turn now to our next story out of Washington. Kris Van Cleave looking at those new rules for airlines that could save you some money.\par \par \uc2 Kris, Chris, that sounds like good news, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The next time you run into travel troubles, you could be eligible for a cash refund. These new Department of Transportation rules that will be going into effect say you can get a refund for a canceled flight, but if you`re delayed more than three hours for a domestic flight or more than six hours internationally, you can also get a refund if you choose not to fly.\par \par \uc2 Also bag fees must be refunded if a checked bag is significantly delayed, and refunds must be given if any services you paid for aren`t provided like broken Wi-Fi.\par \par \uc2 Airlines are also required now to disclose what fees they charge upfront for things like a checked bag, a carry-on bag, a cancel or a change fee. Now most airlines in the US already do that. These new rules will be phased in over the next 12 months --Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Broken Wi-Fi, that`s the fine details. Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now if you signed a contract with your employer, parts of it could be null and void under some new rules that Jo Ling Kent is going to explain for us. It is a change that could affect millions of workers.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning to you. What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Hey, Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  wants to make it easier for you to get a new job. It plans on banning non-compete clauses and those are parts of employer contracts that bar workers across industries from leaving for a competitor or starting a competing business, as some say non-competes help employers protect their trade secrets and allow them to invest in training for employees without fear of losing them, while others argue the clauses are overly restrictive on the workers.\par \par \uc2 The FTC says its ban would translate to 524 more dollars each year for the average worker, yield as many as 29,000 more patents and create at least 8,500 new businesses, but of course this is not set in stone yet. The \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  called the ban an unlawful power grab and said it would sue the FTC to block it -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be following it. Jo Ling, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Well, this morning the Supreme Court here`s a high profile case over abortion access. The case from Idaho could affect laws in other states. It`s the first time the justices are reviewing a state restriction on abortion since they overturned the Roe vs. Wade ruling almost two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court for us.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning. So you know the Biden administration is challenging Idaho`s pretty strict restrictions with a really creative legal argument. They`re saying that that state law which bans almost all abortions actually conflicts with an existing federal law that requires hospitals to provide emergency care for pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Two years after the Supreme Court sent the issue of abortion back to the states, the justices will take up a near total ban in Idaho that some doctors there say is putting pregnant women at risk.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB (OBGYN): It feels like everything has gotten so extreme.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Dr. Stacy Seyb is one of only five high risk pregnancy doctors left in Idaho. He says the law is compromising care because doctors are afraid to perform abortions, even in cases where the state laws seemingly would allow it.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB: Pregnancy is really complex and there are things that can go wrong. We need to be able to help take care of people at this point.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And you know, you could face criminal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB: Yes, yes and jail time.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Biden administration sued to block Idaho`s law, which bans almost all abortions with narrow exceptions to save a pregnant woman`s life or in cases of rape or incest, arguing it conflicts with a federal law that requires hospitals to provide necessary stabilizing treatment for pregnant women, treatment that may include abortions.\par \par \uc2 RAUL LABRADOR\uc2  (Idaho attorney general): They continue to say that this is putting women`s lives in jeopardy, and it is not.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Idaho Attorney General \uc2 Raul Labrador\uc2  says the state`s law already allows an abortion if the woman`s life is at risk and accuses the Biden administration of trying to find a backdoor way to broaden abortion access in states like Idaho.\par \par \uc2 RAUL LABRADOR\uc2 : There will be more abortions happening in hospitals, because they will create an exception that will allow any type of abortion to happen in the state of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, this case could have far-reaching implications. A ruling for example that blocked Idaho`s law could undermine abortion restrictions in more than a dozen states -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Jan.\par \par \uc2 Now to revealing testimony in former President Donald Trump`s criminal trial from former "National Enquirer" publisher, David Pecker.\par \par \uc2 As Robert Costa reports, Pecker`s testimony could -- could -- spell trouble for the former president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made his frustration known leaving court.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : It is a very unfair situation.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: After hours of testimony by the former "National Enquirer" publisher, David Pecker, who described his strategy ahead of the 2016 presidential election to buy stories and kill them to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , while promoting fake stories about his political opponents, including Senators Ted Cruz and \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors zeroed in on an August 2015 meeting at Trump Tower, where Packer says he met with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his former fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  two months after Trump announced he was running for president.\par \par \uc2 At the meeting, Pecker said Trump and Cohen asked him how he could help the campaign and Pecker told them he would act as the eyes and ears for any negative stories on Trump.\par \par \uc2 The catch and kill plan was the origin of Cohen`s $130,000.00 payment to former adult film star, Stormy Daniels according to prosecutors, to keep her quiet about an alleged sexual encounter with Trump, who denies it all.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: The most damaging part of David Pecker`s testimony so far is that he actually had a meeting with \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that this catch and kill scheme would go forward to affect the results of the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pecker described other stories he bought in never published including a $30,000.00 payment to Trump`s doorman for a false story, as well as paying "Playboy" model Karen McDougal, $150,000.00 who claimed to have had a 10-month long affair with Trump in 2006, which he denies.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: By the time we get to Stormy Daniels, that is really story number three in this catch and kill scheme, and the prosecution is talking about a conspiracy to violate the 2016 election by keeping information from the voting public.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The New York trial will resume Thursday and David Pecker will be back on the stand. That same day here in Washington, the Supreme Court will hear arguments in Trump`s presidential immunity case that could have a big impact and whether he can be prosecuted in the federal January 6 case - - Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be following it.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We want to tell you now about a major update on the lawsuit against the \uc2 US government\uc2  by survivors of abuse by former \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2  doctor, Larry Nasser.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice has agreed to a settlement of $139 million, all stemming from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s mishandling of this investigation into Nasser`s sexual abuse. It involves 139 claims including from Olympic stars like Simone Biles, McKayla Maroney and Aly Raisman.\par \par \uc2 A watchdog report had found that agents failed to follow up on the allegations for over a year allowing Nasser to continue to prey on athletes. He is expected to spend the rest of his life behind bars.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has announced a potentially troubling discovery. Traces of bird flu in some samples of pasteurized grocery store milk. Health officials say they think it is unlikely the particles could infect people, but they`re conducting more tests just to be safe.\par \par \uc2 This could mean bird flu is more widespread than previously known in US cows, but they say the milk we buy is still safe to drink.\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up, a woman makes history with experimental surgery including a transplant from a very unlikely donor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jon LaPook has the story. But first, it is 7:15 and time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, new details about an American family`s legal nightmare during a Caribbean vacation. What the State Department is telling tourists.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our new series on the "Cost of Caregiving" focuses on the so-called sandwich generation. We`ll look at the challenges of caring for older parents who are raising young kids.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  is back with another installment of our series "Anything For Love" on the rise of romance scams, Jim.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Vlad, you remember yesterday, we brought you the story of Laura Kowal. Remember she was that mother in Illinois who found what she thought was love online only to be robbed of her life savings.\par \par \uc2 Today, we look at whether dating sites do enough to protect users and even ask one CEO if they invest enough in trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by booking.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: This morning we look at an experimental procedure that is giving hope to people who need organ transplants.\par \par \uc2 More than 89,000 Americans are waiting for a donor kidney. So far this year only 6,900 have gotten one; just over 27,000 transplants were performed last year.\par \par \uc2 Well, 12 days ago, a New Jersey patient became the first woman and the second living human to receive a kidney transplant from a pig.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook shows how a strategy never attempted before is giving her a second chance at life.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: When you came into the hospital, how sick were you?\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: I was pretty much done.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: At age 54, Lisa Pisano`s heart and kidneys were failing.\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: I couldn`t go up the stairs. I couldn`t drive. I couldn`t play with my grandkids. So when this opportunity came to me, I said, I am taking it.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY (Transplant Institute at NYU Langone Health): When we admitted her to the hospital, she had two cardiac arrests. So that`s how close she was to death.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Dr. Robert Montgomery heads up the Transplant Institute at NYU Langone Health.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 FDA\uc2  permission, he and his team decided the only way to save her life was with a first ever approach, giving her both a mechanical heart pump and a kidney transplant from a pig.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY: -- about to do something that no one has done.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The human immune system rejects organs from animals, but Dr. Montgomery and his team used a pig kidney with one gene altered to make it more compatible.\par \par \uc2 How did you know that the kidney was working?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY: So you release the clamps and Lisa`s blood goes into the kidney and it suddenly turns this beautiful pink color. It is a healthy organ. This isn`t just about keeping somebody alive, it is restoring them to their lives.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: How are you feeling today?\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: I feel great today, compared to other days.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: As she recovers from surgery, Pisano dreams of playing with her two young grandchildren for the first time in years.\par \par \uc2 What`s the scene look like? Draw it for me.\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: My granddaughter is, g`ma, g`ma, come on. Running around in the backyard. My grandson is getting into anything and everything you can get into, you know, trying to do it all, and it`s a wonderful scene.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Wow. Dr. LaPook, you`re a professor of Medicine at NYU Langone, let`s start off.\par \par \uc2 I mean, she looks great right now. What is the long-term prognosis for? Is this viewed as a permanent fix?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Well, fingers crossed. I mean, she was very sick.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You heard, she had a couple of cardiac arrests when she came in. This was her last option. So I spoke to Dr. Montgomery. He thinks she is doing very well right now of course.\par \par \uc2 You know, she is only the second person to get this last month. Nancy Chen reported about up at Harvard, they did the first male who got this, but you know, fingers crossed, in Medicine, you`re just always going step by step. This was used under \uc2 FDA\uc2  Compassionate Use Protocol, so it is not approved yet. There`s ways to go but you know, what an amazing, amazing technological tour de force.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so great that it goes from I`m pretty much done to, g`ma, g`ma, come and play.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And about a hundred people at NYU Langone were involved in this care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Jon LaPook.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The team -- it is a team effort.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, more of our CBS News Investigation about romance scams.\par \par \uc2 Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It is 7:30.\par \par \uc2 Our CBS News Investigation, we are calling it "Anything For Love" about the wave of online romance scams turns now to the role of dating apps.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve reported that these romance scams are costing tens of thousands of Americans more than a billion dollars -- that`s with a B -- a year. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  tells us that scammers have used online dating apps to broaden their reach.\par \par \uc2 Chief investigative correspondent, \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  is back with that part of the story. I`m enjoying this series so much, Jim, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: So much heartbreak, so much heartbreak.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Three in 10 Americans say they have used a dating app at some point. In the case of Kelly Gowe`s mother, she believed it was the safest place to find love.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE (Lauren Kowal`s daughter): My mom was actually very specific about wanting to do online dating.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Online dating felt safer.\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : It`s ironic.\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: I know.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Ironic, because after Kelly Gowe`s mom, Laura Kowal found a partner on the dating site, match.com, she was scammed out of one-and-a- half million dollars by someone posing falsely online as Frank.\par \par \uc2 Did she show you her profile on match.com?\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: She never did. I thought she could navigate that very well and would never put herself in any position where she would get hurt.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Laura was like millions of Americans who have logged on to a dating app looking for love. But according to a Pew survey, more than half the people using dating apps believe they`ve been approached by a scam account. Take the profile picture of Frank who Laura met on match.com.\par \par \uc2 We tracked the stolen photo. It`s actually a doctor who lives in Chile.\par \par \uc2 How is it possible that romance scammers can create these fraudulent profiles on online dating sites?\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES (Garbo): I mean, it`s quite easy. They don`t want friction on these apps.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Kathryn Kosmides` nonprofit, Garbo, partnered in 2021 with match.com`s parent company to provide users with background checks. But after a leadership change at Match, the partnership broke down.\par \par \uc2 Kosmides says the entire industry shortchanges safety.\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES: So we`ve talked to every online dating platform out there from the largest to the smallest. They all have similar thoughts and feelings around trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And what are those feelings?\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES: That trust and safety is not generating revenue enough for them to invest in it.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Kosmides is one of several people who worked on security with Match, who told us dating apps don`t ask a lot of background questions of new users. The idea, she says is to keep signups easy and the numbers up for these subscription-based businesses that`s essential to boost revenue.\par \par \uc2 So they don`t do enough in your view to combat the fraudulent profiles that are being established.\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES: Correct.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : In 2019, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  filed a lawsuit alleging from 2013 to 2018, as many as 25 to 30 percent of match.com members who registered each day, were using match.com to perpetrate scams.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Match Group\uc2  told us: "We believe the FTC allegations have no merit."\par \par \uc2 Three former executives who were at Match as recently as last year, told us that fake accounts were a serious and persistent problem for overmatched and underfunded Trust and Safety Departments, the corporate offices that handle customer security.\par \par \uc2 Match told us it spends $125 million every year on trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 JAMES BARNACLE (\uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , Financial Crimes Section chief): Prior to 2017, we didn`t have a significant number of romance fraud complaints in the United States.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: What changed?\par \par \uc2 JAMES BARNACLE: The proliferation of the dating sites.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : James Barnacle is the head of the Financial Crimes section of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JAMES BARNACLE: We think it`s really important for the tech companies to step up and curtail the abuse of American citizens.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: After six months of asking for an interview.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kim, how are you?\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2  (\uc2 Match Group\uc2  chief executive officer): Hi. How are you?\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Good \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  from CBS News.\par \par \uc2 We finally approached \uc2 Bernard Kim\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 Match Group\uc2 , which operates at least a dozen dating platforms, including Hinge, OKCupid, and Tinder.\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2 : Look, we invest a tremendous amount of capital, incredible talent on trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: We talked to several former Match employees including former high profile executives. It`s funny you bring up trust and safety because they said that it never gets adequate resources.\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2 : I mean if you look at our investment over the last several years, we`ve only increased the number of talent to people, leadership that`s focused in this area. So it`s existential to our business to remain safe.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Match told us, they removed 96 percent of improper accounts within a day, and use both AI and human moderators to remove bad actors.\par \par \uc2 We tracked down a former romance scammer in Ghana, and shared with him what the Match CEO told us.\par \par \uc2 How hard was it for you to set up a fake profile on the sites like match.com?\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL (Former romance scammer in Ghana): or us, it is easy.\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2 : We`ve really shifted the way that we`ve addressed people that can come into our platforms. We`re working really, really hard every single day to make sure that people are authentic.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL: I think maybe he`s not aware of what is really going on, on match.com. So maybe he might think he has that security on match.com, but people have their way around it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: So what can law enforcement do to tackle this growing threat? We`ll have more on that on the "CBS Evening News" tomorrow and you can see the whole story all in one place on Sunday night in our streaming documentary, "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Reports."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, I appreciate Mr. Kim talking to you as opposed to jumping in the car and running away.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Very important point there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But then he stood there.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Most people run. He answered every question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did, and he didn`t stutter. But it does sound like that there is still a problem there.\par \par \uc2 So what can people do to protect -- what can we do to protect ourselves?\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Well, I know this is going to sound simple and basic, but if you`ve never met somebody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: You think you have, because you`ve established a relationship- -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: -- through your fingers, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Like by typing texts or e-mails. You don`t know that person. Don`t ever send them money. And if somebody suggests coming offline, off the dating platform and talking on the phone or texting or e-mailing, run.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Run. Nothing good will ever come to that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Jim, as you pointed out yesterday, they don`t just say, now, give me some money.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, you`ve engaged in a relationship and then they say, oh, I`m having some problems. Can you help me out?\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Because it`s a heart transaction. It`s not a head transaction.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Nobody says, oh, how could this happen? Heart overrules the head. I do want to say one thing though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: This entire series only happens because Kelly Gowe chooses to share her deeply personal painful story with us in hopes of preventing other people from living the trauma that she lives every day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She lost her mother in all of this.\par \par \uc2 The tension seems unresolvable. On the one hand, the dating sites want to bring in customers and make it easy for new signups. But on the other hand, that`s what allows the unsafe elements to come in.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And it`s a target rich environment for the scammers.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: It certainly is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It sure is. Jim, thank you very much. This is a really powerful series.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is very interesting.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Great reporting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kelly Gowe, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, a guitar is found in an attic after more than a half century. Why it could bring in nearly a million dollars at auction. Vlad is going to make a bid, I think.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, our new series "Cost of Caregiving" looks at the plight of the so-called Sandwich Generation. We are talking about Americans caring for aging parents while also raising children of their own.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, Lisa Ling is here. She`s been looking into this.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Thank you so much, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, about 80 million Americans are sandwich caregivers including my family. We will show you how my husband and I balance caring for his mother who is 92 and faces some health challenges, while also raising our two young daughters.\par \par \uc2 And you`ll meet Lauren Shin (sp?). She had both of her parents living with her young family until her mother`s condition was more than they could handle.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`ll look at the hard decisions they faced and the big role finances play in making care decisions for our older loved ones -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a lot of people, Lisa. Thank you very much.\par