ourists are trapped in a legal nightmare in a popular vacation destination.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNING. I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil and that`s Natalie Morales.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Lisa Ling looks at the challenges of the so-called Sandwich Generation. That`s people caring for aging parents and kids at the same time. That`s a lot. It`s part of a new series. We`re calling it the "Cost of Caregiving."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And country megastar, Blake Shelton takes us to Las Vegas to show us his new music venue there. He`s also getting personal in a conversation with Cassie DiLaura of "Entertainment Tonight."\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And our good friends, Drew Barrymore and Ross Mathews, they are here from "The Drew Barrymore Show" for a special "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to begin this hour with a warning for Americans traveling to the Caribbean. The territory of Turks and Caicos has enacted strict new laws with a mandatory minimum prison sentence of a dozen years for carrying guns or even merely ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Watson from Oklahoma is currently behind bars there and has a bail hearing today. The Oklahoma man was arrested at the airport when security found bullets in his luggage, something he says were in there by mistake.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Valerie Watson`s return to Oklahoma was emotional. The mother of two is home after 10 days in legal limbo, but without her husband, Ryan.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE WATSON (Ryan`s wife): Just to get to hold my babies.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: He is in jail in Turks and Caicos facing a potential mandatory minimum sentence of 12 years behind bars over four rounds of hunting ammo airport security found in his carry-on bag as the couple were set to travel home April 12th.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: This went from what was supposed to be a dream vacation to a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: We spoke with the Watsons Sunday before they learned Valerie would not be charged and could return home.\par \par \uc2 How do you forget that there are bullets in your bag?\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: The best of my knowledge, I have to assume that it happens while taking a trip to Texas to hunt deer.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Bryan Hagerich from Pennsylvania is awaiting trial after ammo was found in his checked bag in February.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN HAGERICH (From Pennsylvania): I subsequently spent eight nights in their local jail, some of the darkest, hardest times of my life, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Possessing ammunition is prohibited in Turks and Caicos. However, tourists were often able to pay a fine until a February court order mandating even tourists leaving the country are subjected to prison time. That includes at least eight Americans.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GRIM (In prison for ammunition): The judge`s sentencing was completely predicated on the fact that I was an American.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: That judge found Michael Grim from Indiana had exceptional circumstances when he pleaded guilty to accidentally having ammo in his checked bag last year, but sent them to this prison for more than five months anyway, hoping to send a message to other tourists.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GRIM: No clean, running water. You`re kind of exposed to the environment 24/7. Mosquitoes and tropical illnesses are a real concern.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Last September, the US Embassy warned check your luggage for stray ammunition because we will not be able to secure your release from custody.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: I just so desperately want to be back with my son and my daughter.\par \par \uc2 I want to hold them and let them know that this is just an honest mistake.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A mistake landing American tourists visiting an island paradise in a potential prison hell.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh man --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This story is eye opening and heartbreaking, too.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s hard to believe that -- and the government says our hands are tied here. We have to hope that there is going to be some resolution for this guy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. You really have to check the fine details of the rules of the country you`re traveling to it. I mean, people are going to say, I don`t know how this could happen. I can understand how it could happen if --\par \par \uc2 You know, I once went through airport security and they found a knife in my bag that I`d forgotten from some other trip where I needed it. So if you`re in the business of going on hunting trips, you might have it in carry-on bags, some stray bullets, really will cost you there.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes. Check the luggage before you travel for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the meantime, I hope that they can work something out. This is a very, very sad story to me.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Lisa Ling, she shares a very personal story for our new series we`re calling it the "Cost of Caregiving," how her family is caring for an aging parent while also raising young daughters.\par \par \uc2 Part of the so-called Sandwich Generation is what it is known.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our friends, that would be Drew Barrymore and Ross Mathews from "The Drew Barrymore Show." They are here today for a very special edition of "Talk of the Table" and we always love it when they drop by, but first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "God`s Country"/Blake Shelton/Warner Music Group)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is country music superstar, Blake Shelton`s hit, "God`s Country."\par \par \uc2 Ahead he talks about his relationship with wife, Gwen Stefani stepping away from "The Voice" and his new bar and music venue in Las Vegas. What I want to know is who names the babies and who names the dogs? A great Blake Shelton song as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re launching our new series today, we call it the "Cost of Caregiving" about how we care for our country`s aging population.\par \par \uc2 So listen to this number, between now and 2030, about 10,000 people in this country will turn 65 every single day. Many experts say the country is unprepared to care for them, and that care often falls on their adult children. As Americans have children later in life, many end up taking care of their parents and their young kids at the same time.\par \par \uc2 They are referred to as the Sandwich Generation.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, Lisa Ling understands this very personally and she is diving into this issue for us.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, good morning. Glad to have you here.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 So you are about to meet my family who are part of that Sandwich Generation. And sadly, we are not alone.\par \par \uc2 About a quarter of US adults fall into this category, and in this series, we`re going to introduce you to some of them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Mornings in our home are pretty hectic. She said there was a mistake on the study guide. My husband, Paul, and I travel a lot for work, so usually just one of us gets our two girls off to school.\par \par \uc2 But today, Paul has to rush back to take his 92-year-old mother, Grace, to doctors` appointments.\par \par \uc2 GRACE (Paul Song`s mother): Is it cold outside?\par \par \uc2 PAUL SONG: No.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It`s been a tough couple of months for her with multiple trips to the ER and rehab facilities.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SONG: That`s the main hospital.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: We are part of the nearly 80 million Americans taking care of children, as well as our elderly parents at the same time.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SONG: Step up.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And we have a name, the Sandwich Generation.\par \par \uc2 At a certain point, your parents become like kids.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Shin (sp?) is dealing with something similar, until recently, she had both of her parents living with her and her husband while raising two young kids.\par \par \uc2 When did your parents move into your home?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN (Both parents living with her): They moved in in 2017 when my first child was born. They came to offer to stay with me for a year to help raise them and then their health started to deteriorate, so I said, you`re staying I`m going to take care of you.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It was during the pandemic that she realized it wasn`t just her mom`s physical health that was starting to deteriorate, it was her mind.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about how her behavior changed and how the mom that you had always known really started to change?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: In the first few months, it was still okay. It was just -- it seemed like a lot of nonsense talking from her, but then there was a switch. She would tell me to shut up with profanity words sometimes. She would look at me as if she could kill with her eyes.\par \par \uc2 And I would say something and she`d be like, you don`t know what you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Three years after moving in, Shin`s mom was diagnosed with Alzheimer`s disease. Alzheimer`s disease can lead to delusions and sometimes violent behavior. This makes care very difficult.\par \par \uc2 At first, it was Shin`s father who took it on.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: He was spoon feeding her. He was sitting next to her. He was talking to her all day. There was not a moment that he wasn`t by her side.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: After two years, her mother`s condition continued to decline, but it wasn`t until Shin gave birth to her second child that she knew something had to change.\par \par \uc2 What were those first few months like when your daughter was born and your mother was still living here and going through the height of her disease?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: It was hard. I was trying to heal and take care of a newborn and a six-year-old, so it was a lot of nights of screaming and crying into my pillow.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Hi.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: Lisa.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Shin`s family decided that it would be better and safer for everyone if her mom could move into a memory care facility. They found what they were come for an hour away in Anaheim, California where the majority of the residents are Korean American like her mom.\par \par \uc2 Do you know how she`s been doing here?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: She has actually adjusted a lot better. The first month or two was hard. She -- I heard she was screaming for my dad, and then a shift happened, and they said this would happen and she met some friends that she always hangs out with and sits with and eats with.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Shin was able to pay for her mother`s care through California`s Medicaid program called Medi-Cal. It`s for individuals with little to no income.\par \par \uc2 But in many states, costs for memory care facilities can exceed $10,000.00 a month out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JORWIC (Caring Across Generations chief of Advocacy and Campaigns): Medicaid is a state and federal joint program. It`s complicated, which I sometimes think is by design.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Nicole Jorwic is with the organization, Caring Across Generations. She says most Americans should not expect that Medicaid will cover facilities. She says its waitlist is over 750,000 people long.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JORWIC: States can limit how many people they are going to serve, and also the federal government has limits about how much money people can have in order to receive those services.\par \par \uc2 So Medicaid really requires people to spend down their assets and remain in poverty in order to access long-term care.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: So far, we`ve been lucky that Paul`s mom has been able to stay in her home with our kids keeping close eyes on her.\par \par \uc2 My own father spent his final days in a facility that he paid for with a government pension and what he had in the bank. He passed last November, but had he lived, his entire life savings would have lasted him a maximum of two years.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Shin and her family are grateful to have found a safe place for her mom. But as a sandwich caregiver, Shin still has young kids and an aging father at home.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything you would want to say to all of those out there who are struggling and juggling, taking care of their kids and taking care of elderly people as well in their lives?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: It does get better. The kids will get older, they will become a little bit more self-sufficient and they won`t need you as much.\par \par \uc2 And the elderly, I just want people to know, they don`t want to be the ones needing help either.\par \par \uc2 (LAUREN SHIN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: And this is my turn to give back for all their hard work.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: While Lauren Shin made the difficult decision to move her mom into a facility, for many, that`s not an option due to not being able to afford it, their loved one doesn`t qualify, or there`s not enough room at these facilities to take them in, and we will have more on that tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, what Lauren said right there just really resonated with me where you go, you don`t want to be the ones needing help either.\par \par \uc2 But I feel for the sandwich generation because on one hand, you want to do right by your parents. On the other hand, you now have these young children of your own. It just seems like a vise coming out on both sides, Lisa.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Absolutely. I mean, there`s such an emotional toll.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: But also, the financial toll is so overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: I mean, over 26 percent of Americans are supplementing care for their parents while trying to take care of kids themselves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am so glad we`re covering this. There`s no shortage of love for the kids or the parents, but there is a shortage of so many other things. It`s very difficult.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Curious about the reaction you`re going to get from this because you`re here for the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Yes. I mean, I`ve been posting about it on social media.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And I`ve been inundated with people because I think that people are just hungry for information.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because a lot of people do not know what to do.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Ling will be here tomorrow. She is going to introduce us to a family taking care of a loved one at home and show us the challenges that they face as sandwich caregivers.\par \par \uc2 And for more on caregiver resources and organizations like Caring Across Generations and the \uc2 National Council on Aging\uc2 , we invite you to head to CBSMornings.com, it is all there.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Well, ahead, country music star, Blake Shelton takes us inside his new restaurant and music venue in Las Vegas and why he says he wants Beyonce to play there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good luck.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Neon Lights"/Blake Shelton/Warner Music Group)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: That`s country superstar, Blake Shelton performing at his new restaurant and music venue. Shelton has taken his trademark bar, Old Red from Nashville to Las Vegas. It opens up on The Strip at a time when the singer has taken a step back from a featured role on primetime TV.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment Tonight correspondent, Cassie DiLaura spoke with Shelton at the bar about his life with Rock Queen, Gwen Stefani and her sons.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Happy Anywhere/Blake Shelton/\uc2 Warner Music Group\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: For Blake Shelton, it`s all about family.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON (Singer): They`ve changed my life a lot.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Since becoming a stepdad, the country star says he is loving life with his rockstar wife, Gwen Stefani and her three sons.\par \par \uc2 I just think of the beautiful bond that you have and the role that you have taken on as a stepfather. How have they changed your life?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Well, in every possible way.\par \par \uc2 I get to teach them the country, whether it`s music, whether it`s literally, you know, getting out and getting their hands in the dirt or, you know, being out on the ranch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Blake Shelton/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: The couple first met 10 years ago as judges on the singing competition, "The Voice." It`s the seat that took Shelton from Nashville to Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Voice"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: A year later, Shelton and Stefani both went through high - profile divorces. The couple opened up to Gayle King about how their relationship started.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were both very vulnerable in your lives at that particular time. What happened between the two of you that this has worked so beautifully?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Strangely enough, I didn`t feel vulnerable with Gwen. I felt like I had met somebody that had my back.\par \par \uc2 If you would have told me the first season that we met what was going to happen, I would have been like no, we actually don`t even have anything to even talk about, you know, what are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 It`s crazy how things work out.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Right? Did you ever imagine a love like this?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: No. Oh my God. But I don`t know how you can. I don`t know how you can imagine when you find that life partner, that missing piece.\par \par \uc2 It is like, golly, you can go ahead and kill me if this ever, ever goes away or end.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jimmy Kimmel Live"/ABC)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Shelton left "The Voice" last year to focus on new projects, not just pairing up with his wife with her new song "Purple Irises" but also on the newest location of his honky-tonk empire, Old Red Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 How the heck is this tractor staying up there?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: You know it`s a very like a thin cotton rope.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Oh, great.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: It really does feel like you`ve stepped in through a portal and you`re in Nashville when you come through the doors.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Who would you want to see grace the stage?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Dolly would be great. I was hoping to get Beyonce to do a little something.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Texas Hold `Em"/Beyonce/Columbia Records)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: She wants to make it in the country music industry, and I think she must. It ain`t happen until she plays Old Red.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Shelton did land a major booking for Old Red, his wife. In the middle of headlining Coachella with her band, No Doubt.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Coachella)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: The greatest concert I`ve ever seen like it -- it was just - - I`ve never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: You told me a couple of years ago that everything takes a backseat to Gwen and the boys.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: What do you hope the next 10 years looks like for you two?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: If I could have a repeat of these past 10 years would be perfect.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: For CBS MORNINGS. I`m "Entertainment Tonight`s" Cassie DiLaura in Las Vegas. (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He seems like a very happy man with some great music.\par \par \uc2 All right, clear your schedule, you`re not making plans, you`re sticking around because our friends Drew Barrymore and Rossi Ross Mathews from "The Drew Barrymore Show" will join us for a very special "Talk of the Table" -- it doesn`t get better than them.\par \par \uc2 Local news, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are leveling up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I love that song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s time to bring you a very special extra-large edition --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Level Up" -- level up, Drew and Ross. Level up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- of "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: We are here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Featuring our lovely friends, Drew Barrymore Rossi Ross Mathews.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Hi. Rossi Ross.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hosts of "The Drew Barrymore Show." You will always be Rossi Ross.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: You better believe it.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes. Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: He has been Rossi Ross since he came and rescued me at that news desk on our show. And now we`re families with your show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We love it when you guys come.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Had been since day one, so thrilled to be here, and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, you`re up first, Drew.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I am.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: You are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: All right my "Talk of the Table" -- I`m sharing an early look at my eye-opening interview with happiness expert and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best-selling author, Arthur Brooks.\par \par \uc2 Also, I want to give credit to your bestie, Oprah Winfrey, for really helping bring him to the forefront with the extraordinary book that they wrote. My copy is annotated to the nines. He is sharing tips for worrying less, including keeping a worry journal, which was something I had never heard of or thought of, but take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS (Author): Write down your worries, write them down. You will make them specific. And then when you come back to them, you`re going to see progress and say, yes, I used to be worried about that, but that resolved itself. And that means that the thing I was really worried about, wasn`t as big a worry as I thought. And maybe the thing I`m worried about now isn`t as big as I thought.\par \par \uc2 Write it down, own it. You know, I promise you that as your kids grow up, you`re going to treasure that. You`re going to go back to that. You`re going to look at it when they`re 20 and say, I`m so worried about college. You go back, it`s so funny, because 15 years ago, I was worried about them, you know, falling down on the playground on the first day of kindergarten, or whatever it happened to be.\par \par \uc2 And worries are natural. But the more they`re specific, the better off you`re going to be.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh my god, I keep a gratitude journal. I need to keep a worry journal.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS: Totally. Totally.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I`ve never heard that suggestion in my life, Arthur.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: He also just wrote an article in "The Atlantic," which actually suggest that you write down the things that you have to do, say it`s about 20, knock off the bottom 15, because you`re more likely to meet the top five and then feel more successful.\par \par \uc2 He`s just so brilliant, and what he and Oprah did with their book, "Build The Life You Want," which I love so much, because it is so full of true, honest takeaways that you can really apply to your life. He`s a genius, and I think your bestie for really pushing him out to the forefront.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We can`t wait to see this interview.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes. It is interesting, I got to be in the audience for that with people asking questions and just like you were all during that clip, and maybe people at home --\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: All understand that. We all worry. There`s something that affects each and every one of us. It`s a really fabulous conversation.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I`m a big worrier, too.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, who is next, guys?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s wonderful. What`s up?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Oh, it is me. Okay, I am ready, so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You, Ross.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: My "Talk of the Table" is a new study that found the older people are, the later they think old age begins.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Interesting, right? Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know this is true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is confirmed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who needs a study for that?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I now think 85 is old.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Well, that`s what happened to the study published this week in "Psychology and Aging" surveyed nearly 15,000 adults aged 40 to 85 and the researchers found that 64-year-olds believe old age starts now at about 75.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: And at 75, participants said old age starts at 76.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: So, on average, every four to five years of actual aging, the participants` perception of old age increased by about one year. Remember when you were like 20 and you thought 40 was old.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Well, they all --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: George Clooney came on the show, and it goes, you know, when I was young, I used to think like 50, if I lived till 50 or 60, I`d have a great life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Now I`m 50 or 60 and I`m thinking wait, what`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: You know, it`s so true. It is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kick it up to 90.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I`m going to be 50, I think 50 is the new 30.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Absolutely. Well, that`s what we tell ourselves.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: But it is the new 30.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: There you go.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Even the way we dress and act and behave just doesn`t feel like we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They did back -- because Drew, I remember when my mom turned 50 and she seemed so old and I said, God, I hope I make it to 50. And now for many people, 50, you are just getting started.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that`s right.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes, that`s facts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But I will sometimes -- in my mind, I`m young, I`m walking around, I`m feeling young, I`m feeling good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But then I`ll catch a look at myself in the mirror and I`ll be like who`s that old guy?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh, Tony, how --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who is that father figure?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Come on, Tony, you`re the young one --\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: He looks -- if you look at yourself in the mirror, you don`t like it, there is no hope. There is no hope.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you. Thank you. That was the intended effect of my comment. I appreciate the compliments.\par \par \uc2 Arthur Brooks, you recommend that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Needy much?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: No mass --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony`s turn.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- is a very good thing going on at the Barclays Center. If you`re not doing this in stadiums where you are, they should already be doing this.\par \par \uc2 They take recipes from local businesses and scale them up so that people inside the stadium can have fantastic food.\par \par \uc2 So I am going to spotlight three places that I put on social media here.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Likkle More Jerk. It`s a chicken place. Jamaican patties, beef patties, and also marinated hickory smoked chicken. It is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yum.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Gosh. That looks amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Next up is Nay Nay`s Taqueria, and again, these are local businesses in the New York City area that the Barclays Center, a big stadium, has decided to put in the arena for tens of thousands of fans.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Paizano`s. By the way, that green sauce, I drink it like it`s a green juice. It`s so, so tasty.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: This is a brilliant business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Brilliant idea.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes, can I volunteer to go to the restaurants and pick which ones --\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: -- to highlight, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can apply to Chris Giacalone, who is the head of the taste program.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Okay, good that you said that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Always looking for scouts, I`m told.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Chris, hi, call me.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: How often do they switch them out then?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a good question. I don`t know the answer. But there`s about two dozen of them right now. And so like they sign a little nondisclosure. They give the secret recipe over and then they take it from a local thing to a big national thing.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It`s brilliant. More of this, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" -- I just adore this guy. I`m very smitten -- country superstar, Jelly Roll. He is quitting social media. Why? According to his wife, Bunnie Xo, Jelly Roll, has faced relentless online bullying about his weight.\par \par \uc2 On her recent podcast, she spoke about how the comments have affected him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Geez.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BUNNIE XO (Jelly Roll`s wife): My husband doesn`t show it to you, guys. But I`m going to have a very vulnerable moment here. It hurts him.\par \par \uc2 The internet can say whatever the (bleep) they want about you and they say, well, you`re a celebrity, you`re supposed to be able to handle it. No, the (bleep) we`re not.\par \par \uc2 Don`t bully people because you never know where they are mentally.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. Thank you, Bunnie for saying this. Jelly Roll is 39, recently lost weight. He is training for a 5K race in May. And you know, when you meet him, you just fall in love with him and you fall in love with his story. You fall in love with the person that he is.\par \par \uc2 And people always think that celebrities don`t have feelings, that you all can take whatever it is that is thrown at you, but it does.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m glad that she`s speaking up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And saying that he is very vulnerable and that it hurts his feelings and that everybody, we live in a society where people are so mean and they are encouraged and rewarded to be their most mean selves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whether it`s a celebrity or a kid you don`t talk to in your math class.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or an -- your right, your right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you wouldn`t say to their face--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- or at a table where they`re sitting, then don`t say it in public on social media.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Drawing the line saying I`m not going to subject myself to this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You`re so right.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I love your friendship with him. I saw you on at the awards show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I do. I really do.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: You`re so cute together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is called groveling. Please, come on our show.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he knows I really, really, really care about him.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Well, his story is incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was great.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: All right, my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Natalie.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I am going to give you all a good laugh, okay. It`s an interesting contest in Belgium. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Niel Van Herck/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Seagulls. Yes, seagulls.\par \par \uc2 Drew was like, is that a lobster? No. It is a gull screeching championship. It happened last Sunday. Competitors did their very best impressions of a screeching seagull and listen to this year`s winner.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Niel Van Herck/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: That is nine-year-old Cooper Wallace from Britain, taking home the junior title.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seagull boy.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: He says he picked up the talent after being pecked by a seagull at the beach. He says he actually has a lot of respect for seagulls. He`s not afraid of them. But apparently you have to get very much in character.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to be the gull to win the prize.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I see.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And he did.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Also have to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that falls into the --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have so many questions.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: How did everyone --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: They really want to be a seagull, you`ve got to stare at everyone`s food, like you want it really part badly.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Oh, that`s part of the competition. He pecked his sister`s French fries.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: So, that put him over the top.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am sure, you don`t know the answer to this, but there was a dangling banana for some reason?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I saw that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is happening with that?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Don`t ask questions, honey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That falls into the different strokes for different folks.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Too much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we should all take a trip to Belgium.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Drew Barrymore and Rossi Ross Matthews, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lovely to see you, guys.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: We love you.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Thank you, guys, so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tune in to "The Drew Barrymore Show" later this morning for a very special segment featuring Nate, Vlad, and me.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yay.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yay.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How about that?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Look at us together.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It was so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I need more compliments. Yes, I did great.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: The boys are here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Drew, how did Tony do?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Always the best.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Check your local listings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Oh, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Drew. Thank you, Ross.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad said, he did great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a tie, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is someone who had one of the most sought-after fun jobs in Washington. Her name is Deesha Dyer. She became the White House social secretary for President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Deesha will tell us about her unexpected journey. There she is. Hello, Deesha Dyer, looking pretty in blue.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:39. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is sharing her inspiring and unexpected path to the White House, and unexpected is the word here.\par \par \uc2 Deesha Dyer was 31 years old, a hip-hop journalist and part time community college student from Philadelphia when she landed an internship at the White House in 2009.\par \par \uc2 So listen to this. Over six years, she rose from intern to White House Social Secretary for President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 --\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- and First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  -- drop the microphone, Miss Dyer. You`re right now, wow.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER ("Undiplomatic" author): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was in charge of every White House event from state dinners to concerts and visit from world leaders and the Pope. She shares her journey in her new book. It`s called "Undiplomatic: How My Attitude Created the Best Kind of Trouble." We`re very happy to say that Deesha Dyer is with us first on CBS MORNINGS and welcome to you.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes, thank you so much. It`s so great to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved reading this book, Deesha because I would see you at the White House and you were always such a badass, so poised so in control, so on top of everything.\par \par \uc2 So to read that you in that position felt like an impostor even when you reach that level, I didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Help us -- explain that.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: The thing about impostor syndrome really, is that you have it and it comes and it goes at different times. And I think that I was in his new environment at the White House, not even with a college degree.\par \par \uc2 So I walked in and I was like, everyone else is more qualified than I am.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But let`s back up, June 2009, you get a call to interview for an intern. You`re 31 years old.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re in community college. You end up getting that job and then after you finish that job, they call you for another job, which says to me, they saw something in you that you didn`t see in yourself.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes, they did and when they called me, I was -- it was late one night and I was so shocked and I said are you sure?\par \par \uc2 Because for me when you`re an intern, you can -- you know, you`re in college or you just graduated. So that`s acceptable not to have a college degree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: But a full-time job?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: And when I said to her over the phone to Alyssa (sp?), I said, "Are you sure?" And she said, Yes, we are very sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: And so, you know, I was very shocked and I couldn`t even believe that they offered me the job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they did.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I know people really connect with you over the impostor syndrome. It`s something you talk about a lot, you write about a lot in the book. In fact, when you were applying for the internship, when you got the job, you show up first day wearing fake glasses.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: I did.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: To make yourself look smarter.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: I did.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: How did you get over that? You know, what did it take?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. I think I looked around and I said to myself, no one here knows what qualifications I have or what I don`t have. And so why am I making this the burden of this great internship at the White House for Barack Obama? Why is this leading my life?\par \par \uc2 And so I really got over it by realizing that I am able, I am capable. I was getting promoted. And I was like, well, they`re not going to promote me, if I`m an impostor. They see what I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: They see how great I am, and I decided to also dig up the root of why do I even feel this way? Like where does this even start? You know, so that`s really where it came from\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the other complexities for people coming up in their career is you get feedback.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re not sure what is valid feedback that you should take on and what you should reject, and so some of the things you write about hearing are that when you give your opinion, your voice is too loud or too harsh and then, that brushes up uncomfortably against not being yourself, right?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So how do you do -- how do you handle that kind of feedback?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: I think how I handled it is I just said, you know what, this is who I am, I can`t change that. And there`s a good way to use my voice.\par \par \uc2 And so I think I had to figure out how to use my voice to be loud about things that weren`t right about injustice and about things I saw that I didn`t agree with. And I said, I have to deal with the consequences of people just saying, you`re just a little too loud.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but then --\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Or you`re just a little too bold, I had to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But then, you also get very poignant and very personal in this book. I mean, here you are, the Pope is coming to visit.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The first Papal visit.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You also have the first State Dinner with the Chinese Prime Minister --\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- President. And you were in the bathroom throwing up because you just discovered that you`re six weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so you have to put on the face that despite all of the morning sickness that you`re having, you still have to do your job.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take us how you did that, and then at the end of the day, president -- end of the night, President Obama says you did a really good job.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not knowing all that was going on.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Oh, they had no -- they had no idea. And I think for me, the impostor syndrome made me feel like I have to push through this, like no matter how sick I am, no matter how I feel, because I was like, if I just mess up one small thing, I`m going to go back to thinking I`m a fraud, and so will everybody else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said, there`s no room for error or for mistakes in the White House.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Oh, there is no room for error. I mean, the Pope does not visit weekly, you know? I mean, like, you know, China State Dinner does not happen, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: -- every month and so there was not room for error. And I think I also put myself on a very high bar. You know, there was a newspaper article that said, you know, is she up for this? Is she up for this week? Can she do it? We`re not sure. And so I said I`ll show you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You almost didn`t apply for the internship in the first place. We`re so glad you did because it led to everything else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Including this book, which is perfect.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, she ends with this: "You`ll never be able to see your light standing in your own shadow, so move out of the way."\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo to you, Deesha.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I love this.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Thank you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now, you have your own company.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. Hook & Fasten, my social impact agency.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you feel --\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you still feel like an impostor? I`ll go first. No.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: No, I don`t know. No, I feel great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, and your company?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Hook & Fasten is my company and we work with corporations and communities on authentic partnerships.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Bravo, Deesha Dyer, Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Deesha, woo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are cheering you on always.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Undiplomatic" is the name of the book. It`s a great read. It`s on sale now wherever you`d like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In case you missed it yesterday, I didn`t, but if you did on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we showed you some wireless earbuds.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS (Lifestyle expert): These are Groovz. This is all of that sound quality and beautiful connectivity of the big guys but at a fraction of the price, and I think you might notice as we`re panning over this gorgeous floral print.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that floral one. That looks nice.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS: Yes, we`re having fun with colors. Okay, I love that for Mother`s Day, by the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS: Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS: So they`re fun with colors. You can stock up on these, given that price. You get the same technology, crystal clear sound is the big guys, but like I said they are a \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deal today.\par \par \uc2 I would definitely run not walk towards these because they sell out every time they`ve been presented on air.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can get the Groovz wireless earbuds for $39.99, that is 50 percent off the retail price. Nate`s probably sleeping on a plane somewhere with them in right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To the shop for this and other items, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com and be sure to follow \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to some of our most popular deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And while he is enjoying himself, we`re having a great time, but tomorrow Jerry Seinfeld and Jim Gaffigan will be here in studio to talk about their new movie, "Unfrosted" about the race to create the Pop Tart.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See you tomorrow on your favorite show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: See you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240424ek4o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for April 23, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, Jim Axelrod, Nancy Cordes, Robert Costa, Kris Van  Cleave, Nancy Chen, Carter Evans \par \uc2 3,671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I`m not sure that anybody`s ever seen anything like this before.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Tonight: Trump on trial, ex-tabloid publisher David Pecker telling jurors about a secret deal between him and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to bury negative stories during the 2016 presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , May 2, 2016)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And the former president blasts the judge and gag order --\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: An unconstitutional gag order by a highly conflicted judge.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: -- President Biden --\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You have the worst president in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: -- and the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It`s freezing, by the way.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and thank you for being with us. I`m Maurice DuBois. Norah is on assignment.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with day two of testimony in the criminal trial of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Former "National Enquirer" publisher David Pecker was back on the stand, where he said he worked with Trump and his longtime fixer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  to hide negative stories about him during the 2016 campaign. The prosecution claims it was all a part of a conspiracy to influence the election.\par \par \uc2 Before today`s testimony, there was a tense hearing over whether Trump repeatedly violated a gag order by attacking witnesses and jurors on social media. Prosecutors urged the judge to fine Trump thousands of dollars and hold him in contempt, while the former president complained that his right to free speech was being taken away.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa was inside the courthouse and starts us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Former President Trump aired his frustration with being in court today --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): That`s a very unfair situation.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: -- after listening to former "National Enquirer" publisher David Pecker, a man he`s known for over three decades, explain the strategy he testified they agreed upon ahead of the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 The paper would bury any negative news about Trump, while promoting fake stories about his political opponents, including Senators Ted Cruz and \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . Pecker testified the plan was concocted at Trump Tower in 2015. Trump and former fixer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  allegedly asked him how he could help the campaign.\par \par \uc2 He told them, "I would be your eyes and ears," explaining the practice of catch-and-kill. "The Enquirer" would buy the rights to any damaging story about Trump and never publish it. Prosecutors say that plan was the origin of Cohen`s $130,000 payment to former adult film star Stormy Daniels to keep her quiet about an alleged sexual encounter with Trump, who denies it all.\par \par \uc2 One example of catch-and-kill discussed in court today involved a Trump building doorman who was selling what turned out to be a fake story about Trump fathering a son. Pecker told the jury today he bought the story because of the potential embarrassment it would have to the campaign and Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: David Pecker is a very strong witness for the prosecution. He seems very solid on the stand. He certainly seems like he doesn`t have any axe to grind with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and seems almost apologetic for having to tell this story.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: On the stand, Pecker said Trump called him about another story he wanted kept quiet, that of former Playboy model Karen McDougal, who claimed to have had a 10-month-long affair with Trump in 2006, which he denies.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CNN, March 2018)\par \par \uc2 KAREN MCDOUGAL (Former Model): After we had been intimate, he -- he tried to pay me, and I actually didn`t know how to take that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Four days before the 2016 election, "The Wall Street Journal" reported that \uc2 American Media\uc2 , which owned "The Enquirer" at the time, had paid McDougal $150,000 for the rights to her story, which was never published.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In the pros -- in the gag order hearing, the prosecution argued that Trump should be held in contempt for all of his scathing commentary about this case.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s lawyer Todd Blanche said his client`s being careful with his words. To that, the judge said: "That`s just not credible" -- Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Robert Costa, once again at the criminal courthouse in Lower Manhattan tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to those escalating clashes and threats of violence on college campuses. Protests are growing as more students across the country are staging demonstrations against Israel`s war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Chen tonight on what protesters are demanding.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voiceover): Pro-Palestinian demonstrators clash with police at Cal Poly Humboldt in Northern California after a group of students use chairs and other furniture to barricade themselves inside one of the school`s main buildings.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 NYU\uc2 , \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers in riot gear cleared out a pro-Palestinian encampment last night after students defied the university`s order to leave.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #1: We want to see an acknowledgement from our university that there is a genocide happening.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Leave the plaza now, or you will be arrested for trespass.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A hundred and twenty people were arrested.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #2: Free Palestine! Free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: If you`re going to host a protest, you should host it in a spot that`s not so busy and dependent upon students` classes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Today, New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  said students who protest peacefully are not the problem.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): We can`t have outside agitators come in and be destructive to our city. There was -- there was someone wanted to something to happen at that protest at \uc2 NYU\uc2  that police officers didn`t respond to.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: From coast to coast, campus to campus, protests are growing louder in solidarity. Students are also joining in at \uc2 U.C. Berkeley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MALAK AFANEH (U.C. Berkeley Law Student): Quite frankly, I think it`s important that people start to align themselves with Palestinian resistance.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Here at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , the seventh day of protests, demonstrators want the school to divest from all business that supports Israel.\par \par \uc2 RASHIDA MUSTAFA (U.C. Berkeley Law Student): I think that it`s time to divest from those man -- those weapons manufacturing and re-divest them into other important things.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: They`re also demanding amnesty for all students who have been punished.\par \par \uc2 Is the goal to stay here until those demands are met?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #3: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And, as you can see behind me, demonstrators both on and off campus are still demanding to be heard.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there are growing calls for the university`s president to resign -- Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And no real end in sight tonight.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen at \uc2 Columbia University in New York\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 It is being called a monumental settlement for the survivors of sexual abuse by the disgraced former U.S. gymnastics Dr. Larry Nassar, the Justice Department announcing today that it`ll pay more than $138 million for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s mishandling of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Olympic stars Simone Biles, McKayla Maroney and Aly Raisman among the more than 100 people who sued the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for failing to respond to their complaints with urgency. The combined payouts to Nassar`s victims now top $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 At a campaign event today in Florida, President Biden blamed his opponent, former President Trump, for a growing wave of restrictive abortion laws across the country.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes has more on what is shaping up to be a key issue in the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): This isn`t about states` rights. It`s about women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): President Biden traveled to Tampa today just eight days before Florida`s abortion crackdown is set to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 The nation`s third most populous state will ban abortions at six weeks, a point at which many women still don`t realize they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : There`s one person responsible for this nightmare, and he`s acknowledged and he brags about it, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: When it comes to abortion, there is one thing Biden and Trump agree on, that it was Trump`s appointment of three deeply conservative Supreme Court justices that changed the balance of the court and brought about an end to Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from April 12)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We broke Roe v. Wade, and we did something that nobody thought was possible.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Since then, about half of all U.S. states have rolled back reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 And yet polling by CBS News shows only a third of Americans say Trump deserves the blame.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : He described the Dobbs decision as a miracle. Maybe it`s coming from that Bible he`s trying to sell.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Today, Biden highlighted the recent spike in pregnant women being turned away from emergency rooms.\par \par \uc2 Kaitlyn Joshua was turned away by two hospitals in Baton Rouge, Louisiana, when she was having a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA (Reproductive Rights Advocate): I was told that I would be sent home with prayers, but would not get a diagnosis of miscarriage on paper verbally.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Five months after Florida`s abortion ban goes into effect, voters in that state will have a chance to reverse it.\par \par \uc2 There`s a referendum on November`s ballot in Florida that the Biden team hopes will drive Democratic voters to the polls there, though Florida hasn`t gone for a Democratic presidential candidate since 2012 -- Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Nancy Cordes at the White House tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now a story that should serve as a warning for gun owners traveling to the Caribbean islands of Turks and Caicos. An American tourist is behind bars tonight, facing up to 12 years in prison after officials found ammunition in his luggage.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave reports on the State Department travel alerts.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): A tearful reunion at the Oklahoma City Airport this morning was not the way Valerie Watson thought her Turks and Caicos vacation would end.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE WATSON (Wife of Arrested Tourist): Just getting to hold them again, it just filled my mama heart.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: She`s home, but her husband and father of two, Ryan, is in jail on the islands, facing a potential mandatory minimum sentence of 12 years behind bars over four rounds of hunting ammo airport security found in his carry-on bag as the couple was set to leave Turks and Caicos earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON (Arrested Tourist): We were trying to pack more shorts and flip-flops, you know? You know, packing ammunition was not at all our intent.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: We spoke with the Watsons Sunday, before they learned Valerie would not be charged and be allowed to return home.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: This went from a -- what was supposed to be a dream vacation to a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Bryan Hagerich from Pennsylvania is awaiting trial after ammo was found in his checked bag in February.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN HAGERICH (Arrested Tourist): Subsequently spent eight nights in their -- their local jail, some of the darkest, hardest times of my life, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Possessing a gun or ammunition is prohibited in Turks and Caicos. However, tourists were often able to pay a fine, until a February court order mandating even tourists in the process of leaving the country be subject to prison time.\par \par \uc2 Last September, the U.S. Embassy posted this travel alert online warning people to check your luggage for stray ammunition.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: I can`t even begin to think that this very innocent, regrettable mistake would prevent me from being able to, you know, watch my son graduate or teach him to shave or take my daughter to, you know, dances.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A mistake that could land American tourists visiting an island paradise in a prison hell.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now to breaking news tonight.\par \par \uc2 CBS News is confirming that the FDA has found evidence of the bird flu virus in grocery store milk, but expressed confidence that there is no threat to human health. The government agency said tests are being conducted to determine how well pasteurization works to reduce the risk of infectious bird flu in the milk supply.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we continue our CBS News investigation into the growing wave of online romance scams.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  reports on a sinister new twist, when the victims themselves become accomplices.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE (Daughter): My mom was actually very specific about wanting to do online dating, and it felt safer.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Online dating felt safer?\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD (voiceover): If it wasn`t painful enough that Kelly Gowe`s mother, Laura Kowal, was scammed out of $1.5 million, then found dead in the Mississippi River, when Kelly started investigating what happened, she discovered her mom`s role in something criminal.\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: She got so deep into this scam. And when she tried getting out of it, she became what is known in these scams as a money mule. They got her hooked into it.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Laura wasn`t alone.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA (Scam Victim): My name is Glenda.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: It`s the latest scammer tactic, now so widespread that federal agents have broadcast warnings.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA: Police officers told me that my love was a scam.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: This feels like it moves to a whole other level of sinister.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE REDA (Retired U.S. Postal Inspector): They use victims until the end.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Retired postal inspector Natalie Reda investigated cases where victims fell so hard for their scammers, they would do anything they were told, like depositing other victims` money in their accounts to launder it and stay one step ahead of the authorities.\par \par \uc2 At first, the victims might not have realized they were committing a crime.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE REDA: They checked their reasoning at the door.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Kelly says her mom opened bank accounts, created fake corporations and even set up phony dating profiles for the scammers.\par \par \uc2 Could your mom have been compromised and actually been working for these guys?\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: I believe so.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: At the time of her death, police said Kelly`s mom, Laura, was actually under investigation for money laundering.\par \par \uc2 Federal agents had traced some of the money Laura unwittingly sent to scammers to Chicago and arrested this woman, Jennifer Gosha, along with two Nigerian nationals, for alleged involvement in romance fraud.\par \par \uc2 While prosecutors say Gosha was an active participant, she has pleaded not guilty and told us she too was a money mule, duped by one of the Nigerians, and says she never made a nickel.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GOSHA (Defendant): It wasn`t like a ding, ding, ding, something`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Gosha says she dated one of the Nigerians in real life, but he operated behind her back.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GOSHA: I served in the Iraqi war. I raised my children. I am not someone who decides, after all I have accomplished in my life, now I`m going to become a criminal mastermind. If I`m going to come up with the scheme, it`s not going to be this dumbass scheme.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: If convicted, Gosha could face up to 30 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever think to yourself, I played a part in someone`s life being ruined?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GOSHA: I have thought that many, many times. And I didn`t play a part in it knowingly.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: You`re saying you`re a victim of the scammers as well?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GOSHA: I`m absolutely a victim. But I --\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And you`re a victim who may end up doing time.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GOSHA: And only because they feel like I should have known.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: As for Kelly Gowe, her thought is simple: Someone has to pay.\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: And I have so much anger towards them. They took everything away from her. And I, my family will never get that time back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Wow, eye-opening, disturbing.\par \par \uc2 Jim, where does your investigation go now?\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Well, Maurice, law enforcement tells us that the explosion of romance scams is tied directly to the increased popularity of online dating apps.\par \par \uc2 They are, after all, a target-rich environment for scammers. So, tomorrow, we will dive much more deeply into that on CBS Mornings.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That`s must-see.\par \par \uc2 Jim, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Officials overseas investigating a midair crash between two helicopters. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: There were no survivors today when two helicopters collided in midair during a rehearsal for a military parade in Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 One helicopter clipped the other`s rotor, sending both crashing to the ground and killing all 10 crew members on board. The accident prompted Malaysia`s government to cancel Saturday`s 90th anniversary celebration for its navy.\par \par \uc2 The future of the popular security screening company Clear appears to be murky in the state of California. We will explain why next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENINE NEWS, you can listen, subscribe wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Tonight, the popular security screening service Clear is facing some possible turbulence in California.\par \par \uc2 The company could be banned from the state`s airports after lawmakers say it`s created a system of haves and have-nots when it comes to skipping the long lines.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): In long airport security lines, one thing is perfectly Clear: Those who pay extra get rewarded.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR JOSH NEWMAN (D-California): What the current system does is, it allows a Clear customer to cut the line.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: California State Senator Josh Newman introduced a first-in- the-nation law that would stop customers who pay the $189 annual fee from leapfrogging the security line at the state`s airports.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR JOSH NEWMAN: All Clear really does is, it -- it moves me in front of you, and we get to the same place in front of the conveyor belt. Only, by then, you`re mad, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  agent is all stressed out, and I paid $189 because I think I`m better than you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: How is this different than any other service that we pay to jump the line?\par \par \uc2 HENRY HARTEVELDT\uc2  (Travel Industry Analyst, Atmosphere Research Group): People pay to jump the line at theme parks. They pay to jump the line going into music concerts, sports stadiums.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The company says it has nearly one million California customers and pays airports there $13 million. Several airlines have taken a stake in Clear and say the lost revenue could mean higher fares.\par \par \uc2 But Clear, which uses fingerprinting and eye scanning in its screenings, has had issues in recent years with security lapses. That`s the main reason the union representing flight attendants supports the bill.\par \par \uc2 And lawmakers don`t want to ban Clear entirely, just separate the lines.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR JOSH NEWMAN: If you pay for Clear, go do your line somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 HENRY HARTEVELDT\uc2 : That`s financially and logistically impractical. \uc2 TSA\uc2  doesn`t have the funding it needs now to adequately staff all of the security lanes that exist.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And Clear is also facing another business challenge.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 TSA\uc2  and airlines are already investing in similar technology at checkpoints around the country, including right here at LAX. The goal is to move everyone through security faster, whether you have got Clear or not -- Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Carter, we will keep an eye on that one.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans in Los Angeles tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next with a \uc2 NASA\uc2  accomplishment that is truly out of this world.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally here, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And we meet the \uc2 NASA\uc2  scientist who repaired a spacecraft from 15 billion miles away.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 1977)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The Voyager 1 is known for being the first spacecraft to go beyond our solar system. So, when it stopped sending data back to earth last November, the team faced a daunting task. They traced the problem to a single manufacturing computer chip and announced yesterday -- that`s a malfunctioning computer chip.\par \par \uc2 They announced yesterday that they`d finally made contact once again.\par \par \uc2 Suzanne Dodd, the Voyager project manager, said this was all about teamwork.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE DODD (Voyager Project Manager, \uc2 NASA\uc2 ): It`s just incredible to be a part of this mission that -- that continues to this day and that has inspired so many generations of people to go into space exploration or science.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Suzanne Dodd and the Voyager 1 team at \uc2 NASA\uc2 , tonight`s Heart of America. All about teamwork.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Maurice DuBois. Thanks so much for joining us here.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240424ek4n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for April 12, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian, Robert Costa, Errol Barnett,  Debora Patta, Weijia Jiang, Katiera Winfrey, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,581 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON COUNTY PUBLIC SAFETY: Stolen vehicle that crashed into the DPS office. There is entrapment in the building.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The breaking news: a deadly crash police say was intentional, deliberate.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR LOIS KOLKHORST (R-Texas): We will prosecute this person to the full extent of the law.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A stolen 18-wheeler plows into a Texas government building, killing at least one person and hurting more than a dozen others. What authorities are saying tonight about the possible motive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ATWOOD KENJURA (Mayor of Brenham, Texas): The suspect was backing the vehicle up and with the intent of going into it again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell. And thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin tonight with a deadly and terrifying day in Texas. Police say a stolen big rig semitruck was intentionally crashed into a government office building, killing one person and injuring more than a dozen others. Two are now in critical condition tonight.\par \par \uc2 According to authorities, the driver was in a high-speed police chase, before deliberately smashing into the building about an hour west of Houston. A 42-year-old is in custody tonight, and police are now revealing the suspect visited that very same office earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Katiera Winfrey from our CBS Houston station will start us off tonight with the developing details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON COUNTY PUBLIC SAFETY: Stolen vehicle that crashed into the DPS office.\par \par \uc2 KATIERA WINFREY (voiceover): Tense moments tonight after 42-year-old Clenard Parker of Chappell Hill, Texas, rammed this 18-wheeler into the Department of Public Safety building in Brenham, Texas, leaving a gaping hole. He was arrested at the scene.\par \par \uc2 GERALD BROWN (Regional Director, \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 ): He had stolen an 18-wheeler earlier. And he was being chased by a deputy, and he ran into our building. We have several people that were injured and one is deceased at this time.\par \par \uc2 KATIERA WINFREY: The truck was carrying a heavy load of rebar, steel enforcement, when Parker slammed it into the building where various licensing services are provided. Authorities say he had been there the previous day.\par \par \uc2 SGT. JUSTIN RUIZ (\uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 ): Parker did come to the DPS Brenham office yesterday at approximately 3:00 or 4:00 p.m., where he was denied his commercial driver license.\par \par \uc2 KATIERA WINFREY: The mayor said it could have been even worse had Parker not been pulled out of the driver`s seat and put in handcuffs by law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 ATWOOD KENJURA (Mayor of Brenham, Texas): Had it not been for their quick action, as you can see across the street, the suspect was backing the vehicle up and with the intent of going into it again.\par \par \uc2 KATIERA WINFREY: Tonight, the state`s \uc2 Texas Rangers\uc2  have launched a criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR LOIS KOLKHORST (R-Texas): What we have seen today was an act of violence. We will prosecute this person to the full extent of the law, because this will not be tolerated.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KATIERA WINFREY: And the suspect is facing multiple felony offenses, including serious bodily injury.\par \par \uc2 He`s being held at the Washington County Jail tonight without bond -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Katiera Winfrey, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Much of the world is on alert tonight for an imminent attack on Israel by Iran. President Biden tonight has a simple one-word warning for the Iranians: "Don`t."\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned the U.S. military is sending additional firepower to the region to increase force protection for American troops that are already there.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang is at the White House with new reporting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): A U.S. official tells CBS News that the size of the planned attack aimed at military targets in Israel has grown in recent days.\par \par \uc2 It could include more than 100 drones, 150 cruise missiles, and even ballistic missiles used for long-range strikes. Tonight, a warning from President Biden to Tehran.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mr. President, what is your message to Iran in this moment?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Don`t.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Iran has vowed to retaliate, blaming Israel for an airstrike that demolished its consulate in the Syrian capital of Damascus. Iranian state media claims 13, including two senior Iranian generals, were killed.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says, Israel is not only on the defensive...\par \par \uc2 (REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: "But we are also prepared to go on the offensive. We are prepared to strike with a variety of capabilities," he said.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Pentagon`s top commander for the Middle East arrived in Israel as preparations continue, and Biden once again pledged the U.S.` ironclad support.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We are devoted to the defense of Israel. We will support Israel. We will defend -- help defend Israel, and Iran will not succeed.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The State Department is warning American diplomats not to travel outside Tel Aviv or Jerusalem, while the U.K. has gone further, advising people to leave Israel if they can.\par \par \uc2 General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , the former head of Central Command, said an Iranian attack could lead to a broader conflict in the region.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET.) (Former Commander, U.S. Central Command): If there`s a significant mass casualty event inside Israel, then I think the possibility of escalation is very, very high.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Tonight, President Biden says he expects Iran to attack Israel sooner, rather than later.\par \par \uc2 As for those additional U.S. assets you mentioned, the Pentagon is repositioning a Navy destroyer in the region, joining another warship that is already there -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Weijia Jiang at the White House with that new information, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go now to Israel, where tensions are high, with its Iron Dome missile defense system already being tested today, with rockets being launched by an Iranian proxy group.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta joins us from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Debora.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian-backed group \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fired a barrage of missiles from Lebanon into Northern Israel earlier. This is what the night sky looked like a short while ago. The \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2  says most of the missiles were intercepted, the rest falling on empty land, with no injuries.\par \par \uc2 Israel remains on high alert, bracing itself for the possibility of an attack by Iran in retaliation for the bombing of its consulate in Damascus. A former \uc2 Mossad\uc2  official, Sima Shine, told us that there is a real fear that the region could be on the brink of an expanded conflict. She believes the attack could either come from a number of Iranian proxies in Lebanon, Iraq or Yemen or directly from Iran.\par \par \uc2 And if it resulted in mass casualties, this would have the potential for significant escalation -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Memphis today, a police encounter with two teenagers ended in a hail of bullets. An officer was killed, along with an 18-year-old suspect. And we are learning tonight that that teenager was recently released from jail.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Errol Barnett.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEMPHIS POLICE AND SHELBY COUNTY SHERIFF: About five or six, five to six shots, shots fired.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (voiceover): This shoot-out began around 2:00 a.m., when Memphis police say officers responded to a call of a suspicious vehicle.\par \par \uc2 MEMPHIS POLICE AND SHELBY COUNTY SHERIFF: Officer down.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: As they were approaching the stationary vehicle with two teens inside, gunshots rang out from the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 C.J. DAVIS (Memphis, Tennessee, Police Chief): Three officers were struck by gunfire. Tragically, one of our officers lost his life.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Officer Joseph McKinney, with the department three years, was killed. A second officer was taken to an area hospital with non-life- threatening injuries, and a third was grazed by a bullet, treated at the scene.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF C.J. DAVIS: We have a family that`s grieving now. We have a wife that`s grieving now.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Officials say both teenage suspects were shot during the gunfire, killing 18-year-old Jaylen Lobley. In March, police say Lobley was arrested in a stolen vehicle and was armed with a semiautomatic weapon altered to shoot like a machine gun.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF C.J. DAVIS: He was also charged at that time for two stolen vehicles and having a programming device commonly used to steal cars. The suspect was released at that time without bond.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: In the U.S. so far this year, 34 other officers have died in the line of duty, with 11 killed by gunfire.\par \par \uc2 Memphis Mayor Paul Young:\par \par \uc2 PAUL YOUNG (Mayor of Memphis, Tennessee): We know that we, as a community, have to do more to hold violent offenders accountable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, the local district attorney -- that`s Steve Mulroy -- made clear today his office argued against releasing Lobley just last month because they already identified him as a high-risk offender.\par \par \uc2 An investigation is under way, but it raises the painful question, Norah, if both of today`s deaths could have been avoided.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s a real issue in a lot of cities.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Mike Johnson is with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight at the former president`s Mar-a-Lago resort after appearing together publicly for the first time since Johnson took that post. The top House Republican flew to Florida as he`s facing a revolt from conservative members of his conference.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa reports tonight from Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and House Speaker Mike Johnson, two men who fought to overturn the 2020 election, called a press conference today to argue noncitizens should not be able to vote, despite the fact it`s already illegal.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): The House Republicans are introducing a bill that will require proof of citizenship to vote. It -- it seems like common sense.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act of 1996 explicitly bans noncitizen voting in federal elections.\par \par \uc2 Yet Trump and his allies have falsely claimed noncitizens constantly vote in elections. The nonpartisan Brennan Center, which tracks voting issues, says examples of noncitizens voting are extremely rare.\par \par \uc2 For the embattled speaker, today`s pilgrimage to Mar-a-Lago was a chance to get a needed boost from the former president, as some Trump allies say Johnson is failing.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-Georgia): I explained all the reasons why he failed as our speaker.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In recent weeks, Johnson has seen his grip on the gavel loosened, as he has stumbled to fine votes on surveillance legislation and funding for Ukraine. And his work on some bipartisan measures has sparked talk of a possible coup.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I think he`s doing a very good job. He`s doing about as good as you`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: For Trump, the visit underscored his immense influence over the party, despite his legal issues, much like when then-Speaker Kevin McCarthy came to Mar-a-Lago three years ago in the wake of the Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And, on Monday, former President Trump`s criminal trial over hush money payments in 2016 is scheduled to begin.\par \par \uc2 Trump told me today he plans to take the witness stand and that jury selection will be the luck of the draw -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s a big headline out of today.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  lashed out at former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Arizona today, calling him the architect of the restrictive abortion bans nationwide. It comes in the wake of the state`s recent near-total ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  Janet Shamlian reports, the issue is now front and center in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): Good afternoon, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Tonight, the blame game.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We all must understand who is to blame.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this. During his campaign in 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said women should be punished for seeking an abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , campaigning in Arizona, blaming former President Trump for the state`s Supreme Court ruling reviving a near-total abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The overturning of Roe was, without any question, a seismic event. And this ban here in Arizona is one of the biggest aftershocks yet.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Anti-abortion rights protesters were also in Tucson today.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LINANAN (Anti-Abortion Advocate): And we want to protect those people that are in the womb still, because they deserve to be protected.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: They hope to defeat a constitutional amendment protecting the procedure likely to appear on the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 JOSH CRADDOCK (Senior Legal Fellow, James Wilson Institute): I think that when Arizonans are -- come out to vote this November, I think that they`re going to side against this abortion extremism in the referendum and support the right to life.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Pro-abortion rights advocates say they have the almost 400,000 signatures needed, but continue to gather more.\par \par \uc2 JEAN FLETK (Arizona Resident): I`m ashamed as to what it says about Arizona. It`s -- it`s -- it`s a law that they`re -- they`re trying to enforce that was before women had the right to vote, before Arizona was a state. It`s just awful that they have gone so far backwards.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. DeShawn Taylor runs one of the eight abortion clinics in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 DR. DESHAWN TAYLOR (Founder and President, Desert Star Institute for Family Planning): It`s infuriating, because abortion is health care.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: She says patients are confused about when the law will take effect. Abortion is currently legal up to 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 DR. DESHAWN TAYLOR: For the last couple of days, it`s been fairly quiet.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Because they think they can`t get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 DR. DESHAWN TAYLOR: Correct.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Democrats see this as an issue that will likely get voters out in November, Arizona among more than a dozen states likely to have abortion on the ballot, the latest being Colorado -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Janet Shamlian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The former interpreter and longtime friend of baseball superstar Shohei Ohtani surrendered to federal authorities and appeared in a Los Angeles courtroom today. The 39-year-old is facing federal bank fraud charges for allegedly stealing $16 million from Ohtani to place about $19,000 in bets going back more than two years. He was released on $25,000 bond in order to get treatment for gambling addiction.\par \par \uc2 A young swimmer swept away by a fast-moving river -- the latest on the tragic discovery next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A desperate search in California for two young swimmers ended in tragedy today.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Sonoma County say two teenagers were swept away while playing with friends in a local river yesterday. One of the teens was rescued, but the body of the second child was discovered today.\par \par \uc2 Legendary broadcaster Robert MacNeil has died. MacNeil reported from the scene of the assassination of President \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2 . He and his longtime partner in journalism, Jim Lehrer, earned an Emmy for their coverage of the Senate Watergate hearings. They later launched the award- winning MacNeil/Lehrer Report, which eventually went to a full hour and still runs today as PBS NewsHour.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 PBS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert MacNeil was 93.\par \par \uc2 We have another death to report. Italian fashion designer Roberto Cavalli has died. Cavalli became famous in the early 1970s for his animal prints and bold and sensual styles. He also was known for creating the sandblasted look on jeans. Cavalli`s high-end fashions have been worn by celebrities, including Cindy Crawford, Jennifer Lopez, and Madonna.\par \par \uc2 Roberto Cavalli was 83.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next with the moon, the sun, and a little magic from the great eclipse of 2024.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This week`s total solar eclipse brought millions of Americans together for a moment of inspiration and awe.\par \par \uc2 That includes \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman and his family On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Like many families, we went to a lot of trouble this week, driving five hours for a four-minute vacation.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN (Son of Steve Hartman): Because it`s not just four minutes. It`s something that you can look back on in your old age and say, I was there.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This is my oldest son, George. He`s been into astrophysics since kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: By a strange coincidence, the moon is 400 times smaller than the sun, but 400 times closer to the Earth.\par \par \uc2 Where`s the sun now?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He begged to go to the last eclipse in 2017 and wanted to go again this year. So, we traveled to the Top of the World Drive in Vermont`s Northern Kingdom, crashed a party there, and set up for an encore performance by the world`s biggest star.\par \par \uc2 All right, the greatest show on Earth.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: Except, it`s not on Earth.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Only difference, last eclipse, George was an engaging 9- year-old who thought his father hung the moon. And, this time, he`s a teenager.\par \par \uc2 What are you thinking, George?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: Do I have to think in a normal sense in such moments of pure beauty?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 So, while George sulked up the sun, the rest of us cheerfully waited for our window.\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: Yes, it`s in the bottom right corner.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: At this point, the shadow had already passed through most of the country. And we talked about that, how it traveled through red states and blue...\par \par \uc2 BOY #2: I see the moon.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- how it fell on faces of all colors, stirred souls of all creeds, and how, by the time it reached us, more than 30 million Americans...\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- who just moments before seemed so different...\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- would all fall under --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- the same exact wonder.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Oh!\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Like, the country comes together, and we all find it amazing.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: And look around us. Look at all the similarities.\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: A moment that kind of unites us all was -- was pretty cool to see.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: We decided that unity was almost as inspiring as the event itself.\par \par \uc2 Totality!\par \par \uc2 Of course, within seconds, the heavens returned us to our own devices. But our wish at the end of this wondrous week is that a ray of that harmony remains, which leads me now to this.\par \par \uc2 Are you glad you got to have this time with your family?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: Hmm. I think maybe the answer to that is yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A glimmer of hope --\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: But also maybe not.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 -- quickly eclipsed by snark. I guess, when it comes to throwing shade, not even the moon can trump a teenager.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, On the Road, in Newport, Vermont.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It was a delight to see it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Heart of America is next with the Piano Man and his record- breaking performance at the world`s most famous arena, \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America, celebrating an historic milestone for a rock `n` roll legend.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Billy Joel`s performance)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The Piano Man, Billy Joel, is marking his 100th show of his record-breaking residency at New York`s Madison Square Garden. He`s played the famed arena since 1978.\par \par \uc2 And, Sunday night, you can watch his first ever show to be broadcast on TV right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Billy Joel`s performance)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In addition to all the hits, including My Life and New York State of Mind, Joel performs his first new song in nearly 20 years, "Turn the Lights Back On." He`s also joined on stage by some of his famous friends, Jerry Seinfeld and Sting.\par \par \uc2 The five-time Grammy winner will end his decade-long residency at the Garden in July. So, catch him while you can, or just tune in to \uc2 CBS\uc2  on Sunday. Billy Joel, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240413ek4c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for April 10, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jo Ling Kent, Debora Patta, Elaine Quijano, Nancy  Cordes, Mike Bettes, Mark Strassmann, Nikole Killion, Ben Tracy \par \uc2 3,625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we`re tracking flooding and tornado threats. Millions of Americans under severe weather alerts from Texas to Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Big, big tornado.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Multiple tornadoes reported, as damaging wind leaves thousands without power.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Water slamming against the windows, debris flying everywhere.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell. And thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We are coming on the air tonight with more than 25 million Americans in the path of severe and dangerous storms across the South and the Ohio River Valley. Thunderstorms, torrential rains, life-threatening flooding, and tornadoes are impacting states across the Gulf Coast. An EF-2 tornado hit the city of Port Arthur, Texas, destroying homes and a church.\par \par \uc2 There is catastrophic damage in Louisiana after a suspected tornado ripped the roof off an apartment building outside New Orleans. And in Mississippi, dozens of homes were hit and one person was killed when storms slammed the Magnolia State.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, tornado watches are in effect across the region.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann will start us off tonight from a New Orleans suburb that took a direct hit.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): As many as three tornadoes may have touched down in and around Slidell, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Oh.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Wind gusts of up to 110 miles per hour tore through the city just north of New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: It came through fast and did a lot of damage, and it left fast.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: About 50 people needed rescuing after winds sheared the roof off an apartment building.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It happened really fast. While we were trying to pack a few things, my ceiling caved in, his ceilings caved in.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: The damage is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Officials in St. Tammany Parish reported the impact was catastrophic and extensive, with more than 100 buildings damaged.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: This storm was no joke. It`s something that we haven`t seen here in Slidell in a very long time.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Flash flooding in New Orleans swelled roads, overwhelming drivers, and marooning vehicles.\par \par \uc2 As the storm moved east, this was the scene on I-10 in Mobile, Alabama, water everywhere, flooding highways and causeways. It`s the same storm system that lit up the sky overnight and brought heavy rain and hail to East Texas. In Port Arthur, a tornado flattened this church, and another shredded this strip mall outside Houston.\par \par \uc2 More than a foot of rain fell in some parts of the state. And in Dallas, a month`s worth of rain fell in just three hours. One death from the storm has been reported in Mississippi outside Jackson, where the storm churned a swathe of downed trees and power lines.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m standing in front of a ruined building.\par \par \uc2 It`s a general contracting business. And the owner told us earlier this was his life`s work. And now, Norah, it`s gone in seconds.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And these storms are far from over, as they move east overnight.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): And, Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Some of the violent weather we saw play out today, and especially in places like Louisiana, is going to hit again tomorrow, just areas farther to the east. We have overlaid the high-resolution future radar on top of the areas that will be the biggest targets, including the Panhandle of Florida, up into Georgia, really heavy storms for you in places like Atlanta, all the way down toward Tallahassee.\par \par \uc2 Some of these storms could affect early tee times for the Masters tomorrow. They could get rained out, likely flooding across the Gulf Coast here, but it could extend all the way up toward the Ohio Valley., areas from Tampa and I-4 into Orlando, Jacksonville to Savannah, all areas where severe storms could hit. That may include tornadoes as well.\par \par \uc2 There will be a second area up across the Ohio Valley that could be a target, Cincinnati, Columbus, Cleveland, Pittsburgh, and Charleston, and these areas, Norah, watching for high winds and hail to hit.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, some Republicans across the country are rhetorically trying to distance themselves from Arizona`s Supreme Court ruling that upholds a near-total abortion ban. But, late today, Republican state lawmakers in Arizona blocked efforts by Democrats to overturn that law.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes has more on the political uproar.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Angry, let down, frustrated.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): Anxiety in Arizona tonight after the state Supreme Court upheld an 1864 law that bans nearly all abortions and criminalizes those who perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It`s really upsetting that our politicians who are supposed to represent us are doing this.\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Democrats protested on the floor of the state legislature today. And with swing state Arizona up for grabs in November, some Republicans are slamming the ruling too, including former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who just two days ago said abortion should be left to the states.\par \par \uc2 Today, he said Arizona went too far.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): And I`m sure that the governor and everybody else are going to bring it back into reason and that will be taken care of.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Biden, who has vowed to restore Roe v. Wade, was asked today what his message is to Arizonans.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Elect me. I`m in the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The controversial law could go into effect in Arizona this summer, forcing patients to travel to neighboring states with less restrictive laws.\par \par \uc2 At the Camelback Family Planning clinic in Phoenix, phones were ringing off the hook today, calls from women concerned about abortion access.\par \par \uc2 DR. ATSUKO KOYAMA (Camelback Family Planning): As long as we can practice medicine here, we will continue to provide services, whether that means that people need ultrasounds to see how far along they are, to see if they do need to go to a different state.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Arizona`s highest court warned yesterday that doctors who perform the procedure could face between two to five years in prison, though the state`s Democratic attorney general says she won`t prosecute them.\par \par \uc2 DR. ATSUKO KOYAMA: You are now having physicians who are scared to do their job that they did without batting an eye before the overturn of Roe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The uproar in Arizona is reigniting this issue at a national level, with Trump insisting today that, if elected, he would not sign a federal abortion ban if Congress sent one to his desk.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats cast major doubt on that claim, noting that he has repeatedly endorsed a ban at 20 weeks in the past, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A large celebration marking the end of the Muslim holy month of Ramadan ended in a volley of at least 30 gun shots today in Philadelphia. Police say three people were shot. The city`s police chief says it appears to have been a gunfight between four or five people who are now in custody. Federal officials are assisting in the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the family of a 15-year-old boy is demanding an Akron, Ohio police officer be fired after the release of disturbing bodycam video. It shows the shooting of the teen, who the family says was holding a toy gun.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Quijano reports, the city`s mayor is vowing a thorough investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 911 CALLER: There is a guy walking down Newton Street, and he was aiming a gun at people`s houses.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): The call to Akron police came just after 7:00 p.m. on April 1st. Minutes later, Officer Ryan Westlake arrived.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WESTLAKE (Akron, Ohio, Police Officer): Can I see your hands real quick?\par \par \uc2 (Gunshot)\par \par \uc2 RYAN WESTLAKE: Oh (expletive deleted).\par \par \uc2 TAVION KOONCE-WILLIAMS (Shooting Victim): Hey. It`s fake. It`s fake.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WESTLAKE: Shot fired. Shots fired.\par \par \uc2 TAVION KOONCE-WILLIAMS: It`s fake. It`s fake.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Westlake shot 15-year-old Tavion Koonce-Williams once in the risk. Police say he was holding this, a replica of an assault weapon.\par \par \uc2 Federal law requires such replicas to have distinguishing markings, like a permanently attached bright orange plug in the barrel.\par \par \uc2 TAVION KOONCE-WILLIAMS: I`m a good kid, bro. I get As in school.\par \par \uc2 IMOKHAI OKOLO (Attorney for Family of Tavion Koonce-Williams): He was doing everything that he absolutely could to comply with the police officer`s orders.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Attorney Imokhai Okolo represents the family.\par \par \uc2 How is Tavion doing?\par \par \uc2 IMOKHAI OKOLO: It`s tough. It`s very traumatic. He`s been having nightmares, not being able to sleep.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Officer Westlake has been placed on paid administrative leave. He`s been on the force nine years and has been disciplined more than once.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, he was fired, reinstated, and then suspended for several incidents, including pointing a gun at his then-girlfriend while drunk.\par \par \uc2 In this case, former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Detective Sergeant Keith Taylor says the officer only had seconds to act.\par \par \uc2 KEITH TAYLOR (John Jay College of Criminal Justice): I believe that the officer thought that this was a very dangerous situation, based on the 911 call. He was probably focused on trying to stop this individual from actually hurting somebody.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WESTLAKE: Elevate your hand.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: We tried contacting Officer Westlake for comment, but were unable to reach him.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the local police union stood by Westlake, saying in part, quote, "The officer acted within policy and procedure and according to his training". Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elaine Quijano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A major setback today for hostage negotiations. An Israeli official tells CBS News Hamas is unable to locate 40 Israeli hostages for a possible cease-fire deal, this all raising fears tonight for families that their loved ones have died.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta reports tonight on the war that just entered its seventh month.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voiceover): Palestinians pray amidst the rubble and ruin of Gaza, marking the end of Ramadan with the Muslim holiday of Eid al-Fitr.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: For many, the day was spent in mourning at the grave sites of loved ones killed in over half-a-year of war.\par \par \uc2 (ANAM ABDA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "It`s enough, God," Anam Abda (sp?) sobbed. "Enough with war."\par \par \uc2 Instead of a joyful occasion filled with children`s laughter, for this grandmother, it was a day of heartbreak, as she said a final farewell to her grandchildren, while their father cradles the body of his youngest son. His wife and three children were killed in Central Gaza.\par \par \uc2 And for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  chief \uc2 Ismail Haniyeh\uc2 , the conflict became personal. This is the moment he received the news that his three adult sons and four grandchildren were killed in an Israeli airstrike.\par \par \uc2 As the war drags on and the humanitarian crisis worsens, the wider the rift grows between the U.S. and Israel, with President Biden yesterday sharply criticizing the way Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  was conducting this war.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I think what he`s doing is a mistake.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And today reiterating that he expected Netanyahu to make good on his promise to flood Gaza with aid.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : And the fact is that they`re getting in somewhere in the last three days over 100 trucks. It`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: The rift between the U.S. and Israel comes at a precarious time.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are learning that the commander of U.S. forces in the Middle East is heading to Israel amid increasing concerns that Iran is preparing to launch a major attack against Israel in retaliation for last week`s bombing of the Iranian consulate in Damascus. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: They`re on high alert.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, the former chief financial officer of Donald Trump`s real estate empire was sentenced to five months behind bars today for lying under oath during Trump`s civil fraud trial in New York. \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  pleaded guilty to perjury last month, admitting that he lied when he testified that he didn`t know how Trump`s Manhattan penthouse was valued at three times its actual size. Weisselberg previously served 100 days in jail for dodging taxes.\par \par \uc2 Now to some breaking news. Wall Street took a hit, with all three major indexes finishing the day in the red. The Dow fell more than 400 points after the Labor Department reported a higher-than-expected spike in inflation. The Consumer Price Index for March rose at an annual rate of 3.5 percent, and that is the biggest jump in six months.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s MoneyWatch, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent shows us how consumers are feeling the pinch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For the third straight month, prices have gone up more than expected, from the rising cost of car insurance and repairs, from how much you`re paying at the grocery store every week.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Do you know how much one of these costs? Two freaking dollars.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: This was $200.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: We supposed to skip the power bill this month so we can buy groceries or the mortgage?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Before the pandemic, all of these groceries, about 30 items, cost $100 on average. Now, five years later, according to NielsenIQ, all this costs 33 percent more, meaning you would have to skip about 10 items, like chicken, bread, milk, and bananas, to make your $100 budget.\par \par \uc2 Five years ago versus today, the difference is remarkable. Is that normal?\par \par \uc2 CARMAN ALLISON (Vice President, NielsenIQ): Oh, goodness, it`s not normal at all. So, that`s why, really, consumers are really, really scrutinizing their behaviors, because, you know, prices are leveling off, but they`re leveling off at these record high levels.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): Higher gas prices and rent also helped push inflation up 3.5 percent over the last year, also more expensive, baby food and formula spiking nearly 10 percent, elder care up over 14 percent, and veterinary care jumping almost 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 President Biden responding today.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We`re better situated than we were when we took office, where inflation was skyrocketing. And we have a plan to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: But until then, shoppers continue to cut corners where they can.\par \par \uc2 CARMAN ALLISON: We asked Americans, have you changed your behavior? And 87 percent of Americans have said yes to that. We may be seeing more white meat on the barbecue than red meat this summer, because beef prices have gone up 9 percent. Everybody`s been impacted by this. There`s no way you can get around it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The good news here is, wage growth last month did outpace inflation.\par \par \uc2 But this March inflation number is the biggest annual jump in six months, and it really makes it very unlikely that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will lower interest rates any time soon. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, many people waiting on that.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 There`s breaking news from Dallas. A Super Bowl champion is wanted by police in connection with a terrifying crash. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, Dallas police have issued an arrest warrant for \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  wide receiver Rashee Rice and another driver, who are accused of speeding on an expressway and causing a chain reaction crash two weekends ago.\par \par \uc2 Rice, who was driving a Lamborghini, is wanted on charges including aggravated assault and collision involving serious bodily injury.\par \par \uc2 Bird flu has been detected in America`s largest city, with a small number of birds in New York City parks and green spaces testing positive. A human infection from the outbreak was reported earlier this month in Texas, where a worker at a dairy farm had symptoms similar to pink eye. The CDC says there`s no sign of bird flu spreading between people.\par \par \uc2 The cost of a stamp may be going up again. The \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  wants to raise the price of a first class Forever Stamp from 68 cents to 73 cents. If approved, the change would happen in July.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is cracking down on so-called forever chemicals in America`s drinking water. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Today, the Biden administration laid out the first national standards limiting what are known as forever chemicals in drinking water.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ben Tracy reports, the move is aimed at preventing thousands of deaths and tens of thousands of serious illnesses.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGAN\uc2  (\uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  Administrator): Everyone should be able to turn on their tap and trust that the water that they`re drinking and giving their children is safe.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY (voiceover): \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  announced the new drinking water standard in North Carolina, where, in 2017, it was discovered a chemical plant had been contaminating the local water supply by dumping PFAS into a nearby river.\par \par \uc2 EMILY DONOVAN (Co-Founder, Clean Cape Fear): It was gut-wrenching when we first learned about our contamination crisis.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Emily Donovan lives near the river and has been fighting for PFAS regulations for years.\par \par \uc2 How concerned have you been about the water you and your children have been drinking all these years?\par \par \uc2 EMILY DONOVAN: I mean, I have been terrified. No mother wants to be told that she contaminated her children.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: An estimated 200 million Americans are exposed to PFAS chemicals through drinking water. These forever chemicals last for thousands of years and are used in everything from food packaging to water-resistant clothing and cosmetics. They have been linked to certain cancers, liver damage and developmental issues in children.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You can`t smell these chemicals. You can`t taste them. You can`t see them. So that`s why these new rules are so important.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Now, water utilities must reduce PFAS levels to near zero in the next five years. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  says six to 10 percent of the 66,000 public drinking water systems will likely need to make changes to comply. That could cost billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Water Works Association\uc2 , which represents the utilities, says polluters, not communities, should be held responsible.\par \par \uc2 But for Emily Donovan --\par \par \uc2 EMILY DONOVAN: This, I think, is a monumental change in how we look at public water in America.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: -- what matters is making the water safe to drink.\par \par \uc2 Ben Tracy, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Heart of America is next with one of the nation`s highest civilian honors for Rosie the Riveter, the women of World War II.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America with a long-overdue honor for the millions of women who answered the call during World War II.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): In trademark red and white polka dots --\par \par \uc2 (Singing)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: -- more than two dozen women representing Rosie the Riveter received recognition with a Congressional Gold Medal for working on the home front in shipyards and factories during World War II.\par \par \uc2 MAE KRIER (World War II Riveter): Remember these four little words: We can do it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Accepting on behalf of the mostly centenarian group, 98- year-old Mae Krier, an original Rosie who produced B-17 and B-29 bombers for \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 MAE KRIER: But a lot of women were treated poorly by the men. They resented having the women in there.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Marian Sousa worked in a shipyard in Richmond, California.\par \par \uc2 Why is the recognition important?\par \par \uc2 MARIAN SOUSA (World War II Riveter): You want to know that you accomplished something during your life. And I guess this is it, although I raised six kids, and I think that`s a -- that`s some accomplishment too.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Susan King was a riveter from Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN KING (World War II Riveter): For the few of us still living, any time will do to prove to America that we were important too.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Trailblazing then --\par \par \uc2 MAE KRIER: We were doing it to save our country.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: -- and now.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion, CBS News, the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we are grateful to those women, Rosie the Riveters, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240411ek4a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for April 9, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Roxana Saberi, Debora Patta, Elise Preston, Alex  Wilson, Andres Gutierrez, Nancy Cordes, Lisa Ling \par \uc2 3,611 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JILL SOAVE (Mother of Shooting Victim): You have failed your son and you have failed us all.\par \par \uc2 STEVE ST. JULIANA (Father of Shooting Victim): They blame everyone but themselves.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG SHILLING (Father of Shooting Victim): The blood of our children is on your hands too.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A landmark sentencing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE BEAUSOLEIL (Mother of Shooting Victim): Not only did your son kill my daughter, but you both did as well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: A judge sentences the parents of a school gunman for up to 15 years in prison, as they`re the first to be convicted for their child`s mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE CHERYL MATTHEWS (Oakland County, Michigan, Circuit Court): Each act or inaction created a ripple effect.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get to that emotional scene in the courtroom in just a moment, families coming face to face with the parents convicted of involuntary manslaughter for the role in their son`s mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 But we do want to begin tonight with a major abortion ruling that once again is putting the issue at the forefront of the 2024 presidential campaign. Arizona`s Supreme Court today ruled that a 160-year-old near- total abortion ban is still enforceable. The law dates back to 1864, on the books since before Arizona was a state and before women had the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 Arizona will be the 18th state to severely restrict or outright ban the procedure since Roe v. Wade was overturned in 2022. The decision comes the day after former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  claimed he doesn`t support a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 But, today, President Biden said Trump is directly to blame for the ruling, calling the decision extreme and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  Nancy Cordes reports now on the fallout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATIE HOBBS (D-Arizona): It is a dark day in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): Arizona`s Democratic governor begged the legislature to step in today after the state`s conservative Supreme Court reinstated a Civil War era abortion ban, with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The author of the 4-2 decision wrote: "Physicians are now on notice that all abortions, except those necessary to save a woman`s life, are illegal," with doctors facing a two-to-five-year mandatory prison sentence.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATIE HOBBS: We are 14 days away from this extreme ban coming back to life. It must be repealed immediately.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The decision does away with the state`s current 15-week ban, which anti-abortion rights activists had challenged in court.\par \par \uc2 JAKE WARNER (Senior Counsel, \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 ): It`s always the best decision to protect life as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Arizona is now poised to join 17 other states that have imposed near-total abortion bans since Roe v. Wade was struck down.\par \par \uc2 But, unlike many of those states, Arizona is a pivotal swing state that went for President Biden in 2020. The state`s Democratic A.G. announced today: "As long as I am attorney general, no woman or doctor will be prosecuted under this draconian law."\par \par \uc2 Does that give doctors the certainty they need to keep performing abortions if they feel they need to?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LOVE (Arizona for Abortion Access): Well, I think that medical providers are going to have to weigh their options and their risk with their own legal counsel, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Chris Love helps lead Arizona for Abortion Access, a group that has already gathered half-a-million signatures for a November state ballot measure that would establish a constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LOVE: We had told the Arizona voters what`s at stake. I think today is a clear example that we were being serious about that, right?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Biden campaign is counting on that ballot measure and others like it to help drive Democrats to the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 The issue is a proven motivator, which could help explain why several top Republicans in Arizona came out against the Supreme Court ruling today, saying, Norah, that it goes too far and is out of step with the state.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Republicans were denouncing it.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to that other groundbreaking court case, the sentencing of James and Jennifer Crumbley, the first parents in the U.S. to be held criminally responsible for their child`s school shooting.\par \par \uc2 A Michigan judge today sentenced each parent to 10 to 15 years in prison in connection with the shooting deaths of four students at Oxford High School in 2021.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Andres Gutierrez was inside the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG SHILLING (Father of Shooting Victim): The blood of our children is on your hands too.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ (voiceover): One by one, family members of the four students killed at Oxford High School addressed James and Jennifer Crumbley just before they were sentenced.\par \par \uc2 JILL SOAVE (Mother of Shooting Victim): You have failed your son and you have failed us all.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE BEAUSOLEIL (Mother of Shooting Victim): And while you were running away from your son and your responsibilities, I was forced to do the worst possible thing a parent could do. I was forced to say goodbye to my Madisyn.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ (voiceover): The Crumbleys were found guilty of involuntary manslaughter earlier this year. Separate juries found they willfully failed to prevent their son, Ethan, from bringing a handgun to his Michigan school in 2021 and opening fire.\par \par \uc2 Today, they each asked for leniency and forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CRUMBLEY (Defendant): I am sorry for your loss as a result of what my son did.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER CRUMBLEY (Defendant): We were good parents.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: But the judge sentenced them to a minimum of 10 years in prison, minus time already served.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE CHERYL MATTHEWS (Oakland County, Michigan, Circuit Court): These convictions confirm repeated acts or lack of acts that could have halted an oncoming runaway train.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: During their trials, prosecutors painted the couple as absent parents more concerned with themselves than their son`s mental health and accused them of gross negligence for buying a \uc2 SIG Sauer\uc2  handgun as a present for Ethan four days before the shooting and then failing to secure it.\par \par \uc2 Today, Craig Shilling, the father of Justin, said the families have been waiting for this moment.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG SHILLING: I feel that there was some justice here today, for sure. The sentence was appropriate. And I`m glad that we received that.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: Criminal defense attorney Joe Tamburino predicts, this historic case will set a precedent for other parents.\par \par \uc2 JOE TAMBURINO (Attorney and Legal Analyst): If your child is expressing the same type of behavior, the same type of mental state attitude that Ethan Crumbley was expressing, you must do something about it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: And in handing down her sentencing today, the judge said she hopes her decision will act as a deterrent to prevent future school shootings.\par \par \uc2 The Crumbleys will be eligible for parole in under eight years. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Andres Gutierrez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, severe weather is causing havoc across the South. Strong winds knocked down trees in Shreveport, Louisiana. This one crashed into a house and flattened the owner`s carport. And, nearby, this 18-wheel \uc2 FedEx\uc2  truck flipped onto its side during downpours, causing major delays for the morning commute.\par \par \uc2 For a look at the threats still to come, let`s go to Dallas and meteorologist Alex Wilson with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WILSON (Meteorologist, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 It`s already been a very active day of severe weather. That threat will continue, actually goes up into our Wednesday, so, unfortunately, an even more dangerous day expected tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with what`s happening right now. We have active watches across the South. Going to be a dangerous evening and overnight for parts of Texas and into Louisiana, where the tornado threat will remain. As we move through the overnight, in addition to these storms that will bring the severe weather, the tornadoes, the hail, the gusty, damaging winds, we will also be watching for the threat of very heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 So, unfortunately, flash flooding and flooding will be a concern. Now, we start the morning tomorrow with storms ongoing. They will continue through the afternoon. Take a look at the TORCON values as high as a seven. So that just means we could be seeing tornadoes that are stronger, those that are the EF-2 or greater and long track.\par \par \uc2 Again, morning brings the severe weather. It continues all the way through the day. Even into the overnight, you can see those storms will be moving into parts of the Southeast. Heavy rain potential means flash flooding likely all across the South. Some areas could see as much as three to five- plus inches of rain. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Alex Wilson, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, an Idaho teenager is facing federal terrorism charges for an alleged plan to attack churches in the name of ISIS. The Justice Department says the 18-year-old was arrested on Saturday, just one day before launching a plot where he planned to use guns, knives and flammable chemicals.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston reports, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was already on nationwide alert.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  believes 18-year-old Alexander Mercurio was plotting deadly terror attacks on multiple Christian churches in Coeur d`Alene, Idaho.\par \par \uc2 According to court documents, Mercurio had pledged his allegiance to ISIS and his intention to die while killing others using makeshift flamethrowers, a machete and other weapons found in his home by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , which says he communicated with other radicalized individuals on his school-issued computer.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: The profile of this individual is not surprising. The large majority of them are male and typically are quite young. ISIS and its proxies do retain the ability and the intent to try to conduct an attack here in the homeland.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Today, this from \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2  (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): The potential for a coordinated attack here in the homeland is now increasingly concerning.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: It`s a concern worldwide. ISIS-K claimed responsibility for this attack at a Russian concert hall last month. At least 144 were killed.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, a just-released intelligence bulletin warns of a potential threat of violence targeting mass gatherings, such as sports stadiums, concert venues or houses of worship.\par \par \uc2 It definitely sounds the alarm to the average American at home.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: It is a fact that we`re in a heightened threat environment. We, as Americans, need to live our lives, but we also need to do so while being incredibly vigilant.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: There are at least 21 known extremist groups operating in Idaho, according to the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Court documents show the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was tracking Mercurio for nearly two years. He`s expected in court tomorrow. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow, that`s some good work by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the war in Gaza, today, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  declared no force in the world will prevent Israeli forces from entering the besieged city of Rafah. It comes as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  today met with families of several Americans taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , assuring them that the Biden administration is doing all it can to bring them home.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta reports from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voiceover): Rafah has become a tent city of mostly women and children who have been running from war for months.\par \par \uc2 Once again, they fear the worst, as Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  doubles down on his plans to invade Rafah, despite the U.S. warning, that would be a mistake.\par \par \uc2 Huriya (sp?) sought refuge here with her nine children after her husband was killed in an Israeli airstrike.\par \par \uc2 (HURIYA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "What hurts me the most," she said, "is I have lost my dignity. I feel humiliated here."\par \par \uc2 But after six months of war, if they are forced out, where do they go to next? The \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2  has mostly withdrawn from Southern Gaza, allowing residents to return to Khan Yunis. But this is not the home they remember, the destruction everywhere so complete, it is unlivable.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "Everything is dead," this woman said.\par \par \uc2 Across this wasteland, there are also still more than 100 hostages, including Americans, being held, who have now spent more than half-a-year in captivity. Today, their family members met with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , once again pleading for their release.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN DEKEL-CHEN (Father of Hamas Hostage): What we have heard is that there is a deal on the table right now that all of the parties agree to and are willing to work with. We are waiting now and the world waits for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to get to yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  has said there is a serious offer on the table and that the ball is firmly in \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` court, while National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan says he has spoken with Qatar`s prime minister and urged him to get a response from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as soon as possible. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Debora Patta with that new reporting, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There are new developments tonight in last year`s devastating train derailment in East Palestine, Ohio. The railroad company \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  has agreed to pay $600 million to settle a class action lawsuit related to the incident.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Roxana Saberi reports, some residents are still suffering from the impact of the toxic crash.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI (voiceover): In the 14 months since the toxic train derailment forced nearly 2,000 people from their homes, Linda Murphy says she and her husband, Russ, have developed numbness and leg pain.\par \par \uc2 LINDA MURPHY (East Palestine, Ohio, Resident): We really need long-term health care.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: So you`re worried about getting diagnosed with cancer years down the road?\par \par \uc2 LINDA MURPHY: Absolutely, cancer, neurological diseases.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Murphy says the $600 million class action settlement won`t be enough to cover future health care.\par \par \uc2 LINDA MURPHY: I almost feel like it`s giving a grain of rice to the starving. Is it really going to help people the way that it should?\par \par \uc2 JAYNE CONROY (Plaintiff`s Attorney): We appreciate and understand that anxiety.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Jayne Conroy, an attorney for the plaintiffs, acknowledges that, with up to 100,000 people eligible for compensation, some might receive very little or choose to opt out.\par \par \uc2 Still, she says, the settlement is significant.\par \par \uc2 JAYNE CONROY: The derailment itself was totally avoidable, and just the pattern of conduct all resulted in a settlement of this size.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  said in a statement today: "The agreement does not include any admission of wrongdoing or fault" and that it`s already committed over $100 million to the community.\par \par \uc2 If East Palestine becomes a cancer cluster, what would \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  do? Will it --\par \par \uc2 WILL HARDEN (Senior Claims Director, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 ): From a long-term health standpoint, we are addressing that with the, with the state and local authorities.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: So, is it an option that`s on the table?\par \par \uc2 WILL HARDEN: It is an option that is on the table. That is correct.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Plaintiff`s attorneys tell CBS News that, if the settlement announced today is approved by a judge later this month, the first payouts could be made by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  still faces several other lawsuits. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Roxana Saberi, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A helicopter with two people on board plunges into a pond. We have the details on their rescue next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Federal officials are investigating the cause of a helicopter crash today in Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 The chopper with two people on board went down in a pond and then sank. A man who happened to be fishing helped them out of the water. The pilot was treated at the scene, and a female passenger was taken to the hospital for evaluation.\par \par \uc2 A group of Jewish and Arab women in Michigan find peace through dialogue, in hopes of bringing people together.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Six months after the October 7 attack, life has forever changed in both Israel and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, a group of women with strong connections to both places are working to find common ground.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lisa Ling traveled to Michigan to meet this special sisterhood.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING (voiceover): These women, six Jewish and six Palestinian, have been meeting twice a month for over 20 years. They call themselves Zeitouna, the Arabic word for olive tree. Their motto? "Refusing to be enemies."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Every woman in the group here, I feel, is like a sister.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I don`t even see Jewish or Palestinian anymore. I just see human beings.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: The safety of this environment has allowed these women to remain committed to each other in the face of October 7 and after.\par \par \uc2 WADAD ABED (Zeitouna Member): You absorbed my pain, as I absorbed your pain. It`s important to just have a space, a place where everybody is there with open arms.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Have you all talked about ways in which you think that we could get to a better place?\par \par \uc2 DIANE BLUMSON (Zeitouna Member): There`s room in the humanitarian way to recognize the trauma of the other. And people have lost that ability right now.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: A message these women are spreading far beyond this room, including on college campuses, many of which have become deeply polarized.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , two students, one Jewish and one Palestinian, started the Arab-Jewish Alliance more than a year ago to foster better relations between cultures.\par \par \uc2 EVAN ROTKER (Co-Founder, Arab-Jewish Alliance): I grew up Jewish, and the only time when I ever met Arab students was in my Arabic class. I was like, how can we bring Arab students and Jewish students together? And this kind of set us down this path.\par \par \uc2 WELLY ALTAII (Co-Founder, Arab-Jewish Alliance): I think, like, the week after October 7, I was looking on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and I saw the rhetoric, the dehumanizing rhetoric. And so I thought I want to, like, find a club where I can actually interact with people on the other side, because I hadn`t had a chance to interact with people.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: You are the future.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: For the Zeitounas, these young students embody the mission they have spent decades building.\par \par \uc2 WELLY ALTAII: I think that, when you meet a group like Zeitouna, who`s been around for so many years, I think it reassures you that these friendships, they can last forever.\par \par \uc2 IRENE BUTTER (Zeitouna Member): As a Holocaust survivor, this is what I learned. All human beings are the same. And if we could only realize that, then I think we could build a better world.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: The power of conversation to inspire meaningful change.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Ling, CBS News, Ann Arbor, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Very important lesson about finding common ground and treating people with humanity.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next with a special dog taking on an important job to help those in need.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of the CBS EVENING NEWS is sponsored by BREZTRI. Visit us at breztri.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet the guide dogs of Guiding Eyes for the Blind. This New York-based nonprofit has been providing trained guide dogs to those with vision loss, and they do it free of charge. Thanks to the work of over 1,000 volunteers, Guiding Eyes is able to provide 150 dogs a year to those in need.\par \par \uc2 We sat down with president and CEO Thomas Panek and his own guide dog, Ten, who was handed off to him from fellow board member and former Giants quarterback Eli Manning.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS PANEK (President and CEO, Guiding Eyes for the Blind): Blindness isn`t easy to deal with. You know, finding my shoes in the morning is tough. I can`t see the stars at night. Couldn`t see the eclipse last night.\par \par \uc2 But I do have this wonderful dog by my side that helps me, and I want to provide that to anyone who`s going through the same thing that I have been through.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The Guiding Eyes for the Blind, they are tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240410ek4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Major Earthquake in Taiwan; Tornado Outbreak; Severe Weather Forecast; Aid Workers Killed in Gaza; Trump on the Trail; New Footage of Amber Alert Shooting; Financial Aid Funding Malfunction; Sen. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  Live; Artists Versus AI; Lizzo Addresses Quitting Rumors; National Teacher of the Year; "Ghosts" Star, Asher Grodman Live; Bailey Zimmerman`s Rise to Fame; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Dave Malkoff, Stephanie  Abrams, Imtiaz Tyab, Holly Williams, Ed O`Keefe, Elise Preston, Vladimir  Duthiers, Scott MacFarlane, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 14,315 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets. We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: Taiwan is rocked by a powerful earthquake damaging buildings and leaving dozens trapped. We`ll have the latest.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Very, very violent. The whole building swayed, I actually had to hang on to my kitchen countertop for stability.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Violent storms bring widespread destruction to multiple states and it is not over yet.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: And by the way, on the heels of the storms comes a snow threat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Outrage grows after Israeli airstrikes killed seven aid workers in Gaza, and the US calls for an investigation.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE (White House press secretary): Aid workers must be protected.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Taylor Swift has earned a place in an elite group. The pop star landed on Forbes` World`s Billionaires List.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: A cicada apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Trillions are expected to emerge this year.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: Two different groups are set to emerge at the same time.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): This spring, the overlap in a small portion of Illinois around Chicago, that`s a phenomenon known as "the bugs."\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #1: The Iowa-LSU rematch in the Elite Eight lived up to the hype.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #2: Twelve point three million people tuned in.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #3: The most watched college basketball game ever on \uc2 ESPN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RYAN RUOCCO (Sportscaster): From Schenectady. She is simply ridiculous. She`s possessed.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON ("After Midnight" host): Caitlin Clark had so many big shots that the guy was clearly running out of things to say.\par \par \uc2 She`s ridiculous. She is possessed. She`s a witch -- drown her. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved his commentary. He was running out of -- what more adjectives can you say? I love everything he said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When Nate and I were joking about this yesterday, you were like how many threes can she make?\par \par \uc2 He probably had a list of 10 things to say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe even 20.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The way she plays.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Such a great story still.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got breaking news to tell you about as you wake up this morning. There`s been a major earthquake in Taiwan overnight, causing massive damage there. At least nine people are dead, and hundreds have been injured.\par \par \uc2 Major transportation links have been cut. We`ll have more on all of that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we began here in the US with a second day of severe weather affecting millions of people. Extreme winds tore the roofs off of houses and snapped trees in Kentucky, Tennessee, and Indiana, where at least 10 people were hurt.\par \par \uc2 There were a dozen reports of tornadoes touching down from Alabama all the way up to Ohio, that includes the small city of Sunbright, Tennessee where we find Dave Malkoff today.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Good morning. Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Look at this car. It was crushed when a large building came down right in the heart of the city. I just talked to the National Weather Service in the area, they say that there was at least 15 to 20 minutes` worth of warning for the families here, but look at all this. Nothing can really prepare you for something like this, and this is just a small part of a big day of bad weather all across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Holy --\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Destructive and powerful winds barreled through parts of West Virginia, Tuesday, including downtown Charleston and to the west in Hanging Rock, Ohio --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Campers on their side. Oh Dear Lord.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Violent winds decimated this RV park.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This literally took 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Those violent storms hammered a dozen states Tuesday, tearing neighborhoods to shreds.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 University of Kentucky\uc2  in Lexington, the winds were so extreme, the student was knocked off his feet.\par \par \uc2 MAN: At 5:17, our first outdoor warning sirens went off across the city.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Violent storms around Louisville left behind so much damage that the area now has a huge cleanup project to tackle.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I will be declaring a state of emergency for the entire Jefferson County.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The high winds not just causing damage to homes and businesses, but also mayhem on the roads from Louisville to Jeffersonville, Indiana where big rigs proved no match for the winds, where this driver was fortunately able to escape.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Tennessee, residents in Sunbright were shocked to see the wreckage left behind.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Well, it`s just a gut punch to see your town and see these old buildings just demolished.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The tornado likely struck just after 5:15 PM leaving the local funeral home in ruins, and the owner stunned.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I really didn`t think a whole lot about it. I was really surprised when I got up here and seen all this damage.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Yes, that funeral home is right on the other side of this building. I just talked to another neighbor here who said that he heard the warning, he ran across the street. He got his neighbors and they were almost sucked out of the house, but everyone survived and nobody was killed here -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on this system and a possible mess in the Northeast, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The biggest threat today will be the damaging winds from the Delmarva to Florida. We could also have an isolated tornado and hail. Rain will be off the Carolina Coast by this evening. Florida is not going to be as lucky.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, a snowstorm will kick off in the Western Great Lakes and tomorrow, the Northeast is going to get in on the action. The snow is going to be heavy and wet, that means tough to shovel, great for snowballs. The storm is going to linger into our Friday.\par \par \uc2 We need this double-digit snow as we`re down to two over three feet in several cities. The system will also have 40 to 50-plus mile per hour winds that could take down trees power lines, cause travel delays and a wind chill.\par \par \uc2 This coming Monday, it is not all about the total solar eclipse. A big chunk of the country will see a partial eclipse, and parts of the Northeast should have clear skies.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Nate, if you miss this total solar eclipse, the next one in the US is in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so from the weather to the eclipse, our eyes will be on the skies.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to turn back to that powerful earthquake in Taiwan with extensive damage, buildings left teetering on the edge of collapse. Take a look at these pictures.\par \par \uc2 Officials say now at least nine people are dead, hundreds others injured and dozens still trapped in the rubble you see on your screen.\par \par \uc2 This was a 7.4 magnitude quake centered near the island`s East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is following the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Parts of a building crashed to the ground as people across Taiwan were getting ready for the morning rush. Inside cafes and bars, destruction. Panicking patrons as the 7.4 magnitude earthquake rocked the island nation.\par \par \uc2 The force of the quake was so powerful, some buildings tipped onto their sides as some of the smallest survivors were carefully rescued.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "Things just started to fall off the building," this woman says. "Everything is damaged."\par \par \uc2 In the eastern coastal Hualien City, rescue workers had to use cranes to free people stuck inside damaged buildings.\par \par \uc2 YOLI SABAN (From California): It was definitely a shock when it happened.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Yoli Saban (sp?) from California had arrived in Taiwan just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 YOLI SABAN: We were actually sleeping and we were definitely woken up by it because the whole room was shaking. We tried to find like somewhere to hide, under a table, but we didn`t have that.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: But it is the countryside, which saw some of the worst damage. The force of the quake triggered serious landslides, blocking some tunnels, trapping dozens inside.\par \par \uc2 But in Taiwan, earthquakes are common and the initial panic quickly faded as the public were kept informed via cellphone messages and school drills saw some children put on protective head coverings as they waited for the all clear.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now this is the most serious earthquake to hit Taiwan since 1999 when over 2,400 people were killed and hundreds of buildings were destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Now, while this quake hasn`t caused anywhere near that kind of devastation, it has certainly caused an awful lot of alarm -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imtiaz Tyab reporting in London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re learning new details today about the seven aid workers from \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  who were killed by Israel`s military in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 All seven victims have now been identified including an American. The airstrikes destroyed the victims` cars on Monday in what Israel claims was a case of mistaken identity.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  has named Jacob Flickinger, a 33- year-old dual US-Canadian citizen as one of seven of its team members killed by the Israeli military in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 It appears their convoy of three cars was targeted with strikes multiple times, even though the nonprofit says it was coordinating its movement with Israel`s military, which called it a grave mistake.\par \par \uc2 HERZI HALEVI (Chief of the General Staff of Israel): It was a mistake that followed a misidentification -- at night, during a war, in very complex conditions.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): These people are heroes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The \uc2 US government\uc2  says, it is outraged by the deaths calling on Israel to carry out a swift and impartial investigation.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 : We`ve impressed upon the Israelis the absolute imperative of doing more to protect innocent civilian lives, be they Palestinian children, women, and men or be they aid workers.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  called the incident tragic and unintentional, also saying --\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "This happens in war."\par \par \uc2 Relations between the US and Israel appear increasingly strained, even though America is Israel`s most important ally. Nowhere is the tension more evident than over Israel`s planned offensive in Rafah in Southern Gaza, where around one-and-a-half million Palestinians are sheltering, many in tents.\par \par \uc2 Israel says it needs to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` remaining battalions, the US is warning against an assault without a credible plan to protect civilians.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: After sending hundreds of tons of food aid to Gaza, \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  has now paused its operations there. Its founder, the Spanish-American chef, Jose Andres wrote in a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " opinion piece: "Israel is better than the way this war is being waged" -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly Williams for us in Israel. Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  returned to the campaign trail yesterday stressing law and order. Trump and President Biden won four presidential primaries last night; however, the results show that many voters still prefer someone else.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is covering the campaign for us.\par \par \uc2 Ed, quite an output here. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Despite dropping out of the race almost a month ago, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  had noticeable support in last night`s GOP primaries, pulling double digit percentages in all four states. It came as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was firing up his base in two key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: From Michigan to Wisconsin --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Criminals like never before, just pouring into our country --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pushing a message of law and order Tuesday, slamming President Biden`s immigration policies. And despite recent backlash from both parties, he used the word "bloodbath" again. This time, to describe crimes committed by immigrants.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : -- Joe Biden`s border bloodbath, and that`s what it is, it is a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Flanked by police officers, Trump is hoping his tough stance on crime and immigration, both top issues for Republican voters, according to our recent \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll, will motivate a key voting bloc this fall.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : The suburban housewives actually like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You know why? Because I`m the one that`s going to keep them safe.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And the Biden campaign has also been focused on women voters and abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Trump proudly says: "After 50 years with no one coming close, I was able to kill Roe v. Wade." That`s his statement.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Meanwhile, Trump has only suggested supporting a federal abortion ban with exceptions. He teased more to come on his stance.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We`ll be making a statement next week on abortion.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In Arizona, where the president narrowly defeated Trump by less than a percentage point, a reproductive rights organization said Tuesday, it has collected enough signatures needed to put a constitutional abortion measure on the ballot, possibly joining a handful of other states, including Florida.\par \par \uc2 DOMINICA (Voter): We, as people should be able to choose what we want to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now, also of note in last night`s primaries, the political protest vote against President Biden`s handling of the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war continued, but it was lower than earlier primary standing between eight percent and 14 percent -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Ed. Got it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Newly released video from an armed standoff is raising big, big questions about how deputies handled a deadly shootout in California.\par \par \uc2 Now, this happened back in 2022, fifteen-year-old Savannah Graziano was allegedly kidnapped by her father, Anthony John Graziano. He was eventually cornered and during a shootout, a deputy shot and killed Savannah.\par \par \uc2 At the time, they said that she was a participant.\par \par \uc2 But as Elise Preston reports, this newly released footage seems to tell a very different story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Shots are fired. Shots are fired.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Unsealed helicopter footage from the San Bernardino County Sheriff`s Office shows a new vantage point. A 15-year-old, Savannah Graziano`s 2022 death.\par \par \uc2 MAN: She`s out of the passenger`s side.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Wearing a helmet and tactical gear, she heads toward law enforcement, apparently at one deputy`s urging.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY: Come to me. Come to me. Come, come, come, come. Walk, walk, walk, walk, walk. Walk.\par \par \uc2 Hey. Stop. Stop shooting her. He`s in the car.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Despite those warnings, Graziano is gunned down.\par \par \uc2 (Gunfire)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The teenager`s death came a day after her father allegedly shot and killed her mother, then kidnapped Savannah, prompting an Amber Alert and the high-speed chase that culminated with the fatal shootout.\par \par \uc2 At the time, the county sheriff said this.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON D. DICUS (San Bernardino County Sheriff-Coroner): Evidence suggests that Savannah Graziano was a participant in shooting at our deputies.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: But nearly two years later, the helicopter footage appears to suggest otherwise. A \uc2 California Justice Department\uc2  investigation remains ongoing.\par \par \uc2 FELIPE RODRIGUEZ (Former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  detective sergeant): That definitely was one of the most chaotic scenes I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Felipe Rodriguez is a former detective sergeant with the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  and worked in law enforcement for more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 FELIPE RODRIGUEZ: Those gunfire coming from everywhere. Well, now you have someone rushing at you that has a tactical helmet, and some sort of body armor. What one officer saw doesn`t always mean another officer saw the same thing.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: When officers know that there`s a 15-year-old girl who has been abducted. How can something like this still play out?\par \par \uc2 FELIPE RODRIGUEZ: These officers will still have a lot to explain, you know, and each officer has to be accountable for every round that he discharged.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For CBS MORNINGS, Elise Preston, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Heartbreaking tragedy.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, how our bureaucratic mess is blocking the college applications of hundreds of thousands of high school kids. That`s coming up in just a bit, but first, it`s 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, we will exclusively reveal the National Teacher of the Year. The winner will join us in studio.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Asher Grodman plays a pantless ghost on \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` aptly named "Ghosts." We will talk with him about the role, the role`s lack of pants and what he wants to see for his character`s love life, and what it`s like being Tony Dokoupil`s doppelganger.\par \par \uc2 All of that and the stories we`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is the time of the year when hundreds of thousands of young people are trying to make that big decision on where to go to college, and that choice is really hard to make until they know how much financial aid they`re going to get.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they are in limbo because of a massive mishap by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is at the University of Maryland with more.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This was the year the US Education Department unveiled a simpler, shorter college aid application. It`s shorter, but it`s been a fiasco.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Paige Fogle (sp?) was ahead of the game.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE FOGLE (Aid applicant): I had it figured out. I was ready to apply.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A high school senior in Washington, DC, she is already planning to major in business and knows her dream school.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE FOGLE: If everything works out, I`ll be going to \uc2 Hampton University\uc2  in the fall.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Paige submitted for federal financial aid within days of the application portal opening. After weeks of waiting, she is still left in limbo.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE FOGLE: Without these award packages, I can`t make my final decision, which is ultimately delaying me getting to the finish line.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Paralyzed by a new system Washington is using for aid applications, Congress mandated changes to the so-called FAFSA form to simplify it, like streamlining 103 questions down to 20, but the result has been anything but simple.\par \par \uc2 Glitches and technical delays have plagued the forum`s rollout for millions of students. And just last week, the Education Department announced an aid calculation error that could impact hundreds of thousands of families.\par \par \uc2 The new application was launched months later than normal with many students up against the traditional May 1st deadline to choose a school, summer now pushing back their commitment date like \uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 KARA TURNER (Morgan State University Enrollment Management and Student Success senior vice president) There`s a lot that has to happen between the time a student says yes, I want to come and the time that they actually are sitting in class.\par \par \uc2 They need to apply for housing. They need to figure out how they`re going to pay college, enroll in a payment plan. They have to get registered for classes.\par \par \uc2 So all of that is shortened now.\par \par \uc2 JAMES RICHARD KVAAL (Under secretary of Education): Well, it`s certainly been a rocky launch to the FAFSA this year.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: James Kvaal is an undersecretary of education.\par \par \uc2 JAMES RICHARD KVAAL: We know how important the FAFSA is in helping low and middle-income students choose the college that is best going to serve their needs, and our overarching goal is to get the financial aid offers in their hands as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The mess adds strain to an already tense time for students who want to avoid drowning in student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Is this starting to get nerve racking?\par \par \uc2 PAIGE FOGLE: It is. I can`t -- every time I see the cost sheet of tuition costs and fees, I don`t know what to do. I am kind of just stuck.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The US Education Department tells CBS News, it is sending staff and experts to about a hundred schools nationwide to deal with the backlog and crunch, but Nate, they also acknowledged, overall aid applications are down. That`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  of Pennsylvania will join us next to talk all the big issues.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s 7:30 CBS MORNINGS, and one year ago, Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  of Pennsylvania walked out of Walter Reed National Medical Center after seeking treatment for depression and also following a stroke in 2022, a lot going on with him.\par \par \uc2 But since then, he has been on his own two feet, an outspoken critic and commentator on a number of issues, not always what some of his supporters would want him to say and what they want to hear.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Yes, it is great to be here. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We should point out you have the iPad in front of you and a stenographer working over there in the corner, the lingering effects of the stroke mean that sometimes audio processing is difficult.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): It is and it`s really just about captioning. You know, for -- it`s really the perfect example is like you`re wearing glasses, and it allows somebody to read more efficiently. And this is what this is will do.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, after the stroke, it didn`t have any impact on my intellect, but processing language. This allows me to be more precise, when we`re doing interviews and things and situations just like this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It makes sense.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it`s good to know your intellect is fine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, let`s talk about one year on here. I mean, that`s why you came on today. You walk out of Walter Reed.\par \par \uc2 At the time you went in thinking of harming yourself, thinking there was worthlessness in your life. You didn`t know how you could help your family, let alone the people of Pennsylvania. One year later, where are you? How are things going?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I`m grateful. And I`m just so happy to be in a much different situation right now.\par \par \uc2 I have the opportunity to sit with all of you today to talk about mental health, and how incredibly important that is to do that. And I didn`t think I was going to be in that same place when I was coming out there.\par \par \uc2 When I decided to check myself in, I thought this could be the end of my career, and I may have even lost my family. But to having all of that and to be able to have the conversation whether or not it`s a political winner, but it`s an important conversation that I think we really have to have in this nation and that`s what I`ve been able to do.\par \par \uc2 And I`m grateful for anyone to allow me to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we have some --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have to talk about news today, of course, but I`m curious about what turned it around for you before we get into --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And what advice do you have for those that might be hesitant to seek help?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): There`s no -- get help. It works. And if you`re skeptical, I was too, like, oh, this doesn`t change, you know, like nothing`s going to help, but it did and it works.\par \par \uc2 And I`m just -- I`m very grateful to have that opportunity. And I would want to have that same kind of opportunity for anyone that is suffering, but I`d also want to remind that that is a red county problem and that`s a blue county one, and it`s like, and if it may not be you, then it`s somebody that you love, somebody that you know, somebody that you`re work with that they`re having these kinds of issues, and we really need to have the conversation and to make that part of health overall, because that is just part of the health conversation overall.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So let`s have a hard conversation about Israel and Gaza and what`s going on there.\par \par \uc2 Everybody agrees Israel has a right to defend itself. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacked them. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has to go. It is the means and the methods that people are in agony over.\par \par \uc2 They see the thousands of dead children, the thousands of dead civilians, and they wonder, is there no better way to do this, Israel?\par \par \uc2 I know you support Israel`s efforts, do you see an alternative?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Yes. No. Well, it is absolutely tragic. It`s heartbreaking. And you know, I do not -- I don`t assign any more value in the life of a Palestinian child than my own children and I see that kind of a pain and it`s a war and it`s absolutely terrible.\par \par \uc2 And I -- we have to -- what`s going on and where \uc2 Hamas\uc2  now is hiding behind civilians and things, and Israel as you said that they have the right to defend themselves and to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , I think that is that is critical.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think everybody wants \uc2 Hamas\uc2  destroyed, but I think the position Netanyahu seems to be taking, and I`m wondering how you feel about him these days -- is that destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2  at all cost.\par \par \uc2 You know, they say most of the -- many of the victims now are women and children. Clearly very, very innocent. I know what you`re saying no life is better than the other, we understand that.\par \par \uc2 But at what point do you say we have to come up with another strategy that at least keeps the innocent, the women, and the children safe?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): It is very important to minimize the kind of collateral damage and protecting civilization lives as well and I would hope that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  decides to not start to hide behind civilians and creating those circumstances that makes it even more difficult as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that seems to be their strategy, too, Senator to hide behind the innocent people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Yes, that is their strategy and now even in hospitals or in the tunnels and everything and we also can`t forget about the hostages as well, too. They`ve been in custody now since October 7th. So it`s an awful situation.\par \par \uc2 And you don`t have to agree -- you don`t have to agree with every leaders` views, but you know, we have an opportunity to stand with Israel in this situation and that`s what I`ve decided to do.\par \par \uc2 And the other --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is Netanyahu the right person at this particular time?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There seems to be a lot of incoming for him as well.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Yes, well, absolutely. And then, of course, the tragedy of the awful situation where the aid workers were killed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Seven aid workers, yes.\par \par \uc2 I want to shift gears to the presidential election. a recent poll from "The Wall Street Journal" shows that Trump leads in six of the seven swing states, with that margin of error being so slim, what does Biden and the Biden administration and the campaign need to do moving forward?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): You know, I am having a real, honest conversation about this and I really do believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to win, and I believe that because Americans now have to confront this. It`s a very stark choice right now.\par \par \uc2 Do you want chaos? Or do you want to have a decent man who has done and got has through the pandemic, addressed inflation. Now has waded in on this war in Gaza. And now, I really believe we have a very stark choice here.\par \par \uc2 And I think when America comes to the polls, they`re going to decide that they don`t want -- I mean, this this -- like, were we better four years ago? I mean, has people forgotten a lot of bad things that have happened four years ago, as well too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Senator, the people that support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do not think he is chaotic. In fact, they think he is saving America, that he is making America a better place. Certainly, when it comes to the issue of immigration. They don`t think he`s chaotic.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): No, well, what is -- well, it happens to be true that there is a situation on the border, and we do have to address that and it is incredibly disingenuous that they walked away from that border deal that we all worked on that --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Would you call it more than a situation?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I would call -- I would call it a crisis. Absolutely. Hundreds of thousands of people are showing up and they`re desperate to come to our nation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is an executive action needed?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Well, I`m not sure. What -- I think we need as a comprehensive solution as well. I think that`s what`s necessary as well, too. And I believe that I want to have that conversation. And we can perhaps make it even more -- more stronger than what the border deal as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): And you know, my wife, and she`s here with me today. I mean, she was a former DREAMer. I am absolutely supportive of immigration, and I think two things are true at the same time that we can be very supportive of immigration and celebrate immigration, but we also need a more secure border. And I think we need to achieve that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Katie Britt, Republican senator from Alabama. She has fallen under a lot of criticism after her rebuttal to the State of the Union, people were making fun of her, people were saying mean things about her.\par \par \uc2 You actually reached out to her when you were in Walter Reed, invited her to visit. She did visit with you. Why did you want to do that? And what`s the message that the two of you have about I guess, bipartisanship and also support for people with mental health problems?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Yes, yes. Well, she actually did visit me at Walter Reed we`ve been friends and she has a beautiful family as well, and people don`t realize what`s behind the headlines as well.\par \par \uc2 And I -- her children were visiting DC as with their whole class, and this was all before all of that happened with the union state speech, and their children, I saw the kind of pain that they were in -- having all of this happen and my own children were part of the headlines, the national headlines, and nothing is so heartbreaking when children are like, please don`t -- I don`t want to go to school today because all of that.\par \par \uc2 And people have to remember that there`s always going to be kids in those situations that they never signed up for that kind of a situation as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): And I would never pile on and I would just acknowledge that it was a difficult situation and the family are actually part of all of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and I`m sure she appreciated it. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Senator Fetterman, thank you so much for joining us. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, hundreds of recording artists from Billy Eilish to Katy Perry to Nicki Minaj take a stand against what they call a predatory artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A long list of performers including some of the biggest names in music have signed an open letter calling for an end to what they call predatory use of artificial intelligence in the industry.\par \par \uc2 This follows repeated high-profile uses of AI in songs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Beatles)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: From a 2023 Beatles song using AI to clean up an old John Lennon recording --\par \par \uc2 AI GENERATED: I got my heart on a sleeve with a knife in my back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To the now removed fake Drake song mimicking the singer`s voice, artificial intelligence has already infiltrated the modern music industry.\par \par \uc2 But now more than 200 artists including Nicki Minaj, Noah Kahan, and the estates of Bob Marley and Frank Sinatra are standing against what they call its unchecked usage.\par \par \uc2 An open letter posted Monday by the Artists Rights Alliance nonprofit acknowledges the creative potential of AI, but calls on developers, tech companies and platforms to stop using it to infringe upon and devalue the rights of human artists.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It comes as Tennessee Governor Bill Lee signed a first of its kind bill last month prohibiting people from using AI to copy an artist`s voice without permission. A move applauded by many including country star, Luke Bryan.\par \par \uc2 LUKE BRYAN (Singer): Stuff comes in my voice on my phone and I can`t tell it`s not me. It`s a real big deal now. Hopefully, this will curb it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Forensic musicologist, Joe Bennett says many artists believe AI has a place in music`s future.\par \par \uc2 JOE BENNETT (Forensic musicologist): They are just really trying to find a way to incorporate AI in related technologies into the music industry in a way that doesn`t harm the livelihood of creators.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There`s certainly a place for it, but when an artist themselves says they can`t recognize whether it`s their voice or artificial intelligence --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- you`ve got to get ahead of the problem.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the problem. Not good at all. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not good at all.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next the stories that you`ll be talking about today, but first, it is 7:45, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Kacey Musgraves from our news -- well, coming up in our eight o`clock hour, only on CBS MORNINGS, we love when that happens, we`re going to tell you the National Teacher of the Year. I just met this person in the greenroom.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, actor, Asher Grodman from "Ghosts" is here. Hit is one of our favorite shows in the room. He plays the one who is not wearing any pants. Tony did him one year for Halloween. He did a good job, too.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Religiously")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Jan Crawford talks to CMT Award nominee, that`s Bailey Zimmerman about how he became a country star, it seems almost overnight. He`s got a great backstory.\par \par \uc2 So that`s coming up in our next hour. But right now, Vlad and I heading over to the table because Vlad -- not -- Klaus and I.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Klaus, that`s Klaus.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Klaus.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Klaus and Vlad look so much alike.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, should I do a pantless tease with that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think Tony should be pantless in the greenroom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Klaus and I at the table because we`re going to do --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hot, hot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be right back -- with pants or without?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, it is time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 Lizzo fans, we are