udge says they can move forward to trial.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Move forward, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, we will reveal the National Teacher of the Year and talk with the educator who is being honored.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, a man who spends most of his time on TV with no pants on. Who? And what will he wear?\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, it is one of our favorite days of the year. Why? Because only on CBS MORNINGS, we`re going to reveal the National Teacher of the Year, and the winner will be right here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Actor Asher Grodman will be here in studio. He plays the pantless Trevor in "Ghosts," one of my family`s favorite shows. He`ll tell us what it`s like to work half naked.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Country star, Bailey Zimmerman has been on a journey from blue-collar worker to CMT Music Award nominee. Jan Crawford talks to him about his meteoric rise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember his first hit. But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: A massive earthquake rocks Taiwan toppling buildings, and the death toll is rising.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It just wanted -- it was trying to suck you out of the building.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another day of severe weather causes damage in multiple states, and the system isn`t done.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: The biggest threat will be the damaging winds from the Delmarva to Florida. We could also have an isolated tornado and hail.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Israel faces growing outrage for the air strikes that killed seven \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  aid workers.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 : We shouldn`t have a situation where people who are simply trying to help their fellow human beings are themselves at grave risk.\par \par \uc2 DERMOT O`LEARY (British TV host): Brilliant. Thanks, Don.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HAMMOND (British TV host): Thanks, Don.\par \par \uc2 DERMOT O`LEARY: The thing is out --\par \par \uc2 ALISON HAMMOND: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And British TV host, Dermot O`Leary pulls off a viral April Fools` Day prank on co-host, Allison Hammond, pretending to break a guest`s expensive vase.\par \par \uc2 DERMOT O`LEARY: I`m so sorry. I`ll pay for it. I`m so sorry, everybody. I mean, I have only got one thing to say which is April Fool`s.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ALISON HAMMOND: You`ve actually done it. You are so going to pay for that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was that funny?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I think we should have gotten something together for Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I bet she is like -- yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we didn`t --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As a kid, April Fool`s was the most nerve-racking holiday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It was hilarious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You just knew something was coming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to start with severe weather spread across hundreds of miles for the second day in a row. High winds caused damage all across the South and Midwest, snapping trees and ripping up buildings in Kentucky, Ohio, and Indiana.\par \par \uc2 The system produced multiple tornadoes including one in Sunbright, Tennessee, which is northwest of Knoxville. That`s where we find Dave Malkoff today, and he is sitting there as people struggle to clean up.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 We are just less than an hour away from the National Weather Service coming here and figuring out how powerful was this tornado. How did they figure that out? Because they look at the damage like this building that came apart and crushed this car right here in Sunbright, a city of only about 500 people, so everybody knows each other.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the damage right here in Sunbright. You can see what it left behind. Here we can see roofs and windows, they are completely blown out, buildings and homes that are destroyed.\par \par \uc2 The debris left scattered across the street, trees uprooted from the ground, and that was the scene that thousands of people saw through several states.\par \par \uc2 Here in Louisville, Kentucky, where we saw strong winds that knocked over a semitruck, as you can see crews were busy turning over those trucks, and they had to get smaller vehicles as well that were tossed around.\par \par \uc2 It was a similar scene in Hanging Rock, Ohio, where strong winds leveled this RV park, just look at the utter devastation everywhere right here, tossing RVs to their side. The roofs hoisted off their structures, and adding to the misery, they were leaving thousands of people without power.\par \par \uc2 We`re without power here, and also there`s flooding. The storm hit the East Coast and it caused major flooding, inundating homes, and that overtook rivers.\par \par \uc2 So right now we have this storm that is actually pushing towards Boston. So Tony, we could see more devastation there. We could see flooding. We could see snow up there. So, it ain`t over yet. We have more to come.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope not. It`s bad enough already there, Dave.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, we`re going to reveal the National Teacher of the Year and talk with the winner about how we can all help teachers succeed.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus, actor, Asher Grodman who plays the pantless Trevor on the show "Ghosts" will give us a sneak peek at that week`s episode.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for a check of your local weather. It is 8:04.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rock and a Hard Place")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is the hit song, "Rock and A Hard Place," by rising country music star, that`s Bailey Zimmerman on your screen right there.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, he goes backstage with our Jan Crawford to talk about his meteoric rise.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, and guess what? We`re on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve been talking about this person all morning long. We are so psyched about this because it`s one of our favorite moments of the year. Don`t we say, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We really do love this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t we say, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of our favorite moments, and only on CBS MORNINGS, we love when this happens, too, we are revealing the National Teacher of the Year.\par \par \uc2 This year`s winner is -- I wish we had sound effects -- our drum roll, please -- welcome, Missy Testerman. She is a longtime elementary school teacher at Rogersville City School, that`s in Rogersville, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 We`re happy to tell you that Missy has been a teacher for more than 30 years. She`s also currently the English as a Second Language Program Director.\par \par \uc2 Missy, we`re so glad to have you here to say congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN (2024 National Teacher of the Year): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So when we say National Teacher of the Year and then it`s followed by your name, what goes through your head at that moment?\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: The first thing is, is this real?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Missy, it is real.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: The next thing is it`s just an amazing honor to be named the National Teacher of the Year. There are 3.5 million teachers in this country, so to be named as the teacher who gets to advocate for our teachers and our students is just an incredible honor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But tell us why you love it so much. You.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: I love teaching because I love being the person who gets to unlock the love of learning in students.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: And I did that in the regular classroom for 30 years teaching first and second grade. And now I get to work with our school`s English language learn --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But guess what, Missy? The way I look at it, we`re still always learning.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, we are always students, so we are always going to need you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you should know this, we spoke to people who care about you, people back home and they wanted to tell us why they believe that you deserve this honor of the year.\par \par \uc2 Please roll the tape, Katherine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: I love being a teacher.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SMITH (Colleague, former student assistant): I first met Missy when I was in the seventh grade. I walked in that room when I was eleven, twelve. When I`m 40, it doesn`t matter. She makes everyone feel important.\par \par \uc2 MARIAM EL KAMMASH (Former student): Whenever I was in first grade, my cousins, both of them had had Miss Testerman in second grade. She was really nice. She really believed in us.\par \par \uc2 ALI AGLAN (Former student): She was the first teacher I had whenever I came back from Egypt. After second grade, I actually remembered how to speak English.\par \par \uc2 NADEEN AGLAN (Former student): I was in kindergarten, that was like my first memory, just being excited to have her.\par \par \uc2 And then the joke after that was that she couldn`t retire until she had my little sister.\par \par \uc2 JASMEN AGLAN (Former student): Miss Testerman helped us feel more welcome when my brother started school. She`s really nice to my family.\par \par \uc2 BRIDGET BAIRD (Parent of former student, friend): She cares about her students -- their home, their life, their family life, as well as their academic life.\par \par \uc2 ALLIE JUNE MCMAKIN (Former student): My dad passed away right after first grade. During the summer, she came and took me from my mom like to give my mom breaks.\par \par \uc2 She had young kids herself, so she added another kid. She didn`t have to do that. She just made sure that I was taken care of.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE HICKS (Former student): She`s really kind to others, and every time I see her in the hallway, she always makes me smile all the time, and I really like that.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SMITH: She`s a great person who makes the kids feel welcome, makes them feel proud of themselves. But holds them to an extremely high standard. She`s the best.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY RUSSELL (Colleague, parent of former student): She`s taught children that actually want to come into this profession, so that`s wonderful because there`s a shortage of teachers at this time.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER EWING (Colleague, parent of former student): I spent some time really watching her teach. It really inspired me to not only be a teacher, but I wanted to did it like how she was doing it.\par \par \uc2 NADEEN AGLAN: She lets kindness and compassion shine through anything she does, and she is an amazing person.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY RUSSELL: I can always count on her, and I know that our nation can count on her as being a wonderful spokesperson for our education system.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow, Missy. I can tell you`re very touched. You knew all the people in that tape.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: I know, and love all of the people in that tape.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. What does it mean to hear them -- to speak about you that way?\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: It`s very, very touching. I just feel so incredibly blessed that I`ve been able to spend my career at my school, Rogersville City School, in Rogersville, Tennessee is the absolute best place to teach, it is the absolute best place to get --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But to know the difference that you`ve made in people`s lives - - when did you know that you wanted to be a teacher?\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: I think I knew that I wanted to be a teacher the minute that I walked into Mrs. Brown`s room in kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is Mrs. Brown?\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: She was my kindergarten teacher.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kindergarten. Wow.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: I was very scared, and she took me by the hand, led me over to where my classmates and the toys were, and from that moment on, I wanted to be like Mrs. Brown.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mrs. Brown.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s emotional for me to watch the effect you`ve had on kids. So I can only imagine what it`s like for you. It is such a meaningful profession.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony`s mom is a teacher.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My mom`s a teacher and I was texting my mom before the segment and I was like, we`re going to have the teacher of the year on. What should I ask her? What`s the biggest issue for teachers?\par \par \uc2 She is retired now. She said pay. It`s pay. It really is. We have so many people who want to do what you do.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But they can`t make that decision because they just need to make a little more money.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And some who have made the decision are forced out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because of the money.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we have those people, as well.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Yes. I maintain that there`s not a teacher shortage. A lot of times you hear there`s a teacher shortage. We have a lot of trained teachers out there, people who were even in our classrooms who were highly effective educators. But they left because they need to make more money for their families.\par \par \uc2 The average new college grad coming out of an educator program makes 25 percent less than most other career fields, and so we have young people who are still enrolling, getting a teaching degree, but they`re choosing to do other things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Other things.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Due to the pay differential.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Miss Testerman, there are so many things to talk to you about, so we`d like you to stay. I checked with your team, they said you could stay. That you could stay.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we want to continue --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re not in trouble. We just want you to stay after class.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, not in trouble. That`s good, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got to stay.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We want you to stay after class.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When we come back, we`re going to take a break and continue our conversation with the National Teacher of the Year. We`re thinking we need to come up with a surprise. We`ll come up with a surprise in the break just for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, we`re working on something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re working on something.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we`ve got something cooking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Up in the morning and off to school.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the National Teacher of the Year, that`s Missy Testerman. She is from Rogersville, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 We kept her here because we have a very special surprise just for you, Miss Testerman.\par \par \uc2 We have somebody here who wanted to personally come to New York City and say congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 Could we bring out our special guest, please? We need some trumpets or something. And she`s bringing flowers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yay.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN (First Lady of the United States): Hi, Missy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dr. Jill Biden, our First Lady.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: This is amazing. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Thank you so much and thank you for coming. This is incredible.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You may sit there, Miss Testerman.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Biden, please. We`re very excited to have you here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Oh, thanks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What a surprise -- so that`s the surprise you were working on, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. We`ve been working on that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that all we`ve got?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dr. Biden, why did you want to be here personally? Listen, we know you are very busy, too.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Well, I`m a teacher as you well know.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: And I`ve been teaching over 30 years, just like Missy has and I always say teachers are our heroes, and I wanted to be here today to celebrate Missy as I love celebrating teachers, and I have a special announcement, Missy.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Ooh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: When you come to the White House, we are going to have a state dinner for the teachers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. That`s huge.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: You`ll have to pick your dress.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: So, it is the first time ever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s a huge deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let me say, have you ever done that before?\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: No. It has never been done and we want to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I wonder whose idea that was. I wonder whose idea that was. What do you have to say, Miss Testerman? You walk out and there`s the First Lady -- the First Lady walks out bearing flowers for you.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Yes. That is -- that is absolutely amazing. I`m incredibly touched. I know how devoted you are to teaching, continuing to teach even when you`re the First Lady of the United States. First one ever to do that.\par \par \uc2 And I know that we share the joy that there is to be found in education.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: We do. We do. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Our State Teacher of the Year cohort is made up of 55 of the most talented, amazing people, and they are going to be so incredibly excited to hear --\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: To hear this news.\par \par \uc2 So thank you for doing this for us.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: It`s one of my favorite events at the White House when we bring in the Teachers of the Year and we celebrate them every year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I was talking to Miss Testerman before we went on the air, and we were talking about children. You said there`s no such thing as bad kids. What did you tell me about bad kids?\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: There are no such thing as bad students. There may be kids who are having bad days or kids who are struggling with the circumstances they`re in, but there is no such thing as a bad kid.\par \par \uc2 Everyone can have a bad day, and you can always start over a little later in the day. You can start over the next day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Biden --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that philosophy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, definitely. Speaking of struggling, we find more and more -- Tony just mentioned it -- that these teachers struggle because of burnout --\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Low pay. What can we do to help retain these heroes that we all deeply appreciate and need so much?\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Well, I think it`s up to the teachers who are already in the classroom to recruit other teachers and to mentor them because that`s exactly what Missy does, she mentors other teachers. And I think it`s so important because we understand what a typical day is like and what our students are like and what they need.\par \par \uc2 And it is true, students are struggling with mental health issues since the pandemic, and so are teachers because the one thing you have to know is our students trust us and they come to us every day with what they`re struggling with, and they expect us to help them and find answers.\par \par \uc2 And I think we have such a strong bond with our students.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: And it`s such a great part of the job that we -- that connection, don`t you think, Missy?\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: That connection. That`s when learning takes place, when students realize you`re on their side, you want them to be able to create a good life for themselves. That`s when the learning takes place because they know that someone believes in them.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dr. Biden, we are so thrilled to have you here, taking a little break from the campaign trail, because you have stuff to do, I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s a lot going on, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a lot going on. You should know, people change -- I changed clothes three times today because the First Lady was coming. Our big cheese, Shawna Thomas wore a dress and make-up because the First Lady is in the house.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am wondering, did you change clothes? How many times because you knew you were going to meet Missy Testerman today? What did you do?\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Well, I just chose, you know, a typical outfit.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: You look great. We look like we`re headed to a baby shower.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How is the campaign going before we go?\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Oh my gosh. It`s going great. I have been traveling every day that I`m not in the classroom. People are excited. And I really feel like, you know, people know the choice this election. They can choose chaos or they can choose, you know, steady, wisdom, experience. So--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is not a part of you that`s a little worried because it seems to be off kilter a little bit.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: No. No. I feel that Joe will be re-elected.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But when these polls, like "The Wall Street Journal" one land in the White House and he is losing in the battleground states --\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: No, he is not losing in all the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All but one --\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: He is coming up and he is even or doing better. So you know what? Once people start to focus in and they see their two choices, it`s obvious that Joe will win this election.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, the computer is going to take us off. But we want to thank you guys so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Missy, congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Enjoy the White House.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dr. Biden, thank you very much for being here. We appreciate both so much.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning. Is this a good song?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a great song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You like what`s going on? All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. That`s why I`m grooving over here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate, Nate, I`m not in your way. You`re going first.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you love Tony going, is this a good song? It is a good song.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so have you ever gotten a late-night text or call from your boss? If so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- I need you to listen up right now. My "Talk of the Table" is a proposed new law in California. It would give workers the legal right, the legal right, to ignore after-hour work calls and texts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The so-called Right to Disconnect Law will be the first in the country. Employers would have to clear -- they would have to clearly define working hours and guarantee their workers that they can have uninterrupted personal and family time outside of those hours.\par \par \uc2 Now there would be some exemptions when it comes to this, including emergencies or scheduling issues. So the question I have for everyone at home, hit us up on social media and us here, do you like this?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it`s a good idea. I just think it`s unrealistic for us in the news business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You just -- you can`t just say I`m unavailable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, we`re working around the clock.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we really are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because the world is constantly changing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I think it`s a good idea, I actually do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, in general. I mean, a lot of people are working around the clock like plumbers get calls in the middle of the night, electricians get calls in the middle of night. Workers --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, they can`t be exempt because if your toilet is stopped, you want to see a plumber.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People get calls at all hours. Police, obviously.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First responders and all the rest, but for the general person, if you`re just working in marketing somewhere and not that your job is not important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But if you think it can wait until the next day, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And those that struggle with the work-life balance, I mean, you have burnouts, stress, depression, especially with women and parents.\par \par \uc2 So you need to disconnect, if your job allows you to, when you go home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, do you like it? Do you like that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I think for people in certain industries.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Industries.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But maybe we don`t need a law, just respect each other a little more. Maybe hold that text or call.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Or just ignore it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Make it -- hard to ignore when you see it though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" -- so, let`s see if they can tell you opened it.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is Taylor Swift. She is joining a very elite group. Have you heard of that Billionaire`s Club? She is now on "Forbes" Magazine list of billionaires.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With a net worth of $1.1 billion. This is for the Celebrity Billionaire Club. Basketball legend, you go, Magic Johnson, he is there, joining for the first time. Fashion designer, Christian Louboutin he`s got that red bottom shoe. "Law and Order" and "\uc2 FBI\uc2 " producer, Dick Wolf were also added to the list of celebrity billionaires this year.\par \par \uc2 They join a very elite group, we`ll say, Jay-Z is there. \uc2 Oprah\uc2  is there. Michael Jordan is there. Steven Spielberg, George Lucas is number one on the list.\par \par \uc2 But Swift, this is the thing about Taylor Swift. She is the first person to become a billionaire based solely on songwriting and performing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s according to "Forbes." So bravo, bravo, bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And at one point, didn`t she lose part of her catalogue because of what happened?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but she has clearly worked that out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She bounced back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And if people were saying, well, where`s Elon Musk? Where`s Jeff Bezos? We said this was a Celebrity Billionaires Club.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Celebrity. But those guys --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is different than "Forbes" richest people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think those guys --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good list.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those guys think they`re celebrities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I mean, they kind of are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, they are celebrities. But I mean --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bezos and Elon?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, but think about it, make a billion dollars for things you just you know -- things that came into your head, your creative process.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. I think that is truly amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s their power.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good for her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Taylor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She works hard. Good for her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Again, bravo, bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right for my "Talk of the Table" we are talking with someone who stars in one of my favorite \uc2 CBS\uc2  shows, "Ghosts." Asher Grodman plays Trevor, that`s the one with no pants, the `90s Wall Street bro, the pantless one. He`s just one of the ghosts that reside at the Woodstone Mansion, which has been turned into a bed and breakfast by owners Sam and Jay.\par \par \uc2 And in this week`s new episode, we meet Trevor`s brother, who is still alive.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, Asher Grodman joins us now.\par \par \uc2 You have some of the best lines in this show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are a lot of good lines.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, Tony, before he gets started. He just met Jill Biden, First Lady in the greenroom and he said, you know what, I used to be a teacher. Asher used to be a teacher.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN ("Ghosts" actor): Yes, yes, yes. That`s right. Yes, I was a substitute teacher in South Central LA for a while. It was like my side hustle until I got, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: To support myself as an actor, and then I was at Rikers Island and then Hunter College here in New York.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She got a kick out of hearing that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Teaching acting.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Teaching acting. Well, I was a substitute teacher, elementary school in South Central LA, and then teaching acting.\par \par \uc2 I`m sorry, I`m still buzzing. I can`t -- I follow the First Lady, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pressure.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Oh my God. Ugh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s tough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No pressure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So I was texting with my mom who is a retired teacher and she was like, teacher pay, bring it up, bring it up.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then the next text message was, but forget teachers. I`m excited about Asher Grodman.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She`s a "Ghosts" fan as well, all right? Not only in my household, all the way to mom.\par \par \uc2 What`s it been like with this new season?\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: You guys are --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We get to explore aspects of your character`s past.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: I mean, listen, shout out to our writers. This season is so creative, and they`re constantly reinventing the show. There`s some stuff coming up that`s going to blow your mind. I cannot wait for these episodes to come out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For people to see it.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just when I think you can`t do anything more creative on that show, you do something like bringing back a brother. It goes to a flashback when you guys were younger.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were kind of obnoxious back then, Asher.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Well, listen, if you grew up -- I bet you did, playing football video games with your siblings.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: This is the episode for you. I do that, it`s not always -- you know, everything is fair in love and war, especially around Madden.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was fun. It was fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you think makes this show so special? Because the premise when you first hear it, you`re like, really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What?\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: I think there`s something -- I think there`s something about -- we`re able to bring people together even if they`re separated by different points of view in different histories. And there`s something -- I heard this quote recently of like, I would never die for something I believe in because I could be wrong.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts")\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: And I feel like that it is very appropriate for all of these characters, is that we have these old ideas and juxtapose them against each other and there`s this is weird kind of found family thing that happens.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You also have experience writing and directing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Specifically for your favorite \uc2 NFL\uc2  team. We all talk about this, and we talk about it quite often.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 Jacksonville Jaguars\uc2  and the team schedule release video, you joke that the playbook is more like a script.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: I do.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Football is easy. Acting is hard. It`s not a threat, but anybody can be written out --\par \par \uc2 MAN: But you drafted me here.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Cast, don`t say drafted. Football may have gotten you here, but acting will keep you here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s your film you made.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: I was just nominated for three Webby Awards. So congrats on that.\par \par \uc2 Webby Awards Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Jacksonville Jaguars\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tell us about this.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Look at my guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can see you beaming with pride. That`s your team.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Listen, they took such a huge risk and this leap and they said, come, we want you to write and direct this thing. We want to do something that no one`s really ever done before and kind of reinvent the schedule release.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Jacksonville Jaguars\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: And it became a little bit of a trendsetter in the sports world with this whole \uc2 NFL\uc2  scripted.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everybody started doing it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Tennis is doing it now, and it was just such a dream and to see -- I mean, you know, to see these athletes in a light that you could never see them otherwise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: They`re playing themselves. I mean, that shot of Tyson Campbell, I mean, looking terrified.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The thing with Trevor Lawrence who is hilarious.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Trevor is great in that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a thing, there is line that says football is easy, acting is the thing that`s hard.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All you guys were.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: We`ve been saying that football got you here but acting will keep you here in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaking of acting, you`re kind of acting adjacent. I`ve got a technical kind of mechanical question about your pantlessness there on set, right?\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Oh sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you`re doing --\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Well, first of all, let me just say thank you for this Webby shoutout and please go vote. Watch the video.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How can people see it? How can people see it, Asher?\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: It`s on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . It`s on my socials. The Jaguars socials. Enjoy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you`re pantless. You`re acting, cut or scene, are you then putting on a windbreaker? A trench coat? Do you just go and sit down and everyone`s like ah. No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Asher.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts")\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes, that happens a lot and Rose especially complains. But there is a little bit of like getting into a cold pool that like once you get out, you kind of -- it`s worse. So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: And I tend to keep the pants off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, I see what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: We`ve talked about the rip away pants.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Someone`s going to have to button those things up, some poor soul, and I would never want someone to do that. So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Occasionally, sweatpants go on, bur for the most part, you`ve just got to kind of stay in the --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got to keep the set warm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you pull it off, Asher.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You pull it off.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have nightmares that you show up on set wearing pants?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The opposite.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not sure --\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: No, the other day I was -- I was like in the park and I was hot and I really came close to taking my pants off because you get a little too comfortable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s --\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: You ever get that normal --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can get arrested for that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They make a thing called shorts. You can use them.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you find yourself in that predicament again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You forget, I`m in public now. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHER GRODMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Asher Grodman, thank you very much. You can watch an all-new episode of "Ghosts" tomorrow night, 8:30/7:30 Central right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t forget the Webby. Don`t forget that. It`s hilarious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Jan Crawford talks with Bailey Zimmerman about his amazing journey from a blue-collar job to country music star, almost overnight. But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are gearing up for a big, big country music weekend with the CMT Music Awards right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And today we`re introducing you to one of country`s biggest new stars.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Religiously")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the music video for Bailey Zimmerman`s song, "Religiously," which is nominated for a CMT Award this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Zimmerman will also perform at the awards, the biggest stage in country music.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford recently met up with the 24-year-old ahead of two sold out shows at Hammerstein Ballroom in New York City. They went backstage and discussed the nontraditional way he broke into the business and how his career has taken off.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Religiously")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: No one is more shocked by Bailey Zimmerman`s meteoric rise than Bailey Zimmerman.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN (Singer): There`s like this thing, it is called awake in a dream.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Awake in a dream.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: Awake in a dream. This is not supposed to be real.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It was just around three years ago the 24-year-old was working a blue-collar job laying gas pipelines in West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bailey Zimmerman performance)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Almost overnight, Zimmerman`s life changed.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think that you are striking such a chord with people? What is it about?\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: I ask that every day, because I don`t know. I just write songs, and then I also cut other outside songs. And if that sends chills up my back, then it probably won`t my fans, too.\par \par \uc2 Like, when I first heard "Rock and A Hard Place," I was like, that`s it. Holy cow. That`s it, man.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rock and A Hard Place")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In 2022, Zimmerman`s song "Rock and A Hard Place" solidified his spot on the country music charts where it spent six consecutive weeks at number one.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Religiously")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In his first full record that came out last year, "Religiously" showed he had staying power. It was the biggest streaming country debut of all time.\par \par \uc2 But growing up in rural Southern Illinois, Zimmerman didn`t ever dream of becoming a country music star.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: I just would, like, party and be like karaoke and you know, and like having fun.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Did you know that you`re a good singer, or you just --\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: I was like, I can keep a tune with the radio, but I didn`t think it was like good. I just was like, yes, I guess I can sing. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Soon, there was no question after he posted a video singing on \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Can you just sing what you sang?\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: Oh, yes, it is like, sell my soul just to see your face.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And that was the first time.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: His buddies encouraged him to keep going. So in 2020, he wrote this song with a friend and put it on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bailey Zimmerman on TikTok)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And that was it.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: That was it. And then I quit my job the next day and told my mom.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You literally quit your job the next day.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: Yes, I did. I posted it like 3:00 AM, and then I woke up like 7:30 or eight. And it was like two million views, and I was like --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In that short a time.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: Yes. And I was like, whoa.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Never Comin` Home")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: That`s song "Never Comin` Home" would eventually become Zimmerman`s first single, and it`s been streamed almost 80 million times on \uc2 Spotify\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nashville says they want this, and Nashville tries to change you to be that, yet you did this all on your terms and then Nashville came to you.\par \par \uc2 You go from putting stuff on TikTok one day, and then a year later, you have a record deal with a major label.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: I just want to like preface with I have no idea what I`m doing. You have to take advice, but you also have to know where you`re at inside and know who you are and go, this is who I am now.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "This is Where it Ends")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You`re country, but you`re not country.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: I have more country in my pinky than almost I`d say 90 percent of the world, but I`m in the top 10, I can do some country stuff, okay?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And being himself -- sneakers, electric guitars, backward baseball caps and all is what people relate to.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: I don`t, like, dress country and I don`t -- like, I guess, my guitars aren`t that country sometimes. It`s like my music isn`t always country.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bailey Zimmerman performance)\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: It feels like country music is like embracing me and like accepting me and it`s cool. And now, I`ve got a CMT nom and big songs, and sold-out stuff. It`s a blessing. Very crazy. It is --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A blessing.\par \par \uc2 BAILEY ZIMMERMAN: It is such a blessing. It`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Where it Ends")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jan Crawford in New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How cool is that?\par \par \uc2 Now, you can watch the CMT Music Awards with host Kelsea Ballerini live this Sunday April 7th at 8/7 Central on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming live and demanded on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 And Gayle, you are going to be there, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa. Whoa. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going to be there, but Bailey Zimmerman, I picked him for one of my "Mixtape Moments" back in February of last year because I heard his voice and I saw the "Never Leave" was the song, the video.\par \par \uc2 There`s something about -- his enthusiasm to me just jumps off the screen. I love his backwards baseball cap. I love that he`s so cute, and I love that he`s so enthusiastic about what he is doing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I will be there on Sunday because I get to present.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is it just me? Or is country music evolving in a great way?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love country music.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More country in his little pinky than 90 percent of the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is one of those days where I feel we had a little bit for everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we really do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Politics, whether it was music, whether it`s Teacher of the Year, congratulations to you, Miss Testerman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And thank you First Lady, Dr. Jill Biden for coming by.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What a show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See you tomorrow on your favorite morning show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tomorrow is going to be better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240403ek4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for April 2, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Kris Van Cleave, Nancy Cordes, Elise Preston,  Holly Williams, Alex Wilson, Omar Villafranca, Justin Michaels, Nikki  Battiste, Amelia Mugavero \par \uc2 3,854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: tornado threat, the powerful spring storm bringing flash floods and strong winds, with the danger continuing late into the night, as cleanup begins after twisters touch down in the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DORIS PARKER (Barnsdall, Oklahoma, Resident): I was praying so hard that the lord would save me and my house.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We are coming on the air tracking areas bracing for severe weather tonight, including thunderstorms and tornadoes. At this hour, parts of the Ohio Valley, Indiana, Kentucky, and Ohio are under a tornado watch. Plus, tens of millions more are experiencing dangerous weather. This is the same fierce storm system that caused serious damage from Texas to West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky`s governor today declaring a state of emergency after twisters demolished homes and stopped traffic after knocking down power lines. A different tornado leveled buildings in Oklahoma, destroying nearly everything in its path. More than a third of the country right now is under a weather alert. There are blizzard warnings in Michigan and flood watches in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 So, with all of that news, we have got team coverage, starting with Justin Michaels from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS (National Correspondent, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): It happened fast, and it was furious. Dangerous winds knocked someone at the \uc2 University of Kentucky\uc2  clear off their feet.\par \par \uc2 Vicious storms cut a path of destruction across the Ohio Valley.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mindy Broughton)\par \par \uc2 MINDY BROUGHTON (Ohio Resident): Oh, dear God, this is bad.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: Mindy Broughton posted this video after whipping winds tossed and flipped mobile homes in Hanging Rock.\par \par \uc2 MINDY BROUGHTON: I thought we were going to die, but our camper is fine.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: In Columbus, two people were rescued after the Scioto River flooded.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR MIKE DEWINE (R-Ohio): We`re in a very dangerous, dangerous situation. People just need to be very, very careful.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: A possible tornado touched down late afternoon in Louisville, Kentucky, the governor of Kentucky declaring a state of emergency. Traffic on this highway ground to a halt after high winds blew down power lines, roofs blown off buildings in Nelson County, leaving debris scattered everywhere.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh (expletive deleted).\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: In West Virginia, some ran for cover, escaping the fast- moving storm just in time.\par \par \uc2 And this massive billboard came down on top of this vehicle in Dunbar, West Virginia. In Oklahoma, the same storm system left a mountain of debris yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is there.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Last night`s storm ripped through this neighborhood in Barnsdall, Oklahoma, just outside of Tulsa, and turned small buildings and homes into a pile of rubble like this behind me.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Weather\uc2  Service`s preliminary report says an EF-1 tornado with wind speeds up to 110 miles an hour caused all this damage. The tornado leveled 81-year-old Doris Parker`s storage shed, but spared her life.\par \par \uc2 DORIS PARKER (Barnsdall, Oklahoma, Resident): I was praying so hard that the lord would save me and my house. I didn`t think about asking him to save my garage.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: Back here in the Ohio Valley, people are also starting the recovery process after the storm.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: Authorities have been taking this storm very seriously today. In fact, school districts here in Kentucky and also in Ohio let out early today, out of an abundance of caution, making sure kids in school buses weren`t on the road during severe weather.\par \par \uc2 And that severe weather, Norah, is happening right now. Off in the distance, I hear tornado sirens.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow, that`s scary.\par \par \uc2 Justin Michaels, thank you very much for that update.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned, that severe weather far from over, so let`s bring in \uc2 Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist Alex Wilson.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Alex. So, what are you watching?\par \par \uc2 ALEX WILSON (Meteorologist, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Norah, it has been a very active day of severe weather, remaining dangerous through the evening and into the overnight hours.\par \par \uc2 TORCON`s a seven, especially up towards the Ohio Valley, where the tornado risk is especially high. Those storms remain strong through the later evening, even the overnight hours all the way across the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 So, while you`re sleeping, don`t be surprised to see those warnings come in. Have a way to get those alerts. Tomorrow morning, starting off on the soggy side. Heavy rain could lead to flooding. We have got flood watches, also the risk of flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, on the heels of the storms comes a snow threat second half of the week, Thursday into Friday, especially through portions of New England. Northern sections of New York and our New England states could be looking at totals that approach two feet -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there is outrage at the White House after an Israeli airstrike in Gaza killed at least seven aid workers from the \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 . President Biden called the organization`s founder, chef Jose Andres, to express his heartbreak today.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams reports on the response from Israel`s prime minister.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  says its team members were traveling through Central Gaza in three vehicles when they were killed by an Israeli strike. It appears their convoy was hit multiple times, including a direct strike to an armored car, though it was clearly marked with the group`s name.\par \par \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  says it was coordinating its movements with Israel`s military, which has promised to investigate what happened. The U.S.- Canadian victim hasn`t been named. Three British citizens were killed, an Australian woman, Zomi Frankcom, and a Polish man, Damian Sobol. Their Palestinian driver, Saif Abu-Taha, was buried today.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  called the incident tragic and unintentional in a statement, also saying -- quote:\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "This happens in war."\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): These people are heroes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The U.S. has demanded a swift and impartial investigation, with strong words for its ally.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: We have impressed upon the Israelis the absolute imperative of doing more to protect innocent civilian lives, be they Palestinian children, women and men or be they aid workers.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  was founded by Jose Andres, the award-winning Spanish-American chef, and feeds the hungry everywhere from the U.S. to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JOSE ANDRES (Founder, \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 ): Ukraine needs our help because we need them to win this war, because we need to be feeding the world.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "The Israeli government needs to stop this indiscriminate killing," Andres posted on social media last night, "and stop using food as a weapon."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Funded by donations, his charity has been shipping hundreds of tons of food to the Gaza Strip, even building a makeshift pier, and then distributing the aid by truck.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The United Nations says, at least 196 aid workers have been killed in Gaza and the West Bank since October.\par \par \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  says it`s decided to pause its operations in this region -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That will be devastating for so many.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden spoke with Chinese President Xi Jinping for nearly two hours today, amid tensions between the two world powers. It`s their first conversation since November, and President Biden says they spoke candidly about maintaining peace in Taiwan and the South China Sea. The leaders also discussed TikTok and the flow of fentanyl entering the U.S. and election meddling.\par \par \uc2 Now to the 2024 race for the White House. President Biden`s campaign is hitting the airwaves with a hard-hitting political ad focusing on abortion rights. And they`re using Donald Trump`s own words in an effort to warn voters.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes reports, the former president is on the campaign trail in the Midwest, where he`s focusing on another wedge issue, immigration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Joe Biden`s border bloodbath, and that`s what it is. It`s a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): Despite criticism from both sides, former President Trump used the word bloodbath again today, this time to describe crimes committed by migrants.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: The Democrats say, please don`t call them animals. They`re humans. I said, no, they`re not humans. They`re not humans. They`re animals.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It was two-and-a-half weeks ago, while talking about the future of the U.S. auto industry, that Trump warned of a bloodbath if he isn`t elected in November.\par \par \uc2 Today, his campaign went a step further, launching an immigration-related Web site, though there is no evidence that undocumented migrants commit violent crimes at higher rates than U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 What`s the White House reaction to the use of that term bloodbath?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): Our response is, we have to denounce any -- any violent rhetoric that we hear, certainly from our leaders, right?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Biden campaign was focused today on abortion, launching this new ad --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Biden campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: -- one day after Florida`s Supreme Court cleared the way for a six-week abortion ban to go into effect in the state next month.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Biden campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t trust women. I do.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The state court also allowed an abortion rights measure to appear on Florida ballots come November. The Biden camp hopes that will help drive Democrats there to the polls, just as similar initiatives have in other states.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I will be voting this November. If I have to be in there with my crutches or my wheelchair, I will be there.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump was campaigning in another key state, Michigan, where he brought up the $175 million bond he just posted in his New York civil fraud case.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m the only one that has to put up a bond. You know, I put up a bond. I didn`t do anything wrong.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes is with us.\par \par \uc2 A hundred and seventy-five million dollars, a lot of money. What do we know about the man who gave Trump this financial lifeline?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Norah, he is a Los Angeles billionaire. His name is \uc2 Don Hankey\uc2 . He is known as the king of subprime auto loans.\par \par \uc2 And this isn`t the first time he has come to Trump`s financial rescue when many others wouldn`t. It has happened at least twice before when Trump was overleveraged, including in 2022, when he gave Trump a loan to refinance Trump Tower.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, following the money, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there are growing questions about the police shooting of a 15- year-old girl on the side of a California highway in 2022. Newly released video appears to show the teenager attempting to surrender before being killed in a shoot-out.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston reports, California`s Department of Justice is now investigating.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): It`s taken a year-and-a-half for this video to be made public, one that appears to show deputies gunning down the victim of a kidnapping.\par \par \uc2 Deputies had just cornered the father of 15-year-old Savannah Graziano in a high-speed chase. Officers had been on the hunt for her father, Anthony, who allegedly murdered her mother and abducted the girl.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: She`s out on the passenger side.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Tense moments, as Savannah gets out of the truck. Deputies are heard coaxing her to safety, when it all went horribly wrong.\par \par \uc2 (Audio clip from Deputy Audio Belt Recording)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Come to me! Come to me! Come, come, come, come, come! Walk, walk, walk, walk, walk, walk, walk!\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Hey!\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Stop! Stop shooting her! He`s in the car! Stop!\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And from one officer monitoring from above --\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For months, the only official version was what the San Bernardino County sheriff said just after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON DICUS (San Bernardino County, California, Sheriff): Preliminarily, again, there may be some indications that the passenger of the vehicle, which we believe is Savannah, may have been also involved in some of the fire exchange.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The indication was, Savannah could have fired at deputies. But this video and audio just released under the California Public Records Act shows she was trying to get to safety, while apparently shot by those tasked with trying to save her.\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The \uc2 California Department of Justice\uc2  is investigating the shooting and how this could have even happened.\par \par \uc2 One retired police sergeant told CBS Los Angeles that, in the chaos:\par \par \uc2 PATRICK O`MALLEY (Retired Police Sergeant): You had deputies over here that are firing at the truck. So you had a crossfire situation also.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Tonight, Savannah`s family says they`re too distraught to talk about the tragedy. But they want answers on how deputies are trained for incidents like this.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we`re getting our first look at the broken and twisted wreckage -- wreckage of the Francis Scott Key Bridge that`s under the surface of the water, this as crews just opened a second channel to allow small barges and tugboats to move through the port.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave has the latest on this daunting mission.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): New sonar images show what can`t be seen from the surface, up to 90 percent of the Francis Scott Key Bridge sunk to the bottom of the shipping channel, leaving a maze of twisted, mangled steel and concrete, work made more challenging by the storm pounding the Eastern U.S. Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 COL. ESTEE PINCHASIN (\uc2 U.S. Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 ): The wreckage on the bottom of the 50-foot channel is far more extensive than -- than we could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But signs of progress. The first ship passed through one of two temporary channels now open. A larger third one is planned, but none will be able to handle large cargo ships, leaving one of the country`s biggest ports largely shut down and 8,000 jobs in limbo.\par \par \uc2 FABRIZIO SCOTTO (Manager, Vinny`s Cafe): It`s been a little quiet.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Vinny`s Cafe relies on those port workers. Fabrizio Scotto is the manager.\par \par \uc2 FABRIZIO SCOTTO: It`s like a chain effect, really, you know, not just us. All the businesses around are -- I`m sure are really feeling it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Dozens of local businesses are already applying for low- interest loans of up to $2 million to cover lost revenue stemming from the bridge collapse.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE (D-Maryland): We can try to provide some measure of certainty at a time that just feels very uncertain.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: While the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigation is expected to take 12 to 24 months, the Singapore-based owner of the cargo ship Dali that struck the bridge last week filed a motion in federal court asking a Baltimore judge to rule it`s not liable for any loss or damage from the fatal crash and destruction of the Key Bridge.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The work at what`s left of the bridge continues as a 24/7 effort.\par \par \uc2 Now, they`re working very hard at getting that third channel back open. The governor says that`s critical, because it will allow smaller commercial vessels to access the port. Each one of those ships that gets through supports a hundred port jobs, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Very important.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A man is diagnosed with a rare case of bird flu. How it`s linked to dairy cows and what it means for the country`s milk supply, we have got those details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, a dairy worker in Texas has tested positive for bird flu after being in contact with sick cows who got the highly contagious virus from migrating ducks and geese.\par \par \uc2 The CDC telling CBS News there has never been human-to-human spread in the U.S., but they are stepping up their posture and being vigilant.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Amelia Mugavero reports on what impact the outbreak could have on the country`s milk supply.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMELIA MUGAVERO (voiceover): Tonight, Texas officials are warning dairy farmers to take precautions after cases of bird flu have been found in cows for the first time in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 MARTY MCKINZIE (COO, Dairy MAX): They`re identifying animals who may be at risk of any symptoms, and those animals are being removed from the herd to be monitored and treated separately.\par \par \uc2 AMELIA MUGAVERO: Now the Texas Department of Health revealing the highly infectious virus has spread to a dairy worker who was working with sick cows.\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN RIVERS (Epidemiologist, Johns Hopkins Center for Health Security): There was one human case in a dairy farmer, someone with close contact with an infected cow. Influenza is one of the viruses that likes to change and mutate, and we know that it has the potential, the virus has the potential to cause a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 AMELIA MUGAVERO: The patient had conjunctivitis, was told to isolate, and is being treated with Tamiflu.\par \par \uc2 Five states are reporting dairy cows have tested positive for bird flu since the end of March, suggesting it`s likely being transmitted within cattle herds. The CDC says the risk of humans getting bird flu is low, and the \uc2 USDA\uc2  tells CBS News, although the situation is rapidly evolving, consumers shouldn`t worry, saying there is no concern about the safety of the commercial milk supply.\par \par \uc2 SID MILLER (Texas Agriculture Commissioner): Consumers need to know that none of the milk entered the food chain. It wouldn`t have mattered anyway, because we pasteurize milk, which kills all the pathogens, and we haven`t destroyed enough milk to make a dent in the milk supply, so the prices of dairy products should remain stable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 AMELIA MUGAVERO: Now, the \uc2 USDA\uc2  is not issuing an official quarantine, but is really urging dairy farms like the one behind me to really limit the movement and transporting of cows.\par \par \uc2 And even this dairy farm is restricting access to the public and only allowing workers and dairy farmworkers on its property -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That is some good news about the milk supply.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Amelia Mugavero, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, a former hangout of Hollywood stars, the legendary Tropicana Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas, closes its doors after 67 years. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: An American teenager was among three people killed Monday in an avalanche at a luxury resort in the Swiss Alps.\par \par \uc2 The body of a 15-year-old boy was recovered, along with an unidentified man and woman. A 20-year-old survivor was seriously injured and airlifted to a hospital.\par \par \uc2 It was a historic day on the Las Vegas Strip. The Tropicana, once synonymous with glitz and glamour, closed its doors for good after welcoming guests and gamblers since 1957. In its heyday, it was a hangout for the likes of Elizabeth Taylor, Debbie Reynolds, Frank Sinatra, and Sammy Davis Jr.\par \par \uc2 The iconic hotel even appeared --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Diamonds are Forever, 1971, United Artists)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- in the 1971 James Bond movie "Diamonds Are Forever." But, in Sin City, few things are forever, and the Tropicana will be imploded this fall to make way for a new Major League Baseball stadium. Wow. What will that do for traffic?\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next with a hot meal and friendly faces at a restaurant staffed by people with developmental disabilities.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, our Heart of America on this World Autism Awareness Day.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the seven million Americans with intellectual and developmental disabilities have a hard time finding work.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Battiste takes us to one restaurant serving up good meals and opportunities.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voiceover): Serving coffee at Cafe Joyeux in New York City has changed Rachel Barcellona`s life.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BARCELLONA (Cafe Joyeux Employee): I have been rejected so many times and told I was a burden because of my autism.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Now 27 years old, Rachel learned she is autistic when she was 3. Despite being crowned Miss Florida for America last year, she says she`s had a tough time finding a job.\par \par \uc2 Take me to the moment you found out you got this job.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BARCELLONA: I was in shock. I just never had anyone believe in me like that before. And I was going to get paid, too.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Cafe Joyeux`s mission is to promote the inclusion of men and women in the community by hiring and training them to work in its cafes by managers like Sarah Parkes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH PARKES (Head Trainer and Assistant General Manager, Cafe Joyeux): You know, some of our crew are really talkative and cheerful and welcome you as soon as you come to the door. Some are a little bit more shy.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Founded by a French social entrepreneur, there are 20 Cafe Joyeuxs across Europe, all employing people who are neurodivergent. This is the first in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BARCELLONA: That`s the whole point of Cafe Joyeux is to give us the opportunities to never give up.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: What does joyeux mean?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BARCELLONA: Joyful.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Joy is exactly what they are serving up here.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All Cafe Joyeux profits go toward training and employing men and women with disabilities. And that`s why they`re tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240403ek4200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 26, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Carter Evans, Jan Crawford, Ramy Inocencio, Kris  Van Cleave \par \uc2 3,679 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BALTIMORE CITY FIRE: The entire Key Bridge has fallen into the harbor.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The catastrophic bridge collapse in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yo, what the (Expletive Deleted)!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: the search for answers and the missing, as a construction company says six of its workers are presumed dead. What we`re learning about the vessel`s loss of power and that mayday call that may have saved lives.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This bridge carries some 30,000 people every day. It is a major commuter route. And now it is completely shut down.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell. And thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We are here tonight not far from that catastrophic collapse of the Francis Scott Key Bridge in Baltimore. The devastation is beyond words, with this massive structure in ruins, twisted and broken. By daylight, the extent of what happened here became more clear.\par \par \uc2 The video is stunning, and it tells us a lot about what happened after the ship lost power and collided with the bridge. Tonight, we`re also learning more about the survivors and the missing. Construction workers were fixing potholes on this heavily traveled -- traveled bridge in the early hours of the morning, the community here devastated, many telling us this view will never be the same, with the bridge that has stood for decades no longer in the skyline.\par \par \uc2 A state of emergency now issued by Maryland`s governor, and everyone from the federal government to local law enforcement pitching in to help a community in shock.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voiceover): Dramatic video captured the horrifying moment the massive cargo ship, nearly 1,000-feet-long, crashed into a support column on the Francis Scott Key Bridge in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 MARYLAND TRANSPORTATION AUTHORITY\uc2 : The whole bridge just fell down. Start -- start whoever, everybody. The whole bridge just collapsed.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Within seconds, a large portion of the bridge collapses, plunging into the Patapsco River.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE (D-Maryland): We are still investigating what happened, but we are quickly gathering details. The preliminary investigation points to an accident. We haven`t seen any credible evidence of a terrorist attack.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A closer look at the video shows the ship heading south at about 1:24 a.m., when the lights go dark, but quickly on again. A minute later, plumes of black smoke can be seen billowing from the ship`s chimney.\par \par \uc2 Once again, it appears the ship loses its lights. A minute after they come back on, the vessel slams into the bridge.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SCOTT (D-Mayor of Baltimore, Maryland): Never would you think that you would see, physically see the Key Bridge tumble down like that. It looked like something out of a -- an action movie.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sources tell CBS News multiple alarms rang out on ship. After several failed attempts to fix the loss of propulsion, the pilot issued a mayday call, giving officials time to stop traffic on the bridge.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: These people are heroes. They saved lives last night.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The impact of the crash was so loud, it jolted Donald Heinbuch, who lives nearby, out of his sleep.\par \par \uc2 What did it feel like?\par \par \uc2 DONALD HEINBUCH (Baltimore Resident): It felt just like an earthquake. We have had some earthquakes here before, and that`s exactly what it felt like. And associated with that was the rolling thunder, which I thought was very unusual. We took...\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What was the rolling thunder?\par \par \uc2 DONALD HEINBUCH: It was the sound of the bridge collapsing into the Patapsco River.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, it`s just incredible to see this firsthand. I mean, we are not far from this. And, of course, this bridge carries some 30,000 people every day. It is a major commuter route, and now it is completely shut down.\par \par \uc2 You can see there`s an active search-and-rescue operation going on right now, more than 12 hours after this accident, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  using robotic cameras underwater to search for six construction workers who were on the bridge. They are now presumed dead by their employer. Two survivors were pulled from the chilly water this morning.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY (Chair, \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 ): A search- and-rescue is still under way.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Twenty-four members of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  are on the site leading the investigation. They want to examine the voyage data recorder.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY: It will be critical. It`s a critical piece of our investigation, which is why we have a recorder`s team here.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Authorities say the Singapore-flagged vessel was headed from Baltimore to Sri Lanka going nine miles per hour. None of the crew was hurt.\par \par \uc2 Today, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said the federal government will -- quote -- "move heaven and earth" to reopen the port and pay for the rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I told them we`re going to send all the federal resources they need as we respond to this emergency, and I mean all the federal resources. And we`re going to rebuild that port together.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now, federal officials tonight are here on the scene, including the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They`re here to investigate.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave reports on the timeline of the incident and a similar accident that took place more than four decades ago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): By daybreak, the force of the collision was clear. Pieces of the mangled Francis Scott Key Bridge and crushed cargo containers littered the deck of the Dali, but the first sign of trouble here, when the lights flickered and went out on board.\par \par \uc2 The giant ship had lost its engine, its electronics and much of its ability to steer, leaving it on a collision course with the bridge and little time to avert disaster. A mayday call went out as the nightmare scenario unfolded.\par \par \uc2 Michael Kucharski is a former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  senior Marine investigator.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KUCHARSKI (Former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  Senior Marine Investigator): Losing power as you`re approaching a bridge would be just about the worst-case scenario.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Loaded down with heavy containers, the captain tried to quickly slow down the more-than-900-foot-long cargo ship by dropping its port anchor. Dark smoke could be seen billowing from its stacks, likely the backup generator coming online.\par \par \uc2 But the ship never regained engine power, and it struck one of the two main structural support columns of the bridge.\par \par \uc2 Ben Schafer is a structural engineer.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHAFER (Civil and Systems Engineering Professor, Johns Hopkins): Once the bridge was struck by a container ship of that size, there`s not a bridge that we build that`s any level of economy and efficiency that -- that`s going to survive that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from May 1980)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It`s happened before. In 1980, during a blinding thunderstorm, a cargo ship slammed into a support column of Tampa`s Sunshine Skyway Bridge. A 1,200-foot span collapsed; 35 people were killed.\par \par \uc2 Since then, cargo ships have more than quadrupled in size.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHAFER: In the world of infrastructure system, it`s a failure, right? I mean, the bridge is gone. The port is closed. People have died.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Kris Van Cleave is with us now.\par \par \uc2 OK, so, this investigation is just getting under way. What`s the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  looking for?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, Norah, they`re going to have to contend with a lot of debris. This was like ramming a Nimitz-sized aircraft carrier into this bridge.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s debris everywhere, but they want to get on board. They want to find the data and voice recorder that should be on board. They`re also going to look at the maintenance history of this ship, including reports that the propulsion system may have failed an inspection over the summer. They also want to look at the bridge`s maintenance history and its design.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, how does the power go out?\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 A short time ago, we spoke with Maryland Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  to ask about the families of the missing and the survivors.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And what do we know about the victims` families? How are they doing?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE (D-Maryland): Well, I had the chance to spend time with the victims` families.\par \par \uc2 And they`re distraught and they`re heartbroken. But I tell you, the hopefulness that they have, these are -- these are very prayerful families. And I had the chance to pray with them and pray for them. They define what it means to be -- to be Maryland tough and to be Baltimore strong.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Two people were miraculously rescued. What do we know about the survivors?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: So, there are -- there are two people that we know, one who`s actually in shock trauma right now. And so we have a remarkable shock trauma unit here in the city of Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 And they are -- they`re actively working on one individual. And another individual, who was -- who was actually the leader of that group, who watched it all take place. And I had a chance to spend time with him. And, literally, he tells a story where he was actually walking away from the bridge when the bridge began to collapse.\par \par \uc2 And so he`s a -- watching what happened to him and even, as he said, he said, that was all God`s grace.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 So, essentially, he barely escaped?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: He barely escaped. I mean, this was -- this was an absolutely catastrophic event.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And, Governor, I know so many people depend on this bridge to get to work. How quickly can this bridge be rebuilt?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: This is going to be a top priority for us to make sure that we can get this bridge built back up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Part of our conversation with Maryland`s Governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He`s actually been at the site of this collapse since early this morning. And he also told us that, every day, $191 million of economic activity crosses over that bridge. And the Port of Baltimore is responsible for at least 8,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 So we asked \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio to look at the impact for the city, state and our country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voiceover): Collapsing in seconds, Baltimore`s Francis Scott Key Bridge was the gateway to much of the country. Thousands of trucks transported goods on it daily, goods, much of which would end up at this 350,000-square-foot warehouse.\par \par \uc2 John Schmidt is vice president and chief operating officer.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SCHMIDT (Vice President and COO, Capitol Express & Warehousing Inc.): It`s going to add a probably a couple hundred dollars minimum to every truckload.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Each truckload carrying everything from dry goods like food to reams of paper and office machinery.\par \par \uc2 What`s your warning to consumers?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SCHMIDT: Your product might take a little longer to get to you.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: The Port of Baltimore is one of three ports along the East Coast that are at least 50-feet-deep and can handle large ships like the Dali. It`s the U.S.` busiest port for cars, trucks and sugar, handling more than $80 billion in goods last year, employing more than 15,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG (U.S. Transportation Secretary): There is no question that this will be a major and protracted impact to supply chains.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and other multinationals tell CBS News they`re still assessing the economic impact.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MARCOTTE (SVP of Cross-Industry/Cross-Border and Technology, Kantar Consulting): The best case for the port in terms of getting it to be working again is probably about a month.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And worst-case scenario?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MARCOTTE: You`re looking a minimum of a year, and I`d be surprised if it`s not two years.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And before the Port of Baltimore can reopen, authorities first need to remove all of the debris and then re-dredge that part of the river.\par \par \uc2 But, Norah, there`s no timeline at all for when that might happen.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 In today`s other major news, nearly two years after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, abortion rights returned to the nation`s highest court. At issue is access to the widely used abortion pill mifepristone, along with the FDA`s authority to regulate other prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford was there for the oral arguments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Mifepristone kills!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): Protests made clear the controversy, but, inside the court, there appeared to be agreement, with a majority of justices on both sides of the ideological aisle highly skeptical of arguments to restrict use of the so-called abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 A key question, why should courts be second-guessing scientific decisions by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  on how a drug is prescribed?\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2  (U.S. Supreme Court Associate Justice): Do you have concerns about judges parsing medical and scientific studies?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: At issue in the case, the drug mifepristone, used with another drug in nearly two-thirds of abortions. A group of anti-abortion doctors argued the \uc2 FDA\uc2  wrongly expanded access to the pill in 2016, when it extended the window women can take it from seven to 10 weeks and, during the pandemic in 2021, said an in-person doctor`s visit was not needed, allowing mail-order pharmacies to ship the drug nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Erin Hawley represents the doctors who say those changes led to increased emergency room visits and compromised women`s health.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HAWLEY (Attorney For Respondents): Women are now left to receive these drugs in the mail or at their dorm room without ever having been checked by a doctor for life-threatening conditions. That`s reckless.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Abortion rights supporters say mifepristone is the safest method of abortion and the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , not the court system, has the final word on drug safety.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA ELLSWORTH (Danco Laboratories): The \uc2 FDA\uc2  acted on an extensive body of evidence and well within its authority and in accordance with the law.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But, in court, most of the 90-minute argument focused not on the merits of the FDA`s decision to expand access, but whether the doctors even have legal standing to sue.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Jan Crawford joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Jan, did we get a sense of how the justices might rule?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, I mean, I think the argument suggested it`s going to be a narrow procedural ruling for the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , and that would preserve nationwide access to the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 But we should have a decision by the end of June -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jan Crawford at the Supreme Court, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was hit with another gag order today. This time, it was imposed by the New York judge overseeing the trial connected to Trump`s hush money payments to former porn star Stormy Daniels. The gag order came after the former president lashed out at the judge and his daughter on social media.\par \par \uc2 In other news, shares of Trump`s media company soared more than 16 percent in the first day of trading, which now has a market value of nearly $8 billion.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the first public statement from music mogul \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  following raids on two of his homes as part of a possible sex trafficking investigation.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans reports on what authorities may have been looking for.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): There was nothing subtle when federal agents conducted high-profile raids at two large properties in Los Angeles and Miami owned by music mogul \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2 , raids that, tonight, his attorney calls a gross overuse of military-level force.\par \par \uc2 Why a simultaneous cross-country search?\par \par \uc2 LAURIE LEVENSON (Former Federal Prosecutor): Oh, I think they wanted this to be a bit of a surprise. It`s a big raid, and what they were going in was to try to find all types of evidence.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: There are two distinct images of Combs --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2 ` performance)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- the iconic performer and the one named in multiple lawsuits accusing him of everything from rape to sex trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Last December, Combs posted a statement saying: "I did not do any of the awful things being alleged."\par \par \uc2 The lawsuits so far have been in civil court. As for the latest searches:\par \par \uc2 LAURIE LEVENSON: If it involves sex trafficking or drugs or weapons or other illegal behavior, it can easily go from a civil investigation to what we see now, a criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from TMZ)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: TMZ published video it says was after the raids began. It showed Combs walking around Opa Locka Airport near Miami. A short time later, at the same airport, Miami-Dade police arrested Brendan Paul for drug possession. Paul was described in a previous lawsuit as Diddy`s mule, someone used to get drugs and guns.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE LEVENSON: So, what Sean Combs needs to do is follow his lawyers` advice, which undoubtedly is not to talk to people, not try to hinder the investigation, and not try to flee.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, Diddy`s lawyer calls the law enforcement action an unprecedented ambush.\par \par \uc2 He says his client is innocent, and he emphasized that Combs was never detained. In fact, he cooperated and even spoke with investigators -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Carter Evans, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  met with his Israeli counterpart at the Pentagon today, the tensions between their administrations at a wartime high. Austin told defense minister Gallant that the number of civilian casualties in the Gaza Strip is far too high and the amount of humanitarian aid is far too low.\par \par \uc2 Israeli forces continue to pound the city of Rafah, where more than a million Palestinians are seeking shelter from the war.\par \par \uc2 Robert Kennedy Jr. picks a running mate for his independent run for the White House. We will tell you who it is. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert F. Kennedy Jr. introduced his running mate today, choosing Silicon Valley lawyer and investor and philanthropist Nicole Shanahan.\par \par \uc2 Shanahan is 38 and the ex-wife of \uc2 Google\uc2  co-founder \uc2 Sergey Brin\uc2 . Kennedy, running as an independent, also considered \uc2 NFL\uc2  quarterback Aaron Rodgers, who shares his skepticism of vaccines, and former Minnesota Governor and wrestler Jesse Ventura.\par \par \uc2 An important note: Kennedy is currently on the ballot in only one state, Utah, in the general election.\par \par \uc2 Today, a Russian court tacked on an additional three months of pretrial jail time for "Wall Street Journal" reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2 . It was a year ago this week that he became the first American journalist arrested on spying charges in Russia since the Cold War. The U.S. considers birth both Gershkovich and former U.S. Marine Paul Whelan as wrongfully detained on bogus charges.\par \par \uc2 The lottery jackpots keep piling up. We will tell you how much is up for grabs tonight when we return.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Americans were lining up today for a shot at two huge lottery jackpots.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s Mega Millions grand prize now tops $1.1 billion. And tomorrow night`s Powerball jackpot has grown to $865 million.\par \par \uc2 College basketball superstar Caitlin Clark led the Iowa Hawkeyes into the Sweet 16 of the women`s NCAA Tournament.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 NCAA\uc2 /\uc2 ESPN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Clark scored 32 points in their win over West Virginia, breaking the season scoring record. Clark`s season total now stands at 1,113 points. She will try to add to that on Saturday against Colorado.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a final note on the Francis Scott Key Bridge and its iconic namesake.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We wanted to end tonight here, near the Francis Scott Key Bridge, which has significant meaning for the country.\par \par \uc2 This is where it`s believed Francis Scott Key witnessed the battle that inspired him to write America`s national anthem, "The Star-Spangled Banner." He saw the rockets` red glare just a few hundred yards from where the bridge bearing his name stood for decades. This red, white and blue buoy marks the location.\par \par \uc2 In the War of 1812, Key was detained aboard a boat while the Battle of Baltimore raged for 25 hours. "By the dawn`s early light," as he wrote, "the American flag yet waved." His patriotic poem set to music officially became the national anthem in March 1931.\par \par \uc2 Almost exactly 47 years ago, this majestic steel span across the Patapsco River opened to traffic. It was among the marvels of 20th century engineering, this bridge tonight in ruins, but the message of Key`s anthem still true, a message of American resilience, perseverance in the face of adversity.\par \par \uc2 By the dawn`s early light of another day, this community will surely rise again.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240327ek3q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 19, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ramy Inocencio, Charlie De Mar, Manuel Bojorquez,  Errol Barnett, Robert Costa, Ed O`Keefe, Ben Tracy \par \uc2 3,800 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is a "Show me your papers" law.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news: Texas can now arrest and detain anyone suspected of illegally crossing into the state from Mexico. The fight over who can police the border escalating, and the Supreme Court weighing in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DYLAN CORBETT (Executive Director, Hope Border Institute): Nearly half-a- million people here in El Paso have to basically justify their presence in the United States because of the color of their skin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What this means for the battle over immigration.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (R-Texas): Texas, very simply, is enforcing the laws.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin tonight with a ruling from the Supreme Court that allows a sweeping Texas immigration law to go into effect for now. In a 6-3 decision, the nation`s highest court said it won`t block the law known as S.B.4 that gives local and state police the power to arrest and detain migrants suspected of crossing the border illegally.\par \par \uc2 The federal government has been the sole authority of immigration enforcement for decades, and critics say this ruling is an affront to federal law. The case now heads back to the Court of Appeals to decide if it is constitutional or not. But, in the meantime, it`s a major victory for Texas and Republican Governor Greg Abbott.\par \par \uc2 The White House said that they fundamentally disagree with the Supreme Court`s order.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe is on the campaign trail with the president, and he will start us off tonight from Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Razor wire and Texas National Guard troops along the U.S.-Mexico border reinforced tonight by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now Lone Star State sheriff and police departments can arrest, jail and judges can prosecute migrants accused of illegally entering the U.S.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (R-Texas): He`s not denying illegal entry. President Biden is aiding and abetting illegal entry.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Texas Governor Greg Abbott, who`s been locked in a yearslong fight with the federal government over border security, last year signed what`s known as the S.B.4 law amid the historic increase in migrant border crossings.\par \par \uc2 Today, he called the Supreme Court`s ruling a positive development. The White House sought to block the law and today said it`s "another example of Republican officials politicizing the border while blocking real solutions."\par \par \uc2 It called on Congress to pass a bipartisan border security plan currently being blocked in the House. The Supreme Court allowed the new law to take effect, but it still faces a challenge in a lower federal court. Today, conservative Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  urged the appeals court to rule quickly.\par \par \uc2 Liberal Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  questioned the laws constitutionality and said it`ll upend the longstanding federal state- balance of power and sow chaos. Multiple law enforcement officials tell CBS News they`re already short-staffed and don`t have the training from the state on how to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s not like, oh, he looks illegal and we`re going to put him in jail. We can`t do that. You know, we can`t stereotype someone -- you know, someone from Mexico and -- and do that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And that potential for racial profiling across Texas is what concerns migrant activists like Dylan Corbett.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN CORBETT (Executive Director, Hope Border Institute): It`s not just against recently arriving migrants, migrants who are coming to the border today, but this -- this really goes after Texans throughout the state.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tonight, the Mexican government says it won`t accept migrants that Texas officials try to send back under the new law.\par \par \uc2 Campaigning here in Nevada, President Biden had no comment on the ruling. Coincidentally, he`s headed next to the border states of Arizona and Texas -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Today, former Trump White House adviser \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  became the first person from the Trump administration to go to prison over matters related to the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa reports, Navarro was convicted of contempt of Congress and remained defiant to the very end.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETER NAVARRO\uc2  (Former Director, White House Office of Trade and Manufacturing Policy): Navarro is going to prison today.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  made it clear he understood his fate this morning. And, addressing reporters outside Miami`s Federal Correctional Institution, the 74-year-old former Trump trade adviser said he wasn`t happy about it.\par \par \uc2 PETER NAVARRO\uc2 : I`m pissed. That`s what I`m feeling right now. But I`m also afraid of only one thing. I`m afraid for this country, because this, what they`re doing, should have a chilling effect on every American, regardless of their party. If they come for me, they can come for you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Navarro will live in an 80-person dormitory reserved for older inmates. He will have access to a phone, e-mail and TV.\par \par \uc2 PETER NAVARRO\uc2 : The president`s going to sign another order.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Once a combative fixture at Trump`s side, Navarro now becomes the first ever former White House official jailed for contempt of Congress, the four-month sentence handed down after he defied a subpoena from the House January 6 Committee probing his role in trying to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from November 25, 2020)\par \par \uc2 PETER NAVARRO\uc2 : The election is over, the vote is over, but the outcome is not.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Navarro was an advocate of a proposed scheme to help Trump stay in power.\par \par \uc2 The former president, who continues to falsely claim the election was stolen and praises those who face prosecution for their conduct on January 6, said Navarro was wronged.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I think it`s a shame. I think it`s a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Behind the scenes, Republican sources tell me Trump now faces intense stress politically and financially.\par \par \uc2 His prized properties, like Trump Tower and 40 Wall Street, could be seized on Monday by the New York attorney general if he doesn`t secure its $454 million bond.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, two former Mississippi sheriff`s deputies are behind bars after being sentenced for taking part in the racially motivated torture of two black men last year. The deputies were among a group of white law enforcement officers who called themselves the Goon Squad because of their willingness to use excessive force.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Errol Barnett reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (voiceover): "Egregious and despicable," those words from a federal judge in Mississippi describing the actions of former cops Hunter Elward, sentenced to 20 years, and Jeffrey Middleton, sentenced to more than 17 years.\par \par \uc2 They, along with four others, some of whom referred to themselves as the Goon Squad, pleaded guilty to breaking into this home early last year without a warrant, then terrorizing and torturing two black men, all apparently because they stayed at this house with a white woman.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN CLARKE\uc2  (Assistant U.S. Attorney General): The six white police officers involved in this assault sought to dehumanize their victims.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: During the ordeal, both were handcuffed, as deputies poured milk, alcohol and syrup on their faces, calling them racial slurs. At one point, Michael Jenkins was shot in the mouth.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JENKINS (Police Abuse Survivor): He was looking at me. I was looking at him. And we sat there for a minute. He just pulled the trigger.\par \par \uc2 EDDIE PARKER (Police Abuse Survivor): You can see the bullet, you know, coming out of the gun, I mean, the fire coming out. And it was just -- I mean, it was -- it was horrific.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The federal charges were brought by the \uc2 Department of Justice`s Civil Rights Division\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2  (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): Hard to imagine a more atrocious set of civil rights violations than those carried out by these guys.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: An investigation by the Associated Press revealed that, since 2019, some of these deputies had been involved in at least four violent assaults on black men. They resulted in two deaths and another suffering lasting injuries.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 : They`re not just depriving victims of their civil rights. They`re degrading the public`s trust in our criminal justice system.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, there are still four additional deputies to be sentenced later this week.\par \par \uc2 And during today`s hearing in the federal courthouse you see behind me, Hunter Elward addressed the court, said he accepted responsibility and said to the two victims, Norah, that he apologized. At that point, one of the victims, Eddie Parker, stood, turned to him and said: "I forgive you."\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Oh, my goodness. Wow.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to Haiti, where the U.S. is considering a new plan to airlift desperate Americans out of the country, which is in deep political turmoil. With the main airport in Haiti`s capital still closed, helicopters may be used to fly Americans to the Dominican Republic, where they could book flights to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 As CBS` Manuel Bojorquez reports, the only option for now is a dangerous six-hour drive to another airport in Haiti`s north.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Another day at Cap-Haitien International Airport, as Americans are desperate to flee a country in crisis; 82-year- old Gregoire Laconte (sp?) is one of hundreds of U.S. passport holders trying to leave. He`s hoping to get home to his wife in New York, but still no luck securing a seat.\par \par \uc2 You`re back here waiting to see when the next one will go.\par \par \uc2 GREGOIRE LACONTE (American Attempting to Flee Haiti): Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But no idea right now when it might happen?\par \par \uc2 GREGOIRE LACONTE: No -- no idea. No idea.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: This woman, who did not want her identity revealed, came to the airport early this morning with no baggage in hand, scared for those she leaves behind.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The thing is, people go inside your house, killing, raping, all those things, burning your house. You don`t know, tomorrow, what`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: CBS Miami reporter Tania Francois was also in Cap- Haitien, where she was on board one of the several missionary flights bringing aid and evacuating people.\par \par \uc2 TANIA FRANCOIS: Airport workers tell me that this is the first flight to come in from the United States with passengers, also with supplies.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Residents in the capital of Port-au-Prince see no end to a crisis that has their city plagued by gang violence.\par \par \uc2 More than 86,000 people are displaced, with no access to food or gas. People on the streets are on the brink of starvation. Widespread power outages are leaving some stranded and still hoping for a way out.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez, CBS News, Cap-Haitien, Haiti.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to an alarming new study that estimates, in Chicago, that nearly 70 percent of children under 6 years old are exposed to lead- contaminated drinking water.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie De Mar reports on the latest major city to face this widespread problem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR (voiceover): Tonight, danger lurking in Chicago`s drinking water, according to a new study that used A.I. to estimate lead levels in unfiltered tap water over a seven-year period.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Huynh is the study`s lead researcher.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HUYNH (Assistant Professor, \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2 ): You know, the goal is to have zero lead in water at all. And we know from the science that even small amounts of lead in the water can have an impact on your child.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Black and Hispanic children are more likely to be affected and less likely to have their household water tested, according to the study estimation.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HUYNH: I don`t think we need to be alarmist. It`s not as bad as the Flint crisis. Your kid is not going to be hospitalized from the levels of lead that we`re seeing, but even these low levels of lead, these are things that can affect your child without you noticing.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Lead in drinking water is a nationwide issue. Children in Flint, Michigan, were found to have learning delays after the lead water crisis began there 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Today, Chicago`s Department of Water Management told CBS News it has an issue with the study`s methodology, saying its results only indicated whether there is a lead service line, not routine exposure.\par \par \uc2 Chicago environmental advocate Chakena Perry is calling on the city to immediately replace lead water pipes.\par \par \uc2 CHAKENA PERRY (Senior Policy Advocate, \uc2 Natural Resources Defense Council\uc2 ): So, not only is this a public health crisis, but it`s also an environmental justice issue. And so, when you look at it through that lens, that should create a sense of urgency to say that we can`t wait any longer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: The Biden administration has vowed to remove all lead pipes nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Here in Chicago, the Department of Water Management says it`s already introduced programs to remove the city -- the city`s 400,000 lead service lines and offers residents with free water testing -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie De Mar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The number of abortions in the U.S. topped one million last year for the first time in over a decade. More than 60 percent were medication abortions, using a two-pill regimen to terminate early pregnancies. The increase is despite the 2022 Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade that allowed 13 states to ban abortion with few exceptions, forcing many women to travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is set to hear arguments next week in a case that puts access to one of those medications used in abortions at stake.\par \par \uc2 New video of Princess Catherine has not ended speculation online and in the British press about her whereabouts months after she had abdominal surgery.\par \par \uc2 And, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio reports, the authenticity of another royal photo is coming under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voiceover): The optics of royal business as usual.\par \par \uc2 Prince William supported a homelessness project today, while \uc2 King Charles\uc2  greeted Korean War veterans at Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 But those optics have been blurred in this 2022 photo of Queen Elizabeth, her grandchildren and great-grandchildren, the image digitally enhanced, according to global picture agency \uc2 Getty Images\uc2 , the second royal photo in two weeks after Princess Catherine apologized for doctoring this portrait.\par \par \uc2 The picture was taken by Kate, who last officially appeared in public at Christmas, before disappearing from public view to undergo abdominal surgery.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN PAYNE: They may discover that, these days, in a world with social media, where people like to have a running commentary on every single issue, that it isn`t possible now for us to have three months with absolutely no news.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: But Kate made big news when she was filmed walking with Prince William in Windsor in this new video obtained by TMZ, further fueling the debate about transparency in the royal family.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH (Royal Editor, "The Sunday Times"): I think it`s been made clear to me from several of their friends, you know, their thinking on this, that I think both of them will want to be more open about what has gone on at a point where Kate feels ready to do that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And Kensington Palace has maintained all along that Kate would return to public duties after Easter.\par \par \uc2 The late Queen Elizabeth`s unofficial motto was "Never complain, never explain." But for the next-generation royals, that might be, do explain, but just a little -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ramy Inocencio, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A mother desperate to see her missing son again calls in a famous group of rescuers to help find him. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The search for a missing college student in Nashville is expanding.\par \par \uc2 Riley Strain`s family said today they have asked the volunteer group -- rescue group the United Cajun Navy to help look for the 22-year-old. Officials have been searching the Cumberland River and Strain`s bank card because that was found near the shore on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2  senior disappeared 11 days ago during a fraternity trip. His anguished mother says she usually speaks with her son a few times a day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WHITEID (Mother of Riley Strain): He`s my best friend. He`s everything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tennessee officials are investigating whether Strain was overserved alcohol at a bar owned by country music singer Luke Bryan.\par \par \uc2 A new report on climate change issues a red alert while -- warning to the world due to rising sea levels and record temperatures.\par \par \uc2 We will show you one ambitious plan to fight the problem next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: 2023 was the hottest year on record.\par \par \uc2 And a new report on climate change includes a dire warning for the planet. Rising temperatures have resulted in record low sea ice in the Antarctic, while sea levels are rising twice as fast as they were two decades ago. And if the world continues on the same path, ocean levels could rise in America as much as seven feet by the year 2100.\par \par \uc2 That`s leading top climate scientists to combat the problem. The Biden administration is committing billions toward jump-starting a new carbon capture industry in America.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ben Tracy shows us two unique approaches to tackling the crisis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: So, this is where carbon goes to die?\par \par \uc2 BARCLAY ROGERS (CEO, Graphyte): Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY (voiceover): This field in Central Arkansas will soon be a graveyard for planet-warming pollution.\par \par \uc2 BARCLAY ROGERS: It`s going into the ground, and it`ll stay here forever.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Barclay Rogers is CEO of Graphyte, a start-up that takes leftover material from timber and rice mills and locks away the carbon dioxide that plants naturally remove from the atmosphere by turning it into bricks, wrapping them up, and burying them in the ground.\par \par \uc2 BARCLAY ROGERS: We`re taking the carbon captured by plants and keeping it out of the atmosphere for 1,000 years or more.\par \par \uc2 So, you have got a lot of space here.\par \par \uc2 BARCLAY ROGERS: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Graphyte plans to turn this empty warehouse into the world`s largest carbon removal facility, eventually removing 50,000 tons of CO2 per year. That`s like taking 10,000 cars off the road. \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  is paying Graphyte to offset some of the pollution from its flights.\par \par \uc2 This brick of dead plants and trees is really going to be a solution?\par \par \uc2 BARCLAY ROGERS: It`s going to be a very big solution.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: To avoid the worst impacts of climate change, scientists say we need to stop burning fossil fuels and switch to cleaner forms of energy, but also need to remove billions of tons of carbon dioxide we have already put up into the atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 SHASHANK SAMALA (CEO, Heirloom): This is not a nice-to-have anymore. This is a must-have.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Shashank Samala runs \uc2 Heirloom Carbon\uc2 . It just opened the nation`s first commercial carbon capture plant in Central California.\par \par \uc2 The automated facility stacks trays of limestone 40 feet high, allowing the rock to suck carbon dioxide from the air like a sponge. In nature, that takes months. Heirloom`s technology does it in just days.\par \par \uc2 Carbon that might have come out of somebody`s tailpipe...\par \par \uc2 SHASHANK SAMALA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: ... is now being sucked down into this?\par \par \uc2 SHASHANK SAMALA: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Heirloom says its pilot plant removes just 1,000 tons of CO2 per year, but plans to build facilities that capture 1,000 times more.\par \par \uc2 Carbon removal is often knocked for being too expensive. But given the climate crisis we now face, Samala says it`s an essential part of the solution.\par \par \uc2 SHASHANK SAMALA: We need to start turning back the clock on climate change. You know, what carbon removal offers us is the closest thing to a time machine.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Ben Tracy, CBS News, Central California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Heart of America is next with a remarkable graduation ceremony for a premature baby born at just 26 weeks.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet Ellyannah Lopez. When Ellyannah was born in May of 2023, she was the smallest premature baby ever at \uc2 Cedars-Sinai Medical Center\uc2  in Los Angeles, weighing only 12 ounces. And she was the size of a soda can. After spending 10 months in the NICU with a devoted staff of over 200 doctors and nurses taking care of her, she was finally able to go home last week.\par \par \uc2 The staff at Cedars-Sinai Guerin Children`s gave her a full graduation ceremony, look, complete with a cap and gown. Ellyannah`s parents, Cecia and Boris, said the staff became like family.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CECIA JUAREZ (Mother of Ellyannah Lopez): We knew that this day would come. We were very hopeful, and we had faith that things would work out, and we trusted this hospital. And we knew we were in good hands. And, today, we get to take our daughter home after 10 months.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Well, Ellyannah is now 17 pounds, but she`s still got a long road ahead. Ellyannah Lopez and the incredible staff at Cedars-Sinai Guerin Children`s, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240320ek3j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  Executive Director Catherine Russell Discusses the Humanitarian Concerns for Civilians Trapped in Rafah and Unfolding Crisis in Haiti Amid Widespread Gang Violence; Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  Discusses the 2024 Campaign and Decision to not Endorse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  For President; Rep. \uc2 Raja Krishnamoorthi\uc2  and Rep. \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  are Interviewed about the TikTok Bill; Kara Swisher is Interviewed about the TikTok Bill; Catherine Russell is Interviewed about the Humanitarian Situation in Gaza.\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Chris Livesay \par \uc2 8,918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Congress tries a takedown of TikTok`s Chinese ownership, and the split between some allies of Israel and the Netanyahu government grows.\par \par \uc2 With the arrival of spring in the nation`s capital, House lawmakers moved with surprising speed to push a bipartisan bill to try and force the social media platform TikTok to change the way they do business. But with 170 million American TikTok users, what`s not so surprising is the politics playing a part in the back-and-forth over a ban, since both parties see the app as a platform to woo young voters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): This is not an attempt to ban TikTok. It`s an attempt to make TikTok better, tic-tac-toe, a winner.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk with the heads of the House Committee on Communist China, \uc2 Raja Krishnamoorthi\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 , and tech journalist Kara Swisher.\par \par \uc2 Plus, with the aid package to Ukraine and Israel languishing on Capitol Hill, new criticism of the Israeli government`s handling of the war in Gaza makes its outlook even more uncertain.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): Prime Minister Netanyahu has lost his way by allowing his political survival to take the precedence over the best interests of Israel.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk with Maryland Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 . He`s pushing for tighter control on aid to Israel.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will talk with the head of \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  about the dire situation facing hundreds of millions of the world`s children.\par \par \uc2 And one year after his public split with the former president over January 6, we will sit down with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . He says he won`t endorse Trump, but can he convince others to follow his lead?\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation on this St. Patrick`s Day.\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  joins us in person, in studio.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, and Happy St. Patrick`s Day.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE (R-Presidential Candidate): Good morning, Margaret. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s a lot to get to with you today.\par \par \uc2 But I want to begin with some remarks Mr. Trump made yesterday at a rally in Ohio. He walked onto the stage to a song recorded by a choir of prisoners facing charges for their role in the violence of January the 6th.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): You see the spirit from the hostages, and that`s what they are is hostages.\par \par \uc2 They`ve been treated terribly and very unfairly, and you know that, and everybody knows that. And we`re going to be working on that soon. The first day we get into office, we`re going to save our country, and we`re going to work with the people to treat those unbelievable patriots, and they were unbelievable patriots and are.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know, in the past, you`ve said Mr. Trump`s reckless words endangered you and your family on that day.\par \par \uc2 What do you think when you hear him refer to those people facing charges as hostages and patriots?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I think it`s very unfortunate, at a time that there are American hostages being held in Gaza, that the president or any other leaders would refer to people that are moving through our -- our justice system as hostages.\par \par \uc2 And it`s just -- it`s just unacceptable. I was there on January 6. I have no doubt in my mind, Margaret, that, that some people were caught up in the moment, and that entered the Capitol. And -- and they`re certainly entitled to due process of law for any nonviolent activities that day.\par \par \uc2 But the assaults on police officers, ultimately, an environment that claimed lives, is something that I -- I think was tragic that day. And I will -- I will never diminish it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the legal system is processing these individuals through and giving them trials.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what you think, though, about Mr. Trump and whether the public needs to hear and see some of the evidence in regard to the federal charges related to his alleged role in January 6? Do you think that needs to happen before Election Day?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I -- look, I think the American people lived through that moment.\par \par \uc2 I and my family and my team lived through it at the Capitol that day. I think most Americans know what happened. I -- you know, as I have said before, I -- I truly do believe that -- that the judgment about the president`s conduct that day should have been left to the American people.\par \par \uc2 I know it`s been brought into a criminal trial now. And -- and we have cases around the country that have been brought related to that, notably in Georgia. But, at the end of the day, I think the American people know what happened that day.\par \par \uc2 And I -- I said, the people that -- the people that ransacked the Capitol need to be held to the fullest account of the law. And I believe that they are. But the judgment about the president`s efforts that day, I think can be left to the American people.\par \par \uc2 But -- but, look, I -- I said last week that, after a lot of prayer and reflection, I have come to the conclusion that I`m -- I won`t be endorsing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I mean, look, I`m very proud of the record of our administration, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 It was a record that left America more prosperous, and -- and more secure and -- and our liberties and the sanctity of life stronger than ever before in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you`ve said Mr. Trump`s walked away from conservative principles.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I do.\par \par \uc2 I said it during my presidential campaign. The president and I have profound differences. And many people think it`s just over January 6. And, frankly, the fact that the president continues to insist that -- that I had the right to overturn the election that day is a fundamental difference.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: But I want to be clear that I -- I have forgiven the president in my heart for what happened that day.\par \par \uc2 I -- as a Christian, I`m required to do that. I have prayed for him in that regard. But the -- the issue of fealty to the Constitution is not a small matter.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: But it`s not just that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the reason that I -- I cannot in good conscience endorse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this year also has to do with the fact that he is walking away not just from keeping faith with the Constitution on that day, but also, Margaret, with a commitment to fiscal responsibility, a commitment...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: ... to the sanctity of life, a -- a commitment to American leadership in the world.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the president`s reversal just in the last week on -- on TikTok...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: ... following an administration where -- where we literally changed the national consensus on China, is the reason why I -- after a lot of reflection, I just concluded I -- I cannot endorse the agenda that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is carrying into this national debate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that the final word from you? Can you be persuaded, if he changes tacks, to vote for him? Would you vote for him?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I -- I won`t be endorsing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I want to be clear...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But would you vote for him?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: ... I respect the right of Republican voters who`ve made it clear who they`re for, who they want to be our standard-bearer this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: What I`m going to focus my energies on is in the -- what remains of this year on what we should be for, because I joined this party when I was in college...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: ... because I heard the voice and the values of another good Irishman named \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , who -- who said that we need to be -- have a strong national defense, but also recognize we`re leader of the free world, and we need to stand for traditional values, the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 We need to stand for the Constitution and limited government. And in the balance of this year and in the years ahead, I`m just going to continue to champion those values that I think are not just good for the Republican Party, Margaret, but I think that they are the right policies for a stronger, more prosperous America.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But I -- I ask you about persuasion and voting because this is not just Mr. Trump. He has taken the party with him. He has taken the Senate Republican leadership, who are endorsing him. He has taken the House Republican endorsements with him too in terms of leadership.\par \par \uc2 Are all of those people, from \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  to Mike Johnson, walking away from the conservative principles you say?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I -- I don`t want to say that they are. I want to say that my deep conviction is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They are endorsing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: ... that this is a conservative party with -- this is still a party, I believe, that stands for less government, less taxes, American leadership in the world, traditional moral values, and the right to life.\par \par \uc2 I just think, frankly, the disaster of the Biden administration has -- has resulted in people going back to what`s familiar, and I understand that. I...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I worked day in and day out for four and a half years to build that record of the Trump/Pence administration. I`m incredibly proud of it.\par \par \uc2 But as I look at what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is running on now...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: ... I simply cannot in good conscience endorse his candidacy this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But on the issues of the economy, which many Republicans refer to, as you know, something the Trump/Pence administration did well with until COVID, Trump`s campaigning on that.\par \par \uc2 Last night, he was talking about electric vehicles, in particular, and said, when it comes to Chinese companies who manufacture in Mexico, he`s going to put in tariffs.\par \par \uc2 But I want you to listen to exactly what he said.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re going to put a 100 percent tariff on every single car that comes across the line.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And you`re not going to be able to sell those cars, if I get elected. Now, if I don`t get elected, it`s going to be a bloodbath for the whole -- that`s going to be the least of it. It`s going to be a bloodbath for the country. That will be the least of it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 And given how divided the country is, does that language concern you, particularly when it comes to political violence?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, first, I want to -- I want to commend you, Margaret, for putting that in context.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I woke up this morning seeing online all the discussion about bloodbath. And, as you`ve just reflected, the president was clearly talking about the impact of -- of imports devastating the American automotive industry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Was that clear to you? Because it was a little muddled.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I think it was.\par \par \uc2 Look, I want people to know, my -- look, the president and I -- it was very clear for four years, the president and I have -- have different styles.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: We`re different men.\par \par \uc2 But my differences with him are not over style. It`s not over language. And, as I said before, it`s not personal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I mean, for me, the reason why I won`t endorse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this year is because I see him departing from the mainstream conservative agenda that has defined the Republican Party over the last 40 years and still has the best hope for the future of the country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it sounds like you`re trying to move the party back in that direction, which is why I`m asking you if you`re persuadable here.\par \par \uc2 But on that point you made about the commitment to the sanctity of human life, you say Mr. Trump`s walking away from it. President Biden is telling voters that Donald Trump`s trying to ban access to abortion, even though he really hasn`t given any specifics as to what he actually thinks.\par \par \uc2 What -- would you like him to say that -- that he will endorse a 15-week restriction, like you did?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I`m pro-life. I don`t apologize for it. I couldn`t be more proud to have played a role in the administration that appointed three of the justices that sent Roe v. Wade to the ash heap of history.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there doesn`t seem to be a conservative consensus on this issue, which is so motivating to so many voters.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, but, look, the states around the country have been advancing the principles of life.\par \par \uc2 I was very disappointed during our presidential campaign when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  denounced and said a pro-life bill, a six-week bill enacted in Florida, which had already been enacted in Georgia and in Ohio, was, I think, in his words, terrible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Look, we`re -- we`re a pro-life party.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I really do believe that there is a national role. I would like -- I would like to see our nominee endorse a minimum 15-week ban, but, also, I would like to see a nominee that`s affirming the sanctity of life as it`s debated in states across this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And protections for IVF?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: This is a pro-life party.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Protections for IVF?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And endorsing -- should embryos be considered children?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I -- look, I`m -- I`m -- I`m someone that believes in the sanctity of human life, that life begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 My family, as you know, Margaret has benefited by fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We have discussed that.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: And I think they should be protected.\par \par \uc2 But I -- I also believe that, in the days ahead, we would do well to ensure that -- that -- that unborn human life, including embryos, are properly treated and properly respected in the law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we will track that.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pence, thank you for coming in and sharing your views with us today.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be back in a minute, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The push to get food and humanitarian aid into Gaza is intensifying.\par \par \uc2 But, at this point, it`s still far short of what is needed. The \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  said last week that famine was imminent if the amount of aid going into Northern Gaza was not increased exponentially.\par \par \uc2 Our Chris Livesay reports from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY (voice-over): \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2  pilots glide above Northern Gaza, dropping three tons of food, water, and other aid, lifesaving to those who can reach it, but, according to one pilot, a drop in the bucket meant to offset this leaky supply line, more than 200 trucks entering the strip daily, Israel says, but much of it lost to looters, the dangers of open combat, and a lack of truckers brave enough to drive through it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That they cannot collect and distribute.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: But aren`t aid groups getting shot at over there? Is it fair to put that burden on them?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We acknowledge that there are challenges, but, mostly, they`re distributing for the last two-and-a-half weeks without any violence.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Additional aid is on its way by sea for the first time in this war. Nearly 200 tons from Europe arrived Friday on a humanitarian ship.\par \par \uc2 It couldn`t come fast enough for these children.\par \par \uc2 "We want food. We want flour. We want the barest essentials of life," they demand.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "Gaza is slowly dying, if not from the missiles, then from hunger," says this mother.\par \par \uc2 In one instance, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  accused the Israeli military of opening fire on hungry Gazans as they waited for food, killing 21. Israel adamantly denies it, pointing instead at Palestinian gunmen.\par \par \uc2 The humanitarian cost of the war and more than 100 hostages still in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity for five months are too much to tolerate for these Israeli protesters.\par \par \uc2 Protesters don`t only want the hostages back. They want \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to step down. October 7 happened on his watch, and they have not forgotten.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: I think now we should get all the people that are kidnapped back to their homes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: So, even if that means retreating, pulling out of Gaza entirely and handing over all of the prisoners back to...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: I think they should do any deal to get them home. This is the first thing that a country owes to her citizens, is their security. And -- and this is the reason why they should do any effort.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: And what`s notable about this latest proposal from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is that, reportedly, they`re no longer insisting Israel completely withdraw from the Gaza Strip or on a permanent cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 And now, Margaret, three U.S. officials tell CBS News they`re optimistic that both \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Israel can come to an agreement about a pause, albeit temporary, in the fighting.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s Chris Livesay reporting from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by Maryland Democrat Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, a lot to get to with you.\par \par \uc2 But I do know you met with some families of hostage members this past -- family members of hostages -- excuse me -- this past week. Talks are about to resume. What did the hostage families tell you?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN (D-Maryland): Well, they -- they came and talked about the -- the terror every day of not knowing what`s happening to their family members.\par \par \uc2 And they were very clear we need to focus on getting the hostages back and a cease-fire. And this is why I`m for a cease-fire and the return of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that cease-fire would also allow for aid to move in. That`s what the Biden administration is championing.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to some specific things that I know you`re focusing on. There`s this government funding debate happening in the next few days. Some Republicans want to cut off support for the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agency \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 . That supports about six million Palestinian refugees, Lebanon, Jordan, Gaza Strip, West Bank.\par \par \uc2 You said last week, senators need to read the classified report prepared by the director of national intelligence about the Netanyahu government claims about that agency. You seemed to be implying that the links to terror groups are unfounded.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Oh, there`s no doubt that the -- the claim that Prime Minister Netanyahu and others are making that somehow \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  is a proxy for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are just flat-out lies. That`s a flat-out lie.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the person who`s in charge of operations on the ground in Gaza for \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 , it`s about a 20-year \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  veteran. You can be sure he is not in cahoots with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Netanyahu has wanted to get rid of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  since at least 2017. That`s been his goal, not just in Gaza, but also in the other places you talked about.\par \par \uc2 And if you get rid of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  in Gaza today, it is the primary distribution system for food and aid. So, if you cut off funding for \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  in Gaza entirely, it means more people will starve, more people won`t get the medical assistance they need. And so it would be a huge mistake to cut them off.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you explain why you believe that the prime minister is trying to dismantle \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 , which is the agency that has 13,000 people distributing aid? No one else has that kind of infrastructure in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Well, that`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 And there have been allegations made by the Netanyahu government that up to 14 of those 13,000 people were involved in the horrific October 7 attacks. We should investigate it. We should hold all those people accountable.\par \par \uc2 But, for goodness` sakes, let`s not hold two million innocent Palestinian civilians who are dying of starvation, let`s not hold them essentially accountable for the bad acts of 14 people.\par \par \uc2 Netanyahu has wanted to get rid of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  because he has seen them as a -- a means to continue the hopes of the Palestinian people for a homeland of their own.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: And he has been opposed to a two-state solution. And this has been his primary objective, stopping a two-state solution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Back in February, you pushed the Biden administration to craft what ended up being a national security memorandum that requires countries who receive U.S. weapons, all countries, to certify that they are in compliance with U.S. law, and they are not blocking the delivery of humanitarian aid.\par \par \uc2 Israel has until March 24 to turn in a letter. They reportedly did so. Are they in compliance?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: No, they`re not, not as of today.\par \par \uc2 And you`re right. This is a very important tool that the Biden administration`s put forward. It applies to Israel and any other country that receives U.S. military assistance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Will President Biden`s administration say that they`re not in compliance? Are you confident?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: I hope they will, because President Biden himself has repeatedly said that the Netanyahu government is unnecessarily restricting desperately needed humanitarian assistance. I mean, the president has said it a number of times. He`s said no excuses.\par \par \uc2 So it may be that the minister of defense in Israel signed this. But I cannot imagine a scenario right now where Secretary Blinken can find that that promise is credible and reliable, when, the day it was signed, clearly, the Netanyahu government is not in compliance, because we see that -- that they`re continuing to restrict humanitarian assistance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll watch to see if Secretary Blinken affirms it or denies it.\par \par \uc2 But what`s the consequence here? I know you`ve said you don`t want to cut off all military support for Israel. You`re talking about offensive weapons.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Yes, this -- this would not apply to the Iron Dome or any defensive systems, but it would say no more bombs for use in Gaza until you come into compliance.\par \par \uc2 So it`s not a total cutoff. I mean, the Netanyahu government can open the Erez Crossing to help starving people in Northern Gaza. They can stop turning away maternity kits, you know, claiming that, somehow, they pose a -- a dual-use danger, that they could be used for military purposes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: They could stop turning away water purifiers, because, when they do that, they send the whole truck back for four weeks.\par \par \uc2 So they need to allow more assistance in, and they need to really deal with that deconfliction issue. People need to be able to deliver assistance without getting killed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 Well, and the U.S. ambassador, David Satterfield, who works for the Biden administration, has said that police have been hit while helping \uc2 U.N.\uc2  convoys be delivered.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that the Israeli government is deliberately targeting to stop humanitarian aid deliveries? Because the Israeli government denies that.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Well, we know, in the case of, I think, about six of these so-called blue helmeted security escorts, that they were targeted, because Israel claimed that they were part of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , when they were sort of -- so -- so, we know, in that case, that was a deliberate targeting.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right. There have been other cases where Israel has -- has, you know, used force. And a convoy, for example, in the north passed the inspection and then was hit by a naval missile.\par \par \uc2 I do want to point out also, Margaret, that Ambassador Satterfield has said very clearly that, when it comes to U.N.-provided humanitarian assistance, like humanitarian assistance through \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 , there`s been no evidence of diversion to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: So, he said this very, very clearly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And all of Congress has received that information?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: I have tried to tell my colleagues who keep coming back from meeting with...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: ... Netanyahu government officials, spreading this...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: ... this -- this lie, this myth about diversion from \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Now, there may be diversion in other places, but not from \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator, we`ll watch what happens in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For all the political news during the week, be sure to tune into America Decides at 5:00 p.m. weekdays on our streaming network.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Last week the House passed a bill requiring TikTok to divest from its Chinese owner or face removal from the U.S. app stores.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now are the leaders of the House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party, Republican \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  and Democrat \uc2 Raja Krishnamoorthi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both.\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-IL): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman Krishnamoorthi, I want to go to you as the lead Democrat here.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said he would sign this bill if it makes it to his desk. Have you gotten any indication from Leader Schumer that the Senate will take this up or whether this legislation will die in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI: Well, we`ve got an indication that leader is going to take it up in his process. And, obviously, we respect his process.\par \par \uc2 Mike and I have had conversations, very positive ones, with different members of the Senate who are very interested in this bill and who were very surprised by the - the size or the margin of the overwhelming bipartisan support in the House.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, the White House has asked the Senate to take up this bill quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, as you know, President Biden has his campaign on TikTok. Do you agree with Senator Warner that it looks like he`s basically speaking out of both sides of his mouth on this one? Is it unhelpful?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI\uc2 : No, I - I - look, I - I`m not going to tell the president how to campaign. I don`t have it on my personal app - on my personal phone. And it`s, of course, banned from all of our government devices.\par \par \uc2 I would just say that it`s legal now, but I - I would ask everyone to exercise caution with regard to the app because of the various risks that we`ve outlined as part of this bill.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman Gallagher, on the risks, we are eight months out from the presidential election. And in the U.S. intelligence assessment that was made public on Monday, it specifically mentioned that the Chinese government propaganda arm used TikTok to target candidates from both political parties in those midterm elections in 2022. So, we can only assume this is going to be happening in 2024 as well.\par \par \uc2 Do you actually believe a forced sale will happen before November?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 : It absolutely could. I would be in the financial interest of ByteDance`s investors to effectuate a forced sale. I think the user experience on the app would improve and you wouldn`t have this concern over being propagandized. The opaque algorithm influencing what information you see, that would go away. So, I imagine it would actually increase in value.\par \par \uc2 And I think all the more reason why we have to tackle this now, the closer we get to an election, the risk just gets greater and greater. You mentioned that unclassified DNI report. Every single intelligence community official that testified before the intelligence committee last week suggested under its current ownership structure TikTok is a threat to national security, which is why we are trying to force TikTok to separate from ByteDance and by extension from the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But we have been hearing, respectfully, for four years or more that this is a national security threat and something needs to be done, and the it dies in Congress or in the courts. If this version becomes law, how does this actually -- how do you actually stop it because this app is already on 170 million phones? Can you claw it back?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 : Well, if a divestiture happens, if the sale happens, you wouldn`t need to claw it back. People can continue to use the app and post weird dance videos, they can campaign on the app.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But how do you force a company?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 : The force - the -- the forcing function we have in the bill is if they do not pursue a sale, then they would no longer have access to the app store so you couldn`t do new downloads. The website would actually - would also be affected, and so it would affect the functionality of the app. But, again, that`s not the outcome we want. The outcome we want is a forced separation. We think that`s the healthiest for all parties involved. And just given the reporting we have, and TikTok`s own behavior in the last month, look at the pop-up notification they forced on millions of users that resulted in kids calling with school bells in the background saying, you know, if you don`t - if you don`t rescind this bill I`m going to kill myself. That`s just a taste of how this app could be weaponized. Imagine a more consequential vote going forward about an authorizing force to defend Taiwan or altering permanent, normal trade relation status with China. That`s the risk we`re trying to guard against.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But is it really, gentlemen, just TikTok? I mean there are no guardrails virtually in place for any kind of social media that those kids or us adults have access to, Congressman Krishnamoorthi.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI: I agree. And Mike and I would love to work with others with regard to putting guardrails in place with regard to other apps, regardless of their ownership.\par \par \uc2 But this one is different in the sense that we have a foreign adversary controlling this app. Just to give your viewers a sense of it, essentially ByteDance is the 100 percent owner of TikTok. ByteDance basically has its editor in chief who`s also the secretary of the Chinese Communist Party cell embedded at the highest echelons of the company to control all of its products. And he has been tasked with, quote, "making sure all of its products, including TikTok, adhere to correct political direction." And so this is a different type of threat than we`ve seen before.\par \par \uc2 And this divestment is not without precedent. There was an app called Grinder -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI: A popular LBGTQ app that was acquired by a Chinese company. And when the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  found that the CCP had access to sensitive data about LBGTQ members of the military and \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  officials, it required the divestment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI: It happened very quickly. Why? Because the app is very -- was very valuable, much like TikTok, and there was no disruption to users. That`s what will happen with regard to this divestment as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman Gallagher, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was here saying how disappointed he was that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has changed his views on TikTok. This week he was on CNBC and said that, you know, oh, well it will just benefit \uc2 Facebook\uc2  if you crack down on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 Doesn`t that just incentivize the slow rolling of any legislation until after November? You know, the lobbyists can really grab right on to that.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 : While there is a - an unprecedented lobbying campaign, they`re really weaponizing the swamp against legislative action. Over half a million dollars spent last quarter alone on seven different lobbying firms. It`s disgusting. Former members of Congress lobbying on behalf of TikTok. By the way, that should be illegal. Members of Congress should not be allowed to lobby in general, let alone on behalf of foreign adversaries. Side note.\par \par \uc2 But I do think the fact that we got a vote, really a huge vote in the House, bipartisan, 352, makes it impossible to ignore. And that`s a testament to Raja`s leadership, the fact that we were able to come together at a time when the parties don`t trust each other on anything.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 : Far better for Congress to step up and legislate a solution so that we`re not bouncing back and forth between different presidents, different executive orders. That`s a recipe for chaos.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And to be fair here, I mean TikTok --\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI\uc2 : Margaret, can I jump in for a second on that?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure. I to just want to say that TikTok, as you know, insists that it is not under the control of the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI: That is completely - that is completely false and that is exactly why TikTok is in trouble on Capitol Hill. Their CEO has appeared on Capitol Hill repeatedly and made various misrepresentations, Margaret, that have caused members on both sides to really scratch their heads and vote for this bill.\par \par \uc2 Just two - I`ll just point out two very briefly. One is that he claimed that ByteDance, China based employees, never had access to American user data. And that was proven to be false. They repeatedly access it, even unbeknownst to TikTok USA employees. And the second misrepresentation is that he said that this data, American user data, was never used against Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI: That was proven false. And the company admitted that they spied on journalists who were covering TikTok.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI: And they used their geolocation data to surveil them.\par \par \uc2 So, this is why they`re in such hot water on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, congressmen, thank you for joining us in this rare bipartisan action very quickly on TikTok. We`ll be following what happens next. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to tech journalist and podcast host Kara Swisher, who is the author of "Burn Book." She joins us now from Ann Arbor, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Kara, good to have you back on the program.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER (Author, "Burn Book"): Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, we just heard from two of the congressmen really pushing this legislation right now. But notably, of a key Republican senator, \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , who supports the bill, said "The Senate will nickel and dime it, death by a thousand cuts. Nothing that big tech doesn`t want moves across the Senate floor."\par \par \uc2 Do you think he`s right?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why would this be different than any time in the past?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Well, I think -- you know, there`s a lot of lobbying going on about this bill and the Senate is slower and there`s some issues in the bill that probably they`re going to want to correct. And so, you know, slow rolling, it`s the best way to - to have this thing not pass.\par \par \uc2 It is unprecedented that it moved so quickly in the House, although it`s been a long time coming. You remember Donald Trump`s the one that had the executive order around TikTok and - and its service. And so this has been brewing for a long time. It`s just that it will take a lot longer in the Senate to do it. And there`s opponents like \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  and others and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  hasn`t been particularly supportive or non-supportive of it.\par \par \uc2 So, it will just take longer and that`s in - in the favor of - of TikTok.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I know you`ve said in the past on this program, you use TikTok but only on a burner phone. In other words, you don`t trust --\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That you could carry it on a phone with you into private spaces and not be spied on?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Look, Margaret, I don`t trust any of them, but I particularly don`t trust a Chinese owned company. That`s all. I had it on a burner phone. I wrote a column in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " about four or five years ago about it. I think it`s an exciting app. I think it`s a broadcast network. It`s obviously entertainment. And it does it really well. I just feel like every Chinese company I`ve covered has had the tentacles of the Chinese Communist Party involved in it, whether they like it or not. You know, in the case of some of them, they don`t like it, but that`s the case.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s not to be said, there`s not issues with every single U.S. based company, \uc2 Meta\uc2  and Alphabet. But this is a very different step level as far as I`m concerned. And these are -- this is a foreign adversary and it`s very different. China -- a Chinese-owned company would not be able to own \uc2 CBS\uc2 . And I consider -- this is a media company. And so that`s the question, are we going to allow a media company from China to have so much purview around the United States? We are not allowed to be in China. Our companies are not allowed to be there, and so there`s not reciprocity, because they would be worried about the exact same things we should be worried about, which is propaganda, surveillance, anything else.\par \par \uc2 And I get the First Amendment arguments and everything else, but TikTok`s not going away, kids. This is the thing that sort of -- that TikTok is using a little bit that is, I think, disingenuous in that it`s not going away. Someone`s either going to buy it or they`re going to be allowed to continue to operate with some guide - guardrails, which they`re - which is happening now with Project Texas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Which I won`t go into details.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: But there`s got to be some way to monitor this particular service more than the others. At the same time, we should have privacy bills, anti-trust bills, algorithmic transparency bills, which the - which Congress has not act on - acted on in two decades of internet hegemony, essentially.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly. Exactly. There aren`t really guardrails period.\par \par \uc2 TikTok is valued at approximately $100 billion according to \uc2 Wedbush Securities\uc2 , but just $40 billion without the source code.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Who would buy this?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Oh, a lot of companies. You could see \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  picking it up. Maybe \uc2 Apple\uc2 . There`s a whole bunch of consortiums. Steve Mnuchin has one. \uc2 Bobby Kotick\uc2 , who - who just sold \uc2 Activision\uc2  to \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . There`s a lot of people who would buy this.\par \par \uc2 No, I think the question is - and I had a really interesting discussion with Mark Cuban about this recently, what do you buy precisely because the algorithm is not coming with this company? The Chinese government would never let that happen, because the algorithm will actually, if you could reverse engineer it, prove possibly that there is problems with it or anything else.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if there is. I just know, should there be allowed to have the opportunity? And so what are you buying? You`re buying the 170 million say U.S. users and a great brand and - but the algorithm isn`t there and one of the magic of TikTok is the algorithm and the history that you have there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The thing that targets you.\par \par \uc2 This past week, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who you cover in depth, posted that this bill is all about censorship and government control and it`s far too broad. There have been concerns about foreign investors in X and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . What do you make of this argument from him?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Well, this is the guy who just fired someone for give -- doing an interview. I don`t know. I don`t believe anything about what he says. I don`t -- you know, you could make that argument, but why is it if it`s owned by a U.S. entity? It will be able to - you will be able to do whatever you want. And again, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is chock full of foreign investors and everything else.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t know. I don`t - I don`t really take that into account much. He just likes to say free speech a lot and then does the exact opposite. So, I don`t know what to say.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re referring to an interview that Don Lemon did with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and the controversy over that (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes. I`m sorry, I`m not being specific.\par \par \uc2 No, it`s constant like that. Like, talks about free speech and then freezes out journalists and stuff like that. I just - anyone that goes on and on about free speech, really, you have to really look more carefully about what they actually do rather than what they say.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. But I -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: In any case, you know, that`s the argument from that side. That is that argument. So --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. But I raise him particularly because Mark Warner, the chair of the Senate Intelligence Committee, had said there`s not a single person more beholden to the Chinese Communist Party than \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  because of his other businesses, and yet he owns this --\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, I would - I would agree. I don`t know. I just - you have to - you know, consider the source. That`s how I would put it. I always consider the source, Margaret. In this case, really consider the source.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you - you know this business and this industry so well.\par \par \uc2 Kara Swisher, thank you for your insight.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: According to \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 , 81 percent of households in Gaza don`t have enough access to clean water, and nine in ten people do not have enough food to survive. The organization`s executive director is Catherine Russell, and she`s here with us.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL (Executive Director, \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 ): Thank you very much. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You focus in particular on the children. We heard this week from the leaders of the U.S. intelligence community that there will be a generational impact from what is happening in Gaza. The implications of that they were looking through the national security lens.\par \par \uc2 From your perspective, what does that mean? What does a generational impact mean?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Well, it means - it means that what`s happening now is more than 13,000 children already have been killed, which is an astronomical, horrifying number. Thousands more have been injured or we can`t even determine where they are. They may be stuck under rubble. Thousands more have lost one or both parents. Some of these children, you`ve seen them on the news, they`re just by themselves managing their younger siblings. I mean it`s a horrifying situation.\par \par \uc2 So, when you think about the impact of that on those children as they grow up, even on their children, right, it is an impact that is so profound because of the stress that they`re living under and the - the grief and the loss and the fear that they`re living under, it`s - it`s bound to have impacts on them the rest of their lives.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Since October 7th, 33 Israeli children have been killed in those horrific attacks of that day. As you just said, 13,000 in Gaza that you know of.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: That we know of.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s just horrific for the youngest -\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: It`s all horrific. You know, honestly, the - the children who were killed in Israel, the children who were taken hostage -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: There are still children who have not been returned to their families in Israel. All horrific, right? Every one of those cases is so heartbreaking for that child and that family. I think these numbers that we`re seeing out of Gaza are just staggering. I mean we haven`t seen that rate of death among children in almost any other conflict in the world. It`s really shocking.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, your organization says 31 percent of children, one in three under the age of two in northern Gaza suffer from acute malnutrition. This isn`t just trauma, this is - this is stunting them for life.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes. Well, if they survive.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, I`ve seen a lot of children, unfortunately in my job, around the world who suffer from malnutrition. And it is a shocking thing to see. I mean essentially the body starts to consume itself because it has nothing else. And it`s a painful, painful death for children.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been in wards of children who are suffering from severe malnutrition. The whole ward is absolutely quiet because the children and the baby have - - don`t even have the energy to cry. I mean it is a devastating thing to see. And you`re right, if they survive it, if we can manage to get -- what we do is provide therapeutic feeding for them - if we can get that to them, they can survive, but often they`re stunted for life. And stunting means that your ability, your cognitive ability is impacted as well. So, it is a lifelong challenge for these children if they survive. But we know now that children are dying from malnutrition in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The CIA director testified about children starving in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What are you able to actually get in at this point? And we know there are air drops happening. You can`t air drop vaccines. You can`t air drop things that need to be refrigerated. So, what`s getting in and what do you need to get in?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Well, first of all, the one thing we know for sure is, not enough is getting in. And the air drops are - are, as you say, come -- some things are coming in that way. Some things came in through this maritime corridor. But it`s a drop in the bucket in both cases.\par \par \uc2 And what we need to get in for children is, what I said, this therapeutic feeding, which is something called plumpy`nut, I don`t know if you`ve ever seen it, but it`s fantastic and it can save their lives, but we need to get it to them. And we have so little access right now. And it`s very challenging.\par \par \uc2 We also are facing very great bureaucratic challenges moving trucks in. Trucks and moving things by land is by far the most efficient, effective way to get aid in. But there are a lot of challenges to that where we can`t, you know -- I think, excuse me, Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2  mentioned, if things are dual used, sometimes they get rejected. So, we can`t get plastic pipes in, we can`t get some medical kits if they have a little scissors in them. I mean it`s -- it`s completely - almost (INAUDIBLE) sometimes trying to figure out how we get things in through this bureaucratic mess.\par \par \uc2 And I think, at the end of the day, those are choices that are made, right? If the choice is --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Those are political choices.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: They are choices. And I think of the choice is to say, we need to get as much humanitarian aid flooded into this area as possible, everyone can do better in that regard. And I think that the population there is suffering in such a terrible way. And I think one of the big challenges is, because there`s such limited access for the press, in addition, it`s hard to see that, right? And I think it would be great if there were more transparency, if everyone could see what the challenges are, what the legitimate bottlenecks are and how we could all do better.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. We rely on our producer who lives in Gaza, Marwar al-Ghoul, but we cannot get in, you`re correct.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes. Terrible. Exactly. Which is not - right, it should be - you should be able to get in. You should be able to see what`s happening. The world should be able to see what`s happening and make their own judgments about what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, the Israeli government has not permitted that to date.\par \par \uc2 The ongoing crisis in Haiti, I need to ask you about that.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is so close to U.S. shores. Americans are watching it now. What is happening there? I read that two-thirds of children need aid.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Yes. Haiti is a horrific situation. I was there several months ago. There was so much violence even then and there were gangs who were controlling parts of the capital. Now they`ve basically taken over the capital. They`ve taken over the airport.\par \par \uc2 What that means for people is that we can`t get aid in, again, it`s very difficult for us to do that. Many, many people there are suffering from serious hunger and malnutrition and we`re not able to get enough aid to them. It`s - it`s - it`s almost like - it`s like a scene out of "Mad Max," honestly. That`s what it seems like. Gangs, vigilantes responding to the gangs. I mean somehow we need to get more control over that situation so that we can get the humanitarian response in and so that this, you know, population that has suffered kind of one thing after another over the years, earthquakes, cholera, Covid, I mean it`s literally one thing after another for Haiti. And I think right now it`s the worst that anyone has seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s saying a lot.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: That`s saying a lot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Catherine Russell, thank you for sharing your insights.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: Thank you, Margaret. Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020240317ek3h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 14, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jo Ling Kent, Nicole Sganga, Cristian Benavides,  Nancy Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Chris Warren, Dave Malkoff, Kris Van  Cleave \par \uc2 3,836 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tornado on the ground!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, cross-country weather threats, tens of millions in the path of a dangerous storm from the Midwest to the South, with so- called gorilla hail and tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, heavy snow in Colorado leaves people stranded.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I was crying all day yesterday because I wanted to be home, and I can`t.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with more than 40 million Americans under threat from two major storm systems. Heavy snow is falling across the Rockies, with three feet already on the ground in parts of Colorado, causing travel nightmares at the airports and on the roads. Hundreds of flights at \uc2 Denver International Airport\uc2  were canceled today, and more severe and dangerous weather is sweeping across the middle of the country today, with several tornadoes touching down in Kentucky and Indiana.\par \par \uc2 This funnel cloud was spotted over a small town in Kentucky. Communities are surveying the aftermath with downed trees and severely damaged homes and businesses. Now, that same system is moving east, with more than a dozen states under severe weather threats tonight from Dallas to Indianapolis with thunderstorms, damaging hail, and more tornadoes in the forecast.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Dave Malkoff will start us off tonight with more on these life- threatening storms.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Tornado on the ground.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): Tonight, the severe weather threat keeps growing in the Midwest, with multiple tornadoes touching down in Indiana. In Hanover, a tornado ripped through homes, causing roofs to collapse, knocking down trees and power lines.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Total destruction, the inside --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: -- everything.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: In Madison, another tornado crossed the Ohio River. Video shows debris spinning in the air. In Milton, Kentucky, at least 100 structures were damaged after a tornado touched down.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: It`s on the ground. It`s on the ground. It`s on the ground.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Last night, a supercell thunderstorm spun up a tornado, as severe weather moved through Alta Vista, Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Hail battered parts of Northern Kansas, some hail bigger than golf balls. In Colorado, record-breaking snowfall is the problem. Last night, some parts of the state have got 20, 30 inches of snow. Now try digging out of all this, especially if you rarely see snow like this.\par \par \uc2 Hazardous winter conditions created a nightmare in Colorado Springs, leaving some drivers trapped, people stuck on highways like I-70 causing a standstill, leading to major closures west of Denver.\par \par \uc2 BOY: I came here to ski.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN PINA (Traveler): Our flight got canceled. We rented a car, came up here, tried to get to Steamboat. But, unfortunately, the blizzard set us in, and we were lucky enough to get a place to stay here.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The snow also causing more than 800 flight cancellations at \uc2 Denver International Airport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I was crying all day yesterday, because I wanted to be home. And I can`t.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: At this blustery exit in Golden, Colorado, there are hundreds of people from all over the country who are just simply stuck here.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, all of this snow, every single last flake of it, has fallen since 10:00 p.m. last night.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 For a look now at where this severe weather is headed next, let`s bring in meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 As Dave just showed us, it is still snowing in Colorado. And with this lingering system, still a couple more feet of snow is possible in the higher elevations. The big threat, though, we`re tracking tonight is a severe weather threat that has been picking up, very large hail and tornadoes possible, with a damaging wind threat also going through the overnight hours, as, tomorrow, the threat shifts now to the South.\par \par \uc2 We will watch these storms roll through during the daylight hours and then into the evening dropping to the South. The storms are now starting to pick up in Texas. Going into the overnight hours, this front is also going to bring some heavy rain that`s going to stall out with, Norah, the potential for some flash flooding going right into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chris Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a political review causing shockwaves from Washington to Tel Aviv. The top Democrat in the Senate and the highest-ranking Jewish elected official is slamming Israel`s prime minister, calling for new elections to replace \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports, Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is now facing backlash, some backlash following this Senate floor speech.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): Nearly half-a-year into the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , one of Congress` staunchest supporters of Israel, uncorked a blistering criticism of the Israeli leader.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): Prime Minister Netanyahu has lost his way.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a 40-minute address on the Senate floor, Schumer called for new elections and new Israeli leadership.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 : He has been too willing to tolerate the civilian toll in Gaza, which is pushing support for Israel worldwide to historic lows.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Schumer`s words come amid growing concerns among Democrats and potential Democratic voters about Israel`s aggressive military response to the October 7 attack by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- and Netanyahu`s resistance to let more humanitarian aid into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 More than 31,000 Palestinians have died. And Gaza is on the brink of famine. President Biden has voiced his own frustration with Netanyahu, but has never called to replace him. White House officials, who knew in advance what Schumer would say, have expressed deep concern about Israel`s plans to conduct a ground invasion of the crowded city of Rafah.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s ambassador to the U.S. called Schumer`s comments "unhelpful" and said, "Israel is a sovereign democracy."\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): This is not only highly inappropriate; it`s just plain wrong.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: While congressional Republicans blasted Schumer for undercutting a U.S. ally.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-Kentucky): It is grotesque and hypocritical for Americans who hyperventilate about foreign interference in our own democracy to call for the removal of the democratically elected leader of Israel.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tonight, there`s still uncertainty about the fate of hostages being held in Gaza, including five Americans.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Netanyahu has rejected the latest cease-fire proposal from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , arguing, it`s based on unrealistic demands -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  today became the first sitting vice president or president to visit an abortion clinic. The Biden campaign hopes that, by putting reproductive rights front and center of this election cycle, it`ll motivate voters to go to the polls.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes reports tonight from Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): The reporter said, "Well, you`re -- you have made history, I guess, again," because I did. But --\par \par \uc2 (Crowd cheers)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): The vice president made history with a half-hour private tour of an unassuming spot, a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic in St. Paul.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The reason I`m here is because this is a health care crisis.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The clinic has seen a 25 percent surge in abortions since two neighboring states, along with several others, enacted full abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And, Doctor, please.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Sarah Traxler is the chief medical officer.\par \par \uc2 DR. SARAH TRAXLER (Planned Parenthood): Our new abortion landscape is difficult. It is dangerous. And it is putting my patients and health care providers at severe risk.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The visit was the last stop for the V.P. in a six-state swing that has been solely focused on reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: These extremists, they`re trying to take women back to the 1800s. We must restore the protections of Roe.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The issue is a top focus for the Biden campaign. And here`s why. 57 percent of Americans say the overturning of Roe v. Wade nearly two years ago was mostly bad for the country.\par \par \uc2 Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is this the most animating issue for Democrats in your state right now?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (D-Minnesota): Well, it`s one of them. I think people are passionate. We`re passionate people. We vote in very high numbers. But I think, at the end of the day, it`s the one that is most personal and feels the most threatening. And it`s the one that`s real.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who attended a hearing in his classified documents case today, has strategically declined to say if he backs the 15- week national abortion ban favored by some Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News, January)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): You have to win elections. Otherwise, you`re going to be back where you were. And you can`t let that ever happen again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We got some legal news today in another one of the four criminal cases that Trump is currently facing.\par \par \uc2 The Manhattan DA offered a 30-day delay in a trial that was supposed to get under way in just a couple of weeks. Now, this is the case that has to do with hush money payments that were allegedly made to adult film star Stormy Daniels. The defense wants 90 days to review tens of thousands of pages of new documents that were just handed over to them. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Haitian government is extending its overnight curfew to at least Sunday. Street gangs control much of the capital, and violence flared up again today. The U.S. Diplomatic Security Service shared these night- vision photos showing heavy security outside the U.S. Embassy.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Cristian Benavides reports on Americans still trapped in the chaos.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voiceover): Fire trucks in Port-au-Prince race to Haiti`s largest prison set on fire after a mass prison break two weeks ago of 4,000 prisoners was orchestrated by gangs that sparked violence and chaos.\par \par \uc2 It`s been three days since Prime Minister \uc2 Ariel Henry\uc2  resigned, leaving a power vacuum, where gangs now control 80 percent of the capital city. It`s believed there are several hundred Americans still in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 GEN. LAURA RICHARDSON (Commander, U.S. Southern Command): We have our contingency plan.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: General Laura Richardson, the head of U.S. Southern Command, said they are ready to evacuate Americans.\par \par \uc2 Dennis Hankins was confirmed today as U.S. ambassador to Haiti. The post has been vacant since October 2021. Here at the border with the Dominican Republic, tensions remain high and deportations continue. But thousands of other Haitians were allowed to cross less than 100 yards to buy badly needed goods at this open market for the first time in days.\par \par \uc2 Rosyli Peralta (sp?) runs a shop here.\par \par \uc2 (ROSYLI PERALTA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: So, you have noticed that you -- you get less sales and you have gotten less sales since the crisis started.\par \par \uc2 Also, Americans making it out of Haiti. Missionaries Joel and Yvonne Trimble have been splitting their time between Florida and Cap Haitien for the past 49 years. They managed to evacuate today.\par \par \uc2 JOEL TRIMBLE (Missionary): It`s -- it`s never been this bad.\par \par \uc2 YVONNE TRIMBLE (Missionary): Absolutely. Absolutely. But the violence, the guns, it`s the worst it`s ever been.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: I`m standing in front of the market, which has now closed.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials, Norah, say that Guantanamo Bay is ready to be used as a processing center in case of a surge of Haitian migrants trying to flee the violence -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Cristian Benavides, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a number of new developments in what`s being called the biggest cybersecurity attack on America`s health care system. The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  is now investigating \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2  after hackers shut down the company`s payment processing arm. Three weeks later, the pharmacy network is now back online, but many providers are still struggling.\par \par \uc2 CBS Nicole Sganga reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA (voiceover): A ransomware attack turned national crisis.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTINE MEYER (Founder and Owner, CMMD & Associates): It`s been one of the most stressful things we have gone through as a practice. And that`s saying something, given that we survived COVID.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: And for doctors like Christine Meyer, a personal nightmare.\par \par \uc2 On an average weekday, you would get?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTINE MEYER: Twenty, $30,000, $40,000, $50,000 in deposits.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: And, today, you got?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTINE MEYER: Seventy-seven.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The hack targeted \uc2 Change Healthcare\uc2 , a unit of \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2 , forcing the nation`s largest medical payment system offline on February 21st, leaving hospitals and medical practices in a cash crunch.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTINE MEYER: So, to find ourselves suddenly looking at our home and its value, and can we afford to put it up to pay our employees is a terrible feeling.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: White House officials cracking down on \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  Tuesday, \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  demanding the company front hospitals and doctors more emergency funds.\par \par \uc2 How are you holding them accountable?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2  (U.S. Health and Human Services Secretary): We will not continue to work with a clearinghouse exchange operation if it can`t provide the payment for the services that our patients need.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Dr. Meyer stresses, it`s not just a payroll problem. It`s a patient problem.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTINE MEYER: I`m most worried about having to reduce our hours. Our patients that can`t come here for their routine things, their sore throats, their cough, their blood pressure checks, where are they going to go?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Potentially landing patients in emergency rooms.\par \par \uc2 What is preventing another ransomware incident like this from happening tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY XAVIER BECERRA: Everyone should be asking themselves, what`s the answer to the question? What do I need to do to make sure I`m not the next target? And what do I need to do to make sure that, if I am a target, I don`t bring a whole bunch of folks down with me over that cliff?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Concerns about a potential patient data breach have also prompted at least six class action lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2  said in a statement that it`s committed to providing relief to patients and practices affected. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sganga, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, TikTok is fighting back after the House passed a bill calling for it to be banned in the U.S. unless it cuts ties with China. The Senate is now reviewing the legislation.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent takes a look at what`s next for the world`s most popular app.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clips from TikTok)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: This is not what we want.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): The fight over the future of TikTok is bringing out angry users and a determined CEO.\par \par \uc2 Today, on Capitol Hill, TikTok`s CEO held private meetings and made a public plea.\par \par \uc2 SHOU ZI CHEW (CEO, TikTok): But I haven`t heard exactly what we have done that`s wrong. This is a ban on the app in this country. It`s going to impact 170 million Americans who use our app.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: He`s personally attempting to stop a possible ban after the House passed a bill on Wednesday that would mandate TikTok separate from its Chinese parent company. It would have to sell to a U.S. buyer to avoid a ban.\par \par \uc2 Today, former Treasury Secretary in the Trump administration, \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2 , says he wants to buy it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CNBC Squawk Box)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN MNUCHIN\uc2  (Former \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary): I understand the technology. It`s a great business, and I`m going to put together a group to buy TikTok.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You`re trying to buy TikTok?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN MNUCHIN\uc2 : I am, because this should be owned by U.S. -- U.S. businesses.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: But that may not even be possible.\par \par \uc2 SAPNA MAHESHWARI (Business Reporter, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 "): Let`s say all of these things come together and a sale is poised, the Chinese government could very well stop it. It`s unclear if the algorithm is something that could even be exported to another country.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: If TikTok is banned?\par \par \uc2 SAPNA MAHESHWARI: The way it would work is, the app just wouldn`t be updated. It would be removed from stores, and it would degrade over time.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @annathingbutanimals/TikTok)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: This is not just an attack on freedom of speech. This is an attack on small businesses.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Meanwhile, the company`s CEO is weighing every option.\par \par \uc2 SHOU ZI CHEW: We will continue to do all we can, including exercising our legal rights, to protect this amazing platform that we have built with you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Jo Ling Kent joins us now.\par \par \uc2 There`s so much to talk about. I mean, this is so unprecedented. So, what do you think happens now?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Norah, it`s up to Majority Leader Schumer to schedule a Senate vote.\par \par \uc2 And if the bill passes, President Biden says he will sign it. And then, if China is willing to sell, it could be the equivalent of selling \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  or \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  or \uc2 Shopify\uc2 . TikTok is reportedly currently valued at about $100 billion. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Just incredible.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent, thank you for this.\par \par \uc2 A 14-year-old girl is nearly kidnapped on her way home from school. Why police are praising what she did. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Terrifying new video shows a 14-year-old girl escaping from an attempted kidnapping while walking home from school last week.\par \par \uc2 Police in Glendale, Arizona, say an SUV stopped and a 28-year-old man got out and started following her. Well, the girl sensed something was wrong and ran away, until she saw another man walking his dog, who helped her. The man was arrested the next day. He was already on probation for similar crimes.\par \par \uc2 Police are praising the girl for being aware of her surroundings and doing exactly what she should.\par \par \uc2 The world`s most powerful rocket breaks apart in its return to Earth. Why \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is calling this historic test flight a success. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 `s massive new Starship blasted off from Texas today. It didn`t go exactly as planned.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave explains why \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  considers the test a giant leap forward.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 PEOPLE: Six, five, four, three --\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): The most powerful rocket ever built blasted off this morning. It was \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 `s third test of its enormous 397-foot-tall super heavy Starship rocket, technology crucial to NASA`s return to the moon.\par \par \uc2 The previous two launches ended in dramatic explosions.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Stage separation.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: This time, the rocket`s 39 engines powered Starship safely into space.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: OK, we got a Starship on its way to space and a booster on the way back to the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But not everything went to plan. Moments after these dramatic images of reentry, heating the craft to more than 2,500 degrees, Starship broke apart.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: We are making the call now that we have lost Ship 28.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The rocket`s booster was also lost after the launch. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  had hoped to attempt rocket-controlled descents with both.\par \par \uc2 BILL HARWOOD: Getting the Starship part of that up into space and beginning reentry, collecting all that data is a huge step forward for them.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: CBS News space analyst Bill Harwood.\par \par \uc2 BILL HARWOOD: They`re trying to do something no one has ever even attempted. And the fact that they lost the Starship on re-entry, I don`t view that as very significant at all in the third test flight. They`re going to collect enough data from this to figure out how to fix that down the road.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A step closer to human boots on the moon, with many more still ahead.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Heart of America is next with a new class for New York`s bravest.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet the newest members of New York`s bravest. Nearly 300 FDNY firefighters graduated this week, joining the nation`s largest fire department. The probationary firefighters, known as probies, were filled with pride. And they`re committed to keeping New Yorkers safe.\par \par \uc2 Among the graduates is Jerome Nedd Jr. of Brooklyn, whose father was a chef in the North Tower of the World Trade Center.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEROME NEDD JR. (FDNY): My father died on 9/11. So it was -- it was a decision for me, because firefighters went into that building to try and get him out. And those brave heroes that died in that building, I wanted to be like one of them. And I wanted to help people as well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 Well, this new class also includes 10 women and 34 veterans. Officials said half of the graduates are people of color, as the department continues to look more like the city it protects.\par \par \uc2 So, congratulations. And thank you to the newest members of the FDNY, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240315ek3e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 The Future of TikTok; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Training Exercise Explosion; Dangerous Colorado Snowstorm; Trump Charges Dropped; The Voice of Wisconsin; James Crumbley Jury Deliberations; Early 401(k) Withdrawal Surge; Olivia Munn`s Breast Cancer Diagnosis; Spreading Sandy Hook Conspiracies; Students Surprise Security Guard; UnitedHealthcare Hack Fallout; Who You Gonna Call; A New Sense of Purpose; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Scott MacFarlane,  Dave Malkoff, Stephanie Abrams, Nikole Killion, Nancy Cordes, Jill  Schlesinger, Elaine Quijano, Nicole Sganga \par \uc2 15,281 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear trumpets. Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, but we`re still here.\par \par \uc2 So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Keep TikTok.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A bill that may ban TikTok is passed by the House, what that could mean for users and influencers making a living.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: What would you do if it went away tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 GOHAR KHAN (Influencer): I would have to figure out a whole new strategy for bringing customers in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A massive storm is threatening millions in the West with snow and high winds.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: The forecast the biggest snow of the season, 14 to 18 inches across the Metro.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Actor, Olivia Munn reveals a secret battle with breast cancer and what saved her life.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m going to be there with you.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN (Actor): Thanks. I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: An explosion at an \uc2 FBI\uc2  training facility in Southern California sent 16 SWAT officers to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: A 12-year-old boy in India and the leopard just walked in off the street, so kind of kept an eye on the leopard, just closed the door behind him.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: The craziest play you`ll see all day. TCU long half court pass, stolen by Oklahoma from the TCU`s Micah Peavy, steals it back, heaves it, Peavy still had a shot. Oh my goodness. They`re going to count it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA ("The Daily Show"): For more on the TikTok ban, we go live to Washington DC with Ronny Chieng.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 DESI LYDIC ("The Daily Show"): What choice our leaders have here