 is now headed to the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill for us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Scott, a lot of action.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. What do we know?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Tony. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TikTok`s massive lobbying campaign didn`t stop this bill from moving forward with huge bipartisan support. The president has said he`ll sign the bill, but the Senate hasn`t given any indication when it will act or what it will decide.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: TikTok is famous for its fast moving viral videos, but the momentum in Congress against TikTok is spreading almost as fast.\par \par \uc2 REP. BETH VAN DUYNE (R-TX): The yeas are 352, the nays are 65.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Less than two weeks after it was introduced, a bill requiring TikTok to separate from China-based owner ByteDance or face a ban in the US easily passed in the US House.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): This is a commonsense measure to protect our national security.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Now, TikTok is trying to stop the legislation in the Senate arguing it undercuts First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Keep TikTok.")\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The company paid for airfare and lodging for some of its most influential users to descend on Capitol Hill this week, including Gohar Khan of Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 GOHAR KHAN: You`ve probably seen me on your For Your page a few times.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Who post strategy videos on college applications to TikTok savvy high schoolers.\par \par \uc2 GOHAR KHAN: So most of my income comes from TikTok.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Most of it does?\par \par \uc2 GOHAR KHAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: TikTok also sent waves of push alerts urging its users to reach out to their congressperson by typing in their zip codes, and some lawmakers said the bill went too far too