t`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Vlad, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start in Colorado where we could see up to three feet of snow in the Mile High City, a month`s worth could pile up. March is typically Denver`s snowiest month with 11-and-a-half inches. Today, there could be power outages with the heavy snow and the winds gusting as high as 50 miles an hour. The low causing this mess hangs around the four corners this weekend, but thankfully it won`t be as potent.\par \par \uc2 The severe threat continues today for the center of the country. Hail big enough to cause damage and a few tornadoes possible. The Midwest starts today on the stormy side. This pushes into the Northeast this evening. That`s also when we`ll see severe weather popping off in the South. This lasts into our Friday morning when there could be flooding from Texas all the way into Georgia.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices. Vlad, the warmth helping fuel these storms lasts into the weekend, but there is a big cool off next week.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m not looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected in a Florida court. His lawyers are pushing for charges to be dismissed in the case where he is accused of illegally holding classified documents.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime in Atlanta, the judge in Trump`s election interference case has thrown out some of the charges against him and his co-defendants.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is there with that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE SCOTT MCAFEE (Fulton County, Georgia): All right, we are on the record with --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Fulton County Judge Scott McAfee dismissed six counts against former President Trump and five other co-defendants including top Trump allies \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , and conservative attorney, \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 . While the 41-count racketeering case largely remained intact, McAfee throughout charges that accused the defendants of soliciting Georgia officials to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 McAfee said the indictment wasn`t specific about the violations, writing: "The lack of detail is fatal."\par \par \uc2 The dropped charges against Trump include one for this January 2021 call to Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Look, Brad, I`ve got to get -- I have to find 12,000 votes and I have them times a lot, and therefore I won the state.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Trump`s attorney called the ruling the correct application of the law, but former senior DOJ official Tom Dupree says it`s only a partial victory for the former president.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE (Former Deputy Assistant Attorney General): The judge`s decision effectively has the result of narrowing the case against Trump and the other defendants. It doesn`t eliminate it entirely though.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The ruling comes ahead of a highly anticipated decision this week about whether to disqualify Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  and special prosecutor Nathan Wade from the case over allegations their romantic relationship created a conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2  (Fulton County, Georgia district attorney): I`m not on trial, no matter how hard you try to put me on trial.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: If Willis is disqualified, the election interference case could be referred to a special prosecuting counsel here in Georgia that would then have