there, the testimony is there. We want the justice just like anybody else does.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Elaine Quijano.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Heartbreaking story.\par \par \uc2 More Americans are taking money out of their 401(k) retirement accounts early -- it`s what I`m trying to say -- but here`s the question, is that a good idea? Well, we`ve got \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 . She`s here to answer that. Is it a good idea, Jill?\par \par \uc2 Oh, no. That doesn`t sounds so good.\par \par \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 , okay, thumbs down. She she`ll be here to answer the question for you in more detail. But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s funny.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, the extra step doctors took after Olivia Munn tested negative for breast cancer. She says, it saved her life. We`ll show you how it can help you as well.\par \par \uc2 And first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghostbusters: Frozen Empire")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who you going to call? The legendary cast of the new "Ghostbusters" movie, they are here. Some of us are already geeking out. This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In today`s "Money Watch," a new report finds that more Americans are dipping into their 401 (k) retirement savings early.\par \par \uc2 A record number of people took money from their retirement accounts in 2022 and 2023, that`s according to the investment company, \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 . CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here with more. Good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So why are people doing this?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, you know, we have to understand that you could always take money out of your retirement accounts and by law, it is for a hardship. What`s a hardship? It`s foreclosure. You can`t meet your rent. It is medical expenses, funeral expenses -- these are the kinds of things that would motivate you to go into that very sacred account.\par \par \uc2 Now, in this particular survey, we also know that over the last couple of years, inflation is up and so that has actually put a lot of pressure on households. And by the way, the rules have eased on taking money out of these accounts and that has actually allowed people to go in, grab the money, pay off whatever debt is cumulated or meet their bills and move on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what`s the impact or consequences of taking it out early?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why are you so against it? I figure if the bill comes down --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you said --\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes, okay, because it`s really hard to save for retirement, let`s be honest, and you work really hard to get that money in.\par \par \uc2 You know, before 2018, the rules really forced you to take a loan instead of a withdrawal and here`s the problem with the withdrawal, you take the money out, it is taxed at whatever tax bracket you`re at. So whatever you take out, that`s going to be taxed. It hasn`t been taxed yet, right?\par \par \uc2 If you`re under age 59-and-a-half, there is an additional 10 percent whack of a penalty. This is a double tax whammy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Crazy.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Also, it`s really hard once you get that money out, to get it back in and I think that`s the problem that retirement experts are really worried. We are using these accounts too frequently to pay for maybe not emergencies, maybe just day to day bills.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Speaking of getting it back in, the people are saving more also, you were telling me.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : This was an incredibly happy occasion for me, because we found out that \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  looked at their people who were participating. Participation is up. Eighty-five percent of people are participating. They`re putting more money in. Savings increased by 43 percent from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Meaning, the number of people put a little bit more money in. By the way, that is also due to the fact that these plans allow you to automatically escalate your contribution.\par \par \uc2 So a little good news, a little bad news.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems like a contradiction.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s America. A little good news, a little bad news. Jill, thank you very much. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, ahead, TikTok should it banned? Maybe. Can it actually be banned? We`ll talk about it. "The Atlantic`s" Nick Thompson is here to tell us what it means for millions of users of the app.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Nick.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s 7:30. CBS MORNINGS. We are back and the future of TikTok in America is hanging in the balance amid growing pressure from Congress.\par \par \uc2 The House passed legislation yesterday that says the social media app needs to cut ties with its China-based owners, ByteDance or risk a total ban.\par \par \uc2 The bill is now headed to the very slow moving \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , so we want to take you to know some big questions here like, can this really happen if the Senate goes forward? How will it work if it happens? What will the impact be on the users of TikTok?\par \par \uc2 To answer those questions and more Nick Thompson, CEO of "The Atlantic" and former editor-in-chief of Wired is with us. Nick, thank you very much for