opulations. You imagine a conflict with China, how useful would that be?\par \par \uc2 Second, propaganda manipulation. You can imagine, just put a little push on the algorithm. So news that`s bad for Taiwan gets a little extra amplification.\par \par \uc2 Or third, perhaps most likely, let`s just make Americans a little more emotional, a little more angry and you could do that through the algorithm as well.\par \par \uc2 So there are legitimate national security concerns and national security hawks are very pro this ban.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So beyond just the social aspect, there is an economic aspect as well, 170 million Americans are on TikTok. They`re not growing users. That`s interesting to me that TikTok isn`t growing users, but for those that are there.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And sometimes depend on their livelihoods to create content, what does that mean for them?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Well, it means that they have to move somewhere else or find -- I mean, it has a real economic impact. There are a lot of reasons why you wouldn`t want to do this ban. One of which is there is a real economic impact, people advertise it, creators. They make their livings there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, by the way, the president is on TikTok recently.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Well, the president may have gotten on tic tac in order for TikTok not to know that the president was helping out the bill banning them. It`s unclear exactly how that went.\par \par \uc2 But every politician should be on TikTok to get their voice out to young people, but there are legitimate reasons why you would not want to do the ban, including the economic impact, including free speech implications, including the legal fight that is coming.\par \par \uc2 This could be a real mess if it gets through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do we care though, if they have the data? Most people think they`re young users. Why should we care if they have the data of the young people that are using TikTok?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Well, it`s not just they have the data, it`s the ability to influence them. Right?\par \par \uc2 You can imagine China saying we would like the young people in America to have a particular viewpoint and we are going to push that. In fact, one of the reasons this happened, I think, is because the Biden administration and the Democrats who were kind of against a TikTok ban when Trump initially proposed it four years ago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: They saw public opinion, particularly among young people shifting on the conflict between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and they blamed TikTok for that. They saw videos praising Osama bin Laden going viral on TikTok, and they said, whoa.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Is this good for American democracy?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have to add one thing, Gayle, our colleagues over at "60 Minutes" have reported that TikTok, the version that`s available inside China for young people there is basically vegetables and science experiments. It`s like library trips. And here we`ve got the fentanyl opium version. Right?\par \par \uc2 So they are already --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Aware in China.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: That could be them and that could be us. I mean, TikTok is serving what people want. It could be a cultural difference. It could be all kinds of reasons, but it could also be someone turning the dial.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, but they are also not a free speech society, right? So they don`t have \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . They don`t have other social media platforms, because they don`t want them.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: And the other social media platforms, it is so interesting, because if you think about the other economic impacts, \uc2 YouTube\uc2  will gain a lot of users, but \uc2 Google\uc2  will have to pull the app and will actually potentially be leading the legal fight.\par \par \uc2 So you can see \uc2 Google\uc2  which owns \uc2 YouTube\uc2  being extremely conflicted and a lot of people meeting with a lot of lawyers this morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, well, it`s in the hands of the Senate. The president says he`ll sign it if it lands on his desk.\par \par \uc2 Nick Thompson, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Thank you for having me, my friend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to see you. That was great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, coming up, Olivia Munn says a hidden case of breast cancer could have killed her. We will ask a top surgeon about a tool that led to her diagnosis and how it can assess your risk as well.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Actor, Olivia Munn is talking about her battle with breast cancer and a tool that she says saved her life.\par \par \uc2 Munn revealed on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  just yesterday that she had a double mastectomy last year, four surgeries in all in a 10-month period, she said. She posted this emotional video taken in the hospital during her treatment.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are going to handle it for your baby. This stuff doesn`t matter. I swear to God, I would just -- I had a patient say, we are given them to be alive.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is nothing, you know.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN: Dr. Allabadi, thank you much.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Love you. I`m going to be there with you. Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN: I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my, this got to me.\par \par \uc2 Before the diagnosis, Munn says she had a normal mammogram, tested negative for 90 different cancer genes. She wrote on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 : "I wouldn`t have found my cancer for another year at my next scheduled mammogram, except that my OB GYN decided to calculate my breast cancer risk assessment score. The fact that she did that saved my life."\par \par \uc2 With us now is Dr. Elisa Port. She is chief of breast surgery at Mount Sinai Health System in New York City.\par \par \uc2 This story, so anyway, somebody very close to me, I`m going through something similar with this, and so this just struck a chord with me. So I`m in awe and so grateful to her, Olivia Munn that she is telling her story this way.\par \par \uc2 I had never heard of this breast cancer risk assessment tool. This is something we should all be aware of, it seems. I`d never heard of this.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT, CHIEF OF BREAST SURGERY, MOUNT SINAI HEALTH SYSTEM, NEW YORK CITY: Definitely Gayle.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think the most important thing is empowering patients to know about their individual risk. Most women, the average risk of getting breast cancer in their lifetime is about 10 to 12 percent, and then on the other far end of the spectrum are women who carry genetic predisposition, and their risk is about 60 to 80 percent. That`s the highest risk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she had all these positive tests, though, Elisa.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: She had all of the negative --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, everything turned out negative is what I mean.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: The issue is, is that there`s a lot of risk in between -- between 10 and 80 percent, and the question is, how do you figure out what your risk is? And there are other factors that weigh in, separate from genetic predisposition like just having a family history, or having prior breast biopsies, that shows some funny looking cells called atypical cells.\par \par \uc2 These are all things that a lot of women don`t know can drive up their risk for getting breast cancer, and could lead to the concept of added screening, which it seems like Olivia had and detected her breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how do you measure this score? Or how do you calculate this? We put up some of the tools on the screen. But what -- is it a checklist? Or is it an actual physical test?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: It`s -- no, it`s actually -- and there`s a few of them, just so you know, there are different risk models. You can go online and do this. It`s like a survey, a yes or no survey, and it asks you a number of different questions, questions related to your family history, questions related to whether or not you`ve had a breast biopsy or not in what it showed, questions related to reproductive history.\par \par \uc2 What age did as a woman start getting her period? What age did she have children? Believe it or not, the reproductive factors have less of an effect overall, than the other factors on pushing your risk higher.\par \par \uc2 The two big ones that really push your risk higher, are having a family history, which most women know about already. Remember, the risk assessment tool is important. It is very helpful to spit out a quantifiable number, but most women should know already, if they have a family history of breast cancer, that they`re at higher risk than the average person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But so many people who don`t have a family history of breast cancer are still getting breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: That`s exactly true, and none of us is not at risk. Eighty-five to 90 percent of women, Gayle, as you point out, who get breast cancer have no family history. And so we`re all at risk and that`s why when people come and say, you know, I didn`t think to get my mammogram even though I`m 45 or 50, when the starting age is 40. That`s the recommended guidelines for the general population.\par \par \uc2 And women say, I didn`t think to do these general screening tests because I didn`t think I was at risk. But even the average woman`s risk with no other risk factors, her risk is 10 to 12 percent over her lifetime. That`s enough to be doing the appropriate screening tests.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For people who were hearing Olivia`s story today, what should we be doing?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: I think all women should be empowered. Know your risk and whether that`s going online by yourself or going to a doctor who can run this algorithm for you. Those things will all give you a better idea of what you should be doing to be proactive about your health.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Dr. Port, we really thank you for being here and thank you for the work you do.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As we were going to be watching Olivia Munn`s video, you mentioned you`re going to have four surgeries just today.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "What to Watch" coming up next. Right now, it is 7:44. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 We will be interviewing the "Ghostbusters" --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a big day here in our eight o`clock hour. First on CBS MORNINGS, we`re talking about the cast of the highly anticipated new movie "Ghostbusters: Frozen Empire" including original "Ghostbusters," listen to this -- Bill Murray is here, Dan Ackroyd and Ernie Hudson. Can`t wait to see those guys.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Vlad is speaking to an acclaimed former bartender who got everything he wanted and then he nearly lost it all, how running, yep, running helped him outrun his demons, and all that`s co