but I need to hear the commercials, and you could hear the local --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how much are we talking here?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN: So this deal is incredible today. We are doing it for 43 percent off, $39.99.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN: So think of your cable bill.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN: $39.99 once.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Ashley Bellman, I`m already working on my skincare routine here.\par \par \uc2 We partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw today, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com and be sure to text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to some of our most popular deals.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I want to say looking youthful, you know. Everybody says that.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BELLMAN: You already have it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I already got it. I already got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us with youthful Vlad and the lovely Ashley.\par \par \uc2 Tony and I are still here. We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240314ek3e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 13, 2024, \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 13, 2024,\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Roxana Saberi, Nancy Cordes, Cristian Benavides,  Mike Bettes, Nikole Killion, Scott MacFarlane, Janet Shamlian, Kris Van  Cleave \par \uc2 3,881 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, is time up for TikTok?\par \par \uc2 (Clips from TikTok)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 FRANK PALLONE\uc2  (D-New Jersey): Beijing, China, should not have the control over Americans that TikTok gives them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The ultimatum for the popular app used by 170 million Americans after the House passes a bill that would force its Chinese parent company to sell the platform or face a ban.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Keep TikTok! Keep TikTok!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The protests tonight from users.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is 90-plus percent of my family`s income. It`s how we feed our family.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin tonight with the fate of social media app TikTok hanging in the balance. In a rare moment of bipartisanship, the House voted overwhelmingly to pass a bill that would ban the popular platform unless it severs ties with the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 President Biden vowed to sign the bill if it passes the Democratic- controlled Senate. And if the bill becomes law, it would give TikTok`s Chinese-based parent company, \uc2 ByteDance\uc2 , six months to sell the video- sharing app, or it would be removed from app stores and Web hosting services here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Now, TikTok`s massive lobbying efforts were not enough to stop the crackdown, despite a flood of phone calls to members of Congress and these online influencers who were here in Washington visiting Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Scott MacFarlane is going to start us off tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): TikTok has lured 170 million American users with its quick viral videos.\par \par \uc2 (Clips from TikTok)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But perhaps nothing has spread faster than a plan in Congress requiring TikTok separate from its China-based owner or be cut off from its U.S. users.\par \par \uc2 Just eight days after it was introduced, the bill passed the U.S. House overwhelmingly.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KAT CAMMACK (R-Florida): Why in the hell would we want and allow the Chinese Communist Party to have access to our private data?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Despite an 11th-hour flurry of lobbying by TikTok --\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Keep TikTok!\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- which, CBS News has learned, paid to fly and lodge some of its most popular users to Washington this week to make their case.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from TikTok)\par \par \uc2 GOHAR KHAN (TikTok Influencer): My name is Gohar.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Including Gohar Khan of Connecticut, who posts strategy videos for college applicants to millions of TikTok-using high schoolers.\par \par \uc2 What would you do if it went away tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 GOHAR KHAN: I would have to figure out a whole new strategy for bringing customers in.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: TikTok has launched tidal waves of push alerts urging its users to reach out to their congressperson by typing in their zip code.\par \par \uc2 Some in Congress already worry this might be an example of how China could misuse users` personal information.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-Illinois): That, basically, the company had used their geolocation data, along with their zip code information, to target these kids.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: President Biden says he`d signed the bill into law, but it would have to pass a sluggish \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  first. Leaders of the Senate Intelligence Committee have expressed support.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (D-Virginia): This is a national security issue.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But whether China would allow a sale and who would buy TikTok are open issues, and questions remain about First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ROBERT GARCIA (D-California): Passing a bill that could lead to an interruption or an elimination of the app is a huge, monumental decision that I wish more people understood.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a statement of CBS News, TikTok says the legislative process was secretive and that the bill was jammed through, they argue, because they say it`s a ban.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump has opposed this bill. And, Norah, that could be impactful with the now-pivotal Republican U.S. senators.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to that sweeping racketeering case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Georgia, a Fulton County judge today dismissing six of the 41 counts against the former president and top allies \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  . It`s considered a legal setback for prosecutors, but Trump still faces 10 charges that could result in decades in jail.\par \par \uc2 Here \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Fulton County Judge Scott McAfee threw out charges that accused former President Trump and several of his co- defendants of pressuring public officers to violate their oaths as part of an alleged effort to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 McAfee said district attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  and her prosecutors were not specific enough about the violations, writing: "The lack of detail concerning an essential legal element is fatal." Just three of Trump`s 13 counts were tossed, including one for his January 2021 phone call to Georgia`s Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (Audio clip from January 2, 2021)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I just want to find 11,780 votes.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Trump`s legal team applauded the ruling.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA (Attorney For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ): It was a step in the right direction. It goes to the sloppiness of, frankly, the -- the prosecutors down there.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Former federal prosecutor \uc2 Tom Dupree\uc2  said it was only a partial victory for Trump.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS H. DUPREE JR. (Former Deputy Assistant Attorney General): The judge`s decision effectively has the result of narrowing the case against Trump and the other defendants. It doesn`t eliminate it entirely, though.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from February 15)\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS (D-Fulton County, Georgia, District Attorney): It is a lie.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: McAfee`s ruling comes out of a highly anticipated decision this week about whether Willis and special prosecutor Nathan Wade should be disqualified from the election interference case.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the pair admitted to having a romantic relationship for about a year after Wade was hired, but denied any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The romantic relationship ended before the indictment was returned, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 DISTRICT ATTORNEY FANI WILLIS: To a man, yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The sprawling racketeering case against the former president and his associates remained largely intact.\par \par \uc2 But if Willis is disqualified, another attorney could be assigned to the case, which could significantly impact the proceedings -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And potentially delay them.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, a landslide destroyed one home and damaged several others early today in the Sherman Oaks neighborhood of Los Angeles. Video from the scene shows large cracks in the ground near a heavily damaged pool area. No one was hurt, but several people were forced to leave their hillside home. This community was hit with nearly two inches of rain just last week.\par \par \uc2 Yet another winter storm is taking aim at the Rockies tonight, with possibly the biggest snowstorm in years set to slam Denver, while severe storms could bring in hail and tornadoes to parts of the South.\par \par \uc2 So, for more, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  . He`s with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Three major weather stories playing out here over the next couple of days all caused by the same low-pressure storm system. First, it`s the snow, major snow across the Four Corners, including winter storm warnings in Denver, the forecast, the biggest snow of the season, 14 to 18 inches across the Metro. That begins tonight, lasts through the day on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 You can see the timing here, heavy snow throughout the day. It gradually tapers going into Friday and Saturday, but really plagues us here across Utah, Colorado, New Mexico, and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 The other story we`re following, severe weather from Chicago all the way down to Waco, numerous major metropolitan areas, hail, high winds, maybe even tornadoes that comes out of this, storms overnight and through the day on Thursday, more storms again as we get into Friday, really hitting the South with very heavy rain, and then all behind that, very dry conditions, extreme winds igniting a fire danger for us here, especially in Western Texas -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the political turmoil and violence in Haiti, a special team of Marines is now in Port-au-Prince providing additional security at the U.S. Embassy there, this as a small group of the military heads in. Some Americans are being airlifted out, including a famous author.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Cristian Benavides reports tonight on the unrest and the continuing gang violence.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voiceover): A dramatic rescue mission overnight to get Americans out of Haiti, Mitch Albom, bestselling author of books like "Tuesdays with Morrie," airlifted to safety along with his wife and eight others out of Haiti`s violence-torn capital of Port-au-Prince.\par \par \uc2 Albom was visiting his orphanage, Have Faith Haiti, with a group of volunteers when the state of emergency was declared, trapping him and the others for 10 days. The helicopter arrived in the middle of the night, a private mission spearheaded by U.S. Rep. \uc2 Cory Mills\uc2  and Lisa McClain.\par \par \uc2 MITCH ALBOM (Author and Founder of Have Faith Haiti): It landed right out of as you see in movies, you know? I mean, somebody jumps out and says, "Go, go, go," and you run underneath the blades and you jump in.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Albom grateful to be back on U.S. ground, but can`t shake the thoughts of what he left behind.\par \par \uc2 MITCH ALBOM: You know, everybody inside started clapping, but my wife and I, it was a gut punch, because, you know, all of our kids are still at the orphanage.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: A team of Marines have been deployed to provide additional security and prepare for evacuation of the U.S. Embassy.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER ARIEL HENRY speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: This just a day after the nation`s Prime Minister \uc2 Ariel Henry\uc2  announced his resignation.\par \par \uc2 The transition to find his replacement is unlikely to be a smooth one, Haiti`s capital, home to approximately three million, facing widespread gang warfare.\par \par \uc2 Here at the border crossing of Dajabon, people are being deported by the truckloads, some stranded Americans fleeing. We met Troy Stalker (sp?), who is trying to get back home to Illinois.\par \par \uc2 TROY STALKER (American): Tires burning in the street, they will block you. They don`t want you to cross. They don`t want you to get through. So today was a good day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: A sign of how tense things are here at the border, less than a mile from where we are, two Haitian nationals that were attempting to cross the border were shot by members of the Dominican army.\par \par \uc2 Officials here in the Dominican Republic say that a scuffle had taken place and one of them was killed -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Cristian Benavides, thank you for being there.\par \par \uc2 Well, back here at home, the 2024 general election is officially under way, with both President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  securing enough delegates to get their party`s nomination. Today, the president hit the campaign trail in the battleground state of Wisconsin, which he won by only 20,000 votes four years ago.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Listening to America, \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Nancy Cordes spoke to voters in the Badger State to hear what`s on their minds.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): Jay Whisenten co-owns a barbershop on Milwaukee`s North Side, just a few blocks from where President Biden spoke today. Jay voted for Biden in 2020, but is now undecided.\par \par \uc2 JAY WHISENTEN (Undecided Voter): I have been seeing it from both perspectives, and I have been seeing it from the Black voters as well, like: "Yes, we`re going to vote for Trump this year. You know, we need big T. back in office."\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We sat down with him at the Rise & Grind coffee shop, which is owned by Bobbi Tatum, a lifelong Democrat.\par \par \uc2 BOBBI TATUM (Biden Supporter): I`m a Biden fan. I feel like he`s -- unemployment is down. And I feel like he`s helping small businesses. I`m a small business owner.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Patti Granger is a project manager, a mom of two and plans to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 PATTI GRANGER (Trump Supporter): He is a very strong personality, but I like the fact that he pushes boundaries or doesn`t come in as a politician.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Inflation is one of her biggest issues.\par \par \uc2 PATTI GRANGER: Almost six dollars for four sticks of butter, and we`re the Dairy State.\par \par \uc2 It shouldn`t be that expensive.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: How much of it do you attribute to the after-effects of the pandemic and how much of it do you attribute to President Biden`s policies?\par \par \uc2 PATTI GRANGER: It`s been years now since the pandemic. I`m not buying that anymore.\par \par \uc2 BOBBI TATUM: I`m still buying the pandemic, because we`re still getting the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: You feel like the economy is improving?\par \par \uc2 JAY WHISENTEN: It is. Actually, it has. We ain`t where we need to be, but I feel like we`re going -- we`re headed in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: All three are uneasy about sending more aid to Ukraine, something Biden has been pushing for.\par \par \uc2 PATTI GRANGER: It`s like, how can you send billions of dollars over there, yet our communities need it here?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A big issue here in Wisconsin is abortion.\par \par \uc2 BOBBI TATUM: For me, it`s be -- being a woman and also having two daughters, it`s important for -- for them to have a choice for their body.\par \par \uc2 JAY WHISENTEN: That`s their own personal decision. They should be able to have a choice whether or not if they want to keep a child or not.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And how are they feeling about age?\par \par \uc2 You have got an 81-year-old running against a 77-year-old.\par \par \uc2 BOBBI TATUM: I feel like the choice is very limited.\par \par \uc2 JAY WHISENTEN: It`s very limited when it comes to that.\par \par \uc2 PATTI GRANGER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BOBBI TATUM: So, unfortunately, that`s -- that`s what we have to choose from.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And that choice, they all told me, has them paying extra attention to Vice President Harris and whoever Trump chooses as his running mate.\par \par \uc2 Harris made her sixth trip here to this battleground state since she took office last week. And her focus, Norah, was reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Hmm. Nancy Cordes, what a really interesting conversation. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, actor Olivia Munn is revealing her yearlong battle with an aggressive form of breast cancer that led to a double mastectomy, and the biggest surprise, how she learned of her diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Roxana Saberi tells us about the test every woman should know about.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Olivia Munn, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  )\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Good for him. Good for your baby.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI (voiceover): Revealing her diagnosis, Olivia Munn shared this emotional video on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN (Actress): Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Love you.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from X-Men Apocalypse, 20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The 43-year-old, known for her roles in Marvel`s "X-Men: Apocalypse" and HBO`s "The Newsroom," says --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Newsroom, HBO)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: -- she was diagnosed after testing negative for 90 different cancer genes and two months after having a normal mammogram.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Olivia Munn, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  )\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: She credits her OB-GYN with saving her life, after she calculated her breast cancer risk assessment score, 37 percent, and recommended additional testing, which led to her diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Elizabeth Comen is a breast oncologist.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN (Breast Oncologist, Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center): If you have a score, for example, that`s greater than a 20 percent lifetime risk of breast cancer, you may be eligible to include MRIs in your screening, in addition to potentially mammograms and ultrasounds.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The assessment includes questions like current age, family history, if the patient has given birth, and when.\par \par \uc2 How unusual is it for someone who tests negative on a genetic test to still have cancer?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH COMEN: Even if someone goes through genetic testing and for the genes that we are aware of does not come up with an inherited risk for breast cancer, it doesn`t mean that they won`t be diagnosed with breast cancer in their lifetime.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Beautiful. Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Munn appeared at the Academy Awards just last Sunday.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: And right here one more time.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: She says she kept her diagnosis private until now because she needed to get through the hardest parts before sharing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: And by sharing her experiences, Munn says she hopes to inspire others.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Comen recommends women talk to their doctors about getting a breast cancer risk assessment and that women at average risk be screened every year starting at age 40 -- Laura.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: I`m glad she`s sharing her story. I had never heard of that test.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, a major discount chain is now closing 1,000 stores in the U.S., limiting options for shoppers. We will have the details and the reason why next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: One of America`s largest discount retail chains is planning to shut down about 1,000 of its stores nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  said today it`s closing 600 of its \uc2 Family Dollar\uc2  stores in the first half of this year. Another 400 \uc2 Family Dollar\uc2  and \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  locations will have their leases expire over the next few years, mostly because they`re underperforming.\par \par \uc2 A top auto safety group put more than a dozen automated driving systems to the test. We have the results. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to an important consumer alert about those semi- automated driving systems in many new vehicles. Are they doing enough to keep drivers and passengers safe?\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Kris Van Cleave has the results of an eye-opening new report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Holding phone now.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Those increasingly common systems that can help drive your car are not doing enough to ensure drivers are staying focused on the road, according to first-of-its-kind testing from the \uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARKEY\uc2  (PRESIDENT, \uc2 Insurance Institute for Highway Safety\uc2  ): They`re not doing a good job.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: IIHS President \uc2 David Harkey\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARKEY\uc2  : It can be very dangerous. They are not self-driving vehicles. And so you see people who either intentionally or unintentionally misuse these systems and get themselves into trouble.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The new Insurance Institute ratings assess how well the systems monitor a driver, issue alerts, encourage shared control with a driver, and react when safety features are disengaged, like taking off a seat belt.\par \par \uc2 Of the 14 systems from nine automakers tested, none earned a top rating. Just one scored acceptable, the Lexus Teammate with Advanced Drive. Two others rated as marginal.\par \par \uc2 What needs to change?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HARKEY\uc2  : The biggest things that need to change are improvements in monitoring -- monitoring both the head, the eyes, as well as the hands, to make sure you`re ready to take control of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The report comes as lawmaker concern over these driver assistance systems is growing, prompting this exchange with \uc2 NTSB\uc2  Chair Jennifer Homendy last week.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from March 6)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR EDWARD MARKEY (D-Massachusetts): It sounds like, to me, it`s -- it`s not ready for prime time.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY (Chair, \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  ): If it`s only designed to be operated in a certain type of environment, it should be limited to those environments.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In a statement, the trade group representing automakers argues the systems "help reduce roadway crashes and injuries," but "this technology is meant to support a human driver operating behind the wheel. It requires the human driver to be attentive and engaged."\par \par \uc2 Dialed-in driving even in our ever smarter cars.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, CBS News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Heart of America is next with a teacher`s lesson in love.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America with the kindness of a stranger that became a lifesaving moment.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Janet Shamlian reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MALLORY SOBIERALSKI (Mother of Baer Sobieralski): Wow!\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Baer Sobieralski was born with a rare genetic condition and was in kidney failure before his first birthday.\par \par \uc2 KEITH SOBIERALSKI (Father of Baer Sobieralski): I love you.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: With no family fit to donate theirs, Baer was on a wait- list, but time was running out. Seeing a plea online, Jane Callahan made a life-changing call, offering one of her kidneys to a child she did not know.\par \par \uc2 JANE CALLAHAN (Kidney Donor): I just knew, if I was going to be tested, that I would be a match.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: You had that feeling?\par \par \uc2 JANE CALLAHAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A five-hour transplant surgery in December at \uc2 Texas Children`s Hospital\uc2  , then this:\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Your baby did really well. Everything`s great.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The woman who made it possible, a single mom with two children of her own, told Baer`s mom she`d love to meet.\par \par \uc2 MALLORY SOBIERALSKI: It was one of the most profound moments of my life.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Now, two, Baer is recovering.\par \par \uc2 MALLORY SOBIERALSKI: There is your new kidney.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Callahan says she feels great.\par \par \uc2 JANE CALLAHAN: Hi, there.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And two families are one.\par \par \uc2 MALLORY SOBIERALSKI: It`s so great to see you.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: What does this say to you about humanity?\par \par \uc2 KEITH SOBIERALSKI: You get flooded with negative things all the time in the world, and, very often, there are beautiful things that are out there, and you just have to choose to look at them.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Tonight, that beauty is Baer.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, CBS News, Houston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Baer and Jane, tonight`s Heart of America. What a beautiful story.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Hope you have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240314ek3d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 7, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian, Omar Villafranca, Nikki Dementri,  David Martin, Jonathan Vigliotti, Ed O`Keefe, Mark Strassmann, Scott  MacFarlane \par \uc2 3,747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, one of the most important speeches of Joe Biden`s career, as the president delivers an election-year State of the Union, the economy, the border and the other topics he will cover, as the president aims to convince America he`s fit for a second term in office.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell. And thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the countdown is on, just hours from now, the president delivering a critical address to the nation before what may be the largest TV audience he will get this election season. It is a chance for the commander in chief to reframe the political debate about not only the state of this country, but also his stewardship of it.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, I went to the White House for a conversation with the president about what he will talk about. He is eager to engage politically with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 And, Ed, the president tonight, will use the phrase America`s comeback.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s right, Norah.\par \par \uc2 And, as he engages with Republicans, he plans to call out how they have lost elections for supporting the repeal of Roe v. Wade and promise to sign a federal law protecting access to abortion services if it`s ever passed.\par \par \uc2 The president`s also planning to take at least a few jabs at his likely Republican opponent, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): President Biden has spent the past year touting a strong economy.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Wages are up more than prices. Unemployment has reached historic lows.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But polls show most voters aren`t feeling it. Inflation, though down in recent months, has soared on his watch.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, he will use his State of the Union to push Congress to cap out-of- pocket drug costs at $2,000 a year for Americans with private health insurance and to restore the expanded child tax credit for low- and middle- income families.\par \par \uc2 The president also plans to touch on immigration, an area where he`s vulnerable and under constant attack by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Let there be no doubt, this is Joe Biden`s invasion.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The number of migrant crossings spiked to more than 2.4 million in the last fiscal year, compared to under a million at a similar point in the Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 Biden`s own allies have criticized his handling of the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, the rising death toll in Gaza. Thousands voted against him for that reason in recent Democratic primaries.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the president will announce the U.S. military is set to build a temporary port along the coast of Gaza to help deliver humanitarian aid. White House officials say the president`s also expected to draw contrasts with Trump, especially on the subject of reproductive rights, with IVF patients and fertility doctors expected in the audience.\par \par \uc2 Since the repeal of Roe v. Wade, 13 more states have approved complete bans on abortion services.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Stop playing politics with women`s lives and freedom. Let doctors do their job.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Kate Cox, who was blocked by the Texas Supreme Court from terminating a nonviable pregnancy, is scheduled to be in the first lady`s box.\par \par \uc2 With the president`s age a major concern for voters, his performance tonight will be closely watched.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We can all see Joe Biden`s weakness.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Trump`s super PAC released this blistering ad this morning, timed to tonight`s speech.\par \par \uc2 And with hundreds of Trump allies in the House chamber tonight, Speaker Mike Johnson told CBS Mornings he advised his members to be professional.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): We have been trying to turn the temperature down here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now, according to excerpts of the speech, the president will include a riff on his age and Trump`s.\par \par \uc2 He will say his long public life has taught him to embrace themes like freedom and democracy. "Some other people my age," he will say, "embrace themes like resentment, revenge, and retribution," a way of reminding voters his opponent is also an old guy -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Our coverage of the president`s State of the Union address and the Republican response will begin tonight at 9:00 p.m. Eastern/6:00 p.m. Pacific, and we will see you then.\par \par \uc2 Now to this breaking news, tense moments in the sky over California today after a tire flew off a plane just as it lifted off the ground in San Francisco. The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  777 with 249 people on board diverted to Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti shows us the terrifying video.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Just after United Flight 35 took off, a camera captured the moment one of the plane`s rear tires fell off.\par \par \uc2 PILOT: Yes, there`s a tire going down the runway.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: No fire, no sparks, but a back left wheel crashed into a parked car that was empty at the time.\par \par \uc2 AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL: We had to close the runway because somebody lost a tire.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  plane departed \uc2 San Francisco International Airport\uc2  at 11:23 this morning and was headed to Osaka, Japan, but was rerouted to Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has been under investigation since January after an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  plane lost a plug door mid-flight, this United plane has been in service for more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT\uc2 : I know people are going to look at this and say, oh, my goodness, this is another \uc2 Boeing\uc2  problem. I highly suspect it has anything to do with the manufacturing or design of the airplane by \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: On a separate united flight Monday, a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737`s engine caught fire after bubble wrap was sucked into the engine during its flight to Fort Myers. It was diverted safely back to Houston.\par \par \uc2 Today`s flight landed safely in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And an airport employee did sustain minor injuries while responding to the debris field.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating. They`re going to look into the maintenance records for that landing gear, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jonathan Vigliotti, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, we`re learning about a disturbing story of an active-duty soldier with top secret security clearance. He is accused of selling American military secrets to China. The new indictment claims the sergeant was recruited and that some of the documents are related to U.S. weapons systems.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin reports on a story straight out of a spy movie.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SGT. KORBEIN SCHULTZ (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): I have many roles here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN (voiceover): Sergeant Korbein Schultz, an intelligence analyst with the 101st Airborne Division, allegedly told his Chinese handler --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Bourne movie)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: -- he wished he could be Jason Bourne, the daredevil spy played by Matt Damon. But he ended up on this poster.\par \par \uc2 HENRY LEVENTIS (U.S. Attorney): The defendant is charged with entering into a multiyear conspiracy to illegally exploit his access to national defense information for his own financial benefit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: According to the 25-page indictment, Schultz received 14 payments totaling $42,000 from a Chinese agent for handing over sensitive technical data and tactical information about the F-22 stealth fighter, the Army`s newest combat rescue helicopter, and intercontinental ballistic missiles.\par \par \uc2 Schultz is also accused of selling information about the HIMARS long-range rocket system, which the U.S. has given Ukraine to fight off the Russian invasion. He did it online from Fort Campbell, Kentucky, pretending the money was for customizing a car.\par \par \uc2 HENRY LEVENTIS: He traded our national defense information for cash.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: When the Chinese agent promised to pay extra for more highly classified documents, Schultz responded: "I hope so. I need to get my other \uc2 BMW\uc2  back."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Schultz is the fourth serviceman in the past eight months to be arrested for compromising sensitive information.\par \par \uc2 He is scheduled to make his first court appearance tomorrow -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Military secrets for $42,000.\par \par \uc2 David Martin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, an urgent manhunt under way tonight in Philadelphia after a group of teenagers were shot at a bus stop Wednesday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Dementri reports, city officials are promising to use every resource available to find the shooters and the getaway car driver.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI (voiceover): This was the scene, high school students lying in pools of their own blood after a brazen daylight shooting. Three gunmen opened fire on a group of teens waiting for a bus.\par \par \uc2 Eight students, age 15 to 17 years old, were shot. Police say a 16-year-old was shot nine times and is in critical condition tonight.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY CASTOR (Witness): So, I run down there, and the kid was laying in the street, shot in his back. And I put the blood pressure on him. And I looked over, and there was four more kids.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: In a span of 20 seconds, surveillance video shows three people getting out of a car that police say was stolen, firing more than 30 rounds before returning to the car and driving off.\par \par \uc2 FRANK VANORE (Deputy Commissioner of Investigations, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, Police Department): There`s some kind of dispute that happens before. We don`t know where. We don`t know how. And it led to this violence, so we`re -- we`re working on that.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: This shooting came two days after three students from a different high school were shot. One was killed.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE PARKER (D-Mayor of Philadelphia, Pennsylvania): We are going to do everything that we can to ensure your public health and safety.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: The number of shootings is down 34 percent in Philadelphia from this time last year, but police are asking for more public support.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN BETHEL (Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, Police Commissioner): We can`t do this by ourselves. Like, we need the community, we need parents that get engaged and engaged with what their young people are doing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Officials say the teens who were wounded go here to Northeast High School just behind me.\par \par \uc2 So far this school year, district officials note, 60 students have been shot in the city. Seven of them, they say, have died -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Dementri, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there is outrage in Uvalde, Texas, after a new City Council report cleared the Uvalde police of wrongdoing after that school shooting that left 19 children and two teachers dead. Just a reminder, it took police an hour and 17 minutes to confront the gunman.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca has the families` emotional reactions.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: How dare you?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Today, anger over accountability.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two years since the massacre at Uvalde`s Robb Elementary, tempers flared after the latest report on the Uvalde Police Department`s response during the school shooting.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: My son`s birthday is on Tuesday. You know how I`m going to celebrate that? At the cemetery. I should not be doing that.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: For the first time today, the community and the City Council heard the findings of a monthslong independent investigation requested by the city.\par \par \uc2 Former Police Detective Jesse Prado led the investigation.\par \par \uc2 JESSE PRADO (Independent Investigator): All of the officers, Lieutenant Martinez, his actions I found were in good faith.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: He concluded that many officers in the school that day acted in good faith and did not violate policy in their reaction to the shooting. He said the officers didn`t have the proper training that day or the tools, like a rifle-rated shield to overtake the gunman.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: How do you go to bed at night?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Parents were outraged, saying the officers put their safety first.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: They stood there 77 minutes and waited after they got call after call that kids were still alive in there.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The Uvalde police internal investigation is ongoing, but none of the roughly two dozen officers that responded have been terminated.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You fire those officers. You fire them. And you do so with your head held high because you know that that is the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Today, some of the members of the council expressed their outrage over that report.\par \par \uc2 Now, earlier this year, the DOJ released their own scathing report on the entire response to the massacre, finding fault with the officers in action and the lack of communication -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Omar Villafranca, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, two of Alabama`s in vitro fertilization clinics are resuming treatments after a new law signed by Republican Governor \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2 . Nearly half the state`s IVF clinics had paused procedures after a controversial state Supreme Court ruling that gave embryos the same legal rights as children.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian reports, some don`t believe this new law goes far enough.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): A champagne toast at Birmingham`s Alabama Fertility.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Cheers to more Alabama babies.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Celebrating the return of IVF.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The great news came yesterday. We are ready to proceed.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Three embryo transfers were performed here today just hours after the new law was passed.\par \par \uc2 What was it like seeing patients today?\par \par \uc2 DR. MAMIE MCLEAN (Alabama Fertility): Incredibly exciting. Patients, we were able to talk about IVF care. We were able to timeline, lots of smiles, lots of hope and optimism.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: It comes after bills were rushed through the legislature shielding clinics from criminal liability after the state Supreme Court`s controversial ruling that embryos are children.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Senate Bill 159, the Senate concurs.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The court`s decision led to nearly half of Alabama`s providers pausing IVF treatments, the clinic that was the subject of the suit telling CBS News tonight it won`t reopen until it has more clarification on the law.\par \par \uc2 CODY CARNLEY (IVF Patient): We`re hopeful and we`re excited.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Cody Carnley`s embryo transfer was canceled. They have a toddler through IVF and want to grow their family.\par \par \uc2 When do you think your embryo transfer will happen now?\par \par \uc2 CODY CARNLEY: We`re hopeful that that transfer will actually be able to take place at the end of March or the 1st of April.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But reproductive rights advocates say the law is just a fast fix, likely to be challenged because it doesn`t directly address the court`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 Does the Alabama legislation go far enough?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH (D-Illinois): No, the Alabama law does not go far enough.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In Washington, Democratic Senator Tammy Duckworth introduced a bill to give federal protection to IVF. She had both her daughters via the procedure and wants it available for everyone.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: It does not address the issue of, is a fertilized egg a human being, an extrauterine child, in the words of the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2 , with equal or even greater rights than -- than the person who`s going to carry it? It doesn`t address that issue.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And while the mood was celebratory here, the director of Alabama Fertility told me, as long as that state Supreme Court ruling stands, there will be a shadow over their shoulder and IVF care will be under threat -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such an important story for so many.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Congress flooded by phone calls as it gets one step closer to banning the popular social media app TikTok. What may happen, that`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Congress, which doesn`t agree on much, took a big bipartisan step toward -- today towards banning TikTok in the U.S. And lawmakers are hearing about it from their constituents.\par \par \uc2 Here`s CBS Scott MacFarlane.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): TikTok is famous for its viral videos. But, within months, the music could stop.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from TikTok)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The social media giant mobilized some of its estimated 170 million users to call their congressmen or congresswomen to object to new legislation that would require TikTok to find a new owner, divest itself from China-based ByteDance, or be banned from app stores in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And the wave of calls hit midday today.\par \par \uc2 Republican Congresswoman Ashley Hinson of Iowa reposted a video of her manning the phones as she helped manage the calls.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ASHLEY HINSON (R-Iowa): They`re spying on you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A TikTok spokesman told CBS News only users over 18 years old were asked to call.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DAN CRENSHAW (R-Texas): Today, if you want to use your TikTok account, you have to put in your zip code, so that TikTok can tell you which representative you should call. Imagine when China wants to use it more nefariously. Imagine when they want to truly engage in psychological warfare against the American people.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But the effort might have backfired. Just 48 hours after the legislation was introduced, it passed unanimously 50-0 minutes after debate began in the powerful House Energy and Commerce Committee.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a sign of the political -- political disconnect, the White House supports this legislation, but they have a watch party for the State of the Union tonight with TikTok influencers in attendance.\par \par \uc2 And the company, Norah, says this bill tramples First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That is so interesting.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s growing frustration in the U.S. over a shortage of ADHD medications. Our Health Watch report is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we`re shining a light on the nationwide shortage of medications to treat ADHD. These drugs can be crucial for millions who have trouble paying attention and staying focused.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Health Watch, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann has one family`s story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CORONADO (Mother): Try from right here.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): For Kristin Coronado, finding the ADHD drugs her son Dom needs is like a series of longshots, mostly misses.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CORONADO: I`m a mother looking for my son`s medication. I`m not a drug dealer. That`s how they make you feel. I tried another pharmacy, and that led to, like, pharmacy to pharmacy to pharmacy.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You`re on your own.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CORONADO: You`re on your own. Deal with it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Six-year-old Dom takes a generic version of a drug called Focalin XR made by \uc2 Lannett\uc2 . Like other ADHD drugs, it contains a controlled substance tightly regulated by the \uc2 Drug Enforcement Administration\uc2 , or DEA.\par \par \uc2 America`s drug makers claim they`re manufacturing all they can. But patients and doctors` offices have to keep pharmacy-shopping to find it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER HOLTON (Medical Director, Child and Adolescent Psychiatry Clinic, \uc2 Emory University\uc2 ): As soon as they`re without medication, you see a return of untreated ADHD symptoms.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And it can get dark or even dire quickly.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER HOLTON: They take an action that can harm them or even take their lives.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CORONADO: So this is the spreadsheet I made.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Coronado calls all 25 local pharmacies on this list.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CORONADO: Hi. I`m calling to see if my son`s prescription is ready.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This call, another miss.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CORONADO: Not ready yet.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And Dom`s monthly dose is running out.\par \par \uc2 What keeps you up at night thinking about all this?\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN CORONADO: Tomorrow, I have to count the pills that I have left, see what day that ends on, and then, like, start the process all over again.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Drugmakers say the DEA needs to release more of the controlled substance. The DEA counters that drugmakers have yet to use up their supply.\par \par \uc2 Lannett\uc2  and the DEA both had no comment to CBS News. Caught in the middle, kids like Dom.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER HOLTON: It has to be addressed. These children, these families deserve better.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Good news, Kristin Coronado has finally tracked down a refill. Dom`s all set, at least for another month.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Redwood City, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a story.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next with an American woman who`s setting records on the high seas.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet 29-year-old Cole Brauer from Long Island, New York.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Global Solo Challenge)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: She just became the first American woman to sail solo nonstop around the world.\par \par \uc2 Early this morning, Brauer arrived in Spain, completing one of the most extreme and difficult sporting events on Earth, the Global Solo Challenge. Less than 200 people have ever managed to finish it. But that didn`t deter Brauer. Despite suffering bruised ribs when a massive wave slammed her boat and severe dehydration that required an I.V., she continued on.\par \par \uc2 After 30,000 miles and more than four months alone on the high seas, Brauer is back on dry land, where she was greeted by family and friends` hugs, and, yes, some champagne.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations to Cole Brauer, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to join us for the president`s State of the Union address. That`s beginning at 9:00. We will see you then.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240308ek3700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 6, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Scott MacFarlane, Robert Costa, Ed O`Keefe, David  Martin, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Mike Bettes, Elise Preston, Elaine Quijano,  Carter Evans \par \uc2 3,826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): These brazen, heinous attacks on our subway system will not be tolerated.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: crime in America. New York`s governor deploys National Guard troops to patrol the country`s largest mass transit system amid an uptick in violent crime.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Because I don`t think that makes people safe.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Sometimes, it -- I would rather the bus, because it`s -- it`s not safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 And we do want to begin tonight with the rising crime rate in New York on America`s largest subway system, with millions of commuters and tourists riding every day. Well, now an extraordinary new approach by the governor there to bring down crime in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 , the Democratic governor announcing a major crackdown.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of National Guard will begin tonight patrolling subway platforms and checking riders` bags. The show of force comes after a recent spike in crime in the underground transit, including three killings since January. And even today, just hours after the announcement, a train conductor reported that she was hit in the head with a glass bottle.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Quijano spoke with subway riders about the new security.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): New York Governor Kathy Hochul`s plan is aimed at improving subway rider safety, adding 1,000 more personnel, including 750 National Guard troops, to help with patrols and random bag searches.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): Anyone looking to do harm or spread fear on our subways, you will be caught.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: The move comes after a more than 46 percent spike in major crimes in the city`s transit system in January compared to 2023.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, CBS News polling shows crime is a top concern for voters. But data shows crime is down in big cities, including New York, Chicago and Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO SINGLETON (New York City Commuter): In the daytime, I feel safe. Nighttime, I don`t know, man, because I have been saying, where`s the cops? You don`t see them that much.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Already this year, there have been several high-profile crimes in New York`s subway system, including three killings and a brutal attack on a conductor.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): But you have to feel safe in the system, because I know how it plays on your psyche when you hear about some random acts of violence.\par \par \uc2 Danny Pearlstein is with the transit advocacy group Riders Alliance.\par \par \uc2 DANNY PEARLSTEIN (Riders Alliance): With stationing troops there, that`s going to create a heightened climate of fear of the subway. It`s not getting at the root causes of the problems.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: The governor`s plan does include $20 million for mental health outreach.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In the morning, you see nothing but the homeless on the trains. I don`t feel safe. Sometimes, I just take the bus.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: More than three million people ride the New York City transit system every day. But, Norah, that is down from a peak of five million people per day before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. I know that`s controversial. We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Elaine Quijano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to some breaking news, a verdict just in, in the trial of the weapons expert on Alec Baldwin`s movie "Rust." Hannah Gutierrez-Reed was found guilty for her role in the accidental shooting that killed the movie`s cinematographer. They deliberated just two-and-a-half-hours.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston has the late breaking details from the courthouse in New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We find the defendant, Hannah Gutierrez, guilty.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): The jury found Hannah Gutierrez-Reed guilty of manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 KARI MORRISSEY (Special Prosecutor): This case is about constant, never- ending safety failures that resulted in the death of a human being and nearly killed another.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Gutierrez-Reed was in charge of nearly 20 weapons during the filming of the movie "Rust," including the prop gun held by actor Alec Baldwin that discharged, killing Halyna Hutchins and injuring director Joel Souza in October 2021.\par \par \uc2 KARI MORRISSEY: And what do you know?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Prosecutors argued Gutierrez-Reed was negligent because she mixed live ammunition with dummy rounds on the set.\par \par \uc2 KARI MORRISSEY: She had three hours in the morning waiting for the camera crew to arrive. She had every opportunity to go through that box of dummies.\par \par \uc2 JASON BOWLES (Attorney For Hannah Gutierrez Reed): That`s something you can consider.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The defense tried to shift the blame to Baldwin, who they argue never should have pointed the gun in the direction of Hutchins. They say, at the time of this shooting, Gutierrez-Reed wasn`t inside the church and didn`t give Baldwin the gun during the rehearsal.\par \par \uc2 JASON BOWLES: He`s going off-script. That defeats any idea that that was foreseeable to Ms. Gutierrez-Reed. If she doesn`t know what`s happening, she can`t foresee it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, Gutierrez-Reed was taken into custody as she awaits sentencing.\par \par \uc2 As for Baldwin, he was charged with involuntary manslaughter, but always maintained that he did not pull the trigger. Baldwin`s trial is expected to start in July -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston in Santa Fe for us, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the weather, with millions of people under flood watches tonight in the Northeast, while severe storms are threatening the South.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 . He`s with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Another wet stretch ahead for the Northeast now through tomorrow, some heavier rain across the Northeast, with more than 30 million Americans under flood watches, especially that I-95 Corridor, generally speaking, one to two, but pockets of three to five, which could cause that flooding.\par \par \uc2 We will get a break on Friday, right back into it for the weekend. Another storm complex comes in, rain and wind. What`s different about this one, it`s a little bit colder. The tail end of this event brings some snow from Ohio up to Maine. Some that could be very heavy, especially in the mountains in New England, but from Boston southbound, it`s all rain for you here.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s the Southeast, several days of heavy rain and thunderstorms back in our forecast. As much as five inches of rain is possible, Norah. Also can`t rule out severe weather, including hail, high winds and a threat of tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 The stage is all but set for November`s presidential election, now that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has dropped out of the race. Polls show it`s a matchup few Americans wanted, but last night`s dominant Super Tuesday victories for both President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and predecessor \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will now lead to eight months of a brutal, bitter campaign.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R-Presidential Candidate): I said I wanted Americans to have their voices heard. I have done that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): After a year on the campaign trail, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  said she had no regrets. But a Super Tuesday near-sweep for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  convinced her it was time to leave the race.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 : And although I will no longer be a candidate, I will not stop using my voice for the things I believe in.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: While she congratulated the former president, she declined to endorse him.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 : It is now up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to earn the votes of those in our party and beyond it who did not support him. And I hope he does that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But that could be a challenge. Voter exit polls in North Carolina showed 79 percent of Haley`s supporters said they won`t necessarily vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER MAICHIN (Voter): Well, I always say, if it`s a Biden-Trump rematch, I have no idea who I would vote for.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Trump invited Haley supporters to join his movement, but not before criticizing her, saying she got trounced last night.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We want to have unity and we`re going to have unity.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: One sign of party unity, an endorsement today from Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who had been the highest-ranking Republican to withhold his support.\par \par \uc2 President Biden also made direct overtures to Haley supporters, saying: "There is a place for them in my campaign." Biden won overwhelmingly in most Super Tuesday states. But it came with a warning in Minnesota, where tens of thousands voted uncommitted in political protest to his handling of the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 Yet, one of Biden`s few challengers, \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 , also dropped out today and endorsed him, saying he would work to "help keep a man of decency and integrity in the White House."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And Trump could win enough delegates next week to officially clinch the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, after skipping all of the Republican debates, he`s challenging Biden to a debate, saying he will meet him any time anywhere -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, there`s lots to talk about, so let`s dive deeper with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa and Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 Robert, all right, former President Trump, resounding victory, but still a number of challenges ahead.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Norah, top Republicans tell me behind the scenes today that President Biden`s overture to Nikki Haley`s voters, that`s a warning sign that Trump can`t take anything for granted.\par \par \uc2 Biden`s coming right for those suburban Republicans who have real concerns about Trump`s conduct and character. But that`s not the only issue Trump is facing.\par \par \uc2 He`s going to be sitting in a courtroom in New York in just a few weeks for that criminal hush money trial, logistically, so much on the horizon for him, not just that trial in New York, but potentially a Supreme Court decision on presidential immunity and a possible trial on January 6 and classified documents.\par \par \uc2 That will cloud the campaign. And then, finally, raising money. Just on Sunday, Trump met with billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . Now, Musk said today he`s not going to donate a cent to any candidate. But it`s clear Trump and his allies want Musk to be involved in some way politically.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And, Ed O`Keefe, a big night for President Biden ahead with the State of the Union address. He has some work to do to convince voters about his record.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He sure does.\par \par \uc2 But overarching all of that is the concern that he may be too old to do the job. So now he`s trying to bat away those questions with a little bit of humor, in suggesting that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had some mumbles and stumbles of his own.\par \par \uc2 The big argument we`re expecting him to continue making: don`t judge me by my age, but the age of my ideas, trying to be forward-looking, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans are trying to turn the country back on issues like reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Defusing voter frustrations. We saw in Minnesota, again, high percentage of people voted uncommitted, criticizing his position on the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war. If he can`t turn that around, there`s a real risk base voters -- base Democratic voters, won`t show up in the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 And then messaging on the economy. Polling continues to show us Americans upset about the direction of the economy. He`s got eight months to reverse that, suggest, I feel your pain, I`m trying to do something to help, the State of the Union obviously a unique opportunity to try.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Ed O`Keefe, Robert Costa, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And CBS News` prime-time coverage of the State of the Union address and the Republican response begins tomorrow at 9:00 p.m. Eastern/6:00 p.m. Pacific. And we hope you will join us.\par \par \uc2 Now to the Middle East and the dangerous situation in the Red Sea. Tonight, for the first time since this conflict began, a Houthi attack on commercial ships has led to deaths.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin reports on the escalating situation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN (voiceover): The aftermath of the first fatal Houthi attack on commercial shipping, three dead, three more in critical condition, the rest of the crew forced to abandon ship.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, the Indian navy had to come to the rescue of another burning ship. And over the weekend, the Rubymar, leaking both fuel and its cargo of fertilizer, became the first vessel to sink as a result of a Houthi attack, damaging undersea Internet and telecommunications cables as she went down.\par \par \uc2 The ship the Houthis sank ended up causing the severing of these undersea cables?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (NSC Coordinator For Strategic Communications): Our assessment is the same, that those cables were cut most likely by an anchor dragging from the Rubymar as she sank.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The severing of three of 14 cables running under the Red Sea disrupted up to 25 percent of communications between Asia and Europe, forcing companies to reroute traffic until they can be repaired.\par \par \uc2 TIM STRONGE (Undersea Cable Analyst): You have to go to the point of where the last known fault was, find the cables, bring them up to the surface, and fiber -- splice the fiber and repair the cable.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: That sounds like a pretty involved operation.\par \par \uc2 TIM STRONGE: And it`s a dangerous area to be operating in.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin is with us now.\par \par \uc2 David, I want to ask you about another dangerous area, amid the major conflict in Ukraine. I understand the Ukrainians are saying tonight there was a close call, that a Russian missile strike happened less than 2,000 feet from Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy. Do they believe this was a targeted attack?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, I asked John Kirby about that, and he says that the U.S. assessment is, the Russians were not deliberately targeting Zelenskyy. They were going after some other target in Odesa, and he just happened to be nearby.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A call for air defenses.\par \par \uc2 David Martin at the Pentagon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the popular social media app TikTok is facing a potential ban in the United States due to national security concerns for its ties to the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports what a bipartisan group of lawmakers are demanding from the parent company, ByteDance.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clips from TikTok)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): Fueled by viral videos, TikTok offers a stage for millions. But soon, the curtain could be coming down, including for Kim Pham, who says she leans heavily on TikTok to lure customers --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @weareomsom)\par \par \uc2 KIM PHAM (Co-Founder, Omsom): So follow us to come along on this journey.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- to her company in California.\par \par \uc2 What happens to you if it`s just banned one day?\par \par \uc2 KIM PHAM: It wouldn`t kill us tomorrow, but TikTok as a platform has represented a very kind of meaningful and new way that we reach consumers.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: New legislation set for review by a U.S. House committee tomorrow requires TikTok to separate itself or divest from its Chinese- based-owner, ByteDance, or risk a ban from app stores in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-Illinois): We implore ByteDance to sell TikTok so that its American users can enjoy their dance videos, their bad lip sync, everything else that goes along with TikTok.\par \par \uc2 (Clips from TikTok)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Amid growing fears, the personal information TikTok devours from its users could fall into the hands of Chinese adversaries.\par \par \uc2 There may be millions of TikTok users who say, don`t ban this. We love this.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE CATHY MCMORRIS RODGERS (R-Washington): Well, the choice is up to TikTok. They have a choice to make as to whether or not they want to remain with ByteDance that we know is controlled by the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: TikTok blasts this plan, saying they already have fire walls in place to protect user data.\par \par \uc2 And they say it would trample on the First Amendment rights of 170 million Americans -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But I do know a lot of parents that want to ban TikTok.\par \par \uc2 All right, Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A hero police dog helps to rescue a little boy who wandered away. We`ve got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, a 55-year-old Pennsylvania woman is recovering from a rare bear attack.\par \par \uc2 It happened north of Pittsburgh last night when she let her dog outside. She`s expected to be OK after suffering injuries to her arm, face, neck, and head. Wildlife officials put down the mother bear that attacked her and moved her three cubs to another era -- area.\par \par \uc2 Near Kalamazoo, Michigan, a 3-year-old boy who climbed out a window of his grandmother`s home is back with his family after being spotted by a police dog and a drone. A deputy`s bodycam video shows him closing in on the boy about an hour-and-a-half after he wandered off on Monday wearing only a diaper. The boy was found near a pond shaken and crying, but not hurt.\par \par \uc2 Police say the 2017 Kuno -- since 2017, Kuno, the police dog, has helped to rescue five children. That`s a good dog.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 TSA\uc2  is trying out a new screening fast lane that could change the future of airport security. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: With spring break travel starting to pick up, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  is experimenting with new technology it hopes will make going through airport security as easy as self-checkout at the grocery store. It`s a temporary fast lane for PreCheck fliers.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans shows us how it works.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): The future of airport security could be self- service.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: You have everything tucked in -- inside your bin?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: A remote agent now greets passengers at the TSA`s Innovation Checkpoint at Las Vegas International.\par \par \uc2 A real agent, real person.\par \par \uc2 DAVID PEKOSKE (\uc2 TSA\uc2  Administrator): Right, who doesn`t have to physically be in the checkpoint with you. Officers can read X-rays from any machine that generates them, not necessarily the machine they`re standing next to.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The agency wants to make the process more do-it-yourself.\par \par \uc2 DIMITRI KUSNEZOV (System Designer): Something where people have a little more self-pacing and control of -- of how they move through the airport.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Passengers get instructions on screen, walk into a secure scanner while an automatic conveyor directs bags for re-screening or hand search.\par \par \uc2 The idea is, it`s supposed to be like a regular \uc2 TSA\uc2  checkpoint, except fewer \uc2 TSA\uc2  agents and hopefully streamlined. So now we go into the body scanner here. It`s a little different. OK. Overall, it`s pretty simple. There is my bag waiting for me.\par \par \uc2 But, for some, the machine was very sensitive.\par \par \uc2 I see, the hair clip.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The hair clip? Oh, my God. Are you serious? Normally, I wouldn`t have a hiccup. I also don`t like getting scanned four or five times.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Others breezed right through.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: I seemed to go through no problem.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The \uc2 TSA\uc2  says it won`t sacrifice security for speed. Last year, agents confiscated a record of more than 6,700 weapons nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Is this as secure as a regular \uc2 TSA\uc2  checkpoint?\par \par \uc2 DIMITRI KUSNEZOV: Yes, it is. We do independent test and evaluation against the kinds of threats and things that pose risks to air travel.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The agency admits the new system is a learning process for passengers and agents.\par \par \uc2 DAVID PEKOSKE: The bottom line is, all the technology we put in place is much better for security.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, passengers will be able to use the new self-service checkpoint in Las Vegas beginning next week.\par \par \uc2 But the entire system won`t appear in other airports any time soon. Once testing is complete, the best elements of the system will be integrated into current checkpoints -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, I love that you brought us this story.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next with a daughter who ran the distance thanks to a mother`s love.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet Berenice Alfaro of Little Rock, Arkansas. On Sunday, Berenice completed her first marathon in six hours and seven minutes. And while she wanted to finish, her biggest goal was making her mother proud.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, Berenice`s mother, Eva (sp?), was diagnosed with stage three breast cancer. And while that news would stop many in their tracks, Berenice decided to keep moving forward by focusing on a healthier lifestyle. It started with local breast cancer walks. But she soon realized she could go even further and faster.\par \par \uc2 And in doing so, she discovered a new passion for running. It was that passion, along with support from her mother, now a breast cancer survivor, that helped her finish the line.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BERENICE ALFARO (Runner): I just love the fact that she`s still here. It`s very encouraging for her to say: "You can do this. You can do this."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Look at that. Berenice Alfaro, she is tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240307ek3600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for February 27, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jo Ling Kent, Omar Villafranca, Ed O`Keefe, Chris  Warren, Weijia Jiang, Charlie D`Agata, Meg Oliver \par \uc2 3,808 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: the shutdown showdown, tensions over government funding, the southern border, and Russia`s war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The writing is literally on the wall here: "We are not asking too much. We just need artillery shells and aviation. The rest, we will do ourselves."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We`re in Ukraine, as Russian forces advance with aid at the center of an urgent meeting between President Biden and top congressional leaders.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (D-New York): This is an existential moment for the free world.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): We must take care of America`s needs first.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, more than 40 million people are in the path of a severe storm threat that is rolling across the country. Heavy snow from Colorado to Minnesota caused travel chaos at the nation`s airports, with more than 3,000 flights delayed. And the biggest threat from this dangerous weather will come overnight from Missouri to Michigan with dangerous winds, massive hail, and possible tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to have more on that dangerous system in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we want to begin tonight here in Washington with the age-old story of Congress leaving it to the last-minute to get its job done. Tonight, the issue is a potential government shutdown Friday night and the decision to fund Ukraine, or risk an international fiasco.\par \par \uc2 There are some new developments today after an intense Oval Office meeting with President Biden and congressional leaders. And this comes as Russian forces are gaining ground and Ukrainian troops are running short on ammunition.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata is on the ground in Ukraine to give us the scope of why the aid is so pressing.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good evening to you, Norah.\par \par \uc2 We have visited many front-line towns since this war began, and troops are always jittery. But, today, there was a real sense of fear, mixed with frustration. The Russians are on the move, and there`s a genuine sense and a concern that Ukraine just doesn`t have the weapons to stop them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): Following the relentless bombardment in the battle for Bakhmut, Russian troops repeated those brutal tactics in the recent capture of the city of Avdiivka.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Kremlin`s forces are bearing down on Chasiv Yar, where, today, we saw a town under siege and calling out for help.\par \par \uc2 The writing is literally on the wall here, and the message couldn`t be clearer: "We are not asking too much. We just need artillery shells and aviation. We will do the rest ourselves."\par \par \uc2 Hardly a building has been left untouched after months of shelling. But soldiers say the past few days has seen an increase in the ferocity of the offensive. The exchange of fire is nonstop. You can hear the explosions. It`s hard to tell whether that`s incoming or outgoing, but we are within easy reach of Russian artillery and mortars.\par \par \uc2 Chasiv Yar sits only around six miles west of Bakhmut and north of Avdiivka, cities that have become longstanding symbols of Ukrainian resolve, but were ultimately unable to withstand Russia`s sheer number of forces and firepower.\par \par \uc2 Ruben Serakhanian (sp?) is a soldier with the Fifth Assault Brigade.\par \par \uc2 (RUBEN SERAKHANIAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "Right now, we`re facing problems with artillery shells," he said. "We rely on the support of the United States. We`re facing problems, and our men are dying."\par \par \uc2 While Congress argues over the $60 billion Ukraine aid package, President Zelenskyy says Russia is exploiting the delay. The vast majority of residents have already abandoned this place. Some may feel the West has abandoned them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Soldiers repeat, the most pressing need is artillery, Norah, that they simply cannot fight Russia toe-to-toe.\par \par \uc2 They won`t say how much longer they can hold that territory with the stocks they have left, but warehouses are already running empty.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata with that reporting from the front lines, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that military aid for Ukraine was the subject of today`s high-stakes meeting between President Biden and congressional leaders. Also at the center of the Oval Office conversation, that looming government shutdown and the crisis at the U.S. border, the president even spending one-on-one time with Republican House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): President Biden told congressional leaders this morning that the time to send Ukraine more money is now.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I think the consequences of inaction every day in Ukraine are dire.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: House Speaker Mike Johnson, who has refused to allow a vote on new funding for Ukraine, was pressed by everyone in the room, including his Republican colleague Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): The meeting on Ukraine was one of the most intense I have ever encountered in my many meetings in the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But what made this meeting one of the most intense you have ever had here at the White House?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 : The urgency of supporting Ukraine and the consequences to the people of America --\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But Speaker Johnson came out of the meeting undeterred, insisting on action to address the southern border first.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): We must take care of America`s needs first. When you talk about America`s needs, you have to talk first about our open border.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Another top focus today, the looming government shutdown. Several federal agencies run out of money in three days, and all of them could close one week from Friday.\par \par \uc2 Hard-right Republicans, who have brought the government to the brink of a shutdown three times in six months, are now pressuring Johnson to include a list of demands that could kill a funding deal, like cutting funding for background checks to buy guns, cutting incentives for buying electric vehicles, and defunding \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BESA PINCHOTTI (CEO, \uc2 National Military Family Association\uc2 ): Families are living paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: \uc2 Besa Pinchotti\uc2  runs a support group for military families that rely on the federal nutrition program, WIC, which would not be available if the government closes.\par \par \uc2 BESA PINCHOTTI: That`s a huge problem. That`s the safety net that so many families rely on.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: President Biden and House Speaker Johnson also spoke privately in their first one-on-one meeting ever.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, both say they`re optimistic that Congress can strike a deal, but time is running out. The House does not return to Washington until tomorrow evening, just two days before that partial shutdown kicks in -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow, leaving it to the last-minute.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to the dangerous weather we talked about that is happening all across the country. Evacuation orders have been issued, as several wildfires burn out of control along the Texas Panhandle, while severe storms take aim at parts of the Midwest. A large area from Chicago to Louisville is under a tornado threat tonight.\par \par \uc2 For the forecast, let`s bring in meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Dangerous, severe weather expected for parts of the Midwest and Ohio Valley. Not just possible, but likely some of these storms will produce large hail, damaging winds, even significant tornadoes, all a possibility. Overnight, heavy rain bringing a flood threat to many areas, damaging wind the main threat during the day.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, the storms will be pushing into the big cities by the evening commute after what will be a near or record warm day, which is not going to last long, going from the 50s and 60s in the Northeast, Norah, to temperatures that will be well below freezing by Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Chris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s Michigan presidential primary is under way, as one of the key battleground states that will likely decide the general election in November. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is looking to continue his winning streak over former South Carolina Governor \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe explains why President Biden is facing a voter backlash in a state he won in 2020.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Michigan is the biggest test of the anti-Biden vote so far, because, while many Americans have no personal connection to the conflict in the Middle East, thousands here do.\par \par \uc2 The city of Dearborn has the largest concentration of Arab and Muslim Americans in the country and generally votes overwhelmingly for Democrats. But they say the president`s refusal to support an enduring cease-fire is pushing many here, like the Democratic mayor, Abdullah Hammoud, to vote uncommitted in today`s primary.\par \par \uc2 ABDULLAH HAMMOUD (D-Mayor of Dearborn, Michigan): I think, for me, voting uncommitted today is sending a message that we`re trying to hold our elected official accountable.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The president won Michigan by 154,000 votes four years ago. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won it in 2016 by just 10,000. That means tens of thousands of votes against Biden could cost him the state in the general election.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH (Former Biden Campaign Volunteer): This is a clear warning to the Democratic Party that, unless they do change course, they`re in trouble in November. This gives us an opportunity to express our concern and to express it now, in February.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Adam Abusalah volunteered for the president`s 2020 campaign, but by the time we first met him in late November, he was angry with the president for standing by Israel as tens of thousands of civilians in Gaza died.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: If you were to tell me that my vote would be the vote whether Biden wins in Michigan or not, I will still not vote for Biden.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now he`s actively campaigning for uncommitted.\par \par \uc2 The president`s still expected to easily win the primary tonight. On Monday, he said ongoing negotiations to release hostages might mean a temporary pause in fighting.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): My hope is, by next Monday, we will have a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And he reiterated his strong support for Israel, but criticized the government`s plans to push further into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Late Night with Seth Meyer, NBC)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : They`re going to lose support from around the world, and that is not in Israel`s interest.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On the Republican side, should \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  win tonight, he`s hoping it`ll propel him into Super Tuesday next week, when more than a dozen states will be voting.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is still running and says she has no plans to drop out -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Ed O`Keefe at a polling place there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 Well, there are new details tonight regarding the death of 16-year-old Nex Benedict, a nonbinary student who died just one day after getting into a fight with three other students.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca reports, Benedict`s death has sparked nationwide demonstrations against anti-LGBTQ rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): From rallies in New York --\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Nex Benedict!\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: -- to walkouts in Oklahoma --\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Justice for Nex!\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: -- members of the LGBTQ community showed their support for Nex Benedict, a 16-year-old nonbinary student who died earlier this month after a fight at their Owasso, Oklahoma, high school.\par \par \uc2 A preliminary autopsy report says Nex did not die from injuries in the fight.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN INGERSOLL (Friend): It`s been really tough to lose someone that I cared about, to see all of this hate manifesting into something so powerful.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The fight took place in the girls` bathroom, and Owasso police released videos showing everyone involved walking out on their own.\par \par \uc2 A school nurse sent Nex to the hospital, where police bodycam video shows them sitting next to their grandmother.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hi, mom. (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Nex told the officer the girls were harassing them. Nex says they reacted by throwing water on the girls, which started a three-on- one fight.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So, they just up and decided to start messing with you? There was -- there was --\par \par \uc2 NEX BENEDICT (Student): Yes, because of the way that we dress.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The harassment is familiar to LGBTQ youth, like Alex Deroin.\par \par \uc2 ALEX DEROIN (Northeastern Oklahoma Indigenous Safety and Education): What happened on that individual level is, an individual situation that happens because of a systematic -- systematic problem.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Deroin puts some of the blame on Oklahoma leaders who have pushed anti-LGBTQ laws in the state, like Oklahoma`s state superintendent, Ryan Walters, who has been accused of enacting policies that limit transgender students.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WALTERS (Oklahoma Superintendent of Public Instruction): I`m going to completely reject the notion that commonsense policies that there`s two genders, that there shouldn`t be gender ideology pushed in schools has any connection whatsoever to students feeling safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Police are waiting on the autopsy and toxicology reports to determine the cause of death.\par \par \uc2 And people in this community here are waiting to see if the district attorney will end up filing charges on that initial fight -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Democratic Senator Tammy Duckworth says she will bring her bill to protect in vitro fertilization and other fertility treatments to the Senate floor tomorrow, trying to put lawmakers on the record for where they stand on the issue. Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  and Democrats argue Republicans have gone too far with restricting reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 And, today, the Biden administration`s top health official was there in Alabama to meet with patients and doctors impacted by that controversial state Supreme Court ruling giving embryos the same rights as children. Lawmakers in the state are now scrambling to approve legislation to protect access to IVF treatments, and Alabama`s Republican governor says she hopes to have a bill on her desk soon.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s MoneyWatch: The price of a burger and fries could be going up, depending on what time of day it is. That`s right. Fast-food giant Wendy`s says it will introduce surge pricing in 2025 during its busiest hours.\par \par \uc2 Well, it`s getting a frosty reception, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent takes a look at the latest way companies are squeezing consumers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): Taking a bigger bite out of shoppers` incomes, the most in three decades. And be prepared to shell out more.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Wendy`s ad)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Wendy`s announced it`ll start testing dynamic pricing on digital menu boards next year, a form of surge pricing like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Artificial intelligence will suggest menu items and determine whether to raise prices based on demand, location and time. Wendy`s says, for example, it might suggest "a cool Frosty on a warm summer day. This will allow us to be more nimble and flexible with pricing."\par \par \uc2 Why introduce this when customers can easily just go somewhere else?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Clearly, the company is trying to find ways to boost its share price and improve its profit margins. But it could come at the expense of losing some faithful customers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Kellogg\uc2  ad)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Beyond the burgers, \uc2 Kellogg\uc2  is also under fire after its CEO suggested consumers struggling with inflation could eat cereal for dinner.\par \par \uc2 GARY PILNICK\uc2  (CEO, \uc2 Kellogg\uc2 ): You think about the cost of cereal for a family versus what they might otherwise do, that`s going to be much more affordable.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Backlash online was swift.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from James Li)\par \par \uc2 JAMES LI: This fool making four million bucks a year, you think he`s feeding his kids cereal for dinner?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It`s a tough sell, because cereal prices have surged more than 25 percent compared to pre-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Well, it rings a little bit like the French Revolution: Let them eat cereal. And it does sort of not read the room very well.\par \par \uc2 Families are really struggling to afford bringing food to the table to feed everyone at the table. And now they`re suggesting cereal for dinner? It just seems like he`s missing the point here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: What you pay for groceries continues to rise.\par \par \uc2 The financial firm Moody`s Analytics says that the average American right now is paying $1,200 more per year on groceries alone compared to three years ago -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 One of America`s oldest department stores announces a major downsizing and a new vision for its future. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, a possible labor agreement could be brewing between \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and its workers.\par \par \uc2 The coffee giant and the union Workers United have agreed to begin talks aimed at improving labor relations and resolving lawsuits between them. The two sides have been at odds in recent years, as workers at hundreds of stores have voted to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Big changes tonight at Macy`s. The retail chain is closing about 150 stores, while investing to improve the remaining 350 stores. Macy`s also plans to expand its Bloomingdale`s department stores and its beauty chain, Blue Mercury. The former CEO of Bloomingdale`s took over as Macy`s CEO earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 A new tech industry designed to track women`s heart health is booming. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we`re casting light on a growing tech industry aimed at improving women`s health and closing the gender gap in health care.\par \par \uc2 More companies run by women are creating devices designed to better track the health of women`s hearts.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA CHONG RODRIGUEZ (Co-Founder and CEO, Bloomer Tech): So it`s not metal. It`s very soft.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA CHONG RODRIGUEZ: It`s very comfortable.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): Alicia Chong Rodriguez is the co-founder of the Bloomer Bra, an undergarment with a secret weapon, sensors that track health information to detect and fight heart disease in women.\par \par \uc2 What kind of data are you collecting, and how does that help the doctor?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA CHONG RODRIGUEZ: So, we collect data to detect arrhythmia triggers. We also collect breathing patterns, temperature, posture and movement. These symptoms might get dismissed or unrecognized.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The data is sent to a cell phone app and allows the woman to share it with her doctor. Chong Rodriguez`s company, Bloomer Tech, is hoping to get \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for the bra as a medical device.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA CHONG RODRIGUEZ: So, most of the data we have always collected has been predominantly male, but we actually needed data from woman to build better tools to early-detect and treat patients with heart disease.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: More than 60 million women in the United States are living with some form of heart disease, but a study by the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  shows women are not getting the same level of care as men.\par \par \uc2 MAAYAN COHEN\uc2  (Co-Founder and CEO, \uc2 Hello Heart\uc2 ): You put it on here. It`s super simple.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: That trend is inspiring more female entrepreneurs to develop technology specifically for women.\par \par \uc2 MAAYAN COHEN\uc2 : So, it has all of your heart risk factors.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Like \uc2 Maayan Cohen\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 Hello Heart\uc2 , who designed a monitor to track everything from blood pressure and cholesterol to weight and activity.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Hello Heart\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The data is sent in real time to an app which is available through employer health plans.\par \par \uc2 MAAYAN COHEN\uc2 : We have A.I.-based digital coaching that help you improve your health in real time, and we also have risk alert that help you catch risk in time.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: For these women, the mission is personal.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA CHONG RODRIGUEZ: My grandma, we lost her to a heart attack when I was only 13 years old. We need better tools, and now we can do something about it.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Taking care of women`s hearts to help them beat the odds.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Love hearing from those entrepreneurs.\par \par \uc2 All right, Heart of America is next with a police department that made a little boy`s dream come true.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet 5-year-old Ethan Hierro, a brave young boy from Tarrytown, New York, who`s battling brain cancer. Last week, Ethan had his dream come true by becoming Officer Ethan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tarrytown Police Department)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JOHN BARBELET (Tarrytown, New York, Police Chief): How are you Ethan?\par \par \uc2 ETHAN HIERRO (5 Years Old): Good.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JOHN BARBELET: I`m the chief. Are you going to work with us?\par \par \uc2 ETHAN HIERRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JOHN BARBELET: How about this? Would you like to go with mom, your sisters (sp?) --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, the Tarrytown police chief himself picked up their newest recruit and brought him and his family to the police station for his swearing-in.\par \par \uc2 And he was treated like the VIP he is, with a police escort, with lights and sirens the entire way. And after being sworn in by Mayor Karen Brown, Ethan was given his own shield, as well as a tour of the station. And, of course, every officer needs his own patrol car, so that was provided as well.\par \par \uc2 Police Officer Ethan Hierro, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240228ek2r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for February 23, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Charlie D`Agata, Ed O`Keefe, David Martin, Mark  Strassmann, Errol Barnett, Robert Costa, Steve Hartman, Manuel Bojorquez \par \uc2 3,632 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: fertility treatments under threat, and now the candidates for president are weighing in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R-Presidential Candidate): We want to make sure embryos are protected.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We want to make it easier for mothers and fathers to have babies, not harder.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The fallout from that landmark IVF ruling is taking center stage.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I think the government should stay out of the business of women.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: All life should be protected.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, what Alabama`s attorney general is saying about prosecuting IVF families and providers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 There is breaking news tonight in the trial of the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  `s former longtime leader, \uc2 Wayne LaPierre\uc2  , after he was accused of using the gun lobbying group as a personal piggy bank. We will get to that news in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 But we do want to begin with the future of reproductive rights here in America and the growing backlash to that \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  decision that declared embryos should be treated as children.\par \par \uc2 A growing number of conservative Republicans are speaking out against the ruling. There are also a number of key developments in Alabama that have possible nationwide implications. One of the country`s largest embryo shipping companies today saying they have reportedly paused doing business in Alabama. And that means many of the people who hope to continue their treatment out of state have lost that option.\par \par \uc2 Also this, the Alabama attorney general, for his part announced today he has no intention of prosecuting IVF clinics or its patients.\par \par \uc2 Now, this all comes just ahead of the key Republican primary vote tomorrow in South Carolina, and that`s where \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa is tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Republican primary front-runner former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he strongly supports the availability of IVF.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Today, I`m calling on the Alabama legislature to act quickly to find an immediate solution to preserve the availability of IVF in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: As former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  aims for a breakthrough in her home state, a national reckoning over the Alabama Supreme Court`s ruling on in vitro fertilization is upending the final hours of the South Carolina primary.\par \par \uc2 How should Republicans talk about IVF treatments?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R-Presidential Candidate): What I will say is, don`t have a knee-jerk reaction over this. We want to make sure embryos are protected. This decision needs to be decided between the parents and the physicians, no one else. Government doesn`t need to start intruding into this.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The debate over Alabama`s decision is now putting 2024 battleground Republican candidates in political crosshairs, causing the campaign arm of the Senate to encourage Republican Senate candidates to clearly and concisely reject efforts by the government to restrict IVF.\par \par \uc2 But amid the fallout, an intense push for votes in South Carolina for the last two Republican presidential candidates.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  : We have a country to save.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Top Republicans tell CBS News they`re urging Haley to stay in the race as long as possible, in case Trump is convicted in the criminal hush money case set to begin next month in New York.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, her supporters are weighing their options if she were to drop out.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I would sit this election out if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  were the only nominees.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In our interview, Haley sounded the alarm, saying that Republicans nationwide need to sit up and pay attention, open their eyes that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be sitting in a courtroom for much of the coming months.\par \par \uc2 But so many voters here in South Carolina, especially Trump`s supporters, say they just don`t care -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa, ahead of that key vote tomorrow, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to that breaking news about the powerful \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  . The NRA`s former leaders, including the longtime leader \uc2 Wayne LaPierre\uc2  , were accused of using millions of \uc2 NRA\uc2  donor funds as a personal piggy bank. And, tonight, in a stunning rebuke, a jury in Manhattan found them liable.\par \par \uc2 We get details now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Errol Barnett.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Wayne, how do you feel?\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (voiceover): After a week of deliberation, a New York jury tonight found \uc2 Wayne LaPierre\uc2  liable of fraudulently spending \uc2 NRA\uc2  donor funds. He`s been ordered to pay back more than $4.3 million.\par \par \uc2 But the New York attorney general`s office had claimed LaPierre improperly spent even more money, including millions of dollars on private jets, hundreds of thousands of dollars for trips to the Bahamas, and excessive amounts to one vendor in exchange for international trips and personal use of a luxury yacht.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from December 2012)\par \par \uc2 WAYNE LAPIERRE\uc2  (Former Executive Vice President, \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  ): The only thing that stops a bad guy with a gun is a good guy with a gun.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: It is yet another victory for New York Attorney General Letitia James, who`s been investigating the NRA`s spending practices for four years.\par \par \uc2 LaPierre`s decades-long influence and strategic alliance with Republicans prevented the still-debated ban on assault weapons, despite the ongoing plague of mass shootings and gun violence across America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, LaPierre has denied the -- state`s allegations against him.\par \par \uc2 Two other \uc2 NRA\uc2  executives were also found liable of violating their statutory obligations. They also deny culpability. But \uc2 NRA\uc2  membership and revenue has been declining in recent years. Still, though, Norah, it remains a powerful force both politically and culturally across the country.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Errol Barnett, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to this. A suspect is in custody tonight in connection to the death of a 22-year-old girl in Georgia. The dean`s list nursing student was found on a popular running trail with what police call visible injuries.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann reports tonight on renewed concerns of safety on college campuses.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Laken Riley.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): For Laken Riley, an aspiring nurse, this white jacket ceremony was a rite of passage.\par \par \uc2 The 22-year-old honor student was murdered as she jogged alone, jarring Athens, home of the University of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 ZOE TILLEY (University of Georgia Student): She was a sweet girl. It`s obviously devastating for my sorority. All the girls were, like, at the house crying yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Athens is your textbook college town. Lake Herrick is another option for students to jog, walk, relax. But the woods behind this lake is where police found Laken Riley`s body, her murder, the first on this campus in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 JEFF CLARK (University of Georgia Police Chief): The individual was unconscious and not breathing and had visible injuries.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Late this afternoon, police announced they had a murder suspect in custody, but gave no other details.\par \par \uc2 Friday felt different here, edgy for many. All day, police scoured this apartment complex. And Friday, classes were canceled --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: So, definitely a fear factor.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: -- for Georgia students like Haley Pearson (sp?) and Addison Turkin (sp?).\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Everyone`s just in their dorm or went home. And it`s just a quiet, eerie feeling.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Adidas commissioned a global survey about women joggers like Laken Riley; 92 percent said they were concerned and 51 percent said afraid of being physically attacked, especially now in Athens.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: I have always run with a group, and part of the reason is because I don`t want to run alone.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Safety.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Yes, safety, that`s the number one thing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And many people here will feel safer again now that police have a suspect in custody -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Just an awful story.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the governor of Mississippi says two National Guardsman have died after their helicopter crashed this afternoon during a routine training exercise. The AH-64 Apache helicopter crashed in the northeast corner of the state near the Alabama and Tennessee borders. That was around 2:00 p.m. local time. It`s just the latest in a series of military training crashes over the last several years.\par \par \uc2 Also, in some more breaking news, the U.S. is tracking a high-altitude balloon flying over the western part of the country. And it comes just more than a year after the U.S. military shot down that Chinese balloon off the coast of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 We want to bring in our Pentagon correspondent, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin.\par \par \uc2 And so, David, what are they doing about this balloon?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, NORAD sent up aircraft to take a look at the balloon after it was first detected over Utah, and says it does not appear to present a threat to national security since it`s not armed and not carrying any visible intelligence sensors.\par \par \uc2 Who it belongs to and exactly what it`s doing remain unclear. It cannot maneuver and is being blown by the wind from west to east at 43,000 to 45,000 feet, where the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says it does not pose a threat to commercial aviation. It`s described as a small Mylar balloon about 50-feet-tall with a two-foot-square box hanging underneath.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, David, I do need to ask you, because there`s such high interest in this because of that previous Chinese spy balloon. So, how does this one compare?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, the Chinese spy balloon was traveling at 60,000 feet. It was 200-feet-tall, and it was carrying a payload of intelligence sensors and solar panels the size of two freight cars.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the U.S. military shot it down when it reached the Atlantic Ocean. This small balloon is traveling at about 60 miles an hour, and NORAD says it will continue to monitor it to make sure it does not present a hazard to aviation -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Got it, so, much smaller, but they are on alert.\par \par \uc2 David Martin, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, President Biden announced hundreds of new sanctions on Russia on the eve of the two-year mark of Russia`s invasion of Ukraine and the mysterious death of opposition leader \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 CBS` Ed O`Keefe reports tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Make no mistake, Putin is responsible for Alexey`s death.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): The sweeping sanctions unveiled by President Biden include penalties on those specific individuals who ran the Arctic penal colony where detained opposition leader \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2  died one week ago.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  : We in the United States are going to continue to ensure that Putin pays the price for his aggression abroad and repression at home.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In total, there are roughly 600 new sanctions on individuals and entities in Russia and 11 other countries, including China, Serbia and the United Arab Emirates.\par \par \uc2 They include Russia`s top steel producer making parts for military helicopters, a railroad company used by the Russian army, a major Moscow bank, and a Russian-Iranian supply network making lethal attack drones used in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): These sanctions, these restrictions have had a significant impact on Russia, both in its ability to prosecute its aggression. But, even more, we see that they`re going to have a growing and long-term impact on Russia, its ability to modernize its industries.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But CBS News contributor Samantha Vinograd says, sanctions so far haven`t been effective, in part because Putin has maintained strong financial ties with countries that have kept the war machine functioning.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: While U.S. sanctions are designed to have an impact over time, to trickle down in the Russian economy, what`s happening now is more important. Ukraine is poised to run out of ammunition, and Russia has enough money to keep fueling its war machine, at least in the near term.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And news tonight about \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 His mother, who`s been pushing for the release of his body, was told by Russian authorities she could agree within three hours to a secret funeral with no public farewell, or he`d be buried at that penal colony.\par \par \uc2 No word yet on what happened, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Ed O`Keefe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Overseas now to Ukraine. The military is losing ground on the battlefield, which President Zelenskyy blames on a shortage of badly needed weapons.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata reports tonight from Western Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): Just as the full-scale invasion enters its third year, Ukraine suffers its worst loss in months, forces making a hasty retreat under fire from Avdiivka.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  , filmed taking a ride in a nuclear-capable bomber, had mocked what he called Ukraine`s chaotic flight from the city.\par \par \uc2 At a different photo-op, the Ukrainian President, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  , laying wreaths for the war dead at a cemetery in the western city of Lviv today. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and a visiting congressional delegation also paid their respects --\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): We saw the faces of the brave men and women who had lost their lives.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: -- and pledged to push through the $60 billion aid package stalled in Congress. But he told us there`s no time to waste.\par \par \uc2 What kind of impact is that going to have here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  : You know, I -- they had told us they need it in a matter -- in a short period of time. If this waits a year, it`s over. It`s over.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: This may be Zelenskyy`s biggest challenge since the early dark days of the war, when he declared: "We`re here protecting the country. Glory to Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: When unrelenting airstrikes had military analysts predicting Ukraine would fall in a matter of weeks and Russian ground troops were slaughtering civilians in the suburbs of the capital.\par \par \uc2 But with U.S. and allied support, Ukraine fought back, forcing a Russian retreat. But now, starved of ammunition and facing a much larger enemy, with each moment, Ukraine is losing more ground and more lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Senator Schumer said President Zelenskyy told him, if Ukraine gets that new aid package, they will win the war; if they don`t, they will lose.\par \par \uc2 But, Norah, with tens of billions of dollars in U.S. aid already invested here, that may be a hard sell back home.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s Health Watch is next, with a measles outbreak in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In tonight`s Health Watch: Florida`s surgeon general is defying more than 50 years of CDC guidelines in response to a measles outbreak at a local school.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez spoke with some frustrated parents.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): The end of the school day at Manatee Bay Elementary in Weston near Fort Lauderdale comes with the hope of no new measles cases.\par \par \uc2 TRIANA ROA (Grandmother): It`s very disruptive. It`s not really fair.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The Broward County School District says 33 out of nearly 1,100 students are not vaccinated for measles. So far, there are six confirmed cases of the highly contagious infection, which starts with cold- like symptoms seven to 14 days after exposure, then a rash.\par \par \uc2 For some, the disease can be deadly.\par \par \uc2 PETER LICATA (Superintendent, Broward County, Florida, Public Schools): I have not seen any outbreak of measles in my 30 years as being an educator.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Peter Licata is the Broward County Schools superintendent.\par \par \uc2 There`s been some questions about allowing unvaccinated students into the school during this time because it is so highly contagious.\par \par \uc2 PETER LICATA : And that would be the determination of the Health Department and the state.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But Florida`s Surgeon General, Dr. Joseph Ladapo, is leaving attendance decisions to parents, breaking with established medical guidelines, not recommending the vaccine, nor requiring unvaccinated people to stay home.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Randy Katz manages the emergency room at Joe DiMaggio Children`s Hospital, where the cases were treated.\par \par \uc2 Would you recommend unvaccinated children stay home?\par \par \uc2 DR. RANDY KATZ (Joe DiMaggio Children`s Hospital): I think, if you follow the CDC guidelines, they do recommend an unvaccinated child who`s been exposed to somebody with measles should stay home for at least 21 days.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The CDC reports there have been 35 recent cases of measles across 15 states. Today, Michigan reported an additional case, bringing the number to 16. The disease was declared eliminated from the U.S. in 2000. But vaccination rates have since fallen below the 95 percent needed to achieve herd immunity.\par \par \uc2 DR. RANDY KATZ: The measles, mumps, and rubella vaccine is extremely safe. We have been giving it to kids for decades.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Tonight, Broward County is reporting a seventh measles case in a child under 5.\par \par \uc2 The vaccine is required in order to attend classes here. But there are religious and medical exemptions. And the district says students can always attend classes online -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such an important story and update.\par \par \uc2 Manny Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Two major airlines raise their baggage fees. We will tell you which ones next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The price of checking a bag for your flight is going up again.\par \par \uc2 Starting tomorrow at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  , the cost is going up to $40 for the first checked bag. American Airlines raised its baggage fees by up to $10 this week.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman goes On the Road with a story about the power of forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Nikia Cherry in the black and Staci Green in the bright may look like besties from way back, but this is a bond born from bitterness.\par \par \uc2 NIKIA CHERRY (Charged with Vehicular Homicide): I love you so much, Ms. Staci.\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN (Daughter of Accident Victim): Oh, Nikia, oh.\par \par \uc2 NIKIA CHERRY: I`m so sorry.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Do a twirl.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Four years earlier, Staci`s mom, Rosie, was killed in a car crash. The other driver, Nikia Cherry, was doing 73 in a 45. Atlanta area police charged her with vehicular homicide, and Staci was glad to see her suffer.\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: Yes, I was consumed.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: By what?\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: Anger, sadness, loss.\par \par \uc2 JEB BUTLER (Attorney): I mean, Staci was furious.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Attorneys Jeb Butler and Tom Giannotti represented Staci in the civil trial, and they made sure to keep the parties in the case apart.\par \par \uc2 JEB BUTLER: Because I was worried that, if they got together, the result would be incendiary. I was very pleasantly wrong.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Instead, last October, Staci went up to Nikia in this courthouse. She thought of what her minister mother would say, and then told Nikia: "I forgive you."\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: And when I forgave her, it`s like I was reborn again.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You make it sound like a miracle.\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: Nothing short.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It was an extraordinary step, but only the first step. From then to now, Staci has gone so far beyond the words "I forgive you" to the actions of "I love you."\par \par \uc2 NIKIA CHERRY: She`s like a godmama to me. I talk to her every day.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Nikia lost everything after the crash. She now lives in a motel.\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: So I am committed to her life getting better.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Like how?\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: So, I have helped her with money for food.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You have given her money?\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: Yes. Rent. I was her daughter`s secret Santa. I booked a trip for her to go to Miami for her 40th birthday.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Her attorneys say they have never heard of anything like it.\par \par \uc2 TOM GIANNOTTI (Attorney): She`s a remarkable, you know, remarkable person.\par \par \uc2 JEB BUTLER: And all that`s great. That separates conversation from conviction.\par \par \uc2 NIKIA CHERRY: Staci didn`t have to do that. I`m going to cry.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Actually, Staci says she did have to do all that, or she could have never forgiven herself.\par \par \uc2 STACI GREEN: We`ve got to make the best out of this situation.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman On the Road in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We can all learn something about the power of forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240224ek2n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for February 22, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Carter Evans, Jonathan Vigliotti, David Martin,  Nancy Cordes, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Jo Ling Kent, Mark Strassmann, Elise  Preston \par \uc2 3,695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: massive cell service outage.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 COMPUTER VOICE: Some of our customers are experiencing wireless service interruptions this morning.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The widespread blackout for America`s largest wireless provider. The mystery behind what could have caused the problem for tens of thousands of \uc2 AT&T\uc2  users.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It was like radio silence from the moment I woke up.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I see an SOS. Now I can`t even use my GPS, my music.\par \par \uc2 COMPUTER VOICE: We apologize for this inconvenience.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin tonight with that nationwide \uc2 AT&T\uc2  outage that left a lot of people scratching their heads wondering what is going on and leading to confusion and concern about the ability to dial 911, the ripple effect inundating emergency centers from New York to Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 AT&T\uc2  tonight is apologizing, but they are not explaining what happened. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the \uc2 FCC\uc2  and the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  are now investigating the cause, and the White House says there is no evidence that a malicious attack was behind the network shutdown.\par \par \uc2 But there are still so many questions, the outage also highlighting concerns about how the lack of communications could impact us all in a real emergency and renewing concerns about cybersecurity in the U.S. The top Republican on the Senate Intelligence Committee tonight has a dire warning, saying a Chinese cyberattack will be 100 times worse.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` senior business and technology correspondent, Jo Ling Kent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): A rude awakening for tens of thousands of \uc2 AT&T\uc2  customers.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I woke up this morning, turned on my phone, and no Internet.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Check my phone, it`s like, what`s going on? I don`t even see -- I see an SOS. Now I can`t even use my GPS, my music, call my wife, tell her where I`m going to be.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: From Chicago to Dallas, Boston to Los Angeles, more than 74,000 users reported widespread problems.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: So, on a personal level, it was -- it was definitely confusing and uncomfortable, but it wasn`t the end of the world.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Late today, \uc2 AT&T\uc2  apologized to customers and said service had been fully restored. Nearly three-quarters of adults live in households without a landline, so losing cell service in a disaster can cripple communications.\par \par \uc2 That was the case in the Maui wildfires. But today`s outage inundated emergency call centers with people dialing them up to test their phones, \uc2 Massachusetts State Police\uc2  posting that many 911 centers were flooded with calls. And in San Francisco:\par \par \uc2 MARY ELLEN CARROLL (Executive Director, San Francisco, California, Department of Emergency Management): Please don`t call 911 to verify if your phone works if you don`t have an emergency.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is in contact with \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , and the \uc2 FCC\uc2  is actively investigating. The National Security Council says there`s no reason to think that this was a cybersecurity incident.\par \par \uc2 Does this incident worry you about the future?\par \par \uc2 MARY ELLEN CARROLL: I always worry about our dependency on technology. I think it`s also a good wake-up call and just a reminder that we can`t depend 100 percent all the time on the technology that we depend on 100 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In case of emergency, during an outage, experts recommend using the emergency call or text function on your phone, or go find Wi-Fi to make a call.\par \par \uc2 You always want to have an emergency list, as well of your neighbors and your family and friends. And at a total last resort, experts say you can always go in person to your local fire or police department -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s good advice.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 There is more fallout tonight from that controversial Supreme Court ruling in Alabama giving embryos the same rights as children. Two fertility clinics in two of the largest cities in the state paused IVF treatments today over the potential legal risks, bringing the total in the state to at least three.\par \par \uc2 We get details now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Tonight, Gabby Goidel and her husband, Spencer, say they`re in a state of desperation, leaving their home in Alabama and rushing to catch a flight to Texas, where she will now have to continue her IVF treatment.\par \par \uc2 GABBY GOIDEL (IVF Patient): Our clinic called us this morning and let us know that they are no longer going to be providing services or doing any transfers.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Goidel was halfway through her IVF cycle.\par \par \uc2 GABBY GOIDEL: I just kind of broke down into tears, and then we had to suck it up and start making a game plan, because it is such a time- sensitive issue.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Goidel`s provider, Alabama Fertility, was one of two clinics in the state that paused IVF treatment today, writing in a social media post: "We are working as hard as we can to alert our legislators as to the far-reaching negative impact of this ruling on the women of Alabama."\par \par \uc2 And the other clinic, Mobile Infirmary, said: "We understand the burden this places on deserving families who have no alternative options for conceiving."\par \par \uc2 In Michigan today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  addressed the issue during the White House`s Fight for Reproductive Freedoms Tour.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): The access to reproductive health care through IVF is being taken from countless individuals and families.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And on the presidential campaign trail, after \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  said in an interview yesterday, embryos are babies, she clarified her position.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CNN)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R-Presidential Candidate): I didn`t say that I agreed with the Alabama ruling. I do think that, if you look in the definition, an embryo is considered an unborn baby.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A Republican state senator in Alabama is planning to file a bill that he says could add protections for IVF, because it would clarify that embryos are not viable until they are transferred into the uterus.\par \par \uc2 But, Norah, reproductive rights advocates say that is not a solution.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We`re going to be following this story.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to some breaking news. The Justice Department just unsealed charges against four foreign nationals for their alleged part in an operation that left two Navy SEALs dead.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin is at the Pentagon with the latest news.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN (voiceover): The four were arrested after Navy SEALs boarded this vessel off the coast of Somalia and found missile parts, including a warhead, the U.S. says were being smuggled from Iran to the Houthis in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 Two Navy SEALs, Petty Officers Chris Chambers and Gage Ingram, were lost at sea while attempting to board in rough seas. The boarding occurred last month on the same date the U.S. and Britain launched its first round of strikes against Houthi targets in Yemen, strikes which have yet to put an end to attacks against commercial shipping.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH (Pentagon Deputy Press Secretary): We have certainly seen in the past 48, 72 hours an increase in attacks from the Houthis.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The criminal complaint unsealed today says the four suspects carried Pakistani identification cards and that the alleged captain of the crew had a satellite phone on which he communicated with a member of Iran`s Revolutionary Guards.\par \par \uc2 After the vessel was searched and its cargo of missile parts discovered, the crew was taken aboard the USS Puller and transferred to jail in Richmond, Virginia, four charged with crimes and eight others held as material witnesses. The \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  has since intercepted another ship carrying arms to Yemen.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY SABRINA SINGH: We know that the Houthis maintain a large arsenal. They are very capable. They have sophisticated weapons, and that`s because they continue to get them from Iran.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The U.S. military is conducting its own investigation into the boarding which resulted in the loss of those two Navy SEALs to determine, among other things, if it should have been attempted in such rough seas -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: David Martin with that breaking news, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden today met with the widow and daughter of Russian opposition leader \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2 , who died last week in prison under mysterious circumstances. It comes as Russian authorities are pressuring Navalny`s mother to agree to a secret burial of his body without mourners.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes is traveling with the president in California.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): The pictures show President Biden embracing and sitting with Alexey Navalny`s widow and daughter in a San Francisco hotel room one week after Navalny`s suspicious death in a Russian penal colony.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): He was a man of incredible courage, and it`s amazing how his wife and daughter are -- are emulating that.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Navalny`s daughter, Dasha, is a student at nearby \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 . Her mother flew to California to comfort her after meeting with European leaders and vowing to carry on her husband`s work.\par \par \uc2 (YULIA NAVALNAYA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: "By killing Alexey, Putin killed half of me," she said.\par \par \uc2 In Russia, Navalny`s mother said today that she had finally been taken to a morgue to see the body. She was told he died of natural causes and said that she was being pressured to hold a private burial ceremony, as Russian police try to head off more protests over his death.\par \par \uc2 (LYUDMILA NAVALNAYA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: "They want it done secretly," she said. "I don`t agree with that."\par \par \uc2 A defiant Putin flexed his muscle today, flying on a nuclear-capable strategic bomber, even as the U.S. and its allies prepare to unveil new sanctions tomorrow aimed at hobbling his military.\par \par \uc2 At a California fund-raiser last night, President Biden called Putin a -- quote -- "Crazy S-O-B."\par \par \uc2 MAN: President Biden.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Putin shrugged it off in an interview with state TV.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2  speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: "We understand what is happening there from a domestic political point of view," he said, "and this reaction is absolutely adequate."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Tonight, we are getting a better sense of the scope of the sanctions President Biden is set to announce tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned that these sanctions will target nearly 600 banks, companies and individuals both inside and outside Russia -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Very severe sanctions.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, I also want to ask you about these new developments in the case of that ex-\uc2 FBI\uc2  informant who lied to investigators about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . What do we know tonight?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Alexander Smirnov, who was just freed on bond two days ago, has been rearrested in Las Vegas. Federal prosecutors considered him a flight risk. They wanted him detained. In court documents, they said that he has been -- quote -- "actively peddling new lies that could impact U.S. elections."\par \par \uc2 And they said, Norah, that he had met with Russian intelligence contacts as recently as November.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to a terrifying attack on board an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight. Federal authorities have charged a man with assault with a dangerous weapon after he allegedly stabbed another passenger during a flight between Seattle and Las Vegas last month.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Carter Evans reports this is just the latest in a series of mid-flight incidents in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): It happened on an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight on approach to Las Vegas in January.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say Julio Alvarez Lopez bound pens together with rubber bands before the flight and used a makeshift weapon to stab the man sitting across the aisle. Photos show the victim with cuts and bruises. A federal grand jury indicted Lopez Wednesday. Court documents say he planned on attacking and killing the man and felt the mafia had been chasing him.\par \par \uc2 Sam Vinograd is a \uc2 CBS\uc2  contributor.\par \par \uc2 SAM VINOGRAD: Based upon just the sheer number of incidents we have been seeing over the past few years is that we really do need a new approach to airline safety.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Somebody get some help!\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Just this week, passengers had to forcibly restrain a man who allegedly tried to open the door on this \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight to Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Inside Edition)\par \par \uc2 ZACH ETKIND (Passenger): He was saying like: "I got to get off this plane. Get me off this plane."\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Passengers also helped break up a fistfight on this Southwest flight to Hawaii earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 SAM VINOGRAD: TSA continually tries to think about how the threat environment is evolving. But what`s clear is that we`re behind the curve.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, even though these unruly passenger incidents seem to be increasing in intensity, the number of them is actually on the decline since a peak in 2021.\par \par \uc2 But I want to put it all into context for you. The numbers we are seeing these days are still double what we saw before the pandemic -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Important context.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  doctor will be arraigned in Washington state tomorrow for what could be one of the largest sexual assault prosecutions in the Army`s history.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti has our investigation, including exclusive interviews with two of the accusers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Videotaped interviews made by the Army`s Criminal Investigation Division and obtained by CBS News detail disturbing accounts.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: He said: "Now I need you to drop trou."\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I`m thinking to myself, like, what the hell is this for? And I`m here for my neck.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: They say they`re survivors of sex crimes at the hands of their doctor, Major Michael Stockin, a pain management anesthesiologist at Joint Base Lewis-McChord in Washington state.\par \par \uc2 Charging documents obtained by CBS News show Stockin faces more than 50 counts of sexual misconduct while treating 42 patients, all of them men.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Being in the military at the time for 19 years, I trusted the medical doctor I was seeing. I trusted Dr. Stockin.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: We sat down with two of Stockin`s accusers, who are sharing their stories publicly for the first time. They asked to speak anonymously, out of fear of retaliation.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: I knew something wasn`t right after the first interaction, the first procedure I had with him.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Dr. Stockin, he was face level with my groin, and he started touching my genitals.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: You went in for a longstanding chronic issue of shoulder pain. What is a doctor doing inspecting you in an area down there near your groin?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Exactly. I was -- I was very confused.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Both men, now retired from the Army, completed three combat tours.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: In the military, there`s a term to put what happens to you inside your duffel bag, shove it down, and continue to march on. So I was willing to do that. I was willing to shove it into my duffel bag and march on.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The Army told CBS News the investigation remains open.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: I think there are victims out there that might not even know they`re victims.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The scale of the case is historic for the military, according to their attorney, Ryan Guilds.\par \par \uc2 How many potential victims are we talking about?\par \par \uc2 RYAN GUILDS (Attorney): Hundreds. I`m convinced it`s hundreds.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: In a statement to CBS News, Stockin`s lawyer stated, "He is presumed innocent," adding, "This legal fight is just getting started."\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A popular former talk show host is diagnosed with the same form of dementia as Bruce Willis.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Former talk show host Wendy Williams has been diagnosed with a form of dementia, as well as aphasia, her team announced today.\par \par \uc2 The 59-year-old went through tests last year after her memory started to fail and she began to lose words. Actor Bruce Willis suffers from the same issues, and an estimated two million people in the U.S. have the disease.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news from the moon -- that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nearly 55 years after Neil Armstrong took one small step for man, a private U.S. company has taken one giant leap for mankind`s future of space exploration.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann reports on today`s historic moon landing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Intuitive Machines)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Tonight, the odyssey of Odysseus has ended on the moon, the first American made spacecraft kicking up lunar dust in more than a half-century.\par \par \uc2 MAN: As our equipment is on the surface of the moon.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Jubilant mission control of Intuitive Machines in Houston, the first commercial moon landing ever.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Intuitive Machines)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The final descent from 18 miles above the lunar surface a continuous engine burn for 10 minutes. By touchdown, Odysseus had slowed to two miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2  (Co-Founder, Intuitive Machines): This is the assembly room.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Intuitive co-founder \uc2 Steve Altemus\uc2  showed us the historic moon lander up close last year in Houston. His company envisions a $100 billion lunar market.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : We`re delivering scientific payloads, engineering demonstration payloads, but not the humans themselves.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And you believe the moon is the next economic frontier?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : The moon is certainly that next step for space exploration.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Intuitive Machines)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Odysseus landed near the lunar south pole carrying less than 300 pounds of cargo, including six instruments for \uc2 NASA\uc2 , an Intuitive customer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NASA Animation)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The space agency hopes to have astronauts moonwalking there by the end of the decade. Some craters there never see light, so cold they may contain ice. And water is critical to long-term space exploration.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM HARWOOD: \uc2 NASA\uc2  plans to basically hire these companies to send payloads to the moon, like -- like you would call an Uber or a \uc2 Lyft\uc2 . But these companies have to make it work first, and that`s what we`re seeing today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Not since the end of the Apollo era has an American lander parked itself on the surface of the moon.\par \par \uc2 This mission will last roughly a week, until lunar night freezes the solar batteries. Quite an achievement, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, really, really exciting.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next with a look at the woman who made history in Barbie world and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And in honor of Black History Month, we introduce you to the woman who created the first black Barbie more than 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston now on the doll`s lasting impact on girls and women around the world.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): A big curly fro, bold gold jewelry, and a bright red dress looks dynamite on this brown-skinned Barbie.\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS (Black Barbie Designer): Everything that Barbie was, I wanted black Barbie to be the opposite.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Kitty Black Perkins, now 76, designed Mattel`s first black Barbie, which debuted in 1980 after calls from doll collectors.\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS: They were asking for more -- more black dolls.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: In the `60s and `70s, the only diverse dolls in Barbie`s world were sidekicks.\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS: She`s a little bit shapelier.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: But this doll, made more in the image of black women, would bear the marquee name.\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS: I wanted her also to be able to do the same things that the white doll did --\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS: -- but just add a little bit more spice.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Just a dash.\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS: Just a dash.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Perkins, Mattel`s first black designer, grew up in the Jim Crow South.\par \par \uc2 Did you have any black dolls growing up?\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS: I did not. And I would actually color the skin of the paper dolls.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Why?\par \par \uc2 KITTY BLACK PERKINS: Because I wanted them to look like me.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Kansas mom Sonya Larson collects black Barbies.\par \par \uc2 SONYA LARSON (Collector): People don`t understand that being able to see yourself in a doll, it`s -- it`s everything.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Perkins retired from \uc2 Mattel\uc2  as chief Barbie designer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Barbie, 2023, Warner Bros. Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Today, Barbie is more inclusive than ever, and taking over Hollywood. But, for Perkins, black Barbie was the mold to break the mold.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kitty Black Perkins, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240223ek2m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Alabama IVF Ruling Fallout; Biden Considers Executive Action at the Border; Gunmen Open Fire in West Bank; Guarding NATO`s Eastern Flank; Bomb Threat on United Flight; \uc2 Boeing\uc2  7373 Official Out; Wet Winter Weather; Historic Moonshot; Army Doctor Under Scrutiny; Eagles` Legal Battle; Biden`s Bad Dog; Immigration Fight; US Military Exercises; Gayle Celebrates Charles Barkley; Underground Sushi; One-On-One With Ziggy Marley; Remembering Activist Hydeia Broadbent; Office Has A Nose For In-Person Work; A Picture Perfect Pair; Jenny Slate Live, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Meg Oliver, Nancy  Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Holly Williams, Charlie D`Agata, Stephanie  Abrams, Mark Strassmann, Jonathan Vigliotti, Nancy Chen, Ed O`Keefe, Jamie  Yuccas, Elizabeth Werner \par \uc2 14,122 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets. Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, but we`re still here. So, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Alabama`s largest hospital halts IVF treatments after a ruling that frozen embryos have the same rights as children.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Is IVF your last hope to have a baby?\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK (Mother): Yes. There is no other way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: CBS News speaks exclusively to victims of what may be the largest abuse scandal in the army`s history.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think there are victims out there that might not even know they`re victims.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The classic hit "Hotel California" is at the center of a criminal trial. The latest on the decades` old drama.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: What the prosecution is saying is we`re dealing with stolen property.\par \par \uc2 MAN: New documents from the Secret Service reveal that President Biden`s dog, Commander bit agents on at least 24 occasions.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Tiger Woods` son, Charlie will compete in a pre-qualifying event this week in hopes of securing a spot in a PGA Tour event next week.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He`s 15. I`ve got a 15-year-old. And the pressure -- but you know what? His dad is a little bit more talented than Matthew`s dad.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Dillingham jumper -- got it. Kentucky takes the lead.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Number 17 Kentucky at \uc2 LSU\uc2 . Wildcats up by one.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Final second of the game, it got wild.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Wright with three, driving, pass it, block, gets it back. The shot -- good. Tyrell Ward puts it in.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: It falls at the buzzer. \uc2 LSU\uc2  pulls off the upset. More fans storming the court.\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: And \uc2 LSU\uc2  wins it, 75-74.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exciting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the way you play the game.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Buzzer beaters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: College basketball.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: College basketball. So -- we`re on the brink of March Madness.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I love March Madness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Make us even more excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you always an upset. You always wonder who is it going to be this year?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are going to begin though with the fallout from the Alabama Supreme Court first-of-its-kind ruling, I know you`ve heard about it, on frozen embryos created during in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 Now Alabama`s largest hospital now says it will put a pause on IVF treatments while it analyzes this ruling. The court decided that frozen embryos have the same rights as children.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver is here with more on this decision.\par \par \uc2 Meg, good morning.\par \par \uc2 There is a heated discussion on both sides of this issue.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: There certainly is. Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 This case stems from a wrongful death lawsuit from three couples who lost embryos at an Alabama fertility clinic, and now the court`s decision has left people with fertility problems worried about the future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Thirty-four-year-old, Jasmine York is from Alexander City, Alabama. She is the proud mother of a 13-year-old daughter, but can no longer have children, so she turned to IVF doctors at the \uc2 University of Alabama at Birmingham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK: I have no fallopian tubes due to ruptured ectopic pregnancies. It was first transferred back in January and it had had a couple of mishaps, and so we were really looking forward to March 20th was when I was supposed to transfer.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Is IVF your last hope to have a baby?\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK: Yes. There is no other way.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The university said in a statement that it was pausing IVF out of concern physicians and even aspiring parents could face criminal punishment following the state court`s decision even if the hospital adheres to the proper standard of care.\par \par \uc2 What are you telling patients in Alabama today?\par \par \uc2 DR. MAMIE MCLEAN (Alabama Fertility): We`re telling them that there remains a lot of uncertainty. We`re not certain what is safe and legal for us to do with regards to IVF.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Dr. Mamie McLean is a physician at one of Alabama`s largest fertility clinics.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAMIE MCLEAN: We`re taking this day by day. Ultimately we`re worried there`s going to be fewer babies born in Alabama because of this ruling.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Last night, Republican presidential candidate, Nikki Haley spoke to our Gayle King on CNN`s "King Charles" about the court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) (2024 presidential candidate): What -- the question I was ask is do I believe an embryo is a baby? I do think that if you look in the definition, an embryo is considered an unborn baby.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: York firmly disagrees.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK: These are not living children. If they were, things would be a lot different in my world and all of these other women`s worlds, and we want living children. That`s our goal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The university of Alabama at Birmingham told us it has no idea how long IVF will be on hold. Dr. McLean, who works in a private practice told us yesterday, she was in meetings all day with lawyers trying to figure out next steps -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meg, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn to another high-stakes fight, this over immigration at our southern border. Sources confirm to CBS News that President Biden is considering using sweeping executive authority to tackle the crisis there.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in Congress, you`ll recall, blocked a compromise immigration deal earlier this month. Now, there might be movement from the White House. Nancy Cordes has the details of this potential plan.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 What the White House is floating here would be a return to the Trump border policy in one significant respect. It would deter migrants from coming to the US by restricting access to the overloaded asylum program.\par \par \uc2 This is a big development especially for a Democratic administration, and officials are considering it because of the record numbers of migrant crossings over the past few years, 2.4 million migrants apprehended at the border just in the past fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 And so now, they are looking at a law known as 212-F that has been on the books for more than 70 years. It allows the president to suspend the entry of foreigners when it`s determined that their arrival is detrimental to the interests of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Now if that sounds familiar, it`s because the Trump White House used that same law to justify an attempted ban on visitors from some Muslim majority countries. That faced legal challenges, and this almost certainly would again.\par \par \uc2 So why would Mr. Biden attempt it then? Well, because he has gotten an earful from Democratic governors and mayors who say their cities are inundated by new migrants. He does not poll well on this issue, and it is an election year.\par \par \uc2 White House officials say that a decision on whether to go this route could come in the next two weeks -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans are pushing on with their impeachment inquiry into President Biden. The president`s brother, James testified behind closed doors yesterday. Meantime, GOP lawmakers are facing new questions about the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant charged with lying about the president and his son.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Vlad. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The ex-\uc2 FBI\uc2  informant`s alleged ties to Russian intelligence is raising questions, but Republicans dismissed Democrats` claims this deals a fatal blow to the impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re not commenting now.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The president`s younger brother, James was the latest to appear before the Republican-led impeachment inquiry into President Biden on Wednesday, telling congressional investigators the president had no direct or indirect role in his business ventures, but Republicans were also answering questions about former \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant, Alexander Smirnov who appeared in federal court just a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 Any concerns about what happened with this \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant?\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): I -- you know, I`m looking at it. Just want to see what they`ve got, why they say what they say.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You have seconds thoughts about this?\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): No, no.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Smirnov is charged with providing false information about President Biden and his son, Hunter. Some of it allegedly coming from Russian intelligence officials. Republicans had often cited the evidence Smirnov presented.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCCARTHY (R) (Former Speaker of the House): Even a trusted \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant has alleged a bribe to the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But Smirnov has claimed that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his father forced Ukrainian energy firm, \uc2 Burisma\uc2  to pay them each $5 million in bribes, prosecutors now say was a lie.\par \par \uc2 For the second day in a row, prosecutors asked the judge to jail Smirnov until trial, alleging he had recent contact with Russian foreign agents and arguing he is a serious flight risk who is actively peddling new lies that could impact this year`s election.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say these recent developments should end the Republican-led investigation of the president.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): And I`m restating my call to Chairman Comer, to Speaker Johnson, to fold up the tent to this circus show. It`s really over.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: These same congressional committees questioned \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  behind closed doors on Wednesday about his business dealings after a very public battle over several months over how that deposition should take place -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Scott. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to breaking news overnight, this, from the Middle East. There has been a new explosion of violence in the West Bank that`s left one Israeli dead and at least eight others have been wounded here.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has more from East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning. What`s the latest there?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Three Palestinian gunmen opened fire near a busy checkpoint in the Israeli- occupied West Bank early this morning according to authorities here. They say Israeli security forces killed two of the attackers, and a third was wounded and detained.\par \par \uc2 Now tensions have been running high between Israelis and Palestinians in the West Bank following the October 7th attack. Israel`s national security minister, Itamar Ben-Gvir visited the scene with some strong words, saying --\par \par \uc2 (ITAMAR BEN-GVIR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "The right to our lives prevails on their freedom of movement."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the Gaza Strip, there are reports that Israel has intensified its bombardment of the south. Around 1.5 million Palestinians are now sheltering in Rafah Southern Gaza; many living in desperate conditions.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -run Ministry of Health says that nearly 30,000 people in Gaza have now been killed since this war began -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those Israelis just sitting in traffic when the gunmen opened fire. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  called it a heroic act.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams in East Jerusalem for us. Holly, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We are going to turn now to Eastern Europe where nearly two years after the Russian invasion of Ukraine, thousands of US soldiers are guarding NATO`s eastern frontier and training hard to resist any attack there.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata got exclusive access to an American military exercise.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: US live fire combat exercises in Northern Poland close to Russian territory. The scenario, an unnamed adversary has launched an attack on NATO`s eastern flank.\par \par \uc2 The mission for the soldiers of the Third Infantry Division from Georgia is to recapture this ground and force an enemy retreat.\par \par \uc2 Bradley fighting vehicles roll in, infantry troops roll out, explosives take out the minefields, soldiers advance.\par \par \uc2 Exercises like these aren`t just meant to make sure that individual soldiers are ready for combat, but the military is ready in case there is an incursion of a regional enemy.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a reason the war games are being played out right here. To military planners, the Suwalki Gap is NATO`s Achilles heel, just 40 miles separates the Russian enclave of Kaliningrad and Putin ally, Belarus.\par \par \uc2 If the Kremlin launched an incursion here, it would cut off the Baltic states from other \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 And troops are preparing for any eventuality says Lieutenant Colonel Timothy Decker.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. TIMOTHY DECKER (\uc2 US Army\uc2 ): We remain engaged in our training to develop our tactical proficiency and, you know, remain a lethal force that is capable of deterring aggression.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But that training could be in jeopardy with Ukraine funding package stalled in Congress.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Army\uc2  has been footing the bill not just for exercises like these, but training and supplying Ukrainian forces.\par \par \uc2 COL. MARTIN O`DONNELL (\uc2 US Army\uc2 , Europe and Africa spokesman): Worst case scenario, we`re out of funds by the summertime.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: \uc2 US Army\uc2 , Europe and Africa spokesman, Colonel Martin O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 How crucial is it right now?\par \par \uc2 COL. MARTIN O`DONNELL: There`s a lot of risk right now. If we don`t get money, what we do, what the \uc2 US Army\uc2  does on two continents, in Europe and Africa and what we do to support Ukraine, it`s at risk.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: That fundamental. That`s the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 COL. MARTIN O`DONNELL Fundamentally, yes. Absolute risk.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Colonel O`Donnell told us if that Ukraine funding package is not approved soon, the \uc2 US Army\uc2  in Europe will run out of money as early as May or June. That will severely impact not only the flow of weapons to Ukraine, but critical \uc2 US Army\uc2  operations throughout this region -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Charlie D`Agata in Poland for us. Thank you, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 Now to a couple of stories about safety in the sky. A \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  plane carrying more than 200 people made an emergency landing yesterday after a bomb threat. Police say the threat was written on the mirror in one of the plane`s bathrooms.\par \par \uc2 Flight 1533 landed in Chicago about 90 minutes into the flight from Newark to Los Angeles. Passengers were evacuated and officers searched the plane. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating but said there is no indication of a public safety threat at this time.\par \par \uc2 And in a major shakeup at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , the head of the 737 Program left the company yesterday following criticism of its safety practices. Last month, you`ll recall, a door panel blew out of a 737 Max 9 plane midair on an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight. The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executive, Ed Clark, oversaw the factory where final assembly took place for the jet involved in the incident.\par \par \uc2 His departure comes after investigates said bolts to keep the door plugged in place appeared to be missing from the aircraft before it was delivered to \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, the good news is, it finally stopped raining in California. However, the state is still having trouble with mudslides this morning. Take a look at these pictures. Officials have closed down not one, not two, but several highways in Southern California, so let`s go to meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  with the latest.\par \par \uc2 What are these storms doing right now, Steph? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The storms have made their way into the Midwest. Today, rain will spread from the Great Lakes to the south and then continue to the I-95 corridor for Friday, clearing out as we head into our Friday evening.\par \par \uc2 Through tomorrow, both rain and snow will be light with one to three-inch totals. The temps on the other hand, they will be up there. Above average warmth continues for a big chunk of the country through the weekend, and of course, it`s winter, so you wouldn`t think we`d bring out our scorching car scale, but tragically our first hot car death last year occurred in late February.\par \par \uc2 Today`s high in Houston, 78, just 10 minutes inside of the car could be 97 degrees, and it doesn`t even have to be that hot. Nashville`s high in the low 60s, within 30 minutes it could be in the mid to upper 90s.\par \par \uc2 Tony, the hot temperatures will last into Monday when several records could be broken.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will keep an eye on it. \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, we`re going to take you inside an historic lunar mission aiming to land a US spacecraft on the moon later today for the first time -- listen to this -- in more than 50 years. All right.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:15, time now to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Ziggy Marley who helped produce the new hit movie "Bob Marley: One Love," why he says it`s important to share his father`s journey now.\par \par \uc2 Plus, gets ready to laugh, Jenny Slate will tell us about her new comedy special, and we will give you a sneak meek.\par \par \uc2 And we know President Biden`s dog had to leave the White House for biting some people, the question is, how many? We`ll have that answer in "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Later today, a Texas company could become the first private business to land a spacecraft on the moon. The lunar lander named Odysseus is aiming for a landing site near the moon`s south pole. It captured these photos after arriving in lunar orbit yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann has more on the historic moonshot.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ignition and liftoff.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Odysseus began its odyssey one week ago.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Separation confirmed.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The six-legged spacecraft, 14-feet tall, is the first built and flown by Intuitive Machines in Houston.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2  (Intuitive Machines co-founder, president, and chief executive officer): This is the assembly room.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: We met co-founder and CEO, \uc2 Steve Altemus\uc2  last year as Odysseus was being prepared for launch.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : We are delivering scientific payloads, engineering demonstration payloads, but not the humans themselves.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And liftoff.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But space is unforgiving. Last month, the first moon- bound lander built by a rival American company, \uc2 Astrobotic\uc2 , suffered a mission-ending failure of its propulsion system.\par \par \uc2 Confident you`ll stick first landing?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : You know, all the missions mounted to the moon, there`s only been a 40 percent success rate. We believe we can do better than that. I put odd our odds at 75 percent success.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For a successful touchdown, the lander`s engine has to perform. From behind a blast wall, we watched Intuitive Machines conduct a test fire.\par \par \uc2 On descent, the main engine needs to fire for ten straight minutes, then throttle down for a soft landing.\par \par \uc2 Wow.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : Just land softly. That`s the key.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Bouncing doesn`t help here.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : If you`re bouncing, you stay upright that might be okay.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But if you bounce and topple --\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : That`s no good.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ten miles to go.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Nothing American-made has touched down on the moon since \uc2 Apollo\uc2  17 more than a half century ago.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey, Houston, the Challenger has landed --\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Five countries have landed spacecraft there, but never a company.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the moon now, what do you see?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN ALTEMUS\uc2 : I see a little glint of an Intuitive Machine`s lander that will be on the moon forever and ever, a billion years. It`s a fantastic thought, and I can`t look at the moon any differently now.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And if they succeed, neither will we.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Mark Strassmann in Houston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mark does the coolest stories, and the moon still a very, very long way away. I am wishing them the best of luck there at intuitive.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead CBS News investigates troubling allegations of abuse inside the \uc2 US Army\uc2 . We`re looking into the case of a doctor who is accused of betraying the men in his care, male soldiers.\par \par \uc2 It is a CBS News exclusive. Local news though coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It is 7:30.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Army\uc2  is facing what could be one of the largest abuse scandals in its history. CBS News is investigating the case of an Army doctor who is accused of sexual misconduct. His name is Major Michael Stockin. He faces allegations from 42 male patients who say he has abused their trust.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti sat down exclusively with two of the alleged victims whose identities we are not revealing, speaking publicly for the first time. They say this doctor, instead of healing them left them with very deep wounds.\par \par \uc2 We should warn you, this story may be triggering in language and its content.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: CBS News has obtained these videotaped interviews made by the Army`s Criminal Investigation Division.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: He said, "No, I need you to drop trou."\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I`m thinking to myself, like, what the hell is this for? And I`m here for my neck.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Disturbing accounts by victims of alleged sex crimes whose identities we concealed.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It`s definitely not normal.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I feel like a (bleep) victim.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: They detailed claims of sexual abuse at the hands of their doctor, Major Michael Stockin, a pain management anesthesiologist at Washington State`s Joint Base Lewis-McChord.\par \par \uc2 According to the charging documents obtained by CBS News, Stockin faces over 50 counts of sexual misconduct that spans more than two years while treating 42 patients, all of them men.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I trusted Dr. Stockin.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: We sat down with two of Stockin`s accusers, patients who saw him at Madigan Army Medical Center who are sharing their stories publicly for the first time. They asked to speak anonymously out of fear of retaliation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: He would take a knee and that`s where he would kind of conduct his examination. Everything was pretty much mid-calf to waist level.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: When you say waist, you mean groin?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Groin area, yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Myself and Dr. Stockin were left alone in the room. He first checked my shoulders and then asked me to stand up. Dr. Stockin, he was face level about my groin and he started touching my genitals.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: You went in for a long-standing chronic issue of shoulder pain. What is a doctor doing inspecting you in an area down there near your groin?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Exactly. I was very confused.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Both men now retired, each spent more than 20 years in the Army, both completing three combat tours.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Even with my wife, I couldn`t bring myself to talk through what happened to her. It just felt very uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: In the military, there`s a term to put what happens to you inside your duffel bag, shove it down, and continue to march on, so I was willing to do that. I was willing to shove it into your duffel bag and march on.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Stockin`s alleged pattern of behavior calls to mind the chilling details of infamous \uc2 USA gymnastics\uc2  doctor, Larry Nassar who preyed on patients under the guise of treatment.\par \par \uc2 In this case, patients say they also tried to give the doctor, an Army major, the benefit of the doubt.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Being in the military at the time for 19 years, I trusted the medical doctor I was seeing.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I think there are victims that might not even know they are victims.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The scale of the case is historic, according to their attorney, Ryan Guilds.\par \par \uc2 RYAN GUILDS (Attorney): We are aware of cases like the gymnastics case, we`re aware of cases where medical professionals have abused trust. That`s not unique to the military. But the scope and scale of it, for a doctor to have been alleged to have abused that trust for so very many victims is unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: How many potential victims are we talking about here?\par \par \uc2 RYAN GUILDS: Hundreds.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Hundreds?\par \par \uc2 RYAN GUILDS: I`m convinced it`s hundreds.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Male servicemembers are much less likely to report sexual assault than their female counterparts, according to the Defense Department.\par \par \uc2 Guilds, who is representing seven of Stockin`s accusers pro-bono says the Army failed to support victims.\par \par \uc2 RYAN GUILDS: It is the actions that matter, and these unforced errors and failing to care for our survivors undermines the credibility of the Army and is a real shame.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The Army told CBS News that the investigation will remain open through trial, and it will further investigate should additional victims come forward.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Stockin has been suspended from patient care.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to CBS News, Stockin`s lawyer stated that his client is presumed innocent, adding: "This legal fight is just getting started."\par \par \uc2 Both men we spoke with intend to share their story again at the Court Martial scheduled to begin in October.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It`s emotional. It`s not something I`m used to. I can deal with a lot of things. I can deal with anger. I can deal with combat. But emotions and things like that, it`s personal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And all of these charges relate to Stockin`s time in Madigan Army Medical Center, but the investigation is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Stockin had previously been assigned to duty stations in Maryland, Hawaii, and Iraq. If convicted, he faces a maximum sentence of more than 300 years in prison -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Horrific details, Jonathan. And we commend those soldiers for speaking out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say the same thing. I admire and applaud them for speaking up, because A., you know it`s painful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very personal. And you also feel -- you do trust your doctors. I mean, I go to see the doctor, I automatically assume he or she knows more than me about whatever I`m coming to see them for.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that`s in the civilian world, so now, you take that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The trust of doctors --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you add the military chain of command, which is the bedrock of the military.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The superior officer, this guy is a major. He is also a doctor. So you`ve got that exacerbation --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You should be stuffing this pain in to your duffel bag, to use that metaphor which is a fantastic one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of men, that will resonate with them. I hope justice is done in this case.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jonathan, thank you again for that reporting. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about the Eagles. Their hit "Hotel California" is one of the best-known songs of all time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead why -- yes, we love that song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everybody can sing it. Everybody can air solo -- guitar solo. Ahead, we`re going to talk about the lyrics from that song, which are now at the center of a landmark court case in New York.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hotel California.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is, of course, the 1970s Eagles` classic "Hotel California" off the multiplatinum album "Hotel California." That album now at the center of a New York criminal trial that began yesterday where prosecutors say handwritten lyrics with a value of more than a million dollars were stolen from the Eagles more than four decades ago. They want them back.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen looks at the allegations and band member, Don Henley`s role in getting those documents back where they belong.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Eagles Hell Freezes Over/\uc2 MTV\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Eagles` frontman, Don Henley has taken his fight over original band documents to court where the Manhattan district attorney claims three defendants conspired to sell stolen manuscripts of popular Eagles` songs, "Life in the Fast Line," "New Kid in Town" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hotel California")\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And "Hotel California," from one of the band`s most celebrated albums.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors allege an author obtained around a hundred pages of Henley`s handwritten notes and lyrics while working on a biography of the band in the late 1970s and sold them to rare books dealer, Glenn Horowitz in 2005. Then Horowitz allegedly sold them to former Rock & Roll Hall of Fame curator, Craig Inciardi and memorabilia seller, Edward Kosinski.\par \par \uc2 According to court documents, Kosinski sold some "Hotel California" lyric sheets back to Henley for $8,500.00 in 2012. But he and Inciardi later peddled others to Sotheby`s and Christie`s Auction Houses.\par \par \uc2 The DA`s office first got involved in December 2016, executing a series of search warrants to retrieve the allegedly stolen manuscripts and charges were filed against Horowitz, Kosinski, and Inciardi in July, 2022, including conspiracy to possess stolen property. All three men have pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: The prosecution has to make the connection. Each time there was a transaction, the defendants were, in fact, aware that this evidence was stolen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "New Kid in Town.")\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: For CBS MORNINGS, Nancy Chen, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Henley is expected to be a key witness in the case. In the opening statements yesterday, during them, one defense attorney said the prosecutors had, "accused three innocent men of a crime that never occurred."\par \par \uc2 When I say Henley wants these documents back where they belong, it`s where he thinks they belong. Clearly, there is another side to this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, it is his handwritten notes. Okay, let`s just take them at their word, that they didn`t know that they were stolen. But now you know they have been stolen. Why wouldn`t you just give them back to Don Henley?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you asked a question, Tony, well, what if you paid for them? I went "uh." I don`t know. I just think he should -- they should be returned to him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If they can prove that they were stolen, the Eagles, Don Henley probably has a case.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right, if they bought it legitimately thinking it was a legitimate purchase, it is trickier. That`s why I`m not a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaking as a nonmusical genius, all my notes are --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But a genius of all other things --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They can sell them, auction them, you can grab them any time. Here, Vlad, you can have these.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Check out any time you guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Give Don Henley back his music.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s for us, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It should never leave.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Give Don Henley back his notes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know, yes, so not going with the flow.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new information on exactly how many people were bitten by President Biden`s German Shepherd, Commander.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yikes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Blood on the floor in some cases.\par \par \uc2 Right now though, 7:44 exactly. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guess what? We`ve got a great eight o`clock hour ahead for you.\par \par \uc2 Comedian, Jenny Slate will be here today to talk about her new comedy special. She`s going to give us a sneak peek.\par \par \uc2 And Vlad caught up -- listen to the music -- going to be all right -- caught up with Bob Marley`s son, that would be Ziggy here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: . where the "One Love" movie made its debut. Marley tells Vlad why he waited until now to reveal his father`s story, so that`s coming up in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 But right now, Klaus and I, let`s go -- going over to the table because there`s Vlad and Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mr. Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All ready for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am -- I am really -- I am really excited for your Ziggy Marley conversation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like Ziggy Marley. We had him on "King Charles."\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is time now for "What to Watch." But first, a very happy birthday wish from \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To our friend, Drew Barrymore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, Drew, you can ring my bell --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bell. Ring my bell.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ring my bell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My bell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was the song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Drew is what -- what is happening?\par \par