t was finally time to tell his father`s story on the big screen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was number one last week, but first, here`s today`s "Eye- Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 The largest hospital in Alabama stops IVF treatments after the state Supreme Court rules frozen embryos have the same rights as children.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAMIE MCLEAN Ultimately, we`re worried that there`s going to be fewer babies born in Alabama because of this ruling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden considers returning to a border policy that would restrict some types of immigration.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: CBS News gets exclusive access to US military exercises in Poland as Russia`s invasion of Ukraine approaches the two-year mark.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "King Charles" CNN)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope you make it to 80, but this is what we have for you because word is you like lemon. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY (TV analyst): Lemon`s my favorite dessert.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Gayle wishes her friend and CNN co-host Charles Barkley a very happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY: They went all out, y`all. They love me around here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You should know that --\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY: They call -- what`s his name? Buddy Valastro?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You share the same birthday. Listen to who shares your birthday. Rihanna, Trevor Noah.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY: Yes. When are you going to introduce me to Trevor Noah?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we do do it big at CNN. Lemon tart with a candle on. But before the show, we always go out and talk to people and ask people how old do you think Charles is. Somebody said 35.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody said 70 and then said they were kidding. But most people landed on 55, 58. He looks good. Happy birthday, Sir Charles.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How old is he?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s 61.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I would not have known.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doesn`t he look good?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, he looks great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He looks great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anywhere from 35 to 75. I got no idea. I can no longer judge ages at all. It`s awful. It`s a limitation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You okay, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know. I`ll get it checked out neurologically. All right, but we`re going to be with the news here.\par \par \uc2 The big news out of Alabama, that decision leaving many Alabama couples who desperately want children, in a state of limbo about how the state`s largest hospital has stopped offering in vitro fertilization treatments after the Alabama Supreme Court ruled that frozen embryos have the same legal protections as children.\par \par \uc2 The issue has now reached the campaign trail as well as we knew it would. Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley backed the Alabama court`s conclusion on what embryos are by definition in her view.\par \par \uc2 Gayle asked her about it on CNN just last night.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "King Charles" CNN)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re hearing reports already about fertility clinics that are shutting down because they`re worried. What do you say to women that are now concerned about that and your position in that particular issue?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) (2024 presidential candidate): Well, first of all, I didn`t -- I mean, this is -- again, I didn`t say that I agreed with the Alabama ruling. What the question that I was asked is, do I believe an embryo is a baby? I do think that if you look in the definition, an embryo is considered an unborn baby. And so, yes, I believe from my stance that that is. The difference is --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, let`s go now to senior political correspondent Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 Ed, there`s some dancing around there, some parsing of the language. Could this ruling, just big picture, be a problem for Republican candidates who have to answer questions like Gayle`s?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Oh, absolutely. I think last night was a definition of a needle threat.\par \par \uc2 It`s exactly the kind of challenge that Republican candidates who are trying to be sensitive to public opinion and to women who are obviously very concerned about the future of access to these kinds of services, you know, are feeling, but also trying to represent the base of their party and the concerns of Republicans who, remember, have pushed for decades for these kinds of changes and the changes that may yet still be to come as courts continue to hear cases like this one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So let`s talk about Democrats for a second. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  just posted online calling the ruling, which cites a Christian God, a holy God, repeatedly, she called it "outrageous." How are Democrats likely to utilize this ruling for their benefit politically?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They continue to believe it`s one of the key ingredients for victories this year, not only at the presidential level, but potentially in congressional races, gubernatorial races across the country. It could be a ballot measure in places like Florida and Nevada to help boost turnout in those states as well.\par \par \uc2 Watch the vice president today who just conveniently happens to be going to Michigan to discuss this very issue as she continues to tour across the country, primarily to college campuses to remind younger voters of the potential threat to their health services and their access to abortion services and IVF treatment and things like that.\par \par \uc2 It`s expected that she`ll highlight this case and continue talking about it. Democrats believe it could be a big help for them come November.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. All right Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still to come, we speak with Ziggy Marley, the son of the legendary reggae artist Bob Marley.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What it was like producing the hit biopic about his dad. But first, it is 8:05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jamming")\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL:" Ahead in our series, "The Dish" meet the LA native and his Japanese mentor who run one of the city`s hottest sushi spots. Don`t you dare just order the avocado roll.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come on, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I do. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not comparing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle, we`re going to talk about this one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Tuff Gong Records")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That of course is multiple Grammy winner, Ziggy Marley, the son of reggae legend Bob Marley. He is one of the producers of the new film "Bob Marley: One Love" which opened on Valentine`s Day. The biopic blew away the competition at the box office and has already raked in more than $80 million worldwide.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with Ziggy to find out how he is keeping his dad`s legacy alive in this new movie.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY (Musician): His voice in the film became very important.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "One Love")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When Ziggy Marley began piecing together his vision for the biopic about his dad.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Get up, Stand Up" Island Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He knew it needed one thing above all, authenticity.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Just have an actor talk like him or sing like him, but he has to be a part of his own story.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That authenticity is heard throughout the film.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: As the reggae legends real vocals weave throughout "One Love."\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: It`s Bob Marley singing in his own film about him about him, and he, I mean, I respect that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: I don`t know him, but I love that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eagle Rock" Tuff Gong Studios)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not only am I a huge fan of yours, but obviously I`ve been a fan of your father`s since I was a kid. Like I wore my old college like my college T-shirt that I`ve been sporting for years. I got my Exodus on the back.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Oh, nice, nice, oh, wow, wow. Cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, this is just a moment to be sitting here with you.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Yes, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Talk to me about the genesis for this project.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: I mean, there`s been many instances of people coming to the family throughout the years wanting to do this project. I`m mature enough now where I can take this on as a part of my responsibility.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ziggy, now 52, was just 12 years old when his father died of skin cancer.\par \par \uc2 The film chronicles Bob Marley`s rise to fame during the 1970s. The biggest challenge, Marley says, was picking the right actor to play his father.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Kingsley Ben-Adir landed the role and nailed it.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Some people look just like Bob, or talk like Bob, but you don`t have that emotional connection. Then it can`t work. So we went with the emotional connection. You know?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Marley told us it was a certain kind of familiarity that won the role for Lashana Lynch, who plays his mom, Rita, in the film.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Lashana was different because Lashana of Jamaican heritage. She knew the culture and she kind of she reminded me of my mother.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Your mother is also a producer on the film. And you do explore that he did have instances where he was unfaithful.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: The spiritual bond that they have is what is much stronger than the physical bond. But it`s a love that is grown from the commonality of what they`ve been through together.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Those trials and tribulations include an assassination attempt on Bob and Rita Marley amid violent unrest in Jamaica, parallel years of abandonment during Bob Marley`s childhood and rejection by his father.\par \par \uc2 Losing your father at such a young age, did you find some level of a symbiotic relationship with what he was going through and what you went through as a young man?\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Obviously not in the same way, because my father loved me. For me, his abandonment issue was not just his father, but his country, his own people, his own community. He was very upset that they tried to hurt him and hurt his wife.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But iconic songs by "Bob Marley and the Wailers" may be the film`s biggest draw.\par \par \uc2 At one point in the film, when he`s asked about "Redemption Song" he said he`s been writing it his whole life.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What did he mean?\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: All his experiences that he`s been through, especially the internal stuff. I`m not talking about the concerts and the crowds and the record sales. That song didn`t come from that. That song came from something inside of him.\par \par \uc2 So whatever he`s been through all his life has led him to finally create this masterpiece.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We watched that scene together, one where Ziggy Marley himself is portrayed on screen by actor Xavier Woolry\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bob Marley: One Love" \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m watching you watch it, and you seem incredibly moved.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Yes, I mean, I don`t even see it as an actor. Like, I`m not the actor, done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s your dad.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: Yes, it`s like I`m seeing, like, I`m there at this place watching my father singing to me and my mother. It`s like we`re hearing Bob singing that. You know, it`s powerful, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eagle Rock" Geffen Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Today, Marley says his father`s story and his voice may be especially worth listening to.\par \par \uc2 ZIGGY MARLEY: We have to live together better as humanity. We`re not just marketing a movie. We`re marketing a consciousness for the people of the world. One love, unifying, less divisiveness. We need it. I`m proud of that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Can I just also say one thing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- that we talked about? Thank you. One thing we talked about is, to my recollection, this is the first time a major Hollywood picture has premiered in Jamaica. And the Marley family was very, very grateful that Paramount chose to premiere the movie in Jamaica.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Ziggy said there was no other place on Earth where you could premiere this movie but in Jamaica. And the Jamaican people loved it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He looks so much like his dad, it`s almost eerie. And I love - - I didn`t know much about Bob Marley. I knew his music. But I really love the movie. And it`s great to see it`s doing so well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Ziggy always said that what his dad cared about more than anything is the people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The people and he wanted to--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Paramount.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s really good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very well done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Movement of the people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bob Marley. Vida Weisblum was the producer on that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vida is wonderful as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Vida.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in our series, "The Dish," we take you to a sushi restaurant like no other. You`re going to see why it`s not just the menu that makes this place unusual. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In our series, "The Dish" we are visiting Los Angeles where sushi hotspots are not hard to find, but rarely does a kid from the San Fernando Valley with the last name Aguilar grow up to run his own sushi counter in one of the most unusual places.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Yuccas met the chef and his mentor to see how "Sushi Note Omakase" is grooving to its own beat in more ways than one.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: While the address for "Sushi Note Omakase" reads the extravagant Rodeo Drive, you have to go underground just past valet to find it.\par \par \uc2 EARL AGUILAR (Culinary director): We`re in a parking garage.\par \par \uc2 It`s a super small space, but I think it definitely works. A lot of people feel like they`re in a very intimate spot. First time head chef and native Angeleno, Earl Aguilar runs the joint with his mentor, Japanese chef, Kiminobu Saito.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: How many people can be seated in here?\par \par \uc2 KIMINOBU SAITO (Chef): There are 14.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: 14?\par \par \uc2 KIMINOBU SAITO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s it?\par \par \uc2 KIMINOBU SAITO: Yes. I like small. I can say every customer is, you know.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Aguilar began his career at age 18 and was soon working with high-end seafood.\par \par \uc2 EARL AGUILAR: I always loved cooking. I didn`t know I was going to be a sushi chef, but I knew that I was going to be in this industry.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: But after a meal at Saito`s original Sushi Note location, inspiration struck.\par \par \uc2 EARL AGUILAR: I was maybe about three quarters in, and then I had probably my favorite piece on the menu was Kohada, and I knew that this is the spot I wanted to work at, and I asked him if he was hiring.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: So you ate and you walked up and you said, "I want a job with you."\par \par \uc2 EARL AGUILAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That`s gutsy.\par \par \uc2 KIMINOBU SAITO: Oh, yes. That`s all. He`s a good boy.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: As a jazz fan in his native Japan, Saito first came to Los Angeles seeking out the city`s music scene. As a restaurateur, he pays tribute to this love with the musically inspired name, Sushi Note.\par \par \uc2 KIMINOBU SAITO: I saw Miles Davis, I was 19 years old in Japan. He`s amazing. I love modern jazz, and Miles making fusion, it`s more fun.\par \par \uc2 EARL AGUILAR: When I started making my menus, it`s really important that there`s a beginning, middle, and end, like a good jazz song. Sometimes he plays jazz, sometimes I play "A Tribe Called Quest" and that`s kind of a cool combination.\