ople.\par \par \uc2 MAGIC JOHNSON: Aw, you don`t have to cry. You don`t have to cry. Because we are normal people, okay? We are.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Magic Johnson posted on social media, "Thanks to Hydeia, millions were educated, stigmas were broken, and attitudes about HIV and AIDS were changed."\par \par \uc2 Broadbent also spoke at the 1996 Republican National Convention when she was only 12 years old. And she was featured in this very moving episode of the \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  Show.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Republican National Convention 1996)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Oprah Winfrey Show" 1996)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hydea Broadbent was not expected to live past five. She died at 39. No word on the cause of her death.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember this little girl. I`m so glad you`re reminding us about her. Magic Johnson was so tender with her in that moment. At the time, people were so freaked out by AIDS. No one wanted to talk about it. You could see her face was a little puffy from the medication. She was an amazing little girl. I was sorry to hear that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it`s just, you go back to that time when no one was talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And here`s this little girl.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And of course, Magic was also HIV positive by that time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And bringing awareness to this issue and the fact that she did that for the rest of her life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She died so young.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Let`s try to lighten it up a little bit. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll go. My "Talk of the Table" it`s about a building in Houston. They may have found a trick to get peopl