ience laughs)\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: that when the session is over, you`re not allowed to be like, okay, bye-bye, I love you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, bye-bye, I love you. Jenny Slate is back in the house. Jenny, it`s so good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Thanks for having me back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You hit on so many notes that are very relatable. I love what you were talking when you took us to the birth of your daughter, Ida, and you were very sharing in that moment.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you also say, "Can you imagine if men had to deliver babies through their penises?"\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The story that we`d be telling like, "Hey, did you hear the story about Sam?" But it`s hilarious the way you do it.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Thank you. I`m glad. It`s definitely meant to be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Mission accomplished.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Yes, but it`s also really good to be able to even within comedy just like gently put down some of your observations that might not be like that you thought of first, you know, like going into a comedy set like, "Am I really going to do this, my entire birth story?" But it turned out that I really enjoyed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it`s very funny. And you also, the title, I like "Seasoned Professional." You said you went to a hypnotist.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You really did.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the hypnotist said you were a seasoned professional.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that mean to you? What did that mean to her telling you that?\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: I think that what it meant, and by the way, that`s like the only thing that really got implanted in my psyche. Everything else, because I went for stage fright.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say, why did you go? Yes.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: I went for stage fright. I, unfortunately, I won, I guess, or whatever. Like I pushed through the hypnosis and continued on stage fright. But he implanted this one thing that`s like, "You know how to do this. You know how to do your job." And before I go on stage, I find myself saying, "I`m a seasoned professional." Even though I still have self-doubt and all of that, this is my version of what it means for me to be a full-fledged professional.\par \par \uc2 And I`m happy that it`s this complex. I`m like, I`m pretty proud of it at this point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s hard to imagine you having stage fright.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re so commanding on stage. And you work through so many different registers and voices. And it`s really nimble. I mean, it`s very funny also. I love the story about wanting to confess love to your now husband when you`re just starting to date and knowing it`s going to be too much.\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: Totally.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chill people don`t really exist. That was me, by the way, in my relationship. The roles were reversed. How does that story develop into a whole bit? I mean, you just start telling it on stage and open mics or you just knew it was funny in the moment.\par \par \uc2 Like, what`s your process like?\par \par \uc2 JENNY SLATE: I think first it starts in conversation with my friends. And I`m like, what do I really want them to know about my life and how I`m doing and how can I be honest about it? But you know, when you`re like out to dinner with your friends, you want to be usually entertaining, I think, you know. You have a good personality. And then it`s like, I enjoyed that. This is interesting to me. There`s an edge to it because it`s personal. So that`s kind of like the fuel for me. And then I`ll start to improvise on stage with it.\par \par \uc2 And eventually it becomes more formed, but not so 