Yes, here you go. Here you go, here you go.\par \par \uc2 Yes, and by the way, it does have this divider that you can move so you can have different breads, you can use your pastries take your bread out of the bag take your pastries out of the clam shell, put them in here. It`s going to extend the life --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it. I used to, like when I get a loaf of bread, I`m rushing to eat it all before it goes bad.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Exactly $19.99, 33% off and this will pay for itself within a couple of months.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do that too, you end up eating bread even though even though I don`t necessarily want it. I just don`t want it to go bad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, this is it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was just me.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Here`s your answer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So true.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Grab these, take them home, leave them out on the counter. Don`t put them in the fridge.\par \par \uc2 Okay now we were all talking --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Aromatherapy.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: We were talking this week. Gayle, may I put this on your back?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And then we were talking this week about sleep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And our issues with sleep, right? And I heard you all talking about it. Part of what we need to do is we need to relax, we need to have that calm time. This is cozy and this is like a spa, you`re going to put these on do you feel the warmth?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Warm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So you can put these in the microwave.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did you -- oh, the microwave? Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Just a couple of minutes. It`ll stay nice and warm. Yes, it`ll stay nice and warm for a couple of hours. You can also put them in the freezer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Put this in the freezer.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So you can cool them off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You put that on your eyes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You put this on your eyes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Here`s what`s neat, you feel the weight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it`s weighted, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So weight is shown to like help us relax, Tony, this is fabulous like you`re in it to win it. This will help us relax. Inside, what makes it weighted is actually wheat so it`s natural. Flaxseed is inside that is what holds and retains the heat or the cool temperature and then the aroma therapy is all natural. So lavender.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I knew it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love it.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Lavender, rosemary, mints, spearmint.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you see our last story?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, that`s why I was like, you got it, this is all for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You okay, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle with a little lavender.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You got to put -- we`ll put the speaker back on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m just kidding, Mrs. Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 I`m just kidding.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Here you go, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 I got some music on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wherever you go.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So these cozy wraps, they start at just $20.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Put the music on.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Go ahead, go on now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much, always a pleasure --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wait, wait, what do you use the lavender for?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And by the way --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So inside, there`s lavender and all these fun herbs and they give that great aroma.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that lavender love the mint as well. We`re very proud to partner with all these companies to get the deals on your screen to buy anything you saw today or scan the QR code, visit cbsdeals.com be sure to text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to some of our most popular deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are we going to say bye now?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Bye.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, your favorite morning show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240222ek2m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for February 21, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Charlie D`Agata, Carter Evans, Manuel Bojorquez,  Nancy Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Meg Oliver, Roxana Saberi \par \uc2 3,670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK (IVF Patient): I`m angry. I`m sad. I mean, it`s completely just derailed a lot of hope.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, fertility treatment showdown.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We foster the culture of life and protect the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The nationwide implications, as Alabama`s largest hospital system says it`s pausing IVF following a landmark ruling from the state Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Doctors now have to question everything they do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin tonight with shockwaves being felt in the world of reproductive health care following a bombshell IVF ruling by Alabama`s Supreme Court. In Friday`s unprecedented decision, the all-Republican court ruled that frozen embryos are the legal equivalent of children.\par \par \uc2 Now Alabama`s largest hospital is pausing all in vitro fertilization treatments, as health care providers across the state and the country evaluate what this means. The ruling could have a devastating effect for people who desperately want to have children in a state that already has one of the nation`s strictest abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 The decision may also make the already expensive cost of infertility skyrocket because of rising liability costs. To understand the possible impact, look at these numbers from the CDC. Nearly 100,000 babies were born in the U.S. in 2021 using IVF treatments. That`s more than 2 percent of all births.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver speaks tonight to a woman who has spent years and thousands of dollars trying to get pregnant.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK (IVF Patient): I`m angry. I`m sad. I`m just a whole slew of things.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Thirty-four-year-old Jasmine York turned to IVF to get pregnant. But now, her dream of having another child has come to a sudden halt.\par \par \uc2 Is IVF your last hope to have a baby?\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK: Yes. There is no other way.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: This morning, York got a phone call from her fertility doctor at the University of Alabama with the news. The hospital system is pausing all IVF treatments while it takes a closer look at the state Supreme Court`s ruling that frozen embryos are considered people.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to CBS News, the University of Alabama says it is saddened: "But we must evaluate the potential that our patients and our physicians could be prosecuted criminally or face punitive damages for following the standard of care for IVF treatments."\par \par \uc2 DR. SERENA CHEN (Founder, Doctors for Fertility): It is disturbing and ironic that the judgment in this case talks about trying to promote families and help people, and yet they are doing exactly the opposite.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The Alabama court`s ruling could also have a chilling effect nationwide.\par \par \uc2 DR. SERENA CHEN: How can we help our patients if we are going to be threatened with jail time or fines or crimes for just doing our best to take care of our patients?\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Like Alabama Fertility doctor Mamie McLean.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAMIE MCLEAN (Alabama Fertility): Ultimately, we`re worried that there`s going to be fewer babies born in Alabama because of this ruling.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: But, tonight, Alabama IVF patients are left in limbo.\par \par \uc2 How scared are you for the future right now?\par \par \uc2 JASMINE YORK: I am scared, but I do have hope still that this can get turned around and things can be made right.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Tonight, the University of Alabama told us it doesn`t know how long IVF is on hold, leaving many women wondering if they will be able to have a baby -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So many people scared about this.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, fallout on Capitol Hill after a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant was charged with lying about President Biden and his son Hunter`s ties to a Ukrainian energy company. House Republicans have used the now- indicted informant as one of their main sources in the investigations of the president and his family.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports from Capitol Hill that the president`s brother, James Biden testified behind closed doors today as part of the Republicans` impeachment probe.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): The president`s younger brother James arrived ahead of his interview with congressional investigators as Republicans tried to salvage their impeachment inquiry into President Biden.\par \par \uc2 You got any thoughts on this?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ANDY BIGGS (R-Arizona): No, no, because -- because there`s other corroborating evidence with other -- other links to -- to the president.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: James Biden told the panel the president had no role in his business ventures, saying: "I never asked my brother to take any official action on behalf of me or anyone else."\par \par \uc2 But even before the interview began, the Republican probe suffered a major blow. Longtime FBI informant Alexander Smirnov, seen here covered in a scarf, hat and sunglasses, has been charged with providing false information about President Biden and his son Hunter, some of it allegedly coming from Russian intelligence officials.\par \par \uc2 Republicans had championed the evidence he presented.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 12, 2023)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Even a trusted \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant has alleged a bribe to the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: That claim from Smirnov that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his father sought from \uc2 Burisma\uc2 , a Ukrainian energy firm, $5 million in bribes, prosecutors now say it was a lie.\par \par \uc2 Republicans today struggled to answer questions about Smirnov`s indictment.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JIM JORDAN (R-Ohio): All I`m saying is, you got to ask the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about that. He -- he may in fact have given a false statement. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Prosecutors argue, Smirnov, a dual U.S.-Israeli citizen with more than $6 million in his bank account, is a flight risk who`s actively peddling new lies that could impact the election, given that he had contact with Russian intelligence officials this past November.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say the Republicans` impeachment probe is forever tarnished.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE DANIEL GOLDMAN (D-New York): Any further investigation of this impeachment inquiry is now a witting accomplice to Russian intelligence.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The same congressional investigators question \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  next Wednesday about his business dealings.\par \par \uc2 As for Alexander Smirnov, he`s not yet entered a plea in his case, but, Norah, a judge has released him under restrictions pending trial.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane on the Hill for us tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is slamming GOP front-runner \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for refusing to criticize Russia`s \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  following the death of \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2 . The former president instead compared Navalny`s death to his own legal troubles.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes is traveling with the president in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Why does Trump always blame America?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): A suspicious death in Russia is now a U.S. election issue, after former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  equated last week`s demise of a famed opposition leader to his recent fine for business fraud.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): A form of Navalny. It is a form of communism or fascism.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: At a town hall in South Carolina, Trump called Alexey Navalny`s death in an Arctic penal colony a -- quote -- "sad situation," but declined once again to blame Russia`s president.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Putin is responsible for Navalny`s death. Why can`t Trump just say that? Putin`s responsible.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Biden administration is finalizing what it describes as major sanctions to punish Putin.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Treasury Department`s top sanctions official met with allies in Europe to coordinate on the new economic measures that are timed to mark the two-year anniversary of Russia`s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2  (State Department Spokesman): We are always looking at -- at additional ways that we can choke off the Russian war machine, that we can deny the Russian military industrial complex components that it needs to use to fund its war effort.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And yet that war effort rages on. The White House is intensifying its attacks on House Republicans for failing to approve another aid package for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The embattled nation is running low on ammunition, forcing it to withdraw this week from a strategic city on the front lines.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Between that setback and Navalny`s death, pressure is mounting on Republican House Speaker Mike Johnson to buck a vocal minority in his party and bring the Ukraine aid up for a vote as soon as Congress returns from a two-week recess -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to a tragic story out of Florida. A 7-year-old girl has died after a hole she was digging on a beach north of Miami collapsed on top of her and her 9-year-old brother.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez has the heartbreaking details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 911 CALLER: There is a little girl buried under the sand, and they have not gotten to her yet.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Digging with their bare hands, bystanders rush to try to rescue a little girl buried after the hole in the sand she was playing in collapsed.\par \par \uc2 Christina Bleckinger was there and tried to help.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BLECKINGER (Tourist): It was horrifying. My husband grabbed a bucket and started helping. The boy, you can see his head. And even that took a long time to get out. He was, like, stuck under the sand, and you could see him. The girl, you -- we couldn`t see.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The child, identified as 7-year-old Sloan Mattingly, was playing with her 9-year-old brother on Tuesday at the beach in Lauderdale- by-the-Sea. Her family was visiting from Indiana.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BLECKINGER: They were just together as a family digging a hole to have fun in.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The children were rushed to the hospital, where the girl was pronounced dead.\par \par \uc2 I do get the sense that this is the kind of thing that literally in a second, it can happen.\par \par \uc2 TOM GILL (Vice President, \uc2 U.S. Lifesaving Association\uc2 ): Sand, again, is not going to stay in a stable manner at any time. It`s constantly moving, just -- just -- really just like the ocean is. But when this falls in on you, there`s no escaping it. It`s very similar to an avalanche on the ski slopes, where you`re -- you`re not going to be able to escape this once you`re in there.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Experts warn beachgoers not to dig deep holes in the sand. But, if you do, fill it before leaving, and do not dig a hole deeper than your knees.\par \par \uc2 TOM GILL: This is one -- one bit of danger that you don`t need to put yourself in.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Though rare, these accidents do happen. In 2022, there were two similar deaths in other states in just one month.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the 9-year-old boy who survived this accident is now out of the hospital -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there are growing concerns that Vladimir Putin`s invasion of Ukraine launched two years ago could spill over into \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata reports tonight from Northeast Poland, where there are now more than 10,000 U.S. troops stationed near the Ukrainian border.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): U.S. live-fire exercises in Poland today near Russian territory. An adversary has crossed onto this strip of \uc2 NATO\uc2 - controlled soil.\par \par \uc2 U.S. soldiers from the 3rd Infantry Division in Georgia are the first line of defense.\par \par \uc2 This exercise isn`t just about making sure that individual soldiers are ready for combat, but the military itself is ready in case there`s an incursion from a regional enemy.\par \par \uc2 And while no one here will name that regional enemy, there`s a good reason they`re war-gaming an invasion right here and right now.\par \par \uc2 To military planners, the Suwalki Gap is NATO`s Achilles` heel. Just 40 miles separates the Russian enclave of Kaliningrad and Putin-allied Belarus. If the Kremlin launched an incursion here, it would cut off the Baltic states from other \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries. But this training could be in jeopardy, with funding for Ukraine stalled in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Critical U.S. Army exercises for both American and Ukrainian forces, including supplying those forces, is at risk of screeching to a halt.\par \par \uc2 Spokesman for the U.S. Army Africa and Europe Colonel Martin O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 COL. MARTIN O`DONNELL (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): We are running out of money. We have got enough funds right now. If nothing -- if nothing changes, we expect to run out of money before summertime, if nothing changes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Before summertime?\par \par \uc2 COL. MARTIN O`DONNELL: Before summertime.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And what happens then?\par \par \uc2 COL. MARTIN O`DONNELL: Don`t really want to think about it, both in terms of, you know, what we do on the two continents and both in terms of support for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It`s the first time a senior \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  official has gone on camera about the urgency of that Ukrainian funding package, Norah, telling us, the lack of funds not only threatens critical supplies to Ukraine, but overall \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  operations here in Europe.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Very important reporting.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, more than 150,000 borrowers had their student loans canceled today. President Biden said this afternoon he`s forgiving some $1.2 billion of debt for those currently enrolled in a new repayment program. It comes as the annual tuition at a private nonprofit four-year college or university tops $41,000. And that is up 4 percent from just one year ago.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans takes an in-depth look at the high price of a college education.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TALIA DAY (College Student): I knew that college was going to be quite expensive no matter where I went.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Freshman Talia Day (sp?) is among 1,700 students currently enrolled at Pomona College, a prestigious school with a hefty price tag.\par \par \uc2 Gabrielle Starr is Pomona`s president.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE STARR (President, Pomona College): Every time we raise tuition, it doesn`t feel good.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Between 1980 and 2023, the average price of college tuition, fees and room and board in the U.S. skyrocketed 155 percent.\par \par \uc2 What would you say is the single biggest expense?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE STARR: Single biggest expense is people.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Many colleges and universities now operate like small cities, and critics say that`s led to administrative bloat. There are now three times as many administrators and staffers as there are teaching faculty at leading schools.\par \par \uc2 Demand for degrees is also driving up costs.\par \par \uc2 BETH AKERS (Economist, \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 ): I feel like young people have gotten the sense that, in order to be a part of the American dream today, you have to have your bachelor`s degree diploma hanging on the wall.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Economist Beth Akers says easy access to loans compounds the problem.\par \par \uc2 BETH AKERS: We can`t just be telling people a bachelor`s degree at any cost is the golden ticket. And so people are signing on the dotted line basically at whatever price it takes to get them in and through.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So what`s the solution here?\par \par \uc2 BETH AKERS: If we get students and their parents to think about what am I paying here versus what am I getting, then we really force institutions to, you know, check themselves.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But even at the same school, the actual cost can be different for each student.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE STARR: We give out a lot of financial aid.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: At Pomona, 58 percent of students get some sort of aid, bringing their tuition closer to $16,000 a year. But many still rely on loans that will take years to pay back.\par \par \uc2 Is it worth it?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE STARR: All of the studies show that, if you graduate from college, it`s worth it. It`s worth it in terms of the salary that you earn. And it`s worth it in terms of the other opportunities that it opens to you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Carter Evans, CBS News, Claremont, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A bomb threat forces a \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  jet to make an emergency landing. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating a bomb threat aboard a \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  jet.\par \par \uc2 A flight from Newark had to make an emergency landing in Chicago this morning after a fake bomb note was found in a bathroom. Passengers had to be temporarily evacuated. And that came one day after an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight to Chicago was forced to turn back to Albuquerque after a man had to be subdued by passengers after he allegedly tried to open the plane door mid-flight.\par \par \uc2 Several days of heavy rain triggered landslides today in Southern California. A stretch of the Pacific Coast Highway was closed in both directions, along with several other roads in the Malibu area. Beverly Hills ended up with four inches of rain in five days, while more than eight inches fell in the Santa Monica Mountains.\par \par \uc2 Despite recent price cuts, many Americans are still struggling to pay for lifesaving insulin. Our Health Watch report is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: An estimated 8.4 million Americans rely on insulin to treat diabetes.\par \par \uc2 For seniors on Medicare, that cost is capped at $35 a month. But for everyone else, it can be far more expensive.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Health Watch, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Roxana Saberi has one mother`s story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TARA GRAY (Mother): So your blood sugar is 192.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI (voiceover): Fourteen-year-old Cassie Gray says she doesn`t want diabetes to define her, but it can take a toll.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE GRAY (Patient): I feel defeated sometimes, like it takes over my life.\par \par \uc2 TARA GRAY: This is her insulin pen.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: For her mother, Tara, it brings an added worry.\par \par \uc2 What is it like for you financially to pay for these treatments?\par \par \uc2 TARA GRAY: We try to hide some of our stress from our daughter, because she already has enough on her plate.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Insulin prices recently decreased, as Medicare, some states and drug manufacturers moved to cap monthly costs. But not all patients qualify.\par \par \uc2 LARRY LEVITT (Executive Vice President For Health Policy, KFF): Exactly how much help you will get could depend on the drug itself, on the -- your insurance, how big your deductible is and, in some cases, even on your income.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Tara says she was shocked when her private insurance company sent her this letter in 2022, stating that one form of insulin Cassie needs was no longer covered.\par \par \uc2 TARA GRAY: It was not medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Not medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 TARA GRAY: That`s what the letter said. I think I just sat there, like, not medically necessary? This is her air.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But families on the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group that she runs for diabetes patients gave her a tip. She could download a coupon to lower the monthly price from $600 to $99.\par \par \uc2 TARA GRAY: My worry for the future of coupons would be that they go away.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The three top insulin manufacturers told CBS News they have several programs to lower the cost of the drug. \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  says it supports bipartisan federal legislation capping prices at $35 for everyone. So does Tara.\par \par \uc2 TARA GRAY: Every insulin should have a cap.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Then she`d know Cassie could always afford the lifesaving medicine she will need.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi, CBS News, Mechanicsville, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Heart of America is next with a heartwarming story about an incredible family reunion months after the devastating Maui wildfires.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet Mahina. She was missing for months after wildfires ravaged her family`s home in Hawaii last year.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Maui Humane Society)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Her owners eventually gave up hope of finding her after they moved thousands of miles away to Montana. The 3-year-old cat survived in the burn zone for 100 days before she was rescued by the heroes at the Maui Humane Society.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Mahina was reunited with her family. The nonprofit covered the cost to fly her all the way to the mainland. And her owner had this message for Mahina`s rescuers:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you so much, not even just for my kitty, but for all the other animals out there that they have rescued from this fire, just reuniting families and bringing hope. It`s a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: As the Maui Humane Society puts it, it takes a village.\par \par \uc2 And that story of community is tonight`s Heart of America. I love this story, meow and forever.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240222ek2l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump Wins New Hampshire; Biden`s Re-Election Focus; US Retaliation in the Middle East; Russian Military Plane Crash; Southern Flooding Threat; Driveway Shooting Conviction; \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Plane Loses Wheel on Runway; "Barbie" Backlash; Sen. Tim Scott Live; Super Bowl Soulful Celebration; Music Icon Dolly Parton; Remembering Charles Osgood; Cam Newton and Brandon Marshal; State of the Race; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS "Deals"; Country Legend Dolly Parton; Tony and Nate`s Cupcake Contest; Talk of the Table; Dinner Bill Debate; Bills Fans Are Feline Generous; "I Am Iconic"; "Madness: Race and Insanity", \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Caitlin Huey-Burn,  Robert Costa, Nancy Cordes, Charlie D`Agata, Stephanie Abrams, Vladimir  Duthiers, Terry Sullivan, Jane Pauley \par \uc2 14,849 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is back. All is right with the world. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins the New Hampshire primary, but \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  vows to keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) (2024 presidential candidate): A great night here tonight. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Who the hell was the imposter that like claimed a victory, she did very poorly actually.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Biden hits the campaign trail in Virginia, but he is repeatedly interrupted by pro-Palestinian demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): This is going to go on for a while. They got this planned.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Legendary \uc2 CBS\uc2  anchor, Charles Osgood has died. We`re going to look back at his remarkable career.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: And I`ve got to be drifting along.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Millions of people under flood and winter weather alert, all that heavy rain is very slowly shifting eastward.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Despite a record of 30 and 13 and currently in second place in the east, the \uc2 Milwaukee Bucks\uc2  have fired first year head coach, Adrian Griffin.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They have the third best record overall, and you go to this guy and they go, you`re have fired. Really?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON ("After Midnight" host): Oscar nominations were announced today and the story everyone is talking about is the monumental snub of "Barbie" director, Greta Gerwig and "Barbie" herself, Margot Robbie.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Margot Robbie and Greta Gerwig were not nominated for acting or directing, whereas Ryan Gosling was. He got a nomination for playing Ken, which ironically was kind of the plot of the "Barbie" movie.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jimmy Kimmel is right. A lot of dog head movement on that like "huh?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ayi. That did make a lot of sense.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have a say later the show about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s actually right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin though with the battle for the GOP presidential nomination. It`s getting more personal after the New Hampshire primary.\par \par \uc2 Now CBS News projects a win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in yesterday`s contest against former \uc2 UN\uc2  ambassador, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . But in her speech last night, Haley said she is not dropping out of this race; instead, she is vowing to fight on and she is sharpening her attacks on the former president.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And speaking of attacks, former President Trump gave a victory speech that at times felt angry to some and included some threats against -- threats of investigations veiled and not so veiled in many people`s view if \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  does not drop out of this race and soon.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got team political coverage today, including how the Biden campaign is responding, but we`re going to begin with Caitlin Huey-Burns in New Hampshire, where I was just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hey, Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, you got out just before the snow came down here in New Hampshire, it had held off yesterday, which led to record turnout and Independents showed up big, too. But still, it wasn`t enough for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  to overtake \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, she is vowing to plow on and this remains at least for now, a two- person race.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "USA.")\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: With New Hampshire, it is back-to-back victories for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as he moves closer to the Republican presidential nomination.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We have to do what`s good for our party.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Trump`s win in the state where nearly half of the voters identify as Independents adds to questions about former Ambassador Nikki Haley`s path to victory, and Trump spent a majority of his speech last night appearing almost angry as he fixated on his only remaining GOP opponent.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : This is not your typical victory speech, but let`s not have somebody take a victory when she had a very bad night. She had a very bad night.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Trump`s success in New Hampshire comes down to the strength of his base.\par \par \uc2 According to CBS News exit polls, 80 percent of Trump`s voters describe their support as strongly for him while only 30 percent of Haley`s voters said they strongly favor her. Most of her backers said they were voting for her with some reservations or because they disliked Trump.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 : New Hampshire is first in the nation, it is not the last in the nation.\par \par \uc2 [Cheering and applause]\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Still Haley is vowing to stay in the race criticizing her 77-year-old rival.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 : With \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you have one bout of chaos after another. This court case, that controversy, this tweet, that senior moment.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: While Trump appeared to forecast a bruising campaign against Haley if she stays in the fight and he leveled baseless accusations.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And just a little note to Nikki, she is not going to win.\par \par \uc2 [Laughing]\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : But if she did, she would be under investigation by those people in 15 minutes, and so would Ron have been, but he decided to get out. He decided to get out.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But Haley says it`s on to her home state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 : Every time I`ve run for office in South Carolina, I`ve beaten the political establishment.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I did hear Nikki say, and now it`s off to South Carolina. Well, I love South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And speaking of South Carolina, that contest doesn`t happen for another month, but if you live there, get prepared for the ad wars.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley`s campaign is spending nearly $4 million on ad buys there and she is headed to South Carolina tonight for a rally in North Charleston -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lots of money being spent. Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to chief election and campaign correspondent, Robert Costa.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 When we were speaking yesterday, you said \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  needed single digits to convince donors to give her the money to stay in this race for the long term. It looks like she`s in double digits behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Is that enough?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 As Caitlin just said, there is a month between now and the South Carolina Republican primary and in politics, a month can be a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 Based on my conversations last night with top Haley allies, they are going to spend the coming days trying to figure out, is the money there? Is the momentum there? Meeting with donors, meeting with campaign officials in these further voting states not only in South Carolina, but on Super Tuesday in early March.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what are you hearing Bob about the Trump`s strategy going forward? He took a lot of personal potshots at her last night.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Oh, Gayle, you are so right.\par \par \uc2 We are now not just in a political campaign, we are in a season of political war. Look for a barrage of attacks on the airwaves, on social media from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and from many members of the Republican Party. They are going to start coming out with more endorsements in the coming days, pressuring Haley to get out of the race and say it`s time to coalesce around Trump.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bob, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is talking about chaos on the Trump side. He`s got all of these legal entanglements, but so far unless I`m missing something, it seems to be helping him, not hurting him. What`s your read on that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The key there is what you just said, Tony, so far. Right now, Trump campaign officials believe all of this on the legal front is helping Trump. It is stoking the grievances of his supporters, it is helping him generate momentum in this Republican race at the same time.\par \par \uc2 What`s notable is behind-the-scenes, many Republicans are telling me they`re concerned about what`s on the horizon. If Trump is the nominee and that he is in court all of the time, even during the Republican Convention, facing a trial on January 6th, classified records, and so many other things, that could be a drag on the entire Republican ticket.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Bob Costa, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know we haven`t talked about it a lot, but there was actually a Democratic primary in New Hampshire yesterday. It wasn`t sanctioned by the National Democratic Party and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wasn`t actually on the ballot, but he easily won it as a write-in candidate over other challengers.\par \par \uc2 Biden`s campaign is already turning its attention to the general election, and Nancy Cordes is at the White House and has more on that.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Nate, yes, they are looking past the primaries. Mr. Biden`s allies say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is knocking out some of his top GOP rivals more quickly than they expected, which means they need to speed up some of their general election plans. That means some staffing moves at the top of the campaign and stepping up their attacks on Trump.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is betting we won`t -- you won`t vote on this issue.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In Northern Virginia, President Biden argued one man is responsible for the full or partial abortion bans that now exist in 21 states.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : It was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his Supreme Court who ripped away the rights and freedoms of women in America. It`ll be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and all of you that are going to restore those rights for the women of America.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Even as he spoke, his campaign was keeping an eye on events unfolding in New Hampshire. Biden campaign officials saying "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has all but locked up the GOP nomination" and labeling Trump`s victory speech, "bitter and confused sounding."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : They use COVID to cheat and they did a lot of other things, too.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Abortion rights will be a major theme for the Biden-Harris campaign, and here is one reason why. Exit polls from New Hampshire last night found that two-thirds of Republican primary voters oppose a national ban on abortion that`s been proposed by some congressional Republicans.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): Extremists are trying to pass a national abortion ban to outlaw --\par \par \uc2 [Booing]\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: To outlaw abortion in every state, but what they need to know is that we will not allow it.\par \par \uc2 [Cheering and applause]\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And yet another issue is likely to dog President Biden throughout the campaign season.\par \par \uc2 (PRO-PALESTINIAN PROTESTERS protesting.)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Four more years.")\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Pro-Palestinian protesters interrupted his speech over and over last night and had to be escorted out.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We`ve got a couple more of these.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Four more years.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We learned last night that President Biden is going to be sending two of his top White House aides to join his campaign sooner than expected to the relief of Biden allies who had been urging such a move for some time.\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign is also signaling that the GOP presidential primary race is over by selling these new t-shirts with the slogan "Together We Will Defeat Trump Again." -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nancy, we shall see. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The growing number of American airstrikes in the Middle East could also become an issue on this campaign trail. US military hit targets in both Iraq and Yemen last night after attacks on American forces.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata reports on the risks of a wider war in the region.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: With each F-18 fighter jet taking off from the USS Eisenhower and yet more overnight bombing raids, America`s involvement in what has now become a regional fight has escalated by the day and night.\par \par \uc2 (Explosion)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The new strikes against both Iranian-backed militias in Iraq and Houthi targets in Yemen follow US-led bombing raids just the night before, conducted alongside British typhoon fighters.\par \par \uc2 Defense officials say the strikes were retaliation for more than 150 attacks directed at US forces throughout the region since the war in Gaza, began endangering the roughly 900 elite US troops in Syria who we have visited in recent years and witnessed fighting alongside local Kurdish forces battling ISIS, to American troops on bases across Iraq where the military maintains a force strength of around 2,500 soldiers.\par \par \uc2 (Explosion)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But it was the massive missile strike on the Al-Asad Airbase in Iraq over the weekend, injuring four US servicemembers that triggered last night`s counterattack. The local Iraqi militias and the Houthi rebels in Yemen, all backed by Iran.\par \par \uc2 On the strikes in Yemen, US officials downplayed any talk of escalation.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK S. RYDER (\uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  press secretary): Well, first of all, I wouldn`t define it as a conflict. Again, I would define it as action that`s being taken to disrupt and degrade Houthi capability to conduct attacks against the international community.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The strikes have not gone down well in Iraq, and these are US partners. The Iraqi National Security adviser calling it a flagrant violation of Iraq`s sovereignty, saying the US should pile pressure on Israel to halt the offensive in Gaza rather than bombing parts of Iraq -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie D`Agata for us in Tel Aviv. Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got breaking news from Russia where a military plane carrying dozens of Ukrainian prisoners crashed this morning killing everyone on board.\par \par \uc2 Amateur video shows the plane going down or a plane going down not far from Ukraine`s northeastern border, and you can see the fireball rising in the distance. The cause of the crash not clear, but Russia`s Defense Ministry says 74 people have died, 65 of them prisoners, three escorts and a six- person crew.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here at home, a new round of storms is causing serious flooding in parts of the south. Up to seven inches of rain fell in Texas yesterday turning this underpass into a lake. The rising water flooded many homes and businesses in the eastern part of the state.\par \par \uc2 For more on today`s flood threats, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning. How much more rain is the south likely to get?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, we could get another one to two months` worth. We have the threat for flash flooding yet again today; tornadoes, damaging winds, all along the lower Mississippi River Valley.\par \par \uc2 The heaviest rain will be in the south, but the system stretches into the Midwest and to the northeast. Tomorrow, many of the same areas will be wet and we could see more strong thunderstorms rumbling across the Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 Through the end of the week, five to eight inches could accumulate in the south, one to two inches in the northeast, and it is mainly liquid precip, thanks to our highs being 10 to 20 degrees above average today into tomorrow. Highs will get into the 50s all the way into New England.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s a lot of weather happening in the West, too. By the end of the week, multiple storms will crank out three to five inches of rain and feet of snow and there`s no stopping it into next week. We will see moisture continue to pour ashore and the extended outlook shows it is very likely that precip in the west will be above average.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Nate, all of this weather should put a dent in the drought that we have in the northwest and in the south.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, well, I guess that`s good news.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A man in New York who shot and killed a 20-year-old woman who was in a car that took a wrong turn was convicted yesterday of second- degree murder. The jury needed less than an hour to find Kevin Monahan guilty of the murder of Kaylin Gillis. She was a passenger in her boyfriend`s SUV when they pulled into a driveway looking for a party.\par \par \uc2 Monahan came out with a shotgun, fired it, hitting Gillis in the neck. He testified that the fatal shot was accidental. Monahan faces 25 years to life in prison and prosecutors plan to ask for the maximum sentence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another bump in the road for \uc2 Boe