e saw it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Dolly, we wanted to do something fun for you today. So we`re pitting former athletes, Tony Dokoupil and Nate Burleson, against each other in a very different sort of competition. They will have the length of this interview to decorate some of those Duncan Hines cupcakes to the best of their abilities. And at the end, we`re going to ask you to crown a winner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, using your product, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Using your product. So there they are. They`re going to be decorating those cupcakes. Are the men in your life good bakers?\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON: Some of them are. Yes, I have a few good friends that are pretty good cooks and bake pretty well. So I think everybody likes to cook something sweet. I think everybody has a big sweet tooth for the most part. So we learn how to do it, whether it`s pretty or not. So I will judge those cakes. I hate to judge anything, but I`m going to make an exception in this case.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You turned 78, Dolly. Look at you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You look so good. Did you bake your own birthday cake or how were you treated? Yes, I see you. I see you.\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON: Well, to be honest, though, I did. But I used some of my own cake mixes because I was actually working on my birthday. My sister Rachel, she cooked my birthday dinner, but she said, I`m going to let you do your own cake. And so that`s what I did. So I did one of my -- one of my famous cakes. I actually went back and did one of the coconut cakes that we actually started the line with.\par \par \uc2 We started this a couple of years ago and it did so well. I thought, well, we`re just going to keep expanding. And now we`re here. And I actually am very excited because I`ve made a, you know, a partnership deal with Conagra, who actually they own the "Duncan Hines" and so many other things. And later on, I`m going to have a whole lot of things like frozen foods and other things besides just the baking.\par \par \uc2 So you`ll have some good food to eat before you eat your dessert.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON: So, but yes, I had a good birthday. And if you can -- go ahead --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to try your pancakes. I want to try your pancake.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s check in on Nate and Tony real quick to see how they`re doing. Yes, Tony`s got a lot of sampling. It doesn`t look like they`re decorating.\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON: So that`s what I do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly. We all do that when it comes to good baking. Let me ask you about this rock album --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because you perform with some of the most incredible musicians in the history of popular music. And you`re in that studio with them. You`re with Steve Perry. You`re with Ringo and \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2  recording a version of "Let It Be." What was that like?\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON: Well, I`ve known Paul for quite a while. I actually was not in the studio with him. He did that from his own studio because he had just come off of a tour. And so he was trying to get it all done. But I was in the studio with many of them. And I made a great connection with all the artists that I sang with.\par \par \uc2 And it was a thrill for me because these are people that I`ve admired for so many years. And when I started into my rock album, I was singing all these great songs, never had a thought about asking anybody to join me. And about halfway through it, I was hearing these great songs, and I was just hearing their voices in my own heart and head. And I thought, "Wow, wouldn`t it be great if I could get a few of these people to join me?"\par \par \uc2 And so many of them did. I think we got about 20, 24, 25 of the artists that actually were singing on some of their original hits. So it was a great honor and a great thrill. And I just really felt blessed to have them on the album.\par \par \uc2 It`s doing really well, and I`m very proud of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it`s doing so well that on your birthday, you gave us a gift of extra songs, which I thought was very nice. Thank you. I love your album. Word on the street about you, Dolly Parton, is that you like surprises. Do you have a surprise for the audience today? Word on the street is you might.\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON: Do I have a surprise today? I`m not really sure that I have a surprise other than to say that I`m just happy to be here and happy to be with all of you. I think my big surprise is just the fact that I`m doing all of these things. What -- what did you hear that surprise was?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, no, Dolly, we`re always glad to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, we`re always glad to see -- we`re looking at Tony and Nate. We got like a few seconds. What do you think? How`d they do?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dallas Cowboy theme here, Dolly.\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON: Oh, wow. I think they`re the ones with the surprise, maybe that`s what they meant. They were going to surprise me by making me judge. Let me check you out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at those hearts. I love that. Dolly, we love you. You`re an American institution.\par \par \uc2 You`re getting the big hearts boys. Thank you so much, Dolly. Dolly`s latest "Duncan Hines" products are arriving in stores right now. Dolly, we love you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You didn`t use this.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are remembering longtime colleague, broadcaster Charles Osgood. For decades, he was the host of "CBS Sunday Morning" and the iconic voice behind the \uc2 CBS\uc2  radio show, "The Osgood Files." He died yesterday at 91. Sunday Morning`s Jane Pauley remembered his remarkable career.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD (Longtime Sunday Morning host): Good morning. I`m Charles Osgood and this is Sunday Morning.\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: He was the admired, yet approachable host of Sunday Morning for more than 22 years.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: The Osgood File. This is Charles Osgood.\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: And that reassuring voice of \uc2 CBS\uc2  radio`s "The Osgood File" for nearly 46. Charles Osgood knew his was a storied life. Born Charles Osgood Wood in the Bronx, he grew up in Baltimore. He remembered it this way.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: In 1942, milk was delivered in bottles. The mail was delivered twice a day. And that boy named Charlie Wood had a paper route.\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: In the 1950s, he served three years as the Army Band`s announcer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Charles Osgood singing "White Christmas")\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: He had a love of music.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: You are only as old as you feel. Want to see what Lucille is now able to do?\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: Playful poetry.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: These are one-inch pine boards, not just one board, but two.\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: And especially time spent chewing the fat with the likes of Julia Child.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: Did you imagine that you were a natural for television when you first started doing that show?\par \par \uc2 JULIA CHILD (American chef and author): I`m a natural ham and it helps a lot.\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: And many others.\par \par \uc2 KEITH HARING (American artist): They come out fast, but I mean, it`s a fast world.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: We have actors and artists, not just politicians.\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: His beloved Sunday morning was the beneficiary of his passion.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: Our Sunday Mornings are filled with such things.\par \par \uc2 I cannot think of anything that has given me more pleasure professionally than Sunday Morning. Because, first of all, it feels great to be part of something that people love, and I know that they do.\par \par \uc2 I have sung this song, but I will sing it again. I knew I`d be leaving but I didn`t know when.\par \par \uc2 JANE PAULEY: He is survived by Jean, his wife of 50 years, their five children and six grandchildren, and all of us at CBS News.\par \par \uc2 Charles Osgood was 91 years old.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES OSGOOD: And I`ve got to be drifting along.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The "Sunday Morning" of Charles Osgood is the show I fell in love with. It`s the reason I`m here, and I`m far from the only one. He will very much be missed. This Sunday, Sunday morning, we`ll honor the life and career of Charles Osgood with a 90-minute broadcast, 9 am eastern. Tune in.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. And Tony, you`re going to kick it off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I`m going to kick it off. I want to bring the full moral force of our thinking to a key question that a lot of people face.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A woman posted a TikTok video, it`s gone viral, where she said she only had about $50 in her account. She went to a group birthday dinner to celebrate her friend.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Aryan Strauss`s TikTok account)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So she stayed roughly within her own budget and expected to get an individual check. But instead, was on the hook for $150, which she didn`t have as people exited early and didn`t pay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how much had she eaten? How much, what would she say her bill was?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She would say, and there`s some complications here, but she would say she only ate $25 worth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So obviously the dine and dashers, we don`t like them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They eat the dine and dash. I understand why the birthday girl was irritated to have to pick up some more of the check because your friend didn`t have it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But what do we think about the friend going out so short of money? Should she have not gone out at all? Or is it understandable that she would want to participate and other people should step up?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think you should still participate, but you have to be honest. You have to be honest at the table.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think you have to settle that conversation before the bill comes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it is bad when you`re at a group dinner and you`ve only eaten $25 and the bill comes to $150. Then you think, but I don`t have $150.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think if you settle it ahead of time. But I hate that moment at the table. How much did you have? How much did you have?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That awkward moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, why can`t we just agree ahead of time? This is what we`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about you? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I think I agree with this fellow here, MDHJoe (sp?). This has got millions of views, by the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll bet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He said separate checks, always bring your own car. That`s the key to life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Separate checks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But even separate checks can get a little messy too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Separate checks are hard with 10 people. Yes. Separate checks are hard with 10 people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s the best way to handle it though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s the only way to handle it. And don`t share a ride with anybody. When you want to go, you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do agree with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sneak out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  fan showing love, big love for kicker Tyler Bass. We all know Tyler Bass. He missed that crucial field goal on Sunday as the Bills lost to the \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2 . He fell out of the playoff. So Bass received, I hate this, widespread hate online.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People were vicious, people were mean, threats. It makes no sense to me. But others are showing their support by donating to an organization that`s very close to his heart. So it turns out Bass is an ambassador for the 10LivesClub. It`s a cat rescue shelter in Buffalo. The organization posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , "Tyler does not deserve any of the hate he`s receiving." And they noted his work helping rescue cats last year. The club says it received more than $25,000 in donations in Bass`s name on Monday. But this morning we`re happy to tell you the total is now more than $100,000.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The club says many of the individual donations have been $22. That`s a nod to the number two on his jersey. I thought it was so mean, Nate, and so hateful that people would be so unkind to him. You know he wanted to make it, it didn`t work out. But for him to get the threats and just people just being vicious to him, I thought was wrong.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s awful, you`re right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fans are so reactive, he missed the kick.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But here`s the thing, if you`re a real fan and you know the game, you know that the Bills had plenty opportunities for four quarters. So why blame the kicker when your team could have did that job earlier in the game to not be in that position. So shout out to Tyler Bass.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so we`ll stick with football for my "Talk of the Table"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My talk of the table is two former NFL greats, and I mean that, who were among the most dominant players in the league. Quarterback Cam Newton was selected first overall by the \uc2 Carolina Panthers\uc2  in 2011. And in 2015, he won league MVP among plenty of other accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 Brandon Marshall was a wide receiver who played 13 seasons. He was named to six Pro Bowls and was dominant every single year. Now the pair have teamed up off the field for their "I Am Iconic" Tour. It`s a live show in front of an audience that includes candid conversations about sports, mental health, and current events. Brandon and Cam, they`re joining us now. What`s up y`all?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL (Former NFL Player): Hold on, hold on, hold on. Who`s your X, who`s your Z? Got Nate Burleson, wide receiver.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON (Former NFL Player): X, Nate`s a F.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, F.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m in the slot, torching the Nickelbacks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know what you guys are talking about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is this football talk?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes, yes, basically where you going to place us?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Cam, you said when you saw Nate on the field in Detroit, you didn`t think what?\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: I didn`t think he had the star power that he has.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t?\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Yes, just to see him morph into a more sports vertical, you know, the NFL networks, the \uc2 CBS\uc2  sports.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: And then all of a sudden, now you see him on a more broader scale and he`s holding his own.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is holding his own.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: He`s our hero.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As we know, it`s all about opportunities with us.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re so much more than athletes. Brandon I want to start with you so you said it took two years for you to convince cam to do this tour with you. So explain to us what is "I Am Iconic"?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I want to know what your pitch was to him to finally get him to do it.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: Cam, five years from now We`re going to be on Broadway, and they`re going to say "I Am Iconic." We`re going to win a Tony This has never been done before, Cam Newton, we`re going to bring a live show your show and also elements of Broadway together. We`re going to take some of our biggest moments in sports, our host and our guests biggest moments in their lives and bring it into a scripted almost play type environment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what you said?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: No I ain`t say that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Make it make sense. So how did it make sense to you to finally connect and do this for real, Cam?\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Well, I witnessed his "I Am Athlete" tour in Atlanta and one thing about Brandon. He`s very persistent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Almost like, it`s like that gnat at the barbecue, you know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you mean annoying, Cam? Annoying.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: You know, he`s persistent. Same thing, it`s a fine line. And he kept asking me that, he`s like "Yo, bro. It`s something that you would be inspired from." I Witnessed it and I said to myself. It was like yo, I have to be a part of you know a live show. This is probably the closest thing and I ask you, Nate, like what`s the closest thing being away from the game that gives you that same kind of --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Rush.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Feeling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Being on stage was like you know the improvisation that you have to have when somebody just blurts out something rather than somebody saying "hey, I love you."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Or "Hey boo."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s like a two-minute drill in football.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Right, real talk. You have to be equipped with the proper tools.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: You know kind of different than an actual show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: But it when it`s live in front of people,you know you still have to have that same --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you guys say one of the goals is to show that athletes have more in common with the public in general. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: I think we`ve already answered that I think over the last five years, six years, I mean it started with a lot of us, but LeBron, big moment, more than an athlete and now to come you have guys being themselves wearing our hair. Nate, you`re on \uc2 CBS\uc2  when you`re here different styles, right?\par \par \uc2 So I think we`ve already answered that and people just want more of it. And so seeing these shows that we have "Funky Friday" "Fourth and One" (sp?) "I Am Athlete."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Funky Friday."\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: Yes, "Paper Route" those type of shows, you`re seeing us in our truest form for sometimes the first time for some of these athletes.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Oh, yes, for sure. I think and I said and we`re living in the golden age of athlete generated content, and I was saying behind the scenes it was like we needed the \uc2 CBS\uc2  special interview just to be able to say it really didn`t happen that way, nowadays we just got X or \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or \uc2 Instagram\uc2  or you know \uc2 YouTube\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Go straight to the source.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it hard to be vulnerable guys and open up on stage the way you do?\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: It`s hard to unteach yourself that being authentic is okay. And I mean there`s so much you have to protect being a player. You can`t just say what you really want to say because it`s a brand deal. It`s the franchise. It`s the fan base. It`s team continuity, but now --\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: I never worried about that. That`s why I was always in trouble.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s bring some of that authenticity into some NFL analysis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We got four games left Yes, 50 million plus people watch the last playoff game on \uc2 CBS\uc2  on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Four teams left two games.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Four teams left, two games. Who do you like to win the Super Bowl on \uc2 CBS\uc2  by the way this year?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To win or go to the Super Bowl.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can take it either way.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: Baltimore Lamar Jackson, it was a 2015 Cam Newton unstoppable. That`s the year he`s having. It`s his Super Bowl to lose, I mean everything goes through Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everything goes through Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it`ll be Baltimore and who else?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: It`s going to be Baltimore and I hate to say this but it`s going to be \uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why you hate to say it? Relax.\par \par \uc2 Cam, I need to hear your opinion because we have four quarterbacks.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All different, some say game managers to superstar elite quarterback.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: I like what you --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m just saying I`m keeping it real and to be honest I agree with you on a lot of your thoughts when it come to QB`s. But what do you think about this field of guys?\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: I put it like this. When you don`t have a horse in a race, you don`t care which horse wins.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, right.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: So I could care less.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But as a guy who`ve been to the big game, I need to know what you think.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Facts But the NFL as we know in TV, we all love an underdog story. We all love a storyline.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, true.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: What greater underdog story than Detroit? What better storyline than the Swifties.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh so you saying the Chiefs against the Lions.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: I want you know for just a black empowerment what Lamar brings to the game --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Their black --\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Not to say, Patrick Mahomes isn`t adequate, but just what Lamar has just been able to do has just been brilliant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: And just proving to the NFL pundits that this game can be attacked very viciously, strategically by a person who, you know, plays the game how Lamar plays it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And was perceived as a guy who couldn`t play the quarterback position.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: Correct.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We just want good football. Are you a Swifty, Cam?\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: I`m more of a Travis than a Swifty. I just love, you know, pretty much what the Kelsey family --\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MARSHALL: We love love. I love love.\par \par \uc2 CAM NEWTON: I like--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen, we appreciate you guys joining us, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love love too, Brandon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cam Newton, Brandon Marshall, thank you so much. And good luck on the tour when it pops off. And everything else you guys got going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, we can`t wait for Super Bowl 58 Sunday, February 11th. You can watch that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where is it, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And on Nickelodeon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talk about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right. We got it. But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I said the same thing. Our next guest spent a decade, I know, so much happens during the commercial breaks, investigating one of the last racially segregated psychiatric hospitals in this country.\par \par \uc2 Award-winning journalist Antonia Hylton is the author of "Madness: Race and Insanity in a Jim Crow Asylum." She uncovered inhumane conditions. She uncovered inhumane conditions, the mistreatment of patients, and experimentation on some -- without their consent at Maryland`s Crownsville State Hospital. It was built back in 1911 by its patients before closing in 2004.\par \par \uc2 Now this place was a microcosm of America`s struggles with slavery, racial segregation, and poor civil rights. About 1,700 patients are still buried on its ground, the vast majority in unnamed graves. Antonia Hylton, we`re glad to say, joins us right here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Antonia.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON (Author): Good to see you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This book made me so mad and made me so sad and just, it felt so many different emotions when I was reading, and to think that this is a true story. How did you hear about this place?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON I first heard about this place when I was just 19 years old. I was a college student studying the history of psychiatry, and I think I was pulled into this work because I grew up in a very big family of 20- something cousins. I`m one of seven kids, and we were very tight-knit, but we really struggled to talk about emotions and mental health.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And you said you had a wide variety of things in your family, depression, anxiety.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON Right, and I knew that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And no one talked about it.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: You know as a child, but you can see that your parents and your grandparents really struggled to talk about these incidents or these memories that they carry. And so I think when I was a teenager and first knew how to go study this by myself to discover this work on my own, I took the opportunity. And one of the things you realize when you look at the history of mental health in this country is that it`s often taught from the perspective only of White Europeans and White Americans.\par \par \uc2 And so when I came across Crownsville`s name, I just had to know more. And what I found was this very American story in which Jim Crow had seeped into the medical system, and it had really changed generations of black families in Maryland. And when you talk to black people in Baltimore and Annapolis to this day, many of them have a connection to this place --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Many of them.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: But very few people have ever given them the chance to talk about what happened there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and talk about what happened there. That`s the thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Help people understand how difficult it was. There was a Pulitzer Prize reporter that did a story and said it was the most shameful thing that had ever been done in the state. It was hard to comprehend what was happening to the people there, who`ve gone there for help, by the way.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: Exactly. This is supposed to be a place for healing and rehabilitation, but in those early years, that`s not what we find. They brought 12 black men into a forest and forced them initially to build this asylum, and they start to bring more and more patients in. And I think that tells you a lot from the very beginning, right? There are already hospitals around the state for white people in Maryland, but they`re not allowed to get treatment there. And those patients didn`t have to build their own hospital, but black people in Maryland had to build their own mental hospital before they could even get any care.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not until the 50s and 60s when black men and women first start getting jobs there, because originally they were barred from being able to serve their own community.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said that there was a shift when that happened.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: There`s a shift, and what you see is they start to uncover the injustices. Patients that have been left in wards just unclothed and unkept.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The shock treatments, Antonia.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: Shock treatments. I spent time with a woman who is still living named Gertrude Belt, who came in in the 50s, one of the first black employees, and one of her first acts of kindness was to sit patients down in between her legs and shampoo their hair and detangle their curls, because nobody had been doing that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was so matted.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, it`s now closed. It closed back in 2004. There was a ceremony where people went back and said, "Say the names of the patients who were there." That`s very moving the way you tell it in the book.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: Thank you. That is one of my favorite parts of the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mine too.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: It`s such a special ceremony that actually still happens every year. It`s led by a woman named Janice Hayes-Williams in Maryland, and it`s open to the public. She invites people to come and stand in a circle and say some of the names of the 1,700 people who are buried there, mostly in unmarked graves, as a way to help families who might be looking for loved ones and clues, and as a way to bring some healing, some recognition for a story that has been so under-wrapped, so under-covered for so long. It closed in 2004, within all of our lifetimes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: But this is really the first time that it`s being told to a national audience.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The hope and the expectation as this process continues that the unnamed graves will bear names and a proper memorial?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: Well, I actually have. This is compiled by Janice. All of the names of the 1,700 are in here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: And there are a couple hundred, she thinks maybe 200 or more so, that they are going to discover soon. But she is working to connect people and communities in Maryland and beyond with their loved ones who are buried there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Honoring and remembering the forgotten.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you also say if you swallow your pain, it never digests, and treated pain curdles your blood and changes your code. And that`s why it`s important to tell these stories.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Madness" is on sale now. We`ll be right back. Antonia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`re still talking about Cam Newton`s hat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m thinking about those cupcakes and Dolly Parton. Are they still backstage?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I`m thinking about Antonia`s dress to be honest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That all happened today?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That happened today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240124ek1o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for January 22, 2024, \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for January 22, 2024,\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mike Bettes, Elise Preston, Charlie D`Agata,  Nancy Cordes, Caitlin Huey-Burns,Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 3,844 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R-Presidential Candidate): It is now a two-person race. Do we want more of the same or do we want a new, generational leader?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So, who are you supporting?\par \par \uc2 DOUG KRAUS (New Hampshire Voter): I`m supporting Trump. I think he did very good for the country.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: She`s wonderful. She represents everything I`m looking for in a president.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The battle for New Hampshire. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  go head to head, with just one day to go until the country`s first primary.\par \par \uc2 The CBS EVENING NEWS begins now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. We`re live from here in Manchester, New Hampshire, with just hours until the first-in-the-nation primary. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 And it is now a two-person race. And, today, we spoke with \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  , who is going head to head with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Haley is canvassing the Granite State. She`s hoping for an upset tomorrow night, but is trailing in the polls. We spoke to her about the former president and the issues, including immigration and abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Another major issue in this race, the economy. Wall Street hit record highs in both the Dow and S&P 500. But, on Main Street, it`s a different feeling for millions of Americans. Even with low unemployment and growing wages, \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Tony Dokoupil spoke with people who say they`re still struggling to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 With the final push ahead of Election Day, both Trump and Haley are holding campaign rallies tonight.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s begin with \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Caitlin Huey-Burns, who has been out speaking with voters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is campaigning across New Hampshire like her political life depends on it.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (R-New Hampshire): To a big win. To a big win!\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: With Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  now out of the race and throwing his support behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , Haley is scrambling to make up her double-digit deficit in the polls.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R-Presidential Candidate): I have watched the political class line up with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . America doesn`t do coronations.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: At every stop today, Haley hoped to sway an undecided voter. And for one independent we spoke with, it worked.\par \par \uc2 So you came in here undecided.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE HUCKINS (Independent Voter): Correct.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And after meeting her, did you make up your mind?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE HUCKINS: I did. I did. Definitely voting for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  . And --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So you made up your mind right here in this booth after meeting her and asking her questions?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE HUCKINS: Correct.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: That is the New Hampshire way.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS AGER (New Hampshire GOP Chairman): Our independent voters --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: New Hampshire GOP Chairman Chris Ager says independent voters don`t all think alike.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS AGER: Well, independent voters, I believe, will break both for Trump and I`d say against Trump, which is really a \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  vote.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: What do you think people misunderstand about independent voters here?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS AGER: It`s that swing vote that`s relatively small that can make a huge difference.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Trump is campaigning here after a detour to New York, where the E. Jean Carroll defamation trial was delayed when a juror fell sick. And he`s increasingly confident the nomination could be wrapped up by tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  )\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Maybe she will drop out on Tuesday. Let`s see what happens.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Independent voter Kevin St. Pierre (sp?) is holding out hope for Haley.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ST. PIERRE (Independent Voter): Anybody but Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But he hasn`t been able to persuade his longtime friend and neighbor, Doug Kraus (sp?).\par \par \uc2 DOUG KRAUS (New Hampshire Voter): I think he`s an honest guy, and what he says, he did.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ST. PIERRE: We love each other --\par \par \uc2 DOUG KRAUS: It`s all good.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ST. PIERRE: I`m just laughing because -- when he says he`s an honest guy, which I think opposite.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And the New Hampshire secretary of state is projecting record turnout in a Republican presidential primary, expecting 37,000 more voters than in the last competitive GOP race in 2016.\par \par \uc2 And rallies like the one behind me are aimed at turning out every last vote -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, while the Republicans are expecting record turnout, what about the Democrats? Because President Biden`s not on the ballot tomorrow. Explain.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yeah, the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  had changed their primary calendar to prioritize South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So, Biden will not be on the ballot here in New Hampshire in the primary. So Democrats are urging a write-in campaign -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to our conversation with former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  . We met her at a campaign stop in Windham, New Hampshire. That`s about 30 minutes from here in Manchester.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You wanted a two-person race. Now you have it. How do you win it?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R-Presidential Candidate): I mean, we feel really good. You know, the goal was always to be strong in Iowa. We started at 2 percent. We finished with 20 percent. Now it`s to be stronger in New Hampshire and then to get even stronger in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  mentally fit to be president?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  : I think he`s mentally fit, but I think he`s declining. And that`s the -- look, do we really want two 80-year-old candidates running for president? Because the concern I have is, look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  two years ago. Look at how much he`s declined in these two years.\par \par \uc2 What I`m saying is, why can`t we go and finally get all of these people out of D.C. and go with new, generational people? Do they have to stick around this long, when we see what a mess the country is in?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Today is the 51st anniversary of Roe v. Wade. That is no longer the law of the land, thanks to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said today that tens of millions of women now live in states with extreme and dangerous abortion bans. Is President Biden right?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  : What I will tell you is right is the fact that we didn`t need unelected justices deciding something this personal. So, what happened is, you had unelected justices say this shouldn`t be with us. It should be within the hands of the people. The people should decide.\par \par \uc2 I am unapologetically pro-life. But I don`t judge anyone for being pro- choice any more than I want them to judge me for being pro-life.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So you don`t think a woman who has a miscarriage should fear getting proper medical treatment?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  : I think every state needs to make sure that women are safe and that physicians are involved, that we do everything we can to support life, but we do everything we can to support moms.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Let`s talk about immigration. You are being pummeled in this state by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with flyers, I have seen them, and on TV.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters are saying that you are too weak and too liberal to fix the border, that you have refused to call migrants criminals.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  : \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has lied in every single commercial. When I was governor, I passed the toughest illegal immigration law in the country.\par \par \uc2 President Obama sued me over it, and we won. So, if anything, what I have said is, we need to go harder on the border. I think we need to have a nationally verified program that requires businesses to prove that people are in this country legally. I think we need to defund sanctuary cities once and for all.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to put 25,000 Border Patrol and ICE agents on the ground and let them do their job.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We talked about the issues with \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 And it`s interesting. There were some undeclared and undecided voters there. And after that interview, they said they are now open to voting for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The economy is one of the big issues in every election cycle, including here in New Hampshire, and even though consumer confidence has soared in the last two months, many people say they don`t feel it.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Tony Dokoupil spoke with voters to find out more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL (voiceover): We go to the one spot we know people can`t avoid thinking about their finances.\par \par \uc2 We`re out here talking to people about their money.\par \par \uc2 LISA KEISER (Consumer): You really want to know?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really -- do I really want to know? Of course, I really want to know.\par \par \uc2 LISA KEISER: I haven`t seen any prices go down on anything.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we find Lisa Keiser (sp?) feeling frustrated about hers outside this grocery store in Derry, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Well, according to the government, the inflation used to be at 9 and now it`s at 3.\par \par \uc2 LISA KEISER: I don`t know what pocketbook they`re looking at, but it ain`t mine. I have got to take on two jobs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two jobs?\par \par \uc2 LISA KEISER: Yeah, you have to.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The need for multiple incomes is a fact of life in the Raji (sp?) household.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Because we all work now, but --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yeah, we have like six of us in the house. Five of us work. You know, it`s like, if you go back like 30 years, two incomes would have covered all of our expenses, probably, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe even one.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And not just them. Nationally, in fact, most Americans 30 to 64 now say their standard of living feels worse than their parents at the same age.\par \par \uc2 The president, for his part, points to improving economic indicators.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): In case you haven`t noticed, inflation is down, too, and it`s going lower.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And in the town of Portsmouth, we heard about some at Buff & File Nail Bar.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: All right. Ready?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Owners Andree Connell and Alex Sullivan (sp?) say, whatever the bad vibes out there, business in here is booming.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure people here talk about everything. What do you hear?\par \par \uc2 ANDREE CONNELL: I think people might not be as optimistic as they have been in the past, but we have had a great year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But if you`re not happy with Joe Biden`s economy, what`s the alternative?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: I don`t know yet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really? A lot of people have already made up their mind, or they`re totally disgusted and they`re not going to vote for anybody.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Well, I`m disgusted. I`m going to vote, because, if you don`t vote, then you can`t complain, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tony Dokoupil is back with us.\par \par \uc2 Really getting a pulse from the voters there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, she`s not wrong.\par \par \uc2 But, look, consumer confidence, it is up. Inflation is down. But those food prices year over year, they continue to go up. And it`s those things that people deal with every day -- groceries, snack food, that can really shape their opinion.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: I did notice that gas prices are down.\par \par \uc2 Tony Dokoupil, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And CBS News will have full coverage of New Hampshire`s first-in-the-nation primary all day tomorrow, and right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  , the CBS News Streaming Network and on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Today, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  are putting abortion rights at the center of the political debate.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Nancy Cordes reports tonight on their hope that the issues get voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): How dare he?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): The Biden/Harris campaign kicked into high gear today, going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the issue of abortion in op-eds and ads around the country.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Biden-Harris 2024)\par \par \uc2 DR. AUSTIN DENNARD (Sued Texas Over Abortion Ban): In Texas, you are forced to carry that pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The goal, to tie the recent rollback in reproductive rights directly to the GOP front-runner.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We did the Roe v. Wade thing, which, I mean, they have been trying to get it done.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Since Roe v. Wade was overturned 19 months ago, 21 states have established full or partial abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 The issue has galvanized voters on the left, who have turned out to protect abortion rights in seven states so far. Come this November, the issue will be on the ballot in up to nine more states, including battlegrounds like Arizona and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER KLEIN (White House Gender Policy Council): People have made it really clear that they want this right to be restored.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Jennifer Klein leads the abortion policy effort at the White House.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER KLEIN: You have got folks on the other side who are really quite clear that, if they could pass a national abortion ban, they would do so.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Do you want to see a national ban on abortion?\par \par \uc2 At the March for Life in Washington Friday, most people we spoke to said yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yeah, I absolutely do believe that.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Not just in your state, but across the country?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Yes, ma`am, everywhere.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I want to see them outlaw abortion.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But, so far, the GOP front-runner hasn`t committed to a national ban.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NBC/Meet the Press)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m not going to say I would or I wouldn`t.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Here`s why he`s cautious. Polls show two-thirds of all voters oppose it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You have to win elections. Otherwise, you`re going to be back where you were.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: With voter enthusiasm still lagging on both sides, the Biden campaign hopes that keeping abortion rights front and center will help motivate Democrats to vote come November.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the issue is so central to their strategy that it will be the subject of the president and vice president`s first joint campaign rally of the season in Virginia tomorrow -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In another big development today, the Biden administration scored at least a temporary victory at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  , the justices ruling 5-4 that border agents can cut through razor wire that was installed by Texas officials. Conservative Justices Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch, and Kavanaugh were outnumbered in the ruling. Texas argues that it needs the razor wire because the federal government has failed to stop the flow of migrants.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to some dangerous weather impacting the country, ice storms are causing havoc on the roads across the Midwest, and heavy rains and flooding are prompting water rescues in Southern California.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Elise Preston reports tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: This is insane.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Southern California deluged, hours of intense downpours flooding neighborhoods in San Diego. This car washed into a fast- moving storm channel. It`s unknown if anyone was inside.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Everything underwater inside here.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Homes were inundated with water knee-deep, these pets carried to safety.\par \par \uc2 A major freeway shut down, leaving hundreds of cars stranded, drivers with nowhere to go, too much water in the West, ice and snow a major problem in the Midwest and the East. The Coast Guard rescued several people trapped on an ice floe, on Lake Erie, first responders rappelling down to pull them to safety.\par \par \uc2 And these were the treacherous conditions just outside St. Louis, ice making roadways incredibly slick.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, my God!\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Even emergency vehicles, including this fire truck, unable to find traction.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, my God!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, all of California has seen significant rainfall over the last few days, but San Diego, which is about a hundred miles south of here, really bore the brunt of this storm.\par \par \uc2 There are so many emergency calls, drivers are being warned to stay off the roads -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For a look at where these storms are headed next, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Our weather problems, they certainly persist from coast to coast. Here in California, the flood risk is definitely there from now through tomorrow morning, really focused there in Southern California. We saw the issues in San Diego. That continues.\par \par \uc2 But it may extend right over into the desert, including places like Havasu, Phoenix or even Tucson. Now, in the Southeast, it`ll be day after day after day of rain. We think three different storm systems move through now through Thursday. That could increase our flood risk here, with as much as five inches of rain or more in our forecast. Particularly, that is places like Texas, Louisiana and Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 And then, across the Midwest, yes, ice is in our forecast. That will happen for some of us overnight tonight, including around Chicago, warming up tomorrow, with the snow going farther north. But, as temperatures warm here, Norah, everyone sees just plain old rain, instead of the ice.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to some breaking news from the Middle East. U.S. military jets from the aircraft carrier USS Eisenhower attacked Houthi targets in Yemen today. This is now the eighth U.S. strike against that Iranian-backed militia, which has been attacking cargo ships and oil tankers in the Red Sea.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Charlie D`Agata has more tonight from the region.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): The joint U.S.-U.K. strike hit eight Houthi targets today in attacks smaller in scope compared to when allies launched jets from the same Eisenhower aircraft carrier and Cyprus 11 days ago, striking more than 60.\par \par \uc2 In that ongoing U.S.-led fight against Houthi militants, U.S. military officials say the search for two missing Navy SEALs has ended, declaring them deceased; 37-year-old Christopher J. Chambers from Maryland and 27- year-old Nathan Gage Ingram from Texas were on a nighttime raid of this boat, confiscating weapons bound for the rebels in Yemen, when one of them was swept overboard. Following protocol, the other jumped in to attempt a rescue.\par \par \uc2 This new video is said to show one of those Houthi missiles hitting a Greek cargo ship last week. The Houthis say they`re attacking Red Sea targets in support of Palestinians in Gaza, where the war rages on.\par \par \uc2 Today, CBS News producer Marwan Al Ghoul almost became a victim of that war while he and his team were filming in Southern Gaza, narrowly escaping injury in an explosion just a few feet away that killed a passing cyclist.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They shoot us. They shoot us.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: One more life lost adding to a death toll \uc2 Hamas\uc2  health officials say has now surpassed 25,000 Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Now, more on those joint airstrikes that took place tonight.\par \par \uc2 We`re learning they included strikes on an underground storage site and targeted missile and air defense capabilities -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is recommending expanding plane inspections after a door panel flew off a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  jet. We have got those new details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Federal officials are recommending that airlines inspect the door plugs on older \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737s.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes after a door panel blew off a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737 MAX 9 earlier this month, prompting that plane to be grounded nationwide. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  wants airlines to check the nearly 400 older Boeings with the same door plug design.\par \par \uc2 One week after Martin Luther King Jr. Day was observed across the country, Dr. King`s youngest son, Dexter Scott King, died today after a battle with prostate cancer. Dexter King was just seven when his father was assassinated. In an interview along with his siblings back in 2018, he told \uc2 CBS\uc2  ` Michelle Miller he and his brother learned about their father`s death while watching TV.\par \par \uc2 Dexter King went on to continue his father`s work fighting for equality. He was just 62 years old.\par \par \uc2 A college basketball coach does something that no man or woman has ever done before. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: History was made on the college basketball court this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Stanford`s Tara VanDerveer is now the winningest college basketball coach in \uc2 NCAA\uc2  history, men`s or women`s. With win number 1,203, the 70-year-old VanDerveer surpassed Duke`s Coach K, who called it yet another milestone to add to an amazing legacy. The Hall of Fame coach has won three national championships and an Olympic gold medal in her storied 45-year career.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations, coach.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we remember the Hollywood director whose hit movies included "Moonstruck." That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We learned today that acclaimed Hollywood director \uc2 Norman Jewison\uc2  has died.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Moonstruck, 1987, MGM)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The romantic comedy Moonstruck with an all-star cast, including Cher and Nicolas Cage, earned Jewison one of three nominations for best director. He was also nominated for directing the racial drama "In the Heat of the Night" and the musical "Fiddler on the Roof."\par \par \uc2 Norman Jewison\uc2  was 97.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell in Manchester, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240123ek1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For January 21, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, Robert Costa, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,033 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation, we will talk exclusively with one of the two candidates standing between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican nomination, former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Democrats ramp up their efforts to motivate voters on the issue of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 With just hours to go before the polls open in the first-in-the-nation primary, former President Trump actually isn`t exaggerating.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): This is a big deal. The whole world is watching now New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nikki Haley agrees with him. The world is watching. But she says people are looking for a new generation and a different tone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R-Presidential Candidate): I mean, I think they both sow chaos. Look at our country right now.\par \par \uc2 We want people at the top of their game. We -- these are people making decisions on our national security.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that`s only one side of campaign 2024.\par \par \uc2 We traveled to another early primary state, Michigan, where we sat down with the governor and co-chair of the Biden campaign, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . Her message is loud and clear.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER (D-Michigan): Abortion`s on the ballot in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The challenge for Democrats, using that message to build enthusiasm about President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas Governor \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  will also join us to talk about the issues Republicans hope will motivate their voters.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 The New Hampshire primary is just two days away. Can anyone stop the Trump steamroll towards the Republican nomination?\par \par \uc2 We go now to former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador Nikki Haley, who is hoping to be the one to stop him. She`s on the campaign trail in Derry, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: As you know, our \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling shows that you are the Republican candidate with the best chance at beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in a head- to-head race, and yet \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the party`s front-runner.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think, Ambassador, that the Republican establishment isn`t doing more to help you?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, I mean, this is what primaries are all about. This is about the fact that people get to decide which way they want to go forward.\par \par \uc2 What I have said is, you can either pick more of the same, or you can go forward with a new, generational leader. More of the same is the fact that you`ve got 70 percent of Americans don`t want to see a Trump-Biden rematch. More of the same as the fact that both of these presidents put us trillions of dollars in debt, and our kids are never going to forgive us for it.\par \par \uc2 More of the same is the fact that we`re going to have two presidential candidates in their 80s. That`s not what our kids want. That`s not what we should want. You know, more of the same is the fact that we can`t be a country in disarray and have a world on fire and go through four more years of chaos. We won`t survive it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But 26 Republican senators at this point and all of the House Republican leadership have lined up behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Why? Are they afraid of him?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Not surprised at all.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you why. You`ll see a lot of the South Carolina legislature line up behind him too. And it`s because I have never really taken care of elected officials. I call out elected officials because I think they need to be accountable to the people.\par \par \uc2 I call out Republicans and Democrats when they don`t do the right thing. So it`s not surprising that that set is going towards Trump because he`s going to take care of them. I`m not going to do that. I`m going to take care of the taxpayers. I have always done that my entire career, and I will keep on doing it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you win in North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I won twice as governor.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Former President Trump on Friday repeatedly and wrongly said that you were in charge of the January 6 security response at the Capitol, and he said your name four times. I want to play this for our viewers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They never report the crowd on January 6.\par \par \uc2 You know, Nikki Haley, Nikki Haley, Nikki Haley, you know, they -- do you know they destroyed all of the information, all of the evidence, everything deleted and destroyed all of it, all of it, because of lots of things? Like, Nikki Haley is in charge of security.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s unclear what he`s talking about as being deleted.\par \par \uc2 But you said yesterday, hearing this made you question Donald Trump`s mental fitness. Is that the first time you questioned his mental fitness?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: If you look recently, there have been multiple things. I mean, he`s claimed that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was going to get us into World War II. I`m assuming he met World War III. He said that he ran against President Obama. He never ran against President Obama.\par \par \uc2 He says that I`m the one that kept security from Jan -- from the Capitol on January 6. I was nowhere near the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 But, Margaret, you -- don`t be surprised if you have someone that`s 80 in office, their mental stability is going to continue to decline. That`s just human nature. We know that. What I`m saying is, first of all, you`re talking about somebody who`s only going to be in office four years.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, you`re talking about someone who continues to -- I mean, look, I don`t know if he was confused. I don`t know what happened. But it should be enough to send us a warning sign that if you, look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he`s very different than he was two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Are we really going to go into a situation where we have wars around the world, and we`re trying to prevent war, and we`re going to have someone who we can or can`t be sure that they`re going to get confused?\par \par \uc2 It`s a real issue. That`s not being disrespectful. It`s just a fact.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why do you think Trump`s voters care about that? And when you worked in his Cabinet, did you ever question his mental fitness?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: When I worked in his Cabinet, I called him out if he was doing something wrong, I mean, I would show up. I would pick up the phone and say: You can`t do this. Instead, you could do X, Y, or Z.\par \par \uc2 You know, so I always told him what I thought was in the best interest of the country when I was in his Cabinet. But this is different. I mean, we`re seeing he`s just not at the same level he was at 2016. I think we`re seeing some of that decline.\par \par \uc2 But, more than that, what I will say is, focus on the fact that, no matter what it is, chaos follows him. Rightly or wrongly, chaos follows him.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does he cause it?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: And America`s tired.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, he causes it. In some cases, he doesn`t. But, regardless, he`s like a magnet to it. And so what happens is, that puts the rest of America in chaos, and people are tired, and they`re worried.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about an ad that you have released that includes video and testimony from the mother of \uc2 Otto Warmbier\uc2 , who was an American student from the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 , who was taken prisoner in North Korea and died, as you know, after the Trump administration did bring him home.\par \par \uc2 Pundits say national security does not matter to voters. Why do you think this story is one you need to tell?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I think this really does show the contrast.\par \par \uc2 So I worked with Cindy and Fred Warmbier. They`re amazing people. And I will tell you, as a parent, no parent wants to see their child returned to them the way they saw Otto returned. I mean, this was a happy, smart kid. He went to North Korea. And I mean, the thugs in North Korea tortured him and returned him back in a state that -- that is unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 And the difference is, I told Fred and Cindy, speak up. Get loud. Make sure. I will help you. Let`s partner. We`re going to call North Korea out. I passed the largest set of sanctions against them in a generation by pushing China and Russia to do it.\par \par \uc2 But what did Trump do? Instead, he talked about love letters going back and forth to Kim Jong-un. Cindy would contact me. She was so upset. And he went and said: Oh, but Kim said that he wasn`t aware of any torture that happened to Otto.\par \par \uc2 All you had to do was look at Otto when he was returned back to his parents. But this goes back to a pattern. I mean, we saw this over and over again. It`s not just that. He congratulated China`s President Xi a dozen times after China gave us COVID. He congratulated the Chinese Communist Party on their 70th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 We don`t congratulate the Communist Chinese Party. I remember at the United Nations, I had to sit him down and tell him to stop this bromance with Putin. I mean, you can`t have someone who`s trying to buddy up with dictators that want to kill us. Instead, you have to let them know what we expect of them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about clarifying your position here, because you`ve spoken about the complexity of the abortion -- abortion issue personally for you.\par \par \uc2 And you`ve said there`s no national consensus, so there won`t be a federal law. But you`ve also said, if there were one passed, if it were six weeks, it was 15 weeks, or it was restrictions after 20 weeks, which you signed when you were governor, that you`d be OK with that.\par \par \uc2 When we spoke yesterday to \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  of Michigan, she said that you would sign a national abortion ban. Would you?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You know, it`s so interesting, because this is a -- you can`t say that to the American people, because all you`re doing is putting fear and judgment in them.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I`m unapologetically pro-life, but I don`t judge anyone for being pro- choice. I have said I`m fine with a federal law. But the thing is, in order to get a federal law, you have to have a majority of the House...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: ... 60 senators and a signature of the president.\par \par \uc2 Margaret, we haven`t had 60 Republican senators in over 100 years. So no Republican president can ban abortions, any more than a Democrat president can ban any state law. What we can do is, let`s find consensus. I think we can find consensus to ban late-term abortions. I think we can find consensus to encourage adoptions and good-quality adoptions.\par \par \uc2 I think we can find consensus that doctors and nurses who don`t believe in abortion shouldn`t have to perform them. I think we can find consensus that contraception should be accessible. And I think we should find consensus that any woman who has an abortion, no state law should say she`s going to jail or getting the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I will not demonize this issue. It`s too personal. We have to start doing that.\par \par \uc2 Democrats use fear, and Republicans use judgment. This is too personal of an issue to use fear or judgment. And I won`t be a part of any of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, but you`re not taking a ban off the table, which allows for Democrats to say that, that you would be on board. Can you say...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You are not...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... you will not?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Sixty senators -- 60 senators would never approve a ban on abortion. That`s a fact. That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 So why put out something that`s implying something`s going to happen to the American people that is a lie? That`s never going to happen. You`re not going to get 60 senators to agree to a ban.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you know Democrats are using this as a way to motivate voters.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are using this as a way to motivate voters...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Democrats...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... so that they do get the math to add up one day.\par \par \uc2 So, if the math does add up...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... one day, would you sign a ban?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: This is the problem with Democrats. Democrats put fear in the American people. The American people don`t need fear. They need confidence.\par \par \uc2 They need security. They need to know that everything`s going to be OK. When it comes to the abortion issue, I will never put fear in the American people. I will tell them the hard truth. The hard truth is, 60 senators will never agree to a full ban on abortion. That`s a fact, Republicans or Democrats; 60 senators are not going to agree to that.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what 60 senators are going to agree to. Right now, they don`t even agree to 15 weeks. So we`ve got a long way to go. Our goal should be, how do we save as many babies as we can and support as many moms as we can?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, we have to leave it there. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in one minute with Michigan Democratic governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 We sat down yesterday with Michigan`s governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , and talked about why Democrats want to draw attention to this issue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Abortion is on the ballot in all 50 states. Abortion is on the ballot for every one of us, because if we...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Theoretically speaking, you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Well, if we elect a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or a Nikki Haley, or a \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , they all have pledged to sign a national abortion ban. And so in a state like...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They haven`t signed -- no, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hasn`t said what he`s going to do. He just said six weeks is too much for him.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: He`s also the one out there taking credit for the Supreme Court ripping this right away with the Dobbs decision that overruled Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 So it is very clear that abortion is on this ballot for all of us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To pass a federal law, you would need to restore what was in Roe vs. Wade, Democrats would need 60 Senate votes. They`d need to be able to get through the House and they`d need the presidency.\par \par \uc2 That math isn`t there. So, that claim that Democrats could secure the right, isn`t that giving people false hope?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I don`t think so, because, right now, one in three women in this country live in a state where they have no access to reproductive freedom, to make their own decision.\par \par \uc2 If they have a partial miscarriage at home or sepsis, or they`re at -- their reproductive health is at risk, they have no ability to get that -- that service. And so this is why I think, if a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is president or any of the people on the Republican side right now, unfortunately, is, they are going to promote an abortion ban for all of us.\par \par \uc2 Right now, this president has said he is absolutely going to fight for reproductive freedom. Even if he doesn`t have a Congress that will send that bill to his desk, him being in the White House keeps a national ban from happening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re saying if, somehow, Republicans were to sweep the Senate and sweep the House, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would veto that bill?\par \par \uc2 But...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You actually think that that`s a legitimate promise to make to voters? Because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn`t talk about abortion much, and, in fact, he has said he`s not big on it because of his faith. Does he need to talk about it more?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I think it would be good if he did.\par \par \uc2 I know that one tenet of his belief system is that women, and only women at -- with their families and -- and health care professionals are the ones who know what decision is right for them, and that he is fighting and going to continue to fight to make sure that that is squarely the ability of -- of an American woman to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You think he needs to be the messenger on that more?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I don`t think it would hurt. I think people want to know that this is a president that is fighting. And I think he has said that. To use maybe more -- more, you know, blunt language, maybe that would be helpful.\par \par \uc2 But he`s -- that`s his position.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So here, in Michigan, it is now protected under law, access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Viability is decided by a health care professional who determines the likelihood of the fetus` survival outside the uterus. Practically speaking, science is going to improve. Viability is going to move closer and closer to conception. This is one of those challenges.\par \par \uc2 Is this an issue that just gets litigated again and again and again?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I think I have come to the conclusion that a right that was there for almost my whole life -- I`m 52 -- is now very much in jeopardy, and that I`m going to have to continue to fight to protect this right.\par \par \uc2 The Roe standard was a question about viability, did have, I think, real -- made a lot of sense. And -- and I think that`s why you see people coming out in states all across America and saying, we`re demanding this right. And that should be the standard.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Roe had viability, the presumption being that was roughly around 24 weeks. That is moving closer and closer to conception. So, you`re saying even though you had this win in Michigan, it`s not a closed matter, it -- it`s a continued fight?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I -- really, I mean, we made great strides here. But no one should feel complacent that this work is done. A national ban would upend everything that we`ve accomplished here, everything they accomplished in Ohio or...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you say national ban, what do you mean?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I -- you look at the speaker of the House right now has -- has absolutely vowed that he supports a national ban, sending something to the president`s desk that bans, whether it`s after six weeks\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He`s also said the votes aren`t there for it. He admits the math...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: At this moment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: At this moment. But that can change. And that`s why codifying this right, having someone in the White House who would veto it if we see legislation like that passed through the Congress is going to be really important.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you actually think there is a national consensus on abortion now?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I think that the majority of people expect to have the right to make their own decisions about their body.\par \par \uc2 The most important, profound economic decision a woman and her family will make over the course of their lifetime is whether and when to bear a child.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are a lot of economic issues that go alongside raising children.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the Biden administration needs to campaign more on the issue of expanding access to -- to more affordable childcare?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: We want to make sure that Americans have the ability to raise children when they decide that they want to have a child, that it`s easier for them to find childcare that is affordable, that is high-quality.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure that when they enroll their children in schools, that they`re getting the kinds of supports they need to be successful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So how do you make up for the challenge that we`re seeing -- and we`re seeing it in our polling -- because women think access to reproductive care is getting harder, not easier?\par \par \uc2 And more than half of those polled by \uc2 CBS\uc2  say it`s becoming more dangerous. How do you make up for those health care deserts in parts of the country?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Well, the worst thing you can do is cut off access to -- to medical ability, to -- whether it`s around abortion or obtain contraception, cut off access to women being able to get health care on -- through telehealth, for instance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you satisfied with the Biden administration`s messaging on these specific matters related to women?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: You know, I know that this administration is doing the work.\par \par \uc2 And they roll up their sleeves, and I appreciate that. They`ve been phenomenal partners to us here. I think all Democrats and all people who are -- are on the right side of this issue need to use their voices, need to be very clear to the American public.\par \par \uc2 There`s so much noise out there, there`s so many stressors that people are confronting, that it`s hard to -- to cut through sometimes, and it`s no fault of anyone. We`ve got to be very clear about how high the stakes are and what our priorities are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because our polling is showing that the president is underperforming with the Democratic base. This is black voters. This is Hispanic voters.\par \par \uc2 Is the issue of abortion access enough of a halo effect to make up for that lack of enthusiasm and the frustration?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I`m not dismissing polls. I think that they are an important piece of data that should inform additional outreach.\par \par \uc2 But I`m also not -- I`m not getting -- I`m not freaking out. What I hear from people is a sense of urgency, a sense of how serious this moment is in this country. And I -- I respect that and know that`s why we`ve got to continue to show up and continue to talk about these fundamental issues that Americans and American families need solved.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you concerned here in Michigan about this state staying blue?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: I -- I think everyone should always focus on Michigan. It`s always going to be close in this state.\par \par \uc2 You cannot make any assumptions about what the next election is going to bring, based on the last one in a state like this. You got to show up, you got to do the work and show people that you really care about them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s still a purple state?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And Michigan is still up for play.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Absolutely. Oh, I think it always will be.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk a little bit about the economy.\par \par \uc2 Our polling, by a 49-to-21 margin, voters believe former President Trump will be better for their finances than President Biden. So why do you think this perception exists? How do you fix it?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: We`re seeing unemployment at historic lows. We`re seeing take-home pay going up. I think that we have seen a lot of progress happen, inflation coming down.\par \par \uc2 These are important factors that take time for people to really see the benefit from.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The cost of living is still high.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: The cost of living is still high.\par \par \uc2 And for a young person to buy a home, that is out of reach for more people than -- than it has been in a long time. And so I think that all the work around affordable housing, the story that this president is going to be able to tell as people start to tune in closer, as we get closer to the election, is going to be powerful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, this state in particular, it`s Michigan, it`s an auto state.\par \par \uc2 The future of the industry and electric vehicles, in particular, which President Biden has placed a very big bet on, is very dependent on the outcome of this next election and the federal subsidies to make that transition to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Are you disappointed that the Auto Workers Union, the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , has not endorsed President Biden yet?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: No, I think that they will endorse the president.\par \par \uc2 But I also think it`s a good thing that it`s not just a foregone conclusion that that happens. You get to earn the support of people, whether it`s voters or a union, or, you know, a business executive.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to earn individual support. And I think that their process will make sense. And I`m confident the president will be -- will be the person that they end up supporting.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This could be, as you said, another close election.\par \par \uc2 So, I have to ask you, since there are roughly, what, 300,000 people in this state who identify as Arab American, you have a large Muslim American population. There is a lot of pain and frustration with the president`s support of Israel and its military campaign.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How will he be received by this community when he comes to visit this month?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Well, no community is monolithic. I will start with that.\par \par \uc2 I will say that one of the great things about the state is, this is where people came to from around the world for a good-paying job and a high quality of life. It`s true today, but it`s why we have such a robust and beautiful Arab community in Michigan and a robust Jewish community in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 These two communities have lived as neighbors in harmony for decades. And what`s happening in Israel and Gaza has certainly, I think, caused pain for everyone.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We spoke to one of your constituents last night, who said he went door to door for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020, but he`s lost his vote in 2024. And he said he plans to protest against him.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is President Biden going to face protesters when he comes here because of this one issue?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER: He may.\par \par \uc2 A lot of voters are going to vote for things like individual freedoms, like the basis of our democracy, climate change. So, there are a lot of things that are going to come into play as we get closer and closer to the election.\par \par \uc2 But, certainly, these are legitimate and raw feelings that -- that people have, and they`re entitled to their opinions.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our full conversation with Governor Whitmer is on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Join us Tuesday for our live coverage of the New Hampshire primary results starting at 7:00 p.m. Eastern on our free CBS News app.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We go now to the Republican governor of the state of Arkansas, \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2  (R-AR): Good morning, Margaret. It`s great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s good to talk to you.\par \par \uc2 You are one of the youngest, if not the youngest governors in the state, the first female governor of Arkansas. I want to ask you about a number of things, including the current Arkansas law -- we`ve been talking a lot about reproductive health. The law bans abortions except to save the mother`s life in a medical emergency. No exceptions for rape or incest. Your attorney general has twice recently rejected ballot measures that would repeal the ban and give a limited right to abortion up until 18 weeks of conception. As governor, are you open to any ballot initiative?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : Look, I`m proud of the fact that Arkansas is one of the most pro-life states in the country. I`m unapologetically pro-life. I believe that we are a culture that protects life, that values life. I think that`s who we are as a country and I`ll continue to support those measures.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But I know those are your personal convictions, but would you seek the opinion of your constituents on this? I mean some of the attorney general`s objection -- one of the things he objected to was replacing the word "conception" with "fertilization." In another one it was narrowing a medical emergency to threat to physical health and defining it just as that. I mean they seem to be tweaks.\par \par \uc2 On the premise, though, would you be open to seeking the opinion of your constituents in a ballot initiative?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : Arkansas is overwhelmingly pro-life state. I`m proud of that fact. I`m proud of where we are and will continue to push for things that I think protect all innocent human life. It`s why we haven`t just focused on pro-life legislation, but we`ve also done things in the foster and adoption care space. It`s why I`ve spent so much time focusing on education, empowering every single Arkansan to have a great quality of life. We are looking at every aspect in making sure that we`re doing what we can to protect and value life at every stage here in the state of Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So it sounds like a no, you wouldn`t want to put it on a ballot?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS: I`m not going to put a blanket on anything that could come forward. But as it stands right now, I haven`t seen anything that I would be supportive of.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So your state -- you`re talking about the sanctity of life. Your state had one of the highest maternal mortality rates in the country according to the CDC, up until about 2021. Arkansas is one of the few states that hasn`t extended postpartum care for mothers. Why don`t you want those moms to get care for a full 12 months, as is being offered, instead of just 60 days?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : Well, I`m going to have to disagree with the premise of your question, saying that I don`t want that. I certainly want us to do everything that we can to help during pregnancy and well after a child is born, which is why we have done things like focus on the foster and adoption care. We`ve put significant funding into our pregnancy crisis centers. We`re focusing on things that help our mothers, including bring your kids to work at state government. We`ve expanded maternity leave for state employees. We included that in our education package. We have taken a number of steps that are very positive in this front, and we`re going to continue to do that as long as I`m governor.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the states of Mississippi, Wyoming, Montana, South Dakota, they did extend for 12 months, rather than the 60 days. So, I`m just wondering, specifically on that option, why you opted out?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : We`re going to continue to look at options that we feel like best help people here in the state of Arkansas. We`ve done that in a number of ways, and we`re going to continue to do that over the course of hopefully the next seven years while I`m governor of Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, I want to ask you as well about what is happening with the kids in your state? I was interested to see that you are not among the 15 Republican governors who rejected a new federal program to give food assistance to 8 million children during the summer months. You opted into that. A number of Republican governors --\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : I did. I - sorry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Say on premise that this -- this violates conservative principles. So, why are they wrong and why are you OK with this federal program?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : Well, I want to focus on why I think it helps our state. Arkansas, in the past, has ranked at the bottom when it comes to food insecurity for children. I don`t think any child should ever go hungry. If we have options available to us to help improve that, that`s exactly what we`re going to do. That`s why we`ve opted into this program.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to continue to look for ways to help and protect kids in our state. And I was proud to be part of that program and will continue to look for options to help move Arkansas out of the bottom when it comes to food insecurity and into the top.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are, as we said at the get-go there, the youngest governor in the country. And I wonder, when you look at your party right now, what does it say about the party and about our politics that not only is the president of the United States at such an advanced stage, but the Republican frontrunner, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 77 years old here, are these much older individuals really the new generation that you`ve been calling for?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : I think this election right now is very simple. It`s a very clear contrast. You have two individuals who have a four-year record to run on. One has a record of success, coming from a posture and a position of strength in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and one who comes from a position of weakness. Every single thing that voters actually care about, every single thing that drive voters to show up and cast their ballot, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is winning on, whether it`s the economy, whether it`s securing the border, whether it`s national security, whether it`s taking a hardline against China, every single one of those major issues that really drive voters, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is dominating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on. And they both have clear records in which to run from. And I have no doubt that the match-up in November will declare \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a clear victor because of that contrast.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, he`s only four years younger than the president whose age you`ve criticized. But your predecessor as governor, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , just recently ended his presidential bid and he endorsed Nikki Haley. He said, "anyone who believes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will unite this country has been asleep over the last eight years. Trump intentionally tries to divide America."\par \par \uc2 Do you honestly, Sarah - sorry, Governor Sanders, I`m used to calling you Sarah from - from the front row at the White House there, do you honestly believe Trump is going to unite the country this time when in the first term, that you were part of, the country was very divided?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : You know, one of the things that I think is so often left out of Donald Trump`s story is the patriotism and the love of country that he brought back. We haven`t seen that in this president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : In fact, we`ve seen the total opposite. We see people who believe in America again, who see the strength of our country. We brought back American manufacturing. We secured our border. We had a strong economy. Our enemies abroad actually feared us and our allies actually respected us. Instead of the people now are across the - the other side of the world are laughing at us and taking advantage of the weakness of this president.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know, you joked a minute ago, you called me Sarah instead of governor. You know, your colleagues called me a lot of other things. I`ll take Sarah all day over some of the things that the media and the left called me. But when those things were happening, the person who was defending me, empowering me to do my job was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well --\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : I know that he can deliver again because he`s done it before.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, and, Governor, I think we`ve always had a respectful exchange, you and I, so I don`t think we`re part of the media group you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : I didn`t say you, I was -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. But -- but on the substance of the question, in terms of uniting the country, I mean some of the policy things you just rattled off, as you know, immigration has been broken for decades, and those border problems were border problems under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 The Middle East policies he had didn`t solve issues. I mean, in fact, we are seeing the conflict in Israel really flare up. In a way, he didn`t broker the peace deal he promised. He didn`t get Russia out of Ukraine. He didn`t improve relations with China. So, how can you point to that as a - as a high point without recognizing that even he says the work was not finished?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : Well, that`s why we need him to come back for four years because he didn`t get to finish, but he certainly made significant progress. Our border was far more secure under President Trump than under President Biden.\par \par \uc2 I had the chance to go to the border myself. And while I was there met with those who are standing on the front lines, including members of the Arkansas National Guard that we deployed because the federal government is not doing their job. States are having to step up. And in meeting with those individuals, they told us that more people had come across in just that month, just that month, on the terrorist watch list than in the entire four years of Donald Trump`s presidency. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was actually taking steps to secure our border to strengthen our country. It`s hard to argue that having a good economy -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS: Having safe and secure borders, taking a hardline against China, those are empowering and unifying things for our country and only --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you be his VP if asked\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : Hold on, I want to just finish this one point. And only one of two people in the race has actually delivered on each of those things, and it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`d be open to vetting to be his vice president potentially?\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : Look, I absolutely love the job I have.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2 : I think it`s one of the best jobs I could ever ask for. And I am honored to serve as governor. And I hope I get to do it for the next seven years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Next seven years. All right. That sounds like two terms, maybe a no.\par \par \uc2 Governor Sanders, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to our panel. Political correspondent Caitlin Huey-Burns is up in Manchester, New Hampshire, and chief election and campaign correspondent Robert Costa is here with us in studio. His flight to the granite state is later today.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, let`s start with you.\par \par \uc2 As we spoke earlier to Nikki Haley, the former governor finished third in Iowa. She is -- she was 30 percentage points behind there. She`s behind Trump in New Hampshire. What are the voters telling you?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, New Hampshire has always been kind of the hope for losers in Iowa because the electorate here is very different. Forty percent or so are registered as undeclared or independent. So, that provides some favorable terrain.\par \par \uc2 But I will say, Republican sources that I`ve been talking to here on the ground still acknowledge that this is going to be an uphill climb for Haley. Yes, she`s able to compete with independent voters with this message of trying to turn the page from both Biden and Trump, but it`s still a tall order because Trump has such a hold on the Republican base of support here.\par \par \uc2 So, she will have to over perform with independent voters and that also is no sure bet because if you remember in 2016 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won with independent voters. So, it`s a nuanced group and she`s going to have to get every last one to show up in a Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, in his many criticisms, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said that Nikki Haley supporters are Democrats. What`s the reality of the crossover vote?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, and to be clear, Democrats cannot vote in the Republican primary here. They will -- would have had to switch their party registration back in October. That was the deadline. And we did see about 4,000 Democrats, according to the secretary of state, did switch their party registration to Republican or undeclared, but that`s still about a half percentage point of the general electorate here.\par \par \uc2 I will say, talking to supporters of Nikki Haley, I`ve talked to a lot of them who are undeclared or independent voters, and overwhelmingly they have told me that they believe that they see their vote as a vote against Trump, not necessarily a vote for Haley. So, the enthusiasm question - the enthusiasm question, are enough of them going to show up in a Republican primary?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`ll be bringing us the results.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 Bob, you`re here in studio. As we talk about being able to motivate and coalesce around a candidate, as I asked Governor Haley, it looks like the establishment is lining up behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 26 senators, all of the House Republican leadership. Why?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: To build off Caitlin`s excellent reporting, to go back to that question you asked Ambassador Haley, where is the Republican establishment. The Republican establishment, my sources on Capitol Hill, in both the House and the Senate, they say privately, and sometimes even publicly, they no longer run the Republican Party. The voters run the Republican Party, and many of those voters are inclined toward former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, inside the House you have the speaker behind the former president, and in the Senate, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , for example, he has said repeatedly publicly that he does not want to get involved in the Republican primary race.\par \par \uc2 And so many donors who are sources of mine say they`re waiting to see if Haley can come within single digits of Trump in New Hampshire and maybe they will start to pour millions more into her campaign. But at this point they`re moving towards acceptance of Trump being the nominee.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, of course, Leader McConnell had a very troubled relationship with the president - the former president when he was in office. What about Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida? Can he last? We`re talking about this as a Haley-Trump matchup. Where`s DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Many Republicans are watching how he`s changing his schedule seemly by the hour. He`s trying to focus right now on South Carolina, but our reporting shows there is a debate among his allies about whether he has a path ahead and whether he should stay in the race?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What will decide that?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s ultimately going to come down to his conversations with his wife, Casey, and his inner circle. And it`s also going to come down to money. Is there enough money to fund him going into Super Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ve been talking to Governor Whitmer about her very key state of Michigan. What are you hearing from your sources about just the level of concern among Democrats about whether they can keep it in 2024?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Democrats and Republicans, everybody`s watching Michigan. It`s so important. You were on the ground there talking to Whitmer, who`s on the front lines for President Biden. But also Republicans are wondering, can they start to win over suburban voters in Michigan on issues like the economy. Can they win over working voters who may not buy the Biden administration`s position on industrial policy. This is the swing state. And there is concern that younger voters in Michigan especially, because of the issue with Palestine, are getting alarmed about their support for Biden.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we heard some of that as well.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: During our trip to Michigan, we sat down with a group that included Michigan State students and community activists. Four of them identified as Democrats. We talked about a range of topics, including the challenges Mr. Biden faces in a state that supported him in 2020.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNE (Democrat): While I`m not pleased with everything he`s done, I still just think he`s the better choice, he`s the safer choice, he`s the more stable choice.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be the Republican nominee?\par \par \uc2 ANNE: I do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thasin, you did change your mind?\par \par \uc2 THASIN (Democrat): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On the president. Why?\par \par \uc2 THASIN: I was a champion for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  until October 7th. I feel he`s disowned us, disenfranchised us with his stance on Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 THASIN: He`s not listening to us. We are asking for a cease-fire this time. It`s a humanitarian catastrophe in Gaza. Too many lives are being lost at this time. I was never a single-issue voter. In fact, I used to argue with people not to be single-issue voters. But, for me, this is a deal breaker. Way too many lives have been lost.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you say us, you`re Muslim, is that is what you mean?\par \par \uc2 THASIN: Yes. Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You think the Muslim community here feels as you to?\par \par \uc2 THASIN: Yes. I think a vast majority of Muslims, Arab Americans, are -- even progressives who -- I define (ph) myself as a progressive. And many people that I talk to in my circles do not -- are not going to be voting for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Kathy, how do you feel about this issue?\par \par \uc2 KATHY (Democrat): Well, I feel like it`s really a difficult issue, but I`m glad we have someone like Biden with his wisdom and experience trying to decide how to handle this. I believe that he -- I like when he says things like, Israel has the right to defend itself and to do what it needs to do to protect its citizens from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . I think he stresses that Israel`s got to be really careful not to commit war crimes. I think he`s been really clear on that. I just don`t know what else he can do. I think people think he has more power over Netanyahu than he does.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, abortion is legal and protected in the state of Michigan. Your state voting for that. But do all of you have any vision for what should happen at the national level? If you see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  elected, Sean, do you think that there will be federal limits on abortion access?\par \par \uc2 SEAN (Republican): No, I don`t believe so because I think the court has been quite clear. And this was through their opinion in the Dobbs case, allowing states to tailor their own methods for regulating abortion. And then in the several concurrences by the justices who wrote that opinion, that it wasn`t going to be federally regulated. The only way you can do that is if you change the Constitution. So, I can see -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Or write a new law?\par \par \uc2 SEAN: Well, they could write a new law, but unless they - unless they added in to the - to an amendment, then I could see the court swiftly striking it down, like the court does with many things it -- in either presidency for Trump or Biden. And so I`m not exactly worried about that issue. I would like to see instead what I just said where Congress does make an amendment protecting the life of everyone, including people in the womb.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Some of the Republican candidates have different visions on what you just laid out there. You are shaking your head vehemently.\par \par \uc2 KATHY: Yes, I - I have to - yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, I have to note, you feel strongly. Please tell me. Yes, how much of reproductive rights are a factor in your presidential campaign vote?\par \par \uc2 KATHY: Oh, huge. The president chooses our Supreme Court justices. I wish I had confidence that you say in - in our Supreme Court justices. I - I think there`s probably - it`s one of the most important things that the president does and that we have had justices put in under President Trump that are, you know, really changing precedent in major ways that I find very disconcerting. And I`m very concerned about reproductive freedom in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you are as well?\par \par \uc2 ANNE: Same. I mean I think it was, you know, looking bigger picture just the last few Supreme Court nominees, the process was totally up ended and totally disregarded. And I think it starts with that. And then freedoms were rolled back, such as - such as reproductive freedoms. So, I`m fortunate that we have protected them in the state of Michigan. I think just again the American people are for some type of abortion protect. Certainly there`s a sliding scale. But for the federal government to just disregard that and go the other way I think is appalling.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Saba, you said reproductive rights are a huge factor for you, but that you probably won`t vote for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 SABA (Democrat): I think it would be hypocritical of me to use reproductive rights as a way to justify voting for Biden when Biden is aiding and sending military aid to Israel, which is air striking Gaza and blocking humanitarian aids leading to women there who are pregnant either getting c- sections without anesthesia, not being able to be provided with prenatal care.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s interesting that, just to button this up, Anne, you are not very excited about the Democratic candidate, but I saw the both of you -- you are excited -- but I saw the both of you jump when we talked about reproductive access.\par \par \uc2 KATHY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That is something that`s going to make you show up to vote, even though you`re kind of eh on the candidate?\par \par \uc2 ANNE: Yes, I -- I think so. I mean, I`m certainly a frustrated Democrat at the moment, I would say. I think Joe Biden`s made a lot of missteps. I think he`s not - he`s not a perfect man. He`s not a perfect candidate. But nobody is. And I think he`s done the best, but I think it`s - it`s been alarming to see the things that have gone on, particularly in the Supreme Court, around things like reproductive rights. It`s been alarming and I just think it`s a huge wake-up call to women, to young women.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. But before we go, Speaker Johnson`s office tells us that he has not called for an absolute ban on abortion, but what he has said is that there is no national consensus on the issue. And until there is one, there won`t be a bill on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020240121ek1l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Bitter and Dangerous Winter Blast; Race for New Hampshire; Widening Middle East Tensions; Defense Secretary Controversy; New Murder Charge in Gilgo Killings; Controversial Police Raid; Deadly Seatback Failures; Rising Cancer Rates; Songwriters Hall of Fame; Timbaland on Top; The Unstoppable Maggie Nichols; Clive Owen Live, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Carter Evans, Nikki  Battiste, Stephanie Abrams, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Chris Livesay, Nancy  Cordes, Meg Oliver, Elise Preston, Kris Van Cleave, Jon LaPook, Nancy Chen,  Anthony Mason \par \uc2 14,013 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 January 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony`s back. We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A deadly Arctic weather system wreaks havoc across the country causing travel chaos and claiming more lives.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The cold air really does remains entrenched here. Power outages and significant travel delays anticipated.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Republican candidates are in New Hampshire before next week`s primary. Polls show a closer race.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Vote for your favorite president of all time, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , that`s me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The US launches more airstrikes on Houthi targets in Yemen linked to recent Red Sea attacks.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (White House National Security adviser): A group like the Houthis can basically hijack the world as they are doing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Sources say Jason Kelce of the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  told teammates he`s retiring after the Eagles` Monday night playoff loss.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Watch this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A fan at Indiana State had the chance for a cross-court putt to win some money. Drive for show.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Putt for dough.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey. Ten grand.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was awesome. The crowd went crazy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Comedian, Taylor Tomlinson premiered her new show, "After Midnight" right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON ("After Midnight" host): It`s kind of a talk show, but there are no conversation, it`s a game show, but the points are fake. It`s a vanity project but it somehow makes me look worse.\par \par \uc2 Panelists, what a word would "After Midnight" win at the Emmys? Kurt?\par \par \uc2 KURT: Best female late night host. Sorry -- only -- only female.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: That`s absolutely right. Not a lot of competition right now.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like her already. Pulling for you, Taylor. You know who else we`re pulling for, Nate? Tony Dokoupil is back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, welcome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We said Tony Dokoupil is back. Give yourself a round of applause.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can I just say it is so, so good --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Surviving in the tundra.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So good to be back in the 25-degree cold.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here in New York City. Relatively warm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it cold? This cold?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re doing the polar plunge later today in the Hudson, if you want to join.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Have a listen --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is the joke on a day you`re --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good to have you back, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fantastic coverage in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s actually where we`re going to begin with the weather.\par \par \uc2 Today, we have some extreme weather affecting nearly the entire country. Bitter cold temperatures are making life miserable for tens of millions of Americans from coast to coast. It`s especially dangerous right now in Portland, Oregon, and the rest of the Pacific Northwest where an ice storm warning is in effect.\par \par \uc2 More than 70,000 people there are without power in Oregon, and at least seven deaths have been reported. Carter Evans is there.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Well, good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 If you look around me here, the series of storms that blew through here knocked down more than 300 trees in just the Portland area. Take a look at this one uprooted. Imagine what it took to do that. Landed on this truck over here.\par \par \uc2 Two more trees smashed right through the roof of that house.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Portland is covered in ice. The city has been hammered with strong winds gusting to 45 miles per hour combined with up to a quarter inch of ice, weighing down trees and power lines that have knocked out electricity all across western Oregon.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Ice and snow is getting accumulated on the power equipment that is causing heavy damages.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Downed trees are also blocking roads and ice build-up is making travel dangerous.\par \par \uc2 This is a section of I-84 that they`ve closed down, and if you see on the ground here, there is just a thin coating of ice everywhere. Temperatures in the teens right now and it is not just the ice on the road, it`s the wind, gusting really hard at times making it dangerous.\par \par \uc2 The wrath of winter is hitting coast to coast. In Buffalo, New York, the city pulled out its massive snowblower to make a path through the piles of snow that buried the community ahead of another one to three feet expected this week.\par \par \uc2 Down south, frigid temperatures and icy conditions caused this semi-truck to spin out of control and crash into the median on a highway near Birmingham, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My brakes slid, and it was very scary.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And it`s not just roads, the strong arctic blast is also affecting air travel.\par \par \uc2 At New York`s LaGuardia Airport, half of the flights were delayed Tuesday, and more than 25 percent were canceled.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Battiste was there.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Many frustrated travelers have been stuck here at LaGuardia Airport for hours, and even overnight, some sleeping on benches like these behind me. One woman told me she is on her third day of trying to leave New York to get home to her six-month-old baby.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Frustrated. I was crying down at baggage claim.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m frustrated, too. Like, handling all of it has been really stressful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Frustrated but warm in the airport there. Just in the Portland area alone during this storm, 12 people were treated for carbon monoxide poisoning in local emergency rooms.\par \par \uc2 Now, firefighters said some people were using generators and propane heaters to stay warm -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Carter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on the nasty weather, let`s bring in \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Today in the northwest, it is rain and snow from the Coast to the Rockies. We could also see some freezing rain this morning. Ice and snow, so take it slow.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the day, feet of snow will fall in the interior, three to five inches of rain near the coast. And this energy will make its way east.\par \par \uc2 By Thursday, we have light flakes in the Plains, lingering Lake Effect Snow, maybe even some frozen precip in the south again. Friday, it is snow from Illinois to Massachusetts. By Saturday, the storm is out, but the lakes are still open for business.\par \par \uc2 Through Saturday, the heaviest snow will fall at elevation and just off the water, and it won`t go anywhere fast. The cold air is firmly in place with our highs 20 to 30 degrees below average Friday and Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Sunday, we moderate slightly, but still stay chilly.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Nate, I can finally deliver great news. Come Monday, we start a warming trend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is great news. \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tony, over to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We turn to the race for the Republican presidential nomination, and it is still a race, at least for now, focused on New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 All three GOP candidates will be in the Granite State today ahead of next week`s pivotal primary there, and polls are showing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ahead by not nearly as much as he was in Iowa. Nikki Haley is pulling a close second, and Caitlin Huey-Burns is following it for us in Manchester -- snowy Manchester.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hey, good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Well, New Hampshire is just the second stop on the campaign calendar, but here on the ground, it very much feels like it could be one of the final ones as Trump opponents see it as their last best chance to slow his momentum.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m thrilled to be back with the people of the great state of New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: As snow descended on the Granite State, so, too, did the Republican presidential candidates.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is splitting his time this week between the campaign trail and the courtroom as he faces a second defamation suit in Manhattan. All a sign he sees 91 charges and four criminal cases against him as a political advantage among republican voters. He denies any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : If I didn`t get indicted all these times and if they didn`t unfairly go after me, I would have won. But it would have been much closer, I tell you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But the reaction among Republican primary voters here appears mixed.\par \par \uc2 SANDY GRENIER (Trump supporter): I think I can overlook that, I think his policy is so important to me and what he is going to do for the country.\par \par \uc2 CAROLE ALFANO (Haley supporter): I`m done with the drama. We know that the nation is looking at us now, and we`re going to turn out.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And in a pitch to the state`s independent sensibilities, former \uc2 UN\uc2  Ambassador Nikki Haley is urging voters to turn the page.\par \par \uc2 You`ve described this as a two-person race between yourself and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Does that mean you need to take the case more aggressively to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) (2024 presidential candidate): Yes. It`s about the differences between Trump and with me, and I`ll tell you, look, we don`t want two 80-year-olds to be our options.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But another controversy is following Haley as she was asked yesterday whether she thinks the GOP is a racist party.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: No. We`re -- we`re not a racist country, Brian. We`ve never been a racist country.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And after eking out a second place finish in Iowa, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wants to slow Haley`s momentum in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL) (2024 presidential candidate): So, she does not have the ability to build the type of coalition that you need to win a Republican primary, period, much less take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now there are going to be plenty of candidate events here over the next six days. But Gayle, there aren`t going to be any debates before New Hampshire voters weigh in next week.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley was scheduled to have one tomorrow with potentially \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , but she bowed out because she said she would only show if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, thank you, Caitlin. We`re still thinking about Nikki Haley`s comment about never been a racist country. I think she needs to look up the Emmett Till story. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go overseas now to the newest is signs that the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is widening. Pakistan is the latest country to get caught up in all of this violence blaming Iran for, "unprovoked attack on its territory."\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay has more from Israel where Iran`s activities are drawing close attention.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Houthi militants celebrating, even dancing aboard the cargo ship they seized off the coast of Yemen in the Red Sea.\par \par \uc2 (HOUTHIS chanting in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Disrupting global trade and upending global stability.\par \par \uc2 For months, the armed militants have been attacking merchant ships, and the US military there to protect them in what they claim is opposition to the war in Gaza and America`s support of Israel. They are also known proxies of Iran.\par \par \uc2 Last week, US Navy SEALs raided a vessel headed for the Houthis in Yemen. Inside, they found advanced weaponry from Iran, two Navy SEALs fell overboard and remain missing.\par \par \uc2 In an alarming turn, hostilities are now boiling over into the greater Middle East with Iran launching attacks in Iraq, Syria, and Pakistan in the last two days. Dizzying flash points that risk spiraling even further out of control sparked by the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  massacre on October 7th and Israel`s devastating counteroffensive in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (YUSEF ABU-ISHAK speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "My house was bombed, and I fled here without bringing anything with me, not even money," says Yusef Abu-Ishak (sp?). "We need food, bread, and blankets."\par \par \uc2 And more is now on the way. A deal has just been brokered between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to deliver additional aid to Palestinians in exchange for medicine to Israeli hostages. More than 130 remain in \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` captivity.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Now it was Qatar that helped broker that deal. The same country who late last year helped broker a ceasefire between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that also led to the release of some Israeli hostages in exchange for Palestinian prisoners.\par \par \uc2 The hope is that there will now be more of the same before this regional conflict risks exploding into something much bigger throughout the Middle East -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Chris Livesay in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A newly released 911 call is revealing details about Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin`s emergency trip to the hospital.\par \par