ecommendations that the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  has issued over and over and over again to \uc2 NHTSA\uc2  and others, and there hasn`t been action. That tells me you`re not serious about safety. So get serious.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY (D-MA): I`m going to turn up the heat on \uc2 NHTSA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Massachusetts Senator \uc2 Ed Markey\uc2  led the effort in Congress passing legislation as part of the 2021 bipartisan infrastructure bill, giving the \uc2 National Highway Traffic Safety Administration\uc2  two years to draft a new strength standard.\par \par \uc2 Why is it this hard to get something done up here?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY (D-MA): It shouldn`t be hard. This is actually very simple. We`re not trying to put somebody on a mission to Mars. We`re just trying to make sure kids in the backseat are protected.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Markey and Connecticut Senator Richard Blumenthal are among ten senators now demanding answers.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Frankly, I`m going to the president of the United States, and I`m going to say you don`t want this agency to be delaying and dallying when kids` lives are at stake.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 NHTSA\uc2  told the senators, it is proceeding as expeditiously as possible plans to publish a proposed new seatback regulation in the coming months according to this letter sent days before Christmas obtained by CBS News.\par \par \uc2 What are you going to do about this auto safety regulator missing key deadlines like this?\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG (\uc2 US Department of Transportation\uc2  secretary): When it comes to safety, the one thing that matters more than doing something in time for a congressional deadline is doing it right.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 Every week that goes by safety experts believe another child loses their life. Why can`t this get done faster?\par \par \uc2 PETE BU