 finding that colon and breast cancer cases are rising among younger adults. We`re talking about people under the age of 55.\par \par \uc2 Colon cancer, now the leading cause of death in men under 50, and second for women in the very same age group. CBS News chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, thank you so much for being here. What can you tell us about the why here, why is this happening?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Well, first of all you know, I spoke last night to Rebecca Seigel, who is the lead author for the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 . She gave me the inside.\par \par \uc2 The possible reasons why are, you know, are all theoretical. For one thing, obesity is increasing, so you know, it is linked to obesity. Something in our diet, something in the environment.\par \par \uc2 An interesting theory is the microbiome, you know, the trillions of bacteria, thousands of species in our gut, and there are certain types of species that seem to be linked to an increased risk for colon cancer, and we`re changing our microbiome, we know that, with antibiotics, with our diet, and things like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The screening for colon cancer used to be 50. Now, it`s down to 45. Should it be even lower than that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m hearing more and more cases of men who are getting it under the age of 45.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes. And we`ve been -- I`m a gastroenterologist as you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: We`ve been seeing that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are my doctor.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: For years, and you said it, not me, right. We`ve been seeing this for years, and I will just say this. The guidelines that said we`re going to move it from 50 for people at average risk down to 45, that just changed like a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 So there hasn`t been time yet for the statistics to catch up with that. And don`t forget something else, which is we`ve been seeing an increased risk of colon cancer incidents for years, right?\par \par \uc2 It takes a number of years for that then to translate to increased deaths because you don`t die right away. And what Rebecca Seigel told me last night that she is concerned is six of the top ten causes for colon cancer, for cancer deaths, are increasing.\par \par \uc2 And so even though the deaths haven`t increased yet, it was a warning signal that said, hey, let`s look at this because it could translate into increased deaths down the line.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of increasing, breast cancer cases are increasing for women under 50.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s going on with the rise there?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes, Well, you know, the -- again, Rebecca Seigel gave me a lot of insight into this. The rise is less steep than we`re seeing, you know, say for colon cancer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And it`s mostly sort of early stage estrogen receptor positive cases that have a better prognosis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But still, why is that? And you know, we spoke for about 20 minutes about this one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: In younger people, we`re seeing -- the idea is that if you delay pregnancy and you have fewer children, that actually increases the risk of breast cancer. And then in post-menopausal women, women after menopause, obesity is a risk factor, but not before then.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We haven`t seen you since the death of Norman Lear, your father-in-law. How is your family doing? How are you guys doing?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Thank you. We are holding up. We`re sad, you know, it is very sad. I`m wearing Norman`s tie here. I like to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: 