d Anthony and Gayle sat down with a guest.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, we`ve got a big reveal only on CBS MORNINGS. You know we love when that happens.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason is here with this year`s class for the Songwriters Hall of Fame, and we have one of the class members.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Maggie Nichols won gold as a gymnast with Team USA, and she has shown incredible courage off the mat, as well. She`s going to be here with a new book telling her story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Oscar-nominated actor, \uc2 Clive Owen\uc2  is here with his new show, "Monsieur Spade." He plays an iconic private detective.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is so good in this.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Another Arctic blast freezes Portland, Oregon on the heels of a destructive weekend storm.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nothing is worse than ice on the road. That`s pretty much the scariest kind of condition we can get on the roads.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: GOP presidential candidates shift their focus to New Hampshire ahead of next week`s primary.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Trump`s opponents see it as their last best chance to slow his momentum.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A suspected Long Island serial killer is formally charged in a fourth murder.\par \par \uc2 NICOLETTE BRAINARD-BARNES: The indictment by the grand jury has brought hope for justice for my mom and my family.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Now with it being Australia -- there is some critter action going on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if the job of a tennis ball kid wasn`t hard enough at the Australian Open, they can now add bug wrangling to their resumes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This one kid has the toughest job in town.\par \par \uc2 Okay, well, less said about that, the better.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The critter situation has been resolved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always need a good bug wrangler, huh?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bug wrangling. Yes. I want to quit that job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t want that job. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is not for me.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to begin this hour with something I`m very excited about. We are unveiling the new inductees to the Songwriters Hall of Fame for the writers of all the songs we love, there is really no greater honor.\par \par \uc2 They have literally, literally written the soundtrack of our lives, and Anthony Mason joins us now with the announcement only on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Tom Petty once said: "Music is probably the one real magic I have encountered in my life." If that`s so, these songwriters are all magicians.\par \par \uc2 Here are the five new inductees to the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Losing My Religion.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Leading the 2024 inductees, REM.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Losing My Religion.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Michael Stipe, Mike Mills, Peter Buck, and Bill Berry came out of Athens, Georgia in the `80s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The One I Love.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And took indie rock to the top of the pop charts.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Man on the Moon.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "Music is way beyond rational thinking," Stipe has said. "It doesn`t have to make any sense, but there`s something there."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It`s the End of the World As We Know It.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: REM enters the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wasted.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Hillary Lindsey has written 27 number one country hits, many with Carrie Underwood.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jesus Take The Wheel.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: With Lori McKenna and Liz Rose, she is part of the love junkies. The trio that wrote the Grammy-winning "Girl Crush" for Little Big Town.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Girl Crush.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Lady Gaga went to Lindsey to co-write "Million Reasons."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Million Reasons.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "There have been so many days that I think I`ll never write another good song," Lindsey said, and "Yet I show up. I don`t want to, but I show up."\par \par \uc2 Hillary Lindsey has now shown up in the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 As a kid growing up in Virginia, Timothy Mosley wanted to be a DJ. "Then my mom bought me a Casio keyboard," he recalled, "And I started to sample sounds."\par \par \uc2 As Timbaland, he put a stutter in the beat of R&B and became one of music`s most influential producers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Pony.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Co-writing monster hits with Missy Elliot.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Get Ur Freak On.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Justin Timberlake --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sexy Back.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And Nelly Furtado.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Promiscuous.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Timbaland has been elected to the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 Dean Pitchford has written some of the most memorable movie themes of our time from "Fame" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fame.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To "Footloose."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Footloose.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And even the "The Lizzie McGuire Movie."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Lizzie McGuire Movie.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: An actor and dancer, he played Pippen on Broadway, but made his mark as a songwriter.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Pop.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And songs became hits for Melissa Manchester --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Pop.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Whitney Houston --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All The Man That I Need.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And Deniece Williams.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Let`s Hear it From the Boy.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Give the boy a hand, Dean Pitchford is now in the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 Donald Fagen and Walter Becker mat at Bard College in New York in 1967.\par \par \uc2 "When we started, we were going to be the White Sam and Dave," Fagen said.\par \par \uc2 Instead, as Steely Dan, they created a brazen fusion of jazz, pop, and R&B marked by complex chord changes and often inscrutable lyrics.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from VH1 Storytellers)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A universe of characters came alive in their music.\par \par \uc2 Steely Dan is now in the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Just a few songs we know in there, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Geez. Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a walk down memory lane, but it reminds you how crucial and how important --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Songwriting --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Same thing. Songwriting is.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s so interesting because you know, I`ve experienced this a lot over the years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You meet a lot of musicians, they get in to the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame, they`re happy, they are honored and all of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they say, this one --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But to get into the Songwriters Hall of Fame is a whole different level.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It really is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because so many people identify themselves as songwriters first. It`s not that they don`t see themselves as musicians, but the real art is songwriting.\par \par \uc2 And when you get all of those people in the room on the night that they actually the induction take place, it`s just -- everybody is so honored to be with each other.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Such great music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mariah Carey and Jay-Z said the same thing to me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of all the awards that they`ve gotten --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of course, they like those, but getting into the Songwriting Hall of Fame took things to a different level. It meant something very different.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It hit here. It hit here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s go on a road trip, guys. We`ve got the soundtrack.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know. That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, my turn.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Your turn, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anthony, don`t go anywhere. Anthony, don`t go anywhere because ahead and only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS -- I`m thinking, why isn`t anybody talking?\par \par \uc2 Anthony is going to join us because we get to talk to one of the newly elected members of the Songwriters Hall of Fame. It`s a surprise. Who do you think it is? We`ll introduce you right after the break.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:09, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re thinking about the weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, actor, \uc2 Clive Owen\uc2  is making a famous character his own. He plays detective Sam Spade in the new limited series "Monsieur Spade."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Monsieur Spade.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He joins us in a minute, so make sure you stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Work It.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That of course is Missy Elliott`s 2002 hit song, "Work It" produced and co-written by our next guest, Timothy Mosley, or as we know him, Timbaland.\par \par \uc2 And as we revealed just moments ago, he has been elected to the Songwriters Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 The multi-platinum, Grammy award-winning producer has worked with some of the biggest names in music including Beyonce, Jay-Z, Madonna, Justin Timberlake.\par \par \uc2 Timbaland is behind some of the most iconic beats and songs we all know and love.\par \par \uc2 Now only on CBS MORNINGS, he joins us now from Coral Gables, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Tim, good morning. Congratulations. This is just such a huge honor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we`re giving you a round of applause. Around round of applause, Timbaland, yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND (Grammy winner and Songwriters Hall of Fame inductee): Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How does it feel to be on the Songwriters Hall of Fame?\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: It kind of caught me off guard because, you know, for me, I don`t ever look at my work to be put up on a pedestal, I just love to keep working and doing what God gave me the gift to do.\par \par \uc2 It was kind of like caught by surprise, and I was like, oh, wow, because I -- you know, to be honest with you, when the years go by as you get older, you don`t really never think -- you think you look past, you know, I never thought that I was recognized like this.\par \par \uc2 I know what I meant to the world, but you know, sometimes you just don`t ever wish or never think that you`re going to get such an accolade such as this. So it was kind of surprising.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t know I was noticed like this. I didn`t know. So it was like, oh, wow, so I am noticed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Tim, have you looked -- have you looked at your catalogue of work that you`ve done?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you said I never thought -- have you looked at your catalogue? I want to go -- take us inside your brain for just a second.\par \par \uc2 You`re writing these words on a piece of paper, on your computer, and now all of a sudden we`re all singing it. Do you have a certain process? And what this means to you to be recognized this way? What`s your process that you`re still doing? You`re still doing.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: I`m really -- I think just never falling out of love for what I do and what the gift God gave me to do. I think that`s -- I think that`s my process. It`s always been my process.\par \par \uc2 I never got caught up into my highlights. I always was one who just stayed in the work, you know. So that`s really my process, always to be better than the last hit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: Always, you know, because people -- people always had a saying that scared me, they say, you`re only good as your last hit.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: I`m like, wait a minute. What does that mean? You know what I`m saying like -- so after this hit, we`re off and it is like, you`re forgotten about.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: You know, so for me, it is always trying to stay in the race. I never looked at accolades.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, you just --\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: I just wanted to stay in the race.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Tim, you`ve described yourself I think as an introverted kid. So what has music given you over all these years?\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: Music -- yes, because I`m very like -- I`m more extroverted a little bit more now, but music really gives me a way to speak because I don`t -- I am not great with like going out speaking or giving a speech and doing, you know, how I really feel.\par \par \uc2 But music expresses what I`m feeling. So when I make a beat or a song and people like, wow, you did that? Well, yes. I kind of speaks without me speaking, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: Like it sounds very poetic without me speaking a word, you know, because the music speaks for me. And that was a way of me saying, hey, I`m here, like -- but this is how I talk.\par \par \uc2 I almost consider myself, you know, when I saw "Transformers" as Bumblebee. You know, somebody who changed the sound of the radio, and that`s how I convey my words. So music was a way of me speaking without speaking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Bumblebee. I like your analogies this morning, Tim. But I like what you said, it`s using my gift. What do you think your gift is? You`ve touched on it a little bit. What do you think your gift is?\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: I believe my gift is to make people forget about their everyday problems and to laugh and to love and to come together because I think I have the most beautiful gift in the world that doesn`t have a color base, it just has a feeling, you know, and I think that`s what the world is based on, feelings.\par \par \uc2 And I think we forget about our feelings, and we get desensitized on what makes us feel good and when I do a great song that tops the chart, it gives that feeling to make people feel good and forget about their everyday problems and come together with people that you might not would have come together to, came together with.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: And it brings people closer. You know, music is the thing that I always was -- this is my say, and I feel if you wants to change the atmosphere in the room, puts on some music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMBALAND: That changes everything about the 