2 Here`s a look at how she does it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN JUSZCZYK (Kyle Juszczyk`s wife): It`s the NFC championships, so you already know I`m making a custom game day outfit.\par \par \uc2 First, I found a fabric to match my jersey, then I move on to my top. I take my corset pattern, trace it, cut it out.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is time to move on to the pants. In order to make the pants, I took my favorite trousers and traced them on pieces of paper.\par \par \uc2 Here you have your pattern. Now we`re going to stitch the crotches together. There`s got to be a better word, but I just can`t think of it and then here we go. You have your pants.\par \par \uc2 Here is the finished product. Go 9ers.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at what she does. Kristin, you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She turns t-shirts, jerseys, even footballs into stunning clothing for people like there you so Simone Biles, Taylor Lautner, and Brittany Mahomes, who is married to Patrick.\par \par \uc2 She says she learned to sew using \uc2 YouTube\uc2  tutorials.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her husband, Kyle Juszczyk is very proud.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always love a man who hypes up his wife. He does that. He calls her online an absolute star. You go, Kyle.\par \par \uc2 Kristin says that she was -- she started sewing Halloween costumes -- she started -- got interested in it because she was sewing Halloween costumes for the two of them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can see some of her stuff, DesignsByKristin.com. So, you know what I want because we`re all going to the Super Bowl. I want me a Kristin Juszczyk jacket.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m putting it out there. We are calling her now. I think we should do a story on her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hook us up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to meet her. Should it say CBS Super Bowl, something - -\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: King on the back --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Broadway star. Just as long as it says King on the back, we`re good. Yes. That`s all you`ve got to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am calling her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" was the pushups thing. So,. that`s over. We`re going to go now to our guest because we`ve got a world champion gymnast in our greenroom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Next, we talk with Maggie Nichols about her new memoir and becoming an advocate for others. Hey, Maggie. We will see you in just a second, but first --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have a book for you to read, America. It`s written by our next guest. She is a world champion gymnast. Her name is Maggie Nichols.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You remember back in 2015, he helped Team USA clinch gold at the Gymnastics World Championships.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She had her eyes set on the Rio Olympics. That same year, she and her coach reported Team USA doctor, Larry Nassar for sexual abuse to \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2  officials. She was one of the first to do so. She became later known as Athlete A. More than 500 women and girls later came forward.\par \par \uc2 Now as you know, he pleaded guilty to federal child pornography charges and state sexual abuse charges and will spend the rest of his life in prison where many believe that`s where he deserves to be.\par \par \uc2 Nichols retired from elite gymnastics in 2016 and competed at the collegiate level. She became an eight-time NCAA national champion.\par \par \uc2 Simone Biles, her very close best friend says she is a Michael Jordan of collegiate gymnastics. I like that description.\par \par \uc2 She shares her story in a new memoir, it is called "Unstoppable," great title. "My Journey from World Champion to Athlete A to Eight-Time NCAA National Championship Gymnastics Champion and Beyond."\par \par \uc2 Maggie Nichols, it`s an honor to have you in the studio. Your book is so good, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS ("Unstoppable" author): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let me just start with that.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you open the book telling us about your love for this sport. You said I was willing to sacrifice and do anything for the sport. I loved it that much.\par \par \uc2 Something I never thought about until you pointed it out -- Nate, you`ll like this -- she said other athletes are throwing balls in the air, we are throwing our bodies in the air.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s when we`re doing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you love about it -- what did you love about it so much?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: I loved everything about it. Ever since I started, I loved to learn all the new skills. But as I got older, I loved the challenge of it. Every single day brings a new challenge, whether that`s a new skill. You know, making it to World Championships or anything like that.\par \par \uc2 I just loved working so hard and being disciplined, and just loved it so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you were on track to make the Olympic team. Things were going very well for you.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you happened to ask another athlete, another gymnast, does this happen to you with Larry Nassar? What was your question?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes. So what was happening to me with Larry Nassar. I`ve seen a lot of doctors in my life and I`ve had a lot of injuries, so I have seen a lot of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your dad is a doctor.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: My dad is a doctor as well and lot of PTs and so I kind of felt like what he was doing was wrong. And so, I asked someone who was a little bit older than me if this was happening to her, too. And my coach overheard me and you know, the rest is history after that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really was an "Oh, boy." I mean, once you came out with -- and this was 2015.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your life changed after that.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were treated differently. You and your family were treated differently.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you went public in 2018.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It had to be one of the scariest moments of your life.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why did you make that decision, and how did you feel after?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes. It took me a long time to decide whether or not I wanted to come out publicly. But I felt like I would help someone else who may be, you know, experiencing something similar and empower them to share their story, because some of the girls that were coming out publicly before me inspired me to come forward. And I felt like I helped a lot of people by doing so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they had reported it first back in 1997. We should be very clear about this and not to be too graphic, but he was giving genital exams, that he said was helping you with ungloved hands, with all of these young girls.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there are reports that he was getting aroused at the time. What did you think this was supposed to do?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Well, I was having back pain. I was at the World Selection Camp when it first happened. And I couldn`t barely touch my toes, and so he told me that it was going to help me be able to do all my skills and be able to compete with no pain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ad people liked Nassar, didn`t they?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes, everyone --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the beginning? Yes. What was your --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For what they thought he was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: I mean, everyone said he was the most friendly doctor and everything like that. That`s what we trusted -- I mean, we were young athletes at the time, and so we trusted everything that, you know, they told us at USAG.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It went so long where people didn`t believe you. Steve Penny, who was the head of \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2 . Your parents were saying, look, what are you all doing? And they -- what would he say to your parents?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Don`t worry about Maggie, we will take care of Maggie, so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s an investigation under way, if you say anything, it will derail everything.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems the \uc2 FBI\uc2  let you down as well.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How so?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Just not approaching the situation correctly and not doing what they`re supposed to do, not protecting us. After we reported, so many other athletes were abused by Larry Nassar. So it`s really disappointing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, he is where he belongs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Hundreds of women came forward, though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, hundreds came forward.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And part of what makes this book fantastic, particularly for young gymnasts is there`s so much about the sport. There`s a glossary. Your whole resume is there in the back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you hope aspiring gymnasts take away from this?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes, I hope they take a lot away from it, but I think something unique in my story is sometimes when you go through disappointments, you know, you think that your dream is crushed, but then another door swings wide open with so many more amazing opportunities than you could ever imagine.\par \par \uc2 You know, me not making the Olympic team, I thought was my dreams were dashed, you know. But then I got to go to the \uc2 University of Oklahoma\uc2  and I had the most amazing career there, and you know, broke some records. And --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, broke a whole lot of records.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A whole lot of records.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You had a great coaching staff there. I love the KJ that you write about.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Simone Biles, who wrote your foreword, says "Maggie taught me that gym, business first and fun later."\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You two are very close. You were at her wedding. You`ve got news. You`re engaged.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: I am engaged.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Maggie Nichols. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE NICHOLS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This book will help a lot of people, a lot of young women in particular. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 "Unstoppable" is on sale now wherever you buy your books today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, for sure.\par \par \uc2 Actor \uc2 Clive Owen\uc2  is in our progressive greenroom, and coming up, he`ll tell us about playing Detective Sam Spade in a new series, "Monsieur Spade."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Monsieur Spade.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Monsieur --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Actor, \uc2 Clive Owen\uc2  is an Oscar nominee known for movies like "Closer" and "Children of Men." Now, he is the executive producer and star of the limited series "Monsieur Spade."\par \par \uc2 (Clip form "Monsieur Spade.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He plays Detective Sam Spade who is enjoying retirement in France in the 1960s until six nuns are killed.\par \par \uc2 In this scene, Spade meets the woman who inspires him to stay in France.\par \par \uc2 (Clip form "Monsieur Spade.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: From a big dog in the front seat to a big dog in the industry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Clive Owen\uc2 , how are you doing? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There he is.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN ("Monsieur Spade" actor): Doing good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 So the legendary, Humphrey Bogart, he played this role in the 1941 film, "The Maltese Falcon." And you are a huge Bogart fan.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: Huge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What did you think when you got this role and landed it?\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: I was so thrilled. I mean, I knew of Scott Frank who wrote and directed it. He did "The Queen`s Gambit." And I knew of his writing, I was always a huge fan.\par \par \uc2 He called me up and pitched me the idea, and I`ve got a small original "Maltese Falcon" poster on my wall.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: So I took a shot and I send it to him, and said, you`ve come to the right guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m your guy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: So, I was thrilled, yes, really thrilled.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What did it take to perfect the delivery in this show? Because part of what makes it work is your character has this history of violence, we are led to believe, and you don`t ever see that violence at first.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Except it`s in every line. It`s just there beneath the surface.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s just like a menace.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think something scary is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. You can sense it.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: Because you know, we`ve taken Sam Spade, we`ve jumped 20 years ahead. He`s now living in the south of France trying to live a quiet life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Monsieur Spade.")\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: But for me because I was surrounded with a lot of French actors, I had to make sure the origins of the part was `40s, private detective, Bogart-like.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: So to be honest, I kind of drowned in Bogart a little bit, and I listened to him constantly, just to bed myself in in that period, in that time. And --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You captured it.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: That`s what I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I was going to say mission accomplished, Mr. Owen, because you nailed it. Also, your French accent -- I thought, do you actually speak French? I wondered --\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: I don`t, no. They all asked me at the beginning, how is your French? Is it good? I went "It will be."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Was it a good French accent? I mean, somebody -- one of the characters said it was like a flock of crows.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I just thought --\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: No, I did have license because it didn`t have to be good. Scott kept saying it doesn`t have to be good. Don`t worry. You`re a fish out of water.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you didn`t speak -- so what did you do? Just learn the lines --\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: I thought because I had like a lot of time before we started shooting, I thought I`m going to learn French. So I go to a French school teacher, and we started to learn it traditionally in the classic ways if you`re at school.\par \par \uc2 And suddenly after like a while of this, I thought, I`m learning so much that I`m not going to need. I don`t need -- I`m not going to be tested on the grammar. I`m not going to pass the exam.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: So I switched tact and I learned it all phonetically.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Phonetically.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: So, I learned it like an accent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: So, I had to totally understand the scene and what everybody was saying, and then the French, I learned by sound, like you would an accent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but I loved watching you. This movie, you know, there`s six episodes. I was just going watch one just to get a sense of it.\par \par \uc2 But the first ends with the murder of nuns, Clive. I`ve never seen nuns being murdered.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: I`m trying to solve it. I didn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but I mean, the storyline is such a wild storyline that takes us on so --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Didn`t you guys think --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Twists and turns, but it is set up perfectly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So many twists and turns, yes. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Katy and I are going to watch every episode. I`m going to have to watch the first one again. It`s that good.\par \par \uc2 But I`m curious, what`s great about the show is it`s fresh, but also has a nostalgia to it.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: Well, I think that`s the thing because the thing about noir is that you know, everyone feels like they`ve seen it before. As soon as you do all of those tropes, like we go, oh, we know what that is. We know what noir is.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s a very clever idea. Take an iconic `40s private detective but throw him 20 years later in the south of France. Already, we`re in a new -- it`s a new version of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a new feel.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: But still the heart of it, the essence of it, it still has that kind of `40s PI vibe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Clive, you are so awesome, man. We appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is an incredible series.\par \par \uc2 Make sure you check it out. New episodes of "Monsieur Spade" Sundays at 9:00 PM on AMC or stream them on AMC+ or Acorn TV.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And by the way --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I took French in high school.\par \par \uc2 (GAYLE KING speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That means I love you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We spend a lot of time trying to figure out how to say "Monsieur."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I say it`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a tough --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Monsieur, that will do it for us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Monsieur.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, \uc2 Clive Owen\uc2 . Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CLIVE OWEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will see you tomorrow. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240117ek1h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Torrential Rain Batters East Coast; Tornadoes Rip Through the South; Deadly Weather; Defense Secretary`s Cancer Diagnosis; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Presidential Immunity Hearing; Iowa Caucuses Loom; Gunmen Storm Ecuador TV Network; \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO`s Emotional Response; Virginia`s Historic New House Speaker; Bottle Water Safety Concerns; Sara Sidner`s Moving Message; Simone Biles Cover Shoot; Destructive Weather; Sec. Austin`s Cancer Diagnosis; Trump Immunity Hearing; \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO Speaks Out; Honoring Angela Bassett; Road to the Grammys; In The Studio With Finneas; \uc2 NBA\uc2  Legend Charles Barkley; Top Geopolitical Risks; 2024 Geopolitical "Top Risks"; Charles Barkley Joins Talk of The Table; "Can`t Get Enough" of JLO`s Music; Draymond Green Returns; Charles Barkley on CBS MORNINGS; Charles Barkley discusses "King Charles"; Managing Credit Card Debt; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Lana Zak, Dave Malkoff,  Stephanie Abrams, Weijia Jiang, Robert Costa, Ed O`Keefe, Manuel Bojorquez,  Kris Van Cleave, Scott MacFarlane, Celine Gounder, Vladimir Duthiers, Rikki  Klieman, Anthony Mason, Jill Schlesinger, Elizabeth Werner \par \uc2 14,779 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this hump day, Wednesday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Heavy rain and high winds bring multiple tornadoes as brutal weather slams tens of millions of Americans across the country.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Now on the heels of this, one comes another one Friday into Saturday. It also produces a large swaths of snow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Donald Trump`s lawyers argue that presidential immunity can extend as far as ordering the assassination of a political rival.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): As a president, you have to have immunity. Very simple.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new study raises alarms, about microplastics in bottled water, and whether it`s safe to drink.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It can actually be carried in your blood and end up in your organs.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Armed men raided a public television station in Ecuador during a live broadcast, no one was killed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Charles always says this, we never have to worry about having stuff to talk about because people do dump [bleep] all the time.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY (Former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player): That`s it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a quote.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY: Always.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like the thing that happened with the door that flew out of the plane, and then you hear that maybe there were never bolts there.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY: If you`re putting a door on an airplane and you don`t put the bolts on, that`s a fireable offense.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): In response to this fiasco, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has grounded all 200 Boeing Max-9 planes in United States saying it could take four to eight hours to inspect each plane.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think I speak for all travelers when I say "Take your time." Okay. Do not rush. Be thorough.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stephen is so right. Please be thorough.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charles is so right as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he said, no second chances. You are gone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have one job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Put those bolts on that door.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or else, you`re out of here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`ll be here a little bit later on this morning. We`re excited about that.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Millions of US -- millions of us, rather, went to bed listening to the roar of those wicked storms last night. Did you hear them? It was bad.\par \par \uc2 The nasty weather system dumped a lot of rain and brought strong winds and some flooding to this New York area. In the south, tornadoes caused fatalities and major damage.\par \par \uc2 Our team coverage this morning includes Dave Malkoff. He is in the Florida Panhandle. \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , she`s at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 . We`re all over the place, but we`re going to start with a lovely\par \par \uc2 Lana Zak. She`s in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lana. Good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Hey, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You know where I`m standing, just two hours ago it was completely dry. The waters here are rising and it is rising pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 And residents, they are used to flooding in these areas, but they tell me that they have never experienced it at this time of year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: In New York City, behind the wheel and on the pavement, New Yorkers fought the commute home last night, battling swamped streets and whipping winds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s really windy out here and forget about the umbrellas. Try to use a hood. Umbrella is not going to work.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Those gusts that reached between 40 and 50 miles per hour are part of a system that walloped the East Coast, inundating streets and turning them into rivers, including Ridgewood, New Jersey, which saw about four inches of rain in less than 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 The storm also slamming the Philadelphia area flooding businesses and damaging homes like this one after a tree fell onto its roof in West Chester, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 In Annapolis, Maryland, the nearby Severn River spiked to a historically high level of five feet, pouring onto roads and leaving them so impassable, this person swapped their car for a kayak.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Kind of alarming to see it. I didn`t realize how crazy it gets.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Now back here in New Jersey, crews have been working so hard overnight to try and restore power. There is only about 55,000 people who are still without it at this point, but the dangers are not over, even though many towns which flood staged overnight, many of these rivers aren`t expected to crest until Thursday or Friday -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lana, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In the south, at least four people were killed when the storm spun off five suspected tornadoes in multiple states. Thousands of homes are still without power.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff in Panama City Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Look at this. What a mess when you look at all of this left behind from this tornado. We were here last night without the TV lights, and there were residents who were walking through these apartments with flashlights looking for their belongings.\par \par \uc2 The tornado itself hurt five people, but when you see all of this, you understand why statistically, most of the injuries happen after the storm.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: A string of tornadoes, heavy rain, and powerful winds pummeled the Florida Panhandle on Tuesday, ripping off roofs, shredding homes, and crumbling buildings.\par \par \uc2 GARY MICHAELS (Panama City Beach resident): It was a freight train hitting a brick wall.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The storm damaged Gary Michaels` Panama City Beach home where he took cover with his family.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s kind of like a warzone now on that street. Oh my goodness, look at that crooked house.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: But just across the street, he watched as a tornado tore this home from its foundation, lifting it into the air before slamming it back down and leaving it on its corner.\par \par \uc2 GARY MICHAELS: These poles right here all the way down to the side of the house. They just, like a toothpick, snapped.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Another suspected tornado touched down near a mobile home park north of Charlotte, North Carolina killing one person.\par \par \uc2 Near Atlanta, Georgia, a 78-year-old driver died after a tree smashed into his car, pinning it to the highway.\par \par \uc2 Roadways there were flooded when the system dumped more than three inches of torrential rain in just hours.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The water was up to my door, so I had to roll my pants up, put my shoes in my bookbag. The water was really cold, but yes, I had to actually walk through that water to get out of there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: This is the tornado damage, but Tony, five years ago, this same neighborhood was hit by Hurricane Michael.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dave, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on today`s wind and flood threats and the next big storm pushing east, we are going to go now to meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The winds will take their sweet time winding down. We start the day mainly in the 30 to 40-mile power range, some of us higher. This could make for bumpy flights, more power outages and with the saturated soil, tree damage.\par \par \uc2 By this evening, they are going to start to lighten up from west to east, and tomorrow morning we`ll still have breezy conditions into New England.\par \par \uc2 Though the rain will be out by this afternoon, the flood threat actually remains elevated around rivers along the East Coast. Thankfully, they are going to have time to drop before our next system takes shape, that happens on Thursday night when there is another chance for severe weather in the south.\par \par \uc2 Storms will continue to Friday when we also have snow and potentially whiteout conditions in the Great Lakes. The northeast joins the action on Friday. Saturday, the storm is going to linger from Michigan to Maine.\par \par \uc2 Behind all of this, the most frigid air of the season. The cold air starts draining in tomorrow. By Monday, nearly 170 million will be below average.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices. Tony, speaking of Monday, we have yet another storm taking shape. It will be our fourth since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The skies are very busy this time of year, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thanks for tracking it all for us.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: That they are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is new information, there are some questions too about the health of Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We learned last week that he was hospitalized in intensive care without telling President Biden and other top officials. As it turns out, he had an infection related to prostate cancer.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Secretary of Defense \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  had surgery to treat prostate cancer under general anesthesia nearly three weeks ago, but President Biden did not find out about the diagnosis until yesterday.\par \par \uc2 ADM. \uc2 JOHN KIRBY\uc2  (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): It is not optimal for a situation like this to go as long as it did without the commander-in-chief knowing about it. It`s not the way this is supposed to happen.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Seventy-year-old Austin had the procedure December 22. January 1st, he was admitted to the ICU with complications.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK S. RYDER (\uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  press secretary): . including nausea with severe abdominal, hip and leg pain. Initial evaluation revealed a urinary tract infection.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Duties were transferred to his deputy, but the White House was left in the dark about Austin`s hospitalization for three days.\par \par \uc2 Some House Republicans have sent a letter to President Biden calling for Austin`s resignation, accusing the four-star general of covering up his hospital stay, but the White House has said Austin would keep his job through the rest of the president`s term.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, other lawmakers want to see a congressional investigation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETE RICKETTS (R-NE): There must be consequences for this stunning incompetence. Americans deserve answers. Congress deserves answers.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: On Tuesday, White House chief-of-staff, Jeff Zients sent a memo to members of the president`s Cabinet launching a review of protocols and ordering agencies to alert the White House about delegations of authority right away.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK S. RYDER: Clearly, we could have done better and we will do better.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Secretary Austin`s spokesman says one reason for the delay in disclosure is that discussions about prostate cancer treatment and support are often deeply personal and private. Doctors say since they cut Austin`s cancer early, he has an excellent chance of making a full recovery -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Weijia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, an appeals court is trying to answer the question, should the former president be immune from prosecution? That was the topic being debated in court as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looked on.\par \par \uc2 His lawyer say he should not be charged for any acts as president, even if he ordered a political assassination.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was defiant insisting he cannot be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I feel that as a president, you have to have immunity. Very simple.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In the crowded courtroom, Trump sat in the front row next to his lawyer who argued the former president and all presidents should be immune from prosecution over conduct while in office.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SAUER (Donald Trump`s attorney): To authorize the prosecution of a president for his official acts would open a Pandora`s Box.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The judges pressed Trump`s lawyer proposing dramatic hypothetical scenarios to test the bounds of Trump`s argument.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE PAN, (United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit): I asked you to yes/no -- yes or no question. Could a president who ordered SEAL Team Six to assassinate a political rival who was not impeached, can he be subject to criminal prosecution?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SAUER: If he were impeached and convicted first as --\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE PAN: So, your answer no.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SAUER: It is -- my answer is qualified yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Special Counsel Jack Smith`s team pushed back.\par \par \uc2 JAMES PEARCE (Federal prosecutor): It would mean that if a former president engages in assassination, selling pardons, these kinds of things, and then isn`t impeached and convicted, there is no accountability for that -- for that individual.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE (Former deputy assistant attorney general): The judges seem skeptical of Trump`s argument that he is immune for his conduct while he was in office.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Tom Dupree is a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE: You have to keep in mind that today`s argument in the Court of Appeals was really a waystation. I think the final battleground on this will be the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President Trump is expected to be in another court tomorrow, this time in New York for closing arguments in the civil fraud trial against him and his company. Sources say Trump has been talking privately to his advisers about potentially delivering some of his legal team`s closing remarks, underscoring how he sees these stops as a stage, just days before the Iowa caucuses -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All the world is a stage for that former president.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The former president will be counterprogramming, speaking of a stage, his two closest Republican rivals tonight in Iowa. He is going to participate in a televised townhall on One Network, meanwhile, former \uc2 UN\uc2  Ambassador Nikki Haley and Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  will be debating one another on another network.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is following the candidates in these final days before Monday`s Iowa caucus.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER (Fox News Channel chief political anchor): Good evening.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On television --\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL) (2024 presidential candidate): I`m the candidate who is most in tune with the values here of Iowa Republicans, and I think if you`re a conservative, I`m your guy.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And driving through the snow to be on the stump.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) (2024 presidential candidate): Don`t complain about what happens in a general election if you don`t play in this caucus. It matters.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Nikki Haley are now in Iowa for the final five days.\par \par \uc2 Governor, how are you preparing for the debate tomorrow night?\par \par \uc2 And are set to face off in a one-on-one debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 While DeSantis and Haley look to be vying for second in Iowa, a new poll shows Haley has cut former President Donald Trump`s lead in New Hampshire down to just single digits.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: The only way we`re going to win the majority of Americans is if we go forward with a new generational leader that leaves the negativity and the baggage in the past.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That argument resonates with first time Iowa caucus voter, Lauren Vanderiet.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN VANDERIET (Voter): A conservative female president is something that I think America could use right about now.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Bob Rawlings voted for Trump in 2020, but says he is leaning to supporting either Haley or DeSantis, and will be closely watching the debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 BOB RAWLINGS (Voter): To me, they are pretty even, that`s why I`m undecided.\par \par \uc2 I think with me, obviously, one of the issues is border security, and the lack of good policy towards that. So, I want to hear what they both have to say about plans for border security.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We`ve been hearing a lot about border security and immigration. Now, the snow, blowing wind, and below zero temperatures are a real concern here in the closing days of the caucus. Big concern about whether turnout will be affected by that.\par \par \uc2 But up in New Hampshire, those numbers showing Haley now within single digits of the former president. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  airing new attack ads and making baseless claims online about her in recent days. Those attacks really have picked up. He is likely to focus on that tonight in his televised townhall -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re going to go overseas to a shocking scene in Ecuador and shocking is the word, wait until you see this video.\par \par \uc2 A South American country where there has been an alarming spike in gang violence, men with guns broke into the studios of a TV station there taking several staffers hostages before the police arrived.\par \par \uc2 We should mention this video is disturbing. It`s not maybe disturbing, it is very disturbing as Manuel Bojorquez reports, it`s the latest in a series of violent attacks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from TC Television.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Cameras were broadcasting live when masked men firing guns and waving apparent explosives stormed one of Ecuador`s state-owned TV stations Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The crew was ordered to the ground while an anchor was told to relay this message.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: The police should leave.\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: National Police flooded the area arresting 13 people. The attack happened just a day after Ecuador`s new president declared a 60- day national state of emergency.\par \par \uc2 WILL FREEMAN (Foreign Relations Council fellow): For years, Ecuador was a drug transit country. There was a surge in demand for cocaine around the world after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Will Freeman is a fellow at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WILL FREEMAN: So you all of a sudden have Ecuador emerge as this very valuable turf to control.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Locals have experienced a sharp increase in violence.\par \par \uc2 (Explosion.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Including a series of explosions and police abductions within the past few days.\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The crime wave kicked off after notorious gang leader, Adolfo Macias went missing from his prison cell over the weekend for the second time.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT DANIEL NOBOA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: We will not negotiate with terrorists.\par \par \uc2 WILL FREEMAN: We`ve seen attacks today in Ecuador using terrorist tactics that really have no precedent in Latin America since the days of Pablo Escobar.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Could the turmoil mean that more Ecuadorians will try to get into the United States?\par \par \uc2 WILL FREEMAN: That`s already happening. We`re already seeing a surge in migration from Ecuador, which the country hasn`t experienced for 25 years and I really only see it adding to the pressures on the US-Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: For CBS MORNINGS, Manuel Bojorquez, Miami.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s CEO chokes up talking about that scary mid-air incident aboard an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight, but first, it`s 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, a new study finds we are drinking plastic, a lot of it, when we drink bottled water.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder will tell us how you can cut back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO is talking for the first time about Friday`s terrifying incident where a panel flew off an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 ` jet full of passengers.\par \par \uc2 As Kris Van Cleave reports, he says \uc2 Boeing\uc2  wants to make sure this never happens again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The haunting image of this gaping hole in the Alaska 737 Max-9 shook \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Dave Calhoun\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVE CALHOUN (\uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO): I didn`t know what happened to whoever was supposed to be in the seat next to that hole in the airplane. I`ve got kids, I got grandkids and so do you.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: His first public remarks since the door panel blew off the plane were to \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employees, Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 DAVE CALHOUN: We`re going to approach this number one acknowledging our mistake. We are going to approach it with 100 percent in complete transparency every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Investigators are honing in on the four bolts designed to hold that door panel in place, bolts that so far, they cannot find.\par \par \uc2 United and Alaska have both identified other planes where those same bolts were loose. Calhoun says \uc2 Boeing\uc2  must now rebuild trust.\par \par \uc2 DAVE CALHOUN: We`re going to have to demonstrate it by our actions, our willingness to work directly and transparently with them and to make sure they understand that every airplane that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has its game on that`s in the sky is in fact safe.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now, Capitol Hill is demanding answers from the \uc2 FAA\uc2 . \uc2 Billy Nolen\uc2  is the former acting administrator.\par \par \uc2 Is there a sufficient amount of \uc2 FAA\uc2  oversight of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and its suppliers today?\par \par \uc2 BILLY NOLEN (Former acting \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator): I would argue that there is. This is the right case to ask ourselves these questions.\par \par \uc2 What have we missed? That`s a valid question to ask, and what else should we be doing.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: With all Max-9s in the US grounded, airlines are still awaiting guidance about the new inspections.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Portland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, an incredible story, how the new leader of Virginia`s legislature served time in prison before being elected.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Virginia will make history later today. Why? As the first ever Black Speaker the House is sworn in the State Capitol in Richmond.\par \par \uc2 Democrat, Don Scott is his name. He`s a \uc2 US Navy\uc2  veteran and a lawyer who turned to politics a few years ago defying expectations and overcoming some pretty big hurdles. That`s because the man that the Democrats have chosen to lead the assembly was once a federal prison inmate.\par \par \uc2 He shared his story with Scott MacFarlane.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Can`t wait to hear this story.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gayle, for Virginia leaders, there is a unique history and tradition. They refer to their legislature as the oldest, deliberative, lawmaking body in the world.\par \par \uc2 But for the new Speaker of the House, he is anything but traditional.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Inside Virginia`s Capitol, which was designed by Thomas Jefferson himself three centuries ago, it`s Don Scott, who will now be in charge, a remarkable accomplishment for Virginia`s next Speaker of the House who served nearly eight years in a federal prison.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT (Speaker of the Virginia House of Delegates): I made the dreadful mistake of going to pick up some money -- some drug money in Mobile, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Scott pleaded guilty in the 1990s in a drug conspiracy case after being arrested in his 20s.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT: I had never been in any trouble before, and I ended up getting a slap, you know, for 10 years.\par \par \uc2 I remember my mother in the courtroom. I can hear the little yelp that she made when the judge said 10 years. I still hear that sometimes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Scott`s reemergence after his release is a story of redemption, but also have an elected leader who is making a uniquely rapid rise winning the top post in Virginia`s General Assembly after just four years in the legislature.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT: Damaged good sometimes can turn out to be okay, and we`re a lot more interesting than the people that followed all the rules and did everything perfect.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: He`d already served in the Navy.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT: I was a little skinny kid --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And was in his third year of law school when he was arrested. Scott says he was newly focused when he returned from prison. He passed the bar exam, opened a law practice and eventually convinced himself he could win office despite being an ex-felon.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT: My God, well, I think he`s a great comedian. He will use the people that folks think have nothing to offer and shock the world with them.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Which is why it`s so striking that his friends point us to a much different accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY MORRISON (Portsmouth, Virginia judge): He laid down his life for me.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Portsmouth, Virginia judge, Johnny Morrison is Scott`s neighbor and a mentor.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT: That`s my guy.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In 2021, after Scott`s election to office and a year after Morrison personally oversaw Scott swearing in, Scott noticed something was wrong.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT: He was having this issue with his kidney and kind of going through dialysis, he might end up having to come off the bench.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Morrison was in severe pain and his health deteriorating from kidney failure.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY MORRISON: It is emotional for me. He said, we have the same blood type, and I am going to donate my kidney. I am going to donate you a kidney.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: How did he receive that news?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: I think he was shocked. You know, and I think he was shocked that I was a match. Right? I`m your next door neighbor. You know, it`s been a blessing to be able to give that gift.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY MORRISON: Hey there, Mr. Speaker.\par \par \uc2 DON SCOTT: Hey, come on out.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Ever since Morrison helped convince Scott to run, the 58-year-old has had big aspirations and no shortage of confidence, but faces a uniquely tough job. A narrow majority, the governor of the opposing party and fiercely divisive issues he is targeting including guns and abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 As he makes history, the moment he is sworn in, in the one-time capital of the Confederacy.\par \par \uc2 We`re only a few miles from where the first slaves came into this country in 1619, now 405 years later, you have your first Black Speaker, so I`m very cognizant of the shoulders that I stand on. I take it very seriously, and I`m humbled and I`m grateful for the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Don Scott will be sworn in 30 years to the day or 30 years to here after his federal criminal case more than 400 years after the founding of the General Assembly. And Nate, two years after they took down a Confederate statue of Robert E. Lee down the street from the same State Capitol.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scott, unbelievable story. Thank you so much. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I mean, say it all the time --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t think we`ve heard the last of Don Scott. He is -- he is -- he is going somewhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is going somewhere bigger than I think what he is doing now.\par \par \uc2 Didn`t you guys like what he said? Damaged goods can turn out to be okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, I say all the time that what you did on your worst day, a bad decision --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, 30 years ago, it does not define you. That`s not all you can be or what you will be.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I hear you oftentimes say that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have to prove that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re spot on with that. I mean, he was in law school, made a mistake.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Came back out and finished the job and then continued to build the rest of his life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What a story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up, what`s in that bottle of water next to you? Besides water.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder looks at a new study that found a whole lot of plastic might be in there. Stick around, you need to hear this.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A new study has people alarmed about the safety of plastic bottled water after a scientist at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  found that some bottles of water contain hundreds of thousands of tiny plastic fragments in every liter. That`s up to a hundred times more than previous estimates and those plastics could be linked to a whole range of health problems.\par \par \uc2 Now this study did not mention any brand names, I`ll be the first to say, not helpful study. Dr. Celine Gounder is a CBS News Medical contributor and editor-at-large for public health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 The fact they don`t mention brands is going to have -- you`re alarmed and then you want to know what to avoid. With no brand names, that`s tough. So I guess, talk to me about what was found and then in the absence of brands, how people can try to minimize their exposure.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So this study was really focused on plastic bottled water, not other types of bottled water. And what they found was very high levels of nanoplastics. When plastics break down, even just like unscrewing a cap, you`re going to have a little bit of friction, a little bit of breakdown of the plastic, and you find that in the water that you`re consuming.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What kind of health effects can that have?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, the issue is that with these tiny fragments of plastic, we don`t know if the plastic itself can cause health effects, but they are bonded to or combined with other chemicals like phthalates, for example, which are what make plastic more flexible.\par \par \uc2 So when you get these tiny pieces of plastic, you`re also getting exposed to these other chemicals. Those are known as what are called endocrine disrupters. In other words, they have hormonal effects.\par \par \uc2 They can mimic for example, estrogen and other hormones, and those hormones or higher exposures to those kinds of hormones can be associated with anything from diabetes to neurocognitive effects in little kids, to cancer, and so that is why we have some real concerns about these exposures.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you know, I heard about this, as I was watching Norah last night, as I`m chugging on a bottle of water. So you instantly hold up the bottle of water and you say, I don`t see anything in there. You`re saying it`s so small that we can`t see it, it doesn`t matter the brand.\par \par \uc2 So what are we supposed to do with this information? Because you hear it and it`s very frightening, like, ah. Should I just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we here nano and micro --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And plastic and you`re drinking it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and plastic, and we`re all drinking from bottled water, so what should we be doing with this information.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: And it`s not just in the water, frankly, you know, this is all in any number of beverages we consume, which also come you know, many of them in plastic bottles. It`s in the food supply, it`s in the environment.\par \par \uc2 So it`s very hard to avoid this kind of nanoplastic exposure. Some of the things -- basic things you can do would be to try out to avoid plastic packaging, that will help reduce some of the risks. Don`t microwave your food in plastics.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear that all the time.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, and then try to use containers like glass or stainless steel, at least that`ll help reduce your exposure a little.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But are you saying it`s not safe to drink bottled water? That`s what I`m trying to figure out?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, bottom line.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I mean, yes, bottom line. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Should we do -- yes, aluminum cans, glass bottled water?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Glass bottled water, we`re talking that`s what you do, Tony, is definitely an option. Stainless steel bottles that you can refill from the tap. In fact tap water, unless you`re in a place --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Filters?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can this be fixed?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Use filters and --\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Filters can actually introduce more plastic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just start eating plastic pellets for breakfast, people. Train your body. Get your body ready to handle it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But are you saying people should stop drinking bottled water? That`s what I`m trying to get to here. What is it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: What I would say is try to, if you`re going to drink bottled water, don`t do it from a plastic bottle. Do it from glass or stainless steel, from the tap is generally going to be safe unless you have an area where there`s lead pipes or something like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Now, there`s a lot of ifs, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: And then ultimately, this is so confusing and hard for the average person. This is why you have the \uc2 EPA\uc2  and the FDA that are out there that are supposed to be protecting us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I mean, this is their job. We can`t do all of this ourselves.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Just try to avoid it if you can.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just open your mouth when it rains like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that. Old school life.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, gold medal gymnast, Simone Biles, the GOAT, some would say, talk seriously about her life and her chances of one more trip to the Olympics next year.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going big in our eight o`clock hour. You hear the music only on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Anthony as in Mason talks to singer-songwriter, Finneas, he is the brother, Billie Eilish. They co-wrote that beautiful hit song for "Barbie" in just 45 minutes, and you know the other day it won a Golden Globe.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend, that`s Charles Barkley always brings a lot to the table. He is the co-host of "King Charles." He is joining us at our table today just in time for "Talk of the Table" -- I want to say a table one more time -- table.\par \par \uc2 All of that in our next hour, but first, Vlad is at the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With the stories.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, how are you?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are you going to be talking about in "What to Watch." What are you thinking about today, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my God. Well, I`m excited to see Charles Barkley in the studio.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to see how big he really is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what I was thinking, Tony. I really want to see how tall.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is ginormous. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, I like them heels.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s see if we can both --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mr. Duthiers here on a Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Signed, sealed and delivered.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m here. I want to talk about somebody very important to me, my dear friend, Sara Sidner, who is an anchor at CNN, and she is revealing that she has been diagnosed with stage three breast cancer in an emotional message that she put on her show on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Sara says she is in her second month of chemotherapy and will need radiation and a double mastectomy. She discovered a lump back in October and said her reason for 