 an eight time Grammy winner.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is so good, so talented. Only on CBS MORNINGS. We brought Anthony Mason into the studio.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You`re sitting where these days, Billie sits, and you`d sit here, you know, co piloting the microphone that`s over your shoulder. It`s like a boom mic, and she`ll just swing it around.\par \par \uc2 Finneas O`Connell works out of a small studio in the basement of his Los Angeles home.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Billie Eilish and Finneas in studio)\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL (American Singer-songwriter): And then this is just being used as a midi keyboard right now.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s where he collaborates with his younger sister, Billie Eilish.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Billie Eilish and Finneas in studio)\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: I`m fairly certain that was how we wrote Barbie, I was sitting at the piano.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: And she was sitting here or maybe on the couch with the mic.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right here. The "Barbie" song,"What Was I Made For?" was born.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of "What I Was Made For?")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Where did that melody come from?\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: The chords were just at my fingertips, like playing a chord and playing another chord and playing another chord. And I`ve had a couple times in my professional life where I`ve gotten super lucky. One of them was "Ocean Eyes." "Ocean Eyes" was like playing those chords and just literally at the same time being like I`ve been watching for some time. Can`t stop staring at those ocean like all at the same time. Improv, straight up improv.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ocean Eyes")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In 2015, Billie`s recording of the song went viral and launched their careers.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: And the "Barbie" thing was very similar it was at that piano and Billie singing that I used to float now I just fall down.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "What Was I Made For?")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Director Greta Gerwig had shown Finneas and his sister 40 minutes of the film.\par \par \uc2 Had Greta said to you I need a song for this?\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: She`d said I don`t have Barbie`s heart song But she hadn`t said this scene.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: And we wrote the whole song in like 45 minutes with a bad, with a terrible bridge that we ended up rewriting.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Barbie" Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Their melody was woven throughout the film`s score, culminating in the emotional end to Barbie`s journey.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "What Was I Made For?")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What do you like about writing for film?\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: Seeing a piece of music coexist with a great scene of a movie is so powerful, you know it feels like making a piece of furniture for somebody`s house which I love.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "No Time To Die" Interscope Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But a previous project the theme for the James Bond film "No Time To Die" didn`t come as easily. How much pressure was that?\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: It was a lot of pressure, I mean James Bond has such a ethos and a sort of a signature thing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: That if you fail, you really fail, you really miss the mark.\par \par \uc2 So that one was all like minor nine chords, this is a minor nine ,pretty Bond-y right there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. They went to London to work on the theme with iconic composer Hans Zimmer.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: With no promise that it was going to be our song.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: Like not in a threatening way but there was they just had never said "Congratulations, you got it"\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "No Time to Die")\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: And then once we got the orchestra recorded, I muted most of it and thought I`m going to get fired. It was all good but I -- but I wanted Billie`s voice to be the star of it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "No Time to Die")\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: I sent it to Hans and was like did that and he was like I love it, sounds great.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It won them a Golden Globe, a Grammy, and an Oscar.\par \par \uc2 (Finneas and Billie Eilish singing)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Finneas and Billie began working together when he was 18 and she was 13.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe the collaborative relationship with your sister?\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: I think that it`s the closest I`m, I am with Billie to like total trust of anyone and total vulnerability. I`m sure there`s something that she would be embarrassed to say in front of me but not much.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Finneas and Billie as children with their parents)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Finneas and Billie grew up in a musical household, their mom Maggie Baird also wrote songs.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: So it was the demystification of the idea that you could write something.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But he says she suggested a lyric to him just once.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: And it was the line napalm skies and ocean eyes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: And I`m trying to write about burning cities and what else and she was like napalm skies which is a great like napalm as an image is--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s fabulous.\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: So good.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So that was her line .\par \par \uc2 FINNEAS O`CONNELL: Yeah so she I should -- paid off her mortgage is fine.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "What They`ll Say About