who go into credit card debt, about a quarter of them, are doing it because of those expenses that have risen. But we also look at about 43% who say their emergency medical bills, there`s emergency home repairs and auto repairs, and put it all together, and you`ve got that big jump in the number of people who are carrying a balance forward month to month.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How are interest rates impacting credit card debt?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, you know, if you go back, before the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  started raising interest rates, average credit card debt, 14.5%. Now, it`s over 21%. So, what are you going to do? Let me give you three ways to start negotiating this mess.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Negotiating this mess.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes. And I say that very clearly. Number one, when you have credit card debt, one really good way to actually approach this is to ask the credit card company for some help. So, if you don`t ask, you don`t get.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Closed mouths don`t get fed.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yeah, no kidding. And what`s interesting is when you ask for a lower rate, they`ll often give it to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good advice.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Most people don`t know that.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Number two, if you`ve got decent credit scores, and credit scores have risen, actually, over the last five years, you may get a transfer to a zero balance card. Now, remember, that can often be for a very specific period of time. So, you get a zero percent transfer balance, you get it for a year, it`s going to pop right back up if you don`t pay it off in time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : And I think that the last thing to remember when you`re looking at your credit card bills is to automate as much as you can so you don`t miss a payment, you don`t add to it. That`s critical.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s a lot of people that say, "You know what? I`m going to pay now instead of get on this pay later plan." What do you say about that?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Okay, buy now, pay later. It`s basically an installment plan. Maybe you pay some of it up front. You see it every time you check out online. "Oh, can we interest you in this?" Just be careful. Sometimes buy now, pay later gets you in a bad habit. Sometimes there can be fees associated. Read the fine print.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Real quick, if you`re just drowning in debt, bankruptcy isn`t an option?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It is absolutely an option. It is important to remember that we don`t judge this. Don`t judge this, gang.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Because when you have a real problem, sometimes bankruptcy is the best option. You must see a bankruptcy attorney to actually think about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jill, you`re the best. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Time now for CBS MORNINGS "Deals" this morning, we`ve got three items all at exclusive discounts so start shopping now. Scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com. Lifestyle expert Elizabeth Werner is here. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Hello, Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Happy New Year, my friend. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Thank you for having me. So New Year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: We`re cleaning up our houses, cleaning our --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Some of us, yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: We want to. We need to clean our devices too. Our computers, our tablets, our phones, just like a closet, the storage gets full.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc