onal\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For December 24, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anthony Salvanto, David Martin, Jan Crawford, Robert Costa,  Catherine Herridge, Jeff Pegues, Margaret Brennan, Nikole Killion, Ed  O`Keefe, Major Garrett, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 8,684 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 December 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation, we will look ahead to 2024 with our year- end correspondent roundtables. Plus, we reflect on the good news from 2023.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation on this Christmas Eve.\par \par \uc2 It`s tradition here at CBS News to gather our Washington reporters and look forward to the year ahead.\par \par \uc2 We begin with our beat reporters here in the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 David Martin is CBS News national security correspondent. Jan Crawford is our chief legal correspondent. Robert Costa is our chief election and campaign correspondent, Catherine Herridge, senior investigative correspondent. And Jeff Pegues is our chief national affairs and justice correspondent.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you all for celebrating with us and being here at the table.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jan, I want to start with you, because we are heading into a year where the Supreme Court is going to play such a central role to our politics.\par \par \uc2 We saw just this past week the Colorado Supreme Court ruled that the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could be disqualified from holding office because he engaged in insurrection leading up to January 6.\par \par \uc2 How firm of a legal -- legal ground is this on here?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, yes, I mean, the court is going to be front and center and the presidential campaign. And that`s just one of a number of issues that these justices are going to have to confront in the upcoming year.\par \par \uc2 This one asked whether or not he`s even qualified for being president -- I mean, for running for president because of a clause in the 14th Amendment. Now, the Colorado Supreme Court was sharply divided on this 4-3. And that`s a decision it reached, saying he wasn`t qualified under this provision, that other state Supreme Courts have seen differently.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of problems with the arguments that they adopted, that they kind of just blow right by, and the Supreme Court, I don`t think, is going to kind of give it that kind of gloss.\par \par \uc2 I mean, some of the questions that I think the court will look very closely at if they take this up, and I think they have to take this case up, is how Trump can be considered an insurrectionist if he was not charged or convicted of insurrection, and whether or not the 14th Amendment and this section specifically would even apply to him at all, as someone who`s a former president or running for president.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But apart from this particular case, there are others that the Supreme Court may take up in relation to the former president and current front-runner for the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, I mean, it`s kind of like Whac-A-Mole, right? You know, like, you have got one over here, and you got to try to -- he`s going to try to put this one up and knock it down.\par \par \uc2 Another major issue, legal issue brewing is whether he can be prosecuted criminally for some of the actions around January 6. Now, a federal district court judge here in D.C. said that he was not absolutely immune from prosecution, as a former president.\par \par \uc2 The special counsel, who has, of course, charged the former president with the actions around January 6, has asked the Supreme Court -- and, of course, we`ll see how they handle this -- has asked the Supreme Court to jump over an appeals court and decide whether or not the former president United States is immune from criminal prosecution for actions he took in office.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is a big request to come from the special counsel. It`s unusual to try to bring it all the way to the court this quickly, right?\par \par \uc2 And this will have major implications, obviously, Bob Costa, on the campaign you`re covering. But tell me what you`re hearing about what the special counsel is thinking in going through the steps.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: As I look ahead as a reporter, the campaign is going to come back again and again likely to the High Court and how it`s going to consider Trump`s conduct in and around January 6, whether it`s the immunity question, whether it`s about the January 6 defendants, who have their cases coming before the Supreme Court, whether it`s about how the court is going to proceed, if Trump`s convicted in the special counsel case.\par \par \uc2 All this comes down to, what was an insurrection? What`s an insurrectionist type act? What was the president`s role? What did he not do? Did he conspire against the United States?\par \par \uc2 Based on our reporting at CBS News, the special counsel has phone records. He has memos and diary entries from key witnesses like former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , key eyewitness testimony from people who were inside the Oval Office with Trump.\par \par \uc2 And we got a bit of a taste of this with the January 6 Committee in recent years. But they had something in the special counsel`s office the January 6 Committee never had, which is subpoena power to really go deep with witnesses and not just get public testimony and some depositions. They`ve gone deep.\par \par \uc2 And I have talked to people who`ve participated in this investigation as lawyers, sometimes even as witnesses. And it`s evident to me, based on my conversations with sources, that Jack Smith has a sprawling case against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Catherine and Jeff, I know you talk to a lot of law enforcement sources as well.\par \par \uc2 What`s the degree of concern about what happens, how -- as this plays out in the coming year?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Already, law enforcement across the country is dealing with an uptick in domestic terrorism cases, because there is this concern about, how will the public react if there is a conviction in any of these cases?\par \par \uc2 And, already, the number of domestic terrorism cases that they`ve been investigating at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  specifically, compared to the number of international terrorism organizations and those kinds of cases, they`re about running even. So, law enforcement is also very focused on preventing any kind of domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: We`re in this incredibly dynamic threat environment right now.\par \par \uc2 And the focus squarely is on lone actors or lone offenders, individuals who are inspired by events overseas, or they`re inspired to act, radicalized by domestic events, and very opportunistic.\par \par \uc2 So it talks about car-ramming attacks. It talks about weapons. It talks about knives, and a very short, what law enforcement calls flash to bang, that period in between wanting to act and then making the decision to act.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, David Martin, this -- here`s where I want to turn to you, because we already have all this sort of kindling out here.\par \par \uc2 And then you have a huge event, like we saw with October 7, the attack in Israel, and the subsequent very brutal war...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... that continues to play out that the United States is really trying to bring to a conclusion.\par \par \uc2 How far out are we, because that is raising concerns about U.S. national security at home?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, the Israelis have told the U.S. they think they can wrap up the current phase, which is this general offensive against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza, in early January.\par \par \uc2 But then they say, this war is going to go on for months. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been compared to ISIS. So, it`s worth remembering that our war with ISIS started during the Obama administration. We overran the last ISIS stronghold in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: And here we are in the Biden administration, and special operations forces are still conducting the occasional raid into eastern Syria to go after an ISIS leader.\par \par \uc2 Now, Gaza is a lot more restricted than Eastern Syria, but...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s just 25 miles.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: And that, hopefully will keep this from being a yearslong battle.\par \par \uc2 But terrorist organizations die hard. That`s just the fact.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`ve had the defense secretary basically say this could create more radicalization because of what`s happening there, but also the impressions and what the world is watching in terms of this use of American force.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Win the battle, but lose the war.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but become less secure because of your tactics, which are losing you international support and creating a whole new chain generation of young people that want nothing but to see the destruction of Israel.\par \par \uc2 That`s -- that`s the deadly game that Israel is playing here. But they -- they sure don`t show any signs of backing off their original intention to eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm. No, and that`s spilling out publicly now in these differences between Prime Minister Netanyahu and President Biden in the vision for what happens next and how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 In the Middle East as well, David, the administration likes to say, while they may not be able to persuade the Israeli prime minister to do what they want, they think they`ve largely contained this from becoming the worst- case scenario they imagined of a wider regional war. But there`s still a lot of dangerous activity happening.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, you`ve had over 100 attacks by Iranian-backed militias against American troop locations in Iraq and Syria.\par \par \uc2 The Houthis, who most people had never heard of before the start of this war, these -- these rebels in -- in Yemen, who were also backed by Iran, have come in on the side of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and have fired more than 100 missiles and drones either at Israel or at any ship passing by that they think may be coming either to or from Israel.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. is trying very hard not to let either of those situations get out of hand. After 100 attacks on American troops, you have to retaliate some. You can`t let the other guys just get free shots.\par \par \uc2 And they have taken some retaliatory strikes, but they`re very limited. And I think that will stay the same, unless and until one of these mortars, one of these rockets, one of these drones gets through and kills or seriously injures Americans.\par \par \uc2 And then the whole calculus changes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Catherine, you have this extraordinary job of covering the president`s own son and his legal issues, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , with these indictments, three related to a firearm...\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... felony counts, nine related to tax issues. Where does this go?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: 2024 is going to be a year of incredible legal exposure for the president`s son. And these criminal prosecutions are going to unfold at the same time that his father is running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 In January, he will be arraigned in a California Court on the tax charges. And I would pay special attention to the California case. I had two lawyers look at the 56-page indictment. And they reached the same conclusion, that it is a shot across the bow by the special counsel.\par \par \uc2 He identifies \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  as a lawyer, a consultant, and a lobbyist and then goes into considerable detail about his business transactions with Ukraine, with China, Romania and others.\par \par \uc2 And they see this as an indicator that the special counsel at the very least is investigating potential violations of foreign lobbying laws, maybe even a superseding indictment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spends a lot of time talking about the president`s son and these legal issues. As this plays out in a courtroom, how important are the facts to voters and how important is the perception, and how do you make sense of this?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Until Democrats believe there is evidence presented, if ever, that ties President Biden directly to his son`s business endeavors, in terms of being a lobbyist or being someone who is influencing policy coordinated with his son, they are going to continue, from the rank-and- file to the leadership, be behind President Biden.\par \par \uc2 And you haven`t seen any Democrat of note come out about President Biden`s connection with his son as anything more than familial and something that presents a problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jan, just bigger picture, you`ve watched the Supreme Court for so long, a court that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he takes credit for shaping the -- in terms of its conservative direction.\par \par \uc2 Is there still faith in it and how it functions now that they are directly inserted right into our politics?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, I mean, President Trump`s nominees, three, certainly changed the Court and a much more conservative direction and we`ve seen that very clearly.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is the Supreme Court that overturned Roe vs. Wade. I mean to that -- that -- and we`ve seen the political fallout from that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For years now.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And -- and so I think what`s going to be really interesting for the Court right now is that they`re getting these cases, as Bob laid out too, that involve the president on any number of levels. And it`s a real opportunity for the Supreme Court, and -- and particularly the chief justice, to show that they are above politics.\par \par \uc2 The court has had all of these contentious issues, abortion, affirmative action. They`ve taken a hit in polling, some of their -- the kind of the faith and confidence in the court right now, according to polls, is at among its lowest point ever, still higher than the other two branches, and the media, by the way.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that is what you`re going to see. I think Trump`s going to win some, and he`s going to lose some.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: David, one of the institutions that still has some faith in it is the military, when -- when you look at public polling.\par \par \uc2 The year we are about to start feels very consequential on the national security front, not only because of the Middle East, because of the war that is playing out in Europe at a decisive point, as we debate this Ukraine aid package. You look at Asia, the rising China, this upcoming January election in Taiwan that will be consequential as well.\par \par \uc2 What`s happening inside the Pentagon right now as they gear up for 2024?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, they`re going in all sorts of different directions.\par \par \uc2 But there`s sort of two Pentagons. There`s the one Pentagon that develops systems and plans for future wars. They`re now focused on China.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: And, you know, that is just a totally different ball game than Ukraine or what`s -- what`s going on in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 And then they still have to worry about -- about terrorist organizations. So, they`re going in all these different directions. But, look, during the Biden administration, we`ve had the withdrawal from Afghanistan, we`ve had the start of the war in Ukraine, and we`ve had the start of the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They`re kind of on -- on emergency standby all day, every day as it is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was reading: "China`s top military official warned China will show no mercy to anyone who supports independent Taiwan."\par \par \uc2 Is the American military and the Chinese military in contact yet, so that this doesn`t escalate?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, they`re trying to -- what they`re called is talks at the working level...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: ... to do the high-level contacts.\par \par \uc2 But with everything having to do with China, it`s complicated.\par \par \uc2 But even if they get these contacts going, I mean, China`s behavior is not going to change overnight. They`re still going to harass American surveillance planes in the South China Sea. They`re still going to keep building up these disputed islands in the South China Sea. It`s just another mechanism for managing a very difficult relationship.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 And, at least, in 2023, they started talking at -- at the diplomatic level.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have to take a break here. Face the Nation will be back in one minute.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re back with our panel of Washington correspondents.\par \par \uc2 And here`s where you get to predict, Jeff Pegues, the future.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Too much pressure...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, what`s -- what`s your big prediction for the year ahead?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Let me see. Let me rub this thing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your informed prediction.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: All right, my informed prediction?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: OK, well, that`s a little easier, I think.\par \par \uc2 Crime is going down across the country. You wouldn`t think that, based on what you hear, in terms of some of the crime out there, but violent crime is down 13 percent. The final numbers come out early next year.\par \par \uc2 Police officers across the country are understaffed and have managed to essentially get their arms around this problem. I predict that this trend will continue.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: However, there are some bad areas. Carjackings, are -- they continue to skyrocket.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Why?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Well, and those -- it`s -- it`s a good question. Some people think that some of it is fueled by these pictures on social media giving people ideas.\par \par \uc2 And then there -- it`s hard to catch people who are breaking into cars and theft, especially when you don`t have enough cops on the street, you don`t have cops walking the beats. So that`s a -- still a challenge for law enforcement. But, in terms of violent crime, it is going down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Catherine?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Well, mine`s a little dark.\par \par \uc2 I just feel a lot of concern that 2024 may be the year of a black swan event. This is a national security event with high impact that`s very hard to predict. There are a number of con -- concerns I have that factor into that, not only this sort of enduring, heightened threat level that we`re facing, the wars in Israel, also Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And we`re so divided in this country in ways that we haven`t seen before. And I think that just creates fertile ground for our adversaries, like North Korea, China and Iran. And that`s what concerns me most.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A lot of people up at night with that concern, Catherine, at least in this town.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Bob?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Talking to my top Republican sources for months, I have been trying to get an answer to the question of, what happens if former President Trump is convicted in a federal trial or in Georgia, but more likely in a federal trial, before the Republican Convention?\par \par \uc2 Is there a plan B? Elected officials and campaign strategists, they say almost a refrain: No.\par \par \uc2 So, if Trump is the nominee, we`re looking at a Republican Convention this coming summer where there really is no plan to move to another candidate. The Republican Party, because of the way Trump has his fingerprints on everything, the state parties, the delegates are very much in his image politically, he could hold onto the nomination even if he`s convicted of a federal crime.\par \par \uc2 So my prediction is, you might have a crisis inside the GOP come summer, if Trump`s a convicted felon, but still no real plan of how to handle that in a general election campaign.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I predict that the Supreme Court is not going to save \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the criminal trial.\par \par \uc2 He -- they are not going to rule that he is immune from criminal prosecution. And I don`t think it`s even going to be close. It could be 9- 0, with the chief justice writing the opinion that a former president does not have absolute immunity from criminal prosecutions for actions they took while in office.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the Supreme Court is also -- my other prediction is, they are not going to say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is disqualified from running for president, that California -- I mean, that Colorado Supreme Court decision. So I think he`s going to stand trial. The Supreme Court is not going to save him. And he will be on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ninety-one different indictments? Is that the tally?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, if you include what`s going on with the hush money payments in New York.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He`s also facing the ongoing civil fraud trial, Georgia. There`s the E. Jean Carroll case.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You`re going to have to have another hour for -- to -- really to dig into all of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. So, if your prediction plays out, this -- this could be a really interesting year.\par \par \uc2 Get your rest in now.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: David, you correctly predicted last year the next chairman of the Joint Chiefs.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: I got one right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What`s your prediction this year?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: So, this year, I would have to predict the discovery of alien life to compete.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What? I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Excuse me. I said I would have to. I`m not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: But in order to compete with a shocker...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Oh, OK.\par \par \uc2 I think, David, when you speak everyone listens.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I believe it`s absolutely 100 percent true fact. So, you really threw me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: I will tell...\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: He also -- also scares us all every year.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Like, oh, no.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: I will take it from the top.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: In order to compete with the shockers that we`ve got coming up in this election year, I would have to predict something like the discovery of alien life, but I`m not going to go there.\par \par \uc2 Instead, I am going to predict that North Korea`s Kim Jong-un will reap the rewards of having provided Putin with artillery for his war in Ukraine. And those rewards will take the form of technical aid to his nuclear weapons programs. And we won`t know it until we see North Korea testing new and improved weapons.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And perhaps that seventh nuclear test everyone`s been waiting for.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Everyone`s waiting for that shoe.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, I -- you have all set the table beautifully for my conclusion, which is that the only certainty is uncertainty, and anyone who tells you what is going to happen with this election and how it`s going to play out over the next year is selling you something, because there are just so many different variables that all of us are tracking and all of us are weighing, which is why you need to watch \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But it`s also why none of us will sleep very much in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: As we wind up 2023, we want to take a look at the positive this year.\par \par \uc2 Our Anthony Salvanto and his team surveyed Americans to see if they`re happy. And the results are actually good. More than three-quarters, 76 percent, in fact, say they`re very or fairly happy.\par \par \uc2 When asked what`s going well in their lives, seven in 10 cite family. Mental health and hobbies and leisure are other top responses. When we asked about the prospects for 2024, almost half, 47 percent, of Americans say they`re hopeful, more so hopeful than discouraged, although some are mixed.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We want to bring in our political team. Robert Costa is our chief election and campaign correspondent. Major Garrett is our chief Washington correspondent. Nikole Killion is our congressional correspondent. And Ed O`Keefe is our senior White House and political correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for being here in your holiday best.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, 2023, Nicole, kept you very busy. You had a new speaker of the House, this record number of retirements, resignations, departures. And now we start the new year potentially on the cusp of another government shutdown. Will they be able to legislate in 2024?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, I think that`s the million-dollar question. You know, one would hope, but the reality is, it`s going to be a very heavy lift for Congress in the new year, not only trying to fund the government, but also dealing with this unresolved issue of the national security supplemental, whether they can get an agreement on the border. You know, they have ten legislature days before that first government funding deadline.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the issue have very real-world implications. I mean, Ed, some of these border figures are pretty staggering, 10,000 people per day at a time crossing. Does the president need to get directly involved to close this deal or is it too politically complicated for him to do so?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I think they`re trying to keep him from having to engage in the particulars and trying to bring him in towards the end. The fact that, in the closing days of the year it was the homeland security secretary and the chief of staff doing much of the negotiating. That was a signal that they were working through the actual details and the mechanics of what it would mean at the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and other agencies to get certain changes made and whether the lawyers were OK with it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For the first time in decades.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Exactly. And the politics of it though are so much more difficult for the president. He`s got to promise first with Democrats, especially progressives who don`t want to see any semblance of Trump era immigration policy reenacted, but perhaps more importantly on the margins, Latino lawmakers who say you`re now going after a values proposition with a key bloc of voters that need to support you if you expect to win again. There was also concerns that they just weren`t consulted at all in the beginning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there`s a political incentive for him to close a deal here, Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: If he can. And what is important about this immigration conversation, Margaret, it`s not like the ones we`ve gone through for the last 10 years where we talk about comprehensive, meaning the right gets something and the left gets something. The contours of this current debate are all on what Republicans want and are demanding on a policy side. Not even just about money. And that is a seismic shift. And it is something that the White House has come to terms with but hasn`t found a legislative solution or a political way of talking about it. And if there has been one issue for this White House that has been, I think, a blind spot, it has been immigration. And now all of that political upheaval is coming home to the White House and they`re on the cusp of, as Ed indicated, major concessions that will bring policy very close if not identical to Trump policy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you talk to senators about what this will look like, what do we know?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: We don`t know a lot. We do know, for instance, that, you know, the White House has put on the table a willingness to accept limits on asylum, expand detention and deportation efforts. But, you know, there`s also this big sticking point of parole, which the Biden administration has been using, particularly to help, for instance, Afghan evacuees, Ukrainians who are displaced by the Russian war, and Republicans feel that authority is being abused. So, that really has kind of slowed down these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Zelenskyy said that it was an issue of morale, not just money. That it was a statement that the United States was backing away potentially from Ukraine, even if that wasn`t the intention. What are you hearing from your sources on Capitol Hill, because against the backdrop of all these really important issues, we now also have some pretty tricky politics in the House that a new speaker would have to navigate if they get that deal in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s beyond almost something that`s tricky politically. It`s foundationally now complicated for President Biden as he interacts with the Republican Party leadership because on one hand he has a willing partner in Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who looks at Ukraine as a traditional Republican on foreign policy, believes the United States has a role in western Europe, encountering Russian influence in the region. At the same time, President Biden`s also dealing with this new speaker of the House, Mike Johnson, who comes from the Trump wing of the party. And that Trump wing is now fueled by this sense of nationalism, sometimes incoherent, but very certain in its direction in not wanting to give more aid to U.S. allies, to \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries, and in this case also to Ukraine. And that`s something that`s going to confront President Biden for the rest of his presidency, whether it`s until the end of this term or through a second term.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But part of this is just the mechanics, right, of, what does Speaker Johnson want to do? Who does he want to be? He`s from this more conservative element of the party, the Freedom Caucus. What are the choices he wants to make if he also could be ousted, like Kevin McCarthy?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I mean, what choices can he make?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Based on a very narrow majority, more narrow than it was when he became speaker by two seats, and what will the caucus, or the conference rather, that used a tool to oust the previous speaker, because he cut a deal with Democrats to keep the government open, what if Mike Johnson is in a position where, to keep the government open, my goodness, he has to cut a deal with Democrats? How do you look at that existential issue?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He will - right.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: I mean we did see that to a certain extent when they passed this laddered or two-tiered CR, right? Actually more Democrats voted for it than Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The short term funding deal that expires January (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Exactly. And so, you know, it`s possible that may have to happen again. Now, Speaker Johnson has made clear that he does not want to do any more short-term fixes. And so if they can`t figure out this appropriations process, then both Republicans and Democrats may have to accept across-the-board cuts, which neither side wants to do.\par \par \uc2 But I think, you know, if you look at Speaker Johnson`s leadership, he also has shown times where he`s been able to wrangle Republicans together, most notably with the impeachment inquiry vote, where Republicans voted unanimously for it. So, it is a very narrow tight rope that the speaker has to walk, but ultimately I think he`s shown that he can tow both lines when necessary. So, we`ll just have to see how he plays things come the new year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To the campaign trail. Bob Costa, I know you`ve been out there a fair amount. What are you taking away from what people across the country are hearing? They`ve already written off Washington as broken a long time ago, right, but what are they focused on and concerned about?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, Republican voters are looking to see if former President Trump is going to remain in this front running position through the Iowa caucuses and through the New Hampshire primary. It`s evident based on CBS News` latest polling, in my conversation with New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  and others in that state, that Nikki Haley is getting a foothold in New Hampshire. The question is, in the coming weeks can she move from around 25 to 30 percent of support among Republicans in New Hampshire, to 45 to 50 to 50 plus and really make New Hampshire a place where she gets a bounce into her home state of South Carolina and then later into Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Talking to sources on Wall Street, there`s likely going to be a migration of cash toward Haley in the coming weeks to help her survive late into the race. But ultimately, when I`m talking to Republican voters, they still have the same grievances in many cases that Trump has when it comes to the perception of a political establishment, a legal establishment they believe is aligned against the Republican Party. And those grievances are fueling Trump`s support at this time, even as he faces so many other issues.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s interesting that that sense of grievance, you`re saying, is what`s resonating above kitchen table issues, above national security issues? What is that coming from?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It comes from how so many voters channel Trump`s own anger over the 2020 elections. Some share his false claims that he won the election in 2020. He did not. And they believe he deserves a second shot.\par \par \uc2 And it`s so unusual to have a former president running for the nomination again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: I mean I can`t even imagine \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  running in 1984, or George H.W. Bush running in 1996. It`s historically almost unconceivable, but it`s happening and Republican voters, for the most part, are looking at him.\par \par \uc2 But I have detected, Margaret, the support for Trump in some places, especially New Hampshire, noticeably soft. They like Trump but they`re not totally committed to voting for him at this late juncture.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: There is a component of Trump`s message that does address the other two things, I`m not going to get you in wars, we didn`t have any wars when I was president, that`s on the national security, isolationist or nationalist approach, and better off with Trump. They just rolled that campaign slogan out recently.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Meaning, economically you were happier, more secure, felt better when I was there. So, he is trying to address that, but the grievance is the thing that is the rocket fuel and has solidified him all through the long summer of indictments.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Ed, tell me what the president`s message for his re- election is. I was speaking to someone at the White House recently who told me the two issues of most concern to him are gas prices and the numbers at the border leading up to the election.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So, it`s an economic and sort of public safety government management argument, right, is what they`ve been trying to make, that if you look at the economic indicators, inflation is going down, unemployment is staying low, consumer confidence is up. That`s a good thing. Border security, crime, are two issues that they certainly worry about because they know on the margins independent voters all over the country, the Republicans that might be compelled to vote for him could be dissuaded if they see those things, not necessarily handle (ph). He also knows he has to deal with immigration because it is such a base issue of concern, not only an issue for independent voters.\par \par \uc2 They fully expect it will be Trump. They fully expect to be able to throw up the contrast and say, the economy is in better shape, the pandemic`s over, we`re dealing with the global challenges that have occurred. He would do a worse job of it. And you should stick with this guy.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And abortion and (INAUDIBLE) democracy.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And abortion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s what they`re hoping creates momentum.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: They will be there. Those are acts second and third. Acts two and three.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Vice President Harris is going to be out front on abortion rights based on my reporting.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. So, can she motivate those young voters that they need to actually be excited?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: There is evidence that she can. They came out of this college tour she did this fall quite impressed and pleased with the reaction. It`s part of why there`s now this plan for a college tour focused on abortion services, mostly in battleground states, to draw attention to it. But, absolutely, they saw something there that said she can bring back black voters, she can bring back women, she can bring back younger voters, and serve as that sort of partisan cudgel that a running mate often does.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And that coalition is going to be key for Biden if he`s looking to be re-elected in terms of young voters and voters of color in particular. But, you know, some of that support has softened. And so while the Biden campaign argues, well, people just still need to hear our message, we need to get our message out more, people need to understand what we`ve done and what we`ve accomplished. Part of that is using the vice president, I think, to convey that message, but it is one that they are going to have to hammer home because there are many voters saying, we may just sit this one out. We don`t necessarily like Trump, and we`re not going to vote for him, but we may not vote for Biden. And I think that`s going to be a big hurdle for the Biden campaign as they move forward into `24.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to make sure that I ask you, because I know you have spent a lot of time talking to election officials.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We heard this incredible warning from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  about foreign interference, but domestically there`s concern about security.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Sure. Foreign interference is going to be a concern perpetually. And it is. We all love campaigns. For most of my political coverage career I took the process by which we run elections for granted. Overlooking the hundreds of thousands of Americans who do this work either as election administrators, as poll observers or poll workers.\par \par \uc2 I have a deeper appreciation for them now than ever before after 2020. They`re stressed. Many of them are retiring or just quitting under that stress. That is a problem for institutional knowledge in a lot of swing states. That is something that people in this space who watch election administration very closely are worried about. They`re under resourced. Our elections are decentralized.\par \par \uc2 There`s no federal management of elections. We have a lot of elections, they`re costly, they`re local and they`re complex. Those are weaknesses and strengths. We do it pretty well, but this particular part of America that does this heroic work oftentimes unnoticed is under tremendous scrutiny, stress, anxiety, and some are leaving and that does create a gap of knowledge, a gap of experience, that may not help us through 2024. It`s an open question, but there are those in this space who are nervous about it, very much so.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m sure they are. And very real threats as we saw in the last election.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Threats that manifest themselves in lots of different ways. But if you`re an election worker, it doesn`t matter if it`s a text message or if it`s a phone call that`s left on a voicemail in the office, you feel threatened.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: They walk to their cars wondering, should they be looking over -- they actually are looking over their shoulders. And that`s not a space we want our fellow Americans, our neighbors, our friends, to be in when they`re doing this work of helping us cast, count, and certify our votes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And it is incredible. I mean to your point about the brain drain, how -- I remember talking to the Arizona secretary of state about this. All of what happened in the last four or five years is coinciding with a lot of people just aging out. So, it`s not even that they`re quitting because they`re intimidated, they`re quitting because it`s time -- time to retire. Time to not do this anymore and pass it off the next generation.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And a new generation of (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And the concern is that next generation is going to be wearing blue or red visors and sunglasses while they`re counting the votes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, we have to leave this part of the conversation here. We`ll continue our conversation after a quick break.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re back with more from our panel of political correspondents.\par \par \uc2 This is one of my favorite parts, you guys get to predict the future. What do you expect in 2024?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Look, there`s been a lot of talk about micro targets and the ways that candidates are going to try to reach the voters. I predict, however, one of the tried and true pieces of presidential campaigns will endure and that there will be at least one televised debate between the Democrat and Republican presidential nominees\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which would be standard fare in any other year.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It would be, but despite the -\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: But it`s very much uncertain.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, yes, very uncertain.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But is very much uncertain right now given concerns about the Nonpartisan Commission on Presidential Debates -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And the willingness of the potential nominees as we currently imagine them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But if it`s Biden and Trump, they can`t help themselves and they`ll want at least one go at it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anything`s possible this year.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, what about you?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, if former President Trump ends up being the Republican nominee, I predict that he will pick a female running mate. And there are a number of females that are being talked about, female lawmakers. Whether that`s \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 , whether that`s Nancy Mace, for instance, you have Governor \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 , it - it makes sense potentially.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , possibly.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  another one, governor of South Dakota. So, there are a lot of options. Remains to be seen. But I think there potentially will be a woman on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  not the running mate, of course, for obvious reasons.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: No. No, not the running mate. No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, my prediction is kind of 30,000 foot. I think we`re going to look back at 2024 as the year when our relationship to social media changed fundamentally as a country, not only economics, politics, conversation. Up until -- for the last ten years I think our net idea about it was ubiquitous and positive. I think in 2024 it will be sill ubiquitous but net negative. And elites are pulling away from social media platforms. There`s more concern about children being on too much, too much distraction, too much data scraping, too many privacy concerns. There will be, I think, behaviorally a pulling back and a reassessment of social media and all of our behaviors and patterns.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right. And this year we have a new idea, which is to try to find something good and something positive.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It was hard.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s - you know, for people at home, it is overwhelming. It is overwhelming for us in this industry as well, the amount of change, and what a difficult year it has been.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ed, do you have a glimmer of hope?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Took a few days, Margaret, I`m not going to lie, but I did -- I did come up with a story that I was reminded of that happened here in the Washington region to some extent. There`s a guy named Don Scott who was poised to become the speaker of the house of delegates of Virginia next year. He would become the first African American man to hold that position. But, more importantly, he`d be the first former felon. He went through the process in Virginia of getting his record cleared. And it was cleared by a former Republican governor. He became an attorney. Got elected into public office. And now has risen through the ranks in the state legislature to serve in that role. Set his partisanship and the other history aside, it`s just nice to see somebody given a second chance to do something and to try to do it in the public realm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: New beginnings.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, Bob and I both spent a lot of time at the beginning of this year in Georgia, in Plains, Georgia, more specifically when we got that scare about former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , when he entered hospice care. And the fact that he was able to make it for the better part of this year, made it to his 99th birthday, made it to the Peanut Festival in Plains, Georgia, and obviously the tragic news of his wife`s passing, but even made it to that trip in Atlanta to watch her memorial, I think is just a testament to his strength and his grit and continues to be an inspiration for a lot of Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Continues his public service in many ways.\par \par \uc2 Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: My positive story, my good news story is forbearance and the power of concessions. We had two gubernatorial elections in 2023, in Kentucky and Mississippi. The Republican in Kentucky lost, conceded, wished his victor well. In Mississippi, Brandon Presley, the Democrat, lost, wished his Republican well on behalf of the state, and they both conceded willingly, eagerly and, importantly, wished their opponent well on behalf of their state. That is a fundamental strength of our democracy. It`s coming back. It needs to be encouraged everywhere it`s seen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Bob?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: I`m hopeful about younger people in this country, teenagers, college students, so often they`re portrayed in the media, frankly, as people who are addicted to their phones, living on TikTok and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . But when we`re on the campaign trail, we`re often encountering younger people who are attending events, who are knowledgeable about politics, following, you know, their civic culture in wherever they live, and I`m hopeful whenever I meet some of these younger people that not everything is going to be negative, that the younger generation does care. We just got to listen to them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s the only way democracy continues to function if you continue to participate in it. That`s a good, bright note, Bob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTAS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thanks to all of you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Here on FACE THE NATION we`ve covered a lot of tough stories this year. So we asked Mark Strassmann to go back and recap some of the good news.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): Ladies first. Women headlined all over in 2023. Taylor and her jubilant Swifties.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s amazing that like everybody comes together, and we`re all dressed up and we all participate.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): Beyonce and the beehive. Both tours generated billions for local economies, mostly from women cheering their heroes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I have never been more confident and proud to be in my own skin because of her.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): And "Barbie," not just a Hollywood hit, a cultural conversation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Barbie is like such a strong and a hard woman. She has like 90 jobs.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): Good news, speaking of jobs, in 2023 America`s rate of working women between 25 and 54 hit a record high. And the overall economy, inflation is down, recession fears, fading.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL (Federal Reserve Chairman): And we`re seeing inflation making real progress. These are the things we`ve been wanting to see.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): Core inflation is down to 4 percent. Back in January, it was 5.6.\par \par \uc2 MAN: No justice.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No Jeeps.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): 2023 emboldened workers, especially union workers. On strike, nurses, autoworkers, Hollywood. And they all picketed a path to a happy ending on payday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We were finally realizing, wait, we know our worth.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): Fans of comeuppance, or just telling the truth, also hit the jackpot. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  ordered to pay millions for lies he told about two Georgia election workers. And George Santos, the prince of make believe, drummed out of Congress.\par \par \uc2 In entertainment, Michelle Yeoh became the first Asian woman to win the best actress Oscar. And a salute to Admiral Lisa Franchetti. She`s the first ever female member of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.\par \par \uc2 In sports, the Buffalo Bill`s Damar Hamlin played again in an \uc2 NFL\uc2  game, ten months after he nearly died from cardiac arrest on the football field.\par \par \uc2 DAMAR HAMLIN (Buffalo Bills Safety): To be able to still do what I love at the highest level in the world is amazing.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): And Brittney Griner playing again in the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  after languishing for ten months in Russian custody.\par \par \uc2 BRITTNEY GRINER (Phoenix Mercury Player): The love from, you know, the fans when I came out was amazing.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): And, finally, 2023 was a great year for 10 Americans released last week from Venezuelan custody and five Americans released in September by Iran. Home in time for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. We want to wish you all a very happy holiday and we`ll see you again next Sunday. For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020231224ejco00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for December 13, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Meg Oliver, Nicole Sganga, Carter Evans, Ed  O`Keefe, Jan Crawford, Scott MacFarlane \par \uc2 3,758 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: a busy day here in the nation`s capital, the major news from the Supreme Court about the abortion pill, the pause in Donald Trump`s election interference case that could delay his trial, plus breaking news. Congress votes to formally launch an impeachment inquiry into President Biden.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot to get to.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN (Son of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ): I am here.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The dramatic speech from President Biden`s son. Why \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  says he will only testify in his dad`s impeachment inquiry if it`s in public.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Republicans say they`re pursuing the truth. Democrats accuse them of pursuing pure, ugly politics.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Two people under arrest after a scary afternoon at an elementary school, students eating gummy bears contaminated with fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 And our series about the pandemic`s lingering effects on America`s children -- tonight, protecting the mental health of teens.\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK MURTHY (U.S. Surgeon General): I do think that we should have restrictions on phones in the school setting.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for being with us on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with breaking news right here in Washington Capitol Hill, where, just a short time ago. Republicans in the House voting to begin a formal impeachment inquiry into President Biden.\par \par \uc2 The next step follows the yearslong scrutiny of the president and his family that has turned up no proof of high crimes and misdemeanors. Now, Democrats tonight are calling the move an extreme political stunt that has no credibility and no legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 Today`s vote comes on the same day that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  showed up on Capitol Hill, not to testify, but to blast Republicans for what he said were attacks on him to embarrass and damage his father.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane will start us off tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 It has been a busy day.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 It was just hours after the president`s son made that dramatic appearance outside the Capitol defending his father that House Republicans launched an effort to impeach the president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): A party-line vote tonight --\par \par \uc2 MAN: The resolution is adopted.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- will launch another attempt to impeach a U.S. president. Republicans approved a formal impeachment inquiry alleging President Biden might have benefited from his son`s foreign business dealings and has stonewalled them.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RALPH NORMAN (R-South Carolina): What are you scared of? What facts do you not want to come out?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Democrats blasted the move.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (D-Maryland): They have been at it for so long. And, of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to keep it going into the next year for the campaign season.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You think Trump`s directing this?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN: Oh, completely.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The launching of a formal impeachment inquiry gives Republicans more firepower and better legal standing to issue subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 Does this end anywhere short of impeachment articles?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TIM BURCHETT (R-Tennessee): I don`t think so, and --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It`s inevitable?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TIM BURCHETT (R-Tennessee): I think it is.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Ahead of the vote, a House committee subpoenaed the president`s son for a closed-door deposition. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  declined.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN (Son of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ): I`m here today to acknowledge that I have made mistakes in my life and wasted opportunities and privileges I was afforded.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Saying he`d only testify in a public hearing.\par \par \uc2 Speaking before cameras at the Capitol, he described the depths of his drug addiction.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 : During my battle with addiction, my parents were there for me. They literally saved my life.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And he defended his father.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN: There is no evidence to support the allegations that my father was financially involved in my business.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: By snubbing the committee, House Republicans today recommended \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  be investigated for a contempt of Congress criminal charge.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 : They belittled my recovery, and they have tried to dehumanize me, all to embarrass and damage my father, who has devoted his entire public life to service.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a statement tonight, President Biden said of Republicans: "Instead of doing anything to help make Americans` lives better, they`re focused on attacking me with lies" -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Supreme Court agreeing today to hear two politically charged cases involving abortion and one that could impact former President Trump`s election interference trial. That federal case was temporarily placed on hold today.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford is here with us to help us break it down.\par \par \uc2 OK, Jan, the first issue, let`s talk about that. The Supreme Court says they`re going to look at the drug mifepristone, which is the medication used in most common form of abortions.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yeah, I mean, it could have a tremendous impact on how and at what point in a woman`s pregnancy she can access that so-called abortion pill, even in states where it`s legal.\par \par \uc2 Now, a federal appeals court said the \uc2 FDA\uc2  was wrong in how it approved that drug mifepristone for abortions up to 10 weeks. And it said women didn`t need to see a doctor in person to get it. Those lower court decisions, they are on hold while this case plays out. So the pill will remain available for now.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And then let`s talk about this second case. This is by several January 6 defendants who say prosecutors went too far. This could have a big impact, right?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Huge impact. I mean, it literally could affect hundreds of January 6 defendants, including former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, they`re all facing other charges for January 6, but this one is under a federal law that prohibits obstructing any official proceeding. It sounds pretty clear, right? I mean, on January 6, certifying the election, that`s an official proceeding.\par \par \uc2 But the defendants say, no, that law is talking about people who destroyed documents and records, and it shouldn`t apply to us. Now, if the Supreme Court agrees, that could have a big impact on Trump`s election interference case. It could cut two of the four charges against him or potentially delay that trial.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, who covers the Supreme Court for us, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to the White House, where President Biden and Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  met with several family members of Americans being held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe is at the White House, where the families were looking for answers as to what is being done to get their loved ones home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): The two-hour meeting was the president`s first face-to-face encounter with families of Americans being held hostage in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN DEKEL-CHEN (Father of Hamas Hostage): We could have no better friend in Washington or in the White House than President Biden himself and his administration.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But little is known about the state of those still being held.\par \par \uc2 Was the president able to share anything about their condition?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN DEKEL-CHEN: We are going to keep the -- the content of the conversation private.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The White House says there are eight Americans still unaccounted for, including Americans still being held hostage. It is believed 135 total hostages of varying nationalities remain in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The meeting came a day after the president, for the first time publicly since the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks, questioned Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , saying he has to change. Israel`s coalition government is making it difficult to establish a Palestinian state.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden also said Israel is losing global support for its indiscriminate bombing of Gaza. Attacks deemed indiscriminate are usually considered violations of international law.\par \par \uc2 But did he misspeak yesterday when he said that Israel was carrying out indiscriminate attacks?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (NSC Coordinator For Strategic Communications): The president speaks and has spoken, Ed, about our concerns over civilian casualties in Gaza and about urging our Israeli counterparts to be more careful and more deliberate.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Today, in Israel, Netanyahu defiantly said: "We are continuing until the end," adding: "In the face of international pressures, nothing will stop us."\par \par \uc2 In Gaza, heavy fighting continued, leading to the deaths of 10 Israeli soldiers, their highest single-day losses so far.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: While the president met with families of hostages being held in Gaza, it`s incredibly rare for him to ever have contact with families of the dozens of other Americans being wrongfully detained, something advocates for those being held in countries like Afghanistan, China and Venezuela called out today -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It was a historic day on Wall Street. The Dow soared more than 500 points, closing over 37,000 for the first time ever. The gain of nearly 1.5 percent came after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  kept interest rates unchanged and signaled at least three interest rate cuts next year. Good news for the economy.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to that major recall involving more than two million \uc2 Tesla\uc2  cars. That`s nearly all of the electric vehicles \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has sold in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Carter Evans tells us how the company intends to fix a problem with the car`s autopilot system.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): There`s almost nothing left of this \uc2 Tesla\uc2  after it slammed into a fire truck on a Northern California freeway last February. The driver was killed, and investigators believe autopilot was engaged when it happened.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of dozens of deadly crashes since 2016 allegedly involving the technology. And it led to a \uc2 National Highway Traffic Safety Administration\uc2  investigation.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  owner Susie Magged hopes the resulting recall leads to safer use of autopilot.\par \par \uc2 SUSIE MAGGED (\uc2 Tesla\uc2  Driver): I was driving on the freeway and saw a man with his seat almost fully reclined, feet out the window, and hands, like, behind his head going about 95 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Nearly every \uc2 Tesla\uc2  sold in the U.S. is affected. An over- the-air software update will now enable the cars to give drivers more warnings when they`re not paying attention and even suspend the technology if drivers use it incorrectly.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: This recall is actually written in blood.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Robert Sumwalt is former chair of the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 . He says he spoke to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  personally during the agency`s own investigation into autopilot six years ago.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: And, as the chairman of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , I`m talking to him about this investigation, and he hung up on me. I think that shows an arrogance within the organization that is leading to safety problems and killing people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, the software fix has already rolled out to some customers.\par \par \uc2 We reached out to \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , but have not heard back. However, on Monday, the company posted that it believes the cars that use its autopilot system are safer than those without it -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Carter, powerful interviews and such important context.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to a frightening incident in Central Virginia. Two people have been arrested in connection to seven elementary students falling ill after being exposed to fentanyl at their school.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nicole Sganga reports on the disturbing details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA (voiceover): Tonight, shock inside an elementary school after residue from the highly lethal drug fentanyl was found inside a bag filled with gummy bears and shared by fourth graders.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM WELLS (Superintendent, Amherst County, Virginia, Public Schools): The fentanyl came from somebody`s house, right? And so we need to -- we need to wake up as a county and as a community to realize that these things are real, these things are happening.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Two suspects, Clifford Dugan and Nicole Sanders, were arrested early this morning in connection to the incident. Both face charges, including contributing to the delinquency of a minor.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM WELLS: We don`t feel this was an intentional event, but we are continuing our investigation.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Police say five students were taken to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 HAYDEN FLOYD (Student): We felt hot. We felt nauseous.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Including fourth grader Hayden Floyd. The 10-year-old said he ate 10 gummies before heading to the school nurse, then was loaded into an ambulance with his mother.\par \par \uc2 HAYDEN FLOYD: I was shaking a lot because I was really scared. And my mom came. She was right beside me the whole time. And I -- I was just like: "Sorry, mom, sorry, because she was crying."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: All students have been discharged from the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Hayden`s mother, Kristina Wright, tells us she`s so grateful her son is OK, but she never thought she`d have to have a conversation about fentanyl with her 10-year-old -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Oh, so scary.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sganga, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to part three in our series, The COVID Generation. Students isolated during the lockdown turned to their cell phones and social media. And it has taken a toll on their mental health; 57 percent of high school girls in the U.S. said they felt persistently sad during the pandemic. That is double that of the boys.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver shows us what one school is doing to help.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): At Newburgh Free Academy in New York, cell phones are locked away for the entire school day, including lunch.\par \par \uc2 Students like Tyson Hill and Monique May say it`s a relief after constantly being on their phones during the COVID lockdown, when screen time among adolescents more than doubled.\par \par \uc2 How much do you blame the phones and social media for taking a toll on your mental health?\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE MAY (Student): All of it.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: All of it.\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE MAY: For me personally, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TYSON HILL (Student): I blame my darkest moments because of my phone.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Have you ever been bullied online or felt left out after looking at social media?\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE MAY: Of course. Throughout my middle school experience, like, there was a lot of people talking about you, whether it be on Snapchat, posting a story that made fun of the way you looked, and it made me feel depressed.\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK MURTHY (U.S. Surgeon General): The youth mental health crisis is the defining public health issue of our time. If we do not address it with urgency, then I worry we will lose an entire generation of children to depression, anxiety and suicide.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: This year, U.S. Surgeon General Vivek Murthy issued an advisory on the effects of social media on youth mental health.\par \par \uc2 Would you consider advising the country to ban smartphones during school hours?\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK MURTHY: Well, I do think that we should have restrictions on phones in the school setting.\par \par \uc2 We fundamentally have to understand that these devices, and, in particular, social media is behaving largely as an addictive element.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Newburgh Free Academy assistant principal Ebony Clark says banning phones has helped cut down online bullying.\par \par \uc2 EBONY CLARK (Assistant Principal, Newburgh Free Academy): All I`m doing is giving them the opportunity to engage in school and leave the drama outside these doors.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Now that you don`t have that distraction during the school day, how much has it helped you as a person?\par \par \uc2 MONIQUE MAY: I`m more confident in who I am. And I think that just comes from not being able to worry about what other people are saying about me, just being me.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Meg Oliver, CBS News, Newburgh, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The nation`s poison centers report an alarming number of calls due to the popular weight loss drugs.\par \par \uc2 We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In tonight`s Health Watch: growing reports of dangerous reactions to the main drug in the weight loss and diabetes medications Wegovy and Ozempic.\par \par \uc2 From January through November, America`s poison centers reported almost 3,000 calls involving the drug semaglutide. That`s up 1400 percent since 2019. A doctor for the organization says most of the calls were related to dosage errors.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to CBS News, drugmaker \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  says patient safety is its top priority and referred patients to the Web site semaglutide.com.\par \par \uc2 A hiker from California who fell 1,000 feet from a trail in Hawaii last week is telling a remarkable story of survival; 34-year-old Ian Snyder says he landed near a waterfall and was able to drink from a stream. That helped him to hang on for three days until he was finally spotted by a rescue team and then airlifted to safety. Snyder broke several bones, but says he`s otherwise OK and grateful for being rescued.\par \par \uc2 What a story.\par \par \uc2 Siblings who are believed to be the last surviving twins from the Holocaust share their inspiring story. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, you can listen, subscribe wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight marks the seventh night of Hanukkah, the Jewish celebration of the miracle of light that kept shining through the darkest of times.\par \par \uc2 It calls to mind the miraculous survival of some Jews during the Holocaust. One such miracle includes the story of what is believed to be the last surviving twins from the Holocaust, Steven and Marion. They were just 6 years old when they were sent from their home in Amsterdam to a concentration camp.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voiceover): The Hess family, like millions of Jews, was taken from their home by the Nazis.\par \par \uc2 MARION EIN LEWIN (Holocaust Survivor): The Holocaust seems like ancient history, so we have to find a way for -- not to be that, for that to be a lasting lesson.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sent by train in 1944 to Bergen-Belsen, a concentration camp where more than 50,000 died, including Anne Frank. Marion and Steven credit their parents for keeping them together.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN HESS (Holocaust Survivor): They never, ever gave up. And they were just determined to keep us alive. The food in Bergen-Belsen was kohlrabies, turnips, calories -- about 600 calories, to keep you alive.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Their father was assigned to heavy labor. Eight decades later, they still remember their mother`s sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN HESS: She realized that my father needed a lot more nourishment than she did, and even though we were all starving, she gave half of her portions to my father. It still gets to me.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It gets to me.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN HESS: Yeah, to keep him going.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Where do you think that will to survive came from, that determination?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN HESS: I had a real sense of inner courage and strength.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Steven and Marion, now 85 years old, hope their story can be a lesson of remembrance.\par \par \uc2 What has it been like for you since October 7th?\par \par \uc2 MARION EIN LEWIN: It`s something that makes you watch television morning, noon, and night.\par \par \uc2 There`s got to be a better way, that it can`t always be kind of the scorecard of how many get murdered and how many get displaced. I hope that something will happen where, when these conflicts happen, that there`s a real kind of effort to have a long-term solution, you know, where both sides feel like they have a chance for a future.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The Hesses found their future here in the U.S. arriving by boat in 1947.\par \par \uc2 What do you remember about that? You remember your parents.\par \par \uc2 MARION EIN LEWIN: A lot.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN HESS: A lot. A lot. Our parents got us up early to -- to pass the Statue of Liberty. And late in life, it became a very precious memory.\par \par \uc2 MARION EIN LEWIN: But whenever I see the Statue of Liberty, it rings bells, because that was the symbol of our freedom and the ability of -- for us to have a new life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And you can read more about their incredible story of survival in the book "Inseparable."\par \par \uc2 The 25 new movies being preserved for history -- that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: The \uc2 Library of Congress\uc2  has announced the 25 new titles being added to the National Film Registry.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Apollo\uc2  13, 1995, \uc2 Universal Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The only problem this year was narrowing down the list.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Apollo\uc2  13" was joined by other box office hits of the `90s--\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Home Alone, 1990, 20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Terminator 2: Judgement Day, 1991, Tristar Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- like "Home Alone" and "Terminator 2: Judgment Day" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Lady and the Tramp, 1955, \uc2 Walt Disney Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- along with the animated classics "Lady and the Tramp" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Nightmare Before Christmas, 1993, Buena Vista Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- and "The Nightmare Before Christmas."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 12 Years a Slave, 2013, \uc2 Fox Searchlight Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The newest films are from 2013, the drama "12 Years a Slave" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 20 Feet from Stardom, 2013, \uc2 The Weinstein Company\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- and the documentary "20 Feet From Stardom," winners of best picture and best documentary at the Academy Awards. The movies selected for preservation are based on their cultural and historic importance to America`s film heritage.\par \par \uc2 Well, that does it for tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231214ejcd00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for December 11, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Robert Costa, Manuel Bojorquez, Nikole Killion,  Charlie D`Agata, Ed O`Keefe, Meg Oliver, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 3,764 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the breaking news out of the Supreme Court, just hours after an unusual request from the special counsel investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the nation`s highest court agreeing to fast track the process.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the South cleans up after a deadly tornado outbreak.\par \par \uc2 The massive cleanup as thousands are without power and cold temperatures roll in. Pressure mounts as faculty members come to the defense of Harvard`s president.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the new financial fallout at elite universities.\par \par \uc2 A mechanical issue forces a Delta flight to divert to a small Canadian airport, the travel nightmare that happened next.\par \par \uc2 ADITI SHANKAR (Passenger): It`s been more than 15-plus hours since any of us have had a real meal.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We start a new series looking at the pandemic`s lingering effects on America`s children.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In Jefferson County, Kentucky, the number of students chronically absent has skyrocketed since the pandemic. We`ll follow along as they search for students.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Barbie, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from The Bear)\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from \uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 , 2023)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the nominees are in. Who received the most Golden Globe nominations? We`ll tell you.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Succession)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we begin with breaking news. The Supreme Court just moments ago taking a step forward in a historic case involving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Late this afternoon, the Supreme Court agreeing to hear an extraordinary petition from Special Counsel Jack Smith, who is pursuing this criminal prosecution of Trump for his efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Now, the nation`s highest court is now set to weigh in for the first time on the historic criminal prosecution of a former president. The Special Counsel is asking the justices to break from tradition and decide quickly on whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has immunity.\par \par \uc2 Here is what the Special Counsel wrote in this petition: "This case presents a fundamental question at the heart of our democracy: whether a former president is absolutely immune from federal prosecution for crimes committed while in office."\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is our Chief Election and Campaign Correspondent. He`s here to help us understand these big developments.\par \par \uc2 So, Robert, the Special Counsel is trying to make sure that his case moves forward on March 4th, right?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Norah, that`s exactly right. Jack Smith is looking into the future. He knows when the January 6th trial begins next year. Former President Trump will claim it`s illegal and try to raise that with the highest court. That view is backed up by our sources who say Trump is pushing his lawyers to appeal, appeal and appeal, and argue he should be immune from prosecution about his alleged conduct while serving as president.\par \par \uc2 But now, Smith is trying to get ahead of Trump`s strategy to make sure he`s on solid ground legally and won`t be delayed by a flurry of motions from Trump.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s team pushed back against him today saying Smith is attempting to bypass the appeal process. They now have until December 20th to respond before the high court weighs in, if they do at all. All of this, of course, is going to take place as the Republican presidential race heats up next spring and Trump could be wrapping up the nomination.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Also, in this petition, why did the Special Counsel reference U.S. versus Nixon?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Norah, history hovers over everything, especially with this case. And remember, back in 1974, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  said President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  couldn`t protect his case -- his tapes. Now, the special counsel Jack Smith is looking for a similar ruling from the court to say prosecutors can move forward and try to prosecute a president or a former president.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And do it expeditiously.\par \par \uc2 Robert, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to severe weather and heavy rain and snow across the country, flooding still a concern in New England over the next few days. This has the same storm system that led to a tornado outbreak with at least eight twisters touching down across Tennessee, Kentucky, Mississippi and North Carolina killing six people.\par \par \uc2 There was one EF3 tornado with winds up to 150 miles per hour. It was on the ground for an hour, and it traveled 40 miles.\par \par \uc2 CBS`s Manuel Bojorquez shows us the devastation tonight from hard-hit Clarksville, Tennessee. That`s about an hour outside Nashville.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Residents captured the disaster unfolding over Middle Tennessee Saturday.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Oh, my god.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Electrical equipment blowing up, lighting up the night sky. Daylight revealed the devastation. After more than a dozen possible tornadoes tore through the state, leaving nearly a hundred injured and at least six dead, including 10-year-old Arlan Burnham, 31-year-old Floridana Gabriel Perez and her two-year-old son, Anthony Mendez also died when the tornado tossed a nearby trailer, crushing their mobile home.\par \par \uc2 (FELIPE MENDEZ DOMINGO, Victim`s Family Member, speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Perez`s husband said in Spanish that he and their other two children were also home and lucky to have survived.\par \par \uc2 WANDA MCLEMORE (Madison, TN Resident): One of the firemen, I said, "Did they find the baby?" And they said, "Yes, they couldn`t find it because she was holding it, covering it, protecting it."\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Churches and emergency shelters are trying to provide the basics to those who lost all, as small armies of volunteers help with cleanup, like Ryan Puckett.\par \par \uc2 How great is the need here?\par \par \uc2 RYAN PUCKETT (Volunteer): It`s -- it`s pretty great. I mean, we need people. We need donations. It`s cold and it`s only going to keep getting colder, and these people need hope, quite plain and simple.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The system`s impact is far-reaching. It also spawned tornadoes in North Carolina and Alabama, bringing heavy rain to New England, where flood watches are still in effect tonight.\par \par \uc2 Back in Tennessee, Tonya Osborne says she`s lucky she wasn`t home when her house was ripped apart. But the recovery won`t be easy.\par \par \uc2 TONYA OSBORNE (Tennessee resident who lost home): My whole life has been reduced to several garbage bags and what I can get in them and what I can salvage.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And as the cleanup continues here in Clarksville tonight, there are still more than 14,000 customers in this area who do not have power. And temperatures are expected to dip below freezing once again overnight. Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the fallout for elite college presidents continues after that congressional testimony about how to handle antisemitism on college campuses. One of the three presidents is out of a job and as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion reports, she may not be alone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Amid calls for her removal, supporters of Harvard president Claudine Gay rallied to her defense, from alumni to staff. More than 700 faculty members signed a letter urging Harvard`s governing boards to resist political pressures that are at odds with Harvard`s commitment to academic freedom. History professor, Alison Frank Johnson, co-authored the letter.\par \par \uc2 ALISON FRANK JOHNSON (\uc2 Harvard University\uc2  History Professor): Those 700-plus signatories all agreed on the fundamental importance of the university from political interference.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tuesday)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-New York): Does calling for the genocide of Jews violate Harvard`s rules of bullying and harassment? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE GAY (\uc2 Harvard University\uc2  President): It can be, depending on the context.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Gay`s response at a congressional hearing last week, along with the presidents of MIT and \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , ignited a political firestorm.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tuesday)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH MAGILL (Former President of \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 ): It is a context-dependent decision, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2 : It`s a context-dependent decision, that`s your testimony today?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Saturday, Penn president, Liz Magill, resigned.\par \par \uc2 EYAL YAKOBY (University of Pennsylvania Student): I`d say it`s bittersweet.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Eyal Yakoby is a senior then who attended the hearing.\par \par \uc2 EYAL YAKOBY: And I think what it exposed is what a lot of us have been saying for a while now, is that there`s this sense of moral ambivalence when it comes to antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Following Magill`s resignation, Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  posted on X, "One down. Two to go." \uc2 Michael Roth\uc2  is president of \uc2 Wesleyan University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ROTH\uc2  (\uc2 Wesleyan University\uc2 . President): And I hope they don`t lose their job because they would then be subject to these outside forces, the Republican congresswoman on the one hand, but also these big donors who are trying to throw their weight around. And I think that`s not good for the long range health of these schools.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Before Magill`s resignation here at Penn, at least one major donor threatened to pull a $100 million donation. And a prominent Harvard alum claims the university is missing out in over a billion dollars in terminated donations in light of the controversy. Harvard`s president has apologized for her testimony. Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to some more breaking news from New York City. First responders are on the scene at an apartment building in the Bronx that partially collapsed this afternoon. You can see a corner of this seven-story building crumbled to the ground, exposing several rooms to the street.\par \par \uc2 Officials say they have evacuated everyone, but they continue to search through the rubble for any victims. So far, there are no reports of injuries.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas now to the war in Gaza, the Israeli military says it now controls the main square in Gaza City, the headquarters of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  calls it the beginning of the end for the terror group.\par \par \uc2 CBS`s Charlie D`Agata reports from Israel.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): The Israeli military released new videos said to show troops advancing further into southern Gaza, a house-by-house battle of urban warfare. With \uc2 Hamas\uc2  officials updating the death toll past 18,000 today, envoys from the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  visited the Egyptian border crossing with Rafah.\par \par \uc2 The U.S.` sole veto against a ceasefire has fueled anti-war protests in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 The anger and the outrage is growing here in the West Bank with every day the war goes on and every victim inside Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Aimed not just at Israel, but increasingly against America.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: My message is to the United States is not to use the veto against the ceasefire because they believe in democracy and they support democracy. And democracy is saving lives.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Despite those frustrations, the U.S. is already part of post-war plans for Gaza, according to Palestinian Minister of Social Affairs, Ahmad Majdalani.\par \par \uc2 AHMAD MAJDALANI: There are discussion. They have idea.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Those ideas may not be the same. Their plan includes an element of what may remain of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . But Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has called for the group`s total destruction.\par \par \uc2 AHMAD MAJDALANI: Maybe Israel, they can destroy this military forces, but they cannot destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as an organization.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: An organization not only blamed for the massacre of more than 1,200 Israelis, but reiterating threats that not a single hostage will leave unless all its demands for a prisoner release are met. Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war is creating political pressure on President Biden back here at home. Dozens of protesters were arrested at the Capitol today as they demanded a ceasefire in the war. And in a new CBS News poll finds most Americans disapprove of the president`s handling of the conflict and that number is growing as the war stretches into its third month.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe travels to the battleground state of Michigan to understand how the war could impact Biden`s re-election hopes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Twenty-three-year-old Adam Abusalah concerns about President Biden are an alarm bell for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: As a community, we`re tired of voting for the lesser of two evils.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Four years ago, he campaigned for the President, urging neighbors in predominantly Arab-American Dearborn, Michigan, to vote against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: If you were to ask me two months ago if I was going to vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , yeah, I would have held my nose and voted for him.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But as the administration continues standing by Israel`s deadly attacks in Gaza --\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: In November, we remember.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: In November, we remember.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Abusala says he and his community can no longer stand by the President.\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: The same way that they`re being silent right now in the face of injustices, we`re going to be silent in November 2024.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Abdullah Hammoud is Dearborn`s first-ever Arab-American mayor and he says many here have lost loved ones during the fighting.\par \par \uc2 ABDULLAH HAMMOUD (Dearborn, MI Mayor): What`s unfolding overseas, it`s not a political crisis and it`s not complicated. It`s a humanitarian one.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: A Democrat, the mayor applauds the president`s domestic accomplishments, but is now struggling with his support.\par \par \uc2 If you face the binary choice of re-electing the president or re-electing the former president, what do you do?\par \par \uc2 ABDULLAH HAMMOUD: Well, you have an issue where you have both individuals with blood on their hands. One is spewing obviously very hateful rhetoric, but we have a current president that -- whose policies overseas have resulted in the loss of nearly 20,000 Gazans.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Holding Michigan is key to a Biden victory in 2024, 146,000 Muslim Americans voted here in 2020, with nearly 70 percent nationally going for the President. But Abusalah warns the anger here could cost the President the election.\par \par \uc2 So then what will the people do, stay home?\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: No, we`re going to go vote, but we`re going to keep the top of the ticket empty.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You would skip voting for president in 2024?\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Even if it meant \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might win the presidency?\par \par \uc2 ADAM ABUSALAH: If you were to tell me that my vote would be the vote whether Biden wins in Michigan or not, I will still not vote for Biden.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, from the campaign trail, now Ed O`Keefe joins us from the White House as another issue facing President Biden is Ukraine funding. President Zelenskyy in Washington tonight as Congress blocks aid. What`s at stake here, Ed?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, Norah, this really is a last-ditch attempt by President Zelenskyy to save needed war funding that`s been caught up in a fight between Congress and the President over border policy.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned President Biden personally invited Zelenskyy to Washington, first for bipartisan meetings on Capitol Hill. Then they`ll have a press conference together here at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy is so concerned about the impasse that in a speech earlier today here in Washington, he warned, quote, "If there`s anyone inspired by unresolved issues on Capitol Hill, it`s Putin and his sick clique." Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s pretty strong. Ed O`Keefe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now we begin a new series looking at what`s been dubbed the COVID generation, students whose lives were forever changed by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver reports how young people continue to feel the effects not to their health but to their education.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In Louisville, Kentucky, Chris Lantz is part of the attendance team, making up to 30 house calls a week, searching for chronically absent students.\par \par \uc2 First stop of the day, what happened?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LANTZ (Jefferson County Public School Pupil Personnel Assistant Director: Yep, no answer.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: No answer again?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LANTZ: No answer again. Same.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Same thing?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LANTZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In this school district in Jefferson County, 63 percent of families rely on schools for meals. Chronic absenteeism, defined as at least 10 absences within the school year, has always been an issue here. But since the pandemic, 38 percent more students are missing weeks, sometimes months of school.\par \par \uc2 Last year, nearly 14 million students nationwide were chronically absent, nearly twice as many compared to previous years.\par \par \uc2 MARTY POLLIO (Jefferson County Public School Superintendent): I think it is a major crisis.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Jefferson County superintendent, Marty Pollio.\par \par \uc2 MARTY POLLIO: When you think of housing instability, food instability, a student who doesn`t have clean clothes may not come to school.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: There is a youth resource center in each Jefferson County school that provides everything from clean clothes and hygiene products to food for struggling students.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LANTZ: We don`t want to punish them for not going to school. We want to take away all the barriers that might be keeping them from coming to school.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Students like 19-year-old Hector, who in addition to school, works 40 hours a week to help support his family.\par \par \uc2 How many school days did you miss last year?\par \par \uc2 HECTOR: Like half of the year, I didn`t come to school.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Half of the year, you didn`t come to school?\par \par \uc2 HECTOR: Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: After several house calls, Lantz was able to convince Hector to return to school. How does it feel that this school helped bring you back to school after you missed almost half a year last year?\par \par \uc2 HECTOR: That feels like I`m special.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Mm-hm.\par \par \uc2 HECTOR: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: With added support, Hector will graduate high school in 2025 at 20 years old.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel like you`re making a difference, especially when you`re going to these doors and no one`s even answering?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LANTZ: I think if you affect one person, that`s a difference. Especially, in some of our communities, you could save a kid.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Meg Oliver, CBS News, Jefferson County, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We`ve got some breaking news on that historic Texas abortion case. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we`re learning that the woman who sued Texas so she could have an abortion is leaving the state to get the procedure. It comes after what`s being described as a hellish week for Kate Cox. Late Friday, the Texas Supreme Court blocked an order stopping the 31-year-old from having an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And to make the legal limbo more confusing, Texas` high court just vacated its order since she left the state. Doctors say her fetus has a fatal condition and without emergency care, the mother of two`s life is at risk. And she may not be able to get pregnant again in the future.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of Delta passengers flying from Amsterdam to Detroit get stranded in a remote part of Canada. We`ve got the details of their travel nightmare. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Passengers aboard a trans-Atlantic Delta Airlines flight got stranded over the weekend at a remote military base in Canada.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave has more on their unexpected detour.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADITI SHANKAR: You can see outside it`s starting to rain and snow.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): A mechanical issue stranded 270 Delta passengers flying from Amsterdam to Detroit in remote Goose Bay, Canada.\par \par \uc2 ADITI SHANKAR: The issue they said was there was a problem with the deicing on one of the engines and they had to make a landing urgently.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Goose Bay is a military base that has a long runway, that`s what you need. The key is to safety, get it on the ground first, figure out how you get everybody off later.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Delta sent a rescue plane. But the airline says due to weather and runway conditions, the crew timed out, leaving passengers stuck on board for hours before finally getting into military barracks as temperatures approached freezing.\par \par \uc2 ADITI SHANKAR: All we`ve gotten are some snacks and the last real meal we had was 15 hours ago. And most of us, we weren`t anticipating to be in Canada in this cold area, so we don`t even have jackets.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Delta sent a second rescue flight this morning, hoping to get passengers out before a major winter storm.\par \par \uc2 ADITI SHANKAR: We`re very anxious, and also angry that this has taken so long.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: After more than 24 hours, passengers finally took off for Detroit late this afternoon. Delta is apologizing. Kris Van Cleave, CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That is a travel nightmare.\par \par \uc2 All right. Taylor Swift`s golden year shines on with a big Hollywood honor. We`ve got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the two blockbuster movies that dominated the summer box office are leading this year`s Golden Globe nominations.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Barbie, 2023, Warner Bros. Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: "Barbie" is up for nine awards, including Best Musical or Comedy.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from \uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 , 2023, \uc2 Universal Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: On the drama side, "\uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2 " is up for eight Golden Globes, facing off against films, including Martin Scorsese`s "Killers of the Flower Moon" --\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Killers of the Flower Moon, 2023, Apple Studios)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- and Bradley Cooper`s "Maestro."\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Maestro, 2023, Netflix)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, Taylor Swift`s concert movie is among eight movies - -\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour, 2023)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- that were nominated in the new box office achievement category.\par \par \uc2 The 81st annual Golden Globe Awards will air live on Sunday, January 7th, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and Paramount Plus.\par \par \uc2 Always a reminder, I need to watch some more movies, because they all look good.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell, good night.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231212ejcb00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for December 9, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Paul Goodloe, Ramy Inocencio, Willie James Inman,  Jarred Hill, Joy Benedict, Cristian Benavides, Kris Van Cleave, Janet  Shamlian \par \uc2 3,665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tonight, wild weather. Storms sweep the country, snow in the west and a tornado warning in the south and high winds and flooding rain headed to the northeast. We will have the latest forecast.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, deepening crisis in Gaza. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says the most dangerous place in the world to be a child is Gaza, where half the population is starving. This comes after the U.S. vetoes a \uc2 UN\uc2  vote for an immediate ceasefire. We`ll tell you why.\par \par \uc2 Plus, abortion battle: A short-lived legal win for a Texas woman fighting the state`s abortion ban. Why she is now in legal limbo.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BERG (Attorney): Both sides can go to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And inflation forecast. With falling gas prices, easing mortgage rates and lower employment, will the numbers add up for you in 2024?\par \par \uc2 And later, friends with benefits. Why connecting with someone outside your age group can make your life better.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: What`s next for you two?\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN: I think just endless adventures. I can`t wait to see what`s next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from Washington with Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 It is a rough weekend across the nation with severe weather from coast-to- coast. This tornado terrified people northwest of Nashville knocking down trees and power lines.\par \par \uc2 Dime-sized hail battered Arkansas and now, all of that soaking rain and wind are heading northeast.\par \par \uc2 While out West, they`re dealing with snow. Let`s bring in meteorologist, Paul Goodloe from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PAUL GOODLOE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Hey, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 We`re still tracking severe weather on this early December day here, strong thunderstorms, heavy rain damaging winds is going to be around as we head overnight and into your Sunday as well, so heavy downpours, flash flooding concerns, but everyone dealing with the gusty winds. I`ll show you that coming up.\par \par \uc2 But also, we are not just dealing with this across the south, we are dealing with this across the northeast as well where, today, it was a mild day. Tomorrow, temperature is warmer, but wetter.\par \par \uc2 All the rain coming in here, also, the wind. And the backside, we crash down -- the temperatures are crashing down some snow. But it is the wind which sticks around.\par \par \uc2 All this rain, two, three, maybe five inches of rain will cause some flood concerns, maybe a foot of snow on the backside after the rain, but everybody dealing with the winds at times. The Cape and the Islands, hurricane force winds are possible as we head into your Monday morning. Big time impact to the airports here.\par \par \uc2 Also right now, of course the West Coast, we are dealing with an offshore flow, Santa Ana winds, fire dangerous, sky high we have wind advisories even high wind warnings for much of Southern California including the Vegas area in southern Nevada -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Paul Goodloe of \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Israel is widening its hunt for members of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says half the population in Gaza is starving.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio is in Tel Aviv with more.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Adriana, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s ambassador to the United Nations is thanking President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  after the United States issued a veto against a \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  resolution calling for a ceasefire between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here in Hostage Square in Tel Aviv, the focus though is solely on getting those 100 or so abductees still in Gaza home. Israel maintains that military pressure will make that happen faster.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Nine weeks to the day since \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` surprise terror attack and Israel`s military pounded Gaza from the north to the south.\par \par \uc2 This new video said to show soldiers targeting militants in Jabalia in Israel`s mission to destroying \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but destroying families, too.\par \par \uc2 Some here forced to bury their dead in the streets with official cemeteries too full or too dangerous to reach.\par \par \uc2 The devastation stretching as far south as Rafah, on Egypt`s border, supposedly the Strip`s safest area.\par \par \uc2 HASAM SHARIF (University student): This place was a shelter, just a very simple home. They got out from the rubbles, maybe more than four kids. They were injured.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Eighteen-year-old university student, Hasam Sharif (sp?) has a message for President Biden he shared with our \uc2 CBS\uc2  news producer, Marwan Al Ghoul.\par \par \uc2 HASAM SHARIF: I hope there are still people in America who have a gold of heart -- a heart of gold and just to -- just to go out. I want all of you to go out to stop all these massacres.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: The \uc2 World Food Programme\uc2  now says about half of Gaza`s population of more than two million don`t have enough food.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "I came here to find starvation," said this father who fled south to Rafah. "This isn`t milk. It`s basically water." And clean drinking water is a luxury, too.\par \par \uc2 In Tel Aviv, on this third night of Hanukkah, families of hostages still hold out hope, like Mikhail Levy (sp?), his wife was murdered by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , his brother is still missing.\par \par \uc2 You`re lighting the menorah every night without your brother. How is that feeling?\par \par \uc2 MIKHAIL LEVY (Brother still missing): Difficult. It`s even more difficult because Or in Hebrew means light.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Do you believe in miracles?\par \par \uc2 MIKHAIL LEVY: I believe he will be back. You can call it miracle. You can call it whatever you want, but I believe he will be back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And tonight, we have confirmation an Israeli hostage has been killed, his name is 25-year-old Israeli soldier, Sahar Baruch. His kibbutz confirmed that. His family is saying that the return of his body should be part of any future deal.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So much misery. Ramy Inocencio, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is moving ahead with plans to get more weapons to Israel. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Willie James Inman is in Washington with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Willie, good evening.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is moving forward with a sell of $106 million worth of tank shells to Israel bypassing a congressional review process as calls grow for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Chants of "Free Palestine" could be heard outside a Biden campaign fundraiser last night in Santa Monica, which the President attended.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. flexed its veto power over a \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  resolution for an immediate ceasefire. The U.S. delegation said the resolution was divorced from reality and noted its lack of condemnation against the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` October attack.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. abstained from the vote.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. veto drew sharp criticism from allies and human rights groups including \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2 , which said that the veto puts the U.S. at risk of being complicit of war crimes.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. officials continue to stress for the protection of civilians in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Willie James Inman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, the president of the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  resigned. Liz Magill drew criticism for her congressional testimony Tuesday, when she was asked whether calls for the genocide of Jews violated her school`s policies. She said it depended on the context.\par \par \uc2 The presidents of Harvard and MIT are also being scrutinized for their answers.\par \par \uc2 Now to legal whiplash in Texas. The pregnant woman with a fatal fetal diagnosis who was granted the right to an abortion one day before that ruling was put on hold by the State Supreme Court weighs her options.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jarred Hill has the latest including where her case could lead.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: At 20 weeks and four days pregnant, Kate Cox is living in legal limbo. Thirty hours after a Democratic judge approved an emergency abortion Thursday.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE MAYA GUERRA GAMBLE (Texas 459th District Court): I am going to grant the temporary restraining order.\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: The all Republican Texas State Supreme Court put a hold on it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BERG: It`s an unsigned order from the court. But generally, what that means is that the Supreme Court sees merit in the state of Texas opposition to the -- to the order.\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: Trial attorney, David Berg, a member of the Texas Bar says Cox meets the exception under the law.\par \par \uc2 Her doctors say her pregnancy is not viable, and she has a risk for multiple complications, including losing the ability to have another child in the future.\par \par \uc2 But state attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  argued Cox had not demonstrated that she would suffer immediate and irreparable harm without an abortion as the law states.\par \par \uc2 He also warned the doctor and hospitals, that they could be sued and prosecuted if they perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Cox`s lawyers say the fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 "While we still hope that the court ultimately rejects the state`s request and does so quickly, in this case, we fear that justice delayed will be justice denied."\par \par \uc2 Berg says there is a very likely path ahead.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BERG: Both sides can go to the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: The Texas Supreme Court did not give a timeframe for when they might rule, but Cox`s attorneys have argued here that with every day that passes, the risk to her health increases -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Jarred, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Holiday shoppers jammed the nation`s malls today. Cash registers are jingling and \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Joy Benedict found consumers moods are merry and bright.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOY BENEDICT: `Tis the season for spending and a new survey shows shoppers are finding more joy in doing so.\par \par \uc2 So what brought you here today?\par \par \uc2 Consumer sentiment soared 13 percent this month.\par \par \uc2 MARK HAMRICK (Bankrate senior economic analyst): People are seeing some opportunities now to retain some of their money.\par \par \uc2 JOY BENEDICT: That`s a positive sign after high inflation hurt American pocket books.\par \par \uc2 MARK HAMRICK: I think that really boils down to one thing right now and that`s really gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 JOY BENEDICT: The price at the pump dropped another 20 cents this month with a nationwide average of almost $3.20 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 In more good economic news, mortgage rates have fallen to their lowest level since August, and job growth remains steady.\par \par \uc2 Is that going to have an immediate effect on the holiday shopping season right now?\par \par \uc2 MARK HAMRICK: They may be feeling better, but their ability to pay is another question.\par \par \uc2 JOY BENEDICT: Consumer prices are still up 20 percent from pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 MARK HAMRICK: Americans don`t feel like they`ve been delivered from that evil of inflation. They merely feel as if it`s not hurting them as much as it did before.\par \par \uc2 JOY BENEDICT: But for some shoppers, it`s enough relief to have them filling their bags with holiday cheer.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yeah, it is once a year (sp?). You know, it is Christmastime.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOY BENEDICT: Some economists are predicting that things will be even better next year as inflation is expected to fall and they hope that`s enough to avoid a recession, at least for now -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Let`s hope so. Joy Benedict, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Big political news from American ally, Argentina.\par \par \uc2 The former Argentinian TV personality, Javier Milei who claims to get advice from his dogs has been called eccentric by those being polite, and he is now about to be sworn in as Argentina`s new president.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Cristian Benavides has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Supporters of Javier Milei call him El Loco. The former TV personality can sometimes look the part of a madman.\par \par \uc2 (Clips of Javier Milei)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Known for wielding a live chainsaw at his campaign rallies, now, the libertarian populist is Argentina`s new president.\par \par \uc2 NORBERTO SPANGARO (Radio show host): And as I said, politicians never tell you the truth.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 /Mi Argentina)\par \par \uc2 (NORBERTO SPANGARO speaking in foreign n language)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Norberto Spangaro hosts a radio program in Miami geared to Argentinian-Americans. He voted for Milei.\par \par \uc2 NORBERTO SPANGARO: So he brings hope.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Hope to a nation in dire straits, going through its worst economic crisis in a generation.\par \par \uc2 Milei plans to replace the Argentinian peso with the U.S. dollar in a bid to battle sky high inflation, which has reached nearly 150 percent.\par \par \uc2 (JAVIER MILEI speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: He promised to eliminate government agencies, favors outlawing abortion, a self-described anarcho-capitalist, Milei supports legalizing the sale of human organs and calls global warming a socialist lie.\par \par \uc2 His unexpected win, leading to comparisons to former Presidents \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  in Brazil and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: It has also led to protests by women`s rights groups, environmentalists, and public workers.\par \par \uc2 EDUARDO GAMARRA: Is he going to do a dramatic shock therapy? Or is he going to pursue a gradualist approach? Because the reality is that if he pursues a shock approach, he`s going to have people in the streets.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Not just controversial, he is also eccentric, known for cloning his beloved dog, Conan, who died in 2018, reportedly after hiring a medium to communicate with him.\par \par \uc2 Milei now claims taking economic advice from the five identical copies of his original pet. Argentina has a 40 percent poverty rate.\par \par \uc2 Milei won by 11 percentage points, a resounding rejection of the status quo.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides, CBS News, Miami.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, why pilots say they face a difficult choice when it comes to getting mental health care.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: A former \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  pilot accused of trying to cut the engines of the plane midflight has brought renewed focus to mental health in the cockpit. Now, two federal agencies are reviewing regulations.\par \par \uc2 And as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave shows us, pilots say the rules can backfire.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Nineteen-year-old student pilot, John Hauser was struggling with his mental health in silence for months. As a private pilot, he feared seeking help would lead him to losing his \uc2 FAA\uc2  medical certification required to fly.\par \par \uc2 Flying alone, Hauser took his own life by crashing this plane in October 2021.\par \par \uc2 ANNE SUH (John Hauser`s mother): John did not have a known history of depression.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: His mother, Anne Suh.\par \par \uc2 ANNE SUH: In a letter describing the turmoil that John was silently facing, he wrote: "I want to seek help more than anything. I just know if I try, I will have to give up on aviation. And frankly, I`d rather not be here than do that."\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Disclosing mental health issues can lead to a pilot being grounded by the \uc2 FAA\uc2 . \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  first officer, Troy Merritt grounded himself a year ago to get treatment for anxiety and depression.\par \par \uc2 He says he is taking an approved \uc2 FAA\uc2  medication. But it could take another year out of work before regulators allow him to fly again as he is spending thousands out-of-pocket for \uc2 FAA\uc2 -required evaluations to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 TROY MERRITT (\uc2 United Airlines\uc2  first officer): Had the barriers that are in place today not been there, I know I would have sought treatment earlier.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: One study found more than 56 percent of U.S. pilots avoid seeking medical treatment out of fear of losing their \uc2 FAA\uc2  certification to fly.\par \par \uc2 LAILA STEIN (Western Michigan University College of Aviation recent graduate): We got the message loud and clear from all sides. If you want to fly, you can`t admit anything is wrong.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Off-duty \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  captain, Joseph Emerson pleaded not guilty Thursday to dozens of charges including 83 counts of reckless endangerment and was released from custody after more than a month behind bars.\par \par \uc2 He allegedly attempted to turn off the engines of an airliner in midflight. Forty-eight hours earlier, court documents claimed, he had taken psychedelic magic mushrooms reportedly telling police he had been battling undiagnosed depression. His wife, Sarah.\par \par \uc2 SARAH STRETCH (Captain Joseph Emerson`s wife): I am saddened that this situation had to happen to my husband and to these people that it affected. But I know that this has created a movement and momentum to help thousands of other pilots and people in mental health situations.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Following that October incident, the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  called this first of its kind Mental Health Forum.\par \par \uc2 Board chair, Jennifer Homendy.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like from where you sit as the chair of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , the existing regulations aren`t good enough.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY (\uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  Chair): The existing rules are arcane. At the very least, pilots and others need to be able to sit down, talk to a therapist, and not worry about the impact on their jobs.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says it does provide a pathway for pilots to return to the cockpit. But ahead of the forum, the agency announced a panel to review existing mental health regulations.\par \par \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator, \uc2 Michael Whitaker\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WHITAKER\uc2  (\uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator): We need to have a system that allows people to be more forthcoming and to have treatment for issues that shouldn`t keep you out of the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  has conducted similar reviews in the past that yielded few changes. A 2016 survey from Harvard, so pre-pandemic, found about one in eight pilots could qualify as clinically depressed.\par \par \uc2 The nation`s largest pilot union is joining the call for change saying the existing system is outdated.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, CBS News, Reagan National Airport, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, superstar free agent, Shohei Ohtani chooses his dream team. We will tell you where he is going.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Two retired New York City Police officers were honored today for their role in stopping an arson attack at the home where Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. was born.\par \par \uc2 Brothers Kenneth and Axel Dodson were visiting the Atlanta landmark Thursday when a woman began dousing it with gasoline. They took action and subdued her until police arrived.\par \par \uc2 Kenneth says he was just happy to help and even happier to do it with his brother.\par \par \uc2 And today, baseball superstar, Shohei Ohtani slugged through the record books signing the richest contract in the history of American professional sports.\par \par \uc2 The two-time MVP pitcher and hitter just signed with the \uc2 Los Angeles Dodgers\uc2  for an astounding 10 years, and listen to this, $700 million. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, thank you for being a friend from another generation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Finally tonight, friendship, one of the great things about the holidays is celebrating with those closest to us. But if all those people are about your age, you might be missing out.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian explains.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Professional dancer, Laura Kaufman forged friendship at the bar -- not that kind -- a dance class at New York City`s iconic Steps on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 When you first saw Carol, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN (Professional dancer): I was honestly enthralled from the moment I met her.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The woman who became a bestie, Carol Teten, is older, by 52 years.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TETEN (Friend): She had an energy and a warmth about her.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: So, it sounds like you were both instantly sort of attracted to each other?\par \par \uc2 CAROL TETEN: Love at first sight. What can I say?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The age-defiant attachment isn`t that unusual.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-seven percent of adults have a close friend at least fifteen years younger or older than they are.\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN: Dance on a rainy day.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: While this 32-year-old and 84-year-old bonded over ballet, they soon started exploring restaurants.\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN: Say hi, Carol.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And with birthdays one day apart, they celebrate together every year.\par \par \uc2 (People singing Happy Birthday)\par \par \uc2 CAROL TETEN: I don`t think of age when I`m with her. We share a passion for life, and even that one element is not easy to find.\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN: And so when did you get back from Paris?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Experts say we tend to stick to same-age relationships, but one with a significant age gap brings benefits through sharing life experiences.\par \par \uc2 Bryn Mawr College psychology professor, Marc Schulz.\par \par \uc2 MARC SCHULZ (\uc2 Bryn Mawr College\uc2  psychology professor): The payoff that we get from connections with people that are different from us have the potential to be even bigger than the connections we make with people that occupy a similar place in life as us, or are right next door to us.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE singing)\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN: I feel like she`s taught me that getting old doesn`t mean you have to actually get old.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Both women say they are energized by their four-year bond.\par \par \uc2 What`s next for you two?\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN: I think just endless adventures. I can`t wait to see --\par \par \uc2 CAROL TETEN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LAURA KAUFMAN: -- what`s next.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A relationship spanning decades and for the ages.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: A timeless friendship.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS. I`m Adriana Diaz in Washington. Goodnight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020231210ejc900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for December 7, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mark Strassmann, Adam Yamaguchi, Janet Shamlian,  Nikole Killion, Charlie D`Agata, Omar Villafranca \par \uc2 3,557 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, our new reporting into the investigation into that deadly shooting on a college campus in Las Vegas, as we learn more about the victims, and police say the gunman had recently been denied a job at the university.\par \par \uc2 A city on edge. The new details, as sources tell CBS News it was university police who confronted the shooter.\par \par \uc2 The news from the White House. Where do negotiations stand for another pause in fighting in Gaza?\par \par \uc2 Congress opens an investigation into Harvard, Penn, and MIT after their presidents testified about antisemitism on campus.\par \par \uc2 America`s border crisis. We are there as migrant crossings reach near- record levels.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: This is the breach in the wall that the smugglers have cut through, and this is what the Border Patrol is up against.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On this Georgia barrier island, time, taxes, and zoning laws may have caught up with a piece of unique African-American history.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI WILLIAMS (Sapelo Island Descendant): We have thrived. And we want to continue to thrive, but we cannot if it`s snatched away.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We are going to be intimate with new details about that mass shooting on a college campus. We are just learning the identities of two of the three people killed at the \uc2 University of Nevada Las Vegas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We want to show you their pictures, Patricia Navarro-Velez, a 39-year-old mother, and Jerry Chang, 64. They were both business professors. All those killed and injured were faculty members, no students. Police just revealing that the gunman had a target list, but that nobody on that list became a victim. And we`re learning tonight about what was inside at least 22 envelopes that he put in the mail just hours before his rampage.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we know about the shooter. Authorities say he was a former college professor, the motive not yet determined, but police say the gunman recently applied for a job at the university and was denied.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca is on the campus with all the new details.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Omar.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 There`s still a heavy police presence here outside of the business building at UNLV, even though the campus is essentially closed. But, tonight, we`re learning more about the suspect and the faculty members and the community here and the police response, while students here cope with the latest mass shooting in America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Tonight, Las Vegas police releasing this video of the gunman being taken down by officers just outside the campus building where he killed three faculty members.\par \par \uc2 They say he was carrying this legally purchased 9-millimeter handgun and 11 magazines.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCMAHILL (Las Vegas, Nevada, Metropolitan Sheriff): We do not know how many rounds he has fired.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: CBS News has learned the suspect was a former college assistant business professor in North Carolina at \uc2 East Carolina University\uc2  for almost 20 years, resigning in 2017. He applied to work at UNLV, but did not get the job.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say he sent at least 22 envelopes containing an unknown white powder to faculty at UNLV and in North Carolina. Yesterday`s horrific shooting leaves this campus in shock.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Try to hang on. Just follow their instructions.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Students hid in dark classrooms, before being ushered out to safety by police.\par \par \uc2 Junior film student Hannah Werner`s (sp?) screenwriting class stacked desks against the door when they heard the gunfire.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH WERNER (Student): We don`t know where the shooter is. We don`t know what happened, so we all just decided to stay in the room.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: She showed us the alerts students received on their phones.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH WERNER: I was like, wow, this is real. This is actually happening.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Werner recorded this video when officers arrived a short time later and escorted students to safety.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH WERNER: And so they said, like, "Come out with single file line."\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Your hands up at this point?\par \par \uc2 HANNAH WERNER: Hands at your sides, just like that, yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: You felt this was going to happen eventually.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH WERNER: Yes, because I also work in the school district, not while I`m in school, while I`m teaching, but it could also happen while I`m at the movie theater. It could happen at the mall.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Are you going to allow yourself to cry to deal with this?\par \par \uc2 HANNAH WERNER: Yeah, eventually.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Eventually.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH WERNER: Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: President Biden will be in Las Vegas tomorrow as part of a planned trip, and we are expecting him to make comments about the shooting.\par \par \uc2 As for students here, they were getting ready for final exams next week. Those are now put on hold, as the university tries to figure out a way to move forward -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to the war in Gaza. President Biden spoke with Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  this afternoon, and he blamed \uc2 Hamas\uc2  for the end of the cease-fire after the terrorist group refused to release the rest of the young female hostages.\par \par \uc2 We get new reporting now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 And a warning: Some of the images are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): The hunt for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders in and around the city of Khan Younis has brought the very worst of the war to Southern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 In overwhelmed hospitals, exhausted medics treat patients, so many of them children, toddlers, on bloodied floors.\par \par \uc2 (GIRL speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A little girl screams over and over for her brother.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  confirmed to CBS News tonight that video had emerged online showed its soldiers detaining men they claim are suspected of terrorist activities in an unspecified location in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 In Tel Aviv tonight, crowds gathered to mark the first night of Hanukkah. Normally, this would be the start of a joyous occasion to spend time with loved ones and relatives. It`s a more somber event tonight, remembrance of the lives lost two months ago and to remember those who remain missing.\par \par \uc2 Like Gil Dickmann, whose 39-year-old cousin, Carmel Gat, was taken by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What does tonight mean for you?\par \par \uc2 GIL DICKMANN (Cousin of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Hostage): Tonight, we celebrate Hanukkah. It`s about lighting the first candle of hope in the darkness of misery that we`re in. I think that being together in a moment like this is a little light of hope for us.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And there may be hope that specialist Israeli teams combing through captured territory may locate further hostages.\par \par \uc2 DORON AVITAL (Former Sayeret Matkal Commander): You need the right intelligence. We can do it. The special forces are there. They are waiting. They are -- they are together with the troops. It`s their -- it`s the only thing on their mind. It`s a very dynamic landscape.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And there`s only one thing on the mind of relatives in Israel tonight: Bring them home.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In President Biden`s phone call to Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  tonight, the White House said the president stressed the critical need to protect civilians and allow them to move safely away from areas of hostilities -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, the presidents of Harvard, MIT, and the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  are under fire for their responses on how to handle calls for Jewish genocide on campus.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, second gentleman Doug Emhoff, who is the first Jewish spouse of an American president or vice president, speaking at the National Minor lighting, just said -- quote -- "The lack of moral clarity is simply unacceptable."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion reports, the backlash is also coming from school donors and elected officials.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Tonight, the presidents of Harvard University of Pennsylvania, and MIT facing a tough grade after this question was posed to them at a House Education hearing this week on antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-New York): Does calling for the genocide of Jews violate MIT`s code of conduct or rules regarding bullying and harassment, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 SALLY KORNBLUTH (President, \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 ): If targeted at individuals, not making public statements.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ELISE STEFANIK: Does calling for the genocide of Jews violate Penn`s rules or code of conduct?\par \par \uc2 LIZ MAGILL (President, \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 ): If the speech turns into conduct, it can be harassment, yes. It is a context-dependent decision, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2 : It`s a context-dependent decision. That`s your testimony today? Calling for the genocide of Jews is depending upon the context?\par \par \uc2 Does calling for the genocide of Jews violate Harvard`s rules of bullying and harassment, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE GAY (President, \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 ): It can be, depending on the context.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ELISE STEFANIK: It does not depend on the context. The answer is yes. And this is why you should resign. These are unacceptable answers across the board.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Today, New York Republican \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  announced a congressional investigation with subpoena power into the universities, which are facing widespread condemnation.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders like \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 `s CEO called it "one of the most despicable moments in the history of U.S. academia." Hedge fund manager \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  said they must all resign in disgrace.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Penn Office of the President)\par \par \uc2 LIZ MAGILL: I want to be clear. A call for genocide of Jewish people is threatening, deeply so.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Despite Penn`s president trying to clarify, the university`s Board of Trustees met today on short notice, and the state`s governor called her testimony failed leadership.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JOSH SHAPIRO (D-Pennsylvania): I thought her comments were absolutely shameful.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: \uc2 Jonathan Greenblatt\uc2  is CEO of the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN GREENBLATT\uc2  (CEO and National Director, \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 ): There are some who have tried to hide behind arguments that it`s a matter of free speech.\par \par \uc2 But I`m sorry. Freedom of speech is not the freedom to slander people because of their ethnicity.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  official tells CBS News there is no board plan for an imminent change in leadership.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Harvard`s president has also walked back her comments, stating calls for violence or genocide have no place at the university. The House Education Committee says its investigation will focus on policies and disciplinary procedures at the schools -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion, with all that new information, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to a major court decision granting a woman in Texas an emergency abortion. Now, Texas is one of more than a dozen states that imposed a near total ban on the procedure after last year`s Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian tonight explains why a judge is allowing this unprecedented exception.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): A historic decision.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE MAYA GUERRA GAMBLE (Travis County, Texas, District Court): I am going to grant the temporary restraining order.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A Texas judge ruling in favor of Kate Cox, who asked the court for an abortion in a state that doesn`t allow them and threatens to prosecute anyone who helps women get them.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE MAYA GUERRA GAMBLE: The idea that Ms. Cox wants desperately to be a parent and this law might actually cause her to lose that ability is shocking.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Cox, who teared up at the hearing, is 20 weeks` pregnant with a fetus that has a fatal genetic abnormality.\par \par \uc2 The suit filed Tuesday also states, giving birth could affect the Dallas woman`s ability to have more children. The emergency order applies only to the 31-year-old mother of two and ensures her doctor will not face penalties.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY DUANE (Senior Staff Attorney, Center for Reproductive Rights): This case proves that abortion is essential, lifesaving health care, and the judge recognized that immediately.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Texas has one of the nation`s most restrictive abortion laws, a near-total ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (President, Texas Right to Life): What the judge did today will create more confusion and ultimately more harm than -- and that`s what we`re concerned about.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For her part, \uc2 Cox\uc2  in a newspaper op-ed this week wrote: "I do not want to continue the pain and suffering that has plagued this pregnancy, adding: "I do not want my baby to arrive in this world, only to watch her suffer."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Texas attorney general`s office issued a statement today, saying her doctor could still be prosecuted if she performs the abortion.\par \par \uc2 It had argued that Cox should not be permitted to get the abortion. It has not said whether it will appeal the decision -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, a remarkable turn of events.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the growing crisis at the Southern border, the number of migrants crossing into the U.S. from Mexico is nearing an all-time high. CBS News has learned that nearly 10,000 asylum seekers were detained on Wednesday alone. Now, all of this comes as Republicans in Congress are demanding a change to the nation`s immigration policy as part of an emergency funding bill for Israel and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adam Yamaguchi traveled to the border town of Lukeville, Arizona, to see for himself.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI (voiceover): Daybreak at the Lukeville border crossing in Arizona. Hundreds of migrants have reached the most important moment in their journey to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol officials are starting to line the individuals up based on language and where they`re from. Processing may -- may begin momentarily.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, people built campfires for warmth. Some have gone days without food or water. This man traveled from West Africa.\par \par \uc2 Did you sleep?\par \par \uc2 MAN: How to sleep? Yeah, very cold. Nowhere to sleep.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Did you expect this when you came into the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 MAN: No.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Daniela (sp?) is a mother of four from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA (Mother, Through Translator): I`m very tired of walking so much with my children, with the baby in my arms and the little girl on my shoulders.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: This is one of the border`s most remote stretches, now one of the busiest. In the last year alone, there`s been a 140 percent spike in migrant apprehensions in this area.\par \par \uc2 DAN ABBOTT (Humane Borders): I have been working in this sector of the border for almost 20 years. And we have never seen anything like this. They`re cutting through the wall.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Our cameras captured one of those breaches.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Hey, guys.\par \par \uc2 Smugglers hid their faces. This is the breach in the wall that the smugglers have cut through, and this is what the Border Patrol is up against. This is not an anomaly, by any means. Here`s another one rod that was just cut and repaired today.\par \par \uc2 For nearly everyone here, the U.S. represents a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA (through translator): I`m fighting as much as I can, so my children can have something better.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Now, we reached out to Customs and Border Protection, and they tell us they`re sending more personnel and launching new operations to more aggressively target the smugglers.\par \par \uc2 They`re also asking Congress for additional resources -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Adam Yamaguchi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A former \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  pilot accused of trying to shut off a plane`s engines mid-flight pleaded not guilty today to a reduced charge of reckless endangerment. Joseph Emerson was initially charged with attempted murder for threatening the lives of the 83 people on board. Emerson, who was in the cockpit as a passenger, says he was struggling from depression and a lack of sleep and had taken psychedelic mushrooms just shortly before the flight.\par \par \uc2 He was released after posting a $50,000 bond and ordered to get help for his mental health and not to fly.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, we take a look at a unique African-American community off the coast of Georgia that`s fighting for its future.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann reports, the battle concerns changing zoning laws that threaten to turn their ancestral home into a summer playground for the rich.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): By ferry, Nikki Williams brought us to Sapelo Island, deeply rooted in West African Gullah Geechee culture; 44 families settled here after the Civil War, their Gullah Geechee community all formerly enslaved people.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI WILLIAMS (Sapelo Island Descendant): All day, every day, we -- we will fight for it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Among today`s descendants, Nikki Williams.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI WILLIAMS: This is the closest thing that you can get to being in Africa.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You feel it in your bones.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI WILLIAMS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This is Hog Hammock, little more than 400 acres, fewer than 30 full-time families. There is no main street here, just scattered homes, scattered like more and more of the original families in this historic enclave.\par \par \uc2 Back in September, the McIntosh County Commission changed Sapelo`s zoning laws. Home sizes can double. Potentially ahead, million-dollar properties, raised property taxes, and current homeowners priced out and pushed out.\par \par \uc2 ROGER LOTSON (McIntosh County, Georgia, Commissioner): They have an extreme uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Commissioner Roger Lotson, one of only two no-votes and the only commissioner who agreed to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 Is this a battle of rich versus poor?\par \par \uc2 ROGER LOTSON: In part, it is.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Black versus white?\par \par \uc2 ROGER LOTSON: In part, it is, simple because there are not as many rich black folks as there are rich white folks.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Now at risk, one of America`s last intact Gullah Geechee communities.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI WILLIAMS: I can`t see how you can`t see how that hurts. And it`s not just me. It`s all of the descendants.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A community born in blood and tears now sweating out its future.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Sapelo Island, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A hot air balloon comes down hard with nine people on board.\par \par \uc2 We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A hot air balloon slammed to the ground today in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Officials say the balloon with nine people on board was low on fuel and attempting to land when the wind changed direction. The basket holding the passengers was on the ground when the balloon got caught on a street light. Fortunately, no one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a consumer alert and a warning about scanning those Q.R. codes that are on your phone. Well, that`s straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, an important consumer alert to think before you scan one of those Q.R. codes on your cell phone.\par \par \uc2 Restaurants and other retailers use those digital black and white squares to store information. But the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  is warning that scammers are using Q.R. codes to hide harmful links to steal personal information. So, to protect yourself, the FTC suggests that consumers inspect link addresses closely to make sure they aren`t misspelled or contain a switched letter, and don`t scan Q.R. codes you weren`t expecting especially, when they are sent in e-mails and texts urging fast action.\par \par \uc2 We remember America`s day of infamy at Pearl Harbor. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We end tonight in Pearl Harbor, Hawaii, where a remembrance ceremony was held for the more than 2,400 U.S. service members and civilians killed on December 7th, 1941. It`s been 82 years since the attack that will live in infamy.\par \par \uc2 The surprise attack propelled the United States into World War II. Only five survivors were at today`s ceremony. Six had been expected, but 102- year-old Lou Conter of California -- he is the last known survivor of the sunken USS Arizona -- couldn`t make the trip.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Veterans Affairs isn`t sure how many Pearl Harbor survivors are still alive. It is important that we never forget and forever honor the sacrifices of America`s Greatest Generation.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 And on this first night of Hanukkah, we leave you with the annual lighting ceremony of the National Menorah right here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231208ejc700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for November 27, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jon LaPook, Kris Van Cleave, Mark Strassmann,  Errol Barnett, Ed O`Keefe, Holly Williams \par \uc2 3,697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the hope that more American hostages will be released, as the pause in fighting in Gaza is extended for two more days. And the breaking news, 11 more hostages are home. What we`re learning about the women and children just released.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Families reunited, as dozens of hostages come home. Our interview with the family of the American girl who turned 4 in captivity.\par \par \uc2 ELLA MOORE (Aunt of Abigail Mor Edan): It`s like a dream come true, because we were in a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And in the West Bank, crowds of Palestinians gathered to greet released prisoners.\par \par \uc2 The suspect in the shooting of three college students of Palestinian descent pleads not guilty, as we learn more about the victims` injuries.\par \par \uc2 RADI TAMIMI (Uncle of Kinnan Abdalhamid): It`s a real tragedy for the nation, not just for these boys.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A major recall to tell you about, the seat belt issue forcing \uc2 Honda\uc2  to recall hundreds of thousands of vehicles. What drivers need to know.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: How busy is Cyber Monday for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SCOT HAMILTON (Vice President of Planning and Routing Technology, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 ): It`s like our Super Bowl. We practice for it for months in advance.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: We`re at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  R&D facility. We`re going to show you what the future of shopping looks like.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURIE MARGOLIES (Director of Breast Imaging, Mount Sinai Medical Center): Wait a second. What that A.I. marked, that might really be something.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the fight against breast cancer. How artificial intelligence could help.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURIE MARGOLIES: A.I. may have saved this woman`s life.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin tonight with big news out of the Middle East. Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  have agreed to extend their pause in fighting for two more days. Now, the deal is expected to follow the same conditions as the original temporary cease-fire, three Palestinian prisoners released for every Israeli hostage freed.\par \par \uc2 Now, these are the faces of the 69 hostages all kidnapped during the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror attacks on October 7, and they have been freed. Fifty of the hostages were released under this deal that the U.S. helped broker. And 30 of them are children.\par \par \uc2 Now, on Sunday, the hostage-for-prisoner exchange included the freeing of the youngest American captive, 4-year-old Abigail Mor Edan. The agreement has also freed 150 Palestinian prisoners.\par \par \uc2 And, in Israel today, we saw Elon Musk touring kibbutz Kfar Aza, visiting the house where Abigail was kidnapped and her parents were murdered in front of her. The trip comes after the billionaire faced international backlash for endorsing an antisemitic post. Musk was scolded by Israel`s president over the content on his social media platform, X.\par \par \uc2 Now there`s a lot to get to tonight, and \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams will start us off from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Holly.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 The temporary cease-fire was due to end tomorrow morning at 7:00 local time. But, tonight, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  confirmed to CBS News that it`s committed to releasing 20 more hostages in return for a two-day extension of the cease- fire, as, this evening, another group of captives has been freed.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): Another 11 hostages were released by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tonight, including eight children, the youngest a pair of twins just 3 years old.\par \par \uc2 In Israel, there`s more joy and more relief.\par \par \uc2 (ABIGAIL MOR EDAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It follows the release last night of Abigail Mor Edan, a dual U.S.-Israeli citizen who turned 4 while held captive.\par \par \uc2 Her parents were killed in the October 7th attacks. She ran to a neighbor`s house, only to be kidnapped by the militants.\par \par \uc2 What`s it been like for your family to have her come home?\par \par \uc2 ELLA MOORE (Aunt of Abigail Mor Edan): Wow.\par \par \uc2 It`s like a dream come true, because we were in a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ella Moore is Abigail`s aunt.\par \par \uc2 Does she know that she`s lost her parents?\par \par \uc2 ELLA MOORE: I don`t know. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It`s so much for a 4-year-old.\par \par \uc2 ELLA MOORE: It`s so much, yeah. So it`s something I can`t answer.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The temporary cease-fire has held since Friday, allowing people in Gaza to take stock of the devastation wreaked by Israeli airstrikes and fighting and hundreds of trucks to deliver aid, including desperately needed fuel.\par \par \uc2 Israel so far held up its end of the hostage deal, releasing over a hundred Palestinian prisoners, many of them teenagers. There were celebrations on the streets of the West Bank to welcome them home, where some waved the green flag of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One of those freed was Nourhan Awad, who was just 16 when she was arrested in 2015 for the attempted stabbing of an Israeli man.\par \par \uc2 (NOURHAN AWAD speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "I can`t describe my joy," she said. "Yesterday, I was in prison. And, today, I`m in my home with my family."\par \par \uc2 Here in Israel, families have also reunited. Hagar Brodutch and her three children thought their father, Avihai, had been killed by the militants, until they were released.\par \par \uc2 And Maayan Zin finally has her daughters Ela and Dafna back in her arms. But for Iris Weinstein Haggai, so far, there`s been no good news. Her 70- year-old mother, Judi, is believed to be one of the American hostages, a former English teacher originally from New York.\par \par \uc2 IRIS WEINSTEIN HAGGAI (Daughter of Hostage): The hostages that -- that were released didn`t see her, didn`t hear about her. No information still about her. You know, I don`t have any proof of life for my mom. Nothing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The releases over the last four days have been bittersweet for many hostage families, including some in which a mother and her children have been released, but the father is believed to remain in captivity -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Holly Williams, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, the White House is welcoming the extension of this cease-fire, and it is a deal that the president actually helped broker. But there are new questions tonight about when the remaining Americans will be released. And that`s one of the reasons Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  will travel to the Middle East. We have just learned that America`s top diplomat will visit Israel, the West Bank and the United Arab Emirates later this week.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Tonight, the White House is applauding the deal to extend the pause in fighting in Gaza for two days.\par \par \uc2 Senior officials spoke to the Qatari prime minister, who`s a lead in the negotiations, four times today, hoping to prolong the cease-fire, get additional hostages out, and let humanitarian aid in.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (NSC Coordinator For Strategic Communications): Another two days, I mean, my goodness, that gives you a chance for dozens more trucks and tens of thousands of more gallons of fuel that can get into Gaza to people in need.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The administration is closely tracking the fate of what it believes are eight or nine Americans still being held hostage in Gaza. The president spoke with the family of the youngest known U.S. captive, 4-year- old Abigail Mor Edan, who was released late Sunday.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): She`s now safely in Israel, and we continue to press and expect for additional Americans will be released as well.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Meanwhile, American forces in the region remain on heightened alert for more attacks by Iranian-backed militant groups.\par \par \uc2 The most recent came Sunday, when missiles fired from the Houthi-controlled territory inside Yemen landed in the Gulf of Aden near the USS Mason, part of the Eisenhower strike group now in the region. The Mason had been responding to distress calls from an Israeli-owned tanker that had been briefly seized by five Somali pirates.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian-backed Houthi rebels have seized other vessels in the Red Sea, including an Israeli-linked cargo ship. U.S. officials today didn`t rule out striking back.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY: We have responded forcefully against the threats to our forces in Iraq and Syria, and now our forces in the Gulf region, the Gulf of Oman, Gulf of Aden.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Is striking in Yemen still an option?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY: We will take the steps appropriate to protect our troops and our forces in the Middle East region.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And while U.S. naval ships face threats in the waters of the Middle East, a defense official tells CBS News that, in the last six weeks, U.S. forces in Iraq and Syria have been attacked at least 73 times by drones, rockets and close-range ballistic missiles -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe with that new information from the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to a disturbing act of violence in Vermont that`s now being investigated as a possible hate crime. A 48-year-old Burlington man pleaded not guilty today in the shooting of three college students of Palestinian descent over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Errol Barnett reports that, when law enforcement arrived at the suspect`s door, he said: "I have been waiting for you."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Are you Jason Eaton?\par \par \uc2 JASON EATON (Defendant): Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (voiceover): Shooting suspect Jason Eaton is charged with three counts of attempted second-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARTY (Attorney For Jason Eaton): Mr. Eaton enters a not-guilty plea to all three counts.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Police officer!\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Authorities say, Saturday night, as the 20-year-olds, all of Palestinian descent, were walking down this street, two wearing keffiyehs, traditional Arab scarves, the shooter allegedly stepped out from a porch and fired four rounds, injuring all three.\par \par \uc2 One man sustained a spinal injury.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GEORGE (Chittenden County, Vermont, State`s Attorney): Although we do not yet have evidence to support a hate crime enhancement, I do want to be clear that there is no question this was a hateful act.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Family members have identified the college students as Tahseen Ahmed, Kinnan Abdalhamid, and Hisham Awartani. Two of the men are American citizens, and the third is a legal resident.\par \par \uc2 Police found a pistol and magazine of bullets during a search of the suspect`s apartment, which is across the street from the crime scene.\par \par \uc2 JON MURAD (Burlington, Vermont, Police Chief): ATF tests have since connected that firearm to the casings that were recovered at the scene of the shooting.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Rich Price and Radi Tamimi are uncles of two of the men. Tamimi says he`s struggling with the decision to bring his nephew, Kinnan, to the U.S. from the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 RADI TAMIMI (Uncle of Kinnan Abdalhamid): We figured it`d be good for him to be here. It would be safe. We feel shame and betrayal, almost, in the sense that we sent him here with the hope for all of that, and something so tragic occurred.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, one of the three young men has been released from the UVM Medical Center, which you see behind me. The other two, CBS News can report, remain together in the ICU.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, if convicted, the suspect faces 20 years to life in prison, Norah, on each of the three counts he faces.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Errol Barnett there, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, mourners are paying their respects to former First Lady \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2 , who died last week at the age of 96. It is the first day of three days of memorials for Mrs. Carter.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann is in Atlanta with new details.\par \par \uc2 And good evening, Mark. I understand there`s some new reporting about whether former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  at age 99 will be there tomorrow. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 We`re hearing tonight that Mr. Carter will, in fact, attend tomorrow`s special service. At least, that`s the plan. There could be a last minute change of heart, however. Georgia is staging this three-day farewell for Mrs. Carter, but this is an American moment.\par \par \uc2 And right now, live inside the \uc2 Carter Center\uc2 , everyday Americans file past Mrs. Carter`s casket, paying their respects to the daughter of tiny Plains, Georgia, who became first lady of the United States. Earlier today, her Secret Service agents past and present escorted Mrs. Carter`s hearse from Central Georgia to Atlanta. Her family helped lay wreaths at her Georgia alma mater.\par \par \uc2 Now, tomorrow that special tribute service to celebrate the life of Mrs. Carter, among the guests, President Biden, Vice President Harris, and their spouses, the Clintons and former First Ladies Bush, Obama, and Trump.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Carter was a maverick and a pioneer. As first lady, she sat in on Cabinet meetings, had her husband`s ear on policy and politics, and, for the rest of her life, championed mental health therapy. At the White House, this is her official first lady portrait draped in black.\par \par \uc2 She will be buried Wednesday in Plains after a private funeral -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a send-off there.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, much of the country is in store for a big chill, with more than 200 million Americans set to experience subfreezing temperatures tomorrow. Cold air from the north will dip down as far south as Atlanta on Tuesday and into Florida Wednesday morning. Every state will have at least one spot below 32 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Along the Great Lakes, lake-effect snow is expected, especially from Cleveland to Watertown, New York. Some spots could end up with three feet of snow.\par \par \uc2 All right, if you flew on Sunday, you were part of a record wave of air travelers. The \uc2 TSA\uc2  said today that more than 2.9 million people went through security at the nation`s airports on Sunday, making it the busiest day of air travel in U.S. history. More than 30 million people were expected to go through \uc2 TSA\uc2  screenings during the 12-day period that ends on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 From holiday travel to holiday shopping, online sales are expected to smash records on this Cyber Monday, with shoppers spending more than $12 billion.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave reports, digital sales also beat expectations on Black Friday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Tonight, the holiday shopping surge means \uc2 Amazon\uc2  delivery facilities like this one outside Boston will handle 110,000 packages a day, nearly twice its usual number.\par \par \uc2 Americans already spent record amounts this Thanksgiving weekend, an estimated $37.2 billion of it online, up 5.4 percent from last year.\par \par \uc2 SCOT HAMILTON (Vice President of Planning and Routing Technology, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 ): It`s like our Super Bowl. We practice for it for months in advance.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Amazon\uc2  V.P. Scot Hamilton:\par \par \uc2 SCOT HAMILTON: Within 24 hours of that order being placed, that inventory is here on the floor, getting put into vans and out ready to go.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The online retail giant is working to increase the number of same- or next-day deliveries using A.I. to forecast daily demand for more than 400 million products.\par \par \uc2 The technology helps predict where they will likely be ordered from, allowing \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to stage items nearby and deploying mapping technology for drivers that identifies the most efficient routes while updating in real time for traffic and weather.\par \par \uc2 Would you be able to move these volumes, handle these huge days without A.I.?\par \par \uc2 TYE BRADY (Chief Technologist, Amazon Robotics): We simply could not.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Amazon Robotics chief technologist Tye Brady:\par \par \uc2 TYE BRADY: A.I. will touch just about every piece of our supply chain. Our A.I. systems allow us to not only forecast demand better, but allow us to move those goods inside our buildings even quicker.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: On top of already strong discounts, Cyber Monday shoppers are seeing record markdowns on electronics, toys, TVs and apparel.\par \par \uc2 Appliances and sporting goods are expected to see deeper discounts in the coming days. To get them out the door faster, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is deploying Sequoia, an A.I.-driven sorting system that speeds up the processing of incoming goods by 75 percent and sorting for delivery by 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now, within 11 minutes of clicking buy on a same-day order, facilities like this one are already working on delivering it.\par \par \uc2 That`s an hour faster than most \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facilities and something the company says wouldn`t be possible without A.I. It allows this facility to push out more than 55,000 same-day packages every day and deliver them all across the Phoenix area, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  isn`t alone in using A.I. Doctors are now using artificial intelligence to detect breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 A new study out today shows that a new tool from researchers at Northwestern School of Medicine is sparing patients from unnecessary treatments.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Health Watch, CBS News chief medical correspondent Dr. Jon LaPook talks to doctors and patients about how the technology could save lives.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK (voiceover): Tehillah Harris was only 32 when her mother died of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 TEHILLAH HARRIS (Patient): My mom was very concerned about my level of risk.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Harris gets screened regularly at Mount Sinai in New York, where A.I. is used to assist reading mammograms and breast sonograms.\par \par \uc2 TEHILLAH HARRIS: The doctor said that they have this new technology with A.I., and would I be interested? I`m like, sure, sign me up. I can have an extra set of eyes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Preliminary results from a study found physicians assisted by A.I. and mammography screening detected 20 percent more cancers.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURIE MARGOLIES (Director of Breast Imaging, Mount Sinai Medical Center): Why is that important? You might need less chemotherapy, when you might not need a mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Dr. Laurie Margolies is the director of breast imaging at Mount Sinai. She demonstrated how A.I. analyzes mammograms and sorts them into three levels of risk -- low, intermediate and elevated. A.I. is also used to read breast sonograms.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURIE MARGOLIES: So we take our owl, we put it on the finding, and we`re going to analyze it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And it happened in about three seconds.\par \par \uc2 A radiologist also reads the scans.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURIE MARGOLIES: I think A.I. is here to help us in the same way that 30 years ago, a magnifying glass helped us. People look at A.I. and said, it`s as good as me.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Why won`t their jobs be eliminated?\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURIE MARGOLIES: Doctors` jobs won`t be eliminated for many reasons. A.I. is not there to be empathetic. It just gives an opinion. It may not know somebody`s family history, and it certainly can`t provide that hug.\par \par \uc2 TEHILLAH HARRIS: You know, you want someone to come in and explain it to you and, if needed, hold your hand.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: A combination of care, A.I. with a human touch.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jon LaPook, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s some promising technology.\par \par \uc2 Now this story: Passengers aboard a \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  jet scramble for safety after a man opens the emergency exit and jumps off the plane.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: On America`s busiest travel day ever, a \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  flight was delayed in New Orleans for several hours on Sunday after a man got off the plane through the emergency exit while it was parked at the terminal.\par \par \uc2 Video posted online shows passengers exiting the plane and the man being arrested on the tarmac. The good news, no one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 Why \uc2 Honda\uc2  is recalling hundreds of thousands of cars. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now this consumer alert.\par \par \uc2 Honda\uc2  is recalling more than 300,000 cars because of a defect in some front seat belts. The models being recalled include 2023 and 2024 Accords and HR- Vs. \uc2 Honda\uc2  says the recalled vehicles are missing a rivet that makes the seat belt tighten properly. Luckily, no injuries or deaths are being blamed on the problem.\par \par \uc2 They`re the best in show at keeping America safe. The \uc2 TSA\uc2  picks its top canines for its annual calendar. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight, we have got the perfect story for the end of a "ruff" holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 TSA\uc2  announced its 2024 canine calendar with the cutest pooches who keep the skies safe, thanks to their incredible nose for explosives. This year`s top dog is Dina, a 3-year-old German shorthaired pointer who works at \uc2 Harry Reid International Airport\uc2  in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Just 15 of the more than 1,000 canines working across the nation were selected for this year`s honor. And you can print your own calendar at TSA.gov. I`d say that`s "picture paw-fect."\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231128ejbr00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For November 12, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Cordes, Marwan Al Ghoul, Debora Patta, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 7,697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: pressure on Israel for a cease-fire, even a humanitarian pause, increases, while the conditions inside the Gaza Strip continue to deteriorate.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s efforts to decimate \uc2 Hamas\uc2  continue, but the humanitarian situation and escalating Palestinian civilian death toll have intensified the protests around the world, as some Gazans attempt to flee the hardest-hit parts of the region. Others struggle to stay alive in areas that are under attack and running out of resources.\par \par \uc2 We will have reports from the region, and we will speak with White House National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan and Israeli President \uc2 Isaac Herzog\uc2 . Plus, House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman Mike McCaul joins us from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Then: There are some bright spots for Democrats following last week`s off- year elections. Can they win on issues, despite an unpopular president?\par \par \uc2 Virginia Democratic Senator Mark Warner joins us.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin the sixth week following \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` brutal attack in which 1,200 Israelis died. That number has been dropped from 1,400, according to the Israeli government, as remains are still being identified.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations over releasing the 200-plus hostages continue behind the scenes. Publicly, it`s a war that includes allegations, counterallegations, and disturbing images. We will continue to do our best to bring you only factual information and context.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin our coverage today with Debora Patta, reporting from East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): They came in their thousands, chanting, "Now, now," Israelis demanding the immediate release of the nearly 240 loved ones abducted by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on October 7, a reminder, when it comes to pain, there are no winners after more than five weeks of war.\par \par \uc2 In Gaza City, fierce street-to-street battles around several hospitals, including the largest, Al Shifa, now a front line. Hollow-eyed doctors and nurses work in torchlight in a health system that is on its knees. They came to save lives, but fear that, together with their patients, they could die here.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Mohammed Obeid sent out this desolate message.\par \par \uc2 DR. MOHAMMED OBEID (Surgeon): And they hit those people many times. The situation, as I said before, very, very bad. We are nearly sure that we are alone now. No one hear us.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Medical staff want guarantees that the around 600 patients still inside can be safely evacuated.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military has kept up its drumbeat of accusations. \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , it says, is using the hospital as an operating base, a charge the group denies. Palestinians continue to flee southwards, but it is a perilous journey.\par \par \uc2 "My son was shot dead in the chest," Ghaith Hasune (sp?) sobs. "I had to leave his body behind."\par \par \uc2 (GHAITH HASUNE SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And when they do reach the south, even as far as Rafah, the bombs continue to fall.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "We`re not involved. We don`t have fighters in our house," this man weeps. "I just operate a donkey cart."\par \par \uc2 This is the true cost of this war, thousands of civilians killed, sometimes with only a number to identify them, men, women and children now being buried in nameless mass graves.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And the Israeli military says it`s now opened an evacuation route from the Al Shifa Hospital.\par \par \uc2 But, Margaret, for many of the patients there in a critical condition, this is simply not possible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our Debora Patta.\par \par \uc2 CBS News producer Marwan Al Ghoul filed this report last night from Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARWAN AL GHOUL: In Gaza City, there are more and more severe suffering. It`s a matter of die or life in Gaza City, as no enough food, no electricity, no fuel at all.\par \par \uc2 But if I want to talk about Gaza City, I have to talk about Al Shifa Hospital. This hospital contains 500 medical beds, but all occupied by injured people and sick as well. These displaced people live in the hospital and the courtyards.\par \par \uc2 I had a call phone with Dr. Samir Al-Bosh (sp?), who told me that smell of deaths everywhere in the hospitals, as there are 100 dead bodies in the hospital. But they don`t know where to bury those bodies, which attempted, the administrations of the hospital, to dig mass grave to bury the bodies over there.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Samir said that the hospital and doctors cannot apply any services to the injured people, as no fuel, no electricity, and everything ran out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Producer Marwan Al Ghoul reporting from Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Christos Christou is president of Doctors Without Borders International, and he joins us now from Rome.\par \par \uc2 I understand many of your doctors are working at Al Shifa Hospital, and said two infants died due to lack of electricity, the inability to keep incubators on. There are 40 premature babies who need to evacuate. Will the Israeli military help these children evacuate?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOS CHRISTOU (International President, \uc2 Doctors Without Borders\uc2 ): So far, we don`t have any news about any coordinated action about evacuating at least these neonate patients.\par \par \uc2 And we don`t have any news about any proper evacuation. What we see are hundreds of patients that they have underwent surgery very soon and -- very recently, and now they cannot be just -- take and walk out of the hospital.\par \par \uc2 And next to them, we have newborn babies that they needed -- premature babies that they needed support. They were supposed to be in incubators. No incubator works this moment in Al Shifa. And, actually, Al Shifa doesn`t work. It`s not functional.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The prime minister of Israel has said there`s no reason why we can`t just take the patients out of the hospital.\par \par \uc2 What`s your response to that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOS CHRISTOU: To evacuate -- to evacuate the hospital, you need time. You need weeks. It`s not within 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And we said that several times in the past also, that it takes a lot of good organization to evacuate the hospital. And next to this coordination, it takes also a cease-fire. We have reports that people trying to leave the hospital also have been shot down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: By who?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOS CHRISTOU: And we had also accidents -- snipers. And we don`t -- and we don`t know what is happening also sometimes inside the hospital in several places where they are bombed. There are airstrikes in the hospitals as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Israeli military denies attacking the hospital complex. I know it`s a large complex.\par \par \uc2 But they claim that \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , as you know, has a command center in that hospital. I realize you are speaking about very sensitive matters, but the lives of your staff are also in danger here. How does this get resolved?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOS CHRISTOU: First of all, we need to understand that, at this right moment inside hospitals are people -- are people in need, our patients, our children.\par \par \uc2 Our children, we have this acronym, the WCNSF, wounded children of non- surviving families. We have several families that they are there. And these are patients. We need to understand that. We have to protect the hospitals. Any attack in the medical care at the moment is an attack on humanity.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But Israel`s military is going to go at that hospital if it is indeed \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` command center. I`m sure you understand that.\par \par \uc2 You say it would take weeks to get everyone out. Is there any planning?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOS CHRISTOU: Well, there`s no planning. Everyone now lives -- even -- even the health workers have been so overwhelmed and exhausted. And they are in a position at the moment that they cannot even offer anything.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, what seems more likely is not a proper evacuation, but it is a panic, is a panic that will end up with rubbles.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Allowing fuel in has been a big sticking point for Israel, which worries that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will get the fuel. You need the fuel to keep the generators on.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any source of fuel to keep the electricity on, to keep these people alive?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOS CHRISTOU: At the moment, there`s no fuel in the hospital. And we run out of fuel also in other hospitals.\par \par \uc2 And I cannot speak on behalf of all the hospitals this right moment in Gaza. I know that there`s no electricity. We do not have connection in Internet. All these testimonies that you get also from our colleagues like Dr. Mohammed Obeid before was from yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We lost contact later at night. We don`t know what is happening in this right moment. And we know since a long time that people do not have even access to drinkable water.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 It is -- I don`t even have the words to respond to what you just laid out, Doctor. But thank you. And we wish your doctors well, along with their patients.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOS CHRISTOU: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by White House National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 Jake, welcome back to the program.\par \par \uc2 The fighting seems very intense in and around Gaza. \uc2 Doctors Without Borders\uc2  has called on the Israeli government to -- quote -- "end its unrelenting assault on Gaza`s health system."\par \par \uc2 Does the U.S. believe that this is an accurate description of what`s happening? Is the U.S. telling Israel not to hit hospitals?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (U.S. National Security Adviser): Well, Margaret, without getting into intelligence information, we can just look at the open-source reporting that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is using hospitals, as it uses many other civilian facilities, for command and control, for weapons storage, to house its fighters.\par \par \uc2 And this is a violation of the laws of war.\par \par \uc2 That being said, Margaret, the United States does not want to see firefights in hospitals, where innocent people, patients receiving medical care are caught in the crossfire. And we`ve had active consultations with the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  on this.\par \par \uc2 In fact, just yesterday, the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  themselves said that they -- on the record, publicly, said that they are looking for ways to be able to ensure the safety and security of individual patients in those hospitals, while they also try to figure out a way to deal with the fact that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is operating in a way that`s outside the bound -- bounds of any civilized concept of how you would think about using a hospital, using human shields.\par \par \uc2 So it`s an active conversation, but the bottom line is, we don`t want to see firefights in hospitals.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 You don`t dispute, then, the Israeli assertion that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has a command center underneath Al Shifa Hospital?\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: I`m not going to get into a specific assertion related to a specific hospital, because that would be getting into intelligence matters.\par \par \uc2 But, broadly speaking, we can see from open-source reporting that there is a track record, a pattern of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  historically and in this conflict using hospitals and other civilian facilities for these purposes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about the hostages, roughly 240, still being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We had the opportunity to speak with Congressman Mike McCaul from Israel, where he had just met with the Israeli prime minister. And he told us that Israel is considering \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` proposal that Israel release Palestinian women and children from prison in order to obtain the hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is that something the U.S. thinks should happen?\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: Well, Margaret, you`ll understand if I`m very careful in how I answer this question, because the negotiations are delicate. They`re high-stakes.\par \par \uc2 What is at stake is the recovery of a significant number of innocent people, including innocent Americans, currently being held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , a murderous terrorist organization. It is the case that there are active negotiations under way between Israel and Qatar, who is communicating with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and the United States is involved in those discussions, very much involved in those discussions.\par \par \uc2 But I`m not going to get into the specifics of what`s on the table, only to say that we are actively working to ensure the safe return of every American being held hostage and every other person being held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are still roughly 400 Americans stuck in Gaza, according to the State Department. Will they get out?\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: We are determined to ensure that they get out.\par \par \uc2 Many have gotten out. Many are still there, as you mentioned, not just American citizens, but also their immediate family members. And we`re trying to create a circumstance where every one of them can get safe passage out of Gaza. The gate has been open and closed. The lists have included Americans some days and not other days.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, today, the gate is open. We are moving American citizens and their family members out. And in the days ahead, we hope to ensure that every American who wants to leave Gaza is able to do so safely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jake, this past week, a top State Department official testified to Congress that the Gaza Health Ministry`s estimates of 11,000 Palestinian dead may in fact be an undercount.\par \par \uc2 Does the White House share that assessment?\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: We don`t have fidelity on the numbers of casualties, fatalities in Gaza. We don`t know how many are fighters and how many are innocent civilians.\par \par \uc2 But, as I have said before, and as Secretary Blinken has said before, we do know there are thousands of innocent civilians who have been killed in the military operations that began after October 7. And every loss of life of an innocent person, whether it`s Palestinian, Israeli, anyone, is an absolute tragedy.\par \par \uc2 And we grieve for every one of those lives. And we continue to reinforce the proposition that Israel has the right, indeed, the responsibility to go after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  who continues to represent a threat to the State of Israel. But it must do so in a way that`s consistent with the laws of war.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And Secretary Blinken also said too many Palestinians had died to date.\par \par \uc2 I want to zero in on the future, which I know you have talked about from day one, what will happen in Gaza next. And you`ve pressed the Israeli government on this point.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken has been clear that it`s the West Bank and Gaza that needs to be under unified control, and the Palestinian -- \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  likely to govern that. It doesn`t sound like the Netanyahu government is on the same page as the Biden administration, because the prime minister said something very different just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: Well, from our perspective, the way forward -- the basic principles of the way forward are straightforward.\par \par \uc2 And this is something that Secretary Blinken laid out publicly this past week, no reoccupation of Gaza, no forcible displacement of the Palestinian people. Gaza can never be used as a base for terrorism in the future. And Gaza`s territory should not be reduced.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken also said that, ultimately, we do want to see the reconnection, the reunification of control between the West Bank and Gaza under Palestinian leadership. \uc2 The Palestinian Authority\uc2  is the current leadership on the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 But, ultimately, it`s going to be up to the Palestinian people to decide their future, who governs them. And the United States will support a process...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There haven`t been elections held in ages.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: Well, that`s right, Margaret. There haven`t been elections held since the early 2000s.\par \par \uc2 But, post-October 7, we can`t go back to the way things were on October 6. And that goes for ensuring that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  cannot represent a continuing threat to Israel. And, in fact, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  spokespeople have said they want to repeat October 7 again and again until Israel is wiped out, that they want to be in a permanent state of war with Israel.\par \par \uc2 That was on the front page of "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 "...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: ... out of the mouth of a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  spokesman.\par \par \uc2 So, Israel is going to prosecute this campaign against a terrorist group. But we also can`t go back to October 6 when it comes to governance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER JAKE SULLIVAN: And it will ultimately be up to or should be up to the Palestinian people to decide what their future governance looks like. And that should involve a reconnection, as Secretary Blinken has said, of the West Bank and the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jake Sullivan, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re back now with Senate Intelligence Committee Chairman Mark Warner of the state of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 And the Democrats were -- were celebrating what happened in Virginia this past week with your elections.\par \par \uc2 You said it was -- you told the White House what happened in Virginia was the first election of the 2024 national election cycle.\par \par \uc2 What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (D-Virginia): Well, I meant that, while there was a lot of focus on the Wisconsin Supreme Court race, the Ohio abortion question, in Virginia, a purple state, where not only abortion, but gun rights, voting rights were all on the ballot, in terms of Democrats` positions versus the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And I think it sets the stage well for the president. And I think one of the things that has not gotten into the analysis -- I mean, clearly abortion was a big issue, but I also think Virginia very reliant on the federal government, we have a lot of defense establishment, a lot of federal workers.\par \par \uc2 I think the overall craziness that`s come out of the House of Representatives left a message that, even when the Republicans in Virginia would try to appear moderate, I think Virginians were afraid, if they got total control of the state government, it would be the extremists, the MAGA crowd, that would drive the bus, the same way that`s happening, unfortunately, at the national level.\par \par \uc2 And I think that played into the Democrats, you know, taking back the House, keeping the Senate and stopping some of that agenda.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the president`s own approval rating is low.\par \par \uc2 So, when people draw a direct line and say Democrats did well here, so therefore, a year from now, President Biden`s coasting to victory, do you agree with that? Or do Democrats have a Biden problem, given that his approval ratings are -- are not high?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Listen, the president`s approval ratings are tough. I`m the first to acknowledge that.\par \par \uc2 But I also think the president has repeatedly said, at the end of the day, this is not a choice between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and a perfect alternative, particularly if the choice is between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think Virginians, I think Americans will reject that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That also comes down to a question of enthusiasm and turnout, right, in tight elections.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen some particularly young progressives on this issue we`ve been talking about, Israel and Gaza, be very vocal in their frustration. And in tight races, states like Michigan matter a lot. And there are over 200,000 Muslim Americans in that state.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you see this divide on this issue hurting the president with Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: I think it has a potential to.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I think all of us are urging Israel -- clearly, Israel has the right to defend itself, has the right to take out \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is holding many of the Palestinians, people, using them as shields.\par \par \uc2 But we all saw those images on your show today. They`re horrific. So, what Israel has to realize -- and this is why we`ve been pushing for these pauses, why we`ve been pushing to make sure that the settler violence on the West Bank is controlled. This is a battle for hearts and minds for Israel, not just a military battle.\par \par \uc2 And those hearts and minds go across our country. They go across the region. And if they are -- if there`s not more consideration about Palestinian casualties, you could see this already tragic event spill over into violence on the West Bank, coming out of Lebanon, and obviously making it harder in America to maintain our traditional support for Israel.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 America is Israel`s number one arms dealer. That`s what we do. So, does something need to change, in terms of this potential aid package that is being debated in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, I think questions have been raised about how this aid will be used. I have raised some of those questions privately.\par \par \uc2 We also have to pair that with humanitarian assistance. And that is absolutely critical. We`ve, again, seen these horrific images.\par \par \uc2 But I also think, as we think about this package, we also need to realize - - I -- I hear a lot of the Republicans who want to bash Iran, bash Iran, bash Iran.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: And I get it, all right? The Iranians are bad guys.\par \par \uc2 But they then seem to forget the connection between Iran and Russia. And some of these very same folks who want to help Israel, but then want to walk away from Ukraine? I think walking away from Ukraine at this time would be a historic mistake that would pay negative repercussions literally for years to come.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you confident that package, Ukraine and Israel and border and Taiwan, can pass?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: I`m confident, if we can put all those pieces together. And I believe that, not only money, but we do need to make some policy changes on border.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: The devil`s in the details, but I think, if we get that package combined, it will definitely pass.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re going to take a break and come back and talk to you on the other side of it.\par \par \uc2 So, please stay with us, Senator.\par \par \uc2 We have more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yesterday, more than 300,000 pro-Palestinian protesters marched through Central London, calling for a cease-fire in the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 Far right counterprotesters clashed with police, and almost 100 people were arrested for trying to disrupt the main demonstration.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We continue our conversation now with Senator Mark Warner of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 You, in your position as head of the Intelligence Committee, have been tracking a lot of the competition with China, particularly in the technology space. The White House seems to be very focused on establishing military-to-military ties with China. What are you watching for out of this summit with Xi Jinping?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, I do think re-establishing these military ties and communications are important because we saw when we shot down the Chinese balloon, nobody picked up the phone on the other end. And unlike Russia, where we`ve had 75 years of conflicts but lots of back-channel communications, because our - our contest with China is newer, we don`t have that level of communication, so I think that`s very important to be reestablished.\par \par \uc2 I also think the president needs to push President Xi on the continuing intellectual property theft that some years accounts for close to $500 billion of intellectual property being stolen. And I firmly believe, when we`ve seen no retreat from China in terms of its level of technology investment, I absolutely believe that national security in the 21st century is more than tanks and guns. It is who wins the battle for artificial intelligence and quantum computing and advanced energy. The level of Chinese investment, for example, on rare earth minerals coming out of Africa is huge. And we need to be able to counter that with America and with our friends.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, our -- you want guardrails, I imagine, set up with China on artificial intelligence and the like, but legislating around a lot of this is really hard and it`s not moving very quickly.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Margaret, let me tell you, I did the bill that was terribly bipartisan, 13 and 13, Democrats/Republicans -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: About saying we need an approach that`s not simply focused on TikTok, but on all foreign technology that might pose -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: A national security risk from China or Russia or Iran. And suddenly we had the extremes on both the left and the right come out against that legislation. It needs to move because, particularly now as you see literally 40 percent of young people get all their news from TikTok. So I hope those kind of issues will be -- we can get some energy behind them now coming after this summit.\par \par \uc2 But this technology competition, we`ve had military competitors like Russia. We`ve never had an economy that`s making the investments the way China is in these new technology domains.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , this week, came out with a report that said Russia, Iran and China will interfere in the 2024 presidential race. Not -- they were certain about this. And other close presidential races worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Is your legislation going to stop something like that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: I think our legislation will take on an issue like a TikTok, which is, I am afraid, could be used as a propaganda channel for the CCP, the \uc2 Communist Party of China\uc2 . And my beef is with the CCP, it`s not with the Chinese people or the Chinese deaspra (ph).\par \par \uc2 But around artificial intelligence where I think we need to be humble because candidly we`ve done nothing on any guardrails on social media and on technology writ large. The two areas where artificial intelligence could have an enormous negative effect tomorrow is interfering in our public elections, but it also could have a tool in terms of interfering in our public markets. And I`ve been surprised we`ve not seen more manipulation, for example, of stocks using AI tools. So, my hope is, perhaps we can get a coalition between those who favor making sure our public markets are not interfered with as well as those who were concerned about interference in our election to build the kind of coalition because taking on big tech -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: I`ve been there for years and got a -- pretty much a zero record and nobody in Congress has got a good record on this. I think we need that kind of coalition because the election interference could be enormous.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Very quickly, is that "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " report that Ukraine was behind the attack on Nord Stream accurate and is attacking civilian infrastructure acceptable?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Margaret, I`m not going to comment on anything that comes out of classified intelligence. We do know that Russia has a history of misinformation and disinformation, but we`ll continue to follow that issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we spoke earlier today with the chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Michael McCaul, from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you`re wrapping up what has been a pretty quick and busy trip to Israel. You met with Prime Minister Netanyahu. Did you get a sense that his government is close to wrapping up this military operation?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): No, I don`t think so. I think it`s -- you know, the air strikes, I think the ground game is just now starting.\par \par \uc2 And I will say, Margaret, when we walked in, the first thing we saw was the horrific \uc2 Hamas\uc2  video showing the gruesome killings of these barbaric terrorists. And it was a very intense, kind of powerful, moment.\par \par \uc2 You know, the prime minister was in tears. Really teary-eyed about what happened. And, remember, this is sort of like their 9/11. And they - they want to eliminate the threat, you know, as they should. And I think that - and we talked a lot about the ground game operations, humanitarian, and we also met with the minister of defense as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you just raised humanitarian aid. I know the U.S. is the largest donor to the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  relief agency that operates inside of Gaza. Many in the Republican caucus do not support any aid going to them or to Palestinians, even Palestinian civilians living in Gaza. Do you believe that the aid package going through Congress needs to include this kind of help?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: Well, I do. And I think the Israelis do as well. I think, you know, I talked about this with the prime minister. They have put a buffer zone in the middle of Gaza to create this humanitarian zone in southern Gaza. They opened up the Rafah gate, that`s Egypt. About 100 trucks a day are coming in. But it has to be like food, water, and medicine. It can`t be fuel because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  can use that to light their tunnels and operate them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are still roughly 400 Americans stuck inside of Gaza according to the State Department. Did you get any update on how they`re doing?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: We did. And very sensitive negotiations with the Qataris. They`re kind of the lead broker, if you will, of this negotiation. You know, I`m meeting with, right after this interview, with a bunch of families of the hostages. And I met with them in the United States. Very dicey issue.\par \par \uc2 And I think what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  wants would be a swap of prisoners in Israel of Palestinians in exchange for these hostages, both Americans and Palestinians, in the Gaza. That`s an ongoing thing. I hope it can happen. A cease-fire would be very difficult without an agreement to release all hostages.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To be clear, are the Palestinian Americans who are trapped in Gaza being included in that hostage negotiation? Is that what you`re saying as well?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: No, this is a -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: No, I`m sorry, it`s the Palestinian -- Palestinians in - in prison in Israel that are being, you know, negotiated in exchange for prisoners of both Israelis and Americans held captive in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood. So that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  proposal is being entertained. Interesting to know that.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about what Congress can deliver in terms of aid to Israel. Do you think that the new speaker of the House has the clout necessary with your caucus to move through a package that includes Israel aid, Ukraine aid, and other national security items?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: Our aid to Israel is urgent and time is of the essence. We have to deliver this package.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know that we have offsets, and that`s fair, but I don`t think we can play political games with this to support our ally because, you know, if - if they fail, and we fail, it would have a very bad effect across the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Into Ukraine, that`s vitally important as well. So is Taiwan. And so is the last line of defense, the southern border. So, really, if you think about it, all these threats are really tied together. Now, whether we handle it all altogether or separately with say Ukraine and border security, all that has yet to be figured out. The stage to, if you will, a CR that the speaker is trying to implement, I think will actually move the process forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that - that deadline in terms of government funding is next week, November 17th. Yesterday the speaker made this proposal, it`s kind of an unusual way to do it, called a laddered, short-term funding deal.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The White House says it`s just not serious and it`s a waste of time. Are we going to see a government shutdown?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: Well, you know, we have the power of the purse. And, look, we need - we need more time. The CRs always hurt the military, always hurt our national security. And so the first CR until January could resolve the four -- last four appropriation bills that we can`t pass to date. So we could pass those and then have until February to negotiate with the Senate to get this done and put it on the president`s desk. That will also give us time --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but first you need the votes to actually pass this. So - so can Republicans vote together to pass this proposal, this short-term funding agreement?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: We`re going to have to. I mean, there`s no choice here. I mean the world is on fire from where I sit. It is too, you know, urgent. We can`t sit back and do nothing. And talking to Prime Minister Netanyahu, they needed this yesterday, in his words. Ukraine needed it yesterday. The border, for certainly, need it yesterday. And we know that Chairman Xi is threatening Taiwan and the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCCAUL: So, you know, I think what the Senate`s going to do, they`re going to come up with a package, probably after Thanksgiving, that they will send over to the House.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. And we`ll see if that government funding proposal can get passed by next week to avoid that shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for joining us from Israel this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the president of Israel, \uc2 Isaac Herzog\uc2 , who joins us from Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG (President of Israel): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your country lost 1,200 of its civilian citizens on October the 7th. And I know you`re still reeling from that. But what we`re seeing in Gaza right now with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and this concentration of very intense fighting around al-Shifa Hospital, if that is indeed \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` command center, does that mean this war ends if you take control of it?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: Well, the war we`ve - we`ve taken on against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is a war that - which matters to the entire well-being and safety of - of the world, I would say, and therefore our main aim at this stage following the atrocities we`ve gone through on October 7th is to uproot the capability of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . It doesn`t end in Shifa Hospital. They are based all throughout Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you have personally met with many of the families of the roughly 240 hostages being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and other terror groups. Can you explain why Israel is reportedly now considering releasing Palestinian women and children from your prisons in exchange for some of these hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: Well, I don`t want to give any illusions. Qatar is trying to mediate. So is Egypt. In order to get a humanitarian release of a - of some of the hostages, we demand all the hostages. That`s the worldwide demand. But there are - there are talks, of course, behind the scenes. I cannot confirm any details.\par \par \uc2 What I can say is that there is nothing substantial right now on the table. The United States is also heavily involved in it. So, there`s nothing tangible that I can report.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I asked because in the past Israel has flatly refused that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  proposal to release Palestinians from prison. Are you saying that`s a false report?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: It`s not the same. It`s not the same. There are four dozens say that Israel is willing to release our -- murderers who are sentenced for a life sentence. In my speech in Congress, to a joint session of the House in - in July, mid-July -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: I said that the entire problem with the peace process in the region is terror (ph). Terror is undermining the entire ability to move forward. All of those who remember decades of trying to make peace with our neighbors know that we can make pace with those who adhere to the agreements and fight terror. Unfortunately a nation, which supports and aides terror, we cannot be a partner.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: And we`ve seen the atrocious results.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I understand. And the United States of America considers \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to be a terrorist organization and has for - for decades. They`re very clear on that.\par \par \uc2 But as you just mentioned, you have long been an advocate for peace with the Palestinians, but there are many in the current government who before October 7th were not and do not favor two states for two people.\par \par \uc2 Do you still believe that is the way ultimately to have peace.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: Why would anybody agree right now to any progress with our neighbors when we don`t see truly their ability to fight terror and many, many of them, including those in Gaza, are hailing and smiling and celebrating in the most atrocious way the world`s greatest atrocity almost since World War II.\par \par \uc2 May I also add and tell you that part of the whole issue is that it`s kind of a culture that celebrates these awful atrocities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which culture?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: These atrocities cannot be agreed - accepted in any way. It means it has nothing to do with the conflict. It has nothing to do with the borders, with settlements or anything. This is simple. Simple, clear, barbarism, Jew hate, other hate, which is advocated by ISIS, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , al Qaeda, which, if we weren`t here, then Europe will be next and probably the United States is the end game for all of them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I understand, and I`m not discounting in any way anti- Semitism around the globe, which is on the rise.\par \par \uc2 When it comes specifically, though, to the long-term security of the Democratic and Jewish state of Israel, it sounds like you have lost some hope for that peace process. But I wonder, given what you are seeing happen in Gaza right now, and the wish you have for security for your people, aren`t you concerned that more individuals will be radicalized by the level of civilian casualties right now?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: If we eradicate the capabilities of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , we`ll give hope to the Palestinians in Gaza too, to run their life decently, as we`ve expected in the accords we had with the Palestinians. We enabled the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  to take control of Gaza and they lost it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: They lost it to a brutal coup of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in 2007.\par \par \uc2 But the issue is, I kept on asking President Abbas of the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  in -- on numerous occasion, would you go in if Israel takes \uc2 Hamas\uc2  out? And he said no. Now I don`t know exactly where he is at and if they`re even capable of doing so. But one thing is clear, we first and foremost have to give safety and security to Israel.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood. But even the \uc2 United States government\uc2  says that death toll of 11,000 Palestinians may be an undercount. They say they can`t separate civilians from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters. But it`s still a tremendous amount of death.\par \par \uc2 So, given that, how can you say with certainty that Israel`s military and intelligence is hitting the right targets right now, that the dying that is happening is necessary and helpful to your security?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC HERZOG: I thank you for this question first of all because I want to express my utter pain and sorrow for any innocent casualty on the Palestinian side. We are doing our utmost, according to international humanitarian law. First and foremost, we alert people. I mean what`s the story here? The enemy, these terrorists, fought and tried to kill us and send missiles and terrorists from their homes. So, we have to go to these homes and we have to blow up all their infrastructure, tunnels, missile, everything.\par \par \uc2 So, we go and we sent leaflets, we call and we phone and we send text messages to all the civilians in that area, we tell them move out, we give them time to move out and we`ve opened humanitarian corridors and we have increased dramatically the humanitarian aid to those who are moving out to a safety zone.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, there could be casualties. Some of them are also by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fire. And their reporting and the numbers are unclear to me. But I`m saying outright, we are doing our best. Unfortunately, these things happen, and we are sorry for it, but, at the end of the day, we have to uproot that infrastructure because we have to defend ourselves according to our right and duty to defend our people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Herzog, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democrats celebrated a series of high-profile wins in this past Tuesday`s elections, including an abortion rights amendment in Ohio and full control of the state legislature in Virginia. But there was another notable outcome, liberals won a series of school board races in red and blue states alike. Why?\par \par \uc2 Here`s chief White House correspondent Nancy Cordes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): When election season rolls around, school board races haven`t always gotten a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: If you`re going to yell, just do -- stand up, yell and leave.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): But that was before school board hearings around the country became a flashpoint for fights over book banning --\par \par \uc2 MAN: They`re molesting our kids` minds.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): Diversity --\par \par \uc2 MAN: What am I being arrested for?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): And LGBTQ plus rights.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The movie had not been approved and it was not appropriate.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Know that you are making Orlando (ph) County unsafe and an unproductive working and learning environment.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): 2021 saw a surge in conservative school board candidates pushing back on pandemic-era school closures and mask mandates. This past Tuesday, the pendulum appeared to swing the other way, with more liberal candidates picking up school board seats in states like Pennsylvania, Iowa, Ohio, and Virginia.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I started to see the effects of some of the chaos and disfunction on our school board in our classrooms.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): Valan Rodas (ph) is a teacher and newly elected school board member in Spotsylvania County, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You`re not going to cut my mic off when I go to vote.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): That board drew national attention in 2021 when it voted to pull what it called sexually explicit books from school libraries.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: From there it just seemed to continue spiraling into other distracting culture war issues that aren`t the problems that are needed - needed addressing to help our students.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): On Tuesday, Spotsylvania`s school board flipped from a 4-3 conservative majority to 5-2 for progressives.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: What message do you think voters were trying to send about the school board?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I believe they want us to work together.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): April Gillespie is one of the two remaining conservatives.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I believe they`re tired of the fighting and the lawsuits and -- on both sides. Just everything. Both sides. I think that they want us to move forward and focus on the business of the division and not so much the politics of things.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice over): Last Tuesday`s school board races are being viewed as a test case for 2024 when voters in all 50 states will go to the polls. Democrats are hoping this issue, like abortion, will help to drive turnout and make up for what polls show is a dip in enthusiasm for the team at the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s Nancy Cordes reporting.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Today some tragic breaking news. The Pentagon has just announced that five U.S. service members were killed yesterday in an aircraft training mission in the Mediterranean Sea. Our condolences to those families and our deepest respect and thanks for all of those who serve or have served in the military.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020231112ejbc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 US Airstrikes Hit Syria; US Drone Shot Down; Gaza Under Siege; Status of the Conflict; GOP Debate Showdown; Back to the Big Screen; \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  Testifies; Medical Breakthrough; New Weight-Loss Treatment Approved; Breaking Down the GOP Debate; Michael J. Fox`s Push Forward; Jon Batiste Live; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nancy Cordes, Ramy  Inocencio, Robert Costa, Carter Evans, Scott MacFarlane, Jon LaPook, Nancy  Chen, Vladimir Duthiers, Terry Sullivan, Joel Payne \par \uc2 14,545 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A new US airstrikes in Syria against a target tied to Iran after dozens if US troops are injured in multiple attacks.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): The attacks on our troops in Iraq and Syria are resourced by the IRGC, which has direct ties to the Supreme Leader.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Negotiations are underway for a brief ceasefire in Gaza to get aid in and some hostages out.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It has nothing to do with the conflict, we, the families just want them home.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hollywood is back in business. The actors strike is over after a tentative deal the union calls a victory.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: All of our fabulous actors can get back to work.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Southeast Texas, a massive explosion at a chemical plant sent plumes of toxic smoke into the air.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The 57th annual CMA Awards, it was hosted for the second time in a row by Peyton Manning and Luke Bryan.\par \par \uc2 PEYTON MANNING (Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player): We`re back and you know what that means?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BRYAN (Singer-songwriter): Yep. Travis and Taylor weren`t available.\par \par \uc2 PEYTON MANNING: Yup. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For the third time this year, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  skipped out on the chance to debate his Republican rivals for president.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The debate field was narrowed to just five candidates.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): The who`s who have who has no chance to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It is ridiculous to have these debates without the frontrunner. I mean, the guy who they`re trying to beat. You know things have gone sideways when you`re watching something and you`re thinking, "God, I wish \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was there."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve heard many people say what Jimmy said right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that`s not where we are.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin today with new concerns that the Middle East conflict may be spreading. The Pentagon has announced a new US airstrike inside Syria after weeks of drone and rocket attacks that have injured dozens of US servicemembers.\par \par \uc2 Officials say the target in eastern Syria was a weapons warehouse with links to Iran.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes has more on this part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The Defense secretary says that the orders to strike came from President Biden himself and that this was a move in self-defense after more than 40 attacks on US bases in the region just in the last three weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The strike was carried out by two US F-15s. The target, the Pentagon says was a weapons storage facility used by Iran`s Revolutionary Guard located in eastern Syria. It was the second retaliatory strike by the US in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 It came in response to dozens of drone and rocket attacks on American bases in Iraq and Syria that wounded at least 46 servicemembers, 25 of them with traumatic brain injuries.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH (Deputy Pentagon press secretary): We are seeing an uptick in attacks.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: US officials blame militant groups tied to Iran`s Revolutionary Guard. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  warned Iran this weekend not to use the war in Gaza as an excuse to target US troops.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): We`re not looking for conflict with Iran, we`ve made that very clear, but we will do what`s necessary to protect our personnel, be they military or civilian.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In another provocative move, an American Reaper drone worth about $30 million was shot down over the Red Sea by Iranian-backed Houthi rebels. The Houthis claimed the drone was in Yemen`s airspace, but a US official tells CBS News it was shot down while in international airspace over international waters.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Since the start of the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, the US military has been steadily building up its presence in the region as a deterrent. Two carrier strike groups have been sent, and on Sunday, it was revealed that an Ohio class nuclear submarine had crossed through the Suez Canal, a rare disclosure of its location by the Defense Department -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations are underway for a three-day humanitarian ceasefire in Gaza to allow more aid to get in and about a dozen hostages to get out, possibly including Americans.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is that a border crossing where aid trucks are being inspected by Israeli authorities.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 This here is a firsthand look at the high security inspection process of aid trucks going into Gaza. We have been looking inside these boxes and we`ve been seeing everything from food to biscuits, detergents, even toothpaste, and the goal here, the mission here, the priority is that everything going into Gaza must be inspected to make sure that food and medical supplies do go in, getting into the civilian hands of the Palestinians, and to make sure that weapons and fuel don`t get into the hands of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those reports of a ceasefire for up to three days would let trucks like these in and to let Gazans flee the pounding by Israel`s military in the north. That area is now like a demolition site, a wasteland from the war.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UN\uc2  says 50,000 Palestinians fled northern Gaza yesterday. The day before that, it was 15,000, and the day before that was 5,000. So we`re seeing a surge in evacuees, many leaving with just the clothes on their back and any way they can.\par \par \uc2 And what`s going in is just a fraction of what`s needed. There are shortages and fights over food, water, fuel, medical supplies. The \uc2 WHO\uc2  is now warning of an outbreak of disease.\par \par \uc2 And the humanitarian crisis in Gaza will continue unless and until a ceasefire or a temporary pause is called, but last night, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said there will still be no ceasefire until all hostages are released. TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re joined now by John Kirby. He is the National Security Council`s coordinator for Strategic Communications.\par \par \uc2 Admiral, good morning to you. Always good to have you on.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about these attacks on U.S. installations and forces in the Middle East. We`ve put our assets militarily in the region, and yet, since the last time we spoke, the attacks on U.S. have only gone up. If our message is deterrence, are you concerned they`re not getting that message?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): We`re certainly concerned about additional attacks on our troops and our facilities in Iraq and Syria, and you`re right, since our last set of strikes, there have been additional ones, which is why we took another retaliatory strike last night to send a strong message to these proxy groups and to the Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps, the \uc2 Quds Force\uc2 , that these are unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 Now, they`ll have some choices to make, and if they continue to choose to hit our troops, if they continue to launch these rocket and drone attacks, then we will continue to do what we need to do to protect ourselves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Admiral, in essence, the U.S. has gone over there with our fists up and it doesn`t seem like Iran or any of these militia groups have scattered as a result. Should Americans at home watching this be worried that we`ve lost our power of deterrence?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): I don`t think that you can look at the map and look at the force posture that we have in the region, look at the influence we have across the Middle East, and be worried that the United States and our leadership is somehow in doubt in that part of the world.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have sent a strong signal not just to Iran, but to other actors in the region that we don`t want to see this conflict with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  widen or deepen, and thus far, so far, we haven`t seen that happen. We haven`t seen another actor jump all in to try to help \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in their fight against Israel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Admiral, that`s for right now, but we`ve also got to talk about the medium and the long term, and on that subject, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  laid out a pretty specific plan for how he sees Gaza being governed in the aftermath of Israel`s efforts to root out \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and that would involve \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2  from the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  in a governing role.\par \par \uc2 But Abbas has said, I`m not going to ride in there on the back of an Israeli tank, and meanwhile, Netanyahu and all his spokespeople in recent days, none of them are talking about a two-state solution. In fact, they`ve been saying it`s failed.\par \par \uc2 So this seems to be nowhere from a public perspective. Do you have assurances otherwise behind the scenes?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): Look, nobody is asking \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2  to ride in on an Israeli tank into Gaza. What we`re saying is actually nothing new, Tony. We`re saying that we think that the Palestinians ought to have a vote and a voice in their future, that whatever happens in post-conflict Gaza, they`ve got to be able to have a determining seat at the table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Netanyahu, of all the Israeli prime ministers in the country`s history, has been maybe the least direct in pursuit of a two- state solution. And now just recently, he said there would be an indefinite security apparatus, an Israeli one inside Gaza.\par \par \uc2 To a lot of people, that sounds like another occupation. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): We don`t support a reoccupation of Gaza by \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 . We`ve made that clear plainly, publicly and privately to our Israeli counterparts and to the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 Now, as Secretary Blinken also said yesterday in Tokyo that there could be some transitional period in the immediate aftermath of the conflict, wherein \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  might be on the ground for immediate security needs. Certainly, that`s something that is not -- it`s going to, you know, certainly could be possible.\par \par \uc2 But again, we don`t see that as a long term solution for the peace and security of the Palestinian people in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Admiral Kirby, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): Yes, you bet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now we turn to presidential politics. Five Republican candidates squared off last night in Miami in the third GOP campaign debate. They traded jabs that got a little personal at times, but the man to beat, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he was not there.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa covered the debate for us and joins us now to sort it all out.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure if I can sort it all out, but it was a testy and insult-filled night for the Republican presidential contenders who were on stage here with tense exchanges on Israel, on China, and on character.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R) (2024 presidential candidate): You might want to take care of your family first.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) (2024 presidential candidate): Leave my daughter out of your voice.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : Before preaching to anybody else -- your adult daughter. The next generation of Americans --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: A tense showdown in Miami Wednesday as Republican presidential candidates pulled no punches.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : You have her supporters propping her up, that`s fine. Here`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You`re just scum.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : The easy answer --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: All while trying to emerge as credible alternatives to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R) (2024 presidential candidate): Anybody who`s going to be spending the next year-and-a-half of their life, focusing on keeping themselves out of jail in courtrooms cannot lead this party or this country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: As the former president rallied in Hialeah, Florida with his mugshot on merchandise for sale.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former president of the United States): I`m standing in front of tens of thousands of people right now and it`s on television. That`s a hell of a lot harder to do than a debate.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  made it a point to take on Trump and argued the party needs a jumpstart after disappointing results in Tuesday`s elections.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL) (2024 presidential candidate): I`m sick of Republicans losing.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Those losses leaving candidates grappling with a path forward on abortion.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We`ve got to do a better job on these referenda. I think of all the stuff that`s happened to the pro-life cause, they have been caught flat footed on these referenda.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC) (2024 presidential candidate): I would certainly, as president of the United States, have a 15-week national limit.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The candidates were edging for breakthroughs by addressing how the crises in Israel and Gaza are playing out at home.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: For everybody that`s protesting on these college campuses in favor of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , let me remind you something. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  said "Death to Israel and death to America." They hate and would kill you, too.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): To every student who have come to our country on a visa to a college campus, your visa is a privilege, not a right. I would deport you from those campuses.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And with former Ambassador Nikki Haley gaining support in the non-Trump wing of the party, she became a target.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : Do you want a leader from a different generation who is going to put this country first? Or do you want \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  in three-inch heels?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I`d first like to say there are five-inch heels and I don`t wear them unless you can run in them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Last night, several top Republicans told me here that they believe this race could soon become a real battle between Haley and DeSantis for the support of traditional Republicans. They also said there`s time for others to have a breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 The next debate will be in early December in Alabama -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bob, thank you. A lot of drama there.\par \par \uc2 All right now to breaking news overnight out of California, Hollywood actually, the longest actor strike in history is finally over.\par \par \uc2 Last night union leaders voted unanimously in favor of a tentative deal, which they are calling a major victory. Carter Evans has more from outside the studios -- Sony Studios in California.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 What do you know about the deal?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The exact details of this new contract are limited. \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  is promising to tell us more after its national board reviews the contract tomorrow, but the union does say it is a deal of extraordinary scope.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The celebrations have begun now that the actors` strike is finally over.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s been a long 118 days and we are so happy.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN CRABTREE-IRELAND (Chief union negotiator): So far. It`s been a very, very strong chorus of excitement and approval.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Chief union negotiator, Duncan Crabtree-Ireland says details of the tentative three-year agreement which striking actors had been fighting for since mid-July won`t be released until tomorrow at the earliest.\par \par \uc2 Are actors going to be happy with this deal?\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN CRABTREE-IRELAND: I think so. I mean, this is the first time that we`ve ever had a deal that was valued at over a billion dollars in gains during the term of the contract and that really means a lot.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Here is what we do no. \uc2 SAG\uc2  says the tentative deal includes not only a first-time bonus for streaming, but also unprecedented provisions for consent and compensation that will protect members from the threat of AI.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN CRABTREE-IRELAND: We intentionally designed, especially our AI protections, to set standards that can actually evolve with changes in the industry. We don`t want to see performers replaced, we want to see performers augmented and lifted up.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The