 his left eye.\par \par \uc2 And when you saw his face unbandaged for the first time?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN JAMES: I just took a deep breath and said, all right, this is what we`re dealing with.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Good. All right.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The Arkansas native also lost his nose and front teeth and couldn`t eat or drink normally, so he agreed to undergo a partial face transplant, including the world`s first transplant of a donor`s eye.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDDIE RODRIGUEZ (Plastic surgeon): Could we include the eye with the face transplant?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Why would people think that you couldn`t?\par \par \uc2 DR. EDDIE RODRIGUEZ: A whole eye transplant has never been done.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: This isn`t the first, first for plastic surgeon, Dr. Eddie Rodriguez. In 2020, he performed the first simultaneous face and double hand transplant.\par \par \uc2 So then when you suggested doing an eye transplant, and you spoke to ophthalmologists or neurologists or your colleagues, what did they say?\par \par \uc2 DR. EDDIE RODRIGUEZ: It`s not possible. It`s not going to work.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: When you went to Mr. James, and you suggested doing something that had never been done before, what was his response?\par \par \uc2 DR. EDDIE RODRIGUEZ: He didn`t hesitate. He said, if I can help out other people, and then I can help out other soldiers, it`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And he had been in combat, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. EDDIE RODRIGUEZ: Yes. He had had three tours -- Iraq, Afghanistan and Egypt.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: So in late May, Dr. Rodriguez and his team at NYU Langone Health spent 21 hours transferring the donor`s face, eye socket, and the eye itself to James. A huge challenge was providing blood flow to the transplanted eye.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDDIE RODRIGUEZ: It was an exhilarating moment to just see that blood supply coming to the retina.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: This is the 46-year-old now. It`s been just over five months since the eye and face transplant.\par \par \uc2 I want to just put it out there right now that you have some trouble speaking still, right?\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES (Eye and face transplant patient): Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Does it seem to be getting better?\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: Yes. It is getting better. Until I can get some feeling in my lips and get my jaw to open more and get some teeth.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You also have a lot of things that are working a lot better than before.\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: Yes. Everything. It just blows my mind.\par \par \uc2 I tell everybody, if I get close to a mirror, I just stare at it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You`re now looking for mirrors.\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: That`s right. You know, now, I want to go outside and let people see me, you know?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: After the surgery, a huge question remained, would light shined into the transplanted eye be recognized by the part of his brain that processes vision? To find out, James has had two functional MRIs.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m sure for visual, it will be even more.\par \par \uc2 DR. VADA HIDADANYA (Eye specialist): Yes, for this one, it`s usually a lot more.\par \par \uc2 Then we covered his good eye and checked the transplanted eye.\par \par \uc2 Oh wow.\par \par \uc2 And we saw a response back there.\par \par \uc2 Point to where you feel it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Retina expert, Dr. Vada Hidadanya (sp?) is his eyes specialist.\par \par \uc2 DR. VADA HIDADANYA: Can we be more astonished? Every level, every test has further increased our understanding of how this eye is functioning within his body.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Million dollar question, maybe billion dollar question, is it possible that the brain could rewire itself in a different way to be able to actually see an image?\par \par \uc2 DR. VADA HIDADANYA: You never say never.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But you never say never at the beginning of this and it worked.\par \par \uc2 DR. VADA HIDADANYA: A lot of what we have seen does not necessarily support that, but that being said, this is unprecedented so the question is, is it possible? Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Okay, I`m going to take a look at your eye now.\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Okay, it`s pretty healthy.\par \par \uc2 DR. EDDIE RODRIGUEZ: We don`t have sight yet, but I`m an optimist, and the one thing for sure that I can say now is that we are one step closer.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Whether or not he will ever see out of his new eye, Aaron James is committed once again, this time to advancing medical science.\par \par \uc2 It could potentially help somebody with blindness from any cause or macular degeneration or whatever.\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But also, it could help somebody who was in combat, who lost their eyes. I mean, did that go through your head?\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: If me doing it first kick starts it, you know, I`m all for it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Over time, you think you`d be able to open up that eye?\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: The nerves are starting to come up. I`m getting over here.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Really? Isn`t that amazing?\par \par \uc2 Years ago, they would have said, there is no chance at all that it will work, and I suspect that a lot of people right now said there is no chance it will work. And yet --\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: After I went home, every once in a while, I would \uc2 Google\uc2 , is eyes transplant possible? And it would say, you know, at this time, it is not medically possible.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: ChatGPT and \uc2 Google\uc2  are going to have to catch up to you.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN JAMES: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 AARON JAMES: Yes, I know. It`s a good feeling.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The donor was a young man in his 30s who not only gave Aaron James a chance at a more normal life, but helped three other people by donating his kidneys, liver, and pancreas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beautiful thing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have to say, this is extremely exciting. It`s jaw dropping when you realize what doctors and what Medicine can do. He looks good, Jon.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: He looks good and it is astonishing. It`s a technological tour de force to be able to do a face transplant, of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But then to say, you know what? I think I`ll also do something that`s never been done before. I`m going to try transplanting an eye and then, you shine a light in that eye. He can`t see.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But with a functional MRI, you can detect a signal in the back of his brain, the back of his cortex that processes vision. Now, they`re being cautious. This thing is the first step.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But, wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. And it only happened in May and his body seems to be waking up and responding, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Talking about the tingles in the face.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it, and I know some would say well, he has nothing to lose, but you have a lot to lose because you get your hopes up, it might not work. There are more possible surgeries after that. I mean credit to him for saying you know what, let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And to go through it, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To go through it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And to go through it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And you know, he was in combat, and he want to potentially help.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: . not only those people who had an IED injury, but maybe other people with other causes of blindness, very excited. You can see I`m excited about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And cautiously optimistic, but let`s see what happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we love your enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Jon LaPook, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approves another drug for weight loss that might help tens of millions of Americans. When will it be available? That is the question, and also, how much might it cost?\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has approved a new drug to help Americans lose weight and it`s set to hit the market later this year.\par \par \uc2 Now this new drug is called Zepbound. It`s a new formula of the popular diabetes treatment, Mounjaro. It joins other medications that you`ve heard of including Ozempic and Wegovy and is giving doctors and the patients a series of weapons against obesity, a disease that affects about 100 million Americans in this country.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It`s being hailed as a milestone in treating obesity.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It works by slowing down digestion, by having the brain think the stomach is full and thereby reducing appetite.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  approving Eli Lilly`s drug tirzepatide, previously found in diabetes drug, Mounjaro. It comes after a trial showed that patients without diabetes lost up to 22.5 percent of their body weight, on average around 52 pounds using the drug.\par \par \uc2 How does this differ from competitors that are already out there like Ozempic?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: The main difference we see is that the degree of weight loss is bigger.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Although common side effects include nausea, stomach pain, and indigestion, doctors note it`s 