pman, the first Black person in Country Music Awards history to win Song of the Year honors.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that he brought that song back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very cool. I love it, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a different version. It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Made it his own.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tracy, of course is -- and made it his own.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that she gets the credit too as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, can I talk about this?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lay it on me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A new luxury bag is hitting the market, but you`re going to need a million dollars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stop it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To buy it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stop it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pharrell Williams, who is Louis Vuitton`s men`s creative director previewed the pricey bag over the summer. It is made with crocodile leather, it features a gold zipper and a Cuban link handle and it closes with a diamond laced padlock.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sheesh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We don`t know when the Louis Vuitton millionaire speedy will be made available to the public. There`s been a little criticism for the price tag and also for using crocodile skin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: PETA, the \uc2 People for the Ethical Treatment of Animals\uc2  has said, they want to take Pharrell to show him alligator farms where they breed them for fashion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you want to spend --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it comes with that case.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, how many did you order?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I asked Nate about it. He hesitated.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle is like, see you later alligator.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He hesitated for a second. He wants one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, the great Jon Batiste is here. Boy, does he have a powerful story to tell. We`ve got an exclusive look at a new documentary about his life. It`s called "American Symphony."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Last night`s third GOP presidential debate got heated at times, but can any of these candidates beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Terry Sullivan and Joel Payne are here to break it down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We also have my exclusive interview with Michael J. Fox. We talked about his personal battle with Parkinson`s and what could be a medical breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I sure hope so.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 The US carries out a new airstrike in Syria after attacks on American forces in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Defense secretary says that the orders to strike came from President Biden himself and that this was a move in self-defense.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The actors` strike is over. \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  reaches a tentative contract agreement with studios.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN CRABTREE-IRELAND: In the end, I think we all recognize that the industry needs to get back to work. It can only happen with a deal that`s fair.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): I`m sick of Republicans losing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Five Republican presidential candidates face off in the third GOP presidential debates.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : We have her supporters propping her up, that`s fine. Here`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You`re just scum.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : The easy answer --\par \par \uc2 JELLY ROLL (Rapper): There is something poetic about a 39-year-old man winning New Artist of the Year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the musical artist, Jelly Roll gets fired up after his big win at the CMA Awards.\par \par \uc2 JELLY ROLL: I want to tell you, it`s going to be okay. I want to tell you that the wind sailed is better than the wind that blew through and it`s for a reason. Because what is in front of you is so much more important than what`s behind you. Let`s party Nashville.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s great, Gayle. You love Jelly Roll.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Big fan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody cheers for him. Everybody -- he has got a great backstory.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They were cheering so loudly, I couldn`t even hear what he was saying and he was yelling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. He is very well liked.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But with apologies to Jelly Roll, we are going to jump right in the conversation this hour after last night`s Republican debate in Miami.\par \par \uc2 Five GOP presidential candidates took the stage, notably without once again the frontrunner, that would be former President Trump who has skipped all of these events. Tense moments in the debate over issues like foreign policy, also abortion.\par \par \uc2 And former \uc2 UN\uc2  ambassador and South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley is rising in the polls and was a repeated target.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : Do you want a leader from a different generation who is going to put this country first or do you want \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  in three-inch heels?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I first like to say they`re five-inch heels and I don`t wear them unless you can run in them.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 : So we`ve got two of you on stage.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: The second thing I will say is I wear heels, they are not for a fashion statement, they are for ammunition.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With us now, our two CBS News political contributors, Terry Sullivan, former campaign manager for Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 ; and Joel Payne, chief communications officer for the liberal advocacy group, Move On.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  said "they`re two of you on stage in heels."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People caught that little comment. I think he`s referring to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Terry, let me start with you on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis did not address the heels controversy. What else stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Well, I was watching Jelly Roll on the CMA last night. I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 Look, what stood out to me is no one addressed the elephant not in the room.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: I mean, look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still has over 50 percent of the vote in this primary. They have a math problem. This is common core math. This isn`t political strategy, and they need to figure out some way to start chipping away at that and it is not by going after each other`s clothing choices.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But him not coming to any of the debates is not hurting him at all. If you`re running for president, shouldn`t you have to participate in the debates? Is that an issue at all?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: There`s lots of things -- not that voters really care about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They don`t.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: I mean, at the end -- maybe a little bit on the fringes, but he is running a smart campaign. We should give credit to his campaign and his folks, that they`re running a much smarter campaign than he`s ever run before.\par \par \uc2 He is more disciplined. He`s making smart choices. He`s not flailing around nearly as much by Donald Trump`s standards.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: I mean, why would you go to a debate when you`re winning?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it seems to be a race for second place, a distant second.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Last night, who stood out? Clearly, there is no love lost between Nikki Haley and Vivek. Nothing.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, what stood out to me is, I do think all of the candidates have improved at debating at least each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, all of them.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: And I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  actually did what he needed to do to stay viable in a place like Iowa. What his team wants to show their donors, their supporters is that hey, don`t pay attention to the Nikki Haley rise, I`m still the Alternative B to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I think he probably did enough to do that.\par \par \uc2 But as Terry said, the math problem still exists for all of these candidates who aren`t \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You have to be able to put fifty plus one together in the Republican primary field and I don`t know if anything about last night changed the dynamics of that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, math ain`t nothing is the problem, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Let`s take out the shots fired between the candidates and let`s take out some of the circus. Who do you believe had a stronger night?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: You know, look, I think -- I agree with Joel. I think DeSantis had his best debate performance thus far. There was no sizzle, but he`s moving the ball down the field. He is still best positioned to be -- to take on Trump. Out of any of them, he has got the organization in Iowa. He`s got the political pedigree He`s got the record, the money, like all of the things that --\par \par \uc2 Like he is -- he appeals to base primary voters. He is still the most favorably looked at voter amongst -- I mean, a candidate amongst Republicans. He has got higher faves than anyone else and that matters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Isn`t Nikki Haley gaining?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Yes, I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sure, but?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I think, she is gaining, but you know, as Terry made a good point, she is getting with people like me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are people like you?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: She is this era`s \uc2 John McCain\uc2 . She is every Democrats` favorite Republican in this field, just like Joe Manchin is every Republicans` favorite Democrat on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which seems like bad news if you`re a Republican.\par \par \uc2 So "The Wall Street Journal" has got an op-ed today, are Republicans tired of all the losing? They`ve underperformed in 2018, 2020, 2022 and now 2023, just on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Joel, it`s funny. The Republicans seem to be spiraling, and yet, when you talk to Democrats, they are terribly worried as well. How are you feeling after Tuesday about --\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I am terrified.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You were terrified, okay.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Terrified.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it was a big win for Democrats, Joel. A big win.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I think there`s this idea that there`s hubris on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: President Biden and his team don`t see the terrible headwinds out there, they do. It`s good that you have a cut through issue like abortion that you know is effective with this electorate, but I don`t think any Democrat feels like this is better than a coin flip race in 2024, and I think nothing about what happened on Tuesday changed that.\par \par \uc2 What does matter, I think is that abortion still is very resonant with this electorate right now at this moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t see what happened on Tuesday as a boost for President Biden because they certainly are doing a victory lap about it?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, it was not a bad night for President Biden and I think that`s good enough. But look, is President Biden, Andy Beshear in Kentucky who`s running 20 points ahead of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Does President Biden have the favorables of some Democrats around the country? He doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 He has time to rebuild that, but I think you can divorce the winds of Tuesday night from the overall challenges that President Biden is facing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Terry, what should Republicans be doing to change their strategy, particularly on some of these hot button issues like abortion?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Look, they need to take it to Democrats on all the litany of other issues they are weak on -- the economy, inflation, mortgage rates through the roof. It is too expensive to buy a home right now. We are involved in two wars. You know, there`s a crisis at the southern border, crime is out of control. There are some real issues that are kitchen table issues that Republicans need to be talking about. How -- are you really better off than you were four years ago?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you think on a large scale, Republicans are wasting time on issues that don`t matter?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: I think -- I think they could focus more on issues that matter to voters more.`\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joel, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Democrats are not excited. They are scared like you or when I talk to Democrats, a lot of them are dreaming of some third candidate to come in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Some kind of Hail Mary or something.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is there a third party? Or some other Democrat at the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he is losing in the battleground states right now as we sit here today.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Let me give some hope for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Biden`s coalition is an anti-Trump coalition. The path for that coalition to win in `24 still exist. Those folks can come together, young voters, voters of color, Independents, moderates. That is still there. The dynamics are still there.\par \par \uc2 There`s still concern about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , on abortion, on democracy, on the issues that really motivate those voters to turn out, that dynamic still exist and I think Biden can still put that winning coalition together from `20 and `22. He can put it together again in `24.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Yes, but the issue is, is that he barely beat Trump last time. He barely beat Trump and he had no record. And now his record is terrible.\par \par \uc2 And so he hid in his basement, he didn`t have to campaign. Now, he has to actually go out and campaign. He doesn`t look like he`s doing a lot of training for that kind of thing at the moment, and I think he`s in a tough spot.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason he`s losing every single battleground state, ones that he should be -- that he won last time.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I wouldn`t underestimate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I remember we were talking about Democratic primaries in 2020. He looked down and out in a number of occasions there. He showed a path to victory there. I think he can show it again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Terry, is it fair to say, he hid in his basement. We were in the middle of COVID.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Yes, but there were plenty of other people that were out in the world doing things, doing events. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was out doing events and you know, he is not the healthiest person looking on the Planet Earth, I know he is what? Six-eight and 220? But --\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: According to his medical record.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: But no --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Right, right. Yes, but --\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I thought you were talking about Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I guess, Trump has built like an \uc2 NFL\uc2  prospect.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Right. Right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I will just say this: The Biden agenda is popular, whether it`s the cheaper insulin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: All the things that he`s been working on in Congress.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: It is very popular and they are counting on that to guide them directly in `24.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. Joel Payne, Terry Sullivan --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is good to have you, two.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: By the way, Terry --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Little Thanksgiving preview with the back and forth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like your boots as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those are nice.\par \par \uc2 TERRY SULLIVAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is there an inch in there?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, my exclusive interview with Michael J. Fox. We get an update on his health and he shares the latest advancements in medical research on Parkinson`s. But first, it`s 8:10, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: A new documentary follows Grammy-winner, Jon Batiste and his wife, Suleika Jaouad on one of the most difficult journeys of their lives. We have an exclusive look at the trailer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "American Symphony.")`\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Unwavering faith, he`s got that. Jon Batiste will join us to talk about how he handled the very dark moments and what we can all learn from their love story.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, your favorite morning show. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we`re hearing exclusively from award-winning actor and activist, Michael J. Fox ahead of his foundation`s annual fundraiser this Saturday here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Fox was diagnosed with Parkinson`s disease in 1991. He was just 29 years old. Now 62, he has lived with the disease for more than half his life. Since its creation back in 2000, \uc2 the Michael J. Fox Foundation\uc2  has funded nearly -- check this number out -- $2 billion worth of Parkinson`s research.\par \par \uc2 We caught up with Fox to talk about the evolving changes of living with the disease in the global impact of his foundation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX (Actor and activist): We didn`t have money, we didn`t have a voice, and I fell -- I stepped in for these people and raise some hell.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After more than three decades of living with Parkinson`s disease, Michael J. Fox is raising a lot more than just hell.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: It`s impossible to put into words --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is raising the bar and a whole lot of money for Parkinson`s research.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, a landmark clinical study led by his foundation discovered a biomarker, a protein found in spinal fluid that can help detect Parkinson`s in patients, and in some cases, even years before the first symptoms of the disease appear.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: There`s a cure, but, but, but, but it`s big spotlight -- we are willing to do and what we need to focus on. So we know we`re on the right path and we`re very proud.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Parkinson`s disease is a progressive brain disorder that over time can strip away a person`s movement and speech. There is no cure.\par \par \uc2 Have you had moments where you didn`t always have this handsome smile on your face and things may be seemed a little bleak and dark and you found yourself battling what might seem like depression or uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Yesterday at 3:57 in the afternoon. I mean, always.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Always? Really?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Always. Positivity is really sincere, I really feel that and it is genuine, but it`s hard fought. I`m sorry, it is hard won, I should say.\par \par \uc2 This fear, we can find ways to just give us a break, sort of credit for getting through life on life`s terms and in order to do that, you have to stop and say, it isn`t that bad. It`s not that bad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Last time we talked, I learned a little bit more about you. I learned a little bit more about myself and just right now, that was another moment where you were very honest saying, yes, yesterday.\par \par \uc2 I had those moments where things were --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Fear.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You had fear, and as an athlete with an ego and bravado, I hid that fear for a long time, and now, I`m a little bit more open with myself and my family about when I`m struggling, when things are dark. So, I just want to say thank you for saying that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Yes, the absence of fear is faith.\par \par \uc2 The whole idea is, if you`re true to yourself and you`re true to the people that love you, everything is going to be okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In Fox`s corner for the last 35 years has been his biggest advocate, fan, and partner. His wife, Tracy Pollan who he met on the set of "Family Ties" in 1985.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Family Ties.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is it like to have her with you every step of the way?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: It was great for me, yes. I don`t know for her, but yes, I love Tracy, and obviously, she is an amazing person, and she has gone through a lot.\par \par \uc2 TRACY POLLAN (Michael J. Fox`s wife): So the most important question will remain, what will it take to eradicate this disease once and for all?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Then I realized that, she has a life separate and apart from me having Parkinson`s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Family Ties.")\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Apart from me beyond this --\par \par \uc2 (Clip form "Family Ties.")\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Back to the Future.")\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Or my little --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Back to the Future.")\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: She`s a person and I think that`s why it`s gone okay. I figured that out.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Up top. Up top.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: I had no idea what to expect and neither did she, but she, she, she, she had indicated to me by saying that for better for worse; in sickness and in health, that she was -- she was going to get me through it, go through it with me and she have for 35 years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For better for worse, those words have a different meaning.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL J. FOX: Like how we say, we knew the bus was coming. It was going to hit, but we didn`t know how far away it was and how fast it was going. So it really -- at any time, she would have been forgiven to say, I am -- I am just going to step out. But she didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is still fighting and still have got a great sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He still has his sense of humor, and at the end, he said there, we knew that a bus was coming, we didn`t know when or how fast it was coming before it hit us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he`s been living with that for so long with this partner by his side. One thing I can say about him, he has dedicated his life to Parkinson`s and the research and he has done so much for the cause and he is just getting started.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does he feel optimistic? Do you get the sense of that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get the sense that he does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This was a breakthrough in the medical space when it comes to the research for Parkinson`s, finding these biomarkers which can identify this alpha synuclein is what they call it, a protein that clumps up.\par \par \uc2 And you can identify it earlier, which helps people with the rest of their lives.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It keeps getting better. I hope you keep checking in with them as well, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. And I`ll just say this last thing, I said it to him and I`ll say it to the people because there`s a lot of people that are dealing with things that we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 His public fight gives millions private strength and we thank him for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are here in the progressive greenroom, I say this all the time. We meet the nicest people. Here is one, the great Jon Batiste, five- time Grammy winner is here to talk about his new documentary, it`s called what?\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE (Five-time Grammy winner): "American Symphony."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He can`t help himself, even through the darkest times, you find joy.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: You`ve got to have it. Always.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got to have it. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KI