ashed potatoes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now part two of my "Talk of the Table" is the most Willy Wonka thing I have ever witnessed in real life outside of the movie. It is Campbell`s partnering with a chocolatier named Phillip Ashley to put condensed Campbell`s Soup, side dishes really, inside gourmet chocolate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you have some samples here that I would like you to try.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. That`s pretty wild.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The kind of purple one, Gayle is not leaning forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m just going to watch y`all. Go ahead. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The purple one is, the everything bagel mashed potatoes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And chocolate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But there is soup inside.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is soup inside.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m going to put the whole thing in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does it taste like?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Um --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s why Gayle is not leaning forward right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you do? What did you taste?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is the everything mashed potato, everything bagel mashed potato.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What I like about it is how efficient it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You like it? Do you like it? Do you like it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t have to finish your soup and then go to dessert. You get to have dessert and the soup at the same time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got nothing for me?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate. Try the green one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think we`ve got to move on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, man. I`m going to have the jalapeno mac and cheese. You know, I like sweet stuff in the morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And can we give a shout out to Katy who stayed with your broke butt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was really broke, man. I was so broke.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who stayed with your broke butt. But it`s all worked out in the Dokoupil-Tur household.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is our next guest. You okay?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was terrible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh boy. Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is five-time Grammy winner, that`s Jon Batiste.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This mac and cheese are --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nothing terrible about him. He is also an Oscar-winner the same week that Batiste made history with not one, not two, not three, but 11 Grammy nominations.\par \par \uc2 The love of his life, Suleika Jaouad learned that her leukemia had in fact returned.\par \par \uc2 A new film called "American Symphony" documents their love story of art and survival is they fought Suleika`s leukemia while Jon Batiste, in the middle of all this, composed an original symphony.\par \par \uc2 So only on CBS MORNINGS and you know we like when that happens, we`ve got an exclusive first look at the trailer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "American Symphony.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The fight shot. I love that shot.\par \par \uc2 Jon Batiste is also an executive producer on the film, and he is here in the studio. Jon, I`m so glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: I`m so glad to be here. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let us talk about "American Symphony." I`m so -- it`s so many conflicting emotions for me when they say for better or worse, to certainly exemplify that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what happens is, you are going to the best time and the worst time, all at the same time. The duality of that.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you start making this documentary, you were going to concentrate on your music and your Grammy nominations.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you get hit with the news of your wife.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: In the same week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you still kept filming, yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: In the same week, we get the news, you`re nominated for 11 Grammys. Your wife, leukemia is here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Has returned. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: We don`t know if she`s going to make it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: And the thing about that is you realize in that moment, what you really care about and what`s most important. Awards are nice and it is nice to win awards. Having music as a part of your creative survival mechanism is how we made it through.\par \par \uc2 She started to paint in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: And finding that real truth and purity in the creative process and in our love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: That was the key of that entire thing unfolding in a way that we couldn`t have predicted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But cameras --\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: We wouldn`t want the cameras.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You wouldn`t have wanted cameras to film --\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Who would want cameras in these intimate, vulnerable moments in the bone marrow transplant unit?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Or in your bedroom at the end of the night when you`re trying to decompress and debrief and be with each other. And you don`t know how much of those moments you`re going to have. It`s a gray area at this point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you decided to share it with us. I`m glad you did, but you decided to share it with us.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: We have to continually reassess that decision every other week. You know, this is an incredible documentarian and filmmaker, Matthew Hyneman, and he is known for risking his life to make films, literally embedding with drug cartels, or being in Afghanistan, and he put that approach into this documentary and embedding into our lives.\par \par \uc2 So you have 14 hours a day of filming, and 1,500 hours of footage by the end of this, to make this documentary. So there`s so much vulnerability, there`s so much truth and it felt at a certain point that it was bigger than us. It felt like it was an ordained calling by God to continue to film.\par \par \uc2 It didn`t feel comfortable, it didn`t feel like something that we would have decided to do on our own.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: But in the moment that it was happening, I started to ask myself the question: Why is this happening? And we started to ask as a family, what is the purpose of us having these cameras in this moment? It has to be happening for a reason. I always knew that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was the answer you got? What was the answer you got to that question?\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: It was meant to empower other people through our experience, and it was meant for us to learn a lesson on what it truly means to say those vows and to be together, and what ultimately, in this world of, you know, the celebrity and privacy as a premium to kind of create a mystique and distance, what is the purpose of the platform and of the creative arts?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: What is the purpose of these things that we have at our disposal? It is to heal and is to bring people together and to show, we are not alone out there. Everybody`s going to have these ups and downs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: That`s like -- that`s the symphony of life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the symphony of life. Well put.\par \par \uc2 You know, because of the time this was shot around COVID and then also the hospital visits, you were wearing masks and I found it very poetic that, as we were watching everybody with masks on, you are unveiling yourself, moment after moment in front of the world.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What was it like to watch this all back right now in the state that you are in?\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: I mean, you know, it`s hard for me to be completely honest, it is hard for me to watch it back. I`ve gotten to the point where I can have some form of objectivity with it.\par \par \uc2 To be honest, I also felt that I didn`t remember a lot of things that would happening when I watched it back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You were in the moment.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: I didn`t remember. I didn`t remember that there was a camera there, let alone that it happened. I just remember being in the moment and in the flow, and being pulled by something so much greater than myself, and really just wanting to be there for Suleika because she`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: I know, I tell Gayle as I would talk her up, when we come and get together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: She`s absolutely incredible how she faces darkness and hardship and looks it right in the eye and dominates it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she looks at it and she says, face it, but one thing Suleika said the doctor was saying, you know you have to live every day like it`s your last and her philosophy is, don`t say that to me. That`s not helpful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell me to live every day like it`s my first.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Yes, yes. I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s good, Nate.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never forgotten that. Live every day like it`s my first.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: That`s right. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just a change in that perspective.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Changing -- setting your intention and focusing on what you`d like to see happen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Ultimately, it is -- that`s the power you have in the moment, and all we have is the moment and that also puts into perspective what`s most important, I`d say, for us, we`ve figured out each other, our creative arts, our family, our freedom, and understanding that the present is a gift.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: It`s a literal present.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: It`s something that God has given us and we are living through it with such joy and such positivity in the face of all odds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Would you get a little bit more specific on something that everybody wrestles with, which is the balance between their professional ambitions and their family life? Can you about that perspective? Every great work of art has some cost in the creation.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Someone`s personal life. How do you now at this stage in your life, think about the two?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Wow. That`s Tony, it`s ultimately a cost. You said it, this is sacrifice. You have to decide and prioritize and you have to know that if I give myself to this, I can`t give myself to that.\par \par \uc2 In a moment where you`re being pulled in all these directions -- 11 Grammy nominations -- all of these moments you have, you`ve been working towards, to meet, that`s the thing that I realized in that moment, and also ultimately, writing the symphony and understanding that the intrinsic value of this piece meant more than a career milestone.\par \par \uc2 It was a way for us to express these deeper truths, not only ancestrally.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Musically, but our truth of what we were going through in this moment and channel that into the piece.\par \par \uc2 So then, you know, finding ways to integrate what you`re doing creatively with your career and your life. That`s the ultimate synergy and it`s not always possible.\par \par \uc2 When you can do that, it creates something that is remarkable for yourself, even if people don`t quite understand the depth of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but Jon, how is she doing? I know we have to go, but how is she today? Tells people -- because she is --\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: She is doing great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Thank God. It`s a miracle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amen to that.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Praise God. It`s unbelievable how she is moving through the world right now. We`re moving through the world together, and this film is a celebration of so much, and I`m grateful that we can share it with the world and that it was accomplished in the way that it was accomplished.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait for people to see this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so powerful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so well done. You are walking joy to me. Really.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are walking --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re a walking instrument.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: Hello. Oh, yes, Nate.\par \par \uc2 (NATE BURLESON howling.)\par \par \uc2 