NATE BURLESON: And you`ve got a headset.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And you can buy that as well because it`s seven hours, take it the car.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Perfect holiday gift.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Speaking of the car.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: All right, Drop Stop. Now, this is one of Shark Tank`s best sellers and here`s the reason why. It saves lives. We`re going to place this over our seatbelt latch and then in between the driver`s side of the car and the center console. That`s the black hole that your phone drops down, your keys drop down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The fries.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Everything, the fries. This prevents all of that. LAPD put them in their cars, 3,500 cars in just one year --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s the price on this?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: They saw the accidents go down 43 percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, the accidents go down.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: $14.99.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that`s a great deal. You`ve got to get that.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: All right, Sparkles Home. Let`s elevate our home.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: With beautiful rhinestones. This is all high quality, but affordable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like this.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Designed for everyday use. Placemats, napkins, look at this, chairs. There are rhinestones in the stem.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There are little diamonds in here.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Think of gifting, think of buying a set of these, giving them to all of your friends, bringing them as hostess gifts. But you can dress up your table for every day, for Thanksgiving, Christmas, Hanukkah, Kwanzaa -- dress your tables beautifully and you`re going to see a difference.\par \par \uc2 And all of this starts at just $12.99. I know you can just take this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, this has Gayle written all over it.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: It does have. It matches her dress too, today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. All right, Elizabeth Werner, we appreciate you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com to start shopping now.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to an exclusive reveal of the newest cover model of "Vogue" in America. We are talking about one of the greatest rappers of her time, Nicki Minaj.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the December issue, Minaj opens up about her new album and giving birth to her son. She told "Vogue:" "I think that deep down inside I believe that once I had a family, I would just lose the desire to make music, maybe subconsciously, I hope my focus would just be on being a mother and I look forward to that idea. It felt like a relief, but what happens is that you find out you have to work."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, she looks great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She has been working it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She looks great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She has been. Looks amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nicki Minaj is one of the greatest artists out there. So we are looking forward to this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats to Nicki Minaj, cover of "Vogue."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like it. That will do it for us. We will see you tomorrow on your favorite show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020231109ejb900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for November 8, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Adriana Diaz, Scott MacFarlane, Jericka Duncan,  Charlie D`Agata, Weijia Jiang \par \uc2 3,607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, breaking news: The U.S. military carries out airstrikes in Syria, warplanes targeting a weapons storage site used by Iranian-backed groups after attacks on American service members in the Middle East. The new details.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon strikes back after U.S. troops in Iraq and Syria come under attack more than 40 times. And what we`re learning tonight about a $30 million American Reaper drone shot down off the coast of Yemen.\par \par \uc2 Plus, growing calls for a pause in Gaza, as the Israeli army claims control of Northern Gaza from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , blowing up more than 100 terrorist tunnels.\par \par \uc2 America decided. Major wins for Democrats in Kentucky, Virginia and Ohio, abortion rights a key issue driving voters to the polls. So what could it mean for the presidential election in 2024?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A marathon day of testimony for \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , as she`s grilled about the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 `s business practices, which the judge calls fraud.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news: a brothel bust, what the Justice Department calls a high-end network serving elected officials, military officers and tech executives.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA LEVY (Acting U.S. Attorney): Pick a profession, they`re probably represented in this case.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The fiery explosion at a chemical plant forcing neighbors to shelter in place.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF HARFIELD (Owner, Sound Resource Solutions): We employ almost 40 people. And what we`re grateful for is that every one of those people are going to be home eating dinner with their family tonight.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We are behind the scenes at the panda habitat at the Smithsonian`s National Zoo in Washington, where we are getting a sneak peek at what they`re packing up for the pandas, plenty of bamboo and some fruits and vegetables for the pandas` big return to China.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The end of an era. The panda express takes off from Washington. Why the National Zoo pandas are heading back to China.\par \par \uc2 And profiles in service, a story of perseverance and a Marine veteran who proves it`s never too late to chase your dreams.\par \par \uc2 MATT GANYARD (University of Virginia Placekicker): I don`t think the wow factor ever has really faded. Every moment, you know, gives me a little bit of chills.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 And we begin tonight with breaking news, because we are just learning from the Pentagon about airstrikes in Eastern Syria, and the target, a weapons storage facility linked to Iran and Iranian-backed groups.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of defense revealing that the U.S. military is responding to more than 40 attacks on U.S. forces in Iraq and Syria. These attacks have left dozens of our soldiers and troops with traumatic brain injuries.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get straight to the breaking news with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Good evening, Weijia. So what are you learning?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening to you, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, at the direction of President Biden, two F-15 fighter jets launched airstrikes in Eastern Syria at that weapons facility that is used by Iran`s Revolutionary Guard and proxy forces that are backed by Iran. They were in response to attacks by those groups on U.S. troops in both Syria and Iraq, over 40 in the last three weeks alone that have injured nearly 50 American service members.\par \par \uc2 And, just today, U.S. troops were attacked again in Syria. And, in another sign of the increased tensions in the region, an American Reaper drone costing about $30 million was shot down in the Red Sea by the Iranian- backed Houthi rebels. They had fired missiles last month toward Israel which were shot down by a U.S. Naval ship.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has repeatedly warned Iran, a longtime adversary of Israel, against getting involved in the war in the Middle East -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And they are sending a message tonight, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , amid heavy airstrikes on Gaza, the Israeli army claims to have destroyed some 130 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnels since the beginning of its ground operations, now this as more than 80 trucks carrying aid have entered Gaza in the past 24 hours, as there is still this push for a humanitarian pause.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata reports from Tel Aviv tonight that there is a deal -- or that there was a deal, I should say, to release some of the hostages, and it fell apart at the last minute.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): While more global leaders may be joining the U.S. in calling for a humanitarian pause here, in the skies above Gaza and the streets below, there is no hint of a letup.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  released new video said to show troops blowing up tunnels on the coastline of Gaza, even finding and destroying a tunnel shaft in an amusement park.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s defense minister said the \uc2 IDF\uc2  had reached the heart of Gaza City itself and they are tightening the noose.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants released this video today, said to show fighters scrambling through bombed-out buildings, fighting house to house and attacking Israeli tanks. The violent clashes have sent tens of thousands of residents streaming from the north.\par \par \uc2 But even the South provides no sanctuary.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals in the main southern city of Khan Yunis are overflowing with bomb victims. Each life lost adds to the international condemnation for the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned that, before Israel`s ground invasion, diplomats were on the cusp of a deal to get roughly 50 hostages out in exchange for a pause in bombing and delivery of aid, but the deal fell apart. The hunt for hostages remains a top priority, says \uc2 Mossad\uc2 `s former director of intelligence Zohar Palti.\par \par \uc2 ZOHAR PALTI (Former \uc2 Mossad\uc2  Intelligence Director): But, right now, in the tunnels of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , in the dark, there are some children 3, 5, 6 years old as hostages, without their parents. Guys, that is what the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , that is why it is in Gaza right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Tonight, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  reiterated that there will not be a cease-fire without the release of hostages -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Still that focus on the hostages.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, Republican candidates are squaring off in the GOP`s third debate. And it comes after what the party`s own strategists say was a major defeat at the polls last night. There was one key issue that dominated, abortion.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jericka Duncan is on the ground in Ohio, where voters overwhelmingly approved a constitutional amendment protecting reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): Abortion rights supporters are still celebrating after voters passed a state constitutional amendment enshrining a woman`s right to an abortion in Ohio, setting up a key issue ahead of the 2024 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): The voters said, look, the government should not be telling a woman what to do with her body.\par \par \uc2 BEN GUESS (Executive Director, ACLU of Ohio): We have always known that this was a national campaign, because what happens in Ohio was going to immediately become the -- the flash point for the way people were talking about issues in other states.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In Kentucky, where Republicans control the state legislature, incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  defeated Republican Attorney General \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DANIEL CAMERON\uc2  (R-Kentucky Gubernatorial Candidate): Well, that didn`t turn out exactly how I wanted it to.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Cameron said he would amend the state`s total abortion ban if elected to allow for exceptions in cases of rape or incest, but -- quote -- "only if the court made us change the law."\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, Democrats who campaigned on abortion rights took control of the state legislature there. Today, the state`s Republican Governor, Glenn Youngkin, spoke on the issue.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-Virginia): This is a very difficult topic across Virginia and across the nation.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Over 20 states either ban or restrict abortion access. But, since 2022, abortion rights supporters have prevailed in seven out of seven states where the issue has been on the ballot, including in Ohio last night.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS (President, \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 ): I think our country is just getting harder and more selfish. And the unborn child is the one that`s paying the price.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: National Right to Life president Carol Tobias says they will continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 There are 11 states right now, as you know, that are considering abortion on the ballot next year. What is your strategy?\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS: We have to find different and better ways to -- to reach out and get people to understand that killing the unborn child is not going to solve problems.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And abortion opponents say one of those key strategies is raising more money.\par \par \uc2 They acknowledge that they were not only outspent here in Ohio, Norah, but in other places where they lost.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Fascinating evening.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, in New York City, \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  spent much of the day testifying, not as a defendant, but as the state`s final witness at her family`s civil fraud trial. The judge has already ruled that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  committed fraud for years. This trial will determine how much the former president and his sons will pay for that fraud and whether they can continue to do business in New York.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is at the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: How are you doing, Ivanka?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  strode confidently into the courtroom this morning in New York, but, once on the stand, Ivanka, a former executive vice president in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , had trouble recounting many key details.\par \par \uc2 Answering "I don`t recall" dozens of time, Ivanka explained she had no knowledge of her father`s financial statements, as she did in a deposition last summer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from August 2022)\par \par \uc2 IVANKA TRUMP (Former Assistant to President Trump): I never prepared one. I don`t know. I have never made one. I`m not an accountant.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The attorney general`s office says she was an active liaison with lenders during a time when the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  landed sweetheart loans by lying about the former president`s wealth.\par \par \uc2 E-mails revealed to the court showed her involvement on the financing for the Doral Golf Club in Florida in 2011. She was copied on a submission to \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  of her father`s statement of financial conditions, which portrayed his net worth as $3 billion higher than it actually was. That resulted in a deal with much better loan rates than had been previously offered.\par \par \uc2 She then wrote to her fellow executives: "It doesn`t get better than this."\par \par \uc2 Ivanka said she didn`t remember sending the e-mail, but state lawyer Louis Solomon said it was clear she was negotiating the deal from top to bottom. The case has also focused attention on Ivanka`s negotiation of the 2011 lease of the Old Post Office building in Washington, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  turned into the Trump Hotel and then sold in 2022 for $139 million. Ivanka and brothers Eric and Don Jr. each reaped $4 million.\par \par \uc2 New York Attorney General Attorney General Letitia James said Ivanka will not be able to hide from the truth.\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES (New York Attorney General): At the end of the day, this case is about fraudulent statements of financial condition that she benefited from. She was enriched. And, clearly, you cannot distance yourself from that fact.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Sitting in the courtroom, the tone and tenor was so different than when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  testified and had flashes of anger. Ivanka spoke so softly and quietly, the judge frequently urged her to speak louder and into the microphone.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, whereas Trump had seething efforts towards the judge, \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  smiled at the judge and laughed at some of his jokes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans escalated their impeachment inquiry into President Biden today, issuing subpoenas for testimony from the president`s son Hunter and his brother James and also demanding written interviews from other associates and family members.\par \par \uc2 The House Oversight Committee claims there is evidence that the Biden family members set up shell companies to hide money from overseas, adding that Biden, his family, and associates allegedly received more than $24 million from foreign nationals and companies. They have not yet been able to confirm that.\par \par \uc2 An attorney for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  because the subpoenas a political stunt and the Republicans` claims discredited conspiracies.\par \par \uc2 A head-on crash today in South Texas left eight people dead. Officials say police were chasing a suspected human smuggler when the driver crashed into an SUV, killing two people from Georgia. The 21-year-old driver and five passengers were killed. Officials have confirmed that several of those killed were from Honduras.\par \par \uc2 An explosion and massive fire at an East Texas chemical plant prompted a shelter-in-place order today, as thick -- thick plumes of black smoke rose in the air. One worker was left with minor burns. Emergency officials say the company makes solvents for glue and paint remover. A nearby school was evacuated. Air quality in the area is being tested.\par \par \uc2 Now to the end of an era here in Washington. Hundreds of people gathered at the Smithsonian`s National Zoo today to say goodbye to the beloved giant pandas, who are heading home to China.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adriana Diaz was there and reports, the departure brings us closer to the end of more than a half-a-century of so-called panda diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHILD #1: Panda! Panda!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voiceover): These might be the heaviest hitters here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Did you see pandas?\par \par \uc2 CHILD #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHILD #3: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How --\par \par \uc2 CHILD #2: There`s one straight behind you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Oh, there is.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Drawing swarms to the National Zoo to see its last three pandas for the last time.\par \par \uc2 Brandie Smith is the zoo`s director.\par \par \uc2 BRANDIE SMITH (Director, Smithsonian National Zoo): They are just a joy. People watch them. They are happy. They smile. And I think joy is in short supply these days.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Look at how huge.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Under the zoo`s contract with China, all bears go back.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: The building is just going to be quiet, and that is going to be hard.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: An empty nest?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Empty nest.\par \par \uc2 Good boy.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Leaving zookeepers who have been with some since birth bracing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: I`m trying not to think about the end.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The zoo`s first pandas arrived in 1972.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 1972)\par \par \uc2 PAT NIXON (Former First Lady): I think pandemonium is going to break out right here at the zoo.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: After President Nixon and the first lady`s historic visit to the then-closed-off China.\par \par \uc2 Americans` love only grew with the panda cam. Bao Bao`s first snow day got over six million views. They were hooked on their Bei Bei`s famous, but shaky love of heights, before the birth of Xiao Qi Ji, which means Little Miracle.\par \par \uc2 Today, he and his parents headed home, with bamboo snacks for the plane. And their caregivers` long faces say it all. Analysts point to poor U.S.- China relations for the panda drawdown. But the zoo`s director is hopeful.\par \par \uc2 BRANDIE SMITH: I cannot imagine Washington, D.C., without giant pandas, and I will do everything I can to bring them back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Not all is lost.\par \par \uc2 Zoo Atlanta still has a pair of pandas, but they`re expected to go back to China early next year. Panda fans are now watching a meeting between President Biden and Chinese President Xi Jinping next week in San Francisco, hoping that a diplomatic breakthrough could increase chances for future pandas coming to America -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, fingers crossed for that, right, Adriana, fingers crossed. Need those pandas.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And there`s big news tonight about a newly approved weight loss drug. What you need to know next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Federal investigators say they busted a network of brothels in Massachusetts and Virginia that catered to well-connected clients, including elected officials and military officers.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say potentially hundreds of people, including doctors, lawyers, and pharmaceutical executives, paid up to $600 an hour in cash for sex, primarily with Asian women. Three people accused of operating the ring are in custody.\par \par \uc2 Well, in tonight`s Health Watch, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approves a new drug in the battle against obesity. A new version of the diabetes drug Mounjaro will now be marketed as Zepbound. It is for chronic weight management. In trials, people who took the highest dose lost, on average, 48 pounds. The price of the weekly shot is over $1,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 Drugmaker Eli Lilly says the cost will likely be lower if patients have insurance.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to introduce you now to a college football player in his 30s who is living proof of never giving up on your dreams.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to a story of perseverance against all odds.\par \par \uc2 In our series, Profiles in Service, we introduce you to one man who got to live out two of his dreams, a Marine veteran coming off the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from ACCN/\uc2 ESPN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voiceover): Kickers aren`t supposed to make tackles.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And taken down by Ganyard near the 40-yard line.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Matt Ganyard isn`t supposed to be a football player.\par \par \uc2 MATT GANYARD (University of Virginia Placekicker): The body is not responding the same way at 34 that it did at 18.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Woah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But the father of two and former soccer star is making a play after 16 years on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 MATT GANYARD: My dad and brother looked at me and said: "You have got a big leg for a soccer player. Have you ever thought about kicking? How far could you kick it?"\par \par \uc2 So, I took a ball out and kicked for the first time. It was absolutely terrible. That could have been it, but it kind of gave me a little bit of an itch to scratch and really go after learning this new craft.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ganyard`s football career took a time-out when he spent 10 years flying Cobra helicopters in the Marine Corps.\par \par \uc2 MATT GANYARD: In my mind, I was going to be a jet pilot, like my dad.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: His father spent nearly three decades in the Marines, but the road for Ganyard had a few speed bumps.\par \par \uc2 MATT GANYARD: I got to flight school, and, you know, I had some initial issues with airsickness. I remember being, you know, in tears talking to my dad and thinking this dream was going to fall apart before it ever really started.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: He persevered, and his dream took off, making way for the dream he is now living out on the field.\par \par \uc2 Ganyard`s service paused his \uc2 NCAA\uc2  eligibility, giving him his final chance.\par \par \uc2 MATT GANYARD: I don`t think the wow factor ever -- ever has really faded in stepping onto the field, whether just coming out of the tunnel for warmups or stepping onto the field for a kickoff.\par \par \uc2 Every moment, you know, gives me a little bit of chills. But, at the same time, you kind of have to compartmentalize it and focus on the job at hand. And that`s kicking off and putting the ball in the right position.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Matt graduates with his MBA in May, meaning he has only three more games as UVA`s placekicker, but he is still living out his dreams. A good reminder for all of us.\par \par \uc2 Diamonds are forever, and we will show you one in a class of its own. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: A record-setting piece of jewelry sold at Christie`s auction house in Geneva.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this. One lucky collector is the new owner of this ring known as the Bleu Royal featuring a rare pear-shaped blue diamond. At 17.61 carats, it was the star item up for bid at Christie`s Magnificent Jewels auction that included pearls worn by Audrey Hepburn and a self-engraved watch once owned by Marlon Brando.\par \par \uc2 This spectacular gem sold for $44 million, making it one of the top 10 most expensive diamonds ever sold.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231109ejb800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for November 7, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jericka Duncan, Jan Crawford, Jo Ling Kent,  Nikole Killion, Charlie D`Agata, Elise Preston \par \uc2 3,542 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: America decides. It`s election night for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the world marks one month since \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` deadly terror attack that killed more than 1,400 Israelis, and now Israeli forces are in the heart of Gaza City, as thousands of Palestinians escape south.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 We are inside Gaza tonight. The Israeli military takes us further inside the territory than any other journalists.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The commander here told us there were about 1,000 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants here to begin with. Now there are a few dozen.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: As Israeli soldiers advance, thousands of Palestinians flee, as the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  says the level of death is hard to fathom.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The military objective here has always been to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . But there`s growing international outrage, that means destroying everything.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news: A man is in custody, accused of having an assault-style rifle near the U.S. Capitol. The new details.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Did you have a gun?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A Jewish man dies after Israeli, Palestinian protesters clash. What police are saying tonight.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FRYHOFF (Ventura County, California, Sheriff): Investigators have not ruled out the possibility of a hate crime, and this is being investigated as a homicide.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: America decides. Millions head to the polls on this Election Day, one of the top issues, abortion. We speak to voters in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: While Ohio is the only state with abortion on the ballot today, key races in Pennsylvania, Virginia and Kentucky could impact the future of abortion access in those states.\par \par \uc2 ARTURO BEJAR (Former Engineering Director, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 ): It doesn`t need to be this way.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Protecting America`s teens on social media. The disturbing details from a former engineer for the parent company of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WeWork\uc2  files for bankruptcy. How the pandemic led to the fall of the tech company.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bruce Springsteen`s performance)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The Boss is back, Bruce Springsteen`s surprise performance, his first since suspending his tour.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bruce Springsteen`s performance)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are learning that President Biden is personally urging Prime Minister Netanyahu for a humanitarian pause in Gaza. But Israel is insisting a halt in fighting will only come after the more than 200 hostages taken by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are released.\par \par \uc2 It may not be the only disagreement between the two allies, Netanyahu saying he sees Israel playing a role in Gaza for -- quote -- "an indefinite period," specifically talking about security responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Well, you may recall President Biden told "60 Minutes" it would be a big mistake if Israel occupies Gaza after the war.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the fighting in the heart of Gaza City is intensifying. Look at these pictures. This comes as today marks one month since the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack that killed more than 1,400 people, the deadliest attack on Jews since the Holocaust. And now thousands of civilians are fleeing Gaza City as the battle between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  draws closer.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata, embedded with the Israeli army in Northern Gaza, he will start us off tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Charlie. And tell us what you saw.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 We saw destruction, destruction as far as the eye could see, no civilians. Even the soldiers that we were with said they had seen no civilians. But they were coming up against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants. In fact, while we were there, they had to reinforce other troops who were coming under fire.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): We joined the paratroopers of Israel`s 501st Airborne Brigade, among the first wave of forces to invade Gaza after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants went on their murderous rampage one month ago.\par \par \uc2 Gunners kept lookout as we crossed the desert scrubland of Northeast Gaza, until we reached Beit Hanoun. As we arrived, the sound of ongoing gun battles could be heard nearby.\par \par \uc2 The city had an estimated prewar population of more than 60,000 people. Looking around, it`s hard to imagine anyone lived here at all.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Colonel Ido Kass said there was little choice.\par \par \uc2 I know that the military objective is to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. IDO KASS (\uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 ): Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But you destroyed everything.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. IDO KASS: Yeah, because, look, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is using infrastructure. I mean, just 10 minutes ago, we had a serious battle with a group of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  inside the school. We found these rocket launchers.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: He showed us a missile site hidden in the backyard of a home next to what used to be a swimming pool.\par \par \uc2 The commander here told us there were about 1,000 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants here to begin with. Now there are a few dozen left. It`s not the threats above ground that they`re worried about, but the ones below it.\par \par \uc2 It was before the fight even began.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. IDO KASS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: As we were speaking, explosions and gunfire erupted a few blocks away. We were told a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighter emerged from a tunnel near a school and fired an RPG at troops.\par \par \uc2 Southwest of here, in Gaza City, Israeli troops closing in on the heart of the city have triggered a mass exodus south, splitting the territory in two.\par \par \uc2 On a highway south of the city, our CBS News colleague Marwan Al Ghoul spoke with some of those fleeing the fighting.\par \par \uc2 MARWAN AL GHOUL: Walking for hours to cross from Gaza north to the south, carrying nothing.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Except a white flag of surrender and the will to survive.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are civilian people. We want to live in our land with the peace. But nobody in the world let us to live.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: One month since this war began, residents face an uncertain future, first and foremost, when and how it will end.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Now, in terms of that embed, the Israeli military has tight restrictions on coverage, mainly having to do with not showing anything that might give away, like, locations or reveal the identities of their soldiers.\par \par \uc2 So, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  viewed our raw footage as a condition of entry into Gaza, but had no control over our final report -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, back here at home, protests and demonstrations on both sides of the conflict are taking place almost daily in cities across the country. While most are peaceful, some rallies have turned violent. Tonight, an investigation is under way after the death of a 69-year-old Jewish man who was fatally injured at a rally near Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston reports on the disturbing details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Thousand Oaks, California, is trying to come to grips with why 69-year-old Paul Kessler lay dying after a confrontation. Moments earlier, he had held an Israeli flag nearby pro-Palestinian demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FRYHOFF (Ventura County, California, Sheriff): Investigators have not ruled out the possibility of a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: But no one has been charged. And police say they still have no video showing why and how Kessler fell to the ground, fracturing his skull. He died hours later.\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF JAMES FRYHOFF: These witnesses providing conflicting statements about what the altercation and who the aggressor was.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: With emotions often reaching a fever pitch nationwide and with attacks on the rise fueled by antisemitism and Islamophobia, this community may be the latest impacted.\par \par \uc2 Does this incident change your safety and patrol measures at demonstrations, regardless of the size?\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF JAMES FRYHOFF: No. So, we are not changing our protocol. Fifteen minutes prior to this confrontation, there was no indication of impending violence.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The Los Angeles chapter of the \uc2 Council on American-Islamic Relations\uc2  said in a statement: "We join local Jewish leaders in calling on all individuals to refrain from jumping to conclusions."\par \par \uc2 At a makeshift vigil, tributes and anguish.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My heart hurts that someone innocently left home and he never came back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, investigators tell us there have been many conflicting statements about who actually started this incident.\par \par \uc2 They`re asking anyone with video to come forward. They`re actually hoping someone who drove by captured the altercation on their car cameras -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There are cameras everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here in Washington, there was a security alert on Capitol Hill today when a man carrying a semiautomatic rifle was arrested by police in a park near the Senate office building.\par \par \uc2 We get the latest from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): A tense scene near the U.S. Capitol, as police took down a 21-year-old man who was carrying a semiautomatic weapon.\par \par \uc2 TOM MANGER (\uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  Chief): He was holding the gun down as he walked. They ordered him to stop. He did not. One officer Tasered him in the back. He fell forward onto his gun.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The suspect was identified as Ahmir Lavon Merrell of Atlanta, Georgia. A law enforcement source tells CBS News the gun seized was a modified AR-15 rifle. Police did not say why he came to the Capitol, but he may have suffered from mental health issues.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): It`s good that they found this person, but we`re keeping a constant vigilance on security.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Authorities say there was no threat to the congressional community. But the incident follows another one this week where \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  confiscated a pair of Glock handguns after two suspects crashed a stolen vehicle into a barricade.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JOE MORELLE (D-New York): I`m very concerned about the -- the heightened threats against members. This is something we`re continuously dealing with.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The investigation is still ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Police are trying to figure out how Merrell may have gotten the gun. He faces a warrant and several charges tonight, including having an unregistered firearm -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion on the Hill for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There was also an important hearing on Capitol Hill today about the threats teenagers face on social media. A former \uc2 Facebook\uc2  engineer told a Senate panel that \uc2 Meta\uc2  dismissed warnings about unwanted sexual advances and widespread bullying aimed at teens on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , including his own daughter.\par \par \uc2 We have more on this from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): Tonight, \uc2 Meta\uc2  under fire once again.\par \par \uc2 ARTURO BEJAR (Former Engineering Director, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 ): I appear before you today as a dad with firsthand experience of a child who received unwanted sexual advances on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Former Meta engineer Arturo Bejar testified before a Senate subcommittee today, alleging that the social media giant has not done enough to address the harmful effects of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  on teens.\par \par \uc2 ARTURO BEJAR: My 14-year-old daughter joined \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . She and her friends began having awful experiences, including repeated unwanted sexual advances, harassment. She reported this incident -- incidents to the company, and it did nothing.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: While working as a consultant in October 2021, Bejar says he sent this e-mail to CEO and founder \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  head \uc2 Adam Mosseri\uc2 , sharing his daughter`s negative online experiences, but says his outreach was ignored by the founder.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR PETER WELCH (D-Vermont): And just the disregard for the mental health of our kids is -- is truly shocking.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: What should parents be taking away from this?\par \par \uc2 DANNY WEISS (\uc2 Common Sense Media\uc2 ): Parents need to become more aware of the digital lives that their children are leading. Companies need to take more responsibility for the way their products are designed. And policymakers need to enact new laws to hold those companies accountable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: A bipartisan bill aimed at protecting kids online is now making its way through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in response to Bejar`s claims, \uc2 Meta\uc2  tells me that, every day, countless people inside the company are trying to keep young people safe, and it has already introduced more than 30 tools to support teens and their families online -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such an important story for parents and families.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 A case argued at the Supreme Court today could put the safety of people in abusive relationships at serious risk, while also impacting gun laws in many states. Now, at the center of the case is a federal law that bans those under domestic violence restraining orders from having guns.\par \par \uc2 We get more from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RUTH GLENN (President, Survivor Justice Action): The real fear of losing your life when you leave is real. It`s a real fear.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): Ruth Glenn is head of a national advocacy group for victims of domestic violence. She also is a survivor.\par \par \uc2 RUTH GLENN: Eventually, he later found me a few months -- a few weeks later, and shot me three times and left me for dead.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Her organization was one of many today urging the Supreme Court to uphold a 30-year-old federal law banning guns for people under restraining orders for domestic violence, like defendant Zackey Rahimi.\par \par \uc2 Rahimi, under a protective order for assaulting and threatening to shoot his ex-girlfriend, was convicted of violating the law after he was involved in several other alleged shooting incidents in Texas. In the Supreme Court today, his lawyer argued the law violated the Second Amendment right to keep and bear arms, prompting this exchange with Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2  (Chief Justice of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 ): You don`t have any doubt that your client`s a dangerous person, do you?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW WRIGHT (Assistant Federal Public Defender): Your Honor, I would want to know what dangerous person means. At the moment --\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE JOHN ROBERTS: Well, it means someone who`s shooting, you know, at people. That`s a good start.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Rahimi`s argument could have wide-ranging implications for modern-day gun laws, as well as a system of background checks. Over the last 25 years, the law has stopped people under protective orders from buying guns about 78,000 times.\par \par \uc2 The court seemed almost unanimously poised to keep it in place.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice): You don`t give guns to people who have the kind of history of domestic violence that your client has.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, ever since the court ruled that there`s an individual right to bear arms, there`s been a lot of confusion in the lower courts over what kind of gun laws are constitutional.\par \par \uc2 This case could also provide some much-needed guidance on some of those other gun regulations, including efforts to ban specific kinds of weapons - - Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jan Crawford, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to Election Day across the country, voters from coast to coast are going to the polls to weigh in on important races, including governor, state assemblies and mayors in several major cities. Kentucky and Mississippi voters will decide if their governors deserve four more years or if they should be replaced.\par \par \uc2 But there`s one issue that`s hanging over several races, abortion. Abortion rights are on the ballot in Ohio, and it`s also played a central role in the campaigns in Virginia for control of the General Assembly.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jericka Duncan is on the ground in Columbus, Ohio, to find out what`s on the minds of voters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): Abortion rights supporters rallied this afternoon in a last-minute effort to get voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to voters in a district that traditionally leans Democratic.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE ANDERSON (Ohio Voter): I remember what illegal abortions are like. They`re not good. I`d rather have women have a choice.\par \par \uc2 LEAH OCHSENHIRT (Ohio Voter): I think it`s important as women to keep, you know, fighting for our rights.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The proposed amendment, called Issue 1, would guarantee a woman`s right in Ohio to an abortion. It would allow the procedure up until fetal viability, usually around the 23rd week, according to doctors. Exceptions beyond that point would be permitted if a physician determines an abortion is necessary for the health or life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Protect Women Ohio)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Under this amendment, any kind of abortion is going to be fine. This is too extreme for Ohio.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Ohio`s Republican Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  calls the amendment radical and says it will allow abortion at any point during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR MIKE DEWINE (R-Ohio): The majority of Ohioans do not believe that abortion should be allowed at any point.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: DeWine signed a six-week abortion ban into law in 2019 that is currently on hold in the courts.\par \par \uc2 Amy Natoce, a spokesperson with Protect Women Ohio, is against Issue 1.\par \par \uc2 AMY NATOCE (Protect Women Ohio): We`re stripping ability away from the legislature to go forward and pass protections for women and the unborn.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: While Ohio is the only state with abortion on the ballot today, key races in Pennsylvania, Virginia and Kentucky could impact the future of abortion access in those states.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, all 140 General Assembly seats are up for grabs, with Republicans pushing for a 15-week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Since Roe v. Wade was overturned last year, abortion rights supporters have prevailed in six out of six states when the issue of abortion is on the ballot -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: An important note.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And CBS News will bring you all the results in an election night special, America Decides, starting at 8:00 p.m. Eastern on the CBS Streaming Network, and we hope you will join us.\par \par \uc2 Heavy smoke and fog are blamed for another deadly crash in Louisiana. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A dangerous combination of fog and wildfire smoke is being blamed for another series of crashes outside New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 One man was killed and several others were injured today on Interstate 10. Officials are urging drivers to use extreme caution in the area this week. This comes after -- two weeks after seven people were killed in a massive smoke-and-fog-related pileup on Interstate 55 involving nearly 170 vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, officials in Southern California are investigating what caused a fire today that destroyed a historic building once used to store blimps during World War II. In recent years, it appeared in TV shows and movies, including JAG, The X-Files, Austin Powers, and Star Trek. Helicopters used to battle wildfires were sent in to knock down the flames.\par \par \uc2 It`s a stunning fall for the office-sharing company \uc2 WeWork\uc2 . What led to the once-$47 billion company`s collapse? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The office-sharing company \uc2 WeWork\uc2  has filed for bankruptcy protection.\par \par \uc2 The New York-based company once valued at $47 billion is now struggling with debt and trying to get out of long-term leases. \uc2 WeWork\uc2  started to crater during the COVID pandemic when demand for office space drastically fell.\par \par \uc2 The Boss is back. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight: The Boss is back and better than ever.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bruce Springsteen`s performance, courtesy of EMW212/\uc2 YouTube\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Bruce Springsteen appeared on stage for the first time since postponing his 2023 tour dates to seek treatment for peptic ulcer disease.\par \par \uc2 The 74-year-old rock legend gave a surprise performance last night at the Stand Up For Heroes fund-raiser in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Bob Woodruff Foundation\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: He joined John Mellencamp for a duet of their song "Wasted Days" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bruce Springsteen and John Mellencamp`s performance; \uc2 Bob Woodruff Foundation\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- at the event to honor injured veterans and their families. The New Jersey native thrilled the audience as he played four solo songs, including his classic "Dancing in the Dark."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bruce Springsteen`s performance, courtesy of EMW212/\uc2 YouTube\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s good to see the Boss.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231108ejb700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for October 31, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Adriana Diaz, Mike Bettes, Jonathan Vigliotti,  Jeff Pegues, Scott MacFarlane, Charlie D`Agata \par \uc2 3,535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the new warning from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 : \uc2 Hamas\uc2  could inspire terror attacks right here in the United States, with the threat against the homeland reaching a -- quote -- "whole other level." Plus, a deadly airstrike on a refugee camp in Gaza, Israel saying it killed a top \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Part of a refugee camp leveled, the catastrophic damages and civilian deaths. Plus, Israeli troops move deeper into Gaza, as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters emerge from its vast tunnel network.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2  (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): It is a time to be concerned. We are in a dangerous period.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the greatest threat to America since ISIS, the alarming testimony from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 : This is not a time for panic, but it is a time for vigilance.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: New disturbing details about warning signs in that Maine mass shooting, as we learn the family of the gunman went to police nearly six months before he killed 18 people.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We`ve got to go!\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We`re seeing for the first time chaotic bodycam footage of officers in Maui racing to evacuate residents from that deadly wildfire, the police outmatched, trying to fend off the flames with garden hoses.\par \par \uc2 Abortion in America. Why a near-total ban in one state left a doctor hopeless and frustrated after her patient was forced to travel out of state for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 BECCA VINCEN-BROWN (Mother): The trauma that I experienced, I blame completely on the legislation.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Miraculous rescue after a small plane crashes in the Everglades.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: We`re very grateful that he`s OK.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A chilling forecast for Halloween. The only state in the continental U.S. not facing a frigid forecast, we will tell you.\par \par \uc2 And a special visit for one hospital`s little superheroes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They`re like family to us, you know. So, to be able to have a little bit of fun, bring some smiles to their faces is what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with Israel`s invasion of Gaza growing more intense, with troops and tanks advancing further into enemy territory, this as \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  warned senators on Capitol Hill that war could inspire extremist groups to launch attacks right here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Israeli forces are engaged in urban combat with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters as they advance toward the densely populated center of Gaza City. Two Israeli soldiers were killed, the first since the ground incursion. Israel says it hit about 300 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  targets with airstrikes in the last 24 hours, including at the Jabalia refugee camp in Gaza, causing catastrophic damage.\par \par \uc2 Several apartment buildings were destroyed, killing dozens of people. And we learned today that the U.S. is sending 300 more U.S. troops to the Middle East. They won`t go into Israel, but they will help with things like communications, disposal of explosives and other support.\par \par \uc2 And back here in Washington, protesters calling for a cease-fire disrupted Secretary of State Antony Blinken`s testimony on Capitol Hill. Senators today confirmed former Treasury Secretary Jack Lew is the new U.S. ambassador to Israel. That`s actually a position that`s been vacant for more than three months.\par \par \uc2 We have team coverage of the war.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata will start us off tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, fierce fighting is under way inside Gaza. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has fired a barrage of rockets into Israel, several aiming here at Tel Aviv throughout the day, while rescue teams tonight are racing to find survivors in the latest series of Israeli airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): The vast craters left behind as deep as they are wide, the sheer destruction of the surrounding residential buildings, the terrible human toll are all evidence of the ferocity of the Israeli airstrike north of Gaza City, right in the middle of the densely packed refugee camp.\par \par \uc2 One survivor said it felt like an earthquake. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  said dozens were killed, with another 150 badly wounded, making it one of the deadliest single attacks since fighting began.\par \par \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  released more video today said to show its troops advancing through a desert scrubland, bulldozers paving away through the sand and debris for tanks, and soldiers on patrol working their way through the remains of bombed-out buildings after weeks of airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has its own video, fighters emerging from tunnels on the Gaza-Israeli border, saying its fighters have been using machine guns and anti-tank missiles against advancing troops. Both sides report fierce fighting at multiple locations along the strip.\par \par \uc2 This map gives a clearer picture, with Israeli ground assaults from the north and the east, while previous Israeli naval commandos have targeted \uc2 Hamas\uc2  positions from the sea. The Israeli military says it struck 300 targets in the past day, aiming for the vast \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnel systems and the command centers located within them.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been able to retaliate, raining rockets down on Israeli cities north of Gaza like Ashdod, even reaching areas as far as Tel Aviv. In another dangerous development, the Southern Red Sea resort of Eilat found itself under attack again, fighter jets intercepting drones and the Arrow defense system shooting down a missile fired from Iranian-backed Houthi rebels in Yemen before they reached Israeli airspace.\par \par \uc2 (REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "\uc2 IDF\uc2  forces identified an aerial target," the \uc2 IDF\uc2  spokesman said. "Alerts were sounded. There is no threat, and there is no danger."\par \par \uc2 (YAHYA SAREA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The Houthi military spokesman not only claimed responsibility, but warned of more to come unless the Israeli aggressive in Gaza stops.\par \par \uc2 The massive strike on Gaza`s largest refugee camp, more images of the dead and wounded will only inflame a region already outraged by Israel`s military operation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And, tonight, the Israeli military claimed that airstrike in the refugee camp killed a top \uc2 Hamas\uc2  commander who was one of the leaders of the October 7 massacre here, as well as a number of other militants -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to those growing terror threats on the U.S. homeland. \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  was on Capitol Hill today, warning that the risk of a terrorist attack on the U.S. has risen -- quote -- "to a whole other level" not seen since the emergence of ISIS.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): A sober warning from the director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  could lead to attacks in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2  (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): Our most immediate concern is that violent extremists, individuals or small groups, will draw inspiration from the events in the Middle East to carry out attacks against Americans going about their daily lives.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  said domestic terrorists could target Jewish or Muslim communities, and he specifically mentioned the arrest of a man in Houston earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 : Who had been studying how to build bombs and posted online about his support for killing Jews.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: He was referring to Sohaib Abuayyash. In court records obtained by CBS News, prosecutors allege the 20-year-old Jordanian living in Texas, seen here at a firing range, trained with weapons to possibly commit an attack and talked of killing people of particular religious faiths.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 : We shouldn`t stop conducting our daily lives, going to schools, houses of worship, and so forth. But we should be vigilant.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is also probing antisemitic threats against students at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 . And, today, New York`s governor announced a person of interest in custody.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): Every single New Yorker has a right to feel safe and to be safe.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meanwhile, in Nevada, a judge has scheduled a November 13th hearing in the case of John Anthony Miller, charged with making violent, vulgar, antisemitic threats toward Democratic Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  and showing up outside her Las Vegas office.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JACKY ROSEN (D-Nevada): Look at the protests around the world. Jews are feeling under attack.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  director also said al Qaeda and ISIS have called for attacks on the U.S., but said, at this time, they`re not tracking any imminent credible threats -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane on the Hill for us, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we`re getting a clearer picture of the warning signs, some going back months, that perhaps could have prevented the mass shooting in Maine that killed 18 people.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jeff Pegues reports, families are asking why more wasn`t done.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB YOUNG (Family Member of Shooting Victims): Bill was his hero.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES (voiceover): Rob Young`s older brother Bill and his 14-year- old nephew, Aaron, were enjoying a father-son night out last Wednesday when the gunman burst into the bowling alley and started shooting.\par \par \uc2 ROB YOUNG: It`s a tragedy that it had to be at a bowling alley where they were taken.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: He`s frustrated enough wasn`t done to stop Robert Card before the deadly rampage.\par \par \uc2 Are you frustrated, angry?\par \par \uc2 ROB YOUNG: Honestly, I can`t get angry, because I will snap. I have been trying really hard not to -- not to dwell too hard on it. You know, they -- he killed one of our children.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: In newly released documents, the Sagadahoc County Sheriff`s Department says that, in May, the Card family reached out to them to tell them that Robert Card`s mental health was deteriorating.\par \par \uc2 In July, Card`s Army unit had him admitted for a psychiatric evaluation for 14 days, noting that he was hearing voices and making threats against the base. In September, after continued threats to shoot up a drill center, the Army Reserve requested the sheriff`s department do a wellness check.\par \par \uc2 On back-to-back days in September, a deputy visited Card`s home, but did not reach him. And that prompted a statewide alert that went out saying he was armed and dangerous. Card`s brother also told a deputy that he would work to secure any firearms that Card had access to.\par \par \uc2 A month later, the statewide alert was canceled, just one week before the massacre.\par \par \uc2 Bill Bratton\uc2  is the former New York City police commissioner.\par \par \uc2 What should have happened?\par \par \uc2 BILL BRATTON (Former New York City Police Commissioner): A couple of welfare visits was not enough, quite clearly. A full investigation will determine exactly, what actions did the deputies take?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Another warning sign came from the Army, telling law enforcement here that Card should not be around ammunition or firearms.\par \par \uc2 Norah, the funerals for the 18 victims named on these crosses behind me will begin over the next several days.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jeff Pegues, thank you for being there.\par \par \uc2 Now to some newly released videos showing the police response to the nation`s deadliest wildfire in modern history. The bodycam video shows how officers tried to get residents out of harm`s way, but were quickly overwhelmed by the Maui wildfire.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti covered the catastrophe on the ground in Lahaina that left 99 dead.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We got to go!\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): They go door to door --\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Got to evacuate now. There`s a fire.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: -- neighborhood to neighborhood, these Maui police officers knowing time is running out, this bodycam video revealing not just the urgency --\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: There`s a fire.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: -- but also care.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: We won`t let you fall.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It was a chaotic last-minute evacuation after Maui officials failed to sound warning sirens. For many, this was their first alert.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what the world saw on August 8th as flames raced through Front Street in Lahaina.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: Come out. Come out. Come out.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: This is what police saw at 6:42 p.m., a group huddled inside a coffee.\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: Come with me. Come with me.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Fifteen rescued with embers flying all around.\par \par \uc2 MAN #7: I`m sorry, dude.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Later, an officer helps a man with severe burns.\par \par \uc2 With no fire trucks in sight, officers cut fences and even tried to fight what became America`s deadliest wildfire in a century with garden hoses. At the start of the fast-moving fire, only 13 Maui police officers were on duty, outmatched.\par \par \uc2 MAN #8: Please turn around. We have too many casualties.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Ninety-nine died in the fire. Thousands of homes and businesses lay in ruins. But Maui`s police chief says something else is evident in the 20 hours of video.\par \par \uc2 JOHN PELLETIER (Maui County, Hawaii, Police Chief): You saw officers saving lives. You saw officers going into houses and getting people out of harm`s way.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And the county, including the police department, is currently under investigation for its emergency response.\par \par \uc2 And it is important to point out tonight the Maui Police Department released 911 calls and now all of this video only after media organizations, including CBS News, requested public records, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jonathan Vigliotti has been pressing for answers from day one. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, turning now to the weather, it will be a freezing Halloween for millions of trick-or-treaters in all the Lower 48 states, except for Florida.\par \par \uc2 For the cold forecast, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Happy Halloween, and good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 A very frigid forecast this Halloween across much of the country. If you look at this map, you see all these blue dots. It indicates locations that will set record low temperatures or record low high temperatures. And you can see it is a ton of states.\par \par \uc2 For trick-or-treating, very cold, many spots down into the 30s, including a 32 in Kansas City, 36 with snow in Chicago, temperatures in the morning the coldest of the season in many spots, including freezing in Dallas, 27 in Nashville. And what a contrast in Nashville, over the weekend, temperatures in the upper 80s, tomorrow morning, temperatures that will be 60 degrees colder at 27.\par \par \uc2 Cold air returns again Thursday morning. The good news here, Norah, is, if you don`t like the cold, temperatures warm up gradually heading into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good news.\par \par \uc2 Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last year, 15 states have passed near-total bans on abortion. One of the most restrictive laws is in Idaho, where abortion is a felony under most circumstances.\par \par \uc2 In a CBS News investigation, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adriana Diaz traveled to Idaho, where even supporters of the ban say there have been unexpected consequences for women`s health.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BECCA VINCEN-BROWN (Mother): I can`t describe the amount of love that I have for this little girl.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (voiceover): Becca Vincen-Brown was overjoyed about having a sibling for her daughter. But at 16 weeks, tests revealed the fetus had a fetal diagnosis with multiple abnormal organs.\par \par \uc2 BECCA VINCEN-BROWN: That really kind of hit both my husband and I that it wasn`t going to be a viable baby.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Her options were, wait until she miscarried or had a stillbirth or leave the state for an abortion. They drove seven hours to Portland with their 2-year-old, because they didn`t have childcare.\par \par \uc2 After the first night of a two-day procedure, she went into labor in her hotel bathroom.\par \par \uc2 BECCA VINCEN-BROWN: I had to stifle cries, because my child is on the other side of the wall.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Sleeping.\par \par \uc2 BECCA VINCEN-BROWN: Sleeping. And my other child is sitting in the hotel bathroom.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: When Dr. Anne Feighner, Vincen-Brown`s O.B. in Boise, heard what happened, she was overcome with emotion.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNE FEIGHNER (OB/GYN): Sadness, feeling like there was so much harm that happened to her that didn`t need to happen.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How dangerous was it for her?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNE FEIGHNER: Very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Why is there so much confusion over the exemption that abortions are allowed if it`s to save a woman`s life?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNE FEIGHNER: Because it`s really hard to define at what point you`re saving that woman`s life.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Idaho`s laws are fueling a maternal care exodus. More than half of the state`s high-risk O.B.s will be gone by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel there were unintended consequences?\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE JOHN VANDER WOUDE (R-IDAHO): Obviously.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Idaho State Representative Republican John Vander Woude voted for the abortion ban, but now says his position has evolved.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE JOHN VANDER WOUDE: I mean (sp?), we have to have something in there that includes the health of the mother, not just the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNE FEIGHNER: Hello. How are you (inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Vincen-Brown is expecting again --\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNE FEIGHNER: Good. How have things been going?\par \par \uc2 BECCA VINCEN-BROWN: Good.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: -- and doesn`t want anyone else to go through what she did.\par \par \uc2 BECCA VINCEN-BROWN: The trauma that I experienced, I blame completely on the legislation. And it`s really pushing me to try to make a change.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Vincen-Brown is now suing the state of Idaho, along with other patients and doctors, over its abortion ban, which they say is resulting in -- quote -- "grave harms to pregnant patients."\par \par \uc2 The Center for Reproductive Rights, which filed the suit, has brought similar suits in three other states, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: An important story.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A small plane crashes in the Everglades, leaving the pilot stranded for hours. The dramatic rescue next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There was a remarkable rescue today in South Florida.\par \par \uc2 A man crashed his small plane into the gator- and bug-infested Everglades overnight, and then he was stranded for nine hours until first responders finally got to him. Officials say they didn`t learn he crashed until 10:00 a.m. You can see -- look at this -- he was sitting on the wing of his mostly submerged plane when a helicopter crew got to the scene and hoisted him to safety with minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 Now that crash is under investigation.\par \par \uc2 A major court victory for \uc2 Tesla\uc2  today, a jury in California finding Tesla`s autopilot technology was not to blame for a deadly crash in 2019. Survivors of the crash accused the automaker of knowing its driver-assistant technology was defective. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  argued the crash was caused by human error and that the driver still has to keep their hands on the wheel while using autopilot.\par \par \uc2 The jury ruled that the driver`s Model 3 was not defective when it went off the road.\par \par \uc2 Passengers are trapped aboard \uc2 Disney\uc2 `s world-famous monorail. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A ride on \uc2 Disney\uc2  World`s iconic monorail system came to a sudden halt today when it suddenly blew a tire.\par \par \uc2 The monorail got stuck near EPCOT. It was about 15 feet above the ground, and workers used a lift to evacuate more than 70 passengers. No one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 The Caped Crusader and your friendly neighborhood Spider-Man visit patients at a California hospital. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: Pediatric patients at one California hospital got an early treat on this Halloween night.\par \par \uc2 Batman and Spider-Man came to the rescue of kids at the Ventura County Medical Center. They rappelled down the wall, to the delight of the children, parents and staff. When they were done, they came back down to earth to take photos and hand out gifts.\par \par \uc2 And that actually wasn`t Bruce Wayne or Peter Parker, but actually volunteers from the Ventura County Fire Department`s urban search-and- rescue team. Our firefighters are our real-life heroes.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231101ejav00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Second Stage of Conflict; Anti-Semitic Mob Storms Airport; Gun Violence Outbreak; Pence Drops Out; Trump Leads in Iowa; Maine Mass Shooting Aftermath; Remembering Matthew Perry; \uc2 UAW\uc2  Agreements Reached; Underground Warfare; Breast Cancer and Young Women; Josh Groban Live; A Lesson in Vision, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Debora Patta, Holly  Williams, Robert Costa, Jeff Pegues, Kevin Frazier, Kris Van Cleave,  Vladimir Duthiers, Ramy Inocencio, Christina Ruffini, Celine Gounder, Steve  Hartman, Meryl Hartman, Emmett Hartman \par \uc2 13,389 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday. It`s a new week, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 An anti-Semitic mob storms an airport in Russia looking for Jewish passengers as Israel faces growing pressure over Gaza.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (White House National Security adviser): We do not stand for the killing of innocent people, whether it be Palestinian Israeli or otherwise.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The auto workers expand their strike against \uc2 General Motors\uc2  after reaching tentative deals with Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (\uc2 UAW\uc2  president): We truly are saving the American Dream.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  goes from court to the campaign trail as his former VP drops out.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): People are leaving now and they`re all endorsing me. I don`t know about \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , he should endorse me.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Tampa, Florida, two people were killed and 18 others were hurt in the shooting there during Halloween celebrations.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Now look at Travis Kelce today and Taylor Swift is not here.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Broncos upset the Chiefs.\par \par \uc2 MAN: "Shake it Off" play as the Broncos exited the field.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tributes are pouring in tonight for actor, Matthew Perry. The beloved "Friends" star died in an apparent drowning at the age of 54.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Singer, Charlie Puth and an arena full of "Friends" fans sang the theme song during his concert.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Charlie Puth concert.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Charlie Puth`s voice so beautiful, because that song is normally so upbeat, but when you realize why it`s being played, it is very haunting when you hear it that way.\par \par \uc2 We were all surprised at the death of Matthew Perry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It gives the words different meaning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually thought that, too. Kevin Frazier is going to join us a little bit later on to discuss that very sad news.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with Israeli troops inside Gaza this morning for what Israel calls the next stage of the war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  released this video of infantry and armored vehicles on the move. You see them there. They`re entering Gaza from the north after another weekend of very intense bombing, causing large numbers of civilian casualties. That`s according to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  officials.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the Israeli government says they have now confirmed the death of a young woman believed to have been kidnapped by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  at a music festival and held hostage. You`ve seen her picture for several days now. They say this is -- they found the body of Shani Louk. It was discovered in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta is in East Jerusalem for us following all the latest developments.\par \par \uc2 Debora, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: A very good morning.\par \par \uc2 Tensions are mounting here in Jerusalem and the West Bank. Earlier this morning, four Palestinians were killed in clashes with Israeli forces coming as this war intensifies.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Determined to show he is in full control, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  visited Israeli troops over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "You`re surrounded by a sea of love," he told them. "I salute you."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: This is the public face of Israel`s war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , grainy \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  footage showing a clean clinical operation.\par \par \uc2 But many here view the war very differently. In a small protest in Ashdod, Reuma Kadeem (sp?) who lost her daughter and grandchildren in the brutal October 7th attack is furious. Her anger directed at Netanyahu.\par \par \uc2 (REUMA KADEEM speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "How long will we continue with this bloodshed?" She said, "If this man does not go, we won`t have a solution."\par \par \uc2 In the West Bank, Palestinian teenager, Yazan Najar (sp?) told us he is outraged the children of Gaza have been forgotten.\par \par \uc2 YAZAN NAJAR (Palestinian teenager): It makes me sick that the world is turning its back on us and it`s not doing anything to protect us.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: As Israel pounds Gaza, Palestinians trapped inside this living hell have nowhere safe to hide. They are running out of everything.\par \par \uc2 Some so desperate, they broke into a United Nations warehouse grabbing anything they could get their hands on.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  news producer, Marwan Al-Ghoul drove through what`s left of the northern part of Gaza City.\par \par \uc2 MARWAN AL-GHOUL: Suddenly, massive airstrike hit this house. I can see the bodies of a woman here. A little boy is here, and others are looking for more deaths and more victims.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Ambulances rush to rescue the critically injured. Many are sheltering in hospitals, which have received repeated orders from the Israeli military to evacuate, but the Red Crescent says, if they move the hundreds of patients in intensive care, they will die.\par \par \uc2 And the key from Gaza is growing louder, "We need peace."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And both the United Nations and US officials are urging Israel to prioritize the protection of civilians -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Debora Patta for us in East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Debora, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In all this, President Biden has a tricky balance to strike as the war intensifies, and on a call yesterday with Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , Mr. Biden reiterated Israel`s right to defend itself. However, he noted that is within the bounds of international law, the laws of war.\par \par \uc2 To talk about that and more, we are joined now by John Kirby, the National Security Council`s coordinator for Strategic Communications.\par \par \uc2 Admiral, good morning to you. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to dive right in because there`s a lot to get to.\par \par \uc2 For two weeks or more now, we`ve seen thousands of bombs and civilians suffering in Gaza. Israel obviously says here, there are military targets amid all of that rubble. Can you confirm that the US has intelligence that underscores that Israel is right to target these particular buildings at these particular times?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): Well, I certainly won`t speak to intelligence matters, and I obviously am not going to talk for the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  and their operations, that`s really for them to speak about.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  does represent a significant and real threat to the Israeli people and, frankly, to the Israeli state and they proved that on October 7th, and so there is absolutely every right and responsibility for Israeli leaders to go after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as a terrorist organization and to eliminate that threat.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, how they are doing that, that`s really for them to speak to. We also believe and President Biden has said multiple times, that this puts an added burden on Israel, because it`s such a densely populated area, Gaza, in particular there in the north, they`ve got that extra burden of making sure that they`re doing everything they can to protect innocent life. That`s hard for any military fighting in an urban environment.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Admiral, I mean, the US conditions aid, weapons, money to any partner, and that includes Israel on following the law. So I`m asking you a pretty simple question, which is, can you confirm that from the US` understanding currently, all of these strikes we`re seeing in Israel are in fact, within the laws of war?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): We are not evaluating each and every strike they take, and we`re not going to react in real time to each and every operation that they conduct. We have been in constant communication with the Israelis, including over the weekend, as you rightly noted, from the president, at his level with Prime Minister Netanyahu to stress the importance of respect for innocent civilian life, to respect the law of war, and to minimize casualties to the maximum extent possible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, the point of war is to win a more stable peace. So, I want to jump a little bit ahead and talk about the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 I was in Israel last week and senior members of that administration, and also the \uc2 IDF\uc2  told me that they expect to work with partners in the region and the world to rebuild Gaza after this and create a new security regime.\par \par \uc2 Who are these partners? And are there any Arab states among them?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): Well, as President Biden said in the Rose Garden last week out there with the Australian prime minister, we can`t go -- we can`t just go back to October 6th, the day before these attacks. There has got to be a better vision for the Palestinian people going forward.\par \par \uc2 He still believes in a two-state solution, and he is obviously working with partners throughout the region -- Arab partners, certainly our Israeli counterparts, the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2 , we`re working with all of those to try to find a way to get to a two-state solution so that the Palestinian people can live in peace and security.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Admiral, I want to talk about the American hostages. Something else I heard last week in Israel is that the \uc2 IDF\uc2  cannot confirm that the hostages are even alive at this point. Does America have the ability to say yes, we know these people are alive and we`re working to free them?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): We have a very limited amount of information to work with right now, because we`re not on the ground. This is an \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2  operation. We`re doing the best we can to try to ascertain as much information as we can about all the hostages. Certainly, the Americans that are being held hostage as well. We want to get them home to their families. I can tell you this is something we are working literally by the hour.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Admiral John Kirby, always a pleasure to talk to you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning now to a disturbing situation in southern Russia where an anti-Semitic mob stormed an airport yesterday looking for Jewish passengers on a flight from Israel. Twenty people were injured. It was a moment with troubling echoes of the past.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Video posted on social media appears to show a frenzied mob storming the main airport in the Dagestan region of southern Russia, searching for Israeli passengers and surrounding a plane that reportedly arrived from Tel Aviv in Israel.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Where did the passengers go?" They yell at this airport staffer. "I don`t know," he tells them.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Inside this plane, the captain tells his passengers to stay calm. "Please don`t try to leave," he says. "There`s an angry crowd."\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Dagestan is a majority Muslim region, and in Moscow, Russia recently hosted a visit by leaders of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , the militant group behind the deadly attacks in Israel on October 7th.\par \par \uc2 But Russian officials have condemned this attack, calling it a gross violation of the law and detaining 60 people.\par \par \uc2 Amid a worldwide upsurge in attacks on Jewish people, the US said "There is no excuse or justification for anti-Semitism."\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Holly Williams, London.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s very disturbing.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, in the wake of the mass shooting in Lewiston, Maine, at least 13 more shootings around the country killed 11 people over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The Gun Violence Archive provided these numbers. The most alarming incident happened after a night of Halloween fun.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, this video from Tampa shows people in their costumes, scattering outside bars and nightclubs when shooting began early Sunday morning. Two people were killed here, 16 others were injured and one suspect is under arrest.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the Republican presidential race, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  dropped out over the weekend saying he has "no regrets" though one imagines disappointment.\par \par \uc2 Pence struggled in the crowded GOP field, and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , well, he still leads the race while he fights his courtroom battles.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is in Washington with more on Pence`s withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning. This one surprised me.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 A little bit surprising and months ago I was in Iowa and spoke with the former vice president on the day he announced. He was upbeat and hopeful his conservative message could break through, but months later, it became evident that many Republican voters just would not give him a hearing, especially those Trump supporters who can`t forgive Pence for his role in certifying the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R) (Former vice president of the United States): After much prayer and deliberation, I have decided to suspend my campaign for president effective today.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  suspended his campaign this past weekend in a speech in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: This is not my time.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: With a nod to how hard it has been to gain traction in a primary race so dominated by his one-time ally, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: We always knew this would be an uphill battle, but I have no regrets. The only thing that would have been harder than coming up short would have been if we`d never tried at all.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pence`s decision might be the beginning of a shakeup of the Republican presidential contest, potentially prompting others to assess their own campaigns and whether they should follow suit and consider rallying around a Trump alternative.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R) (2024 presidential candidate): I think in the end, it just means this race is narrowing whichever one said that it would.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But Trump rivals like former New Jersey governor, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  are in no rush to leave or to push others out.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : It`s not my place to tell people when to get out.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Sources close to Penn say he is unlikely to quickly make an endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Everybody that leaves seems to be endorsing me.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But Trump called on Pence to endorse him, despite having pressured Pence relentlessly to overturn the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : He should endorse me. I chose him, made him vice president, but people -- people in politics can be very disloyal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bob, we`ve got a new Des Moines Register poll showing that former President Trump is still leading the pack there in Iowa and by a wide margin. However, and this is interesting, Nikki Haley now tied with Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with 16 percent each.\par \par \uc2 The poll also shows that 65 percent of Republican caucus goers there in Iowa say Trump can beat Biden despite the legal challenges he is facing, 91 felony counts. So what do these numbers tell you about the race?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Clearly, this is now a race for second place in Iowa, with Haley continuing to gain support, not only there, but from major donors across the country who see her as a viable Trump alternative who could take on President Biden on foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 But now with Pence out, can she land his endorsement? And can she weather Governor DeSantis` mounting attacks? At this point, still a long way to go, and in politics, Tony, a few months can be a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Bob, you`re right about that. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot can happen.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we are going to remember Matthew Perry, the beloved star of "Friends" who unfortunately died on Saturday. He was only 54 years old, but first it is 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Maine`s second largest city holds a vigil to remember victims of last week`s mass shooting. We will talk with a 10-year- old survivor of the attack.\par \par \uc2 Plus, striking auto workers celebrate a second record-setting contract. We`ll show you what it means for the last car company still holding out.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thousands of people in Maine are returning to school and work this morning after last week`s mass shooting there and the city of Lewiston held a vigil just last night to honor the 18 people killed by Robert Card who was found dead on Friday after a massive two-day search.\par \par \uc2 Since then, we`ve also been told that a statewide alert warning law enforcement about this gunman was sent out last month.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Pegues has more now from Lewiston.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: This is where police say they found the body of the mass shooter, Friday night, inside a trailer at a recycling center roughly 10 miles from where the shootings took place.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CARL L. SHELINE (Lewiston, Maine): Just an incredible sense of relief for our city.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The gunman, 40-year-old Army reservist, Robert Card used to work at the recycling center, but was fired two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 MIKE SAUSCHUCK (Maine Commissioner of Public Safety): That particular location was cleared twice previously.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: This comes as the Associated Press reports a statewide alert warning Maine law enforcement about Card went out last month. Nearby, Sagadahoc County Sheriff, Joel Merry says he issued the alert after the Army Reserve notified him about threats Card made against his military base and other soldiers.\par \par \uc2 He sent a deputy to Card`s home for a welfare check, but Card was not there.\par \par \uc2 Lewiston Police chief, David St. Pierre declined to comment on Sheriff Merry`s claims.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything that you would do differently?\par \par \uc2 DAVID ST. PIERRE (Lewiston Police Chief): You know, we`re going to learn from some mistakes. I`m sure we`ve made a couple along the way.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: What did all of those gunshots sound like to you?\par \par \uc2 TAMMY ASSELIN (Mother of Toni Asselin): It`s so hard to describe, because behind every single one, I know that somebody was lined up there.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Tammy Asselin who was in the Just-In-Time Recreation bowling alley with her 10-year-old daughter, Toni, Wednesday night, but got separated when the gunman opened fire.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY ASSELIN: I can`t even begin to explain the amount of things that go through your mind in those precious, you know --\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Asselin called her parents who rushed to the scene. Her father says he waited for news on the 10-year-old for what felt like hours, then he got a call from an unknown number.\par \par \uc2 FERN ASSELIN (Grandfather): And I just said "hello" and when I said hello, I`ve heard four of the most beautiful words in the world I`ll never forget.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: What did you say to your grandpa?\par \par \uc2 TONI ASSELIN (10 -year-old witness): I`m not dead, Pappi.\par \par \uc2 FERN ASSELIN: And I just broke down and it was pretty tough, but I was happy.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Why did you think that was important to tell him.\par \par \uc2 TONI ASSELIN: To start off with that, so he could not be so worried anymore.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: It was another survivor who led Little Toni to safety. The little girl says that she did see at least one person get shot and killed and this morning, her mother is concerned about the long-term effects of what she witnessed -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jeff, it is such another important reminder that even when you survive, it is still very traumatic and dramatic and just so jarring and unsettling.\par \par \uc2 I`m glad that community got to mourn though together as a group.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Jeff Pegues.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ve got the latest reaction to the death of Matthew Perry, the "Friends" star who spent the last part of his life devoted to helping others.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s 7:30.\par \par \uc2 The shock still has not worn off from the news that we got over the weekend that actor, Matthew Perry died suddenly at the age of 54.\par \par \uc2 He was of course best known for his brilliant comic performance as Chandler Bing on the iconic sitcom, "Friends," we all love that show, but Perry himself said he wanted to be remembered for his efforts to help others who like himself suffer from addiction.\par \par \uc2 For more on Matthew Perry`s legacy and the reaction to his death, we are joined by "Entertainment Tonight" co-host, Kevin Frazier in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 I remember that big interview he did with Diane Sawyer. He talked very candidly about his struggles based on his book. He seemed to turn a corner. This was so stunning to hear and so sad to hear this news.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: I think you said it right, Gayle. Good morning, by the way.\par \par \uc2 That Hollywood is in shock, we`ve all lost a friend. You know, Matthew Perry was known for his sarcasm and dry humor, but he was also so honest about his battle with substance abuse.\par \par \uc2 He opened up about it in his memoir just published last year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: Engine 23, Rescue 23. EMS 9, all on radio, respond to the drowning.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Fifty-four-year-old, Matthew Perry was found unresponsive in a hot tub and his Pacific Palisades home Saturday at 4:00 PM local time. The investigation is ongoing, but authorities say they found no sign of foul play.\par \par \uc2 It was only a week ago that Perry had posted an image of himself in his hot tub on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We were hoping it was not true.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: On Sunday, fans left flowers in front of both his Los Angeles home and the New York City building where his fictional friends character lived.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Perry was the son of actor, John Bennett Perry and Suzanne Marie Langford. He grew up between Ottawa, Canada and Los Angeles. There, he pursued acting and as a child and teenager, landed several small roles.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: But it was his portrayal of the quick-witted, never short on jokes Chandler Bing --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: That made him a household name and earned Perry his first of five Emmy nominations.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: The role and the show`s juggernaut ratings launched Perry to the big screen playing alongside Bruce Willis in "The Whole Nine Yards."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Whole Nine Yards.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Perry earned accolades for his dramatic role as Republican lawyer, Joe Quincy in the West Wing.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "West Wing.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Big night nomination for the "West Wing." Interesting that it`s not "Friends," but "West Wing."\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY (Actor): Yes. Interesting, isn`t it? Two hundred and seventy episodes of "Friends" and I get nominated once and three episodes of "West Wing" and now twice.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Odd Couple.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: There was also a turn as executive producer and star of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  revival sitcom "The Odd Couple" starring alongside Yvette Nicole Brown.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Odd Couple.")\par \par \uc2 YVETTE NICOLE BROWN (Actor): He could hear the music as the way I described it. He could hear the ba-da-da-da-da of a line, and then he would find a way to improv.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: But amid all his fame and success, Perry struggled for decades with substance abuse, addictions to alcohol and amphetamines led to multiple stints in rehab.\par \par \uc2 In a memoir released last year, "Friends, Lovers and the Big Terrible Thing," he wrote he nearly died from opioid abuse in 2018 before he found his way to sobriety.\par \par \uc2 "It`s very odd," he writes, "To live in a world where if you died, it would shock people, but surprise no one."\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY: The best thing about me, bar none, is if somebody comes up to me and says I can`t stop drinking, can you help me? I can say yes and follow up and do it.\par \par \uc2 When I die, I don`t want "Friends" to be the first thing that`s mentioned. I want that to be the first thing that is mentioned and I`m going to live the rest of my life proving that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Shocked friends and co-stars posted heartfelt tributes online.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Maggie Wheeler, who played Chandler`s on again off again girlfriend Janice, wrote: "The joy you brought to so many in your two short lifetime will live on."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: "Friends" co-creators Marta Kauffman and David Krane and executive producer, Kevin Bright said in a statement: "We will always cherish the joy, the light, the blinding intelligence he brought to every moment not just to his work, but in life as well." -- guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Kevin, we have not heard from the main cast members of "Friends." What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: You know, Nate. Here`s a group that was bonded by going on one of the wildest rides in Hollywood history. You could say that they were the greatest ensemble cast in TV history, and they won`t rush out to just give a statement. They are processing this moment. They didn`t lose a cast member, they lost a friend, and someone who they were bonded with forever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That makes sense to me. Matthew Perry, when he started that show, he was only 24.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kevin, I know you interviewed him many, many times. Is there anything that stands out to you that makes you smile when you think about him?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Gayle, when he would walk in a room or he would step on the red carpet, he was so unassuming. He would walk over, shake your hand, ask how you were doing. It wasn`t about being this megastar. It was about Matty Perry who was doing his job and that`s the thing.\par \par \uc2 Everyone that I`ve talked to, they say Matty. They don`t say Matthew, they say our good friend, Matty. They loved him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Kevin, we heard Matthew or Matty say that he doesn`t want his legacy to be "Friends." That shouldn`t be the thing people remember, he wants it to be how he helped others in the throes of addiction. How far was he able to make it in that dream?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Well, he had a sober living home for men in Malibu, and he also -- you heard him say that anyone who came up to him, he wanted to help them. That was the main focus of the second half of his life.\par \par \uc2 And you know, he also found a passion in pickleball, and I talked to his pickleball coach and he said that, you know, Matty really had turned the corner. He was enjoying life. He wanted to put a court in his backyard and he played two hours on the day he passed away. He really seemed to have embraced life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, I know with that almost perfect timing, he is making those angels laugh up there.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Frazier, thank you so much. Appreciate you waking up early for us.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, auto workers claim two big victories in their long strike. We will have the details of the groundbreaking deals.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The strike against Detroit`s Big 3 automakers is two-thirds settled this morning. Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , that`s the owner of Chrysler and \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , have agreed to new contracts that provide record pay raises and also bonuses.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 United Auto Workers Union\uc2  is pushing \uc2 General Motors\uc2  to settle on terms as well. Kris Van Cleave reports now on the latest on these historic contract deals.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: Our union is again victorious.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: As the president of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , Shawn Fain announced a second historic contract with the Detroit automaker within four days --\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN: We truly believe that we got every penny possible out of this company. We left nothing on the table.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The Union increased pressure on the last remaining hold out, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , with workers from the company`s largest North American plant hitting the picket line Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 MEN: One, two, three, solidarity.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Recent deals with \uc2 Jeep\uc2  parent, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , and Ford ended more than 40 days of strikes against the companies. Union leaders say the tentative contracts include a 25 percent wage increase with cost of living adjustments and improvements in retirement benefits.\par \par \uc2 Fain also said the \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  agreement saves jobs at a factory the company had planned to close.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s definitely going to be life changing for the people.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m just -- I`m ecstatic. I can`t believe it to be honest. So I`m just super excited.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK ANDERSON (Auto economist): It`s an unambiguous. Big win for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Auto economist, Patrick Anderson says it will be hard for GM to avoid a similar deal.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK ANDERSON: They`re going to be highly pressured to agree to something like this, it`d be difficult for them to avoid it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In a statement, GM said it is disappointed by the expanding strike, adding its goal remains to reach an agreement as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  members still have to vote to approve the deals at Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, what we know about the huge network of tunnels in Gaza that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  prepared for Israel`s ground attack, but first, it`s 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Mr. Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming in to a Monday. How are you?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m doing well, but I`m still sad over learning about Matthew Perry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we all are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, "Friends" was such a big -- I mean, I got out of college right at the time that "Friends" was becoming a thing, like a couple of years right after a premiered, and it was just -- it felt like when I was watching that show that it was sort of my life in the early or mid-1990s, you know, because they were about my age. And some of the things that were depicted on the show were things that you know, 22 to 23-year-old were going through.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Were going through.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everybody wondered that, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And even if it wasn`t your life, it was just so well done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was so well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like all the --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was so relatable.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Matthew Perry to me was the MVP of that show. He was so, so very good as Chandler, and that`s what we want to talk about today because he actually as you pointed out with Kevin did not want to be remembered for his role on "Friends."\par \par \uc2 He was very open about his struggles with substance abuse and he later dedicated his life to helping others on their recovery journey. In his memoir, "Friends, Lovers and the Big Terrible Thing," he revealed he spent more than $7 million on his own drug treatment.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And as Kevin Frazier mentioned earlier, Perry turned his Malibu home, his house into a sober living facility to help other men. In 2015, the actor was honored by Phoenix House, a nonprofit drug and alcohol rehab program for his advocacy.\par \par \uc2 His friend, Hank Azaria says Perry helped him on his road to recovery. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HANK AZARIA (Actor): The night I went into AA, Matthew brought me in. The whole first year I was sober, we went to meetings together.\par \par \uc2 As a sober person, he was so caring and giving and wise that he totally helped me get sober.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m sure that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what he said he wanted to be remembered for.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly, Gayle. I was going to say if he could hear those words, and I`m sure he probably can, that`s what he wanted to be remembered for. He said, that was the greatest accomplishment, that he hoped "Friends" was very far down on the list of things that people talked about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and I know at times like this, it is easy to point out the flaws of you know, these actors or these people that we admire so much, but it`s good that we point out the impact that they had made on and off camera. That`s the legacy --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And he fell into the substance abuse problem the way so many other people do. He was injured and he started taking those pills to help with his injury, and the result was that he nearly died.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the harder drugs and prescription drugs, but he also wrote in the memoir about how during those first two seasons of friends, he was drinking and I mean, talk about talent.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been drunk before thinking I`m funny.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not funny.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, not funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was saying and he was struggling.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Won an Emmy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And he was unbelievably good. The timing was remarkable. He was so knockout funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He said, sometimes when he looks at all episodes, he can`t believe it because he knows the state he was in when he was doing that show. He said, I look at that guy and I feel sorry.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He couldn`t even remember.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sometimes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Speaks of the talent.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s how great he was as an actor. He`ll be missed.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to turn a little bit here and talk about something a little bit more lighthearted. Last week`s -- last night`s rather \uc2 Milwaukee Bucks\uc2  game had a very special guest singing the National Anthem leaving the crowd saying "Ye boy."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not bad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Milwaukee Bucks\uc2 /X.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, that is six-time Grammy nominee, Flavor Flav who had a very shaky start and it was very long, but by the end the crowd was cheering him on.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a little bit background, the founding member of Public Enemy, this was a long time bucket list item for him. He wanted to be respectful. He tried really hard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think he was doing it with love, and that`s what makes it --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Shannon Sharpe said ye, boy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Flavor Flav, we`ll do.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in his eight o`clock hour, Josh Groban. We just love, love, love to hear his voice. He`s here to talk about his new music, his dream job on stage, and a new relationship.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Studies show that breast cancer rates are up among younger women. We`ll share one woman`s story and what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Today`s "Kindness 101" is about vision and we get to meet another young member of the Hartman family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They have a nice family. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Israel says its troops have entered Gaza as the war escalates.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: But the United Nations and US officials are urging Israel to prioritize the protection of civilians.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: An anti-Semitic mobs storms are Russian airport looking for Jewish passengers on a flight from Israel.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY ASSELIN: I can`t even begin to explain the amount of thinking that goes through your mind in those precious moments.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A mother and daughter tell their harrowing story after surviving the mass shooting in Maine.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: What did you say to your grandfather?\par \par \uc2 TONI ASSELIN: I`m not dead, Pappi.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fans remember "Friends" actor, Matthew Perry today.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: After his sudden death over the weekend at the age of 54.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We were all shocked. We were hoping it was not true. We`re still in disbelief.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: After much prayer and deliberation, I have decided to suspend my campaign for president.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  drops out of the race for the Republican presidential nomination.\par \par \uc2 NATE BARGATZE ("Saturday Night Live"): He made the decision after he consulted with God and God said, dude, you`re embarrassing both of us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ouch. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s what "Saturday Night Live" does so well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ouch.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ouch.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to be in this hour with the news though. The next stage of Israel`s war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Over the weekend, there was still more heavy bombing in parts of Gaza that are unfortunately still packed with civilians and Israel also began to move troops and armored vehicles over the border into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 But much of this war may be fought not on the streets of Gaza, but beneath them where \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is believed to have built an elaborate network of tunnels where they are also hiding hostages.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is in Tel Aviv with more on this.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, they call it the Gaza Metro, in a kind of grim joke. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that`s right. The new phase in Israel`s invasion into Gaza has now continued on into its third day. As for Israel aiming for those tunnels, for those \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnels, they are trying to eliminate them and they`ve been saying over the past few days, they`ve been targeting hundreds of them underground under the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHARONE LIFSCHITZ (Yocheved Lifshitz daughter): A network of tunnels underneath, it looks like a spider web.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: When \uc2 Hamas\uc2  took 85-year-old Israeli, Yocheved Lifshitz hostage, she saw with their own eyes, a secret subterranean world few outside Gaza have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Beneath the streets of Gaza lies an underground city.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Israel`s army claims this is where \uc2 Hamas\uc2  plans and carries out attacks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Destroying \uc2 Hamas\uc2  means destroying this tunnel network.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: A labyrinth of passages, shafts, and rooms believed to stretch more than 300 miles and possibly to a depth of more than 200 feet. Nicknamed the Gaza Metro, used to ambush and assault.\par \par \uc2 How crucial are the tunnels under Gaza to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JOEL RASKIN (Bar-Ilan University geomorphology professor): They`re pivotal for anything that the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has planned to do, and will probably also execute.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Joel Raskin is an expert on Gaza`s tunnels, studying their evolution over half a century. Dug by hand and basic tools, early narrow tunnels were used to smuggle goods in from Egypt, later for weapons. Now, modernized for attack and virtually undetectable.\par \par \uc2 JOEL RASKIN: The geology of the Gaza Strip is ideal for tunnel digging and maintaining, but it is very complex for tunnel detection, based on the abundant layers of sediment.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Raskin shows me his rendering of a cross section that Israel`s military could be up against. Attack shafts near the Israel border, defend shafts further back, artillery pads below the surface and tunnels linked to apartment blocks and hospitals for escape.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI (Israel`s chief military spokesperson): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists operate inside and under Shifa Hospital, and other hospitals in Gaza with network of terror tunnels.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Al-Shifa Hospital is Gaza is biggest. The tunnels that Israel claims run under it in focus. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  denies there are tunnels under the hospital, which says it is sheltering 40,000 displaced Palestinians, treating the wounded and the helpless from Israel`s intensifying ground operations.\par \par \uc2 Amir Ulo is an Israeli reserve colonel and first went into a Gaza tunnel in 2007.\par \par \uc2 AMIR ULO (Israeli reserve colonel): It`s a small place, crowded and dark place.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Since then, Israel`s military has been training in its own mock tunnels, built by the \uc2 US Army\uc2 . In the past three days, Israel has been dropping bombs to cave in \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` networks.\par \par \uc2 And there are international hostages in those tunnels. How does that complicate the invasion?\par \par \uc2 AMIR ULO: We are realistic. I am not telling you that we are not going to face losses. We are not seeking for war. We are seeking for peace. But when it`s time to war, we know how to fight and we will do it and we will prevail.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has framed this conflict as the battle for Israel`s very survival. But Nate, the families of those hostages are concerned, are fearful that they will not come out of this alive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I completely understand those fears.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, a new push to warn younger women about the potential threat of breast cancer. One patient talks about her diagnosis. She was just 38 years old.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Broken Vow.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s always gets to me, every time. That is Josh Groban and David Foster performing the song, "Broken Vow." Coming up, Josh Groban will be here in the studio. He has just arrived to talk about his new music and his Tony nominated role is "Sweeney Todd."\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Your favorite morning show, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As you may know, October is Breast Cancer Awareness Month. That`s why you see a lot of people wearing pink this month.\par \par \uc2 This morning we`re taking a look at the alarming rise in breast cancer cases among women under the age of 50. Now recent study shows an almost eight percent increase in diagnosed cases over the 10 years to 2019.\par \par \uc2 Breast cancer is the second most common cancer in women in this country, and the second most common cause of cancer deaths for American women.\par \par \uc2 Our Christina Ruffini spoke with a 38-year-old woman who tells us about her shocking diagnosis and how she says it had gone undetected.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD (Breast cancer patient): Do you see the birds right now?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Stefanie Gerard is many things, a grad student --\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Down came the rain --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: A mother, a new homeowner.\par \par \uc2 What was a bigger shock to the system? The actual chemo or the fact that you were having to have it at 38?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: I think probably having to have it at 38.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Now, she is also a woman with cancer.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: When you start treatment and you look in the mirror and you`re like so sad and so depressed that you`re losing your hair and then on the flip side, you`re like: What is hair? Who cares? All I care about is life. I want to live. I want to watch my daughter grow up.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Gerard has a family history of breast cancer and a genetic mutation that puts her at higher risk.\par \par \uc2 For more than a year, she says she also had persistent pain in her left armpit and breast. Doctors ran annual sonograms, even a breast MRI, but found nothing.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: They always said, you don`t need a mammogram until 40. No mammogram. You`re fine. You`re fine. You`re fine. They`ve said it`s normal. My doctor said cancer doesn`t hurt.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: In May, she saw a new doctor who ordered a mammogram, which came back positive for low-stage breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: The radiologist told me no, this doesn`t show up on a breast MRI because these are microcalcifications that can only be picked up by a mammogram.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: So you needed the mammogram?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: You needed the mammogram.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: She also needed a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: When I found out that I was going to have a mastectomy, I thought, like I want to remember my body like the way it was. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: I want to remember my body the way it was, and so I put out onto \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , you know if anybody knew of photographer.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: That`s gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: In her head, this is how Stefanie still sees herself.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: That`s my hair.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Yes, and boobs.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: With a mane of dark waist-length curls and her preop curves proudly displayed. She gave us emphatic permission to show the unedited photos, but we blurred them for the sake of morning TV.\par \par \uc2 Have you thought about taking them again, when you`re done with this whole process?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: I have. I have. It`s still my body. It`s just got some battle wounds.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Following the procedure and subsequent tissue testing, Gerard`s cancer was upgraded to stage two ductal carcinoma, an invasive form of the disease that spread to her lymph nodes. She now needs chemo and radiation.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: You have to make screening earlier. Like by the time -- what if I had waited until I was 40?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: If there is something in the back of your mind telling you this is not right, then you have to push for it any way that you can get that test.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Whatever it takes, she says to be around to watch her kids grow up.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIE GERARD: Every moment I look at my daughter, it`s like I`m trying to hold on to like every moment. I just want to know like I`m going to be okay. I`m going to be here. I`m going to watch her grow up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stefanie look great. I would have taken those pictures, too. She gets her third round of chemo next week. Her doctors will rescan her in a couple of months to see how the treatment works.\par \par \uc2 She says she is hopeful. She is looking forward to spending the holidays with her family. She`s also very excited to get back to grad school in January.\par \par \uc2 Stefanie has a lot to do.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in CBS News medical contributor. That`s Dr. Celine Gounder, who is editor-at-large for public health at KFF. Good morning to you, Dr. Celine.\par \par \uc2 Is her story an anomaly? It sounds like she did all the right things. She alerted people, I feel something, and still they didn`t find anything right away.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: We are seeing breast cancer rates increasing among women under 50.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, there are a number of different reasons. One, women are delaying having children until later in life until their 30s or 40s and that is associated with an increased risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is? It is?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It is. It is. The delay. And then also, we increased rates of obesity, overweight. What many people don`t realize is the fatty tissue actually produces estrogen, which is why people who are overweight man or woman will have larger breasts.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, we think that some environmental factors are at play here. So the combination of those three seem to be driving more breast cancer in younger women.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what`s the downside to getting a screening earlier? Why is that not even just a normal thing for everybody?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: The field is really moving away from age-based screening to risk factor. So family history, genetics, which Stefanie had both, which were indications to have screening earlier and I think one key message, if there is anything I would get across today is that people need to be sharing family history with each other.\par \par \uc2 You know, we all know family members who are estranged, they`re not on speaking terms. Maybe they`re a little shy about sharing those personal medical details, but that information can be life-saving, because that`s how doctors know what extra tests to be getting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still want to know what`s the downside, though, to getting it earlier. Why wouldn`t I -- if I have the option, why would I do that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, because when you get a mammogram or you know other testing, especially in younger women, you have denser breasts, it`s going to mean a lot of extra biopsies and biopsies are not risk free. And those can be you know, scarring, et cetera. I don`t think you want all these twenty, thirty-something-year-old women ending up with unnecessary biopsies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And get a false --\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: False positives.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Positive.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dr. Gounder, can you help clear up what women should be looking for when it comes to breast cancer screenings? We heard Stefanie talk about pain. I recently had a friend also who felt pain and then was diagnosed positive with breast cancer. So the pain you say is not really an indicator. So what is an indicator?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So when women have breast pain, it`s almost always due to hormonal changes, hormonal shifts during your period, during pregnancy and so that keeps going, right, regardless of whether you have breast cancer or not. And so I think it can be easy to confuse that as a symptom of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 But what we`re really looking for are a mass. If you have skin changes, it could be like rashy skin changes or dimpling of the breast or bloody discharge from the nipple.\par \par \uc2 Pain, very occasionally can be a symptom of breast cancer, especially if it`s one-sided and if it`s localized, not the entire breast, but usually it`s not a symptom of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, Stefanie isn`t the first woman to say that she felt dismissed by her doctor. What advice do you give to those that need to advocate for themselves a little bit more?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, and this is not an excuse, but I think a lot of doctors feel like that "I Love Lucy" episode, where it is the conveyor belt with the candies where she`s trying to keep up, right? And I think sometimes, doctors don`t pause enough when a patient brings up an issue.\par \par \uc2 And so what you need to do is, you know, if you feel like you`re being dismissed, bring a family member, bring a friend whose entire job is to advocate for you to say, you know, hold on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: We need to talk about this more, and if you still don`t feel heard, get another doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To ask a second opinion.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Second opinion.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stefanie said that the doctors told her cancer doesn`t hurt. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: That is true. By and large cancer -- breast cancer is not -- breast pain is not a symptom of breast cancer. But again, a mass, skin changes, rashy skin changes or dimpling, or breast nipple discharge. Those are really the three symptoms you want to be looking for.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Dr. Gounder, thank you very much. Some great information there.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in our "Kindness 101" series, Steve Hartman and his kids share a lesson in vision. How one boy who has a special relationship to Steve reminds us all that nothing can hold you back if you want to achieve something if you really want it.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In case you missed it on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we were joined by Leshem Ben Hamo last week who showed us the Gotze kitchen shears and peeler set. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS (Lifestyle expert): Can I try?\par \par \uc2 LESHEM BEN HAMO (Gotze): Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You`re an expert. They need to see how you do this.\par \par \uc2 LESHEM BEN HAMO: Yes. You expert. Now, look, I am going to give you -- excuse me -- three carrots\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And I am going to use it, one hand. Boom. There you go.\par \par \uc2 LESHEM BEN HAMO: You see?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: So he designed this with his grandmother in mind, so people who have grip strength problems, it`s easy for them to use it.\par \par \uc2 LESHEM BEN HAMO: Yes, yes, and look at a pineapple.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Are you cutting that pineapple right now?\par \par \uc2 LESHEM BEN HAMO: See?\