 out to Magic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on the show.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  going something really cool.\par \par \uc2 So imagine a couple of people going to a game with their tickets, good tickets, and then the team surprises them with sideline passes. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: You won the rad prize.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What`s --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So they are sideline passes. If you want to come and watch the warm up on the sideline, I can take you guys down there right now if you want to come.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bingo.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Right now?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ladies and gentlemen, the real Garden of Eden right here.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is great. So we see them like live.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Not just at the game. I`m on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Cynthia, you`ll never believe where I am? I am on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got to call everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got to call everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s what they did. You know, this is really cool. I love what the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  did, just making dreams come true. They got to see DeSean Jackson who is one of their favorite players.\par \par \uc2 But that`s cool though. You just go to the game.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are the Eagles the only one who does that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know. I think it`s going to be popular across the league right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s such a great idea. Yes. It`s such a great idea.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not going to believe where I am, on CBS MORNINGS. That`s what Josh Groban is saying right now.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to our next guest, singer, songwriter, actor, his name is Josh Groban is going to leave Broadway revival of "Sweeney Todd" very soon, which he called nothing short of a life-changing experience.\par \par \uc2 He plays Sweeney Todd, the demon barber of Fleet Street, who is out for revenge. Groban and his co-star, that`s Annaleigh Ashford recently released the much anticipated cast album. It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, "Sweeney Todd" reunites with his old friend, a silver razor blade.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sweeney Todd.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen to that voice. Couldn`t you just listen to it all day? I`ll go first. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Josh Groban joins us now in the studio. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Gayle, your voice is giving me a run for my money. I`ve got to say, hearing you, you came into my dressing room.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: She came into my dressing room singing "Barber and His Wife."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then Josh started howling.\par \par \uc2 Josh, you should know that --\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: That`s when I like something, that`s what I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your role is Sweeney Todd has inspired costumes. Max on camera one, Nate Burleson had a -- there he is, behind you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yep, yep, yep.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Oh, yes?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: You were Sweeney?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was Sweeney.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate Burleson had a Halloween party and he came as Sweeney Todd.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Razor and everything.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Oh my God. That`s awesome. We get people who dress up at the show, too. Like, when I go out the stage door, we see people in full cosplay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Full costume.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: It`s just --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I mean, I love it. It`s Sondheim. To have this excitement over a show that I`ve loved since I was five years old is the coolest thing. And I try not to look at specific people in the audience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: In case I see somebody that I might be nervous to be singing in front of.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: But you know the cast will always say backstage, we have a Lovett in aisle three. We have a Todd in mezzanine aisle five.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s not a party that comes out and goes "Security. Security."\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Only if they`re holding an actual blade.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we just saw you and Annaleigh recording the cast album.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How different is that than being on stage? Because I think it would be harder, is it not?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: In some ways it is because you know that you`re recording something that, you know, it`s a great privilege to go and see live theatre.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: And you know that for me I didn`t get to see live theater until long after I listened to my favorite cast recording. So you know that you have the opportunity to be introducing the show to a whole new generation before they might even be able to see you on stage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I hadn`t thought of that.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: So you`re keeping that in mind when your voice acting, when you`re singing the songs, you know that people are closing their eyes and immersing themselves in the audio only, and it is a whole different process.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I had not thought about that. So what is it like? I hadn`t thought about it that way. What`s it like for you though, because you listen to the cast album when you were a kid.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now you`re recording the cast album, Josh Groban.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: It`s just -- it is truly --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Goosebumps?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I mean, I don`t -- I really -- it is the greatest privilege of my career to be doing this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Because I was a theater kid at heart growing up. I had an amazing fork in the road. I love singing my solo music. It`s been an extraordinarily blessed life and career and I`ll go back into recording and all that stuff.\par \par \uc2 But I was the kid locking my door and singing along with Len Cariou and George Hearn, and Angela Lansbury and you know -- and so to see young people out there, to see fans out there, who again, a stage door will say this is my first "Sweeney Todd," this is my first Broadway show.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: That`s a privilege to be able to do that and one that I don`t take lightly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So this is a show with revenge, murder, also comedy. How do you balance those three things?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: This show has everything.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it has it all.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Yes, it really does have everything. It`s a perfect show, and Emily Ashford, who`s such a brilliant comedian, and actor, she brings the light, I bring the dark.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sweeney Todd.")\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: There are these Aria-esque musical moments while hands (ph) are doing the most horrible things up there, and that`s the juxtaposition that Sondheim always wanted, was to make sure that all of those dualities were at play and having their own beautiful light.\par \par \uc2 And so when people walk out of there laughing and crying, we`re just -- we`re thrilled that they`re getting it and with the 26-piece orchestra, that is the first time since the 70s it has been on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And speaking of laughing, I was reading that you and Annaleigh try to make each other laugh.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On stage.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Not just me and Annaleigh, the whole cast. We have little secret moments. You know, my friend, Nick Christopher, who is my understudy as Sweeney and also plays Pirelli so brilliantly, both roles, we have little moments on stage where we`re always trying to make each other laugh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: He will give me -- he`ll be -- spoiler alert -- he`ll be like in a box at some point, and then I`m opening the box and he`ll give me like little funny looks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That would be funny.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Little things after show 250, you start to do this to keep your sanity because it is a very dark show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: But no, we`re having a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A moment ago, you mentioned you had a tremendous fork in the road. What happened at that fork in the road because --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was wondering that, too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because it is interesting, because we all have that fork.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The fork, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s people that are watching right now that are sitting at that fork in the road and are paralyzed with their decision.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What happened with your fork in the road?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I love talking to students about especially students who are interested in the creative arts, about what those moments look like, what they feel like, and how to know when you`re ready for them.\par \par \uc2 Because I was a theater kid, I was at \uc2 Carnegie Mellon\uc2 , wonderful Carnegie Mellon University studying musical theater and I got a call from David Foster, who is, you know, a gazillion times Grammy Award winning producer, our friend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was my voice coach, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: And your voice coach and I hear, he recorded a song with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Cannot wait to hear after this interview and you know, I knew that theater was my passion. And I also knew that a door that was open like this wouldn`t come around, maybe ever again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: In fact, I could almost guarantee it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: And so I flipped a coin, and I said to myself --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You flipped a coin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Well, I flipped -- I flipped a coin. It was the greatest advice I ever got and the person who told me said, flip a coin and listen to your gut the moment it lands. If your gut goes, eh-eh. Just flip it back over, you know? You`ll know in that moment whether that was the right thing or not and I decided to view it as an education.\par \par \uc2 I said I`m going to go, I`m going to learn from David and I probably won`t sell any records, but you know whatever happens, happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It happens.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: You can teach a lot of things, sometimes those moments happen where you realize the life experience is going to teach you a lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: And I`m always a proponent of doing that as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what`s your gut telling you these days? Because you`ve said you`re leaving in January.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re ready to go, I guess.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: My gut is always trying to find things that challenge me, to keep me scared, because, you know, I`ve been doing this now, I am celebrating 20th anniversary of my "Closer" album, which I can`t believe it`s been 20 years, 25 years since my first album and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s when I first fell in love with you, Josh.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I remember when I surprised you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One and -- yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: That`s the first time I heard that beautiful voice. And "Sweeney Todd" is the scariest thing I`ve ever done by a longshot and I loved being able to climb that mountain. And so whatever I do next, I want it to challenge me and teach me. I want to learn new things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No matter who we are, we can always learn new things and get better.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Although you`re pretty damn good.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Josh Groban, congratulations.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: If you say so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have until January to see -- who`s replacing you, Josh?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I can`t give you that exclusive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you can.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I certainly -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Initials.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: I can`t do it. I can`t do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel sorry for.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Corner office is watching.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel sorry for the person that`s following you, but I know they`ll be great because they are all on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: They will be great. I know that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Sweeney Todd: The Demon Barbara of Fleet Street" currently playing at the Lunt-Fontanne.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Lunt-Fontanne. There we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lunt-Fontanne. Say it for me, Josh.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN: Lunt-Fontanne.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lunt-Fontanne, and the "Sweeney Todd" cast album we`re happy to say is available wherever you listen to your music.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in just a moment, in our series "Kindness 101," Steve Harmon introduces us to his nephew, Ted, to share an important lesson for all of us about vision.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 Josh, that was great.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time now for our "Kindness 101" series.\par \par \uc2 This morning, Steve Hartman and his kids share a lesson about vision.\par \par \uc2 Steve is introducing us to his nephew, Ted, this week who is overcoming a major challenge.\par \par \uc2 Together, they`ll teach us how any goal is possible as long as you look to the future and stay determined.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. I`m here with my daughter and sidekick, Meryl.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Hello, everyone, and welcome to our lesson on vision.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And for a definition, we go now to my son, Emmett over at the Dictionary Desk.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Good morning. So for the purposes of this lesson, I`m defining vision as the power to see into the future.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sounds magical, almost like having your own personal crystal ball.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: That is so cool.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: I taught you how to do that.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: With vision, you`re able to look past whatever problems you have in front of you today, to the person that you can become, a vision of your future, and who better to teach us about vision than a person who is blind.\par \par \uc2 And this story pulled from our archive, we see how getting through even your darkest hour is possible with good vision.\par \par \uc2 I chose to tell this story, mainly for an audience of one.\par \par \uc2 TED (Steve Hartman`s nephew): Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This 12-year-old named Ted.\par \par \uc2 TED: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Ted is my nephew and he says sometimes his blindness feels insurmountable. I see.\par \par \uc2 TED: I felt like I was doomed. I know that that does sound a little immature, but --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A woe is me kind of a feeling.\par \par \uc2 TED: Yes. I really want to be like everybody else sometimes, you know?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And that`s why.\par \par \uc2 When I heard about this drag racer, attempting to set a new world speed record, I thought Ted and others like him had to meet the driver.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, Dan Parker of Columbus, Georgia gotten a crash. He suffered a traumatic brain injury so severe it blinded him.\par \par \uc2 DAN PARKER (Blind racecar driver): I never imagined I would be back in the seat of a race car, but I`ve been a racer my whole life. I just had to figure out another way to do it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A machinist by trade, Dan got adaptive equipment so he could make parts, and then designed this entire race car.\par \par \uc2 TED: That`s just amazes me. What does he look like?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A mustache and a beard?\par \par \uc2 TED: I have a mustache.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You have a mustache?\par \par \uc2 TED: See? Whiskers? Nobody sees them.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Don`t worry about it. That won`t be an issue. Anyway, back to our story.\par \par \uc2 Dan was attempting to set the Guinness record for fastest car driven blindfolded. Of course, no blindfold was needed, but he did have a special audio guidance system, and for safety purposes, a sighted driver next to him, hands hovering over the steering wheel just in case.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t necessary. Dan went 211 miles an hour, set a record, and more importantly, an example.\par \par \uc2 DAN PARKER: Ted, I want you to know that blindness is not what is stopping you. Surround yourself with believers and go for your dreams. You can make excuses or make it happen.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A new Guinness World Records title.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Dan says inspiring the Ted`s of the world is the main reason he did this. And if my nephew was any indication, it was well worth the drive.\par \par \uc2 TED: If he can do that, well then I think I could easily pursue my dream. What about flying planes?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That`s exactly what I wanted to come from this.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now my nephew, Ted. Welcome to "Kindness 101."\par \par \uc2 TED: It`s great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: How`s your facial hair coming, Ted?\par \par \uc2 TED: It`s great. I mean, it is long.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: It looks like you shaved.\par \par \uc2 TED: I did. I did shave.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I should also mention that after that story first aired, you got an offer?\par \par \uc2 TED: Yes, I got an invitation by a pilot. He was very generous --\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: You ready to go.\par \par \uc2 TED: I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Okay, pull back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: What was that like?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TED: This is so fun.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TED: It was amazing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TED: I`m flying.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TED: It`s like driving a car.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Well, wait, have you driven a car before?\par \par \uc2 TED: When I was younger, I did go down your driveway on a --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This is news to me.\par \par \uc2 TED: Well, I had my mom`s help. But you know --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: So how have these examples and experiences changed your thinking?\par \par \uc2 TED: Like it`s opened the door and showed me everything that`s possible in this world.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: What do you feel like you can`t do now?\par \par \uc2 TED: What I can`t do is be pessimistic.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Ted, thanks so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 TED: Of course. It`s been a great pleasure. Great pleasure.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You know, people always say to live in the moment and that`s great when things are going well, but when times are tough, it is just as important not to dwell, to look past the present using not your eyes but your vision.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I wrote down the same line, you could make excuses or you can make it happen. Make it happen, people. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are tight on time, we`ve got to go, so we`re inviting you to come back and see us tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020231030ejau00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for October 26, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Adriana Diaz, Nikole Killion, Charlie D`Agata,  Elaine Quijano, Jeff Pegues \par \uc2 3,438 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, the urgent manhunt across New England, after a mass shooter goes on a deadly rampage in Maine at a bowling alley and a local bar. Police are looking for the suspect on the ground and in the water, with the Coast Guard joining the search.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS (D-Maine): This is a dark day for Maine.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: At least 18 people killed, and a dozen others are injured in a mass shooting, this time in the small city of Lewiston, Maine.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON (Witness): I slid basically into where the pins are and climbed up to the machine, and was on top of the machines for about 10 minutes, until the cops got there.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The second shooting scene is right down this street in a restaurant. After that, the gunman took off, launching this massive manhunt.\par \par \uc2 COL. WILLIAM ROSS (Maine State Police): This is someone that should not be approached.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What we`re learning about the alleged shooter, the weapon used and his military background.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m Adriana Diaz in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Here are the rest of tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Israeli tanks execute a targeted raid in Gaza, the army calling it preparation for the next stages of combat.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): The House will be in order.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Mike Johnson takes up the gavel as speaker of the House and begins defending his record as an election denier, climate skeptic and his anti-LGBTQ views.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Otis tears through the Mexican resort city of Acapulco, destroying buildings, roads and leaving at least 27 people dead.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN (President, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ): \uc2 UAW\uc2  family, our stand-up strike has delivered.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Striking autoworkers make big gains, reaching a tentative agreement with Ford after 41 days out of work.\par \par \uc2 And, back in Maine, a close-knit community in mourning.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS: I want every person in Maine to know that we will heal together.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Thursday night. I`m Jericka Duncan. Norah is on assignment.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are in Lewiston, Maine, the scene of the 565th mass shooting in this country this year.\par \par \uc2 We will get to \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adriana Diaz in Washington with the day`s other top stories, including the war in the Middle East. But we begin tonight here in Maine with a community reeling; 18 people were killed and more than a dozen others injured after a gunman opened fire inside a bowling alley and then in a nearby bar.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the suspect remains on the run, with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  joining State Police in the search for 40-year-old Robert Card, who is considered armed and dangerous. Authorities are asking for the public`s help in tracking down the Army Reservist who was hospitalized this summer for mental health issues, including hearing voices.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, there is \uc2 FBI\uc2  activity, including multiple vehicles and heavily armed agents around the suspect`s last known residence in Bowdoin, Maine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): Tonight, a massive manhunt by air, land, and sea for the suspected gunman behind a mass shooting in Southern Maine, with police on alert from Canada to New York.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: We have an active shooter. We have multiple injuries.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Gunfire rang out shortly before 7:00 p.m. Wednesday at Just-In-Time Recreation bowling alley, the first of two shooting scenes in Lewiston, Maine. Seven people were killed. A witness described the chaos.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON (Witness): -- a weapon, I just booked it down the lane, and I slid basically into where the pins are and climbed up to the machine, and was on top of the machines for about 10 minutes, until the cops got there.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Minutes later, at 7:08 p.m., frantic 911 calls began streaming with reports of an active shooter at Schemengees Bar and Grille four miles away. Eight people there were found dead.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen people were injured in both shootings. Three victims later died at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN ALEXANDER (Chief Medical Officer, Central Maine Healthcare): We had approximately 100 team members who were off-duty come into the hospital to help ensure that we were able to care for not only the patients that we received from this horrible scene, but also take care of the patients that we have there.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Multiple law enforcement agencies, including the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and State Police, responded to the scenes, searching for the suspect, 40-year- old Army Reservist Robert Card, shown in these surveillance photos carrying a semiautomatic rifle.\par \par \uc2 COL. WILLIAM ROSS (Maine State Police): He should be considered armed and dangerous. Based on our investigation, we believe this is someone that should not be approached.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Three hours after the shooting began, police discovered an SUV registered to the suspect at a boat launch in the town of Lisbon.\par \par \uc2 Residents in three nearby communities were advised to shelter in place. Today, many schools and businesses were closed.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS: This is a dark day for Maine.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The mass shooting has shattered a sense of security in Maine, considered one of the safest states in the country.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Janet Mills\uc2  spent years working in Lewiston.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS: This city do not deserve this terrible assault on its citizens, on its peace of mind, on its sense of security. No city does, no state, no people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: With that multistate manhunt under way, residents here are still being told to shelter in place tonight.\par \par \uc2 Investigators are looking into a possible motive into the shootings, including why the two locations for the attacks were chosen.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jeff Pegues has more new details about the suspect`s military experience, as well as his history of mental illness.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES (voiceover): CBS News has learned that investigators are looking into whether Robert Card was targeting his current or former girlfriend in last night`s mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 Sources say he went to the bowling alley first and then the bar nearly four miles away. It`s unclear if the person was at either location. Today, police also found a note at the gunman`s house, though it`s unclear what it says.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ST. PIERRE (Lewiston, Maine, Police Chief): This is an all-hands-on- deck approach.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Law enforcement officials say Card is an Army Reservist. The Army says he enlisted in the Reserves in 2002 and is now a sergeant 1st class. He worked as a petroleum supply specialist and has had no combat deployments.\par \par \uc2 In July of this year, Card was with his unit at West Point when leaders reported that he was acting erratically. New York State Police took him to an Army hospital for an evaluation. A Maine law enforcement bulletin said that Card was released about two weeks later. The alert also noted Card was hearing voices and had threatened to shoot up a military installation in Southern Maine.\par \par \uc2 Card`s cousin, Michael Mercier.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MERCIER (Cousin of Suspect): Yeah, he`s got a lot of problems up in his head right now. One of them is schizophrenic.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Tom Chittum, a former associate deputy director of the ATF, says, despite the mental evaluation, federal law would not prohibit Card from having such a weapon.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS CHITTUM (Former ATF Associate Deputy Director): Simply being diagnosed with a mental health disorder is not disqualifying, and neither is a commitment, when it is voluntary, or a brief commitment for observation.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Last night, investigators found Card`s vehicle at a boat launch. Sources say law enforcement is searching for a boat that he could have used to escape.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SORUKAS (Retired U.S. Marshal): He has a military background and may have an advantage, but, keep in mind, many of these law enforcement officers, they are hunters. They are fishermen. And they have military backgrounds as well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The gunman was heavily armed for this assault.\par \par \uc2 Investigators believe that he may have used an extended magazine, as well as a scope. A firearm was recovered in that abandoned vehicle. And, Jericka, intelligence sources are telling CBS News that they believe that that recent breakup may have been a significant destabilizing event in the gunman`s life.\par \par \uc2 That said, there is still no clear motive right now for this shooting.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Jeff Pegues for us tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden ordered flags to be lowered to half-staff in memory of the victims, noting that the number of gun deaths in America is -- quote -- "not normal and we cannot accept it."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Quijano is learning more about the lives lost.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): Tonight, loved ones remember the lives lost.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BARNARD (Father of Shooting Victim): He could always laugh at something, no matter how bad it was.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Forty-two-year-old Arthur Strout was a father of three.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BARNARD: You know, he tried to be tough with his kids, you know, but, at the same time, he was just -- he was a great dad. He just was. I`m proud of him.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Strout had been at Schemengees Bar and Grille playing pool with his father, Arthur Barnard.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BARNARD: He was supposed to leave with me, because he didn`t bring his car. I said, "OK." And he said, "I love you," like always. All my kids tell me that every time we see each other.\par \par \uc2 You know, 10 minutes later, I got a phone call.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Kristy Strout was Arthur`s wife. They had been married for almost seven years.\par \par \uc2 KRISTY STROUT (Widow of Shooting Victim): He has helped me raise my children since they were very, very little. You know, his -- his daughter is only 13, and without a dad because of all of this. Because of one man`s choices, you know, my daughter has to grow up without a father.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Seventy-six-year-old Bob Violette was an avid bowler who was teaching kids at Just-In-Time Recreation bowling alley when the shooting happened, according to CBS Boston station WBZ.\par \par \uc2 Lewiston resident Paul Englehart knew Violette.\par \par \uc2 PAUL ENGLEHART (Friend of Shooting Victim): He was actually kind of like a mentor to the young kids that were over there yesterday as, like, the -- a bowling coach, a wonderful man, great man. I mean, I could never -- I would not be able to say a bad word about him.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Many people in this quiet town of 38,000 woke up to the news that someone they knew had been killed, and, tonight, they are trying to process the loss.\par \par \uc2 APRIL STEVENS (Lewiston Resident): Just, everybody pray. Pray for those that are hurt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Friends and family of Arthur Strout have gathered tonight outside the family home to show their support.\par \par \uc2 And, Jericka, Arthur`s wife tells us that their daughter`s birthday is coming up on Halloween.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So many horrific stories.\par \par \uc2 Elaine, I know you have been here all day covering this for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We will have more news from Maine later in the broadcast.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, let`s go to \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adriana Diaz in Washington for the rest of today`s top stories.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Thank you so much, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas to the war in the Middle East, Israeli airstrikes are intensifying, with ground forces conducting a targeted raid into Gaza in their hunt for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata is in Tel Aviv, where the military is preparing for the next stage of the war.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  released this overnight footage said to show its tanks crossing into Northern Gaza, conducting a targeted raid.\par \par \uc2 Armored bulldozers pave the way. Tanks open fire on suspected \uc2 Hamas\uc2  targets before withdrawing. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  described the operation as preparations for the next stage of combat. But even before that next stage begins is the question of what comes next, growing concerns about Israel`s long-term strategy after invading Gaza, one reason the U.S. has dispatched Lieutenant General James Glynn as a top adviser here, a specialist in urban warfare on the battlefields of Fallujah and Mosul in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 In Gaza today, with the death toll mounting to more than 7,000, according to officials there, so too is the sadness, anger, and desperation. They count the shrouded bodies, even as more arrive. At least 50 hostages are among those killed, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  health officials claim tonight, victims of Israeli bombardment. CBS News can`t independently verify those numbers.\par \par \uc2 Locating the remaining captives hidden in the maze of tunnels beneath Gaza will be extremely difficult, says former Israeli intelligence chief Haim Tomer.\par \par \uc2 HAIM TOMER (Former Israeli Intelligence Chief): It is kind of a mission impossible. To find the hostages, intelligence-wise, is very, very very difficult, almost on the line of impossible.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Best-case scenario, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  releases at least some of the hostages. Worst-case scenario doesn`t end well for anyone.\par \par \uc2 HAIM TOMER: The moment you kidnap babies and old ladies, there are no rules anymore, no rules.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Tonight, the Israeli military spokesman said the army will continue to conduct limited raids, including tonight, and they will continue all the more forcefully in the coming days -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Thank you, Charlie, and your team.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, the new speaker of the House, Louisiana Republican Mike Johnson, met with President Biden today at the White House. Until now, the staunch conservative was little known outside his state and party.\par \par \uc2 And, as Nikole Killion reports, he is remaining tight-lipped about some of his positions.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): On his second day on the job, Speaker Mike Johnson continued to dodge questions about his efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe the 2020 election was stolen, sir, just yes or no?\par \par \uc2 After the election, Johnson, in coordination with then-President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , recruited more than 100 Republicans to sign onto a brief supporting a lawsuit to overturn the results. He also promoted conspiracy theories about rigged voting machine software in a radio interview.\par \par \uc2 (Audio clip from Rep. Mike Johnson`s 2020 radio interview)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): When you have, you know, a software system that is used all around the country that is suspect because it came from Hugo Chavez`s Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Democrats are concerned Johnson could again try to block certification of the Electoral College results next year if President Biden wins again.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (D-New York): His track record with respect to trying to overturn the results of the 2020 election speaks for itself.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Johnson`s opponents are also singling out his views on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from May 2022)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: There is no right to abortion in the Constitution, period.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Before entering office, Johnson called abortion a Holocaust that has been repeated every day for 32 years, since 1973`s Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 As a congressman, he has co-sponsored at least three bills that would ban the procedure nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Do you intend to pursue a national abortion ban as Speaker, sir?\par \par \uc2 But he had nothing to say today about how he will govern going forward. Johnson is also staunchly anti-gay rights. In 2004, he called homosexuality inherently unnatural and voted against legalizing same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Moderate Republicans argue that, despite views they may disagree with, he will be an efficient speaker.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And he recognizes the need that, irrespective of prior positions, he`s going to have to weave us together towards a common goal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Several Republican lawmakers told us they don`t think Speaker Johnson will inject his personal views into his new role.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Johnson also expressed a willingness to work across the aisle, calling his meeting today with President Biden productive -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The off-duty \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  pilot accused of trying to bring down a flight on Sunday with more than 80 people on board appeared in federal court today in Portland, Oregon. A lawyer for Joseph Emerson said the pilot thanks the flight crew for their swift action against him.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the crew stopped Emerson from trying to cut the plane`s engines and subdued him. Emerson`s attorney says he was not suicidal or trying to hurt anyone. He told investigators he was suffering a nervous breakdown and had taken psychedelic mushrooms two days before the incident.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. military -- the U.S. military has released video of yet another Chinese fighter jet harassing and flying dangerously close to a U.S. military plane. Officials say this happened Tuesday over the South China Sea, the Chinese jet coming within 10 feet of the American B-52. The Pentagon says the Chinese military has made similar reckless maneuvers on U.S. planes more than 180 times in the past two years.\par \par \uc2 China claims most of the sea as theirs, but the U.S. says it`s international waters.\par \par \uc2 Finally a breakthrough in the autoworkers strike -- thousands are heading back to work at one of the Big Three automakers, but what about the others?\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Thousands of autoworkers are returning to Ford plants after their union reached a tentative contract agreement to end the six-week-old strike.\par \par \uc2 The deal, which still has to be approved by union members, includes a 25 percent pay raise over 4.5 years. Ford says today that it has lost $1.3 billion in production. Thousands more workers remain on strike at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , as those talks continue.\par \par \uc2 A man suspected of killing a Maryland judge last week was found dead today in a wooded area about a mile from his abandoned SUV. Investigators believe he was angry about losing custody of his kids, when he allegedly shot and killed the judge who presided over the divorce case.\par \par \uc2 A Mexican resort city picks up the pieces after a devastating hurricane. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tonight, heartbreaking scenes of destruction in the Mexican resort city of Acapulco after the monster Category 5 Hurricane Otis pounded the region on Wednesday, killing at least 30 people.\par \par \uc2 Windows are blown out and trees and power lines knocked down. The powerful storm caught everyone by surprise, exploding in strength over the warm Pacific waters.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we go back to Maine for some final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Finally tonight: This small city of Lewiston, Maine, is home to fewer than 40,000 people.\par \par \uc2 It`s the kind of place where you know your neighbors and see friends just walking down the street. But no one can understand why it now joins the list of American cities forever linked by the tragedy of a mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): Just weeks ago, one survey named Lewiston America`s 13th safest city.\par \par \uc2 It`s a former mill town, now home to colleges and health care systems. It hosts an annual Balloon Festival and a Liberty Festival on July 4. And it`s where children gathered on a Wednesday night at a bowling alley, children and a community whose lives are now forever changed.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Janet Mills\uc2  says Lewiston is a city with a big heart and she knows it well. It`s where she met her husband and got married.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS: I know it`s hard for us to think about healing when our hearts are broken. Let us wrap our arms around one another, offer comfort and solace and love.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And it`s what we will continue to do until something changes.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 For Norah O`Donnell and Adriana Diaz, I`m Jericka Duncan in Lewiston, Maine.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231027ejaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 McCarthy Out as House Speaker; Five Hurt in Campus Shooting; Health Care Strike; Trump Under Gag Order; Hunter Biden Pleads Not Guilty; Inside Secretive Ukrainian Unit; Robotaxi Fears; Inside the Battery Belt; Catholics Consider Change; Student Loan Scams; Liver Disease Warning; Making Waves of Change; The Poetry of Arlo Parks; "In the Form of a Question," \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nikole Killion, John  Dickerson, Nicole Sganga, Catherine Herridge, Ramy Inocencio, Kris Van  Cleave, Ben Tracy, Chris Livesay, Anna Werner, Vladimir Duthiers, Dana  Jacobson \par \uc2 14,576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy becomes the first House speaker in history to be voted out of a job after a revolt by some far-right Republicans.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Eight people can throw you out as speaker, I think you`ve got a real divide. My fear is the institution fell today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Multiple people wounded after gunfire erupts at \uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Well, it`s hitting glass behind my head. It`s just -- it is another day here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: During his trial for fraud, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is hit with a partial gag order for attacking a court clerk with false claims on social media.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I think it`s going very well.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Northern Italy, at least 21 people were killed and 18 others hurt when a passenger bus careened off an overpass.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A deadly plane crash near Portland, Oregon. This single engine plane falling out of the sky crashing into a home.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Jury selection is underway in the criminal fraud trial of fallen crypto king, Sam Bankman-Fried.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): Which is surprising because by the looks of him, he already got the electric chair.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Mere minutes before I walked out here on this stage, Republican Kevin McCarthy was ousted as speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 Back in January, Congress had to vote a record 15 times for McCarthy to get the speaker`s job, but to remove him, just one.\par \par \uc2 (Cheering and applause.)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: That`s progress, Kev. Feel good about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a stunning turn of events and that`s where we`re going to get right to the news today with history being made on Capitol Hill. The uncertainty over what is going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy is now the first speaker of the House ever to be removed from leadership. Eight members of his own party went after him and ultimately took him down.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy said it was personal and now the race is on to pick a replacement. Till then, Congress is at a standstill.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is following all of this for us all.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning. What a day yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, pretty unprecedented, Gayle, and good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 You know, this leaves a huge leadership vacuum at a very critical time, the speaker is second in line to the presidency, and Congress has a lot of work to do, including trying to avoid a shutdown in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Office of Speaker of the House of the \uc2 United States House of Representatives\uc2  is hereby declared vacant.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: This morning, a House divided, leaderless after Kevin McCarthy was forced out as speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I don`t regret standing up for choosing governance over grievance.\par \par \uc2 It is my responsibility. It is my job.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: McCarthy was pushed out by eight members of his far-right flank led by Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): Chaos is Speaker McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Florida Republican introduced the motion to remove McCarthy and accused him of breaking promises to conservatives and cutting deals with Democrats on spending.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): The reason Kevin McCarthy went down today is because nobody trusts Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): This is a serious, solemn and sober moment.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: House Democrats joined GOP hardliners in part after circulating this clip in a meeting from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "Face the Nation" when McCarthy blamed them for holding up a recent bill to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I wasn`t sure it was going to pass. You want to know why? Because the Democrats tried to do everything they can not to let it pass.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democrats were the ones who voted for this.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Did you -- did you watch --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In a larger number than Republicans to keep the continuing resolution alive.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): Democrats seized the day, and he goes on TV hours later and says Republicans did it. Republicans were leading, which is insane.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: McCarthy`s ouster marks a stunning sequence of events for the California Republican who battled his members at every turn and went through 15 rounds to secure the speaker`s gavel in January when he made this prediction.\par \par \uc2 How confident are you that you will have this job for a full two-year term?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): A thousand percent.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: With no consensus for a replacement, North Carolina Republican, \uc2 Patrick McHenry\uc2  was designated acting speaker. McCarthy`s deputies like Majority Leader Steve Scalise are being floated for the job and some posted last night, they nominate former President Trump for speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): If I lose my job over doing what I truly believe what`s right, I`m very at peace with it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Scalise is battling blood cancer, but says he feels great. He has been reaching out to members and a candidate forum is set for next week.\par \par \uc2 As for the Trump option, the speaker does not have to be a current member of the House, but whoever it is, will continue to face a slim five-vote majority, so every vote is going to count -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know who`s going to want the job at this point.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on the fallout from all of this, we are joined now by CBS News chief political analyst, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 John, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 What is going on in this country? What is going on in Congress? What`s going on in the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, when you gain power by criticizing what it takes to wield power, it`s really hard to govern and use that power, and that`s essentially the problem the Republican Party has.\par \par \uc2 Governing requires compromise, working with the other party, and believing in institutions. The energy of the Republican Party, not the majority of physical members, but in the base and a lot of -- and the age who voted out the speaker, they believe that compromise is capitulation, they believe the other party is evil, and they believe institutions are corrupt.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you fan those flames to get elected, then once you have to wield power by compromising working within institutions and working with the other party, this very powerful loud base says: Wait, you`ve sold out. And that`s what happened to Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 He often fomented concern about institutions. I mean, he denied the outcome of the 2020 election. So when he says \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  doesn`t care about the institution, McCarthy in part came to power by not trusting institutions.\par \par \uc2 So you can`t -- it makes a very big conflict, and in the end, it creates chaos, and the challenge for the Republican Party is what are the two biggest acts of chaos in American politics in this modern era? There`s this vacating of the speaker, and there is also the attack on the Capitol, which all of the top Republicans say the front runner of the party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  encouraged and had his supporters commit that attack.\par \par \uc2 So the two last acts of chaos are on the doorstep of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Kevin McCarthy also said bring it and they did. I think, even -- he seemed so confident that this was not going to happen, and that`s why it`s so stunning.\par \par \uc2 Was there not one Democrat, not one? Or clearly, they needed more than one that could come and help them at this particular time? Because they did work together to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They worked -- they did. They worked together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he ticked people off on Margaret`s show.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And in fact -- we should -- still, it`s an irony that \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  who knocked the speaker for working with Democrats --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : -- needed Democrats to in the end undo the speaker.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To take down Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And why does that matter? It reinforces the idea that when you`re in a legislative body --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : -- with a small margin, you have to work with the other side, and that`s what Kevin McCarthy did. Democrats worked with him. Why didn`t they vote to save Kevin McCarthy? Because I think, you know, as Nikole mentioned in her piece, they worked with Kevin McCarthy, and then he turned around, and by their view, kneed them in the groin for having worked with him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On Margaret`s show, yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And also, I think they go back and see the way in which he`s done a number of other things that have been -- that the Democrats have --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So the House can`t do anything until they elect a new speaker. So what`s next? And what does it mean for the American people?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We`re stuck at the moment. I mean, it means that until that -- until there`s a new speaker, business will basically come to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A halt.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I mean, they`ll be able to work on some stuff about -- but the thing is also, what`s going to be required to move forward on government funding and addressing any of the problems that America faces is compromise, working together, all of those things that this small number of Republicans kicked out a speaker for engaging.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But then, they take a week off, John. It seems like this ought to be an all hands on deck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I think their hope, if this is even possible is to find a consensus leader and have one vote instead of 15.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know --\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I mean, given the way things are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I always bring up this name in moments like this, Dolly Parton for speaker of the House. I think she could bring everybody together. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Sending that East Tennessee wisdom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We need something here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Tupac Shakur or --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, this is why people are exhausted with Washington, right? We have problems in this country and they`re not getting solved.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes. Dolly Parton second in line to the presidency is perhaps some people`s vision.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First in line in my heart.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or Taylor Swift seems to be working for unity.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Would be one as well, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure. John, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Making the complicated digestible.\par \par \uc2 All right, turning now to a shooting overnight at a Historically Black College. Police say at least five people were shot at a dorm on the campus of \uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  in Baltimore. They are expected to survive, which is good news.\par \par \uc2 Authorities do not know how many shooters were involved.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sganga is at Morgan State for us.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 This campus, home to about 9,000 students was on lockdown for three hours last night. Just hours before shots rang out, students gathered for their annual homecoming coronation ceremony, an event that kicks off homecoming week. Now instead, it would be remembered by students as a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 POLICE: I got a shot out window it appears.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: It`s the shattered windows at a dorm building that led police to believe chaos was unfolding on campus Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WORLEY (Baltimore Police Commissioner): Multiple windows shattered made our officers believe possibly we had an active shooter.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Law enforcement says campus police heard the first shots around 9:25, Tuesday night. Minutes later they found five victims, four of those are students.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRANDON SCOTT (D) (Baltimore, Maryland): This unfortunate and unacceptable tragedy joins as we know a long line of colleges and schools.\par \par \uc2 We have to stop saying not one more.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Police say the shooting took place outside of Thurgood Marshall Hall.\par \par \uc2 POLICE: Got to check and make sure.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: But inside, officers were going door to door scanning rooms in that dorm while students were sheltering in place.\par \par \uc2 While a shooter still remains on the loose, officials remain firm that the campus is safe.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER RICHARD WORLEY: They cleared every single floor twice, and after that, we realized the campus was most licensed likely safe, the shooter was nowhere around that we could find.\par \par \uc2 NAISIR (Attendee): Families were just taking pictures with their children who won events. As soon as we were walking, we started just hearing the shots go off.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Naisir (sp?) was attending a packed coronation ceremony, part of a ritual in the run up to the school`s homecoming week. He says it was just across the street from the gunfire.\par \par \uc2 NAISIR: I was shaken up by it. Two bullet holes went through the glass windows. I heard them as they were going by, so I was really just praying that I would get inside safely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: And it`s a scene all too familiar for students here as there were shooting incidents during homecoming weekend last year and in 2021. Meanwhile, classes are canceled today. As for the rest of homecoming week, well, it`s still up in the air. We do know that \uc2 FBI\uc2  is assisting in this investigation -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Some more news breaking overnight: A strike involving more than 75,000 health care workers is indeed getting underway after \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  and a coalition of unions failed to strike a deal.\par \par \uc2 If the strike is fully implemented, hundreds of Kaiser medical facilities will be affected across five states and Washington, DC. The striking workers would include nurses, pharmacists, technicians, therapists, and cleaners. They are asking for higher wages. They`re asking for additional staffing. These have been major challenges lately across industries.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser says it has offered increases across the board saying its goal is a fair and equitable agreement. That`s a direct quote. Hospitals and emergency departments will remain open or are set to during this three-day strike, but there could be delays. There could be cancellations as well. So watch out.\par \par \uc2 The union say this is expected to be the largest strike of its kind in American history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The judge in Donald Trump`s civil fraud trial has issued a limited gag order after the former president posted a phony and personal criticism of the judge`s law clerk.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s social media posts which has since been deleted, included a photo of the clerk with Democratic Senate Majority Leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  falsely calling her Schumer`s girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 The judge quickly called Trump and his lawyers to meet in private and then ordered all parties in the case to stop making remarks about court staff. Trump was in court for the first two days of testimony in a civil fraud trial.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has pleaded not guilty to charges of lying about his drug abuse to buy a handgun in 2018. The president`s son attended a court hearing in Delaware after the judge in that case refused to let him enter a plea over a video link.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge was inside the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 I was not more than five feet away from \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  as he entered the courtroom and took his seat when the hearing came to order. There was a nervous smile and some laughter with his attorneys.\par \par \uc2 The last time the president`s son was in the same federal courthouse, a plea deal for two misdemeanor tax charges and a felony gun offense collapsed opening the door to more serious charges. Asked by the judge on Tuesday if he understood the alleged crimes and penalties, which can include up to 25 years in prison if convicted, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  responded in a soft but determined voice, "Yes, Your Honor."\par \par \uc2 At his side, lead attorney, Abbe Lowell at one point arguing a statute Biden is accused of violating a ban on gun possession for drug users is unconstitutional. The ongoing special counsel investigation could still file tax charges in this case -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s head overseas now to Ukraine this morning where we continue our look at special units in that country`s military seeking to push back on Russia`s invasion.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio met with some elite snipers who are playing a key role in the battlefields.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: American bullets from American rifles crack through the air near Ukraine`s frontline with Russia. This sniper unit granted CBS News special access as they keep their skill to kill sharp.\par \par \uc2 Always working in teams of two, a spotter checks windspeed. His sniper adjust angle, then between heartbeats, fires, hitting a target nearly a quarter of a mile away.\par \par \uc2 So is it easy to hit this target?\par \par \uc2 COMMISSAR (Sniper): Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Yes?\par \par \uc2 COMMISSAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: You sound very confident. Yup.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSAR: Yup.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: This young man is codenamed Commissar.\par \par \uc2 How important is your job as a sniper in the Defense of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (COMMISSAR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "We bridge the gaps where infantry can`t," he says. "We liquidate top targets like commanders and machine gunners."\par \par \uc2 With progress along the war`s more than 600-mile front slope, if not static, snipers have become more heralded.\par \par \uc2 And in contrast to the high tech war of high flying drones and high visibility hardware, the power of a sniper is low tech, low visibility, and relatively low cost, killing with a single bullet.\par \par \uc2 Everything about snipers is secretive. We can`t even tell you where we are. We can`t even show you their faces. That`s because they are high-value targets.\par \par \uc2 (NIKOLAI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "An experienced sniper is priceless," says Nikolai, their commander. "A tank is just a bunch of metal and can be easily replaced, but it takes a lot of money and years to train a sniper."\par \par \uc2 Along with Ukrainian firearms, American scopes and American 338 caliber rifles, 90 percent of their ammunition they say is also American.\par \par \uc2 You have an American flag there. Why?\par \par \uc2 (COMMISSAR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "Americans have helped Ukraine a lot. I wear this as a sign of respect."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: As for the US funding freeze on Ukraine, Tony, the White House says that existing aid will only last about another couple of months. President Biden has called allied leaders and told them he is confident that bipartisan support for this country will stay strong.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ramy Inocencio for us in Ukraine. Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, stay with us, a woman is seriously injured after being hit by a driverless taxi, but was the self-driving vehicle really at fault in this case? We`ll tell you how this is reigniting the conversation around autonomous vehicles, but first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, how people repaying student loans can avoid online scams that claim to help them avoid big bills for a price.\par \par \uc2 And there`s panic in Paris ahead of the Summer Olympics over a guest that nobody wants to have at home. We`ll explain in a bit.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Police in San Francisco are investigating after a woman was hurt in an accident involving a self-driving rideshare and another car, but here`s the story: The car with a human driver hit the woman first and then she wound up in the path of the driverless robotaxi. This accident is putting new attention on the safety of those driverless vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: San Francisco Police are investigating an apparent hit- and-run that left a woman critically injured and pinned under a Cruise self-driving robotaxi in San Francisco Monday night.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The patient was transported within 10 minutes of the arrival of the first rescuers.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Video from the Cruise vehicle reviewed by CBS News shows the woman crossing against the light and getting hit by a sedan. She is then launched into the path of the self-driving car. Cruise says its vehicle braked aggressively to minimize the impact.\par \par \uc2 There is some chance that this self-driving car was just in the wrong place at the wrong time.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: The real question that needs to be answered is, could a human driver have avoided this person in the road?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Cruise says its vehicles are involved in 65 percent fewer collisions than rideshares driven by people, but in August, cut its Bay Area fleet by 50 percent after two accidents within hours of each other.\par \par \uc2 The autonomous taxis have drawn public outcry with some residents disabling them by putting traffic cones on their hoods.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BRANDENBURG (San Francisco Bay Area resident): We already have taxis, we already have Lyfts and Ubers. It doesn`t seem like we need them.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Cruise has about 400 of these self-driving vehicles on the roads in several cities across the US including here in Phoenix. The company has never had a fatal accident, and this morning says it is cooperating with police as they try to find this apparent hit-and-run driver.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And by hit-and-run driver, he is talking about the initial car that hit the lady.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not the self-driving car, which clearly was staying right there. I mean, I think we have to keep in mind that the data shows that the self-driving vehicles overall --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are safer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- are safer than human drivers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People make mistakes. The computers make fewer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but some people love them, some people don`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s a ton of these vehicles in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they`re coming everywhere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If they`re there, they`re going national.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the question they`re asking is, a human driver, they said probably couldn`t have done any better than the driverless vehicle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The human driver caused the incident.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would say so as well. At least, began anyway.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in our series, "Protecting the Planet," we will go to a part of the country being called the Battery Belt for the rapid growth of plants there.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us, local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Our series "Protecting the Planet" looks at the manufacturing boom driven by new electric vehicle battery plants.\par \par \uc2 As the auto worker strike enters its third week, a key sticking point is worker pay in EV plants. Many are being built in the south where the workforce is largely non-union.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy shows us how the EV transition is transforming communities.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This is the kind of town that assumed its best days had rolled by, but now Commerce, Georgia, population 7,700 and climbing finds itself back on track.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CLARK HILL (Commerce, Georgia): Well, I think money follows money.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Mayor Clark Hill says new businesses like this brewery are pouring into downtown. There`s even something approaching rush hour on once sleepy streets.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CLARK HILL: When you have a company investing more than two-and-a- half billion dollars in your community, it attracts a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That company is just down the road inside this massive manufacturing plant where they`re building battery cells for electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 How big is this place and how many batteries can you actually produce?\par \par \uc2 JAEWON CHEY (SK On executive vice chairman): This place as large as 13 football fields, and this place we make battery for over 400,000 cars.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Jaewon Chey is executive vice chairman of SK On, SK stands for South Korea and the flagpole out front is a sign that after decades of US jobs being offshored to Asia, the script is being flipped.\par \par \uc2 Foreign battery manufacturers are opening dozens of new US plants to be close to the automakers they supply. SK builds many of its batteries for Ford.\par \par \uc2 Despite some recent job cuts, it currently employs more than 3,000 people at this one plant including Desmond Salmon.\par \par \uc2 DESMOND SALMON (SK On employee): I live, you know, 20 minutes from here. This is a great opportunity for me to be a part of this.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: SK plans to employ up to 15,000 people by 2026 in five battery plants across three US states.\par \par \uc2 JAEWON CHEY: One more in different location in Georgia; two in Kentucky, one in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In the past three years, more than $90 billion in battery investments have been announced nationwide, creating an estimated 70,000 manufacturing jobs. Much of this is clustered into about eight states stretching from Michigan down into the southeast, a part of the country now known as the Battery Belt.\par \par \uc2 Many of these states are red ones, where Republican lawmakers rarely mention climate change and even oppose the climate legislation that is now supercharging US battery manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 Georgia, which has seen the most investment even imposed a new tax on public EV charging this summer.\par \par \uc2 MIKE O`KRONLEY\uc2  (Ascend Elements CEO): For right now, this plant just opened around six months ago. By year end, this plant will be at capacity and we`re going to need another plan.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Mike O`Kronley\uc2  is CEO of Ascend Elements.\par \par \uc2 Here in Covington, Georgia, it recycles used EV batteries, extracting about 98 percent of all the valuable metals inside them.\par \par \uc2 MIKE O`KRONLEY\uc2 : Specifically nickel, cobalt, lithium.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: This is another critical part of the new domestic EV supply chain.\par \par \uc2 MIKE O`KRONLEY\uc2 : I`m really proud to be part of that, and I know a lot of our team members are also very, very proud to be part of that.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And for towns like Commerce, where spotting an actual electric vehicle is still rare, being a notch on the Battery Belt means more than bragging rights.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CLARK HILL: We feel like it`s not only going to be good for our citizens, but it`s also going to provide a better quality of life for the whole region.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy, in Commerce, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so interesting to see how far we`ve come with electric vehicles because remember in the beginning, people were like, whoever is going to drive one of those? And now, a lot of us want to drive one of them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do remember those days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, they are building batteries and they`re really changing these little towns. They`re getting bigger. They`re getting a lot of energy. New bars, new restaurants, new folks coming in. It`s an amazing time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it is supposed to create more revenue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As people transition to more electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is doing that.\par \par \uc2 Moving on, the Roman Catholic church has opened a formal meeting that`s likely to address major controversies like female priests and the church`s response to its gay members.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis celebrated mass this morning to open that meeting saying the church needs to lower its barriers and welcome everyone.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay is in Rome covering this meeting.\par \par \uc2 Chris, good morning\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 It`s been called the Super Bowl of the Catholic Church. Bishops gathered from around the world to discuss hot button topics such as whether or not priests can get married or whether women can become deacons or whether or not divorced and remarried Catholics can have communion.\par \par \uc2 The Synod is already historic. It`s the first time women and lay people are being allowed to vote. However, 80 percent of participants are still bishops, but the biggest bombshell dropped earlier this week when Francis opened the door for priests to bless same sex unions.\par \par \uc2 Now, this came with caveats. Francis emphasized that blessings shouldn`t be seen as elevating same sex unions to the sacred place of heterosexual weddings.\par \par \uc2 Up until now, the Church`s position has been that same sex unions cannot be blessed because, "God cannot bless sin." But in his statement, Francis said, "We cannot be judges who only deny, reject, and exclude."\par \par \uc2 LGBTQ groups have welcomed the important change while conservative Catholics have blasted Pope Francis for what they say is diluting church doctrine and sowing confusion. All of this in the Synod has only just begun -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chris Livesay for us in Rome.\par \par \uc2 Chris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next in just a moment, as student loan repayments resume, the government is warning borrowers to watch out for scammers.\par \par \uc2 Anna Werner will be here with what you need to know to avoid getting ripped off.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are some new warnings this morning about scammers trying to take advantage of the millions of Americans now resuming payments on their student loans. We`re talking 27 million people who have federal student loan debt, and that totals approximately, $1.1 trillion. That`s a lot of people in a vulnerable position, a lot of scammers trying to get that money.\par \par \uc2 Our consumer investigative correspondent, Anna Werner has more.\par \par \uc2 Anna, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 And you will not be surprised to hear that government agencies and nonprofit groups say the scammers have been reaching out to people through robocalls, online ads, e-mails, even snail mail. They often ask for fees to provide a bogus service, or try to get your personal information -- surprise. Here`s what some of those robocalls sound like.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Press one now to see if you are eligible to get your loans completely forgiven. This recent announcement could potentially eliminate 100 percent of your federal student debt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So many of these scammers offer to help borrowers avoid repayment, to lower their payments, get the loans forgiven. Some scammers provide some legitimate information, then they claim they`ll do the paperwork for you for a price, of course.\par \par \uc2 So you really have to be paying attention. I think a lot of people get caught because somebody calls and they just aren`t quite listening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, they also say "completely forgiven."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People want to -- you know, people --\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Either you don`t have the money or you`re just looking to save some money. That`s everybody, right? And so you`re susceptible, you want to hear them out. And then you feel like, oh, this is an easy, cheap path.\par \par \uc2 So what are the warning signs, because you want to walk down, but you`re walking down a bad path.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Right. Okay. So if they`re trying to get your personal information, first of all, they ask for your federal student aid loan ID, you want to watch out for that. Don`t give out personal information.\par \par \uc2 They might say they`re with the Department of Education. You should go to the Department of Education, or StudentAid.gov website makes sure that you`re talking to the real people. They might offer, as I said, to manage or process your paperwork. You don`t have to pay fees for that. You can go through the federal government, you don`t need that help, which will take a bunch of your money.\par \par \uc2 So you really -- I think a lot of it though, is people just paying attention and thinking for a minute about now, wait a minute, why is somebody wanting to help me so much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re talking to these people who have been scammed. How do they say they got tricked?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Well, so we spoke to one woman who said for example, they called and they said she had until the end of the month to submit an application, she would pay $1,000.00 and her loans would be dismissed, of course, and the urgency to complete by a deadline is part of the con.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Fortunately, she reached out to a nonprofit agency and they were like, no, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: It`s a scam. Don`t do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, my momma said and I`m pretty sure we all heard this, if it sounds too good to be true --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- it probably is.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your Momma sounds like my momma.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anna, thank you so much. This is valuable information.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories you will be talking about today. But first, it is 7:42, times to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." There are bed bugs in Paris apparently?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh man. We`re going to get to that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can probably tell Katy, which is why we`re not going, Katy. This is why we`re not going.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Boy, all right --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the reason.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the reason.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m going to tell you what`s going on in Paris, but let`s get right into this --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- because we have an e