rnance over grievance. It is my responsibility. It is my job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An historic first in the House as Speaker Kevin McCarthy is removed from his position.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We are stuck at the moment. Until there`s a new speaker, business will basically come to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 NAISIR: I just saw everybody running. I heard the shots going off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Five people are hurt at a shooting at a Historically Black University in Baltimore\par \par \uc2 DAVID WILSON (\uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  president): \uc2 Morgan State University\uc2  will not be deterred. We will move forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More than 20 people are dead after a tourist bus plunges off an overpass in Italy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The main question that the investigation will look to answer is exactly how this could have happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  pleads not guilty to federal firearms charges in a Delaware courtroom.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The ongoing special counsel investigation could still file tax charges in this case.\par \par \uc2 DOROTHY HOFFNER (World`s oldest skydiver at 104): It was wonderful. I had to keep myself awake so I could see the scenery.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re never too old for fun. A Chicago woman goes tandem skydiving at the age of 104.\par \par \uc2 DOROTHY HOFFNER: I couldn`t understand why those people were there, but as I say, all I did was get old -- older. I`m not old. I`m older.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she`s not old, she is older.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And listen Bellamy is here. Hi, Bellamy. We all want to get older.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG (Actor): Yes. Oh, yes. The alternative is worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that something you would do?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I haven`t done it yet, but maybe at her age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is not a bad time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s done it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate`s done it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He had the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I`ll do, I`ll do it at her age.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with an interview you`ll see only on CBS MORNINGS and we love when that can happen. So I`m going to say it again, only on CBS MORNINGS, actor, Bellamy Young, best known for shows like "Scandal" and "The Other Black Girl" is opening up for the very first time about the impact of chronic liver disease on her family.\par \par \uc2 So Bellamy`s father had cirrhosis, which led to a chronic liver condition known as HE. Now that had devastating effects on his brain. That`s a great picture.\par \par \uc2 Thirty million Americans have some form of liver disease, nearly one in five have a chronic condition, which for many leads to HE. It can produce symptoms like extreme personality changes, disorientation, confusion, and uncoordinated movements, and inappropriate behavior.\par \par \uc2 And Bellamy joins us at the table.\par \par \uc2 We`re glad you`re here, because I had never heard of HE until this meeting yesterday, when they told us that you were coming on.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I`m so grateful to have this time with you guys, because that`s how it is. This affects so many people in America and across the world, and a lot has changed.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of information to know. We can find out how to handle this condition, and no one is having this conversation. So I`m very, very grateful to be here with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how did it affect your family? We should say your dad died when you were 15.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: He did. So it was a bit of a time ago. But we felt very isolated and alone. There wasn`t a lot of information. Even our doctor couldn`t really help us.\par \par \uc2 So he was diagnosed very late because we didn`t know what to look for. We never imagined after a diagnosis of cirrhosis, which is a severe form of chronic liver disease, we never imagined that a liver disease could affect your cognition and your behavior. So we didn`t even know what to keep our eyes open for.\par \par \uc2 And now that`s why I partner with Salix and I`m hoping I can encourage everyone to look at a website called UnderstandingHE.com because you can find out about the symptoms, you can notice them early.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how did it show itself, Bellamy?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s talk about -- yes, let`s talk about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I am not sure exactly what --\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still don`t understand what was happening in your house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes, so, well, at the time, I was young and so a lot of this annoyed me as things annoy teenagers. So maybe a change in the smell of his breath and I`d be like: Ugh, he`s drinking in the daytime, or he`d forget to pick me up from school and I`d be like: Oh, great, you know, he forgot to pick me up.\par \par \uc2 But when things became progressively worse, like one night, he forgot how to get home from the job he`d had forever, and we thought, okay, that`s something -- is something going on?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really bad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s bad.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: But at that point, he was in a very what they call overt hepatic encephalopathy and what happens with hepatic encephalopathy or HE is the toxins in your liver, they sort of back up in your bloodstream until they get to your brain, and they cause disorientation and they cause a lack of coordination and a lot of different, very obvious symptoms if you know what you`re looking for.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: But we didn`t know, so we didn`t catch it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Was this tied to alcohol use?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: His diagnosis of cirrhosis was because of alcohol use. But you know, chronic -- severe chronic liver disease can be caused by so many things.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. It seems like at one point, you came to a realization about these things that were going on because you weren`t aware.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When did you come to this, you know, realization? And how did you come to the point where you want to talk about it?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Well, we came to the realization very late, and that is exactly why I want to talk about it because so much has changed.\par \par \uc2 Now there is information, people understand this disease and how to treat it and how to walk along the path. But most of all, how to recognize it when it`s happening to the person that you love. Because as a person who is sick, or as a caregiver, it`s just agonizing to watch and you can get in front of it.\par \par \uc2 If you talk to your doctor, you can plan a path forward and also to have community. We felt very alone. So just to feel like there are other people out there to talk to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Who can help you, have great conversations with your doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think there`s a stigma because of the association with alcohol? Is that why you think people don`t talk about it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I do think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I think people get embarrassed about liver disease, but it affects like 30 million people in America. That`s a lot of people, and we need to release the stigma.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Especially for people --\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: And enhance the education.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bellamy, like us who have never heard about it.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we have heard about you in "Scandal."\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Well, hello?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know it is running -- I know it is on another network, but I loved you in that show, and I love that show and what`s so interesting is that, you weren`t even supposed to be on the show for very long, right?\par \par \uc2 I read that you were only supposed to be in the first couple of episodes. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Gayle, I am so lucky. No, she came -- yes. She came -- Shonda came around, our first official table read once we`ve gotten picked up to tell everybody what their arc was going to be that first season and she kind of put her hand on my shoulder.\par \par \uc2 And I dressed up and I`m very southern, so I was very excited for the day. And she said: So you`re going to be here three episodes. I want to write a presidential divorce, and I tried to smile because you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like, yay, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: I was grateful to be there and I was dying inside. But it didn`t turn out that way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you became such a key figure in that show.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: It was fun. It was fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Such a keeper --\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Madam President. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Bellamy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much for coming here.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. And that website again for people you want to check that out.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: UnderstandingHE.com.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, very good. Thank you very much for bringing awareness to this.\par \par \uc2 BELLAMY YOUNG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Appreciate it. Bellamy Young. Come back soon.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, our series "Pushing the Limits," Dana Jacobson goes into the water, the dirty water of the Hudson River.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, for the Hudson -- the Hudson River. It`s cloudy, can`t see very far in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cloudy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a story about one man`s aquatic adventure.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up on CBS MORNINGS, take a swim with us in the Hudson River and find out why this guy is swimming all 315 miles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it`s pretty up there, I am not worried about that part of it. But anyway, okay, but first it is time anybody -- 8:08, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Purple Phase.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Grammy-nominated artist, Arlo Parks, who will be here in studio with us. She will talk to us about her new book of poems, very personal, and why she decided to release them now after keeping them private.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our series, "Pushing the Limits" share stories of ordinary people accomplishing the truly incredible. In this case, it is Lewis Pugh, he is an endurance athlete. He just wrapped up a 315-mile aquatic adventure, swimming from the source of the Hudson River in New York`s Adirondack Mountains all the way down to New York Harbor, accompanied by a support team.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Saturday Morning" co-host, Dana Jacobson joined Pugh on the journey and later at New York`s Pier 57 to find out more about the inspirational message behind the mission.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Endurance swimmer, Lewis Pugh knows exactly how to make a splash. The 53-year-old is the only person to have completed a long- distance swim in every ocean in the world. He is willing to test the waters no matter the adventure.\par \par \uc2 Over the past decade, he has become the first athlete to swim across the North Pole, the English Channel, the Red Sea, even under the Antarctic ice sheet.\par \par \uc2 When did swimming come into your life?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH (Endurance swimmer): When I was about 17 years old.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You ready?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You`re feeling well? Good luck, my boy.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: When did it go from enjoying swimming to finding these endurance type of swims?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: In 2007, I went to the North Pole and I did a swim there, and this was across an open patch of sea.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: And I remember going into that swim and then coming out the other end, and actually feeling that I was a different person because I came out of that feeling, wow, you know, this place is melting so quickly, I now have a big responsibility to share this message with the whole world.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It changed your perspective, obviously.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I think it changed me.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Since then, Pugh has been on a mission to complete record breaking swims in jaw-dropping places, not for the titles or accolades, but to make waves in the conversation about ocean and now river preservation.\par \par \uc2 His latest challenge turned quite a few heads as he expected it would. In mid-August, Pugh set out on a month-long journey that`s never been done before, swimming unassisted from the tip of the Hudson River in the Adirondacks down to the very bottom in Lower Manhattan. That`s 315 miles of the infamous river contaminated by toxic waste throughout the last century before cleanup efforts began in the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I want people to know what has happened in the Hudson River.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I think this is a blueprint for people all around the world, that their rivers can be saved.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That there is something that can be done.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Absolutely. People around the world have heard about this as an amazing story about a river, which was so badly misused.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: And so badly polluted, and it was one of the most polluted rivers here in America, if not the world. And then things have been turned around.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Enough so that Pugh spent 32 consecutive days in the river swimming twice a day, both early in the morning, and well into the night to align his swim times with the rivers current.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Sometimes, I`ll be swimming at night, and I`d turn onto my back and start doing backstroke and I`m looking at the heavens and you`ve got the big blue moon, and all of these stars and it was really, really comforting.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to want to get in here, it`s a lot cooler.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I decided to jump in myself.\par \par \uc2 All right, you convinced me.\par \par \uc2 On day 25 of the journey, when Pugh had just 50 miles to go, long distance from my swim team days, I eased into my stroke. But trust me, Lewis Pugh makes it look easy.\par \par \uc2 People that I told that I was going to swim with you, there are still people who said, you`re getting in the Hudson River? Where?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So, it still has some of that reputation.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I hope we can change that reputation.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: I hope more people will swim in this river. This is such a special river.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: One that Pugh has come to know intimately. And one that drew a crowd of spectators to downtown Manhattan, to take in his final stretch within it.\par \par \uc2 But there`s one crowd Pugh hopes to rally in even larger numbers, members of the United Nations who have the chance to ratify a new treaty aimed to protect 30 percent of the world`s High Seas by 2030.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: This is the last piece of the jigsaw puzzle which we needed in order to protect the planet properly. So for me, the mission isn`t complete.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Because it`s not just the swim.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Because it`s not just a swim. I`ve got to get to the diplomats. I`ve got to get the Environment Ministers, I`ve got to get to the heads of state now and deliver the message.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Your determination as one person to make world leaders listen, to make people listen. Where does that come from?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS PUGH: Deep down in here. I`ve seen oceans change. And for me, this is a defining issue of our generation.\par \par \uc2 In a short period of time, we really have to protect the planet. I love being in the oceans. I love being in rivers. I am absolutely determined to spend my life doing this work.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Dana Jacobson in New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo to him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, he did it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean -- yes, he did it and he is totally right that the Hudson is a success story in the sense that it has come a long, long way from terrible, terrible pollution and the more people are in it and active with it, the more we care about it, the more we`ll keep it clean.\par \par \uc2 But just for the record, the New York state environmental officials say that the New York City portion, the very end there, still not considered to be an everyday safe swimming river.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I`m getting texts from people I work with at the "NFL Today." Shout out to Ellen. She basically is saying the same thing like there`s a reputation but I swim in this river all the time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I was surprised that she told me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: After the rainstorm --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ellen swims in there all the time?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes. She said --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The issue is the runoff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is the runoff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the sewer system, so after a big rainstorm like we had last week --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what she said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Then you`ve got to stay out of it, and I`m sure he was timing it like that.\par \par \uc2 I`ll tell you what though? Swimming at night, something brushes you in the water, I couldn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it just give you an appreciation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For what we have and often we take for granted.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We should go for a night swim together as a show.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is one of those things that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you`re talking about, Willis?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A night swim?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, retired \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend, that would be Michael Jordan joins a very exclusive list and we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not even a swim. A night swim?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here is your "Jeopardy!" clue. She is the most successful female champion in "Jeopardy!" history. The answer: Who is Amy Schneider?\par \par \uc2 She is getting ready on our set right now.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, she will tell us about her new memoir and the questions she has yet to answer.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where I`ve always been able to control myself and will never conga.\par \par \uc2 Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, you can`t control it any longer. Get up, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on, baby. Shake that conga.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would love to see it though, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is Michael Jordan, who is still setting records two decades after retiring from the \uc2 NBA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His Airness is now the first athlete to rank among the 400 wealthiest people in the US.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And most of his money was made off the court.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After selling his majority share in the \uc2 Charlotte Hornets\uc2 , Jordan`s net worth is now calculated by Forbes magazine at around $3 billion.\par \par \uc2 He made most of his fortune close to two-and-a-half billion through partnerships with big brands like \uc2 Nike\uc2 , a lot of \uc2 Nike\uc2  money, Gatorade, McDonald`s, and Hanes. He made $93.9 million as a player at a time when players were not earning what they make right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was that movie we were talking about? It is called "Air."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Air."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Air."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is about the deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So good. I love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because that`s a large part of his fortune.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was like the first athlete to get a portion of every shoe sold in his likeness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A different type of revenue share as an athlete and listen, the Jordan brand has transcended basketball.\par \par \uc2 You can see that logo in NASCAR --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anything, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Football, boxing, baseball.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you know what it means.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it was a hoop shoe at one point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then, his mom came in and according to "Air" came in and said: Oh no, we`re going to do things differently here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But don`t we just love him being on the list?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t we just love Michael Jordan?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, Gayle, you make a good point. That`s momma`s fortune.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because she was the one that started it all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, according to "Air," which is a very good move.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very good movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is about how the classic southern accent is apparently fading from this earth, from this country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say it ain`t so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: According to a big time study from the University of Georgia and \uc2 Georgia Tech\uc2 , which looked at like seven generations of Georgians, that deep southern drawl is falling off a cliff.\par \par \uc2 So baby boomers talk differently than the silent generation, talk differently than Gen X, talk differently than current college kids.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, it`s not just Georgia. So this is the interesting thing. According to people who study this, it is happening with the Boston accent. It`s happening with people in Detroit. It`s happening in California.\par \par \uc2 So this thing that was so interesting about America where we all talk a little bit different.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have a Maryland and Florida accent, it is kind of going away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did they say why?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s because we`re moving around more. We`re mixing more, and more of a national culture of media.\par \par \uc2 So I guess the question is, is this a bad thing or a good thing or not a thing at all?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still like the different accents.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think it is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still like the different accents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like it, too. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I enjoy a good accent for sure, but I think it`s a good thing because oftentimes, unfortunately, stereotypes are attached to accents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you are judged.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. So the classic southern accent comes out on words like prize and face, bet, bait and dress.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What words give you a little trouble?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well you know, Nate, that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: From Maryland and Florida.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do a lot of water.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, the water.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: W-A-R-D-E-R. I do a lot of water. Forward, no R and forward, forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then also I can`t -- you know, that little prefix U-N, you know, like unaware, I always say like O-N, unaware, unguard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hear from people who write to me about it, but I`m going to stick to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All I know is, I can still understand every word you say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What about you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think you`re good.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is an actor`s incredible journey to make it on stage on time and all the people that helped get her there.\par \par \uc2 Shout out to \uc2 Delta Airlines\uc2  on this.\par \par \uc2 Mikayla Renfrow is usually a Broadway understudy. In fact, she`s the second understudy, but she was flying back from a European vacation earlier this week -- earlier last week, when she got a text message the show needed her to go on as Jasmine in "Aladdin" that night.\par \par \uc2 Her flight was due to land at JFK airport at five o`clock, two hours before curtain time. She decided to ask for help.\par \par \uc2 So first, she went to the flight attendant. The flight attendant moved her up to first class, so she would be the first one off the plane. The pilot was able to radio ahead to change gates so that the gate was the first one next to Customs, so she could whisk through the airport, to a car, then a helicopter that the Delta crew arranged for her --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Took her from Manhattan where she took the subway and then ran to the theater. She had to take the subway after landing -- Delta set all this up -- getting into costume half an hour before the curtain went up.\par \par \uc2 And I just said, boy, Delta going above and beyond. She made it and then she invited the flight attendant who also helped her, Leicha Richardson.\par \par \uc2 So I just think all things came together to make sure that she got to the theater 30 minutes before curtain time, to get into costume and do it. I love that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shout out to Delta.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shout out to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was a good one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you Mikayla, I hear that you killed that night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, that was good, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Grammy-nominated artist, Arlo Parks is here. We`ll talk with the singer-songwriter and poet about her new book and her decision to share her poems publicly for the first time.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Pegasus.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Arlo Parks in her hit song on Pegasus featuring Phoebe Bridgers.\par \par \uc2 The Grammy-nominated singer-songwriter and poet has been called a poet laureate of Gen Z discontent by "Vogue" Magazine. Parks is now releasing never before seen poems in her new book: "The Magic Border: Poetry Fragments From My Soft Machine."\par \par \uc2 A companion to her second album, her poems explore themes of queerness, Blackness, grief, and love. Arlo Parks joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS (Grammy-nominated singer-songwriter): Good morning. I`m good. Thank you. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You seem to be good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 This collection of poetry is deeply, deeply personal and as you read it, you can feel it. Why did you decide to share this work publicly? And how does this compare to your music?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes, so I`ve been writing poetry since I was about maybe 11 or 12, and I think as you say, it was very much my own personal refuge. It was this thing that I did for myself by myself.\par \par \uc2 And I think recently, you know, touring a lot, I was reading a lot and being so moved by what I was reading, and I just wanted to be able to do that for other people, and I think that`s what gave me the courage to finally kind of share this and create this book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talk about, Arlo, about poetry has a chaotic magic to you, and I like the phrasing of that. But you also talk about having a paper bag of burrata and anchovies. You lost me on that one, I am afraid. Does that taste good? Does that taste good?\par \par \uc2 But talk about poetry having a chaotic magic to you.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a very visual writer, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you mean? Didn`t you think --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Every poem is like a scene.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very visual. Yes. Yes. I can see it.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. Exactly. I take a lot of influence from cinema. And I think just from observing, I think that if you look out at the world, there is this real sense of magic to it. And I think my process is very chaotic. It`s very much kind of pulling fragments from the characters around me and my past and nostalgia and the things I observe.\par \par \uc2 And there`s something quite beautiful about that, to me, I think when I approached my poems, in this kind of whirlwind, very emotional --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stream of consciousness that you`re allowing us to enjoy the ride as well.\par \par \uc2 When you write, it seems like it is therapeutic for the reader.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it has to be therapeutic for you. What is it like releasing this and how you have been feeling about yourself?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. It`s exactly as you say, it feels so cathartic. I think especially being able to actually speak to fans and people who have read the book, and maybe have been inspired to, you know, pick up writing themselves, or you know, like people who wanted to write for 20 years being like, okay, I will finally have the courage to do it now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: And I think that kind of gives me a sense of purpose, and just makes me feel like my thoughts and my feelings that I kind of put together in my bedroom are actually having a wider impact on the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to pick up on what Nate thought -- what Nate said, because I thought that too, that it must be healing bruises: "I wish I was bruises, almost every one that I love has been abused and I am included in that." So it seems to me this would be very healing to write about, to sing about.\par \par \uc2 What do you hope people will get out of your book?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. I think I want it to be -- I guess, I want it to be a relief. I want it to be something that people can read and see themselves in, and I want it to be something that people can read and be like: Oh, I should get this from my sister. I should get this for, you know, my partner. It`d be something that you know, people carry through with them on their journeys and share with people they love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you and Phoebe Bridgers, together. I like that combination.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Yes. Yes. No, we met a while ago, we met like maybe four years ago and we`ve been kind of crossing paths and singing together at music festivals and finally came together for my song "Pegasus," which is really special. She`s like a really special person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is your advice to those who want to start writing?\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: I guess just start.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just start.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Just trust your voice and start. I think, the hardest thing is very much you know, being like I want to write, I want to write, but maybe not quite having the courage to kind of put those first few words on the page.\par \par \uc2 So I think just start and trust your words.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you started -- I love your accent.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Started.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You started at 11.\par \par \uc2 ARLO PARKS: Very Brit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Being this transparent isn`t easy. So thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At 11.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So thank you for this work. It is helpful for the reader.\par \par \uc2 All right, "The Magic Border."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats, Arlo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . is on sale wherever you buy your books. Tickets are also currently on sale for your upcoming tour. Okay, Arlo.\par \par \uc2 Up next, "Jeopardy!" record breaker, Amy Schneider is here. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jeopardy!")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a very long time. "Jeopardy!" champion, Amy Schneider became the second longest running player in the show`s history last year, winning 40 consecutive games of "Jeopardy!"\par \par \uc2 Now Schneider is opening up about that moment and so much more in her new memoir. It`s called "In the Form of a Question: The Joys and Rewards of a Curious Life."\par \par \uc2 We are proud to say it`s published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which like \uc2 CBS\uc2  is owned by \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Schneider writes in the book: "Knowledge is a shield and a sword, a joy and a duty. If you have the desire, not just to know but to understand and you will grow more and more powerful every day."\par \par \uc2 And Amy Schneider joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Amy, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER ("In the Form of a Question" author): Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is so much in this book that you open up about, and I want to get to a lot of it. But I want to start with the key message here, which is just that learning can be fun, learning makes you powerful. It can be a joy.\par \par \uc2 Talk about what you want people to take away on that front?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes, you know, I had so many people after I was on "Jeopardy!" asking me like, you know, how do you know all that stuff? And when I thought it through, I realized that it was just because I was raised to love knowing stuff, and to be curious about the world.\par \par \uc2 And that, you know, I also happen to have the sort of memory that like kind of trivia facts stick in my mind. So like, I was lucky in that sense that that skill set applied to "Jeopardy!"\par \par \uc2 But even apart from that, I think I`ve gotten so much value out of just having that curiosity to openness and understanding the world better and ultimately myself. And that`s something that I think anybody can benefit from whether or not they`re going to like remember the capital of Liechtenstein or whatever.\par \par \uc2 But being open and curious, I think has brought me so many benefits.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s the capital of Liechtenstein?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Vaduz.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also like, the key thing is being willing to be wrong and actually being open to being wrong and liking being wrongly, you write, and this is very unique in the beginning of this book that you hope to disagree in the future with what you`re writing right now, what the reader is reading.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Talk to me about that philosophy, that approach.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Right. Yes, I mean, I think that, you know, I feel like -- you know, I feel like I should always look back on myself in the past. And think, boy, I was done back then. Because if I don`t think that, then I haven`t gotten any smarter since.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: And so, and I do -- like, I know that I`ve evolved my beliefs on a lot of things over the years, and I look back at times and things I used to believe and I don`t agree with that anymore.\par \par \uc2 And if that was the case, then it`s reasonable to think that there`s something I believe now that in the future I`m going to disagree with. So I just wanted to partly just like cover myself and you know, for --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because there`s a lot of topics in this book. Gayle, dive in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll say, but I like how you did every chapter with a question. Chapter Two: When did you know you were trans? And I`m wondering, did you have to have a whole thought process before deciding to go on "Jeopardy!" because you are exposing yourself to the whole world in a very different way?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was that something that was a tough decision?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: It was. You know, I`d wanted to be on "Jeopardy!" kind of like my whole life. I grew up watching with my parents but then yes, when I came out as trans, it definitely made me reconsider for a while. Because, you know, trans people can become targets and I`ve seen that happen to people. And you know, it was kind of scary to be exposed in that way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what did you think it was when you were growing up, Amy? What did you think?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes. I mean, you know, growing up in the 80s and 90s in Ohio, you know, trans person was not a concept that that me or anybody in my community knew about.\par \par \uc2 And so, it wasn`t something I could imagine being, and so when I was a kid, more or less, what I figured was, all of the other little boys probably wish that they were girls too, and are jealous of them and wish they could were pretty things and all of that. And we`re all just not allowed to say that, so we`re all hiding it, but probably everybody else feels the same way I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there`s nobody you can talk to really about it.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Right, because that is -- I mean, and also, you know, that is a thing. Like, it`s so strict in our society that men cannot do anything girly or talk about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s changing, I hope.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes, I think so. But that definitely -- you know, made it easy to believe that everyone else was secretly thinking those things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel like most people can`t differentiate knowing something and understanding something and those are two things that you write about. You know yourself. What do you understand about yourself now versus let`s say, 20 years ago?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: I mean, I think, you know, again, first thing that I`m a woman. It took me a long time to understand that.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, you know, once I started living kind of more authentically, I learned a lot more. I learned that I am like kind of an extrovert and social and I learned that I`m a lot more you know, once I started allowing myself to be authentic, I could connect with other people so much better.\par \par \uc2 And I learned that, you know, I`m really interested in getting to know people in a way that I was always kind of frightened of forming those connections before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are many things, but many things you didn`t appear to be was nervous when you were on "Jeopardy!" You always seem so calm, so in control. Were you feeling that?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: No, I was definitely nervous most of the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you?\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes. But, you know, I wanted -- first of all, I just like, I`m a performer. I was a theater kid my whole life. And you know, I wanted to look good on TV, and I also didn`t want my other contestants to know if I was feeling nervous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Poker face, if you will.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes, exactly. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you think you would have that long of a run? Did you think, I got this game? Because you played it for so long.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: No. I mean, like, you know, I thought I could be good -- I like, I told all my friends, I was like, if things go my way, I could win - - I could see myself winning three or four games.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: That was, you know, legitimately about the most I expected.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But hold on, I don`t want people to think this is just an earnest book about learning and knowing. It is that, but it`s also a very frank and honest conversation about drug use, about polyamorous relationships.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you get into a lot of -- you`ve got tarot card reading.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Amy, go see us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, were there things in the book, and we only have about 30 more seconds that you were like: Ooh, I don`t want to put that in.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: No, because I felt an obligation to give an authentic representation of myself as a trans person because I know of many people I represent that community.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY SCHNEIDER: And I want to take a give a full picture.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and that`s why you didn`t wrestle with, do I have a story? You have a story and that matters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have a story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have a platform as well.\par \par \uc2 Amy Schneider, thank you very much. Appreciate it. "In the Form of a Question" is on sale right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is a good book?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. What is a good book? Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`ll do it for us. Very good, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Capital of Lichtenstein. That was impressive, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020231004eja400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 13, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jo Ling Kent, Lana Zak, David Martin, Nikole  Killion, Nikki Dementri \par \uc2 3,792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the stunning new details about how police finally captured the convicted murderer after escaping from prison two weeks ago. He was subdued by a police dog and forcibly taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Found in a stolen \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  sweatshirt. How police used heat- seeking technology and DEA planes to find him.\par \par \uc2 DEB RYAN (District Attorney, Chester County, Pennsylvania): We never lost faith that this capture would occur. We knew that it was just a matter of time.\par \par \uc2 MAN: House Speaker Kevin McCarthy is defending his decision to open an impeachment inquiry against President Biden.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): To be able to get the answers to those questions, you are going to have to subpoena certain documents.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  met with North Korea`s Kim Jong Un amid speculation that Kim could sell Moscow weapons and ammunition to use against the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news: the new ruling from a New Mexico judge on the governor`s temporary ban on carrying a firearm.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ALLEN (Bernalillo County, New Mexico, Sheriff): The Bernalillo County Sheriff`s Office will not enforce this segment of the order.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Devastation in Libya. Flooding has killed thousands of people, leaving entire communities destroyed.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Part two of our in-depth interview with presidential hopeful \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , tonight`s focus, on the issues, like the economy, abortion rights, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Do you believe that voters will elect someone who is facing 91 criminal counts?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Song of the year is Taylor Swift!\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Taylor Swift sweeps at the VMAs.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT (Musician): This means the world to me. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: With `N Sync in sync once again.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Wednesday night. We begin tonight with the dramatic end of a two-week manhunt for an escaped killer in Pennsylvania. Fugitive Danelo Cavalcante was captured early this morning after a DEA plane picked up his heat signal.\par \par \uc2 We are just getting this new mug shot of the man police found with a rifle in his possession. It was a police dog who bit Cavalcante`s scalp and ultimately latched onto his thigh, likely putting him in excruciating pain. He was found in a wooded area about 20 miles from the prison where he broke out.\par \par \uc2 The massive manhunt involved 500 members of law enforcement working around the clock, including officials with the State Police, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , ATF, \uc2 U.S. Marshals Service\uc2 , and the Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Dementri will start us off tonight with new details on how it all came together from Avondale, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nikki.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: State Police say, once in motion, the capture of Danelo Cavalcante today happened within five minutes.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, he is in custody at SCI Phoenix, a maximum-security prison northwest of Philadelphia, to serve a life sentence for first-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PENNSYLVANIA STATE POLICE: The search for Danelo Cavalcante is over. The subject is now in custody.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI (voiceover): Now police are revealing how the massive search came to a dramatic end.\par \par \uc2 DEB RYAN (District Attorney, Chester County, Pennsylvania): Our nightmare is finally over, and the good guys won.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Police say a burglar alarm went off within their search parameter just after midnight. About an hour later, an aircraft using thermal technology picked up a signal.\par \par \uc2 And just before 8:00 this morning, tactical units surrounded Cavalcante as he tried to escape with a stolen rifle. But law enforcement released a canine to hold him.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. GEORGE BIVENS (\uc2 Pennsylvania State Police\uc2 ): He continued to resist, but was forcibly taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Cavalcante was seen wearing a \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  football sweatshirt, his face bloodied from a dog bite injury to his scalp.\par \par \uc2 JIM MARTIN (Pennsylvania Resident): Out of nowhere, everybody started congregating back by the shed.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Jim Martin says he witnessed Cavalcante`s capture from outside the farm equipment store where he works.\par \par \uc2 JIM MARTIN: One camouflaged trooper had his gun, and then they were walking him up, and then they loaded him up.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Law enforcement posed for photos with Cavalcante shortly after the apprehension.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. GEORGE BIVENS: I am not bothered at all by the fact that they took a photograph with him in custody. Again, they are proud of their work.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: The search for Cavalcante ended about 20 miles from the prison where he escaped after being spotted multiple times in different parts of Southeastern Pennsylvania, leaving residents on edge.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN BRADY (Pennsylvania Resident): It was a scary couple of days.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Now Kathleen Brady says her community can finally relax.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN BRADY: Everyone can just exhale finally.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: State Police say Cavalcante was cooperative while being interviewed. His preliminary hearing is scheduled for two weeks from today -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikki Dementri, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Some big news here in Washington, with Utah Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  announcing he won`t seek reelection. One of his reasons? A dysfunctional Congress. And it comes on the same day Republicans are moving forward with their impeachment inquiry against President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` congressional correspondent, Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): House Republicans huddled behind closed doors Wednesday, ready to move full speed ahead with their impeachment inquiry against President Biden unilaterally authorized by Speaker Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 How do you justify this impeachment inquiry without holding a full House vote?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Oh, easily, because \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  changed the rules and the precedent.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Despite saying earlier this month that he would hold a vote, the speaker pointed to former Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s decision four years ago to announce an impeachment inquiry into then-President Trump. It took five weeks until the House voted --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from October 31, 2019)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): The resolution is adopted.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: -- prompting the Trump Department of Justice to declare that impeachment inquiries are invalid unless the chamber takes a formal vote.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TED LIEU (D-California): This is completely impeachment theater. There is no additional power that the Republican Congress has.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The chair overseeing the probe into the business dealings of Mr. Biden`s son and associates countered.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES COMER (R-Kentucky): We are following the money, and we will see where that leads us.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The White House continued to insist there is no evidence of wrongdoing by the president.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): This is a political stunt.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Utah Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , who announced he is retiring next year, said his party needs to move on and the impeachment inquiry lacks merit.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITT ROMNEY (R-Utah): I haven`t heard any allegation of something that would rise to the level of high crime or misdemeanor.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Republican senator and former presidential nominee said the Trump wing of the party is too hung up on resentment and settling scores.\par \par \uc2 Romney is 76, and he said his age was a factor in his decision not to seek reelection and believes it`s time for a new generation of leaders -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, that is big news.\par \par \uc2 All right, Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have now got some breaking news on Hurricane Lee. Portions of New England are now under tropical storm and hurricane watches as the Category 2 storm approaches. Lee is weakening as it heads into cooler waters, but it will bring at least tropical-storm-force winds to New England starting Friday night and dangerous waves and rip currents up and down the East Coast from South Carolina to Maine.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas now to a rare meeting today between two nuclear-armed adversaries of the U.S., North Korean dictator \uc2 Kim Jong Un\uc2  and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  spending more than four hours together in far east Russia, discussing military cooperation.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin reports from the Pentagon, where there is growing concern about this rogue state alliance.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN (voiceover): Kim Jong Un rode his armored mystery train into Russia to meet \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . But there was no mystery about the message the two dictators were sending.\par \par \uc2 "This is our new Cosmodrome," Putin told Kim. And the sight of them touring Russia`s newest space launch facility looked like a down payment on a Russian promise to help North Korea develop more powerful missiles that could threaten the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Probably not by coincidence, North Korea launched two short-range missiles just before the visit began. Kim was all ears, asking detailed questions about the diameter of a rocket. The U.S. was all ears, too.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (NSC Coordinator For Strategic Communications): Any arrangement that would improve North Korea`s military capabilities would be of -- certainly would be of significant concern to us.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Putin, of course, will want something in return. And what he needs most is help to keep firing artillery in Ukraine at the rate of 10 million rounds a year.\par \par \uc2 Joint Chiefs Chairman General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  told CBS News, Russia can`t keep that rate up.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2  (Chairman, Joint Chiefs of Staff): Their consumption rates have gone down a lot because their industry is not producing the amounts that they were, you know, previously in years gone by.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: What does North Korea have that Russia wants?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2  (Chairman, Joint Chiefs of Staff): Well, munitions, the same calibers and types of munitions that the Russians use, or the same types that the North Koreans use.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Whatever the details of an arms deal, \uc2 Kim Jong Un\uc2  has now clearly thrown in with Russia, promising Putin full and unconditional support for his -- quote -- "sacred fight" against the West -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: David Martin at the Pentagon tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there`s a new ruling just in from a federal judge. He says the New Mexico governor is violating constitutional rights with a new ban on the right to publicly carry a firearm in New Mexico`s largest city, Albuquerque.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lana Zak reports on the backlash from both parties.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK (voiceover): In New Mexico, gun culture runs deep, so a temporary ban on where to carry them was bound to cause an uproar.\par \par \uc2 MARK ABRAMSON (Gun Shop Owner): People are angry.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Mark Abramson is a gun shop owner.\par \par \uc2 MARK ABRAMSON: I cannot fathom how this is anything other than an attention-getter.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: The judge`s ruling halts Governor Michelle Lujan Grisham`s 30- day suspension barring private citizens from carrying firearms on public property in Albuquerque and Bernalillo County, where violent crime rates are especially high.\par \par \uc2 She cited a public health emergency after three children were fatally shot recently, including 11-year-old Froylan Villegas, who was killed outside a ballpark last week.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D-New Mexico): I have to take a tough direct stand, or basically I`m just ignoring the fact that we lost an 11- year-old, another child.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Nationally, firearms are the leading cause of death for children and teens.\par \par \uc2 But the county`s Democratic sheriff, John Allen, feels that the governor`s act was unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ALLEN (Bernalillo County, New Mexico, Sheriff): There is not enough law enforcement to go around and enforce this order when we`re supposed to be going after criminals on the street and not after law-abiding citizens.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: So what are the solutions?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ALLEN: The solutions is multifaceted. You have red flag laws. You implement with your senators and your representatives other laws that they think might help bring down gun violence.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: The Catholic archbishop for the area has counseled families ravaged by gun violence. He supports the temporary measure.\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP JOHN WESTER (Archbishop of Santa Fe): We have to be courageous. We have to do something about this terrible status quo that we`re living with in terms of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Norah, this divide doesn`t fall along traditional party lines. Many of the most public critics were Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with the governor`s office just moments ago. They plan to be back here in court on October 3rd. The governor said she will never stop fighting -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s a really interesting story, Lana. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , Bill Gates, and \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  were together today in one room to discuss the future of artificial intelligence, both the dangers and the benefits.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent reports, lawmakers are struggling to keep up with the technology.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): Today, a rare show of force by tech tycoons on Capitol Hill, tackling the growing threat of artificial intelligence to American jobs, safety, and, according to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , our very existence.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (CEO, \uc2 Tesla Motors\uc2 ): I think this meeting may get down in history as being very important for the future of civilization.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Musk, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , Bill Gates, and other top CEOs met behind closed doors with a bipartisan group of senators facing the reality that, if no action is taken, A.I. is expected to eliminate or disrupt 300 million jobs worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Musk, the richest man in the world, says he told senators he`s in favor of having a specific regulator for A.I.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK: It`s important for us to have a referee. I think the consequences of A.I. going wrong are severe. So we have to be proactive, rather than reactive.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Congress is sufficiently ready to regulate A.I.?\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK: No, no, not yet. No, the -- the -- the sequence of events will not be jumping in at the deep end and -- and making rules.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Another top priority today was managing A.I.`s rapid development.\par \par \uc2 Can Americans trust \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  to make A.I. safe?\par \par \uc2 SAM ALTMAN (CEO, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 ): I think trust, but verify. I think it`s really important that the government does put sensible policy in place.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Now, along with that safety, senators tell me another major focus was the race against China.\par \par \uc2 They want U.S. tech companies to set the global standards before China does. But lawmakers also want the tech companies to develop their A.I. responsibly -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What an extraordinary meeting.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you up there asking those tough questions, Jo Ling Kent. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to part two of our America Decides conversation with presidential hopeful \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Florida governor is currently in second place in national polls. But he`s a distant runner-up to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Tonight, we wanted to talk to him about the issues that matter most to American voters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida) (Presidential Candidate): Iowans don`t want the campaign to be about the past.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (voiceover): \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is toughening his attacks on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out on the campaign trail, making the case voters want to look forward.\par \par \uc2 If it`s not about the past, do you believe that voters will elect someone who is facing 91 criminal counts?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: So I didn`t think, even before all this, that -- that the president should -- former president should have run again. I mean, I think that there`s just certain -- there`s too many voters who he`s a deal-breaker for them.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: I mean, Nikki Haley says Americans won`t vote for a convicted criminal. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: I think the chance of getting elected president after being convicted of a felony is as close to zero as you can get.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Let`s talk about some of the issues that matter most to voters. How does a President DeSantis get inflation down?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: Stop spending so much money. We need a president that`s going to be a force for spending restraint, because that`s one of the root causes, with Congress spending so much.\par \par \uc2 Two, open up domestic energy production. Energy costs are a huge part of inflation.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: On that issue of spending, the issue is that, in fact, Republicans, and under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he added $8 trillion to the debt.\par \par \uc2 And as Nikki Haley pointed out during the debate, Republicans have earmarked more in spending bills than Democrats do. Do Republicans have a spending problem?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: Yes, of course. This is Republicans and Democrats in D.C. that have done this. This is not just one political party. Republicans talk big when they`re out of power, but, when they get in, they don`t put their money where their mouth is.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, you`re right. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  added almost $8 trillion to the debt in four years. He ran saying he was going to eliminate the national debt, maybe over an eight-year period, but he did make that promise. And then he did the opposite.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So, that`s a broken promise?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: Well, of course it is.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There is new data out that the number of abortions in Florida has actually increased, and increased since Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 For those who oppose abortion rights, is it time to enact a national ban on abortion?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: Well, here -- the issue with Florida is that the Southeastern states have very, very strong pro-life laws. Florida is litigating under a 15-week.\par \par \uc2 And so we have become, against our wishes, a destination. Now, we have also done the Heartbeat Protection Act, which would go into effect once the courts clear this, which I think would probably stop the whole abortion tourism thing. So we don`t want to be an abortion tourism destination.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Why won`t you answer that question?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: About why you would support a federal ban?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: I support -- I support pro-life policies. I will be a pro-life president. But, at the same time, you know, I have got to chart the course and be honest with people about, OK, how do you advance the ball like we did in Florida?\par \par \uc2 And the way you do that is really bottom up.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We asked the governor whether the penalties mentioned in Florida`s six-week ban would include women.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: We have no criminal penalty. The penalties are for the physician. I mean, a lot of these women are --\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Governor, I read the bill. It says just this. It does include jail time and fines for -- quote -- "any person who willfully performs or actively participates in a termination of pregnancy."\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: Right. And that`s for the providers. That is not for the women.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Is a woman not actively participating in the termination of her pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: No, because she -- she`s not a medical practitioner. So there are no women --\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So you are not for criminalizing women?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS: No, no, absolutely not. And that will not happen in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, there were so many important issues to cover with the governor.\par \par \uc2 We asked him about the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  warning that Florida is openly hostile for African-Americans, people of color, and LGBTQ individuals. His answer is on our CBSNews.com Web site.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, heart-wrenching new images in the aftermath of Libya`s historic floods and a staggering estimate from the mayor of a devastated city.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The mayor of a flood-ravaged city along the Mediterranean Sea in Libya said today 18,000 to 20,000 people could be dead in the wake of Monday`s historic floods.\par \par \uc2 Drone video shows the widespread damage after two major dams collapsed when 16 inches of rain drenched parts of the region on Sunday. Entire neighborhoods were destroyed, leaving tens of thousands without homes.\par \par \uc2 The wife of a notorious drug lord is released from federal custody. We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The wife of the infamous Mexican drug lord known as El Chapo was released from federal custody today.\par \par \uc2 She served nearly two years in a Texas prison and a California halfway house for helping to run his multibillion-dollar cartel. The former teen beauty queen will now serve four years of supervised release. El Chapo, who twice escaped from prison, is serving a life sentence at a Supermax prison in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Swift makes history at the \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards. We will have the details next.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: If you can`t watch the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, you can listen, subscribe wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The era of Taylor Swift continues, following last night`s \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards)\par \par \uc2 The 33-year-old megastar took home a record-tying nine VMA trophies, including the top prize, video of the year, for her smash hit "Anti-Hero."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT (Musician): I just want to say that the fact that this is a fan-voted award means so much to me.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, Swift, who is in the middle of her groundbreaking Eras Tour, now has 23, surpassing Madonna for second place on the all-time list. Only Beyonce has more, with 30.\par \par \uc2 It may be hard to find a blank space in Swift`s trophy case, but we know all too well those are champagne problems to have.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS 