uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Search teams work around the clock in Morocco where the death toll has soared from Friday`s devastating earthquake.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Many of these homes are made out of mud bricks, they crumble and they don`t leave any air pockets for survivors.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In eastern Libya, at least 2,000 people are dead after a massive flood tore through parts of the country. Thousands more remain missing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: North Korea`s Kim Jong-un is in Russia as \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  seeks more weapons to use against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-IL): What they`re going to supply aren`t necessarily the best artillery shells in the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: An incredible rescue as an American explorer is pulled from a cave in Turkey after being trapped underground for more than a week.\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY (American explorer): It is amazing to be above ground again.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A Pennsylvania killer who escaped from prison nearly two weeks ago has been spotted again and police say he is armed.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 NBA\uc2  player, Kevin Porter is charged with beating his girlfriend, a former WNBA player.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Aaron Rodgers injured in his Jets debut just minutes into the game.\par \par \uc2 MAN: An ankle injury, which the team believes is a torn Achilles.\par \par \uc2 PEYTON MANNING (Manning Cast): I know he`s got some things he can use, some -- there`s got to be some magic potion he can put on that thing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The game would go to overtime, and then fireworks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Starts to his left, gets to the 40, turns the corner, at the 45 victory. Brock is in front at the 40, at the Bills` 30, inside the 20. He is on his steps, to the 10, to the five. Touchdown. It`s a Jets touchdown. This game is over.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was over. Let`s hope it`s not over for Aaron Rodgers today, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was thinking how he must be feeling today. Hay-yay-yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, he is feeling like Jets fans are feeling, pretty upset, but they got the win.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They got the win.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No Rodgers no problem. That young fella right there, Gibson said, I`ll handle it, just let me touch the ball. He scored a touchdown in overtime.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does magic potion work on it a torn Achilles?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know. I don`t use that type of medicine, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t know. No. No, no.\par \par \uc2 We are all about answering the big questions today and we`ve got some fantastic guests, including Oprah. There`s only one of her. Her new book is about what it really means to be happy and how to live that way every single day. She seems to do that. She wrote it with a Harvard professor, Arthur C. Brooks is his name. They are both here today. They will share science of happiness lessons with all of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus astrophysicist, Neil deGrasse Tyson is also here to help unpack mysteries of space. His new book has a title, straight from the movies, "To Infinity and Beyond." And if you ever wondered about big questions like can humans actually live on Mars? Well, he`s got the answers for you in just a bit, so stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like Times Square.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to stay right here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re good right here?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mars or Times Square, that`s our choices.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sometimes it looks like Mars out there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Brooklyn for me. That`s true.\par \par \uc2 All right, but we`re going to begin with the breaking news from Pennsylvania and it`s not great. Police say, escaped prisoner Danelo Cavalcante, a convicted killer already dangerous is now armed and dangerous. He`s got a rifle.\par \par \uc2 He was spotted early this morning in a place called saw South Coventry Township, that`s in Chester County and before that, he was seen in a neighborhood, Glenmoore, nearly nine miles away. How he got from point A to point B, they`re not sure.\par \par \uc2 People are being told, lock your doors, lock your cars and be on alert. We`re going to go once again to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Philadelphia`s, Brandon Goldner who has got the latest on what is now a 13-day man hunt. They`re hoping it`s lucky 13 if you`re law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON GOLDNER: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Police activity in South Coventry Township has intensified overnight. We`ve seen many officers wearing tactical gear and holding long rifles as they lock down the area in pursuit of Danelo Cavalcante who police say is now armed with a gun.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BRANDON GOLDNER: On Monday night, a reverse 911 call by a \uc2 Pennsylvania State Police\uc2  gave the residents in South Coventry township outside of Philadelphia an urgent update on the whereabouts of escaped inmate, Danelo Cavalcante. Police warned neighbors he was armed and in any area and to remain inside.\par \par \uc2 The development came after numerous residents reported shots were fired earlier in the area. According to police, a resident reported a rifle was stolen during a confrontation with Cavalcante in their home. It`s unclear who fired the shots or if the suspect was struck.\par \par \uc2 According to sources, police also found a green shirt and shoes similar to what he was last wearing in recent surveillance footage. Now police are combing through the area looking for footprints that may belong to Cavalcante, the escaped