ans they`re very difficult to reach. The death toll has now risen since Friday to more than 2,800 people and that number is likely to go up, but all hope is not lost, they say.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay is in the hard-hit part of Morocco with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 Chris, if I was still searching for someone, I would still have hope. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good morning from high in the Atlas Mountains, the area that`s been most devastated by this earthquake. Just take a look at these mud brick dwellings crumbling under the weight of that initial impact, not to mention the aftershocks. We felt one just moments ago and that village over there on the hills where 48 people were killed by the initial quake.\par \par \uc2 Now, take a look at this, in order to get them any help, vehicles need to come up this road, the only main highway for getting people help in this area, a one-lane highway prone to traffic jams, rock slides, things that could mean the difference between life and death.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Time is ticking, but rescuers are digging in the dirt rescuing their own sisters, wives, and children, and holding back the sand in the hourglass.\par \par \uc2 (MOHAMMED URCHAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "I heard my sister screaming, brother, brother, save us." I rescued her and her son and her husband says Mohammed Urchan (sp?). We used our bare hands because we didn`t have tools.\par \par \uc2 But such scenes of joy more common shortly after the quake are growing increasingly rare. It`s now day four since the quake and only now are rescuers able to reach the most remote victims. The crucial golden period, the best window for finding survivors who might still be struggling to survive beneath the rubble is now closed, and many victims could not be saved.\par \par \uc2 Here in the high Atlas Mountains, the epicenter of the quake, these jagged cliffs, serpentine passages, and rustic dwellings are just as awe-inspiring as they are lethal.\par \par \uc2 Many of these homes are made out of mud bricks, so they don`t just collapse, they crumble and they don`t leave any air pockets for survivors who tend to just choke to death on the dust and if there was anyone to save, locals tell us they had to save themselves.\par \par \uc2 (MOHAMMED AITRI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "The government didn`t come. We didn`t see anyone after the earthquake. They only came to count the number of victims, since then, no one is here with us," says Mohammed Aitri (sp?).\par \par \uc2 So Moroccans are helping each other like here at the blood bank in Marrakech lining up for hours in the sun, but drop by drop, they too our rescue workers.\par \par \uc2 Why are you donating blood?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Actually because of what`s happened, we feel so sorry. So we`d like to help because the people who are injured now, the Moroccan citizens, and I am one of them, so it is a must for all the Moroccans to do the same thing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Now, Morocco has been delivering help of its own -- medicine, food, water. We`ve seen helicopters flying overhead, government vehicles driving by, but we get the sense at this point it`s mostly aid for the living. At this point, so late in the game people tell us it`s not very likely that they`re going to find survivors -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yet, the hope still continues. Thank you very much, Chris. Chris Livesay reporting from Morocco.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are ways for you to help the victims of this earthquake. Visit CBSNews.com for a list of organizations you can donate to.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to more flooding here in this country. Areas around Boston were hammered with as much as nine inches of rain in just about four hours last night. Emergency crews were called in to rescue people from their vehicles, others were trapped inside their homes as raging waters rose, flooding yards, washing away manhole covers and sweeping away parts of roads.\par \par \uc2 And the area could see more flooding this weekend from Hurricane Lee which remains a Category 3 storm and is headed north.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking Lee for us.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The entire East Coast needs to be on alert this week for Hurricane Lee as it could bring dangerous surf and rip currents, and if you live on the Cape of Massachusetts or Maine, keep a close eye on Lee as we head into the weekend as you are now in the cone.\par \par \uc2 The exact positioning of the storm and level of impacts we`ll see in the US will be determined in part by a high pressure off to the east and a trough off to the west. If Lee ends up farther west, we could have stronger winds, heavier rain, larger swells and coastal flooding. If it slides more east, the impacts will be less intense, but we`ll still see moderate to large waves and also rip currents.\par \par \uc2 Another player will be the size of the storm. Watch this wind field expand as it goes up the East Coast. Look at that. That could also not only bring stronger winds for us, but make for higher surge and coastal impacts.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, it`s not just all about landfall, you can have tropical storm and hurricane force winds extend out one to 200 miles from the center.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: North Korean dictator, Kim Jong-un has arrived in Russia`s far east this morning by armored train, there for a meeting with President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . They`re expected to talk about supplying arms to Russia for its war in Ukraine, and the way these things work, Kim will likely want a few things in return.\par \