ickey said he was on the edge of death.\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY: I kept throwing up blood and then my consciousness started to like get harder to hold on to and I reached the point that I was like, I`m not going to live me.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: A multinational rescue effort kicked in, nearly 200 volunteers and medics flew in bringing down blood and fluids.\par \par \uc2 Teams from Europe and Turkey were each assigned stages to help Dickie rise further faster. Three thousand feet up, taller than two Empire State Buildings.\par \par \uc2 Most of the time cocooned on a stretcher, hooked up to an IV and with a medic always at his side. Navigating waterfalls, near freezing cold and narrow passages, some widened with explosions.\par \par \uc2 Then just after midnight, Dickey`s journey from the depths of the earth came to its end.\par \par \uc2 MARK DICKEY: It`s been one hell of a crazy, crazy adventure, but on the surface safely, I`m still alive.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: But for now, he is grateful to be back above ground.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Ramy Inocencio at the Morca cave, Turkey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, the injury that threatens Aaron Rodgers` new career with the Jets, but first, it is 7:16, it is time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead updated COVID vaccines are now FDA approved. We`ll show you how soon they could be available and who should get them. That`s the big question.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 New York Jets\uc2  started their new season last night and they and their fans couldn`t have been more pumped. They were playing an emotional home opener on the night of 9/11 and their new quarterback superstar, Aaron Rodgers led the team onto the field for the first time. Now, they won but lost, because just a few minutes later, 