l going to say a prayer and I`m still going to hold out hope, but my heart is with Aaron right now --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s tough for Aaron Rodgers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because this whole city was so excited to see what he could do with his team.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we were.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I`ll just say this, Jets fans, enjoy the victory. You got the W.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s what matters most.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on, Aaron. Pulling for you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, Oprah will be here to talk about the art and science of getting happier.\par \par \uc2 Your local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s 7:30. CBS MORNINGS and \uc2 Google\uc2  is going on trial today in a lawsuit that could change the way big tech companies do business. That`s because the Justice Department and more than a dozen states accused the company of illegally limiting your online search options. That of course is something \uc2 Google\uc2  strongly denies.\par \par \uc2 Senior Business and tech correspondent, Jo Ling Kent is at the courthouse in Washington for us, joining us for the very first time on CBS MORNINGS, we are very happy to say that.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Hey, Tony, thank you and good morning.\par \par \uc2 This is the most important lawsuit against the US tech giant since the government sued \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  25 years ago. Now, this court battle, it really matters because essentially it comes down to the way you and millions of people are searching the internet on your smartphone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: \uc2 Google\uc2  is my best friend. I love \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Yes --\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: \uc2 Google\uc2  is your best friend?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Why?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I mean, because --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It`s everything you do.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Literally we`re walking right now trying to find a pizza place, and I`m Googling it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: When you search for something online, a lot of people call it the same thing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I \uc2 Google\uc2  weekly.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m Googling everything.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: How often are you Googling stuff on your phone?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Like 30 times, like every day.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: But now, that popularity and how it came to be has landed \uc2 Google\uc2  the company in federal court.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department and more than a dozen states are taking on the tech giant in a landmark antitrust trial beginning today. The government claims \uc2 Google\uc2  illegally pays billions of dollars to \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Samsung\uc2 , \uc2 LG\uc2  and beyond to make \uc2 Google\uc2  the default search engine on your phone and computers shutting out rivals like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Bing and \uc2 DuckDuckGo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This, the government says allows \uc2 Google\uc2  to dominate nearly 90 percent of all searches in the US and 95 percent of searches on mobile devices.\par \par \uc2 Will you ever change?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: To where?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The government also says \uc2 Google\uc2  uses that dominance to charge advertisers higher prices and favors advertising on its own platform and steers advertiser spending towards itself. \uc2 Google\uc2  denies any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 Is \uc2 Google\uc2  breaking the law here?\par \par \uc2 KENT WALKER\uc2  (Chief legal officer): No, we`re not. We believe that people use \uc2 Google\uc2  because it`s helpful. The case comes down to two important principles competition and choice.\par \par \uc2 Competition, you`ve never seen more choice whether that`s TikTok or Reddit or \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or \uc2 Expedia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: \uc2 Google\uc2 `s chief legal officer, \uc2 Kent Walker\uc2  also says, if a user doesn`t want to use \uc2 Google\uc2  as the default, they can change it.\par \par \uc2 KENT WALKER\uc2 : It really couldn`t be easier. You can sit at home on your couch and change what search engine you`re using. It`s as easy as changing your shoes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It`s as easy as changing your shoes?\par \par \uc2 KENT WALKER\uc2 : Well, we see lots of people doing it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Is it easy?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Definitely not. It`s definitely not easy. I feel like here, you probably have to go -- see, I don`t even know where it is.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: And for small companies trying to compete for your business online, critics of \uc2 Google\uc2  say it`s nearly impossible.\par \par \uc2 STACY MITCHELL (Institute for Local Self-Reliance co-executive director): \uc2 Google\uc2 `s dominance means that a lot of innovators that might come along in the tech space, you know, really can`t get a toehold. And if we, you know, take action against these tech monopolies, really insist that they -- you know that they cannot be these dominant gatekeepers, we open up the market, and we`re going to see all kinds of innovations and new developments in online.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Now, this obviously could change the way that small businesses can compete with the giant, \uc2 Google\uc2 , and this trial here is expected to last about 10 weeks. If \uc2 Google\uc2  loses, it could result in fees or damages, or even a potential restructuring of the company. And if any of this sounds familiar, you guys, you have to remember that \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  antitrust lawsuit was in 1998. And it turns out, it was the same exact year, a tiny little startup called \uc2 Google\uc2  was officially founded in Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a coinky-dink.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What a coinky-dink. You know, Jo Ling, \uc2 Google\uc2  has got a pretty good response to this lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, they say people are choosing \uc2 Google\uc2  and one of their data points on that shows that on Bing, the \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  competitor, the number one thing searched, \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That tells you everything you need to know on some level.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Jo Ling, welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`re so happy to have you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, lucky us.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Oh, thank you guys. I am such a fan of this show and it is an honor to be a part of this fantastic team. I`m just glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you to the family.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now you can figure out how is it when you`re using \uc2 Google\uc2  that stuff that you`ve just talked about with your friends all of a sudden just pops up on your phone.\par \par \uc2 Tell me, Jo Ling what that happens?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: I am on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, all right. We will see you later.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: I got you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, bye-bye. Welcome, welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Opening up Pandora`s Box right there, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will have a special segment on conspiracy theories later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We don`t have enough time for that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Neil deGrasse Tyson is coming on later, we`ll ask him. All right --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Aliens though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Updated COVID vaccines could be available as soon as this month, we`ll talk with Dr. Celine Gounder about who should get the shots and the protection that they offer.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The CDC is expected to approve updated COVID vaccines today at a time when COVID cases -- have you heard -- those numbers, they are going up, but concern about the virus, that`s going down. It`s not good.\par \par \uc2 In a new CBS News poll, just 45 percent of people say they were worried that they or someone in their family could get COVID. Now that`s down from 77 percent in April of 2020 and only 43 percent said they plan to get the new booster and you can see these numbers do vary, that`s according to people`s political affiliation.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder is a CBS News medical contributor and editor-at-large at KFF Health News and joins us.\par \par \uc2 This is not a political issue for many people, we just want to be healthy. So when you hear these numbers, and you hear the numbers, people are still reluctant to get it, even though they know COVID cases are going up. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I don`t think this is just a partisan question. I think a lot of people are just over COVID.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: They`re tired of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Which I think is understandable, but there are certain groups that remain at increased risk over everyone else for hospitalization and death. Those folks absolutely should be going out to get the updated COVID vaccine and that includes people over 65, people who are immunocompromised, those who are living in nursing homes, and two other groups of people we may not think about, pregnant women and infants.\par \par \uc2 And what all these groups have in common is they have weaker immune systems, and so are at higher risk for complications.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But why do you think this vaccine in particular is important for us to get?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This particular COVID vaccine, it`s updated for the variants that have been circulating this year. It`s not perfectly matched the variants that are out there right now, but it`s pretty close, and what we`re seeing from antibody levels and testing, this should provide robust protection against the currently circulating strains.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Will people have to pay for this shot this time around?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: If you have insurance, whether that`s private insurance, Medicare or Medicaid, you should not have any out of pocket cost to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Now, if you don`t have insurance, the Biden administration has set up what they`re calling a Bridge Access Program, where you will still be able to get vaccinated for free, but that won`t start until mid-October or so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of people stockpiled COVID tests in the era of like, you`ve got to take them before you go into this and take them before you go into that and show them and take a picture blah-blah-blah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, I`ve got 20 at home.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I have a pack.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I`ve got 20 at home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I bet you, if you read the back of the box, it says expired, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have not yet. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you use an expired test if someone wants one?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. So the FDA has extended expiration dates on some of the tests. So the best way to figure that out because it does vary by brand, is just go online and search for FDA at-home COVID test expiration date and then you`ll come up to a page where you can look up the specific brand that you have at home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And when are the vaccines coming out? \uc2 Moderna\uc2  and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  are both going to be releasing these updated vaccines. Does it matter to you which one you get?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It really doesn`t matter. I would view these as interchangeable right now. The CDC is anticipated to make some recommendations later today, which really paves the way for a rollout and we think that within a few days, vaccines will be available to everyone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. I cannot wait to get mine and I`m scared of needles but I don`t want to get COVID again.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder, also you were in in Morocco when the earthquake hit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were not close to anything, but still it`s got to be very unsettling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very scary, yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. I was in Marrakech and you could definitely feel it, but you know, as tourists you`re not staying in those mud brick buildings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So you know, you`re a lot safer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are glad you are home and safe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Glad you`re okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 To find out if your COVID test is good past its expiration date, we`ve made it very easy for you. Go to our website, CBSMornings.com for a link to the FDA chart.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up next, Vlad will have the stories that you`ll be talking about today. But first it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Vlad Duthiers is at the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But there was a little bit of uncertainty about whether you were going to make it on time. What happened this morning?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jon Tower is telling on you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad will be late, he said.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I still use wake up -- I still use my good old fashion analogue watch and I was in LA this weekend and it was still set to LA time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh you wound it to LA time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, I actually looked up and I was like, wow, it`s getting a little bright. I looked at my watch, I`m like, ah, I`ve still got another hour to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And clearly I did not have another hour to go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you made it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But I am here. Like - -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why you should get the \uc2 Apple\uc2  Watch because it just changes time automatically.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, see, I know. But can`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never lets you down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, for the look, for sure, but you know, digital does work for waking up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I guess, it does.\par \par \uc2 Catch me tomorrow here at this time, 7:47, every morning, hopefully.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s begin with tonight`s \uc2 MTV\uc2  Video Music Awards that will make history because for the very first time, the Artist of the Year category features all female nominees including Doja Cat, Karol G., Nicki Minaj, Shakira, Beyonce, and Taylor Swift who both broke records this summer with their tours.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, you know about that. Gayle was practically at every single Beyonce and Taylor Swift concerts this summer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Summer of concert for Gayle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This year`s Video \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  Award will celebrate multi- award winner, Shakira, featuring a special performance from the Colombian artist. We should mention \uc2 MTV\uc2  is part of our parent company, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The VMA`s air tonight at 8:00 PM. Eastern on \uc2 MTV\uc2 , and they are also streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 And you know, there`s always something that happens at the VMAs, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They always like to have a little surprise that`s quite provocative.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. And I don`t know if it`s always planned, but there was that time that Lady Gaga wore the meat dress. There was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Madonna and Britney.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Madonna and Britney with the kiss heard around the world. And of course, when Beyonce came out with the baby bump.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? That was cool, and my favorite is I`m going to let you finish, but Beyonce had the greatest album of all time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was that at the MTV`s?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ye.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was Kanye West.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But was that -- I thought that was Grammys? Was that was the \uc2 MTV\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No. It was the -- I`m going to let you finish.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was the \uc2 MTV\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa. Okay. I don`t think that one was planned.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, no that was not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That wasn`t on the schedule of events.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tune in is the point that we`re trying to make here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Some of you might have missed some of the big interviews we had yesterday that were preempted by a 9/11 coverage, so we want to give you another chance to see them. Starting with \uc2 University of Colorado\uc2  football coach Deion "Primetime" Sanders.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Pro Football Hall of Famer has led the team to two big wins after the Buffaloes went one and 11 last season. So we asked Sanders about his role in the remarkable turnaround.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you see that other people don`t -- didn`t? Because this is extraordinary what you`ve done.\par \par \uc2 DEION SANDERS (\uc2 University of Colorado\uc2  football coach): Well, I know what I want, and I know how to go get it and I am going to do it unapologetically.\par \par \uc2 I`m not here to run for office. I`m not here to kiss the babies and all of that, although, I love kids. I`m here to win and I`m here to be dominant.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dominant.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dominant. That he is, and Tony, you asked Coach Prime about his star quarterback son borrowing one of his signature moves.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He did his own version, you could say of the Deion shuffle. What do you think of his moves? Not bad, I`ve got to say.\par \par \uc2 DEION SANDERS: It was hot garbage. That was horrible. Like I have five wonderful kids, and my youngest daughter, she is the only one that could dance in the family.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Man, put him on blast like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, he also criticized himself there. He`s saying, look, even Deion can`t really do the Deion shuffle, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We can move, but we can`t dance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very true. Neither can I.\par \par \uc2 All right, we also had an exclusive interview with Novak Djokovic, who stopped by our studio, fresh off his US Open victory. The 36-year-old earn his 24th Grand Slam singles title, but it was this very sweet moment in the stands with his daughter that captured our hearts.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from USTA/\uc2 ESPN\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here`s how he described it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NOVAK DJOKOVIC (US Open Men`s Singles champion): I was just thankful it was -- it was over. And the first thing I wanted to do after I exchanged words with him at the net is to hug my daughter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I saw that. We love that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I saw that as well.\par \par \uc2 NOVAK DJOKOVIC: She was seated courtside.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did she inspire you during that time?\par \par \uc2 NOVAK DJOKOVIC: She did. She`s my guardian angel. She`s my baby. She`s my sweetheart.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t know that she was going to be seated there. We faced each other as I was sitting on the bench, and she was there. And every single time I would need some encouragement, she would be there. She would give me a fist bump. She`ll give me a smile and it would melt me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It melted all of us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He also told us that his dream was to live long enough that his kids would be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Play long enough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Play long enough is a better way to say it, you`re right, Nate -- play long enough that his kids would be old enough to understand what he did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To understand --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you have a life. I mean, the reason you see the tears in his eyes, he was telling us in the greenroom just before he appeared that this is somebody who when he was his kid`s age was on line to get bread for his family at 5:00 AM.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very rough upbringing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, this is Serbia in the 90s, a war-torn country and to go from the bread line to center court to the US Open as a champion is huge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Called it his dream here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: From the bread line to the bread winner, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, Oprah and Harvard professor, Arthur C Brooks --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do you do that, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Will show us how to enjoy a happier life.\par \par \uc2 Stick around we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A simple mind --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil. That`s Nate Burleson. We`re all here.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Oprah is also here in the studio today. She`ll be joined by the co-author of her brand new book, Arthur C. Brooks, it`s called "Build the Life You Want: Art and Science of Getting Happier."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL:` And speaking of happiness for, "The Dish," I spoke to an award-winning chef who says British food is actually much better than its reputation. I tried some of his work and I`ve got to say, he`s got a point.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve also got Neil deGrasse Tyson in studio to help explain the mysteries of space. His new book is "To Infinity and Beyond."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour with Oprah. There is only one. In her new book, she shares her wisdom about the secrets to greater happiness.\par \par \uc2 According to a recent survey -- listen to this number -- only 25 percent of Americans say they are very happy. What? To find out what that means to be happy, Oprah collaborated with Arthur C. Brooks, a Harvard professor who studies and teaches the science of happiness. I want to know what the C stands for \uc2 Arthur Brooks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Their book is called "Build the Life You Want: The Art and Science of Getting Happier." In it, they share practical advice on how to change your life for the better.\par \par \uc2 Oprah and Arthur C. Brooks -- what does the C stand for?\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS ("Build the Life You Want" co-author): The C stands for Charles. I don`t know why I put it in there, but there you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Well, it stands out. So I want to start with you, Arthur, because people would think you must be a naturally happy person.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in your book, I was surprised to see that, nope, you say normally you can be gloomy and anxious. And that even your wife said after all of this research, maybe you need to follow some of your own research.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Yes. I study happiness.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought that was a funny.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: I know. She`s pretty funny. My wife is a naturally very happy person, but the truth is that I study happiness because I want to be a happier person.\par \par \uc2 I became a social scientist to learn about myself. It`s me search rather than me search research. And that was a really important thing. Then over the past 30 years, I haven`t cracked the code entirely, but Oprah and I have put together a handbook, an owner`s manual for emotional self- management. It`s worked for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember when Oprah first discovered you, Arthur, you want to tell that story?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY ("Build the Life You Want" co-author): Well, it was during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And you know, I didn`t leave home to 322 days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And so I was reading "The Atlantic," and reading his column and being inspired and fortified by every week, every Thursday, there was another drop and I had a conversation with him. And afterwards, I thought, wow, this is the kind of guy that if I was still doing the show, we would be calling him every day to be an expert on every subject.\par \par \uc2 Then I invited him over for dinner, best dinner guest ever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll bet.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Because you can probe his brain on happiness. And I thought, you know, we should do something together, and so now we are.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So only 25 percent of Americans say they`re very happy.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But I mean, the premise of the book is that happiness doesn`t just happen.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s something you`ve got to work on, right?\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: That`s right. That`s right. Most people think that -- the biggest mistake people make about happiness is they think it`s a feeling and they are waiting for the feeling to kind of come to them, like a like a dove coming to sit on your shoulder. It doesn`t work that way at all.\par \par \uc2 People have feelings of happiness, but that`s evidence of happiness, you need to work on it. It`s a combination of enjoyment, satisfaction, and purpose, and all of those things we can understand. And we can work on and make it a project and that`s what we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I first started getting really interested in the subject itself of happiness, because at the end of every show, I would sit with the audience, and I would have conversations with them. It was my own focus group for 25 years.\par \par \uc2 And I would always ask people: What do you really want? What do you really want? And people would always say: I just want to be happy. And then I would say, what does that look like?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You take it one step further and then they couldn`t answer.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: What does it look like, and most people could not answer it.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Right.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : You know, it`s an audience made up of mostly women, many times they would say, well, you know, I want my kids to be happy. Okay, so now your kids are out of the house, then what? What does it look like for you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Oprah, let me ask you this. What does happiness look like for you now versus let`s say 30 years ago? I mean, me search did you do while collaborating on this project?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Well, happiness for me now is more a level of contentment and peace of mind and satisfaction, coming from purpose and meaning that I have in my life.\par \par \uc2 So it used to be doing, doing, doing, doing, doing. It used to be a schedule that I was just saying to some somebody this morning -- a schedule that if there was a space in the day it would be filled.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: If there was an hour --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because you want it be productive.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes. It must be productive and always working.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like what you say, though, because people, you get that statistics and it shows that happiness is down, so what do we do? And there`s a thing in the book, Arthur, where you say you can`t change the world, but you can change how you experience it. So how can we be happier?\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: This is super important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Because a lot of people think that I can`t be -- Oprah and I talk about this in the book a lot -- I can`t be happy until something changes, I can`t be happy to my marriage is better, I can`t be happy until something is resolved in my health.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I can`t be happy until I lose weight.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Until my finances sort themselves out. People say this all the time. And the whole point is, you can be happier, no matter what`s going on in your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How? How?\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: And the way to do is to understand the components of happiness and to work on them, and then to put together a portfolio.\par \par \uc2 Look, in this book, we have this, this happiness 401(k). We have the things that you need to be investing in every single day -- your faith, your family, your real friends, not your deal friends, and everybody knows the difference between the two.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: And dedicating your work no matter what you`re doing, offering it up as service to other people. We walk step by step about what the science says, about how to understand this point, how to practice these things, how to teach them to other people.\par \par \uc2 So this is a step by step approach using science and real stories from real people`s lives, by the way, including our lives in this book.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you say, happiness is not a destination, it is a direction.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And that`s why we called it the art and science of being happier, not just being happy.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happier.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s an interesting passage in the book, Oprah where you talk about a period in your life when you were literally on trial, not a criminal trial, it was a lawsuit, you said something about hamburgers, the beef producers were suing you. And it was a six-week trial. It`s exhausting. It`s demanding. There are stakes involved. And yet, you write, you had reason to be happy. How do you find that reason?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Well, one of the things that, you know, when I`m going through any trial, is being able to do -- I was doing this when I didn`t even know it was called metacognition. He talks about metacognition in the book where you can separate the circumstances of what`s going on from what is really going on in here.\par \par \uc2 You can feel the feeling and let the space between the feeling and you allow you to be objective about that`s happening out there and I am here, so that metacognition, which is really thinking about the thinking is really helpful. And for me, it always goes back to gratitude.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gratitude. Oprah, I want to shift gears a little bit and turn to the Hawaii wildfires and efforts to help those in need. The People`s Fund of Maui was created by you and Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson, in which you both poured time effort and millions to kick it off yourself, yet and still, it faced online criticism.\par \par \uc2 I want to give you the opportunity to kind of give your thoughts on that.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Well, this is a really great point about being happier in the midst of an onslaught of being terrorized and vilified online.\par \par \uc2 I will say this, I came out of this experience -- I was just saying this to Arthur this morning -- with so much more compassion for young kids, because I was thinking, what if I didn`t really know who I am? What if I didn`t have the assurance of Invictus, that I am the master of my own fate and the captain of my soul.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It would have taken you down.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: It would have taken -- it will take you out. So all the online, you know, being --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Slander and attacks.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Attacks, lies, conspiracy theories, really took the focus off of what was the most important thing and that was the people of Maui. So this idea came about because, as you know, when you were there, Tony, I was on the ground talking to lots of people trying to figure out how do I best help?\par \par \uc2 And in the beginning, it was just, you know, material things, dropping off generators and towels. And then I started talking to people. People really wanted their own agency.\par \par \uc2 Gayle`s son, Will, sent me an article about Dolly Parton. Dolly Parton in 2017 during the wildfires in Gatlinburg started a people`s fund, she called it My People`s Fund. She had a concert, she raised $12.5 million, and then directly gave it to the people, a thousand a month.\par \par \uc2 I thought, whoa, that`s the idea. So --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Getting the money into the hands of the people.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Directly into the hands of the people, so to set up the infrastructure for that, The Rock and his team and my team we were on the - - literally on, I don`t know how many Zooms, to set up the infrastructure for the verification process to be able to drop money into people`s accounts.\par \par \uc2 And we thought because both of us have given to charities our whole life, I`ve been giving to charity since my first paycheck, a tither.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And still do that. Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I still do that. Okay. So we thought, starting the fund with $10 million would be great idea because any of us who`ve ever been to any benefit, you know you go to a benefit and somebody gives $10 million, that`s called a good night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 So we thought $10 million would start off the fund. We`ll do what Dolly did, get other people to give money, and then we`ll put it directly into the people`s bank accounts.\par \par \uc2 I was so excited. I was so excited about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s still a good idea.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it still should be.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And then I got up the next morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : I was so excited. I got up the next morning and I saw all of this vitriol and I was like, whoa, what happened here? So this is what I want to say. I want to say that, you know, I think in the beginning, so many people were calling and asking, where do we give our money? So I thought, I`m going to give people a place to do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll create that fund.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: We`re going to create something, and I still think it`s a really strong idea.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Dolly Parton was right. Putting money directly into the hands of the people is a significant thing and the model that`s been created, I will say that as of today, 2,200 people had been cleared and verified and those people are going to get a notice from The People`s Fund of Maui and going to have the money going into their bank account.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is working. It made me deeply mad. It made you deeply sad.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: It made me sad that we are at this state in our country, you know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I am wondering how you are coping with that right now?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Right. Everything -- everything leads to this kind of polarization.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Even serving other people, but here`s the polling. Here`s Oprah`s point, if you take your time, your money, your resources, your life, and you give the love that other people need, especially in their times of need, that is the secret to happiness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Happiness is love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happiness is love.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happiness is love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Arthur.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Full stop.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR C. BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Full stop.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Happiness is love. Full stop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Arthur. Thank you, Oprah.\par \par \uc2 Oprah said she wore blue today, Arthur, to match the cover of the book. All right.\par \par \uc2 "Build the Life You Want" is on sale now wherever you like to buy your books, but first, it`s 8:11. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, we`ll talk with "Yellowstone`s" on and off screen chef, Gabriel "Gator" Guilbeau about his new cookbook. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Only on CBS MORNINGS, we like when we can say that, the "Great Food and Wine" Magazine is revealing its annual list of America`s best new chefs.\par \par \uc2 This year`s lineup, this is a national competition, more than eight states around the country and one of them is New York right here in our own backyard, Ed Szymanski.\par \par \uc2 For our series "The Dish," Szymanski is proving at his New York restaurant, Dame and Lords, that modern British cuisine is much more than pot roast.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, my God. This is amazing. Oh, it is so good. It`s really good.\par \par \uc2 Oh my gosh. If you don`t mind, I`d like a moment alone with the French pie.\par \par \uc2 Oh my gosh. It`s delicious.\par \par \uc2 I`m no professional food critic.\par \par \uc2 That is [bleep] amazing. Wow.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI (Chef): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But trust me when I say Ed Szymanski`s cooking is as one actual professional food critic put it, glorious.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: That`s the best part of doing this really. It`s the watching of the satisfaction on customers` faces.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I heard very little of that because of the crunch.\par \par \uc2 As a baby face 30-year-old, Szymanski is head chef of two bustling New York City restaurant.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: May I have a spoon, please?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Place which he runs with his wife, Patricia.\par \par \uc2 One is a casually high end seafood spot called Dame.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: This is a grilled oyster with a green chartreuse hollandaise on top.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: Enjoy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And a short walk away, the other, a larger more meat focus bistro called Lords.\par \par \uc2 Neither spot takes itself too seriously, and neither does Szymanski preferring a t-shirt to a chef`s coat and on his feet, well --\par \par \uc2 They`re like nights slippers or something.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: Yes. Most people who have reached chefdom tend to buy slightly more expensive ones, but humble boy at heart, I guess, they do the job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That job is wowing Americans with food from one of the world`s less appreciated culinary traditions.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: I already get laughed out of the room when I tell people that the hottest new restaurants in New York are British restaurants. I think British food can be incredibly delicious.\par \par \uc2 Britain is a very small island, but one that`s influenced by a lot of different cultures over centuries.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What began with fish and chips --\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: And the fish floats instantly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Has now moved into edgier English classics including a Scotch egg with curry spice lamb.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: It satisfies some of those medieval cravings like the caveman I like fat and acid. I like salt. I like a little spice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is so good. That is lights out.\par \par \uc2 And a full pig`s head, breaded and fried.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s really delicious.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: It is a moral burden on us if we choose to eat meat, to eat every part of the animal and not to throw the other parts in the trash, but that`s only one side of it. The other side is that it`s actually really delicious.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m a little bit confused about your path into the kitchen because you were not meant to be a chef, do I have that right?\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: That`s true. I was the weird kid who decided I wanted to be an investment banker. It`s like, that`s what I want to do in my life at 11 years old, but I pursued it pretty doggedly for four years and realized, after setting foot in an asset management firm, maybe this isn`t for me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Instead, a volunteer job at a restaurant changed his life.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: I stepped into that kitchen and the personalities and the food and smells. And I thought that the restaurant was the coolest place to be.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re going to be a Best New Chef Food and Wine honoree.\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: When I found out the news I was shocked. I didn`t think that`s something that would ever happen to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For more than three decades, "Food and Wine" has published an annual list of best new chefs described as the most exciting emerging culinary talent in America.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ed`s take on modern British food is really fresh, very seasonal, it`s doing something that nobody else in the country is doing and if we think about the history of Best New Chefs, that first class was, Thomas Keller, Daniel Boulud, Rick Bayless, Nancy Silverton, David Chang -- these are the chefs who have helped shape cuisine in America over the past 34 years and this new class will do the same over the next couple of decades.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This year`s awards put a particular focus on great chefs, who are also cultivating kindness in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 In the kitchen, you have a sign that says "Be positive." Where does that come from? How do you maintain it?\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: You know, a lot of it comes from experience and making mistakes of my own and seeing how one is perceived when they raise their voice or do silly things or yell, and I want to set the example from the top down that you need to be very understanding and supportive of your staff.\par \par \uc2 It seems so silly once you see how great it can be if everyone is supportive of each other.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you calling Gordon Ramsay silly and could you speak into this camera right here?\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: I think Gordon is a terrible representative of kitchen culture. I mean that kitchen culture, if it didn`t die 20 years ago, it should be dead now and buried way underground. Like there`s no place for that in this day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But if I had a $20 million check for you to stand in front of a British flag with your arms crossed and a vein popping in your neck, you will take it?\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: $30 million, I would take it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For now, Szymanski is thinking about his next new restaurant, while also hoping the magic never ends.\par \par \uc2 How do you deal with the doubt?\par \par \uc2 ED SZYMANSKI: With these kinds of awards tell a lot. Seeing the customers respond positively to the food. I would give it all away if it meant I had a busy restaurant every night packed with people who are excited to eat the food. That`s what I really care about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is the goal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like him, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The British food he cooks is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Food and Wines" list of Best New Chefs is lights out great. It changes lives. I`m seeing it tonight. We`re going to talk more this week about all of the people on that list.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re emceeing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I am.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am nervous. I like chefs, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, no. That`ll be -- I do, too.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Neil deGrasse Tyson is here talking about his new book "To Infinity and Beyond."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love this song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Neil deGrasse Tyson has spent his life trying to understand the mysteries of science and now, he is tackling some big questions about space in his new book "To Infinity and Beyond: A Journey of Cosmic Discovery."\par \par \uc2 Some of those questions are: Should we try to live on Mars?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And will we find life -- Gayle says no --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or will we find life on planets outside of our solar system?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eventually.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tyson is also director of the Hayden Planetarium here in New York and the host of the podcast "Star Talk." Now, he wrote this book with his senior podcast producer, Lyndsey Nix Walker.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing, sir?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON ("To Infinity and Beyond" co-author): Doing good. Thanks for -- thanks for having me back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: I love what you`ve done with the place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, ,yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We want to put this up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little cosmic feng shui.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Feng shui. We got them with you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So in your book, you dig into what Hollywood gets right and what it gets wrong. You write the "Incredible Hulk" might be the most scientifically implausible superhero ever created. How so?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: You`re going to make me talk smack about the Hulk?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You wrote it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you sit and watch movies and just pick stuff apart?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: No, not while I`m watching it, only after when I`ve compiled my --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After you`re like, that`s so unrealistic.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: No, no. When Hulk was just a cartoon character, let it be anything you want. But when they start making live action movies?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: So the Hulk is whatever is the mass of David Banner.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Okay. Then he has something huge.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Incredible Hulk.")\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Yes, there he goes. So, either at that size, he has the density of a beach ball, because you have -- he thins out to become that or he somehow accumulates mass by converting the energy of the city around him into particles, like equals MC squared. But if you do that, there`d be no energy left in the county, okay, or beyond.\par \par \uc2 And so this this interplay of matter and energy could not happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t we just enjoy the movie? Can`t we just enjoy the movie?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what you`re saying those pills they advertise on TV at 3:00 AM won`t turn me into the Hulk?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Oh, yes, no, you have to eat -- yes, no. That`s the answer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay. No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The thing about you -- oh, I`m sorry. Go on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, go ahead, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, go, go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I was going to just say you also -- you also throw some shade on "The Dark Side of the Moon" album title from Pink Floyd 1973. Is there no dark side?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: It`s -- oh, man. I`ve been working to undo that for 50 years. Okay, 1973, this is the 50th anniversary of "The Dark Side of the Moon." There is no dark side of the moon.\par \par \uc2 No, both sides of the moon get sunlight. A day on the moon lasts a month, but people think there`s a dark side of moon because of that album and I`ve been working on it ever since.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, and if you listen on headphones --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you seeing anybody for this? Are you seeking treatment?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: You`ve got somebody you can interview. So if you listen with headphones to that song "The Dark Side of the Moon" at the very end, it says "There is no dark side of the moon." And I said, oh, so it is just poetic, but then it continues, "It`s all dark." That`s how that song actually ends, so that got worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the lead, we talked to you about it, should we try to live on Mars? A, should we? and B, would you want to?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Yes, so I wouldn`t mind visiting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: You put a little amusement park there, I`ll go. So it`s nine months there and you`ve got to wait until the orbits line up to come back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So is it possible, do you think?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Definitely. But to want to live there permanently? Like why? Earth is -- well, for now Earth is fine to live on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: But you want to terraform Mars so that you don`t need a spacesuit walking off your spaceship.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Okay? Because people analogize exploring Mars with like the explorers of the 16th century.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: The difference is, those explorers come to the new world, they can breathe the air when they get off their ship. There are human beings greeting them, handing them food.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: There is no counterpart to that on Mars. So you`ve got to terraform Mars. And people worry that we want to do that because you might be trashing Earth, and we need Earth 2.0.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They don`t want a Planet B.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you like this for fun, Neil? Was this -- you and Lyndsey - - was this -- you just wanted to do what?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you did pop culture and humor.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Thank you. That`s important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did you do it?\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Yes, so that strands of the podcast. The Star Talk podcast is science, pop culture, and humor. It`s our DNA. We have a three strand DNA.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: So throughout the book, it`s an ascent from Earth to the edge of the universe. And it explores how human beings thought about those problems and then tried to accomplish them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: If you`re staying here on Earth and there`s the moon and it`s a thousand years ago, 500 years ago, how would you get to the moon?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Just think about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I never have.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: You never have. No, no, but some people have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: And so when you do that, you have to invent new kinds of science.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: New ambitions -- new -- and sometimes it has to wait for technology to catch up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Or vice versa. So this book, it`s a journey of mind from caves to conquering our understanding of the origin of the universe, and you`re going to learn about the fits and starts along the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Neil deGrasse Tyson, I appreciate you, just like last time you were here, we`re going to have some commercial conversations in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, for sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So don`t go away. We need to talk about aliens --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the Loch Ness monster.\par \par \uc2 NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON: Uh-oh. Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, "To Infinity and Beyond" is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got lots of options of where you want to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you believe in the Loch Ness monster?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up next, the chef feeding the stars of "Yellowstone" on screen and off the set. His name, got a very cool name, Chef Gator Guilbeau. There he is. Hi Gator.\par \par \uc2 I like him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hi Gator.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hi, Gator. He will tell us about his new "Yellowstone" cookbook with recipes inspired by this huge monster show, but first, it`s 8:35, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m very excited because our next guest is a chef for the cast and crew of "Yellowstone" and he also plays a chef on the show from time to time.\par 