 cast and the crew in such extreme conditions and in such remote locations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you didn`t even know it was "Yellowstone." They just told you it was some Western movie.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: Not a clue. All they told me is that there was a cowboy that was in charge that was very hungry, and perhaps a little bit agitated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That would Taylor Sheridan, yes.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: And that I had to get out there and come feed the cowboy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you guys clicked and then so you end up actually on the show. I think that`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No acting -- I think it`s such a great backstory here.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: Well, I told him right away I said you know I`m not an actor, right? He goes, yes, just go ahead. Do it. Go do Gator stuff. I`ve been doing Gator stuff for years now. So it seems to be working out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is working out great.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: And it is coming out great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now when you talk about in your book your Gator stuff, I just fell in love with you in this book. You said you`ve got Cajun heritage, but sometimes on the set what you like to create is a gator vibe. You like good music, some coffee. Maybe a steak and a good story to tell? What does the gator vibe look like? What do you do?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: If you`re visiting the "Yellowstone" movie set, you are going to make the first stop at my kitchen, 100 percent or you`re - - maybe the wrong person invited you, but that is going to be your first stop. It is a good way to get you in the zone for what we`re all about at the ranch and I think I`m an integral part of being on "Yellowstone."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, "Yellowstone" is --\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: You`ve got to go eat some gator food before you go in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, the Gator vibe.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: Have a coffee, you know, maybe have some more, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gator vibe. I like it.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: It is an important place to start.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure. The show is wildly successful --\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I love that "Yellowstone" is leaving no crumbs -- pun intended -- on the table or success.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: They are not.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But this isn`t easy, to write a book. What was the best part of maybe the most difficult part of writing this book?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: It was incredibly difficult to transfer stuff that I`ve been doing for years at home and for television productions and film productions that I just did by sense, I did by taste, and touch, and I knew that -- I knew the recipes in my head.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, you`re kind of old school with it.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: Very much so and to actually literally put pen to paper and come up with measurements and portions and that was very difficult for me because it was something that I`d never done before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: I was never a recipe guy and I had them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: I had them here, but not here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Gator, the recipes --\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: It was quite a lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The potato corn chowder. I want to lick that page. Ribs fried bread, I want to lick that page.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL "GATOR" GUILBEAU: You know what? I think we need to cook all of these recipes for you at your house is what we need to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \u