.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, that`s good. Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, you have something else to say?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Please, tell me, you also don`t pack this neatly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want to say I love you and thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Your favorite morning show. Yes, I said it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230912ej9c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is Interviewed about China, North Korea and Russia; General Frank McKenzie is Interviewed about Afghanistan; A Look at Power Competition in the World.\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Ed O`Keefe <Guest: Kamala Harris, Frank McKenzie, David Sanger, Josh Rogin, French  Hill \par \uc2 8,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: a network exclusive with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , as top administration officials travel the world to shore up alliances.\par \par \uc2 Fall kicks off with a week of in-person international diplomacy, as President Biden gathers with top foreign leaders in India. Secretary of State Blinken visits with President Zelenskyy in Ukraine, and Vice President Harris travels to Southeast Asia to confer with allies about the growing threat from China.\par \par \uc2 Looming large in all those discussions, three who weren`t there. We met up with the vice president in Jakarta, Indonesia, and talked about her efforts in the region, plus the issues she`s taking on as V.P., abortion rights, root causes of migration and why she thinks Republicans are honing in on her.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you respond to those attacks? That`s not a policy. That`s about you.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): They feel the need to attack because they`re scared.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Plus, do Americans think there should be maximum age limits for politicians? Our new poll has some interesting findings.\par \par \uc2 Then, 22 years after 9/11 and two years following the chaotic withdrawal of U.S. forces from Afghanistan, we will talk with the former head of Central Command General Frank McKenzie McKenzie.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas Republican French Hill joins us to talk about national security and what`s on the congressional agenda, as House members head back to Washington for the fall session.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 The reports and the pictures coming in from Morocco, where an earthquake hit Friday, are stunning. More than 2,000 are dead. And that number is expected to climb, as rescuers desperately try to find survivors in the rubble. The quake is the strongest to hit a North African country in 120 years. And the \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2  put it at a 6.8 magnitude.\par \par \uc2 In other foreign news, President Biden met with leaders of the G20. Those are the 20 largest economies in the world. Notably missing, Russia`s \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and China`s Xi Jinping. We will have more on the president`s trip later in the broadcast.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has also been conferring with allies. We met up with her in Indonesia late last week for the ASEAN Summit, a network of nations in Southeast Asia. While we were there, our polling unit surveyed Americans about the job the vice president is doing back home. Her approval rating tracks similarly to President Biden`s at 41 percent.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have been focusing a lot of their attacks on Harris, highlighting that she would be in line for the job of president should anything happen to President Biden. And it is Republicans who say they have heard a lot about her work, more so than Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Our conversation in Jakarta allowed us the opportunity to ask about her work and a lot more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you`re 58 now. If you win a second term, as you and the president are running to do, he would be 86 at the end of it.\par \par \uc2 And we are seeing Republican candidates hone in on you, in particular, as being next up for that job. Nikki Haley says:\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R-Presidential Candidate): A vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a vote for a President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE (R-New Jersey) (Presidential Candidate): I want to be clear that I pray every night for Joe Biden`s good health, not only because he`s our president, but because of who our vice president is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida) (Presidential Candidate): Harris is his impeachment insurance. People know, if she were president, Katy bar the door, as bad as Biden did, it would get worse.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you respond to all of that?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We`re delivering for the American people.\par \par \uc2 And the reality of it is that, unfortunately, very few of those who would challenge our administration actually have a plan for America. You look at what we have accomplished, Margaret. We have created over 800,000 new manufacturing jobs in America, 13 million new jobs, unemployment at record lows.\par \par \uc2 We have capped the cost of insulin for seniors at $35 a month, capped the cost of prescription drugs on an annual basis at $2,000.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`re talking about...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: ... for seniors.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... what you would do as president as being a risk. They`re honing in on you.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think that is? How do you respond to those attacks? That`s not about policy. That`s about you.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Listen, this is not new. There`s nothing new about that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, listen, I am -- in my career, I was a career prosecutor. I was the first woman elected district attorney of San Francisco, a major city in this country, and reelected. I was the first woman attorney general of the second largest Department of Justice in the United States and reelected.\par \par \uc2 I was a United States senator. I represented one in eight Americans, and I`m now vice president of the United States. They feel the need to attack because they`re scared that we will win, based on the merit of the work that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I and our administration has done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But even Democrats are worried about the president`s age.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" had a poll showing two-thirds of Democrats say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is too old to run again. Are you prepared to be commander in chief?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes I am, if necessary. But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be fine.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you something. I work with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  every day. Under Joe Biden`s leadership, we have transformed and are in the process of transforming America`s infrastructure with a historic investment in, not only roads and bridges, but high-speed Internet, what we are doing around issues like lead pipes. And I could go on and on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you taking the threat of a second Trump presidency seriously enough?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I don`t understand the question.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were dismissive of some of the Republican criticism of you and the president.\par \par \uc2 When you look at current polling, the front-runner for the Republican nomination is the former president, the 45th president.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We will win reelection.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you feel -- you will win?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We will win. We will win reelection. There is too much at stake, and the American people know it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In terms of delivering on promises, you in your portfolio have tackled the issue of the root cause of migration.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Patrol saw a record number of migrant families cross the border in the month of August, this despite that record level of heat. Why is that happening now? You`ve rolled out new policies.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Around the globe, we are seeing an increase in irregular migration. And there are a number of factors at play, an increase in authoritarian regimes, what we`re seeing in terms of extreme weather occurrences, what we`re seeing in terms of, in many places around the globe, increasing food insecurity.\par \par \uc2 So there are many reasons for why this is happening. And -- and America is -- is not immune to that. The point has to be, then, to understand what we must do to deal with, one, ensuring that we have a secure, humane and orderly policy about the border, but also what we must do in the long term to address the root causes of migration.\par \par \uc2 The work that I have been doing in that regard has been to build public- private partnerships, to the extent that we have now raised over $4 billion to invest in the countries in that region.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that migration data, according to CBP, is showing migrants from Guatemala are up, Honduras, Ecuador, Peru.\par \par \uc2 So, when the border crossings went down earlier in the summer, the administration said it was due to your policies working. Now they`re going back up as they did in the month of August. Does that show the strategy is no longer working?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 What it means is that we have to stay focused on a number of issues related to the irregular migration that, again, we`re seeing around the world. And America is not immune. You know, my philos...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you think the strategy is working, despite the numbers being up?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Overall, we are seeing progress, but there is -- we`re not going to have a constant -- there are going to be fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 That is normal, just like the weather fluctuates and -- and circumstances fluctuate, such as elections in those regions and what that might mean. It doesn`t mean that we keep our foot off the gas. We have to stay focused and understand there has to be a long-term strategy, as well as a short-term strategy.\par \par \uc2 And here`s -- here`s the bottom...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, was it too early to say that the strategy was working when the numbers went down for two months?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: My point is that focusing on root causes...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: ... and doing the work that we have been doing to bring U.S. investment into those countries in a way that is supportive of their economies and supportive of fighting corruption actually works.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have been very active on the issue of abortion and rallying behind the idea of this federal law to restore what was upheld in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But, to do that, Democrats need 60 votes in the Senate, they need a majority in the House they don`t have, they need the presidency. The math doesn`t add up. So don`t you need to level with the American people and say this is not a realistic promise to make for 2024?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Congress has the ability to put back in place the rights that the Supreme Court took from the women of America.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In theory. It doesn`t have the votes to do that.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: But that`s why we have elections. And that`s why we are seeing around this country that, when this issue is on the ballot, from Kansas to California, people vote in favor of upholding basic freedoms.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of Americans agree that the government should not be making this decision for the women of America.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is it that you believe? I mean, what week of pregnancy should abortion access be cut off?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We need to restore the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which was...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We`re not trying to do something new.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that was nebulous, because it was about viability, which could be anywhere between 20 to 24 weeks. And...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: But it -- so, no, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 Let`s -- let me be very clear...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s -- that was in the Women`s Health Protection Act that the White House also endorsed.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Let me be -- let me be very clear.\par \par \uc2 From day one, the president has been clear. I have been clear. We need to put back the protections that are in Roe v. Wade into law. Since the Supreme Court took it, Congress has the power and ability to pass legislation to put those protections back in law, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will sign that bill. So, that is what we want.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But does it need to be specific in terms of defining where that guarantee goes up to and where it does not, at which week of pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We need to put back in place the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know why I`m asking you this question though, because ...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: But we`re not trying to -- but we`re not trying to do anything that did not exist before June of last year. We are saying...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, it wasn`t crafted into law. And that`s why I`m asking you for the specifics there...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : But it...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... because Republicans say the lack of a precise date in cutting it off -- you know this -- is, they say that allows Democrats to perform abortions up until birth.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Which is ridiculous, which is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which is statistically not accurate.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And it`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I understand that...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And it`s a mischaracterization of the point.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: No, the point is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But -- but do you need to be more precise?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The president is, we have to -- I am being precise.\par \par \uc2 We need to put into law the protections of Roe v. Wade. And that is about going back to where we were before the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But if -- if there is the possibility through legislation to provide any kind of guarantee at the federal level, any kind of protection, like the Republican proposal, 15 weeks protection...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The Republicans are also...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... is it worth doing something?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: But the Republicans -- member -- members of the Republican Party are also proposing a national ban.\par \par \uc2 The thing that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m talking about the Lindsey Graham bill, as you know...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... and -- and those proposals of giving some access, though that`s 15 weeks. Why do you think it shouldn`t be specific?\par \par \uc2 You know, out in -- out in New Mexico, for example, the governor there says it shouldn`t be nailed down to a week, because it should be a private matter between a woman and her doctor. There shouldn`t be a precise number put on that.\par \par \uc2 Is that what you believe as well?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I believe that we should put the protections of Roe v. Wade into law, and the way that will happen is if we have a \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  who, regardless of their personal view for themselves or their family, would agree that the women of America should be trusted to make decisions about their life and their body, based on what they know to be in their best interest.\par \par \uc2 It`s that simple.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will have more of our interview with the vice president in our next half-hour.\par \par \uc2 We will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Arkansas Republican Congressman French Hill, who joins us from Little Rock.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL (R-Arkansas): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Lawmakers are coming back here to Washington this week.\par \par \uc2 And they have just 11 working days left to fund the government before September 30. Speaker McCarthy has already said it looks like we`re headed towards a short-term patch, versus actually passing all these appropriation bills. Are you confident we can avoid a shutdown?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: I`m hopeful we can avoid a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 And the number one thing that I think House Republicans need to do -- and I have certainly urged all my colleagues on this point -- Margaret, is get the other 11 bills that we have not passed during the summer across the House floor. We`ve only passed one, and the Senate`s passed 12.\par \par \uc2 So, that gives them a distinct advantage over the House in the negotiation for 2024 spending details. And if we want to merit that and have the right kind of negotiation that House conservatives want, then we need to come together and pass those 11 bills as soon as possible, including using a brief continuing resolution, if necessary, in order to get that work done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the right flank of your party opposes that short- term, that continuing resolution that you just mentioned there.\par \par \uc2 Are we going to be looking here at another scenario where, despite having a majority, Republicans will need Democrats to get this over the finish line?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: But, you know, this is what`s -- I think it is -- it is frustrating.\par \par \uc2 We don`t control the Senate. We don`t control the White House. But what we do control is our own appropriations process. And Chairman \uc2 Kay Granger\uc2  has written more conservative bills and more conservative funding levels than the Senate, more conservative than the debt ceiling deal that President Biden and Speaker McCarthy agreed to. So that`s good.\par \par \uc2 But to have that negotiating clout, we need to get all those bills passed across the House floor.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: A government shutdown is not going to improve that situation. And a long-term continuing resolution only institutionalizes last year`s \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  priorities.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think House conservatives want that to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you talking about short term just taking us to December or so?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: No, short term -- to me, short term is shorter than that.\par \par \uc2 I think I would certainly support something in the October time frame to give the House the time it needs to complete -- complete these other 11 bills. They`ve come through committee.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: People have their amendments ready. We just need floor time to debate them and pass them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that`s a lot of work to get done in a short window. And it`s not clear that Republican leadership has control of all parts of your party, as I mentioned.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  said she will not vote to fund the government unless "We have passed an impeachment inquiry on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Is that a tactic leadership is considering?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: I don`t have insight on that. I would doubt that, because I don`t think Jamie Comer, the chairman of the Oversight Committee, or Jim Jordan, the Chairman of the Judiciary Committee, think that`s a good idea.\par \par \uc2 I don`t believe they`ve even remotely completed their work on the kind of detailed investigations and quality work that Speaker McCarthy is expecting both those committees to produce before someone goes to an impeachment activity.\par \par \uc2 We don`t want to repeat the mistakes we think that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  made by prematurely moving to impeachment during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, would -- OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, the other part of the right wing of your caucus that`s raising concerns is specific to the portion of aid that President Biden is requesting to help Ukraine, a supplemental request, $44 billion, 24 for Ukraine, $16 billion for natural disasters, $4 billion to fight the flow of fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to swallow those numbers? How significant are you going to trim them?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Well, look, I think a majority of House Republicans would prefer that those core spending items, not so much the FEMA emergency numbers, but the other numbers, be handled through the existing budget and appropriations process. And that`s going to cause concern for a lot of House Republicans, not just conservatives, in the House conference.\par \par \uc2 We`d like to see that incorporated in the budgets that we`re already negotiating now. I personally support ejecting Putin from Ukraine. I support funding for Ukraine. I just introduced this week the Ukraine Reconstruction Act, which converts Russian assets to pay for the war and pay for reconstruction of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, this is something, a battle that we need to win. And we`re going to work out how best to do that in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, another battlefield where Russia has been active is in Syria.\par \par \uc2 I know you just paid a visit there, the first time a lawmaker visited since 2017, when \uc2 John McCain\uc2  went. I`m wondering why you did and what it is you`re asking U.S. allies to do.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: Well, I wanted to see for myself the death and destruction caused by 12 years of Assad`s brutality against his own people.\par \par \uc2 He said: Keep me in office, or I will destroy the country.\par \par \uc2 And he`s certainly done that. Just last week, Russian aircraft flew 41 sorties, killing some 40 people in Syria, including 15 children. So, I went across the border with my colleagues Ben Cline from Virginia and Scott Fitzgerald from Wisconsin, and we met with orphans that had been orphaned at the Wisdom House orphanage, which is supported by wonderful, generous donors here in Little Rock, Arkansas, to see pictures of their kid -- their parents killed by Assad.\par \par \uc2 What we need is for the whole world to have a political solution to Syria. And let`s recognize that this is the first battleground that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  began killing innocent civilians several years before he went into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 These are -- this is a failure of the national and global leadership. And we need to continue to take action to find a political solution and cut off Assad`s funding, cut off his ability to have a drug trade throughout the world and get help to the people in Northwest Syria that -- that are victims of the February earthquakes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman, there is a lot to unpack on Syria with you, and I hope to do that again another time.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE FRENCH HILL: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We also surveyed Americans about another big issue in politics these days, age and the ability to serve effectively over the age of 75.\par \par \uc2 More than three-quarters of Americans, no matter what their political affiliation is, favor maximum age limits for elected officials. Just over half say the job of president or senator is too demanding for someone over the age of 75, although more than a third of those surveyed said it depends.\par \par \uc2 Although 68 percent of Americans say age brings experience, that is outweighed by the approximately eight in 10 Americans who are concerned that elected officials might be out of touch or unable to do the job past the age of 75.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot more about our poll on our Web site at CBSNews.com.\par \par \uc2 We will be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to senior White House and political correspondent Ed O`Keefe, who is traveling with the president in Hanoi, Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Ed, this is a quick trip, just 24 hours. But it`s part of President Biden`s strategy to counter a rising China.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s right, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 The president met this evening with the top leaders of Vietnam, who are seeking what they call a comprehensive strategic partnership now with the United States, a relationship that would put the U.S. on par with China and Russia, and that White House officials hope means, eventually, Vietnam will end up buying even more U.S. military equipment, as the Biden administration seeks to counter China in this region.\par \par \uc2 The stop here in Hanoi comes after the president made a visit to the G20 summit in India on Saturday. There was a lot of conversation about topics of concern for the president, climate change, economic development, and infrastructure spending.\par \par \uc2 There was also a joint statement from the leaders of the G20 that expressed general concern about the suffering of the people of Ukraine, but no stronger denunciation of the war there. That`s in part because, of course, Russia and China are members of the G20, and India is officially neutral on the war.\par \par \uc2 Still, the White House is pleased that there was any mention at all of the situation in Ukraine. And the visit comes amid new CBS News polling that shows tough criticism for the president when it comes to foreign policy. Fifty percent of Americans believe he is making the United States weaker around the world, 57 percent believe he`s being too easy on China, and seven in 10 Americans have a pessimistic view on the prospects for world peace.\par \par \uc2 The visit here in Vietnam will end Monday with a stop at the memorial to the site where former Senator \uc2 John McCain\uc2  was shot down over the skies of Vietnam. And on his way home to Washington on 9/11, he will commemorate the terrorist attacks with a stop and a visit with troops in Alaska -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ed O`Keefe with the president in Hanoi, Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Although it was not surprising that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  would skip the G-20 Summit, Chinese President Xi Jinping`s absence was noted, both there and at the ASEAN Summit. President Biden told reporters today in Hanoi that he was concerned China was, quote, changing the rules of the game, and that he met with Chinese Premier Li Qiang, who did attend both meetings.\par \par \uc2 Here`s more of our conversation with the vice president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: China`s rewriting the global order. Your national security strategy says they are the only power in the world with the intent and the ability to do that.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: There`s no question that we are very concerned about the Chinese government`s actions on the South China Sea and how that is impacting the security and the future prosperity of the nations that are affected.\par \par \uc2 For example, I was recently in the Philippines. And I`ve been spending a lot of time with President Marcos. The -- what is happening in terms of unprovoked actions against the Philippine interests in the South China Sea is significant. And we have been very clear that we stand with the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`re a treaty alley.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: They are. Absolutely. Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The U.S. will have to militarily defend them.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Not only is there a treaty relationship, but the very basic issue that the American people will experience and feel if we`re not - if we`re not on top of it is what that will mean in terms of commerce flowing freely through those waters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How concerned are the leaders you spoke with here about the potential for a military clash?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think the issue that is most present here among the southeast Asian leaders is that there be a respect for and enforcement of international rules and norms. But they hope that there will be an avoidance of conflict. And that there is a great symmetry between that and our policy toward China. We do not invite conflict, but we absolutely are prepared to and engaged in what is necessary to compete.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So when President Biden said the other day that China was a ticking time bomb, he was talking about their current economic problems that they are experiencing. He said, that`s not good because when bad folks have problems they do bad things.\par \par \uc2 What is the scenario that you are thinking about and worried about the most?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We, as the United States, and our policy is not about decoupling, it is about de-risking. It is about understanding.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s not about pulling out U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: It`s not about pulling out, but it is about ensure that we are protecting American interests and that we are a leader in terms of the rules of the road, as opposed to following others` rules.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But President Biden was talk -- I think he was talking about the economic problems within China right now. He seemed to be suggesting that Xi Jinping might take some kind of action.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: It`s no secret that China is experiencing economic problems. And - and what you will find is -- certainly in my conversations with American business leaders is that they are looking at the future in terms of their capital investments and taking into account which countries are engaged in practices that are about abiding by the rule of law and international rules and norms in a way that they can be guaranteed that there will be some stability so they can make long-term investments. There is increasingly an understanding that China may not be the best bet when you are looking for stability, when you are looking for an investment in a place where there is an adherence to and respect for international rules and norms.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This November the United States will host a big summit of leaders in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: China said a few days ago, their state security service, the U.S. needs to show sufficient sincerity before Xi Jinping agrees to visit.\par \par \uc2 How important is it for Xi Jinping to come to America in November?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Well, it is important to the order of things, if you will, the stability of things, that we keep open lines of communication.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were in the same room with China`s number two today.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes, I have - and I have actually met with President Xi as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did you talk to him? Did you talk to him today?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Well, we exchanged pleasantries, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it`s still a little tense?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I don`t think it`s as simple, if you will, as whether there is tension. Yes, there`s tension when you are in a competition of any sort, but that does not mean that a we are seeking conflict.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And I think it`s important to not conflate the two.\par \par \uc2 Do we have disagreements, for example, very serious disagreements about what`s happening in the South China Sea? Yes. And we`re very clear about that. So, one could argue that there`s tension there.\par \par \uc2 But you look, for example, at trade. Ninety-nine percent of our trade is not influenced by what we are doing in terms of the restrictions on exports.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you want Xi Jinping to come in November?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think he is absolutely an important player in this region of the world. And if he comes, then that will be, I`m sure, something that could be productive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: North Korea`s Kim Jong-un, as you know, is expected, according to U.S. intelligence, to go meet with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Russia, in exchange for some military support potentially here.\par \par \uc2 How destabilizing would that be for Asia?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think it would be a huge mistake. I think it would be a huge mistake. First of all, when you look at Russia`s unprovoked war on Ukraine, and the idea that they would supply ammunition to Russia, well, it`s predictable where that ends up.\par \par \uc2 I also believe very strongly that for both Russia and North Korea, this will further isolate them in --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Some of the most sanctioned countries in the world at this point.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: But at some point it`s just a step too far.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is that step too far? I mean, what is it that Kim Jong-un is seeking? Is it nuclear submarines, as our allies in Seoul have said they`re concerned about? Is it satellites? What does that do here in Asia?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Our allies are telling you -- us about their concern about what`s happening in terms of North Korea. We are all absolutely clear and unequivocal about our goal of the complete denuclearization of North Korea. But when we think about the -- Russia`s aggression in Ukraine, we`re -- many, most, are very clear also. It`s an absolute violation of one of the most important agreements that we have around the world in terms of international rules and norms, which is the importance of sovereignty and territorial integrity. And, Margaret, I -- on that matter, I -- it is very clear that Russia has -- clearly they`re very desperate. They have already experienced a strategic failure. So, clearly this is an act of desperation on the part of Russia. But it would be a huge mistake for Korea to do this - for North Korea to do this.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our full interview with the vice president is on our website and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to retired Marine Corps General Frank McKenzie, who was the head of U.S. Central Command from 2019 to 2022.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to FACE THE NATION, sir.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow --\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET), (Former Commander of U.S. Central Command): Margaret, good to be with you this morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Tomorrow is the 22-year mark after the attacks of 9/11. And this Friday a senior U.S. intelligence official told reporters that al Qaeda is at a historic low point and revival is unlikely in Afghanistan and Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if you think we`re at the point where the U.S. can declare victory?\par \par \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2 : Well, it`s good news about al Qaeda. I think ISIS is actually the more enduring threat right now. Al Qaeda is torn with internal theocratic debates. ISIS is not. And I do believe that ISIS, particularly in Afghanistan, is taking advantage of the vast ungoverned spaces that are there. And I believe they are, in fact, gathering strength. I would not dispute the judgment on al Qaeda.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, your successor, the current CENTCOM commander, said ISIS could do an external operation against the U.S. in under six months.\par \par \uc2 How should Americans understand that threat from Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2 : Well, I believe ISIS has always wanted to attack us here in our homeland as a core tenant, a core belief of theirs. And one of the reasons that we were in Afghanistan was to prevent the use of that country as a base from which to gather strength and either to direct or inspire attacks on our homelands or the homelands of our allies. As a result of our withdraw from Afghanistan, it is now far more difficult for us to pursue those objectives.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you`ve made no secret of the fact that now as a private citizen you opposed both President Biden and President Trump`s decision to withdraw U.S. troops from Afghanistan. In June, Biden was asked about some of the documented failures of that withdraw. And here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President Of The United States): Because remember what I said about Afghanistan. I said al Qaeda would not be there. I said it wouldn`t be there. I said we`d get help from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . I was right.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do you make of the president`s characterization of the U.S. working with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2 : Of course, I don`t see that intelligence reporting anymore, but everything up until April 2022, which is when I stopped reading it, led me to believe that \uc2 Taliban\uc2  would act only in the light of their very best interest. And while they might - they might - they might make some temporary accommodation, as they did when we withdrew from Afghanistan, they weren`t to be trusted and they actually have a long-term familial and customary relationship with al Qaeda. And it`s very difficult to think that would change. I think that relationship is far stronger than any potential relationship they choose with the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You oversaw that evacuation. And you`ve testified to Congress previously that the U.S. did not have any opportunity to take out that suicide bomber who took the lives of the 13 Americans who died August two years ago.\par \par \uc2 But in recent months, a Marine sniper has testified to Congress that he had the bomber in his sights, and he was not authorized to take the shot. Do you stand by your initial statement?\par \par \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2 : Yes, I do, Margaret. And the point I would make is this. As the days wound up to the 26th of August, which was the day of the strike, we were looking at four significant threats. We were looking at a vehicle born IED attack, a car with a bomb in it, that we thought was very real, in fact was being prepared. We were looking at a suicide vest attack of the - of the type that actually occurred on the 26th. We were looking at indirect fire, rockets or mortars, directed against the airfield, and then we were looking at the possibility of an insider attack, somebody who got past our checkpoints with a bomb and was able to set it off either in the crowded terminal area or on an airplane.\par \par \uc2 So, the point I would make is, look, there were a lot of threats being worked all the time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there was nothing that you saw indicating that there was a decision made not to take a shot? Nothing that would substantiate what this individual said under sworn testimony?\par \par \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2 : No. What I can tell you is that tactical units on the ground went in with rules of engagement, what we call ROE in our business, that were robust enough to allow them to defend themselves. And we actually exercised that right on several occasions in defense of the airfield where we engaged targets that appeared to be threatening. So, the rules of engagement were in place.\par \par \uc2 As to what actually happened on the ground at the tactical level down inside a rifle squad or a platoon, well, Margaret, I -- I don`t know the answer to that. So I can`t - I can`t help you with that particular one.\par \par \uc2 But I do know we had good rules of engagement. They were the standing rules of engagement that we`ve had. People were well trained in those rules of engagement and knew how to apply them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to also ask you, as former CENTCOM commander, about Iran. We have seen a buildup by the administration of Marines, warships in the region. At the same time, a bit of a thaw with Iran with this potential prisoner exchange happening and the release of $6 billion in unfrozen Iranian oil reserve money.\par \par \uc2 How should Americans at home think about Iran now given what`s happening?\par \par \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2 : Iran remains the most significant threat to peace in the region and they continue to pursue ultimately a policy of ejecting the United States and other western nations from the region. That hasn`t changed. That - that`s a - and a return to hegemonic power in the region. All those remain core goals of Iran.\par \par \uc2 Now, they will do a lot tactically to gain sanctions relief. And that`s, in my judgement, that`s what they`re doing now. The core problem of Iran has not gone away. In fact it`s worsened because over the last ten years Iran has significantly increased the capabilities and capacity of their ballistic missiles, they have thousands of those, of land attack cruise missiles, low flying missiles, and also drones. And those give them the capability to gain what we would call overmatch against the neighbors.\par \par \uc2 That`s a significant - that`s a significant rise in Iranian capabilities. We tend to overlook that sometimes because we look hard at the nuclear issue. And the nuclear issue is important. But the issues that`s - that poses a threat this afternoon is the Iranian capability with their missiles.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: General, thank you for your assessment and your time today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re back for some analysis with David Sanger of "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Josh Rogin.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER, (White House and National Security Correspondent, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 "): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ROGIN, (Foreign Policy and National Security Columnist, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 "): Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 You know, David, I want to start with you. I know you`ve been looking a lot at great power competition. In the last Cold War, southeast Asia, Asia was an epicenter of this shutdown. And once again we are looking at Asia becoming a real focus here for great power competition, this time directly with China.\par \par \uc2 I wonder how you think the Biden administration is doing so far in countering the balance?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER: Well, so far a lot better, I think, than most of their predecessors. And in that I would - I would include both President Trump and President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . They have got a strategy to try to win back the allegiance of countries throughout the Pacific. And so you`ve seen agreements that have ranged down through the Solomon Islands and New Guinea, places that people really hadn`t thought about in the United States very much, up through the Philippines, as you were discussing with Vice President Harris, and with Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 I remember being on the first trip an American president took to Vietnam, it was \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , and it was pretty much all about reconciliation with the war. What I thought was interesting about the president`s press conference, which ran just as you began the show here, is, there was almost no reference to the Vietnam War. It was really all about China itself and its influences, Russia and its influences. And what the president didn`t say, as my "Times" colleagues have reported, is that Vietnam has just signed an arms deal secretly with Russia.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER: And did so just before the president arrived. So, you`re seeing a lot of countries hedging and trying to keep one foot in both camps.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, Josh, you were with me on that very long transit to Indonesia and you spoke with Vice President Harris as well. One of the things she -- she didn`t lay out there, but the broader strategy seems to be building this constellation of countries that don`t really like Beijing, but aren`t ready to necessarily claim allegiance. They just want a little bit more relationship management here.\par \par \uc2 What is it that you think was accomplished?\par \par \uc2 JOSH ROGIN: Right. Well, I think, you know, we talk a lot about the U.S.- China relationship for obvious reasons. But the fact is that the Indo- Pacific strategy is a lot bigger than China. And where the real action is, is with all of these other countries. And we see the geopolitical shifts in the region, Japan and Korea, coming to Camp David for the first time. We see the Japanese and the Philippines working together more than they ever have before. This is not caused because of a us but there is an opportunity for the United States to take advantage of these demand signals coming from allies, and I think that`s what Vice President Harris was there to do.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t know about your opinion, Margret, but my opinion is that she did it -- represented the United States admirably and skillfully and showed that she had a command of the issues and a good rapport with several of these Asian leaders, whom she has met with on many trips many times. And did she solve all of the world`s problems in 36 hours? No. And can she solve the fact that our Indo-Pacific strategy lacks a robust economic and investment component, no. But she did the hard work of meeting with these people and hearing them out. And but what they said to her and to you and to me is very clear, is that they`re stuck there in that region with China, whether they like it or not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ROGIN: They want America in the region. They don`t know if they can count on our long-term commitment. They would like to see more money on the table. But this was a small step, I think, in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The one part that some of those countries can count on are those countries where there is an actual treaty, right? And I think it is worth reminding people that five of our seven treaty allies are in Asia. So, the U.S. could get drawn into a conflict. So these skirmishes in the Philippines or things that are happening in these far-flung places actually could pull in U.S. military action and require it, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER: This is our biggest risk right now, which is something accidental.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER: You`ll remember that the Bush administration, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , began with its first big foreign policy crisis was an American plane that collided with a Chinese plane, both intelligence plane and a fighter, and (INAUDIBLE) took days to get the crew back.\par \par \uc2 Right now there is so much activity from the South China Sea down through the Philippines that that`s a big risk. And you mentioned North Korea, where they`re in, obviously, you know, the diplomacy that you saw President Trump engage in, ultimately fail. The North Korean nuclear arsenal is larger than it has ever been. They`ve just brought out a submarine that may or may not be able to carry nuclear weapons. And the cooperation that we once had from China and Russia to solve this problem is gone.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER: Why? Because the Russians need arms from the North Koreans and looks like in the next few days we may see Kim Jong-un show up to meet \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly. And that`s worth pointing out because when we hear about international rules based order, that`s based on the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . And two of those actors you just talked about have veto power there. So, we`re really seeing this new territory foreign policy wise.\par \par \uc2 Josh, you know, one of the things that strikes me is how carefully the administration parses and picks its words when it refers to anything having to do with China. Then U.S. Ambassador \uc2 Rahm Emanuel\uc2  tweeted, and he went there, "President Xi`s cabinet lineup is resembling Agatha Christie`s novel, `And Then There Were None`." He`s talking about the foreign minister going missing, the rocket force commanders going missing. The defense minister hasn`t been seen in two weeks. He said, "who`s going to win this unemployment race? China`s youth or Xi`s cabinet?"\par \par \uc2 Fairly provocative but pointing to some real internal issues potentially within China`s top leadership.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ROGIN: Right. I mean we all know \uc2 Rahm Emanuel\uc2  in Japan I`ve been to Tokyo recently. They call him the Rahm-bassador. They call him like a - you know, the - the -- the junkyard dog that you chain up near - near the - the Chinese that can bark at them. And that`s not exactly perfectly coordinated inside the administration, to be honest, but it`s not as if they`re stopping him either. And you saw in your interview with Vice President Harris and President Biden`s press conference, they`re very careful when talking about China.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Very careful.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ROGIN: And then there`s Rahm. And he`s not -- but what he`s saying is true, that the level of secrecy inside Xi Jinping`s regime is unprecedented. And that - in more than anything else is what`s causing a lot of the uncertainty and risk of miscalculation that could lead to the conflict that nobody wants. So, if it has to be \uc2 Rahm Emanuel\uc2  that says the obvious, when is that when the foreign minister disappears off the face of earth, no explanation, that`s weird, OK. And when they are expanding militarily and say, oh, we want to have good military communication, but we won`t establish the military-to-military hotline, then they`re not telling the truth, OK. And these are just facts about our relationship with China that are unfortunate but need to be spoken so that people understand that, in the end, if the Chinese government doesn`t want to engage, like it says it does, there`s not a lot we can do except to rally our allies to defend ourselves, which I think is what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`ll see if Xi Jinping ends up coming to America in November potentially to meet with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID SANGER: We`re -- we`re taking bets.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Unclear. I don`t know what bet you would take on that one. But I will - I will defer. We`re going to have to leave the conversation there though, and we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before we go today, we want to congratulate Coco Gauff, the first American to win the U.S. Open as a teen since 1999 when Serena Williams won at the age of just 17. This is her first major tournament victory. President Biden called her.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230910ej9a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 6, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian, Imtiaz Tyab, Nikole Killion,  Chris Warren, Christina Ruffini, Catherine Herridge, Nikki Dementri \par \uc2 3,625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: Police release a shocking new video, as we learn new details about how a murderer escaped from a Pennsylvania prison -- the urgent manhunt, with residents scared and frustrated, as school is canceled for another day.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The stunning video shows the killer scaling a wall, then pushing through razor wire and running across the roof. Plus, one of the major obstacles facing search teams.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: The undergrowth, it is so thick, searchers can only go a few feet before losing sight of each other.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Federal prosecutors say they plan to bring an indictment against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  by late September. The president`s son has been under investigation in Delaware on gun and tax charges.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: With record-breaking heat, parents are scrambling to find childcare, as the start of the school year is disrupted in multiple states.\par \par \uc2 HUGH BARRETT (Parent): I was shocked. I couldn`t believe that we didn`t have something as basic as air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, the new track for Hurricane Lee just coming in, where the extremely dangerous storm is headed.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: More than four dozen children sent to the hospital after a school bus crash.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: That head-on collision involving a school bus and a tow truck.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is in Ukraine this morning.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Just hours before Secretary Blinken`s arrival, an early-morning Russian missile attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: A Russian attack on a city in the eastern part of the country has killed at least 16 people.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A concerning rise in cancer for people under the age of 50. What the study shows and how you can lower your risk.\par \par \uc2 And rescued just in the nick of time, after multiple sharks attacked a catamaran and almost sunk the boat.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with new details in the brazen breakout and manhunt that has captured international attention. Hundreds of law enforcement officers from multiple agencies are looking for Danelo Cavalcante. And we finally know how the dangerous fugitive escaped from a prison outside of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this new video. The convicted killer is seen scaling between two walls, moments later, escaping to freedom. And we now know this is how another inmate escaped in May.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Dementri reports, the guards didn`t notice Cavalcante escaped for a full hour, giving him a head start.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI (voiceover): Tonight, we`re seeing newly released video of Danelo Cavalcante`s daring escape from prison.\par \par \uc2 The escape murderer crab-walks up the prison wall, then jumps down to a less secure area, before pushing through razor wire to exit the facility.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD HOLLAND (Acting Warden, Chester County Prison): The tower officer did not observe, nor report the escape. The escape was discovered as part of the inmate count.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: The tower guard has been placed on leave. Cavalcante was awaiting transfer to a state prison after he received a life sentence for killing his ex-girlfriend. An inmate who escaped from the same prison in May used a similar method.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE RUNDELL (Concerned Resident): I just wish that they would catch him and also let us know why there have been two people since May that escaped from that prison.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Hundreds of police officers, along with federal agencies, including the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Border Patrol, are flooding yards and searching cars.\par \par \uc2 Today, a train passing through the area was stopped and also searched.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. GEORGE BIVENS (\uc2 Pennsylvania State Police\uc2 ): Of everything that I`m able to see lead me to believe that he is still there in that area.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Police say Cavalcante was last seen overnight near the area they`re searching. On Monday, photos showed him on a trail camera at a nearby botanical garden. And, on Saturday, a home surveillance camera recorded him passing through a yard, all this just a few miles from where police say he was spotted shortly after his escape on Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 Police say they have confirmed seven total sightings of Cavalcante.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. GEORGE BIVENS: We are not going anyplace until we have him in custody.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Something working against police in this search is the undergrowth. It is so thick, searchers can only go a few feet before losing sight of each other.\par \par \uc2 Two Pennsylvania school districts are once again closed as the community grows increasingly frustrated.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Just kind of taking it day by day, even, you know, hour by hour.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI DEMENTRI: Officials doubled down today, saying they will not stop this manhunt until Cavalcante is back in their custody, which means more than 200 officers are searching 24/7 -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Quite a manhunt.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Dementri, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we`re following another urgent manhunt, this one right here in D.C. Police say a murder suspect escaped police custody from George Washington University Hospital, where he was being treated. Christopher Haynes was not handcuffed when he escaped. Traffic near G.W. is gridlocked. University students were told to shelter in place.\par \par \uc2 Now to breaking news concerning \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , federal prosecutors revealing in a court filing today that they will seek to the president`s son before the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 We get the new details from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Catherine Herridge.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE (voiceover): The prosecutor`s status report is the strongest indication that charges are looming in the Hunter Biden case.\par \par \uc2 The president`s son had previously reached a deal for alleged gun possession by a drug user that avoided prosecution if he abided by the terms for two years. But, in July, a plea agreement for misdemeanor tax charges collapsed, leaving the future of the gun deal in question and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  facing legal jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Today, special counsel David Weiss revealed his plans, telling the court: "The government intends to seek the return of an indictment in this case before September 29."\par \par \uc2 Harry Litman is a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 What`s the message from this filing?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN (Former U.S. Attorney): The message from this filing is, they are going full-bore at him on a charge that they normally wouldn`t bring. So they are really playing hardball with Biden.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: In August, Weiss, a Trump appointee, was named special counsel after prosecutors said negotiations with Hunter Biden`s lawyers were at an impasse.\par \par \uc2 An attorney for the president`s son said today: "We expect a fair resolution, based on the evidence and the law, not outside political pressure."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: This development comes as House Republicans have already ramped up their investigations into the president and his son, calling the plea agreements a sweetheart deal.\par \par \uc2 And they want special counsel Weiss to testify -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Catherine Herridge with his breaking news, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to the intense and record-breaking heat gripping major U.S. cities tonight across the South and all the way up to the East Coast to New England; 160 million Americans sweltered in temperatures in the 90s or above today.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Christina Ruffini.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voiceover): For parents and kids already sweating the start of a new school year, the heat hasn`t helped.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE WILDER (Parent): The fact that they aren`t really prepared for these kinds of incidents is a little ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: With the heat index topping triple digits in D.C., some kids at Horace Mann Elementary are trying to learn their ABCs without A.C.\par \par \uc2 HUGH BARRETT (Parent): Luke was coming home sweaty.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Hugh Barrett`s 5-year-old son, Luke, came home complaining about the heat and the noise from fans that didn`t do much. Finally, after more than a week, temporary window units were installed.\par \par \uc2 HUGH BARRETT: There are so many gaps for just basic services like air conditioning not being functional in places like schools, where kids need to learn, teachers need to teach.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: But the hot weather is spelling trouble for schools across the country. Already, schools in nine states have been dismissed or released early this week. In Baltimore, no central air in some schools forced kids back to remote learning.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everybody should have air. I mean, you got air in your car. You got air at your job. Why not in our schools?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And, in Philadelphia, where more than half of schools don`t have adequate cooling, 86 of them will be released early for the rest of the week.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: It was a little, like, stuffy and uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: I was sweating. I was like --\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #3: But it`s so humid, the cafeteria -- it`s like this huge cafeteria. There`s no air at all.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And this problem isn`t going away.\par \par \uc2 According to a recent study, about 14,000 public schools that didn`t need cooling systems in the 1970s will need them by 2025, at a cost of almost $40 billion -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Christina Ruffini, wow, that`s a big number. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on when the heat will break and a storm that`s just become a hurricane out in the Atlantic, we turn to Chris Warren of our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Relief from this unbearable heat is on the way. The Northeast will have another day back into the 90s tomorrow, more significant cooling for the Great Lakes by Friday. Even the Northeast will be back down into the 80s. Still warm, but not as hot as the past couple of days.\par \par \uc2 And now, this evening, new, Hurricane Lee, was a tropical storm, now a hurricane, expected to be a major hurricane by the end of the week. And then, early next week, high pressure and a dip in the jet stream should keep it away from the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 However, Norah, this is next week, and there is still a lot we need to watch.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, Chris Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Moving on now to a frightening accident in Pennsylvania. A tow truck crashed head on into a school bus this morning in Bear Creek. That`s south of Scranton; 49 children were taken to the hospital. Most of their injuries were minor, thank goodness.\par \par \uc2 Well, police believe the tow truck driver suffered a medical episode, causing him to veer into the school bus. Both drivers and two students remain in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 On Capitol Hill today, there were new questions about the longest-serving Republican leader in Senate history. Even one of Mitch McConnell`s colleagues is now doubting the diagnosis of the Capitol physician.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion reports, the 81-year-old says he plans to stay in office.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  dismissed questions about his health this afternoon and vowed to stay on.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-Kentucky): I`m going to finish my term as leader, and I`m going to finish my Senate term.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Kentucky Republican pointed back to a letter from Congress` attending physician, which found no evidence of seizure, stroke, or movement disorder, such as Parkinson`s disease, following two recent incidents in which he froze up.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky`s \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , an eye doctor, expressed support for McConnell, but skepticism about the Capitol physician`s diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAND PAUL\uc2  (R-Kentucky): I have practiced medicine for 25 years, and it doesn`t look like dehydration to me. It looks like a focal neurologic event.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: McConnell met with his Republican colleagues privately to discuss his condition.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel that he is being fully transparent about his health?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITT ROMNEY (R-Utah): He`s a fine leader. If he has a hangnail, I don`t need to know about it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Questions aren`t only swirling around the 81-year-old McConnell. Today, in Jakarta, Vice President Harris was asked about President Biden`s age and whether she would be able to step into the role if necessary.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Do you feel prepared for that possibility? Has serving as vice president prepared you for -- for that job?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And how would you, you know, describe that -- that process?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Well, first of all, let`s -- I`m answering your hypothetical. But Joe Biden`s going to be fine, so that is not going to come to fruition.\par \par \uc2 Every vice president understands that, when they take the oath, that they must be very clear about the responsibility they may have to take over the job of being president. I am no different.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: President Biden is 80 and would be 86 at the end of a second term, if reelected.\par \par \uc2 As for McConnell, he did not take any questions during his meeting with Republican senators today, but he was more candid, calling himself a concussion survivor. He also said he lacks energy and is sleeping more -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Hmm.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion on Capitol Hill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we have breaking news out of Texas. A federal judge is ordering Governor Greg Abbott to move that floating barrier from the center of the Rio Grande to the riverbank by September 15th. The 1,000-foot barrier was designed to stop migrants from entering the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The judge wrote that the buoys are -- quote -- "a threat to human life." Texas plans to appeal.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the war in Ukraine, Russia unleashed one of its worst attacks on civilians in months today, a missile strike in Eastern Ukraine killing at least 17 people and wounding dozens, the attack unfolding as Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  returned to the war zone with a new aid package for America`s besieged ally.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab reports from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voiceover): The moment a Russian missile slammed into a busy outdoor market, bringing fire and death to the streets of Kostyantynivka.\par \par \uc2 The carnage in the Eastern Donetsk region is one of the deadliest Russian assaults in months, and came shortly after missiles targeting Kyiv were shot down by Ukrainian air defenses, as Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  was traveling to the capital by train on an unannounced two-day trip, his first since the start of Ukraine`s counteroffensive nearly three months ago.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian President): President Biden.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And Blinken arrived with the promise of a further billion dollars in military aid that includes controversial depleted uranium munitions.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): In the ongoing counteroffensive, progress has accelerated in the past few weeks. This new assistance will help sustain it and build further momentum.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In recent days, Ukrainian forces have pushed further south in the Zaporizhzhia region, but progress is painfully slow, thanks to Russian airstrikes and minefields, as President Zelenskyy also navigates political minefields.\par \par \uc2 This week, he removed Defense Minister \uc2 Oleksii Reznikov\uc2  for failing to tackle corruption and replaced him with Rustem Umerov, who has a reputation as an anti-corruption campaigner, who is now responsible for overseeing billions of dollars in military funds.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now, as the war grinds on and Ukraine continues to seek outside support, President Zelenskyy wants his Western backers to know he`s willing to take a hard line on corruption, while also continuing to fight off Russia and its deadly attacks -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Imtiaz Tyab, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he would absolutely take the stand to defend himself if asked to testify in any of his upcoming trials. The former president even said today he`s looking forward to it.\par \par \uc2 We learned new details on what a trial may look like in the Georgia election interference case. It would be televised. Prosecutors say they will call at least 150 witnesses, and the proceedings will last four months.\par \par \uc2 And in the Trump classified documents case, a key witness, a Mar-a-Lago I.T. worker -- this is news -- is agreeing to cooperate with prosecutors. He struck a deal with special counsel Jack Smith`s office after he was threatened with perjury charges.\par \par \uc2 All right, now to an alarming study on cancer and young people. We`re talking about younger than 50 years old. Cases worldwide have shot up by nearly 80 percent in the past 30 years, and cancer is killing more young people than ever.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian looks at what`s behind this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Queen Stewart is a lawyer and mom of two who learned she had breast cancer at 35, finding the lump herself, too young for insurance-paid screenings.\par \par \uc2 When you were diagnosed, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 QUEEN STEWART (Cancer Patient): I just cried, and I just worried so much about whether I would be here for my girls.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Cancer is surging in people under 50, according to a new study involving more than 200 countries, increasing 79 percent over an almost-30-year period, the most cases and deaths coming from breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 Prostate and trachea or windpipe cancers had the fastest increases.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER FLOWERS (Oncologist, \uc2 University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center\uc2 ): Those numbers are remarkable.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: What are the ramifications for society of younger people getting cancer?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER FLOWERS: I think it`s important for younger individuals who meet screening criteria to consider and pursue screening at age- appropriate times.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But why is it happening? Researchers say genetics is a factor, but the study cites poor diets, alcohol and tobacco use, physical inactivity, and obesity.\par \par \uc2 What steps can people take to reduce their cancer risk?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER FLOWERS: Look at the importance of screening. There`s breast cancer, colorectal cancer, or cervix cancer.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In the U.S., the recommended age to start colon cancer screening was recently lowered from 50 to 45, and breast cancer from 50 to 40.\par \par \uc2 Stewart had a double mastectomy, chemotherapy and radiation. She`s in remission and says she has a new lease on life.\par \par \uc2 QUEEN STEWART: It had me create a sense of urgency in my life to live now, to find joy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And researchers specifically mentioned dietary factors, consuming too much red meat, too much salt, as contributing to these cancer cases, stressing how much of a role what we eat plays in our overall general health -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, it`s so important to get that early screening.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 An unwanted kiss leads to a criminal case. The latest on the growing scandal involving a Spanish soccer star -- next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The scandal involving the Spanish women`s soccer team took another serious turn today.\par \par \uc2 One of the players, Jenni Hermoso, filed a criminal complaint against the head of Spain`s Soccer Federation, Luis Rubiales, for kissing her on the lips without her consent during the World Cup celebration. Rubiales has been suspended. Under Spanish law, he could face one to four years in prison if convicted of sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 We have got a major recall to tell you about. \uc2 ConAgra\uc2  is recalling more than 245,000 pounds of its Banquet brand frozen chicken strips. It says the chicken could contain pieces of plastic. The products were being recalled having "best if used by" dates of December 11, 2024, or January 1st and 7th of 2025.\par \par \uc2 A dramatic rescue at sea after multiple sharks attacked a catamaran. That`s just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In Central Greece, at least two people are dead and several others are missing after record rainfall unleashed devastating floods. Since Monday, the storm has triggered landslides, destroyed a bridge and swept away dozens of cars.\par \par \uc2 Now to this story. Three sailors are safe tonight after a harrowing encounter with multiple sharks off the coast of Australia. The sharks caused severe damage to their 30-foot boat. They were rescued by a cargo ship. The sailors, two from Russia and one from France, are OK. They were attempting around-the-world sail.\par \par \uc2 America`s young tennis stars making their mark at the U.S. Open. That story is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight, at the U.S. Open, youth is holding serve.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from USTA/\uc2 ESPN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Twenty-year-old Ben Shelton earned his first trip to the semifinals Tuesday night with a four-set win over Frances Tiafoe. It marked the first quarterfinal match featuring two African-American men.\par \par \uc2 American Coco Gauff is another player whose game is as hot as the weather. Tomorrow, she makes her first bid for the finals in Flushing Meadows. At 19, Gauff is just two wins away from her first major title.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS. We will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230907ej9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For August 27, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Cordes, Nikole Killion, Robert Costa, David Becker, Scott  MacFarlane, Cristian Benavides \par \uc2 8,944 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I`m Nancy Cordes in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation, breaking news overnight, as a U.S. Osprey crashes in Australia with 23 Marines on board, and three people are killed in a racially motivated shooting in Jacksonville, Florida. We will have the latest on both stories.\par \par \uc2 Another first for the American presidency last week, a booking photo of former President Trump after he was charged, along with 18 others, with conspiring to overturn the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We did nothing wrong. I did nothing wrong. And everybody knows it. I have never had such support.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump may be right about that support, as his campaign says they have brought in more than $7 million, thanks in part to mug shot merch.\par \par \uc2 And his competitors?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER (FOX News Anchor): Please raise your hand if you would.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Most, including former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , say they`d support him for president even if he`s convicted.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to Pence, along with former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , who feels much differently.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (R-New Jersey) (Presidential Candidate): Someone`s got to stop normalizing this conduct.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Have you seen Donald Trump`s mug shot yet?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I did see it on television.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What did you think?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Handsome guy. Wonderful guy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We will have analysis and talk about the complicated legal calendar with our political panel.\par \par \uc2 Then: a childcare cliff looming next month and a bipartisan duo hoping to prevent it. South Carolina Republican Nancy Mace and California Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  will talk about the millions of children who could lose their care if Congress doesn`t act.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, Russia expert \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2  weighs in on the mysterious plane crash that killed Russian mercenary chief \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  after his failed coup attempt against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We have got a lot to get to today, but we want to begin with that crash of an Osprey aircraft carrying 23 U.S. Marines. Three of those Marines are now confirmed dead, and five more have been seriously injured. It happened during a multinational training exercise on Melville Island in Australia`s Northern Territory.\par \par \uc2 The incident is under investigation. And we will update you as we learn more.\par \par \uc2 The other breaking story, a dollar store in Jacksonville, Florida, is the site of a racially motivated shooting yesterday.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent Cristian Benavides reports from the scene.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voice-over): A heartbroken community left reeling after officials say a gunman opened fire inside a \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  store in Jacksonville, Florida, killing three people before taking his own life.\par \par \uc2 All three of the victims were black. Police say the suspect was motivated by hate when he walked into the store Saturday afternoon, wearing a mask and tactical vest.\par \par \uc2 T.K. WATERS (Jacksonville, Florida, Sheriff): Plainly put, this shooting was racially motivated, and he hated black people.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: In writings left behind by the gunman, who is described as a white man in his early 20s, authorities say he described his hateful ideology.\par \par \uc2 This picture, released by the sheriff`s office, shows swastikas drawn on the AR-15-style rifle he used to carry out the attack.\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF T.K. WATERS: We stand together as a community. We live together as a community. We fight together as a community. And when someone comes into our city and does this, we`re not going to stand for it, and we don`t accept it.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has opened a federal civil rights investigation and will investigate this as a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The shooting happened less than a mile from Edward Waters University, a historically black university.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the school said they believe the suspect was on campus earlier in the day, but left after being confronted by a security officer.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That was Cristian Benavides reporting from Jacksonville.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to politics and former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who joins us from Indiana this morning.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vice President, thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE (R-Presidential Candidate): Thank you, Nancy. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with this shooting in Jacksonville. You know, here we are, it`s the eve of the 60th anniversary of the March on Washington. And yet we`re appearing -- we`re appearing to witness a rise in racial hatred, in white supremacy across the country.\par \par \uc2 What can you do, what can your party do, what can the nation do to address this?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, there`s no place in America for racially inspired violence.\par \par \uc2 And I condemn what occurred in Jacksonville in the strongest possible terms. That wasn`t a criminal act. That was an act of evil.\par \par \uc2 And -- but I -- and our prayers are with the families who lost loved ones and those that are injured. Karen and I prayed this morning for them. And, as a Marine Corps family, we also prayed for the families of our Marines that were lost and injured in the Osprey crash. We believe in prayer at our house, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 But -- but, look, I -- this issue of mass shootings is one that I think we need leadership in this country afresh. I -- I -- I will tell you that I believe that the -- there`s a series of steps that we can take, providing law enforcement with the support that they need, ending this -- this long experiment of defunding the police by the American left.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to provide federal funding to provide armed guards at all of our public and private schools. We need a commitment to institutional health care in this country that I have called for many months ago.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, I also believe that justice delayed is justice denied. And I`m calling for an expedited federal death penalty for anyone engaged in a mass shooting like took place in Jacksonville or -- or, frankly, like the shootings that took place at -- at a baseball park and at a football game.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: We -- we`ve got to send a message to anyone that has evil in their hearts that there is no chance for them to spend the rest of their life behind bars, that they`re going to meet their fate in months, not years.\par \par \uc2 And I believe -- I believe expedited due process of federal death penalty for those that engage in the kind of mass shootings that claim lives in Jacksonville yesterday is an idea whose time has come.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Understood.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to the Republican primary. There was a moment that a lot of people will remember from the debate the other day, and that was when all of the candidates were asked whether they thought you did the right thing on January 6, 2020.\par \par \uc2 Let`s listen quickly to how some of them responded.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R-Presidential Candidate): Vice President Pence did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM (R-North Dakota) (Presidential Candidate): \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  did the right thing on January 6.\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  stood for the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TIM SCOTT (R-South Carolina) (Presidential Candidate): Absolutely, he did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida) (Presidential Candidate): Mike did his duty. I have got no beef with him.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Can you tell us what it felt like to get that kind of validation from your fellow Republican leaders, after all the abuse you`ve taken from the MAGA wing of your party for the past 2.5 years for that, for simply doing your job on January 6?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well -- well, Nancy it was heartening.\par \par \uc2 But it -- it wasn`t anything new. Frankly, as I have traveled around this country for the last 2.5 years, Americans from every walk of life have come up to me and -- and expressed their appreciation for the fact that we did our duty that day.\par \par \uc2 Look, I -- I -- as I said, I understand the disappointment in the 2020 election. I was on the ballot. But, under our Constitution, states certify elections. And once court cases and reviews and recounts are completed, the only duty of the vice president is to preside over a joint session of Congress where those votes are open and counted.\par \par \uc2 And I welcome the fact that almost everybody on that stage made it clear that I did my duty that day. And I think the American people deserve to hear where every single one of the candidates for the Republican nomination stand.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Yes, I want to ask you about one of those candidates, entrepreneur \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You had some criticism of him on the stage. You said the country can`t afford to have a rookie in the White House. I wonder what you make of his voting record. He is listed as an unaffiliated voter in his home state of Ohio. He didn`t vote in the `22 primaries. He has acknowledged that he didn`t vote in most presidential elections, and he describes himself as a - - quote -- "American nationalist."\par \par \uc2 What`s your view of his politics?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, you know -- where to start?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: We had a good, vigorous debate, which is -- you know, which is why you have primaries, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 And I welcome the chance to contrast my longstanding commitment to the conservative agenda to -- and -- and frankly, the fact that I believe, in all humility, that I`m -- I`m the most qualified and the most tested candidate in this race, not only with Vivek, who I have known for several years, but for everybody else on the stage.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I think this country is in a lot of trouble. I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , from that disastrous withdrawal in Afghanistan two years ago this week, to his policies that launched the worst inflation in 40 years -- mortgage rates went up to a 22-year high yesterday, burdening families -- their war on energy, the crisis at the border, the assault on our liberties, that now is the time, I believe, for leadership, that -- that knows how to move the Congress, knows how to move an administration, but also has led a state and made the hard choices of a chief executive at the state level.\par \par \uc2 And so I was happy to contrast that. With regard to his voting record, I -- I...\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But we`re talking about -- we`re talking about Ramaswamy here.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I -- I...\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: ... if you don`t mind, Mr. Vice President.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: He said that he would pardon Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: You were one of six candidates on the stage who said that you would support Mr. Trump even if he is convicted of a felony.\par \par \uc2 Why do you feel that way, especially since you also said on the stage that you felt that he asked you to put him before the Constitution? Why should someone like that be president?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, look, I -- I signed a pledge to be on that stage to say that I would support the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 I remain confident, more confident after Wednesday night, that the Republican nominee will not be the former president, that we`re going to give the American people a standard-bearer for the GOP that`s going to be able to lead us to victory against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and -- and the radical left.\par \par \uc2 But -- so, you know, my -- I raised my hand just to say that I will -- I will support the Republican nominee, because, Nancy, I could never support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I mean, Joe Biden`s policies have been disastrous. He and his family are under an ethical cloud themselves.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has trampled on the Constitution of the United States. He`s failed to faithfully execute our laws at the Southern border of the United States, created the worst crisis in American history. And that -- that student loan giveaway, where he was going to ask truck drivers to pay their taxes to pay off the student loans of graduate students was a -- was, essentially, an unconstitutional power grab that I rejected.\par \par \uc2 So, I will support the Republican nominee and -- and I`m going to continue to work my heart out to make sure that it`s me.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mr. Vice President, we will be looking forward to the next debate and seeing you there.\par \par \uc2 Appreciate you joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Thank you, Nancy. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We turn now to former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, good morning. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : Thanks for having me, Nancy. It`s good to be here.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 Do you take issue with former President Trump making money off of his mug shot?\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : Look, there`s almost nothing anymore that he could do that would surprise me in terms of the ongoing grift.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised the country when he ran in 2016 that he would drain the swamp. Really, all he did was rearrange the swamp, rearranged it so that he could make money off of regular voters to pay his own legal fees, to pay $208,000 for his wife`s stylist, and to set up a $2 billion grift from the Saudis for his daughter Ivanka and his son-in-law, Jared.\par \par \uc2 You know, we see the same thing with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and what he`s doing. We have two ruling families right now in this country who put themselves before the people they`re supposed to represent. We need a wholesale change. And that`s why I`m running for president.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: You think Trump supporters are being ripped off when they donate?\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : Absolutely, you know, when you see $40 million in legal fees in just the first half of the year.\par \par \uc2 And that was before two of the indictments. These fees are only going to go through the roof. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  maybe should sell one of his golf courses or maybe sell his apartment at Trump Tower to fund it. But, instead, he`s taking people who donate an average of $100 to him to try to get him to become president of the United States again, and he`s using that money to pay his legal fees.\par \par \uc2 It`s -- it`s unethical, it`s immoral, and it`s just another part of the grift that`s been going on in Washington D.C. for much, much too long. And you look at him, and you look at what`s going on, as I said, with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and the way he was selling access to his father when he was vice president, I think that we should be tired of both of those acts.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: You know, you said at the debate -- and it got a lot of attention -- that candidates need to stop normalizing Trump`s conduct.\par \par \uc2 But you stood by him after he was accused of rape, after he praised \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  repeatedly, after he was impeached.\par \par \uc2 Didn`t you normalize his conduct for a long time as well?\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : The conduct I`m talking about, Nancy, is the one that is the greatest threat to our country and the greatest threat to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And that was standing in front of the cameras from the White House behind the seal of the president on election night and telling people the election was stolen and for now three years, nearly, continuing to say the same thing, with absolutely no proof and no evidence. That`s the problem, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 You can have policy disagreements with someone. And, believe me, I spoke out at the time about the things that I disagreed with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on. That`s much different than taking classified documents from the White House and hiding them for 18 months from the government, claiming you don`t have them, when these are some of the biggest secrets in our -- in our country`s arsenal.\par \par \uc2 It`s different than undercutting democracy, in the way that he was doing so and leading to the right we had on Capitol Hill on January 6. You know, I`m going to get on that stage and I`m going to tell the truth. Regardless of what other people may think, that is the truth.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I urge people to go to ChrisChristie.com, donate to support the truth, because that`s what we`re standing up for.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: One of Trump`s 18 co-defendants in Fulton County is his former Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , who you know.\par \par \uc2 And he`s going to be in court tomorrow arguing that his charges should be moved to federal court because the conduct in question happened when he was serving in the federal government. You are a former federal -- federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that argument?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE: He`s got an argument to make, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 There`s no doubt that, under the statutes, there`s an argument to make. But, ultimately, all these change of venue decisions are at the discretion of the trial judge here, a federal judge, who`s going to be hearing that. And he`s going to want to hear all the different evidence. And the balancing of the interests in these venue decisions are made on that basis.\par \par \uc2 So, is there an argument to be made? There`s definitely an argument to be made about switching venue to federal court. But, in the end, it really won`t change matters all that much, except that the trial won`t be televised. It still has to be governed under the state laws of Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that will be different are some of the procedural things that lawyers care about. But, in the end, all of the charges must remain the same. So I think that whether \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  wins that motion or doesn`t is not going to make a substantive difference on how ultimately a jury is going to be asked to make these decisions at the time of trial.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I want to move to an issue that a lot of Republican voters in particular care about, which is immigration.\par \par \uc2 On the debate stage, a couple of your opponents seemed to suggest that they would send troops into Mexico to fight the drug cartels. Governor DeSantis said he would deploy U.S. Special Forces.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that that is a viable solution to the Fentanyl crisis? Wouldn`t Mexico view that as an act of war?\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : I think they would.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I see it also as an -- as an act of war by China to be sending the precursor chemicals to these Mexican drug cartels in order to make the fentanyl that they`re sending up into the country.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think there are ways to get this done. I think we have to use our intelligence assets. We have to be much tougher and stronger at the border to prevent people from coming through, finish the wall that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never finished, only built 52 miles of in four years.\par \par \uc2 We need to make sure that we put National Guard at the border to stop those folks from coming across the border with fentanyl. I think the invasion-of- Mexico issue is one that sounds good on the debate stage, but I think, if any of them tried to implement that part of it as president, they would find it`s much more complicated that they`re -- than they`re making it sound.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Wouldn`t it be illegal to have the National Guard serving in a law enforcement capacity at the border?\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : No, Nancy, look, I think that what we`re doing is protecting the border.\par \par \uc2 They would be working with Customs and Border Patrol folks hand in hand. They can effectuate the arrest. But you obviously need greater force there to prevent them from coming over the border in the first place. And so it`s a combination of the two of them. Customs and Border Patrol would still be the law enforcement agency.\par \par \uc2 But they need backup. They need help. And we`re not getting that right now from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , thanks so much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 FORMER \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 : Thanks for having me, Nancy. Have a good Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We are back with South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace and Democratic California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Together, they launched the Congressional Bipartisan Affordable Childcare Caucus -- that`s a mouthful -- to deal with the childcare cliff that is barreling down the pike.\par \par \uc2 And I want to get to that in just a moment, but first want to talk to you about politics.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, President Trump is now facing four indictments, and yet he is still the overwhelming favorite to win the Republican nomination. Can your party win the presidency if he is the nominee?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (R-South Carolina): Well, I think every -- both sides are going to have to work hard to win next year.\par \par \uc2 I -- I talk to a lot of voters that are frustrated with both the Republican and Democrat Party. Both parties have put us on the spending cliff. You talk about childcare cliff, which we`ll talk about in a moment. But both parties have contributed to the inflation that we have today, the out-of- control spending, the debt ceiling that`s going to add almost $20 trillion to the debt over the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 And I think both parties are going to have to make a strong case next year, regardless of who the nominee is. But we`ll see what voters decide next year.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: You think Trump is a good messenger for that case?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: rMD-SU_He`s running away with the nomination process now.\par \par \uc2 We`re still pretty far out, but he just raised over $7 million after the fourth indictment. So, voters will get a say. I know that, in my district and in my state, he`s the standard-bearer for the nomination right now.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Congressman Khanna, you said back in April that you felt that the White House was being too overprotective of the president, that it basically wasn`t letting him get out there enough and be his authentic self.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel that top aides in the White House got that message?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-California): I think they do.\par \par \uc2 The President has a great story to tell. He`s bringing back manufacturing in Ohio, $20 billion in Ohio of new industry. He is focused on the working class. I was actually just in South Carolina yesterday with Bishop Green at AME Church, and the faith community has a lot of support for him.\par \par \uc2 He`s a better politician than anyone who works for him. Let him be out there.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Congresswoman Mace, you are pro-life, but you`ve also said your party`s too extreme on abortion.\par \par \uc2 And on the debate stage the other night, many of the candidates put their support behind a national abortion ban at 15 weeks, perhaps even earlier. Your fellow South Carolinian Nikki Haley was not among them. She said that that`s unrealistic.\par \par \uc2 But do you think that position is going to hold up well with the GOP primary electorate?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, certainly, every candidate talked about being pro-life and what their -- what their limits may or may not be.\par \par \uc2 But the only candidate on the stage that talked about how we should protect women and not demonize them was Nikki Haley. And that is a message that we have to carry through. We have to be pro-woman and pro-life. You cannot go after women and attack them because they make a choice that you don`t like or do not agree with, because it`s a very sensitive subject right now.\par \par \uc2 We want to show that we are going to protect women who`ve been raped, girls who are victims of incest. We want to make sure that every woman in this country, regardless of where she lives or how much money she makes, that she has access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 Those are the kinds of things -- and we`re going to talk about childcare in a minute -- but those are the kinds of things that really matter to women. And we have to show that we -- that we care.\par \par \uc2 And I do want to add that I know that you were praising President Biden, the story that he has to tell, but the story he won`t tell is a story about his family`s business. He has lied every single time he`s been asked about his family`s business. He`s lied about his involvement. He had a pseudonym.\par \par \uc2 Like, you`ve done -- if you`ve done everything up aboveboard, why is there the need to lie for it? That is a story that I want to hear. That is a story that every American deserves.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : If I could just respond to that...\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Very quickly. We don`t have much time left.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : ... Representative Mace and I agree on a lot of things, but this president has shown integrity for 40 years.\par \par \uc2 He has been completely transparent. He has allowed the law enforcement agencies to take their hold without interfering. And I think the American people will see that.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Well, when we come back, we`re going to get to a topic you both do agree on. And that`s childcare.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to take a quick break, but more with Congressman Mace and -- Congresswoman Mace, rather, and Congressman Khanna on the other side.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This week, CBS News presents a special edition of BET`s America in Black with a look the 1963 march.\par \par \uc2 It airs at 8:00 p.m. Eastern time Monday night on BET, and it`s available on CBS News Streaming and the CBS News and Paramount+ apps starting at 8:00 p.m. on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We will be right back with more from Congressman Khanna and Congresswoman Mace, plus our political panel and Russia expert \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION. I`m Nancy Cordes in for Margaret today.\par \par \uc2 And we are back with Republican Nancy Mace and Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the two of you are here because the U.S. is facing a so-called childcare cliff that most parents probably didn`t know about. On September 30th, just a little over a month from now, $40 billion worth of pandemic- era funding for the childcare industry is set to expire. And without it, up to 70,000 childcare facilities could close and an estimated 3.2 million children could lose access to childcare.\par \par \uc2 These numbers are really frightening. What can the two of you do about it and how quickly can you get legislation together so that Congress could consider it before the deadline?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Well, it`s staggering the challenge before us. And Congressman Khanna and I, we`ve been good friends. We`ve been great lawmakers together. We`ve started a childcare caucus. Our approaches may be different from time to time on different issues, but our goals are always the same. And these are issues that parents, especially moms, that - that they care about and we have to address. From the perspective that I`m looking at, you know, we talk about four-year-old pre-k, we talk about making sure that parents have the freedom and the resources to have childcare options, affordable child care options.\par \par \uc2 I approach it from a less government regulation standpoint. We have some really crazy regulations in this country. Some places say you have to have a four-year college degree. Well, that certainly makes it harder to find childcare workers -\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE: There are increase in costs because of it. Other places say, well, if you`re certified in one state, it`s not reciprocal in another. And it`s just - you know, having some of those approaches that can be easy to fix, fast fix, not controversial, that can pass out of a Republican- controlled House, Democrat-controlled Senate and get to a Democrat president`s desk to be signed into law, that`s what we`ve got to be working on.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: So, what do the two of you agree on?\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Well, first of all, as divided as we are as a country, 86 percent of Americans believe that there -- we need more support for affordable childcare.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right.\par \par \uc2 RO KHANNA: This is something that unifies Americans.\par \par \uc2 We have agreement on more training for childcare workers, paying them more and having more flexibility. We have an agreement on some support, government policy support, for childcare.\par \par \uc2 The average cost of a family for childcare is $10,000 a year. And 85 percent of women say that they can`t work because of childcare issues if they`re leaving the workforce. So, we have a -- we need a short-term solution, which is a continuation of some of the grants and funding the Childcare Stabilization Act so we don`t have this cliff and that the president signs, and then we need a long-term solution to reduce the costs to families.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Experts say doing that alone would cost $16 billion.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, you spoke earlier about the fact that you feel that both President Trump and President Biden presided over too much spending.\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But a lot of the solutions here, experts say, costs money. Universal pre-k, fully funding the childcare development fund, subsidizing childcare for low-income families. What could you live with and what do you think you could convince fellow Republicans to get on board for?\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE: Well, I actually think it`s less government, not more, that`s getting in the way of this. We saw in December of 2018, when the Azar (ph) report came through making recommendations to states about health care policy and certificate of need, the same kind of thing could be said for, what are some of the easy, low hanging fruit regulations that we can roll back or eradicate or recommend go away.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Do you think - do you think rolling back regulations alone, though, can really get at the problem?\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE: It`s something we can agree on. It`s a good start because right now we`re going to have a very difficult time with the appropriations process because of the amount of spending that`s going on, because of the additional supplementals. What are some of the small parts that will make a big difference in a mom`s life? And that is one thing that I think a lot of us can agree on. It`s not going to be easy. We do some large, comprehensive spending package. It`s going to have difficulty going through. So, I`m looking for the small parts big difference here policy wise.\par \par \uc2 RO KHANNA: This is an investment that is pro-economic growth. I mean businesses are losing billions of dollars because we don`t have people in the workforce. So, it pays for itself. And then you look at the funding. You`re talking about less than 1 percent of the defense budget. We`re one of the only countries that is putting $10,000 of burden on ordinary families just to take care of their kid. It`s pro-family, it`s pro-economic growth, and we should have a consensus in this country to have the government make a difference here.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And -\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE: We can incentivize businesses too and make sure that parents have the money to be able to afford childcare. Less taxes would be great.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And it is so important because we`ve seen study after study that shows that early childhood education makes such a huge difference in outcomes later in life. So, we really hope you`re successful.\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE: Thank you. We`re working real hard.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Congresswoman Nancy Mace, Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , appreciate it and appreciate you being here today.\par \par \uc2 NANCY MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This Monday marks the 60th anniversary of Martin Luther King`s Jr. "I have a dream" speech that he made at the 1963 March on Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING JR\uc2 .: I have a dream that one day this nation will rise up, live out the true meaning of its creed. We hold these truths to be self-evident that all men are created equal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Yesterday, thousands marched to honor the anniversary. A continuation of Dr. King`s dream with many echoes of the past and concerns about the challenges facing the civil rights movement today. Among those who spoke, Dr. King`s granddaughter, Yolanda King.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA KING: Sixty years ago Dr. King urged us to struggle against the triple evils of racism, poverty, and bigotry. Today, racism is still with us, poverty is still with us, and now gun violence has come for our places of worship, our schools, and our shopping centers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A powerful message. We`ll discuss that with our political panel.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Robert Costa is our chief election and campaign correspondent, David Becker is our CBS News election law contributor, and we`re also joined by congressional correspondents Scott MacFarlane and Nikole Killion, a double billing.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to all of you for being here.\par \par \uc2 What prescient words by the granddaughter of \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , just a few hours before that shooting, talking about racially animated shootings taking place across the country.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, you know, here we are, 60 years later, still a lot of challenges.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, really striking. Of course, that was King`s granddaughter. We also heard from Martin Luther King III, Dr. King`s son, and King`s wife.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, really being out there on the mall yesterday, 60 years later, it was palpable the amount of frustration and concern there is 60 years later. You know, we talk about how far we have come as a country, and yet, for many, particularly in the African American community, there is a sense that we`re still fighting the same battles, whether that`s issues of hate crimes and racial violence, whether that`s the issue of the rollback of affirmative action, voting rights. So, those were some of the concerns articulated at the march yesterday, and also a call to action to continue to keep fighting some of these issues so that one day Dr. King`s dream can be realized. That certainly was the message expressed.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A call to action earlier in the show today by former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 Robert, I want to talk to you about the former president, President Trump, who is now -- he`s - he`s been booked. He`s got a mugshot. He`s raising a lot of money using that mugshot. What can you tell us about the legal strategy that his - his team has right now, particularly after he has changed his legal team in the Atlanta case?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 He has shaken up his legal team in Georgia. What to watch now is the difference between his strategy and the strategy of his co-defendants in this Georgia case. A sprawling prosecution using RICO, racketeering charges, to get this large group of defendants together.\par \par \uc2 Kenneth Chesebro, who authored a memo suggesting alternate electors should be organized by Trump people, he is pushing for a speedy trial. So is \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 , another co-defendant, who infamously urged Trump to use the government to seize voting machines in the weeks after the 2020 election. But Trump himself is not pushing for a speedy trial because it could have political complications based on our reporting here at CBS News. He could be called as a witness during the fall, just as debates are heating up and the primaries and caucuses loom on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 What you`re also watching in Georgia right now is the push by \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , the former chief of staff, to have this trial move from being a Georgia case at the state Fulton County level, to being a federal case. So, there are many dynamics at play, multiple legal fronts, all as the political battleground also unfolds.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: David, you`re a lawyer. Robert just mentioned these co- defendants who are looking for a speedy trial. Why would they want a speedy trial? Why would they want their lawyers to have less time to prepare for this?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Well, they might feel that they already know the facts of this case and they`re prepared to go to trial. There might be a variety of tactical reasons they might be moving in that way. And the court`s ultimately going to have to decide how to server all of these defendants and place them into and do different court cases at different times. Or they could ultimately all be tried together as well if there`s some way to reconcile that. But that`s something that the lawyers and the clients are obviously discussing and trying to figure out. And this is where we`re really going to see the tension between the defendants. These defendants aren`t all unified in their claims. And some of them may do better if others of them do worse. And so we`re going to see that play out over the next several months as these tactical considerations are made.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: We know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not want a speedy trial in this case or any of the cases. In fact, in D.C. tomorrow there`s going to be a hearing about one of his federal cases where his lawyers have pushed to have the trial in 2026. Are they going to be successful with that - with that motion, Scott?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: We will find out tomorrow, 10:00 a.m. Eastern Time. The judge in Washington, D.C., determines the trial date. And this is a Mars and Venus type of world. The prosecution wants it January 2nd. And, as you mentioned, Trump`s team wants it 2026.\par \par \uc2 There are some reasons for optimism for Jack Smith going into this hearing. First of all, the early hearings in this case have moved crisply. They`ve had a quick schedule and the judge has met all the early deadlines. And the judge has said, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues with vitriolic social media posts, she`s inclined to move the trial date earlier to avoid tainting the jury pool. And there`s also the other matter of, there are many other cases going on and this schedule is getting clogged quickly. January or early 2024 trial might suit some purposes. And, Nancy, by so many accounts, this case, the one in D.C., is built for efficiency.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I want to ask you, Nikole, about the vitriolic social media posts that Scott just eloquently described. The judge in - or the D.A., rather, in Atlanta has a different bond agreement with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than with any of his other co-defendants. Basically, he has to refrain from attacking fellow witnesses, defendants, on social media or anywhere else. Based on what we know of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which is that he attacks lots of people all the time on social media, how is it - how likely is it that he is going to live up to that agreement?\par \par \uc2 KILLION: Well, it remains to be seen. I mean we also know that he`s very verbose on the campaign trail and doesn`t really hold back when it comes to going after the prosecutors in this case, but he does have to be very careful. We know that he has a new counsel in Georgia. I would imagine maybe conversations would be had about those parameters to reaffirm them. But certainly the fact that the former president turned around and put his mugshot on social media in such a short period of time, raised some $7.1 million off of that, shows that, you know, he is trying to capitalize off of this case. So, it`s certainly likely he could go after some of these witnesses, continue to go after prosecutors. But if he`s being smart about it, and listening to his counsel, hopefully he won`t.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A lot of people wondering why a billionaire would have to go through a bail bondsman to post a $200,000 bail.\par \par \uc2 COSTA: Well, this is a case where Trump is just trying to get his bearings now in Georgia. He got rid of his lawyer, Drew Findling. But the political consequences of all this, they`re watching Trump get a bail bondsman in Georgia, the rest of the field. I was in Milwaukee this week covering the debate, and they`re just wondering, is there ever a moment where this all becomes too much for now Republican voters are rallying behind Trump. They`re buying his mugshot on coffee cups or on t-shirts. But the Republican race - and I was hearing this from candidates and their top strategists out in Milwaukee this week, they`re saying that down the line this could become such a burden on Trump`s time, on his political energy that maybe they start to break through. That`s why they`re not going at him directly in most cases.\par \par \uc2 You heard some criticism, of course, from Vice President Pence, former Vice President Pence and former Governor Christie in your conversations today. But beyond those two, they`re really hovering around Trump`s candidacy, trying to build their own message should there ever be a collapse with Trump. But as we know with the Republican Party, it might never come. They might always stick by Trump.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It certainly gets difficult to schedule campaign rallies if you`re expected in court every other day.\par \par \uc2 David, I want to go big picture here because you are an election law expert. And I`m just wondering, because you speak to secretaries of state and election officials in various states all the time. How do they feel now that the former president, many of his aides, have actually been charged with crimes connected to the 2020 election, which, as we know, was not rife with fraud, as the former president continues to argue?\par \par \uc2 BECKER: Yes. It`s 1,027 days since that November 2020 election. The most scrutinized election in American history with more paper ballots, audits, recounts, more pre-election litigation, more post-election litigation confirming the results than ever before. And election officials are still, to this day, almost three years after that, having to debunk lies about that election, having to defend their own patriotism, their own service to our country, playing an endless game of Wack-a-mole.\par \par \uc2 But I think they are seeing an accountability coming to those who have spread those lies for so long. It`s not just the criminal cases. It`s also the civil defamation case against Fox News. It`s also disbarment proceedings against people like John Eastman and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . There is a sense that accountability is coming and they want this to play out according to how the legal system want -- is supposed to work. And it is working that way right now.\par \par \uc2 And so what we hope to see is that there is a cooling down, because right now there are tens of millions of Americans who still believe that Republicans and Democrats are conspiring to steal elections, and that`s just false. And we know it`s false.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A lot of election workers who got death threats, who continue to get death threats because of some of the things that people are saying about the election.\par \par \uc2 David Becker, Robert Costa, Nikole Killion, Scott MacFarlane, thank you all for being here today. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Russia`s investigative committee confirmed today that the remains of Wagner leader \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  have been positively identified, along with the nine others who died in a plane crash last week.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined by \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2 , senior fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2 , and former White House Russia expert.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Fiona.\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL (Former National Security Council and Senior Director, European and Russian Affairs): Thanks, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  claims he had nothing to do with this. Is there anyone in the diplomatic community who believes that?\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL: I doubt it. And I - look, I think what we can certainly say is he didn`t order it not to happen because there are plenty of people who were painting a target on Prigozhin`s back. The system itself expected him to be taken out of the picture in some fashion. I guess it was a question about what the method would be. And, in fact, I think over the last two months there`s been more shock, not just internationally but also domestically based on source reporting that the fact that nothing had happened to Prigozhin and that he was allowed to walk around as if he hadn`t indeed perpetrated a push (ph) exactly two months ago.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: You know, usually Putin`s enemies tend to get poisoned or pushed out windows or shot. Why go to the trouble of bombing an airplane?\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL: Well, we don`t know exactly how the aircraft was brought down yet, but I guess we`ll find out more as things go along. I mean I`m sure, again, that the Russian government, the Kremlin, will accuse all kinds of other people about carrying out this act, if it seemed to be the result of an explosive device, you know, for example.\par \par \uc2 But it`s so dramatic. It`s so spectacular that, of course, one has to ask whether this was done for the demonstrative effect of it. And we have had some mysterious plane crashes in the past taking down Russian leaders. There was a very famous general, Alexander Lebed, for example, who died in a helicopter crash. So, it`s not something that is unheard of. And in other settings as well, of course, we`ve had Pakistan, Bangladesh, China, where they have been the loss of key people in plane crashes. So, I think this is par for the course, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And there is some symmetry, isn`t there, to Prigozhin`s dash to Moscow in that aborted mutiny when the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  brought down several Russian aircraft?\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL: Exactly. So, this is also a part of that idea of he who lives by the sword dies by the sword. An eye for an eye. The vengeance factor, it`s baked into the system. There was a lot of clamor from the uniformed military and especially the air force for some kind of retribution for this, whether it was in a legal form.\par \par \uc2 But, look, he took down a proportional number of people in a Russian military aircraft, as you`ve just said here. So, again, there is a symmetry and a symbolism all of it -- in all of this that is inescapable in the Russian domestic context, as well as for the rest of us watching it from the outside.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  has been so pivotal for the Russian military in the war in Ukraine. Now there are reports that the Russian government is requiring Wagner fighters to sign loyalty oaths. What is this going to mean? The death of \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 , what will it mean for the war in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL: Well, I don`t think it will actually mean anything significant for the war in Ukraine in terms of the military campaign itself. We were already seeing that Wagner was being pulled out. It was pulling out after the actions in Bakhmut where they were in the thick of the fighting there in that city that, of course, you know, was so much of the focus on the carnage of the war over the last several months. But it was already a precipitating factor in all of this series of events over the last two months. The idea that the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  were going to be dismantled and reincorporated or incorporated for the first time, rather, into the Russian military, and that was one of the factors that Prigozhin himself was citing as a reason for him making his march on Moscow. He didn`t want to have his guys put under the command of the central Russian military.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, you know, what we can see from all of this is that Wagner was pretty crucial for these early stages, this first couple of parts of the campaign in Ukraine, and now the Russian government and Putin wants to have more centralized control. He gave Wagner a long leash, and Prigozhin clearly took that leash far too far.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: You know, you`re such a close study of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . You`ve sat across the table from him. How should we think about his power in Russia now after this likely assassination? There was a period of time after Prigozhin made that dash to Moscow where it seemed like elites, commentators in Russia felt more comfortable speaking out against Putin, against the war. He appeared to be weak. How does his standing look to you now?\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL: Well, not only were other commentators speaking out, but Prigozhin himself said this war was a mistake. But it was basically -- he was acting because he wanted to make sure that war was won. And that was kind of part of the - the theme of his - of his revolt.\par \par \uc2 Now, other commentators, as you rightly said, including, you know, some senior generals, have been bashed -- literally bashed back and put out of the picture for saying the same kinds of things. What Putin is saying with this assassination, whether, you know, he actually carried it out or not, the message is going to be very clearly transmitted to everybody, no speaking out now. Buckle down. Everyone getting behind this campaign in Ukraine. That`s why I say I don`t think it`s really going to change the way the Russians are approaching this. And there is no room whatsoever for disloyalty.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that Putin said that was very notable when the mutiny, the push (ph), was happening two months ago, was that the traitors who carry this out will have an inevitable punishment. We`ve seen an inevitable death as a result of this that everyone was foretold. I mean we`ve all been expecting something like this for the last two months.\par \par \uc2 The message to the whole system is, don`t try anything. And even don`t criticize, I would say, at this point because we`ve seen so much action against people who have been speaking out.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And Prigozhin may not be the last to be targeted.\par \par \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2 , Russia expert, thank you so much for being here today.\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that`s it for us. Thank you for watching. For FACE THE NATION," I`m Nancy Cordes. Have a good day.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230827ej8r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 60 MINUTES, August 20, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Bill Whitaker, Sharyn Alfonsi, Jon Wertheim \par \uc2 7,864 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: 60 Minutes\par \uc2 SXMN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: We are going to the Capitol --\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Who is Ray Epps? A former member of the Oath Keepers who served in the Marine Corps and was an ardent Trump supporter, or at least he was until conservative media post-January 6 began insinuating that he was a government plant for the Deep State.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tucker Carlson Tonight, Fox News)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 : What exactly was the role of Ray Epps in the chaos of January 6th?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: No matter how many times they push this conspiracy theory, this lie, it`ll never become truth.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Come on.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): The United States is in the middle of a maternal health crisis. Today, a woman in the U.S. is twice as likely to die from pregnancy complications than her mother was a generation ago.\par \par \uc2 You`ve looked at this as a doctor, as a policymaker. What needs to be done?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: We have to prioritize motherhood, right? As a country, we have, and particularly in the Deep South, said that we`re pro-birth. If we`re really going to be pro-birth, we need to be pro-motherhood and pro- family.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Champion," Metropolitan Opera)\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): You`d be forgiven for double-checking your ticket to make sure you weren`t at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . This is New York`s Metropolitan Opera.\par \par \uc2 And in this corner, the maestro, Yannick Nezet-Seguin, only the third music director in the Met`s 140-year history.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting "Champion")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Nezet-Seguin`s eyes reach musicians in the pit and vocalists on stage, seemingly all at once. The bounce in his baton consecrates every note.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting "Champion")\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: I`m Lesley Stahl.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: I`m Bill Whitaker.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: I`m Anderson Cooper.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: I`m Sharyn Alfonsi.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: I`m Jon Wertheim.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA: I`m Cecilia Vega.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: I`m Scott Pelley. Those stories tonight on 60 MINUTES.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 WHO IS RAY EPPS?\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: For millions of consumers of conservative news, Ray Epps is a notorious villain, a provocateur responsible for turning peaceful protests on January 6th into a violent assault on the U.S. Capitol. The irony is that Epps was a passionate supporter of President Trump who went to Washington to protest the 2020 Election.\par \par \uc2 But as we first reported in April, his contradictory behavior on January 6th spawned a full-fledged conspiracy theory, casting him as a government agent who incited an insurrection. Today, Epps is in hiding after death threats forced him to sell his home.\par \par \uc2 So who is Ray Epps?\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you`ll hear from the man himself.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: As soon as President Trump is finished speaking, we are going to the Capitol. It`s that direction.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): At 6`4", in his desert camouflage, bright red Trump hat, and military-style backpack, Ray Epps stood out from the crowd on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 That`s him running toward the U.S. Capitol alongside the vanguard of rioters who first attacked and overran police.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Let`s go!\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: What do you think when you see this now?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Brings back some bad memories. It`s hard to see our Capitol under attack.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): It`s been more than two years since the storming of the Capitol. But Ray and his wife Robyn told us they relive January 6th every day of their lives.\par \par \uc2 ROBYN EPPS: You know, some people have said, well, just let it go, and let it die down.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: It doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 ROBYN EPPS: What they don`t understand is it doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 : What exactly was the role of Ray Epps in the chaos of January 6th?\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): The theory, Epps, a former member of the Oath Keepers, was an \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant who incited the crowd on January 6th, bubbled up from a right-wing news site called Revolver News run by a former Trump speechwriter.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tucker Carlson Tonight, Fox News)\par \par \uc2 DARREN BEATTIE: He is the smoking --\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 : And they have a heart attack.\par \par \uc2 DARREN BEATTIE: -- gun of the entire Fed-surrection.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): And landed on Fox News primetime --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Ingraham Angle, Fox News)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM: According to a new investigation from Revolver, Epps may have led the breach team that first entered the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): The convoluted conspiracy theory made its way to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-Florida): It`s not the Proud Boys who engaged in the initial breach. It`s Ray Epps at that precise moment.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE THOMAS MASSIE (R-Kentucky): How did Ray Epps know that there were going to be pipe bombs?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TED CRUZ (R-Texas): Miss Sanborn, who is Ray Epps?\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): That question animated former Fox News host \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  for nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tucker Carlson Tonight, Fox News)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 : Ray Epps? He is on video several times encouraging crimes, riots, breaches of the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Carlson has focused on Epps more than 20 times on his top-rated show -- a half dozen times this year.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: He`s obsessed with me. He`s going to any means possible to destroy my life and our lives.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Why?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: To shift blame on somebody else. If you look at it, Fox News, Marjorie Taylor Greene, Ted Cruz, Gaetz, they`re all telling us before this thing that it was stolen. So you tell me, who has more impact on people, them or me?\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Epps, once a loyal Fox News watcher, told us he doesn`t understand how he got cast as the villain. The Epps` version is more mundane -- they believed the 2020 Election had been stolen from Donald Trump and considered January 6th a legitimate protest.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: It was a sloppy election. And then to top that off, you have talking heads reporting that there`s problems with the voting machines and different things like that. The election`s stolen. So yeah, we had concerns. I -- I wanted to be there. I wanted to witness this with my own eyes.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Epps went to Washington with his 36-year-old son and almost immediately stepped into trouble. The conspiracy theory starts here, the night of January 5th.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Give me one minute. Give me one minute, okay?\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): On the streets of D.C., tensions were running high at a pro-Trump rally being live streamed on the internet. The Marine veteran tried to take charge.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I`m going to put it out there I`m probably going to go to jail for this, okay? Tomorrow, we need to go into the Capitol. Into the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: What?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: No, no, no --\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Peacefully.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Fed. Fed. Fed. Fed. Fed. Fed. Fed. Fed --\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): To some in the crowd, Epps seemed so over the top, he must have been a government agent -- a Fed sent to entrap them.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: When you said, "We have to go into the Capitol, we have to go into the Capitol," what were you thinking?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I said some stupid things. My thought process, we surround the Capitol, we get all the people there. I mean, I had -- I had problems with the election. You know, it was my duty as an American to peacefully protest, along with anybody else that wanted to.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): The next morning, January 6th, Epps was out by the Washington Monument, still focused on a single goal.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: We are going to the Capitol, where our problems are. It`s that direction.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We`re going to walk down to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): While President Trump was still speaking at the Ellipse, Ray Epps walked toward the Capitol. He told us he wanted to be up front to help keep the peace. What happened next at Peace Circle where protesters first overran police is seen as a smoking gun. Epps pulled this agitated rioter aside and said something. Conspiracists say he was giving marching orders, because seconds later, this happened -- the first \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officer goes down.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Whoa, whoa, whoa, hey, hey, hey.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: As closely as you can remember, what exactly did you say to him?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: "Dude, we`re not here for that. The police aren`t the enemy," something like that.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Did anyone from the federal government direct you to be here at the Peace Circle at this time?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: No.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: No one from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: No.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Your old comrades with the Oath Keepers?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: No.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: I think what is so damning about the video is that there`s a barrier there. The barrier gets knocked down. And a police officer, a female police officer gets knocked down. And the mob, including you, walk over the barrier and march on --\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: -- toward the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Why didn`t you stop to help this police officer who was -- who was knocked over?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: When she was knocked down and I started to go towards her to help her up, and I saw a billy club over here in the corner of my eye. And I thought, "You know, they`re going to think I`m part of this." So I backed off.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: You were part of it.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I was there. I wasn`t a part of that, knocking her down.\par \par \uc2 ROBYN EPPS: And he wasn`t part of the violence. There`s a big difference there.\par \par \uc2 (Protesters shouting)\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Is that you there?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Ray Epps was never seen committing an act of violence that day, or entering the Capitol. Epps told us when he saw the violence, his fervor to enter the building became a desire to play peacemaker.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Hey, guys. Guys, they`re not the enemy. They`re not the enemy. Appreciate you guys.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): And police body cam video backs him up.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I thought I could stop it. So I went back and forth. I talked people down, and worked the line back and forth. "Step down. Step down. We`re good here," that kind of thing. And I kept it that way for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 Maybe step back. We`re holding ground. We`re not trying to get people hurt. They don`t want to get hurt, you don`t want to get hurt. Just back off.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Epps says he left the Capitol grounds to help evacuate an injured man. The time, 2:54 p.m.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I looked back at the Capitol. And there was people crawling up the Capitol walls. And it looked like -- it looked terrible. I mean, I was kind of ashamed of what -- what was going on at that point. So I -- I started to walk out.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): He told us that`s when he sent this text to his nephew. Conspiracists saw it as the true confession of an agent provocateur.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: "I was in front with a few others. I also orchestrated it." Explain this to me.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I was boasting to my nephew. I helped get people there. I -- I was directing people to the Capitol that morning.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: You know how this sounds?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I know exactly how it sounds. I`ve been scolded by my wife for using that word. I shouldn`t have used that word.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: When you add up all of these things, as your critics have done, you`ve given them a lot of ammunition to paint you as this instigator.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: There was an effort to make me the scapegoat.\par \par \uc2 TOM JOSCELYN: If Ray Epps was a covert plant, he is the worst covert plant of all time. If you are part of some elaborate conspiracy against thousands of people in Washington, D.C., I don`t know why you`d want to stand out from the crowd the way Ray Epps did.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Tom Joscelyn is a researcher and author, one of the country`s top terrorism experts, tapped by the January 6th Committee to help write its final report, which found evidence far-right extremists like the Proud Boys planned and executed the breach of the Capitol. He says the committee interviewed Epps and found he wasn`t important enough to put in the report.\par \par \uc2 TOM JOSCELYN: I wouldn`t defend Ray Epps or anybody else who was on the Capitol grounds that day. I would just defend the facts. So the idea that he`s leading the charge or really orchestrating it is just contradicted by this mountain range of evidence.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what the conspiracy theorists want you -- want you to do, right? They don`t want you to look at this mountain range of evidence. They want you to turn around and focus on this pebble on the ground named Ray Epps. They also don`t want you to look at what President Trump was saying and doing.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): He calls Epps` behavior baffling, but not evidence of a conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 TOM JOSCELYN: They`ve got to come up with some sort of connective tissue between Ray Epps and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And they`ve got none. And so they can make up all sorts of ad hoc arguments to justify their beliefs, but that`s all they are. It`s not actual investigative work. It`s not actual evidence.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): The January 6th Committee looked at the evidence -- video, phone records, travel receipts, so did the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . When Epps got back to Arizona on January 8th, a relative told him he was on an \uc2 FBI\uc2  poster "seeking information" about certain rioters.\par \par \uc2 ROBYN EPPS: We literally hung up the phone and walked right into the house, sat down and called the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Do you remember what you said to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: Told them who I was, and that I would cooperate in any way I could. I didn`t break any laws.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Two months later, he met with agents.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: So when we met with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , I mean, it was like, "Oh, finally, we`re going to clear this up." There was no, "I take the Fifth." There was none of that. It was just like we`re talking right now. I went through everything. And they had a lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 In the summer of 2021, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took his picture off the bureau`s website. Epps thought that would end his troubles. But it only added fuel to the conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tucker Carlson Tonight, Fox News)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 : A new piece in "Revolver News" notes that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  removed a photo of Ray Epps from its Most Wanted page this summer.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : How about the one guy? Go in, go in. Get in there, everybody. Epps. Get in there. Go. Go. Go. Nothing happens to him.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): The Epps would dispute that. After former President Trump mentioned Epps by name, harassment and death threats picked up.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: "I pray," to come to you to kill you. What do you think when you open a letter like that?\par \par \uc2 ROBYN EPPS: Scares me to death.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): It got so bad they were forced to sell their 5- acre ranch outside Phoenix. They`re now in hiding, living in this 300- square foot recreational vehicle, somewhere in the Rocky Mountains -- we agreed not to disclose exactly where.\par \par \uc2 ROBYN EPPS: It`s so sad what people have done to Ray, and to us, and to our lives. Sometimes, I`ve used my maiden name just so that we don`t call attention.\par \par \uc2 RAY EPPS: I have a hard time, being a man, being on the -- being a Marine, being on the run. I -- I had to do the necessary things to keep my family safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  issued a statement to 60 MINUTES in April saying, quote, "Ray Epps has never been an \uc2 FBI\uc2  source or an \uc2 FBI\uc2  employee." Last month, Epps sued Fox News for defamation, accusing former host, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , of promoting a reckless conspiracy theory. Carlson was fired by Fox News the day after this report first aired.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 THE DOMINO EFFECT\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: The United States is in the middle of a maternal health crisis. Today, a woman in the U.S. is twice as likely to die from pregnancy complications than her mother was a generation ago.\par \par \uc2 Statistics from the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  show the United States has one of the highest rates of maternal death in the developed world. Women in the U.S. are 10 or more times likely to die from pregnancy-related causes than mothers in Poland, Spain or Norway.\par \par \uc2 As we first reported this past April, some of the worst statistics come out of the South -- in places like Louisiana, where deep pockets of poverty, health care deserts and racial biases have long put mothers at risk.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you`ll hear from some of the women trying to improve maternal health care in Louisiana and why they say last summer`s abortion ban set off a `domino effect`, making a bad situation, worse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: The state of maternal health in the United States is abysmal. And Louisiana is the highest maternal mortality in the U.S. So, in the developed world, Louisiana has the worst outcomes for women having babies.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Hello. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): As an OB-GYN, Louisiana`s former state secretary of health, and founder of Nest, a primary health service for families, Dr. \uc2 Rebekah Gee\uc2  spent a career advocating for better maternal care.\par \par \uc2 Renowned for its rich culture and legendary celebrations, Louisiana also holds the distinction of being one of the riskiest places in the country to give birth.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-nine out of every 100,000 mothers in Louisiana die during or shortly after childbirth.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: How did we get here? Why is Louisiana in this position?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: The high C-section rates have contributed, the lack of access to well woman care before and after pregnancies. Fifty percent of the time, women don`t get that postpartum care, which means they have untreated hypertension, untreated diabetes, untreated depression. The fact that we have racial bias in health care. And so all of these things are compounded especially worse for low-income women.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): As Louisiana`s secretary of health, Dr. Gee helped expand postpartum Medicaid coverage and pushed the state to track how race impacted care and maternal outcomes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Was it under-reported?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: Absolutely. In the prior administration, when I was medical director, we were told we were not allowed to show data that showed health disparities. There was a real --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Because why?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: Because the political establishment didn`t want to admit that there were disparities.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): A state board now reviews and reports every maternal death in Louisiana. Dr. Gee worked with that board and found the results especially upsetting. Data showed 80 percent of maternal deaths in the state were potentially preventable.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You`ve looked at this as a doctor, as a policymaker. What needs to be done?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: We have to prioritize motherhood, right? As a country, we have, and particularly in the Deep South, said that we`re pro-birth. If we`re really going to be pro-birth, we need to be pro-motherhood and pro- family, right? So --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What does that look like?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: Making sure that women have time off to get their medical appointments, making sure that we have affordable childcare, making sure that women have access to well woman care.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Access is a big hurdle for many women in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So what`s it like to raise a family here?\par \par \uc2 THERESA DUBOIS: Uh, a struggle.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Twenty-eight-year-old Theresa Dubois and 32- year-old Brittany Cavalier are both married, and mothers of two.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): They`re expecting their third babies this summer.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: Who`s going to win?\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: Me.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Brittany runs the local day care that Theresa`s younger daughter attends.\par \par \uc2 They live in Assumption Parish, a rural county of 21,000 where sugarcane is plentiful and doctors are scarce.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So to give us a sense of kind of where we are, how close is the nearest pediatrician?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: Almost 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: OB-GYN?\par \par \uc2 THERESA DUBOIS: An hour and 35 minutes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: When you have to go to the OB-GYN and it takes you an hour and a half --\par \par \uc2 THERESA DUBOIS: Oh, it`s a nightmare. It`s a lot. It`s a lot emotionally. It`s a lot in the car. It`s a lot on your body, just waiting that long to get help.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): A third of Louisiana`s parishes are maternal health deserts -- meaning they don`t have a single OB-GYN, leaving more than 51,000 women in the state without easy access to care and three times more likely to die of pregnancy-related causes.\par \par \uc2 The only hospital in Assumption isn`t equipped to deliver babies. So Cavalier and Dubois have to travel more than an hour to get to hospitals in Baton Rouge to give birth -- a harrowing journey when there`s an emergency.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: I mean, we are supposed to be one of the best countries in the world and you`re just leaving the women out there to dry.\par \par \uc2 LATONA GIWA: Starting with the most marginalized --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Latona Giwa saw those disparities when she worked as a delivery room nurse. In 2011, she co-founded the New Orleans- based Birthmark Doula Collective.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Hey, Trinity (sp?). How are you?\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Doulas provide emotional and physical support before, during and after childbirth. Last year, the Collective worked with 2,000 mothers.\par \par \uc2 LATONA GIWA: We work with the most marginalized families who are most at risk for poor birth outcomes. And we prioritize working with black and brown families, with low-income families.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): In Louisiana, black women are up to four times more likely than white women to die during or after childbirth.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What do you see in your line of work that black and brown women are facing when they`re pregnant versus a white woman?\par \par \uc2 LATONA GIWA: We live in a country that does not guarantee insurance coverage and health care to everyone. There is different and discriminatory care. Black and brown people are more likely to be on Medicaid. They`re going to practices that are busier, that take more patients. And that`s where the Doula comes in.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: So you have your baby laying --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Studies show better birth outcomes for black women who`ve had Doula care.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: There you go. Good job.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Birthmark`s work in Louisiana caught the attention of Every Mother Counts.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: How are you?\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): A maternal advocacy group founded by model Christy Turlington after she suffered complications with the birth of her daughter in 2003.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: I hemorrhaged. There was a lot of blood. There was a whole sort of stream of things that needed to happen.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Really scary.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: Scary and painful. But it was when I got home and really started to think, what about everyone else in the world that this happens to but doesn`t have that team of care working together, understanding what`s happening and actively managing it?\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Those questions led Turlington around the world to document the challenges women face giving birth -- stories of midwives in Haiti and mothers in Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: How do you compare U.S. maternal health care to maternal health care in the rest of the world?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: Well, the U.S. is one of eight countries that have actually had an increase in maternal mortality. So we`re certainly at the bottom rung.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Last year, Every Mother Counts distributed more than a million dollars to groups focused on strengthening maternal care in the U.S., a mission that became even more difficult last summer.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Whose body?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My body!\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Whose choice?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My choice!!\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): After Roe versus Wade was overturned by the Supreme Court in June, Louisiana implemented a sweeping abortion ban. The ban set off a domino effect across the state, impacting women like Kaitlyn Joshua, a community organizer.\par \par \uc2 Joshua and her husband were thrilled to learn she was pregnant last summer. The couple have a 4-year-old daughter and were looking forward to expanding their family.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: We started experiencing cramping. I, you know, lost a lot of blood.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): She told us she went to Woman`s Hospital in Baton Rouge where they did an ultrasound, examined and monitored her. Joshua says, that`s where the treatment ended.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: And so I said, "Okay. So is this a miscarriage?" And the young lady, she said, "I can`t really tell you that right now. I don`t know." And I said, "Well, what do you mean you don`t know? We did the ultrasound." I recall her saying, "We`re just sending you home with prayers. We`re going to hope for the best."\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So you`re in pain.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You think you`re miscarrying, and they say, "We`re going to send you home with prayers?"\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Yes. You know, we`re Christians, too, like we pray. But it just was very insulting in the moment just because women come there for answers. So, it would have been nice to get a definite response.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Woman`s Hospital told 60 MINUTES, "It`s complex. When diagnosis of early pregnancy loss is unclear, the standard of care is to wait."\par \par \uc2 The next day, Kaitlyn Joshua told us her pain became unbearable. So she sought care from a second hospital, Baton Rouge General, where a doctor ordered another ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: She straight up said, "This doesn`t look like a baby at all. Are you sure you were ever pregnant? This just looks like a cyst."\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Wait. She --\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: She questioned whether you were pregnant or not?\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Yeah, absolutely. And they discharged me maybe within an hour and a half or so after monitoring me. And on the paperwork it literally said, "potential miscarriage" or "possible miscarriage," but nothing definite.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Knowing what you know now about what your body was going through, could you have died?\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: When I finally did get care from a midwife on, like, day five, she said that you certainly could have died. She said, "The amount of blood you lost, the amount of fluids that you were passing was a lot for someone in such a short period of time."\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Why do you think there was such ferocious pushback from not one, but two hospitals?\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: I -- I just have to believe that it is just the vagueness of the abortion ban in this state that`s caused so much fear around physicians doing their job.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): We reached out to Baton Rouge General. They told us, "Every patient is different," and that since the ban, they have "not changed the way they manage miscarriage or the options available" to treat them.\par \par \uc2 The hospital left Joshua with one option -- to take Tylenol and monitor for worsening symptoms.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, some physicians are now afraid to offer methods typically used to treat miscarriages because those same methods are used in abortion and could be seen as illegal, potentially landing health care providers in jail.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: To be clear, you were not seeking abortion.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You were trying to have a healthy baby. You needed care and nobody would touch you.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Absolutely. I think a lot of times we fail to realize the intersectionality between maternal health care, reproductive justice and abortion care. Until we understand that all of those things interconnect, we probably will not see change any time soon.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: We take an oath to do no harm, and that`s really our north star as a physician. But when the prospect of doing that might cause you to be brought up on criminal charges, that`s a really difficult place for our physicians to be.\par \par \uc2 You know, what`s drastic is --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Dr. Jennifer Avegno has been an ER doctor for 22 years and is the current Director of the New Orleans Health Department.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, Attorney General \uc2 Jeff Landry\uc2  sent a letter to doctors about the new abortion ban that Avegno says paralyzed maternal health care across the state.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: The letter was something that I`ve never seen before as a practicing physician. A non-medical layperson inserting themselves into medical care. And there was a direct line about any physician who violates this will lose their liberty and medical license. And so really, it was a threat.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What kind of criminal penalties does a doctor face?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: At least one year and up to 10 years of imprisonment with hard labor.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: This is about going to jail -\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: This is about going to jail. Doctors don`t want to be in a war with their own state. They want to be able to just practice.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): As the New Orleans Health Department director, Dr. Avegno has seen how doctors are struggling to interpret the language of Louisiana`s new abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: What they`re being told is, well, you can consult with the hospital attorney. But I don`t know of any other disease or process where routinely you`re being told, we`ll get the hospital attorney involved.\par \par \uc2 That also brings up, what if the attorney advises something that the physician really feels is harmful. So really, what our physicians are facing is a terrible choice to make decisions that might not be in the best interest of the patient or risk going to jail.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: We reached out to three urban hospitals, rural hospitals, providers. And they said, "We`d love to talk to you, but we`re afraid."\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: Yes. That has been the consistent echo from providers. There are several providers I think that would love to speak out but were told by their hospitals that it`s too risky.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So why are you sitting here today?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: I am concerned that we are going to see a worsening of our morbidity and mortality rates, simply because of access and simply because of fear.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 CBS\uc2  SPORTS HQ)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Yannick Nezet-Seguin is a conductor operating at triple tempo, at once the director of three major orchestras -- in Philadelphia, in his hometown of Montreal and at the Metropolitan Opera in New York, which has pinned on him the hopes for rebounding from financial crisis and the bold revamping of its artistic mission. Not that you`ll catch Nezet- Seguin sweating it.\par \par \uc2 At 48, he`s obliterating the stereotype of the strict, unapproachable maestro -- joyfully keeping his musicians in time, his hair bleached platinum blonde and a smile all but welded to his face. Known as much for his versatility as his virtuosity, Nezet-Seguin, as we first told you in May, is reimagining the role of the modern orchestra and its place in the soundtrack of the modern city.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Champion", Metropolitan Opera)\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): You`d be forgiven for double-checking your ticket to make sure you weren`t at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . This is New York`s Metropolitan Opera.\par \par \uc2 And in this corner, the maestro, Yannick Nezet-Seguin, only the third music director in the Met`s 140-year history.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting "Champion")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): He`s conducting a jazz-infused opera about a real-life boxing champion of the 1960s and `70s. All this is a radical key change from the standard Met lineup of Puccini and Verdi and Wagner.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting "Champion")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Nezet-Seguin`s eyes reach musicians in the pit and vocalists on stage, seemingly all at once. The bounce in his baton consecrates every note.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting "Champion")\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: It`s the cathartic moment where, "Oh my god, this is so amazing. This is so impressive. This is so intense. This is so emotional." And this cathartic experience we feel transformed as a different person before and after listening to these pieces.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: There seems to be something sacred about your making music. Am I overstating that?\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: No. No, you`re not. I try never to take myself too seriously. But music has to be taken seriously.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting an orchestra)\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): A pianist by training who`s conducted the great philharmonics of Europe, Nezet-Seguin brings his sensibility to bear up and down the Eastern Seaboard. In Philadelphia, he is the current custodian of what`s been a world-renowned orchestra for more than a century. We visited in February, as the city celebrated the contract extension through 2030 of a maestro known to all by his first name.\par \par \uc2 ALL: Yannick.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): That morning, Yannick and his musicians made the pilgrimage up the famed Rocky steps, clad in Eagles gear. He sees the orchestra -- not unlike a sports team -- as an instrument of cohesion in the community.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Hey. Hi, everyone. Hi.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): In the afternoon, Nezet-Seguin stopped by Philly`s premier performing arts high school to guest conduct the students.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin guest-conducting students)\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: I think if we were to agree to bite even more in every note like TA-TA-TA-TA-TA-TA-TA-TA-TA-TA --\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin guest-conducting students)\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: That`s it. That`s what I`m talking about.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Four hundred and fifty miles due north, back home in Montreal --\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: (Inaudible) crescendo --\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): -- they speak his language in more ways than one. He has a lifetime contract with the Orchestre Metropolitain. Nezet-Seguin has led these musicians for more than 20 years. That`s his husband, Pierre Tourville, on viola. The depth of his connection here produces hypnotic sound. Listen to this, a rehearsal of Sibelius` "Fifth Symphony."\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting rehearsal of Sibelius` "Fifth Symphony)\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Some weeks, Yannick leads performances in all three cities. Just last Saturday, he conducted a matinee in New York and the evening program in Philly. Then again, what is a conductor if not someone able to synchronize?\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: I can`t deny that it`s a very demanding schedule. And even the word "schedule" if, should I ever retire, I want to ban that word from my life.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Do you have a favorite child among the three orchestras you conduct?\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: You cannot ask me this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting a rehearsal of Wagner`s "Lohengrin")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Yet one child in particular -- and isn`t this always the case -- demands extra attention and TLC.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Can we have more importance in the third --\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): At the Met, the country`s largest performing arts institution, we watched him wrangle 250 musicians and singers for a production of Wagner`s "Lohengrin," a stalwart opera that gave us the "Bridal Chorus."\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting a rehearsal of Wagner`s "Lohengrin")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): And Nezet-Seguin`s interpretations do right by the masters.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: What I want is to respect what Wagner wrote in the score.\par \par \uc2 So it`s very poetic and magical. What`s really individual is me being completely at the service of the composer.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Yannick took over the podium here in 2018, after his predecessor was fired for allegations of sexual misconduct. Facing weak ticket sales and perpetually strapped for cash, the Met has had a hard time rebounding from COVID and has dipped into its endowment to cover around 10 percent of its $300 million operating budget.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Maestro to the pit, please.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): They`ve tried courting a younger, more diverse crowd here before, but Nezet-Seguin is doing it prestissimo, speeding up the tempo of change. He`s betting that new composers and contemporary operas -- some adapted from bestselling books and Hollywood hits -- will bring in a wider audience and shore up the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 And early results suggest he`s right.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from the opera, "Fire Shut Up in My Bones")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): A recent premiere of the opera "Fire Shut Up in My Bones" -- based on a memoir by journalist Charles Blow and composed by jazz legend, Terence Blanchard -- sold-out and outsold Verdi`s classic "Rigoletto" here that season. What`s more, half the seats were filled by first-time Met goers.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: It`s great, such a success. And yet I`m thinking that means that all of these people never felt compelled, or dare I say, welcomed to come here. And that is my mission.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: First timers were 50 percent. You saw that as both a triumph but also sounds like an indictment.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Mostly, it`s a tremendous encouragement to continue in that vein.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Champion")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): The Met will put on 17 new and recent works over the next five seasons -- this at a place that once went near decades without staging a new opera. This spring brought "Champion," another Terence Blanchard composition based on the complicated life of Emile Griffith, a bisexual prizefighter.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Champion")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: What can`t you write an opera about?\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: How do you thread that needle between experimenting and bringing in new audiences, but also not upsetting the traditionalists?\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: The truth is I`m not necessarily concerned about not upsetting traditionalists. I think people who love our art form are still going to love it because we`re still going to play some Puccini and Verdi. To me, it`s -- it`s never about not upsetting. And if some people are upset, well, too bad. They just don`t have to come to everything we do.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): And that`s about as prickly as you`ll hear him get. Beyond Yannick`s musicianship, his real hallmark might be his light touch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting rehearsal for "Champion")\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Okay, let`s do it one more time.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Take this rehearsal for "Champion," the first time the orchestra, rhythm section, and singers played the piece together, but also that rare collaboration between a conductor and a living composer.\par \par \uc2 TERENCE BLANCHARD: He gets it.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Terence Blanchard, a seven-time Grammy winner, is the first Black composer in the Met`s history.\par \par \uc2 TERENCE BLANCHARD: He gets the story. He gets the whole notion of bringing these different styles of music together.\par \par \uc2 One of the rehearsals with the orchestra, he said, "Listen, don`t follow me, listen to the drums." Most conductors don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting rehearsal for "Champion")\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: I`m there for you.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: (Inaudible) --\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Okay, cool.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): We saw firsthand Yannick`s responsiveness when soprano Latonia Moore asked him to slow things down.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting rehearsal for "Champion")\par \par \uc2 LATONIA MOORE: Can we start again? It`s too fast. It`s too fast. Can we do it a hair slower? I just want to really get everything out.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Yeah. So, right from the bass.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Is there a worry, though, that your authority might be undercut by all these good vibes?\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: I think it`s the old concept of authority, which is because I say so and because I can fire you, I`m going to tell you what to do. That`s not working anymore. And I will go even further.\par \par \uc2 Even the masters of the past, Toscanini, for example, fantastic musician. Of course, I respect a lot of this. But you can hear with some recordings with Toscanini, especially with singers, you could hear the fear in their voice. And to me, that`s not expressive.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: You can discern that?\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Oh yeah. Oh, I`m sure you could. Like they`re trembling, because they fear because already, to open your mouth on the stage is already nerve-wracking. If you have a conductor who is waiting for you to fail, it`s going to be even more nerve-wracking. The music doesn`t win.\par \par \uc2 The music wins when everybody feels free to express who they are.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting an imaginary orchestra in grade school)\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Yannick found that freedom for himself as early as grade school. Here he is leading an imaginary orchestra in front of classmates. He studied at the conservatory in Montreal, a music town with a church on every corner. He led the church choir in his teens and trained his ear in less formal ways, too.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: I would go to a record store and buy CDs and discover repertoire after repertoire, every symphony by Brahms, every symphony by Beethoven, by Bruckner, by Mahler. I wanted then to hear every version and buy every version of every symphony. And I`m not shy to say that it -- it`s shaped a lot of who I am as a musician. Because eventually, it became a collection of, like, 12,000 CDs.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Last winter, we visited his parents at Yannick`s childhood home, the kind of folks who keep every clipping.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Yannick told us when he was a boy, he collected thousands and thousands, maybe 10,000 CDs.\par \par \uc2 SERGE P. SEGUIN: Yeah --\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE NEZET: Oh, more than that.\par \par \uc2 SERGE P. SEGUIN: Yeah, that`s --\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: He wasn`t exaggerating?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE NEZET: Oh, no, no, no --\par \par \uc2 SERGE P. SEGUIN: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE NEZET: -- not at all.\par \par \uc2 SERGE P. SEGUIN: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE NEZET: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Where did you put them?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDINE NEZET: He made the shelves for him.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): And it was his mom who managed Yannick`s schedule throughout his 30s, as he worked the international circuit -- London, Vienna, Rotterdam -- guest conducting 100 orchestras.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin`s conducting debut in Met)\par \par \uc2 He made his Met debut in 2009; persistence paying off. And if today, he`s living the dream, he`s also conscious to live his life. He and Pierre go on the road together. We met them at a spot in Old Montreal, where they had celebrated their wedding. They make a point of going out after every performance.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Friends, family, members of the orchestra --\par \par \uc2 PIERRE TOURVILLE: Colleagues --\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Colleagues, some people together to eat well and drink a little.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "This Is Your Life")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Of all the musical moments in the couple`s history, we were surprised when Yannick highlighted this one --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "This Is Your Life")\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): -- the time Pierre and Celine Dion serenaded him on a popular Quebec talk show, a kind of musical "This Is Your Life."\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Of course, it`s one of the most moving moments of my life.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE TOURVILLE: Not everybody can say that they`ve sung a --\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Duet --\par \par \uc2 PIERRE TOURVILLE: -- duet with Celine --\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: You`ve had a duet --\par \par \uc2 PIERRE TOURVILLE: -- Dion.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: -- with Celine Dion.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: Bocelli and Barbra Streisand --\par \par \uc2 PIERRE TOURVILLE: Yeah --\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: And that`s --\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: And Pierre --\par \par \uc2 PIERRE TOURVILLE: -- Pavarotti.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: -- and Pierre and Pavarotti.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: Wow\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): Maybe it`s a hard turn from Celine to the Met for most conductors, not so for Yannick Nezet-Seguin. Yes, he takes music seriously, but resists being too precious about it.\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: You have to trust the acoustics.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): He`s too busy smudging boundaries that have long kept some audiences away from classical music and opera.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Yannick Nezet-Seguin conducting an opera)\par \par \uc2 YANNICK NEZET-SEGUIN: I would love that when I finish my time on Earth that no one ever says again, "Oh, classical music is not for me. It`s for the educated. It`s for the rich. It`s for the white." Whatever it is, you know, I want everyone to feel, "Oh yeah, I could like it, some of it. I don`t like Mozart but I like Blanchard." Fine, but at least you feel that you could go there because there would be something for you.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM (voiceover): What would Puccini and Verdi and Wagner make of all this? One suspects they would join in the applause. They, after all, were once musical boundary-pushers, too.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: For a look at how 60 MINUTES reports its stories, go to 60minutesovertime.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: I`m Bill Whitaker. We will be back next week with another edition of 60 MINUTES.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SXMN000020230821ej8k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for August 9, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett,Jim Axelrod, Cristian Benavides, Caitlin Huey- Burns,Robert Costa, Ed O`Keefe, Mark Strassmann, Catherine Herridge \par \uc2 3,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Breaking news: at least six people killed and dozens injured as wildfires rage on the Hawaiian island of Maui, some people jumping into the ocean to escape the flames, as rescue crews desperately search for survivors.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane-force winds fuel the deadly fires, as thousands are evacuated after flames engulf a popular tourist town.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR SYLVIA LUKE (D-Hawaii): We need to be brave for each other.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A Utah man is killed during an \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid after allegedly threatening President Biden.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A tornado touched down in Northeast Colorado. It was one of two tornadoes that hit the area.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: New details on a search warrant into former President Trump`s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, as a key memo reveals new information about a plot to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We did it!\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Supporters of abortion rights in Ohio are claiming a huge victory after voters rejected a measure that would have made it harder to amend the state Constitution.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We beat the brakes off of them! Yes!\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: A group of LGBTQ veterans who were kicked out of the military because of their sexuality are now suing the Department of Defense, hoping to change what are currently discriminatory discharges to honorable.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL FARRELL (Former U.S. Navy Service Member): My country is telling me I`m not good enough to serve just because of who I love.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Someone at this supermarket won the $1.58 billion Mega Millions jackpot.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: It`s going to change the whole town. I think it is going to turn upside down from this. We have no idea.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We honor a true hero of our nation.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And a Vietnam War veteran receives long overdue recognition.\par \par \uc2 COL. PARIS DAVIS (RET.) (Medal of Honor Recipient): Never forget who we are and what America stands for. And when you do that, America is a better place to be.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening, everyone. Thank you for joining us. I`m Major Garrett, in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with wildfires in Hawaii that have people fleeing for their lives. The fast-spreading flames have engulfed entire towns on the island of Maui, ripping through historic tourist destinations, burning everything in sight.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of homes and businesses are feared destroyed, as winds from Hurricane Dora have turned small brushfires into raging infernos. A state of emergency has been declared, and officials are preparing to ask for federal funding as soon as they get a better idea of the scale of destruction.\par \par \uc2 FEMA and the military have been called in to help. At least six people have died and 20 are seriously injured. The death toll is expected to rise. Thousands are staying in shelters, and officials are discouraging all nonessential travel from the mainland, at least for the next few days.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1^ (EXPLETIVE DELETED) damn it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Iconic Lahaina, a paradise lost.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Let`s go! Go.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Damn, it`s hot.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Winds from a hurricane south of Maui with gusts up to 70 miles per hour pushed flames that raced across the island and fueled several large wildfires.\par \par \uc2 In a horrifying instant, a wall of fire charred a piece of Maui all the way to the ocean. The Coast Guard rescued at least a dozen people from these waters, their only refuge with flames menacing all around.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Somebody is down right here.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: Yes, somebody is down. We cannot do nothing for her.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Thousands of people were evacuated, including Dan Sonnesyn. He shot these images today on Maui, a horizon of smoke and flames, his vacationing family of three from Colorado now stuck sleeping in their car.\par \par \uc2 DAN SONNESYN (Tourist): It was awful, for all of the natives, especially. And you lose a place like Lahaina, it`s so historic.\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Daybreak showed the scope of the calamity. Flames had wiped out hundreds of homes and businesses and Hawaiian history.\par \par \uc2 Lahaina, a mecca for tourists, used to be the capital of Hawaii`s ancient kingdom and a major fishing port.\par \par \uc2 DAN SONNESYN: This is such a cool old town. It`s really tough to think about.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Witnesses say Lahaina lies in scorched ruins.\par \par \uc2 MAN #7: Everything is (EXPLETIVE DELETED) gone.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR SYLVIA LUKE (D-Hawaii): This is a time we need help. We need to be -- stand together. We need to be brave for each other. We are just struck by the devastation felt on Maui.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Many Maui residents have lost everything. Shelters are overrun, emergency resources pushed to the brink.\par \par \uc2 And more than 4,000 tourists are now scrambling to get off the island far away from paradise -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As so many officials said, there was no way to prepare.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to some breaking news. A Utah man was shot and killed earlier this morning during an \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid on his home.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe reports that the agents were attempting to serve an arrest warrant after the man allegedly threatened to kill President Biden, his family, and several others, including former President Obama.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): According to court filings, just three days ago, Craig Robertson posted that he heard President Biden was coming to Utah.\par \par \uc2 Robertson made a threat, saying he would be cleaning the dust off the M24 sniper rifle.\par \par \uc2 A.T. Smith is a former Secret Service deputy director and CBS News consultant.\par \par \uc2 A.T. SMITH: I think this is about as extreme as it gets, when we are talking about someone like this, who is either a sole participant or a lone wolf.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Early Wednesday, sources tell CBS News an \uc2 FBI\uc2  team tried to arrest Robertson at his Provo, Utah, home, less than an hour`s drive from where the president is traveling tonight.\par \par \uc2 Some kind of confrontation ensued, and Robertson died in a shoot-out. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  had been investigating Robertson for five months for allegedly making threats against other officials, among them, Manhattan district attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , New York Attorney General Letitia James, and Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , all of them overseeing criminal or civil proceedings against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In March, using TRUTH Social, a platform favored by Trump, Robertson said he would be traveling to New York to fulfill his dream of killing Bragg. The criminal complaint includes allegations of more than two dozen other threats against Vice President Harris, prosecutors, and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 A.T. SMITH: There is a line to be crossed, and, clearly, this individual crossed that line, and that is why the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was there today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Neighbors tell CBS News Robertson was active in his church, pro-Second Amendment, and someone known to have a -- quote -- "arsenal of weapons" in his home.\par \par \uc2 A White House official says the president was briefed on the case this morning shortly after the shoot-out. The Secret Service referred all questions on the case to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Ed O`Keefe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Thunderstorms, baseball-sized hail and at least two tornadoes hit Northeastern Colorado last night. This massive funnel cloud tore a path through Yuma County, damaging homes and businesses. It also toppled telephone poles and power lines. Authorities are going door to door to check on residents, but, so far, no injuries have been reported.\par \par \uc2 Now to the January 6 investigation. Tonight, we are learning new details about a Trump campaign memo that lays out a post-election plot to use fake electors to block then-president-elect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from taking office.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa also reports, the investigation now includes former President Trump`s social media account.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Court documents tonight reveal the special counsel obtained a search warrant for former President Donald Trump`s Twitter account, seeking more information as it continues to investigate the attempt to overthrow the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Trump responded immediately on social media, calling the search warrant "a major hit on my civil rights."\par \par \uc2 As prosecutors continue to build their case, we are learning new details about the Justice Department`s probe into Trump`s conduct in and around January 6. A memo authored by Trump lawyer Ken Chesebro written in 2020 and first reported by "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " outlines a plot to encourage the creation of false slates of Trump electors in battleground states where Trump lost.\par \par \uc2 The idea was to compel Congress to consider those false slates on January 6, when it certified the election. In the memo, Chesebro writes that letting matters play out this way would enable Trump to deprive Biden of electoral votes, but he also acknowledged the proposal as a bold, controversial strategy that would likely be rejected by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Chesebro did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN (Former U.S. Attorney): Chesebro was the architect of a strategy to just keep Biden from getting to the magic number of 270, doing something that he knew was almost certainly unlawful to buy time.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But the effort fell short of pushing then-Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to disavow certifying the 2020 election. Pence has since become a key witness in the investigation.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE (R-Presidential Candidate): President Trump asked me to put him over the Constitution, but I chose the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: CBS News has learned Chesebro is also likely one of the co- conspirators in the Trump indictment, but has not been charged.\par \par \uc2 The next standoff in this case comes Friday. A hearing will be held over what Trump can and cannot say about the investigation. This comes after prosecutors sounded the alarm about comments the former president made on the trail and on social media -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A key word in that memo, "deprive."\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to a closely watched battle over abortion. Supporters of abortion rights in Ohio are claiming victory after voters overwhelmingly rejected a ballot measure that would have made it more difficult to add abortion protections to the state`s Constitution.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns reports on the political impact of the vote.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ohio, we did it. We did it!\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Voter turnout far exceeded expectations for a summer special election that sent a message across the country.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They underestimated Ohio.\par \par \uc2 And what -- and they have awakened a sleeping giant.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: At issue, whether to raise the threshold to amend the state Constitution from a simple majority, 50 percent plus one vote, to 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, including Secretary of State \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , had hoped to implement the change ahead of November, when enshrining abortion protections into the state Constitution will be on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2  (R-Ohio Secretary of State): Of course, we don`t want to see a really radical abortion amendment put in our state Constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Changing the rules at this stage of the game, isn`t that stacking the deck?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE FRANK LAROSE: No.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But 57 percent of Ohio voters disagreed. Voters rejected the measure even in areas former President Trump won in 2020. And in each of Ohio`s 88 counties, support for the proposal was less than the Trump vote.\par \par \uc2 Republican strategist Maura Gillespie says the results are a warning sign for her party.\par \par \uc2 MAURA GILLESPIE (GOP Strategist): I think it speaks to this issue being a losing issue for Republicans. They are never going to win if they continue to isolate moderate Republicans, independents, and many in their party who maybe aren`t the loudest voices.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But Ohio Republicans aren`t backing away.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE FRANK LAROSE: We lost one battle, but the war continues, and I have just begun to fight.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And Ohioans will vote directly on abortion rights this November with that constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 And there are efforts under way in other states to get this issue on the ballot next year, a presidential year, and also in that big battleground of Arizona -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A four-month drought without anyone hitting the Mega Millions jackpot ended last night. The very lucky someone purchased a single winning ticket worth nearly $1.6 billion at a \uc2 Publix\uc2  grocery store in Neptune Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Cristian Benavides is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mega Millions)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Let`s see if I can make you a billionaire tonight.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voiceover): An instant billionaire woke up in Neptune Beach, Florida, today, while millions were disappointed to find they weren`t so lucky.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I would have been glad to win $5.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The winner won $1.58 billion, buying the single ticket at this \uc2 Publix\uc2  grocery store.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I could have won. That could have been me.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: This is the third largest jackpot in U.S. history. The lucky winner can now afford to buy just about anything, like 56 Rolls-Royce Boat Tails, nearly 170,000 diamond rings, and six penthouse apartments overlooking New York`s Central Park.\par \par \uc2 Now lottery players are flocking to this store, hoping to strike gold themselves.\par \par \uc2 So you think the store is going to get lucky twice?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: It`s a possibility. Other places have.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Now, the winner has 60 days to decide how they want to receive their winnings.\par \par \uc2 If they choose the lump sum, that is $783 million -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Happy choices ahead.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to an update on a CBS News investigation. As we have reported, at least 35,000 service members were kicked out of the military because of their sexual orientation, but just over 1,300 have had their discharge status changed since they don`t -- since the don`t ask/don`t tell policy was repealed in 2010.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  reports on a new lawsuit against the Pentagon that seeks to restore honor to thousands of veterans.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: This is 24-year-old you.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL FARRELL (Former U.S. Navy Service Member): Correct.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Do you remember what she was thinking?\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL FARRELL: I thought I was proud at that time. Putting on that uniform was everything.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD (voiceover): When Sherrill Farrell joined the Navy in 1985, she thought she had found a career.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : You wanted to do 20 years.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL FARRELL: That was my plan.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : You didn`t even do 10 months.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL FARRELL: No, sir. I wasn`t allowed to.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : She says a casual conversation about gay clubs with a bunkmate landed her in front of Naval investigators. Her military career was over a few months later.\par \par \uc2 She was stunned to read her DD 214, her discharge document.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL FARRELL: "Other than honorable due to homosexuality." It hurt, because my country is telling me I`m not good enough to serve because of who I love, not because of anything else, just because of who I love.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Farrell is part of a group of veterans now suing the Defense Department to get their discharges changed and gain access to benefits like VA loans, tuition assistance, and federal jobs.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN LARKIN (Attorney): This case is not about damages. This case is about simply changing that piece of paper.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Jocelyn Larkin is one of the lawyers representing Farrell and others in a class action lawsuit against the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN LARKIN: Every time they have to show that document, they are essentially outed involuntarily. I mean, imagine if it was on your driver`s license. It`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: The suit calls the existing process of trying to change a vet`s DD 214 inadequate, opaque, and often requires veterans to hire a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN LARKIN: It would take a lot less effort to change these documents than it did to hound them out and take away their dreams.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: As for Sherrill Farrell, she still has hope for an upgraded discharge and plans for when she gets one.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL FARRELL: Once they change my discharge, I`m going to frame both of those DD 214s and hang them up, because I want the country to know that I was willing to serve and die for my country, but you are telling me I am not able to serve because of who I love?\par \par \uc2 And you are not even willing to serve, but you get all of the benefits. How fair is that?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: A Pentagon spokesperson said the department does not comment on pending litigation, but, in an earlier statement, stood by their existing discharge upgrade process -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 The suspected Gilgo Beach serial killer was ordered by a Long Island judge today to turn in a DNA sample. The judge ruled there is probable cause to believe Rex Heuermann committed at least some of the Gilgo Beach murders. Heuermann`s lawyers had argued against submitting DNA.\par \par \uc2 Investigators used DNA found on a pizza crust outside Heuermann`s office to link him to one of the three victims he`s accused of killing.\par \par \uc2 There is an update on an American nurse and her daughter who were kidnapped in Haiti. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A nurse from New Hampshire and her daughter who were abducted in Haiti nearly two weeks ago have been freed.\par \par \uc2 Without offering details, the aid group the Alix Dorsainvil works for said they had been held captive in the city of Port-au-Prince. Dorsainvil and her daughter were abducted on the same day the State Department ordered the evacuation of some Americans due to the threat of kidnapping and other crimes.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we remember rock `n` roll legend Robbie Robertson. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Robbie Robertson`s performance)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A loss tonight in the world of classic rock.\par \par \uc2 Robbie Robertson, the driving force behind The Band, died today in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (Graphics onscreen: Robbie Robertson; 1943-2023)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Robbie Robertson`s performance of The Weight)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That is "The Weight," just one of the string of classics written by Robertson.\par \par \uc2 He and the band gained fame backing Bob Dylan in the 1960s. Robertson went on to critically acclaimed solo career, also producing contracts for his longtime friend director Martin Scorsese, who paid tribute today, saying Robertson`s effect on the art form was profound and lasting.\par \par \uc2 Robbie Robertson was 80.\par \par \uc2 After nearly 60 years, an American hero finally gets his due. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: One of the first black officers to serve in the Green Berets was honored today with the unveiling of his Medal of Honor plaque.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Catherine Herridge has for some time followed the remarkable story of Vietnam War veteran Retired Colonel Paris Davis, now receiving long-overdue recognition.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE (voiceover): When President Biden awarded the Medal of Honor to retired Colonel Paris Davis in March --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from March 3)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We honor a true hero of our nation.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: -- it righted a nearly six-decade-old-wrong for one of the first black officers in the elite Green Berets.\par \par \uc2 Since the White House ceremony, what has changed?\par \par \uc2 COL. PARIS DAVIS (RET.) (Medal of Honor Recipient): Everything.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Americans have taken the 84-year-old Davis into their hearts.\par \par \uc2 COL. PARIS DAVIS: I couldn`t go anywhere where someone didn`t recognize me or come over and say: "Thanks for your service."\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: They have sent handmade cards.\par \par \uc2 COL. PARIS DAVIS: Most of them were: "We love you. Congratulations."\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: He has even thrown out the first pitch at Nationals Park.\par \par \uc2 But some still ask why his Medal of Honor paperwork for rescuing two severely injured soldiers in Vietnam vanished twice at the height of the civil rights movement.\par \par \uc2 COL. PARIS DAVIS: People were really interested in finding out what the hell happened. And I would always say, that was then, this is now.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: And now another honor, his name engraved in the Medal of Honor Wall at the National Museum of the U.S. Army.\par \par \uc2 COL. PARIS DAVIS: Never forget who we are and what America stands for.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: As Davis secures his place in Special Forces history.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Herridge, CBS News, Fort Belvoir, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \